window.SUBJECT_DATA=window.SUBJECT_DATA||{};
window.SUBJECT_DATA["obgnnl"]=[{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The anatomy of the female reproductive tract is broadly classified into which three groups of organs?","options":["External genital, Internal genital, and Accessory organs","Primary, Secondary, and Tertiary organs","Superficial, Deep, and Pelvic organs","Reproductive, Excretory, and Endocrine organs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source flow-chart divides the female reproductive tract into External genital organs, Internal genital organs, and Accessory organs.  Why B wrong: Primary/secondary/tertiary is not the classification given in the notes.  Why C wrong: Superficial/deep/pelvic is not the source classification.  Why D wrong: These are unrelated functional systems, not the tract classification given."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Which feature best characterizes the external genital organs of the female?","options":["They can be seen with the naked eye","They cannot be seen without instruments","They lie entirely within the pelvic cavity","They function only during intercourse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state external genital organs \"Can be seen by naked eyes.\"  Why B wrong: That describes internal genital organs.  Why C wrong: External organs are on the surface, not deep in the pelvis.  Why D wrong: That describes accessory organs."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Why do internal genital organs require instruments to be examined?","options":["Because they can be seen with the naked eye","Because they cannot be seen with naked eyes and need some instruments to see them","Because they help only at the time of intercourse","Because they are covered by hair after puberty"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states internal genital organs \"Cannot be seen with naked eyes\" and \"Need some instruments to see them.\"  Why A wrong: That is true of external organs.  Why C wrong: That describes accessory organs.  Why D wrong: Hair covering after puberty refers to the mons pubis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"According to the notes, what is the primary role attributed to the accessory organs of the female reproductive tract?","options":["Help at the time of intercourse","Production of ova","Menstruation","Support of the uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states accessory organs \"Help at the time of intercourse.\"  Why B wrong: Ova production is an ovarian (internal organ) function.  Why C wrong: Menstruation relates to the uterus, an internal organ.  Why D wrong: Uterine support is not listed as the accessory-organ role."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Which structure is identified as the accessory organ of the female reproductive tract in the notes?","options":["Ovary","Breast","Vagina","Fallopian tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart lists \"Breast\" under accessory organs.  Why A wrong: Ovary is an internal genital organ.  Why C wrong: Vagina is an internal genital organ.  Why D wrong: Fallopian tube is an internal genital organ."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The external genital organ of the female is collectively called by which name?","options":["Vulva / Pudendum","Perineum","Vestibule","Introitus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the external genital organ in the female is \"called as VULVA/PUDENDUM.\"  Why B wrong: The perineum is a separate region, not the collective term for external genitalia.  Why C wrong: The vestibule is only one part within the vulva.  Why D wrong: Introitus refers specifically to the vaginal opening."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Which of the following are the internal genital organs having a role in reproduction, as listed in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fallopian tube","Uterus (lower part = cervix)","Vagina","Ovary","Mons pubis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed list of internal genital organs includes Fallopian tube, Uterus (lower part = cervix), Vagina, and Ovary.  Why E wrong: Mons pubis is part of the vulva (external genitalia), not an internal genital organ."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"In the female reproductive tract, the lower part of the uterus is known as which structure?","options":["Cervix","Fundus","Isthmus","Cornu"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Uterus (lower part = cervix).\"  Why B wrong: The fundus is the upper, rounded part of the uterus.  Why C wrong: The isthmus is a narrow region but the notes specifically name the lower part as the cervix.  Why D wrong: The cornu is the upper lateral horn where tubes enter."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the seven parts included in the vulva as listed in the notes?","options":["Mons pubis","Clitoris","Uterus","Bartholin glands"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The uterus is an internal genital organ, not a part of the vulva.  Why A wrong: Mons pubis is listed as part 1 of the vulva.  Why B wrong: Clitoris is listed as part 4 of the vulva.  Why D wrong: Bartholin glands are listed as part 7 of the vulva."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"How many parts are included in the vulva according to the notes?","options":["5","6","7","8"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The list enumerates 7 parts: Mons pubis, Labia majora, Labia minora, Clitoris, Vestibule (6 openings), Skene glands, and Bartholin glands.  Why A, B, D wrong: The source specifically lists 7 items."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The structure listed as \"VESTU BULE\" in the notes' vulval parts is noted to contain how many openings?","options":["4 openings","5 openings","6 openings","2 openings"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"VESTU BULE (6 openings)\" as part 5 of the vulva. (\"VESTU BULE\" is the source spelling of vestibule.)  Why A, B, D wrong: The source explicitly states 6 openings."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The vulva is lined by which type of epithelium that protects it from injury?","options":["Keratinised stratified squamous epithelium","Columnar epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the vulva is lined by keratinised (printed \"KERTANISED\") stratified squamous epithelium to protect the vulva from getting injured. The printed spelling \"KERTANISED\" is a typo for \"keratinised.\"  Why B wrong: Columnar epithelium lines Bartholin gland ducts, not the vulval surface.  Why C wrong: Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract.  Why D wrong: Simple cuboidal epithelium is found in glands/ducts, not the protective vulval lining."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"What is the purpose of the keratinised stratified squamous epithelium lining the vulva?","options":["To secrete alkaline mucus","To protect the vulva from getting injured","To produce ova","To allow menstrual blood to escape"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state the lining protects the vulva from getting injured.  Why A wrong: Alkaline mucus is produced by Bartholin glands.  Why C wrong: Ova production is an ovarian function.  Why D wrong: Menstrual escape occurs through the hymenal opening."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Which fold of the labia minora covers the clitoris from above?","options":["Prepuce","Frenulum","Fourchette","Mons pubis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state labia minora goes anteriorly and covers the clitoris from above as the PREPUCE.  Why B wrong: The frenulum covers the clitoris from below.  Why C wrong: The fourchette is where the labia minora meet posteriorly.  Why D wrong: The mons pubis lies above the pubic bone, separate from clitoral folds."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The fold that covers the clitoris from below is termed:","options":["Frenulum","Prepuce","Fourchette","Perineal raphe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the labia minora covers the clitoris from below as the FRENULUM.  Why B wrong: The prepuce covers the clitoris from above.  Why C wrong: The fourchette is the posterior meeting point of the labia minora.  Why D wrong: The perineal raphe is a separate midline ridge shown in the diagram."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"In the labelled vulval diagram, the area where the labia minora of the left and right sides meet posteriorly is called the:","options":["Fourchette","Anterior labial commissure","Vestibule","Prepuce"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state labia minora of left & right side meet posteriorly, and this area is called the FOURCHETTE (also shown labelled in the diagram).  Why B wrong: The anterior labial commissure is the anterior meeting point of the labia majora.  Why C wrong: The vestibule is the enclosed triangular area, not the posterior junction.  Why D wrong: The prepuce covers the clitoris from above."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"In the surface anatomy diagram of the vulva, which structure lies immediately anterior to the clitoris and above the pubic bone?","options":["Mons pubis","Perineal raphe","Posterior labial commissure","Anus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The labelled diagram shows the mons pubis at the top, above the prepuce of the clitoris.  Why B wrong: The perineal raphe is a posterior midline structure.  Why C wrong: The posterior labial commissure lies posteriorly.  Why D wrong: The anus is the most posterior labelled structure."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The mons pubis is also known by which alternative name?","options":["Mons veneris / Mon venerum","Fourchette","Introitus","Carunculae myrtiformis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the alternative name \"Mon veneries / Mon Venerum\" for the mons pubis.  Why B wrong: Fourchette is the posterior labial junction.  Why C wrong: Introitus is the vaginal opening.  Why D wrong: Carunculae myrtiformis are hymenal remnants after childbirth."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Where does the mons pubis lie in relation to the pubic bone?","options":["Directly above the pubic bone","Directly below the pubic bone","Lateral to the pubic bone","Posterior to the pubic bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the mons pubis \"Lies directly above pubic bone.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies its position as directly above the pubic bone."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The mons pubis is described as a fatty area because of its underlying:","options":["Adipose tissue","Muscle tissue","Glandular tissue","Cartilage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the mons pubis as a \"Fatty area (underlying adipose tissue).\"  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies adipose (fat) tissue, not muscle, gland, or cartilage."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"What change occurs to the mons pubis after puberty?","options":["It is covered by hair","It disappears","It becomes keratinised","It secretes alkaline mucus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that after puberty the mons pubis \"is covered by Hair.\"  Why B wrong: It does not disappear.  Why C wrong: Keratinisation refers to the vulval epithelial lining.  Why D wrong: Alkaline mucus is secreted by Bartholin glands."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"What is the length of the clitoris according to the notes?","options":["1.5 to 2 cms","2.5 to 3 cms","0.5 to 1 cm","3 to 4 cms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the clitoris is \"1.5 TO 2 cms in length\" (also labelled 1.5 to 2 cms in the diagram on page 5).  Why B, C, D wrong: The source explicitly states 1.5 to 2 cms."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The clitoris is described in the notes as which type of organ in females?","options":["Erectile organ","Secretory organ","Endocrine organ","Excretory organ"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the clitoris is the \"Erectile organ of females.\"  Why B wrong: Secretory function belongs to Bartholin/Skene glands.  Why C wrong: It is not described as endocrine.  Why D wrong: Excretion is a urinary function."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The vascularity of the clitoris is best described as:","options":["Highly vascular","Avascular","Poorly vascular","Vascular only after puberty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the clitoris is \"Highly vascular,\" consistent with its role as an erectile organ.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source states it is highly vascular, without qualification."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The clitoris in the female is homologous to which structure in the male?","options":["Penis","Scrotum","Bulbourethral (Cowper) gland","Penile urethra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The homologous table states the female clitoris corresponds to the male penis.  Why B wrong: The scrotum is homologous to the labia majora.  Why C wrong: The bulbourethral gland is homologous to the Bartholin gland.  Why D wrong: The penile urethra is homologous to the labia minora."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The clitoris and the penis both develop from which common embryonic origin?","options":["Genital tubercle","Genital swelling","Genital folds","Urogenital sinus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The homologous table lists the origin of both clitoris and penis as the \"GENITAL TUBERCLE.\"  Why B wrong: Genital swelling is the origin of the labia majora / scrotum.  Why C wrong: Genital folds are the origin of the labia minora / penile urethra.  Why D wrong: The urogenital sinus is not the origin named for the clitoris."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The labia majora are also known as the:","options":["Outer lips","Inner lips","Vestibular folds","Nymphae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe labia majora as the \"Outer lips.\"  Why B wrong: Inner lips refers to the labia minora.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the terms used for labia majora in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Which of the following structures are contained within the labia majora according to the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fats","Hair follicles","Sebaceous glands","Sweat glands (apocrine glands)","Bartholin glands"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the labia majora as having fats, hair follicles, sebaceous glands, and sweat glands (apocrine glands).  Why E wrong: Bartholin glands are a separate vulval structure, not listed as a component contained within the labia majora."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The sweat glands present in the labia majora are specifically called:","options":["Apocrine glands","Eccrine glands","Merocrine glands","Holocrine glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the sweat glands of the labia majora are \"called APOCRINE gland.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifically names them apocrine glands."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The labia majora in the female are homologous to which structure in the male?","options":["Scrotum","Penis","Penile urethra","Bulbourethral gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The homologous table lists labia majora ↔ scrotum.  Why B wrong: The penis is homologous to the clitoris.  Why C wrong: The penile urethra is homologous to the labia minora.  Why D wrong: The bulbourethral gland is homologous to the Bartholin gland."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The labia majora and the scrotum both develop from which embryonic origin?","options":["Genital swelling","Genital tubercle","Genital folds","Müllerian duct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The homologous table lists the origin of labia majora / scrotum as \"Genital swelling.\"  Why B wrong: Genital tubercle gives rise to the clitoris / penis.  Why C wrong: Genital folds give rise to the labia minora / penile urethra.  Why D wrong: The Müllerian duct is not the origin named here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The labia minora are also referred to as the:","options":["Inner lips","Outer lips","Greater lips","Vestibular bulbs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe labia minora as the \"Inner lips.\"  Why B wrong: Outer lips refers to labia majora.  Why C, D wrong: These are not terms used in the notes for labia minora."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Why are the labia minora not visible in young females?","options":["They are covered by the labia majora","They are covered by the hymen","They are covered by the prepuce","They have not yet developed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in young females the labia minora are not visible as they are covered by the labia majora.  Why B, C wrong: They are not described as being covered by hymen or prepuce.  Why D wrong: They exist but are simply covered, not undeveloped."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"In which group of females are the labia minora seen properly (i.e., become clearly visible)?","options":["Multiparous females","Prepubertal females","Nulliparous females","Postmenopausal females"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that \"In multiparous females, [labia minora] are seen properly.\"  Why B wrong: In young/prepubertal females they are covered by the labia majora.  Why C wrong: Nulliparous is not the group specified.  Why D wrong: Postmenopausal is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"A comparison table in the notes lists which features as ABSENT in the labia minora but PRESENT in the labia majora? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hair","Fat","Sweat glands","Apocrine glands","Sebaceous glands"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The comparison table states labia minora have hair absent, fat absent, sweat glands absent, and apocrine glands absent, whereas labia majora have all of these present.  Why E wrong: Sebaceous glands are present in BOTH labia minora and labia majora, so they are not a distinguishing absent feature."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"According to the labia minora–labia majora comparison, which structure is present in BOTH?","options":["Sebaceous glands","Hair","Apocrine glands","Fat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Sebaceous glands present in Both.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: Hair, apocrine glands, and fat are present in labia majora but absent in labia minora."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The labia minora in the female are homologous to which structure in the male?","options":["Penile urethra","Scrotum","Penis","Prostate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The homologous table lists labia minora ↔ penile urethra.  Why B wrong: The scrotum is homologous to the labia majora.  Why C wrong: The penis is homologous to the clitoris.  Why D wrong: The prostate is homologous to the Skene (paraurethral) glands."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The labia minora and the penile urethra both arise from which embryonic origin?","options":["Genital folds","Genital swelling","Genital tubercle","Urogenital ridge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The homologous table lists the origin of labia minora / penile urethra as \"Genital folds.\"  Why B wrong: Genital swelling gives rise to labia majora / scrotum.  Why C wrong: Genital tubercle gives rise to the clitoris / penis.  Why D wrong: Urogenital ridge is not the origin named here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The paraurethral glands, also known as Skene glands, are homologous to which male structure?","options":["Prostate gland","Scrotum","Bulbourethral gland","Penis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Paraurethral glands/skene glands: Homologous to PROSTATE glands of males.\"  Why B wrong: The scrotum is homologous to the labia majora.  Why C wrong: The bulbourethral gland is homologous to the Bartholin gland.  Why D wrong: The penis is homologous to the clitoris."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The vaginal opening (introitus) is covered by a thin membrane called the:","options":["Hymen","Prepuce","Fourchette","Frenulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the vaginal opening/introitus is covered by a thin membrane called the HYMEN.  Why B wrong: The prepuce covers the clitoris from above.  Why C wrong: The fourchette is the posterior labial junction.  Why D wrong: The frenulum covers the clitoris from below."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The normal opening in the hymen serves what physiological function?","options":["Allows menstrual blood to come out every month","Produces alkaline mucus","Allows urine to pass","Secretes lubricating fluid during intercourse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the hymen has an opening through which menstrual blood comes out every month.  Why B wrong: Alkaline mucus is produced by Bartholin glands.  Why C wrong: Urine passes through the urethral opening.  Why D wrong: Lubrication is a Bartholin gland function."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"A hymen that has no opening is termed:","options":["Imperforate hymen","Cribriform hymen","Septate hymen","Annular hymen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if there is no opening in the hymen, it is called an IMPERFORATE HYMEN.  Why B, C, D wrong: These other hymen variants are not the term the source uses for an absent opening."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"In a female with an imperforate hymen, menstrual blood cannot escape, leading to a condition called:","options":["Cryptomenorrhea (concealed menses)","Menorrhagia","Amenorrhea","Dysmenorrhea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that with an imperforate hymen, menstrual blood cannot come out, which is called CRYPTOMENORRHEA (concealed menses).  Why B wrong: Menorrhagia is heavy menstrual bleeding, not concealed menses.  Why C wrong: In cryptomenorrhea menses actually occur but are concealed, unlike true amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Dysmenorrhea is painful menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The term \"cryptomenorrhea\" as used in the notes specifically means:","options":["Concealed menses","Absent menses","Painful menses","Frequent menses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define cryptomenorrhea as \"(concealed menses).\"  Why B, C, D wrong: The source parenthetically defines it as concealed menses, not absent, painful, or frequent."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"During which event is the hymen typically ruptured, according to the notes?","options":["At the time of intercourse","During menstruation","During urination","At puberty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that at the time of intercourse the hymen is ruptured.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source attributes hymenal rupture to intercourse (and laceration to childbirth), not to these events."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"After childbirth, the lacerated hymen is replaced by remnants known as:","options":["Carunculae myrtiformis","Nabothian cysts","Bartholin cysts","Skene remnants"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that during childbirth the hymen is lacerated and later replaced by CARUNCULAE MYRTIFORMIS (source spelling \"MYRITIFORMIS\").  Why B wrong: Nabothian cysts occur on the cervix.  Why C wrong: Bartholin cysts involve the Bartholin gland.  Why D wrong: \"Skene remnants\" is not a recognized term for hymenal remnants."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"What is the size of the Bartholin glands according to the notes?","options":["Pea sized","Almond sized","Walnut sized","Bean sized"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Bartholin gland size is \"Pea sized glands.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies pea-sized."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"How many Bartholin glands are present?","options":["2","1","4","6"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the number of Bartholin glands is 2.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies 2."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The Bartholin gland is lined by which type of epithelium?","options":["Columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Ciliated epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes' gland diagram states \"Lining = Columnar epithelium.\"  Why B wrong: Keratinised stratified squamous epithelium lines the vulval surface.  Why C, D wrong: These epithelia are not the lining described for the Bartholin gland."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"When does the Bartholin gland perform its main function?","options":["At the time of intercourse","During menstruation","During childbirth","At puberty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the Bartholin gland function occurs \"At time of Intercourse.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies intercourse as the time of function."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"What type of secretion does the Bartholin gland produce at the time of intercourse?","options":["Alkaline mucus","Acidic mucus","Serous fluid","Sebum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the Bartholin gland \"Produces Alkaline Mucus.\"  Why B wrong: The secretion is alkaline, not acidic.  Why C, D wrong: The source specifies alkaline mucus, not serous fluid or sebum."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The alkaline mucus produced by the Bartholin gland serves what function?","options":["Helps in lubrication","Neutralises menstrual blood","Produces ova","Protects against injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes flowchart shows alkaline mucus \"Helps in Lubrication.\"  Why B, C wrong: These functions are not attributed to Bartholin secretion.  Why D wrong: Protection from injury is the role of the keratinised vulval epithelium."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The Bartholin gland is also known by which alternative name?","options":["Greater vestibular gland","Lesser vestibular gland","Paraurethral gland","Prostate gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The homologous table names it \"Bartholin gland/Greater vestibular gland.\"  Why B wrong: There is no such \"lesser vestibular gland\" reference in these notes.  Why C wrong: Paraurethral (Skene) glands are separate.  Why D wrong: The prostate is the male homolog of the Skene glands."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The Bartholin gland (greater vestibular gland) in the female is homologous to which male gland?","options":["Bulbourethral (Cowper) gland","Prostate gland","Seminal vesicle","Testis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The homologous table lists Bartholin/greater vestibular gland ↔ Bulbourethral gland (Cowper gland).  Why B wrong: The prostate is homologous to the Skene (paraurethral) glands.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the homologs named in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The vestibule is described as which shape of area?","options":["Triangular area","Circular area","Oval area","Rectangular area"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the vestibule as a \"Triangular area.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies triangular."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The vestibule (triangular area) is bounded anteriorly by which structure?","options":["Clitoris","Fourchette","Labia minora","Anus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the vestibule is bounded anteriorly by the CLITORIS.  Why B wrong: The fourchette bounds it posteriorly.  Why C wrong: The labia minora bound its sides.  Why D wrong: The anus is not a boundary of the vestibule."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The sides (lateral boundaries) of the vestibule are formed by the:","options":["Labia minora","Labia majora","Clitoris","Mons pubis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the sides of the vestibule are bounded by the labia minora.  Why B wrong: The labia majora are the outer lips, not the vestibular boundaries stated.  Why C wrong: The clitoris bounds it anteriorly.  Why D wrong: The mons pubis is not a boundary of the vestibule."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The vestibule is bounded posteriorly by the:","options":["Fourchette","Clitoris","Labia minora","Urethral opening"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the vestibule is bounded posteriorly by the FOUR CHETTE (fourchette).  Why B wrong: The clitoris bounds it anteriorly.  Why C wrong: The labia minora bound its sides.  Why D wrong: The urethral opening is one of the openings within the vestibule, not its boundary."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"How many openings does the vestibule have?","options":["6","4","2","5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the vestibule \"has 6 openings.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies 6 openings."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"In the numbering of the six vestibular openings, opening number 1 corresponds to the:","options":["Urethral opening","Vaginal opening","Bartholin gland opening","Paraurethral gland opening"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes number opening 1 as the urethral opening.  Why B wrong: The vaginal opening is number 4.  Why C wrong: Bartholin gland openings are 5 and 6.  Why D wrong: Paraurethral gland openings are 2 and 3."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Openings numbered 2 and 3 in the vestibule correspond to which glands?","options":["Paraurethral glands","Bartholin glands","Sebaceous glands","Apocrine glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label openings 2 and 3 as the paraurethral gland openings.  Why B wrong: Bartholin gland openings are numbered 5 and 6.  Why C, D wrong: Sebaceous and apocrine glands are not vestibular openings."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The largest of the paraurethral glands is known as the:","options":["Skene gland","Bartholin gland","Cowper gland","Sebaceous gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that the largest paraurethral gland is the SKENE gland.  Why B wrong: The Bartholin gland is the greater vestibular gland, a separate structure.  Why C wrong: The Cowper gland is a male structure.  Why D wrong: Sebaceous glands are not paraurethral glands."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Vestibular opening number 4 corresponds to the:","options":["Vaginal opening","Urethral opening","Bartholin gland opening","Paraurethral gland opening"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label opening 4 as the vaginal opening.  Why B wrong: The urethral opening is number 1.  Why C wrong: Bartholin openings are 5 and 6.  Why D wrong: Paraurethral openings are 2 and 3."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"The vaginal opening (opening number 4) is specifically called the:","options":["Introitus","Fourchette","Fornix","Os"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the vaginal opening is \"called as INTROITUS.\"  Why B wrong: The fourchette is the posterior labial junction.  Why C wrong: The fornix is the recess around the cervix inside the vagina.  Why D wrong: The os refers to the cervical opening."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Vestibular openings numbered 5 and 6 correspond to the openings of which glands?","options":["Bartholin glands","Paraurethral glands","Sebaceous glands","Skene glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label openings 5 and 6 as the openings of the Bartholin glands.  Why B, D wrong: Paraurethral (Skene) gland openings are numbered 2 and 3.  Why C wrong: Sebaceous glands do not open into the vestibule as numbered openings."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"Where does the Bartholin duct open, according to the notes?","options":["In the vestibule, in the groove between the vaginal opening (introitus) and labia minora, at the junction of the anterior 2/3rd and posterior 1/3rd","Directly onto the mons pubis","Into the urethra","At the anterior commissure of the labia majora"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the Bartholin duct opens in the vestibule in the groove between the vaginal opening (introitus) and labia minora at the junction of the anterior 2/3rd and posterior 1/3rd.  Why B, C, D wrong: These locations contradict the specific site described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"In the labelled photographic vulval diagram (page 2), which structure is labelled between the clitoris (above) and the vestibule (below) on the medial side?","options":["External urethral orifice","Perineal raphe","Posterior labial commissure","Anus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The labelled diagram identifies the external urethral orifice just below the clitoris and above the vestibule.  Why B wrong: The perineal raphe is a posterior midline structure.  Why C wrong: The posterior labial commissure lies posteriorly.  Why D wrong: The anus is the most posterior labelled structure."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"In the surface vulval diagram, the midline ridge labelled between the posterior labial commissure and the anus is the:","options":["Perineal raphe","Fourchette","Frenulum","Introitus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The labelled diagram identifies the perineal raphe in the posterior midline near the anus.  Why B wrong: The fourchette is at the posterior junction of the labia minora, more anterior.  Why C wrong: The frenulum is near the clitoris.  Why D wrong: The introitus is the vaginal opening."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"In the labelled surface diagram of the vulva, the opening of the greater vestibular glands corresponds to which named glands?","options":["Bartholin's glands","Skene glands","Sebaceous glands","Paraurethral glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the \"Opening of greater vestibular (Bartholin's) glands,\" identifying the greater vestibular glands as the Bartholin's glands.  Why B, D wrong: Skene/paraurethral glands are a different set of glands.  Why C wrong: Sebaceous glands are not the greater vestibular glands."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Anatomy of female reproductive tract, vulva and its parts, mons pubis…","question":"In the labelled surface vulval diagram, the anterior meeting point of the labia is called the:","options":["Anterior labial commissure","Posterior labial commissure","Fourchette","Perineal raphe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the anterior meeting region as the \"Anterior labial commissure.\"  Why B wrong: The posterior labial commissure is the posterior meeting point.  Why C wrong: The fourchette is the posterior junction of the labia minora.  Why D wrong: The perineal raphe is a posterior midline ridge."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"A Bartholin cyst forms as a direct result of which of the following events?","options":["Blockage of the duct of the Bartholin gland","Overgrowth of the clitoral tissue","Prolapse of the uterus into the vagina","Atrophy of the labia majora"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if the duct of the Bartholin gland gets blocked, it forms a Bartholin cyst.  Why B wrong: Clitoral overgrowth is unrelated to Bartholin cyst formation.  Why C wrong: Uterine prolapse is a pelvic floor problem, not a cause of Bartholin cyst.  Why D wrong: Labial atrophy does not cause duct blockage or cyst formation."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Which term best describes the clinical character of a Bartholin gland cyst as noted in the text?","options":["Painless","Painful","Pulsatile","Non-tender and mobile only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Painful\" as a feature of the Bartholin cyst.  Why A wrong: The notes specifically state it is painful, not painless.  Why C wrong: It is a fluid-filled cyst, not a pulsatile (vascular) swelling.  Why D wrong: The text emphasizes pain, not a purely non-tender mobile lump."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"According to the notes, a Bartholin gland cyst is typically located on the:","options":["Labia majora","Labia minora","Mons pubis","Clitoral hood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the location of the Bartholin cyst is on the labia minora.  Why A wrong: The notes specify labia minora, not labia majora.  Why C wrong: The mons pubis is not the noted site.  Why D wrong: The clitoral hood is not the noted site."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"What is the most common cause of a Bartholin gland cyst/abscess according to the notes?","options":["Candida albicans","Infection with E. coli","Trichomonas vaginalis","Herpes simplex virus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give \"Infection (E. coli)\" as the most common cause.  Why A wrong: Candida is not listed as the most common cause.  Why C wrong: Trichomonas is not listed as the cause.  Why D wrong: HSV is not listed as the cause."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The surgical management of choice for a Bartholin cyst as given in the notes is:","options":["Total hysterectomy","Marsupialisation","Simple aspiration only","Vulvectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state management is MARSUPILISATION (marsupialisation) — creating a permanent open pouch for drainage.  Why A wrong: Hysterectomy is unrelated to a vulvar gland cyst.  Why C wrong: Aspiration alone is not the management given.  Why D wrong: Vulvectomy is far too radical and not the noted management."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The blood supply of the vulva is derived mainly from which artery?","options":["Internal pudendal artery","Uterine artery","Ovarian artery","Inferior mesenteric artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the blood supply of the vulva is the internal pudendal artery.  Why B wrong: The uterine artery supplies the uterus, not the vulva primarily.  Why C wrong: The ovarian artery supplies the ovaries.  Why D wrong: The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut, not the vulva."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The nerve supply of the vulva is provided by the:","options":["Obturator nerve","Pudendal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the nerve supply of the vulva is the pudendal nerve.  Why A wrong: The obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh.  Why C wrong: The femoral nerve supplies the anterior thigh.  Why D wrong: The sciatic nerve supplies the posterior thigh/leg, not the vulva."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Lymphatic drainage of the vulva is mainly to which group of lymph nodes?","options":["Inguinal lymph nodes (superficial + deep inguinal)","Para-aortic lymph nodes only","Axillary lymph nodes","Internal iliac nodes only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state lymphatic drain is to the inguinal lymph nodes (superficial + deep inguinal lymph nodes).  Why B wrong: Para-aortic nodes are not the primary vulval drainage.  Why C wrong: Axillary nodes drain the breast/upper limb.  Why D wrong: The notes specify inguinal (superficial + deep), not internal iliac alone."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The vulval lymphatic drainage involves which components of the inguinal nodes?","options":["Only superficial inguinal nodes","Only deep inguinal nodes","Both superficial and deep inguinal lymph nodes","Neither; it drains to pelvic nodes only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"Superficial + Deep inguinal lymph nodes.\"  Why A wrong: Deep inguinal nodes are also involved.  Why B wrong: Superficial inguinal nodes are also involved.  Why D wrong: The text names inguinal nodes, not pelvic-only drainage."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"(Extra Edge) The lymphatic drainage of the clitoris is to which node?","options":["Node of Rosenmuller / lymph node of Cloquet","Obturator node","Sacral node","Superior mesenteric node"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Extra Edge box states clitoris lymphatic drainage goes to the node of Rosenmuller, also called the lymph node of Cloquet.  Why B wrong: The obturator node is not the specified drainage of the clitoris.  Why C wrong: The sacral node is not named for clitoral drainage.  Why D wrong: The superior mesenteric node drains the gut."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"\"Node of Rosenmuller\" and \"lymph node of Cloquet\" are two names for the node draining which structure?","options":["Cervix","Clitoris","Fallopian tube","Ovary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Extra Edge box links both names to lymphatic drainage of the clitoris.  Why A wrong: The cervix is not the referenced structure.  Why C wrong: The fallopian tube is not the referenced structure.  Why D wrong: The ovary is not the referenced structure."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The chapter on page 7 is titled Anatomy Part-2 and specifically deals with which region?","options":["Perineum","Uterus","Placenta","Ovary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The heading reads \"ANATOMY (PART-2) (PERINEUM).\"  Why B wrong: The uterus is not the titled subject.  Why C wrong: The placenta is not the titled subject.  Why D wrong: The ovary is not the titled subject."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The pelvic bone is made up of which of the following components?","options":["2 hip bones (innominate bones), sacrum, and coccyx","2 femurs and the sacrum","Sacrum and coccyx only","4 hip bones and the ilium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the pelvic bone comprises 2 hip bones (innominate bones), sacrum, and coccyx (tail bone).  Why B wrong: The femurs are limb bones, not part of the pelvic bone.  Why C wrong: This omits the two hip bones.  Why D wrong: There are 2 hip bones, and the ilium is a part of the hip bone, not separate."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The hip bones are also known by which name?","options":["Innominate bones","Sesamoid bones","Wormian bones","Pneumatic bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the 2 hip bones as innominate bones.  Why B wrong: Sesamoid bones are embedded within tendons (e.g., patella).  Why C wrong: Wormian bones are accessory skull sutural bones.  Why D wrong: Pneumatic bones contain air spaces (e.g., some skull bones)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Which bone is referred to as the \"tail bone\" in the pelvic bone description?","options":["Sacrum","Coccyx","Ilium","Ischium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the coccyx as the tail bone.  Why A wrong: The sacrum is separate and not called the tail bone.  Why C wrong: The ilium is a part of the hip bone.  Why D wrong: The ischium is a part of the hip bone."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The hip bone is composed of how many parts, and what are they?","options":["2 parts: pubis and ilium","3 parts: pubis, ischium, and ilium","4 parts: pubis, ischium, ilium, sacrum","3 parts: sacrum, coccyx, and ilium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Hip Bone: 3 Parts\" — Pubis, Ischium, Ilium.  Why A wrong: It has 3 parts, and the ischium is omitted here.  Why C wrong: The sacrum is not a part of the hip bone.  Why D wrong: The sacrum and coccyx are separate bones, not parts of the hip bone."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Which part of the hip bone contributes to the pubic symphysis?","options":["Ilium","Ischium","Pubis","Sacrum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes link the pubis to the pubic symphysis.  Why A wrong: The ilium is associated with the iliac crest, not the symphysis.  Why B wrong: The ischium bears the ischial tuberosity.  Why D wrong: The sacrum is a separate bone."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The ischial tuberosity is a bony landmark that is part of which portion of the hip bone?","options":["Pubis","Ischium","Ilium","Coccyx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes connect the ischium to the ischial tuberosity.  Why A wrong: The pubis contributes to the pubic symphysis.  Why C wrong: The ilium is a separate part of the hip bone.  Why D wrong: The coccyx is a separate bone, not part of the hip bone."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The cavity that lies between the pelvic bones is called the pelvic cavity and it contains:","options":["External genital organs","Female internal genital organs","The urinary bladder only","The rectum only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the pelvic cavity lies between the pelvic bones and contains the female internal genital organs.  Why A wrong: External genital organs are in the perineum, not the pelvic cavity.  Why C wrong: It contains the internal genital organs, not only the bladder.  Why D wrong: It contains the internal genital organs, not only the rectum."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The region that lies outside the pelvic bone and contains the external genital organ is called the:","options":["Perineum","Pelvic cavity","Peritoneum","Pouch of Douglas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cavity outside the pelvic bone is the perineum, containing the external genital organ.  Why B wrong: The pelvic cavity lies between (inside) the pelvic bones.  Why C wrong: The peritoneum is a serous membrane, not this region.  Why D wrong: The pouch of Douglas is the rectouterine pouch, not the perineum."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The pelvic cavity is separated from the perineum by which structure?","options":["Pelvic floor","Peritoneum","Broad ligament","Pubic symphysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the pelvic cavity is separated from the perineum by the pelvic floor.  Why B wrong: The peritoneum is not the separating structure described here.  Why C wrong: The broad ligament supports the uterus but does not separate these regions.  Why D wrong: The pubic symphysis is a bony joint, not the separating floor."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"What is the anterior boundary of the anatomical perineum?","options":["Coccyx","Pubic symphysis","Ischial tuberosity","Sacrum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the anterior boundary of the anatomical perineum as the pubic symphysis.  Why A wrong: The coccyx is the posterior boundary.  Why C wrong: The ischial tuberosity is the lateral boundary.  Why D wrong: The sacrum is not listed as the anterior boundary."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The lateral boundary of the anatomical perineum is formed by the:","options":["Pubic symphysis","Ischial tuberosity","Coccyx","Ilium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the lateral boundary as the ischial tuberosity.  Why A wrong: The pubic symphysis is the anterior boundary.  Why C wrong: The coccyx is the posterior boundary.  Why D wrong: The ilium is not listed as a boundary of the perineum."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The posterior boundary of the anatomical perineum is the:","options":["Coccyx","Pubic symphysis","Ischial tuberosity","Sacrum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the posterior boundary as the coccyx.  Why B wrong: The pubic symphysis is the anterior boundary.  Why C wrong: The ischial tuberosity is the lateral boundary.  Why D wrong: The sacrum is not the listed posterior boundary (coccyx is)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"What is the shape of the anatomical perineum?","options":["Triangular","Diamond shaped","Circular","Square shaped"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the shape of the anatomical perineum is diamond shaped.  Why A wrong: It is diamond shaped, though it is divided into two triangles.  Why C wrong: It is not circular.  Why D wrong: It is diamond (rhomboid), not square."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The anatomical perineum is divided into how many triangles, and which ones?","options":["2 — urogenital triangle and anal triangle","2 — femoral triangle and anal triangle","3 — urogenital, anal, and vesical triangles","1 — urogenital triangle only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the perineum has 2 triangles: urogenital triangle and anal triangle.  Why B wrong: The femoral triangle is in the thigh, not the perineum.  Why C wrong: There are 2 triangles, not 3.  Why D wrong: There are 2 triangles, including the anal triangle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"In the diagram of the parts of the anatomical perineum, the two named triangular regions are the urogenital triangle and the:","options":["Anal triangle","Pubic triangle","Sacral triangle","Iliac triangle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Page 8 labels the two parts of the anatomical perineum as the urogenital triangle and the anal triangle.  Why B wrong: There is no \"pubic triangle\" named.  Why C wrong: There is no \"sacral triangle\" named.  Why D wrong: There is no \"iliac triangle\" named."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The three corners (angles) that mark the boundaries of the anatomical perineum diamond are the pubic symphysis, coccyx, and the paired:","options":["Ischial tuberosities","Iliac crests","Greater trochanters","Anterior superior iliac spines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Page 8's \"Anatomical perineum boundaries\" diagram marks pubic symphysis (top), ischial tuberosities (sides), and coccyx (bottom).  Why B wrong: The iliac crests are not the marked boundary points.  Why C wrong: The greater trochanters are femoral landmarks, not perineal boundaries.  Why D wrong: The ASIS are not the marked boundary points here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"In the hand-drawn perineum diagram (page 8), which triangle is shown anteriorly (toward the pubic symphysis)?","options":["Urogenital triangle","Anal triangle","Femoral triangle","Lumbar triangle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The labeled drawing places the urogenital triangle toward the pubic symphysis (anterior) and the anal triangle toward the coccyx (posterior).  Why B wrong: The anal triangle is posterior, toward the coccyx.  Why C wrong: The femoral triangle is a thigh structure.  Why D wrong: The lumbar triangle is in the posterior abdominal wall."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The obstetrical perineum is defined as the part of the anatomical perineum that lies between which two structures?","options":["Vaginal opening and anal opening","Urethral opening and vaginal opening","Pubic symphysis and coccyx","Clitoris and urethral opening"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define the obstetrical perineum as the part of the anatomical perineum lying between the vaginal opening and the anal opening.  Why B wrong: It lies between the vaginal and anal openings, not urethra and vagina.  Why C wrong: Those are boundaries of the anatomical perineum, not the obstetrical perineum.  Why D wrong: The clitoris–urethra region is not the obstetrical perineum."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"What is the approximate size of the obstetrical perineum given in the notes?","options":["2 × 2 cms","4 × 4 cms","6 × 6 cms","4 × 8 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the size is 4 × 4 cms.  Why A wrong: The stated size is 4 × 4 cms, not 2 × 2.  Why C wrong: The stated size is 4 × 4 cms, not 6 × 6.  Why D wrong: The stated size is 4 × 4 cms, not 4 × 8."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The obstetrical perineum is also known by which other name?","options":["Perineal body","Perineal membrane","Pelvic diaphragm","Urogenital diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the other name of the obstetrical perineum as the perineal body.  Why B wrong: The perineal membrane is a distinct structure, not the given synonym.  Why C wrong: The pelvic diaphragm is a muscular floor, not this synonym.  Why D wrong: The urogenital diaphragm is a separate structure."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"During delivery of the fetal head, perineal support is given at which site?","options":["The obstetrical perineum (perineal body)","The pubic symphysis","The sacrum","The mons pubis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list, under significance, that perineal support during delivery of the fetal head is given in this (obstetrical perineum) area.  Why B wrong: Support is given at the perineal body, not the pubic symphysis.  Why C wrong: The sacrum is not the site of perineal support.  Why D wrong: The mons pubis is not the site of perineal support."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"A \"perineal tear\" refers to a tear occurring in which region?","options":["The obstetrical perineum area","The cervix","The lower uterine segment","The broad ligament"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a perineal tear means a tear in this (obstetrical perineum) area.  Why B wrong: A cervical tear is a different injury.  Why C wrong: A tear of the lower uterine segment is uterine rupture, not a perineal tear.  Why D wrong: The broad ligament is not the perineal region."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Episiotomy is best defined by the notes as a:","options":["Spontaneous perineal laceration","Surgically planned incision given in the perineal area","Repair of a vesicovaginal fistula","Removal of the Bartholin gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define episiotomy as a surgically planned incision given in this (perineal) area.  Why A wrong: An episiotomy is planned/surgical, not a spontaneous tear.  Why C wrong: Fistula repair is unrelated to the definition of episiotomy.  Why D wrong: Gland removal is not episiotomy."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The role of the pelvic floor / pelvic diaphragm is to:","options":["Separate the pelvic cavity from the perineum","Support the fetal head during crowning only","Form the boundary of the abdominal cavity","Connect the two ovaries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the role of the pelvic floor is to separate the pelvic cavity from the perineum.  Why B wrong: Supporting the fetal head is a function of the obstetrical perineum, not the defined role of the pelvic floor here.  Why C wrong: The pelvic floor is not the boundary of the abdominal cavity (the diaphragm/thorax boundary is different).  Why D wrong: It does not connect the ovaries."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The pelvic floor / pelvic diaphragm is formed by how many muscles?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the pelvic floor/pelvic diaphragm is formed by 2 muscles (levator ani and ischiococcygeus/coccygeus).  Why A wrong: It is formed by 2 muscles, not 1.  Why C wrong: It is formed by 2 muscles, not 3.  Why D wrong: It is formed by 2 muscles, not 4."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Which two muscles form the pelvic floor / pelvic diaphragm?","options":["Levator ani and ischiococcygeus (coccygeus)","Bulbospongiosus and coccygeus","Levator ani and external anal sphincter","Obturator internus and piriformis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the two muscles are the levator ani muscle and the ischiococcygeus/coccygeus muscle.  Why B wrong: Bulbospongiosus is a perineal muscle, not one of the two pelvic diaphragm muscles.  Why C wrong: The external anal sphincter is a perineal muscle, not a pelvic diaphragm component here.  Why D wrong: Obturator internus and piriformis are lateral pelvic wall muscles."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Which muscle is described as the \"main\" muscle of the pelvic floor?","options":["Levator ani","Coccygeus","Bulbospongiosus","Deep transverse perinei"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes mark the levator ani muscle as the \"Main\" muscle of the pelvic floor.  Why B wrong: The coccygeus is the accessory muscle, not the main one.  Why C wrong: Bulbospongiosus is a perineal muscle.  Why D wrong: Deep transverse perinei is a perineal muscle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The levator ani is described as a fan-shaped muscle that hangs like a:","options":["Hammock","Sling of tendon","Dome","Ring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the levator ani as a fan-shaped muscle which hangs like a hammock.  Why B wrong: It is likened to a hammock, not a tendon sling.  Why C wrong: It is described as hammock-like, not dome-shaped.  Why D wrong: It is not described as ring-shaped."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Regarding the left and right levator ani muscles, the notes state that they:","options":["Meet in the center","Never meet","Attach only to the sacrum","Fuse with the coccygeus laterally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the left and right side levator ani muscles meet in the center.  Why B wrong: They do meet in the center.  Why C wrong: The described feature is that they meet centrally, not attach only to the sacrum.  Why D wrong: The notes describe them meeting each other in the center, not fusing with coccygeus."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The levator ani muscle is thickest and thinnest at which locations, respectively?","options":["Thickest on the sides, thin in the center","Thickest in the center, thin on the sides","Uniformly thick throughout","Thickest anteriorly, thin posteriorly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state this muscle is thickest on the sides and thin in the center.  Why B wrong: This reverses the correct description.  Why C wrong: It is not uniform; it varies from sides to center.  Why D wrong: The notes describe sides vs center, not anterior vs posterior."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"What are the two parts of the levator ani muscle?","options":["Pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus","Bulbospongiosus and ischiocavernosus","Coccygeus and piriformis","Superficial and deep transverse perinei"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the parts of levator ani as pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus.  Why B wrong: These are perineal muscles, not parts of the levator ani.  Why C wrong: Coccygeus is a separate pelvic floor muscle; piriformis is a pelvic wall muscle.  Why D wrong: The transverse perinei muscles are perineal muscles, not levator ani parts."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"What is the main role of the levator ani muscle?","options":["It is the main support of the uterus and prevents prolapse","It supplies blood to the vulva","It forms the pubic symphysis","It produces amniotic fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the role of levator ani is the main support of the uterus (it prevents prolapse).  Why B wrong: Blood supply of the vulva is via the internal pudendal artery, unrelated to levator ani's role.  Why C wrong: The pubic symphysis is a bony joint, not formed by muscle.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid is produced by fetal/placental sources, not this muscle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Which statement about the levator ani muscle is TRUE according to the notes?","options":["It forms a part of the perineal body and is cut during episiotomy","It does not form part of the perineal body","It is never cut during episiotomy","It does not support the uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the levator ani forms a part of the perineal body and is cut during episiotomy.  Why B wrong: It does form part of the perineal body.  Why C wrong: It is cut during episiotomy.  Why D wrong: It is the main support of the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Which of the following is TRUE about the ischiococcygeus / coccygeus muscle?","options":["It does not support the uterus, does not form part of the perineal body, and is not cut during episiotomy","It is the main support of the uterus","It forms the perineal body","It is routinely cut during episiotomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the ischiococcygeus/coccygeus does not support the uterus, does not form part of the perineal body, and is not cut during episiotomy.  Why B wrong: The levator ani, not coccygeus, is the main uterine support.  Why C wrong: The coccygeus does not form the perineal body.  Why D wrong: The coccygeus is not cut during episiotomy."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The muscles that form the perineum include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Bulbospongiosus","Superficial transverse perinei","Deep transverse perinei","Ischiococcygeus (coccygeus)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The perineal muscle list is bulbospongiosus, superficial transverse perinei, deep transverse perinei, external anal sphincter, and urinary sphincter — the coccygeus is NOT in this list; it does not form part of the perineal body.  Why A wrong: Bulbospongiosus is listed as a perineal muscle.  Why B wrong: Superficial transverse perinei is listed.  Why C wrong: Deep transverse perinei is listed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Which of the following is one of the five muscles listed as forming the perineum?","options":["External anal sphincter muscle","Coccygeus muscle","Piriformis muscle","Obturator internus muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The external anal sphincter muscle is listed as muscle number 4 of the perineum.  Why B wrong: The coccygeus is a pelvic floor muscle that does not form the perineum.  Why C wrong: Piriformis is a pelvic wall muscle.  Why D wrong: Obturator internus is a pelvic wall muscle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"In the list of muscles forming the perineum, which muscle is number 5?","options":["Urinary sphincter muscles","Bulbospongiosus","Levator ani","Deep transverse perinei"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list urinary sphincter muscles as number 5 of the perineal muscles.  Why B wrong: Bulbospongiosus is number 1.  Why C wrong: Levator ani is added separately (\"1 to 5 + levator ani\"), not numbered 5.  Why D wrong: Deep transverse perinei is number 3."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Match the order: the first two muscles listed as forming the perineum are, in order:","options":["Bulbospongiosus, then superficial transverse perinei","Deep transverse perinei, then external anal sphincter","External anal sphincter, then urinary sphincter","Superficial transverse perinei, then bulbospongiosus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list #1 Bulbospongiosus and #2 Superficial Transverse Perinei muscle.  Why B wrong: Deep transverse perinei is #3 and external anal sphincter is #4.  Why C wrong: These are #4 and #5.  Why D wrong: This reverses the order; bulbospongiosus is #1."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"According to the notes, which set of muscles are cut during episiotomy / attach to the perineal body / support the uterus?","options":["Muscles 1 to 5 (of the perineum) plus the levator ani muscle","Only the levator ani muscle","Only the coccygeus muscle","Only the bulbospongiosus muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bracketed note states muscles cut during episiotomy / attaching to perineal body / supporting uterus = \"1 to 5 + Levator ani muscle.\"  Why B wrong: It includes the five perineal muscles plus levator ani, not levator ani alone.  Why C wrong: The coccygeus is specifically excluded.  Why D wrong: It is the full set (1–5 + levator ani), not bulbospongiosus alone."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"(Exam tip) \"All of the following muscles are attached to the perineal body EXCEPT\" — the answer is:","options":["Ischiococcygeus / coccygeus","Bulbospongiosus","Levator ani","External anal sphincter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Tip box states the answer to this \"except\" question is ischiococcygeus/coccygeus, which does not attach to the perineal body.  Why B wrong: Bulbospongiosus is attached to the perineal body.  Why C wrong: The levator ani forms part of the perineal body.  Why D wrong: The external anal sphincter attaches to the perineal body."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"(Exam tip) \"All of the following muscles are cut during episiotomy EXCEPT\" — the correct answer is:","options":["Coccygeus (ischiococcygeus)","Deep transverse perinei","Bulbospongiosus","Levator ani"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Tip states the coccygeus/ischiococcygeus is the exception — it is NOT cut during episiotomy.  Why B wrong: Deep transverse perinei is cut during episiotomy.  Why C wrong: Bulbospongiosus is cut during episiotomy.  Why D wrong: The levator ani is cut during episiotomy."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"A single-best-answer question asks: \"Name the muscle that supports the uterus, is cut in episiotomy, and is attached to the perineal body — but which is the MAIN support of uterus.\" The best answer is:","options":["Levator ani","Coccygeus","Piriformis","Obturator internus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The levator ani is the main support of the uterus, forms part of the perineal body, and is cut during episiotomy.  Why B wrong: The coccygeus does none of these (it is the \"except\" answer).  Why C wrong: Piriformis is a pelvic wall muscle.  Why D wrong: Obturator internus is a pelvic wall muscle."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following muscles are attached to the perineal body / cut during episiotomy? Select all that apply.","options":["Bulbospongiosus","Superficial transverse perinei","Ischiococcygeus (coccygeus)","External anal sphincter","Levator ani"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The perineal muscles (bulbospongiosus, superficial/deep transverse perinei, external anal sphincter, urinary sphincter) plus the levator ani are attached to the perineal body and cut during episiotomy (options A, B, D, E all qualify).  Why C wrong: The ischiococcygeus/coccygeus does NOT attach to the perineal body and is NOT cut during episiotomy — it is the exception.  Additional note: The deep transverse perinei and urinary sphincter (not listed as options) also belong to this group."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"The pelvic bones, together with the sacrum and coccyx, enclose a cavity. The structures found inside this pelvic cavity are the:","options":["Female internal genital organs","External genital organs","Muscles of the perineum","Inguinal lymph nodes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the pelvic cavity contains the female internal genital organs.  Why B wrong: External genital organs lie in the perineum, outside the pelvic bone.  Why C wrong: Perineal muscles are in the perineum, not the pelvic cavity contents described.  Why D wrong: Inguinal lymph nodes are in the groin, not the pelvic cavity."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Bartholin gland cyst, vulva blood/nerve/lymphatic supply, clitoris…","question":"Which structure forms part of the perineal body and prevents uterine prolapse, making it clinically important during childbirth?","options":["Levator ani muscle","Ischiococcygeus muscle","Pubic symphysis","Sacrospinous ligament"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The levator ani prevents prolapse (main support of uterus) and forms part of the perineal body.  Why B wrong: The ischiococcygeus does not support the uterus or form the perineal body.  Why C wrong: The pubic symphysis is a bony joint, not this muscular support.  Why D wrong: The sacrospinous ligament is not described in these notes as this support."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"In the mnemonic \"BLESSD\" used for the muscles of the perineum, the letter \"B\" stands for which muscle?","options":["Bulbocavernosus reflex","Bulbospongiosus","Biceps femoris","Broad ligament muscle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the mnemonic BLESSD, \"B\" denotes the Bulbospongiosus muscle.  Why A wrong: Bulbocavernosus reflex is a reflex, not a perineal muscle in this list.  Why C wrong: Biceps femoris is a thigh muscle, not a perineal muscle.  Why D wrong: There is no \"broad ligament muscle\"; the broad ligament is a peritoneal fold."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"In the perineal muscle mnemonic \"BLESSD\", the letter \"L\" represents which muscle?","options":["Latissimus dorsi","Longitudinal muscle of vagina","Levator ani","Lateral rectus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"L\" in BLESSD stands for Levator ani.  Why A wrong: Latissimus dorsi is a back muscle.  Why B wrong: The longitudinal muscle of the vagina is not part of this mnemonic.  Why D wrong: Lateral rectus is an extraocular eye muscle."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"In the mnemonic \"BLESSD\", the letter \"E\" stands for which perineal structure?","options":["Erector spinae","External anal sphincter","External oblique","Epicranius"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"E\" in BLESSD denotes the External anal sphincter.  Why A wrong: Erector spinae is a back muscle.  Why C wrong: External oblique is an abdominal wall muscle.  Why D wrong: Epicranius is a scalp muscle."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"In the perineal muscle mnemonic \"BLESSD\", which structure does the letter \"S\" (the first of the two S's) represent?","options":["Superficial transverse perinei","Urinary sphincter","Sartorius","Soleus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first \"S\" in BLESSD stands for the Urinary sphincter.  Why A wrong: Superficial transverse perinei is the second \"S\" in this mnemonic.  Why C wrong: Sartorius is a thigh muscle.  Why D wrong: Soleus is a calf muscle."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"In the mnemonic \"BLESSD\", the second \"S\" and the letter \"D\" respectively represent which two muscles?","options":["Superficial transverse perinei and Deep transverse perinei","Sartorius and Deltoid","Soleus and Diaphragm","Sphincter urethrae and Detrusor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The second \"S\" = Superficial transverse perinei muscle and \"D\" = Deep transverse perinei muscle.  Why B wrong: Sartorius and deltoid are limb muscles.  Why C wrong: Soleus and diaphragm are unrelated skeletal/respiratory muscles.  Why D wrong: Detrusor is the bladder muscle, not part of the perineum mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Which of the following are the internal genital organs of the female located in the pelvic cavity? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Ovary","Fallopian tube","Uterus and cervix","Vagina","Clitoris"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The internal genital organs are the gonads (ovary), fallopian tubes, uterus (with cervix), and vagina — all located in the pelvic cavity.  Why A/B/C/D right: Each is listed among the four internal genital organs.  Why E wrong: The clitoris is an external genital organ, not part of this internal list."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The ovary is best described as which type of structure in the female reproductive system?","options":["Female accessory gland","Female gonad","External genital organ","Endocrine duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary is the female gonad.  Why A wrong: Accessory glands (e.g., Bartholin's) are separate structures.  Why C wrong: The ovary is an internal, not external, organ.  Why D wrong: The ovary is not a duct."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"During embryonic development, the ovary develops from which structure?","options":["Mullerian duct","Wolffian duct","Genital ridge","Urogenital sinus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary develops from the genital ridge.  Why A wrong: The Mullerian duct gives rise to fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper vagina.  Why B wrong: The Wolffian (mesonephric) duct forms male genital structures.  Why D wrong: The urogenital sinus contributes to lower vagina and urethra, not the ovary."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"What is the normal number of ovaries in the female?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are 2 ovaries.  Why A/C/D wrong: The female has a pair (2) of ovaries, not 1, 3, or 4."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"According to the notes, the normal size of the ovary is:","options":["3 cm × 2 cm × 1 cm","5 cm × 3 cm × 2 cm","2 cm × 1 cm × 0.5 cm","4 cm × 4 cm × 2 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary measures approximately 3 cm × 2 cm × 1 cm.  Why B/C/D wrong: These dimensions do not match the stated size."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The normal weight of an ovary is approximately:","options":["1-2 gm","5-10 gm","15-20 gm","25-30 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary weighs 5-10 gm.  Why A/C/D wrong: These weights are outside the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The ovary is located in the ovarian fossa, which lies in which part of the pelvis?","options":["Medial pelvic wall","Anterior pelvic wall","Lateral pelvic wall","Posterior pelvic floor"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ovarian fossa lies in the lateral pelvic wall.  Why A/B/D wrong: The fossa is on the lateral wall, not medial, anterior, or posterior floor."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"What is the normal colour of the ovary?","options":["Bright red","Pearly white","Yellowish brown","Bluish grey"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary is pearly white in colour.  Why A/C/D wrong: These colours do not describe the normal ovary."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The shape of the ovary is best described as:","options":["Spherical (ball shaped)","Ovoid (almond shaped)","Pear shaped","Triangular"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary is ovoid, i.e., almond shaped.  Why A wrong: It is not spherical.  Why C wrong: The uterus, not the ovary, is pear shaped.  Why D wrong: The ovary is not triangular."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Which ligament connects the ovary to the uterus?","options":["Ovarian ligament","Infundibulopelvic ligament","Broad ligament","Uterosacral ligament"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ovarian ligament connects the ovary to the uterus.  Why B wrong: The infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament connects the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall.  Why C wrong: The broad ligament is a wide peritoneal fold.  Why D wrong: The uterosacral ligament connects the cervix/uterus to the sacrum."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The infundibulopelvic ligament (also called the suspensory ligament of the ovary) connects the ovary to which structure?","options":["Uterus","Cervix","Lateral pelvic wall","Sacrum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The infundibulopelvic/suspensory ligament connects the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall.  Why A wrong: That is the role of the ovarian ligament.  Why B/D wrong: It does not attach to cervix or sacrum."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The ovarian vessels and nerves travel within which ligament?","options":["Ovarian ligament","Round ligament","Infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament","Cardinal ligament"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ovarian vessels and nerves are present in the infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament of the ovary.  Why A wrong: The ovarian ligament connects ovary to uterus but is not stated to carry these vessels/nerves.  Why B/D wrong: The round and cardinal ligaments do not carry the ovarian vessels/nerves."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The ovary lies behind the peritoneum, meaning its position is described as:","options":["Intraperitoneal","Retroperitoneal","Subperitoneal","Extra-pelvic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary is found behind the peritoneum, so it is retroperitoneal.  Why A wrong: Intraperitoneal means fully within the peritoneal cavity.  Why C/D wrong: These terms do not match \"behind the peritoneum.\""},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The mesovarium is a part of which ligament, lying just anterior to the ovary?","options":["Round ligament","Broad ligament","Ovarian ligament","Cardinal ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mesovarium is the part of the broad ligament lying just anterior to the ovary.  Why A/C/D wrong: The mesovarium is a subdivision of the broad ligament, not these ligaments."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the ovary? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Oogenesis","Secretion of estrogen","Secretion of progesterone","Secretion of testosterone as its primary hormone","Production of ova"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary performs oogenesis (production of ova) and secretes the hormones estrogen and progesterone.  Why A/B/C/E right: These are the stated ovarian functions and hormones.  Why D wrong: The notes list estrogen and progesterone as the ovarian hormones, not testosterone."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The surgical removal of the ovary is termed:","options":["Salpingectomy","Hysterectomy","Oophorectomy","Myomectomy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical removal of the ovary is called oophorectomy.  Why A wrong: Salpingectomy is removal of the fallopian tube.  Why B wrong: Hysterectomy is removal of the uterus.  Why D wrong: Myomectomy is removal of fibroids."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Which of the following is NOT an alternative name for the fallopian tube?","options":["Oviduct","Uterine tube","Salpinx","Mesosalpinx"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Mesosalpinx is a part of the broad ligament, not a synonym for the fallopian tube.  Why A/B/C wrong: Oviduct, uterine tube, and salpinx are all synonyms for the fallopian tube."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"What is the length of the fallopian tube?","options":["5-7 cms","10-12 cms (about 4 inches)","15-18 cms","2-3 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fallopian tube is 10-12 cms long, equal to about 4 inches.  Why A/C/D wrong: These lengths do not match the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The fallopian tube is derived from which embryonic duct?","options":["Wolffian duct","Mesonephric duct","Mullerian duct","Pronephric duct"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The fallopian tube is derived from the Mullerian (paramesonephric) duct.  Why A/B wrong: The Wolffian/mesonephric duct forms male structures.  Why D wrong: The pronephric duct is an early transient structure, not the origin of the tube."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The lining epithelium of the fallopian tube is:","options":["Simple squamous epithelium","Ciliated columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fallopian tube is lined by ciliated columnar epithelium.  Why A/C/D wrong: These epithelial types are found elsewhere (e.g., alveoli, skin/vagina, bladder), not lining the tube."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Which cell type, along with ciliated columnar cells, is a component of the fallopian tube epithelium as shown in the diagram?","options":["Goblet cell","Chief cell","Parietal cell","Kupffer cell"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ciliated columnar epithelium diagram labels the cilia, goblet cell, and mucus.  Why B/C wrong: Chief and parietal cells are in the stomach.  Why D wrong: Kupffer cells are in the liver."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Peg cells are characteristically found in which structure?","options":["Ovary","Fallopian tube","Cervix","Vagina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peg cells are seen in the fallopian tube.  Why A/C/D wrong: Peg cells are a feature of the fallopian tube epithelium, not the ovary, cervix, or vagina."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Which of the following is a function of the cilia of the fallopian tube? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To push sperm towards the ova","To push the zygote towards the uterine cavity after fertilization","To secrete estrogen","To produce ova","To assist transport of the fertilized ovum"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct: The cilia push sperm towards the ova and, after fertilization, push the zygote (fertilized ovum) towards the uterine cavity.  Why A/B/E right: These reflect the two stated ciliary functions and their combined role in ovum/zygote transport.  Why C wrong: Estrogen is secreted by the ovary, not cilia.  Why D wrong: Ova are produced by the ovary, not by cilia."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"A condition in which the cilia become immotile (cannot move) is known as:","options":["Turner syndrome","Kartagener syndrome","Down syndrome","Asherman syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Immotile cilia is called Kartagener syndrome.  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome is a 45,XO chromosomal disorder.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is trisomy 21.  Why D wrong: Asherman syndrome is intrauterine adhesions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"In Kartagener syndrome, sperms cannot move towards the ova. This most directly results in:","options":["Infertility","Menorrhagia","Endometriosis","Fibroid formation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If sperms cannot reach the ova, fertilization fails, causing infertility.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not the stated consequence of immotile cilia impairing sperm transport."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"In Kartagener syndrome, if fertilization occurs but the zygote cannot move towards the uterine cavity, it may implant in the fallopian tube. This results in:","options":["Molar pregnancy","Ectopic pregnancy","Twin pregnancy","Placenta previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the zygote implants in the fallopian tube instead of the uterine cavity, it results in an ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Molar pregnancy is abnormal trophoblastic proliferation.  Why C wrong: Twin pregnancy involves two fetuses, unrelated to ciliary immotility.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is a low-lying placenta in the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Which is the correct order of the parts of the fallopian tube from medial (uterine end) to lateral?","options":["Infundibulum → Ampulla → Isthmus → Intramural","Intramural → Isthmus → Ampulla → Infundibulum","Isthmus → Intramural → Infundibulum → Ampulla","Ampulla → Isthmus → Infundibulum → Intramural"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: From medial to lateral the order is Intramural (interstitial) → Isthmus → Ampulla → Infundibulum.  Why A/C/D wrong: These sequences reverse or scramble the correct medial-to-lateral order."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The intramural part of the fallopian tube is also known as the:","options":["Interstitial part","Ampullary part","Fimbrial part","Isthmic part"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The intramural part is also called the interstitial part.  Why B/C/D wrong: These name other parts of the tube."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"What is the length of the intramural (interstitial) part of the fallopian tube?","options":["2.5 cms","5 cms","1.25 cms","10 cms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The intramural part is 1.25 cms long.  Why A wrong: 2.5 cms is the isthmus.  Why B wrong: 5 cms is the ampulla.  Why D wrong: 10 cms exceeds the length of any single part."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The intramural part of the fallopian tube has a width (lumen diameter) of:","options":["1 mm","2 mm","4 mm","6 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The intramural part is 1 mm wide.  Why B wrong: 2 mm is the isthmus width.  Why C wrong: 4 mm is the ampulla width.  Why D wrong: 6 mm is the fimbriae width."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Which part of the fallopian tube is the narrowest part of the tube and is called the \"anatomical sphincter\"?","options":["Intramural (interstitial) part","Isthmus","Ampulla","Infundibulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The intramural/interstitial part is the narrowest part of the tube and is called the anatomical sphincter.  Why B wrong: The isthmus is the 2nd narrowest part and is the physiological sphincter.  Why C/D wrong: The ampulla is widest and the infundibulum is the funnel-shaped lateral part."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The intramural part opens into the uterus at which location?","options":["Cornua (uterine angle)","Internal os","External os","Fundus centre"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The intramural part opens into the uterus at the cornua, also called the uterine angle.  Why B/C wrong: The internal and external os are openings of the cervix.  Why D wrong: It opens at the cornu, not the centre of the fundus."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The isthmus is which numbered part of the fallopian tube (from medial to lateral)?","options":["1st part","2nd part","3rd part","4th part"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The isthmus is the 2nd part of the tube.  Why A wrong: The intramural/interstitial part is the 1st (medial-most).  Why C wrong: The ampulla is the 3rd part.  Why D wrong: The infundibulum is the 4th (most lateral) part."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"What is the length of the isthmus of the fallopian tube?","options":["1.25 cms","2.5 cms","5 cms","4 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The isthmus is 2.5 cms long.  Why A wrong: 1.25 cms is the intramural part length.  Why C wrong: 5 cms is the ampulla length.  Why D wrong: 4 cms is not a stated tube-part length here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The width of the isthmus of the fallopian tube is:","options":["1 mm","2 mm","4 mm","6 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The isthmus is 2 mm wide.  Why A wrong: 1 mm is the intramural part.  Why C wrong: 4 mm is the ampulla.  Why D wrong: 6 mm is the fimbriae."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The isthmus of the fallopian tube is described as the 2nd narrowest part and is called the:","options":["Anatomical sphincter","Physiological sphincter","Uterine sphincter","Ovarian sphincter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The isthmus is the 2nd narrowest part and is called the physiological sphincter.  Why A wrong: The anatomical sphincter is the intramural (narrowest) part.  Why C/D wrong: These are not terms used for tube parts here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"During tubal ligation, which part of the fallopian tube is cut?","options":["Intramural part","Isthmus","Ampulla","Infundibulum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In tubal ligation, the isthmus is cut.  Why A/C/D wrong: The isthmus, not the intramural part, ampulla, or infundibulum, is the site cut in tubal ligation."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The ampulla is which numbered part of the fallopian tube, and what is its length?","options":["2nd part, 2.5 cms","3rd part, 5 cms (longest)","1st part, 1.25 cms","4th part, 1.25 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ampulla is the 3rd part and, at 5 cms, is the longest part of the tube.  Why A wrong: 2nd part/2.5 cms describes the isthmus.  Why C wrong: 1st part/1.25 cms describes the intramural part.  Why D wrong: 4th part/1.25 cms describes the infundibulum."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The ampulla of the fallopian tube has a width of 4 mm, making it the:","options":["Narrowest part","2nd narrowest part","Widest part","Shortest part"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: At 4 mm, the ampulla is the widest part of the tube.  Why A wrong: The intramural part (1 mm) is narrowest.  Why B wrong: The isthmus is the 2nd narrowest.  Why D wrong: The ampulla is the longest, not the shortest."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"In which part of the fallopian tube does fertilization normally occur?","options":["Intramural part","Isthmus","Ampulla","Infundibulum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fertilization occurs in the ampulla.  Why A/B/D wrong: These parts are not the normal site of fertilization."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"What is the most common site for an ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Ovary","Ampulla of the fallopian tube","Cervix","Abdominal cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ampulla is the most common site for ectopic pregnancy.  Why A/C/D wrong: Ovarian, cervical, and abdominal ectopics occur but are far less common than ampullary ectopic pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The most common site for genital tuberculosis infection is the:","options":["Ampulla of the fallopian tube","Ovary","Cervix","Vagina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ampulla is the most common site for genital tuberculosis infection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common site for genital TB."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Which part of the fallopian tube is the most lateral part?","options":["Intramural part","Isthmus","Ampulla","Infundibulum"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The infundibulum is the most lateral part of the tube.  Why A wrong: The intramural part is the medial-most.  Why B/C wrong: The isthmus and ampulla lie between the two ends."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"What is the length of the infundibulum of the fallopian tube?","options":["1.25 cm","2.5 cm","5 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The infundibulum is 1.25 cm long.  Why B wrong: 2.5 cm is the isthmus.  Why C wrong: 5 cm is the ampulla.  Why D wrong: 10 cm approximates the whole tube length."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The finger-like projections at the lateral end (infundibulum) of the fallopian tube are called:","options":["Villi","Fimbriae","Microvilli","Cilia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The infundibulum has finger-like projections called fimbriae.  Why A/C wrong: Villi and microvilli are absorptive projections found elsewhere (e.g., intestine).  Why D wrong: Cilia are hair-like structures lining the epithelium, not the finger-like projections."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"According to the notes, the width of the infundibulum and the fimbriae are respectively:","options":["1 mm and 2 mm","2-3 mm and 6 mm","4 mm and 5 mm","6 mm and 2-3 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The infundibulum is 2-3 mm wide and the fimbriae are 6 mm.  Why A/C/D wrong: These pairings do not match the stated widths."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"One of the fimbriae becomes very long and is connected to the ovary. This specialized fimbria is called the:","options":["Ovarian ligament","Ovarian fimbria","Suspensory fimbria","Mesosalpinx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The long fimbria connected to the ovary is called the ovarian fimbria.  Why A wrong: The ovarian ligament is a separate structure connecting ovary to uterus.  Why C wrong: There is no \"suspensory fimbria.\"  Why D wrong: Mesosalpinx is a part of the broad ligament."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Tubal ligation is best described as a:","options":["Temporary method of contraception in females","Permanent method of contraception in females","Emergency contraceptive","Barrier method of contraception"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tubal ligation is a permanent method of contraception in the female.  Why A wrong: It is permanent, not temporary.  Why C wrong: It is not an emergency contraceptive.  Why D wrong: It is a surgical sterilization, not a barrier method."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"In which tubal ligation technique is an isthmus loop made and then cut?","options":["Pomeroy technique","Filshie clip method","Essure method","Uchida technique"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the Pomeroy technique, an isthmus loop is made and cut.  Why B/C/D wrong: Although these are real sterilization methods, the notes specifically describe the Pomeroy technique for the loop-and-cut of the isthmus."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"The surgical removal of the fallopian tube is called:","options":["Oophorectomy","Salpingectomy","Hysterectomy","Salpingitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Removal of the fallopian tube is called salpingectomy.  Why A wrong: Oophorectomy is removal of the ovary.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is removal of the uterus.  Why D wrong: Salpingitis is inflammation, not removal, of the tube."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Salpingo-oophorectomy refers to the surgical removal of which structures?","options":["Fallopian tube only","Ovary only","Fallopian tube plus ovary","Uterus plus cervix"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Salpingo-oophorectomy is removal of the fallopian tube plus the ovary.  Why A wrong: Removal of only the tube is salpingectomy.  Why B wrong: Removal of only the ovary is oophorectomy.  Why D wrong: Removal of uterus with cervix is hysterectomy."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"Inflammation of the fallopian tubes is called:","options":["Salpingectomy","Salpingitis","Oophoritis","Cervicitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Salpingitis is inflammation of the fallopian tubes (tube inflammation).  Why A wrong: Salpingectomy is surgical removal of the tube.  Why C wrong: Oophoritis is inflammation of the ovary.  Why D wrong: Cervicitis is inflammation of the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"As labelled in the internal reproductive anatomy diagram, the \"external os\" is an opening of which structure?","options":["Ovary","Fallopian tube","Cervix","Vagina"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The external os is the lower opening of the cervix, as labelled in the diagram.  Why A/B/D wrong: The external os belongs to the cervix, not the ovary, tube, or vagina."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Perineal muscle mnemonic, Internal genital organs of female, Ovary…","question":"As shown in the fallopian tube diagram, the intramural (interstitial) part represents which end of the tube relative to the uterus?","options":["Proximal end (near the uterus)","Distal end (away from the uterus)","Mid-portion only","Free peritoneal end"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The intramural/interstitial part is the proximal part, closest to the uterus.  Why B wrong: The distal end (near the ovary) is the infundibulum with fimbriae.  Why C/D wrong: The intramural part is the proximal uterine end, not mid-portion or free end."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"According to the key concept, removal of the ovary leads to a decrease in which hormone, producing menopause-like symptoms?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Ovary Remove → Hormones ↓ Estrogen ↓ → Menopause like symptoms,\" so ovary removal decreases estrogen and causes menopause-like symptoms.  Why A wrong: Progesterone is not the hormone specified in this key concept.  Why C wrong: Prolactin is a pituitary hormone for lactation, not the ovarian hormone referenced.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is a posterior pituitary hormone, not decreased by ovary removal here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"Removal of the ovaries produces which type of symptoms because of the resulting hormone drop?","options":["Pregnancy-like symptoms","Menopause-like symptoms","Puberty-like symptoms","Hyperthyroid symptoms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With estrogen falling after ovary removal, the patient develops menopause-like symptoms as stated in the note.  Why A wrong: Pregnancy-like symptoms are not caused by estrogen decline from oophorectomy.  Why C wrong: Puberty is associated with rising, not falling, estrogen.  Why D wrong: The note links the change to menopause, not thyroid function."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The surgical removal of the uterus is termed:","options":["Oophorectomy","Salpingectomy","Hysterectomy","Myomectomy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note titles the section \"Where we do Hysterectomy\" and defines it as \"Uterus Removal.\"  Why A wrong: Oophorectomy is removal of the ovary, not the uterus.  Why B wrong: Salpingectomy is removal of the fallopian tube.  Why D wrong: Myomectomy is removal of fibroids, sparing the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"For a woman younger than 45 years undergoing uterus removal, what is done regarding the ovaries?","options":["Remove both ovaries and tubes","Remove one ovary only","Do not remove the ovaries (do only hysterectomy)","Remove ovaries but keep the tubes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states that if the female's age is <45 years, do not remove the ovaries and perform only hysterectomy.  Why A wrong: Bilateral removal is reserved for age ≥45 years.  Why B wrong: The note does not describe unilateral ovary removal for this age group.  Why D wrong: Neither ovaries nor tubes are removed in a <45-year-old per the note."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In a woman aged 45 years or older undergoing hysterectomy, the recommended additional procedure is:","options":["Do only hysterectomy","Bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy","Unilateral oophorectomy","Myomectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart states that if age is ≥45 years, remove both ovaries and tubes — i.e., hysterectomy + bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy.  Why A wrong: Hysterectomy alone is done for those <45 years.  Why C wrong: The note specifies bilateral (both sides), not unilateral removal.  Why D wrong: Myomectomy removes fibroids and is unrelated to this age-based ovary decision."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In a woman aged ≥45 years having a hysterectomy, which structures are removed in addition to the uterus?","options":["Only the cervix","Both ovaries and both tubes","Only the left ovary","Only the fallopian tubes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart states \"Remove both ovaries & tubes\" for age ≥45 years.  Why A wrong: The cervix removal is not the point emphasized here.  Why C wrong: Removal is bilateral, not just the left ovary.  Why D wrong: Both ovaries and tubes are removed, not tubes alone."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The reason ovaries are generally preserved in women under 45 years undergoing hysterectomy is best explained by which physiological concern?","options":["To avoid premature loss of estrogen and menopause-like symptoms","To keep the uterine cavity intact","To preserve menstrual bleeding","To maintain lactation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key concept links ovary removal to decreased estrogen and menopause-like symptoms; preserving ovaries in younger women avoids this.  Why B wrong: The uterus (and its cavity) is removed in hysterectomy regardless.  Why C wrong: Menstruation ceases after hysterectomy regardless of ovary status.  Why D wrong: Ovaries do not produce milk; lactation is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The uterus is commonly known by which lay term?","options":["Womb","Birth canal","Cervix","Adnexa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The heading explicitly reads \"Uterus (Womb).\"  Why B wrong: Birth canal generally refers to the vagina/passage, not the uterus itself.  Why C wrong: The cervix is the lower part of the uterus, not a synonym for the whole organ.  Why D wrong: Adnexa refers to the tubes and ovaries, not the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"What is the characteristic shape of the uterus?","options":["Spherical","Pear shaped / Pyriform shaped","Tubular","Triangular flat sheet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Shape: Pear shaped / Pyriform shaped.\"  Why A wrong: The uterus is not spherical.  Why C wrong: Tubular describes the fallopian tubes, not the uterus.  Why D wrong: Only the internal uterine cavity is triangular; the organ itself is pear/pyriform shaped."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The normal position of the uterus in the body is:","options":["Retroverted and retroflexed","Anteverted and anteflexed","Midposition and straight","Anteverted and retroflexed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Note 1 states the normal position of the uterus is anteverted and anteflexed.  Why A wrong: Retroversion/retroflexion is seen only in a few females, not the norm.  Why C wrong: The uterus is angled, not straight midposition, normally.  Why D wrong: Normal is anteverted AND anteflexed, not combined with retroflexion."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The angle of anteflexion, which lies between the cervix and the body of the uterus, is approximately:","options":["90°","120°","45°","180°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the angle of anteflexion (between cervix and uterus) as 120°.  Why A wrong: 90° is the angle of anteversion (between vagina and cervix), not anteflexion.  Why C wrong: 45° is not given in the note.  Why D wrong: 180° would mean a straight axis, contrary to the diagram."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The angle of anteversion, formed between the vagina and the cervix, is approximately:","options":["120°","90°","60°","45°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram marks the angle of anteversion (between vagina and cervix) as 90°.  Why A wrong: 120° is the anteflexion angle (cervix–uterus).  Why C wrong: 60° is not indicated.  Why D wrong: 45° is not indicated in the note."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The angle of anteflexion is the angle between which two structures?","options":["Vagina and cervix","Cervix and body of uterus","Fundus and sacrum","Uterus and bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note defines the angle of anteflexion as the angle \"between cervix and uterus.\"  Why A wrong: Vagina–cervix defines the angle of anteversion.  Why C wrong: Fundus–sacrum describes direction in retroversion, not an anteflexion angle.  Why D wrong: The uterus–bladder relationship is not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The angle of anteversion is the angle between which two structures?","options":["Cervix and uterine body","Vagina and cervix","Fundus and symphysis","Ovary and tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the angle of anteversion is \"the angle between vagina and cervix.\"  Why A wrong: Cervix and uterine body define anteflexion.  Why C wrong: Fundus–symphysis relates to fundal direction, not this angle.  Why D wrong: Ovary–tube is unrelated to uterine axis angles."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"When the uterus is anteverted, the fundus of the uterus points:","options":["Towards the sacrum","Anteriorly towards the pubic symphysis","Directly upward towards the umbilicus","Posteriorly towards the rectum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Note 2 states that when the uterus is anteverted, the fundus points anteriorly towards the pubic symphysis.  Why A wrong: Pointing towards the sacrum occurs in retroversion.  Why C wrong: The note specifies anterior direction toward the symphysis, not straight up.  Why D wrong: Posterior/rectal direction is not described for anteversion."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In a woman with a retroverted uterus, the fundus points towards the:","options":["Pubic symphysis","Bladder","Sacrum","Umbilicus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Note 3 states that when the uterus is retroverted (in few females), the fundus points towards the sacrum.  Why A wrong: Pointing toward the symphysis occurs in anteversion.  Why B wrong: Bladder direction is not described for retroversion.  Why D wrong: The umbilicus is not the fundal direction described."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"Retroversion of the uterus, in which the fundus points towards the sacrum, is described in the notes as occurring in:","options":["All females","Only pregnant females","A few females","Only postmenopausal females"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Note 3 specifies retroversion occurs in \"few females,\" making it a variant rather than the norm.  Why A wrong: The normal position for most women is anteverted/anteflexed.  Why B wrong: The note does not restrict retroversion to pregnancy.  Why D wrong: The note does not limit it to postmenopausal women."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The length of a non-pregnant uterus is:","options":["8 cm (3 inch)","35 cm","20 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The size table lists the non-pregnant uterine length as 8 cm (3 inch).  Why B wrong: 35 cm is the pregnant uterine length.  Why C wrong: 20 cm is the length during puerperium.  Why D wrong: 5 cm is the non-pregnant breadth, not length."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The breadth (width) of a non-pregnant uterus is:","options":["8 cm","5 cm (2 inch)","20 cm","12.5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the non-pregnant breadth as 5 cm; 5 cm equals about 2 inches (the printed \"21 inch\" is a scanning/typographic error for 2 inch).  Why A wrong: 8 cm is the non-pregnant length.  Why C wrong: 20 cm is the pregnant breadth.  Why D wrong: 12.5 cm is the puerperium breadth (and pregnant thickness)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The thickness (wall) of a non-pregnant uterus is:","options":["2.5 cm (1 inch)","12.5 cm","7.5 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the non-pregnant thickness as 2.5 cm (1 inch).  Why B wrong: 12.5 cm is the pregnant thickness.  Why C wrong: 7.5 cm is the puerperium thickness.  Why D wrong: 5 cm is the non-pregnant breadth."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The weight of a normal non-pregnant uterus is:","options":["1000 gm","60–80 gm","500 gm","250 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the non-pregnant uterine weight as 60–80 gm.  Why A wrong: 1000 gm is the weight of a pregnant uterus (and during puerperium).  Why C wrong: 500 gm is not listed.  Why D wrong: 250 gm is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The capacity of a non-pregnant uterus is:","options":["10 mL","5 L","20 L","1 L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the non-pregnant uterine capacity as 10 mL.  Why B wrong: 5 L is the capacity of a pregnant uterus.  Why C wrong: 20 L is the maximum pregnant capacity, not the non-pregnant value.  Why D wrong: 1 L is not listed for the non-pregnant uterus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The length of the uterus during pregnancy (at term) is approximately:","options":["8 cm","20 cm","35 cm","12.5 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the pregnant uterine length as 35 cms.  Why A wrong: 8 cm is the non-pregnant length.  Why B wrong: 20 cm is the puerperium length.  Why D wrong: 12.5 cm is the pregnant thickness."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The breadth (width) of a pregnant uterus is:","options":["5 cm","20 cm","12.5 cm","35 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the pregnant uterine breadth as 20 cms.  Why A wrong: 5 cm is the non-pregnant breadth.  Why C wrong: 12.5 cm is the puerperium breadth / pregnant thickness.  Why D wrong: 35 cm is the pregnant length."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The thickness of the pregnant uterus is:","options":["2.5 cm","7.5 cm","12.5 cm","20 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the pregnant uterine thickness as 12.5 cms.  Why A wrong: 2.5 cm is the non-pregnant thickness.  Why B wrong: 7.5 cm is the puerperium thickness.  Why D wrong: 20 cm is the pregnant breadth."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The weight of the pregnant uterus (at term) is approximately:","options":["60–80 gm","1000 gms","500 gms","200 gms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the pregnant uterine weight as 1000 gms.  Why A wrong: 60–80 gm is the non-pregnant weight.  Why C wrong: 500 gms is not listed.  Why D wrong: 200 gms is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The capacity of the pregnant uterus is about 5 L, with a maximum capacity of:","options":["10 mL","5 L","20 L","2 L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the pregnant uterine capacity as 5 L, with a maximum of 20 L.  Why A wrong: 10 mL is the non-pregnant capacity.  Why B wrong: 5 L is the usual pregnant capacity, but the question asks for the maximum.  Why D wrong: 2 L is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"During the puerperium, the length of the uterus is:","options":["8 cm","20 cms","35 cms","12.5 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the puerperal uterine length as 20 cms.  Why A wrong: 8 cm is the non-pregnant length.  Why C wrong: 35 cm is the pregnant length.  Why D wrong: 12.5 cm is the puerperium breadth."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"During the puerperium, the breadth of the uterus is:","options":["5 cm","20 cm","12.5 cms","7.5 cms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the puerperal uterine breadth as 12.5 cms.  Why A wrong: 5 cm is the non-pregnant breadth.  Why B wrong: 20 cm is the pregnant breadth.  Why D wrong: 7.5 cm is the puerperium thickness."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"During the puerperium, the thickness of the uterus is:","options":["2.5 cm","7.5 cms","12.5 cms","20 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the puerperal uterine thickness as 7.5 cms.  Why A wrong: 2.5 cm is the non-pregnant thickness.  Why C wrong: 12.5 cm is the pregnant thickness / puerperium breadth.  Why D wrong: 20 cm is the puerperium length."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The weight of the uterus during the puerperium is:","options":["60–80 gms","1000 gms","10 gms","250 gms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the puerperal uterine weight as 1000 gms (same as the pregnant uterus, before full involution).  Why A wrong: 60–80 gm is the non-pregnant weight.  Why C wrong: 10 gms is not listed.  Why D wrong: 250 gms is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"By how many weeks after delivery does the uterus return to almost the same length, breadth, weight, and thickness as in a non-pregnant female?","options":["2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that 6 weeks after delivery, length, breadth, weight, and thickness of the uterus are almost the same as in a non-pregnant female.  Why A wrong: 2 weeks is too early per the note.  Why B wrong: 4 weeks is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 12 weeks is longer than stated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The height (length) of the uterus immediately after delivery is about 20 cm, which corresponds to the length of a uterus at how many weeks of pregnancy?","options":["12 weeks","16 weeks","20 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states 20 cms = 20 weeks pregnant uterus length, i.e., immediately after delivery the uterus equals a 20-week pregnant uterus in length.  Why A wrong: 12 weeks is not the stated equivalent.  Why B wrong: 16 weeks is not the stated equivalent.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is not the stated equivalent."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"Immediately after delivery, the fundus of the uterus can be palpated at which level?","options":["At the level of the umbilicus","Just below the umbilicus","At the xiphisternum","At the pubic symphysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the level where the uterus can be palpated immediately after delivery is \"just below the umbilicus.\"  Why A wrong: The note specifies just below, not at, the umbilicus.  Why C wrong: The xiphisternum is far too high.  Why D wrong: The pubic symphysis level occurs much later in involution."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The uterine angles, where the fallopian tubes join the uterus (right and left), are known as the:","options":["Cornua","Isthmus","Fundus","Cervix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note defines CORNUA as the \"uterine angles (Right Cornua, Left Cornua).\"  Why B wrong: The isthmus is the narrow part near the internal os.  Why C wrong: The fundus is the dome above the cornua.  Why D wrong: The cervix is the lower neck of the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"How many tube structures are attached to each cornu of the uterus?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"3 tube structures are attached to cornua of uterus.\"  Why A wrong: One is fewer than the three listed.  Why B wrong: Two is fewer than the three listed.  Why D wrong: Only three structures are named (round ligament, fallopian tube, ovarian ligament)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The three structures attached to the cornua of the uterus, listed from anterior to posterior, are:","options":["Fallopian tube → round ligament → ovarian ligament","Round ligament → fallopian tube → ovarian ligament","Ovarian ligament → fallopian tube → round ligament","Round ligament → ovarian ligament → fallopian tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists the structures from anterior to posterior as round ligament, fallopian tube, and ovarian ligament (mnemonic R-T-O).  Why A wrong: The round ligament is the most anterior, so it comes first.  Why C wrong: This reverses the order; the round ligament is anterior, not last.  Why D wrong: The fallopian tube lies between the round and ovarian ligaments."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In the mnemonic for cornual attachments (R-T-O, anterior to posterior), the letter \"T\" stands for:","options":["True ligament","Fallopian Tube","Transverse ligament","Tubal ovary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note defines R = Round ligament, T = Fallopian Tube, O = Ovarian ligament.  Why A wrong: \"True ligament\" is not part of the mnemonic.  Why C wrong: The transverse (cardinal) ligament is not listed here.  Why D wrong: \"Tubal ovary\" is not a defined structure in the note."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In the R-T-O cornual mnemonic, \"O\" refers to which structure?","options":["Ovary","Ovarian ligament","Ovarian artery","Os (internal)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note defines O as the Ovarian ligament.  Why A wrong: The ovary itself is a gland, whereas O in the mnemonic denotes the ovarian ligament.  Why C wrong: The ovarian artery is not part of the mnemonic.  Why D wrong: The internal os is a cervical opening, unrelated to this mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The most common cause of failure of female sterilization (tubal ligation) is:","options":["Age of the patient","Identification of the wrong structure during surgery","Use of local anesthesia","Obesity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Q1 in the note states the most common cause of failure of female sterilization is identification of the wrong structure (e.g., ligating the round ligament instead of the tube).  Why A wrong: Patient age is not cited as the cause.  Why C wrong: Anesthesia type is not cited.  Why D wrong: Obesity is not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"During labor, uterine contractions begin from which part of the uterus, giving it the name \"pacemaker of contractions\"?","options":["Cervix","Isthmus","Cornua of the uterus","Body of the uterus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Q2 states uterine contraction during labor begins from the cornua of the uterus, which is therefore called the pacemaker of contractions.  Why A wrong: The cervix dilates but does not initiate contractions.  Why B wrong: The isthmus forms the lower segment; it is not the pacemaker.  Why D wrong: Contractions spread over the body but originate at the cornua."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The part of the uterus that lies above the cornua and is dome-shaped is the:","options":["Isthmus","Fundus","Body","Cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the fundus is the part of the uterus which lies above the cornua and is dome shaped.  Why A wrong: The isthmus lies near the internal os, below the body.  Why C wrong: The body lies below the cornua, not above.  Why D wrong: The cervix is the lowest part of the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"Which of the following is TRUE about the fundus of the uterus?","options":["It contains the uterine cavity","It does not have a uterine cavity","It is inverted-triangle shaped","It lies below the cornua"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the fundus \"does not have a uterine cavity.\"  Why A wrong: The uterine cavity lies in the body, not the fundus.  Why C wrong: The inverted triangle shape describes the body's cavity, not the fundus.  Why D wrong: The fundus lies above, not below, the cornua."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"A uterus in which the fundus is flat or has a dimple (instead of being dome-shaped) is called:","options":["Bicornuate uterus","Septate uterus","Arcuate uterus","Didelphys uterus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that if the fundus is flat or has a dimple, the uterus is called an ARCUATE uterus.  Why A wrong: A bicornuate uterus has two horns, not merely a flat/dimpled fundus.  Why B wrong: A septate uterus has an internal septum.  Why D wrong: Uterus didelphys is a double uterus, not the flat-fundus variant described."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"Which part of the uterus lies below the cornua and forms the major part of the uterus?","options":["Fundus","Body of the uterus","Isthmus","Cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the body of the uterus lies below the cornua and forms the major part of the uterus.  Why A wrong: The fundus lies above the cornua and is a small dome.  Why C wrong: The isthmus is a short narrow part near the internal os.  Why D wrong: The cervix is the lower neck, not the major body."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The uterine cavity is present in which part of the uterus?","options":["Fundus","Body of the uterus","Cornua","Cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the uterine cavity is present in the body of the uterus.  Why A wrong: The fundus does not have a uterine cavity.  Why B correct as above.  Why C wrong: The cornua are the angles, not the location of the cavity per this note.  Why D wrong: The cervix has the cervical canal, not the uterine cavity."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The shape of the uterine cavity in the body of the uterus is:","options":["Circular","Inverted triangle","Oval","Rectangular"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow diagram states the shape (of the body/uterine cavity) is an inverted triangle.  Why A wrong: Circular is incorrect per the note.  Why C wrong: Oval is not stated.  Why D wrong: Rectangular is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The length of the body of the uterus is:","options":["0.5 cm","6–7 cms","10 cms","20 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow diagram states the length of the body of the uterus is 6–7 cms.  Why A wrong: 0.5 cm is the normal length of the isthmus.  Why C wrong: 10 cm is the isthmus length during labor.  Why D wrong: 20 cm relates to post-delivery uterine height, not body length."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The isthmus of the uterus is the part that lies near the:","options":["External os","Internal os","Fundus","Cornua"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the isthmus is the part of the uterus which lies near the internal os.  Why A wrong: The external os opens into the vagina, further down.  Why C wrong: The fundus is at the top, far from the isthmus.  Why D wrong: The cornua are the upper angles, not near the isthmus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The internal os is best defined as the:","options":["Opening between the cervix and the vagina","Opening between the uterus and the cervix","Opening of the fallopian tube","Opening at the fundus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed note states the internal os is the \"opening between uterus & cervix.\"  Why A wrong: That describes the external os (cervix–vagina).  Why C wrong: The tubal opening is the ostium, not the internal os.  Why D wrong: There is no os at the fundus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The isthmus connects the uterus to which structure?","options":["Vagina","Cervix","Fallopian tube","Ovary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the isthmus \"connects uterus to cervix.\"  Why A wrong: The cervix, not the isthmus, connects to the vagina.  Why C wrong: The fallopian tube connects at the cornua, not via the isthmus of the uterus.  Why D wrong: The ovary is attached by the ovarian ligament, not the isthmus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The normal length of the uterine isthmus is:","options":["0.5 cm (½ cm)","5 cm","6–7 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the isthmus normal length = ½ cm (0.5 cm).  Why B wrong: 5 cm is the isthmus length during pregnancy.  Why C wrong: 6–7 cm is the length of the body of the uterus.  Why D wrong: 10 cm is the isthmus length during labor."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"During pregnancy, the isthmus of the uterus plays an important role by forming the:","options":["Upper uterine segment","Lower uterine segment","Cervical canal","Endometrial lining"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that during pregnancy the isthmus forms the lower uterine segment.  Why A wrong: The upper segment is the contractile body, not derived from the isthmus.  Why C wrong: The cervical canal is a separate structure.  Why D wrong: The endometrium is a tissue layer, not formed from the isthmus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"During pregnancy, the length of the isthmus (lower uterine segment) increases to:","options":["0.5 cm","5 cms","10 cms","20 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the isthmus length during pregnancy is 5 cms.  Why A wrong: 0.5 cm is the normal (non-pregnant) isthmus length.  Why C wrong: 10 cm is the length during labor.  Why D wrong: 20 cm is unrelated to the isthmus length."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"During labor, the length of the lower uterine segment (isthmus) becomes:","options":["0.5 cm","5 cms","10 cms","2 cms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the isthmus length during labor is 10 cms.  Why A wrong: 0.5 cm is the normal non-pregnant length.  Why B wrong: 5 cm is the length during pregnancy (before labor).  Why D wrong: 2 cm is not a stated value."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"How many histological layers does the uterus have?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Uterus has 3 layers\" (endometrium, myometrium, perimetrium/serosa).  Why A wrong: Two undercounts the layers listed.  Why C wrong: Four overcounts the layers listed.  Why D wrong: Five overcounts the layers listed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The innermost layer of the uterus is the:","options":["Myometrium","Perimetrium","Endometrium","Serosa"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists the endometrium as the innermost layer.  Why A wrong: The myometrium is the middle layer.  Why B wrong: The perimetrium is the outermost layer.  Why D wrong: The serosa is another name for the outer perimetrium, not the inner layer."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The middle, muscular layer of the uterus is the:","options":["Endometrium","Myometrium","Perimetrium","Serosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists the middle layer as the myometrium, which forms the muscle.  Why A wrong: The endometrium is the inner lining.  Why C wrong: The perimetrium is the outer layer.  Why D wrong: The serosa is the outermost layer, synonymous with perimetrium."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The outermost layer of the uterus, also called the serosa, is the:","options":["Endometrium","Myometrium","Perimetrium","Decidua"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists the outermost layer as the perimetrium/serosa.  Why A wrong: The endometrium is the innermost layer.  Why B wrong: The myometrium is the middle muscular layer.  Why D wrong: The decidua is the pregnancy-modified endometrium, not the outer serosa."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"Which uterine layer is composed mainly of muscle and is responsible for contractions?","options":["Endometrium","Myometrium","Perimetrium","Cervical canal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note specifies the myometrium as the muscle-forming middle layer.  Why A wrong: The endometrium is glandular lining, not muscle.  Why C wrong: The perimetrium is a serous covering, not the contractile muscle.  Why D wrong: The cervical canal is a passage, not a uterine layer."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The lining epithelium of the uterus is:","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Columnar epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Cuboidal epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Uterus lining = Columnar Epithelium.\"  Why A wrong: Stratified squamous epithelium lines the vagina/ectocervix, not the uterine body.  Why C wrong: Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract.  Why D wrong: The note specifies columnar, not cuboidal, epithelium."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The concept of \"living ligature\" refers to which uterine layer that arrests bleeding after delivery?","options":["Endometrium","Myometrium","Perimetrium","Serosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note explains living ligature in terms of myometrial muscle fibres arranged in a mesh that constrict blood vessels after delivery.  Why A wrong: The endometrium is shed, and is not the contractile ligature.  Why C wrong: The perimetrium is a serous covering, not the muscle that constricts vessels.  Why D wrong: The serosa is the outer covering, not the living ligature."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In the pregnant uterus, the myometrial muscle fibres are arranged in how many layers?","options":["1 layer","2 layers","3 layers","4 layers"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that in a pregnant uterus, the myometrium has muscle fibres arranged in 3 layers.  Why A wrong: One layer undercounts the arrangement.  Why B wrong: Two layers undercounts the arrangement.  Why D wrong: Four layers overcounts the arrangement."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In the \"living ligature\" mechanism, which layer of myometrial fibres is arranged like a mesh with blood vessels running in between?","options":["The inner layer","The middle layer","The outer layer","The serosal layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the middle layer has fibres like a mesh with blood vessels in between.  Why A wrong: The inner layer is not described as the mesh layer.  Why C wrong: The outer layer is not the mesh layer.  Why D wrong: The serosa is not a myometrial muscle layer."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"According to the living ligature concept, how does bleeding stop after delivery?","options":["Platelets plug the placental site immediately","When the uterus contracts, the meshed muscle fibres constrict the blood vessels and bleeding stops","Estrogen causes vasoconstriction of uterine vessels","The cervix closes and blocks blood flow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that after delivery, when the uterus contracts, the blood vessels get constricted and bleeding stops (the muscle acts as a living ligature).  Why A wrong: The note attributes hemostasis to muscular constriction, not primarily platelets.  Why C wrong: Estrogen is not the mechanism described.  Why D wrong: Cervical closure is not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"A postpartum patient develops heavy vaginal bleeding and the uterus feels soft and boggy (atonic). Based on the \"living ligature\" concept, the priority action is to:","options":["Encourage the uterus to contract (e.g., fundal massage/uterotonics)","Give a blood transfusion first","Insert a urinary catheter","Place the patient in Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Since contraction of the meshed myometrium constricts vessels and stops bleeding, promoting uterine contraction (massage/uterotonics) is the priority to control atonic bleeding.  Why B wrong: Transfusion addresses volume loss but does not stop the source of bleeding.  Why C wrong: Catheterization helps the uterus contract but is secondary to directly promoting contraction.  Why D wrong: Positioning does not address the atonic uterus, the source of bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"Which structures are correctly labeled parts of the uterus as shown in the \"Parts of Uterus\" diagram? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fundus","Cornua","Body proper","Isthmus","Cervix"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The labeled parts diagram identifies Fundus (2), Cornua (1), Body proper (3), Isthmus (4), and Cervix (5) — all five are parts of the uterus.  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is explicitly numbered and labeled on the diagram as a part of the uterus, along with the uterine cavity, internal os, and cervical canal."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In the labeled diagram, the opening at the lower end of the uterine cavity leading into the cervical canal is the:","options":["External os","Internal os","Fimbrial ostium","Fornix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the \"Internal OS\" as the opening between the uterus and the cervix (leading to the cervical canal).  Why A wrong: The external os is the lower cervical opening into the vagina.  Why C wrong: The fimbrial ostium is the abdominal opening of the fallopian tube.  Why D wrong: The fornix is a vaginal recess around the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In the female reproductive system figure, which structure has finger-like projections at the end of the fallopian tube?","options":["Fundus","Fimbriae","Ovarian ligament","Cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the \"Fimbriae\" as part of the fallopian tube (the finger-like projections near the ovary).  Why A wrong: The fundus is the dome-shaped top of the uterus.  Why C wrong: The ovarian ligament attaches the ovary to the uterus.  Why D wrong: The cervix is the neck of the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In the female reproductive system figure, the passage running through the cervix is labeled the:","options":["Uterine cavity","Cervical canal","Vaginal fornix","Internal os"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the \"Cervical canal\" as the passage through the cervix.  Why A wrong: The uterine cavity is within the body of the uterus.  Why C wrong: The vaginal fornix is not labeled here.  Why D wrong: The internal os is an opening, not the canal through the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"A 40-year-old woman requires a hysterectomy for a benign condition. Based on the age-based key concept, the nurse anticipates that the surgeon will:","options":["Remove the uterus and both ovaries and tubes","Remove the uterus but preserve the ovaries","Remove only the ovaries","Perform bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because she is <45 years, the note recommends doing only hysterectomy and NOT removing the ovaries, to avoid premature estrogen loss.  Why A wrong: Removing both ovaries and tubes is recommended for age ≥45 years.  Why C wrong: Removing only the ovaries does not treat the uterine condition and is not the plan.  Why D wrong: Bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy applies to age ≥45 years."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"A 50-year-old woman is scheduled for hysterectomy. According to the age-based concept, the expected surgery is:","options":["Hysterectomy only, preserving ovaries","Hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy","Removal of one ovary only","Myomectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Being ≥45 years, the note recommends removing both ovaries and tubes along with the uterus (hysterectomy + bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy).  Why A wrong: Ovary preservation is for women <45 years.  Why C wrong: The note specifies bilateral removal for this age.  Why D wrong: Myomectomy spares the uterus and is not the indicated procedure."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"The term \"pyriform,\" used to describe the uterine shape, means shaped like a:","options":["Kidney","Pear","Triangle","Banana"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note equates pyriform with \"pear shaped,\" describing the uterus.  Why A wrong: Kidney-shaped is reniform, not pyriform.  Why C wrong: Triangular describes the internal cavity, not the term pyriform.  Why D wrong: Banana-shaped is not the meaning of pyriform."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"In the \"Parts of Uterus\" figure, the ovary is connected to the uterus by which structure?","options":["Round ligament","Ovarian ligament","Fallopian tube","Broad ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the \"Ovarian ligament\" connecting the ovary to the uterus (at the cornua).  Why A wrong: The round ligament is the most anterior cornual attachment, not the ovary's tether.  Why C wrong: The fallopian tube transports the ovum but is not the ligament tethering the ovary to the uterus.  Why D wrong: The broad ligament is a peritoneal fold, not labeled as connecting the ovary to the cornua here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Hysterectomy key concept & age-based ovary decision…","question":"Immediately after delivery, a uterine length of 20 cm indicates that the uterus is comparable in length to a pregnant uterus at 20 weeks; this reflects which postpartum process beginning?","options":["Involution","Subinvolution","Lochia formation","Engorgement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The reduction of the uterus from term size toward the non-pregnant state (reaching non-pregnant dimensions by 6 weeks) reflects involution, which begins immediately after delivery when the uterus measures about 20 cm/20-week size.  Why B wrong: Subinvolution is failure/delay of this normal process, not the normal reduction described.  Why C wrong: Lochia is the vaginal discharge, not the uterine size change.  Why D wrong: Engorgement refers to the breasts, not the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The middle layer of the myometrium is functionally referred to by which of the following names?","options":["Living ligature","Broad ligament","Round ligament","Living membrane"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that the middle layer of the myometrium is hence called the LIVING LIGATURE, because its interlacing muscle fibres compress blood vessels after delivery to control bleeding.  Why B wrong: The broad ligament is a peritoneal fold, not a myometrial layer.  Why C wrong: The round ligament is a supporting ligament of the uterus, not the middle myometrial layer.  Why D wrong: \"Living membrane\" is not the term used; the correct term is living ligature."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In the sagittal diagram of the uterus, the space enclosed within the uterine body is labelled as the:","options":["Cavity of the body","Cavity of the cervix","Vaginal canal","Broad ligament space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The uterine diagram labels the upper interior space as the \"Cavity of body\" (the uterine/body cavity), bounded by the uterine wall.  Why B wrong: The cavity of the cervix is the lower canal-like space within the cervix, a separate label.  Why C wrong: The vagina is labelled separately below the cervix.  Why D wrong: The broad ligament is not shown in this internal cavity diagram."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In the labelled uterus diagram, the muscular boundary surrounding the uterine cavity is termed the:","options":["Uterine wall","Cavity of cervix","Vagina","Fundus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the muscular boundary enclosing the cavity of the body as the \"Uterine wall.\"  Why B wrong: The cavity of the cervix is the interior space of the cervix, not the wall.  Why C wrong: The vagina lies below the cervix and is a separate structure.  Why D wrong: The fundus is the dome of the uterus, not labelled as the wall here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In the \"Position of Uterus in Pelvis\" diagram, which organ lies anterior to the uterus?","options":["Bladder","Rectum","Ovary","Sigmoid colon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows Bd (bladder) with the urethra anterior to the uterus (ut) and cervix (Cx), while the rectum lies posteriorly.  Why B wrong: The rectum is shown posterior (\"Post\") to the uterus, not anterior.  Why C wrong: The ovary is not the anterior relation depicted in this positional diagram.  Why D wrong: The sigmoid colon is not labelled in this diagram."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In the pelvic position diagram, which structure is located posterior to the uterus?","options":["Rectum","Bladder","Urethra","Vagina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the rectum posteriorly (\"Post\") in relation to the uterus.  Why B wrong: The bladder (Bd) lies anterior to the uterus.  Why C wrong: The urethra lies anteriorly, below the bladder.  Why D wrong: The vagina is continuous below the cervix, not the posterior relation being asked."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The physiological tilting of the uterus slightly towards the right side, caused by the pelvic colon pushing it, is known as:","options":["Dextrorotation","Levorotation","Anteversion","Retroversion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe that the (sigmoid/pelvic) colon on the left side pushes the uterus towards the right, so the uterus is slightly rotated to the right — this is called Dextro rotation.  Why B wrong: Levorotation would be rotation toward the left, the opposite of what occurs.  Why C wrong: Anteversion is forward tilting of the whole uterus over the bladder, not rotation to the right.  Why D wrong: Retroversion is backward tilting of the uterus, not a rightward rotation."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"According to the notes, the leftward pelvic column pushes the uterus toward which side, producing dextrorotation?","options":["Right side","Left side","Anterior","Posterior"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The column on the left side pushes the uterus towards the Right side, hence the uterus is slightly rotated to the right (dextrorotation).  Why B wrong: The push is away from the left, toward the right.  Why C wrong: The described movement is a rotation to the right, not anterior tilting.  Why D wrong: It is not a posterior movement."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In the peritoneal relations of the uterus, the broad ligament attaches the sides of the uterus to which structure?","options":["Lateral wall of pelvis","Sacrum","Symphysis pubis","Anterior abdominal wall"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram and text show the broad ligament extending from the sides of the uterus to the lateral wall of the pelvis.  Why B wrong: The uterosacral ligament, not the broad ligament, connects to the sacrum.  Why C wrong: The pubocervical/pubic attachments relate to the symphysis pubis, not the broad ligament.  Why D wrong: The broad ligament attaches to the lateral pelvic wall, not the anterior abdominal wall."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In the labelled tube-and-ovary diagram, the part of the fallopian tube that passes through the uterine wall is the:","options":["Interstitial part","Ampulla","Infundibulum","Isthmus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The interstitial part is labelled at the junction of the fundus of the uterus, representing the intramural segment of the tube passing through the uterine wall.  Why B wrong: The ampulla is the wide middle segment lateral to the isthmus.  Why C wrong: The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped lateral end bearing fimbriae.  Why D wrong: The isthmus is the narrow segment between the interstitial part and the ampulla."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In the fallopian tube diagram, which segment is labelled between the interstitial part and the ampulla?","options":["Isthmus","Infundibulum","Fimbriae","Cervical canal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The isthmus is the narrow tubal segment shown between the interstitial part and the ampulla.  Why B wrong: The infundibulum is the terminal funnel-shaped part near the ovary.  Why C wrong: Fimbriae are the finger-like projections on the infundibulum.  Why D wrong: The cervical canal is part of the cervix, not the fallopian tube."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The funnel-shaped lateral end of the fallopian tube bearing finger-like projections is called the:","options":["Infundibulum with fimbriae","Ampulla","Isthmus","Interstitial part"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the terminal funnel end as the \"Infundibulum with fimbriae,\" lying close to the ovary.  Why B wrong: The ampulla is the dilated middle portion where fertilization usually occurs.  Why C wrong: The isthmus is the narrow medial portion.  Why D wrong: The interstitial part is the intramural portion within the uterine wall."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Which statement about the broad ligament is correct according to the notes?","options":["Its name is a misnomer because it is not a true ligament","It is a strong true ligament of the uterus","It provides the main support to the uterus","It is composed of dense fibrous tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state the broad ligament is a misnomer (wrong name) because it is NOT a ligament — it is actually a fold of peritoneum.  Why B wrong: The notes stress it is not a true ligament.  Why C wrong: The notes clearly state it does NOT support the uterus.  Why D wrong: It is a thin peritoneal fold, not dense fibrous tissue."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The broad ligament is best described histologically as:","options":["A thin fold of peritoneum","A band of skeletal muscle","A cord of fibrocartilage","A layer of endometrium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the broad ligament as a thin fold of peritoneum attached from the sides of the uterus to the pelvic wall.  Why B wrong: It contains no skeletal muscle band; it is peritoneum.  Why C wrong: It is not fibrocartilage.  Why D wrong: Endometrium lines the uterine cavity, not the broad ligament."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Which of the following is TRUE regarding the supportive role of the broad ligament?","options":["It does not support the uterus","It is the primary support of the uterus","It supports only the cervix","It supports the bladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes highlight in green that the broad ligament \"does not support the uterus.\"  Why B wrong: True uterine support comes from other structures (e.g., cardinal/uterosacral ligaments), not the broad ligament.  Why C wrong: It does not act as the cervical support either.  Why D wrong: It is not described as a bladder support."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Into how many parts is the broad ligament divided?","options":["3 parts","2 parts","4 parts","5 parts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Parts of Broad ligament: 3 Parts\" — mesosalpinx, mesovarium, and mesometrium.  Why B wrong: There are three, not two, named parts.  Why C wrong: Four is more than the three parts described.  Why D wrong: Five is not correct."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The part of the broad ligament that lies near the fallopian tube is called the:","options":["Mesosalpinx","Mesovarium","Mesometrium","Mesocolon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define the mesosalpinx as the part which lies near the fallopian tube (salpinx = tube).  Why B wrong: The mesovarium lies near the ovary.  Why C wrong: The mesometrium lies near the uterus.  Why D wrong: The mesocolon relates to the colon, not the broad ligament."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The portion of the broad ligament situated near the ovary is termed the:","options":["Mesovarium","Mesosalpinx","Mesometrium","Infundibulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mesovarium is defined as the part of the broad ligament which lies near the ovary.  Why B wrong: The mesosalpinx lies near the fallopian tube.  Why C wrong: The mesometrium lies near the uterus.  Why D wrong: The infundibulum is part of the fallopian tube, not the broad ligament."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The largest part of the broad ligament, lying near the uterus, is the:","options":["Mesometrium","Mesovarium","Mesosalpinx","Myometrium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mesometrium is defined as the part of the broad ligament which lies near the uterus.  Why B wrong: The mesovarium lies near the ovary.  Why C wrong: The mesosalpinx lies near the fallopian tube.  Why D wrong: The myometrium is the muscular wall of the uterus, not a part of the broad ligament."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The main arterial blood supply of the uterus is provided by the:","options":["Uterine artery","Ovarian artery","Vaginal artery","Superior vesical artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the main blood supply of the uterus as the uterine artery.  Why B wrong: The ovarian artery is listed as the \"other\" (accessory) supply, not the main one.  Why C wrong: The vaginal artery mainly supplies the vagina.  Why D wrong: The superior vesical artery supplies the bladder, not the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The uterine artery is a branch of which vessel?","options":["Anterior division of the internal iliac artery","Posterior division of the internal iliac artery","External iliac artery","Abdominal aorta directly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify the uterine artery is a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery.  Why B wrong: It arises from the anterior, not posterior, division.  Why C wrong: The external iliac artery does not give the uterine artery.  Why D wrong: The ovarian artery (not uterine) arises directly from the abdominal aorta."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Besides the uterine artery, which additional artery contributes to the blood supply of the uterus?","options":["Ovarian artery","Renal artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Pudendal artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the ovarian artery as the \"other\" arterial supply of the uterus.  Why B wrong: The renal artery supplies the kidney.  Why C wrong: The inferior mesenteric artery supplies parts of the gut.  Why D wrong: The internal pudendal artery supplies perineal structures, not the uterus proper."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Embryologically, the uterus is derived from which structure?","options":["Mullerian (paramesonephric) duct","Wolffian (mesonephric) duct","Urogenital sinus","Genital ridge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus is derived from the mullerian duct (paramesonephric duct).  Why B wrong: The Wolffian/mesonephric duct gives rise to male genital structures.  Why C wrong: The urogenital sinus forms the lower vagina and urethra, not the uterus.  Why D wrong: The genital ridge forms the gonads, not the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The best investigation to visualize the interior of the uterine cavity is:","options":["Hysteroscopy","Colposcopy","Laparoscopy","Cystoscopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best investigation to see inside the uterus is hysteroscopy.  Why B wrong: Colposcopy visualizes the cervix, vagina and vulva, not the uterine interior.  Why C wrong: Laparoscopy views the external/pelvic surfaces of organs, not inside the uterus.  Why D wrong: Cystoscopy examines the bladder."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The surgical procedure to remove the uterus is called:","options":["Hysterectomy","Oophorectomy","Salpingectomy","Myomectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define hysterectomy as the operation to remove the uterus.  Why B wrong: Oophorectomy is removal of the ovary.  Why C wrong: Salpingectomy is removal of the fallopian tube.  Why D wrong: Myomectomy is removal of fibroids while preserving the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Removal of both the uterus and the cervix is known as:","options":["Total abdominal hysterectomy (TAH)","Subtotal hysterectomy","Radical hysterectomy","Myomectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe removal of uterus + cervix as Total Abdominal Hysterectomy (TAH), which is the most common (M/C) type.  Why B wrong: Subtotal hysterectomy removes only the uterus, leaving the cervix behind.  Why C wrong: Radical hysterectomy (removing additional surrounding tissue) is not the type described here.  Why D wrong: Myomectomy removes only fibroids, not the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Which type of hysterectomy is the most common (M/C)?","options":["Total abdominal hysterectomy (uterus + cervix removed)","Subtotal hysterectomy (only uterus removed)","Vaginal hysterectomy","Radical hysterectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes mark \"M/C\" (most common) next to the Total Abdominal Hysterectomy (uterus + cervix removed).  Why B wrong: Subtotal hysterectomy is labelled as \"rarely done.\"  Why C wrong: Vaginal hysterectomy is not the type flagged as most common here.  Why D wrong: Radical hysterectomy is not discussed as the most common type in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In a subtotal hysterectomy, which structure is preserved (not removed)?","options":["Cervix","Uterine body","Fundus","Endometrium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state subtotal hysterectomy removes only the uterus but NOT the cervix; hence the cervix is preserved. It is rarely done.  Why B wrong: The uterine body is removed in this procedure.  Why C wrong: The fundus (part of the uterine body) is removed.  Why D wrong: The endometrium lines the uterine cavity, which is removed with the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The cervix represents which portion of the uterus?","options":["The lowermost part of the uterus","The uppermost (fundal) part of the uterus","The middle muscular layer of the uterus","The lateral peritoneal fold of the uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define the cervix as the lowermost part of the uterus.  Why B wrong: The uppermost part is the fundus, not the cervix.  Why C wrong: The middle muscular layer is the myometrium.  Why D wrong: The lateral peritoneal fold is the broad ligament."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In the cervix diagram, the opening at the junction between the uterine cavity and the cervical canal is the:","options":["Internal os","External os","Exocervix","Portio vaginalis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The internal os (Int OS) is labelled at the upper end of the cervix where it communicates with the uterine cavity.  Why B wrong: The external os is the lower opening of the cervix into the vagina.  Why C wrong: The exocervix is the vaginal portion of the cervix, not an os.  Why D wrong: The portio vaginalis is another name for the exocervix, not the internal os."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The cervix is divided into how many parts?","options":["2 parts","3 parts","4 parts","5 parts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cervix has 2 parts — the endocervix and the exocervix (ectocervix).  Why B wrong: Three is incorrect; only two parts are described.  Why C wrong: Four is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Five is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The part of the cervix that lies nearer to the uterus and is above the vagina is the:","options":["Endocervix (supravaginal part)","Exocervix (portio vaginalis)","External os","Transformation zone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The endocervix is defined as the part of cervix near the uterus, also called the supravaginal part of the cervix.  Why B wrong: The exocervix/portio vaginalis is the part inside the vagina.  Why C wrong: The external os is an opening, not a part of the cervix per se.  Why D wrong: The transformation zone is a region of epithelial change near the external os, not the supravaginal part."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The endocervix is also known as which part of the cervix?","options":["Supravaginal part","Portio vaginalis","Ectocervix","External os"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the endocervix is also called the supravaginal part of the cervix.  Why B wrong: Portio vaginalis is the alternate name of the exocervix/ectocervix.  Why C wrong: Ectocervix is the same as exocervix, the vaginal part.  Why D wrong: The external os is the opening of the cervix into the vagina."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The exocervix (ectocervix) is defined as the part of the cervix that lies:","options":["Inside the vagina","Near the uterus, above the vagina","Within the uterine cavity","Within the broad ligament"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define the exocervix/ectocervix as the part of cervix inside the vagina.  Why B wrong: The part near the uterus, above the vagina, is the endocervix.  Why C wrong: It lies in the vagina, not within the uterine cavity.  Why D wrong: The broad ligament is a peritoneal fold, unrelated to the exocervix location."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The exocervix/ectocervix is also called by which name?","options":["Portio vaginalis","Supravaginal part","Internal os","Squamocolumnar junction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the exocervix/ectocervix is also called PORTIO Vaginalis.  Why B wrong: The supravaginal part is the endocervix.  Why C wrong: The internal os is the upper opening of the cervix.  Why D wrong: The squamocolumnar junction is the transformation zone, not the whole exocervix."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The external os is defined as the place where the:","options":["Cervix opens into the vagina","Cervix opens into the uterine cavity","Fallopian tube opens into the uterus","Vagina opens into the vestibule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define the external os as the place where the cervix opens into the vagina.  Why B wrong: The opening into the uterine cavity is the internal os.  Why C wrong: The tubal opening into the uterus is the uterine ostium of the tube, not the external os.  Why D wrong: The vaginal opening into the vestibule is the introitus, not the external os."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"On per-speculum examination, the external os of a nulliparous woman typically appears:","options":["Round/circular","Transverse/slit-like","Star-shaped","Irregularly torn"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the external os is round/circular and is seen in the nulliparous female.  Why B wrong: The transverse/slit-like os is seen in multiparous women.  Why C wrong: A star shape is not the described nulliparous appearance.  Why D wrong: An irregular torn appearance is not the described shape for a nullipara."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"A transverse, slit-like external os on per-speculum examination indicates that the woman is:","options":["Multiparous","Nulliparous","Postmenopausal only","Pregnant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the transverse/slit-like external os is seen in multiparous women.  Why B wrong: The nulliparous os is round/circular.  Why C wrong: The slit shape reflects prior vaginal delivery (parity), not menopausal status per se.  Why D wrong: The shape reflects parity, not the current pregnant state."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The length of the cervix in a non-pregnant woman is approximately:","options":["2.5 cm","4–5 cm","1.25 cm","7–8 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the non-pregnancy cervical length as 2.5 cm.  Why B wrong: 4–5 cm is the cervical length during pregnancy.  Why C wrong: 1.25 cm is the length of the endocervix (a component), not the whole cervix.  Why D wrong: 7–8 cm is not a value given."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"During pregnancy, the length of the cervix is approximately:","options":["4–5 cm","2.5 cm","1.25 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cervical length in pregnancy is 4–5 cm.  Why B wrong: 2.5 cm is the non-pregnant length.  Why C wrong: 1.25 cm is the endocervical component length.  Why D wrong: 10 cm is not a value provided."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In the non-pregnant cervix (total length 2.5 cm), the length of the endocervix is:","options":["1.25 cm","2.5 cm","0.5 cm","4 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the endocervix measures 1.25 cm (half of the 2.5 cm total non-pregnant cervical length).  Why B wrong: 2.5 cm is the total cervical length, not the endocervix alone.  Why C wrong: 0.5 cm is not a value given.  Why D wrong: 4 cm exceeds the total non-pregnant cervical length."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Before puberty, how does the length of the cervix compare with the length of the uterine body?","options":["The cervix is longer than the uterine body","The cervix is shorter than the uterine body","They are equal in length","The cervix is absent before puberty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that before puberty, the length of the cervix is more than the length of the uterus (body), consistent with the 1:2 corpus:cervix ratio.  Why B wrong: The cervix being shorter is true only in later reproductive life, not before puberty.  Why C wrong: Equal length (1:1) occurs at menopause, not before puberty.  Why D wrong: The cervix is present before puberty and is relatively longer."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"According to the uterus (corpus):cervix ratio table, the ratio before puberty is:","options":["1:2","2:1","3:1","1:1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the corpus:cervix ratio before puberty as 1:2 (cervix is longer).  Why B wrong: 2:1 is the ratio at puberty.  Why C wrong: 3:1 is the reproductive-age ratio.  Why D wrong: 1:1 is the menopausal ratio."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The corpus (uterus):cervix ratio at puberty is:","options":["2:1","1:2","3:1","1:1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the ratio at puberty as 2:1.  Why B wrong: 1:2 is the pre-pubertal ratio.  Why C wrong: 3:1 is the reproductive ratio.  Why D wrong: 1:1 is the menopausal ratio."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"During the reproductive age, the corpus (uterus):cervix ratio is:","options":["3:1","2:1","1:2","1:1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the reproductive-age ratio as 3:1 (uterine body markedly longer than cervix).  Why B wrong: 2:1 is the pubertal ratio.  Why C wrong: 1:2 is the pre-pubertal ratio.  Why D wrong: 1:1 is the menopausal ratio."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"In a menopausal female, the corpus (uterus):cervix ratio becomes:","options":["1:1","3:1","2:1","1:2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the menopausal ratio as 1:1.  Why B wrong: 3:1 is the reproductive ratio.  Why C wrong: 2:1 is the pubertal ratio.  Why D wrong: 1:2 is the pre-pubertal ratio."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Like the uterus, the cervix is embryologically derived from the:","options":["Mullerian duct","Wolffian duct","Urogenital sinus","Cloaca"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cervix is derived from the mullerian duct.  Why B wrong: The Wolffian duct gives male genital structures.  Why C wrong: The urogenital sinus forms the lower vagina, not the cervix.  Why D wrong: The cloaca contributes to the lower rectum and urogenital sinus, not the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The lining epithelium of the endocervix is:","options":["Columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the endocervix is lined by columnar epithelium.  Why B wrong: Stratified squamous epithelium lines the exocervix, not the endocervix.  Why C wrong: Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract.  Why D wrong: Simple cuboidal epithelium is not the endocervical lining described."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The lining epithelium of the exocervix is:","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Columnar epithelium","Ciliated columnar epithelium","Pseudostratified epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the exocervix is lined by stratified squamous epithelium.  Why B wrong: Columnar epithelium lines the endocervix, not the exocervix.  Why C wrong: Ciliated columnar epithelium lines the fallopian tube, not the exocervix.  Why D wrong: Pseudostratified epithelium is not the exocervical lining described."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The transformation zone is defined as the region where:","options":["Columnar epithelium of the endocervix gradually changes into squamous epithelium of the exocervix","Squamous epithelium of the vagina changes into columnar epithelium of the uterus","Endometrium changes into myometrium","Peritoneum changes into serosa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define the transformation zone (squamocolumnar junction) as the place where columnar epithelium of the endocervix changes gradually into squamous epithelium of the exocervix.  Why B wrong: It is a cervical epithelial transition, not a vagina-to-uterus transition.  Why C wrong: Endometrium-to-myometrium is a uterine wall relationship, unrelated to this zone.  Why D wrong: Peritoneum-to-serosa is not what the transformation zone describes."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Another name for the transformation zone of the cervix is the:","options":["Squamocolumnar junction","Internal os","Portio vaginalis","Fornix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes title this region \"Transformation Zone/Squamocolumnar Junction,\" using the two terms interchangeably.  Why B wrong: The internal os is the upper cervical opening, not the epithelial junction.  Why C wrong: Portio vaginalis is the exocervix.  Why D wrong: The fornix is the recess of the vagina around the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Where is the transformation zone (squamocolumnar junction) located?","options":["Near the external os","Near the internal os","At the fundus of the uterus","In the vaginal fornix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the location of the transformation zone is near the external os.  Why B wrong: It is near the external os, not the internal os.  Why C wrong: The fundus is the upper uterus, far from the transformation zone.  Why D wrong: The vaginal fornix is not the described location."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Why is the transformation zone of the cervix clinically significant?","options":["It is the most common site for cancer of the cervix","It is the site of implantation","It is where menstruation begins","It is the origin of the uterine artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the significance of the transformation zone is that it is the most common site for cancer cervix.  Why B wrong: Implantation occurs in the uterine endometrium, not the transformation zone.  Why C wrong: Menstruation originates from shedding of the endometrium, not this zone.  Why D wrong: The uterine artery arises from the internal iliac artery, unrelated to this zone."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The shape of the cervical canal is described as:","options":["Cylindrical/fusiform (spindle-shaped)","Triangular","Spherical","Funnel-shaped throughout"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cervical canal shape is cylindrical/fusiform (spindle-shaped).  Why B wrong: The uterine cavity is triangular, not the cervical canal.  Why C wrong: Spherical is not the described shape.  Why D wrong: While the ends narrow, the canal is described specifically as cylindrical/fusiform, not funnel-shaped throughout."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Which investigation is used to examine the cervix, transformation zone, vagina, and external os?","options":["Colposcopy","Hysteroscopy","Laparoscopy","Cystoscopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Clinical Connect box lists colposcopy as the investigation that visualizes the vagina, exocervix, external os and transformation zone.  Why B wrong: Hysteroscopy visualizes the inside of the uterus.  Why C wrong: Laparoscopy views the pelvic/abdominal cavity, not the cervical surface.  Why D wrong: Cystoscopy examines the bladder."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"During colposcopy, which of the following structures can be visualized? (Select all that apply)","options":["Vagina","Exocervix","External os","Transformation zone","Endocervix"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list vagina, exocervix, external os, and transformation zone as the structures seen on colposcopy.  Why A/B/C/D correct: Each is explicitly listed among the four structures visible on colposcopy.  Why E wrong: The notes specifically state the endocervix is NOT seen on colposcopy."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"Which cervical structure is NOT visualized during colposcopy?","options":["Endocervix","Exocervix","External os","Transformation zone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state that the endocervix is what is \"not seen\" on colposcopy.  Why B wrong: The exocervix is seen on colposcopy.  Why C wrong: The external os is seen on colposcopy.  Why D wrong: The transformation zone is seen on colposcopy."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"The endocervix is also described in the cervix diagram as which of the following relative to the external os?","options":["The canal lying above/internal to the external os toward the uterus","The vaginal-facing surface of the cervix","The opening into the vagina","The peritoneal covering of the cervix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows the endocervix as the inner canal between the internal os and external os, lying toward the uterus (supravaginal part).  Why B wrong: The vaginal-facing surface is the exocervix.  Why C wrong: The opening into the vagina is the external os.  Why D wrong: The peritoneal fold is the broad ligament, not the endocervix."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Myometrium, uterus cavity anatomy…","question":"A patient asks which part of her cervix a doctor sees with a speculum during a routine examination. The correct answer is the:","options":["Exocervix (portio vaginalis) and external os","Endocervix and internal os","Uterine cavity","Fallopian tubes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The exocervix (portio vaginalis) with the external os is the vaginal portion of the cervix and is visible on speculum/colposcopic examination; the notes list exocervix and external os as visible.  Why B wrong: The endocervix and internal os lie deep and are not directly visualized (endocervix is not seen on colposcopy).  Why C wrong: The uterine cavity requires hysteroscopy to visualize.  Why D wrong: The fallopian tubes are not seen on speculum examination."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The vagina is best described as which type of organ?","options":["A fibromuscular hollow organ/canal","A solid muscular organ","A glandular secretory organ","A cartilaginous tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the vagina is a fibromuscular hollow organ/canal.  Why B wrong: It is hollow, not solid.  Why C wrong: The vagina has no glands of its own.  Why D wrong: It is fibromuscular, not cartilaginous."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The vagina connects which two structures?","options":["Uterus to vulva","Uterus to urinary bladder","Cervix to rectum","Ovary to uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vagina connects the uterus to the vulva.  Why B wrong: The bladder lies anterior to the vagina but is not connected by it.  Why C wrong: The rectum lies posterior but is not the connection.  Why D wrong: The fallopian tube connects the ovary region to the uterus, not the vagina."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Which of the following is a function of the vagina?","options":["Helps in passage of menstrual blood","Produces ova","Secretes progesterone","Stores urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One listed function of the vagina is to help in the passage of menstrual blood.  Why B wrong: Ova are produced by the ovary.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is secreted by the corpus luteum/ovary.  Why D wrong: Urine is stored in the urinary bladder."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The vagina serves as the passage for the birth of the body and is therefore also called the:","options":["Birth canal","Cervical canal","Inguinal canal","Anal canal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the vagina is the passage for birth of the body → BIRTH canal.  Why B wrong: The cervical canal is within the cervix, not the whole passage.  Why C wrong: The inguinal canal is in the groin.  Why D wrong: The anal canal is the terminal part of the rectum."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the direction of the vagina?","options":["Upwards and backwards","Downwards and forwards","Upwards and forwards","Straight vertical"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Vagina direction = Upwards + Backwards.  Why B wrong: This is the opposite of the stated direction.  Why C wrong: The vagina passes backwards, not forwards.  Why D wrong: The vagina is directed obliquely, not straight vertical."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The vagina makes what angle with the ground when a female stands?","options":["45°","90°","30°","60°"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vagina makes an angle of 45° with the ground when the female stands.  Why B wrong: 90° would be vertical, which is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 30° is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 60° is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"In the mid-sagittal figure of the female pelvis, which structure lies immediately anterior to the vagina?","options":["Urinary bladder / urethra","Rectum","Anus","Ovary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the urinary bladder and urethra anterior to the vagina.  Why B wrong: The rectum lies posterior to the vagina.  Why C wrong: The anus lies posteroinferiorly.  Why D wrong: The ovary is located higher, near the uterine tube."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"In the female pelvic figure, the labia majora is located near which structure?","options":["Anus (posteriorly, lateral to the vaginal opening)","Ovary","Uterine body","Pubic symphysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the labia majora adjacent to the anus/vaginal opening region.  Why B wrong: The ovary is intra-abdominal near the uterine tube.  Why C wrong: The uterine body is superior.  Why D wrong: The pubic symphysis is anterior bony landmark."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The angle of anteflexion shown in the figure refers to the angle at which structure?","options":["Between the uterine body and the cervix","Between the vagina and the ground","Between the bladder and urethra","Between the ovary and uterine tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The angle of anteflexion is the forward bend of the uterine body over the cervix (shown in the figure of uterine direction).  Why B wrong: That is the 45° angle with the ground.  Why C wrong: No such angle is described.  Why D wrong: No such angle is described."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the shape of the gross cross-section of the vagina?","options":["H-shaped","O-shaped","Y-shaped","Triangular"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the gross section of the vagina is H-shaped and has 4 pockets.  Why B wrong: It is not O-shaped.  Why C wrong: It is not Y-shaped.  Why D wrong: It is not triangular."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The four pockets seen in the gross section of the vagina are collectively called:","options":["Fornix (fornices)","Cul-de-sac","Rugae","Recess of Morison"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The four pockets are called fornix (plural fornices).  Why B wrong: Cul-de-sac refers specifically to the pouch of Douglas, not all four pockets.  Why C wrong: Rugae are the vaginal folds, not the pockets.  Why D wrong: Morison's pouch is a hepatorenal space, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"How many fornices does the vagina have in total?","options":["4","2","3","6"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There are 4 fornices in total: anterior, posterior, left lateral, right lateral.  Why B wrong: There are more than 2.  Why C wrong: There are 4, not 3.  Why D wrong: There are 4, not 6."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four vaginal fornices?","options":["Inferior fornix","Anterior fornix","Posterior fornix","Left lateral fornix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There is no \"inferior\" fornix; the four are anterior, posterior, left lateral, and right lateral.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are all valid named fornices."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Which is the deepest vaginal fornix?","options":["Posterior fornix","Anterior fornix","Right lateral fornix","Left lateral fornix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the deepest fornix is the posterior fornix.  Why B wrong: The anterior fornix is the shallowest.  Why C, D wrong: The lateral fornices are intermediate, not deepest."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Which is the shallowest vaginal fornix?","options":["Anterior fornix","Posterior fornix","Right lateral fornix","Left lateral fornix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the shallowest fornix is the anterior fornix.  Why B wrong: The posterior fornix is the deepest.  Why C, D wrong: The lateral fornices are intermediate."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the length of the posterior wall of the vagina?","options":["9 cm","7 cm","5 cm","11 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior wall of the vagina measures 9 cm.  Why B wrong: 7 cm is the anterior wall length.  Why C, D wrong: Neither value is stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the length of the anterior wall of the vagina?","options":["7 cm","9 cm","6 cm","8 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior wall of the vagina measures 7 cm.  Why B wrong: 9 cm is the posterior wall length.  Why C, D wrong: Neither value is stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"By how much is the posterior wall of the vagina longer than the anterior wall?","options":["2 cm","1 cm","3 cm","4 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior wall (9 cm) is 2 cm longer than the anterior wall (7 cm).  Why B, C, D wrong: The difference is exactly 2 cm."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The posterior vaginal wall is always _______ than the anterior wall.","options":["Longer","Shorter","Thicker","Thinner"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior wall (9 cm) is always longer than the anterior wall (7 cm).  Why B wrong: It is longer, not shorter.  Why C, D wrong: The comparison concerns length, not thickness."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The local inhabitant (normal flora) bacteria of the vagina is:","options":["Lactobacillus acidophilus","Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus aureus","Clostridium perfringens"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vagina's local inhabitant bacteria is Lactobacillus acidophilus.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the normal vaginal flora described."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Lactobacillus acidophilus of the vagina is also known as:","options":["Doderlein bacteria/bacilli","Gardnerella bacilli","Bacillus vaginalis","Peg cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lactobacillus acidophilus is called Doderlein bacteria (Doderlein bacilli).  Why B wrong: Gardnerella is associated with bacterial vaginosis, not normal flora here.  Why C wrong: No such named bacillus is given.  Why D wrong: Peg cells are in the fallopian tube epithelium."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"At what stage of life do Doderlein bacilli appear in the vagina?","options":["At puberty","At birth","In childhood","At menopause"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Doderlein bacilli appear at puberty.  Why B, C wrong: They are absent in childhood/at birth.  Why D wrong: They disappear at menopause."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Doderlein bacilli remain in the vagina during which period?","options":["Throughout reproductive age","Only during pregnancy","Only during menstruation","Only in childhood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: They remain in the vagina throughout reproductive age.  Why B, C wrong: They persist throughout reproductive age, not only in those events.  Why D wrong: They are absent in childhood."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Doderlein bacilli disappear at which stage of life?","options":["Menopause","Puberty","Reproductive age","Pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Doderlein bacilli disappear at menopause.  Why B wrong: They appear at puberty.  Why C wrong: They are present during reproductive age.  Why D wrong: They increase in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The presence of Doderlein bacilli is related to the level of which hormone?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Doderlein bacilli are related to estrogen levels.  Why B, C, D wrong: The notes specifically link them to estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the function of Doderlein bacilli in the vagina?","options":["Convert glycogen in vaginal epithelial cells into lactic acid, making pH acidic","Produce mucus for lubrication","Digest sperm cells","Convert lactic acid into glycogen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Doderlein bacilli convert the glycogen present in vaginal epithelial cells into lactic acid, making vaginal pH acidic.  Why B wrong: They do not produce mucus.  Why C wrong: They do not digest sperm.  Why D wrong: The direction is glycogen → lactic acid, not the reverse."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"By making the vaginal pH acidic, Doderlein bacilli provide which benefit?","options":["Fewer pathogenic organisms can enter the vagina (natural defence mechanism)","Increase menstrual flow","Promote bacterial overgrowth","Raise vaginal pH to alkaline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The acidic pH means less pathogenic organisms can enter the vagina — a natural defence mechanism.  Why B wrong: Not related to menstrual flow.  Why C wrong: The opposite — it limits pathogens.  Why D wrong: It makes pH acidic, not alkaline."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the pH of the vagina during childhood?","options":["Slightly alkaline (6–8)","Acidic (4–5)","Strongly acidic (3.5)","Neutral (7 only)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In childhood the vaginal pH is slightly alkaline (6–8), with no Doderlein bacilli.  Why B wrong: 4–5 is the reproductive-age pH.  Why C wrong: 3.5 is the pregnancy pH.  Why D wrong: The stated range is 6–8, not neutral 7."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"During childhood, the state of Doderlein bacilli in the vagina is:","options":["Absent (no Doderlein bacilli)","Present in large numbers","Increased","Just appearing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In childhood there are no Doderlein bacilli.  Why B, C wrong: They are absent in childhood.  Why D wrong: They appear at puberty, not childhood."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"At puberty, the vaginal pH changes from:","options":["Alkaline pH to acidic pH","Acidic pH to alkaline pH","Neutral to alkaline","No change"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At puberty the vaginal pH changes from alkaline to acidic (with Doderlein bacilli appearing).  Why B wrong: The direction is alkaline → acidic.  Why C, D wrong: There is a definite change to acidic."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the vaginal pH during reproductive age?","options":["4–5 (acidic)","6–8 (slightly alkaline)","3.5","7.4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During reproductive age the vaginal pH is 4–5 (acidic), with Doderlein bacilli present.  Why B wrong: 6–8 is childhood/menopause pH.  Why C wrong: 3.5 is the pregnancy value.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the vaginal pH during menopause?","options":["Slightly alkaline (6–8)","Acidic (4–5)","3.5","Strongly acidic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During menopause the vaginal pH becomes slightly alkaline (6–8), with Doderlein bacilli disappearing.  Why B wrong: 4–5 is reproductive-age acidic pH.  Why C wrong: 3.5 is the pregnancy value.  Why D wrong: It is slightly alkaline, not acidic."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"During menopause, the status of Doderlein bacilli is:","options":["Disappear","Present","Increase","Just appear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At menopause the Doderlein bacilli disappear.  Why B, C wrong: They are gone at menopause.  Why D wrong: They appear at puberty."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"In pregnancy, what happens to the number of Doderlein bacilli in the vagina?","options":["Increases","Decreases","Remains unchanged","Disappears completely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In pregnancy the number of Doderlein bacilli increases.  Why B, C, D wrong: The notes clearly show an increase during pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"As Doderlein bacilli increase in pregnancy, the conversion of glycogen into lactic acid:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Stops","Reverses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Increased Doderlein bacilli convert more glycogen into lactic acid.  Why B, C, D wrong: The conversion increases."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"During pregnancy, the acidity of the vagina:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Remains neutral","Becomes alkaline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In pregnancy the acidity of the vagina increases.  Why B, D wrong: Acidity increases (pH falls).  Why C wrong: It does not become neutral."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the vaginal pH during pregnancy?","options":["3.5","4–5","6–8","7.4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In pregnancy the pH decreases to 3.5.  Why B wrong: 4–5 is the non-pregnant reproductive-age pH.  Why C wrong: 6–8 is childhood/menopause.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"In pregnancy the vaginal pH falls to 3.5. This is because pH:","options":["Decreases (becomes more acidic)","Increases","Stays the same","Becomes alkaline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: With increased acidity the pH decreases (↓) to 3.5.  Why B, C, D wrong: A lower pH value means increased acidity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"At the time of menstruation, the pH of menstrual blood entering the vagina is:","options":["Alkaline","Acidic","Neutral","Strongly acidic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Menstrual blood entering the vagina has an alkaline pH.  Why B, D wrong: Menstrual blood is alkaline, not acidic.  Why C wrong: It is alkaline, not neutral."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"During menstruation, the vaginal pH becomes:","options":["Alkaline (6–8)","Acidic (4–5)","3.5","Neutral (7)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Because alkaline menstrual blood enters, the pH of the vagina becomes alkaline (6–8) during menstruation.  Why B wrong: 4–5 is the usual reproductive-age acidic pH.  Why C wrong: 3.5 is the pregnancy value.  Why D wrong: The stated range is 6–8."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The vagina is lined by which type of epithelium?","options":["Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Columnar epithelium","Ciliated columnar epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vagina is lined by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.  Why B wrong: Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium lines the vulva.  Why C wrong: Columnar epithelium lines the endocervix/uterus.  Why D wrong: Ciliated columnar epithelium lines the fallopian tube."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Which of the following is TRUE about the glands of the vagina?","options":["The vagina does not have any glands","The vagina has numerous mucous glands","The vagina contains Bartholin glands within its wall","The vagina has sebaceous glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the vagina does not have any glands.  Why B, D wrong: The vagina has no glands.  Why C wrong: Bartholin glands are at the vaginal orifice/vulva, not within the vaginal wall itself."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Vaginal discharge is made up of which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cervical discharge","Endometrial gland discharge","Bartholin gland discharge (only at time of intercourse)","Vaginal gland discharge","Ovarian discharge"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Vaginal discharge = cervical discharge (most common) + endometrial gland discharge + Bartholin gland discharge (only at time of intercourse).  Why D wrong: The vagina has no glands, so there is no vaginal gland discharge.  Why E wrong: No ovarian discharge component is listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Which is the most common component of vaginal discharge?","options":["Cervical discharge","Endometrial gland discharge","Bartholin gland discharge","Vaginal gland discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cervical discharge is labelled as the most common component.  Why B wrong: Endometrial discharge is a component but not the most common.  Why C wrong: Bartholin discharge occurs only at the time of intercourse.  Why D wrong: The vagina has no glands."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Bartholin gland discharge contributes to vaginal discharge only during:","options":["Time of intercourse","Menstruation","Pregnancy","Ovulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bartholin gland discharge occurs only at the time of intercourse.  Why B, C, D wrong: The notes specify only at the time of intercourse."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Which wall of the vagina does NOT have peritoneum covering it?","options":["Anterior wall","Posterior wall","Both walls","Lateral wall"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior wall of the vagina does not have peritoneum.  Why B wrong: The posterior wall has peritoneum.  Why C wrong: Only the posterior wall is covered.  Why D wrong: The notes address anterior vs posterior walls specifically."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Which wall of the vagina is covered by peritoneum?","options":["Posterior wall","Anterior wall","Neither wall","Lateral wall only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior wall of the vagina has peritoneum.  Why B wrong: The anterior wall does not have peritoneum.  Why C, D wrong: The posterior wall specifically has peritoneum."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The peritoneal pouch located between the urinary bladder and the uterus is called the:","options":["Utero-vesical pouch","Rectouterine pouch","Pouch of Douglas","Cul-de-sac"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The utero-vesical pouch lies between the bladder and the uterus, as labelled in the peritoneum figure.  Why B, C, D wrong: The rectouterine pouch / pouch of Douglas / cul-de-sac lies between the uterus and the rectum."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The rectouterine pouch is also known by which of the following names? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pouch of Douglas","Cul-de-sac","Utero-vesical pouch","Recess of Morison","Rectovesical pouch"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The rectouterine pouch is also called the pouch of Douglas and the cul-de-sac.  Why C wrong: The utero-vesical pouch is a different pouch (between bladder and uterus).  Why D wrong: Morison's pouch is a hepatorenal space.  Why E wrong: The rectovesical pouch is a male pelvic pouch."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"In the vaginal fornix figure, which fornix is directly related to the peritoneum of the pouch of Douglas?","options":["Posterior fornix","Anterior fornix","Lateral fornix","Vaginal orifice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure shows the posterior fornix related to the peritoneum, which is why the posterior fornix is used to access the pouch of Douglas.  Why B wrong: The anterior fornix relates to the bladder region.  Why C wrong: The lateral fornices are not related to the peritoneal pouch here.  Why D wrong: The vaginal orifice is the external opening."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The Pouch of Douglas is a pouch located between which two structures?","options":["Uterus and rectum","Bladder and uterus","Uterus and ovary","Vagina and bladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pouch of Douglas is a pouch between the uterus and the rectum.  Why B wrong: That is the utero-vesical pouch.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the described location."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"In Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID), what is present in the pouch of Douglas?","options":["Fluid","Blood","Air","Nothing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In PID, fluid is present in the pouch of Douglas.  Why B wrong: Blood in the pouch of Douglas indicates ectopic pregnancy, not PID.  Why C, D wrong: Fluid accumulates in PID."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"In an ectopic pregnancy, what is present in the pouch of Douglas?","options":["Blood","Clear fluid","Pus","Air"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In ectopic pregnancy, blood is present in the pouch of Douglas.  Why B wrong: Fluid in the pouch of Douglas is a feature of PID.  Why C, D wrong: Blood, not pus/air, is the finding."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"To confirm blood in the pouch of Douglas in suspected ectopic pregnancy, a syringe is passed from which structure into the pouch of Douglas?","options":["Posterior fornix","Anterior fornix","Cervical os","Abdominal wall"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A syringe is passed from the posterior fornix into the pouch of Douglas to confirm blood.  Why B wrong: The anterior fornix is not used; the pouch is posterior.  Why C wrong: Not through the cervical os.  Why D wrong: This is a transvaginal procedure via the posterior fornix."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The procedure of passing a syringe/needle from the posterior fornix into the pouch of Douglas to aspirate fluid is called:","options":["Culdocentesis","Amniocentesis","Paracentesis","Thoracocentesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This procedure is called culdocentesis.  Why B wrong: Amniocentesis samples amniotic fluid.  Why C wrong: Paracentesis drains peritoneal (abdominal) fluid via the abdominal wall.  Why D wrong: Thoracocentesis drains pleural fluid."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Culdocentesis is performed to check for:","options":["Abnormal fluid in the abdominal cavity behind the uterus","Amniotic fluid abnormalities","Pleural effusion","Cerebrospinal fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Culdocentesis checks for abnormal fluid in the abdominal cavity behind the uterus.  Why B, C, D wrong: These involve other body cavities/procedures."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the lining epithelium of the vulva?","options":["Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Columnar epithelium","Germinal epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vulva is lined by keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.  Why B wrong: Non-keratinized stratified squamous lines the vagina.  Why C wrong: Columnar epithelium lines the endocervix/uterus.  Why D wrong: Germinal epithelium lines the ovary."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The exocervix is lined by epithelium that is:","options":["Same as vagina (non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium)","Columnar epithelium","Keratinized squamous epithelium","Ciliated columnar epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The exocervix has the same lining as the vagina (non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium).  Why B wrong: The endocervix, not exocervix, is columnar.  Why C wrong: Keratinized lining is for the vulva.  Why D wrong: Ciliated columnar lines the fallopian tube."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The endocervix is lined by which epithelium?","options":["Columnar epithelium","Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Germinal epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The endocervix is lined by columnar epithelium.  Why B wrong: That is the exocervix/vagina lining.  Why C wrong: Keratinized lining is for the vulva.  Why D wrong: Germinal epithelium lines the ovary."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The uterus is lined by which type of epithelium?","options":["Columnar epithelium","Squamous epithelium","Ciliated columnar epithelium","Germinal epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The uterus is lined by columnar epithelium.  Why B wrong: Squamous epithelium lines the vagina/vulva/exocervix.  Why C wrong: Ciliated columnar (with peg cells) lines the fallopian tube.  Why D wrong: Germinal epithelium lines the ovary."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The fallopian tube is lined by which epithelium?","options":["Ciliated columnar epithelium (with peg cells)","Columnar epithelium without cilia","Non-keratinized squamous epithelium","Germinal epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The fallopian tube is lined by ciliated columnar epithelium and has peg cells.  Why B wrong: The fallopian tube epithelium is ciliated and has peg cells.  Why C wrong: Squamous lining is for vagina/exocervix.  Why D wrong: Germinal epithelium lines the ovary."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"Which specialized cells are present in the ciliated columnar epithelium of the fallopian tube?","options":["Peg cells","Goblet cells","Paneth cells","Kupffer cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The fallopian tube epithelium contains peg cells.  Why B wrong: Goblet cells are in the GI/respiratory tract.  Why C wrong: Paneth cells are in the small intestine.  Why D wrong: Kupffer cells are in the liver."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The ovary is covered by which type of epithelium?","options":["Germinal epithelium","Columnar epithelium","Ciliated columnar epithelium","Keratinized squamous epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary is covered by germinal epithelium.  Why B, C wrong: Columnar/ciliated columnar line the uterus/tube.  Why D wrong: Keratinized squamous lines the vulva."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"According to the notes, the most common variety of cancer of any organ depends on which factor?","options":["Its lining epithelium","Its blood supply","Its nerve supply","Its hormonal receptors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The concept states the most common variety of cancer of any organ depends on its lining epithelium.  Why B, C, D wrong: The notes specify lining epithelium as the determinant."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the most common (m/c) cancer of the vagina?","options":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Germ cell tumor","Transitional cell carcinoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Because the vagina is lined by squamous epithelium, its most common cancer is squamous cell carcinoma.  Why B wrong: Adenocarcinoma arises in columnar (glandular) epithelium such as the uterus.  Why C wrong: Germ cell tumors are ovarian.  Why D wrong: Transitional cell carcinoma is of the urinary tract."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"The most common cancer of the uterus is adenocarcinoma because the uterus is lined by:","options":["Columnar epithelium","Squamous epithelium","Germinal epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The uterus is lined by columnar epithelium, so its most common cancer is adenocarcinoma.  Why B wrong: Squamous epithelium would give squamous cell carcinoma.  Why C wrong: Germinal epithelium is of the ovary.  Why D wrong: Transitional epithelium is of the urinary tract."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"What is the most common cancer of the fallopian tube?","options":["Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Germ cell tumor","Sarcoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common fallopian tube cancer is adenocarcinoma (its epithelium is columnar/ciliated columnar).  Why B wrong: Squamous cell carcinoma corresponds to squamous-lined organs like the vagina.  Why C wrong: Germ cell tumors are ovarian.  Why D wrong: Sarcoma arises from connective tissue."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"A clinician suspects a ruptured ectopic pregnancy and aspirates non-clotting blood from the pouch of Douglas via the posterior fornix. This procedure and finding indicate:","options":["Positive culdocentesis suggestive of hemoperitoneum from ectopic pregnancy","Normal finding","PID with pus","Amniotic fluid leak"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspiration of blood from the pouch of Douglas via the posterior fornix (culdocentesis) supports ruptured ectopic pregnancy with blood collecting behind the uterus.  Why B wrong: Blood in the pouch is abnormal.  Why C wrong: PID gives fluid, not blood.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid is not aspirated by culdocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Anatomy of vagina (structure, function, direction, angle)…","question":"A woman in her reproductive years has a vaginal pH measured at 4.5. This acidic pH is primarily due to:","options":["Doderlein bacilli converting glycogen to lactic acid","Menstrual blood","Bartholin gland secretions","Alkaline cervical mucus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In reproductive age (pH 4–5), Doderlein bacilli convert glycogen to lactic acid, keeping the pH acidic.  Why B wrong: Menstrual blood is alkaline and would raise the pH.  Why C wrong: Bartholin secretions occur at intercourse and do not set baseline pH.  Why D wrong: Alkaline mucus would raise, not lower, pH."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"What is the blood supply of the ovary?","options":["Uterine artery","Ovarian artery","Internal pudendal artery","Middle rectal artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary is supplied by the ovarian artery.  Why A wrong: The uterine artery is the main supply of the uterus, not the ovary.  Why C wrong: The internal pudendal artery supplies the vulva.  Why D wrong: The middle rectal artery contributes to vaginal (not ovarian) supply."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The ovarian artery is a branch of which vessel?","options":["Internal iliac artery","Abdominal aorta","Common iliac artery","Uterine artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ovarian artery arises directly as a branch of the abdominal aorta.  Why A wrong: The internal iliac artery supplies the uterus (anterior division), not the ovarian artery origin.  Why C wrong: The common iliac artery is not the direct origin of the ovarian artery.  Why D wrong: The uterine artery is a separate vessel supplying the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"What is the MAIN arterial blood supply of the uterus?","options":["Ovarian artery","Uterine artery","Internal pudendal artery","Descending cervical artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The main blood supply of the uterus is the uterine artery.  Why A wrong: The ovarian artery only additionally contributes to uterine supply; it is not the main.  Why C wrong: The internal pudendal artery supplies the vulva.  Why D wrong: The descending cervical artery supplies the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The uterine artery is a branch of which division of the internal iliac artery?","options":["Posterior division","Anterior division","Both anterior and posterior divisions","It is not a branch of the internal iliac artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The uterine artery is a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery.  Why A wrong: It arises from the anterior, not the posterior, division.  Why C wrong: It arises specifically from the anterior division only.  Why D wrong: The uterine artery is indeed a branch of the internal iliac artery."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Besides the uterine artery, the uterus also receives blood supply from which vessel?","options":["Middle rectal artery","Internal pudendal artery","Ovarian artery","Descending cervical artery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The uterus is also supplied by the ovarian artery in addition to the uterine artery.  Why A wrong: The middle rectal artery contributes to vaginal supply.  Why B wrong: The internal pudendal artery supplies the vulva.  Why D wrong: The descending cervical artery supplies the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The medial part of the fallopian tube is supplied by which artery?","options":["Ovarian artery","Uterine artery","Internal pudendal artery","Middle rectal artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The medial part of the fallopian tube is supplied by the uterine artery.  Why A wrong: The ovarian artery supplies the lateral part of the fallopian tube.  Why C wrong: The internal pudendal artery supplies the vulva.  Why D wrong: The middle rectal artery contributes to vaginal supply."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The lateral part of the fallopian tube is supplied by which artery?","options":["Uterine artery","Ovarian artery","Descending cervical artery","Internal pudendal artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lateral part of the fallopian tube is supplied by the ovarian artery.  Why A wrong: The uterine artery supplies the medial part of the fallopian tube.  Why C wrong: The descending cervical artery supplies the cervix.  Why D wrong: The internal pudendal artery supplies the vulva."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The descending cervical artery, which supplies the cervix, is a branch of which artery?","options":["Ovarian artery","Internal pudendal artery","Uterine artery","Middle rectal artery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The cervix is supplied by the descending cervical artery, which is a branch of the uterine artery.  Why A wrong: The ovarian artery supplies the ovary and lateral fallopian tube.  Why B wrong: The internal pudendal artery supplies the vulva.  Why D wrong: The middle rectal artery contributes to vaginal supply."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Which of the following arteries supply the vagina? (Select all that apply)","options":["Descending cervical artery","Internal pudendal artery","Middle rectal artery","Ovarian artery","Renal artery"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The vagina is supplied by the descending cervical artery, internal pudendal artery, and middle rectal artery.  Why D wrong: The ovarian artery supplies the ovary and lateral fallopian tube, not the vagina.  Why E wrong: The renal artery supplies the kidney and has no role in vaginal blood supply."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"What is the blood supply of the vulva?","options":["Uterine artery","Ovarian artery","Internal pudendal artery","Middle rectal artery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The vulva is supplied by the internal pudendal artery.  Why A wrong: The uterine artery supplies the uterus.  Why B wrong: The ovarian artery supplies the ovary.  Why D wrong: The middle rectal artery contributes to vaginal supply only."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"In a patient with postpartum hemorrhage (PPH) where medicines have failed and bleeding is from the uterus, what is the underlying principle for surgically stopping the bleeding?","options":["Increase the blood supply of the uterus","Stop the blood supply of the uterus so bleeding will stop","Remove the ovaries","Ligate the renal artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the blood supply of the uterus is stopped (by ligating its arteries), the bleeding will stop.  Why A wrong: Increasing blood supply would worsen the hemorrhage.  Why C wrong: Removing the ovaries does not control uterine bleeding.  Why D wrong: The renal artery is unrelated to uterine blood supply."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Which arteries should be ligated in a case of Post Partum Hemorrhage (PPH)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Uterine artery","Ovarian artery","Anterior division of internal iliac artery","Middle rectal artery","Renal artery"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: In PPH the arteries ligated are the uterine artery, ovarian artery, and the anterior division of the internal iliac artery — all of which supply the uterus.  Why D wrong: The middle rectal artery supplies the vagina, not primarily the uterus, and is not listed for PPH ligation.  Why E wrong: The renal artery has no role in uterine blood supply."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Which endoscopic procedure is used to look inside the abdominal cavity (outer uterus)?","options":["Hysteroscopy","Laparoscopy","Colposcopy","Salpingoscopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Laparoscopy is used to visualize the abdominal cavity (outer surface of the uterus).  Why A wrong: Hysteroscopy looks inside the uterine cavity.  Why C wrong: Colposcopy examines the cervix and vagina.  Why D wrong: Salpingoscopy examines the fallopian tube."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Which procedure is used to look inside the uterus?","options":["Laparoscopy","Hysteroscopy","Colposcopy","Salpingoscopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hysteroscopy is used to visualize the interior of the uterus.  Why A wrong: Laparoscopy visualizes the abdominal cavity/outer uterus.  Why C wrong: Colposcopy examines the cervix and vagina.  Why D wrong: Salpingoscopy examines the fallopian tube."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Colposcopy is used to examine which structures?","options":["Abdominal cavity","Uterine cavity","Cervix and vagina","Fallopian tube"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Colposcopy is used to look at the cervix and vagina.  Why A wrong: The abdominal cavity is examined by laparoscopy.  Why B wrong: The uterine cavity is examined by hysteroscopy.  Why D wrong: The fallopian tube is examined by salpingoscopy."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"During colposcopy, which part of the cervix is NOT seen?","options":["Exocervix","Vagina","Endocervix","Vaginal wall"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: On colposcopy, the exocervix and vagina are seen but the endocervix is NOT seen.  Why A wrong: The exocervix is visible during colposcopy.  Why B wrong: The vagina is visible during colposcopy.  Why D wrong: The vaginal wall is part of the vagina, which is visualized."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Which procedure is used to look inside the fallopian tube?","options":["Laparoscopy","Hysteroscopy","Colposcopy","Salpingoscopy"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Salpingoscopy is used to examine the fallopian tube.  Why A wrong: Laparoscopy examines the abdominal cavity/outer uterus.  Why B wrong: Hysteroscopy examines the uterine cavity.  Why C wrong: Colposcopy examines the cervix and vagina."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The sex of the fetus is decided primarily by which factor?","options":["Maternal hormones","A gene (SRY gene)","Placental weight","Amniotic fluid volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sex of the fetus is decided by a gene — specifically the SRY gene.  Why A wrong: Maternal hormones do not determine genetic sex.  Why C wrong: Placental weight is unrelated to sex determination.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid volume does not decide fetal sex."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"What does \"SRY\" stand for in sex determination?","options":["Sex Reproductive Yield","Sex Related Region on the Y chromosome","Steroid Receptor of Y","Sex Ratio Yield"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SRY stands for the Sex Related Region present on the Y chromosome (the SRY gene).  Why A wrong: This is not the correct expansion.  Why C wrong: SRY is not a steroid receptor.  Why D wrong: This is not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"On which part of the Y chromosome is the SRY gene located?","options":["Long arm of Y chromosome","Short arm of Y chromosome","Centromere of Y chromosome","Telomere of X chromosome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The SRY gene is located on the short arm of the Y chromosome.  Why A wrong: It is on the short arm, not the long arm.  Why C wrong: It is not located on the centromere.  Why D wrong: It is on the Y chromosome, not the X chromosome."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"If the SRY gene is present, what will the sex of the baby be?","options":["Female","Male","Intersex","Depends on maternal karyotype"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the SRY gene is present, the baby develops as male.  Why A wrong: Absence of SRY (Y chromosome absent) leads to female.  Why C wrong: The flowchart gives a binary outcome for SRY presence/absence.  Why D wrong: Sex is determined by the fetal SRY gene, not the maternal karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"If the SRY gene is absent (Y chromosome absent), what will the sex of the baby be?","options":["Male","Female","Intersex","Cannot be determined"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the SRY gene is absent and the Y chromosome is absent, the baby develops as female.  Why A wrong: Male development requires the SRY gene.  Why C wrong: The flowchart gives a binary outcome.  Why D wrong: Absence of SRY/Y clearly leads to female."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"A baby with karyotype 46(XY) will have which sex?","options":["Female","Male","Turner syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 46(XY) karyotype corresponds to a male baby.  Why A wrong: Female corresponds to 46(XX).  Why C wrong: Turner syndrome is 45(XO).  Why D wrong: Klinefelter syndrome is 47(XXY)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"A baby with karyotype 46(XX), with Y chromosome absent, will have which sex?","options":["Male","Female","Turner syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 46(XX) karyotype with the Y chromosome absent corresponds to a female baby.  Why A wrong: Male corresponds to 46(XY).  Why C wrong: Turner syndrome is 45(XO).  Why D wrong: Klinefelter syndrome is 47(XXY)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"A baby with karyotype 45(XO) and absent Y chromosome develops as a female with which syndrome?","options":["Klinefelter syndrome","Turner syndrome","Down syndrome","Mullerian agenesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 45(XO) karyotype (Y chromosome absent) is a female with Turner syndrome.  Why A wrong: Klinefelter syndrome is 47(XXY).  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is trisomy 21, not described here.  Why D wrong: Mullerian agenesis is absence of Mullerian ducts, a separate condition."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"A baby with karyotype 47(XXY) develops with which syndrome and which sex?","options":["Turner syndrome, female","Klinefelter syndrome, male","Down syndrome, male","Mullerian agenesis, female"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 47(XXY) karyotype is Klinefelter syndrome, and because a Y chromosome is present the sex is male.  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome is 45(XO) and female.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is trisomy 21.  Why D wrong: Mullerian agenesis is a Mullerian duct absence condition, unrelated to karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Up until how many weeks of intrauterine life do the gonads develop from the genital ridge and retain the capacity to become either testis or ovaries?","options":["4 weeks","6 weeks","8 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Until 6 weeks of intrauterine life, the gonads develop from the genital ridge and are bipotential (can become testis or ovaries).  Why A wrong: The stated cut-off is 6 weeks, not 4.  Why C wrong: 8 weeks is beyond the bipotential stage.  Why D wrong: 12 weeks is well beyond the bipotential window."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"After 6 weeks, if the Y chromosome is present, the gonads form which structure and by when?","options":["Ovaries by 7–8 weeks","Testis by 6–7 weeks","Testis by 12 weeks","Ovaries by 6 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the Y chromosome is present, the gonads form testis, which are formed by 6–7 weeks.  Why A wrong: Ovaries form when the Y chromosome is absent.  Why C wrong: Testis are formed by 6–7 weeks, not 12 weeks.  Why D wrong: Ovaries form by 7–8 weeks and only when Y is absent."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"After 6 weeks, if the Y chromosome is absent, the gonads form which structure and by when?","options":["Testis by 6–7 weeks","Ovaries by 7–8 weeks","Ovaries by 6 weeks","Testis by 7–8 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the Y chromosome is absent, the gonads form ovaries, which are formed by 7–8 weeks.  Why A wrong: Testis form when the Y chromosome is present.  Why C wrong: Ovaries are formed by 7–8 weeks, not by 6 weeks.  Why D wrong: Testis (not ovaries) form when Y is present, by 6–7 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"According to the concept described, the presence of ovaries depends on which factor?","options":["Presence of the Y chromosome","Absence of the Y chromosome","Presence of the SRY gene","Presence of two Y chromosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The presence of ovaries depends on the absence of the Y chromosome.  Why A wrong: Presence of the Y chromosome leads to testis formation.  Why C wrong: The SRY gene drives male/testis development.  Why D wrong: Ovary formation is linked to absence of Y, not presence of extra Y."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"For proper (complete) development of the ovary, what is required?","options":["Only one X chromosome","Both X chromosomes","One X and one Y chromosome","The SRY gene"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Although absence of Y allows ovary formation, both X chromosomes are needed for proper development of the ovary.  Why A wrong: Only one X leads to improper ovarian development (streak gonads).  Why C wrong: A Y chromosome would drive testis formation.  Why D wrong: The SRY gene drives male development, not ovarian development."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"In Turner syndrome (45 XO), the ovary is improperly developed and forms what?","options":["Testis","Streak gonads","Normal ovaries","Ovotestis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Turner syndrome (45 XO), the Y chromosome is absent so an ovary begins to develop, but since only 1 X chromosome is present the ovary is not properly developed and forms STREAK GONADS.  Why A wrong: Testis require a Y chromosome, which is absent in Turner syndrome.  Why C wrong: With only 1 X the ovaries do not develop normally.  Why D wrong: The condition results in streak gonads, not ovotestis."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"From where do germ cells originate?","options":["Endoderm","Epiblast","Genital ridge","Mullerian duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Germ cells originate from the epiblast.  Why A wrong: Vaginal epithelium (not germ cells) is derived from endoderm of the urogenital sinus.  Why C wrong: The gonads/ovary develop from the genital ridge, but germ cells originate from the epiblast.  Why D wrong: The Mullerian duct forms female internal genital organs, not germ cells."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Until how many weeks are germ cells the same in male and female?","options":["4 weeks","6 weeks","8 weeks","10 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Till 6 weeks, germ cells in male and female are the same.  Why A wrong: The stated point is 6 weeks.  Why C wrong: Differentiation occurs after 6 weeks.  Why D wrong: 10 weeks is beyond the point of divergence."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"When the Y chromosome is present, what are the germ cells called?","options":["Oogonia","Spermatogonia","Oocytes","Spermatids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the Y chromosome is present (male), the germ cells are spermatogonia.  Why A wrong: Oogonia are the female germ cells (Y absent).  Why C wrong: Oocytes are a later female stage, not the term used here.  Why D wrong: Spermatids are a later male stage, not the term used here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"When the Y chromosome is absent, what are the germ cells called?","options":["Spermatogonia","Oogonia","Spermatids","Oocytes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the Y chromosome is absent (female), the germ cells are oogonia.  Why A wrong: Spermatogonia are the male germ cells (Y present).  Why C wrong: Spermatids are a male stage.  Why D wrong: Oocytes are a later female stage, not the term used here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"In early intrauterine life, how many pairs of ducts are present in both males and females?","options":["1 pair","2 pairs","3 pairs","4 pairs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In early intrauterine life, 2 pairs of ducts are present in both males and females.  Why A wrong: There are two pairs, not one.  Why C wrong: There are two pairs, not three.  Why D wrong: There are two pairs, not four."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The Mullerian duct is also known as which duct?","options":["Mesonephric duct","Paramesonephric duct","Metanephric duct","Pronephric duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Mullerian duct is also called the paramesonephric duct.  Why A wrong: The mesonephric duct is the Wolffian duct.  Why C wrong: The metanephric duct is associated with kidney development, not this pair.  Why D wrong: The pronephric duct is not the Mullerian duct."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The Wolffian duct is also known as which duct?","options":["Paramesonephric duct","Mesonephric duct","Metanephric duct","Pronephric duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Wolffian duct is also called the mesonephric duct.  Why A wrong: The paramesonephric duct is the Mullerian duct.  Why C wrong: The metanephric duct is not the Wolffian duct.  Why D wrong: The pronephric duct is not the Wolffian duct."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"In the male, which duct grows and which regresses?","options":["Mullerian duct grows; Wolffian duct regresses","Wolffian duct grows; Mullerian duct regresses","Both grow","Both regress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the male, the Wolffian duct grows and the Mullerian duct regresses.  Why A wrong: This describes the female pattern.  Why C wrong: One grows and one regresses.  Why D wrong: One grows and one regresses."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"In the female, which duct grows and which regresses?","options":["Wolffian duct grows; Mullerian duct regresses","Mullerian duct grows; Wolffian duct regresses","Both grow","Both regress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the female, the Mullerian duct grows and the Wolffian duct regresses.  Why A wrong: This describes the male pattern.  Why C wrong: One grows and one regresses.  Why D wrong: One grows and one regresses."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"In the male, the growing Wolffian duct forms which internal genital organs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Seminal vesicles","Epididymis","Ejaculatory duct","Vas deferens","Fallopian tube"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The Wolffian duct forms male internal genital organs — the mnemonic \"SEED\": Seminal vesicles, Epididymis, Ejaculatory duct, and Vas deferens.  Why E wrong: The fallopian tube is a female internal organ formed from the Mullerian duct."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"In the female, the growing Mullerian duct forms which internal genital organs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fallopian tube","Uterus","Cervix","Upper 2/3rd of vagina","Vas deferens"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The Mullerian duct forms the female internal organs: fallopian tube, uterus, cervix, and the upper 2/3rd of the vagina.  Why E wrong: The vas deferens is a male organ formed from the Wolffian duct."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The upper 2/3rd of the vagina is formed by which structure?","options":["Urogenital sinus","Mullerian duct","Wolffian duct","Genital ridge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The upper 2/3rd of the vagina is formed by the Mullerian duct.  Why A wrong: The urogenital sinus forms the lower 1/3rd of the vagina.  Why C wrong: The Wolffian duct forms male organs and regresses in females.  Why D wrong: The genital ridge forms the ovary."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The lower 1/3rd of the vagina is formed by which structure?","options":["Mullerian duct","Urogenital sinus","Wolffian duct","Genital ridge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lower 1/3rd of the vagina is formed by the urogenital sinus.  Why A wrong: The Mullerian duct forms the upper 2/3rd of the vagina.  Why C wrong: The Wolffian duct forms male organs.  Why D wrong: The genital ridge forms the ovary."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The vaginal epithelium is derived from which germ layer of the urogenital sinus?","options":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The vaginal epithelium is derived from the endoderm of the urogenital sinus.  Why A wrong: It is derived from endoderm, not ectoderm.  Why B wrong: It is derived from endoderm, not mesoderm.  Why D wrong: It is derived from endoderm, not neural crest."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The ovary is formed from which structure?","options":["Mullerian duct","Wolffian duct","Genital ridge","Urogenital sinus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary is formed from the genital ridge.  Why A wrong: The Mullerian duct forms the fallopian tube, uterus, cervix, and upper vagina.  Why B wrong: The Wolffian duct forms male internal organs.  Why D wrong: The urogenital sinus forms the lower 1/3rd of the vagina."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"A female in whom both Mullerian ducts are absent has which condition?","options":["Turner syndrome","Mullerian agenesis","Klinefelter syndrome","Streak gonads"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When both Mullerian ducts are absent in a female, the condition is called Mullerian agenesis.  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome is a 45(XO) karyotype disorder.  Why C wrong: Klinefelter syndrome is a 47(XXY) male disorder.  Why D wrong: Streak gonads describe the ovarian finding in Turner syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"In Mullerian agenesis, which of the following structures are ABSENT? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fallopian tubes","Uterus","Cervix","Upper 2/3rd of vagina","Ovaries"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: In Mullerian agenesis the Mullerian-derived structures are absent: fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper 2/3rd of vagina.  Why E wrong: The ovaries are normal in Mullerian agenesis because they arise from the genital ridge, not the Mullerian duct."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"In Mullerian agenesis, the absence of the uterus leads to which clinical presentation?","options":["Secondary amenorrhea","No menstruation → primary amenorrhea","Menorrhagia","Dysmenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Absence of the uterus means no menstruation, presenting as primary amenorrhea.  Why A wrong: It is primary (never menstruated), not secondary amenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Menorrhagia (heavy bleeding) cannot occur without a uterus.  Why D wrong: Dysmenorrhea (painful menses) does not occur without menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"In Mullerian agenesis, which structures remain NORMAL? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ovaries","Ovulation","Lower 1/3rd of vagina","Uterus","Cervix"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: In Mullerian agenesis the ovaries are normal, ovulation is normal, and the lower 1/3rd of the vagina (from the urogenital sinus) is normal.  Why D wrong: The uterus is absent in Mullerian agenesis.  Why E wrong: The cervix (Mullerian-derived) is absent."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Why do patients with Mullerian agenesis also get renal (kidney) anomalies?","options":["Because the ovaries fail","Because Mullerian ducts also form some parts of the kidney","Because of primary amenorrhea","Because the Wolffian duct forms the kidney"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Patients with Mullerian agenesis also get renal anomalies because the Mullerian ducts also form some parts of the kidney.  Why A wrong: The ovaries are normal in Mullerian agenesis.  Why C wrong: Primary amenorrhea is a consequence, not the cause of renal anomalies.  Why D wrong: The source attributes the renal anomaly to Mullerian (not Wolffian) duct involvement."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"What are homologous organs?","options":["Organs present only in males","Organs in male and female that have the same embryological origin","Organs that perform the same function","Organs derived from the Wolffian duct only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Homologous organs are organs in the male and female that have the same embryological origin.  Why A wrong: They are present in both sexes.  Why C wrong: They share embryological origin, not necessarily identical function.  Why D wrong: They are not limited to Wolffian duct derivatives."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The male penis and the female clitoris are homologous organs derived from which embryological structure?","options":["Genital swelling","Genital tubercle","Genital fold","Genital ridge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The penis (male) and clitoris (female) are both derived from the genital tubercle.  Why A wrong: The genital swelling gives rise to the scrotum/labia majora.  Why C wrong: The genital fold gives rise to the penile urethra/labia minora.  Why D wrong: The genital ridge forms the ovary."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The scrotum (male) and labia majora (female) are homologous, both derived from which structure?","options":["Genital tubercle","Genital swelling","Genital fold","Urogenital sinus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The scrotum and labia majora both develop from the genital swelling.  Why A wrong: The genital tubercle gives rise to the penis/clitoris.  Why C wrong: The genital fold gives rise to the penile urethra/labia minora.  Why D wrong: The urogenital sinus forms the lower 1/3rd of the vagina."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The penile urethra (male) and labia minora (female) are homologous, both derived from which structure?","options":["Genital tubercle","Genital swelling","Genital fold","Mullerian duct"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The penile urethra and labia minora both develop from the genital fold.  Why A wrong: The genital tubercle gives rise to the penis/clitoris.  Why B wrong: The genital swelling gives rise to the scrotum/labia majora.  Why D wrong: The Mullerian duct forms internal female genital organs."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The male prostate gland is homologous to which female gland?","options":["Bartholin gland","Skene gland (para urethral gland)","Greater vestibular gland","Cowper gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The prostate gland (male) is homologous to the Skene gland (para urethral gland) in the female.  Why A wrong: The Bartholin gland is homologous to the bulbourethral/Cowper gland.  Why C wrong: The greater vestibular gland is another name for the Bartholin gland.  Why D wrong: The Cowper gland is a male gland (homologous to Bartholin gland)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"The male bulbourethral gland (Cowper gland) is homologous to which female gland?","options":["Skene gland","Para urethral gland","Bartholin gland (greater vestibular gland)","Prostate gland"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The bulbourethral gland/Cowper gland (male) is homologous to the Bartholin gland (greater vestibular gland) in the female.  Why A wrong: The Skene gland is homologous to the prostate gland.  Why B wrong: The para urethral gland is another name for the Skene gland (homologous to prostate).  Why D wrong: The prostate gland is a male gland homologous to the Skene gland."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Another name for the female Bartholin gland is:","options":["Skene gland","Para urethral gland","Greater vestibular gland","Prostate gland"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Bartholin gland is also called the greater vestibular gland.  Why A wrong: The Skene gland is the para urethral gland (homologous to prostate).  Why B wrong: The para urethral gland is the Skene gland.  Why D wrong: The prostate is a male gland."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Another name for the female Skene gland is:","options":["Greater vestibular gland","Para urethral gland","Bartholin gland","Cowper gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Skene gland is also called the para urethral gland.  Why A wrong: The greater vestibular gland is the Bartholin gland.  Why C wrong: The Bartholin gland is homologous to the Cowper gland.  Why D wrong: The Cowper gland is a male gland."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"How is the number of Barr bodies calculated?","options":["Number of X chromosomes + 1","Number of X chromosomes − 1","Number of Y chromosomes − 1","Number of X chromosomes × 2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The number of Barr bodies is calculated as (Number of X chromosomes − 1).  Why A wrong: The formula subtracts 1, it does not add 1.  Why C wrong: The formula is based on X chromosomes, not Y.  Why D wrong: The formula subtracts 1, it does not multiply."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"How many Barr bodies are present in a normal male (46 XY)?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A male (46 XY) has one X chromosome, so Barr bodies = (1 − 1) = 0.  Why B wrong: 1 Barr body is seen in a normal female (46 XX).  Why C wrong: 2 Barr bodies would require 3 X chromosomes.  Why D wrong: 3 Barr bodies would require 4 X chromosomes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"How many Barr bodies are present in a normal female (46 XX)?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A female (46 XX) has two X chromosomes, so Barr bodies = (2 − 1) = 1.  Why A wrong: 0 Barr bodies is seen in a male (46 XY) or Turner (45 XO).  Why C wrong: 2 Barr bodies would require 3 X chromosomes.  Why D wrong: 3 Barr bodies would require 4 X chromosomes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"How many Barr bodies are present in Turner syndrome (45 XO)?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Turner syndrome (45 XO) has one X chromosome, so Barr bodies = (1 − 1) = 0.  Why B wrong: 1 Barr body is seen in a normal female (46 XX) and Klinefelter (47 XXY).  Why C wrong: 2 Barr bodies would require 3 X chromosomes.  Why D wrong: 3 Barr bodies would require 4 X chromosomes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"How many Barr bodies are present in Klinefelter syndrome (47 XXY)?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Klinefelter syndrome (47 XXY) has two X chromosomes, so Barr bodies = (2 − 1) = 1.  Why A wrong: 0 Barr bodies is seen in a male (46 XY) or Turner (45 XO).  Why C wrong: 2 Barr bodies would require 3 X chromosomes.  Why D wrong: 3 Barr bodies would require 4 X chromosomes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"A student is asked to identify the karyotypes that give ZERO Barr bodies. Which of the following have 0 Barr bodies? (Select all that apply)","options":["Normal male 46(XY)","Turner syndrome 45(XO)","Normal female 46(XX)","Klinefelter syndrome 47(XXY)","A karyotype 47(XXX)"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Both a normal male 46(XY) and Turner syndrome 45(XO) have only one X chromosome, so Barr bodies = (1 − 1) = 0.  Why C wrong: A normal female 46(XX) has 1 Barr body.  Why D wrong: Klinefelter 47(XXY) has 2 X chromosomes → 1 Barr body.  Why E wrong: A 47(XXX) karyotype has 3 X chromosomes → (3 − 1) = 2 Barr bodies (extension of the same formula)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Blood supply of female reproductive organs, artery ligation in PPH…","question":"Which two conditions in the karyotype/syndrome discussion result in a FEMALE phenotype despite chromosomal abnormality or normalcy?","options":["46(XX) and 45(XO)","46(XY) and 47(XXY)","45(XO) and 47(XXY)","46(XX) and 46(XY)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Both the normal female 46(XX) and Turner syndrome 45(XO) result in a female phenotype (Y chromosome absent in both).  Why B wrong: Both 46(XY) and 47(XXY, Klinefelter) are male because a Y chromosome is present.  Why C wrong: 45(XO) is female but 47(XXY) is male.  Why D wrong: 46(XY) is male, not female."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The monthly cyclical bleeding that occurs in a female of reproductive age is called:","options":["Ovulation","Menstruation","Menopause","Menarche"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Menstruation is defined as the monthly/cyclical bleeding that occurs in a female of reproductive age; in common language it is called \"periods.\"  Why A wrong: Ovulation is the release of the secondary oocyte, not bleeding.  Why C wrong: Menopause is the permanent stoppage of the menstrual cycle.  Why D wrong: Menarche is the onset (first occurrence) of menstruation, not the recurring bleeding itself."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"In a female, the reproductive age during which menstruation occurs is:","options":["10–15 years","15–49 years","20–55 years","12–45 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify the reproductive age as 15–49 years.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated range of 15–49 years given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The onset (first appearance) of menstruation is termed:","options":["Menopause","Menarche","Menorrhagia","Ovulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Menarche is defined as the onset of menstruation.  Why A wrong: Menopause is the permanent stoppage of the menstrual cycle.  Why C wrong: Menorrhagia is heavy/prolonged menstrual bleeding, not first onset.  Why D wrong: Ovulation is oocyte release, unrelated to first menses."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The permanent stoppage of the menstrual cycle is called:","options":["Menarche","Menopause","Amenorrhea","Oligomenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define menopause as the permanent stoppage of the menstrual cycle.  Why A wrong: Menarche is the onset of menstruation.  Why C wrong: Amenorrhea is absence of menses but not necessarily permanent stoppage.  Why D wrong: Oligomenorrhea is infrequent menstruation, not permanent stoppage."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"During menstruation, the visible bleeding comes out from the:","options":["Urethra","Vagina","Fallopian tube","Cervical canal only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state menstruation is visible bleeding and the blood comes out from the vagina.  Why A wrong: The urethra carries urine, not menstrual blood.  Why C wrong: The fallopian tube transports the oocyte; it is not the exit route of menstrual blood.  Why D wrong: Blood passes through the cervix but ultimately exits via the vagina."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Menstrual bleeding occurs due to the shedding of which uterine layer?","options":["Myometrium","Perimetrium","Endometrium","Serosa"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state menstruation is due to shedding of the endometrium.  Why A wrong: The myometrium is the muscular layer and does not shed during menses.  Why B wrong: The perimetrium is the outer serosal covering and does not shed.  Why D wrong: Serosa (perimetrium) is the outer covering, not the shed layer."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Which of the following correctly describes menstruation according to the important points listed?","options":["It is a pathological process","It occurs at irregular intervals","It is physiological and occurs generally at regular intervals","It is unrelated to hormonal changes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state menstruation is physiological and occurs generally at regular intervals.  Why A wrong: Menstruation is physiological, not pathological.  Why B wrong: It occurs at regular intervals, not irregular.  Why D wrong: It is due to hormonal changes in the HPO axis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Menstruation occurs due to hormonal changes in which axis?","options":["Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal axis","Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Thyroid axis","Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Ovarian axis","Renin-Angiotensin axis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state menstruation is due to hormonal changes in the Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Ovarian (HPO) axis.  Why A wrong: The adrenal axis governs cortisol, not the menstrual cycle.  Why B wrong: The thyroid axis governs thyroid hormones.  Why D wrong: The renin-angiotensin axis regulates blood pressure/fluid balance."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The three layers of the uterus, from innermost to outermost, are:","options":["Perimetrium, Myometrium, Endometrium","Endometrium, Myometrium, Perimetrium","Myometrium, Endometrium, Perimetrium","Endometrium, Perimetrium, Myometrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the layers as Endometrium (inner), Myometrium (middle/muscular), and Perimetrium (outer).  Why A wrong: This reverses the order; endometrium is innermost.  Why C wrong: Endometrium, not myometrium, is the innermost layer.  Why D wrong: Perimetrium is the outermost, not the middle layer."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The innermost layer of the uterus, which sheds during menstruation, is the:","options":["Myometrium","Endometrium","Perimetrium","Parametrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The endometrium is the inner lining that sheds (\"Shed → Menstruation\").  Why A wrong: The myometrium is the muscular layer that does not shed.  Why C wrong: The perimetrium is the outer serosal layer.  Why D wrong: The parametrium is connective tissue around the uterus, not a shedding layer and not listed in the figure."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The middle muscular layer of the uterus is called the:","options":["Endometrium","Perimetrium","Myometrium","Serosa"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the middle layer as the myometrium.  Why A wrong: Endometrium is the innermost lining.  Why B wrong: Perimetrium is the outermost layer.  Why D wrong: Serosa is another name for perimetrium (outer), not the middle layer."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"In the Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Ovarian axis, which nucleus of the hypothalamus is associated with hormone release?","options":["Paraventricular nucleus","Arcuate nucleus","Supraoptic nucleus","Suprachiasmatic nucleus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the hypothalamic component in the HPO axis as the \"Arcuate nucleus ← Hypothalamus.\"  Why A, C, D wrong: These hypothalamic nuclei are not the one identified in the HPO axis notes for GnRH-related release."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH) is released from the hypothalamus in what manner?","options":["Continuous manner","Pulsatile manner","Only once at menarche","Random unregulated bursts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes clearly state GnRH is released from the hypothalamus in a pulsatile manner.  Why A wrong: It is pulsatile, not continuous.  Why C wrong: It is released cyclically throughout reproductive life, not only once.  Why D wrong: The release is regulated and pulsatile, not random."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"GnRH released by the hypothalamus acts on which gland?","options":["Posterior pituitary","Anterior pituitary","Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state GnRH acts on the anterior pituitary.  Why A wrong: The posterior pituitary stores oxytocin/ADH; GnRH acts on the anterior lobe.  Why C wrong: The adrenal gland is not the target of GnRH.  Why D wrong: The thyroid is stimulated by TSH, not GnRH."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Which two hormones are released by the anterior pituitary in the menstrual cycle?","options":["GnRH and Oxytocin","Estrogen and Progesterone","Follicle stimulating hormone (FSH) and Luteinizing hormone (LH)","Prolactin and ADH"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show the anterior pituitary releasing FSH and LH.  Why A wrong: GnRH is from the hypothalamus; oxytocin is stored in the posterior pituitary.  Why B wrong: Estrogen and progesterone are ovarian hormones, not from the pituitary.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is anterior pituitary but not the pair described here; ADH is posterior pituitary."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"FSH and LH released by the anterior pituitary are together called:","options":["Gonadotropins","Corticotropins","Somatotropins","Thyrotropins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state FSH and LH together are called Gonadotropin.  Why B wrong: Corticotropin (ACTH) acts on the adrenal cortex.  Why C wrong: Somatotropin is growth hormone.  Why D wrong: Thyrotropin (TSH) acts on the thyroid."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The decreased hormone release from the hypothalamus that initiates the axis activity is linked to which developmental stage?","options":["Menopause","Puberty","Infancy","Old age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state hormone release from the hypothalamus (GnRH) is associated with the time of puberty, when the HPO axis becomes active.  Why A wrong: Menopause is the cessation of cycles, not their initiation.  Why C wrong: The HPO axis is quiescent in infancy.  Why D wrong: Old age is post-menopausal."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"FSH and LH act on which organ to release estrogen and progesterone?","options":["Uterus","Ovary","Cervix","Vagina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows FSH and LH acting on the ovary, which then releases estrogen and progesterone.  Why A wrong: The uterus (endometrium) is acted upon by estrogen/progesterone, not by FSH/LH directly here.  Why C wrong: The cervix is not the gonadotropin target.  Why D wrong: The vagina is not a hormone-producing target of FSH/LH."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Estrogen and progesterone released from the ovary act on the uterus, causing the endometrium to eventually:","options":["Proliferate permanently","Shed (menstruation)","Calcify","Become fibrotic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows estrogen and progesterone acting on the uterus/endometrium and the endometrium shedding, which is called menstruation.  Why A wrong: Proliferation is temporary; shedding follows when hormonal support is withdrawn.  Why C wrong: Calcification is not part of the menstrual cycle.  Why D wrong: Fibrosis is not the described outcome."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The sequence of the menstrual cycle at puberty begins with which event?","options":["FSH acts on the ovary","The Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Ovarian axis becomes active","Estrogen release from follicles","LH surge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"At the time of Puberty → Hypothalamic pituitary ovarian axis becomes active\" as the first step.  Why A wrong: FSH acting on the ovary is a later step.  Why C wrong: Estrogen release from follicles occurs after FSH stimulation.  Why D wrong: LH surge occurs much later, near ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"In the menstrual cycle sequence, which hormone is released FIRST by the anterior pituitary after GnRH stimulation?","options":["LH","FSH","Progesterone","Inhibin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"First release FSH\" after GnRH acts on the anterior pituitary.  Why A wrong: LH surge comes later in the cycle.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is an ovarian hormone from the corpus luteum, not first.  Why D wrong: Inhibin is produced later by the corpus luteum."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The ovarian cycle is initiated by which hormone?","options":["LH","FSH","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The highlighted note states \"Ovarian cycle is initiated by → FSH.\"  Why A wrong: LH triggers ovulation later, it does not initiate the ovarian cycle.  Why C wrong: Estrogen is released as a consequence of FSH action, not the initiator.  Why D wrong: Progesterone is a later corpus luteum hormone."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"When FSH acts on the ovary, it stimulates many follicles and causes the release of which hormone?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Testosterone","Inhibin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state FSH acts on the ovary and from the follicles it releases estrogen.  Why A wrong: Progesterone is released mainly later by the corpus luteum.  Why C wrong: Testosterone is not the described follicular hormone here.  Why D wrong: Inhibin is produced by the corpus luteum in this scheme."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"As estrogen level increases, it exerts negative feedback on FSH. The result of FSH↓ is that:","options":["All follicles mature equally","All follicles die except one, called the dominant follicle","The corpus luteum forms immediately","Menstruation begins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state rising estrogen gives negative feedback on FSH → FSH↓ → all follicles die except 1 follicle called the Dominant Follicle.  Why A wrong: Only one follicle becomes dominant; the rest undergo atresia.  Why C wrong: The corpus luteum forms after ovulation, not at this step.  Why D wrong: Menstruation occurs at the end of the cycle, not here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Rising estrogen exerts positive feedback on which hormone, producing a sudden increase?","options":["FSH","LH","Progesterone","GnRH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state increasing estrogen gives positive feedback on LH → LH↑, and this sudden increase is called the LH surge.  Why A wrong: Estrogen gives negative feedback on FSH.  Why C wrong: Progesterone rises later from the corpus luteum.  Why D wrong: GnRH is upstream from the hypothalamus, not the described feedback target here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The sudden, sharp increase in LH caused by rising estrogen is known as the:","options":["FSH surge","LH surge","Estrogen peak","Progesterone withdrawal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the sudden increase in LH is called the LH surge.  Why A wrong: There is no FSH surge described; FSH is suppressed.  Why C wrong: The estrogen peak triggers the LH surge but is not itself called the LH surge.  Why D wrong: Progesterone withdrawal happens later, causing menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"When rising estrogen acts on the uterine endometrium, the endometrium will:","options":["Shed","Proliferate and become thick","Undergo secretory change","Atrophy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state estrogen acts on the uterine endometrium so it will PROLIFERATE → endometrium becomes thick.  Why A wrong: Shedding occurs when hormonal support is withdrawn, not during estrogen-driven proliferation.  Why C wrong: Secretory change is caused by progesterone, not estrogen.  Why D wrong: Estrogen thickens rather than atrophies the endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The LH surge causes meiosis in the primary oocyte to be completed, converting it into a:","options":["Primordial follicle","Secondary oocyte","Ootid","Zygote"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that with the LH surge, meiosis in the primary oocyte gets completed and the primary oocyte is converted to a secondary oocyte.  Why A wrong: A primordial follicle is an earlier stage, not a product of meiosis completion.  Why C wrong: The ootid stage follows fertilization/meiosis II completion, not this step.  Why D wrong: A zygote forms only after fertilization."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The process by which the secondary oocyte is released, occurring due to the LH surge, is called:","options":["Menstruation","Ovulation","Implantation","Luteolysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the conversion/release step as \"This is called ovulation.\"  Why A wrong: Menstruation is endometrial shedding.  Why C wrong: Implantation is embedding of the embryo, later.  Why D wrong: Luteolysis is degeneration of the corpus luteum."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"After ovulation, the secondary oocyte and corpus luteum are formed from the:","options":["Endometrial cells","Follicular cells","Myometrial cells","Cervical cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the secondary oocyte + corpus luteum is formed from the follicular cell(s).  Why A wrong: Endometrial cells line the uterus, not the follicle.  Why C wrong: Myometrial cells are uterine muscle.  Why D wrong: Cervical cells are unrelated to corpus luteum formation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Excessive estrogen leading to a very thick endometrium can ultimately result in:","options":["Cervical cancer","Endometrial (uterine) cancer","Ovarian cyst","Vaginal atrophy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Key Points state excessive estrogen → very thick endometrium → endometrial cancer/uterine cancer.  Why A wrong: Cervical cancer is chiefly linked to HPV, not unopposed estrogen.  Why C wrong: Ovarian cysts are not the described consequence.  Why D wrong: Vaginal atrophy is associated with low estrogen, the opposite state."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Why is estrogen NEVER given alone to a female who has a uterus?","options":["It causes ovarian failure","It can lead to uterine (endometrial) cancer","It stops ovulation permanently","It causes early menopause"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Key Points state estrogen is never given alone to any female who has a uterus because it can lead to uterine cancer.  Why A wrong: The stated reason is uterine cancer risk, not ovarian failure.  Why C wrong: Permanent ovulation cessation is not the reason given.  Why D wrong: Early menopause is not the stated concern."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"To decrease the risk of uterine cancer, estrogen is always given along with which hormone?","options":["Testosterone","Progesterone","Prolactin","Thyroxine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Key Points state estrogen is always given along with progesterone to decrease the risk of uterine cancer.  Why A wrong: Testosterone is not used for this purpose.  Why C wrong: Prolactin is unrelated to endometrial protection.  Why D wrong: Thyroxine is a thyroid hormone, not protective here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Estrogen's effect on the pituitary gonadotropins is best described as:","options":["LH increases (↑) and FSH decreases (↓)","LH decreases and FSH increases","Both LH and FSH increase","Both LH and FSH decrease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show estrogen effect = LH↑ (positive feedback) and FSH↓ (negative feedback).  Why B wrong: This reverses the actual effects.  Why C wrong: FSH is suppressed, not increased, by estrogen.  Why D wrong: LH is increased (surge), not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"In the menstrual cycle, the LH surge occurs because of which hormone?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Inhibin","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Q&A notes state that in the menstrual cycle, LH increases / the LH surge occurs because of estrogen (positive feedback).  Why A wrong: Progesterone gives negative feedback on LH.  Why C wrong: Inhibin suppresses FSH, not causing the LH surge.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is unrelated to the LH surge."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Meiosis I (first meiotic division) in the oocyte is resumed/completed because of which hormone?","options":["FSH","Estrogen","LH surge","Progesterone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Q&A notes state \"Meiosis 1 is resumed because of... LH surge.\"  Why A wrong: FSH initiates follicular growth but does not resume meiosis I.  Why B wrong: Estrogen triggers the LH surge but the direct cause of meiosis resumption is the LH surge.  Why D wrong: Progesterone is a corpus luteum hormone acting later."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Ovulation occurs because of which hormonal event?","options":["FSH surge","LH surge","Estrogen withdrawal","Progesterone rise"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Ovulation occurs because of = LH surge.\"  Why A wrong: There is no FSH surge; FSH is suppressed.  Why C wrong: Estrogen actually peaks and triggers the LH surge before ovulation.  Why D wrong: Progesterone rises after ovulation, from the corpus luteum."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The single follicle that survives and matures while the others die is called the dominant follicle or:","options":["Primordial follicle","Graafian follicle","Atretic follicle","Antral follicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the dominant follicle as the Graafian follicle.  Why A wrong: The primordial follicle is the earliest, immature stage.  Why C wrong: An atretic follicle is a degenerating follicle (the ones that die).  Why D wrong: The antral follicle is an intermediate stage, not the mature dominant follicle name given."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"In the dominant (Graafian) follicle, meiosis I gets completed at puberty due to which event?","options":["FSH release","LH surge","Estrogen negative feedback","Progesterone rise"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that at puberty, due to the LH surge, meiosis I gets completed in this follicle.  Why A wrong: FSH stimulates follicular growth but is not the cause of meiosis I completion.  Why C wrong: Estrogen negative feedback suppresses FSH; it does not directly complete meiosis I.  Why D wrong: Progesterone acts later via the corpus luteum."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"When the primary oocyte completes meiosis I, it forms a secondary oocyte and a:","options":["Second polar body","First polar body","Corpus albicans","Ootid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show Primary oocyte → Secondary oocyte + 1st polar body.  Why A wrong: The second polar body forms during meiosis II, after fertilization.  Why C wrong: The corpus albicans is the degenerated corpus luteum.  Why D wrong: The ootid stage is a later product."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The corpus luteum is formed due to which process?","options":["Menstruation","Ovulation","Implantation","Fertilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Q&A notes state \"Corpus luteum is formed due to Ovulation,\" which itself occurs due to the LH surge.  Why A wrong: Menstruation is shedding of the endometrium, not the cause of corpus luteum formation.  Why C wrong: Implantation occurs after fertilization, unrelated to corpus luteum formation here.  Why D wrong: Fertilization is not required for corpus luteum formation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Ovulation, which leads to corpus luteum formation, itself occurs due to:","options":["Estrogen withdrawal","LH surge","FSH surge","Progesterone peak"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state ovulation (and hence corpus luteum formation) occurs due to the LH surge.  Why A wrong: Estrogen peaks (not withdrawal) triggers the LH surge.  Why C wrong: There is no FSH surge.  Why D wrong: The progesterone peak occurs after corpus luteum forms."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The corpus luteum is maintained by which hormone?","options":["FSH","LH","Estrogen","Inhibin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Q&A notes state \"Corpus luteum is maintained by which Hormone = LH.\"  Why A wrong: FSH initiates follicular development, not corpus luteum maintenance.  Why C wrong: Estrogen is produced by, not the maintainer of, the corpus luteum.  Why D wrong: Inhibin suppresses FSH; it does not maintain the corpus luteum."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"After ovulation, the corpus luteum increases in size and reaches its maximum size how many days after ovulation?","options":["4 days","6 days","8 days","14 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the corpus luteum reaches maximum size = 8 days after ovulation.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the specified value of 8 days."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The corpus luteum starts forming which hormones? (Select all that apply)","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Inhibin","Oxytocin","Testosterone"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the corpus luteum forms Progesterone (main), Estrogen, and Inhibin (and it reduces FSH).  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is a posterior pituitary hormone, not from the corpus luteum.  Why E wrong: Testosterone is not listed among the corpus luteum hormones."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The MAIN hormone produced by the corpus luteum is:","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","FSH","Inhibin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label PROGESTERONE as the main hormone formed by the corpus luteum.  Why A wrong: Estrogen is produced but is not labeled the main one here.  Why C wrong: FSH is a pituitary hormone; the corpus luteum reduces FSH.  Why D wrong: Inhibin is produced but is not the main hormone."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The main source of progesterone in the reproductive cycle is:","options":["Anterior pituitary","Ovary (corpus luteum)","Adrenal cortex","Endometrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the main source of progesterone is the ovary (corpus luteum).  Why A wrong: The anterior pituitary produces gonadotropins, not progesterone.  Why C wrong: The adrenal cortex produces small amounts but is not the main source described.  Why D wrong: The endometrium is a target of progesterone, not its source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Progesterone from the corpus luteum gives negative feedback on which hormone?","options":["FSH","LH","Estrogen","GnRH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone gives negative feedback on LH → LH↓.  Why A wrong: Inhibin/estrogen reduce FSH; progesterone's described feedback is on LH.  Why C wrong: Estrogen is a hormone, not the feedback target described.  Why D wrong: GnRH is upstream; the notes specify LH as the target."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Because progesterone gives negative feedback on LH (LH↓), what happens to the corpus luteum (if pregnancy does not occur)?","options":["It enlarges further","It dies","It becomes the dominant follicle","It secretes more FSH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state LH↓ → \"So corpus luteum dies,\" because LH maintains it.  Why A wrong: Falling LH support causes regression, not enlargement.  Why C wrong: The dominant follicle is a pre-ovulatory structure, not a fate of the corpus luteum.  Why D wrong: The corpus luteum does not secrete FSH; it reduces FSH."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Estrogen produced by the corpus luteum acts on the uterine endometrium and supports it, producing which action?","options":["Proliferative action","Secretory action","Shedding action","Ischemic action"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state estrogen (from corpus luteum) acts on the uterine endometrium and supports it → SECRETORY action.  Why A wrong: The proliferative phase is driven earlier by follicular estrogen before ovulation.  Why C wrong: Shedding occurs after hormonal support is lost.  Why D wrong: Ischemia occurs at the very end when support is withdrawn, not as the supportive action."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"One of the actions of the corpus luteum's hormones is that it:","options":["Increases FSH","Reduces FSH","Increases GnRH","Increases oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Reduces FSH\" among the corpus luteum's actions (via estrogen and inhibin).  Why A wrong: The corpus luteum reduces, not increases, FSH.  Why C wrong: GnRH is not the described target.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is unrelated to the corpus luteum here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Which hormone from the corpus luteum specifically suppresses FSH?","options":["Progesterone","Inhibin","LH","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inhibin is listed among the corpus luteum hormones, and the corpus luteum \"reduces FSH\"; inhibin's role is to inhibit FSH.  Why A wrong: Progesterone's described feedback is on LH.  Why C wrong: LH maintains the corpus luteum; it does not suppress FSH.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not a corpus luteum hormone here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"When the corpus luteum dies, all the hormones formed by it (progesterone, estrogen, inhibin) will:","options":["Increase","Decrease","Stay the same","Convert to FSH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"So all Hormones formed by Corpus Luteum decrease\" once it dies.  Why A wrong: Death of the corpus luteum lowers, not raises, its hormones.  Why C wrong: Levels fall, they do not stay constant.  Why D wrong: These hormones do not convert into FSH."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"When progesterone decreases (↓) after the corpus luteum dies, the immediate consequence to the endometrium is:","options":["The endometrium proliferates","The support to the endometrium is lost and it is shed","The endometrium becomes secretory","The endometrium calcifies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone↓ → support to the endometrium is lost → endometrium is shed → called menstruation.  Why A wrong: Proliferation occurs earlier under estrogen, not with progesterone withdrawal.  Why C wrong: Secretory change requires progesterone; its withdrawal ends this.  Why D wrong: Calcification is not part of the cycle."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The shedding of the endometrium that occurs when progesterone support is lost is called:","options":["Ovulation","Menstruation","Implantation","Proliferation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the shedding of the endometrium (from lost progesterone support) as menstruation.  Why A wrong: Ovulation is oocyte release.  Why C wrong: Implantation is embryo embedding.  Why D wrong: Proliferation is endometrial growth, the opposite of shedding."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"When estrogen decreases (↓) after the corpus luteum dies, the negative feedback on FSH is lost. What happens to FSH?","options":["FSH decreases","FSH increases (↑)","FSH stays unchanged","FSH converts to LH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state estrogen↓ → negative feedback on FSH is lost → FSH↑.  Why A wrong: Loss of negative feedback raises, not lowers, FSH.  Why C wrong: FSH rises rather than remaining unchanged.  Why D wrong: FSH does not convert to LH."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"The rise in FSH (FSH↑) that follows the loss of estrogen's negative feedback signifies that:","options":["Menopause has begun","A new (menstrual) cycle begins","Pregnancy has occurred","Ovulation is happening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state FSH↑ → \"New cycle begins.\"  Why A wrong: This is the normal cyclical restart, not menopause.  Why C wrong: A rising FSH indicates a new cycle, not pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Ovulation is triggered by the LH surge mid-cycle, not by this FSH rise at cycle onset."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"After the corpus luteum dies, inhibin levels will:","options":["Increase","Decrease (↓)","Remain constant","Trigger the LH surge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show Inhibin (↓) among the hormones that decrease when the corpus luteum dies.  Why A wrong: Death of the corpus luteum lowers inhibin.  Why C wrong: Inhibin falls, it does not stay constant.  Why D wrong: Inhibin suppresses FSH; it does not trigger the LH surge."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"A nurse explains that the corpus luteum forms from follicular cells and, if not rescued by pregnancy, regresses. Which hormone maintains it, and its withdrawal causes its death?","options":["FSH","Estrogen","LH","Inhibin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The corpus luteum is maintained by LH; progesterone's negative feedback lowers LH (LH↓), so the corpus luteum dies.  Why A wrong: FSH does not maintain the corpus luteum.  Why B wrong: Estrogen is produced by the corpus luteum but is not its maintainer.  Why D wrong: Inhibin suppresses FSH; it does not maintain the corpus luteum."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"Which sequence correctly represents the HPO axis signalling at the start of the menstrual cycle?","options":["Ovary → Hypothalamus → Pituitary","Hypothalamus (GnRH) → Anterior pituitary (FSH/LH) → Ovary (estrogen/progesterone)","Pituitary → Hypothalamus → Ovary","Ovary → Pituitary → Hypothalamus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show Hypothalamus releases GnRH → acts on anterior pituitary → releases FSH/LH → act on ovary → estrogen/progesterone.  Why A, C, D wrong: These reverse or scramble the correct top-down HPO signalling order."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Menstrual cycle — definition of menstruation, reproductive age…","question":"During the proliferative phase, the thickening of the endometrium is primarily driven by which hormone?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Inhibin","LH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state estrogen acts on the uterine endometrium to make it proliferate and become thick.  Why A wrong: Progesterone causes secretory change, not the initial proliferation/thickening.  Why C wrong: Inhibin suppresses FSH; it does not thicken the endometrium.  Why D wrong: LH triggers ovulation and maintains the corpus luteum, not endometrial proliferation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Which hormone initiates the ovarian cycle?","options":["LH","FSH","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Ovarian cycle is initiated by: FSH\" — FSH stimulates many follicles at the start of the cycle.  Why A wrong: LH is responsible for the mid-cycle surge and ovulation, not initiation of the ovarian cycle.  Why C wrong: Estrogen is secreted later by the growing follicles; it does not initiate the cycle.  Why D wrong: Progesterone dominates the second (luteal) half of the cycle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the ovarian cycle, FSH acts to:","options":["Stimulate many follicles","Trigger ovulation directly","Cause endometrial shedding","Maintain the corpus luteum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows FSH \"Stimulates many follicles,\" which then release estrogen.  Why B wrong: Ovulation is triggered by the LH surge, not FSH.  Why C wrong: Endometrial shedding is caused by a fall in progesterone.  Why D wrong: The corpus luteum is maintained by LH (non-pregnant) or HCG (pregnant), not FSH."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"The growing follicles/ovary secrete which hormone?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","HCG","Inhibin only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Estrogen is secreted by = Follicles/ovary\" and follicles release estrogen after FSH stimulation.  Why A wrong: Progesterone is chiefly from the corpus luteum, not the developing follicles.  Why C wrong: HCG is produced in pregnancy (by trophoblast), not by follicles.  Why D wrong: Inhibin is a corpus-luteum hormone; the main follicular hormone is estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Which hormone proliferates (causes proliferation of) the uterine endometrium?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","LH","FSH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Which hormone proliferates the uterine endometrium = Estrogen\" — estrogen drives the proliferative phase.  Why A wrong: Progesterone causes secretory (not proliferative) changes.  Why C wrong: LH triggers ovulation and luteinization, not endometrial proliferation.  Why D wrong: FSH acts on ovarian follicles, not directly on the endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the effect of estrogen on FSH?","options":["Increases FSH (positive feedback)","Decreases FSH (negative feedback)","No effect","Converts FSH to LH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Estrogen effect on FSH = decreases (Negative feedback).\"  Why A wrong: Estrogen exerts positive feedback on LH, but negative feedback on FSH.  Why C wrong: There is a definite negative-feedback effect on FSH.  Why D wrong: Hormones are not interconverted; feedback modulates secretion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the effect of estrogen on LH?","options":["Decreases LH (negative feedback)","Increases LH (positive feedback)","No effect","Blocks the LH surge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Estrogen effect on LH = increases (Positive feedback)\"; rising estrogen triggers the LH surge.  Why A wrong: Negative feedback is estrogen's effect on FSH, not LH.  Why C wrong: Estrogen clearly increases LH.  Why D wrong: Estrogen actually causes the LH surge, not blocks it."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"On which day of the cycle is estrogen at its maximum?","options":["Day 1","Day 12","Day 14","Day 22"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Estrogen is maximum on = Day 12 of cycle\" (peak just before ovulation triggering the LH surge).  Why A wrong: Day 1 is the start of menstruation with low hormones.  Why C wrong: Day 14 is ovulation; estrogen peaks a bit earlier (Day 12).  Why D wrong: Day 22 is the peak of progesterone/corpus luteum, not estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"The LH surge is due to which hormone?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","FSH","Inhibin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"LH surge is due to = Estrogen\" — high estrogen exerts positive feedback producing a sudden rise in LH.  Why A wrong: Progesterone exerts negative feedback on LH.  Why C wrong: FSH stimulates follicles but does not cause the LH surge.  Why D wrong: Inhibin inhibits FSH; it does not trigger the LH surge."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the best-answer time interval between the LH surge and ovulation?","options":["12–24 hours","24–36 hours","32–36 hours","48–72 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Time interval between LH surge and ovulation = 32–36 hours (Best ans.).\"  Why A wrong: Not given; too short.  Why B wrong: 24–36 hours is listed only as the \"2nd best\" answer, not the best.  Why D wrong: Not supported by the notes."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"According to the notes, the \"2nd best\" answer for the interval between LH surge and ovulation is:","options":["32–36 hours","24–36 hours","12–24 hours","8 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly write \"2nd best (24–36 hours).\"  Why A wrong: 32–36 hours is the best answer, not the 2nd best.  Why C wrong: Not listed.  Why D wrong: 8 days refers to time to maximum corpus luteum size, unrelated to this interval."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the size of the follicle just before ovulation?","options":["8–10 mm","12–14 mm","18–20 mm","25–30 mm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Size of follicle just before ovulation = 18–20 mm\" (the mature Graafian follicle).  Why A wrong: Too small; represents an early follicle.  Why B wrong: Still below the mature ovulatory size.  Why D wrong: Larger than the stated 18–20 mm."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Resumption of meiosis-1 in the oocyte is due to which hormone?","options":["LH","FSH","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Meiosis 1 is resumed due to = LH\"; the LH surge triggers completion of meiosis-1.  Why B wrong: FSH promotes follicular growth, not resumption of meiosis-1.  Why C wrong: Estrogen causes the LH surge but is not the direct trigger for meiosis-1 resumption.  Why D wrong: Progesterone acts in the luteal phase, after ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"On which day does ovulation occur in a typical 28-day cycle?","options":["Day 1","Day 12","Day 14","Day 22"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The cycle diagram marks ovulation at Day 14 in a 28-day cycle.  Why A wrong: Day 1 is the first day of menstruation.  Why B wrong: Day 12 is when estrogen peaks, just before ovulation.  Why D wrong: Day 22 is the peak of corpus luteum activity, not ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In a 28-day cycle, on which day does menstruation begin (end of cycle)?","options":["Day 14","Day 22","Day 28","Day 8"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram marks \"Day 28 — Menstruation\" as the cycle end/new cycle onset.  Why A wrong: Day 14 is ovulation.  Why B wrong: Day 22 is maximum corpus luteum activity.  Why D wrong: Day 8 relates to time from ovulation to maximum corpus luteum, not menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"During follicular development, what happens to all follicles except one?","options":["They all mature and ovulate","They all die except one, called the dominant follicle","They become corpus albicans","They convert into corpus luteum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"all follicles die except one called as Dominant follicle which keeps growing.\"  Why A wrong: Only one follicle (the dominant one) matures and ovulates.  Why C wrong: Corpus albicans is the degenerated corpus luteum, not the fate of the atretic follicles here.  Why D wrong: Only the ovulated follicle becomes the corpus luteum."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"After ovulation, the ruptured follicle transforms into which structure?","options":["Corpus albicans","Corpus luteum","Graafian follicle","Primordial follicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show \"Follicle → Corpus luteum\" after ovulation.  Why A wrong: Corpus albicans forms later, when the corpus luteum degenerates.  Why C wrong: The Graafian follicle is the pre-ovulatory mature follicle.  Why D wrong: A primordial follicle is the earliest resting follicle stage."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"During ovulation, the oocyte is described in the notes as progressing from:","options":["Oogonium to primary oocyte","1° oocyte to 2° oocyte","2° oocyte to ovum only","Zygote to blastocyst"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label ovulation as \"1° oocyte + 2° oocyte\" — the primary oocyte completes meiosis-1 to become a secondary oocyte at ovulation.  Why A wrong: The oogonium-to-primary-oocyte step occurs in fetal life, not at ovulation.  Why C wrong: The notes emphasize the 1° → 2° oocyte transition here.  Why D wrong: Zygote/blastocyst forms after fertilization, not at ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Which sequence correctly represents estrogen's negative-feedback pathway noted in the cycle?","options":["Estrogen → increases FSH","Estrogen (negative feedback) → decreases FSH","Estrogen → decreases LH","Estrogen → increases progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows \"−ve feedback (1) FSH,\" i.e., estrogen decreases FSH via negative feedback.  Why A wrong: Estrogen decreases, not increases, FSH.  Why C wrong: Estrogen exerts positive feedback on LH (increases it).  Why D wrong: Estrogen does not increase progesterone via feedback."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"According to the cycle diagram, the \"+ve feedback on LH\" from estrogen results in:","options":["A gradual decline in LH","A sudden increase in LH (LH surge)","Increased FSH","Endometrial shedding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes chain \"+ve feedback on LH → Sudden ↑ LH → LH Surge.\"  Why A wrong: Positive feedback causes a sharp rise, not a decline.  Why C wrong: FSH is under negative feedback here.  Why D wrong: Endometrial shedding follows the progesterone fall, not the LH surge."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"On Day 22 of the non-pregnant cycle, the main hormone from the corpus luteum is progesterone, whose action on the endometrium is described as:","options":["Proliferative action","Secretory action (supports endometrium)","Ischemic action","Bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram states progesterone \"Supports endometrium called as secretory action.\"  Why A wrong: Proliferation is estrogen's role in the first half.  Why C wrong: Ischemia occurs when progesterone falls, not during its support.  Why D wrong: Bleeding follows loss of progesterone support."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the effect of progesterone on LH (Day 22 diagram)?","options":["Increases LH (positive feedback)","Negative feedback on LH (LH ↓)","No effect on LH","Converts LH to FSH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows \"(2) Negative feedback on LH → LH ↓.\"  Why A wrong: Positive feedback on LH is estrogen's action, not progesterone's.  Why C wrong: Progesterone clearly reduces LH.  Why D wrong: Hormones are not interconverted."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"When progesterone levels fall (↓Progesterone), what is the direct consequence on the endometrium?","options":["Support to endometrium is lost and endometrium sheds","Endometrium proliferates","Endometrium becomes secretory","Ovulation occurs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram chains \"↓Progesterone → Support to endometrium is lost → Endometrium sheds.\"  Why B wrong: Proliferation is estrogen-driven and happens earlier.  Why C wrong: The secretory phase requires progesterone support, which is now lost.  Why D wrong: Ovulation occurs mid-cycle, not with falling progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"From ovulation, after how many days does the corpus luteum reach its maximum size, corresponding to which cycle day?","options":["8 days after ovulation (Day 22)","4 days after ovulation (Day 18)","14 days after ovulation (Day 28)","2 days after ovulation (Day 16)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maximum size of corpus luteum is seen on \"Day 8 after ovulation (Day 22).\"  Why B wrong: The stated interval is 8 days, not 4.  Why C wrong: Day 28 corresponds to menstruation/corpus albicans, not maximum size.  Why D wrong: 2 days after ovulation is too early for maximum size."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Maximum activity of the corpus luteum is seen on which day?","options":["Day 14","Day 8 after ovulation (Day 22)","Day 28","Day 12"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Maximum activity of corpus luteum seen on: Day 8 after ovulation (Day 22)\" — same as maximum size.  Why A wrong: Day 14 is ovulation.  Why C wrong: Day 28 is menstruation.  Why D wrong: Day 12 is estrogen peak."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Which of the following lists ALL the hormones released by the corpus luteum? (Select all that apply)","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Inhibin","FSH","HCG"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"All hormones released by corpus luteum: Progesterone, Estrogen, Inhibin.\"  Why D wrong: FSH is a pituitary gonadotropin, not a corpus-luteum hormone.  Why E wrong: HCG is secreted by the trophoblast/placenta, not the corpus luteum (it maintains the corpus luteum)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the MAIN hormone released by the corpus luteum?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","Inhibin","HCG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Main hormone released by corpus luteum: Progesterone.\"  Why A wrong: Estrogen is also released but is not the main hormone of the corpus luteum.  Why C wrong: Inhibin is a minor secretory product.  Why D wrong: HCG is not made by the corpus luteum."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Which hormone brings about secretory changes in the endometrium?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","FSH","LH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Hormone which brings about secretory changes in Endometrium: Progesterone.\"  Why A wrong: Estrogen brings about proliferative changes.  Why C wrong: FSH acts on ovarian follicles.  Why D wrong: LH triggers ovulation/luteinization, not secretory endometrial change."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the corpus luteum figure, the yellow luteinized cells derived from the granulosa layer are labelled as:","options":["Granulosa lutein","Corpus albicans","Medulla","Cortex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the luteinized granulosa cells as \"Granulosa lutein.\"  Why B wrong: Corpus albicans is the white scar of the regressed corpus luteum.  Why C wrong: Medulla is the inner ovarian region.  Why D wrong: Cortex is the outer ovarian region."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the ovary figure, the degenerated/white remnant of the corpus luteum is labelled as:","options":["Granulosa lutein","Corpus albicans","Corpus luteum","Cortex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the regressed structure \"Corpus albicans.\"  Why A wrong: Granulosa lutein are the active yellow cells of the functioning corpus luteum.  Why C wrong: The corpus luteum is the active yellow body, not the degenerated remnant.  Why D wrong: Cortex is the ovarian region containing follicles."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"The transformation of the ruptured follicle into the corpus luteum (yellow body) after the LH surge is called:","options":["Atresia","Luteinization","Proliferation","Ovulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure states \"luteinization after LH surge,\" describing the follicle's conversion into the corpus luteum.  Why A wrong: Atresia is degeneration of non-dominant follicles.  Why C wrong: Proliferation is endometrial growth by estrogen.  Why D wrong: Ovulation is the release of the oocyte, which precedes luteinization."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the ovarian cross-section, which regions are labelled? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cortex","Medulla","Corpus albicans","Endometrium","Myometrium"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary figure labels Cortex, medulla, and corpus albicans (along with granulosa lutein and human ovary).  Why D wrong: Endometrium is a uterine layer, not shown in the ovary figure.  Why E wrong: Myometrium is the uterine muscle layer, not part of the ovary figure."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In a non-pregnant female, which hormone maintains the corpus luteum?","options":["HCG","LH","FSH","Estrogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states corpus luteum in a non-pregnant female is maintained by LH.  Why A wrong: HCG maintains the corpus luteum in pregnancy, not in a non-pregnant female.  Why C wrong: FSH stimulates follicular development, not corpus-luteum maintenance.  Why D wrong: Estrogen does not maintain the corpus luteum."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the life span of the corpus luteum in a non-pregnant female?","options":["10–12 weeks","12–14 days","8 days","28 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states life span of the corpus luteum in a non-pregnant female is \"12–14 days.\"  Why A wrong: 10–12 weeks is the pregnant-female corpus luteum lifespan.  Why C wrong: 8 days is the time from ovulation to maximum activity.  Why D wrong: 28 days is the full cycle length, not the corpus-luteum lifespan."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In a pregnant female, which hormone maintains the corpus luteum?","options":["LH","HCG","FSH","Progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the corpus luteum in a pregnant female is maintained by HCG.  Why A wrong: LH maintains it only in the non-pregnant female.  Why C wrong: FSH does not maintain the corpus luteum.  Why D wrong: Progesterone is the product of the corpus luteum, not its maintainer."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the life span of the corpus luteum in a pregnant female?","options":["12–14 days","10–12 weeks","40 weeks","8 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states life span of the corpus luteum in a pregnant female is \"10–12 weeks.\"  Why A wrong: 12–14 days is the non-pregnant lifespan.  Why C wrong: 40 weeks is the full gestation, not the corpus-luteum lifespan.  Why D wrong: 8 days is the time to maximum corpus-luteum activity."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"The corpus luteum of pregnancy is the main source of progesterone until how many weeks of pregnancy?","options":["8 weeks","10 weeks","12 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the corpus luteum of pregnancy is the \"Main source of progesterone till 12 week of pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: The stated cutoff is 12 weeks, not 8.  Why B wrong: 10 weeks is not the stated cutoff.  Why D wrong: After 12 weeks the placenta takes over; 20 weeks is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the source of progesterone in a non-pregnant female?","options":["Placenta","Corpus luteum (ovary)","Anterior pituitary","Adrenal cortex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Source of progesterone in Non-pregnant female = Corpus luteum (ovary).\"  Why A wrong: The placenta produces progesterone only during pregnancy (after 12 weeks).  Why C wrong: The anterior pituitary secretes FSH/LH, not progesterone.  Why D wrong: The adrenal cortex is not the stated source here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In a non-pregnant female, on which day of the cycle are progesterone levels maximum?","options":["Day 12","Day 14","Day 22","Day 28"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"In a non pregnant female when will the levels of progesterone be maximum = Day 22 of cycle.\"  Why A wrong: Day 12 is the estrogen peak.  Why B wrong: Day 14 is ovulation.  Why D wrong: Day 28 is when progesterone has fallen and menstruation begins."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"During pregnancy, what is the main source of progesterone AFTER 12 weeks?","options":["Corpus luteum of pregnancy","Placenta","Ovarian follicle","Adrenal gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"After 12 weeks = Placenta\" becomes the source of progesterone.  Why A wrong: The corpus luteum of pregnancy is the main source only until 12 weeks.  Why C wrong: Ovarian follicles secrete estrogen, not the main pregnancy progesterone.  Why D wrong: The adrenal gland is not the stated source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Which hormone maintains pregnancy?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","FSH","LH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Which hormone maintains pregnancy: Progesterone.\"  Why A wrong: Estrogen supports pregnancy but the notes name progesterone as the maintainer.  Why C wrong: FSH is not the pregnancy-maintaining hormone.  Why D wrong: LH maintains the corpus luteum only in the non-pregnant state."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"The menstrual cycle is divided into which two cycles?","options":["Follicular cycle and luteal cycle","Ovarian cycle and uterine cycle","Proliferative cycle and secretory cycle","Estrogen cycle and progesterone cycle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart divides \"Menstrual Cycle\" into \"Ovarian cycle\" and \"Uterine cycle.\"  Why A wrong: Follicular and luteal are phases of the ovarian cycle, not the two main divisions.  Why C wrong: Proliferative/secretory are uterine-cycle phases.  Why D wrong: This is not how the notes divide the menstrual cycle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the ovarian cycle diagram, the phase from Day 1 to Day 14 (up to ovulation) is called:","options":["Luteal phase","Follicular phase","Secretory phase","Menstrual phase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the first 14 days as the \"Follicular phase.\"  Why A wrong: The luteal phase is after ovulation (Day 14–28).  Why C wrong: Secretory is a uterine-cycle phase name.  Why D wrong: The menstrual phase is only Days 1–5, a subset, not the whole first half."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the ovarian cycle diagram, the phase after ovulation (Day 14 to Day 28) is called:","options":["Follicular phase","Luteal phase","Proliferative phase","Menstrual phase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the second half as the \"Luteal phase.\"  Why A wrong: The follicular phase is the first half (before ovulation).  Why C wrong: Proliferative is the corresponding uterine-cycle phase name for the first half.  Why D wrong: Menstrual phase is at the very start of the cycle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"The mature dominant follicle ready to ovulate is also called the:","options":["Primordial follicle","Graafian follicle","Antral follicle","Atretic follicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ovarian-cycle diagram labels the \"Dominant follicle/graffian follicle\" (Graafian follicle) at ovulation.  Why A wrong: Primordial follicles are the earliest resting follicles.  Why C wrong: Antral follicles are an intermediate developmental stage.  Why D wrong: Atretic follicles are degenerating follicles."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the main hormone in the first half of the cycle (preovulatory/follicular phase)?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","HCG","Inhibin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Q1 on the page states the main hormone in the first half (preovulatory/follicular phase) is ESTROGEN.  Why A wrong: Progesterone dominates the second half.  Why C wrong: HCG is a pregnancy hormone.  Why D wrong: Inhibin is a minor hormone, not the main first-half hormone."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"What is the main hormone in the second half of the cycle (postovulatory/luteal phase)?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","FSH","LH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Q2 on the page states the main hormone in the second half (postovulatory/luteal phase) is PROGESTERONE.  Why A wrong: Estrogen dominates the first half.  Why C wrong: FSH acts early in the cycle.  Why D wrong: LH surges at mid-cycle but progesterone dominates the luteal phase."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"According to the key concept, the time (length) of the luteal phase is:","options":["Variable in different females","Always fixed = 14 days","Always 8 days","Equal to the follicular phase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Time of luteal phase is always fixed = 14 days.\"  Why A wrong: It is the follicular phase that varies, not the luteal phase.  Why C wrong: The fixed value is 14 days, not 8.  Why D wrong: The follicular phase length varies, so they are not always equal."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Which phase of the ovarian cycle can vary in length among different females?","options":["Luteal phase","Follicular phase","Both are always fixed","Secretory phase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Follicular phase can vary in different females.\"  Why A wrong: The luteal phase is fixed at 14 days.  Why C wrong: The follicular phase varies, so both are not fixed.  Why D wrong: The secretory (luteal) phase is fixed, corresponding to the fixed luteal phase."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"The formula to calculate the day of ovulation is:","options":["Length of cycle + 14","Length of cycle − 14","Length of cycle ÷ 2","Length of cycle − 28"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give \"Length of cycle − 14 = Day of ovulation\" (because the luteal phase is a fixed 14 days).  Why A wrong: Adding 14 gives a value beyond the cycle.  Why C wrong: Halving is not the given method.  Why D wrong: Subtracting 28 is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"For a cycle length of 38 days, on which day does ovulation occur (per the worked example)?","options":["Day 14","Day 19","Day 24","Day 28"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The example computes 38 − 14 = 24, so ovulation on Day 24.  Why A wrong: Day 14 applies only to a 28-day cycle.  Why B wrong: Day 19 would correspond to a 33-day cycle.  Why D wrong: Day 28 is not the ovulation day for a 38-day cycle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the uterine cycle, which phase corresponds to Day 1 to Day 5 (bleeding)?","options":["Proliferative phase","Secretory phase","Menstrual phase","Luteal phase"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels Day1–Day5 (Bleeding) as the \"Menstrual phase.\"  Why A wrong: The proliferative phase follows menstruation.  Why B wrong: The secretory phase is the second half of the uterine cycle.  Why D wrong: Luteal phase is the ovarian-cycle term, not a uterine-cycle phase name."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the uterine cycle, the proliferative phase (up to Day 14) is driven by estrogen and produces:","options":["Endometrial proliferation","Secretory transformation","Endometrial shedding","Ovulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows Estrogen → \"Endometrial proliferation\" during the proliferative phase.  Why B wrong: Secretory transformation is the progesterone-driven secretory phase.  Why C wrong: Shedding is the menstrual phase.  Why D wrong: Ovulation is an ovarian-cycle event, not a uterine-cycle change."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the uterine cycle, the secretory phase (Day 14 to Day 28) is driven by progesterone, which provides:","options":["Endometrial proliferation","Support to endometrium / secretory action","Menstrual bleeding","Follicle stimulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows Progesterone → \"Support endometrium/secretory action\" in the secretory phase.  Why A wrong: Proliferation is estrogen's proliferative-phase action.  Why C wrong: Bleeding is the menstrual phase.  Why D wrong: Follicle stimulation is an FSH/ovarian action."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"The first half of the cycle is equivalently known by all of the following names EXCEPT:","options":["Follicular phase","Preovulatory phase","Proliferative phase","Secretory phase"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes equate \"1st half of cycle = Follicular phase = Preovulatory phase = proliferative phase\" — secretory phase is NOT in this group.  Why A wrong: Follicular phase is a correct synonym for the first half.  Why B wrong: Preovulatory phase is a correct synonym.  Why C wrong: Proliferative phase is a correct synonym."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"The second half of the cycle is equivalently known by all of the following names EXCEPT:","options":["Luteal phase","Post-ovulatory phase","Secretory phase","Proliferative phase"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes equate \"2nd half of cycle = Luteal phase = Post ovulatory phase = Secretory phase\" — proliferative phase belongs to the first half.  Why A wrong: Luteal phase is a correct synonym for the second half.  Why B wrong: Post-ovulatory phase is a correct synonym.  Why C wrong: Secretory phase is a correct synonym."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"For menstruation to occur, what must happen to progesterone levels?","options":["Progesterone must remain permanently high","Progesterone should initially be good (supporting endometrium) then suddenly decrease","Progesterone must be absent throughout","Progesterone must rise sharply"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The clinical concept states \"Initially progesterone level should be good so that it supports the Endometrium and then it should suddenly decrease.\"  Why A wrong: If levels do not decrease, the female will NOT menstruate.  Why C wrong: With no progesterone at all there is also no menstruation.  Why D wrong: A rise, not a fall, would prevent menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"If progesterone levels do NOT decrease at the end of the cycle, the result is:","options":["Female will menstruate normally","Female will not menstruate","Ovulation occurs","Estrogen surge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"Levels of Progesterone do not decrease → Female will not Menstruate.\"  Why A wrong: Persistent progesterone support prevents shedding.  Why C wrong: Ovulation is mid-cycle and unrelated to end-cycle progesterone fall.  Why D wrong: An estrogen surge is not the described outcome."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"If there is no progesterone at all in a female, the result regarding menstruation is:","options":["Heavy menstruation","Then also no menstruation","Normal menstruation","Continuous bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"There is no Progesterone in any female → Then also no menstruation\" (because progesterone must first build up endometrial support before withdrawal triggers shedding).  Why A wrong: Absence of progesterone does not cause heavy menstruation here.  Why C wrong: Normal menstruation requires progesterone support then withdrawal.  Why D wrong: Continuous bleeding is not the stated outcome."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"During pregnancy (9 months), the absence of periods is termed:","options":["Dysmenorrhea","Amenorrhea","Menorrhagia","Oligomenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Pregnancy (9 months): No period (Amenorrhea).\"  Why A wrong: Dysmenorrhea is painful menstruation.  Why C wrong: Menorrhagia is heavy menstrual bleeding.  Why D wrong: Oligomenorrhea is infrequent menstruation, not absence."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the first 12 weeks of pregnancy, the source of progesterone is:","options":["Placenta","Corpus luteum of pregnancy","Ovarian follicle","Anterior pituitary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page flow chart shows \"First 12 weeks → Corpus Luteum of pregnancy → Progesterone.\"  Why A wrong: The placenta produces progesterone later on throughout pregnancy (after 12 weeks).  Why C wrong: The ovarian follicle secretes estrogen, not the pregnancy progesterone.  Why D wrong: The anterior pituitary secretes gonadotropins, not progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Later on, throughout the rest of pregnancy (after the first 12 weeks), the source of progesterone is:","options":["Corpus luteum of pregnancy","Placenta","Adrenal gland","Ovary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page flow chart shows \"Later on Throughout pregnancy → Placenta → Progesterone.\"  Why A wrong: The corpus luteum is the source only in the first 12 weeks.  Why C wrong: The adrenal gland is not the stated source.  Why D wrong: The ovary/corpus luteum is superseded by the placenta after 12 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"A woman has a regular 32-day cycle. Using the notes' formula, on which day does she ovulate?","options":["Day 14","Day 16","Day 18","Day 20"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Day of ovulation = length of cycle − 14 = 32 − 14 = Day 18.  Why A wrong: Day 14 applies only to a 28-day cycle.  Why B wrong: Day 16 would correspond to a 30-day cycle.  Why D wrong: Day 20 would correspond to a 34-day cycle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"A nurse explains that in a 28-day cycle, the maximum corpus luteum size and activity occur on Day 22. This is because Day 22 is:","options":["The day of ovulation","8 days after ovulation","The first day of menstruation","The estrogen peak"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ovulation is Day 14; Day 22 is 8 days after ovulation, when the corpus luteum reaches maximum size and activity.  Why A wrong: Ovulation is Day 14, not Day 22.  Why C wrong: Menstruation begins around Day 28.  Why D wrong: The estrogen peak is on Day 12."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Which structure, forming from the follicle, secretes progesterone to support the endometrium in the luteal phase of a non-pregnant female?","options":["Corpus albicans","Corpus luteum","Graafian follicle","Placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The corpus luteum (from the ovulated follicle) is the source of progesterone in the non-pregnant female's luteal phase.  Why A wrong: Corpus albicans is the inactive degenerated remnant.  Why B is correct as above.  Why C wrong: The Graafian follicle is the pre-ovulatory follicle secreting estrogen.  Why D wrong: The placenta is a pregnancy source only after 12 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"In the non-pregnant cycle diagram, by Day 28 the corpus luteum has regressed into the:","options":["Graafian follicle","Corpus albicans","Granulosa lutein","Dominant follicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Fate of Corpus Luteum\" diagram shows the corpus luteum regressing to \"Corpus albicans\" toward Day 28 menstruation.  Why A wrong: The Graafian follicle is pre-ovulatory, before the corpus luteum forms.  Why C wrong: Granulosa lutein are the active cells of the functioning corpus luteum.  Why D wrong: The dominant follicle precedes ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Menstrual cycle Part-2, ovarian cycle…","question":"Which pair correctly matches the ovarian-cycle phase with its dominant hormone?","options":["Follicular phase — Progesterone","Luteal phase — Estrogen","Follicular phase — Estrogen; Luteal phase — Progesterone","Both phases — LH"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen dominates the follicular (first-half) phase and progesterone the luteal (second-half) phase, per the notes.  Why A wrong: The follicular phase is estrogen-dominant, not progesterone.  Why B wrong: The luteal phase is progesterone-dominant, not estrogen.  Why D wrong: LH surges at mid-cycle but does not dominate both phases."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the normal length (cycle interval) of a normal menstrual cycle?","options":["21–35 days","24–38 days","28–40 days","15–28 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the normal length of a menstrual cycle is 21–35 days.  Why B wrong: 24–38 days is not the value given in the notes.  Why C wrong: 28–40 days is incorrect; 28 days is the average, not the range upper limit.  Why D wrong: 15–28 days is not the stated normal range."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"According to the notes, the average length of the menstrual cycle is 28 days, seen in what percentage of females?","options":["5% females","10% females","15% females","50% females"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the average length is 28 days, seen in 15% of females.  Why A wrong: 5% is not the stated figure.  Why B wrong: 10% is not the stated figure.  Why D wrong: 50% is not stated; only 15% of females have exactly a 28-day cycle."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the normal number of days of bleeding during menstruation?","options":["1–3 days","2–7 days","4–10 days","5–12 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give normal number of days of bleeding as 2–7 days.  Why A wrong: 1–3 days is below the normal range.  Why C wrong: 4–10 days exceeds the stated normal upper limit.  Why D wrong: 5–12 days is far beyond the normal range."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the average number of days of bleeding during menstruation?","options":["1–2 days","2–4 days","4–6 days","6–8 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the average days of bleeding is 4–6 days.  Why A wrong: 1–2 days is too low.  Why B wrong: 2–4 days is not the stated average.  Why D wrong: 6–8 days exceeds the stated average."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the normal blood loss during a menstrual cycle?","options":["5–80 mL","10–100 mL","20–120 mL","1–5 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state normal blood loss is 5–80 mL.  Why B wrong: 10–100 mL is not the stated range.  Why C wrong: 20–120 mL exceeds the normal upper limit.  Why D wrong: 1–5 mL is far below normal."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"According to the notes, what is the \"best\" (most representative) figure for average menstrual blood loss?","options":["20 mL","35 mL","50 mL","80 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give average blood loss as 35 mL (best) with 50 mL as the 2nd best figure.  Why A wrong: 20 mL is not listed.  Why C wrong: 50 mL is listed as the \"2nd best,\" not the best.  Why D wrong: 80 mL is the upper limit of the normal range, not the average."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In the notes, what is the \"2nd best\" value quoted for average menstrual blood loss?","options":["35 mL","50 mL","80 mL","5 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 50 mL is the 2nd best figure for average blood loss (35 mL being the best).  Why A wrong: 35 mL is the \"best\" figure, not the 2nd best.  Why C wrong: 80 mL is the normal upper limit.  Why D wrong: 5 mL is the lower limit of normal blood loss."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"How is Atypical/Abnormal Uterine Bleeding (AUB) defined in the notes?","options":["Bleeding only after menopause","Any deviation from the normal characteristics of the menstrual cycle","Bleeding only during pregnancy","Bleeding limited to <2 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define AUB as any deviation from the normal characteristics of the menstrual cycle.  Why A wrong: That describes post-menopausal bleeding specifically, not AUB overall.  Why C wrong: Pregnancy bleeding is not the definition given.  Why D wrong: Bleeding <2 days is one specific type (hypomenorrhea), not the overall definition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which type of abnormal uterine bleeding is defined as excessive bleeding?","options":["Hypomenorrhea","Oligomenorrhea","Menorrhagia","Polymenorrhea"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Menorrhagia is defined as excessive bleeding.  Why A wrong: Hypomenorrhea is less bleeding.  Why B wrong: Oligomenorrhea is infrequent (long) cycles.  Why D wrong: Polymenorrhea is frequent (short) cycles."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In menorrhagia, the blood loss is more than what amount?","options":[">5 mL",">35 mL",">50 mL",">80 mL"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state menorrhagia = blood loss more than >80 mL.  Why A wrong: >5 mL is the hypomenorrhea threshold reference.  Why B wrong: 35 mL is the average normal blood loss.  Why C wrong: 50 mL is a normal average value."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In menorrhagia, the number of days of bleeding is more than how many days?","options":["≥8 days","≥5 days","≥2 days","≥12 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state menorrhagia = number of days of bleeding more than ≥8 days.  Why B wrong: ≥5 days is within/near the normal range.  Why C wrong: ≥2 days is the lower normal limit.  Why D wrong: ≥12 days is not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Menorrhagia (excessive menstrual bleeding) is classically seen in which uterine condition?","options":["Polyp in uterus","Fibroid uterus","Asherman syndrome","Imperforate hymen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state menorrhagia is seen in fibroid uterus.  Why A wrong: Polyp in the uterus is associated with metrorrhagia.  Why C wrong: Asherman syndrome is associated with hypomenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Imperforate hymen causes cryptomenorrhea, not menorrhagia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which type of abnormal uterine bleeding is defined as less bleeding?","options":["Menorrhagia","Hypomenorrhea","Metrorrhagia","Polymenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypomenorrhea is defined as less (reduced) bleeding.  Why A wrong: Menorrhagia is excessive bleeding.  Why C wrong: Metrorrhagia is irregular/intermenstrual bleeding.  Why D wrong: Polymenorrhea is frequent short cycles."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In hypomenorrhea, the blood loss is characterized as which of the following?","options":[">80 mL",">5 mL (reduced/scanty flow)","35 mL",">50 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list hypomenorrhea with blood loss shown as \">5 mL\" indicating scanty/reduced flow.  Why A wrong: >80 mL indicates menorrhagia.  Why C wrong: 35 mL is the normal average.  Why D wrong: >50 mL is not the hypomenorrhea descriptor."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In hypomenorrhea, the number of days of bleeding is:","options":["≥8 days","<2 days","4–6 days",">7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state hypomenorrhea = number of days of bleeding <2 days.  Why A wrong: ≥8 days indicates menorrhagia.  Why C wrong: 4–6 days is the normal average.  Why D wrong: >7 days is prolonged bleeding, not hypomenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Hypomenorrhea is classically seen in which condition?","options":["Fibroid uterus","Asherman syndrome","Polyp in uterus","Ovulatory dysfunction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state hypomenorrhea is seen in Asherman syndrome.  Why A wrong: Fibroid uterus is associated with menorrhagia.  Why C wrong: Polyp is associated with metrorrhagia.  Why D wrong: Ovulatory dysfunction is not the answer given for hypomenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What are the two key features of Asherman syndrome as described in the notes?","options":["Enlarged ovaries + thick endometrium","Adhesions in the uterus + thin endometrium","Polyp + thick endometrium","Fibroid + bulky uterus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in Asherman syndrome, adhesions are seen in the uterus and the endometrium is thin.  Why A wrong: Enlarged ovaries/thick endometrium is not described.  Why C wrong: Polyp with thick endometrium is not Asherman syndrome.  Why D wrong: Fibroid/bulky uterus describes menorrhagia causes."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which type of abnormal uterine bleeding is defined as short cycles (frequent menstruation)?","options":["Oligomenorrhea","Polymenorrhea","Menorrhagia","Hypomenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Polymenorrhea = short cycles / frequent menstruation.  Why A wrong: Oligomenorrhea is long/infrequent cycles.  Why B is correct as noted.  Why C wrong: Menorrhagia is excessive bleeding.  Why D wrong: Hypomenorrhea is scanty flow."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In polymenorrhea, the cycle length is:","options":["<21 days","≥35 days","21–35 days",">45 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state polymenorrhea = cycles <21 days.  Why B wrong: ≥35 days indicates oligomenorrhea.  Why C wrong: 21–35 days is the normal cycle length.  Why D wrong: >45 days is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"According to the notes, polymenorrhea is associated with which ovarian finding?","options":["Ovary atrophied","Ovary congested","Ovarian cyst","Ovarian torsion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in polymenorrhea the ovary is congested.  Why A wrong: Atrophy is not mentioned.  Why C wrong: Ovarian cyst is not mentioned for polymenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Ovarian torsion is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which type of abnormal uterine bleeding is defined as long cycles / infrequent menstruation?","options":["Polymenorrhea","Oligomenorrhea","Metrorrhagia","Menorrhagia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oligomenorrhea = long cycles / infrequent menstruation.  Why A wrong: Polymenorrhea is short/frequent cycles.  Why C wrong: Metrorrhagia is irregular/intermenstrual bleeding.  Why D wrong: Menorrhagia is excessive bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In oligomenorrhea, the cycle length is:","options":["<21 days","≥35 days","21–28 days","28–35 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state oligomenorrhea = cycles ≥35 days.  Why A wrong: <21 days indicates polymenorrhea.  Why C wrong: 21–28 days is within normal range.  Why D wrong: 28–35 days is within normal range."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which type of abnormal uterine bleeding is defined as irregular bleeding / intermenstrual bleeding?","options":["Menorrhagia","Metrorrhagia","Oligomenorrhea","Hypomenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Metrorrhagia = irregular bleeding / intermenstrual bleeding.  Why A wrong: Menorrhagia is excessive bleeding.  Why C wrong: Oligomenorrhea is infrequent long cycles.  Why D wrong: Hypomenorrhea is scanty bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Metrorrhagia is classically seen in which uterine condition?","options":["Fibroid uterus","Polyp in uterus","Asherman syndrome","Imperforate hymen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state metrorrhagia is seen in polyp in uterus.  Why A wrong: Fibroid uterus is associated with menorrhagia.  Why C wrong: Asherman syndrome is associated with hypomenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Imperforate hymen causes cryptomenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"A female is having excessive bleeding occurring at irregular intervals. What is this called?","options":["Menorrhagia","Metrorrhagia","Menometrorrhagia","Polymenorrhea"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes' Q states excessive bleeding at irregular intervals = menometrorrhagia (combination of menorrhagia + metrorrhagia).  Why A wrong: Menorrhagia alone is excessive but at regular intervals.  Why B wrong: Metrorrhagia alone is irregular but not necessarily excessive.  Why D wrong: Polymenorrhea is frequent cycles, not the combined pattern."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"A 25-year-old female complains of intermenstrual bleeding. She is a case of which condition?","options":["Menorrhagia","Metrorrhagia","Hypomenorrhea","Oligomenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes' Q states intermenstrual bleeding = metrorrhagia.  Why A wrong: Menorrhagia is excessive bleeding.  Why C wrong: Hypomenorrhea is scanty bleeding.  Why D wrong: Oligomenorrhea is infrequent cycles."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"How is AUB (Abnormal Uterine Bleeding) distinguished from DUB in the notes?","options":["AUB = excessive bleeding due to some cause/pelvic pathology; DUB = excessive bleeding without any reason/absence of pelvic pathology","AUB and DUB are identical terms","AUB = scanty bleeding; DUB = heavy bleeding","AUB occurs only after menopause; DUB only before menarche"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state AUB means excessive bleeding due to some cause (reason)/pelvic pathology (e.g., fibroid, polyp), whereas DUB means excessive bleeding without any reason / in absence of pelvic pathology.  Why B wrong: They are explicitly distinguished.  Why C wrong: Both refer to excessive bleeding, not scanty vs heavy.  Why D wrong: Neither is defined by age of onset here."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"DUB (Dysfunctional Uterine Bleeding) is best described as which of the following?","options":["Bleeding with an identifiable fibroid","A diagnosis of exclusion (excessive bleeding in absence of pelvic pathology)","Bleeding caused by a uterine polyp","Bleeding caused by pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state DUB is excessive bleeding without any reason / in absence of pelvic pathology and is a diagnosis of exclusion.  Why A wrong: A fibroid is a pelvic pathology (that would make it AUB).  Why C wrong: A polyp is a pelvic pathology.  Why D wrong: Pregnancy is a specific cause, not DUB."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Give an example of a pelvic pathology that would classify bleeding as AUB (not DUB).","options":["Fibroid or polyp","Stress","Excessive exercise","Idiopathic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give \"Eg: Fibroid, Polyp\" as causes making bleeding AUB.  Why B wrong: Stress is not a listed pelvic pathology example here.  Why C wrong: Exercise is not a pelvic pathology example.  Why D wrong: Idiopathic (no reason) would be DUB, not AUB."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"The causes of AUB are remembered by the mnemonic PALM-COEIN. What does the \"P\" stand for?","options":["Pregnancy","Polyp","PID","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In PALM-COEIN, P = Polyp.  Why A wrong: Pregnancy is not part of the PALM-COEIN acronym here.  Why C wrong: PID is not a PALM-COEIN letter.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is not part of the mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In the PALM-COEIN causes of AUB, what does the \"A\" stand for?","options":["Anemia","Adenomyosis","Adhesions","Atrophy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A = Adenomyosis.  Why A wrong: Anemia is not in the mnemonic.  Why C wrong: Adhesions are not the \"A.\"  Why D wrong: Atrophy is not the \"A.\""},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In PALM-COEIN, the \"L\" stands for which cause of AUB?","options":["Leiomyoma / Fibroid uterus","Luteal defect","Leukemia","Lymphoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: L = Leiomyoma / Fibroid uterus.  Why B wrong: Luteal defect is not the \"L.\"  Why C wrong: Leukemia is not listed.  Why D wrong: Lymphoma is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In PALM-COEIN, the \"M\" stands for which cause of AUB?","options":["Menopause","Malignancy","Menarche","Mittelschmerz"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: M = Malignancy.  Why A wrong: Menopause is not the \"M.\"  Why C wrong: Menarche is not a cause in the mnemonic.  Why D wrong: Mittelschmerz is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In PALM-COEIN, the \"C\" stands for which cause of AUB?","options":["Cancer","Cervicitis","Coagulation pathology","Cyst"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: C = Coagulation pathology.  Why A wrong: Cancer/malignancy is covered by \"M.\"  Why B wrong: Cervicitis is not listed.  Why D wrong: Cyst is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In PALM-COEIN, the \"O\" stands for which cause of AUB?","options":["Obstruction","Ovulatory cause","Obesity","Oophoritis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: O = Ovulatory cause.  Why A wrong: Obstruction is not the \"O.\"  Why C wrong: Obesity is not listed.  Why D wrong: Oophoritis is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In PALM-COEIN, the \"E\" stands for which cause of AUB (as noted in the source)?","options":["Endometrial cause (Endometriosis)","Ectopic pregnancy","Endocervical polyp","Estrogen excess"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state E = Endometrial cause (Endometriosis).  Why B wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is not listed.  Why C wrong: Endocervical polyp is not the \"E.\"  Why D wrong: Estrogen excess is not the \"E.\""},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In PALM-COEIN, the \"I\" stands for which cause of AUB?","options":["Infection","Iatrogenic","Idiopathic","Inflammation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: I = Iatrogenic.  Why A wrong: Infection is not the \"I.\"  Why C wrong: Idiopathic is not the \"I\" (that overlaps with \"N — Not yet identified\").  Why D wrong: Inflammation is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In PALM-COEIN, the \"N\" stands for which cause of AUB?","options":["Neoplasm","Not yet identified","Necrosis","Nephropathy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: N = Not yet identified.  Why A wrong: Neoplasm is covered under Malignancy.  Why C wrong: Necrosis is not listed.  Why D wrong: Nephropathy is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"When a female presents with excessive bleeding, what is the FIRST thing to assess according to the notes' algorithm?","options":["Do a Pap smear","See the age of the patient","Perform dilatation & curettage","Start tranexamic acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart begins with \"See the age of patient\" to guide further evaluation.  Why A wrong: Pap smear is not the first step in the algorithm.  Why C wrong: D&C is a later/last step in select patients.  Why D wrong: Drugs come after age-based assessment."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Excessive uterine bleeding in a girl who has just started her periods (menarche) is called what?","options":["Puberty menorrhagia","Metrorrhagia","Cryptomenorrhea","Post-menopausal bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state excessive bleeding in a girl who just started her period/menarche is called puberty menorrhagia.  Why B wrong: Metrorrhagia is intermenstrual bleeding.  Why C wrong: Cryptomenorrhea is hidden menstruation.  Why D wrong: Post-menopausal bleeding occurs after menopause."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"How is puberty menorrhagia managed according to the notes?","options":["Manage by drugs","Immediate hysterectomy","Dilatation & curettage","Per vaginal examination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state puberty menorrhagia is managed by drugs.  Why B wrong: Hysterectomy is reserved for females ≥40 years.  Why C wrong: D&C should never be done in puberty menorrhagia.  Why D wrong: Per vaginal exam should never be done in puberty menorrhagia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In a female with puberty menorrhagia, which of the following should NEVER be done? (Select all that apply)","options":["Per vaginal exam","Dilatation & curettage","Drug therapy","Reassurance","Immediate hysterectomy"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Never Do\" box lists (1) Per vaginal exam and (2) Dilatation & curettage in puberty menorrhagia females.  Why C wrong: Drug therapy is the recommended management.  Why D wrong: Reassurance is appropriate, not contraindicated.  Why E wrong: Hysterectomy is not listed as a \"never do\" item in this box, though it is not appropriate here either; the source's explicit prohibitions are PV exam and D&C."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In the age-based evaluation of excessive bleeding, which age range is labeled \"reproductive age\"?","options":["12–24 years","25–39 years","40–50 years","≥40 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label reproductive age as 25–39 years.  Why A wrong: 12–24 is not the stated range.  Why C wrong: 40–50 is not the labeled reproductive age.  Why D wrong: ≥40 is the peri-menopausal category."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In the notes, a peri-menopausal female is defined by which age?","options":["≥30 years","≥35 years","≥40 years","≥50 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define peri-menopausal female as ≥40 years.  Why A wrong: ≥30 is not the stated cut-off.  Why B wrong: ≥35 is not the stated cut-off.  Why D wrong: ≥50 is not the stated cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In a reproductive-age or peri-menopausal female presenting with excessive bleeding, what should be ruled out FIRST?","options":["Cancer","Pregnancy","Fibroid","Thyroid disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm states to first \"Rule out pregnancy\" (and proceed only if negative).  Why A wrong: Cancer workup (endometrial biopsy) comes later.  Why C wrong: Fibroid is found via ultrasound, done after ruling out pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Thyroid disease is not the first step in this algorithm."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"After pregnancy is ruled out (negative) in a female with excessive bleeding, what is the next investigation per the algorithm?","options":["Transvaginal ultrasound","Endometrial biopsy","Dilatation & curettage","Hysterectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state: after ruling out pregnancy (–ve), then do transvaginal ultrasound.  Why B wrong: Endometrial biopsy comes later if bleeding does not stop with drugs.  Why C wrong: D&C is the last step.  Why D wrong: Hysterectomy is a surgical option, not the next investigation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"After transvaginal ultrasound in a bleeding female, what does the algorithm advise doing first before further procedures?","options":["First try drugs to decrease bleeding","Do immediate hysterectomy","Do endometrial biopsy","Do D&C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state after TVS, first try drugs to decrease bleeding.  Why B wrong: Hysterectomy is not the first measure.  Why C wrong: Biopsy is done only if bleeding does not stop after drugs.  Why D wrong: D&C is the final step."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"If bleeding does NOT stop after drug therapy, what should be done to rule out cancer/hyperplasia of the endometrium?","options":["Transvaginal ultrasound","Endometrial biopsy","Pregnancy test","Pap smear"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if bleeding does not stop, do endometrial biopsy to rule out cancer/hyperplasia of endometrium.  Why A wrong: TVS was already done earlier.  Why C wrong: Pregnancy was ruled out at the start.  Why D wrong: Pap smear screens cervix, not endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the FINAL step in the algorithm for excessive bleeding after endometrial biopsy?","options":["Do dilatation & curettage","Do transvaginal ultrasound","Start OCPs","Reassure and discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm ends with \"Do dilatation & curettage.\"  Why B wrong: TVS was performed earlier.  Why C wrong: OCPs are part of the initial drug trial.  Why D wrong: Reassurance is not the final surgical step described."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which of the following drugs is used to treat excessive bleeding in any age group? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tranexamic acid","OCPs (Estrogen + Progesterone)","Progesterone","Warfarin","Aspirin"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list three drugs for excessive bleeding in any age group: (1) Tranexamic acid, (2) OCPs (estrogen + progesterone), (3) Progesterone.  Why D wrong: Warfarin is an anticoagulant and would worsen bleeding.  Why E wrong: Aspirin is an antiplatelet and would worsen bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"OCPs used to treat excessive bleeding contain which hormone combination?","options":["Estrogen + Progesterone","Estrogen only","Progesterone + Testosterone","FSH + LH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state OCPs = Oral Contraceptive Pills = Estrogen + Progesterone.  Why B wrong: OCPs described here are combined, not estrogen-only.  Why C wrong: Testosterone is not a component.  Why D wrong: FSH + LH are pituitary hormones, not OCP components."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the surgical option for treating excessive bleeding in females ≥40 years?","options":["Hysterectomy (Total Abdominal Hysterectomy)","Myomectomy","Endometrial ablation only","Oophorectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the surgical option in females ≥40 years is hysterectomy (total abdominal hysterectomy).  Why B wrong: Myomectomy is not the option stated.  Why C wrong: Endometrial ablation is not the option stated here.  Why D wrong: Oophorectomy (ovary removal) is not the stated surgery."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In a Total Abdominal Hysterectomy (TAH), which structures are removed?","options":["Uterus only","Uterus + Cervix","Uterus + Cervix + Ovaries + Tubes","Cervix only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state TAH removes uterus + cervix.  Why A wrong: Cervix is also removed, not uterus alone.  Why C wrong: Removal of ovaries and tubes describes TAH with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, not plain TAH.  Why D wrong: More than just the cervix is removed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the medical term for pain during menstruation?","options":["Dyspareunia","Dysmenorrhea","Amenorrhea","Menorrhagia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state pain during menstruation is dysmenorrhea.  Why A wrong: Dyspareunia is pain during intercourse.  Why C wrong: Amenorrhea is absence of menstruation.  Why D wrong: Menorrhagia is excessive bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Dysmenorrhea is classified into which two main types?","options":["Acute and chronic","Primary and secondary","Mild and severe","Physiological and pathological"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes classify dysmenorrhea into primary and secondary.  Why A wrong: Acute/chronic is not the classification used.  Why C wrong: Mild/severe is not the classification.  Why D wrong: Physiological/pathological is used for amenorrhea, not dysmenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which statement characterizes PRIMARY dysmenorrhea?","options":["There is a pelvic pathology causing the pain","There is no identifiable reason/pathology for the pain","It starts after age 35","It is caused by endometriosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in primary dysmenorrhea there is no reason for pain.  Why A wrong: Pelvic pathology causing pain describes secondary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Onset after 35 describes secondary dysmenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Endometriosis is a cause of secondary dysmenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In primary dysmenorrhea, when does the patient typically first experience the pain?","options":["Only after age 35","Since menarche (onset of menstruation)","Only after childbirth","Only after menopause"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state primary dysmenorrhea is seen in young females who give a history that since menarche they have pain during menstruation.  Why A wrong: Onset after 35 is a feature of secondary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Post-childbirth onset is not described.  Why D wrong: Post-menopause onset is not described."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In primary dysmenorrhea, the pain is located in which area?","options":["Localized to one side","Centralized / suprapubic area","Right iliac fossa only","Lower back only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state pain in primary dysmenorrhea is centralized/suprapubic.  Why A wrong: Localized pain is a feature of secondary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Right iliac fossa is not described.  Why D wrong: Lower back only is not described."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In primary dysmenorrhea, the timing of pain relative to menstruation is:","options":["Starts 1–2 days before menstruation and subsides with onset of menstruation","Starts 4–7 days before and is not relieved with menstruation","Occurs only at mid-cycle","Occurs 1 week after menstruation ends"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state primary dysmenorrhea pain starts 1–2 days before menstruation and subsides with onset of menstruation.  Why B wrong: 4–7 days before and not relieved describes secondary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Mid-cycle pain describes Mittelschmerz.  Why D wrong: Pain a week after menstruation is not described."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Primary dysmenorrhea is also known by which name?","options":["Congestive dysmenorrhea","Spasmodic dysmenorrhea","Membranous dysmenorrhea","Ovulatory dysmenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state primary dysmenorrhea is also called spasmodic dysmenorrhea.  Why A wrong: Congestive dysmenorrhea is the alternate name for secondary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Membranous dysmenorrhea is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Ovulatory dysmenorrhea is not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which drugs are used to treat primary dysmenorrhea? (Select all that apply)","options":["NSAIDs","OCP","Antibiotics","Warfarin","Insulin"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list NSAIDs and OCP as treatment for primary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Antibiotics are not indicated for primary dysmenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Warfarin is an anticoagulant, not a treatment.  Why E wrong: Insulin is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which statement characterizes SECONDARY dysmenorrhea?","options":["There is no reason for the pain","There is some pelvic pathology causing the pain","Pain since menarche","Pain is centralized/suprapubic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in secondary dysmenorrhea there is some pelvic pathology causing pain.  Why A wrong: \"No reason for pain\" describes primary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Pain since menarche describes primary dysmenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Centralized/suprapubic pain describes primary dysmenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of secondary dysmenorrhea?","options":["Endometriosis","Fibroid","PID","Adenomyosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of secondary dysmenorrhea is endometriosis.  Why B wrong: Fibroid is a cause but not the most common.  Why C wrong: PID is a cause but not the most common.  Why D wrong: Adenomyosis is a cause but not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which of the following are causes of secondary dysmenorrhea listed in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Endometriosis","Adenomyosis","Fibroid","PID","Imperforate hymen"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list endometriosis (most common), adenomyosis, fibroid, and PID as causes of secondary dysmenorrhea.  Why E wrong: Imperforate hymen is the cause of cryptomenorrhea, not listed for secondary dysmenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Secondary dysmenorrhea is most commonly seen in reproductive-age females of which age?","options":[">35 years","<15 years","12–13 years",">50 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state secondary dysmenorrhea is seen in reproductive-age females, most commonly age >35 years.  Why B wrong: <15 years is more typical of primary dysmenorrhea (young girls).  Why C wrong: 12–13 years is the age of menarche.  Why D wrong: >50 years is post-menopausal age."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"A woman states she previously had no pain during menstruation, but now suddenly pain has started. This history is typical of which type of dysmenorrhea?","options":["Primary dysmenorrhea","Secondary dysmenorrhea","Mittelschmerz","Physiological dysmenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in secondary dysmenorrhea the patient says earlier she did not have pain during menstruation but now suddenly pain has started.  Why A wrong: In primary dysmenorrhea pain has been present since menarche.  Why C wrong: Mittelschmerz is mid-cycle ovulation pain.  Why D wrong: \"Physiological dysmenorrhea\" is not a category used here."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In secondary dysmenorrhea, the pain is described as:","options":["Centralized/suprapubic","Localized","Diffuse throughout body","Referred to the shoulder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state pain in secondary dysmenorrhea is localized.  Why A wrong: Centralized/suprapubic pain is a feature of primary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Diffuse body pain is not described.  Why D wrong: Shoulder-referred pain is not described."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In secondary dysmenorrhea, the timing of pain relative to menstruation is:","options":["Starts 1–2 days before and subsides with menstruation","Starts 4–7 days before menstruation and is not relieved with menstruation","Only at mid-cycle","Only after menstruation ends"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state secondary dysmenorrhea pain starts 4–7 days before menstruation and is not relieved with menstruation.  Why A wrong: 1–2 days before and relieved with menstruation describes primary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Mid-cycle pain describes Mittelschmerz.  Why D wrong: Post-menstruation pain only is not described."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Secondary dysmenorrhea is also known by which name?","options":["Spasmodic dysmenorrhea","Congestive dysmenorrhea","Primary dysmenorrhea","Membranous dysmenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state secondary dysmenorrhea is also called congestive dysmenorrhea.  Why A wrong: Spasmodic dysmenorrhea is the name for primary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Primary dysmenorrhea is the opposite category.  Why D wrong: Membranous dysmenorrhea is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the treatment principle for secondary dysmenorrhea?","options":["Treat the cause","Only NSAIDs","Only OCPs","Immediate hysterectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the treatment for secondary dysmenorrhea is to treat the cause.  Why B wrong: NSAIDs alone is the treatment for primary dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: OCPs alone is not the stated approach for secondary.  Why D wrong: Hysterectomy is not the general principle stated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is Mittelschmerz syndrome?","options":["Pain at the time of ovulation","Pain during menstruation","Pain during intercourse","Pain after menopause"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define Mittelschmerz syndrome as pain at the time of ovulation.  Why B wrong: Pain during menstruation is dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Pain during intercourse is dyspareunia.  Why D wrong: Post-menopausal pain is not Mittelschmerz."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"A patient complains of cyclical abdominal pain occurring in the middle of her cycle. This is characteristic of:","options":["Mittelschmerz syndrome","Primary dysmenorrhea","Metrorrhagia","Cryptomenorrhea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe Mittelschmerz as a patient with complaint of cyclical pain in the abdomen in the middle of the cycle (ovulation).  Why B wrong: Primary dysmenorrhea pain is with menstruation, not mid-cycle.  Why C wrong: Metrorrhagia is bleeding, not pain.  Why D wrong: Cryptomenorrhea is hidden menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is dyspareunia?","options":["Pain during menstruation","Pain during intercourse","Pain during ovulation","Absence of menstruation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define dyspareunia as pain during intercourse.  Why A wrong: Pain during menstruation is dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Pain during ovulation is Mittelschmerz.  Why D wrong: Absence of menstruation is amenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"If a female complains of BOTH dysmenorrhea and dyspareunia, which diagnosis should always be considered?","options":["Endometriosis","Fibroid","Asherman syndrome","PCOS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if a female complains of dysmenorrhea and dyspareunia, always think of endometriosis.  Why B wrong: Fibroid is not the classic pairing highlighted.  Why C wrong: Asherman syndrome causes hypomenorrhea.  Why D wrong: PCOS is a cause of secondary amenorrhea, not the dysmenorrhea+dyspareunia pairing."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the definition of menarche?","options":["Onset of menstruation in a female","Permanent cessation of menstruation","Absence of menstruation","Pain during menstruation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define menarche as onset of menstruation in a female.  Why B wrong: Permanent cessation of menstruation is menopause.  Why C wrong: Absence of menstruation is amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Pain during menstruation is dysmenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the normal age of menarche according to the notes?","options":["10–11 years","12–13 years","14–16 years","8–9 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state age of menarche = 12–13 years.  Why A wrong: 10–11 years is not the stated normal age.  Why C wrong: 14–16 years is not the stated normal age.  Why D wrong: 8–9 years would be precocious."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"If menarche occurs in a female less than 10 years of age, it is called:","options":["Precocious menstruation","Premature menopause","Primary amenorrhea","Cryptomenorrhea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if menarche occurs in a female <10 years of age it is called precocious menstruation.  Why B wrong: Premature menopause is early cessation, not early onset.  Why C wrong: Primary amenorrhea is never having menstruated.  Why D wrong: Cryptomenorrhea is hidden menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Menopause is characterized by which ovarian change?","options":["No more follicles in the ovary","Excessive follicles in the ovary","Ovarian congestion","Multiple ovarian cysts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state menopause = no more follicles in the ovary.  Why B wrong: Excessive follicles is the opposite.  Why C wrong: Ovarian congestion is a feature of polymenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Multiple ovarian cysts describe PCOS."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Menopause is defined as:","options":["Permanent cessation of menstruation","Temporary stopping of menstruation","Onset of menstruation","Irregular menstruation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state menopause = permanent cessation of menstruation.  Why B wrong: It is permanent, not temporary.  Why C wrong: Onset of menstruation is menarche.  Why D wrong: Irregular menstruation is metrorrhagia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the average age of menopause in India according to the notes?","options":["45 years","47 years","51 years","55 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state age of menopause in India = 47 years.  Why A wrong: 45 years is not the stated Indian value.  Why C wrong: 51 years is the worldwide value.  Why D wrong: 55 years is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the average worldwide age of menopause according to the notes?","options":["47 years","51 years","45 years","40 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state worldwide age of menopause = 51 years.  Why A wrong: 47 years is the Indian value.  Why C wrong: 45 years is not stated.  Why D wrong: 40 years is the cut-off for premature menopause."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"If menopause occurs in a female less than 40 years of age, it is called:","options":["Premature menopause","Precocious menstruation","Perimenopause","Secondary amenorrhea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if menopause occurs in a female <40 years = premature menopause.  Why B wrong: Precocious menstruation is early menarche.  Why C wrong: Perimenopause is the transition around normal menopause.  Why D wrong: Secondary amenorrhea is a broader category."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"For diagnosis of menopause, a female must have had no menstruation for how long?","options":["3 months","6 months","12 months","24 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for menopause diagnosis a female does not have menstruation × 12 months.  Why A wrong: 3 months is the reference for secondary amenorrhea (regular cycles).  Why B wrong: 6 months is the reference for secondary amenorrhea (irregular cycles).  Why D wrong: 24 months is not the stated period."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is post-menopausal bleeding?","options":["Any episode of bleeding after menopause has been established","Heavy bleeding during menstruation","Bleeding at menarche","Mid-cycle bleeding in a reproductive woman"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in a menopausal female (no menstruation × 12 months) any episode of bleeding after menopause is called post-menopausal bleeding.  Why B wrong: Heavy menstrual bleeding is menorrhagia.  Why C wrong: Bleeding at menarche is normal onset of menstruation.  Why D wrong: Mid-cycle bleeding in a reproductive woman is metrorrhagia, not post-menopausal bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"A female presents with post-menopausal bleeding. What investigation should be done first?","options":["Transvaginal ultrasound (TVS)","Endometrial biopsy","Pregnancy test","Hysterectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in a female with post-menopausal bleeding, first do TVS (transvaginal ultrasound).  Why B wrong: Endometrial biopsy is done based on TVS findings (thick endometrium).  Why C wrong: Pregnancy is not relevant after menopause.  Why D wrong: Hysterectomy is not the initial investigation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In a female with post-menopausal bleeding, at what endometrial thickness on TVS should an endometrial biopsy be done?","options":["> 4 mm","> 10 mm","> 2 mm","> 8 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that on TVS, if the thickness of endometrium is > 4 mm, do endometrial biopsy.  Why B wrong: > 10 mm is not the stated cut-off.  Why C wrong: > 2 mm is not the stated cut-off.  Why D wrong: > 8 mm is not the stated cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the definition of amenorrhea?","options":["Absence of menstruation","Painful menstruation","Excessive menstruation","Frequent menstruation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define amenorrhea as absence of menstruation.  Why B wrong: Painful menstruation is dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Excessive menstruation is menorrhagia.  Why D wrong: Frequent menstruation is polymenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Amenorrhea is broadly classified into which two categories?","options":["Physiological and pathological","Primary and menopausal","Acute and chronic","Spasmodic and congestive"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes classify absence of menstruation into physiological and pathological.  Why B wrong: Primary/secondary is a subdivision of pathological, not the top-level split.  Why C wrong: Acute/chronic is not used.  Why D wrong: Spasmodic/congestive refers to dysmenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which of the following are PHYSIOLOGICAL causes of amenorrhea? (Select all that apply)","options":["Before puberty","Pregnancy","Breast feeding","After menopause","PCOS"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list physiological amenorrhea as occurring before puberty, during pregnancy, during breast feeding, and after menopause.  Why E wrong: PCOS/PCOD is listed as a cause of secondary (pathological) amenorrhea, not physiological."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Pathological amenorrhea is subdivided into which two types?","options":["Primary and secondary","Physiological and iatrogenic","Acute and chronic","Mild and severe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes subdivide pathological amenorrhea into primary and secondary amenorrhea.  Why B wrong: Physiological is a separate top-level branch.  Why C wrong: Acute/chronic is not used.  Why D wrong: Mild/severe is not used."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What defines PRIMARY amenorrhea?","options":["The female has never menstruated / has not attained menarche","The female was menstruating but has now stopped","Bleeding is hidden due to obstruction","Excessive bleeding at irregular intervals"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state primary amenorrhea means the female has never menstruated / has not attained menarche.  Why B wrong: Previously menstruating then stopping is secondary amenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Hidden bleeding due to obstruction is cryptomenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Excessive irregular bleeding is menometrorrhagia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"According to the exact definition, primary amenorrhea is diagnosed if a female does NOT menstruate by what age in the ABSENCE of breast development?","options":["13 years","15 years","16 years","18 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state primary amenorrhea = not menstruating by 13 years of age in absence of breast (development).  Why B wrong: 15 years applies when breast development is present.  Why C wrong: 16 years is not the stated cut-off.  Why D wrong: 18 years is not the stated cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"According to the exact definition, primary amenorrhea is diagnosed if a female does NOT menstruate by what age in the PRESENCE of breast development?","options":["13 years","15 years","16 years","12 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state primary amenorrhea = not menstruating by 15 years of age in presence of breast (development).  Why A wrong: 13 years applies in the absence of breast development.  Why C wrong: 16 years is not the stated cut-off.  Why D wrong: 12 years is not the stated cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What defines SECONDARY amenorrhea?","options":["A female who never menstruated","A female who was menstruating normally earlier but now has no menstruation","Hidden menstruation due to imperforate hymen","Menstruation before age 10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state secondary amenorrhea means the female was menstruating normally earlier but now there is no menstruation.  Why A wrong: Never menstruating is primary amenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Hidden menstruation is cryptomenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Menstruation before 10 is precocious menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In a female with REGULAR cycles, for how many missed cycles is secondary amenorrhea diagnosed?","options":["Past 3 cycles","Past 6 cycles","Past 12 cycles","Past 1 cycle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state secondary amenorrhea = no menstruation for past 3 cycles in females with regular cycles.  Why B wrong: 6 (months) is the criterion for irregular cycles.  Why C wrong: 12 cycles/months is the menopause diagnostic period.  Why D wrong: 1 cycle is not the stated criterion."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"In a female with IRREGULAR cycles, for how long of no menstruation is secondary amenorrhea diagnosed?","options":["3 months","6 months","12 months","1 month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state secondary amenorrhea = no menstruation for 6 months in females with irregular cycles.  Why A wrong: 3 cycles applies to regular cycles.  Why C wrong: 12 months is the menopause diagnostic period.  Why D wrong: 1 month is not the stated criterion."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of secondary amenorrhea?","options":["Pregnancy","Hypothyroidism","PCOS","Asherman syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common (m/c) cause of secondary amenorrhea is pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Hypothyroidism is a listed other cause, not the most common.  Why C wrong: PCOS is a listed other cause.  Why D wrong: Asherman syndrome is a listed other cause."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"other causes\" of secondary amenorrhea? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hypothyroidism","Severe anemia","PCOS/PCOD","Asherman syndrome","Increased prolactin levels"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list hypothyroidism, severe anemia, PCOS/PCOD, Asherman syndrome, stress/excessive dieting, excessive physical exercise, and increased prolactin levels as other causes of secondary amenorrhea — all listed options are included.  Why each is correct: Each is explicitly named in the \"Other causes\" list."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"Which of the following lifestyle/behavioral factors are listed as causes of secondary amenorrhea? (Select all that apply)","options":["Stress","Excessive dieting","Excessive physical exercise","Adequate sleep","Moderate hydration"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list stress, excessive dieting, and excessive physical exercise as causes of secondary amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Adequate sleep is not listed as a cause.  Why E wrong: Moderate hydration is not listed as a cause."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"An elevation of which hormone is listed as a cause of secondary amenorrhea?","options":["Prolactin","Insulin","Cortisol","Progesterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list increased (↑) prolactin levels as a cause of secondary amenorrhea.  Why B wrong: Insulin is not the listed hormone.  Why C wrong: Cortisol is not listed.  Why D wrong: Progesterone elevation is not the listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is cryptomenorrhea?","options":["Hidden menstruation — the female menstruates normally but menstrual blood fails to come out due to obstruction in the passage","Complete absence of menstruation for life","Excessive menstrual bleeding","Pain during menstruation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define cryptomenorrhea as hidden menstruation, where the female menstruates normally but menstrual blood fails to come out after some obstruction in the passage.  Why B wrong: Lifelong absence of menstruation is primary amenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Excessive bleeding is menorrhagia.  Why D wrong: Pain during menstruation is dysmenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of cryptomenorrhea?","options":["Imperforate hymen (no opening in hymen)","Fibroid uterus","Cervical cancer","Asherman syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of cryptomenorrhea is imperforate hymen (no opening in the hymen).  Why B wrong: Fibroid uterus is a cause of menorrhagia.  Why C wrong: Cervical cancer is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Asherman syndrome causes hypomenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"What does \"imperforate hymen\" mean?","options":["No opening in the hymen","A torn hymen","An enlarged cervix","A prolapsed uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state imperforate hymen means no opening in the hymen.  Why B wrong: A torn hymen is not imperforate.  Why C wrong: Enlarged cervix is unrelated.  Why D wrong: Prolapsed uterus is a different condition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"How does a patient with imperforate hymen typically present on diagnosis?","options":["Complains of pain in abdomen but no menstruation","Heavy vaginal bleeding","Painless amenorrhea with breast atrophy","Frequent menstruation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the diagnosis of imperforate hymen is a patient who complains of pain in the abdomen but has no menstruation.  Why B wrong: There is no external bleeding because blood cannot escape.  Why C wrong: Painless amenorrhea with breast atrophy is not described.  Why D wrong: Frequent menstruation is not a feature."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Characteristics of normal menstrual cycle…","question":"On local examination of a patient with imperforate hymen, what is the classic finding?","options":["Tensed, bluish, and bulging hymen","Pale, flat hymen","Ulcerated cervix","Everted vaginal walls"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state local examination shows a tensed, bluish, and bulging hymen (due to trapped menstrual blood behind it).  Why B wrong: A pale, flat hymen is not the described finding.  Why C wrong: Ulcerated cervix is unrelated.  Why D wrong: Everted vaginal walls are not described."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"In a girl with an imperforate hymen, blood collecting and accumulating within the uterus is termed:","options":["Hematocolpos","Hematometra","Hematosalpinx","Hematoperitoneum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state blood can collect in the uterus, which is called Hematometra.  Why A wrong: Hematocolpos is collection of blood in the vagina, not the uterus.  Why C wrong: Hematosalpinx is collection of blood in the fallopian tube (not mentioned as the uterine collection).  Why D wrong: Hematoperitoneum is blood in the peritoneal cavity, not the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Accumulation of menstrual blood within the vagina due to an obstructing hymen is called:","options":["Hematometra","Hematocolpos","Hematocele","Hydrocolpos"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify blood collecting in the vagina is termed Hematocolpos.  Why A wrong: Hematometra refers to blood collection in the uterus.  Why C wrong: Hematocele is a blood collection (e.g., in the tunica vaginalis/pouch), not the described vaginal accumulation.  Why D wrong: Hydrocolpos is accumulation of watery/mucoid fluid in the vagina, not blood."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The recommended treatment to relieve an imperforate hymen with hematometra/hematocolpos is:","options":["Total hysterectomy","Give a cruciate (X-shaped) incision on the hymen","Vaginal dilatation only","Hormonal therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state treatment is to give a cruciate (X) incision on the hymen to release the collected blood.  Why A wrong: Hysterectomy is not indicated for a simple imperforate hymen.  Why C wrong: Dilatation alone does not open an imperforate hymen; a cruciate incision is required.  Why D wrong: Hormonal therapy does not relieve mechanical hymenal obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The most common cause of primary amenorrhea is:","options":["Mullerian agenesis","Turner syndrome (45,XO)","Polycystic ovarian syndrome","Sheehan syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of primary amenorrhea is Turner syndrome (45,XO).  Why A wrong: Mullerian agenesis is listed as the 2nd most common cause, not the most common.  Why C wrong: PCOS typically causes secondary amenorrhea/oligomenorrhea, not the most common primary amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Sheehan syndrome (postpartum pituitary necrosis) causes secondary amenorrhea, not primary."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The karyotype associated with Turner syndrome, the most common cause of primary amenorrhea, is:","options":["46,XX","47,XXY","45,XO","46,XY"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give Turner syndrome as 45,XO (monosomy X).  Why A wrong: 46,XX is the normal female karyotype.  Why B wrong: 47,XXY is Klinefelter syndrome, seen in males.  Why D wrong: 46,XY is the normal male karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The second most common cause of primary amenorrhea, in which both Mullerian ducts are absent, is:","options":["Turner syndrome","Mullerian agenesis (46,XX)","Kallmann syndrome","Asherman syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 2nd most common cause of primary amenorrhea is Mullerian agenesis (46,XX) with both Mullerian ducts absent.  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome is the most common cause, not the second.  Why C wrong: Kallmann syndrome is a cause of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism but is not listed here as the second most common.  Why D wrong: Asherman syndrome (uterine synechiae) causes secondary amenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"In Mullerian agenesis, the karyotype is typically:","options":["45,XO","46,XX","47,XXX","46,XY"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Mullerian agenesis has a 46,XX karyotype (normal female chromosomes but absent Mullerian ducts).  Why A wrong: 45,XO is Turner syndrome.  Why C wrong: 47,XXX is triple X syndrome, not the karyotype given for Mullerian agenesis.  Why D wrong: 46,XY is a normal male karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"In Mullerian agenesis, which structures are absent?","options":["Both ovaries","Both Mullerian ducts","Both fallopian tubes only","The pituitary gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify both Mullerian ducts are absent in Mullerian agenesis.  Why A wrong: Ovaries (Wolffian/gonadal derivatives here are functional); the defect is in Mullerian duct structures.  Why C wrong: It is the entire Mullerian duct system, not only the tubes, that is absent.  Why D wrong: The pituitary is intact; the defect is at the level of the Mullerian ducts."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The best investigation in primary amenorrhea is:","options":["Hormonal assay","Karyotyping","Pelvic ultrasound","Endometrial biopsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best investigation in primary amenorrhea is karyotyping (to detect chromosomal causes such as Turner syndrome).  Why A wrong: Hormonal testing is the best test for secondary amenorrhea, not primary.  Why C wrong: Ultrasound helps assess structure but is not the single best investigation named.  Why D wrong: Endometrial biopsy is not the best investigation for primary amenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The best test to investigate primary (1°) amenorrhea is karyotyping, whereas the best test for secondary (2°) amenorrhea is:","options":["Karyotyping","Hormonal test","Laparoscopy","CT scan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 2° amenorrhea best test = hormonal test (while 1° amenorrhea best test = karyotyping).  Why A wrong: Karyotyping is the best test for primary amenorrhea, not secondary.  Why C wrong: Laparoscopy is not listed as the best test for secondary amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: CT scan is not the best test named for secondary amenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The prostaglandin mainly released during menstruation is:","options":["PGE1","PGF2 (PGF2α)","PGI2","PGD2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the prostaglandin released during menstruation is PGF2 (PGF2α), which causes uterine contractions.  Why A wrong: PGE1 is not the one named for menstruation.  Why C wrong: PGI2 (prostacyclin) is a vasodilator/anti-aggregatory, not the menstrual prostaglandin listed.  Why D wrong: PGD2 is not the prostaglandin identified in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Menstrual bleeding is mainly composed of:","options":["Venous blood","Arterial blood","Lymphatic fluid","Capillary transudate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state menstrual bleeding is mainly arterial blood.  Why A wrong: The notes specifically state arterial (not venous) blood.  Why C wrong: Menstrual flow is blood plus endometrial tissue, not lymphatic fluid.  Why D wrong: It is not merely capillary transudate; the source describes it as mainly arterial blood."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Which layer of the endometrium is shed at the time of menstruation?","options":["Zona basalis","Superficial (functional) layer","Myometrium","Perimetrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the superficial layer of endometrium is shed at the time of menstruation.  Why A wrong: The zona basalis is retained and regenerates the endometrium; it is not shed.  Why C wrong: The myometrium is the muscular layer, not shed during menstruation.  Why D wrong: The perimetrium is the outer serosal layer, not shed."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Which layer of the endometrium is responsible for regeneration of the endometrium in the next cycle?","options":["Superficial (functional) layer","Zona basalis (basal layer)","Compact layer","Spongy layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the zona basalis is responsible for regeneration of the endometrium in the next cycle.  Why A wrong: The superficial layer is shed during menstruation, not the source of regeneration.  Why C wrong: The compact layer is part of the functional layer that is shed.  Why D wrong: The spongy layer is part of the functional layer, not the regenerating basal layer."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"GnRH stands for:","options":["Growth Hormone Releasing Hormone","Gonadotropin Releasing Hormone","Glucocorticoid Releasing Hormone","Glycoprotein Releasing Hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define GnRH as Gonadotropin Releasing Hormone.  Why A wrong: That is GHRH, a different hypothalamic hormone.  Why C wrong: There is no hormone by that exact name; glucocorticoids are regulated via CRH/ACTH.  Why D wrong: GnRH does not stand for glycoprotein releasing hormone."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"GnRH is secreted from which specific nucleus of the hypothalamus, especially at puberty?","options":["Supraoptic nucleus","Arcuate nucleus","Paraventricular nucleus","Suprachiasmatic nucleus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes indicate GnRH originates from the hypothalamus (arcuate nucleus) at puberty.  Why A wrong: The supraoptic nucleus produces ADH/oxytocin, not GnRH.  Why B is correct as above.  Why C wrong: The paraventricular nucleus produces oxytocin/CRH, not GnRH.  Why D wrong: The suprachiasmatic nucleus regulates circadian rhythm, not GnRH secretion."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"GnRH is physiologically released from the hypothalamus in a:","options":["Continuous steady manner","Pulsatile manner","Single daily bolus","Random unregulated manner"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state GnRH is released in a pulsatile manner.  Why A wrong: Continuous release actually suppresses gonadotropins (opposite of natural action); natural release is pulsatile.  Why C wrong: It is not a single daily bolus but repeated pulses.  Why D wrong: Release is rhythmic/pulsatile, not random."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"GnRH acts on which gland to stimulate release of LH and FSH?","options":["Posterior pituitary","Anterior pituitary","Adrenal cortex","Thyroid gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show GnRH acts on the anterior pituitary to release LH and FSH.  Why A wrong: The posterior pituitary stores ADH and oxytocin, not LH/FSH.  Why C wrong: The adrenal cortex is not the target of GnRH.  Why D wrong: The thyroid is stimulated by TSH, not by GnRH."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"LH and FSH are together called:","options":["Corticotropins","Gonadotropins","Somatotropins","Thyrotropins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state LH and FSH together are called gonadotropins.  Why A wrong: Corticotropin (ACTH) acts on the adrenal cortex.  Why C wrong: Somatotropin is growth hormone.  Why D wrong: Thyrotropin (TSH) acts on the thyroid."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"An example of a synthetic GnRH (GnRH analogue) mentioned in the notes is:","options":["Leuprolide (Leukrolide)","Metformin","Clomiphene","Bromocriptine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give Leuprolide (written \"Leukrolide\") as an example of synthetic/made GnRH.  Why B wrong: Metformin is an insulin sensitizer used in PCOS, not a GnRH analogue.  Why C wrong: Clomiphene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator for ovulation induction, not a GnRH analogue.  Why D wrong: Bromocriptine is a dopamine agonist, not a GnRH analogue."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"When synthetic GnRH is given in a pulsatile manner, it:","options":["Acts opposite to natural GnRH","Acts like natural GnRH and increases LH, FSH and estrogen","Decreases LH and FSH","Has no effect on gonadotropins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state pulsatile synthetic GnRH acts like natural GnRH, raising LH, FSH and estrogen.  Why A wrong: Continuous (not pulsatile) administration acts opposite to natural GnRH.  Why C wrong: It increases, not decreases, gonadotropins when given pulsatile.  Why D wrong: It does have an effect—stimulating gonadotropins."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Pulsatile administration of synthetic GnRH is clinically used for: (Select all that apply)","options":["Delayed puberty","Ovulation induction","Fibroid treatment","Endometriosis treatment","Precocious puberty"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the uses of pulsatile GnRH as delayed puberty and ovulation induction (both need stimulation of gonadotropins/estrogen).  Why C wrong: Fibroid treatment is a use of continuous GnRH (suppression), not pulsatile.  Why D wrong: Endometriosis treatment is a use of continuous GnRH (suppression).  Why E wrong: Precocious puberty is treated with continuous GnRH (suppression), not pulsatile."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"When synthetic GnRH is given continuously, it:","options":["Acts like natural GnRH","Acts opposite to natural GnRH, decreasing LH, FSH and estrogen","Increases estrogen levels","Stimulates ovulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state continuous synthetic GnRH acts opposite to natural GnRH, lowering LH, FSH and estrogen (due to receptor downregulation).  Why A wrong: Only pulsatile administration mimics natural GnRH.  Why C wrong: Continuous administration decreases estrogen, not increases it.  Why D wrong: Continuous administration suppresses the axis and does not stimulate ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Continuous administration of synthetic GnRH is clinically used for: (Select all that apply)","options":["Fibroid","Endometriosis treatment","Precocious puberty","Delayed puberty","Ovulation induction"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list continuous GnRH uses as fibroid, endometriosis treatment, and precocious puberty (all require suppression of estrogen/gonadotropins).  Why D wrong: Delayed puberty is a use of pulsatile GnRH (stimulation).  Why E wrong: Ovulation induction is a use of pulsatile GnRH (stimulation)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"A patient with a hormone-dependent uterine fibroid is prescribed continuous GnRH analogue therapy. The therapeutic rationale is to:","options":["Increase estrogen to shrink the fibroid","Decrease estrogen (via LH/FSH suppression) to reduce fibroid growth","Induce ovulation","Stimulate the arcuate nucleus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Continuous GnRH suppresses LH, FSH and estrogen; since fibroids are estrogen-dependent, lowering estrogen reduces their growth—matching its listed use for fibroids.  Why A wrong: Increasing estrogen would worsen an estrogen-dependent fibroid.  Why C wrong: Continuous GnRH suppresses the axis and does not induce ovulation.  Why D wrong: The therapeutic effect is pituitary receptor downregulation, not arcuate nucleus stimulation."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"LH and FSH are released from which part of the pituitary gland?","options":["Posterior pituitary","Anterior pituitary","Intermediate lobe","Pituitary stalk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state LH & FSH (gonadotropins) are released from the anterior pituitary.  Why A wrong: The posterior pituitary releases ADH and oxytocin.  Why C wrong: The intermediate lobe is associated with MSH, not gonadotropins.  Why D wrong: The stalk connects hypothalamus and pituitary but does not itself release gonadotropins."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Estrogen exerts a negative feedback effect that decreases which gonadotropin?","options":["LH","FSH","TSH","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state estrogen → ↓FSH (estrogen has a negative effect on FSH).  Why A wrong: Progesterone (not estrogen, per the notes) is linked to decreasing LH.  Why C wrong: TSH is a thyroid hormone regulator, unrelated to estrogen feedback here.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is not the gonadotropin described in this feedback loop."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Progesterone exerts a negative feedback effect that decreases which gonadotropin?","options":["FSH","LH","TSH","ACTH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone → ↓LH (progesterone has a negative effect on LH).  Why A wrong: Estrogen is the hormone linked to decreasing FSH in the notes.  Why C wrong: TSH is unrelated to this feedback loop.  Why D wrong: ACTH is an adrenal-axis hormone, not affected here."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"In a normal female, the FSH:LH ratio is approximately:","options":["1:1","1:2","1:3","2:1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the normal female FSH:LH ratio is 1:1.  Why B wrong: 1:2 is a ratio seen in PCOS, not the normal female.  Why C wrong: 1:3 is another PCOS ratio, not normal.  Why D wrong: 2:1 is not the stated normal ratio."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"In PCOS (Polycystic Ovarian Syndrome), the FSH:LH ratio characteristically becomes:","options":["1:1","1:2 or 1:3 (with LH markedly increased)","3:1","2:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in PCOS the FSH:LH ratio is 1:2 or 1:3 with LH↑↑↑ (raised LH relative to FSH).  Why A wrong: 1:1 is the normal female ratio, not the PCOS pattern.  Why C wrong: 3:1 (FSH excess) is not the PCOS pattern.  Why D wrong: 2:1 does not reflect the LH predominance seen in PCOS."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"In PCOS, which gonadotropin is markedly elevated, altering the FSH:LH ratio?","options":["FSH","LH","TSH","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show LH↑↑↑ in PCOS, producing an FSH:LH ratio of 1:2 or 1:3.  Why A wrong: FSH is relatively low; it is LH that is markedly raised.  Why C wrong: TSH is not part of this ratio.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is not the gonadotropin altered in this ratio."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"In a menstrual cycle, the best time to check LH and FSH levels is:","options":["Day 14 of the cycle","Day 2 or Day 3 of the cycle","Day 21 of the cycle","Day 28 of the cycle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best time to check LH & FSH is Day 2 or Day 3 of the cycle (early follicular phase baseline).  Why A wrong: Day 14 is near ovulation with the LH surge—not the baseline testing time.  Why C wrong: Day 21 is used to check progesterone/ovulation, not baseline LH/FSH.  Why D wrong: Day 28 is late luteal phase, not the recommended testing window."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Estrogen has a negative feedback effect on FSH and progesterone has a negative feedback effect on LH. Therefore, if both estrogen and progesterone decrease in a female, LH and FSH will:","options":["Both decrease","Both increase","Remain unchanged","Only LH increases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if estrogen and progesterone decrease, the negative feedback is removed, so LH↑ and FSH↑.  Why A wrong: Loss of negative feedback raises gonadotropins, so they do not decrease.  Why C wrong: They change—both rise.  Why D wrong: Both LH and FSH rise, not LH alone."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Which conditions are examples of high LH and FSH (raised gonadotropins) due to low estrogen/progesterone? (Select all that apply)","options":["Menopause","Turner syndrome","Sheehan syndrome","Pregnancy","Kallmann syndrome"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list menopause and Turner syndrome (digenetic/non-functional ovaries → ↓estrogen, ↓progesterone → LH↑, FSH↑) as examples of raised FSH & LH.  Why C wrong: Sheehan syndrome causes low FSH & LH (anterior pituitary necrosis), not high.  Why D wrong: Pregnancy has high estrogen/progesterone with low FSH & LH.  Why E wrong: Kallmann (hypothalamic failure) causes low FSH & LH."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"In Turner syndrome, the ovaries are described as:","options":["Hyperfunctional and enlarged","Digenetic (dysgenetic) and non-functional","Polycystic","Absent from birth with functional testes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe Turner syndrome ovaries as digenetic (dysgenetic), i.e., non-functional, leading to ↓estrogen, ↓progesterone and thus LH↑, FSH↑.  Why A wrong: The ovaries are non-functional, not hyperfunctional.  Why C wrong: Polycystic ovaries characterize PCOS, not Turner syndrome.  Why D wrong: Turner females have dysgenetic ovaries (streak gonads), not functional testes."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Which conditions cause LOW LH and FSH (decreased gonadotropins)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Kallmann syndrome (hypothalamic failure)","Sheehan syndrome (anterior pituitary necrosis)","Pregnancy (raised estrogen & progesterone)","Menopause","Turner syndrome"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes place Kallmann (hypothalamic failure), Sheehan (anterior pituitary necrosis), and pregnancy (estrogen↑/progesterone↑) under FSH↓ & LH↓.  Why D wrong: Menopause causes raised FSH & LH.  Why E wrong: Turner syndrome causes raised FSH & LH."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Kallmann syndrome, a cause of low LH and FSH, results from failure at the level of the:","options":["Ovary","Hypothalamus","Anterior pituitary","Adrenal gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Kallmann syndrome = hypothalamic failure, producing low gonadotropins.  Why A wrong: The primary defect is central (hypothalamic), not ovarian.  Why C wrong: Anterior pituitary necrosis is Sheehan syndrome, not Kallmann.  Why D wrong: The adrenal gland is not involved in Kallmann syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Sheehan syndrome, a cause of low LH and FSH, is due to:","options":["Hypothalamic failure","Necrosis of the anterior pituitary gland","Ovarian dysgenesis","Polycystic ovaries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define Sheehan syndrome as necrosis of the anterior pituitary gland, leading to low FSH & LH.  Why A wrong: Hypothalamic failure describes Kallmann syndrome.  Why C wrong: Ovarian dysgenesis describes Turner syndrome (high gonadotropins).  Why D wrong: Polycystic ovaries describe PCOS."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"A physiological state characterized by high estrogen and progesterone with resulting low LH and FSH is:","options":["Menopause","Pregnancy","Turner syndrome","Kallmann syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if estrogen↑ and progesterone↑ → pregnancy, which is placed under FSH↓ & LH↓.  Why A wrong: Menopause has low estrogen/progesterone with high gonadotropins.  Why C wrong: Turner syndrome has low estrogen with high gonadotropins.  Why D wrong: Kallmann syndrome has low gonadotropins due to hypothalamic failure, not raised estrogen/progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The most important source of natural estrogen in the body is the:","options":["Adrenal gland","Ovary","Placenta","Adipose tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most important source of natural estrogen is the ovary (follicular mass).  Why A wrong: The adrenal gland is not the most important estrogen source in the notes.  Why C wrong: The placenta is a major source during pregnancy but is not listed as the most important overall source here.  Why D wrong: Adipose tissue converts androgens to estrogen but is not the primary/most important source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Within the ovary, natural estrogen is chiefly produced by the:","options":["Corpus albicans","Follicular mass","Ovarian stroma","Hilus cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes indicate the ovary produces estrogen via the follicular mass.  Why A wrong: The corpus albicans is a regressed corpus luteum and is not the estrogen source given.  Why C wrong: The ovarian stroma is not the source named.  Why D wrong: Hilus cells are not the source stated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"From puberty, the female ovary starts producing androgens (e.g., testosterone), which are converted to estrogen in adipose (fatty) tissue by the enzyme:","options":["5-alpha reductase","Aromatase","Sulfatase","Dehydrogenase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state androgens are converted to estrogen in adipose tissue by the enzyme aromatase.  Why A wrong: 5-alpha reductase converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, not to estrogen.  Why C wrong: Sulfatase is not the enzyme named for this conversion.  Why D wrong: A generic dehydrogenase is not the specified aromatization enzyme."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"An example of the androgen produced by the ovary that is subsequently converted into estrogen is:","options":["Cortisol","Testosterone","Progesterone","Aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give testosterone as the example androgen converted to estrogen.  Why A wrong: Cortisol is a glucocorticoid, not an androgen for estrogen conversion.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is a progestogen, not the androgen described.  Why D wrong: Aldosterone is a mineralocorticoid, not the androgen described."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Besides the ovary and adipose-tissue conversion, another source of natural estrogen listed is the:","options":["Corpus luteum","Thyroid gland","Pancreas","Spleen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the corpus luteum as a source of natural estrogen (source #3).  Why B wrong: The thyroid gland is not an estrogen source in the notes.  Why C wrong: The pancreas is not a listed estrogen source.  Why D wrong: The spleen is not a listed estrogen source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"How many types of natural estrogen are described in the notes?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list 4 types of natural estrogen: E1, E2, E3, E4.  Why A wrong: Two is fewer than the four listed.  Why B wrong: Three omits estetrol (E4).  Why D wrong: Five exceeds the four types described."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The natural estrogen E1 is named:","options":["Estradiol","Estrone","Estriol","Estetrol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes identify E1 as Estrone, most common in menopausal females.  Why A wrong: Estradiol is E2.  Why C wrong: Estriol is E3.  Why D wrong: Estetrol is E4."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Which natural estrogen (E1/Estrone) is most common in menopausal females?","options":["Estradiol (E2)","Estrone (E1)","Estriol (E3)","Estetrol (E4)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Estrone (E1) is more common in menopausal females.  Why A wrong: Estradiol (E2) predominates in reproductive age, not menopause.  Why C wrong: Estriol (E3) is the most specific estrogen in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Estetrol (E4) is not listed as the menopausal estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The natural estrogen E2 (the most common one) is named:","options":["Estrone","Estradiol","Estriol","Estetrol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes identify E2 (marked m/c = most common) as Estradiol.  Why A wrong: Estrone is E1.  Why C wrong: Estriol is E3.  Why D wrong: Estetrol is E4."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Estradiol (E2) is the most common estrogen in which age group and state? (Select all that apply)","options":["Reproductive age","Pregnancy (most common in pregnancy)","Menopause","Childhood before puberty","Postmenopausal decades only"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Estradiol (E2) is common in reproductive age and is the most common estrogen in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Estrone (E1) predominates in menopause.  Why D wrong: The notes do not describe childhood predominance for E2.  Why E wrong: Postmenopausal predominance is estrone (E1), not estradiol."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The natural estrogen E3 is named:","options":["Estrone","Estradiol","Estriol","Estetrol"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes identify E3 as Estriol, the most specific estrogen in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Estrone is E1.  Why B wrong: Estradiol is E2.  Why D wrong: Estetrol is E4."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Which natural estrogen is the \"most specific\" estrogen in pregnancy?","options":["Estradiol (E2)","Estriol (E3)","Estrone (E1)","Estetrol (E4)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Estriol (E3) is the most specific estrogen in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Estradiol (E2) is the most common in pregnancy, but the \"most specific\" is estriol.  Why C wrong: Estrone (E1) is the menopausal estrogen.  Why D wrong: Estetrol (E4) is not designated as the most specific in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The natural estrogen E4 is named:","options":["Estradiol","Estriol","Estetrol","Estrone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes identify E4 as Estetrol.  Why A wrong: Estradiol is E2.  Why B wrong: Estriol is E3.  Why D wrong: Estrone is E1."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The correct order of estrogen potency from most potent to least potent is:","options":["E1 > E2 > E3 > E4","E2 > E1 > E3 > E4","E3 > E2 > E1 > E4","E4 > E3 > E2 > E1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Most Potent = E2 > E1 > E3 > E4 = Least Potent.  Why A wrong: E2 (estradiol), not E1, is the most potent.  Why C wrong: E3 is not the most potent; estradiol (E2) is.  Why D wrong: This reverses the order; E4 is the least potent."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Which natural estrogen is the MOST potent?","options":["Estrone (E1)","Estradiol (E2)","Estriol (E3)","Estetrol (E4)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the potency order E2 > E1 > E3 > E4, estradiol (E2) is the most potent.  Why A wrong: Estrone (E1) is second, not most potent.  Why C wrong: Estriol (E3) is third in potency.  Why D wrong: Estetrol (E4) is the least potent."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Which natural estrogen is the LEAST potent?","options":["Estradiol (E2)","Estrone (E1)","Estriol (E3)","Estetrol (E4)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the order E2 > E1 > E3 > E4, estetrol (E4) is the least potent.  Why A wrong: Estradiol (E2) is the most potent.  Why B wrong: Estrone (E1) is second most potent.  Why C wrong: Estriol (E3) is third, not the least."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The most common synthetic estrogen used in oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) is:","options":["Estradiol valerate","Ethinylestradiol","Mestranol","Conjugated equine estrogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common synthetic estrogen in OCPs is ethinylestradiol.  Why A wrong: Estradiol valerate is not the one named as most common in OCPs.  Why C wrong: Mestranol is an older synthetic estrogen but not the one specified.  Why D wrong: Conjugated equine estrogen is used in HRT, not the OCP synthetic estrogen named."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"OCPs are classified as \"high dose pills\" when the estrogen content is:","options":["Less than or equal to 20 µg","30–35 µg","Greater than or equal to 50 µg","Exactly 10 µg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define high dose pills as estrogen ≥ 50 µg.  Why A wrong: ≤ 20 µg corresponds to very low dose pills.  Why B wrong: 30–35 µg corresponds to low dose pills.  Why D wrong: 10 µg is below the high-dose threshold."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"OCPs are classified as \"low dose pills\" when the estrogen content is:","options":["≥ 50 µg","< 50 µg (typically 30–35 µg)","≤ 20 µg","≥ 100 µg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define low dose pills as estrogen < 50 µg (30–35 µg).  Why A wrong: ≥ 50 µg is a high dose pill.  Why C wrong: ≤ 20 µg is a very low dose pill.  Why D wrong: ≥ 100 µg is not among the classifications."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"OCPs are classified as \"very low dose pills\" when the estrogen content is:","options":["≥ 50 µg","30–35 µg","Less than or equal to 20 µg","40 µg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define very low dose pills as estrogen ≤ 20 µg.  Why A wrong: ≥ 50 µg is a high dose pill.  Why B wrong: 30–35 µg is a low dose pill.  Why D wrong: 40 µg is not the very low dose threshold."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The combined oral contraceptive brand \"Femilon\" contains ethinylestradiol at a dose of:","options":["0.15 mg","0.02 mg","0.05 mg","0.5 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Femilon package shown states Ethinylestradiol I.P. 0.02 mg per tablet.  Why A wrong: 0.15 mg is the desogestrel content in Femilon, not ethinylestradiol.  Why C wrong: 0.05 mg is not the labeled dose.  Why D wrong: 0.5 mg is far higher than the labeled 0.02 mg."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"In the OCP \"Femilon,\" the progestogen component is desogestrel at a dose of:","options":["0.02 mg","0.15 mg","1.5 mg","0.05 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Femilon label states Desogestrel B.P. 0.15 mg per tablet.  Why A wrong: 0.02 mg is the ethinylestradiol content, not desogestrel.  Why C wrong: 1.5 mg is ten times the labeled desogestrel dose.  Why D wrong: 0.05 mg is not the labeled desogestrel dose."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"In a non-pregnant female, the natural source of progesterone is the:","options":["Placenta","Corpus luteum","Anterior pituitary","Adrenal medulla"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the source of progesterone in a non-pregnant female is the corpus luteum.  Why A wrong: The placenta is a source only during pregnancy (after 12 weeks).  Why C wrong: The anterior pituitary secretes gonadotropins, not progesterone.  Why D wrong: The adrenal medulla secretes catecholamines, not progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"During pregnancy, progesterone is produced by the corpus luteum of pregnancy up to approximately:","options":["4–6 weeks","10–12 weeks","20 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the corpus luteum of pregnancy produces progesterone during the first 10–12 weeks.  Why A wrong: The corpus luteum sustains progesterone longer than 4–6 weeks (up to 10–12 weeks).  Why C wrong: By 20 weeks the placenta has taken over progesterone production.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is well past the corpus luteal phase of progesterone production."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"After 12 weeks of pregnancy, the main source of progesterone becomes the:","options":["Corpus luteum","Placenta","Ovarian follicle","Adrenal cortex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that after 12 weeks the placenta produces progesterone (luteoplacental shift).  Why A wrong: The corpus luteum is the source only up to about 10–12 weeks.  Why C wrong: The ovarian follicle is not the source after 12 weeks.  Why D wrong: The adrenal cortex is not the main progesterone source in late pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"According to the notes, progesterone is described as the main hormone for:","options":["Ovulation only","Menstruation","Lactation","Puberty onset"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone is the main hormone for menstruation.  Why A wrong: Ovulation is triggered by the LH surge; the notes credit progesterone with menstruation.  Why C wrong: Lactation is driven by prolactin/oxytocin, not progesterone.  Why D wrong: Puberty onset is driven by GnRH pulsatility, not progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Progesterone (as a hormone) can be used clinically to:","options":["Induce ovulation","Delay periods / control periods","Terminate pregnancy","Stimulate FSH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone is a hormone used to delay periods / to control (regulate) periods.  Why A wrong: Ovulation induction uses agents like clomiphene/pulsatile GnRH, not progesterone for delaying periods.  Why C wrong: Progesterone supports pregnancy; it does not terminate it.  Why D wrong: Progesterone suppresses LH; it does not stimulate FSH."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The main hormone that sustains and supports pregnancy is:","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","LH","FSH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone is the main hormone during pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Estrogen rises in pregnancy but progesterone is named the main hormone.  Why C wrong: LH is suppressed during pregnancy.  Why D wrong: FSH is suppressed during pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"An example of a synthetic progesterone (progestin) mentioned in the notes is:","options":["Ethinylestradiol","Levonorgestrel","Leuprolide","Clomiphene"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give Levonorgestrel as an example of synthetic progesterone.  Why A wrong: Ethinylestradiol is a synthetic estrogen, not progesterone.  Why C wrong: Leuprolide is a synthetic GnRH analogue.  Why D wrong: Clomiphene is a SERM used for ovulation induction, not a progestin."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"A contraceptive preparation combining estrogen and progesterone is termed:","options":["Minipill","OCP (combined oral contraceptive pill)","Mirena","Progesterone injection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state estrogen + progesterone → OCP's.  Why A wrong: The minipill is a progestogen-only pill (no estrogen).  Why C wrong: Mirena is a progesterone-only intrauterine system.  Why D wrong: A progesterone injection contains only progestin, not estrogen + progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"A progestogen-only pill (containing no estrogen) is known as the:","options":["OCP","Minipill","Mirena","Depot injection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progestogen-only pill → MINIPILL.  Why A wrong: OCPs combine estrogen and progesterone.  Why C wrong: Mirena is an intrauterine device, not a pill.  Why D wrong: A depot injection is an injectable, not the progestogen-only pill."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The progesterone-releasing intrauterine device mentioned in the notes is:","options":["Copper-T","Mirena","Antara","Mala-D"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone (IUD form) → MIRENA.  Why A wrong: Copper-T is a non-hormonal (copper) IUD, not the progesterone device named.  Why C wrong: Antara is the medroxyprogesterone injection program, not an IUD.  Why D wrong: Mala-D is a combined oral contraceptive pill, not an IUD."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The Mirena intrauterine system releases levonorgestrel at a rate of:","options":["20 micrograms/24 hours","150 mg/ml","0.02 mg/day estrogen","35 micrograms/24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Mirena label shown states levonorgestrel 20 micrograms/24 hours intrauterine delivery system.  Why B wrong: 150 mg/ml is the medroxyprogesterone injection concentration, not Mirena's rate.  Why C wrong: Mirena releases levonorgestrel (progestin), not estrogen.  Why D wrong: 35 micrograms/24 hours is not the labeled Mirena release rate."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The contraceptive progesterone injection (e.g., Antara) contains medroxyprogesterone at a concentration of:","options":["50 mg/ml","150 mg/ml","20 mg/ml","0.15 mg/ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Medroxyprogesterone Injection I.P. label shown states 150 mg/ml.  Why A wrong: 50 mg/ml is not the labeled concentration.  Why C wrong: 20 mg/ml is not the labeled concentration.  Why D wrong: 0.15 mg/ml is far below the labeled 150 mg/ml."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The medroxyprogesterone contraceptive injection (Antara) is administered by which route?","options":["Subcutaneous","Deep intramuscular","Intravenous","Intradermal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The injection label states \"For deep intramuscular use only.\"  Why A wrong: The label specifies deep IM, not subcutaneous.  Why C wrong: It is not given intravenously.  Why D wrong: It is not an intradermal injection."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The action of estrogen on the uterine endometrium is to cause it to:","options":["Undergo secretory change","Proliferate","Shed","Atrophy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state estrogen acts on the uterine endometrium to cause proliferation.  Why A wrong: The secretory (support) change on the endometrium is the action of progesterone.  Why C wrong: Shedding occurs with hormone withdrawal, not as estrogen's direct action.  Why D wrong: Atrophy is not estrogen's endometrial action; estrogen promotes growth."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The action of progesterone on the uterine endometrium, also called the secretory action, is to:","options":["Cause proliferation","Support (maintain) the endometrium","Shed the functional layer","Thin the endometrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone supports the uterine endometrium (called the secretory action).  Why A wrong: Proliferation is the action of estrogen.  Why C wrong: Shedding is due to hormone withdrawal, not progesterone's support action.  Why D wrong: Progesterone maintains/supports rather than thins the endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Under estrogen influence, cervical mucus becomes:","options":["Thick, viscous, and scanty","Thin, watery, abundant, and elastic","Dry and absent","Purulent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state estrogen makes cervical mucus thin, watery, abundant, and elastic.  Why A wrong: Thick, viscous, scanty mucus is the effect of progesterone.  Why C wrong: Estrogen increases (does not eliminate) cervical mucus.  Why D wrong: Purulent mucus indicates infection, not the estrogenic effect described."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"The property of estrogen-influenced cervical mucus that allows it to be stretched between the fingers is called:","options":["Ferning","Spinnbarkeit","Cerclage","Colposcopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the elastic stretchability of cervical mucus between fingers is called Spinnbarkeit.  Why A wrong: Ferning refers to the microscopic fern-like crystallization pattern, not the stretch test.  Why C wrong: Cerclage is a cervical suture procedure, not a mucus property.  Why D wrong: Colposcopy is a diagnostic visualization procedure, unrelated to mucus elasticity."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Under the microscope, estrogen-influenced cervical mucus that dries gives which characteristic appearance?","options":["Cobblestone appearance","Fern-like appearance","Ground-glass appearance","Honeycomb appearance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state estrogenic cervical mucus, when seen under the microscope, gives a FERN-like appearance.  Why A wrong: A cobblestone appearance is not the described pattern.  Why C wrong: Ground-glass is a radiological term, not the mucus microscopy pattern here.  Why D wrong: Honeycomb is not the described crystallization pattern."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Under progesterone influence, cervical mucus becomes: (Select all that apply)","options":["Thick","Viscous","Scanty","Not elastic (cannot be stretched between fingers)","Abundant and watery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone makes cervical mucus thick, viscous, scanty, and not elastic (cannot be stretched between fingers).  Why E wrong: Abundant and watery mucus is the estrogenic effect, opposite to progesterone's effect."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Under progesterone influence, when cervical mucus is seen under the microscope, the finding is:","options":["Prominent fern-like appearance","No fern-like appearance","Spinnbarkeit stretch of 15 cm","Ground-glass crystals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that under progesterone, no fern-like appearance is seen under the microscope.  Why A wrong: A prominent fern pattern is the estrogenic effect, not progesterone.  Why C wrong: Spinnbarkeit (stretchability) is an estrogenic property; progesterone mucus is not elastic.  Why D wrong: Ground-glass crystals are not the described microscopic finding."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Complications of imperforate hymen & cruciate incision treatment…","question":"Ferning (fern-like crystallization) of cervical mucus is a sign attributable to which hormone?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","LH","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes conclude \"Ferning is about [estrogen],\" i.e., ferning indicates estrogen action on cervical mucus.  Why A wrong: Progesterone abolishes ferning (no fern pattern).  Why C wrong: LH triggers ovulation but is not the cause of the ferning pattern.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is not associated with cervical mucus ferning."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"On endometrial biopsy, the endometrial glands under the influence of estrogen appear:","options":["Straight, tubular and simple","Curved, complex and filled with secretion","Corkscrew-shaped","Atrophic and cystic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under estrogen (proliferative phase), the endometrial glands are straight, tubular and simple.  Why B wrong: Curved complex secretion-filled glands describe the progesterone (secretory) effect.  Why C wrong: Corkscrew glands are the progesterone effect, not estrogen.  Why D wrong: Atrophic/cystic changes are not the described estrogen action."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"On endometrial biopsy under progesterone influence, the glands become curved and complex and are specifically called:","options":["Tubular glands","Corkscrew glands","Straight glands","Simple glands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Under progesterone the glands are curved complex, called CORKSCREW glands, and are filled with secretion.  Why A/C/D wrong: Tubular, straight and simple describe the estrogen (proliferative) pattern, not the progesterone (secretory) pattern."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Under progesterone influence, the corkscrew endometrial glands are characteristically:","options":["Empty and collapsed","Filled with secretion","Lined by squamous cells","Necrotic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The progesterone-influenced corkscrew glands are filled with secretion (secretory endometrium).  Why A wrong: They are secretion-filled, not empty/collapsed.  Why C/D wrong: The notes describe secretory filling, not squamous lining or necrosis."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"What is estrogen's effect on blood lipids?","options":["Increases high-density lipid and decreases low-density lipid","Decreases high-density lipid and increases low-density lipid","Increases both HDL and LDL","No effect on lipids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen increases high-density lipid (good fat) and decreases low-density lipid (bad fat).  Why B wrong: This is the opposite of estrogen's action.  Why C/D wrong: Estrogen selectively raises HDL and lowers LDL; it does not raise both or have no effect."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Because estrogen raises HDL and lowers LDL, estrogen is described as being:","options":["Cardiotoxic","Cardioprotective","Neutral to the heart","Pro-atherogenic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state estrogen is cardioprotective due to its favourable lipid profile.  Why A/D wrong: Estrogen protects, rather than harms, the cardiovascular system in the notes.  Why C wrong: It has a definite protective (not neutral) effect."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Which statement follows from estrogen being cardioprotective?","options":["Young females do not have heart disease","Young males have more protection than females","Estrogen causes early heart disease in young females","Estrogen has no relation to heart disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state young females do not have heart disease because estrogen is cardioprotective.  Why B wrong: The protection described is for young females, not males.  Why C wrong: Estrogen protects, it does not cause early heart disease.  Why D wrong: Estrogen is explicitly linked (protectively) to heart disease risk."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Regarding progesterone's effect on lipids, the notes state that which other hormone group has the same action?","options":["Glucocorticoids","Androgens","Mineralocorticoids","Thyroid hormones"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state androgens also have the same lipid action as progesterone.  Why A/C/D wrong: Glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids and thyroid hormones are not cited as sharing progesterone's lipid action."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Estrogen has which effect on blood clotting?","options":["Decreases clotting factors","Increases clotting factors","No effect on clotting factors","Destroys clotting factors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen increases clotting factors.  Why A/C/D wrong: It raises, rather than lowers, removes or destroys, clotting factors — which is why it carries thrombosis risk."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Because estrogen increases clotting factors, which therapy should NEVER be given to a female with thrombosis?","options":["Hormone replacement therapy or oral contraceptive pills","Calcium supplements","Iron tablets","Folic acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HRT or oral contraceptive pills (estrogen-containing) should never be given to a female with thrombosis because estrogen increases clotting factors.  Why B/C/D wrong: Calcium, iron and folic acid do not raise clotting factors and are not contraindicated for this reason."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Estrogen has which effect on bone?","options":["Decreases bone mass","Increases bone mass","No effect on bone","Causes bone fractures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen increases bone mass (protective against osteoporosis).  Why A/D wrong: Estrogen builds bone; loss of estrogen (menopause) reduces bone mass, but estrogen itself increases it.  Why C wrong: Estrogen has a definite bone-preserving effect."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Progesterone acts on smooth muscle as a:","options":["Smooth muscle stimulant increasing uterine contraction","Smooth muscle relaxant decreasing uterine contraction","Skeletal muscle relaxant","Cardiac stimulant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone is a smooth muscle relaxant, so uterine contraction decreases.  Why A wrong: It decreases, not increases, uterine contraction.  Why C/D wrong: The described effect is on uterine smooth muscle, not skeletal or cardiac muscle."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Because progesterone relaxes uterine smooth muscle, it is clinically used to:","options":["Induce labour","Prevent abortion and preterm labor","Increase menstrual flow","Ripen the cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: By relaxing the uterus, progesterone is used to prevent abortion and preterm labor.  Why A wrong: It reduces contractions; it does not induce labour.  Why C/D wrong: The notes describe abortion/preterm-labour prevention, not increased flow or cervical ripening."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Progesterone raises a female's body temperature by approximately:","options":["0.2 to 0.5°C","1.0 to 1.5°C","2.0 to 2.5°C","It lowers body temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone increases the body temperature of a female by 0.2 to 0.5°C (thermogenic effect, basis of BBT ovulation detection).  Why B/C wrong: The rise is only 0.2–0.5°C, not 1–2.5°C.  Why D wrong: Progesterone raises, not lowers, body temperature."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"SERM drugs (selective estrogen receptor modulators) are characterized by:","options":["Acting only as estrogen agonists on all tissues","Acting only as estrogen antagonists on all tissues","Acting as estrogen agonist on some tissues and estrogen antagonist on other tissues","Having no effect on estrogen receptors"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A SERM acts as an estrogen agonist on some tissues and as an estrogen antagonist on other tissues.  Why A/B wrong: SERMs are tissue-selective, not purely agonist or antagonist everywhere.  Why D wrong: They bind estrogen receptors and modulate them."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Which of the following is a SERM (selective estrogen receptor modulator)?","options":["Clomiphene citrate","Danazol","Letrozole","GnRH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clomiphene citrate is listed under SERMs.  Why B/C/D wrong: Danazol, letrozole and GnRH belong to the drugs that decrease estrogen in the body, not the SERM group."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Clomiphene citrate (a SERM) is clinically used for:","options":["Induction of ovulation","Treatment of osteoporosis","Medical abortion","Breast cancer treatment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list clomiphene citrate's use as ovulation induction.  Why B wrong: Osteoporosis is the use of raloxifene.  Why C wrong: Medical abortion is the use of mifepristone.  Why D wrong: Breast cancer treatment is the use of tamoxifen."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Tamoxifen, a SERM, is used clinically for:","options":["Ovulation induction","Breast cancer treatment","Osteoporosis","Preterm labor prevention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tamoxifen's use is breast cancer treatment.  Why A wrong: Ovulation induction is clomiphene's use.  Why C wrong: Osteoporosis is raloxifene's use.  Why D wrong: Preterm labor prevention is progesterone's use."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"A recognized problem/adverse effect associated with tamoxifen is:","options":["Uterine (endometrial) cancer","Hot flushes","Osteoporosis","Ovarian cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state tamoxifen's problem is uterine cancer (endometrial cancer), reflecting its agonist action on the endometrium.  Why B wrong: Hot flushes are listed as the problem of raloxifene.  Why C wrong: Raloxifene treats osteoporosis; tamoxifen is not linked to it here.  Why D wrong: The cited malignancy is endometrial/uterine, not ovarian."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Raloxifene, a SERM, is used for the treatment of:","options":["Osteoporosis","Breast cancer","Ovulation induction","Medical abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Raloxifene is used for treatment of osteoporosis (estrogen agonist on bone).  Why B wrong: Breast cancer treatment is tamoxifen's use.  Why C wrong: Ovulation induction is clomiphene's use.  Why D wrong: Medical abortion is mifepristone's use."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"A recognized problem/adverse effect associated with raloxifene is:","options":["Hot flushes","Endometrial cancer","Ovulation","Bone loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list raloxifene's problem as hot flushes.  Why B wrong: Endometrial (uterine) cancer is the problem listed for tamoxifen.  Why C/D wrong: Raloxifene is used to treat osteoporosis, so it does not cause bone loss, and ovulation is not its adverse effect."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Which mnemonic phrase in the notes (\"Dominos Pizza Got Late\") represents drugs that decrease estrogen in the body?","options":["Danazol, Progesterone, GnRH (continuous dose), Letrozole","Clomiphene, Tamoxifen, Raloxifene, Mifepristone","Estrogen, Progesterone, Testosterone, LH","Danazol, Clomiphene, Letrozole, Tamoxifen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic maps to Danazol (Dominos), Progesterone (Pizza), GnRH continuous dose (Got), Letrozole (Late) — drugs that decrease estrogen.  Why B wrong: Those are SERMs / progesterone antagonist, not the estrogen-lowering group.  Why C wrong: These are hormones, not the listed estrogen-lowering drugs.  Why D wrong: Clomiphene and tamoxifen are SERMs, not part of this mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a drug that decreases estrogen in the body?","options":["Danazol","Clomiphene citrate","Raloxifene","Tamoxifen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Danazol is listed under drugs that decrease estrogen in the body.  Why B/C/D wrong: Clomiphene, raloxifene and tamoxifen are SERMs, not estrogen-lowering drugs in this list."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"According to the notes, GnRH decreases estrogen in the body when given in which manner?","options":["As a single pulsatile dose","As a continuous dose","As an intermittent weekly dose","Only intravenously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify GnRH (continuous dose) decreases estrogen — continuous GnRH downregulates pituitary receptors.  Why A wrong: Pulsatile GnRH stimulates, rather than suppresses, gonadotropins.  Why C/D wrong: The notes specify continuous dosing, not weekly or route-specific dosing."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Letrozole is classified in the notes as a drug that:","options":["Increases estrogen in the body","Decreases estrogen in the body","Is a progesterone antagonist","Is a SERM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Letrozole is listed among drugs which decrease estrogen in the body (aromatase inhibitor).  Why A wrong: It lowers, not raises, estrogen.  Why C wrong: The progesterone antagonist is mifepristone.  Why D wrong: It is not a SERM; it acts by decreasing estrogen production."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Which drug is the progesterone antagonist (a drug that decreases progesterone)?","options":["Mifepristone (RU486)","Clomiphene","Letrozole","Danazol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mifepristone (RU486) is the drug that decreases progesterone (progesterone antagonist).  Why B/C/D wrong: Clomiphene is a SERM; letrozole and danazol are estrogen-lowering drugs, not progesterone antagonists."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Mifepristone (RU486) is clinically used for:","options":["Medical abortion","Ovulation induction","Treatment of osteoporosis","Preventing preterm labor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mifepristone (RU486), a progesterone antagonist, is used for medical abortion.  Why B wrong: Ovulation induction is clomiphene's use.  Why C wrong: Osteoporosis is raloxifene's use.  Why D wrong: Preventing preterm labor is progesterone's action, the opposite of mifepristone."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"In fertilization, the fusion of a sperm with which cell forms the zygote?","options":["Ovum (secondary oocyte)","Primary oocyte","Oogonium","First polar body"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The zygote is formed by union of sperm + ova (secondary/2° oocyte).  Why B wrong: The primary oocyte has not yet completed meiosis I at the time of fertilization.  Why C wrong: The oogonium is the earliest diploid germ cell, not the fertilized cell.  Why D wrong: The first polar body is a discarded product of meiosis I, not fertilized."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"In the diagram of sperm parts, the acrosomal cap contains which enzyme?","options":["Hyaluronidase","Lactase","Amylase","Pepsin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The acrosomal cap contains hyaluronidase, which helps the sperm penetrate the oocyte's coverings.  Why B/C/D wrong: Lactase, amylase and pepsin are digestive enzymes not associated with the acrosomal cap."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Which part of the sperm contains mitochondria that form ATP and is called the \"power house of the sperm\"?","options":["Head","Neck","Middle piece","Tail"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The middle piece has mitochondria that form ATP and is called the power house of the sperm.  Why A wrong: The head carries the nucleus and acrosomal cap.  Why B wrong: The neck connects head and middle piece but is not the energy source.  Why D wrong: The tail (axial filament) provides motility, not ATP generation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The tail of the sperm is also referred to as the:","options":["Acrosomal cap","Axial filament","Neck","Middle piece"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tail is labelled as the tail/axial filament.  Why A/C/D wrong: The acrosomal cap, neck and middle piece are separate, named sperm parts."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Arrange the sperm parts in correct order from anterior to posterior as labelled in the sperm cell anatomy figure.","options":["Head → Midpiece → Tail","Tail → Midpiece → Head","Midpiece → Head → Tail","Head → Tail → Midpiece"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sperm anatomy figure labels Head (nucleus), then Midpiece (mitochondria), then Tail.  Why B/C/D wrong: These orders do not match the anterior-to-posterior arrangement shown."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The nucleus of the sperm is located in which part?","options":["Head","Midpiece","Tail","Acrosomal cap"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the nucleus within the head of the sperm.  Why B wrong: The midpiece contains mitochondria.  Why C wrong: The tail provides motility.  Why D wrong: The acrosomal cap covers the head but the nucleus itself lies in the head."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The sperm (male gamete) carries which chromosome number?","options":["Diploid, 46 chromosomes","Haploid, 23 chromosomes","Haploid, 22 chromosomes","Diploid, 23 chromosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sperm has a haploid number of chromosomes = diploid/2 = 46/2 = 23.  Why A/D wrong: Diploid (46) is the somatic cell count; the gamete is haploid.  Why C wrong: The haploid number is 23 (22 autosomes + 1 sex chromosome), not 22."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The two chromosomal combinations found in human sperm are:","options":["22 + X and 22 + Y","44 + XX and 44 + XY","23 + X and 23 + Y","22 + XX and 22 + YY"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A sperm carries 22 autosomes plus one sex chromosome — either 22 + X or 22 + Y.  Why B wrong: 44 + XX/XY is a diploid count, not a gamete.  Why C wrong: With one sex chromosome the autosome count is 22, so total 23 (not 23 autosomes + sex chromosome).  Why D wrong: A normal sperm carries a single sex chromosome, not XX or YY."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The size of a sperm is stated as approximately:","options":["50 microns","5 microns","500 microns","100 microns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the sperm size as 50 microns.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated size of 50 microns."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The fertilizable span (lifespan capable of fertilizing) of the sperm is:","options":["48 hours – 72 hours","12 hours – 24 hours","6 hours – 12 hours","7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the sperm's fertilizable span as 48 hours to 72 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 48–72 hour span."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Sperm is formed by which process?","options":["Oogenesis","Spermatogenesis","Capacitation","Ovulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sperm is formed by spermatogenesis.  Why A wrong: Oogenesis forms the ovum.  Why C wrong: Capacitation is the maturation that enables fertilization, not sperm formation.  Why D wrong: Ovulation is release of the oocyte from the ovary."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Spermatogenesis begins at which stage of life?","options":["Puberty","Intrauterine life","Birth","Old age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogenesis begins at puberty.  Why B wrong: Intrauterine onset is a feature of oogenesis, not spermatogenesis.  Why C/D wrong: The notes specify puberty as the onset."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The time taken for spermatogenesis is:","options":["70–75 days","28 days","90–120 days","14 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state spermatogenesis takes 70–75 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 70–75 day duration."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The process by which sperm is released is called:","options":["Ejaculation","Capacitation","Ovulation","Implantation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The process by which sperm is released is ejaculation.  Why B wrong: Capacitation is acquisition of fertilizing ability.  Why C wrong: Ovulation is release of the oocyte.  Why D wrong: Implantation is embedding of the blastocyst in the endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The process by which sperm attain the capacity to fertilize the ovum is called:","options":["Capacitation","Ejaculation","Spermatogenesis","Fertilization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Capacitation is the process by which sperm attain the capacity to fertilize the ova.  Why B wrong: Ejaculation is release of sperm.  Why C wrong: Spermatogenesis is sperm formation.  Why D wrong: Fertilization is the actual fusion of gametes, which capacitation enables."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Capacitation of sperm occurs in the:","options":["Female reproductive tract","Male reproductive tract","Testis","Epididymis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Capacitation occurs in the female reproductive tract.  Why B/C/D wrong: The male tract, testis and epididymis are sites of sperm production/storage, not capacitation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Capacitation begins in which part of the female reproductive tract?","options":["Cervix","Fallopian tube","Ovary","Vagina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Capacitation begins in the cervix.  Why B wrong: The majority of capacitation occurs in the fallopian tube, but it begins in the cervix.  Why C/D wrong: The ovary and vagina are not the sites where capacitation begins."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The majority of capacitation occurs in the:","options":["Fallopian tube","Cervix","Uterine cavity","Vagina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The majority of capacitation occurs in the fallopian tube.  Why B wrong: The cervix is where capacitation begins, not where the majority occurs.  Why C/D wrong: The uterine cavity and vagina are not the primary site of capacitation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The time taken for capacitation is:","options":["2 to 6 hours","24 to 48 hours","30 minutes","12 to 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the time taken by capacitation as 2 to 6 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 2–6 hour duration."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Although millions of sperm are ejaculated, the figure indicates that approximately how many sperm reach the site near the fallopian tube/ovum?","options":["300–500","3000–5000","30–50","3–5 million"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The capacitation figure shows only about 300–500 sperm reach the upper tract near the ovum, though millions are ejaculated.  Why B/C wrong: These do not match the labelled 300–500.  Why D wrong: Millions are ejaculated, but only 300–500 reach the ovum's vicinity."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Oogenesis begins during which period of life?","options":["Intrauterine (fetal) life","Puberty","Menarche","Menopause"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oogenesis begins in intrauterine life, unlike spermatogenesis which begins at puberty.  Why B/C wrong: Puberty/menarche is when the arrested process resumes, not when it begins.  Why D wrong: Menopause marks the end of reproductive life, not the onset of oogenesis."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The earliest germ cell in oogenesis, the oogonium, has which chromosome constitution?","options":["46 XX (44 + XX)","23 X","44 + XY","22 + X"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The oogonium is diploid, 46 XX (44 autosomes + XX).  Why B/D wrong: 23 X or 22 + X are haploid gamete counts, not the diploid oogonium.  Why C wrong: XY is a male constitution, not the female oogonium."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The oogonium divides to form the primary oocyte by which type of cell division?","options":["Mitosis","Meiosis I","Meiosis II","Binary fission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oogonia form primary oocytes by mitosis, as labelled in the diagram.  Why B/C wrong: Meiosis begins in the primary oocyte (arrested in prophase); it is not the oogonium-to-primary-oocyte step.  Why D wrong: Binary fission is a prokaryotic mode of division, not applicable here."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"According to the notes, the germ cell is best derived from which of the following?","options":["Epiblast","Ectoderm","Endoderm","Mesoderm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state germ cell is derived from EPIBLAST (best answer), with ectoderm as the second-best answer.  Why B wrong: Ectoderm is labelled the 2nd best answer, not the best.  Why C/D wrong: Endoderm and mesoderm are not given as the source in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"According to the notes, the second-best answer for the origin of the germ cell is:","options":["Ectoderm","Epiblast","Endoderm","Trophoblast"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ectoderm is stated as the 2nd best answer for germ-cell derivation (epiblast being the best).  Why B wrong: Epiblast is the best (first) answer.  Why C/D wrong: Endoderm and trophoblast are not cited as germ-cell sources."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The primary oocyte has which chromosome constitution?","options":["44 + XX","22 + X","23 X","46 XY"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The primary oocyte is diploid, 44 + XX.  Why B/C wrong: These are haploid counts describing the secondary oocyte/polar body, not the primary oocyte.  Why D wrong: XY is a male constitution."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Until puberty, the primary oocyte remains in the ovary in which state?","options":["Arrested state","Actively dividing state","Degenerating state","Fully mature state"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Till puberty the primary oocyte remains in an arrested state in the ovary.  Why B wrong: It is arrested, not actively dividing.  Why C/D wrong: It is neither degenerating nor fully mature during this arrest."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"When the primary oocyte is surrounded by follicular cells of the ovary, the resulting structure is called the:","options":["Primordial follicle","Graafian follicle","Corpus luteum","Corpus albicans"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A primary oocyte surrounded by follicular cells is called the primordial follicle.  Why B wrong: The Graafian follicle is the mature pre-ovulatory follicle, a later stage.  Why C/D wrong: Corpus luteum and corpus albicans are post-ovulatory structures."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The meiotic arrest of the primary oocyte is overcome at puberty due to:","options":["LH surge","FSH surge","Estrogen surge","Progesterone drop"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The arrest is over at puberty due to the LH surge.  Why B/C/D wrong: The notes specifically credit the LH surge, not FSH, estrogen surge, or a progesterone drop."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"In the primary oocyte, meiosis (meiosis I) starts and then gets arrested in which phase?","options":["Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Meiosis I starts and gets arrested in prophase in the primary oocyte.  Why B wrong: Metaphase arrest occurs later, in meiosis II (secondary oocyte).  Why C/D wrong: Arrest occurs in prophase, not anaphase or telophase."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"When the primary oocyte completes meiosis I, it produces a secondary oocyte (23 X) plus a:","options":["First polar body","Second polar body","Female pronucleus","Corpus luteum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Meiosis I yields the secondary oocyte plus the 1st polar body (23 X).  Why B/C wrong: The second polar body and female pronucleus are products of meiosis II, at fertilization.  Why D wrong: The corpus luteum is the post-ovulatory remnant of the follicle, not a meiotic product."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"The release of the secondary oocyte from the primary oocyte (from the ovary) is called:","options":["Ovulation","Menstruation","Implantation","Capacitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Release of the secondary oocyte from the primary oocyte is called ovulation.  Why B wrong: Menstruation is shedding of the endometrium.  Why C wrong: Implantation is embedding of the embryo.  Why D wrong: Capacitation is a sperm maturation process."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"After ovulation, the secondary oocyte begins meiosis II but this gets arrested in which phase?","options":["Metaphase","Prophase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The secondary oocyte begins meiosis II but is arrested in metaphase.  Why B wrong: Prophase arrest occurs in meiosis I (primary oocyte).  Why C/D wrong: The arrest is at metaphase, not anaphase or telophase."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Meiosis II of the secondary oocyte is completed at which point?","options":["At the time of fertilization","At puberty","During ovulation","At implantation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Meiosis II is completed only at the time of fertilization.  Why B wrong: Puberty resumes meiosis I, not completion of meiosis II.  Why C wrong: At ovulation the oocyte is still arrested in metaphase II.  Why D wrong: Fertilization (before implantation) triggers completion of meiosis II."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Completion of meiosis II at fertilization produces the female pronucleus and the:","options":["Second polar body","First polar body","Corona radiata","Zona pellucida"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Completion of meiosis II yields the female pronucleus and the second polar body.  Why B wrong: The first polar body is released earlier, at completion of meiosis I.  Why C/D wrong: Corona radiata and zona pellucida are protective coverings, not meiotic products."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"In the primordial follicle figure, the outer layer of follicular cells shown is labelled as which structures?","options":["Cumulus oophoricus and corona radiata","Zona pellucida and vitelline membrane","Theca interna and theca externa","Granulosa and stroma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The follicular cells are labelled as the cumulus oophoricus and corona radiata.  Why B wrong: Zona pellucida/vitelline membrane are inner coverings of the oocyte, not the labelled follicular cell layers here.  Why C/D wrong: Theca layers and granulosa/stroma are not the labels used in this figure."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"In the structure of the secondary oocyte, the space between the oocyte and the zona pellucida (containing the first polar body) is called the:","options":["Perivitelline space","Cumulus oophoricus","Corona radiata","Ooplasm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The perivitelline space is the space where the 1st polar body lies, shown in the secondary oocyte structure.  Why B/C wrong: Cumulus oophoricus and corona radiata are outer follicular cell layers.  Why D wrong: Ooplasm is the cytoplasm of the oocyte, not the space."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"In the secondary oocyte structure, which two components together constitute the \"secondary oocyte\" proper?","options":["Nucleus and ooplasm","Zona pellucida and corona radiata","Vitelline membrane and perivitelline space","First polar body and cumulus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure brackets the nucleus and ooplasm together as the secondary oocyte.  Why B wrong: Zona pellucida and corona radiata are surrounding coverings.  Why C wrong: Vitelline membrane and perivitelline space are boundary/space structures.  Why D wrong: First polar body and cumulus are not the oocyte proper."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"Which of the following is the outermost covering of the secondary oocyte as shown in its structure?","options":["Corona radiata","Zona pellucida","Vitelline membrane","Perivitelline space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The corona radiata (follicular cells) forms the outermost covering shown around the secondary oocyte.  Why B wrong: Zona pellucida lies internal to the corona radiata.  Why C wrong: The vitelline membrane is the innermost oocyte membrane.  Why D wrong: The perivitelline space is a gap, not a covering layer."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Actions of estrogen and progesterone, estrogen-related drugs…","question":"In the secondary oocyte structure, the membrane lying just internal to the zona pellucida and enclosing the ooplasm is the:","options":["Vitelline membrane","Corona radiata","Perivitelline space","Cumulus oophoricus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vitelline membrane is the innermost membrane enclosing the ooplasm, labelled inside the zona pellucida.  Why B/D wrong: Corona radiata and cumulus oophoricus are outer follicular cell layers.  Why C wrong: The perivitelline space is the gap, not a membrane."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"When does oogenesis begin?","options":["During intrauterine (fetal) life","At birth","At puberty","At the time of first ovulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Oogenesis = begins in intrauterine life\" — the process of forming ova starts while the female is still in the womb.  Why B wrong: The process is already underway before birth, not initiated at birth.  Why C wrong: Puberty is when the arrested first meiotic division resumes, not when oogenesis begins.  Why D wrong: Ovulation occurs long after oogenesis has begun in fetal life."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The first meiotic division of the oocyte is arrested in which phase?","options":["Metaphase","Prophase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 1st meiotic division is \"arrested = in PROPHASE.\"  Why A wrong: Metaphase arrest applies to the 2nd meiotic division, not the 1st.  Why C wrong: Anaphase is not the arrest point of either meiotic division here.  Why D wrong: Telophase is not the described arrest phase."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The arrest of the first meiotic division is over (resumes) at what time?","options":["During intrauterine life","At birth","At puberty","At fertilization"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for the 1st meiotic division \"arrest is over = Puberty.\"  Why A wrong: The arrest begins in fetal life; it does not end there.  Why B wrong: The oocyte remains arrested in prophase from before birth until puberty.  Why D wrong: Fertilization ends the arrest of the 2nd meiotic division, not the 1st."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"Ovulation is defined as the release of which structure?","options":["Primary oocyte from the ovary","Secondary oocyte from primary oocyte","Mature ovum from a polar body","Oogonium from the germinal epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define \"Ovulation – Release of secondary oocyte from primary oocyte.\"  Why A wrong: It is the secondary oocyte, not the primary oocyte, that is released at ovulation.  Why C wrong: Polar bodies are byproducts of meiosis, not the source of the released cell.  Why D wrong: Oogonia are earlier precursor cells, not what is released at ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The first polar body is released at what time?","options":["At puberty","At the time of ovulation","At the time of fertilization","During implantation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"1st polar body released at the time of ovulation.\"  Why A wrong: Puberty is when the 1st meiotic arrest is over, but the polar body release is timed to ovulation.  Why C wrong: The 2nd polar body (not the 1st) is released at fertilization.  Why D wrong: Implantation occurs days after polar body release."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The second meiotic division of the oocyte is arrested in which phase?","options":["Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 2nd meiotic division is \"arrested in METAPHASE.\"  Why A wrong: Prophase arrest applies to the 1st meiotic division.  Why C wrong: Anaphase is not the described arrest point.  Why D wrong: Telophase is not the described arrest point."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The arrest of the second meiotic division is completed (resumes) at what time?","options":["At puberty","At ovulation","At the time of fertilization","At implantation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for the 2nd meiotic division \"arrest is over : at the time of fertilization.\"  Why A wrong: Puberty ends the 1st division arrest, not the 2nd.  Why B wrong: Ovulation releases the secondary oocyte still arrested in metaphase II.  Why D wrong: Implantation happens after fertilization has already completed meiosis II."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The second polar body is released at what time?","options":["At the time of ovulation","At puberty","At the time of fertilization","When the morula enters the uterus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"2nd polar body released at the time of fertilization.\"  Why A wrong: The 1st polar body (not the 2nd) is released at ovulation.  Why B wrong: Puberty relates to resumption of meiosis I, not polar body II release.  Why D wrong: Morula entry into the uterus is a later event unrelated to polar body release."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"What is the size of the ovum (largest cell in the body)?","options":["60–80 microns","90–100 microns","120–130 microns","150–160 microns"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Size of ova = 120–130 microns (largest cell in body).\"  Why A wrong: 60–80 microns is below the stated range.  Why B wrong: 90–100 microns is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 150–160 microns exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The ovum is known as which of the following?","options":["The smallest cell in the body","The largest cell in the body","The most motile cell in the body","The only anucleate cell in the body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly label the ovum as the \"largest cell in body.\"  Why A wrong: The sperm is among the smallest cells; the ovum is the largest.  Why C wrong: The sperm is the motile gamete; the ovum is non-motile.  Why D wrong: The ovum contains a nucleus; it is not anucleate."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"What is the fertilizable span (lifespan) of the ovum?","options":["6–12 hours","12–24 hours","24–48 hours","48–72 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Fertilizable span of ova: 12–24 hours.\"  Why A wrong: 6–12 hours is shorter than the stated span.  Why C wrong: 24–48 hours exceeds the stated span.  Why D wrong: 48–72 hours exceeds the stated span."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The maximum number of ovarian follicles is seen at which time of intrauterine life?","options":["3rd month","4th month","5th month (20 weeks) of intrauterine life","7th month"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Maximum number of follicles seen: 5th month of intrauterine life (20 weeks of intrauterine life).\"  Why A wrong: 3rd month is earlier than the peak.  Why B wrong: 4th month is before the stated peak.  Why D wrong: 7th month follicle numbers have already declined from the peak."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The maximum number of follicles seen at the 5th month of intrauterine life corresponds to how many weeks of intrauterine life?","options":["16 weeks","18 weeks","20 weeks","24 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes equate the 5th month with \"20 weeks of intrauterine life.\"  Why A wrong: 16 weeks corresponds to roughly the 4th month.  Why B wrong: 18 weeks does not match the stated 5th month.  Why D wrong: 24 weeks corresponds to about the 6th month."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"During fertilization, how many sperms attack the corona radiata?","options":["100–200 sperms","200–300 sperms","300–500 sperms","500–1000 sperms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"300–500 sperms attack to (Corona radiata).\"  Why A wrong: 100–200 is below the stated number.  Why B wrong: 200–300 is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 500–1000 exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"Which enzyme is released by sperms to help penetrate the corona radiata?","options":["Acrosin","Hyaluronidase","Amylase","Lipase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fertilization flow chart shows \"Hyaluronidase released\" after sperms attack the corona radiata.  Why A wrong: Acrosin is another acrosomal enzyme but the notes specifically name hyaluronidase here.  Why C wrong: Amylase digests carbohydrates in the gut, not corona radiata.  Why D wrong: Lipase digests fats, not the corona radiata matrix."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the fertilization process flow chart, after the sperm penetrates the corona radiata (CR), which layer does it reach next?","options":["Vitelline membrane","Zona pellucida","Perivitelline space","Ooplasm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows the order CR → Zona Pellucida → Acrosomal reaction → ZP3.  Why A wrong: The vitelline membrane lies deeper, encountered after the zona pellucida.  Why C wrong: The perivitelline space is reached only after crossing the zona pellucida.  Why D wrong: The ooplasm is the innermost cytoplasm, reached last."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"Which reaction occurs at the zona pellucida to allow sperm penetration?","options":["Cortical reaction","Acrosomal reaction","Zona reaction","Capacitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show \"Acrosomal reaction\" occurring at the Zona Pellucida in the fertilization flow chart.  Why A wrong: The cortical reaction is a block to polyspermy but is not the named event in this flow chart.  Why C wrong: The zona reaction is a subset of polyspermy blocking, not the named step here.  Why D wrong: Capacitation prepares sperm earlier in the female tract, before reaching the ovum."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The zona pellucida is composed of which glycoproteins?","options":["ZP1, ZP2, ZP3","ZP1, ZP4, ZP5","GP1, GP2, GP3","ZA1, ZA2, ZA3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"ZP3 (ZP1, ZP2, ZP3)\" — the zona pellucida consists of ZP1, ZP2 and ZP3.  Why B wrong: ZP4/ZP5 are not the components listed in the notes.  Why C wrong: The correct nomenclature is ZP (zona pellucida), not GP.  Why D wrong: ZA1–ZA3 is not the labeling used in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The zona pellucida has a protein receptor for the sperm known as:","options":["ZP1 receptor","ZP2 receptor","ZP3 receptor","GnRH receptor"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Zona pellucida → has protein receptor → ZP3 receptor.\"  Why A wrong: ZP1 is a component of the zona but not the named sperm-binding receptor here.  Why B wrong: ZP2 is a component but not the named receptor in the notes.  Why D wrong: GnRH is a hypothalamic hormone, unrelated to zona pellucida sperm binding."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the fertilization diagram, \"CR\" pointing to the outer layer around the oocyte stands for:","options":["Cell rim","Corona radiata","Cortical region","Central ridge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the outer cellular layer \"CR,\" standing for corona radiata (300–500 sperms attack the corona radiata).  Why A wrong: \"Cell rim\" is not an anatomical term used here.  Why C wrong: The cortical region is not the labelled structure.  Why D wrong: \"Central ridge\" is not a structure in the oocyte diagram."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the labelled secondary oocyte diagram, \"ZP\" stands for:","options":["Zonular process","Zona pellucida","Zygote plate","Zonal periphery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram uses \"ZP\" to label the zona pellucida surrounding the oocyte.  Why A wrong: \"Zonular process\" is not the structure shown.  Why C wrong: \"Zygote plate\" is not an anatomical term here.  Why D wrong: \"Zonal periphery\" is not the labelled structure."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the fertilization diagram, the nucleus labelled belongs to which cell?","options":["Nucleus of primary oocyte","Nucleus of secondary (2°) oocyte","Nucleus of the sperm","Nucleus of the polar body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels \"Nucleus of 2° oocyte\" (secondary oocyte).  Why A wrong: The released cell is the secondary oocyte, not the primary oocyte.  Why C wrong: The labelled nucleus is of the oocyte, not the sperm.  Why D wrong: The polar body is a separate small structure, not the labelled nucleus."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the secondary oocyte diagram, which membrane lies just beneath the zona pellucida and surrounds the cytoplasm?","options":["Plasma membrane only","Vitelline membrane","Basement membrane","Tunica albuginea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels \"Vitte membrane\" (vitelline membrane) around the oocyte.  Why A wrong: While the vitelline (oolemma) is a plasma membrane, the specific labelled structure is the vitelline membrane.  Why C wrong: The basement membrane is not a labelled oocyte layer here.  Why D wrong: The tunica albuginea is a covering of the ovary/testis, not the oocyte."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The narrow fluid-filled space between the oocyte membrane and the zona pellucida is called:","options":["Blastocoele","Perivitelline space","Uterine cavity","Antrum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the \"Perivitelline\" (perivitelline space) between the vitelline membrane and zona pellucida.  Why A wrong: The blastocoele is the fluid cavity inside a blastocyst, formed much later.  Why C wrong: The uterine cavity is the interior of the uterus, unrelated to the oocyte.  Why D wrong: The antrum is the fluid cavity of an ovarian follicle, not this space."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"What is the main function of the zona pellucida during fertilization?","options":["To provide nutrition to the sperm","To allow entry of a single sperm and prevent polyspermia","To secrete progesterone","To transport the ovum through the tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Function of zona pellucida: Allows entry of single sperm and prevents POLYSPERMIA.\"  Why A wrong: The zona regulates sperm entry; it is not a nutrient source for sperm.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is secreted by the corpus luteum, not the zona pellucida.  Why D wrong: Tubal cilia/peristalsis transport the ovum, not the zona pellucida."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"Polyspermia refers to:","options":["Entry of a single sperm into the ovum","Entry of more than one sperm into the ovum","Absence of any sperm","Fusion of two ova"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The zona pellucida \"prevents POLYSPERMIA,\" i.e., prevents entry of more than one sperm into the ovum.  Why A wrong: Single-sperm entry is the normal, desired event the zona permits.  Why C wrong: Absence of sperm is not polyspermia.  Why D wrong: Polyspermia involves multiple sperm, not fusion of two ova."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"A single sperm is able to enter the zona pellucida with the help of which structures?","options":["ZP1 receptors","ZP3 receptors","FSH receptors","LH receptors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"SINGLE SPERM ENTERS ZONA pellucida (with help of ZP3 receptors).\"  Why A wrong: ZP1 is a zona component, but ZP3 is the specific sperm-binding receptor named.  Why C wrong: FSH receptors are on granulosa cells, not involved in sperm zona entry.  Why D wrong: LH receptors relate to ovulation triggering, not zona sperm entry."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"After the sperm penetrates the zona pellucida, which space does it reach next?","options":["Blastocoele","Perivitelline space","Antral cavity","Uterine cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows after entering the zona pellucida the sperm \"Reach the perivitelline space.\"  Why A wrong: The blastocoele forms only in the blastocyst stage.  Why C wrong: The antral cavity is a follicular structure, not relevant post-penetration.  Why D wrong: The uterine cavity is far downstream of fertilization events."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"After the sperm reaches the perivitelline space, what event is completed?","options":["First meiotic division","Second meiotic division and secondary oocyte formation","Cleavage into 2 cells","Formation of the morula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states the \"2nd meiotic division is completed and secondary oocyte\" then splits into female pronucleus and 2nd polar body.  Why A wrong: The first meiotic division was completed earlier at ovulation.  Why C wrong: Cleavage into 2 cells occurs after the zygote is formed.  Why D wrong: Morula formation is a much later cleavage stage."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"Completion of the second meiotic division of the secondary oocyte produces which two products?","options":["Two secondary oocytes","Female pronucleus and the second polar body","Male pronucleus and the first polar body","Two zygotes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart branches into \"Female Pronucleus\" and \"2nd Polar body release.\"  Why A wrong: The division yields one functional pronucleus, not two oocytes.  Why C wrong: The male pronucleus comes from the sperm, and the polar body released here is the 2nd, not the 1st.  Why D wrong: The zygote forms only after the pronuclei fuse."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"Fertilization is precisely defined as the meeting/fusion of which two structures?","options":["Two sperms","Sperm and the female pronucleus","Two polar bodies","Sperm and the corona radiata"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Sperm + Female pronucleus → meet → called as FERTILIZATION.\"  Why A wrong: Fusion of two sperms does not occur in fertilization.  Why B correct as above.  Why C wrong: Polar bodies are discarded byproducts, not fusion partners.  Why D wrong: The corona radiata is a surrounding cell layer, not a fusion partner."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The immediate product formed when the sperm and female pronucleus fuse is:","options":["A morula","A single-cell zygote","A blastocyst","A secondary oocyte"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows FERTILIZATION → \"Single cell ZYGOTE.\"  Why A wrong: The morula is a 16-cell later stage.  Why C wrong: The blastocyst forms after the morula reaches the uterus.  Why D wrong: The secondary oocyte existed before fertilization."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"At what stage is the sex of the individual decided?","options":["At ovulation","When the single-cell zygote is formed","At implantation","At the morula stage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows Single cell ZYGOTE → \"Sex decided.\"  Why A wrong: Ovulation only releases the oocyte (which always carries X); sex is not yet determined.  Why C wrong: Implantation occurs after sex has already been determined at fertilization.  Why D wrong: By the morula stage the sex was already decided at zygote formation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"A sperm carrying 22+X combining with an ovum carrying 22+X produces a zygote of which chromosomal makeup and sex?","options":["44+XY, male","44+XX, female","45+XXX, female","46+XY, male"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show 22+x sperm + 22+x ovum = \"44+XX Zygote Female.\"  Why A wrong: 44+XY (male) results from a Y-bearing sperm.  Why C wrong: 45+XXX is an abnormal trisomy, not the depicted normal outcome.  Why D wrong: The total autosomes are written as 44 in the notes and the combination gives XX, not XY."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"A sperm carrying 22+Y combining with an ovum carrying 22+X produces a zygote that is:","options":["Female","Male","Either sex randomly","Sexless"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show 22+Y sperm + 22+X ovum → zygote; a Y-bearing sperm produces a male (XY).  Why A wrong: An X-bearing sperm (not Y) would produce a female.  Why C wrong: A Y-bearing sperm deterministically produces a male.  Why D wrong: The zygote has a defined sex (male here)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The sex of the baby is decided by:","options":["The mother (ovum)","The father (sperm)","Both parents equally at random","The hormonal environment of the uterus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Sex of Body is decided by Father (sperm)\" because the sperm carries either an X or Y chromosome.  Why A wrong: The ovum always contributes an X chromosome, so it cannot determine sex.  Why C wrong: The determining chromosome (X or Y) comes solely from the sperm.  Why D wrong: The uterine hormonal environment does not set genetic sex."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"What is the normal site of fertilization?","options":["Fundus of the uterus","Ampulla of the fallopian tube","Isthmus of the fallopian tube","Cervical canal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Site of fertilization → Ampulla of Fallopian Tube → Zygote is formed.\"  Why A wrong: The uterine fundus is the site of implantation, not fertilization.  Why C wrong: The isthmus is a narrower tubal segment, not the usual fertilization site.  Why D wrong: The cervical canal is not where fertilization occurs."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"After fertilization, the single-celled zygote begins to divide. The first division (to a 2-celled zygote) occurs approximately how long after fertilization?","options":["After 12 hours","After 30 hours","After 48 hours","After 72 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the zygote (single celled) divides \"After 30 hours of fertilization.\"  Why A wrong: 12 hours is too early per the notes.  Why C wrong: 48 hours is later than the stated 30 hours.  Why D wrong: 72 hours corresponds to the tubal transport window, not the first cleavage."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"Arrange the correct sequence of cleavage stages of the zygote.","options":["2-celled → 4-celled → 8-celled → 16-celled (morula)","4-celled → 2-celled → 8-celled → 16-celled","8-celled → 4-celled → 2-celled → morula","16-celled → 8-celled → 4-celled → 2-celled"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show zygote → 2 celled → 4 celled → 8 celled → 16 celled (MORULA).  Why B wrong: Cleavage does not go from 4 back to 2 cells.  Why C wrong: Cell number increases, not decreases, during cleavage.  Why D wrong: This reverses the natural increasing sequence."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The individual cells produced during cleavage of the zygote are called:","options":["Blastomeres","Trophoblasts","Pronuclei","Oogonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the dividing cells as \"Blastomere\" during the 4-celled stage.  Why B wrong: Trophoblast is the outer layer of the blastocyst, formed later.  Why C wrong: Pronuclei are the haploid nuclei before fusion, not cleavage cells.  Why D wrong: Oogonia are germ cell precursors in the fetal ovary."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The 16-celled stage of the dividing zygote is known as the:","options":["Blastocyst","Morula","Gastrula","Zygote"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"16 celled Zygote (MORULA).\"  Why A wrong: The blastocyst forms after the morula enters the fluid-filled uterine cavity.  Why C wrong: The gastrula is a later embryonic stage not mentioned here.  Why D wrong: \"Zygote\" refers to the single cell / early dividing stages, but the 16-cell stage is specifically the morula."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"On which day of a 28-day cycle does fertilization typically occur (per the notes)?","options":["Day 7","Day 14","Day 18","Day 21"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"When did fertilization happened = Day 14 of cycle\" and the timeline marks \"14 ovulation / 14 fertilization.\"  Why A wrong: Day 7 is within the follicular phase, before ovulation.  Why C wrong: Day 18 is when the morula enters the uterine cavity, not fertilization.  Why D wrong: Day 21 is the mid-luteal phase, after fertilization."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the labelled Morula structure, the outermost layer is the:","options":["Trophoblast","Zona pellucida","Inner cell mass","Blastocoele"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Structure of Morula\" diagram labels the outer covering as \"Zona pellucida.\"  Why A wrong: The trophoblast is a blastocyst feature, formed after the morula stage.  Why C wrong: The inner cell mass is a blastocyst structure.  Why D wrong: The blastocoele is the blastocyst cavity, not present in the morula."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"From where does the early zygote/morula obtain its nutrition while in the fallopian tube?","options":["Maternal blood via the placenta","The fallopian tube (secretory cells)","The uterine endometrium","The corpus luteum directly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes ask \"Early zygote from where does it get nutrition → Fallopian Tube,\" and secretory cells of the tube provide nutrition.  Why A wrong: The placenta has not yet formed at this stage.  Why C wrong: The endometrium nourishes only after implantation in the uterus.  Why D wrong: The corpus luteum secretes hormones, not direct nutrition to the tubal zygote."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The fallopian tube lining contains how many types of cells (per the notes)?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show a box \"3 Types of Cells.\"  Why A wrong: Two understates the number.  Why C wrong: Four exceeds the stated three.  Why D wrong: Five exceeds the stated three."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"Which of the three tubal cell types pushes the zygote toward the uterine cavity?","options":["Ciliated columnar cells","Secretory cells","PEG cells","Goblet cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Ciliated columnar cells → Cilia pushes zygote towards uterine cavity.\"  Why B wrong: Secretory cells provide nutrition, not propulsion.  Why C wrong: PEG cells have a function that is \"not known\" per the notes.  Why D wrong: Goblet cells are not one of the three listed tubal cell types."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"Which of the three tubal cell types provides nutrition to the zygote?","options":["Ciliated columnar cells","Secretory cells","PEG cells","Trophoblast cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Secretory cells → Provide nutrition to Zygote.\"  Why A wrong: Ciliated columnar cells move the zygote, they do not nourish it.  Why C wrong: PEG cells' function is unknown per the notes.  Why D wrong: Trophoblast is part of the blastocyst, not a tubal cell type."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"According to the notes, what is the function of PEG cells of the fallopian tube?","options":["Provide nutrition to the zygote","Push the zygote via cilia","Function not known","Secrete estrogen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for PEG cells \"Function not known.\"  Why A wrong: Nutrition is the role of secretory cells.  Why B wrong: Cilia-based propulsion is the role of ciliated columnar cells.  Why D wrong: The notes do not attribute estrogen secretion to PEG cells; their function is listed as unknown."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"For how long does the morula remain in the fallopian tube?","options":["Till 1 day after fertilization","Till 3 days after fertilization","Till 5 days after fertilization","Till 7 days after fertilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Till how much time will Morula remain in fallopian tube = Till 3 days after fertilization.\"  Why A wrong: 1 day is too short per the notes.  Why C wrong: 5 days is when the zona pellucida is lost, not tubal residence.  Why D wrong: 7 days is well beyond the stated tubal residence period."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"On which day after fertilization does the morula enter the uterine cavity?","options":["3rd day","4th day","5th day","6th day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"On 4th Day after fertilization → Morula enters the uterine cavity.\"  Why A wrong: On day 3 the morula is still in the tube.  Why C wrong: Day 5 is when the zona pellucida is lost, after uterine entry.  Why D wrong: Day 6 is later than the stated day 4."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"By which two mechanisms does the morula enter the uterine cavity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Movement of cilia","Peristalsis in the tube","Active swimming of the morula","Uterine suction","Gravity alone"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Movement of cilia\" and \"Peristalsis in tube\" as the mechanisms of morula transport into the uterine cavity.  Why C wrong: The morula does not swim actively; it is passively moved.  Why D wrong: \"Uterine suction\" is not a mechanism listed in the notes.  Why E wrong: Gravity is not the stated transport mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"If the morula does not enter the uterine cavity and instead implants in the fallopian tube, the condition is called:","options":["Molar pregnancy","Ectopic pregnancy","Abruptio placentae","Placenta previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that failure to enter the uterine cavity with implantation in the tube \"is called: ECTOPIC Pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: A molar pregnancy is abnormal trophoblastic proliferation, not tubal implantation.  Why C wrong: Abruptio placentae is premature placental separation, unrelated.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is low placental implantation over the cervix, not tubal."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In which cellular form/stage does the zygote enter the uterine cavity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Morula","16-celled stage zygote","16–64 cell staged zygote","Single-celled zygote","Blastocyst before fluid entry"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the entering form as \"MORULA,\" \"16 celled stage zygote,\" and \"16–64 cell staged zygote.\"  Why D wrong: The single-celled zygote is the initial stage still in the tube, not the entering form.  Why E wrong: The morula becomes the blastocyst only after entering the fluid-filled uterine cavity, so it does not enter as a blastocyst."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"Entry of the morula into the uterine cavity on the 4th day after fertilization corresponds to which day of the menstrual cycle?","options":["14th day","16th day","18th day","21st day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the morula enters the uterine cavity on the \"4th day after fertilization\" which is the \"18th day of the cycle\" (fertilization day 14 + 4 days).  Why A wrong: Day 14 is the day of ovulation/fertilization.  Why B wrong: Day 16 does not match 14 + 4 days.  Why D wrong: Day 21 is later than the calculated day 18."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"On the menstrual-cycle timeline in the notes, ovulation and fertilization are both marked at:","options":["Day 1","Day 14","Day 18","Day 28"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline marks \"14 ovulation / 14 fertilization\" at day 14.  Why A wrong: Day 1 is the start of the cycle (menstruation).  Why C wrong: Day 18 is when the morula enters the uterine cavity.  Why D wrong: Day 28 is the end of the cycle."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"When the morula enters the uterine cavity, what happens to it that changes its name?","options":["It loses its cells","It gets filled with fluid and becomes a blastocyst","It fuses with the endometrium","It divides into two morulae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"When Morula enters uterine cavity it gets filled with fluid → Now Morula is called BLASTOCYST.\"  Why A wrong: It does not lose cells; it accumulates fluid.  Why C wrong: Fusion with endometrium is implantation, a separate later event.  Why D wrong: The morula does not split into two."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"On which day after fertilization is the zona pellucida lost?","options":["Day 3","Day 4","Day 5","Day 6"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"On Day 5 after fertilization: Zona pellucida is lost.\"  Why A wrong: Day 3 is when the morula is still in the tube with intact zona.  Why B wrong: Day 4 is uterine entry; the zona is still present.  Why D wrong: Day 6 is later than the stated day 5."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the structure of the blastocyst, the cluster of cells that will form the embryo is called the:","options":["Trophoblast","Inner cell mass","Zona pellucida","Corona radiata"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The blastocyst diagram labels the \"Inner cell mass\" (embryoblast).  Why A wrong: The trophoblast is the outer cell layer that forms the placenta.  Why C wrong: The zona pellucida is lost by the blastocyst stage.  Why D wrong: The corona radiata surrounds the oocyte, not part of the blastocyst."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the blastocyst, the outer single layer of cells that surrounds the cavity is called the:","options":["Inner cell mass","Trophoblast","Embryoblast","Blastocoele"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The blastocyst diagram labels the outer layer \"Trophoblast.\"  Why A wrong: The inner cell mass is the inner cluster, not the outer layer.  Why C wrong: The embryoblast is another name for the inner cell mass, not the outer layer.  Why D wrong: The blastocoele is the fluid cavity, not a cell layer."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The fluid-filled cavity of the blastocyst is called the:","options":["Antrum","Blastocoele (blastocyst cavity)","Perivitelline space","Amniotic cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Morula and Blastocyst figure labels the \"Blastocyst cavity (blastocoele).\"  Why A wrong: The antrum is the fluid cavity of an ovarian follicle.  Why C wrong: The perivitelline space is between the oocyte and its zona.  Why D wrong: The amniotic cavity forms later around the developing embryo."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"The inner cell mass of the blastocyst is also known as the:","options":["Trophoblast","Embryoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels \"Inner cell mass (embryoblast).\"  Why A wrong: The trophoblast is the outer layer.  Why C wrong: The cytotrophoblast is a trophoblast sub-layer, not the inner cell mass.  Why D wrong: The syncytiotrophoblast is a trophoblast sub-layer, not the embryoblast."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the labelled secondary oocyte image, which structure is the outermost cellular layer?","options":["Zona pellucida","Corona radiata","Vitelline membrane","Ooplasm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Image of Secondary Oocyte\" labels the corona radiata as the outermost cellular (radiating cell) layer.  Why A wrong: The zona pellucida lies just inside the corona radiata.  Why C wrong: The vitelline membrane is deeper, surrounding the ooplasm.  Why D wrong: The ooplasm is the innermost cytoplasm."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the secondary oocyte image, the cytoplasm of the oocyte is labelled as:","options":["Germinal vesicle","Ooplasm","Nucleus","Zona pellucida"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The image labels the cytoplasm of the oocyte as \"Ooplasm.\"  Why A wrong: The germinal vesicle is the large oocyte nucleus, not the cytoplasm.  Why C wrong: The nucleus is a distinct labelled structure, not the cytoplasm.  Why D wrong: The zona pellucida is a surrounding glycoprotein layer."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the secondary oocyte image, the large nucleus of the oocyte is also labelled as the:","options":["Germinal vesicle","Blastocoele","Perivitelline space","Trophoblast"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The image labels \"Nucleus\" and \"Germinal vesicle,\" the germinal vesicle being the oocyte's nucleus.  Why B wrong: The blastocoele is the blastocyst cavity.  Why C wrong: The perivitelline space is a fluid space, not the nucleus.  Why D wrong: The trophoblast is the outer blastocyst layer."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the fertilization image, which structures are labelled around/within the ovum? (Select all that apply)","options":["Corona radiata","Cytoplasm","Nucleus","Plasma membrane","Zona pellucida"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The FERTILIZATION image labels all five — corona radiata, cytoplasm, nucleus, plasma membrane, and zona pellucida.  Why (each) correct: Each is explicitly present as a labelled line in the figure.  Why none excluded: No listed option is absent from the figure labels."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the \"Figure of Uterus → Implantation,\" which layers of the uterine wall are labelled? (Select all that apply)","options":["Endometrium","Myometrium","Serosa","Perimetrium cartilage","Peritoneal muscle"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The uterus/implantation figure labels the endometrium, myometrium, and serosa (plus the uterine cavity).  Why D wrong: \"Perimetrium cartilage\" is not a real/labelled structure; the uterus has no cartilage.  Why E wrong: \"Peritoneal muscle\" is not a labelled uterine layer."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the uterus/implantation figure, the innermost layer where implantation occurs is the:","options":["Endometrium","Myometrium","Serosa","Ut cavity wall muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The endometrium is the innermost lining layer labelled, into which the blastocyst implants.  Why B wrong: The myometrium is the middle muscular layer.  Why C wrong: The serosa is the outermost covering layer.  Why D wrong: There is no separate labelled \"Ut cavity wall muscle\"; the cavity is the space lined by endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the uterus/implantation figure, the thick middle muscular layer of the uterus is the:","options":["Endometrium","Myometrium","Serosa","Perimetrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the middle muscular layer \"Myometrium.\"  Why A wrong: The endometrium is the inner lining.  Why C wrong: The serosa is the outermost layer.  Why D wrong: The perimetrium is not the label used here (the outer layer is labelled serosa)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the uterus/implantation figure, the outermost covering layer of the uterus is the:","options":["Endometrium","Myometrium","Serosa","Mucosa"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the outermost layer \"Serosa.\"  Why A wrong: The endometrium is the innermost layer.  Why B wrong: The myometrium is the middle layer.  Why D wrong: \"Mucosa\" corresponds to the endometrial lining, not the outer layer here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"In the Morula/Blastocyst comparison figure, which label denotes the zona pellucida of the morula?","options":["ZP","ICM","TB","BC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The morula in the figure is labelled \"ZP\" for zona pellucida.  Why B wrong: ICM (inner cell mass) is a blastocyst label.  Why C wrong: TB is not the label used; trophoblast is spelled out.  Why D wrong: BC is not the label used; the blastocyst cavity is spelled out."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"A woman's ovum is fertilized in the ampulla on cycle day 14. On day 18 of her cycle, in what form is the conceptus most likely entering her uterine cavity?","options":["Single-celled zygote","Morula (16–64 celled)","Fully implanted blastocyst","Primary oocyte"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Day 18 of the cycle = day 4 after fertilization, when the morula (16–64 celled) enters the uterine cavity.  Why A wrong: The single-celled zygote stage was ~30 hours after fertilization, in the tube.  Why C wrong: Implantation and blastocyst transformation occur after uterine entry, not during entry.  Why D wrong: The primary oocyte stage precedes ovulation and fertilization."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Oogenesis and ova…","question":"A blastocyst is examined 5 days after fertilization. Which finding is expected?","options":["Intact zona pellucida is present","The zona pellucida has been lost","It is still a single-celled zygote","It is still in the ampulla of the tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that on Day 5 after fertilization the zona pellucida is lost, which is required before implantation.  Why A wrong: By day 5 the zona pellucida is lost, not intact.  Why C wrong: By day 5 it is a multicellular blastocyst, not single-celled.  Why D wrong: By day 4 it has entered the uterine cavity, so it is not in the ampulla."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The process of attaching of the blastocyst to the endometrium is called:","options":["Fertilization","Implantation","Capacitation","Ovulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define implantation as the attaching of the blastocyst to the endometrium.  Why A wrong: Fertilization is the fusion of sperm and ovum, not attachment to endometrium.  Why C wrong: Capacitation is the functional maturation of sperm in the female tract.  Why D wrong: Ovulation is the release of the ovum from the ovarian follicle."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Which type of implantation occurs in humans?","options":["Superficial implantation","Eccentric implantation","Interstitial implantation","Central implantation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the type of implantation occurring in humans is INTERSTITIAL IMPLANTATION.  Why A wrong: Superficial attachment is the initial contact but not the human type of implantation.  Why B wrong: Eccentric refers to the side location of implantation, not the human implantation type named here.  Why D wrong: Central implantation is not the human pattern described."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Interstitial implantation means that the blastocyst:","options":["Attaches only superficially to the endometrial surface","Goes deep inside the endometrium and then implants","Implants in the fallopian tube","Remains floating in the uterine cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define the \"Means\" of interstitial implantation as the blastocyst going deep inside the endometrium and then implanting.  Why A wrong: Superficial attachment is the opposite/earlier step, not interstitial implantation.  Why C wrong: Tubal implantation is ectopic, not the normal interstitial process.  Why D wrong: A floating blastocyst has not implanted at all."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The limit of penetration of the blastocyst into the endometrium is decided by:","options":["Nitabuch's layer","Decidua basalis","Basement membrane","Chorion frondosum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the limit of penetration of the blastocyst into the endometrium is decided by NITABUCH's LAYER.  Why B wrong: Decidua basalis forms the maternal side of the placenta, it does not limit penetration.  Why C wrong: The basement membrane is not named as the penetration limit here.  Why D wrong: Chorion frondosum forms the fetal side of the placenta."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"After implantation, the endometrium is called:","options":["Myometrium","Serosa","Decidua","Perimetrium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that after implantation the endometrium is called Decidua (pregnant endometrium).  Why A wrong: Myometrium is the muscular layer of the uterus.  Why B wrong: Serosa is the outer serous covering of the uterus.  Why D wrong: Perimetrium is the outer uterine layer, not the transformed endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The decidua (pregnant endometrium) is notable because it:","options":["Persists throughout life","Sheds after delivery","Turns into myometrium","Becomes the fetal membrane"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the decidua sheds after delivery.  Why A wrong: It is shed, not permanent.  Why C wrong: It is transformed endometrium, not muscle.  Why D wrong: Fetal membranes arise from the chorion/amnion, not the decidua."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Which hormone converts the endometrium to decidua?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone converts the endometrium to decidua.  Why A wrong: Estrogen proliferates endometrium but the notes name progesterone for decidual conversion.  Why C wrong: hCG maintains the corpus luteum, not the direct converter named.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin causes uterine contraction, unrelated to decidualization."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The hormone responsible for the decidual reaction is:","options":["Progesterone","Prolactin","Relaxin","Estrogen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"Hormone Responsible for Decidual Reaction: PROGESTERONE.\"  Why B wrong: Prolactin stimulates milk production, not the decidual reaction.  Why C wrong: Relaxin softens the cervix/ligaments; not named here.  Why D wrong: Estrogen is not the hormone named for the decidual reaction."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Implantation occurs in the form of a:","options":["Zygote","Morula","Blastocyst","Gastrula"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state implantation occurs in the form of a BLASTOCYST.  Why A wrong: The zygote is the single-cell stage right after fertilization.  Why B wrong: The morula is the solid ball of cells preceding the blastocyst.  Why D wrong: Gastrula formation occurs later, after implantation."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Implantation begins on which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 3","Day 6","Day 10","Day 14"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state implantation begins by Day 6 after fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 3 is too early; the blastocyst is still forming.  Why C wrong: Day 10 is when implantation is completed, not when it begins.  Why D wrong: Day 14 is when mesoderm is formed, unrelated to the start of implantation."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Implantation begins on which day of the menstrual cycle?","options":["Day 14","Day 20","Day 24","Day 28"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state implantation begins by Day 20 of the cycle.  Why A wrong: Day 14 is the time of ovulation/fertilization.  Why C wrong: Day 24 is not the stated start of implantation.  Why D wrong: Day 28 marks the end of the cycle."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Implantation is completed by which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 6","Day 8","Day 10","Day 12"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state implantation is completed by Day 10 after fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 6 is when implantation begins.  Why B wrong: Day 8 is when the trophoblast differentiates, not implantation completion.  Why D wrong: Day 12 is when two cavities appear, after implantation is complete."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"In a normal 28-day cycle, on which day does ovulation and fertilization typically occur according to the timeline?","options":["Day 6","Day 14","Day 20","Day 28"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline in the notes marks Day 14 as both ovulation and fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 6 is when implantation begins.  Why C wrong: Day 20 corresponds to the start of implantation.  Why D wrong: Day 28 is the last day of the cycle."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The most common site of implantation is:","options":["The fundus of the uterus","The posterior wall of the upper part of the uterus","The anterior wall of the lower uterine segment","The cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common site of implantation is the posterior wall of the upper part of the uterus, and explicitly note it is NOT the fundus.  Why A wrong: The notes specifically exclude the fundus (\"NOT FUNDUS\").  Why C wrong: Lower segment implantation would be abnormal (placenta previa).  Why D wrong: Cervical implantation is an ectopic/abnormal site."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Slight bleeding that occurs in some females at the time of implantation is known as:","options":["Hartman sign","Piskacek sign","Goodell sign","Chadwick sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that bleeding occurring at the time of implantation is the HARTMAN SIGN.  Why B wrong: Piskacek sign is asymmetrical uterine enlargement, not bleeding.  Why C wrong: Goodell sign is cervical softening.  Why D wrong: Chadwick sign is bluish discoloration of the cervix/vagina."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The implantation bleeding of Hartman sign roughly corresponds to the time of:","options":["The next expected ovulation","The actual (expected) menstrual period","Delivery","Fertilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Hartman sign roughly corresponds to the time of the actual period.  Why A wrong: It coincides with the expected period, not the next ovulation.  Why C wrong: Delivery is at term, far later.  Why D wrong: Fertilization is around day 14, earlier than implantation bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Hartman sign (implantation bleeding) occurs due to:","options":["Central implantation","Eccentric implantation (implantation on the side of the uterus)","Fundal implantation","Tubal implantation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes attribute Hartman sign to eccentric implantation, i.e., it occurs on the side of the uterus.  Why A wrong: Central location is characteristic of a pseudo gestational sac (ectopic), not the cause of Hartman sign.  Why C wrong: The fundus is not the most common implantation site per these notes.  Why D wrong: Tubal implantation is ectopic and not linked to Hartman sign here."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Asymmetrical enlargement of the uterus in early pregnancy is called:","options":["Hartman sign","Piskacek sign","Hegar sign","Braxton Hicks sign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state PISKACEK sign is asymmetrical enlargement of the uterus in early pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Hartman sign is implantation bleeding.  Why C wrong: Hegar sign is softening of the lower uterine segment (not asymmetrical enlargement).  Why D wrong: Braxton Hicks are painless intermittent contractions, not asymmetrical enlargement."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"On ultrasound in early pregnancy, the gestational sac appears on one side (eccentrically located). This corresponds to which clinical sign?","options":["Hartman sign","Piskacek sign","Goodell sign","Osiander sign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes link Piskacek sign to the gestational sac being visible on one side (eccentric) on USG.  Why A wrong: Hartman sign is implantation bleeding.  Why C wrong: Goodell sign is cervical softening.  Why D wrong: Osiander sign relates to lateral vaginal pulsations, not eccentric sac location."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"A true gestational sac, seen in an intrauterine pregnancy, is located:","options":["Eccentrically (on one side)","Centrally","In the fallopian tube","In the cervix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a true gestational sac is ECCENTRIC and is seen in intrauterine pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Central location is characteristic of a pseudo gestational sac (ectopic).  Why C wrong: Tubal location indicates ectopic pregnancy, not a true intrauterine sac.  Why D wrong: Cervical location is not the described true-sac position."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"A centrally located gestational sac on ultrasound is a pseudo gestational sac and is seen in:","options":["Normal intrauterine pregnancy","Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a pseudo gestational sac is CENTRALLY LOCATED and is seen in ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Intrauterine pregnancy shows a true (eccentric) sac.  Why C wrong: Molar pregnancy is not the association given.  Why D wrong: Twin pregnancy is not the association given."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"How many types of decidua form after implantation is complete?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe 3 types of decidua (basalis, capsularis, and parietalis/vera).  Why A wrong: There are three, not two.  Why C wrong: Only three types are described.  Why D wrong: Only three types are described."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The decidua that separates the blastocyst from the myometrium is the:","options":["Decidua capsularis","Decidua basalis","Decidua parietalis","Decidua vera"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define decidua basalis as the decidua which separates the blastocyst from the myometrium.  Why A wrong: Decidua capsularis separates the blastocyst from the uterine cavity.  Why C wrong: Decidua parietalis is the remaining endometrium of the entire uterus.  Why D wrong: Decidua vera is another name for decidua parietalis, not the basalis."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Which type of decidua forms the maternal side of the placenta?","options":["Decidua basalis","Decidua capsularis","Decidua parietalis","Decidua vera"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state decidua basalis is the decidua which forms the maternal side of the placenta.  Why B wrong: Decidua capsularis covers the blastocyst toward the uterine cavity.  Why C wrong: Decidua parietalis is the remaining endometrium.  Why D wrong: Decidua vera = decidua parietalis, not the placental maternal side."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The decidua that separates the blastocyst from the uterine cavity is the:","options":["Decidua basalis","Decidua capsularis","Decidua parietalis","Decidua vera"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define decidua capsularis as the decidua which separates the blastocyst from the uterine cavity.  Why A wrong: Decidua basalis separates blastocyst from myometrium and forms maternal placenta.  Why C wrong: Decidua parietalis lines the rest of the uterus.  Why D wrong: Decidua vera is the parietalis, not capsularis."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The decidua parietalis is also known as:","options":["Decidua basalis","Decidua capsularis","Decidua vera","Decidua frondosum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly label DECIDUA PARIETALIS as Decidua Vera.  Why A wrong: Decidua basalis is a distinct type.  Why B wrong: Decidua capsularis is a distinct type.  Why D wrong: \"Decidua frondosum\" is not a term; chorion frondosum is the fetal placental part."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Which decidua represents the remaining endometrium of the entire uterus (not directly involved with the blastocyst)?","options":["Decidua basalis","Decidua capsularis","Decidua parietalis","Chorion laeve"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define decidua parietalis (vera) as the remaining endometrium of the entire uterus.  Why A wrong: Basalis lies between blastocyst and myometrium.  Why B wrong: Capsularis covers the blastocyst toward the cavity.  Why D wrong: Chorion laeve is a fetal membrane structure, not a decidua."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"At what gestation do the decidua parietalis and decidua capsularis fuse with each other?","options":["8–10 weeks / 2 months","12 weeks / 3 months","14–16 weeks / 4 months","20 weeks / 5 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Q1 of the notes states decidua parietalis and capsularis fuse at 14–16 weeks / 4 months.  Why A wrong: Fusion is later than 8–10 weeks.  Why B wrong: The notes specify 14–16 weeks, not 12 weeks.  Why D wrong: Fusion occurs by 4 months, not 5 months."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"At what gestational age is the uterine cavity obliterated?","options":["8 weeks / 2 months","14–16 weeks / 4 months","20 weeks / 5 months","24 weeks / 6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Q2 of the notes states the uterine cavity is obliterated at 14–16 weeks / 4 months.  Why A wrong: Obliteration occurs later than 8 weeks.  Why C wrong: The notes specify 14–16 weeks, not 20 weeks.  Why D wrong: The notes specify 14–16 weeks, not 24 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"During further development, the blastocyst differentiates into which two components?","options":["Ectoderm and endoderm","Trophoblast and inner cell mass","Chorion and amnion","Cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show further development of the blastocyst gives rise to the trophoblast and the inner cell mass.  Why A wrong: Ectoderm and endoderm are later germ layers derived from the inner cell mass.  Why C wrong: Chorion and amnion are fetal membranes formed later.  Why D wrong: Cyto- and syncytiotrophoblast are subdivisions of the trophoblast, not the initial two components."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The trophoblast differentiates on which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 6","Day 7","Day 8","Day 12"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label trophoblast differentiation as occurring on Day 8 after fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 6 is when implantation begins.  Why B wrong: Day 7 is when the ectoderm forms.  Why D wrong: Day 12 is when two cavities appear."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The single cell layer of the trophoblast is called the:","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Chorion laeve","Decidua basalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the single cell layer of the trophoblast is the cytotrophoblast.  Why A wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is the multicellular layer.  Why C wrong: Chorion laeve is a smooth fetal membrane, not the cell layer name.  Why D wrong: Decidua basalis is maternal tissue, not trophoblast."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The multicellular layer of the trophoblast is called the:","options":["Cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast","Amnion","Yolk sac"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the multicellular layer is the syncytiotrophoblast.  Why A wrong: Cytotrophoblast is the single cell layer.  Why C wrong: Amnion is the inner fetal membrane formed by ectoderm.  Why D wrong: The yolk sac is derived from the inner cell mass region, not the trophoblast layer."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Which structure is known as the \"hormone factory of the placenta,\" producing hCG and HPL?","options":["Cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast","Decidua basalis","Chorion laeve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the syncytiotrophoblast as the hormone factory of the placenta producing hCG and HPL.  Why A wrong: Cytotrophoblast forms villi and chorion, not the primary hormone factory.  Why C wrong: Decidua basalis is maternal placental tissue.  Why D wrong: Chorion laeve is the smooth fetal membrane."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"hCG and HPL are formed by which structure?","options":["Cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast","Inner cell mass","Decidua parietalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The single-liner in the notes states hCG and HPL are formed by the syncytiotrophoblast.  Why A wrong: Cytotrophoblast forms villi/chorion.  Why C wrong: Inner cell mass gives rise to germ layers.  Why D wrong: Decidua parietalis is remaining maternal endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Near the decidua basalis, the cytotrophoblast forms villi-like structures known as the:","options":["Chorion laeve","Chorion frondosum","Amnion","Yolk sac"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that near the decidua basalis, the cytotrophoblast forms villi-like structures called the chorion frondosum.  Why A wrong: Chorion laeve is the smooth chorion formed elsewhere.  Why C wrong: Amnion is the inner membrane from ectoderm.  Why D wrong: The yolk sac is a separate structure."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The chorion frondosum forms which part of the placenta?","options":["Maternal side","Fetal side","Umbilical vein","Amniotic membrane"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the chorion frondosum forms the fetal side of the placenta.  Why A wrong: The maternal side is formed by decidua basalis.  Why C wrong: The umbilical vein is a cord vessel, not formed by chorion frondosum here.  Why D wrong: The amniotic membrane is formed by ectoderm."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"In areas away from the decidua basalis where the trophoblast remains smooth, the cytotrophoblast forms the:","options":["Chorion frondosum","Chorion laeve","Decidua capsularis","Syncytiotrophoblast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that everywhere else the trophoblast remains smooth and forms the chorion laeve.  Why A wrong: Chorion frondosum is the villous chorion near decidua basalis.  Why C wrong: Decidua capsularis is maternal tissue.  Why D wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is the multicellular hormone-producing layer."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The chorion laeve goes on to form the:","options":["Chorion (fetal membranes)","Maternal placenta","Umbilical cord","Decidua vera"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the chorion laeve forms the chorion (fetal membranes).  Why B wrong: Maternal placenta is decidua basalis.  Why C wrong: The umbilical cord forms from the connecting stalk.  Why D wrong: Decidua vera is maternal endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The inner cell mass gives rise to which type of cells?","options":["Trophoblast cells","Pluripotent stem cells","Decidual cells","Syncytial cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the inner cell mass gives rise to pluripotent stem cells.  Why A wrong: Trophoblast is the other lineage of the blastocyst.  Why C wrong: Decidual cells are maternal (endometrial) in origin.  Why D wrong: Syncytial cells are part of the trophoblast lineage."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The ectoderm is formed on which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 7","Day 8","Day 12","Day 14"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the ectoderm is formed by Day 7 after fertilization.  Why B wrong: Day 8 is when the endoderm forms.  Why C wrong: Day 12 is when two cavities appear.  Why D wrong: Day 14 is when the mesoderm forms."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The endoderm is formed on which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 7","Day 8","Day 12","Day 14"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the endoderm is formed by Day 8 after fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 7 is when the ectoderm forms.  Why C wrong: Day 12 is when two cavities appear.  Why D wrong: Day 14 is when the mesoderm forms."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The mesoderm (third germ layer) is formed on which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 7","Day 8","Day 12","Day 14"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the mesoderm (3rd layer) is formed on Day 14 after fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 7 is when the ectoderm forms.  Why B wrong: Day 8 is when the endoderm forms.  Why C wrong: Day 12 is when two cavities appear (cavity formation)."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The bilaminar disc is composed of which two germ layers?","options":["Ectoderm and mesoderm","Ectoderm and endoderm","Endoderm and mesoderm","Mesoderm and trophoblast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show the bilaminar disc forms after the ectoderm (Day 7) and endoderm (Day 8), i.e., two layers before the mesoderm appears.  Why A wrong: Mesoderm is the later third layer, not part of the bilaminar (two-layer) disc.  Why C wrong: Mesoderm is not part of the bilaminar disc.  Why D wrong: Trophoblast is not a germ layer of the disc."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"On which day after fertilization do two cavities appear (cavity formation)?","options":["Day 8","Day 10","Day 12","Day 14"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that on day 12 after fertilization, 2 cavities will appear (cavity formation).  Why A wrong: Day 8 is trophoblast/endoderm differentiation.  Why B wrong: Day 10 is when implantation is completed.  Why D wrong: Day 14 is when the mesoderm forms."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The entire fetus is formed from how many germ layers?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the entire fetus is formed by the 3 germ layers (ectoderm, endoderm, mesoderm).  Why A wrong: There are three germ layers, not two.  Why C wrong: Only three germ layers form the fetus.  Why D wrong: Only three germ layers form the fetus."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"At what gestational age does the embryo start to look like a human?","options":["6 weeks","8 weeks","12 weeks","16 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the embryo looks like a human at 8 weeks.  Why A wrong: The notes specify 8 weeks, not 6 weeks.  Why C wrong: The notes specify 8 weeks, not 12 weeks.  Why D wrong: The notes specify 8 weeks, not 16 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Posteriorly, the germ layers are all connected to the trophoblast, which goes on to form the:","options":["Future placenta","Future umbilical cord","Amniotic sac","Yolk sac"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that posteriorly the germ layers all connected to the trophoblast form the future umbilical cord (via the connecting stalk).  Why A wrong: The placenta forms from chorion frondosum and decidua basalis.  Why C wrong: The amniotic sac is formed from the amnion/ectoderm.  Why D wrong: The yolk sac is a distinct early structure."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Which structure forms the future umbilical cord?","options":["Connecting stalk","Yolk sac","Amnion","Decidua capsularis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the connecting stalk forms the umbilical cord.  Why B wrong: The yolk sac decreases in size and does not form the cord itself.  Why C wrong: The amnion forms the amniotic membrane.  Why D wrong: Decidua capsularis is maternal tissue."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Which of the following is a function of the cytotrophoblast?","options":["Forms the maternal side of the placenta","Forms the fetal side of the placenta","Produces hCG and HPL","Forms the decidua"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"forms fetal side of placenta\" as a function of the cytotrophoblast.  Why A wrong: The maternal side is formed by decidua basalis.  Why C wrong: hCG and HPL are produced by the syncytiotrophoblast.  Why D wrong: The decidua is maternal endometrium, not formed by cytotrophoblast."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Which fetal membrane is formed by the cytotrophoblast (a function of the cytotrophoblast)?","options":["Amnion","Chorion","Yolk sac","Allantois"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Form CHORION (fetal Membrane)\" as a function of the cytotrophoblast.  Why A wrong: The amnion is formed by ectoderm.  Why C wrong: The yolk sac is a distinct structure.  Why D wrong: The allantois is not the membrane named here."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"According to the notes, the cytotrophoblast is protective because it:","options":["Prevents pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH)","Prevents gestational diabetes","Prevents preterm labour","Prevents anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list that the cytotrophoblast prevents pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH).  Why B wrong: Gestational diabetes prevention is not a listed function.  Why C wrong: Preterm labour prevention is not a listed function.  Why D wrong: Anemia prevention is not a listed function."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"As development proceeds, the amniotic cavity:","options":["Decreases in size","Increases in size","Disappears completely","Turns into the yolk sac"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the amniotic cavity increases in size.  Why A wrong: It enlarges; it is the yolk sac that decreases.  Why C wrong: It does not disappear; it enlarges.  Why D wrong: It does not become the yolk sac."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"As development proceeds, the yolk sac:","options":["Increases in size","Decreases in size","Forms the placenta","Forms the amnion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the yolk sac will decrease in size.  Why A wrong: It is the amniotic cavity that increases; the yolk sac decreases.  Why C wrong: The placenta forms from chorion frondosum and decidua basalis.  Why D wrong: The amnion is formed from ectoderm."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The yolk sac is the first site of which fetal process?","options":["Fetal hematopoiesis","Fetal respiration","Fetal digestion","Fetal urine production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the yolk sac is the first site of fetal hematopoiesis.  Why B wrong: Respiration/gas exchange occurs via the placenta.  Why C wrong: Digestion is not the described yolk sac role here.  Why D wrong: Urine production is a later renal function."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Regarding the fate of the yolk sac, which of the following is correct?","options":["It remains in the connecting stalk and contributes to forming part of the gut","It becomes the placenta","It becomes the umbilical vein","It forms the amniotic fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the yolk sac remains in the connecting stalk and forms a part of the gut (in addition to being the first site of hematopoiesis).  Why B wrong: The placenta arises from chorion frondosum and decidua basalis.  Why C wrong: The umbilical vessels form from the connecting stalk vasculature, not directly named as the yolk sac fate.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid is not formed by the yolk sac."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The fetal membranes consist of an outer and an inner membrane. The OUTER membrane is the:","options":["Amnion","Chorion","Decidua","Yolk sac"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the outer fetal membrane is the chorion.  Why A wrong: The amnion is the inner membrane.  Why C wrong: The decidua is maternal tissue, not a fetal membrane.  Why D wrong: The yolk sac is a separate early structure."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The outer fetal membrane (chorion) is formed by the chorion laeve, which is derived from the:","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Ectoderm","Endoderm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the chorion is formed by the chorion laeve (cytotrophoblast).  Why A wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is the hormone-producing layer.  Why C wrong: Ectoderm forms the amnion.  Why D wrong: Endoderm is not the source of the chorion."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The INNER fetal membrane is the:","options":["Chorion","Amnion","Decidua vera","Chorion frondosum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the inner fetal membrane is the amnion.  Why A wrong: The chorion is the outer membrane.  Why C wrong: Decidua vera is maternal endometrium.  Why D wrong: Chorion frondosum is the fetal placental part."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The inner fetal membrane (amnion) is formed by the:","options":["Ectoderm","Endoderm","Mesoderm","Cytotrophoblast"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the amnion is formed by ectoderm.  Why B wrong: Endoderm does not form the amnion.  Why C wrong: Mesoderm is the third germ layer, not the amnion source here.  Why D wrong: Cytotrophoblast forms the chorion."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The maternal side of the placenta is formed by the:","options":["Chorion frondosum","Decidua basalis","Chorion laeve","Amnion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the placenta's maternal side = decidua basalis.  Why A wrong: Chorion frondosum forms the fetal side.  Why C wrong: Chorion laeve forms the smooth fetal membrane.  Why D wrong: Amnion is the inner fetal membrane."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The fetal side of the placenta is formed by the:","options":["Decidua basalis","Chorion frondosum (cytotrophoblast/trophoblast)","Decidua parietalis","Amnion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the placenta's fetal side = chorion frondosum (cytotrophoblast) trophoblast.  Why A wrong: Decidua basalis forms the maternal side.  Why C wrong: Decidua parietalis is the remaining maternal endometrium.  Why D wrong: The amnion is the inner fetal membrane, not the placental fetal side."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"In the connecting stalk formation diagram, which structures are shown connected to the trophoblast? (Select all that apply)","options":["Amnion","Ectoderm","Endoderm","Yolk sac","Myometrium"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The connecting stalk diagram labels the amnion, ectoderm, endoderm, and yolk sac all connected posteriorly to the trophoblast (forming the future umbilical cord).  Why E wrong: The myometrium is maternal uterine muscle and is not part of the connecting stalk structures shown."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"The chorion frondosum represents which pattern of the trophoblast/chorion?","options":["The smooth, non-villous chorion","The villous (bushy) chorion near the decidua basalis","The maternal endometrium","The inner amniotic membrane"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe chorion frondosum as the villi-like (bushy) structure formed near the decidua basalis, forming the fetal placenta.  Why A wrong: The smooth chorion is the chorion laeve.  Why C wrong: The maternal endometrium is the decidua.  Why D wrong: The inner membrane is the amnion."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Implantation, Decidua and its 3 types, Further development of blastocyst…","question":"Which layer of the trophoblast forms villi-like structures near the decidua basalis?","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Amnion","Mesoderm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cytotrophoblast forms villi-like structures near the decidua basalis (becoming chorion frondosum).  Why A wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is the multicellular hormone-producing layer.  Why C wrong: Amnion is the inner fetal membrane.  Why D wrong: Mesoderm is a germ layer, not a trophoblast layer."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Which germ layer gives rise to the oral mucosa lining, nasal epithelium lining, and anal lining?","options":["Endoderm","Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Trophoblast"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the germ-layer derivatives table, ectoderm forms the oral mucosa lining, nasal epithelium lining, and anal lining.  Why A wrong: Endoderm forms the visceral lining (bladder, urethra, GIT, respiratory lining), not the oral/nasal/anal linings.  Why B wrong: Mesoderm forms the cavities lining (peritoneal, pericardial, pleural).  Why D wrong: Trophoblast is an extraembryonic structure, not a primary germ layer for these linings."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The peritoneal, pericardial, and pleural cavity linings are derived from which germ layer?","options":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the cavities lining (peritoneal, pericardial, pleural) as mesodermal derivatives.  Why A wrong: Ectoderm forms oral/nasal/anal linings.  Why C wrong: Endoderm forms visceral linings (bladder, urethra, GIT, respiratory).  Why D wrong: Neural crest is not listed as forming these serous cavity linings."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The visceral lining of the bladder, urethra, GIT, and respiratory tract is derived from which germ layer?","options":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Amnion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists visceral lining (bladder, urethra, GIT, respiratory lining) under endoderm.  Why A wrong: Ectoderm forms oral/nasal/anal linings.  Why B wrong: Mesoderm forms serous cavity linings.  Why D wrong: Amnion is an extraembryonic membrane, not a germ layer."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Which of the following organs is a derivative of the ectoderm?","options":["Kidney","Heart","Central nervous system (CNS)","GI organs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists CNS, PNS, pituitary, and adrenal medulla as ectodermal organ derivatives.  Why A wrong: Kidney is mesodermal (mnemonic \"Kitna = Kidney\").  Why B wrong: Heart is mesodermal (mnemonic \"Hum = Heart\").  Why D wrong: GI organs are endodermal."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Which part of the adrenal (suprarenal) gland is an ectodermal derivative?","options":["Adrenal cortex","Adrenal medulla","Both cortex and medulla","Neither"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists adrenal medulla as an ectodermal organ derivative.  Why A wrong: Adrenal cortex is mesodermal (mnemonic \"CORONA = Adrenal cortex\").  Why C wrong: Only the medulla is ectodermal; the cortex is mesodermal.  Why D wrong: The medulla is clearly listed as ectodermal."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"In the mnemonic for mesodermal organ derivatives, \"CORONA\" stands for which structure?","options":["Adrenal cortex","Kidney","Heart","Reproductive organ"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mesoderm mnemonic states CORONA = Adrenal cortex.  Why B wrong: Kidney corresponds to \"Kitna.\"  Why C wrong: Heart corresponds to \"Hum.\"  Why D wrong: Reproductive organ corresponds to \"Rulayega.\""},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Which of the following organs is derived from the mesoderm?","options":["Pituitary","Heart","Peripheral nervous system (PNS)","Respiratory lining"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mesoderm mnemonic lists heart (\"Hum = Heart\"), along with adrenal cortex, suprarenal gland, kidney, and reproductive organ.  Why A wrong: Pituitary is ectodermal.  Why C wrong: PNS is ectodermal.  Why D wrong: Respiratory lining is endodermal."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"According to the mesoderm mnemonic, \"Rulayega\" refers to which organ?","options":["Kidney","Suprarenal gland","Reproductive organ","Heart"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic states \"Rulayega = Reproductive organ.\"  Why A wrong: Kidney = \"Kitna.\"  Why B wrong: Suprarenal gland = \"Sabko.\"  Why D wrong: Heart = \"Hum.\""},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Which germ layer gives rise to the GI organs?","options":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Ectoderm and mesoderm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"GI Organs\" as endodermal derivatives.  Why A wrong: Ectoderm forms neural and glandular structures like CNS, PNS, pituitary.  Why B wrong: Mesoderm forms heart, kidney, adrenal cortex, reproductive organs.  Why D wrong: GI organs are purely listed under endoderm."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The epidermis of the skin, nails, hairs, sweat glands, and sebaceous glands are appendages derived from which germ layer?","options":["Endoderm","Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Trophoblast"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists epidermis of skin, nails, hairs, sweat glands, and sebaceous glands as ectodermal appendages.  Why A wrong: Endoderm has no appendages listed (\"X\").  Why B wrong: Mesoderm forms bones, cartilage, and muscle.  Why D wrong: Trophoblast is not a germ layer."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Bones, cartilage, and muscle are appendage derivatives of which germ layer?","options":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Neuroectoderm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists bones, cartilage, and muscle as mesodermal appendages.  Why A wrong: Ectoderm forms epidermis, nails, hairs, sweat and sebaceous glands.  Why C wrong: Endoderm has no appendages (marked \"X\").  Why D wrong: Neuroectoderm is not the listed source for bone/cartilage/muscle."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"In the germ-layer derivatives table, which germ layer has NO appendage derivatives (marked \"X\")?","options":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","All three have appendages"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table marks the endoderm \"Appendages\" cell with an \"X,\" indicating no appendage derivatives.  Why A wrong: Ectoderm forms epidermis, nails, hairs, sweat/sebaceous glands.  Why B wrong: Mesoderm forms bones, cartilage, muscle.  Why D wrong: Endoderm's appendage cell is explicitly \"X.\""},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"According to the important concept, gestational age is equal to which of the following?","options":["Fetal age","Period of amenorrhea = period of pregnancy","Time since ovulation","Time since implantation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Gestational age = Period of Amenorrhea = Period of Pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Fetal age differs from gestational age (by 2 weeks).  Why C wrong: Time since ovulation corresponds to fetal/fertilization age, not gestational age.  Why D wrong: Implantation timing is not the definition of gestational age."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Pregnancy (gestational age) is calculated from which day?","options":["Day of ovulation","Day of fertilization","1st day of last menstrual period (LMP)","Day of missed period"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows gestational age is calculated from the 1st day of LMP.  Why A wrong: Ovulation (~14 day) is the start of fetal age, not gestational age.  Why B wrong: Fertilization occurs around day 14; fetal age begins there.  Why D wrong: Missed period occurs around 28 day, not the calculation start point."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Fetal age is counted from which event, occurring around day 14?","options":["LMP","Ovulation/fertilization","Missed period","Implantation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram marks 14 day as ovulation/fertilization, from which fetal age is counted.  Why A wrong: LMP (1st day) is the start of gestational age.  Why C wrong: Missed period is at ~28 day.  Why D wrong: Implantation is not the marked event for fetal age start."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"According to the diagram, the missed period typically occurs around which day?","options":["Day 14","Day 21","Day 28","Day 40"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram marks 28 day → Missed period.  Why A wrong: Day 14 is ovulation/fertilization.  Why B wrong: Day 21 is when cardiac activity is seen (after fertilization).  Why D wrong: Day 40 is not marked on this diagram."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"What is the difference between gestational age and fetal (fertilization) age?","options":["1 week","2 weeks","3 weeks","4 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the difference = 2 weeks (Fertilization age + 2 weeks = Gestational age).  Why A wrong: The stated difference is 2 weeks, not 1.  Why C wrong: 3 weeks is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 4 weeks is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The relationship \"Fertilization Age + 2 Weeks = ______\" completes to which term?","options":["Fetal age","Gestational age","Post-term age","Menstrual cycle length"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page explicitly states Fertilization Age + 2 Weeks = Gestational Age.  Why A wrong: Fetal age equals fertilization age, not fertilization age + 2 weeks.  Why C wrong: Post-term is unrelated to this formula.  Why D wrong: Menstrual cycle length is not derived from this formula."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Cardiac activity in the embryo is seen on which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 13","Day 17","Day 21","Day 28"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states cardiac activity is seen on day 21 after fertilization (or 5 weeks gestational age).  Why A wrong: Day 13 corresponds to primary villi formation.  Why B wrong: Day 17 corresponds to tertiary villi formation.  Why D wrong: Day 28 is the missed period, not cardiac activity onset."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Cardiac activity seen on day 21 after fertilization corresponds to which gestational age?","options":["3 weeks","4 weeks","5 weeks","6 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states day 21 after fertilization equals 5 weeks gestational age.  Why A wrong: 3 weeks is incorrect for cardiac activity.  Why B wrong: 4 weeks is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 6 weeks is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The human placenta can be described by three terms. Which term means \"disc-like in shape\"?","options":["Deciduate","Haemochorial","Discoid","Villous"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines \"Discoid\" as disc-like in shape.  Why A wrong: Deciduate means it has to shed after delivery.  Why B wrong: Haemochorial means it lies in contact with maternal blood.  Why D wrong: \"Villous\" is not one of the three descriptive terms listed."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The term \"deciduate\" used to describe the human placenta means:","options":["Disc-like in shape","It has to shed after delivery","It lies in contact with maternal blood","It is rich in blood vessels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines Deciduate = it has to shed after delivery.  Why A wrong: Disc-like refers to discoid.  Why C wrong: Contact with maternal blood refers to haemochorial.  Why D wrong: Vascularity is not the definition of deciduate."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The human placenta is termed \"haemochorial\" because:","options":["It is disc-shaped","It sheds after delivery","Chorion lies in contact with maternal blood","It contains fetal blood only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines Haemochorial = it lies in contact with maternal blood.  Why A wrong: Disc shape = discoid.  Why B wrong: Shedding after delivery = deciduate.  Why D wrong: Both maternal and fetal blood are present; the term refers to chorion-maternal blood contact."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The placenta has how many surfaces?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the placenta has 2 surfaces: placental (fetal) surface and maternal surface.  Why A wrong: There are two, not one.  Why C wrong: Three is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Four is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"What fraction of the placenta is formed by the fetal surface?","options":["1/5","2/5","3/5","4/5"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the fetal surface forms 4/5 of the placenta.  Why A wrong: 1/5 is the maternal surface.  Why B wrong: 2/5 is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 3/5 is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"What fraction of the placenta is formed by the maternal surface?","options":["1/5","2/5","3/5","4/5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the maternal surface forms 1/5 of the placenta.  Why B wrong: 2/5 is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 3/5 is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 4/5 is the fetal surface."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"In the placenta, the maternal surface and fetal surface occupy:","options":["Opposite walls of the uterus","The same area on the uterus wall","Different uterine cavities","The cervix only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the maternal surface and fetal surface occupy the same area on the uterus wall.  Why A wrong: They occupy the same area, not opposite walls.  Why C wrong: They are not in different cavities.  Why D wrong: The cervix is not where the surfaces are described to lie."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The maternal surface of the placenta is formed by which structure (also called the basal plate)?","options":["Chorion frondosum","Decidua basalis","Amnion","Decidua capsularis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the maternal surface is formed by decidua basalis (also called basal plate).  Why A wrong: Chorion frondosum forms the fetal surface (chorionic plate).  Why C wrong: Amnion is a separate fetal membrane.  Why D wrong: Decidua capsularis is not the maternal-surface former described here."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The fetal surface of the placenta is formed by which structure (also called the chorionic plate)?","options":["Decidua basalis","Chorion frondosum","Decidua vera","Yolk sac"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the fetal surface is formed by chorion frondosum (also called chorionic plate).  Why A wrong: Decidua basalis forms the maternal surface (basal plate).  Why C wrong: Decidua vera is not described as forming the fetal surface.  Why D wrong: The yolk sac is a separate structure."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"\"Basal plate\" is another name for which placental structure?","options":["Chorion frondosum","Decidua basalis","Amniotic membrane","Umbilical cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states decidua basalis is also called the basal plate.  Why A wrong: Chorion frondosum is the chorionic plate.  Why C wrong: Amniotic membrane is unrelated to the basal plate.  Why D wrong: Umbilical cord is not the basal plate."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"\"Chorionic plate\" is another name for which structure forming the placenta?","options":["Decidua basalis","Chorion frondosum","Chorion laeve","Decidua parietalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states chorion frondosum is also called the chorionic plate.  Why A wrong: Decidua basalis is the basal plate.  Why C wrong: Chorion laeve (smooth chorion) is not the chorionic plate.  Why D wrong: Decidua parietalis is unrelated here."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"In the diagram of placenta formation, the embryo is also referred to as the:","options":["Trophoblast","Inner cell mass","Syncytiotrophoblast","Blastocyst"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the embryo as the \"Inner cell mass.\"  Why A wrong: Trophoblast is the outer cell layer, not the embryo.  Why C wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is a trophoblast layer.  Why D wrong: Blastocyst is the whole early structure, not specifically the embryo/inner cell mass."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Primary villi are formed by which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 13","Day 15","Day 16","Day 17"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states primary villi formation occurs on Day 13.  Why B wrong: Day 15 is when maternal blood vessels open into the intervillous space.  Why C wrong: Day 16 is secondary villi.  Why D wrong: Day 17 is tertiary villi."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Primary villi (Day 13) are composed of which combination of layers?","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast + Cytotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast + Extraembryonic mesoderm","Syncytiotrophoblast + fetal capillaries","Extraembryonic mesoderm + fetal capillaries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Primary villi = Syncytiotrophoblast (Synctio T) + Cytotrophoblast (Cyto T) = D13.  Why B wrong: Adding extraembryonic mesoderm forms secondary villi.  Why C wrong: Fetal capillaries appear in tertiary villi.  Why D wrong: This combination is not the primary villi composition."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Secondary villi are formed by which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 13","Day 15","Day 16","Day 17"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states secondary villi are formed on Day 16 after fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 13 = primary villi.  Why B wrong: Day 15 = maternal blood vessels open into IVS.  Why D wrong: Day 17 = tertiary villi."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Secondary villi are composed of which combination of layers?","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast + Cytotrophoblast only","Syncytiotrophoblast + Cytotrophoblast + Extraembryonic mesoderm","Cytotrophoblast + fetal capillaries","Extraembryonic mesoderm only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Syncytio T + Cyto T + Extraembryonic mesoderm = 2° (secondary) villi.  Why A wrong: That combination is primary villi.  Why C wrong: Fetal capillaries define tertiary villi.  Why D wrong: Extraembryonic mesoderm alone does not form secondary villi."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"On which day (after fertilization) do maternal blood vessels open into the intervillous space (IVS), the new concept?","options":["Day 12","Day 13","Day 15","Day 17"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page (new concept) states maternal blood vessels open into the IVS on Day 15 after fertilization; Day 12 is the old concept.  Why A wrong: Day 12 is the old concept, not the current one asked.  Why B wrong: Day 13 is primary villi formation.  Why D wrong: Day 17 is tertiary villi."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"In the old concept, on which day were maternal blood vessels said to open into the intervillous space?","options":["Day 12","Day 15","Day 16","Day 17"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page notes Day 12 is the old concept (Day 15 is the new concept).  Why B wrong: Day 15 is the new concept.  Why C wrong: Day 16 is secondary villi.  Why D wrong: Day 17 is tertiary villi."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Uteroplacental circulation is established by which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 12","Day 13","Day 15","Day 17"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Important Point #7 states uteroplacental circulation is established when maternal blood vessels open into the IVS — Day 15 after fertilization (new concept).  Why A wrong: Day 12 is the old concept.  Why B wrong: Day 13 is primary villi.  Why D wrong: Day 17 relates to feto-placental circulation/tertiary villi."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Tertiary villi are formed by which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 13","Day 15","Day 16","Day 17"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The page states tertiary villi are formed by Day 17 after fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 13 = primary villi.  Why B wrong: Day 15 = maternal vessels open into IVS.  Why C wrong: Day 16 = secondary villi."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The appearance of which structure characterizes tertiary villi (Day 17)?","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Extraembryonic mesoderm","Fetal capillaries"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The tertiary villi figure labels fetal capillaries, which appear in tertiary villi on Day 17.  Why A wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is present from primary villi.  Why B wrong: Cytotrophoblast is present from primary villi.  Why C wrong: Extraembryonic mesoderm appears in secondary villi."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Fetal blood vessels open into the villi (feto-placental circulation established) by which day?","options":["Day 13","Day 15","Day 16","Day 17"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Important Point #8 states tertiary villi are formed, fetal blood vessels open in villi, and feto-placental circulation is established by Day 17.  Why A wrong: Day 13 = primary villi.  Why B wrong: Day 15 = uteroplacental circulation (maternal side).  Why C wrong: Day 16 = secondary villi."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"For feto-placental circulation being established, what is listed as the \"second best answer\" for the day range?","options":["Day 12–15","Day 15–16","Day 17–21","Day 21–28"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the best answer is Day 17 and the \"second best answer Day 17 – 21.\"  Why A wrong: Day 12–15 is not stated.  Why B wrong: Day 15–16 is not stated.  Why D wrong: Day 21–28 is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The space present in between the villi of the placenta is called the:","options":["Amniotic cavity","Intervillous space","Chorionic cavity","Yolk sac cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Important Point #2 states the space in between the villi is the intervillous space.  Why A wrong: The amniotic cavity surrounds the fetus, not between villi.  Why C wrong: Chorionic cavity is a different extraembryonic space.  Why D wrong: Yolk sac cavity is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"What is the volume of fetal blood within the villi of the placenta?","options":["150 mL","250 mL","350 mL","500 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Important Point #3 states volume of fetal blood in the villi = 350 mL.  Why A wrong: 150 mL is the maternal blood in the intervillous space.  Why B wrong: 250 mL is not stated.  Why D wrong: 500 mL is the total placental volume."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"What is the volume of maternal blood present in the intervillous space?","options":["100 mL","150 mL","350 mL","500 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Important Point #4 states maternal blood in the intervillous space = 150 mL.  Why A wrong: 100 mL is not stated.  Why C wrong: 350 mL is fetal blood in the villi.  Why D wrong: 500 mL is total placental volume."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"What is the total volume of the placenta (fetal blood in villi + maternal blood in intervillous space)?","options":["350 mL","400 mL","500 mL","650 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Important Point #4 states total placental volume = 350 mL + 150 mL = 500 mL.  Why A wrong: 350 mL is only the fetal blood in villi.  Why B wrong: 400 mL is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 650 mL is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"What is the approximate weight of the placenta?","options":["350 g","500 g","650 g","1000 g"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Important Point #4 states weight of placenta = 500 g.  Why A wrong: 350 g is not the stated weight.  Why C wrong: 650 g is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 1000 g is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"How many spiral arteries (maternal blood vessels) supply the intervillous space?","options":["60 (range 60–100)","120 (range 120–200)","200 (range 200–300)","350 (range 350–500)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Important Point #5 gives the number of spiral arteries in the intervillous space as 120/(120–200).  Why A wrong: 60 is below the stated value.  Why C wrong: The lower value is 120, not 200.  Why D wrong: 350 is unrelated (that is fetal blood volume)."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"In the placenta, maternal blood and fetal blood:","options":["Freely mix with each other","Are present but do NOT mix with each other","Are found only on the fetal side","Are separated only by amnion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Important Point #6 states both maternal and fetal blood are present but they do not mix with each other.  Why A wrong: They explicitly do not mix.  Why C wrong: Maternal blood is also present (in IVS).  Why D wrong: They are separated by the placental membrane/barrier layers, not the amnion alone."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The structures that separate maternal blood from fetal blood are together called the:","options":["Basal plate","Chorionic plate","Placental membrane / barrier","Decidua vera"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast, extraembryonic mesoderm, and endothelium of fetal capillaries together form the placental membrane/barriers.  Why A wrong: Basal plate is the maternal surface (decidua basalis).  Why B wrong: Chorionic plate is the fetal surface (chorion frondosum).  Why D wrong: Decidua vera is not this membrane."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"From outside to inside, which is the correct order of the layers separating maternal blood from fetal blood?","options":["Endothelium → Extraembryonic mesoderm → Cytotrophoblast → Syncytiotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast → Cytotrophoblast → Extraembryonic mesoderm → Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries","Cytotrophoblast → Syncytiotrophoblast → Endothelium → Extraembryonic mesoderm","Extraembryonic mesoderm → Syncytiotrophoblast → Cytotrophoblast → Endothelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Important Point #6 lists (outside to inside): 1. Syncytiotrophoblast, 2. Cytotrophoblast, 3. Extraembryonic mesoderm, 4. Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries.  Why A wrong: This is the reverse order.  Why C wrong: The order and layers are scrambled.  Why D wrong: Extraembryonic mesoderm is third, not first."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Which of the following is the OUTERMOST layer of the placental membrane separating maternal and fetal blood?","options":["Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries","Extraembryonic mesoderm","Cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: In the outside-to-inside list, syncytiotrophoblast is first (outermost, facing maternal blood).  Why A wrong: Endothelium of fetal capillaries is the innermost (4th).  Why B wrong: Extraembryonic mesoderm is third.  Why C wrong: Cytotrophoblast is second."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Which is the INNERMOST layer of the placental barrier (closest to fetal blood)?","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Extraembryonic mesoderm","Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The endothelium of fetal blood capillaries is listed 4th (innermost).  Why A wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is the outermost layer.  Why B wrong: Cytotrophoblast is second.  Why C wrong: Extraembryonic mesoderm is third."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Maternal blood in the intervillous space is rich in which of the following?","options":["CO2 and excretory material","O2 and glucose","Waste products only","Fetal red blood cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states maternal blood in the intervillous space is rich in O2 and glucose.  Why A wrong: CO2 and excretory material are in the fetal blood within the villi.  Why C wrong: Maternal blood carries nutrients and O2 to the fetus, not only waste.  Why D wrong: Fetal RBCs are in fetal blood, which does not mix with maternal blood."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Fetal blood within the villi contains predominantly which substances?","options":["O2 and glucose","CO2 and excretory material from the fetus","Glucose and iron","Maternal antibodies only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states fetal blood in the villi has CO2 and excretory material from the fetus.  Why A wrong: O2 and glucose are in maternal blood in the IVS.  Why C wrong: Glucose and iron are transported from mother to fetus, not the fetal blood's characteristic content.  Why D wrong: Antibodies are transferred but are not the described content of fetal villous blood."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"What does the placenta transfer FROM maternal blood TO the fetus?","options":["CO2 and excretory products","O2 and nutrients","Waste and urea only","Fetal antibodies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the placenta transfers O2 and nutrients from maternal blood to the fetus (and transfers CO2 and excretory products to the mother).  Why A wrong: CO2 and excretory products go from fetus to mother.  Why C wrong: Waste and urea are transferred from fetus to mother.  Why D wrong: The described direction is maternal-to-fetal O2/nutrients."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The placenta transfers which substances FROM the fetus TO the mother?","options":["O2 and glucose","CO2 and excretory products","Iron and calcium","Fat-soluble vitamins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the placenta transfers CO2 and excretory products to the mother.  Why A wrong: O2 and glucose go from mother to fetus.  Why C wrong: Iron and calcium are actively transported from mother to fetus.  Why D wrong: Fat-soluble vitamins go from mother to fetus (direct transport)."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"The main function of the placenta is described as the exchange of materials (O2, CO2, nutrients, excretory products) between:","options":["Amniotic fluid and fetal blood","Maternal blood and fetal blood","Uterine wall and cervix","Fetal lungs and maternal lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the function of the placenta is exchange of material (O2, CO2, nutrients, excretory products) between maternal blood and fetal blood.  Why A wrong: The exchange is between maternal and fetal blood, not amniotic fluid.  Why C wrong: Uterine wall and cervix are not the exchange partners.  Why D wrong: The fetal lungs are non-functional for gas exchange in utero; the placenta serves this role."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"By which transport mechanism does O2 cross the placenta?","options":["Facilitated diffusion","Active transport","Simple diffusion","Direct transport"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The transport table lists O2 under simple diffusion (along with CO2, waste product, Na+, Cl-).  Why A wrong: Facilitated diffusion (GLUT-1) is for glucose.  Why B wrong: Active transport is for Fe, Ca, proteins, water-soluble vitamins.  Why D wrong: Direct transport is for fat-soluble vitamins and lipids."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Which of the following substances crosses the placenta by SIMPLE diffusion? (Select all that apply)","options":["O2","CO2","Glucose","Na+","Cl-"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The transport table lists O2, CO2, waste product, Na+, and Cl- under simple diffusion.  Why C wrong: Glucose crosses by facilitated diffusion (GLUT-1), not simple diffusion.  Why A/B/D/E correct: O2, CO2, Na+, and Cl- are all listed under simple diffusion."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Glucose is transported across the placenta by which mechanism?","options":["Simple diffusion","Facilitated diffusion via GLUT-1","Active transport against a concentration gradient","Direct transport"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists glucose under facilitated diffusion (GLUT-1).  Why A wrong: Simple diffusion is for O2, CO2, waste, Na+, Cl-.  Why C wrong: Active transport is for Fe, Ca, proteins, water-soluble vitamins.  Why D wrong: Direct transport is for fat-soluble vitamins and lipids."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Which transporter mediates the facilitated diffusion of glucose across the placenta?","options":["SGLT-2","GLUT-1","GLUT-4","Na+/K+ ATPase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table specifies facilitated diffusion of glucose via GLUT-1.  Why A wrong: SGLT-2 is not mentioned; it is a renal transporter.  Why C wrong: GLUT-4 is insulin-dependent muscle/fat transporter, not stated here.  Why D wrong: Na+/K+ ATPase is an active-transport pump, not the glucose facilitator."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Which of the following substances is transported across the placenta by ACTIVE transport (against the concentration gradient)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Iron (Fe)","Calcium (Ca)","Proteins","Water-soluble vitamins","Lipids"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Fe, Ca, proteins, and water-soluble vitamins under active transport (against the concentration gradient).  Why E wrong: Lipids are moved by direct transport, not active transport.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are listed under active transport."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Active transport across the placenta occurs:","options":["Along the concentration gradient","Against the concentration gradient","Only through GLUT-1","Without energy expenditure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table heading specifies active transport occurs against the concentration gradient.  Why A wrong: Movement along the gradient describes diffusion.  Why C wrong: GLUT-1 mediates facilitated diffusion of glucose, not active transport.  Why D wrong: Active transport requires energy; it is not passive."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Water-soluble vitamins cross the placenta by which mechanism?","options":["Simple diffusion","Facilitated diffusion","Active transport","Direct transport"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists water-soluble vitamins under active transport.  Why A wrong: Simple diffusion is for O2, CO2, waste, Na+, Cl-.  Why B wrong: Facilitated diffusion is for glucose.  Why D wrong: Direct transport is for fat-soluble vitamins and lipids."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Fat-soluble vitamins and lipids are transported across the placenta by which mechanism?","options":["Simple diffusion","Facilitated diffusion via GLUT-1","Active transport","Direct transport"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists fat-soluble vitamins and lipids under direct transport.  Why A wrong: Simple diffusion is for gases and electrolytes.  Why B wrong: GLUT-1 facilitated diffusion is for glucose.  Why C wrong: Active transport is for Fe, Ca, proteins, water-soluble vitamins."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Iron (Fe) and calcium (Ca) are transported from mother to fetus across the placenta by:","options":["Simple diffusion","Active transport","Facilitated diffusion","Direct transport"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Fe and Ca under active transport (against the concentration gradient).  Why A wrong: Simple diffusion is for gases and electrolytes like Na+/Cl-.  Why C wrong: Facilitated diffusion is for glucose.  Why D wrong: Direct transport is for fat-soluble vitamins and lipids."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"At what rate is O2 supplied to the fetus via the placenta?","options":["4 mL/kg/min","6 mL/kg/min","8 mL/kg/min","10 mL/kg/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states O2 is supplied to the fetus at 8 mL/kg/min.  Why A wrong: 4 mL/kg/min is incorrect.  Why B wrong: 6 mL/kg/min is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 10 mL/kg/min is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Sodium (Na+) and chloride (Cl-) cross the placenta primarily by which mechanism?","options":["Active transport","Facilitated diffusion","Simple diffusion","Direct transport"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Na+ and Cl- under simple diffusion (with O2, CO2, and waste product).  Why A wrong: Active transport is for Fe, Ca, proteins, water-soluble vitamins.  Why B wrong: Facilitated diffusion is for glucose.  Why D wrong: Direct transport is for fat-soluble vitamins and lipids."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"Waste products from the fetus cross the placental barrier to the mother chiefly by which mechanism?","options":["Simple diffusion","Active transport","Facilitated diffusion","Direct transport"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"Waste product\" under simple diffusion.  Why B wrong: Active transport is for Fe, Ca, proteins, water-soluble vitamins.  Why C wrong: Facilitated diffusion is for glucose.  Why D wrong: Direct transport is for fat-soluble vitamins and lipids."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"In the placental developmental sequence, which structure is labeled as lying against the developing villi and is derived from the maternal side?","options":["Chorion frondosum","Decidua basalis","Amnion","Extraembryonic mesoderm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The villi-formation figures label decidua basalis on the maternal side, adjacent to the villi.  Why A wrong: Chorion frondosum is the fetal-side chorionic plate.  Why C wrong: Amnion is a separate fetal membrane.  Why D wrong: Extraembryonic mesoderm is a villous core layer, not the maternal decidua."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Derivatives of germ layers, gestational age vs fetal age…","question":"In the placenta formation diagram, the outer membrane surrounding the embryo/inner cell mass and bearing villi is the:","options":["Amnion","Chorion","Decidua vera","Yolk sac"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The placenta figure labels the outer villous membrane as the chorion, with chorion frondosum forming the villi region.  Why A wrong: Amnion is the inner fetal membrane, not labeled with the villi here.  Why C wrong: Decidua vera is maternal endometrial tissue, not the labeled membrane.  Why D wrong: Yolk sac is a separate early embryonic structure."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"What is the weight of the placenta at term?","options":["250 g","500 g","1000 g","3000 g"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the weight of placenta at term is 500 g.  Why A wrong: 250 g is too low and not the stated value.  Why C wrong: 1000 g is not the term placental weight.  Why D wrong: 3000 g (3 kg) is the approximate weight of the term fetus, not the placenta."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The ratio of the weight of the placenta to the weight of the fetus at term is approximately:","options":["1 : 2","1 : 4","1 : 6","1 : 10"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give 500 g : 3 kg (3000 g) = 1 : 6.  Why A wrong: 1:2 does not match the stated 500 g : 3000 g.  Why B wrong: 1:4 is incorrect for the given values.  Why D wrong: 1:10 overstates the difference."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"At what gestational age does the weight of the placenta equal the weight of the fetus?","options":["12 weeks","17 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state weight of placenta = weight of fetus at 17 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 12 weeks is when the placenta is completely formed, not when weights are equal.  Why C wrong: 24 weeks is not the stated age.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks marks the start of the 3rd trimester, not weight equality."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"By which gestational age does the placenta START forming?","options":["2 weeks","6 weeks","12 weeks","17 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the placenta starts forming by 6 weeks.  Why A wrong: 2 weeks is too early.  Why C wrong: 12 weeks is when placental formation is completed.  Why D wrong: 17 weeks is when placental weight equals fetal weight."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"By which gestational age is placental formation COMPLETED?","options":["6 weeks","10 weeks","12 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state placental formation is completed by 12 weeks.  Why A wrong: 6 weeks is when formation starts.  Why B wrong: 10 weeks is not the stated completion time.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks is well after completion."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The best time to perform ultrasonography (USG) to locate the placenta is during the:","options":["1st trimester","2nd trimester","3rd trimester","Immediately after conception"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that to locate the placenta, the best time to do USG is the 3rd trimester.  Why A wrong: The 1st trimester is too early for reliable placental localization.  Why B wrong: The 2nd trimester is not the stated best time.  Why D wrong: The placenta is not yet formed immediately after conception."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"A normal term pregnancy is considered to be how many weeks?","options":["37 weeks","38 weeks","40 weeks","42 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define pregnancy as 40 weeks.  Why A wrong: 37 weeks is early term but not the value stated.  Why B wrong: 38 weeks is not the stated duration.  Why D wrong: 42 weeks would be post-term."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"According to the notes, the 1st trimester of pregnancy extends up to:","options":["12 weeks","13 weeks + 6 days","14 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 1st trimester = 13 weeks + 6 days.  Why A wrong: 12 weeks is not the exact boundary given.  Why C wrong: 14 weeks marks the start of the 2nd trimester.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks is within the 2nd trimester."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"According to the notes, the 2nd trimester spans which range?","options":["13 weeks to 27 weeks","14 weeks to 27 weeks + 6 days","12 weeks to 24 weeks","14 weeks to 28 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 2nd trimester = 14 weeks → 27 weeks + 6 days.  Why A wrong: It starts at 14 weeks, not 13.  Why C wrong: 12–24 weeks does not match the stated range.  Why D wrong: It ends at 27 weeks + 6 days, and 28 weeks begins the 3rd trimester."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"According to the notes, the 3rd trimester spans which range?","options":["24 weeks – 40 weeks","27 weeks – 40 weeks","28 weeks – 40 weeks","30 weeks – 42 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 3rd trimester = 28 weeks – 40 weeks.  Why A wrong: It begins at 28 weeks, not 24.  Why B wrong: 27 weeks + 6 days ends the 2nd trimester; the 3rd begins at 28.  Why D wrong: 30–42 weeks does not match the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"A 22-year-old pregnant woman with a previous history of placenta previa is now 12 weeks pregnant and worried about recurrence. As a nurse, at what gestational age should you advise USG for localization of the placenta?","options":["Immediately","20 weeks","24 weeks","32 weeks"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The answer key states 32 weeks (3rd trimester), because the 3rd trimester is the best time to localize the placenta.  Why A wrong: At 12 weeks the placenta is not fully migrated/localized; immediate USG is not advised for final localization.  Why B wrong: 20 weeks is still 2nd trimester and may show apparent low-lying placenta that later migrates.  Why C wrong: 24 weeks is still too early for definitive localization."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The placenta contains phagocytic cells known as:","options":["Kupffer cells","Hofbauer cells","Langhans cells","Decidual cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state placenta has Hofbauer cells (phagocytic cells). (Source spelling \"Hoffbacur/Hoffbaeur cells\" corrected to the standard \"Hofbauer cells\".)  Why A wrong: Kupffer cells are phagocytic cells of the liver, not the placenta.  Why C wrong: Langhans cells relate to cytotrophoblast layer but are not the phagocytic cells named here.  Why D wrong: Decidual cells form the maternal decidua, not the placental phagocytes."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The functional characteristic of Hofbauer cells in the placenta is that they are:","options":["Hormone-secreting cells","Phagocytic cells","Oxygen-transporting cells","Contractile cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hofbauer cells are described as phagocytic cells.  Why A wrong: They are not primarily hormone-secreting.  Why C wrong: Oxygen transport is a function of blood, not these cells.  Why D wrong: They are not contractile."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"What is the diameter of the term placenta?","options":["5–10 cm","10–15 cm","15–20 cm","20–25 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the placental diameter is 15–20 cm.  Why A wrong: 5–10 cm is too small.  Why B wrong: 10–15 cm is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 20–25 cm exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The average thickness of the term placenta is:","options":["1.5 cm","2.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state average thickness = 2.5 cm.  Why A wrong: 1.5 cm is below the stated average.  Why C wrong: 4 cm is the maximum thickness, not the average.  Why D wrong: 5 cm exceeds even the maximum."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The maximum thickness of the term placenta is:","options":["2.5 cm","3 cm","4 cm","6 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maximum thickness = 4 cm.  Why A wrong: 2.5 cm is the average thickness.  Why B wrong: 3 cm is not the stated maximum.  Why D wrong: 6 cm exceeds the stated maximum."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The placenta is thickest at which region?","options":["Margins","Centre","Periphery","Cord insertion site only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the placenta is thickest in the centre.  Why A wrong: The margins are thinner than the centre.  Why C wrong: The periphery is thinner.  Why D wrong: The notes specify the centre, not specifically the cord insertion site."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"When placental thickness is greater than 4 cm, the condition is called:","options":["Placenta previa","Placentomegaly","Placenta accreta","Placental infarction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if placenta is >4 cm thick, it is called placentomegaly.  Why A wrong: Placenta previa refers to abnormal placental position over the cervical os.  Why C wrong: Placenta accreta refers to abnormal placental adherence to the myometrium.  Why D wrong: Placental infarction is tissue death from ischemia, not thickening."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"Which of the following conditions is associated with placentomegaly? (Select all that apply)","options":["Twin pregnancy","Diabetes","Rh negative pregnancy","Oligohydramnios","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list twin pregnancy, diabetes, and Rh negative pregnancy as causes of placentomegaly.  Why D wrong: Oligohydramnios is not listed as a cause of placentomegaly.  Why E wrong: Placenta previa refers to placental position, not placentomegaly."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"Placentomegaly (thick placenta) is characteristically seen in which type of blood-group incompatibility pregnancy?","options":["Rh positive pregnancy","Rh negative pregnancy","ABO compatible pregnancy","O positive pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Rh negative pregnancy as a cause of placentomegaly.  Why A wrong: Rh positive is not the specified condition.  Why C wrong: ABO compatible pregnancy is not listed.  Why D wrong: O positive is not the specified condition."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The maternal surface of the placenta is formed by the:","options":["Chorion frondosum","Decidua basalis","Amnion","Chorion laeve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maternal surface is formed by the decidua basalis.  Why A wrong: Chorion frondosum forms the fetal surface.  Why C wrong: The amnion is the innermost fetal membrane.  Why D wrong: Chorion laeve is the smooth, non-placental chorion."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The maternal surface of the placenta is also called the:","options":["Chorionic plate","Basal plate","Amniotic plate","Villous plate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maternal surface is also called the basal plate.  Why A wrong: The chorionic plate is the fetal surface.  Why C wrong: Amniotic plate is not a term used here.  Why D wrong: Villous plate is not the stated name."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"What fraction of the placenta is formed by the maternal surface?","options":["1/5th","2/5th","3/5th","4/5th"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maternal surface forms 1/5th of the placenta.  Why B wrong: 2/5th is not stated.  Why C wrong: 3/5th is not stated.  Why D wrong: 4/5th is the fraction formed by the fetal surface."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The colour of the maternal surface of the placenta is:","options":["Shiny grey","Dull red","Bluish white","Pale yellow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maternal surface is dull red in colour.  Why A wrong: Shiny grey describes the fetal surface.  Why C wrong: Bluish white is not stated.  Why D wrong: Pale yellow is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"Regarding the maternal surface of the placenta, which statement is TRUE about membranes and cord?","options":["Both membranes and cord are attached","Membranes and cord are NOT attached","Only the cord is attached","Only the membranes are attached"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that on the maternal surface, membranes and cord are not attached.  Why A wrong: They are not attached to the maternal surface (they attach to the fetal surface).  Why C wrong: The cord attaches to the fetal surface, not the maternal surface.  Why D wrong: The membranes attach to the fetal surface."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The polygonal areas (LOBES) on the maternal surface of the placenta are further divided into which functional units?","options":["Villi","Lobules / cotyledons","Septa","Trophoblasts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maternal surface has polygonal areas called LOBES, further divided into LOBULES/COTYLEDONS, called the functional unit of the placenta.  Why A wrong: Villi are components within cotyledons, not the polygonal lobe divisions named.  Why C wrong: Septa separate cotyledons but are not the functional units.  Why D wrong: Trophoblasts are cell layers, not lobular divisions."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The fetal surface of the placenta is formed by the:","options":["Decidua basalis","Chorion frondosum","Basal plate","Endometrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetal surface is formed by the chorion frondosum.  Why A wrong: The decidua basalis forms the maternal surface.  Why C wrong: The basal plate is another name for the maternal surface.  Why D wrong: The endometrium is the uterine lining, not the fetal surface."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The fetal surface of the placenta is also called the:","options":["Basal plate","Chorionic plate","Decidual plate","Villous stem"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetal surface is also called the chorionic plate.  Why A wrong: The basal plate is the maternal surface.  Why C wrong: Decidual plate is not the term used.  Why D wrong: Villous stem is not the name for the fetal surface."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"What fraction of the placenta is formed by the fetal surface?","options":["1/5th","2/5th","3/5th","4/5th"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetal surface forms 4/5th of the placenta.  Why A wrong: 1/5th is formed by the maternal surface.  Why B wrong: 2/5th is not stated.  Why C wrong: 3/5th is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The colour and appearance of the fetal surface of the placenta is:","options":["Dull red","Shiny, grey","Bluish black","Bright red and rough"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetal surface is shiny, grey in colour.  Why A wrong: Dull red describes the maternal surface.  Why C wrong: Bluish black is not stated.  Why D wrong: Bright red and rough describes the maternal surface, not the shiny fetal surface."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"On which placental surface is the umbilical cord attached, and where?","options":["Maternal surface, at the margin","Fetal surface, attached to its centre","Maternal surface, at the centre","Fetal surface, at the margin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetal surface has the umbilical cord attached to its centre, along with membranes.  Why A wrong: The cord does not attach to the maternal surface.  Why C wrong: Attachment is to the fetal surface, not the maternal surface.  Why D wrong: The cord is attached to the centre of the fetal surface, not the margin."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The functional unit of the placenta is the:","options":["Villus","Cotyledon","Decidua","Chorion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cotyledon is the functional unit of the placenta.  Why A wrong: A villus is a component within a cotyledon, not the whole functional unit.  Why C wrong: The decidua is maternal tissue.  Why D wrong: The chorion is a fetal membrane."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The total number of cotyledons in the placenta is:","options":["5–10","10–15","15–20","20–30"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the total number of cotyledons = 15–20.  Why A wrong: 5–10 is below the stated range.  Why B wrong: 10–15 is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 20–30 exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"A cotyledon is best defined as:","options":["That area of placenta which has a stem villus and all its branches","The maternal decidua only","The chorionic membrane surrounding the fetus","The umbilical cord with its vessels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define a cotyledon as that area of placenta which has a stem villus and all its branches.  Why B wrong: The decidua alone is not a cotyledon.  Why C wrong: The chorionic membrane is not a cotyledon.  Why D wrong: The umbilical cord is not a cotyledon."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the cotyledon (placental barrier) diagram, which is the OUTERMOST layer facing the maternal blood?","options":["Cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast","Extraembryonic mesoderm","Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The labelled diagram lists the syncytiotrophoblast as layer 1 (outermost, facing maternal blood in the intervillous space).  Why A wrong: Cytotrophoblast is layer 2, deep to the syncytiotrophoblast.  Why C wrong: Extraembryonic mesoderm is layer 3.  Why D wrong: Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries is the innermost, layer 4."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the placental barrier, which layer is labelled SECOND (deep to the syncytiotrophoblast)?","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Extraembryonic mesoderm","Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels cytotrophoblast as the second layer.  Why A wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is the first (outermost) layer.  Why C wrong: Extraembryonic mesoderm is the third layer.  Why D wrong: Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries is the fourth layer."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the placental barrier, which layer is labelled THIRD?","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Extraembryonic mesoderm","Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels extraembryonic mesoderm as the third layer.  Why A wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is the first layer.  Why B wrong: Cytotrophoblast is the second layer.  Why D wrong: Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries is the fourth layer."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the placental barrier, the INNERMOST (fourth) layer, in contact with fetal blood, is the:","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Extraembryonic mesoderm","Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the endothelium of fetal blood capillaries as the fourth (innermost) layer.  Why A wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is the outermost layer.  Why B wrong: Cytotrophoblast is the second layer.  Why C wrong: Extraembryonic mesoderm is the third layer."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"Arrange the four layers of the placental membrane from maternal (outer) to fetal (inner) side. Which is the CORRECT order?","options":["Cytotrophoblast → Syncytiotrophoblast → Endothelium → Extraembryonic mesoderm","Syncytiotrophoblast → Cytotrophoblast → Extraembryonic mesoderm → Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries","Endothelium → Mesoderm → Cytotrophoblast → Syncytiotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast → Extraembryonic mesoderm → Cytotrophoblast → Endothelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The numbered diagram gives the order 1) Syncytiotrophoblast, 2) Cytotrophoblast, 3) Extraembryonic mesoderm, 4) Endothelium of fetal blood capillaries.  Why A wrong: Syncytiotrophoblast is outermost, not cytotrophoblast.  Why C wrong: This is the reverse order.  Why D wrong: Mesoderm and cytotrophoblast are transposed."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the term placenta figure, the villi and intervillous spaces are anchored to which maternal structure?","options":["Chorion frondosum","Decidua basalis","Amnion","Umbilical cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The term placenta figure labels the decidua basalis as the maternal structure from which villi arise (maternal surface).  Why A wrong: Chorion frondosum forms the fetal surface.  Why C wrong: The amnion is a fetal membrane.  Why D wrong: The umbilical cord attaches to the fetal surface."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"Uteroplacental circulation (mother → placenta) is present in which space?","options":["Villi","Intervillous space","Umbilical vein","Chorionic plate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state uteroplacental circulation is present in the intervillous space.  Why A wrong: The villi contain fetoplacental circulation, not uteroplacental.  Why C wrong: The umbilical vein carries fetal blood, not maternal.  Why D wrong: The chorionic plate is a placental surface, not the circulation space."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The volume of maternal blood in the uteroplacental (intervillous) circulation is:","options":["100 mL","150 mL","350 mL","500 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the volume of blood in uteroplacental circulation = 150 mL.  Why A wrong: 100 mL is not the stated value.  Why C wrong: 350 mL is the volume of fetal blood in villi (fetoplacental).  Why D wrong: 500 mL is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"Uteroplacental circulation is established by which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 10","Day 15","Day 17","Day 21"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state uteroplacental circulation is established by Day 15 after fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 10 is not the stated day.  Why C wrong: Day 17 is when fetoplacental circulation is established.  Why D wrong: Day 21 marks the upper end of fetoplacental establishment (Day 17–21)."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The uteroplacental blood flow / intervillous space blood flow at term is:","options":["150–350 mL/min","400 mL/min","500–750 mL/min","1000 mL/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state uteroplacental blood flow at term / intervillous space blood flow at term = 500–750 mL/min.  Why A wrong: 150–350 mL/min is below the stated range.  Why B wrong: 400 mL/min is the fetoplacental blood flow at term.  Why D wrong: 1000 mL/min exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The single best value quoted for uteroplacental blood flow at term is:","options":["400 mL/min","500 mL/min","750 mL/min","900 mL/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specifically state uteroplacental blood flow at term = 750 mL/min.  Why A wrong: 400 mL/min is the fetoplacental blood flow at term.  Why B wrong: 500 mL/min is the lower bound of the range, not the stated term value.  Why D wrong: 900 mL/min is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the intervillous space, how many times is the maternal blood replaced in one minute?","options":["1–2 times","3–4 times","5–6 times","8–10 times"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in the intervillous space, blood is replaced 3–4 times in 1 minute.  Why A wrong: 1–2 times is below the stated value.  Why C wrong: 5–6 times is above the stated value.  Why D wrong: 8–10 times is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"According to the notes, the number of spiral (uterine) arteries opening into the intervillous space is BEST given as:","options":["60","120","200","300"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give 120 as the best answer (with 120–200 as the second-best answer) for spiral arteries opening into the intervillous space.  Why A wrong: 60 is not stated.  Why C wrong: 200 is the upper bound of the 2nd best answer, not the single best value.  Why D wrong: 300 exceeds the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The \"second-best\" answer given in the notes for the number of spiral arteries opening into the intervillous space is:","options":["60–100","100–120","120–200","200–300"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give 120–200 as the second-best answer.  Why A wrong: 60–100 is not stated.  Why B wrong: 100–120 is not stated.  Why D wrong: 200–300 is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"Fetoplacental circulation (fetus → placenta) is present in which structure?","options":["Intervillous space","Villi","Basal plate","Decidua basalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetoplacental circulation is present in the villi.  Why A wrong: The intervillous space contains maternal (uteroplacental) circulation.  Why C wrong: The basal plate is the maternal surface.  Why D wrong: The decidua basalis is maternal tissue."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The volume of fetal blood in the villi (fetoplacental circulation) is:","options":["150 mL","250 mL","350 mL","500 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the volume of fetal blood in villi = 350 mL.  Why A wrong: 150 mL is the maternal blood volume in uteroplacental circulation.  Why B wrong: 250 mL is not stated.  Why D wrong: 500 mL is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"Fetoplacental circulation is established by which day(s) after fertilization?","options":["Day 10","Day 15","Day 17 (Day 17–21)","Day 28"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetoplacental circulation is established by Day 17 (Day 17–21).  Why A wrong: Day 10 is not stated.  Why B wrong: Day 15 is when uteroplacental circulation is established.  Why D wrong: Day 28 is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The fetoplacental blood flow at term is:","options":["150 mL/min","400 mL/min","500 mL/min","750 mL/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetoplacental blood flow at term = 400 mL/min.  Why A wrong: 150 mL is a volume, not this flow value.  Why C wrong: 500 mL/min is the lower bound of uteroplacental flow.  Why D wrong: 750 mL/min is the uteroplacental blood flow at term."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the fetoplacental circulation diagram, secondary (2°) villi appear on which day?","options":["Day 15","Day 16","Day 17","Day 21"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels 2° villi as forming on Day 16 (D16).  Why A wrong: Day 15 is when uteroplacental circulation is established.  Why C wrong: Day 17 is when tertiary (3°) villi and fetal blood vessels appear.  Why D wrong: Day 21 is the upper end of fetoplacental establishment."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the fetoplacental circulation diagram, tertiary (3°) villi and fetal blood vessels appear on which day?","options":["Day 15","Day 16","Day 17","Day 21"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels 3° villi and fetal blood vessels as forming on Day 17 (D17).  Why A wrong: Day 15 is uteroplacental circulation establishment.  Why B wrong: Day 16 is when secondary villi appear.  Why D wrong: Day 21 is the upper limit of the establishment window."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The umbilical VEIN carries which type of blood, and in which direction?","options":["Deoxygenated blood, from fetus to placenta","Oxygenated blood, from placenta to fetus","Mixed blood, from placenta to fetus","Oxygenated blood, from fetus to placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood from placenta to fetus.  Why A wrong: That describes the umbilical artery.  Why C wrong: The umbilical vein carries oxygenated (not mixed) blood.  Why D wrong: Direction is placenta to fetus, not fetus to placenta."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The oxygen saturation of blood in the umbilical vein is:","options":["50–60%","70–80%","85–90%","95–100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state umbilical vein O2 saturation = 70–80%.  Why A wrong: 50–60% is the umbilical artery O2 saturation.  Why C wrong: 85–90% is not stated.  Why D wrong: 95–100% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The pressure within the umbilical vein is:","options":["10 mm Hg","20 mm Hg","40 mm Hg","60 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state umbilical vein pressure = 10 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 20 mm Hg is not stated.  Why C wrong: 40 mm Hg is not stated.  Why D wrong: 60 mm Hg is the umbilical artery pressure."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The adult remnant of the umbilical vein is the:","options":["Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum teres (round ligament of liver)","Ligamentum arteriosum","Median umbilical ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the umbilical vein remnant in the adult is the ligamentum teres.  Why A wrong: The medial umbilical ligament is the remnant of the umbilical artery.  Why C wrong: Ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus.  Why D wrong: The median umbilical ligament is the remnant of the urachus."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The umbilical ARTERY carries which type of blood, and in which direction?","options":["Oxygenated blood, from placenta to fetus","Deoxygenated blood, from fetus to placenta","Mixed blood, from fetus to placenta","Deoxygenated blood, from placenta to fetus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the umbilical artery brings back deoxygenated blood from fetus to placenta.  Why A wrong: That describes the umbilical vein.  Why C wrong: The umbilical artery carries deoxygenated (not mixed) blood.  Why D wrong: The direction is fetus to placenta, not placenta to fetus."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The oxygen saturation of blood in the umbilical artery is:","options":["50–60%","70–80%","85–90%","95–100%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state umbilical artery O2 saturation = 50–60%.  Why B wrong: 70–80% is the umbilical vein O2 saturation.  Why C wrong: 85–90% is not stated.  Why D wrong: 95–100% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The pressure within the umbilical artery is:","options":["10 mm Hg","30 mm Hg","60 mm Hg","90 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state umbilical artery pressure = 60 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 10 mm Hg is the umbilical vein pressure.  Why B wrong: 30 mm Hg is not stated.  Why D wrong: 90 mm Hg is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The adult remnant of the umbilical artery is the:","options":["Ligamentum teres","Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum venosum","Median umbilical ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the umbilical artery remnant in the adult is the medial umbilical ligament.  Why A wrong: The ligamentum teres is the remnant of the umbilical vein.  Why C wrong: Ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus.  Why D wrong: The median umbilical ligament is the remnant of the urachus."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"Which immunoglobulin is able to cross the placenta?","options":["IgA","IgG","IgM","IgE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the Ig which can cross the placenta is IgG.  Why A wrong: IgA is found in breast milk/colostrum, not the placenta-crossing antibody.  Why C wrong: IgM does not cross the placenta.  Why D wrong: IgE does not cross the placenta."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"Which immunoglobulin is predominantly present in breast milk / colostrum?","options":["IgG","IgA","IgM","IgD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the Ig present in breast milk/colostrum is IgA.  Why A wrong: IgG is the placenta-crossing antibody.  Why C wrong: IgM is not the milk/colostrum antibody named.  Why D wrong: IgD is not the milk/colostrum antibody named."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH), the initial change that begins the pathway leading to IUGR is:","options":["Increased maternal blood flow","Decreased maternal blood flow","Increased fetal growth","Increased intervillous space volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows PIH → maternal blood flow decreases, starting the cascade.  Why A wrong: Maternal blood flow decreases, not increases, in PIH.  Why C wrong: Fetal growth is reduced, not increased.  Why D wrong: Blood flow in the intervillous space decreases, not increases."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the PIH pathway, decreased maternal blood flow in the intervillous space leads directly to:","options":["More nutrients reaching the fetus","Less nutrients reaching the fetus","Increased fetal growth","Placentomegaly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows decreased intervillous blood flow → less nutrients reach the fetus.  Why A wrong: Fewer, not more, nutrients reach the fetus.  Why C wrong: Fetal growth decreases as a result.  Why D wrong: Placentomegaly is not part of this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The reduced uteroplacental blood flow in PIH ultimately results in which fetal condition?","options":["Macrosomia","Intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR)","Polyhydramnios","Placenta previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart ends with less fetal growth → IUGR (Intrauterine Growth Restriction).  Why A wrong: Macrosomia (large fetus) is the opposite of the outcome shown.  Why C wrong: Polyhydramnios is not part of this cascade.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa relates to placental position, not this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The overall placental problem depicted in the PIH → IUGR flow chart is best termed:","options":["Uteroplacental insufficiency","Placental abruption","Placental infarction","Vasa previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed label in the flow chart names the condition as uteroplacental insufficiency (source spelling \"Uteroplanetal Insufficiently\").  Why B wrong: Placental abruption is premature separation of the placenta, not shown here.  Why C wrong: Placental infarction is not the labelled concept.  Why D wrong: Vasa previa is fetal vessels crossing the os, unrelated to this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"The important concept figure titled \"Nutrient transport between mother and fetal blood\" describes exchange occurring across the:","options":["Umbilical cord only","Placental membrane / barrier","Amniotic membrane","Cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nutrient transport between mother and fetal blood occurs across the placental barrier (the four-layer membrane between maternal intervillous blood and fetal capillaries).  Why A wrong: The cord transports blood but is not the exchange membrane.  Why C wrong: The amniotic membrane is not the site of maternal-fetal nutrient exchange.  Why D wrong: The cervix has no role in nutrient exchange."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the term placenta gross specimen, which surface appears shiny/greyish with the umbilical cord and membranes attached?","options":["Maternal surface (Figure B)","Fetal surface (Figure A)","Basal plate","Decidua basalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Figure A is labelled the fetal surface, which is shiny, grey with the cord (circled) and membranes attached.  Why A wrong: The maternal surface (Figure B) is dull red with lobes/cotyledons, no cord attachment.  Why C wrong: The basal plate is another name for the maternal surface.  Why D wrong: The decidua basalis forms the maternal surface."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Placenta at term (weight, weight:fetus ratio, formation timeline)…","question":"In the term placenta gross specimen, the dull red surface showing the divided lobes/cotyledons is the:","options":["Fetal surface (Figure A)","Maternal surface (Figure B)","Chorionic plate","Amnion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Figure B is labelled the maternal surface, which is dull red and shows the cotyledons/lobes.  Why A wrong: Figure A is the shiny fetal surface with the cord.  Why C wrong: The chorionic plate is the fetal surface.  Why D wrong: The amnion is a membrane, not the maternal surface."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The maternal surface of the placenta is formed by which structure?","options":["Chorion frondosum","Decidua basalis","Decidua capsularis","Amnion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The maternal surface of the placenta is formed by the decidua basalis.  Why A wrong: Chorion frondosum forms the fetal surface, not the maternal surface.  Why C wrong: Decidua capsularis overlies the conceptus and does not form the placental maternal surface.  Why D wrong: Amnion is a fetal membrane, not the maternal surface."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The maternal surface of the placenta is also called:","options":["Chorionic plate","Basal plate","Amniotic plate","Decidual capsule"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The maternal surface is also known as the basal plate.  Why A wrong: Chorionic plate is the name for the fetal surface.  Why B is correct as above; C and D are invented terms.  Why C wrong: \"Amniotic plate\" is not a recognized placental term.  Why D wrong: Decidual capsule is not the maternal surface designation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The maternal surface forms what fraction of the placenta?","options":["1/5th","2/5th","3/5th","4/5th"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The maternal surface forms 1/5th of the placenta.  Why B wrong: 2/5th is not correct per the source.  Why C wrong: 3/5th is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 4/5th is the fraction formed by the fetal surface, not the maternal surface."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"What is the colour of the maternal surface of the placenta?","options":["Shiny grey","Dull red","Pale white","Bright yellow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The maternal surface is dull red in colour.  Why A wrong: Shiny grey describes the fetal surface.  Why C wrong: Pale white is not the described colour.  Why D wrong: Bright yellow is not the described colour."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Which statement about the maternal surface of the placenta is TRUE regarding the membranes and cord?","options":["Both membranes and cord are attached to it","Only the cord is attached to it","Membranes and cord are NOT attached to it","Only the membranes are attached to it"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: On the maternal surface, membranes and cord are not attached.  Why A wrong: Both being attached describes the fetal surface.  Why B wrong: The cord is attached to the fetal surface, not the maternal.  Why D wrong: Membranes are attached to the fetal surface, not the maternal."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The polygonal areas (LOBES) on the maternal surface of the placenta are further divided into structures called:","options":["Villi","Lobules / cotyledons","Septa","Decidual cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The polygonal LOBES are further divided into lobules/cotyledons, which are the functional units of the placenta.  Why A wrong: Villi are components within cotyledons, not the direct division of the lobes described here.  Why C wrong: Septa separate cotyledons but are not the divisions themselves.  Why D wrong: Decidual cells are maternal tissue cells, not the lobular divisions."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The fetal surface of the placenta is formed by:","options":["Decidua basalis","Chorion frondosum","Decidua vera","Basal plate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fetal surface is formed by the chorion frondosum.  Why A wrong: Decidua basalis forms the maternal surface.  Why C wrong: Decidua vera (parietalis) does not form the fetal surface.  Why D wrong: Basal plate is the name for the maternal surface."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The fetal surface of the placenta is also called:","options":["Basal plate","Chorionic plate","Decidual plate","Villous plate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fetal surface is also called the chorionic plate.  Why A wrong: Basal plate is the maternal surface.  Why C wrong: \"Decidual plate\" is not the fetal surface term.  Why D wrong: \"Villous plate\" is not a recognized designation for the fetal surface."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The fetal surface forms what fraction of the placenta?","options":["1/5th","2/5th","3/5th","4/5th"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The fetal surface forms 4/5th of the placenta.  Why A wrong: 1/5th is the fraction of the maternal surface.  Why B wrong: 2/5th is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 3/5th is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The fetal surface of the placenta is described as:","options":["Dull red","Shiny, grey in colour","Rough and lobulated","Pale and cotyledonous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fetal surface is shiny and grey in colour.  Why A wrong: Dull red describes the maternal surface.  Why C wrong: The lobulated appearance is characteristic of the maternal surface.  Why D wrong: Cotyledons are on the maternal surface."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"On the fetal surface of the placenta, the umbilical cord is attached at the:","options":["Periphery / margin","Centre","Lower pole","Basal plate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On the fetal surface, the umbilical cord is attached to its centre, along with the membranes.  Why A wrong: Marginal/peripheral attachment is an abnormal (battledore) insertion, not the normal description here.  Why C wrong: \"Lower pole\" is not the described attachment site.  Why D wrong: The basal plate is the maternal surface."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The functional unit of the placenta is the:","options":["Villus","Cotyledon","Decidua basalis","Chorion laeve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cotyledon is the functional unit of the placenta.  Why A wrong: A single villus is a component, not the functional unit.  Why C wrong: Decidua basalis is maternal tissue forming the maternal surface.  Why D wrong: Chorion laeve is the smooth, non-villous chorion."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The total number of cotyledons in a placenta is:","options":["5 – 10","10 – 15","15 – 20","20 – 25"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The total number of cotyledons is 15–20.  Why A wrong: 5–10 is below the stated range.  Why B wrong: 10–15 is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 20–25 exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"According to the new concept, a cotyledon is defined as that area of the placenta which contains:","options":["3 or 4 villi","One stem villus and all its branches","Only the decidua basalis","The chorionic plate alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the new concept, a cotyledon is that area of the placenta which has one stem villus and all its branches.  Why A wrong: \"3 or 4 villi\" reflects the old concept, not the new definition.  Why C wrong: Decidua basalis is maternal tissue, not the cotyledon definition.  Why D wrong: The chorionic plate is the fetal surface layer, not a cotyledon."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"In the OLD concept, a cotyledon was considered to be the area of placenta having approximately how many villi?","options":["1 villus","3 or 4 villi","10 villi","15 villi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The old concept defined a cotyledon as the area of placenta having 3 or 4 villi.  Why A wrong: One stem villus with branches is the new concept, not the old.  Why C wrong: 10 villi is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: 15 villi is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"In the cotyledon diagram, \"IVS\" refers to which space?","options":["Intervertebral space","Intervillous space","Intravascular space","Interstitial space"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IVS stands for the intervillous space, the space between the villi where maternal blood circulates.  Why A wrong: Intervertebral space is a spinal term, unrelated.  Why C wrong: Intravascular space is a general vascular term, not the IVS abbreviation used here.  Why D wrong: Interstitial space is not what IVS denotes in placental anatomy."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Uteroplacental circulation refers to the flow of blood from:","options":["Fetus to placenta","Mother to placenta","Placenta to fetus","Fetus to mother"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Uteroplacental circulation is the mother → placenta circulation.  Why A wrong: Fetus to placenta describes fetoplacental circulation.  Why C wrong: Placenta to fetus describes umbilical vein flow, not uteroplacental circulation.  Why D wrong: Fetus to mother is not the definition of uteroplacental circulation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Uteroplacental circulation is present in which space?","options":["Villi","Intervillous space","Amniotic cavity","Umbilical vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Uteroplacental circulation is present in the intervillous space.  Why A wrong: The villi contain the fetoplacental (fetal) circulation.  Why C wrong: The amniotic cavity contains amniotic fluid, not maternal placental circulation.  Why D wrong: The umbilical vein is part of the fetal circulation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The volume of blood in the uteroplacental (intervillous) circulation is:","options":["100 mL","150 mL","350 mL","500 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The volume of blood in uteroplacental circulation is 150 mL.  Why A wrong: 100 mL is not the stated value.  Why C wrong: 350 mL is the volume of fetal blood in villi (fetoplacental).  Why D wrong: 500 mL is part of the blood-flow rate range, not the volume."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Uteroplacental circulation is established by which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 5","Day 10","Day 15","Day 21"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Uteroplacental circulation is established by Day 15 after fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 5 is too early per the source.  Why B wrong: Day 10 is not the stated day.  Why D wrong: Day 21 relates to the upper limit for fetoplacental circulation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The uteroplacental / intervillous space blood flow at term is approximately:","options":["150 – 350 mL/min","400 mL/min","500 – 750 mL/min","1000 – 1200 mL/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Uteroplacental / intervillous space blood flow at term is 500–750 mL/min.  Why A wrong: 150–350 mL/min is below the stated range.  Why B wrong: 400 mL/min is the fetoplacental blood flow at term.  Why D wrong: 1000–1200 mL/min exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The uterine blood flow at term is approximately:","options":["350 mL/min","500 mL/min","750 mL/min","1000 mL/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Uterine blood flow at term is about 750 mL/min.  Why A wrong: 350 mL/min is not the stated uterine blood flow value.  Why B wrong: 500 mL/min is the lower end of the intervillous flow range, not uterine flow at term.  Why D wrong: 1000 mL/min exceeds the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"In the intervillous space, how many times is the blood replaced in 1 minute?","options":["1 – 2 times","3 – 4 times","5 – 6 times","8 – 10 times"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the intervillous space, blood is replaced 3–4 times in 1 minute.  Why A wrong: 1–2 times is below the stated value.  Why C wrong: 5–6 times exceeds the stated value.  Why D wrong: 8–10 times exceeds the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"How many spiral (uterine) arteries open into the intervillous space (best answer)?","options":["60","100","120","200"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The best answer for the number of spiral arteries opening into the intervillous space is 120 (with 120–200 as the second-best answer).  Why A wrong: 60 is not the stated value.  Why B wrong: 100 is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 200 is the upper limit of the \"second best answer\" range (120–200), not the single best answer."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The \"second best answer\" range given for the number of spiral arteries opening into the intervillous space is:","options":["60 – 100","100 – 120","120 – 200","200 – 300"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The second best answer is 120 to 200 spiral arteries.  Why A wrong: 60–100 is not stated.  Why B wrong: 100–120 is not the stated second-best range.  Why D wrong: 200–300 exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Fetoplacental circulation refers to the flow of blood from:","options":["Mother to placenta","Fetus to placenta","Placenta to mother","Uterus to placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetoplacental circulation is the fetus → placenta circulation.  Why A wrong: Mother to placenta describes uteroplacental circulation.  Why C wrong: Placenta to mother is not the definition.  Why D wrong: Uterus to placenta is part of the maternal (uteroplacental) side."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Fetoplacental circulation is present in which structure?","options":["Intervillous space","Villi","Basal plate","Decidua basalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetoplacental circulation is present in the villi.  Why A wrong: The intervillous space holds the maternal (uteroplacental) circulation.  Why C wrong: The basal plate is the maternal surface.  Why D wrong: Decidua basalis is maternal tissue."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The volume of fetal blood in the villi is:","options":["150 mL","250 mL","350 mL","500 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The volume of fetal blood in the villi is 350 mL.  Why A wrong: 150 mL is the volume of blood in the uteroplacental circulation.  Why B wrong: 250 mL is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 500 mL is not the stated fetal-blood volume."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Fetoplacental circulation is established by which day (range) after fertilization?","options":["Day 10 (Day 8–12)","Day 15 (Day 12–15)","Day 17 (Day 17–21)","Day 25 (Day 21–28)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fetoplacental circulation is established by Day 17 (range Day 17–21).  Why A wrong: Day 10 is too early.  Why B wrong: Day 15 is when uteroplacental circulation is established, not fetoplacental.  Why D wrong: Day 25 is beyond the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The fetoplacental blood flow at term is approximately:","options":["150 mL/min","400 mL/min","500 – 750 mL/min","750 mL/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetoplacental blood flow at term is 400 mL/min.  Why A wrong: 150 mL is the uteroplacental blood volume, not the flow.  Why C wrong: 500–750 mL/min is the uteroplacental/intervillous flow at term.  Why D wrong: 750 mL/min is the uterine blood flow at term."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The umbilical vein carries which type of blood, and in which direction?","options":["Deoxygenated blood, from fetus to placenta","Oxygenated blood, from placenta to fetus","Deoxygenated blood, from placenta to fetus","Oxygenated blood, from fetus to placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood from the placenta to the fetus.  Why A wrong: Carrying deoxygenated blood from fetus to placenta describes the umbilical artery.  Why C wrong: The umbilical vein carries oxygenated, not deoxygenated, blood.  Why D wrong: The umbilical vein flows toward the fetus, not toward the placenta."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The oxygen saturation of blood in the umbilical vein is:","options":["50 – 60%","60 – 70%","70 – 80%","90 – 100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The umbilical vein O2 saturation is 70–80%.  Why A wrong: 50–60% is the umbilical artery saturation.  Why B wrong: 60–70% is not the stated range for the vein.  Why D wrong: 90–100% exceeds the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The blood pressure within the umbilical vein is:","options":["10 mm Hg","30 mm Hg","60 mm Hg","90 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pressure in the umbilical vein is 10 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 30 mm Hg is not stated.  Why C wrong: 60 mm Hg is the umbilical artery pressure.  Why D wrong: 90 mm Hg is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"In the adult, the remnant of the umbilical vein is the:","options":["Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum teres (round ligament of liver)","Ligamentum venosum","Median umbilical ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The umbilical vein remnant in the adult is the ligamentum teres.  Why A wrong: Medial umbilical ligament is the remnant of the umbilical artery.  Why C wrong: Ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, not the umbilical vein per this source.  Why D wrong: Median umbilical ligament is the remnant of the urachus."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The umbilical artery carries which type of blood, and in which direction?","options":["Oxygenated blood, from placenta to fetus","Deoxygenated blood, from fetus to placenta","Oxygenated blood, from fetus to placenta","Deoxygenated blood, from placenta to fetus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The umbilical artery brings deoxygenated blood back from the fetus to the placenta.  Why A wrong: Oxygenated blood from placenta to fetus describes the umbilical vein.  Why C wrong: The artery carries deoxygenated, not oxygenated, blood.  Why D wrong: The artery flows from fetus to placenta, not placenta to fetus."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The oxygen saturation of blood in the umbilical artery is:","options":["50 – 60%","70 – 80%","80 – 90%","95 – 100%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The umbilical artery O2 saturation is 50–60%.  Why B wrong: 70–80% is the umbilical vein saturation.  Why C wrong: 80–90% exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 95–100% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The blood pressure within the umbilical artery is:","options":["10 mm Hg","30 mm Hg","60 mm Hg","120 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The pressure in the umbilical artery is 60 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 10 mm Hg is the umbilical vein pressure.  Why B wrong: 30 mm Hg is not stated.  Why D wrong: 120 mm Hg is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"In the adult, the remnant of the umbilical artery is the:","options":["Ligamentum teres","Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum venosum","Falciform ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The umbilical artery remnant is the medial umbilical ligament.  Why A wrong: Ligamentum teres is the umbilical vein remnant.  Why C wrong: Ligamentum venosum is the ductus venosus remnant.  Why D wrong: The falciform ligament is not the umbilical artery remnant."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Which immunoglobulin (Ig) is able to cross the placenta?","options":["IgA","IgM","IgG","IgE"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: IgG is the immunoglobulin that can cross the placenta, giving passive immunity to the fetus.  Why A wrong: IgA is present in breast milk/colostrum but does not cross the placenta.  Why B wrong: IgM is too large to cross the placenta.  Why D wrong: IgE does not cross the placenta."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Which immunoglobulin is present in breast milk / colostrum?","options":["IgG","IgA","IgM","IgD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IgA is the immunoglobulin present in breast milk/colostrum.  Why A wrong: IgG crosses the placenta; it is not the main immunoglobulin of colostrum.  Why C wrong: IgM is not the predominant immunoglobulin in breast milk.  Why D wrong: IgD is not associated with breast milk/colostrum here."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"In the nutrient transport concept between mother and fetus, the mother's blood is described as rich in:","options":["Waste products","Nutrients","Carbon dioxide only","Deoxygenated cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mother's blood is rich in nutrients, which are transported to the fetal blood.  Why A wrong: Waste products flow from fetus to mother, not the defining feature of maternal blood.  Why C wrong: Carbon dioxide is a waste gas carried away from the fetus, not what maternal blood is rich in.  Why D wrong: \"Deoxygenated cells\" does not describe nutrient-rich maternal blood."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"In pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH), what is the FIRST consequence in the pathway leading to fetal growth restriction?","options":["Less nutrients reach the fetus","Maternal blood flow decreases","Intrauterine growth restriction","Less growth of fetus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the PIH pathway, maternal blood flow decreases first.  Why A wrong: Less nutrients reaching the fetus is a later step, following reduced intervillous flow.  Why C wrong: IUGR is the final outcome of the pathway.  Why D wrong: Less growth of fetus is a late step just before IUGR."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"In the PIH pathway, after maternal blood flow decreases, the next step is that blood flow decreases in the:","options":["Umbilical artery","Intervillous space","Amniotic cavity","Renal arteries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal blood flow in the intervillous space will also decrease.  Why A wrong: The umbilical artery is fetal; the source specifies intervillous space.  Why C wrong: The amniotic cavity is not the described site.  Why D wrong: Renal arteries are not part of this placental pathway."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The final outcome of the pregnancy-induced hypertension (uteroplacental insufficiency) pathway is:","options":["Placenta previa","Intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR)","Placentomegaly","Postpartum haemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The endpoint of the PIH/uteroplacental insufficiency pathway is IUGR (intrauterine growth restriction).  Why A wrong: Placenta previa is a location anomaly, unrelated to this pathway.  Why C wrong: Placentomegaly is enlargement, the opposite outcome.  Why D wrong: PPH is a bleeding complication, not the endpoint of this growth pathway."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The reduced placental blood flow condition depicted in the PIH pathway is termed:","options":["Uteroplacental insufficiency","Placental abruption","Placenta accreta","Chorioamnionitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The condition of decreased maternal placental blood flow is called uteroplacental insufficiency.  Why B wrong: Placental abruption is premature separation, not the described insufficiency.  Why C wrong: Placenta accreta is abnormal adherence, unrelated here.  Why D wrong: Chorioamnionitis is an infection, not this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The abbreviation \"IUGR\" stands for:","options":["Intrauterine growth restriction","Intrauterine gestational rupture","Increased uterine growth rate","Intrauterine glandular reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IUGR stands for intrauterine growth restriction.  Why B wrong: \"Gestational rupture\" is not what IUGR denotes.  Why C wrong: \"Increased uterine growth rate\" is incorrect.  Why D wrong: \"Glandular reaction\" is not the meaning."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The normal thickness of the placenta is:","options":["1.5 cm","2.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal thickness of the placenta is 2.5 cm.  Why A wrong: 1.5 cm is below the stated normal.  Why C wrong: 4 cm is the maximum normal thickness, not the typical normal value.  Why D wrong: 5 cm exceeds the maximum and would indicate placentomegaly."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"The maximum normal thickness of the placenta is:","options":["2.5 cm","3 cm","4 cm","6 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The maximum normal placental thickness is 4 cm.  Why A wrong: 2.5 cm is the typical normal thickness, not the maximum.  Why B wrong: 3 cm is not the stated maximum.  Why D wrong: 6 cm exceeds normal limits and indicates placentomegaly."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Placentomegaly is diagnosed when the placental thickness is more than:","options":["2.5 cm","3 cm","4 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If placental thickness is more than 4 cm, it is called placentomegaly.  Why A wrong: 2.5 cm is the normal thickness.  Why B wrong: 3 cm is still within normal limits.  Why D wrong: 5 cm is above the threshold but the defining cut-off is \">4 cm,\" making C the precise answer."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Placentomegaly (enlarged placenta) is seen in all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Twin pregnancy","Diabetic mother","Rh negative pregnancy","Pregnancy-induced hypertension"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Pregnancy-induced hypertension causes a SMALL placenta, not placentomegaly; therefore it is the exception.  Why A wrong: Twin pregnancy is a listed cause of placentomegaly.  Why B wrong: Diabetic mother is a listed cause of placentomegaly.  Why C wrong: Rh negative pregnancy is a listed cause of placentomegaly."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"A small-sized placenta is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":["Twin pregnancy","Diabetic mother","Pregnancy-induced hypertension (increased BP in mother) and IUGR","Rh negative pregnancy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A small placenta is seen in PIH / increased maternal BP and in IUGR.  Why A wrong: Twin pregnancy causes placentomegaly (large placenta).  Why B wrong: Diabetes causes placentomegaly.  Why D wrong: Rh negative pregnancy causes placentomegaly."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"In a normally located placenta, the placenta is situated in the:","options":["Lower uterine segment","Upper part of uterus (upper uterine segment)","Cervical canal","Fallopian tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normally the placenta is located in the upper part of the uterus (upper uterine segment).  Why A wrong: A placenta in the lower uterine segment is abnormal (placenta previa).  Why C wrong: The cervical canal is not the normal site.  Why D wrong: A tubal location would be an ectopic pregnancy, not a normal placenta."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"When the placenta is located in the lower part of the uterus, the condition is called:","options":["Placenta accreta","Placenta previa","Placentomegaly","Circumvallate placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A placenta located in the lower part of the uterus is called placenta previa.  Why A wrong: Placenta accreta is abnormal adherence to the myometrium, not location in the lower segment.  Why C wrong: Placentomegaly is enlargement, not abnormal location.  Why D wrong: Circumvallate placenta is a surface/shape anomaly."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"When a small part of the placenta separates from the main placenta but REMAINS connected by blood vessels, the anomaly is called:","options":["Placenta spuria","Placenta succenturiata","Placenta bilobata","Circumvallate placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A small accessory lobe connected to the main placenta by blood vessels is placenta succenturiata (succenturiate lobe).  Why A wrong: Placenta spuria has NO connecting blood vessel.  Why C wrong: Placenta bilobata is two equal parts connected by a vessel.  Why D wrong: Circumvallate placenta is a surface-ring anomaly, not an accessory lobe."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"When a small part of the placenta separates from the main placenta and is NOT connected by any blood vessel, it is called:","options":["Placenta succenturiata","Placenta spuria","Placenta bilobata","Placenta membranacea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A separated small lobe with no connecting blood vessel is placenta spuria.  Why A wrong: Placenta succenturiata is connected by blood vessels.  Why C wrong: Placenta bilobata involves two equal parts connected by a vessel.  Why D wrong: Placenta membranacea is not the anomaly described here."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"When two EQUAL parts of the placenta are separated from each other but remain connected by a blood vessel, the anomaly is called:","options":["Placenta succenturiata","Placenta spuria","Placenta bilobata","Placenta previa"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Two equal parts of placenta separated but connected by a blood vessel is placenta bilobata.  Why A wrong: Succenturiata is a small accessory lobe, not two equal parts.  Why B wrong: Spuria has no connecting vessel.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is a location anomaly."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Abnormalities in the lobes of the placenta (succenturiata, spuria, bilobata) can commonly lead to which complication?","options":["Postpartum haemorrhage (PPH)","Antepartum haemorrhage only","Gestational diabetes","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These lobe abnormalities can lead to PPH (postpartum haemorrhage), often due to retained lobe tissue.  Why B wrong: The source specifically links these to PPH, not antepartum haemorrhage.  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes is unrelated to these lobe anomalies.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is a separate location anomaly."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Normally, the fetal and maternal surfaces of the placenta are equal, giving the placenta which shape?","options":["Spherical","Disc shaped","Cylindrical","Horseshoe shaped"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When fetal and maternal surfaces are equal, the placenta is disc shaped.  Why A wrong: A spherical shape is not the normal placental form.  Why C wrong: Cylindrical is not correct.  Why D wrong: Horseshoe shape is not the normal description."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"When the fetal surface is small and the maternal surface is big, so that the maternal surface surrounds the fetal surface in the form of a ring, the anomaly is called:","options":["Placenta succenturiata","Circumvallate placenta","Placenta bilobata","Placenta previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the maternal surface surrounds a smaller fetal surface as a ring, it is a circumvallate placenta.  Why A wrong: Succenturiata is an accessory lobe anomaly.  Why C wrong: Bilobata is two equal lobes connected by a vessel.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is a location anomaly."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"In circumvallate placenta, the ring formation is due to a structure described as a:","options":["Valve-like thick wall","Blood vessel bridge","Succenturiate lobe","Chorionic cyst"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The circumvallate placenta figure labels a \"valve-like thick wall\" forming the ring.  Why B wrong: A blood-vessel bridge characterizes succenturiata/bilobata, not circumvallate.  Why C wrong: A succenturiate lobe is a separate accessory-lobe anomaly.  Why D wrong: A chorionic cyst is not the described structure."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Which of the following is a recognized problem associated with circumvallate placenta? (Select all that apply)","options":["IUGR","Fetal death","Antepartum haemorrhage","Placentomegaly","Gestational diabetes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The problems associated with circumvallate placenta are IUGR, fetal death, and antepartum haemorrhage.  Why D wrong: Placentomegaly is a size anomaly, not a listed problem of circumvallate placenta.  Why E wrong: Gestational diabetes is not listed as an associated problem."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Which complication is specifically NOT seen with circumvallate placenta?","options":["Intrauterine growth restriction","Antepartum haemorrhage","Postpartum haemorrhage","Fetal death"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states postpartum haemorrhage is NOT seen with circumvallate placenta.  Why A wrong: IUGR is a listed problem of circumvallate placenta.  Why B wrong: Antepartum haemorrhage is a listed problem.  Why D wrong: Fetal death is a listed problem."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Which surface of the placenta bears the umbilical cord and membranes?","options":["Maternal surface","Fetal surface","Basal plate","Decidua basalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fetal surface has the umbilical cord (attached to its centre) and membranes attached to it.  Why A wrong: The maternal surface has neither the cord nor membranes attached.  Why C wrong: The basal plate is the maternal surface.  Why D wrong: Decidua basalis forms the maternal surface."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Placenta maternal vs fetal surface, cotyledons, placental circulation…","question":"Which surface of the placenta shows the lobulated (cotyledon) appearance?","options":["Fetal surface","Maternal surface","Chorionic plate","Amniotic surface"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The maternal surface has the polygonal LOBES divided into lobules/cotyledons.  Why A wrong: The fetal surface is smooth, shiny and grey, without cotyledons.  Why C wrong: The chorionic plate is the fetal surface.  Why D wrong: The amniotic surface is smooth, not lobulated."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"In the diagram of circumvallate placenta, which surface shows the characteristic \"valve-like thickening\" of the membranes?","options":["Fetal surface","Maternal surface","Both surfaces equally","Neither surface"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the \"valve like thickening\" at the periphery of the maternal surface, the hallmark of a circumvallate placenta.  Why A wrong: The fetal surface is the smooth chorionic side bearing the cord; it is labelled separately and does not show the valve-like ridge.  Why C wrong: The thickening is a peripheral ring feature, not distributed equally across both surfaces.  Why D wrong: The thickening is explicitly present, defining the abnormality."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The placental variant illustrated with a raised ring/valve-like thickening of the membranes near its margin is called:","options":["Succenturiate placenta","Circumvallate placenta","Battledore placenta","Placenta membranous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure is explicitly labelled \"Circumvallate placenta,\" characterised by a peripheral valve-like thickening.  Why A wrong: Succenturiate placenta refers to an accessory lobe, not shown here.  Why C wrong: Battledore refers to marginal cord insertion, a different abnormality.  Why D wrong: Placenta membranous is a thin membrane-like placenta covering the whole uterus, not a ring-thickened placenta."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Normally, the placenta (chorionic villi) is attached to which uterine layer?","options":["Myometrium","Serosa (perimetrium)","Endometrium (decidua)","Parametrium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state normally the placenta (chorion villi) is attached to the endometrium (decidua).  Why A wrong: Attachment to myometrium is abnormal (accreta/increta), not the normal state.  Why B wrong: Attachment to serosa is placenta percreta, an abnormality.  Why D wrong: The parametrium is connective tissue lateral to the uterus, not the normal placental attachment site."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Why does the placenta normally separate early after delivery?","options":["Because the myometrium is very strong","Because the endometrium (decidua) is very weak","Because the serosa detaches first","Because the umbilical cord retracts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explain the endometrium is very weak, which is why the placenta separates early after delivery (deciduate).  Why A wrong: Myometrial strength is not the stated reason for normal separation.  Why C wrong: The serosa is not involved in normal placental separation.  Why D wrong: Cord retraction is not the mechanism of placental separation described."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"If chorionic villi are NOT attached to the endometrium but instead enter the myometrium or serosa, what is the expected clinical consequence after delivery?","options":["Placenta separates faster than normal","Placenta will not be easily removed and the patient continues to bleed","No effect on placental delivery","Uterine involution is accelerated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if villi enter myometrium or serosa, the placenta will not be easily removed after delivery and the patient will continue to bleed.  Why A wrong: Abnormal attachment prevents, not accelerates, separation.  Why C wrong: There is a definite adverse effect — retained placenta and bleeding.  Why D wrong: Retained adherent placenta impairs, not accelerates, involution."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The general term for a placenta whose chorionic villi are abnormally deeply attached to the uterine wall (myometrium/serosa) is:","options":["Morbidly adhered placenta","Placenta previa","Abruptio placentae","Circumvallate placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The heading \"Morbidly Adhered Placenta\" covers accreta, increta, and percreta — abnormally adherent villi.  Why B wrong: Placenta previa is abnormal location (low implantation), not abnormal depth of attachment.  Why C wrong: Abruptio placentae is premature separation, not abnormal adherence.  Why D wrong: Circumvallate refers to a membrane thickening, not villous invasion."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"In placenta accreta, the chorionic villi are:","options":["Superficially attached to the myometrium","Deeply invading the myometrium","Attached to the serosa","Attached only to the endometrium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Type A, Placenta Accreta, is defined as chorionic villi superficially attached to the myometrium.  Why B wrong: Deep invasion of the myometrium is increta.  Why C wrong: Attachment to the serosa is percreta.  Why D wrong: Attachment only to the endometrium is normal, not accreta."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Which type of morbidly adherent placenta is the most common (mc)?","options":["Placenta increta","Placenta percreta","Placenta accreta","Placenta membranous"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes mark Placenta Accreta as \"(mc)\" — the most common type.  Why A wrong: Increta (deep myometrial invasion) is less common than accreta.  Why B wrong: Percreta (invasion to serosa) is the least common.  Why D wrong: Placenta membranous is a developmental abnormality, not a type of adherent placenta."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"In placenta increta, the chorionic villi:","options":["Are superficially attached to the myometrium","Invade the myometrium (deeply attached)","Are attached to the serosa","Do not attach to any uterine layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Type B, Placenta Increta, is defined as chorionic villi invading the myometrium (deeply attached).  Why A wrong: Superficial myometrial attachment is accreta.  Why C wrong: Serosal attachment is percreta.  Why D wrong: Increta is defined by deep attachment, not absence of attachment."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"In placenta percreta, the chorionic villi are attached to which structure?","options":["Endometrium","Superficial myometrium","Serosa","Cervix"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Type C, Placenta Percreta, is defined as chorionic villi attached to (penetrating through to) the serosa.  Why A wrong: Endometrial attachment is normal.  Why B wrong: Superficial myometrial attachment is accreta.  Why D wrong: The cervix is not the defining site for percreta."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"In the diagram of morbidly adhered placenta, which layer is penetrated by the villi in the DEEPEST form of invasion (percreta)?","options":["Decidua only","Superficial myometrium","Full myometrium reaching the serosa","Cervical canal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Percreta is the deepest form, with villi reaching the serosa (uterine surface); the diagram labels Percreta as the outermost invasion.  Why A wrong: Decidua-only attachment is normal.  Why B wrong: Superficial myometrial invasion is accreta.  Why D wrong: The cervical canal is not the depth marker in this classification."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Which two histological/anatomical factors are the two main reasons morbidly adherent placenta occurs?","options":["Nitabuch layer absent and decidua basalis absent","Amnion absent and chorion absent","Wharton's jelly absent and Hoboken folds absent","Endometrium thickened and myometrium hypertrophied"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the 2 main reasons as (1) NITABUCH layer absent and (2) decidua basalis absent.  Why B wrong: Amnion/chorion are fetal membranes, not the stated causes.  Why C wrong: Wharton's jelly and Hoboken folds are cord structures, unrelated to adherence.  Why D wrong: The mechanism is absence of protective decidual layers, not thickening/hypertrophy."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Absence of which layer, normally separating the placenta from the myometrium, is a key cause of morbidly adherent placenta?","options":["Nitabuch layer","Wharton's jelly","Chorion frondosum","Amniotic membrane"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes name the Nitabuch layer's absence as a main reason for abnormal adherence.  Why B wrong: Wharton's jelly is the connective tissue of the umbilical cord.  Why C wrong: Chorion frondosum forms the fetal side of the placenta, not a separation layer.  Why D wrong: The amniotic membrane surrounds the fetus and is not the decidual barrier."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Which of the following are the two main RISK FACTORS for morbidly adherent placenta according to the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Placenta previa in present pregnancy","Previous cesarean section","Multiple gestation","Advanced maternal age","Gestational diabetes"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the 2 main risk factors as (1) placenta previa in the present pregnancy and (2) cesarean section done in a previous pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Multiple gestation is not listed as a main risk factor here.  Why D wrong: Advanced maternal age is not among the two stated risk factors.  Why E wrong: Gestational diabetes is not listed as a main risk factor here."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"A previous cesarean section is a main risk factor for morbidly adherent placenta primarily because it:","options":["Increases amniotic fluid","Damages/scars the uterine wall predisposing to abnormal villous attachment","Weakens the umbilical cord","Enlarges the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A prior cesarean scar disrupts normal decidua, and the notes list previous cesarean section as a main risk factor for abnormal adherence.  Why A wrong: Amniotic fluid volume is not the mechanism cited.  Why C wrong: Cord weakening is unrelated to placental adherence risk.  Why D wrong: Placental size is not the stated risk mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Which of the following are the two main PRESENTING FEATURES of morbidly adherent placenta? (Select all that apply)","options":["Patient continues to bleed after delivery (PPH)","Placenta does not separate after delivery","Severe hypertension","Oligohydramnios","Fetal tachycardia"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the 2 main presenting features as (1) patient continues to bleed after delivery (PPH) and (2) placenta does not separate after delivery.  Why C wrong: Hypertension is not a listed presenting feature.  Why D wrong: Oligohydramnios is not listed.  Why E wrong: Fetal tachycardia is not among the stated presenting features."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Continued bleeding after delivery in morbidly adherent placenta is a form of:","options":["Antepartum hemorrhage","Postpartum hemorrhage (PPH)","Intra-cranial hemorrhage","Fetal hemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify the patient continues to bleed after delivery and label it PPH (postpartum hemorrhage).  Why A wrong: Antepartum hemorrhage occurs before delivery, not after.  Why C wrong: Intra-cranial hemorrhage is unrelated.  Why D wrong: The bleeding here is maternal (postpartum), not fetal."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"What is the BEST management of morbidly adherent placenta according to the notes?","options":["Manual removal of placenta","Hysterectomy","Oxytocin infusion only","Uterine massage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best management is hysterectomy.  Why A wrong: Manual removal may worsen bleeding in adherent placenta and is not the best option cited.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin alone will not detach abnormally invaded villi.  Why D wrong: Uterine massage is inadequate for morbidly adherent placenta."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"If a patient with morbidly adherent placenta REFUSES the definitive surgery, what alternative management is advised?","options":["Immediate manual extraction","Leave the placenta in the uterus plus antibiotics","Uterine artery embolization only","High-dose oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if the patient refuses (hysterectomy), management is to leave the placenta in the uterus plus antibiotics.  Why A wrong: Manual extraction is the option being avoided in refusal scenarios and risks hemorrhage.  Why C wrong: Embolization is not the alternative listed in the notes.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not the stated alternative for refusal."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Normally, the fetal side of the placenta develops from which structure?","options":["Chorion frondosum","Chorion laevae","Decidua basalis","Amnion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetal side of the placenta normally develops from the chorion frondosum.  Why B wrong: Chorion laevae forms the fetal membrane (chorion), not the placenta proper.  Why C wrong: Decidua basalis is the maternal component.  Why D wrong: The amnion is the innermost membrane, not the source of the placental fetal side."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The chorion laevae normally forms which structure?","options":["The maternal surface of placenta","The fetal membrane (chorion)","The umbilical cord","The decidua basalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state chorion laevae forms the fetal membrane: chorion.  Why A wrong: The maternal surface derives from the decidua, not chorion laevae.  Why C wrong: The cord develops from the connecting stalk, not chorion laevae.  Why D wrong: Decidua basalis is maternal tissue."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"If the placenta develops from BOTH chorion frondosum and chorion laevae, the resulting abnormal placenta is:","options":["Thick and localized","Thin, membrane-like, and covering the whole uterus (placenta membranous)","A succenturiate lobe","Circumvallate placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state such a placenta will cover the whole uterus and be thin and membrane-like → Placenta Membranous.  Why A wrong: It is described as thin, not thick and localized.  Why C wrong: A succenturiate lobe is an accessory lobe, not described here.  Why D wrong: Circumvallate is a ring-thickening abnormality, not the whole-uterus membranous type."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The term \"placenta membranous\" describes a placenta that is:","options":["Localized to the fundus and thick","Thin, membrane-like, and covering the whole uterus","Attached abnormally deep into the serosa","Bilobed with a valve-like ring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Placenta membranous covers the whole uterus and is thin and membrane-like, per the notes.  Why A wrong: It is diffuse and thin, not localized and thick.  Why C wrong: Deep serosal invasion is percreta, a different condition.  Why D wrong: A valve-like ring describes circumvallate placenta."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The umbilical cord functionally:","options":["Connects the fetus to the placenta","Connects the placenta to the myometrium","Connects the amnion to the chorion","Connects two twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the umbilical cord connects the fetus to the placenta.  Why B wrong: The cord does not attach to the myometrium.  Why C wrong: Amnion-to-chorion connection is not the cord's role.  Why D wrong: The cord links one fetus to its placenta, not two twins."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The umbilical cord is also known by which name?","options":["Funis","Vernix","Meconium","Lanugo"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the umbilical cord is also called FUNIS.  Why B wrong: Vernix caseosa is the waxy fetal skin coating.  Why C wrong: Meconium is the first fetal stool.  Why D wrong: Lanugo is fine fetal body hair."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The umbilical cord develops from which embryonic structure?","options":["Connecting stalk","Chorion frondosum","Yolk sac","Decidua basalis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the umbilical cord develops from the connecting stalk.  Why B wrong: Chorion frondosum forms the fetal side of the placenta.  Why C wrong: The yolk sac is a separate embryonic structure, not the stated origin.  Why D wrong: Decidua basalis is maternal tissue."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"What is the normal LENGTH RANGE of the umbilical cord?","options":["10–20 cm","30–100 cm","100–150 cm","5–15 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the cord length range as 30–100 cm.  Why A wrong: 10–20 cm is below the stated range.  Why C wrong: 100–150 cm exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 5–15 cm is far below the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"According to Dutta, the AVERAGE length of the umbilical cord is:","options":["20 cm","40 cm","60 cm","100 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state average length is 40 cm (as per Dutta).  Why A wrong: 20 cm is below the stated average.  Why C wrong: 60 cm is above the stated average.  Why D wrong: 100 cm is the upper end of the range, not the average."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"A \"short cord\" is defined by the notes as a cord measuring:","options":["< 30 cm","< 40 cm","< 50 cm","< 20 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define a short cord as < 30 cm.  Why B wrong: 40 cm is the average length, not the short-cord cutoff.  Why C wrong: < 50 cm is not the stated cutoff.  Why D wrong: < 20 cm is not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Which of the following are PROBLEMS associated with a SHORT umbilical cord? (Select all that apply)","options":["Easily broken","Prevents version","Cord prolapse","Cord enlargement leading to fetal distress","Vasa previa"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list short-cord problems as (1) easily broken and (2) prevents version.  Why C wrong: Cord prolapse is a problem of a long cord, not a short cord.  Why D wrong: Cord enlargement → fetal distress is a long-cord problem.  Why E wrong: Vasa previa relates to velamentous insertion, not short-cord length."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"A \"long cord\" is defined by the notes as a cord measuring:","options":["≥ 100 cm","≥ 50 cm","≥ 40 cm","≥ 30 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define a long cord as ≥ 100 cm.  Why B wrong: ≥ 50 cm is within the normal range, not the long-cord cutoff.  Why C wrong: 40 cm is the average, not the long cutoff.  Why D wrong: 30 cm is the lower normal bound, not the long-cord definition."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Which of the following are PROBLEMS associated with a LONG umbilical cord? (Select all that apply)","options":["Can lead to cord enlargement → fetal distress","Cord prolapse","Easily broken","Prevents version","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list long-cord problems as (1) cord enlargement leading to fetal distress and (2) cord prolapse.  Why C wrong: \"Easily broken\" is a short-cord problem.  Why D wrong: \"Prevents version\" is a short-cord problem.  Why E wrong: Placenta previa is not a cord-length problem."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"According to the long-cord problem flow, cord enlargement leads to which intermediate consequence before cord prolapse is considered?","options":["Fetal distress","Maternal hypertension","Placental abruption","Oligohydramnios"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows cord enlargement → fetal distress in the long-cord problems.  Why B wrong: Maternal hypertension is not in this flow.  Why C wrong: Placental abruption is not the stated consequence.  Why D wrong: Oligohydramnios is not listed in this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"What is the average DIAMETER of the umbilical cord?","options":["0.5 cm","1.5 cm","2.5 cm","3.5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the average cord diameter as 1.5 cm.  Why A wrong: 0.5 cm is smaller than the stated diameter.  Why C wrong: 2.5 cm exceeds the stated diameter.  Why D wrong: 3.5 cm exceeds the stated diameter."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The pH of blood in the umbilical cord is approximately:","options":["6.2","7.2","7.8","8.2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the pH of blood in the umbilical cord is 7.2.  Why A wrong: 6.2 is markedly acidotic and not the stated value.  Why C wrong: 7.8 is alkalotic and not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 8.2 is far too alkaline and incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Which of the following are the named structures in the umbilical cord? (Select all that apply)","options":["Folds of Hoboken","Wharton's jelly (connective tissue)","Nitabuch layer","Decidua basalis","Chorion frondosum"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the named structures in the cord as (1) folds of Hoboken and (2) the slippery connective tissue called Wharton's jelly.  Why C wrong: The Nitabuch layer is a decidual layer, not a cord structure.  Why D wrong: Decidua basalis is maternal placental tissue.  Why E wrong: Chorion frondosum forms the placental fetal side, not a cord structure."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The slippery connective tissue of the umbilical cord is called:","options":["Wharton's jelly","Vernix caseosa","Nitabuch's fibrinoid","Reichert's membrane"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cord's connective tissue is slippery and called Wharton's jelly.  Why B wrong: Vernix caseosa is the fetal skin coating.  Why C wrong: Nitabuch's fibrinoid is a decidual layer.  Why D wrong: Reichert's membrane is not the cord connective tissue."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"A \"false knot\" of the umbilical cord is caused by:","options":["Wharton's jelly collecting at some places and appearing as a knot","The fetus passing through a true loop of cord","Twisting of two umbilical arteries","Rupture of the umbilical vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define a false knot as Wharton's jelly collecting at some places and appearing as a knot.  Why B wrong: That describes a true knot, not a false knot.  Why C wrong: Arterial twisting is not the stated cause of a false knot.  Why D wrong: Venous rupture is unrelated to false-knot formation."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"A true knot of the umbilical cord differs from a false knot in that a true knot:","options":["Is an actual knotting of the cord (potentially compromising blood flow)","Is merely a localized collection of Wharton's jelly","Is caused by cord shortening","Only occurs after delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure contrasts a true knot (an actual knot in the cord) with a false knot (Wharton's jelly collection); the true knot is a genuine knotting.  Why B wrong: A localized Wharton's jelly collection is the false knot.  Why C wrong: Cord shortening is not the described cause of a true knot.  Why D wrong: True knots form in utero, not only after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Normal attachment of the cord to the placenta is described as attachment to the:","options":["Margin of the fetal surface","Centre of the fetal surface (central attachment)","Membranes only","Maternal surface"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state normal attachment is to the centre of the fetal surface, called central attachment of the cord.  Why A wrong: Marginal attachment is battledore placenta, an abnormality.  Why C wrong: Attachment to membranes only is velamentous insertion, an abnormality.  Why D wrong: The cord attaches to the fetal surface, not the maternal surface."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"When the umbilical cord is attached to one side (margin) of the placenta rather than the centre, the condition is called:","options":["Battledore placenta (marginal insertion of cord)","Velamentous insertion","Circumvallate placenta","Succenturiate placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define marginal insertion of cord (attached to one side, not centre) as battledore placenta.  Why B wrong: Velamentous insertion is attachment to membranes first, then placenta.  Why C wrong: Circumvallate is a membrane ring-thickening abnormality.  Why D wrong: Succenturiate refers to an accessory lobe."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"In velamentous insertion of the cord, the cord:","options":["Attaches directly to the centre of the placenta","Is not directly attached to placenta — it attaches first to the membranes and then to the placenta","Attaches to the myometrium","Attaches to the maternal surface only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define velamentous insertion as the cord not being directly attached to the placenta; it attaches first to the membranes and then to the placenta.  Why A wrong: Central attachment is the normal pattern, not velamentous.  Why C wrong: Cords do not attach to myometrium.  Why D wrong: The cord relates to the fetal surface/membranes, not the maternal surface."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"In the labelled figure, \"Central attachment\" of the cord corresponds to which type of placenta?","options":["Normal attachment","Battledore placenta","Velamentous placenta","Circumvallate placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels central attachment as the normal attachment (A), contrasted with battledore placenta (B).  Why B wrong: Battledore is the marginal insertion, labelled separately.  Why C wrong: Velamentous placenta involves membrane insertion, shown in a separate figure.  Why D wrong: Circumvallate is a different abnormality not shown here."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Velamentous insertion of the cord can lead to which serious complication because the fetal vessels run unprotected through the membranes?","options":["Vasa previa","Placenta accreta","Uterine rupture","Cord prolapse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The p-090 flow explains how velamentous insertion leads to vasa previa, as the cord/vessels attached to membranes can rupture with the membranes.  Why B wrong: Placenta accreta is due to absent Nitabuch/decidua, not velamentous insertion.  Why C wrong: Uterine rupture is unrelated to velamentous insertion.  Why D wrong: Cord prolapse is a long-cord problem, not the stated velamentous complication."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"In the pathophysiology of vasa previa from velamentous insertion, why do the fetal vessels rupture when the membranes rupture at delivery?","options":["Because the cord (and its vessels) is attached to the membranes, so when membranes rupture the cord ruptures too","Because the placenta separates prematurely","Because the myometrium contracts strongly","Because Wharton's jelly dissolves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explain the cord is attached to the membrane, so when membranes rupture the cord (containing umbilical artery and vein) will also rupture.  Why B wrong: Premature placental separation is abruption, not this mechanism.  Why C wrong: Myometrial contraction is not the stated cause of vessel rupture here.  Why D wrong: Wharton's jelly dissolution is not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The umbilical cord contains which vessels that rupture in vasa previa?","options":["Umbilical artery and umbilical vein","Two renal arteries","Hepatic artery and portal vein","Aorta and vena cava"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the umbilical cord has umbilical artery and umbilical vein, which rupture in vasa previa.  Why B wrong: Renal arteries are not in the cord.  Why C wrong: Hepatic vessels are not umbilical cord contents.  Why D wrong: Aorta and vena cava are fetal great vessels, not cord vessels."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"In vasa previa, the bleeding that occurs is:","options":["Maternal blood","Fetal blood (it comes from the fetus)","Placental maternal-sinus blood","Amniotic fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in vasa previa there is fetal blood loss — \"it comes from the fetus.\"  Why A wrong: The blood is fetal, not maternal.  Why C wrong: It is fetal vessel blood, not maternal placental sinus blood.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid is not blood."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Because the bleeding in vasa previa is of fetal origin, it primarily leads to:","options":["Fetal death and NOT maternal death","Maternal death only","No clinical consequence","Maternal hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state vasa previa leads to fetal death and not to maternal death, because the blood lost is fetal.  Why B wrong: The mother does not typically die since the blood is fetal.  Why C wrong: There is a serious consequence — fetal death.  Why D wrong: Maternal hypertension is not the described outcome."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"The management of vasa previa is:","options":["Vaginal delivery with oxytocin","Cesarean section","Manual removal of placenta","Expectant management at home"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state management of vasa previa is cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Vaginal delivery risks fetal exsanguination when membranes rupture.  Why C wrong: Manual removal of placenta addresses retained placenta, not vasa previa.  Why D wrong: Expectant home management is unsafe given the fetal risk."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Vasa previa can be diagnosed during pregnancy by which investigation?","options":["Doppler","Amniocentesis","Non-stress test","Serum beta-hCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state vasa previa can be diagnosed during pregnancy by Doppler.  Why B wrong: Amniocentesis assesses amniotic fluid/genetics, not cord vessel position.  Why C wrong: A non-stress test assesses fetal heart reactivity, not vasa previa diagnosis.  Why D wrong: Beta-hCG is a pregnancy hormone, not a diagnostic for vasa previa."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"At term, how many blood vessels are present in the umbilical cord?","options":["2 vessels","3 vessels","4 vessels","5 vessels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state blood vessels at term are 3 (two umbilical arteries and one umbilical vein).  Why A wrong: Two vessels is not the term count in these notes.  Why C wrong: Four vessels is the count before 20 weeks, not at term.  Why D wrong: Five vessels is not described."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"According to the notes, the THREE blood vessels present in the umbilical cord at term are:","options":["Right umbilical artery, left umbilical artery, and left umbilical vein","Two umbilical veins and one umbilical artery","Right umbilical artery, right umbilical vein, and left umbilical vein","Two umbilical arteries and two umbilical veins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the 3 term vessels as right umbilical artery, left umbilical artery, and left umbilical vein.  Why B wrong: The cord at term has two arteries and one vein, not two veins and one artery.  Why C wrong: The right umbilical vein disappears; it is not present at term.  Why D wrong: There is only one vein at term, not two."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Before 20 weeks of pregnancy, how many blood vessels are present in the umbilical cord?","options":["2 vessels","3 vessels","4 vessels","6 vessels"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that before 20 weeks of pregnancy there are 4 blood vessels.  Why A wrong: Two vessels is not described for this stage.  Why B wrong: Three vessels is the term count, not before 20 weeks.  Why D wrong: Six vessels is not described."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"According to the notes, the FOUR blood vessels present in the cord before 20 weeks of pregnancy are:","options":["Right umbilical artery, right umbilical vein, left umbilical artery, left umbilical vein","Two arteries and two veins on the left only","Two arteries and two veins on the right only","Three arteries and one vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the 4 vessels as right umbilical artery, right umbilical vein, left umbilical artery, and left umbilical vein.  Why B wrong: The four vessels are bilateral (right and left), not left-only.  Why C wrong: They are not right-only.  Why D wrong: There are two arteries and two veins, not three arteries and one vein."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"During cord development, which vessel is \"left behind\" (persists to term)?","options":["Left umbilical vein","Right umbilical vein","Right umbilical artery","Left umbilical artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the left umbilical vein is left behind (persists), which is why only one vein remains at term.  Why B wrong: The right umbilical vein disappears.  Why C wrong: The right umbilical artery persists but the question asks about the vein that is \"left behind.\"  Why D wrong: The left umbilical artery persists as an artery, but the specific \"left behind\" vein is the left umbilical vein."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"Which umbilical vessel disappears during development, so that only one vein remains at term?","options":["Left umbilical vein","Right umbilical vein","Left umbilical artery","Right umbilical artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the right umbilical vein disappears, leaving only the left umbilical vein at term.  Why A wrong: The left umbilical vein is the one that persists.  Why C wrong: The left umbilical artery persists to term.  Why D wrong: The right umbilical artery persists to term as one of the two arteries."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"A single umbilical artery (only 2 vessels instead of 3 at term) would be an abnormal finding; how many arteries are normally present at term in the cord?","options":["One artery","Two arteries","Three arteries","No arteries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The term list includes right umbilical artery and left umbilical artery — two arteries (plus one left umbilical vein).  Why A wrong: Only one artery would be abnormal; normally there are two.  Why C wrong: There are two arteries, not three, at term.  Why D wrong: Arteries are present, so \"no arteries\" is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"In the circumvallate placenta figure, the smooth surface bearing the umbilical cord attachment is labelled the:","options":["Fetal surface","Maternal surface","Serosal surface","Decidual surface"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the fetal surface as the smooth chorionic side (which bears the cord), distinct from the maternal surface.  Why B wrong: The maternal surface is the rough, cotyledon-bearing side with the valve-like thickening.  Why C wrong: There is no \"serosal surface\" label on the placental figure.  Why D wrong: \"Decidual surface\" is not the label used; the maternal surface is the decidual side."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"A patient with a previous cesarean section AND current placenta previa presents with failure of the placenta to separate and heavy bleeding after delivery. This clinical picture is most consistent with:","options":["Morbidly adherent placenta (e.g., accreta)","Normal third stage of labour","Uterine atony with normal placenta","Circumvallate placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Previous cesarean + placenta previa are the two main risk factors, and non-separation with PPH are the two main presenting features of morbidly adherent placenta.  Why B wrong: Normal third stage involves prompt separation without heavy bleeding.  Why C wrong: Atony causes bleeding but the placenta still separates; here the placenta does not separate.  Why D wrong: Circumvallate does not classically cause retained non-separating placenta with these risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Circumvallate placenta (maternal/fetal surface, valve-like thickening)…","question":"A short umbilical cord \"prevents version.\" In obstetrics, \"version\" refers to:","options":["A procedure/turning of the fetus to a favourable position","Rupture of the membranes","Rotation of the placenta","Contraction of the uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A short cord physically restricts fetal movement/turning, thus preventing version (the turning of the fetus); this is why it is listed as a short-cord problem.  Why B wrong: Membrane rupture is not \"version.\"  Why C wrong: Version refers to the fetus, not placental rotation.  Why D wrong: Uterine contraction is not the meaning of version."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which structure forms the bulk of connective tissue (gelatinous ground substance) protecting the vessels within the umbilical cord?","options":["Adventitia","Wharton's jelly","Endothelium","Perivascular region"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the cross-section of the umbilical cord, Wharton's jelly is the gelatinous connective tissue that surrounds and cushions the umbilical vessels.  Why A wrong: Adventitia is the outer coat of the vessel wall, not the cushioning jelly.  Why C wrong: Endothelium is the innermost lining of the vessels, not the surrounding jelly.  Why D wrong: The perivascular region is the zone immediately around the vessels, not the protective jelly itself."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"How many umbilical arteries and umbilical veins are normally present in the umbilical cord as shown in the cross-section?","options":["Two arteries and one vein","One artery and two veins","Two arteries and two veins","One artery and one vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal umbilical cord cross-section shows two umbilical arteries and one umbilical vein.  Why B wrong: There is one vein, not two, and two arteries.  Why C wrong: There is only one umbilical vein.  Why D wrong: There are two arteries, not one (a single artery is an abnormality)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"In the umbilical cord cross-section, which layer is labelled as the innermost lining of the vessels?","options":["Amnion","Adventitia","Endothelium","Sub-amnion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Endothelium is labelled as the innermost lining of the umbilical vessels.  Why A wrong: Amnion is the outer covering membrane of the cord, not the vessel lining.  Why B wrong: Adventitia is the outer vessel coat.  Why D wrong: Sub-amnion lies just beneath the amnion, not lining the vessels."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which two layers cover the outer surface of the umbilical cord according to the cross-section figure?","options":["Endothelium and adventitia","Amnion and sub-amnion","Wharton's jelly and perivascular region","Chorion and decidua"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cross-section labels amnion and sub-amnion as the outer covering layers of the cord.  Why A wrong: These are vessel-related layers, not the outer cord covering.  Why C wrong: Wharton's jelly and perivascular region are internal to the cord.  Why D wrong: Chorion and decidua are placental/membrane structures, not labelled on the cord cross-section."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"What is the most common vascular abnormality of the umbilical cord?","options":["Two vessel cord with absent vein","Single umbilical artery","Velamentous insertion","True knot of cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Single umbilical artery is stated as the most common vascular abnormality of the cord.  Why A wrong: Absent vein is not described; the abnormality is a missing artery.  Why C wrong: Velamentous insertion is an insertion abnormality, not the one named here.  Why D wrong: A true knot is a structural, not vascular, abnormality and is not the one described."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Single umbilical artery is most commonly seen in all of the following conditions EXCEPT:","options":["Diabetic mother","Twin pregnancy","Post-term pregnancy","Antepartum haemorrhage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Post-term pregnancy is not listed; single umbilical artery is most commonly seen in diabetic, twin, antepartum haemorrhage, and preeclampsia.  Why A wrong: Diabetic is listed as a common association.  Why B wrong: Twin pregnancy is listed.  Why D wrong: Antepartum haemorrhage is listed."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Single umbilical artery is most commonly seen in which hypertensive disorder of pregnancy?","options":["Gestational hypertension only","Preeclampsia","Chronic hypertension","Eclampsia only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Preeclampsia is listed among the conditions in which single umbilical artery is most commonly seen.  Why A, C, D wrong: These specific hypertensive states are not the one listed on the page; preeclampsia is named."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"A single umbilical artery is associated with malformations of which body systems?","options":["Cardiovascular and renal systems, and trisomies","Respiratory and hepatic systems only","Musculoskeletal system only","Gastrointestinal system only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Single umbilical artery is associated with CVS malformation, renal malformation, and trisomy in the body.  Why B, C, D wrong: These systems are not listed as the associations; the page names cardiovascular, renal, and chromosomal (trisomy) associations."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"After detecting a single umbilical artery on ultrasound, which set of investigations should be performed?","options":["CT scan, MRI, and biopsy","ECHO, USG, and karyotype","X-ray, ECG, and CBC","Amniocentesis, HbA1c, and LFT"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After seeing a single umbilical artery, the investigations to be done are ECHO, USG, and karyotype (to evaluate cardiac, structural, and chromosomal anomalies).  Why A, C, D wrong: These investigations are not the ones listed on the page for this workup."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Detection of a single umbilical artery should prompt an ECHO primarily to rule out which type of anomaly?","options":["Renal malformation","Cardiovascular (CVS) malformation","Neural tube defect","Skeletal dysplasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ECHO (echocardiography) evaluates the heart, correlating with the associated CVS malformation.  Why A wrong: Renal malformation is assessed by USG, not ECHO.  Why C wrong: Neural tube defects are not the listed association.  Why D wrong: Skeletal dysplasia is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which two surgical instruments are shown for management of the umbilical cord at birth?","options":["Umbilical cord cutting scissors and umbilical cord clamp","Forceps and vacuum extractor","Episiotomy scissors and suture needle","Amnihook and Kocher's forceps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figures show umbilical cord cutting scissors and an umbilical cord clamp.  Why B wrong: Forceps and vacuum are delivery instruments, not shown here.  Why C wrong: Episiotomy tools are not the ones depicted.  Why D wrong: An amnihook/Kocher are for membrane rupture, not shown here."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which fetal membrane is the inner layer that is shiny and smooth?","options":["Chorion","Amnion","Decidua","Cytotrophoblast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The amnion is the inner layer, described as shiny and smooth.  Why A wrong: The chorion is the outer membrane, described as rough and dull.  Why C wrong: Decidua is maternal endometrium, not a fetal membrane described here.  Why D wrong: Cytotrophoblast forms the chorion (chorion laeve), not the shiny inner amnion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"What is the thickness range of the amnion?","options":["0.02–0.5 mm","1–2 mm","0.5–1 cm","2–5 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The amnion thickness is stated as 0.02–0.5 mm.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated thickness of the amnion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"The amnion is formed from which embryonic germ layer?","options":["Endoderm","Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Trophoblast"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The amnion is formed by ectoderm.  Why A wrong: Endoderm forms gut derivatives, not the amnion.  Why B wrong: Mesoderm does not form the amnion as stated.  Why D wrong: Trophoblast forms placental structures, not the amnion here."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which statement correctly describes the vascularity and nerve supply of the amnion?","options":["Rich in blood vessels and nerves","No blood vessels and no nerve supply","Blood vessels present but no nerves","Nerves present but no blood vessels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The amnion has no blood vessels and no nerve supply.  Why A, C, D wrong: The amnion is avascular and non-innervated; only the chorion is described as having blood vessels present."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Between the two fetal membranes, which is described as \"more strong\"?","options":["Chorion","Amnion","Both are equally strong","Decidua"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The amnion is described as \"more strong.\"  Why A wrong: The chorion is the rough, dull outer membrane and is not described as the stronger one.  Why C wrong: The page specifically states the amnion is more strong.  Why D wrong: Decidua is not one of the two membranes compared here."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which fetal membrane is the outer membrane, rough and dull in texture?","options":["Amnion","Chorion","Yolk sac","Allantois"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chorion is the outer membrane, described as rough and dull.  Why A wrong: The amnion is the inner, shiny, smooth membrane.  Why C, D wrong: Yolk sac and allantois are not the membranes described here."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"The chorion is formed by which structure?","options":["Chorion laeve (cytotrophoblast)","Ectoderm","Syncytiotrophoblast only","Decidua basalis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chorion is formed by chorion laeve (cytotrophoblast).  Why B wrong: Ectoderm forms the amnion, not the chorion.  Why C wrong: The page attributes the chorion to cytotrophoblast (chorion laeve), not syncytiotrophoblast.  Why D wrong: Decidua basalis is maternal tissue, not the fetal chorion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which fetal membrane has blood vessels present?","options":["Amnion","Chorion","Neither membrane","Both equally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood vessels are present in the chorion.  Why A wrong: The amnion has no blood vessels.  Why C, D wrong: Only the chorion has blood vessels present, per the page."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which prostaglandin is present in the fetal membranes?","options":["PGF2-alpha","PGE1","PGE2","PGI2"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The prostaglandin present in the membrane is PGE2.  Why A, B, D wrong: These prostaglandins are not the one stated to be present in the membrane."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"During normal labour, when do the membranes rupture?","options":["At the onset of the first stage","After full dilation of the cervix (10 cm), i.e., at the end of the first stage of labour","During the third stage","Before labour begins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Membranes normally rupture after full dilation of the cervix (10 cm), at the end of the first stage of labour.  Why A wrong: Rupture occurs at the end, not the onset, of the first stage.  Why C wrong: It is not the third stage.  Why D wrong: Rupture before labour begins is PROM, not normal timing."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"At what cervical dilation is full dilation defined, at which normal membrane rupture occurs?","options":["6 cm","8 cm","10 cm","4 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Full dilation of the cervix is 10 cm.  Why A, B, D wrong: These are lesser dilations, not full dilation."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Premature Rupture of Membranes (PROM) is defined as rupture of membranes occurring:","options":["After full cervical dilation","Before labour begins","Only after 37 weeks during active labour","During the second stage of labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PROM is when membranes rupture before labour begins.  Why A wrong: Rupture after full dilation is normal labour rupture.  Why C, D wrong: These describe intrapartum timing, not PROM."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Preterm Premature Rupture of Membranes (PPROM) is defined as rupture of membranes occurring:","options":["Before 37 weeks of pregnancy","After 40 weeks of pregnancy","At full dilation","After delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PPROM is when membranes rupture before 37 weeks of pregnancy.  Why B wrong: 40 weeks is term/post-term, not preterm.  Why C wrong: Rupture at full dilation is normal timing.  Why D wrong: Membranes rupture during pregnancy/labour, not after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"When an obstetrician deliberately ruptures the membranes, the procedure is called:","options":["Amniocentesis","Artificial Rupture of Membranes (ARM) / Amniotomy","Cerclage","Curettage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Obstetrician-performed rupture of membranes is called Artificial Rupture of Membranes (ARM) / amniotomy.  Why A wrong: Amniocentesis is sampling amniotic fluid, not rupturing membranes for labour.  Why C wrong: Cerclage is a cervical stitch.  Why D wrong: Curettage is scraping of the uterine cavity."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"ARM (amniotomy) speeds up labour because rupture of the membranes causes the release of which substance?","options":["Oxytocin","PGE2","Relaxin","Progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the membrane ruptures, PGE2 is released, which makes the process of labour fast.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin release is not the mechanism given here; PGE2 is stated.  Why C, D wrong: Relaxin and progesterone are not the released substances described."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Performing ARM/amniotomy to make an already-established labour progress faster (at term) is best described as:","options":["Induction of labour","Augmentation of labour","Cervical ripening","Active management of third stage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page notes \"Labor fast = Term = AUGMENTATION of LABOR\" — speeding up existing labour is augmentation.  Why A wrong: Induction is starting labour before it begins; augmentation is speeding established labour.  Why C wrong: Cervical ripening is preparing the cervix, not the described action.  Why D wrong: Third-stage management concerns placental delivery, not this."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"What is the most common (primary) site for synthesis of hormones in the placenta?","options":["Cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast","Decidua","Amnion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The syncytiotrophoblast is the most common site for synthesis of placental hormones.  Why A wrong: Cytotrophoblast forms the chorion laeve; it is not the main hormone-synthesis site named.  Why C, D wrong: Decidua and amnion are not the stated hormone-synthesis site."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Placental hormones are derived into which two categories?","options":["Steroid hormones and protein/peptide hormones","Amine hormones and lipid hormones","Catecholamines and thyroid hormones","Growth factors and cytokines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Placental hormones fall into steroid hormones and protein/peptide hormones.  Why B, C, D wrong: These categories are not the two given on the page."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which of the following are the steroid hormones produced by the placenta?","options":["hCG and HPL","Estrogen and progesterone","Oxytocin and prolactin","Insulin and cortisol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The placental steroid hormones listed are estrogen and progesterone.  Why A wrong: hCG and HPL are protein/peptide hormones, not steroids.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the placental steroid hormones listed."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which of the following are the protein/peptide hormones produced by the placenta?","options":["Estrogen and progesterone","hCG (Human Chorionic Gonadotropin) and HPL (Human Placental Lactogen)","FSH and LH","Cortisol and aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The placental protein/peptide hormones are hCG and HPL.  Why A wrong: Estrogen and progesterone are steroid hormones.  Why C wrong: FSH and LH are pituitary hormones.  Why D wrong: Cortisol and aldosterone are adrenal steroids."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"What does the abbreviation HPL stand for?","options":["Human Placental Lactogen","Human Prolactin Level","Human Peptide Ligand","Human Progesterone Lactogen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HPL stands for Human Placental Lactogen.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the correct expansions of HPL."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"hCG is chemically classified as which type of hormone?","options":["Steroid hormone","Glycoprotein hormone","Amine hormone","Fatty-acid derivative"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: hCG is a glycoprotein hormone.  Why A wrong: hCG is a protein/peptide (glycoprotein), not a steroid.  Why C, D wrong: hCG is not an amine or fatty-acid derived hormone."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"hCG is notable for having the highest content of which component among hormones?","options":["Lipid content","Carbohydrate content","Iron content","Iodine content"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: hCG is the hormone with the highest carbohydrate content.  Why A, C, D wrong: The distinguishing feature is high carbohydrate content, not lipid, iron, or iodine."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"How many subunits does hCG have?","options":["One","Two (alpha and beta)","Three","Four"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: hCG has 2 subunits — alpha (α) and beta (β).  Why A, C, D wrong: hCG has exactly two subunits."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"The alpha (α) subunit of hCG is similar to the alpha subunits of which other hormones?","options":["hCG, LH, FSH, and TSH","Insulin and glucagon","Cortisol and aldosterone","Oxytocin and vasopressin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The alpha subunit is non-specific and is similar in hCG, LH, FSH, and TSH.  Why B, C, D wrong: These hormones do not share the common alpha subunit described."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which subunit of hCG is the specific subunit and is tested in all pregnancy tests?","options":["Alpha (α) subunit","Beta (β) subunit","Gamma subunit","Both subunits equally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The beta (β) subunit is the specific subunit; βhCG subunit is tested in all pregnancy tests.  Why A wrong: The alpha subunit is non-specific (shared with LH, FSH, TSH).  Why C wrong: There is no gamma subunit.  Why D wrong: Testing relies on the specific beta subunit."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"What is the first (primary) function of hCG in pregnancy?","options":["To maintain the corpus luteum of pregnancy","To stimulate milk let-down","To cause uterine contractions","To suppress ovulation permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: hCG functions to maintain the corpus luteum of pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Milk let-down is oxytocin's role.  Why C wrong: Uterine contractions relate to oxytocin/prostaglandins.  Why D wrong: This is not a listed function of hCG."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"In males, hCG acts as the first stimulus for which cells to secrete testosterone?","options":["Sertoli cells","Leydig cells","Germ cells","Kupffer cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In males, hCG acts as the first stimulus for Leydig cells to secrete testosterone.  Why A wrong: Sertoli cells support spermatogenesis, not testosterone secretion here.  Why C wrong: Germ cells produce sperm, not testosterone.  Why D wrong: Kupffer cells are hepatic macrophages, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"In a non-pregnant female, the corpus luteum is maintained by which hormone?","options":["hCG","LH","FSH","Estrogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In non-pregnant females, the corpus luteum is maintained by LH.  Why A wrong: hCG maintains the corpus luteum only in pregnancy.  Why C, D wrong: FSH and estrogen do not maintain the corpus luteum as stated."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"hCG is functionally similar to which hormone?","options":["LH","FSH","TSH","Prolactin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: hCG is functionally similar to LH.  Why B, C wrong: FSH and TSH share structural (anatomical) similarity, not functional.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is not the functionally similar hormone."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"hCG is anatomically (structurally) similar to which hormones?","options":["LH, FSH, and TSH","Only LH","Insulin and glucagon","Prolactin and oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: hCG is anatomically similar to LH, FSH, and TSH (due to the shared alpha subunit).  Why B wrong: Functional similarity is only with LH; structural similarity extends to FSH and TSH too.  Why C, D wrong: These hormones do not share the structural similarity."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Synthesis of hCG begins by how many days after fertilization?","options":["2 days","8 days","14 days","22 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Synthesis of hCG begins by 8 days after fertilization (ovulation +8).  Why A wrong: Too early; synthesis begins at 8 days.  Why C wrong: 14 days corresponds to fertilization/ovulation timing, not hCG onset.  Why D wrong: Day 22 of cycle corresponds to the same event but expressed as cycle day, while \"8 days after fertilization\" is the post-fertilization figure."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"In terms of the menstrual cycle, hCG synthesis begins on which cycle day (assuming fertilization at day 14)?","options":["Day 14","Day 22","Day 28","Day 1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: hCG synthesis begins on day 22 of the cycle (ovulation/fertilization at day 14 + 8 days).  Why A wrong: Day 14 is fertilization, not hCG onset.  Why C wrong: Day 28 is the end of the cycle.  Why D wrong: Day 1 is the start of menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"hCG synthesis begins approximately how many days before the missed period?","options":["1–2 days before","5–6 days before","10–12 days before","14 days before"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: hCG synthesis begins 5–6 days before the missed period.  Why A, C, D wrong: These intervals do not match the stated 5–6 days."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which statement about hCG and pregnancy is correct?","options":["hCG is always present in pregnant females and absent in non-pregnant females","hCG is present in both pregnant and non-pregnant females equally","hCG is absent in pregnant females","hCG is present only in males"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: hCG is always present in pregnant females and absent in non-pregnant females.  Why B, C, D wrong: These contradict the stated fact that hCG presence indicates pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"In early pregnancy, hCG levels double approximately every:","options":["24 hours","48 hours","72 hours","96 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In early pregnancy, hCG levels double every 48 hours.  Why A wrong: The range noted is 24–48 hours, but the stated doubling time is 48 hours (24–48 hours range on page 95).  Why C, D wrong: These exceed the stated doubling interval."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"hCG levels reach their maximum at approximately what gestational age?","options":["4–5 weeks","60–80 days of pregnancy / 10 weeks","16 weeks","40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: hCG reaches maximum level at 60–80 days of pregnancy / 10 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Too early for the peak.  Why C wrong: 16 weeks is when hCG reaches its minimum.  Why D wrong: 40 weeks is term; hCG is at its maintained minimum by then."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"At the time of a missed period, the approximate hCG level is:","options":["10 IU/L","100 IU/L (mIU/mL)","50,000 IU/L","100,000 IU/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At the missed period, hCG is approximately 100 IU/L (mIU/mL).  Why A wrong: Lower than the stated missed-period level.  Why C, D wrong: 50,000–100,000 IU/L corresponds to the peak at 60–80 days / 10 weeks, not the missed period."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"At the peak (60–80 days / 10 weeks), the approximate hCG level is:","options":["100 IU/L","500–1,000 IU/L","50,000–100,000 IU/L (10^5 IU/L)","5,000 IU/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: At 60–80 days / 10 weeks, hCG reaches 50,000–100,000 IU/L (10^5 IU/L).  Why A wrong: 100 IU/L is the missed-period level.  Why B, D wrong: These are below the stated peak range."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"After 10 weeks of pregnancy, hCG levels reach their minimum at which gestational age?","options":["12 weeks","16 weeks (100–130 days)","20 weeks","40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After 10 weeks, hCG starts decreasing and reaches a minimum at 16 weeks of pregnancy (100–130 days).  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 16-week (100–130 days) minimum."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"After reaching its minimum level, what happens to hCG for the remainder of pregnancy?","options":["It disappears completely","It is maintained at that minimum level throughout pregnancy","It rises steadily again until term","It doubles every 48 hours again"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At the minimum level, hCG is maintained throughout the rest of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: hCG does not disappear during pregnancy.  Why C, D wrong: hCG does not rise or resume doubling after reaching its minimum."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"After delivery, hCG disappears from the blood in approximately:","options":["2 days","2 weeks","4 weeks","9 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: hCG disappears from blood 2 weeks after delivery.  Why A wrong: 2 days is the disappearance time from urine, not blood.  Why C, D wrong: These are longer than the stated blood clearance after normal delivery."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"After delivery, hCG disappears from the urine in approximately:","options":["2 days","2 weeks","7 weeks","9 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: hCG disappears from urine 2 days after delivery.  Why B wrong: 2 weeks is the blood clearance time.  Why C, D wrong: These correspond to clearance after molar evacuation, not delivery."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"After an abortion, hCG disappears from the blood in approximately:","options":["2 days","2–4 weeks","7 weeks","9 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After abortion, hCG disappears from blood after 2–4 weeks.  Why A wrong: 2 days is urine clearance after normal delivery.  Why C, D wrong: 7 and 9 weeks correspond to partial and complete molar evacuation, respectively."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"After evacuation of a partial hydatidiform mole, hCG disappears in approximately:","options":["2 weeks","4 weeks","7 weeks","9 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After partial mole evacuation, hCG disappears in 7 weeks.  Why A, B wrong: These are shorter than the stated 7 weeks.  Why D wrong: 9 weeks corresponds to a complete mole, not a partial mole."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"After evacuation of a complete hydatidiform mole, hCG disappears in approximately:","options":["4 weeks","7 weeks","9 weeks","2 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After complete mole evacuation, hCG disappears in 9 weeks.  Why A, D wrong: These are shorter than the stated 9 weeks.  Why B wrong: 7 weeks corresponds to a partial mole."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"What is the doubling time of hCG in early pregnancy?","options":["12 hours (6–12 hours)","48 hours (24–48 hours)","72 hours (48–72 hours)","96 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The doubling time of hCG in early pregnancy is 48 hours (range 24–48 hours).  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated doubling time."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"What is the half-life of hCG?","options":["12 hours","24 hours","36 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The half-life of hCG is 36 hours.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the stated 36-hour half-life."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which of the following conditions is associated with INCREASED hCG levels?","options":["Ectopic pregnancy","Hydatidiform mole","Abortion","Trisomy 18 (Edwards' syndrome)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydatidiform mole causes increased hCG levels (mnemonic \"High\" = hydatidiform mole).  Why A, C wrong: Ectopic pregnancy and abortion cause decreased hCG.  Why D wrong: Trisomy 18 (other than 21) causes decreased hCG."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Choriocarcinoma is associated with what change in hCG levels?","options":["Increased hCG","Decreased hCG","No change in hCG","hCG becomes undetectable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Choriocarcinoma causes increased hCG levels (mnemonic \"Court\" = choriocarcinoma).  Why B, C, D wrong: Choriocarcinoma raises, not lowers or abolishes, hCG."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Down syndrome (Trisomy 21) is associated with what change in hCG?","options":["Increased hCG","Decreased hCG","Absent hCG","Normal hCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Down syndrome (Trisomy 21) is associated with increased hCG (mnemonic \"Demands\" = Down syndrome, Trisomy 21).  Why B wrong: Trisomies OTHER than 21 (13, 18) cause decreased hCG; Trisomy 21 raises it.  Why C, D wrong: hCG is increased, not absent or normal."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Rh-negative pregnancy with hydrops fetalis is associated with what change in hCG?","options":["Increased hCG","Decreased hCG","Absent hCG","Unchanged hCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rh-negative pregnancy (hydrops fetalis) is associated with increased hCG (mnemonic \"Riya's\" = Rh negative pregnancy/hydrops fetalis).  Why B, C, D wrong: hCG is increased in this condition."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"A twin (multiple) pregnancy is associated with what change in hCG levels?","options":["Increased hCG","Decreased hCG","Absent hCG","No change"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Twins are associated with increased hCG (mnemonic \"Testimonial\" = Twins).  Why B, C, D wrong: With two placentas, hCG is higher, not lower or absent."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which of the following is associated with DECREASED hCG levels?","options":["Hydatidiform mole","Twins","Ectopic pregnancy","Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Ectopic pregnancy causes decreased hCG (mnemonic \"E\" = Ectopic pregnancy).  Why A, B, D wrong: Hydatidiform mole, twins, and Down syndrome all cause increased hCG."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which trisomies (other than 21) are associated with decreased hCG levels?","options":["Trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome) and Trisomy 18 (Edwards' syndrome)","Trisomy 21 only","Monosomy X only","Trisomy 16 and Trisomy 22"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Trisomies other than 21 — Trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome) and Trisomy 18 (Edwards' syndrome) — are associated with decreased hCG.  Why B wrong: Trisomy 21 causes increased hCG.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the trisomies listed on the page."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Trisomy 13 is also known as which syndrome?","options":["Edwards' syndrome","Patau syndrome","Down syndrome","Turner syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trisomy 13 is Patau syndrome.  Why A wrong: Edwards' syndrome is Trisomy 18.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is Trisomy 21.  Why D wrong: Turner syndrome is monosomy X (45,X)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Trisomy 18 is also known as which syndrome?","options":["Patau syndrome","Edwards' syndrome","Down syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trisomy 18 is Edwards' syndrome.  Why A wrong: Patau syndrome is Trisomy 13.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is Trisomy 21.  Why D wrong: Klinefelter is 47,XXY."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Abortion is associated with what change in hCG levels?","options":["Increased hCG","Decreased hCG","Absent hCG at all times","No change"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Abortion causes decreased hCG (mnemonic \"A\" = Abortion).  Why A wrong: hCG falls, not rises, in abortion.  Why C wrong: hCG decreases but is not immediately absent (clears over 2–4 weeks).  Why D wrong: hCG decreases."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which set of prenatal screening tests for Down syndrome uses hCG?","options":["Triple test, quadruple test, and dual test","Complete blood count and blood grouping","Oral glucose tolerance test only","Non-stress test and biophysical profile"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: hCG is used in Down syndrome screening via the triple test, quadruple test, and dual test.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not use hCG for Down syndrome screening."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Besides Down syndrome screening, what is the other main use of hCG detection listed?","options":["For detecting/confirming pregnancy","For measuring blood glucose","For assessing kidney function","For measuring thyroid function"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: hCG is used for detecting/confirming pregnancy — whenever hCG is detected in blood/urine of a female, it means she is pregnant.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not uses of hCG detection described here."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Detection of hCG in the blood or urine of a female indicates:","options":["She is pregnant","She has diabetes","She has anemia","She is menopausal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Whenever hCG is detected in the blood/urine of a female, it means she is pregnant.  Why B, C, D wrong: hCG detection specifically indicates pregnancy, not these conditions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which two test types can detect hCG to confirm pregnancy?","options":["Urine test and blood test","Stool test and saliva test","Sweat test and hair test","CSF test and pleural fluid test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: hCG for pregnancy confirmation is detected by urine test and blood test.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the sample types used for hCG-based pregnancy testing."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"What is a key ADVANTAGE of the urine pregnancy test over the blood test?","options":["It can be done at home and can detect pregnancy before the missed period","It requires laboratory equipment","It is more accurate in ectopic pregnancy","It measures exact hormone concentration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The urine test's advantage is that it can be done at home; note the page states it can detect pregnancy relative to the missed period at home use.  Why B wrong: Requiring lab equipment describes the blood test, which is a disadvantage there.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the advantages listed for the urine test."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"What is a DISADVANTAGE of the blood pregnancy test as listed on the page?","options":["It cannot be done at home; pregnancy can't be detected before missed period","It has high false positive rates","It requires first morning urine","It cannot detect twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The disadvantage listed is that the blood test cannot be done at home, and (in that context) pregnancy can't be detected before the missed period without lab access.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the disadvantages listed on the page."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"For a urine pregnancy test kit, which urine sample is preferable?","options":["Random daytime urine","First voided urine of the morning","Post-meal urine","24-hour pooled urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first voided urine of the morning is preferable because it is most concentrated (highest hCG).  Why A, C wrong: These are more dilute and less preferable.  Why D wrong: A pooled 24-hour sample is not used for the kit."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Which type of assay is used in the urine pregnancy test kit?","options":["2-site sandwich immunoassay","Competitive radioimmunoassay","Agglutination inhibition only","ELISA spot assay"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The urine pregnancy kit uses a 2-site sandwich immunoassay.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the assay type stated for the kit."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"What is the sensitivity (detection threshold) of hCG in urine for the pregnancy test kit?","options":["5–10 mIU/mL","30–50 mIU/mL","100–200 mIU/mL","500 mIU/mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The kit's sensitivity is 30–50 mIU/mL of hCG present in urine.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 30–50 mIU/mL sensitivity."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"The urine pregnancy test kit becomes positive (earliest) on which day?","options":["Day of implantation","Day of missed period","Day 8 after fertilization","Two weeks after missed period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The urine kit becomes positive (earliest) on the day of the missed period.  Why A wrong: Implantation precedes detectable levels for the kit.  Why C wrong: hCG synthesis begins by day 8, but the kit turns positive at the missed period.  Why D wrong: It becomes positive at the missed period, not two weeks after."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"The site of synthesis of hCG (reconfirmed on the page) is the:","options":["Cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast","Decidua","Corpus luteum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The site of synthesis of hCG is the syncytiotrophoblast.  Why A wrong: Cytotrophoblast forms the chorion laeve, not the main hCG source.  Why C, D wrong: Decidua and corpus luteum are not the hCG synthesis site."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"A pregnant woman at 10 weeks gestation has an hCG level in the range of 50,000–100,000 IU/L. This finding is best interpreted as:","options":["Abnormally high, suggesting molar pregnancy in all cases","The expected peak level for this gestational age","Abnormally low for gestation","Consistent with an ectopic pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: hCG peaks at 60–80 days / 10 weeks with a level of 50,000–100,000 IU/L, so this is the expected peak.  Why A wrong: This is the physiologic peak, not necessarily molar.  Why C wrong: It is the peak (maximum), not low.  Why D wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is associated with decreased, not peak, hCG."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"A woman had a normal vaginal delivery 10 days ago and a urine hCG test is still weakly positive. Based on the disappearance timelines, this is:","options":["Abnormal, indicating retained products","Expected, as urine hCG clears about 2 days after delivery — but blood clears in 2 weeks (reassess sample source)","Diagnostic of choriocarcinoma","Diagnostic of a new pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the notes, hCG disappears from urine ~2 days and from blood ~2 weeks after delivery; a persistent positive at 10 days warrants correlating with these timelines/sample type before concluding pathology.  Why A, C, D wrong: The notes give normal clearance timelines; jumping to retained products, choriocarcinoma, or new pregnancy is not supported by the page alone."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Select ALL conditions associated with INCREASED hCG levels. (Select all that apply)","options":["Hydatidiform mole","Choriocarcinoma","Ectopic pregnancy","Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)","Twins"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Increased hCG is seen in hydatidiform mole, choriocarcinoma, Down syndrome (Trisomy 21), Rh-negative pregnancy/hydrops fetalis, and twins.  Why C wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is associated with DECREASED hCG."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Select ALL conditions associated with DECREASED hCG levels. (Select all that apply)","options":["Trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome)","Trisomy 18 (Edwards' syndrome)","Ectopic pregnancy","Abortion","Twins"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased hCG is seen in trisomies other than 21 (Trisomy 13 – Patau, Trisomy 18 – Edwards'), ectopic pregnancy, and abortion.  Why E wrong: Twins are associated with INCREASED hCG."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"Select ALL the correct descriptors of the amnion. (Select all that apply)","options":["Inner layer","Shiny and smooth","Formed by ectoderm","Rough and dull with blood vessels","No blood vessels and no nerve supply"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The amnion is the inner layer, shiny/smooth, formed by ectoderm, avascular with no nerves, and more strong.  Why D wrong: Rough, dull, with blood vessels describes the chorion, not the amnion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Umbilical cord cross-section anatomy, single umbilical artery abnormality…","question":"A pregnant woman asks why she should use her first morning urine for a home pregnancy test. The best nursing explanation is:","options":["Morning urine is sterile","First voided morning urine is most concentrated, giving the highest detectable hCG","Morning urine has less protein","It reduces the chance of a false positive from food"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first voided morning urine is preferable because it is most concentrated, maximizing detectable hCG (kit sensitivity 30–50 mIU/mL).  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the reasons the sample is preferred for hCG detection."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Which blood test is the MOST sensitive for detecting human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) in pregnancy?","options":["Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)","Radioimmunoassay (RIA)","Latex agglutination test","Haemagglutination inhibition test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most sensitive blood test for hCG is Radioimmunoassay (RIA).  Why A wrong: ELISA is sensitive but the source specifies RIA as the most sensitive.  Why C wrong: Latex agglutination is a less sensitive qualitative urine test.  Why D wrong: Haemagglutination inhibition is an older, less sensitive method."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The sensitivity of the radioimmunoassay (RIA) for hCG in blood is:","options":["0.001 mIU/mL","0.01 mIU/mL","0.1 mIU/mL","1 mIU/mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state RIA sensitivity is 0.001 mIU/mL of hCG in blood.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are higher (less sensitive) values not given in the source; 0.001 mIU/mL is the stated sensitivity."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Using the blood hCG test, pregnancy can be detected earliest at what time relative to the missed period?","options":["On the day of the missed period","5 to 6 days before the missed period","5 to 6 days after the missed period","2 weeks after the missed period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the blood hCG test is earliest positive 5 to 6 days before the missed period.  Why A wrong: It is detectable before, not only on, the day of the missed period.  Why C wrong: The source specifies before, not after, the missed period.  Why D wrong: This is far too late; detection is possible days before the missed period."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Nausea and vomiting during pregnancy (morning sickness) is mainly due to which hormone?","options":["Human placental lactogen (HPL)","Progesterone","Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)","Estriol"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state morning sickness / nausea and vomiting in pregnancy is due to the hormone hCG.  Why A wrong: HPL causes insulin resistance, not morning sickness.  Why B wrong: Progesterone relaxes smooth muscle but the source attributes nausea/vomiting to hCG.  Why D wrong: Estriol is the most specific estrogen, not linked to morning sickness here."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"In twin pregnancy and hydatidiform mole, hCG levels are increased, leading to increased nausea and vomiting. This severe form is called:","options":["Hyperemesis gravidarum","Hyperbilirubinemia","Hyperglycemia","Hyperreflexia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show that in twins & hydatid mole, hCG↑ → ↑nausea & vomiting → Hyperemesis Gravidarum.  Why B wrong: Hyperbilirubinemia is a jaundice condition, not vomiting.  Why C wrong: Hyperglycemia is high blood sugar, unrelated to this pathway.  Why D wrong: Hyperreflexia is a neurological sign (e.g., pre-eclampsia), not vomiting."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Human Placental Lactogen (HPL) is also known as:","options":["Human chorionic gonadotropin","Human chorionic somatomammotropin (HCS)","Human chorionic thyrotropin","Human placental estrogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HPL is also called Human chorionic somatomammotropin (HCS).  Why A wrong: hCG is a separate hormone responsible for morning sickness.  Why C wrong: Human chorionic thyrotropin is a distinct placental hormone.  Why D wrong: This is not the alternate name given for HPL."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Human Placental Lactogen (HPL) is synthesized by which structure?","options":["Cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast","Decidua","Corpus luteum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HPL is synthesized by the syncytiotrophoblast.  Why A wrong: The source specifies syncytiotrophoblast, not cytotrophoblast.  Why C wrong: The decidua is transformed endometrium, not the site of HPL synthesis.  Why D wrong: Corpus luteum produces progesterone early in pregnancy, not HPL."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"HPL can be detected earliest by which week of pregnancy, after which it continuously increases?","options":["1 week","3 weeks","12 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HPL is detected earliest by 3 weeks of pregnancy and continuously increases.  Why A wrong: Detection is stated at 3 weeks, not 1 week.  Why C wrong: 12 weeks is too late; detection begins by 3 weeks.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks is far later than the earliest detection point."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Maximum levels of HPL are seen at approximately which week of pregnancy?","options":["24 weeks","28 weeks","36 weeks","40 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maximum HPL levels are seen at 36 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A, B wrong: 24 and 28 weeks relate to peak insulin resistance, not peak HPL.  Why D wrong: The source specifies 36 weeks, not 40 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"What is the primary aim (purpose) of the hormone HPL during pregnancy?","options":["To provide glucose to the fetus","To stimulate uterine contractions","To initiate lactation","To mature the fetal lungs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the aim of HPL is to provide glucose to the fetus.  Why B wrong: Uterine contractions at term are triggered by maternal estrogen.  Why C wrong: The notes explicitly state HPL has no role in lactation.  Why D wrong: Lung maturation is not the stated aim of HPL."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"How does HPL make more glucose available to the fetus (Function 1)?","options":["By increasing maternal insulin secretion","By bringing about insulin resistance in the mother, increasing maternal glucose","By decreasing maternal blood glucose","By increasing fetal insulin production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HPL brings about insulin resistance in the mother (↑glucose), making it easily available for the fetus.  Why A wrong: HPL causes insulin resistance, not increased insulin secretion effectiveness.  Why C wrong: HPL raises maternal glucose, it does not decrease it.  Why D wrong: The mechanism involves maternal insulin resistance, not fetal insulin."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"According to the notes, HPL brings about gluconeogenesis and increases fatty acids in the mother. What is the purpose of this increased fatty acid?","options":["It is stored as fetal fat","It is used by the mother's body for energy and spares glucose for the fetus","It suppresses maternal appetite","It is converted directly into fetal glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fatty acid is used by the mother's body for energy and spares glucose for the fetus.  Why A wrong: The source describes maternal energy use, not fetal fat storage.  Why C wrong: Appetite suppression is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Fatty acid is used for maternal energy, not converted to fetal glucose."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"What role does HPL play in lactation?","options":["It initiates milk production","It has no role in lactation","It is essential for milk let-down","It suppresses prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes clearly state HPL has no role in lactation.  Why A wrong: HPL does not initiate milk production.  Why C wrong: Milk let-down is mediated by oxytocin, and HPL has no lactation role.  Why D wrong: The source states no role, not suppression of prolactin."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Insulin resistance in pregnancy is mainly due to which hormone?","options":["Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)","Human placental lactogen (HPL)","Progesterone","Estradiol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state insulin resistance in pregnancy is mainly due to Human placental lactogen (HPL).  Why A wrong: hCG causes morning sickness, not insulin resistance.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is a smooth muscle relaxant, not the main cause of insulin resistance.  Why D wrong: Estradiol is the most common estrogen, not the cause of insulin resistance."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Insulin resistance in pregnancy is maximum between which gestational weeks?","options":["12–16 weeks","24–28 weeks","32–36 weeks","36–40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state insulin resistance is maximum between 24–28 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A, C, D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated peak of 24–28 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Because of insulin resistance, pregnancy is considered to be a:","options":["Hypoglycemic state","Diabetogenic state","Ketogenic state","Hypercoagulable state only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that because of insulin resistance, pregnancy is a diabetogenic state.  Why A wrong: Insulin resistance raises glucose, opposite of hypoglycemic.  Why C wrong: The source specifically calls it diabetogenic, not ketogenic.  Why D wrong: While pregnancy is hypercoagulable, the notes here specify diabetogenic due to insulin resistance."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Human placental lactogen (HPL) is a marker of:","options":["Fetal lung maturity","Placental well being","Neural tube defects","Maternal thyroid function"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HPL is a marker of placental well being.  Why A wrong: Lung maturity is assessed by other markers (e.g., L/S ratio), not HPL.  Why C wrong: Neural tube defects are screened by AFP/triple test, not HPL.  Why D wrong: HPL is not a maternal thyroid marker."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The placenta is described as an \"incomplete endocrine organ\" because:","options":["It cannot produce any hormones on its own","Without the help of the fetus, the placenta cannot form estrogen","It lacks the enzymes to produce progesterone","It requires the maternal pituitary to make hCG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the placenta is an incomplete endocrine organ because without the help of the fetus, the placenta cannot form estrogen.  Why A wrong: The placenta does produce hormones (e.g., hCG, HPL, progesterone).  Why C wrong: The placenta can form progesterone from maternal LDL.  Why D wrong: The stated reason is inability to form estrogen without the fetus, not hCG dependency on maternal pituitary."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Which fetal structure produces the DHEA sulphate that the placenta uses to form estrogen?","options":["Fetal liver","Fetal adrenal gland","Fetal kidney","Fetal thyroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the adrenal gland of the fetus forms DHEA sulphate, which is used by the placenta to form estrogen.  Why A wrong: The fetal liver is not stated as the DHEA sulphate source here.  Why C wrong: The fetal kidney contributes urine to amniotic fluid, not DHEA sulphate.  Why D wrong: The fetal thyroid is not the source of DHEA sulphate."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"DHEA sulphate produced by the fetal adrenal gland is used by the placenta to form:","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","hCG","HPL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state DHEA sulphate is used by the placenta to form estrogen.  Why A wrong: Progesterone precursor is maternal LDL, not DHEA sulphate.  Why C wrong: hCG is produced by syncytiotrophoblast, not from DHEA sulphate.  Why D wrong: HPL is synthesized by syncytiotrophoblast, not from DHEA sulphate."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The most COMMON estrogen in pregnancy is:","options":["Estrone (E1)","Estradiol (E2)","Estriol (E3)","Estetrol (E4)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common estrogen in pregnancy is Estradiol = E2.  Why A wrong: Estrone (E1) is not identified as most common here.  Why C wrong: Estriol (E3) is the most specific estrogen, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Estetrol is not the answer given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The most SPECIFIC estrogen in pregnancy is:","options":["Estradiol (E2)","Estriol (E3)","Estrone (E1)","Progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most specific estrogen in pregnancy is Estriol = E3.  Why A wrong: Estradiol is the most common, not the most specific.  Why C wrong: Estrone is not identified as most specific.  Why D wrong: Progesterone is not an estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Which estrogen is measured in the TRIPLE TEST?","options":["Estradiol (E2)","Estriol (E3)","Estrone (E1)","Estetrol (E4)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the estrogen measured in the triple test is Estriol = E3.  Why A wrong: Estradiol is the most common estrogen, not the triple test marker.  Why C wrong: Estrone is not the triple test estrogen.  Why D wrong: Estetrol is not stated as the triple test marker."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Which of the following fetal conditions results in the fetus forming LESS DHEA sulphate (and hence less estrogen)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fetal death","Down syndrome","Anencephaly","Twin pregnancy","Hydatidiform mole"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list fetal death, Down syndrome, and anencephaly as conditions where the fetus forms less DHEA sulphate → less estrogen.  Why D wrong: Twin pregnancy is associated with increased hCG, not decreased DHEA sulphate.  Why E wrong: Hydatidiform mole raises hCG; it is not in the low-DHEA list."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"In anencephaly, which is described as a type of:","options":["Chromosomal aneuploidy","Neural tube defect","Metabolic disorder","Connective tissue disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label anencephaly as a neural tube defect.  Why A wrong: Anencephaly is a neural tube defect, not primarily a chromosomal aneuploidy.  Why C wrong: It is a structural neural tube defect, not a metabolic disorder.  Why D wrong: It is not a connective tissue disorder."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"What is the main problem (defining feature) in anencephaly?","options":["Absent limbs","Skull/cranium of the fetus is absent","Absent kidneys","Absent spinal cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the main problem in anencephaly is that the skull/cranium of the fetus is absent.  Why A wrong: Limb absence is not the defining feature.  Why C wrong: Renal absence (Potter's) is a different condition.  Why D wrong: The main problem stated is absent skull/cranium, not spinal cord."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"In anencephaly, the \"additional problem\" that begins the sequence leading to post-term pregnancy is:","options":["Absent adrenal gland","Absent kidneys","Absent liver","Absent placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the additional problem is an absent adrenal gland, leading to absent DHEA sulphate.  Why B wrong: Kidney absence is not part of this pathway.  Why C wrong: The liver is not implicated in the sequence.  Why D wrong: The placenta is present; the adrenal gland is absent."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Arrange the correct pathophysiological sequence explaining post-term pregnancy in anencephaly.","options":["Adrenal gland absent → Absent DHEA sulphate → Placenta estrogen↓ → Cannot signal uterus → Uterus doesn't contract → Post-term pregnancy","Adrenal gland absent → ↑Estrogen → Uterus contracts → Preterm labor","Absent kidneys → ↓Amniotic fluid → Post-term pregnancy","Absent skull → ↑DHEA sulphate → Post-term pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the flow: adrenal gland absent → absent DHEA sulphate → placenta estrogen decreases → cannot signal uterus → uterus doesn't contract → leads to post-term pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Estrogen decreases (not increases) and pregnancy is prolonged, not preterm.  Why C wrong: The pathway involves DHEA/estrogen, not amniotic fluid volume.  Why D wrong: DHEA sulphate is absent (decreased), not increased."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"What is the role of maternal estrogen at term?","options":["It relaxes the uterus","It tells the uterus to start contracting, so labor starts","It maintains pregnancy","It decreases blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maternal estrogen at term tells the uterus to start contracting, and labor starts.  Why A wrong: Estrogen signals contraction, not relaxation.  Why C wrong: Maintaining pregnancy is the role of progesterone.  Why D wrong: BP-lowering is a progesterone (smooth muscle relaxant) effect."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"In early pregnancy (up to 6 weeks), the main source of progesterone is:","options":["Placenta","Corpus luteum of pregnancy","Fetal adrenal gland","Maternal ovary follicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in early pregnancy (up to 6 weeks), progesterone comes from the corpus luteum of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The placenta takes over only later (6–8 weeks onward).  Why C wrong: Fetal adrenal produces DHEA sulphate, not progesterone.  Why D wrong: The corpus luteum, not a follicle, is the source."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"At around 6–8 weeks of pregnancy, which structure takes over the function of the corpus luteum for progesterone production?","options":["Fetal liver","Placenta","Maternal adrenal gland","Amnion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that at 6–8 weeks, the placenta takes over the function of the corpus luteum.  Why A wrong: The fetal liver is not involved in this takeover.  Why C wrong: The maternal adrenal gland does not take over progesterone production here.  Why D wrong: The amnion is a membrane, not a progesterone source."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Beyond 8 weeks of pregnancy (≥8 weeks), the main source of progesterone is the:","options":["Corpus luteum","Placenta","Fetal adrenal gland","Maternal ovary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that at ≥8 weeks of pregnancy, the placenta is the source of progesterone.  Why A wrong: The corpus luteum supplies progesterone only up to about 6 weeks.  Why C wrong: The fetal adrenal makes DHEA sulphate, not progesterone.  Why D wrong: The maternal ovary is not the ≥8-week source."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"What is the precursor used by the placenta to form progesterone?","options":["Maternal LDL (low-density lipoprotein)","Fetal DHEA sulphate","Maternal HDL","Cholesterol from fetal liver"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the placenta uses maternal LDL to form progesterone.  Why B wrong: DHEA sulphate is the precursor for estrogen, not progesterone.  Why C wrong: The source specifies LDL, not HDL.  Why D wrong: The precursor is maternal LDL, not fetal liver cholesterol."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Progesterone is responsible for converting the endometrium into decidua. This process is known as:","options":["Implantation reaction","Decidual reaction","Trophoblastic invasion","Chorionic reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone converts endometrium to decidua, called the decidual reaction.  Why A wrong: Implantation is a separate process from the decidual conversion.  Why C wrong: Trophoblastic invasion involves trophoblast cells, not the decidual reaction term.  Why D wrong: \"Chorionic reaction\" is not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Which is the main hormone responsible for MAINTAINING pregnancy?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","hCG","HPL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone is the main hormone for maintaining pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Estrogen signals uterine contraction at term, not maintenance.  Why C wrong: hCG supports the corpus luteum early but is not called the main maintenance hormone here.  Why D wrong: HPL provides glucose to the fetus and is a placental marker."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Below which progesterone level does the source state pregnancy leads to abortion?","options":["<25 ng","<50 ng","<100 ng","<10 ng"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if progesterone levels fall below 25 ng, it leads to abortion.  Why B, C, D wrong: These thresholds are not the value given; the source specifies <25 ng."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Progesterone acts on smooth muscle in pregnancy as a:","options":["Stimulant (contractor)","Relaxant","Vasoconstrictor","Diuretic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone is a smooth muscle relaxant in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: It relaxes rather than stimulates smooth muscle.  Why C wrong: By relaxing blood vessels it lowers resistance (vasodilator effect), not vasoconstriction.  Why D wrong: A diuretic effect is not described."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"By relaxing blood vessels, progesterone causes which change in blood pressure during pregnancy?","options":["Blood pressure increases due to increased resistance","Resistance decreases, so blood pressure decreases in pregnancy","Blood pressure remains unchanged","Resistance increases, so blood pressure decreases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone relaxes blood vessels → resistance decreases → BP decreases in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Vascular resistance decreases, not increases.  Why C wrong: BP is stated to decrease, not stay unchanged.  Why D wrong: Resistance decreases (not increases) leading to lower BP."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Constipation in pregnancy caused by progesterone is due to:","options":["Increased peristalsis","Decreased peristalsis","Increased water absorption only","Increased gastric acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state constipation in pregnancy is due to decreased peristalsis (a progesterone smooth muscle relaxant effect).  Why A wrong: Peristalsis is decreased, not increased.  Why C wrong: The stated cause is decreased peristalsis, not water absorption.  Why D wrong: Gastric acid change is not the cause given for constipation."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Heartburn in pregnancy caused by progesterone is due to relaxation of the:","options":["Pyloric sphincter","Lower esophageal sphincter","Ileocecal valve","Anal sphincter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the lower esophageal sphincter relaxes, causing heartburn in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The pyloric sphincter is not the one implicated in heartburn here.  Why C wrong: The ileocecal valve is not involved in heartburn.  Why D wrong: The anal sphincter relates to defecation, not heartburn."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Amniotic fluid is also known as:","options":["Liquor amnii","Meconium","Vernix caseosa","Colostrum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state amniotic fluid ⇒ Liquor amnii.  Why B wrong: Meconium is fetal stool, not amniotic fluid.  Why C wrong: Vernix caseosa is the fetal skin coating.  Why D wrong: Colostrum is early breast milk."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"One key function of amniotic fluid is that it distends the uterus. What is the purpose of this?","options":["To increase maternal blood volume","So that the fetus gets enough space to grow","To trigger labor","To nourish the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state amniotic fluid distends the uterus so that the fetus gets enough space to grow.  Why A wrong: It relates to fetal space, not maternal blood volume.  Why C wrong: Distension for growth is described, not labor triggering.  Why D wrong: It concerns fetal space, not placental nourishment."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The membranes that fuse to enclose the amniotic fluid, as labeled in the figure, are the:","options":["Chorionic and amniotic membranes (fused)","Decidua basalis and capsularis","Myometrium and endometrium","Serosa and mucosa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the \"fused chorionic and amniotic membranes\" enclosing the amniotic fluid.  Why B wrong: Decidual layers are maternal, not the fused fetal membranes labeled.  Why C wrong: These are uterine wall layers, not the amniotic sac membranes.  Why D wrong: Serosa/mucosa are general tissue layers, not labeled here."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The specific gravity of amniotic fluid is:","options":["1.001 to 1.003","1.008 to 1.010","1.015 to 1.020","1.025 to 1.030"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the specific gravity of amniotic fluid is 1.008 to 1.010.  Why A, C, D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated 1.008–1.010."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The pH of amniotic fluid is:","options":["Acidic, between 5–6","Alkaline, between 7–7.5 (about 7.2)","Neutral, exactly 7.0","Strongly alkaline, above 8"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the pH is alkaline, between 7–7.5, approximately 7.2.  Why A wrong: Amniotic fluid is alkaline, not acidic.  Why C wrong: The stated value is about 7.2, in the alkaline 7–7.5 range, not exactly 7.0.  Why D wrong: The pH is 7–7.5, not above 8."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The alkaline pH of amniotic fluid is the basis of which bedside test used to confirm rupture of membranes?","options":["It turns nitrazine/litmus paper blue (alkaline)","It turns it red (acidic)","It has no effect on pH paper","It bleaches the paper white"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Because amniotic fluid is alkaline (pH 7–7.5), it turns pH/nitrazine paper toward blue, distinguishing it from acidic vaginal secretions (this follows from the stated alkaline pH).  Why B wrong: Acidic red is the opposite of the alkaline reaction.  Why C wrong: An alkaline fluid does change pH paper color.  Why D wrong: It changes color rather than bleaching the paper."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The osmolality of amniotic fluid is:","options":["150 mOsm/L","250 mOsm/L","290 mOsm/L","350 mOsm/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the osmolality of amniotic fluid is 250 mOsm/L.  Why A, C, D wrong: These values do not match the stated 250 mOsm/L."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The rate of amniotic fluid turnover is:","options":["100 cc/hour","250 cc/hour","500 cc/hour","1000 cc/hour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the rate of amniotic fluid turnover is 500 cc/hour.  Why A, B, D wrong: These values do not match the stated 500 cc/hour."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Amniotic fluid is completely replaced approximately every:","options":["1 hour","3 hours","6 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state amniotic fluid is replaced every 3 hours.  Why A, C, D wrong: These intervals do not match the stated 3 hours."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The volume of amniotic fluid at 12 weeks of pregnancy is approximately:","options":["50 mL","200 mL","500 mL","800 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the volume at 12 weeks is 50 mL.  Why B, C, D wrong: These larger volumes correspond to later gestation, not 12 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Maximum amniotic fluid volume (about 1 liter) is seen between which weeks of pregnancy?","options":["24–28 weeks","30–34 weeks","36–38 weeks","40–42 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maximum amniotic fluid (Volume = 1 liter) is seen between 36–38 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A, B, D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated 36–38 weeks peak."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The maximum volume of amniotic fluid (seen at 36–38 weeks) is approximately:","options":["500 mL","800 mL","1 liter","2 liters"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maximum amniotic fluid volume equals 1 liter.  Why A, B wrong: These are lower than the stated maximum.  Why D wrong: 2 liters at term is the threshold for polyhydramnios, not the normal maximum."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"What happens to amniotic fluid volume as pregnancy advances after 38 weeks?","options":["It increases","It decreases","It remains constant","It doubles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that as pregnancy advances after 38 weeks, the volume of amniotic fluid decreases.  Why A wrong: The volume decreases, not increases, after 38 weeks.  Why C wrong: It changes (decreases), not stays constant.  Why D wrong: It decreases rather than doubling."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The volume of amniotic fluid at 40 weeks of pregnancy is approximately:","options":["1 liter","800 mL","500 mL","200 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the volume at 40 weeks is 800 mL.  Why A wrong: 1 liter is the peak seen at 36–38 weeks, not 40 weeks.  Why C, D wrong: These are lower than the stated 800 mL at 40 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"If the volume of amniotic fluid is LESS than 200 mL at term, the condition is called:","options":["Polyhydramnios","Oligohydramnios","Anhydramnios","Hydrops fetalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if amniotic fluid volume is less than 200 mL at term, it is oligohydramnios.  Why A wrong: Polyhydramnios is excess fluid (>2 liters), the opposite.  Why C wrong: Anhydramnios is complete absence, not the term used for <200 mL here.  Why D wrong: Hydrops fetalis is fetal fluid accumulation, unrelated to this definition."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"If the volume of amniotic fluid is MORE than 2 litres at term, the condition is called:","options":["Oligohydramnios","Polyhydramnios","Normal pregnancy","Meconium aspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if amniotic fluid volume is more than 2 litres at term, it is polyhydramnios.  Why A wrong: Oligohydramnios is deficient fluid (<200 mL), the opposite.  Why C wrong: >2 litres is abnormal, not a normal pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Meconium aspiration is a neonatal respiratory condition, not a fluid volume definition."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Amniotic fluid is derived from which source(s)?","options":["Only the mother","Only the fetus","Both mother and fetus","Only the placenta"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state amniotic fluid comes from both mother + fetus.  Why A wrong: The fetus also contributes, especially later.  Why B wrong: The mother contributes, especially early.  Why D wrong: The placenta is only a minor contributor, not the sole source."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Who is the main contributor of amniotic fluid in the EARLY weeks of pregnancy?","options":["The mother","The fetus","The placenta","The umbilical cord"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the mother is the main contributor in early weeks of pregnancy.  Why B wrong: The fetus is the main contributor later, not early.  Why C wrong: The placenta is a minor contributor.  Why D wrong: The umbilical cord is not a stated contributor."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Who is the main contributor of amniotic fluid in the LATER weeks of pregnancy?","options":["The mother","The fetus","The placenta","The decidua"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetus is the main contributor in later weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The mother is the main contributor early, not later.  Why C wrong: The placenta is only a minor contributor.  Why D wrong: The decidua is not a stated contributor."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"If the pregnancy is less than 12 weeks, the major contributor of amniotic fluid is:","options":["Ultrafiltrate of maternal plasma","Diffusion from fetal skin","Fetal urine","Pulmonary fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states for pregnancy <12 weeks the major contributor is the ultrafiltrate of maternal plasma.  Why B wrong: Diffusion from fetal skin dominates between 12–20 weeks.  Why C wrong: Fetal urine is the contributor at ≥20 weeks.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary fluid is only a minor contributor."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Between 12 and 20 weeks of pregnancy, the major contributor of amniotic fluid is:","options":["Ultrafiltrate of maternal plasma","Diffusion from fetal skin","Fetal urine","Placental fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states between 12 and 20 weeks the major contributor is diffusion from fetal skin.  Why A wrong: Ultrafiltrate of maternal plasma is the <12-week source.  Why C wrong: Fetal urine is the ≥20-week source (after skin cornification).  Why D wrong: Placental fluid is a minor contributor."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"When pregnancy is ≥20 weeks, the major contributor of amniotic fluid is:","options":["Ultrafiltrate of maternal plasma","Diffusion from fetal skin","Fetal urine","Pulmonary fluid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states for pregnancy ≥20 weeks the major contributor is fetal urine.  Why A wrong: Maternal plasma ultrafiltrate is the <12-week source.  Why B wrong: Diffusion from fetal skin occurs before 20 weeks, before cornification.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary fluid is a minor contributor."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Cornification (keratinisation) of fetal skin occurs at:","options":["Before 12 weeks","After 20 weeks of pregnancy","At 40 weeks","Only after birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state cornification of fetal skin occurs after 20 weeks of pregnancy (which is why skin diffusion stops and urine becomes the main source).  Why A wrong: Diffusion from skin still occurs before 20 weeks, so cornification is later.  Why C wrong: Cornification occurs after 20 weeks, well before 40 weeks.  Why D wrong: It occurs in utero after 20 weeks, not only after birth."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"The OVERALL major contributor of amniotic fluid is:","options":["Fetal urine","Maternal plasma","Fetal skin diffusion","Placental fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the overall major contributor of amniotic fluid is fetal urine.  Why B wrong: Maternal plasma ultrafiltrate is only the early (<12 weeks) contributor.  Why C wrong: Skin diffusion is limited to before 20 weeks.  Why D wrong: Placental fluid is a minor contributor."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"Which of the following are listed as MINOR contributors of amniotic fluid? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pulmonary fluid","Placental fluid","Fetal urine","Ultrafiltrate of maternal plasma","Diffusion from fetal skin"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list pulmonary fluid and placental fluid as minor contributors.  Why C wrong: Fetal urine is the overall MAJOR contributor, not minor.  Why D wrong: Maternal plasma ultrafiltrate is a major early contributor.  Why E wrong: Fetal skin diffusion is a major contributor between 12–20 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"In the fetal amniotic fluid circulation diagram, the fetus removes/regulates amniotic fluid mainly by which action, and adds to it by which?","options":["Swallows fluid (removal) and produces urine (addition)","Urinates (removal) and swallows (addition)","Cries (removal) and breathes (addition)","Absorbs through placenta only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows the fetus \"Swallow\"ing amniotic fluid (a route of removal) and passing \"Urine\" (a route of addition/production).  Why B wrong: This reverses the roles; swallowing removes and urine adds.  Why C wrong: Crying/breathing are not the labeled mechanisms in the diagram.  Why D wrong: Placental absorption alone is not what the diagram depicts."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"A patient at term has an amniotic fluid volume measured at 150 mL. This finding is consistent with:","options":["Polyhydramnios","Normal term volume","Oligohydramnios","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define <200 mL at term as oligohydramnios; 150 mL is below 200 mL.  Why A wrong: Polyhydramnios requires >2 litres.  Why B wrong: Normal term volume is around 800 mL, not 150 mL.  Why D wrong: Twin pregnancy is associated with higher (not lower) fluid volumes typically."},{"section":"Batch 020 · hCG blood test (RIA sensitivity, earliest detection)…","question":"A pregnant woman develops severe nausea and vomiting; ultrasound reveals a hydatidiform mole. The most likely hormonal explanation for her symptoms is:","options":["Markedly elevated hCG","Low progesterone","Elevated HPL","Low estriol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state hydatid mole causes hCG↑, leading to increased nausea and vomiting (hyperemesis gravidarum).  Why B wrong: Low progesterone is linked to abortion risk, not hyperemesis.  Why C wrong: HPL causes insulin resistance, not nausea/vomiting.  Why D wrong: Low estriol relates to fetal problems, not molar-associated vomiting."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Amniotic fluid volume is kept in balance because the fetus performs which two roles with respect to the fluid?","options":["It only produces amniotic fluid","It only swallows amniotic fluid","It both produces and swallows amniotic fluid","It neither produces nor swallows amniotic fluid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state amniotic fluid is kept in balance as the fetus \"not only produces amniotic fluid but it also swallows it,\" so both processes maintain equilibrium.  Why A wrong: Production alone would not maintain balance; swallowing is also required.  Why B wrong: Swallowing alone ignores the fetal contribution to fluid production (mainly urine).  Why D wrong: The fetus is actively involved in both production and removal of fluid."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Fetal swallowing movements begin by approximately which gestational age?","options":["6–8 weeks","10–12 weeks","16–18 weeks","20–24 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetal swallowing movements begin by 10–12 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Too early; swallowing has not yet begun.  Why C wrong: Later than the stated onset of 10–12 weeks.  Why D wrong: Well beyond the stated onset."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"How much amniotic fluid does the fetus swallow per day (as per the notes)?","options":["50–100 mL/day","100–150 mL/day","200–500 mL/day","500–1000 mL/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetus swallows 200–500 mL of amniotic fluid per day.  Why A/B wrong: These volumes are lower than the stated 200–500 mL/day.  Why D wrong: This exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"A fetus with renal agenesis (a kidney problem) produces less urine. What amniotic fluid abnormality results?","options":["Polyhydramnios","Oligohydramnios","Normal amniotic fluid index","Anhydramnios only with normal AFI"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the flow, renal agenesis → less urine → amniotic fluid decreases → OLIGOHYDRAMNIOS.  Why A wrong: Polyhydramnios is excess fluid, caused by swallowing defects, not reduced urine output.  Why C wrong: The AFI would be reduced, not normal.  Why D wrong: The named outcome in the notes is oligohydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In the notes, esophageal atresia in the fetus is used as an example of a defect that leads to which condition?","options":["Oligohydramnios","Polyhydramnios","Normal fluid volume","Anhydramnios"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Esophagus atresia is a swallowing defect → the fetus cannot swallow fluid → POLYHYDRAMNIOS.  Why A wrong: Oligohydramnios results from reduced fluid (e.g., renal problems), not impaired swallowing.  Why C wrong: Impaired swallowing causes fluid to accumulate, not remain normal.  Why D wrong: Anhydramnios is near-absence of fluid; a swallowing defect causes excess (polyhydramnios)."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"What is the normal colour of amniotic fluid at term?","options":["Colourless clear","Straw coloured","Golden yellow","Green"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state amniotic fluid is colorless but at term it is STRAW COLORED.  Why A wrong: Colourless describes early fluid; at term it is straw coloured.  Why C wrong: Golden yellow is abnormal (bilirubin/Rh incompatibility).  Why D wrong: Green indicates meconium (fetal distress), an abnormal finding."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Green coloured amniotic fluid indicates the presence of which substance?","options":["Bilirubin","Meconium","Blood","Bile salts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Green colour means the amniotic fluid has got meconium.  Why A wrong: Bilirubin causes golden yellow colour.  Why C wrong: Blood produces dark red colour (abruptio placenta).  Why D wrong: Bile salts are not the cause listed for green fluid."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Green coloured (meconium-stained) amniotic fluid is seen in which conditions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fetal distress","Breech or transverse lie","Rh incompatibility","Intrauterine death of fetus","Concealed abruptio placenta"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list green/meconium-stained fluid as seen in fetal distress and in breech or transverse lie.  Why C wrong: Rh incompatibility gives golden yellow (bilirubin) fluid.  Why D wrong: Intrauterine death gives tobacco-juice coloured fluid.  Why E wrong: Concealed abruptio placenta gives dark coloured/dark red fluid."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Golden yellow amniotic fluid is due to the presence of which pigment?","options":["Meconium","Bilirubin","Haemoglobin","Melanin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Golden yellow fluid is due to BILIRUBIN.  Why A wrong: Meconium gives green colour.  Why C wrong: Haemoglobin/blood gives dark red colour.  Why D wrong: Melanin is not implicated in amniotic fluid colour."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Golden yellow amniotic fluid (due to bilirubin) is characteristically seen in which condition?","options":["Post-term pregnancy","Rh incompatibility","Intrauterine death","Fetal distress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Golden yellow fluid indicates Rh incompatibility.  Why A wrong: Post-term/post-maturity gives saffron/greenish-yellow colour.  Why C wrong: Intrauterine death gives tobacco-juice colour.  Why D wrong: Fetal distress gives green (meconium) fluid."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In Rh incompatibility causing golden yellow amniotic fluid, what is the Rh status of mother and fetus?","options":["Mother Rh +ve, Fetus Rh –ve","Mother Rh –ve, Fetus Rh +ve","Both Rh –ve","Both Rh +ve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify mother = Rh –ve and Fetus = Rh +ve.  Why A wrong: The combination is reversed.  Why C wrong: Two Rh –ve individuals do not create incompatibility.  Why D wrong: Two Rh +ve individuals do not create Rh incompatibility."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Tobacco juice coloured amniotic fluid indicates which condition?","options":["Fetal distress","Intrauterine death of fetus","Post-maturity","Rh incompatibility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tobacco juice colour amniotic fluid indicates intrauterine death of the fetus.  Why A wrong: Fetal distress gives green meconium-stained fluid.  Why C wrong: Post-maturity gives saffron/greenish-yellow fluid.  Why D wrong: Rh incompatibility gives golden yellow fluid."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Saffron colour / greenish yellow amniotic fluid is characteristic of which situation?","options":["Preterm labour","Post-term pregnancy / post-maturity (≥42 weeks)","Abruptio placenta","Rh incompatibility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Saffron/greenish-yellow fluid is seen in post-term pregnancy / post-maturity (≥42 weeks).  Why A wrong: Preterm labour is not the listed cause.  Why C wrong: Abruptio placenta gives dark red fluid.  Why D wrong: Rh incompatibility gives golden yellow fluid."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Post-maturity is defined in the notes as a gestational age of:","options":["≥40 weeks","≥41 weeks","≥42 weeks","≥44 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define post-maturity as ≥42 weeks.  Why A/B wrong: These are below the stated ≥42 weeks threshold.  Why D wrong: The stated threshold is 42, not 44 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Dark coloured / dark red amniotic fluid is associated with which condition?","options":["Concealed variety of abruptio placenta","Fetal distress","Post-maturity","Intrauterine death"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dark coloured/dark red fluid indicates the concealed variety of abruptio placenta.  Why B wrong: Fetal distress gives green fluid.  Why C wrong: Post-maturity gives saffron/greenish-yellow fluid.  Why D wrong: Intrauterine death gives tobacco-juice colour."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which of the following is NOT a function of amniotic fluid?","options":["It distends the uterus","It provides nutrition to the fetus","It acts as a shock absorber","It helps maintain the temperature of the fetus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly \"Remember: Amniotic fluid does not provide nutrition to fetus.\"  Why A wrong: Distending the uterus is a listed function.  Why C wrong: Acting as a shock absorber is a listed function.  Why D wrong: Maintaining fetal temperature is a listed function."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which function of amniotic fluid gives the fetus room to grow and move?","options":["It distends the uterus","It gives enough space for the fetus to grow & move","It acts as a shock absorber","It maintains temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Function 2 states it gives enough space for the fetus to grow and move.  Why A wrong: Distending the uterus is a separate listed function.  Why C wrong: Shock absorption protects from trauma, not growth space.  Why D wrong: Temperature maintenance is a distinct function."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Amniotic fluid protecting the fetus from external mechanical injury reflects which function?","options":["Distending the uterus","Providing nutrition","Acting as a shock absorber","Providing space to move"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Function 3 states amniotic fluid acts as a shock absorber.  Why A wrong: Distension is a different function.  Why B wrong: The fluid does not provide nutrition.  Why D wrong: Providing space is a separate listed function."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"The composition of amniotic fluid is approximately:","options":["90% water, 10% solids","95% water, 5% solids","99% water, 1% solids","80% water, 20% solids"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state amniotic fluid is 99% water and 1% solids.  Why A/B/D wrong: These water/solid ratios do not match the stated 99%/1%."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"By which investigation is amniotic fluid volume assessed?","options":["MRI","USG (ultrasound)","Plain X-ray","CT scan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state assessment of amniotic fluid is by USG.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the modality named for amniotic fluid assessment."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"During ultrasound assessment of amniotic fluid, the abdomen of the female is divided into how many quadrants?","options":["2 quadrants","3 quadrants","4 quadrants","6 quadrants"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: On ultrasound the abdomen is divided into 4 quadrants, and each quadrant shows many pockets of amniotic fluid.  Why A/B/D wrong: The notes specify 4 quadrants, not these numbers."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"The Amniotic Fluid Index (AFI) is calculated by:","options":["Measuring the single deepest pocket only","Adding the largest pocket of each of the four quadrants (a+b+c+d)","Measuring the total uterine volume","Averaging all pockets in one quadrant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The largest pocket of each quadrant is measured and their sum (a+b+c+d) is the Amniotic Fluid Index (AFI).  Why A wrong: The single largest pocket is a different, separate measurement method.  Why C wrong: Total uterine volume is not how AFI is derived.  Why D wrong: AFI sums one pocket from each of the four quadrants, not several from one."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"What is the normal range of the Amniotic Fluid Index (AFI)?","options":["2–8 cm","5 and 24 cm","8–16 cm","10–30 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state normal AFI is between 5 and 24 cm.  Why A wrong: 2–8 cm is the normal depth of the single largest pocket, not the AFI.  Why C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated 5–24 cm."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"An AFI less than 5 cm indicates which condition?","options":["Polyhydramnios","Oligohydramnios","Normal fluid","Anhydramnios only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if AFI is <5 cm → OLIGOHYDRAMNIOS.  Why A wrong: Polyhydramnios is an increase in AFI (≥ high cutoff), not a decrease.  Why C wrong: <5 cm is below the normal lower limit.  Why D wrong: The named condition for AFI <5 cm is oligohydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"According to the notes, a raised AFI above the upper normal limit indicates which condition?","options":["Oligohydramnios","Polyhydramnios","Normal fluid","Meconium aspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state an AFI above the upper cutoff indicates POLYHYDRAMNIOS (the notes print \">2.5\"; as the normal upper limit is 24 cm this is a typographic error for >24/25 cm — the concept is a raised AFI = polyhydramnios).  Why A wrong: Oligohydramnios is a low AFI (<5 cm).  Why C wrong: A value above the upper limit is not normal.  Why D wrong: Meconium aspiration is a fetal complication, not an AFI category."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"What is the normal depth of the single largest pocket of amniotic fluid?","options":["1–3 cm","2–8 cm","5–24 cm","8–16 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the normal depth of the single largest pocket is 2–8 cm.  Why A wrong: This range is below normal.  Why C wrong: 5–24 cm is the AFI range, not the single-pocket depth.  Why D wrong: This does not match the stated 2–8 cm."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"A single largest pocket depth of less than 2 cm indicates:","options":["Polyhydramnios","Oligohydramnios","Normal fluid","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if depth is <2 cm → OLIGOHYDRAMNIOS.  Why A wrong: Polyhydramnios corresponds to a deep pocket (≥8 cm).  Why C wrong: <2 cm is below the normal 2–8 cm range.  Why D wrong: Twin pregnancy is a cause of polyhydramnios, not a pocket-depth category."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"A single largest pocket depth of ≥8 cm indicates:","options":["Oligohydramnios","Polyhydramnios","Normal fluid","Anhydramnios"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if depth is ≥8 cm → POLYHYDRAMNIOS.  Why A wrong: Oligohydramnios is a shallow pocket (<2 cm).  Why C wrong: ≥8 cm is above the normal upper limit.  Why D wrong: Anhydramnios is near-absence of fluid, the opposite of a deep pocket."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In oligohydramnios, the total amniotic fluid volume is:","options":["<200 mL","<500 mL",">2 L","1–1.5 L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The oligohydramnios column states amniotic fluid = <200 mL.  Why B wrong: The stated threshold is <200 mL, not <500 mL.  Why C wrong: >2 L is the polyhydramnios volume.  Why D wrong: This is not the stated oligohydramnios volume."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In polyhydramnios, the total amniotic fluid volume is:","options":["<200 mL","500 mL–1 L",">2 L","1–1.5 L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The polyhydramnios column states amniotic fluid >2 L.  Why A wrong: <200 mL is the oligohydramnios volume.  Why B/D wrong: These are below the stated >2 L threshold."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"On USG, oligohydramnios is defined by an AFI and single largest pocket of:","options":["AFI <5 cm and single largest pocket <2 cm","AFI ≥24 cm and pocket ≥8 cm","AFI 5–24 cm and pocket 2–8 cm","AFI <2 cm and pocket <5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The oligohydramnios column lists AFI <5 cm and single largest pocket <2 cm.  Why B wrong: These are polyhydramnios criteria.  Why C wrong: These are normal values.  Why D wrong: The values are transposed/incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"On USG, polyhydramnios is defined by a single largest pocket depth of:","options":["<2 cm","2–8 cm","≥8 cm","≥12 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The polyhydramnios column lists single largest pocket ≥8 cm on USG.  Why A wrong: <2 cm indicates oligohydramnios.  Why B wrong: 2–8 cm is normal.  Why D wrong: The stated cutoff is ≥8 cm, not ≥12 cm."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"What is the most common cause of MILD oligohydramnios (and also of mild polyhydramnios)?","options":["Renal agenesis","Idiopathic","Esophageal atresia","Maternal diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of both mild oligohydramnios and mild polyhydramnios is idiopathic.  Why A wrong: Renal agenesis is the most common cause of severe oligohydramnios.  Why C wrong: Esophageal atresia is an example of a severe polyhydramnios cause.  Why D wrong: Maternal diabetes is another (not \"most common mild\") polyhydramnios cause."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"What is the most common cause of SEVERE oligohydramnios?","options":["Twin pregnancy","Congenital renal problem in fetus (e.g., renal agenesis)","Cleft palate","Maternal diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of severe oligohydramnios is a congenital renal problem in the fetus (e.g., renal agenesis), associated with a horseshoe-shaped kidney.  Why A wrong: Twin pregnancy is a polyhydramnios cause.  Why C wrong: Cleft palate causes polyhydramnios (swallowing problem).  Why D wrong: Maternal diabetes causes polyhydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In severe oligohydramnios due to congenital renal problems, which characteristic kidney malformation is mentioned?","options":["Polycystic kidney","Horseshoe shaped kidney","Duplex kidney","Ectopic kidney"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify a HORSESHOE shaped kidney in relation to congenital renal problems/renal agenesis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These renal anomalies are not the one named in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"What is the most common cause of SEVERE polyhydramnios?","options":["Idiopathic","Renal agenesis","Congenital swallowing defect in fetus","Hypertension in mother"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of severe polyhydramnios is a congenital swallowing defect in the fetus.  Why A wrong: Idiopathic is the most common cause of MILD polyhydramnios.  Why B wrong: Renal agenesis causes oligohydramnios.  Why D wrong: Maternal hypertension is an oligohydramnios cause."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which of the following are examples of congenital swallowing defects causing severe polyhydramnios? (Select all that apply)","options":["Esophageal atresia","Duodenal atresia","Intestinal obstruction","Renal agenesis","Amnion nodosum"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list esophageal atresia, duodenal atresia, and intestinal obstruction as examples of severe-polyhydramnios swallowing defects.  Why D wrong: Renal agenesis causes oligohydramnios.  Why E wrong: Amnion nodosum is a cause of oligohydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"According to the grades of polyhydramnios in the notes, mild polyhydramnios corresponds to an AFI of:","options":["8–11 cm","12–15 cm",">16 cm","5–24 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes grade mild polyhydramnios as AFI = 8–11 cm.  Why B wrong: 12–15 cm is moderate polyhydramnios.  Why C wrong: >16 cm is severe polyhydramnios.  Why D wrong: 5–24 cm is the overall normal AFI range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"According to the notes, moderate polyhydramnios corresponds to an AFI of:","options":["8–11 cm","12–15 cm",">16 cm","<8 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate polyhydramnios is graded as AFI = 12–15 cm.  Why A wrong: 8–11 cm is mild.  Why C wrong: >16 cm is severe.  Why D wrong: <8 cm is not a listed polyhydramnios grade."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"According to the notes, severe polyhydramnios corresponds to an AFI of:","options":["8–11 cm","12–15 cm",">16 cm","5–24 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe polyhydramnios is graded as AFI >16 cm.  Why A wrong: 8–11 cm is mild.  Why B wrong: 12–15 cm is moderate.  Why D wrong: 5–24 cm is the normal range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which of the following is listed as an \"other cause\" of oligohydramnios? (Select all that apply)","options":["Post-term pregnancy (≥42 weeks)","Premature rupture of membranes","Pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH)","Maternal diabetes","IUGR of the fetus"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Post-term pregnancy, premature rupture of membranes, raised maternal BP (hypertension/PIH), and IUGR are all listed causes of oligohydramnios.  Why D wrong: Maternal diabetes is a cause of polyhydramnios, not oligohydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which class of drugs (including indomethacin) is listed as causing oligohydramnios?","options":["Prostaglandin synthetase inhibitors and ACE inhibitors","Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Corticosteroids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Prostaglandin synthetase inhibitors (like indomethacin) and ACE inhibitors as drug causes of oligohydramnios.  Why B/C/D wrong: These drug classes are not listed as oligohydramnios causes in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which of the following is a listed cause of oligohydramnios related to a placental/amniotic finding?","options":["Chorangioma","Amnion nodosum","TORCH infection","Thalassemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amnion nodosum is listed among the causes of oligohydramnios.  Why A wrong: Chorangioma is a polyhydramnios cause.  Why C wrong: TORCH infection is a polyhydramnios cause.  Why D wrong: Thalassemia is a polyhydramnios cause."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which chromosomal abnormality is listed as a cause of oligohydramnios?","options":["Trisomy","Triploidy","Monosomy X","Thalassemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Triploidy is listed as a cause of oligohydramnios.  Why A wrong: Trisomy is listed under polyhydramnios causes.  Why C wrong: Monosomy X is not listed in the notes.  Why D wrong: Thalassemia is a polyhydramnios cause."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In the notes, cleft lip / cleft palate causes polyhydramnios because:","options":["The fetus produces excess urine","The fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid","The placenta produces excess fluid","It causes renal agenesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cleft lip/cleft palate → fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid → polyhydramnios.  Why A wrong: Excess urine is the diabetes/polyuria mechanism, not the cleft mechanism.  Why C wrong: Excess placental fluid production is not the stated mechanism.  Why D wrong: Renal agenesis causes oligohydramnios, not polyhydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Explain the mechanism by which maternal diabetes causes polyhydramnios, per the notes.","options":["Maternal high glucose → fetal hyperglycaemia → fetal polyuria → polyhydramnios","Maternal high glucose → decreased fetal urine → polyhydramnios","Maternal high glucose → fetal swallowing defect → polyhydramnios","Maternal high glucose → renal agenesis → polyhydramnios"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow is: same (high) glucose reaches fetus → fetus glucose high → polyuria → polyhydramnios.  Why B wrong: Fetal urine increases (polyuria), not decreases.  Why C wrong: A swallowing defect is a separate mechanism, not the diabetes pathway.  Why D wrong: Renal agenesis causes oligohydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which neural tube defects are listed as causes of polyhydramnios? (Select all that apply)","options":["Anencephaly","Spina bifida","Renal agenesis","Horseshoe kidney","Cleft palate"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list neural tube defects such as anencephaly and spina bifida as polyhydramnios causes.  Why C/D wrong: Renal agenesis and horseshoe kidney relate to oligohydramnios.  Why E wrong: Cleft palate is a separate polyhydramnios cause (swallowing), not a neural tube defect."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of polyhydramnios in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Twin / multifetal pregnancy","Rh incompatibility","TORCH infection in fetus","Chorangioma","Premature rupture of membranes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Twin/multifetal pregnancy, Rh incompatibility, TORCH infection, and chorangioma are all listed polyhydramnios causes.  Why E wrong: Premature rupture of membranes is a cause of oligohydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which chromosomal/haematological conditions are listed as causes of polyhydramnios? (Select all that apply)","options":["Trisomy","Thalassemia","Triploidy","Chorangioma"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Trisomy and thalassemia are both listed as causes of polyhydramnios.  Why C wrong: Triploidy is listed under oligohydramnios causes.  Why D wrong: Chorangioma is a tumour cause (listed), not a chromosomal/haematological condition."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON complication of oligohydramnios?","options":["Cord prolapse","Pulmonary hypoplasia","Postpartum haemorrhage","Abruptio placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common complication of oligohydramnios is pulmonary hypoplasia (due to less space → fetal parts, including lungs, do not grow properly).  Why A wrong: Cord prolapse is a polyhydramnios complication.  Why C wrong: Postpartum haemorrhage is a polyhydramnios complication.  Why D wrong: Abruptio placenta is listed under polyhydramnios complications."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In oligohydramnios, reduced space around the fetus leads to pulmonary hypoplasia because:","options":["The fetal parts (including lungs) do not grow properly","The fetus swallows too much fluid","Excess fluid compresses the lungs","The placenta stops functioning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow is: due to less space → fetal parts do not grow properly → pulmonary hypoplasia.  Why B wrong: In oligohydramnios there is too little, not too much, fluid.  Why C wrong: There is a lack (not excess) of fluid.  Why D wrong: Placental failure is not the stated mechanism for pulmonary hypoplasia here."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Besides pulmonary hypoplasia, which limb complication is associated with oligohydramnios?","options":["Polydactyly","Limb amputation defect / limb reduction defect","Achondroplasia","Clubfoot only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list limb amputation defect / limb reduction defect as an oligohydramnios complication.  Why A wrong: Polydactyly is not listed.  Why C wrong: Achondroplasia is a genetic skeletal disorder, not listed here.  Why D wrong: Clubfoot is not the specific complication named."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which of the following are complications of polyhydramnios? (Select all that apply)","options":["Preterm rupture of membranes","Preterm labour (<37 weeks)","Abruptio placenta","Postpartum haemorrhage","Pulmonary hypoplasia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Preterm rupture of membranes, preterm labour (<37 weeks), abruptio placenta, and postpartum haemorrhage are all listed polyhydramnios complications.  Why E wrong: Pulmonary hypoplasia is the classic complication of oligohydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Preterm labour is defined in the notes as labour occurring at:","options":["<34 weeks","<37 weeks","<39 weeks","≥42 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define preterm labour as <37 weeks.  Why A wrong: The stated cutoff is <37, not <34 weeks.  Why C wrong: <39 weeks is not the definition given.  Why D wrong: ≥42 weeks is post-maturity, not preterm."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Why does polyhydramnios cause respiratory distress in the mother?","options":["The uterus pushes the diaphragm up","The fluid enters the maternal lungs","It causes maternal anaemia","It triggers pulmonary embolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state respiratory distress in the mother occurs because the (over-distended) uterus pushes the diaphragm up.  Why B wrong: Amniotic fluid does not enter the maternal lungs in this mechanism.  Why C wrong: Anaemia is not the stated mechanism.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary embolism is not the mechanism described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Polyhydramnios increases the chances of malpresentation such as:","options":["Occipito-anterior position","Breech / transverse lie","Vertex presentation","Cephalic presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Polyhydramnios increases chances of malpresentation (breech/transverse lie) because of extra room for the fetus to move.  Why A/C/D wrong: Occipito-anterior, vertex, and cephalic presentations are normal presentations, not malpresentations."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which serious cord complication is increased in polyhydramnios?","options":["Cord prolapse","Cord compression from oligohydramnios","True knot only","Nuchal cord only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state polyhydramnios increases the chances of cord prolapse.  Why B wrong: Cord compression is an oligohydramnios (low-fluid) mechanism.  Why C/D wrong: True knot and nuchal cord are not the complications listed for polyhydramnios here."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Which uterine complication after delivery is listed for polyhydramnios?","options":["Subinvolution of the uterus","Uterine rupture during first trimester","Endometriosis","Fibroids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Subinvolution of the uterus is listed as a polyhydramnios complication (the over-stretched uterus involutes poorly).  Why B wrong: First-trimester uterine rupture is not listed.  Why C wrong: Endometriosis is unrelated.  Why D wrong: Fibroids are not listed as a polyhydramnios complication."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In oligohydramnios, cord compression sets off a sequence that ultimately produces which fetal syndrome?","options":["Meconium aspiration syndrome","Respiratory distress syndrome of prematurity","Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome","Fetal alcohol syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cord compression → umbilical vein compressed → fetal distress → fetus passes meconium → green (meconium-stained) fluid → swallowed by fetus → MECONIUM ASPIRATION SYNDROME.  Why B wrong: RDS of prematurity is due to surfactant deficiency, not this cord-compression sequence.  Why C wrong: Twin-to-twin transfusion occurs in monochorionic twins, unrelated here.  Why D wrong: Fetal alcohol syndrome is due to maternal alcohol, not oligohydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In the cord-compression complication of oligohydramnios, which vessel is described as getting compressed first?","options":["Umbilical artery","Umbilical vein","Uterine artery","Ductus venosus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the umbilical vein gets compressed, leading to fetal distress.  Why A wrong: The notes specifically name the umbilical vein, not the artery.  Why C wrong: The uterine artery is maternal, not part of this sequence.  Why D wrong: The ductus venosus is not the vessel named."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In the meconium-aspiration sequence, why does the amniotic fluid turn green?","options":["Because of bilirubin release","Because the fetus passes meconium into the fluid (meconium stained)","Because of maternal bleeding","Because of infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal distress makes the fetus pass meconium into the fluid, so the amniotic fluid becomes green (meconium stained).  Why A wrong: Bilirubin produces golden yellow fluid, not this green colour.  Why C wrong: Maternal bleeding gives dark red fluid.  Why D wrong: Infection is not the stated cause of the green colour here."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Apart from pulmonary and cord complications, oligohydramnios can also lead to:","options":["Preterm labour","Post-term pregnancy","Polyhydramnios","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state oligohydramnios can lead to post-term pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Preterm labour is a polyhydramnios complication.  Why C wrong: Polyhydramnios is the opposite condition, not a complication of oligohydramnios.  Why D wrong: Twin pregnancy is a cause of polyhydramnios, not a complication of oligohydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"The treatment for oligohydramnios shown in the notes involves instilling fluid into the uterus. This procedure is:","options":["Amnioinfusion (with normal saline)","Amnioreduction","Chorionic villus sampling","Cordocentesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For oligohydramnios (low fluid), fluid (normal saline) is instilled via an intrauterine catheter — amnioinfusion. (The heading printed \"AMNIOCENTESIS\" but the labelled diagram and description are of amnioinfusion, which adds fluid; amniocentesis withdraws fluid.)  Why B wrong: Amnioreduction removes fluid and is the polyhydramnios treatment.  Why C wrong: Chorionic villus sampling samples placental tissue, not a fluid treatment.  Why D wrong: Cordocentesis samples cord blood, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"During amnioinfusion for oligohydramnios, the normal saline is warmed to which temperature?","options":["25°C","37°C","40°C","Room temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the temperature of the normal saline = 37°C (body temperature).  Why A/C wrong: These do not match the stated 37°C.  Why D wrong: The fluid is specifically warmed to 37°C, not left at room temperature."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"During amnioinfusion, the intrauterine pressure should never exceed:","options":["15 mm Hg","20 mm Hg","25 mm Hg","30 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state during this procedure the intrauterine pressure should never be more than 25 mm Hg.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 25 mm Hg limit."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"During the amnioinfusion setup, which device continuously measures the pressure inside the uterus?","options":["Intrauterine pressure monitor (via intrauterine pressure catheter)","Sphygmomanometer","Fetal scalp electrode","External tocodynamometer only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels an intrauterine pressure monitor connected via an intrauterine pressure catheter to measure uterine pressure.  Why B wrong: A sphygmomanometer measures maternal blood pressure, not intrauterine pressure.  Why C wrong: A fetal scalp electrode records fetal heart rate, not pressure.  Why D wrong: The notes depict an internal (catheter) pressure monitor, not an external toco alone."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In the amnioreduction (amniocentesis) treatment of polyhydramnios, the amount of amniotic fluid removed per hour should be about:","options":["100 mL/hr","250 mL/hr","500 mL/hr","1000 mL/hr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the amount removed per hour = 500 mL/hr.  Why A/B wrong: These are lower than the stated 500 mL/hr.  Why D wrong: This exceeds the stated 500 mL/hr rate."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"In amnioreduction for polyhydramnios, the maximum amount of amniotic fluid that can be removed is:","options":["500–1000 mL","1000–1500 mL","1500–2000 mL","2000–2500 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maximum amount of amniotic fluid that can be removed = 1500–2000 mL.  Why A/B wrong: These are below the stated maximum range.  Why D wrong: This exceeds the stated 1500–2000 mL range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"Amniocentesis (removal of amniotic fluid) is performed under the guidance of:","options":["Fluoroscopy","Ultrasound","MRI","Palpation alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state amniocentesis is done under ultrasound guidance (the diagram shows an ultrasound transducer over the abdomen with the needle entering the amniotic fluid).  Why A wrong: Fluoroscopy uses ionising radiation and is not used here.  Why C wrong: MRI is not the guidance modality named.  Why D wrong: Blind palpation alone would be unsafe; ultrasound guidance is specified."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"A pregnant woman is found on USG to have an AFI of 3 cm and a single deepest pocket of 1.5 cm. Which condition and most common severe cause should the nurse suspect?","options":["Polyhydramnios due to esophageal atresia","Oligohydramnios due to fetal congenital renal problem (renal agenesis)","Normal amniotic fluid","Polyhydramnios due to maternal diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AFI <5 cm and pocket <2 cm define oligohydramnios; its most common severe cause is a congenital renal problem in the fetus (renal agenesis).  Why A wrong: Esophageal atresia causes polyhydramnios, not oligohydramnios.  Why C wrong: These values are below normal (AFI 5–24 cm, pocket 2–8 cm).  Why D wrong: Maternal diabetes causes polyhydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Amniotic fluid balance, fetal swallowing onset & volume…","question":"A term neonate is born through thick green (meconium-stained) amniotic fluid following an oligohydramnios pregnancy with cord compression. The neonate is at highest risk for:","options":["Meconium aspiration syndrome","Rh haemolytic disease","Transient tachypnoea from polyhydramnios","Neonatal hypoglycaemia from maternal diabetes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Meconium-stained fluid that is swallowed/aspirated after cord-compression fetal distress leads to meconium aspiration syndrome.  Why B wrong: Rh disease relates to golden yellow (bilirubin) fluid/Rh incompatibility, not meconium staining.  Why C wrong: This scenario is oligohydramnios, not polyhydramnios.  Why D wrong: Maternal diabetes/hypoglycaemia is not indicated by meconium-stained fluid."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, which drug is used for managing polyhydramnios?","options":["Terbutaline","Indomethacin","Nifedipine","Magnesium sulphate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page explicitly states Indomethacin (an NSAID/prostaglandin synthetase inhibitor) can be used to manage polyhydramnios by reducing fetal urine output.  Why A wrong: Terbutaline is a tocolytic (beta-agonist), not used for polyhydramnios reduction.  Why C wrong: Nifedipine is a calcium-channel-blocker tocolytic, not the drug named.  Why D wrong: Magnesium sulphate is used for eclampsia prophylaxis/neuroprotection/tocolysis, not for polyhydramnios per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which of the following is a disadvantage of using Indomethacin during pregnancy?","options":["Leads to premature closure of the ductus arteriosus","Causes maternal hyperglycaemia","Increases amniotic fluid volume","Causes fetal macrosomia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Lead to premature closure of ductus arteriosus\" as a disadvantage of Indomethacin.  Why B wrong: Hyperglycaemia is not a listed effect of Indomethacin.  Why C wrong: Indomethacin reduces fetal urine and hence amniotic fluid; it does not increase it.  Why D wrong: Macrosomia is not an Indomethacin effect noted."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Besides premature closure of the ductus arteriosus, the notes list which additional concern that makes Indomethacin contraindicated in pregnancy?","options":["Effect on the fetal kidney","Effect on the fetal liver","Effect on the fetal lungs","Effect on the fetal brain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Effect of fetal kidney\" and that it is \"Contraindicated in Pregnancy.\"  Why B wrong: Liver effect is not the disadvantage listed.  Why C wrong: Lung effect is not listed for Indomethacin.  Why D wrong: Brain effect is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"In the nurse's role for the amniocentesis/amnioreduction procedure, which action must be completed BEFORE the procedure to legally proceed?","options":["Auscultate the fetal heart sound afterward","Get the consent form signed","Explain symptoms after the procedure","Give a tocolytic drug"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The listed nurse's roles include \"Get consent form signed,\" an essential pre-procedure step.  Why A wrong: Auscultating fetal heart sound afterward is a post-procedure step.  Why C wrong: Explaining symptoms is done after the procedure.  Why D wrong: Giving a tocolytic is not listed among the nurse's roles here."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the nurse's roles, why is a free abdomen examination performed before the procedure?","options":["To determine the fetal position","To estimate maternal blood pressure","To measure amniotic fluid index","To assess uterine scar"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the nurse should \"Do a free abdomen examination to determine fetal position.\"  Why B wrong: Blood pressure is not determined by abdominal examination.  Why C wrong: AFI is measured by ultrasound, not abdominal palpation, and is not the stated purpose.  Why D wrong: Uterine scar assessment is not the stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The nurse should auscultate the fetal heart sound at which point(s) around the amniocentesis procedure per the notes?","options":["Only before the procedure","Only after the procedure","Both before and again after the procedure","During the procedure only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The roles include \"Auscultate fetal heart sound\" (before) and \"After the procedure again listen to fetal heart sound.\"  Why A wrong: It is also done after the procedure.  Why B wrong: It is also done before the procedure.  Why D wrong: The notes specify before and after, not during only."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"After the amniocentesis procedure, the nurse educates the patient to report which of the following warning symptoms? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hyperactivity of the uterus","Vaginal discharge (bleeding)/water discharge","Fetal movements","Blurred vision","Painful urination"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the post-procedure symptoms to explain as hyperactivity of uterus, vaginal discharge (bleeding)/water discharge, and fetal movements.  Why D wrong: Blurred vision is not among the listed post-procedure symptoms.  Why E wrong: Painful urination is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Providing emotional support to the patient is listed among the nurse's responsibilities during which procedure context?","options":["The amniocentesis/amnioreduction procedure","Cesarean section","Episiotomy repair","Vaccination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Give emotional support to patient\" is listed within the nurse's roles for this fetal-fluid procedure.  Why B wrong: The list is not about cesarean section.  Why C wrong: The list is not about episiotomy repair.  Why D wrong: The list is not about vaccination."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The interval of \"2 weeks\" shown on the fetal timeline lies between the 1st day of the LMP and which event?","options":["Ovulation (14th day = fertilization)","Implantation","The missed period","Neural tube closure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows 2 weeks between the 1st day of LMP and ovulation, at which the 14th day marks fertilization.  Why B wrong: Implantation is not the labelled endpoint of this 2-week interval.  Why C wrong: The missed period (28 days) is a separate, later point on the timeline.  Why D wrong: Neural tube closure (day 30) is much later."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The missed period is marked at approximately how many days on the fetal age timeline?","options":["14 days","21 days","28 days","30 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline shows \"28 days (missed period).\"  Why A wrong: 14 days corresponds to ovulation/fertilization.  Why B wrong: 21 days is not the marked missed-period point.  Why D wrong: 30 days is neural tube closure, not the missed period."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Age counted from the 1st day of the LMP is called:","options":["Fetal age","Gestational age","Embryonic age","Conceptional age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram links \"1st day of LMP → Gestational age.\"  Why A wrong: Fetal age is counted from fertilization (14th day), not from LMP.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the terms the notes assign to the LMP-based count."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Age counted from the day of fertilization (14th day) is called:","options":["Gestational age","Fetal age","Menstrual age","Postnatal age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram links \"14th = fertilization → Fetal age.\"  Why A wrong: Gestational age is counted from the 1st day of LMP.  Why C wrong: Menstrual age is synonymous with gestational (LMP-based) age.  Why D wrong: Postnatal age is after birth."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, Gestational Age equals:","options":["Fetal age + 2 weeks","Fetal age − 2 weeks","Fetal age + 4 weeks","Fetal age × 2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Fetal age + 2 weeks = Gestational Age.\"  Why B wrong: That formula gives Fetal age, not Gestational age.  Why C/D wrong: These formulas are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, Fetal Age equals:","options":["Gestational Age + 2 weeks","Gestational Age − 2 weeks","Gestational Age + 4 weeks","Gestational Age − 4 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Gestational Age − 2 weeks = Fetal Age.\"  Why A wrong: Adding 2 weeks would give a value larger than gestational age, which is incorrect.  Why C/D wrong: These formulas are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"In pregnancy, all definitions and all events are conventionally based on which age?","options":["Fetal age","Gestational age","Conceptional age","Chronological age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes say \"All definitions given in Pregnancy are based on Gestational age\" and \"All events occurring in Pregnancy are based on Gestational age.\"  Why A wrong: Only one definition (growth period) is based on fetal age; definitions/events generally use gestational age.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the age standard used in pregnancy per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which is the ONLY definition originally based on fetal age/day of fertilization according to the notes?","options":["Trimester","Growth period","Viability","Term"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Note states there is only one definition originally based on fetal age/day of fertilization → GROWTH PERIOD.  Why A wrong: Trimesters are defined by gestational age.  Why C wrong: Viability is defined by gestational age (28 weeks in India per the notes).  Why D wrong: Term is a gestational-age based definition."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The \"Period of fertilized ovum\" (original definition) extends from the day of fertilization up to how long after?","options":["1 week","2 weeks","3 weeks","8 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The growth-period table states the period of fertilized ova runs \"From day of fertilization up till 2 weeks after that.\"  Why A wrong: It is 2 weeks, not 1 week.  Why C wrong: 3 weeks marks the start of the embryonic period.  Why D wrong: 8 weeks is the end of the embryonic period."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Expressed as gestational age, the period of fertilized ovum corresponds to which range?","options":["0 to 2 weeks gestational age","2 to 4 weeks gestational age","5 to 10 weeks gestational age","11 weeks to delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adding 2 weeks to the fetal-age definition (0–2 weeks), the table gives \"+2 weeks = 2 week gestational age\" up to \"4 weeks gestational age.\"  Why A wrong: Gestational age adds 2 weeks, so it begins at 2 weeks, not 0.  Why C wrong: 5–10 weeks gestational age is the embryonic period.  Why D wrong: 11 weeks to delivery is the fetal period."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The embryonic period, by original (fetal-age) definition, spans:","options":["Day of fertilization to 2 weeks","3 weeks to 8 weeks after fertilization","9 weeks after fertilization to delivery","11 weeks of gestation to delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the embryonic period is \"3 weeks after fertilization → 8 weeks after fertilization.\"  Why A wrong: That is the period of fertilized ovum.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe the fetal period."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Expressed as gestational age, the embryonic period corresponds to:","options":["2 to 4 weeks gestational age","5 to 10 weeks gestational age","11 weeks gestational age to delivery","20 to 24 weeks gestational age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adding 2 weeks to 3–8 weeks after fertilization gives \"5 weeks gestational age → 10 weeks gestational age.\"  Why A wrong: That is the period of fertilized ovum.  Why C wrong: That is the fetal period.  Why D wrong: Not a growth-period range in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which growth period is labelled the \"most teratogenic period\"?","options":["Period of fertilized ovum","Embryonic period","Fetal period","Neonatal period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Comment column marks the embryonic period as the \"Most teratogenic period.\"  Why A wrong: The period of fertilized ovum is generally an \"all-or-none\" phase, not marked as most teratogenic.  Why C wrong: The fetal period is after organogenesis and less teratogenic.  Why D wrong: The neonatal period is after birth."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The fetal period, by original (fetal-age) definition, begins at:","options":["3 weeks after fertilization","8 weeks after fertilization","9 weeks after fertilization and continues up till delivery","11 weeks after fertilization"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the fetal period is \"9 weeks after fertilization up till delivery.\"  Why A/B wrong: These fall within the embryonic period.  Why D wrong: 11 weeks is the gestational-age start, not the fetal-age start."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Expressed as gestational age, the fetal period begins from:","options":["5 weeks of gestational age","9 weeks of gestational age","11 weeks of gestational age up till delivery","20 weeks of gestational age"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the fetal period is \"From 11 weeks of gestational age up till delivery.\"  Why A wrong: 5 weeks is the start of the embryonic period.  Why B wrong: 9 weeks is the fetal-age (not gestational-age) start.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks is not the stated boundary."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"How does the notes define \"Teratogenic\"?","options":["Any external exposure to the mother which can lead to congenital malformation in the baby","A genetic mutation transmitted from the father","A hormonal deficiency in the fetus","A maternal autoimmune reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define teratogenic as \"any External exposure to the mother which can lead to congenital malformation in baby.\"  Why B wrong: It refers to external exposure, not inherited paternal mutation.  Why C wrong: It is not defined as a hormonal deficiency.  Why D wrong: It is not an autoimmune reaction."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"How does the notes define a \"Teratogen\"?","options":["The external exposure which leads to congenital malformation in the baby","The fetal organ affected by malformation","The gestational week of maximum growth","A protective maternal factor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define a teratogen as \"That external exposure which leads to congenital malformation in baby.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the definition; a teratogen is the causative external exposure."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The most teratogenic period corresponds to which fetal-age range after fertilization?","options":["0 to 2 weeks after fertilization","3 weeks to 8 weeks after fertilization","9 weeks after fertilization to delivery","11 weeks after fertilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state most teratogenic period = embryonic period → \"3 weeks to 8 weeks after fertilization.\"  Why A wrong: That is the period of fertilized ovum.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe the fetal period, which is less teratogenic."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The most teratogenic period corresponds to which range of pregnancy (gestational age)?","options":["5 weeks to 10 weeks of pregnancy / 1st trimester","11 weeks to 20 weeks of pregnancy","20 weeks to 28 weeks of pregnancy","28 weeks to 36 weeks of pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the most teratogenic period as \"5 Weeks to 10 weeks of pregnancy / 1st trimester of pregnancy.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are later ranges that are not the most teratogenic period."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The three broad categories of teratogens shown branching from \"Teratogen\" are drugs, radiation, and:","options":["Infections such as Rubella and CMV","Maternal anaemia","Gestational diabetes","Multiple pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram branches Teratogen into Drugs, Infections (Rubella, CMV), and Radiation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not listed as the teratogen categories in the diagram."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which infections are specifically named as teratogenic in the notes?","options":["Rubella and CMV (cytomegalovirus)","Tuberculosis and malaria","Hepatitis A and E","Dengue and chikungunya"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The teratogen branch names \"Infections Rubella, CMV.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These infections are not the ones listed as teratogens in the diagram."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Under the FDA classification, a drug that does not cause fetal risk, is supported by both human and animal studies, and is safe in pregnancy belongs to which category?","options":["Category A","Category C","Category D","Category X"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category A drugs cause no fetal risk, have human and animal studies, and are safe in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Category C drugs may cause fetal risk with benefit outweighing risk.  Why C wrong: Category D drugs may cause fetal risk and are otherwise contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Category X is absolutely contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which of the following are examples of FDA Category A drugs per the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Folic acid","Thyroxine","Paracetamol","Phenytoin","Thalidomide"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Folic Acid and Thyroxine as Category A examples.  Why C wrong: Paracetamol is a Category B example.  Why D wrong: Phenytoin is a Category D example.  Why E wrong: Thalidomide is a Category X example."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Paracetamol, Penicillin, and Amoxycillin are given as examples of which FDA pregnancy category?","options":["Category A","Category B","Category C","Category X"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Paracetamol, Penicillin, and Amoxycillin as Category B examples (no fetal risk; human and animal studies).  Why A wrong: Category A examples are Folic Acid and Thyroxine.  Why C wrong: Category C example is Corticosteroids.  Why D wrong: Category X examples are Thalidomide and Isotretinoin."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"A drug that \"may cause fetal risk\" but whose \"benefits outweigh the risk,\" exemplified by corticosteroids, is classified as:","options":["Category A","Category B","Category C","Category X"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Category C drugs may cause fetal risk with benefit outweighing risk; corticosteroids are the listed example.  Why A/B wrong: Categories A and B do not cause fetal risk.  Why D wrong: Category X is absolutely contraindicated with no risk-benefit exception."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The antiepileptic drug Phenytoin, which may cause fetal risk and is contraindicated unless benefits outweigh risk, is placed in which category?","options":["Category B","Category C","Category D","Category X"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Antiepileptic drug (Phenytoin)\" as the Category D example — may cause fetal risk, benefits may outweigh risk, otherwise contraindicated.  Why A wrong: Category B drugs do not cause fetal risk.  Why B wrong: Category C is exemplified by corticosteroids, not phenytoin.  Why D wrong: Category X (thalidomide, isotretinoin) is absolutely contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which additional descriptor distinguishes FDA Category D from Category C in the notes?","options":["Drug is completely safe","No human studies exist","Otherwise they are contraindicated","Used only in the first trimester"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Category D adds the point \"Otherwise they are contraindicated\" beyond the shared risk/benefit description of Category C.  Why A wrong: Category D drugs are not safe.  Why B wrong: Both C and D reference human studies.  Why D wrong: Trimester restriction is not the distinguishing descriptor listed."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which FDA category is described as \"absolutely contraindicated\" in pregnancy?","options":["Category B","Category C","Category D","Category X"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe Category X as \"Absolutely contraindicated.\"  Why A/B/C wrong: Categories B, C, and D are not absolutely contraindicated (C and D permit use when benefits outweigh risk)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which drugs are cited as examples of FDA Category X? (Select all that apply)","options":["Thalidomide","Isotretinoin","Amoxycillin","Folic acid","Corticosteroids"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Thalidomide and Isotretinoin as Category X examples (absolutely contraindicated).  Why C wrong: Amoxycillin is Category B.  Why D wrong: Folic acid is Category A.  Why E wrong: Corticosteroids are Category C."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"What is the SI unit of radiation used in the notes?","options":["Rad","Gray","Sievert","Roentgen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"SI unit = Radiation = GRAY.\"  Why A wrong: Rad is described as the old unit.  Why C/D wrong: Sievert and Roentgen are not the units mentioned in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, \"Rad\" is described as:","options":["The SI unit of radiation","The old unit of radiation","Equal to one Gray","A unit of drug dosage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Rad = old unit.\"  Why A wrong: Gray is the SI unit; Rad is the old unit.  Why C wrong: 1 Gray = 100 Rad, so they are not equal.  Why D wrong: Rad is a radiation unit, not a drug-dose unit."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, 1 Gray equals how many Rad?","options":["10 Rad","100 Rad","1000 Rad","1 Rad"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"1 Gray = 100 Rad.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These conversions contradict the stated 1 Gray = 100 Rad."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, 1 Rad equals how many milli-Rad?","options":["100 milli Rad","500 milli Rad","1000 milli Rad","10 milli Rad"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"1 Rad = 1000 milli Rad.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These contradict the stated conversion."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Per the notes, exposure of a pregnancy to 100 Gray of radiation results in:","options":["25% of fetal cells dying","50% of fetal cells dying","75% of fetal cells dying","100% of fetal cells dying"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Fact\" states exposure to 100 Gray → 50% of fetal cells will die.  Why A/C wrong: These percentages are not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 100% death occurs at 200 Gray, not 100 Gray."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Per the notes, what radiation exposure causes 100% of fetal cells to die?","options":["50 Gray","100 Gray","150 Gray","200 Gray"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The Fact states exposure to 200 Gray → 100% fetal cells will die.  Why A/C wrong: These values are not stated.  Why B wrong: 100 Gray causes 50% cell death, not 100%."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, the maximum permissible radiation in pregnancy is:","options":["1 Rad","5 Rad","10 Rad","100 Rad"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"In pregnancy maximum permissible Radiation is 5 Rod (Rad).\"  Why A wrong: 1 Rad is the pelvic X-ray exposure, not the maximum permissible.  Why C/D wrong: These exceed the stated 5 Rad limit."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which imaging investigations are listed as SAFE in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["MRI","USG (ultrasonography)","Doppler","X-Ray","Barium enema"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Investigation Safe\" column lists MRI, USG, and Doppler.  Why D wrong: X-Ray is in the contraindicated column.  Why E wrong: Barium enema is in the contraindicated column."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which investigations fall in the \"not done routinely but can be done\" category in pregnancy?","options":["MRCP and PET scan","MRI and USG","X-Ray and HSG","Barium enema and ERCP"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The middle column lists MRCP (magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography) and PET scan (Positron Emission Tomography).  Why B wrong: MRI and USG are in the \"safe\" column.  Why C/D wrong: X-Ray, HSG, Barium enema, and ERCP are in the contraindicated column."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"What does MRCP stand for in the notes?","options":["Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography","Maternal renal contrast pyelography","Multi-row computed pelvimetry","Magnetic radiation cardiac protocol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes expand MRCP as \"magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the expansion given."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"PET scan in the notes stands for:","options":["Positron Emission Tomography","Pelvic Echo Test","Prenatal Enzyme Test","Placental Emission Tracer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes expand PET as \"Positron Emission Tomography.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the given expansion."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which of the following investigations are CONTRAINDICATED in pregnancy per the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Contrast MRI","Gadolinium MRI","X-Ray","HSG (hysterosalpingography)","Doppler"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The contraindicated column lists Contrast MRI, Gadolinium MRI, X-Ray, HSG, Intravenous Pyelography, Barium Enema, and ERCP.  Why E wrong: Doppler is in the \"safe\" column."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which of the following is listed among the contraindicated investigations in pregnancy?","options":["Intravenous Pyelography","Ultrasonography","Doppler","Plain MRI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intravenous Pyelography is listed in the contraindicated column (along with Barium Enema and ERCP).  Why B/C/D wrong: USG, Doppler, and plain MRI are in the safe column."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, the radiation exposure from a CHEST X-Ray is:","options":["0.1 – 0.2 Rad","1 Rad","5 Rad","10 Rad"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give \"X-Ray: Radiation Exposure Chest = 0.1 – 0.2 Rad.\"  Why B wrong: 1 Rad is the pelvic X-ray exposure.  Why C wrong: 5 Rad is the maximum permissible dose.  Why D wrong: 10 Rad is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, the radiation exposure from a PELVIS X-Ray is:","options":["0.1 Rad","0.2 Rad","1 Rad","5 Rad"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Pelvis = 1 Rad.\"  Why A/B wrong: 0.1–0.2 Rad is chest X-ray exposure.  Why D wrong: 5 Rad is the maximum permissible dose."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"If, by chance, an X-ray is done during pregnancy, what is the appropriate next step according to the notes?","options":["Immediate MTP (medical termination of pregnancy)","Reassurance (MTP is not indicated)","Emergency cesarean section","Start corticosteroids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed note states the next step is \"Reassurance (MTP is not indicated).\"  Why A wrong: The notes explicitly state MTP is not indicated.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated next step."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The maximum problem of teratogenesis (congenital malformation) occurs when a female is exposed to a drug/radiation at the time of:","options":["Implantation","Organogenesis","Delivery","Puberty"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow states maximum problem occurs \"at the time of organogenesis,\" leading to congenital malformation of the fetus.  Why A wrong: Implantation precedes organogenesis and is not the point of maximum malformation risk here.  Why C/D wrong: Delivery and puberty are unrelated to organogenesis-timed teratogenesis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, most fetal organs are formed between which weeks of pregnancy?","options":["5 weeks to 10 weeks","8 weeks to 20 weeks","11 weeks to delivery","20 weeks to 28 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The organogenesis section states \"Most of fetal organs are formed between 8 weeks → 20 weeks of pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: 5–10 weeks is the most teratogenic period, not the main organ-formation window stated.  Why C wrong: 11 weeks to delivery is the fetal period.  Why D wrong: This range is not the stated organ-formation window."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which two organ systems are the exceptions that start forming BEFORE 8 weeks of pregnancy?","options":["Respiratory and GI systems","CNS and CVS","Renal and hepatic systems","Musculoskeletal and integumentary systems"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that except 2 organ systems — CNS and CVS — start forming before 8 weeks.  Why A/C/D wrong: These systems are not the stated early-forming exceptions."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which organ system is the FIRST to form in the fetus?","options":["CVS","CNS","Respiratory system","Renal system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes answer \"Which organ system is first to form? CNS.\"  Why A wrong: CVS starts forming at 5 weeks but the CNS is stated to be first.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the first system to form."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, the cardiovascular system (CVS) starts forming at how many weeks of pregnancy?","options":["3 weeks","5 weeks","8 weeks","11 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"CVS: Starts forming at 5 weeks of pregnancy,\" after which cardiac activity is seen on USG.  Why A wrong: 3 weeks after fertilization equals 5 weeks gestational age (same event expressed differently); 5 weeks is the stated gestational figure.  Why C/D wrong: These are later than the stated CVS formation."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Cardiac activity can first be seen on USG at which of the following (all equivalent)? (Select all that apply)","options":["5 weeks gestational age","3 weeks after fertilization","21 days after fertilization","10 weeks gestational age","18 weeks gestational age"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed note states cardiac activity is first seen on USG at 5 weeks gestational age, or 3 weeks after fertilization, or 21 days after fertilization — all describing the same time point.  Why D wrong: 10 weeks is when fetal heart sound is heard by Doppler, not when cardiac activity first appears on USG.  Why E wrong: 18 weeks relates to stethoscope auscultation."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"At the time cardiac activity first appears on USG (about 5 weeks gestation), the fetal heart sound:","options":["Can be heard with a stethoscope","Cannot yet be heard","Can be heard with the naked ear","Is heard only with fetoscope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"At this time Fetal Heart Sound cannot be heard,\" even though cardiac activity is visible on USG.  Why A/C/D wrong: Auscultation methods come much later (Doppler at 10 weeks, stethoscope at 18–20 weeks)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"By what gestational age can the fetal heart sound first be heard using a DOPPLER?","options":["5 weeks","10 weeks","18 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetal heart sound can be heard with Doppler at 10 weeks (the earliest method).  Why A wrong: At 5 weeks only USG cardiac activity is seen, not audible FHS.  Why C/D wrong: 18–20 weeks is the stethoscope range."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Using a STETHOSCOPE, the fetal heart sound can be heard by which gestational age?","options":["5–8 weeks","10–12 weeks","18–20 weeks","28–30 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the stethoscope can hear the fetal heart sound at 18–20 weeks.  Why A/B wrong: These are too early for stethoscope auscultation.  Why D wrong: 28–30 weeks is later than the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"The EARLIEST that fetal heart sound can be heard in pregnancy is:","options":["5 weeks with USG","10 weeks with Doppler","18 weeks with stethoscope","20 weeks with fetoscope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Earliest fetal heart sound can be heard in Pregnancy by: 10 weeks with Doppler.\"  Why A wrong: USG at 5 weeks shows cardiac activity but not an audible heart sound.  Why C/D wrong: These methods are later than the earliest (Doppler at 10 weeks)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"In the \"important days\" table (counted from the day after fertilization), which germ layers correspond to Day 7 and Day 8 respectively?","options":["Day 7 → Ectoderm; Day 8 → Endoderm","Day 7 → Endoderm; Day 8 → Mesoderm","Day 7 → Mesoderm; Day 8 → Ectoderm","Day 7 → Endoderm; Day 8 → Ectoderm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Day 7 → Ectoderm and Day 8 → Endoderm.  Why B/C/D wrong: These assign the germ layers to the wrong days."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the important-days table, the mesoderm forms on which day?","options":["Day 8","Day 14","Day 17","Day 21"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"Day 14 → Mesoderm.\"  Why A wrong: Day 8 is endoderm.  Why C wrong: Day 17 is fetoplacental circulation.  Why D wrong: Day 21 is cardiac activity on USG."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"On which day is fetoplacental circulation established according to the notes?","options":["Day 14","Day 17","Day 21","Day 30"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"Day 17 → Fetoplacental circulation was established.\"  Why A wrong: Day 14 is mesoderm.  Why C wrong: Day 21 is cardiac activity on USG.  Why D wrong: Day 30 is neural tube closure."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the important-days table, cardiac activity is seen on USG on which day (counted from day after fertilization)?","options":["Day 14","Day 17","Day 21","Day 30"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"Day 21 → Cardiac activity is seen on USG,\" consistent with 3 weeks after fertilization.  Why A/B wrong: These are mesoderm and fetoplacental circulation.  Why D wrong: Day 30 is neural tube closure."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Neural tube closure occurs on which day according to the notes?","options":["Day 17","Day 21","Day 30","Day 14"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"Day 30 → Neural Tube closure.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These days correspond to other events (fetoplacental circulation, cardiac activity, mesoderm)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Fetal body movement begins at which week of pregnancy (from 1st day of LMP)?","options":["7 weeks","8 weeks","11 weeks","16 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The important-weeks table states fetal body movement begins at 7 weeks.  Why B wrong: 8 weeks marks organogenesis and formation of ovaries/testis.  Why C wrong: 11 weeks is fetal breathing movements.  Why D wrong: 16 weeks relates to fetal skeleton visibility and lanugo appearance."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"At 8 weeks of pregnancy, which of the following occur? (Select all that apply)","options":["Organogenesis begins","Ovaries and testis are formed","Embryo starts looking like a human","Surfactant appears","Baby becomes viable"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The 8-week row lists organogenesis begins, ovaries & testis formed, and embryo starts looking like human.  Why D wrong: Surfactant appears at 24 weeks.  Why E wrong: Viability (India) is at 28 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Fetal breathing movements begin at which week?","options":["7 weeks","11 weeks","12 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"11 weeks → Fetal breathing movements.\"  Why A wrong: 7 weeks is fetal body movement.  Why C wrong: 12 weeks covers insulin/thyroxine/urine/swallowing milestones.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks relates to lanugo, vernix, and meconium."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which fetal functions begin at 12 weeks (3 months) of pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Insulin secretion","Thyroxine secretion","Urine production","Swallowing","Hearing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The 12-week row lists insulin secretion start, thyroxine secretion begins, urine production begins, and swallowing begins.  Why E wrong: Fetus starts hearing at 24 weeks, not 12 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, the urine output of the fetus at term is:","options":["250 mL/day","450 mL/day","650 mL/day","1000 mL/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Urine output of fetus at term 650 mL/day.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These values are not the stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"On USG, the sex of the baby can be determined by which gestational age?","options":["8 weeks","12 weeks (12–14 weeks)","16 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 12-week row states \"On USG–Sex of baby can be determined by 12 weeks (12–14 weeks),\" coinciding with external genitalia differentiation.  Why A wrong: At 8 weeks ovaries/testis form but sex is not yet determinable on USG per the notes.  Why C/D wrong: These are later than the stated 12–14 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"At 16 weeks, an X-ray of the fetus can visualize which feature?","options":["Fetal skeleton (ossification of fetal bones)","Surfactant","Vernix caseosa","Meconium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 16-week row states \"X-Ray: Fetal skeleton is visible (ossification of fetal bones).\"  Why B wrong: Surfactant appears at 24 weeks.  Why C wrong: Vernix caseosa relates to 20 weeks (sebaceous glands).  Why D wrong: Meconium relates to 20 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Lanugo (small brown hair) first appears at which week, and covers the entire body by which week?","options":["Appears at 16 weeks; covers entire body at 20 weeks","Appears at 8 weeks; covers entire body at 12 weeks","Appears at 20 weeks; covers entire body at 24 weeks","Appears at 24 weeks; covers entire body at 28 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state lanugo appears at 16 weeks and \"Lanugo covers entire body\" at 20 weeks.  Why B/C/D wrong: These week combinations contradict the stated milestones."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Vernix caseosa is formed by the sebaceous glands of the fetus at which week?","options":["12 weeks","16 weeks","20 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 20-week row states \"Sebaceous glands of fetus form Vernix Caseosa.\"  Why A/B wrong: These are earlier milestones (functions/skeleton, lanugo appearance).  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is viability, not vernix formation."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Meconium gets its characteristic green colour from which pigment, per the notes?","options":["Bilirubin","Biliverdin (an oil pigment)","Melanin","Haemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 20-week row states meconium is a \"Green substance due to (oil pigment) BILIVERDIN,\" a degradation product of liver enzymes.  Why A wrong: The notes specifically name biliverdin, not bilirubin.  Why C/D wrong: Melanin and haemoglobin are not the named pigment."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, meconium is described as a degradation product of:","options":["Liver enzymes","Renal tubules","Muscle proteins","Amniotic fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state meconium's green substance (biliverdin) is a \"Degradation product of liver enzymes.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"When the fetus is in distress, meconium is passed into the:","options":["Maternal bloodstream","Amniotic fluid","Placenta","Umbilical vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"When fetus is in distress → It passes meconium into Amniotic fluid.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specify amniotic fluid as the destination, not these structures."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"At which week does the fetus start hearing according to the notes?","options":["16 weeks","20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 24-week row states \"Fetus starts hearing.\"  Why A/B wrong: These weeks are associated with skeleton/lanugo and vernix/meconium respectively.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is viability."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Surfactant appears at which week of pregnancy?","options":["20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks","36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 24-week (6 months) row states \"Surfactant appears.\"  Why A wrong: 20 weeks relates to lanugo/vernix/meconium.  Why C wrong: 28 weeks is viability.  Why D wrong: 36 weeks relates to nails/testicular descent/fontanelle."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Surfactant is a soapy material synthesized by which cells?","options":["Type I pneumocytes","Type II pneumocytes","Goblet cells","Clara cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state surfactant is a \"Soapy material synthesized by type II pneumocytes.\"  Why A wrong: Type I pneumocytes are for gas exchange, not surfactant.  Why C/D wrong: These cells are not named as surfactant producers in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"What is the primary function of surfactant according to the notes?","options":["Increase surface tension of the lung","Decrease surface tension of the lung","Increase amniotic fluid","Promote lanugo growth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state surfactant acts to \"Decrease surface tension of lung.\"  Why A wrong: It decreases, not increases, surface tension.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to surfactant function."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, deficient surfactant leads to which condition in the baby after birth?","options":["Respiratory distress","Jaundice","Hypoglycaemia","Hyperbilirubinaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"If it is less it leads to respiratory distress in baby after birth.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the consequence of surfactant deficiency stated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"Which drug is noted to INCREASE surfactant production in the fetus?","options":["Indomethacin","Corticosteroid","Paracetamol","Phenytoin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Corticosteroid → ↑ surfactant production.\"  Why A wrong: Indomethacin is used for polyhydramnios; it does not increase surfactant per the notes.  Why C/D wrong: Paracetamol and phenytoin are not noted to increase surfactant."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"According to the notes, in India, the baby becomes viable at which gestational age?","options":["24 weeks","28 weeks","36 weeks","40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 28-week row states \"India: Baby becomes viable.\"  Why A wrong: 24 weeks is surfactant appearance and hearing.  Why C wrong: 36 weeks relates to nails/testicular descent/fontanelle.  Why D wrong: 40 weeks is term, not the stated viability threshold."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"At 36 weeks, which developmental changes occur according to the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nails grow","Both testes descend into the scrotum","Posterior fontanelle closes","Surfactant first appears","Organogenesis begins"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The 36-week row lists nail growth, descent of both testes into scrotum, and closure of the posterior fontanelle.  Why D wrong: Surfactant appears at 24 weeks.  Why E wrong: Organogenesis begins at 8 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"In the important-weeks table, the weeks are counted from which reference point?","options":["Day of fertilization","Day of implantation","1st day of the last menstrual period (LMP)","Day of the missed period"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table header specifies weeks are counted \"From 1st day to Last menstrual Period\" (i.e., from the 1st day of the LMP / gestational age).  Why A wrong: The important-days table (not weeks) uses day after fertilization; the weeks use LMP.  Why B/D wrong: These are not the stated reference points."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Nurse's role in amnioreduction/amniocentesis for polyhydramnios…","question":"In the important-days table, the days are counted from which reference point?","options":["1st day of the LMP","Day after fertilization","Day of implantation","Day of the missed period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The header states \"Important Days in development of fetus: Day is taken from day after fertilization.\"  Why A wrong: LMP-based counting applies to the important-weeks table, not the important-days table.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated reference points."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"According to the fetal weight chart, what is the approximate weight of the fetus at 4 weeks of gestation?","options":["2 gm","1/2 gm","20 gm","100 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists the weight of the fetus at 4 weeks as 1/2 gm.  Why A wrong: 2 gm corresponds to 8 weeks.  Why C wrong: 20 gm corresponds to 12 weeks.  Why D wrong: 100 gm corresponds to 16 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"As per the fetal weight chart, the weight of the fetus at 8 weeks is:","options":["1/2 gm","2 gm","20 gm","300 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart states 8 weeks = 2 gm.  Why A wrong: 1/2 gm is 4 weeks.  Why C wrong: 20 gm is 12 weeks.  Why D wrong: 300 gm is 20 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The fetal weight at 12 weeks of gestation, per the chart, is:","options":["2 gm","20 gm","100 gm","600 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart records 12 weeks = 20 gm.  Why A wrong: 2 gm is 8 weeks.  Why C wrong: 100 gm is 16 weeks.  Why D wrong: 600 gm is 24 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"According to the chart, the fetal weight at 16 weeks is:","options":["20 gm","100 gm","300 gm","1200 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists 16 weeks = 100 gm.  Why A wrong: 20 gm is 12 weeks.  Why C wrong: 300 gm is 20 weeks.  Why D wrong: 1200 gm is 28 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The fetal weight at 20 weeks of gestation, per the chart, is:","options":["100 gm","300 gm","600 gm","1200 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart records 20 weeks = 300 gm.  Why A wrong: 100 gm is 16 weeks.  Why C wrong: 600 gm is 24 weeks.  Why D wrong: 1200 gm is 28 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"According to the chart, the fetal weight at 24 weeks is:","options":["300 gm","600 gm","1200 gm","500 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists 24 weeks = 600 gm.  Why A wrong: 300 gm is 20 weeks.  Why C wrong: 1200 gm is 28 weeks.  Why D wrong: 500 gm corresponds to about 22 weeks (viability weight), not 24 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"As per the chart, the fetal weight at 28 weeks of gestation is:","options":["600 gm","1000 gm","1200 gm","1500 gm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The chart records 28 weeks = 1200 gm.  Why A wrong: 600 gm is 24 weeks.  Why B wrong: 1000 gm is not listed.  Why D wrong: 1500 gm is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The fetus reaches a weight of 500 gm at approximately how many weeks of gestation?","options":["20 weeks","22 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states weight of fetus = 500 gm at 22 weeks.  Why A wrong: At 20 weeks the fetus weighs 300 gm.  Why C wrong: At 24 weeks the fetus weighs 600 gm.  Why D wrong: At 28 weeks the fetus weighs 1200 gm."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"A fetal weight of 500 gm (at 22 weeks) corresponds to how many days of gestation, as calculated in the notes?","options":["140 days","147 days","154 days","168 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note calculates 22 × 7 = 154 days.  Why A wrong: 140 days = 20 weeks.  Why B wrong: 147 days = 21 weeks.  Why D wrong: 168 days = 24 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"\"Period of viability\" is best defined as the period beyond which:","options":["The fetus stops growing in weight","If a fetus is born, it is capable of independent existence and can be saved","The placenta becomes fully functional","Fetal lungs begin producing surfactant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Period of viability means the period beyond which if a fetus is born it is capable of independent existence and can be saved.  Why A wrong: Fetal weight gain continues; it is not the definition.  Why C wrong: Placental function is unrelated to the definition of viability.  Why D wrong: Surfactant production is a separate concept from viability."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"As per the notes, the period of viability in India is:","options":["20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks","34 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state India = 28 weeks.  Why A wrong: 20 weeks is the USA value.  Why B wrong: 24 weeks is the WHO value.  Why D wrong: 34 weeks is not a viability threshold here."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"According to the notes, the period of viability in the USA is:","options":["20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks","22 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state USA = 20 weeks.  Why B wrong: 24 weeks is the WHO value.  Why C wrong: 28 weeks is the India value.  Why D wrong: 22 weeks corresponds to the 500 gm weight point, not viability."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"As per WHO, the period of viability is:","options":["20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks","37 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state as per WHO = 24 weeks.  Why A wrong: 20 weeks is the USA value.  Why C wrong: 28 weeks is the India value.  Why D wrong: 37 weeks marks term, not viability."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The period of viability differs from country to country. Which sequence correctly matches the values in the notes?","options":["India 24 wk, USA 28 wk, WHO 20 wk","India 28 wk, USA 20 wk, WHO 24 wk","India 20 wk, USA 24 wk, WHO 28 wk","India 28 wk, USA 24 wk, WHO 20 wk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give India = 28 weeks, USA = 20 weeks, WHO = 24 weeks.  Why A wrong: Values are mismatched.  Why C wrong: Values are mismatched.  Why D wrong: USA and WHO values are swapped."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Which cells in the fetal lungs are responsible for producing surfactant?","options":["Type I pneumocytes","Type II pneumocytes","Alveolar macrophages","Goblet cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetal lungs have Type II pneumocytes which produce surfactant.  Why A wrong: Type I pneumocytes are for gas exchange, not surfactant.  Why C wrong: Macrophages perform immune clearance, not surfactant production.  Why D wrong: Goblet cells produce mucus, not surfactant."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"According to the notes, surfactant production begins at approximately what gestational age?","options":["12 weeks","20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks (arrow diagram states \"at 2 weeks\")"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state surfactant is produced but is completely formed between 34 and 37 weeks; production begins in the second trimester (around 24 weeks). Note: the source writes \"at 2 weeks,\" which is a transcription slip for the start of surfactant production; the emphasized fact is complete formation at 34–37 weeks.  Why A wrong: 12 weeks is too early for surfactant.  Why B wrong: 20 weeks precedes typical surfactant appearance.  Why D wrong: The literal \"at 2 weeks\" from the diagram is not biologically valid; complete formation is 34–37 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Surfactant is completely formed in the fetal lungs between which gestational weeks?","options":["24 and 28 weeks","28 and 32 weeks","34 and 37 weeks","37 and 40 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state surfactant is completely formed between 34 and 37 weeks.  Why A wrong: This is earlier than complete formation.  Why B wrong: This precedes complete formation.  Why D wrong: This is post-term to term, later than the stated window."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Surfactant (soapy material) is chemically composed mainly of which substance?","options":["Cholesterol","Phospholipids","Triglycerides","Glycogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the soapy material (surfactant) has phospholipids.  Why A wrong: Cholesterol is a minor component, not the emphasized one.  Why B wrong is not applicable.  Why C wrong: Triglycerides are not the composition of surfactant.  Why D wrong: Glycogen is a storage carbohydrate, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the primary function of pulmonary surfactant?","options":["To increase surface tension of lungs","To decrease surface tension of lungs at the time of breathing","To increase alveolar fluid secretion","To promote fetal red cell production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the function of surfactant is to decrease surface tension of lungs at the time of breathing.  Why A wrong: Surfactant decreases, not increases, surface tension.  Why C wrong: It does not function to increase alveolar fluid.  Why D wrong: Surfactant has no role in red cell production."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"If surfactant is deficient in a newborn, which condition is most likely to develop?","options":["Transient tachypnea of newborn","Respiratory Distress Syndrome","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Bronchopulmonary dysplasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if surfactant is less, it leads to Respiratory Distress Syndrome.  Why A wrong: Not the consequence stated in the notes.  Why C wrong: Meconium aspiration is unrelated to surfactant deficiency here.  Why D wrong: Not the immediate consequence described."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Which class of drugs increases the production of surfactant in the fetal lungs?","options":["Beta-agonists","Corticosteroids","Antibiotics","Calcium channel blockers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the drug which increases production of surfactant is corticosteroids.  Why A wrong: Beta-agonists are tocolytics, not surfactant inducers.  Why C wrong: Antibiotics treat infection, not surfactant production.  Why D wrong: Calcium channel blockers are tocolytics, not surfactant inducers."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Fetal lungs are said to be \"matured\" when the surfactant is completely formed, which occurs at:","options":["24–28 weeks","30–33 weeks","34–37 weeks","38–40 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state when surfactant is completely formed (34–37 weeks) the lungs of the fetus are said to be matured.  Why A wrong: Earlier than lung maturity.  Why B wrong: Earlier than the stated maturity window.  Why D wrong: Later than the stated window."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Which test is most commonly done to assess fetal lung maturity?","options":["Kleihauer-Betke test","Lecithin/Sphingomyelin (L/S) ratio in amniotic fluid","APT test","Nile blue sulphate test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most commonly done lung maturity test is the Lecithin/Sphingomyelin ratio in amniotic fluid.  Why A wrong: Kleihauer-Betke is for Rh-negative pregnancy / anti-D dosing.  Why C wrong: APT test differentiates fetal from maternal bleeding.  Why D wrong: Nile blue sulphate is another maturity test but is not the \"most commonly done\" one per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In the Lecithin/Sphingomyelin (L/S) ratio test, an L/S ratio of ≥2 indicates:","options":["Lungs of fetus are NOT mature","Lungs of fetus are mature","Presence of chorioamnionitis","Fetal anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows L/S ratio ≥2 → lungs of fetus are mature.  Why A wrong: An L/S ratio <2 indicates lungs are not mature.  Why C wrong: The L/S ratio does not indicate infection.  Why D wrong: It does not assess anemia."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In the L/S ratio test, an L/S ratio of <2 indicates:","options":["Lungs of fetus are mature","Lungs of fetus are NOT mature","Surfactant is completely formed","Fetus is at term"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows L/S ratio <2 → lungs of fetus are not mature.  Why A wrong: A ratio ≥2 indicates mature lungs.  Why C wrong: A low ratio indicates incomplete surfactant.  Why D wrong: The ratio does not directly indicate term status."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The \"best test\" for fetal lung maturity looks for which substance in the amniotic fluid?","options":["Lecithin","Sphingomyelin","Phosphatidylglycerol","Bilirubin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the best test as looking for phosphatidylglycerol in amniotic fluid.  Why A wrong: Lecithin is part of the L/S ratio, not the \"best test\" here.  Why B wrong: Sphingomyelin is the denominator of the L/S ratio.  Why D wrong: Bilirubin is measured for hemolytic disease, not lung maturity."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In the phosphatidylglycerol test, if phosphatidylglycerol is PRESENT in amniotic fluid, it indicates:","options":["Lungs of fetus are mature","Lungs of fetus are not mature","Fetal distress","Maternal bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows phosphatidylglycerol present in amniotic fluid → lungs of fetus are mature.  Why B wrong: Absence, not presence, means lungs are not mature.  Why C wrong: It does not indicate distress.  Why D wrong: It does not indicate maternal bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In the phosphatidylglycerol test, if phosphatidylglycerol is ABSENT in amniotic fluid, it indicates:","options":["Lungs of fetus are mature","Lungs of fetus are not mature","Term pregnancy","Presence of surfactant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows phosphatidylglycerol absent → lungs of fetus are not mature.  Why A wrong: Presence, not absence, indicates mature lungs.  Why C wrong: The test does not confirm term status.  Why D wrong: Absence suggests deficient surfactant."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Which is the best test to determine fetal lung maturity specifically in a diabetic mother?","options":["Lecithin/Sphingomyelin ratio","Phosphatidylglycerol","Shake test","Nile blue sulphate test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best test to determine fetal lung maturity in a diabetic mother is phosphatidylglycerol.  Why A wrong: The L/S ratio can be unreliable in diabetic mothers; the notes specify phosphatidylglycerol.  Why C wrong: The shake test is a bedside test, not the best test in diabetics.  Why D wrong: Nile blue sulphate is not the specified best test in diabetics."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The bedside test for fetal lung maturity is also known as the:","options":["APT test","Shake test / Bubble test","Kleihauer-Betke test","Coombs test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the bedside test = shake test / bubble test.  Why A wrong: APT test differentiates fetal from maternal blood.  Why C wrong: Kleihauer-Betke is for anti-D dosing.  Why D wrong: Coombs test detects antibodies, not lung maturity."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In the shake (bubble) test, when amniotic fluid is shaken and bubbles are formed, this indicates the presence of:","options":["Bilirubin","Soap / surfactant","Meconium","Fetal hemoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states if bubbles are formed → soap is present → surfactant is present → lungs of fetus are mature.  Why A wrong: Bubble formation reflects surfactant, not bilirubin.  Why C wrong: Meconium does not create the bubble/soap phenomenon here.  Why D wrong: Fetal hemoglobin is unrelated to the shake test."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Arrange the correct interpretation sequence of the shake (bubble) test as given in the notes.","options":["Bubbles formed → surfactant absent → immature lungs","Bubbles formed → soap present → surfactant present → lungs mature","No bubbles → soap present → lungs mature","Bubbles formed → adult Hb present → maternal blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes chain: shake it → if bubbles are formed → soap is present → surfactant is present → lungs of fetus mature.  Why A wrong: Bubbles indicate surfactant present, not absent.  Why C wrong: Absence of bubbles would not indicate maturity.  Why D wrong: Adult Hb belongs to a different (APT) test."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The Nile blue sulphate test for fetal lung maturity is performed on amniotic fluid because it contains:","options":["Fetal red blood cells","Fetal skin cells","Maternal white blood cells","Amniotic surfactant crystals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Nile blue sulphate test → take amniotic fluid → has fetal skin cells.  Why A wrong: The test targets fetal skin cells, not RBCs.  Why C wrong: It is not based on maternal WBCs.  Why D wrong: It examines cells, not surfactant crystals directly."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"When Nile blue sulphate dye is added to fetal skin cells and the lungs are mature, the cells appear:","options":["Blue","Green","Orange","Colorless"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state when Nile blue sulphate dye is added to fetal skin cells and the lung of the fetus is mature, it appears orange colour cells.  Why A wrong: The dye is blue but the mature-lung cells stain orange.  Why B wrong: Green is not described.  Why D wrong: Colorless is not described."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In the Nile blue sulphate test, fetal lungs are considered mature when the percentage of orange cells is:","options":["≥10%","≥25%","≥50%","≥75%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state orange cells ≥50% → lungs of fetus are mature.  Why A wrong: 10% is below the stated threshold.  Why B wrong: 25% is below the stated threshold.  Why D wrong: 75% is higher than the required threshold; ≥50% suffices."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Which drug is given to accelerate the lung maturity of the fetus?","options":["Betamethasone / Corticosteroids","Oxytocin","Magnesium sulphate","Nifedipine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the drug given to accelerate lung maturity of the fetus is corticosteroids.  Why B wrong: Oxytocin induces labour, it does not mature lungs.  Why C wrong: Magnesium sulphate is for neuroprotection/seizure prevention.  Why D wrong: Nifedipine is a tocolytic, not a lung-maturity accelerator."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In preterm labour at less than 34 weeks, the notes recommend:","options":["Do a lung maturity test first","Always give corticosteroids","Withhold corticosteroids","Give tocolytics only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The indication flow chart shows preterm labour <34 weeks → always give corticosteroids.  Why A wrong: The lung maturity test step is for 34–37 weeks, not <34 weeks.  Why C wrong: Corticosteroids should always be given at <34 weeks.  Why D wrong: The notes emphasize corticosteroids at <34 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In preterm labour between 34 and 37 weeks, the notes advise which first step?","options":["Always give corticosteroids without testing","Do lung maturity test; if lungs not mature, give corticosteroid","Deliver immediately","Give antibiotics only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows 34–37 weeks → do lung maturity test → if lungs not mature → give corticosteroid.  Why A wrong: Testing precedes giving steroids in the 34–37 week window.  Why C wrong: Immediate delivery is not the stated step.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics are not the described step."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Worldwide, the corticosteroid of choice for fetal lung maturity in preterm labour is:","options":["Dexamethasone","Betamethasone","Hydrocortisone","Prednisolone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state worldwide → betamethasone.  Why A wrong: Dexamethasone is the choice in India per the notes.  Why C wrong: Hydrocortisone is not the corticosteroid of choice here.  Why D wrong: Prednisolone is not mentioned as the choice."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In India, the corticosteroid of choice for accelerating fetal lung maturity is:","options":["Betamethasone","Dexamethasone","Methylprednisolone","Triamcinolone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state India → dexamethasone.  Why A wrong: Betamethasone is the worldwide choice.  Why C wrong: Methylprednisolone is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Triamcinolone is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The betamethasone regimen for fetal lung maturity, per the notes, is:","options":["2 IM injections of 12 mg, 24 hours apart","4 IM injections of 6 mg, 12 hours apart","2 IM injections of 6 mg, 12 hours apart","4 IM injections of 12 mg, 24 hours apart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state betamethasone = 2 injections (IM), 12 mg then 12 mg, 24 hours apart.  Why B wrong: That is the dexamethasone regimen.  Why C wrong: The betamethasone dose is 12 mg, not 6 mg, and spacing is 24 hours.  Why D wrong: Betamethasone is 2 injections, not 4."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The dexamethasone regimen for fetal lung maturity, per the notes, is:","options":["2 injections of 12 mg, 24 hours apart","4 injections of 6 mg, 12 hours apart","4 injections of 12 mg, 24 hours apart","2 injections of 6 mg, 12 hours apart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state dexamethasone = 4 injections of 6 mg each, given 12 hours apart.  Why A wrong: That is the betamethasone regimen.  Why C wrong: Dexamethasone dose is 6 mg, not 12 mg.  Why D wrong: Dexamethasone is 4 injections, not 2."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the total number of doses in the dexamethasone protocol for fetal lung maturity?","options":["2 doses","3 doses","4 doses","6 doses"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list dexamethasone as 4 injections (6 mg → 6 mg → 6 mg → 6 mg).  Why A wrong: 2 doses is the betamethasone regimen.  Why B wrong: 3 doses is not the protocol.  Why D wrong: 6 doses is not correct."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the total cumulative dose of betamethasone given for fetal lung maturity per the notes?","options":["12 mg","24 mg","6 mg","48 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Betamethasone is 12 mg + 12 mg = 24 mg total (2 doses).  Why A wrong: 12 mg is a single dose, not the total.  Why C wrong: 6 mg is a single dexamethasone dose.  Why D wrong: 48 mg is not correct."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the total cumulative dose of dexamethasone in the fetal lung maturity protocol?","options":["12 mg","18 mg","24 mg","6 mg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dexamethasone is 4 × 6 mg = 24 mg total.  Why A wrong: 12 mg is under the total.  Why B wrong: 18 mg equals only 3 doses.  Why D wrong: 6 mg is a single dose."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"How long does a corticosteroid need to act after administration for fetal lung maturity?","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state corticosteroid needs 24 hours to act.  Why A wrong: 6 hours is too short per the notes.  Why B wrong: 12 hours is the dexamethasone dosing interval, not the time to act.  Why D wrong: 48 hours is longer than stated."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In which condition is corticosteroid administration for fetal lung maturity contraindicated?","options":["Gestational diabetes","Chorioamnionitis","Preterm labour","Rh-negative pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state corticosteroid is contraindicated in chorioamnionitis.  Why A wrong: Gestational diabetes is not the stated contraindication.  Why C wrong: Preterm labour is the indication, not contraindication.  Why D wrong: Rh-negative pregnancy is unrelated to this contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"By which route is betamethasone administered for fetal lung maturity?","options":["Intravenous (IV)","Intramuscular (IM)","Oral","Subcutaneous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify betamethasone dose = 2 injections (IM).  Why A wrong: IV is not the stated route.  Why C wrong: Oral is not used here.  Why D wrong: Subcutaneous is not the stated route."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The dosing interval between the two betamethasone injections is:","options":["12 hours","24 hours","6 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show the two 12 mg betamethasone doses are separated by 24 hours.  Why A wrong: 12 hours is the dexamethasone interval.  Why C wrong: 6 hours is not stated.  Why D wrong: 48 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The dosing interval between successive dexamethasone injections is:","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show each 6 mg dexamethasone dose is given 12 hours apart.  Why A wrong: 6 hours is not the stated interval.  Why B wrong is not applicable.  Why C wrong: 24 hours is the betamethasone interval.  Why D wrong: 48 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the first (earliest) site of fetal hematopoiesis?","options":["Liver","Bone marrow","Yolk sac","Spleen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the yolk sac as the 1st site for hematopoiesis.  Why A wrong: The liver is the second site (6–20 weeks).  Why B wrong: Bone marrow is the site from ≥20 weeks.  Why D wrong: The spleen accompanies the liver phase but is not the first site."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"During which period is the yolk sac the site of fetal hematopoiesis?","options":["Until 6 weeks of pregnancy","6–20 weeks","≥20 weeks","28–34 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states yolk sac hematopoiesis occurs ≤6 weeks (until 6 weeks of pregnancy).  Why B wrong: 6–20 weeks is the liver/spleen phase.  Why C wrong: ≥20 weeks is the bone marrow phase.  Why D wrong: 28–34 weeks is not a hematopoiesis site period in the table."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Which haemoglobins are the main types formed during the yolk sac phase of hematopoiesis?","options":["Fetal Hb only","Adult Hb only","Gower 1, Gower 2, and Portland Hb","HbA2 and HbS"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Gower 1, Gower 2, and Portland Hb as the main Hb formed at the yolk sac.  Why A wrong: Fetal Hb is the main Hb during the liver/spleen phase.  Why B wrong: Adult Hb is formed in the bone marrow phase.  Why D wrong: HbA2 and HbS are not the yolk sac embryonic haemoglobins."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The liver (and spleen) serve as the site of fetal hematopoiesis during which period?","options":["Until 6 weeks","6–20 weeks of pregnancy","≥20 weeks","After birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states liver (spleen) hematopoiesis occurs at 6–20 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Until 6 weeks is the yolk sac phase.  Why C wrong: ≥20 weeks is bone marrow.  Why D wrong: After birth is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Which is the main haemoglobin formed during the liver/spleen phase of fetal hematopoiesis?","options":["Gower 1","Portland Hb","Fetal Hb","Adult Hb"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Fetal Hb as the main Hb formed during the liver (spleen) phase (6–20 weeks).  Why A wrong: Gower 1 is a yolk sac embryonic Hb.  Why B wrong: Portland Hb is a yolk sac embryonic Hb.  Why D wrong: Adult Hb is formed in the bone marrow phase."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"From which gestational age does the bone marrow become the site of fetal hematopoiesis?","options":["≥6 weeks","≥12 weeks","≥20 weeks","≥28 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states bone marrow hematopoiesis occurs at ≥20 weeks.  Why A wrong: 6 weeks marks the end of the yolk sac phase.  Why B wrong: 12 weeks falls in the liver phase.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is later than the stated onset of ≥20 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Which haemoglobin is mainly formed once bone marrow becomes the site of hematopoiesis (≥20 weeks)?","options":["Gower 2","Portland Hb","Fetal Hb","Adult Hb"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Adult Hb as the main Hb formed in the bone marrow phase (≥20 weeks).  Why A wrong: Gower 2 is a yolk sac embryonic Hb.  Why B wrong: Portland Hb is a yolk sac embryonic Hb.  Why C wrong: Fetal Hb dominates the liver/spleen phase."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the globin chain composition (structure) of fetal haemoglobin (HbF)?","options":["α2β2","α2γ2","α2δ2","β4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Fetal Hb = α2γ2.  Why A wrong: α2β2 is adult Hb (HbA).  Why C wrong: α2δ2 is HbA2, not stated for HbF here.  Why D wrong: β4 (HbH) is a pathological variant, not HbF."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the globin chain composition of adult haemoglobin (HbA)?","options":["α2γ2","α2β2","α2δ2","γ4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Adult Hb = α2β2.  Why A wrong: α2γ2 is fetal Hb.  Why C wrong: α2δ2 is HbA2.  Why D wrong: γ4 (Hb Barts) is a pathological variant."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"A key property of fetal haemoglobin (HbF) that distinguishes it from adult Hb is that it is:","options":["Rapidly hemolyzed by acid/alkali","Resistant to acid and alkali","Unable to bind oxygen","Absent at birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Fetal Hb is resistant to acid/alkali.  Why A wrong: It is adult Hb that hemolyzes with acid/alkali.  Why C wrong: HbF binds oxygen (with high affinity).  Why D wrong: HbF is present at birth (75–80%)."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What happens to adult haemoglobin (HbA) when acid or alkali is added?","options":["It becomes resistant","Hemolysis occurs","It converts to HbF","It precipitates as crystals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state hemolysis occurs when acid or alkali is added to adult Hb.  Why A wrong: It is fetal Hb that is resistant.  Why C wrong: HbA does not convert to HbF.  Why D wrong: Crystal precipitation is not described."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The differing acid/alkali resistance of HbF versus HbA forms the basis of how many important tests, per the notes?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state this difference forms the basis of 2 important tests (APT/Singer test and Kleihauer-Betke test).  Why A wrong: Two tests are described, not one.  Why C wrong: Only two tests are listed.  Why D wrong: Only two tests are listed."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The APT test is also known as the:","options":["Kleihauer-Betke test","Singer alkali denaturation test","Shake test","Coombs test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the APT test as the Singer alkali denaturation test.  Why A wrong: Kleihauer-Betke is a separate test.  Why C wrong: The shake test assesses lung maturity.  Why D wrong: Coombs test detects antibodies."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The APT / Singer alkali denaturation test helps in differentiating between:","options":["HbA2 and HbS","Vasa previa (fetal bleeding) and placenta previa (maternal bleeding)","Preterm and term labour","Rh-positive and Rh-negative blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the APT test helps differentiate between vasa previa (fetal bleeding) and placenta previa (maternal bleeding).  Why A wrong: It does not differentiate HbA2/HbS.  Why C wrong: It does not classify labour timing.  Why D wrong: It does not determine Rh type."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In the APT / Singer alkali denaturation test, which reagent is used?","options":["Nile blue sulphate","KOH / NaOH (alkali)","HCl (acid)","Methylene blue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the reagent = KOH/NaOH (alkali).  Why A wrong: Nile blue sulphate is used in the lung maturity test.  Why C wrong: The APT test uses alkali, not acid.  Why D wrong: Methylene blue is not the reagent here."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In the APT test, if the colour of blood does NOT change after adding KOH, this indicates:","options":["Adult Hb (maternal blood)","Hb is resistant to alkali → HbF (fetal blood) → vasa previa","Placenta previa","Hemolysis of fetal cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if colour of blood doesn't change after adding KOH → Hb is resistant to alkali → HbF (fetal haemoglobin) → fetal blood → vasa previa.  Why A wrong: Adult Hb changes colour (hemolyzes) with alkali.  Why C wrong: Placenta previa corresponds to the colour-change (maternal) pathway.  Why D wrong: Fetal Hb is resistant and does not hemolyze."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In the APT test, if the colour of blood becomes brown/hemolyzed after adding KOH, this indicates:","options":["Fetal Hb → vasa previa","Adult Hb → maternal blood → placenta previa","Resistant Hb → fetal bleeding","Surfactant present"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state colour of blood becomes brown/hemolyzed → there is adult Hb → this is maternal blood → placenta previa.  Why A wrong: Fetal Hb does not hemolyze; the brown change is maternal.  Why C wrong: Resistant Hb would show no colour change.  Why D wrong: Surfactant is unrelated to this test."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"The Kleihauer-Betke test is used in which clinical situation?","options":["To assess fetal lung maturity","In Rh-negative pregnancy to calculate the dose of Anti-D","To induce labour","To measure amniotic fluid volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the Kleihauer-Betke test is used in Rh-negative pregnancy to calculate the dose of Anti-D.  Why A wrong: Lung maturity is assessed by L/S ratio or shake test.  Why C wrong: It does not induce labour.  Why D wrong: It does not measure amniotic fluid."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"During a suspected antepartum haemorrhage where it is unknown whether the mother has placenta previa or vasa previa, the next step described is to:","options":["Perform an emergency cesarean","Collect the blood coming out from the mother's vagina and put it in a test tube","Give corticosteroids","Perform amniocentesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the next step is to collect the blood coming out from the mother's vagina and put it in a test tube (then add KOH).  Why A wrong: Immediate cesarean is not the described next step.  Why C wrong: Corticosteroids are unrelated to this differentiation.  Why D wrong: Amniocentesis is not the step described."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the BEST test to diagnose vasa previa DURING pregnancy (antenatally)?","options":["Singer alkali denaturation test","Doppler USG","Kleihauer-Betke test","Shake test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state to diagnose vasa previa during pregnancy, the best test is Doppler USG.  Why A wrong: The Singer alkali denaturation test is best at the time of bleeding, not antenatally.  Why C wrong: Kleihauer-Betke is for anti-D dosing.  Why D wrong: The shake test assesses lung maturity."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the BEST test to diagnose vasa previa at the TIME of bleeding?","options":["Doppler USG","Singer alkali denaturation test","MRI","Complete blood count"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state to diagnose vasa previa at the time of bleeding, the best test is the Singer alkali denaturation test.  Why A wrong: Doppler USG is best during pregnancy (antenatally), not at time of bleeding.  Why C wrong: MRI is not the stated test.  Why D wrong: CBC does not differentiate fetal from maternal blood."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Which test is best to differentiate between vasa previa and placenta previa, and between HbF and HbA?","options":["Doppler USG","Singer alkali denaturation test","Kleihauer-Betke test","Nile blue sulphate test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the Singer alkali denaturation test is the best test to differentiate between vasa previa and placenta previa, and between HbF and HbA.  Why A wrong: Doppler USG diagnoses vasa previa antenatally but does not differentiate HbF/HbA.  Why C wrong: Kleihauer-Betke is for anti-D dosing.  Why D wrong: Nile blue sulphate assesses lung maturity."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Compared to maternal RBCs, fetal RBCs are described as:","options":["Small in size","Large in size","Nucleated always","Absent at birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states fetal RBCs are large; maternal RBCs are small.  Why A wrong: Small is the maternal RBC description.  Why C wrong: The notes do not state fetal RBCs are always nucleated here.  Why D wrong: Fetal RBCs are present at birth."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the lifespan of a fetal RBC according to the notes?","options":["60 days","90 days","120 days","150 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states fetal RBC lifespan = 90 days.  Why A wrong: 60 days is not stated.  Why C wrong: 120 days is the maternal RBC lifespan.  Why D wrong: 150 days is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"What is the lifespan of a maternal (adult) RBC according to the notes?","options":["90 days","100 days","120 days","150 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states maternal RBC lifespan = 120 days.  Why A wrong: 90 days is the fetal RBC lifespan.  Why B wrong: 100 days is not stated.  Why D wrong: 150 days is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"At the time of birth, the normal haemoglobin (Hb) level of the baby is:","options":["11–13 g/dL","14–15 g/dL","16–18 g/dL","20–22 g/dL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state at the time of birth, baby's Hb = 16–18 g/dL.  Why A wrong: 11–13 g/dL is lower than the neonatal value.  Why B wrong: 14–15 g/dL is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 20–22 g/dL is above the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"At the time of birth, what percentage of the newborn's haemoglobin is fetal haemoglobin (HbF)?","options":["20–25%","50%","75–80%","90–95%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state at birth HbF = 75–80%.  Why A wrong: 20–25% is the HbA fraction at birth.  Why B wrong: 50% is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 90–95% is above the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"At the time of birth, what percentage of the newborn's haemoglobin is adult haemoglobin (HbA)?","options":["20–25%","40–50%","60–70%","75–80%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state at birth HbA = 20–25%.  Why B wrong: 40–50% is not stated.  Why C wrong: 60–70% is not stated.  Why D wrong: 75–80% is the HbF fraction at birth."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"Which statement correctly matches the newborn haemoglobin fractions at birth?","options":["HbF 20–25%, HbA 75–80%","HbF 75–80%, HbA 20–25%","HbF 50%, HbA 50%","HbF 90%, HbA 10%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HbF = 75–80% and HbA = 20–25% at birth.  Why A wrong: The fractions are reversed.  Why C wrong: They are not equal.  Why D wrong: Values do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In a suspected placenta previa, the blood collected and treated with KOH turns brown/hemolyzed because it is which type of blood?","options":["Fetal blood containing HbF","Maternal blood containing adult Hb","Amniotic fluid","Mixed cord blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes trace the brown/hemolyzed KOH reaction to adult Hb, indicating maternal blood → placenta previa.  Why A wrong: Fetal blood (HbF) is resistant and does not hemolyze/change colour.  Why C wrong: Amniotic fluid is not what is being tested here.  Why D wrong: The test distinguishes maternal vs fetal, and brown = maternal."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Weight of fetus by gestational age, period of viability…","question":"In the APT/Singer test, fetal haemoglobin (HbF) resisting alkali denaturation indicates the source of bleeding is:","options":["Maternal (placenta previa)","Fetal (vasa previa)","Uterine atony","Cervical erosion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state resistant Hb (HbF, fetal blood) indicates vasa previa.  Why A wrong: Placenta previa is maternal bleeding with adult Hb.  Why C wrong: Uterine atony is not evaluated by this test.  Why D wrong: Cervical erosion is not evaluated by this test."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"By the time a baby becomes 6 months old, what percentage of haemoglobin is adult haemoglobin (HbA)?","options":["50% HbA","75% HbA","99% HbA","100% HbA"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that by 6 months of age, 99% Hb = HbA and <1% Hb = HbF.  Why A wrong: 50% is not the stated value; the switch to HbA is nearly complete by 6 months.  Why B wrong: 75% understates the adult haemoglobin fraction at 6 months.  Why D wrong: A trace (<1%) of HbF persists, so it is 99%, not 100%."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"By 6 months of age, approximately what proportion of haemoglobin remains as fetal haemoglobin (HbF)?","options":["<1%","10%","25%","50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state <1% Hb = HbF by 6 months of age.  Why B, C, D wrong: These overstate the residual HbF; by 6 months HbF is reduced to less than 1%."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In fetal circulation, the site of oxygenation of blood is the:","options":["Lungs","Placenta","Liver","Kidneys"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In fetal circulation the site of oxygenation is the placenta (fetal lungs are non-functional in utero).  Why A wrong: Lungs are the site of oxygenation in adult (postnatal) circulation, not fetal.  Why C, D wrong: The liver and kidneys are not sites of oxygenation."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In adult circulation, the site of oxygenation of blood is the:","options":["Placenta","Liver","Lungs","Spleen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In adult circulation the site of oxygenation is the lungs.  Why A wrong: The placenta is the fetal site of oxygenation.  Why B, D wrong: Liver and spleen are not sites of gas exchange."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In fetal circulation, oxygenated blood is carried from the placenta to the fetus through the:","options":["Umbilical artery","Umbilical vein","Ductus arteriosus","Pulmonary vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygenated blood is carried from placenta to fetus by the umbilical vein.  Why A wrong: The umbilical artery carries deoxygenated blood from fetus to placenta.  Why C wrong: The ductus arteriosus is a shunt between the pulmonary artery and aorta, not the placental supply.  Why D wrong: The pulmonary vein is part of the pulmonary (postnatal) circuit."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In fetal circulation, deoxygenated blood is carried from the fetus back to the placenta through the:","options":["Umbilical vein","Ductus venosus","Umbilical artery","Inferior vena cava"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Deoxygenated blood is carried from fetus to placenta by the umbilical artery.  Why A wrong: The umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood to the fetus.  Why B wrong: The ductus venosus connects the umbilical vein to the IVC.  Why D wrong: The IVC returns blood to the heart within the fetus, not to the placenta."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Which statement correctly describes how the two sides of the heart work in fetal versus adult circulation?","options":["Fetal — in series; Adult — parallelly","Fetal — parallelly; Adult — in series","Both work in series in fetus and adult","Both work parallelly in fetus and adult"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In fetal circulation both sides of the heart work parallelly; in adult circulation they work in series.  Why A wrong: It reverses the correct relationship.  Why C, D wrong: The fetal and adult arrangements differ (parallel vs series), so a single description for both is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In adult circulation, oxygenated blood is carried through _____ and deoxygenated blood through _____.","options":["Veins; arteries","Arteries; veins","Capillaries; arteries","Arteries; capillaries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In adult circulation oxygenated blood is carried through arteries and deoxygenated blood by veins.  Why A wrong: It reverses the relationship.  Why C, D wrong: Capillaries are exchange vessels, not the described transport vessels."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"How many shunts (bypasses) are present in fetal circulation?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal circulation has 3 shunts (bypasses): ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus.  Why A, B wrong: Fewer than three; the notes list three distinct shunts.  Why D wrong: There are three, not four, shunts."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"How many shunts are present in normal adult circulation?","options":["No shunts","1 shunt","2 shunts","3 shunts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Adult circulation has no shunts.  Why B, C, D wrong: The three fetal shunts close after birth, leaving no shunts in the adult."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In the fetal circulation shunts diagram, the foramen ovale allows blood to pass between which two structures?","options":["Right atrium to left atrium","Umbilical vein to IVC","Pulmonary artery to aorta","Left ventricle to aorta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The foramen ovale connects the right atrium (RA) to the left atrium (LA).  Why B wrong: That describes the ductus venosus.  Why C wrong: That describes the ductus arteriosus.  Why D wrong: This is a normal outflow, not a shunt."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In fetal circulation, the inferior vena cava (IVC) carries which type of blood toward the heart?","options":["Purely deoxygenated blood","Oxygenated blood (as labelled in the fetal diagram)","Lymph","Only plasma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the fetal circulation shunts diagram the IVC is labelled as carrying oxygenated blood (delivered via the umbilical vein/ductus venosus).  Why A wrong: The SVC is labelled deoxygenated (\"SVC Deoxy\"); the IVC is oxygenated in the fetus.  Why C, D wrong: The IVC carries blood, not lymph or plasma alone."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The ductus venosus in fetal circulation connects which two structures?","options":["Right atrium to left atrium","Pulmonary artery to aorta","Umbilical vein to inferior vena cava (IVC)","Umbilical artery to aorta"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ductus venosus connects the umbilical vein to the IVC (inferior vena cava).  Why A wrong: That is the foramen ovale.  Why B wrong: That is the ductus arteriosus.  Why D wrong: The umbilical artery is not connected to the aorta via this shunt."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The ductus arteriosus connects which two vessels in fetal circulation?","options":["Umbilical vein to IVC","Pulmonary artery to the aorta","RA to LA","Aorta to pulmonary vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary artery to the aorta.  Why A wrong: That is the ductus venosus.  Why C wrong: That is the foramen ovale.  Why D wrong: The shunt links the pulmonary artery to the aorta, not aorta to pulmonary vein."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"At the time of birth, which of the following occurs?","options":["Lungs stop functioning and placenta starts functioning","Lungs start functioning and placenta stops functioning","Both lungs and placenta stop functioning","Both lungs and placenta continue functioning together"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At birth the lungs start functioning and the placenta stops functioning.  Why A wrong: It reverses the events.  Why C wrong: The lungs begin, not stop, functioning.  Why D wrong: The placenta ceases to function after birth."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"After birth, which structure becomes the site of oxygenation?","options":["Placenta","Lungs","Liver","Umbilical vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With the first breath, the lungs become the site of oxygenation.  Why A wrong: The placenta stops functioning at birth.  Why C, D wrong: Neither the liver nor the umbilical vein is a site of oxygenation."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Which of the following structures close at the time of birth? (Select all that apply)","options":["Umbilical artery","Umbilical vein","Ductus arteriosus","Ductus venosus","Foramen ovale"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list five structures that close at birth: umbilical artery, umbilical vein, ductus arteriosus, ductus venosus, and foramen ovale.  Why each is correct: Each is a fetal shunt/vessel that becomes non-functional and then closes after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In terms of closure timing, functional closure of the fetal structures means:","options":["They close anatomically over several weeks","All these structures stop functioning immediately after birth","They only close after 1 year","They never truly close"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Functional closure means all these structures stop functioning immediately after birth.  Why A wrong: That describes anatomical closure, which takes weeks to a year.  Why C wrong: Foramen ovale takes 1 year for anatomical closure, but functional closure is immediate.  Why D wrong: They do close both functionally and anatomically."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Which fetal shunt is the LAST to close functionally, and in what time frame?","options":["Foramen ovale, 1 year","Ductus arteriosus, immediately","Ductus venosus, 12–96 hours after delivery","Umbilical vein, 2–3 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ductus venosus is the last to close, at 12–96 hours after delivery.  Why A wrong: Foramen ovale takes 1 year for anatomical closure but is not the \"last to close\" functionally per the note.  Why B wrong: The ductus arteriosus is not the last to close functionally.  Why D wrong: 2–3 weeks is the anatomical closure time of the umbilical vein, not the \"last to close\" functional note."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Anatomical closure of the umbilical vein and ductus venosus occurs at approximately:","options":["12–96 hours","2–3 weeks","2–3 months","1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anatomical closure of the umbilical vein and ductus venosus occurs at 2–3 weeks.  Why A wrong: 12–96 hours is the last-to-close (functional) time of the ductus venosus.  Why C wrong: 2–3 months is the anatomical closure time of the umbilical artery and ductus arteriosus.  Why D wrong: 1 year is the anatomical closure time of the foramen ovale."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Anatomical closure of the umbilical artery and ductus arteriosus occurs at approximately:","options":["2–3 weeks","2–3 months","6 months","1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The umbilical artery and ductus arteriosus achieve anatomical closure at 2–3 months.  Why A wrong: 2–3 weeks applies to the umbilical vein and ductus venosus.  Why C wrong: 6 months is not a stated closure time.  Why D wrong: 1 year applies to the foramen ovale."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The foramen ovale undergoes anatomical closure at approximately:","options":["12–96 hours","2–3 weeks","2–3 months","1 year"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Anatomical closure of the foramen ovale occurs at 1 year.  Why A, B, C wrong: These are the timings for the ductus venosus (functional), umbilical vein/ductus venosus, and umbilical artery/ductus arteriosus respectively."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The ductus arteriosus becomes which adult remnant after birth?","options":["Ligamentum teres","Ligamentum arteriosum","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum venosum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum.  Why A wrong: Ligamentum teres is the remnant of the umbilical vein.  Why C wrong: Fossa ovalis is the remnant of the foramen ovale.  Why D wrong: Ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The ductus venosus becomes which adult remnant?","options":["Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum venosum","Ligamentum teres","Fossa ovalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum.  Why A wrong: Ligamentum arteriosum is from the ductus arteriosus.  Why C wrong: Ligamentum teres is from the umbilical vein.  Why D wrong: Fossa ovalis is from the foramen ovale."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The foramen ovale becomes which adult remnant?","options":["Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum teres","Ligamentum venosum","Superior vesical artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis.  Why B wrong: Ligamentum teres is from the umbilical vein.  Why C wrong: Ligamentum venosum is from the ductus venosus.  Why D wrong: The superior vesical artery is derived from the proximal umbilical artery."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The umbilical vein becomes which adult remnant?","options":["Ligamentum venosum","Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum teres","Medial umbilical ligament"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres.  Why A wrong: Ligamentum venosum is from the ductus venosus.  Why B wrong: Ligamentum arteriosum is from the ductus arteriosus.  Why D wrong: Medial umbilical ligament is from the distal umbilical artery."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The proximal part of the umbilical artery persists in the adult as the:","options":["Medial umbilical ligament","Superior vesical artery","Ligamentum teres","Fossa ovalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The proximal part of the umbilical artery becomes the superior vesical artery.  Why A wrong: The distal part becomes the medial umbilical ligament.  Why C wrong: Ligamentum teres is from the umbilical vein.  Why D wrong: Fossa ovalis is from the foramen ovale."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The distal part of the umbilical artery persists in the adult as the:","options":["Superior vesical artery","Ligamentum venosum","Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum arteriosum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The distal part of the umbilical artery becomes the medial umbilical ligament.  Why A wrong: The proximal part becomes the superior vesical artery.  Why B, D wrong: These are remnants of the ductus venosus and ductus arteriosus respectively."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"What is the total duration of a normal pregnancy in months (as stated in the notes)?","options":["9 months exactly","9 months + 7 days","10 months","8 months + 7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total duration of pregnancy = 9 months + 7 days.  Why A wrong: An extra 7 days is added beyond 9 months.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 9 months + 7 days."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The total duration of a normal pregnancy expressed in weeks is:","options":["38 weeks","40 weeks","42 weeks","36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total duration of pregnancy = 40 weeks.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated value is 40 weeks, calculated from the 1st day of LMP."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The total duration of pregnancy expressed in days is:","options":["266 days","270 days","280 days","294 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Total duration of pregnancy = 280 days.  Why A wrong: 266 days is from conception, not from LMP.  Why B wrong: 270 is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 294 days (42 weeks) is the post-term threshold, not the normal duration."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The total duration of pregnancy (40 weeks / 280 days) is calculated from:","options":["Day of conception","Day of implantation","1st day of the last menstrual period (LMP)","Day of the missed period"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The duration is calculated from the 1st day of the last menstrual period (LMP).  Why A, B wrong: Conception/implantation occurs about 2 weeks after the LMP; the standard calculation uses LMP.  Why D wrong: The missed period (28th day) is a later landmark, not the reference point."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"On the pregnancy timeline, ovulation (fertilization) typically occurs on which day?","options":["1st day","7th day","14th day","28th day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 14th day corresponds to ovulation (fertilization).  Why A wrong: The 1st day is the 1st day of LMP.  Why B wrong: 7th day is not a stated landmark.  Why D wrong: The 28th day corresponds to the missed period."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"On the pregnancy timeline, the 28th day corresponds to:","options":["Ovulation","Missed period","Implantation","First day of LMP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 28th day corresponds to the missed period.  Why A wrong: Ovulation is on the 14th day.  Why C wrong: Implantation is not the labelled event here.  Why D wrong: The 1st day is the first day of LMP."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"\"Period of pregnancy / gestational age / period of amenorrhea\" is calculated from which point?","options":["Day of ovulation","Day of fertilization","1st day of LMP","Missed period"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Gestational age (period of pregnancy/period of amenorrhea) is calculated from the 1st day of LMP.  Why A, B wrong: These occur roughly 14 days after the LMP.  Why D wrong: The missed period is a later event, not the reference point."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Which formula is used to calculate the expected date of delivery (EDD)?","options":["Johnson's formula","Naegele's formula","McDonald's rule","Haase's rule"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The EDD is calculated using Naegele's formula.  Why A wrong: Johnson's formula estimates fetal weight.  Why C wrong: McDonald's rule relates fundal height to gestational age.  Why D wrong: Haase's rule estimates fetal length."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"According to Naegele's formula as written in the notes, EDD is obtained by taking the 1st day of LMP and:","options":["Adding 9 months + 7 days","Subtracting 3 months + 7 days","Adding 7 months + 9 days","Adding 9 months only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state: 1st day of LMP + 9 months + 7 days = EDD.  Why B wrong: The classic \"minus 3 months + 7 days\" is an alternative form but not the version written here.  Why C wrong: It swaps the months and days values.  Why D wrong: The 7 days must also be added."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"If the LMP is 15th August 2020, what is the EDD by Naegele's formula (as worked in the notes)?","options":["22nd May 2021","22nd May 2020","22nd April 2021","15th May 2021"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The worked example gives August + 9 months = May, 15 + 7 = 22, so EDD = 22nd May 2020 (as written in the notes).  Why A wrong: The notes' worked answer is dated 2020, not 2021.  Why C wrong: +9 months from August is May, not April.  Why D wrong: 15 + 7 = 22, not 15."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"What percentage of females deliver on their exact expected date of delivery (EDD)?","options":["40%","50%","80%","90%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Only 40% of females deliver on their exact EDD.  Why B wrong: 50% deliver within 1 week before or after the EDD, not on the exact date.  Why C, D wrong: These overstate the proportion delivering exactly on the EDD."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"According to the notes, 50% of females deliver:","options":["Exactly on the EDD","Either 1 week before or 1 week after their EDD","More than 2 weeks after the EDD","Only preterm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 50% of females deliver either 1 week before or 1 week after their EDD.  Why A wrong: Only 40% deliver exactly on the EDD.  Why C, D wrong: The note specifies within 1 week of the EDD, not beyond 2 weeks or exclusively preterm."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The \"period of viability\" is best defined as:","options":["The period after which delivery must be induced","The period after which, if delivery occurs, the fetus is capable of independent existence","The period during which the placenta forms","The first trimester of pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Period of viability is the period after which, if delivery occurs, the fetus is capable of independent existence.  Why A wrong: It is not about mandatory induction.  Why C, D wrong: It is not defined by placental formation or the first trimester."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"According to the notes, the period of viability depends on:","options":["Maternal age only","The medical facility available in that country","The fetal sex","The season of birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The period of viability differs from country to country and depends on the medical facility available in that country.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the determinants stated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The period of viability in the United States (US) is:","options":["20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks","37 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the US period of viability as 20 weeks.  Why B wrong: 24 weeks is the WHO value.  Why C wrong: 28 weeks is the India value.  Why D wrong: 37 weeks is the term-delivery threshold, not viability."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"The period of viability in India is:","options":["20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks","22 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The India period of viability is 28 weeks.  Why A wrong: 20 weeks is the US value.  Why B wrong: 24 weeks is the WHO value.  Why D wrong: 22 weeks is when fetal weight = 500 g, not the India viability period."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"According to the WHO, the period of viability is:","options":["20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks","40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO period of viability is 24 weeks.  Why A wrong: 20 weeks is the US value.  Why C wrong: 28 weeks is the India value.  Why D wrong: 40 weeks is the total duration of pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Which term describes the death of a fetus BEFORE the period of viability?","options":["Abortion","Intrauterine death (IUD)","Stillbirth","Post-term delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Abortion is death of the fetus before the period of viability.  Why B wrong: IUD is death of the fetus after the period of viability.  Why C wrong: Stillbirth is death of the fetus during the process of delivery.  Why D wrong: Post-term delivery refers to timing (≥42 weeks), not fetal death."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"By the WHO definition, an abortion is the death of a fetus when the fetal weight is:","options":["> 500 gms","< 500 gms","> 1000 gms","= 2500 gms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO defines abortion as death of the fetus when weight is < 500 gms.  Why A wrong: > 500 gms corresponds to the IUD definition.  Why C, D wrong: These weights are not part of the definition."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Which term describes death of the fetus AFTER the period of viability?","options":["Abortion","Intrauterine death (IUD)","Stillbirth","Preterm birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IUD (intrauterine death) is death of the fetus after the period of viability.  Why A wrong: Abortion is death before viability.  Why C wrong: Stillbirth is death during the process of delivery.  Why D wrong: Preterm birth is a live birth before 37 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"By the WHO definition, intrauterine death (IUD) is death of the fetus when its weight is:","options":["< 500 gms","> 500 gms","< 1000 gms","exactly 500 gms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO defines IUD as death of the fetus when weight is > 500 gms.  Why A wrong: < 500 gms corresponds to abortion.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the WHO definition."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Death of the fetus DURING the process of delivery is termed:","options":["Abortion","IUD","Stillbirth","Neonatal death"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Stillbirth is death of the fetus during the process of delivery.  Why A wrong: Abortion is death before viability.  Why B wrong: IUD is death after viability but before delivery.  Why D wrong: Neonatal death occurs after birth, not during delivery, and is not the term given."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"At approximately what gestational age does the fetal weight reach 500 gms?","options":["20 weeks / 140 days","22 weeks / 154 days","24 weeks / 168 days","28 weeks / 196 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetal weight = 500 gms at 22 weeks / 154 days.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 22 weeks (154 days) value."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A delivery occurring at less than 37 weeks of gestation is classified as:","options":["Term delivery","Preterm delivery","Post-term delivery","Abortion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delivery at < 37 weeks is preterm.  Why A wrong: Term is 37 weeks to 41 weeks 7 days.  Why C wrong: Post-term is ≥ 42 weeks.  Why D wrong: Abortion occurs before the period of viability, well before 37 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A delivery occurring at 42 weeks or more of gestation is classified as:","options":["Term delivery","Preterm delivery","Post-term delivery","Early-term delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Delivery at ≥ 42 weeks is post-term.  Why A wrong: Term is 37–41 weeks 7 days.  Why B wrong: Preterm is < 37 weeks.  Why D wrong: \"Early-term\" is not a category in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Post-term delivery (≥ 42 weeks) corresponds to how many days?","options":["280 days","266 days","294 days","300 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 42 × 7 = 294 days, as stated in the notes.  Why A wrong: 280 days is the normal 40-week duration.  Why B wrong: 266 days is the duration from conception.  Why D wrong: 300 days is not a value given."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A term delivery is defined as delivery occurring between:","options":["34 weeks and 40 weeks","37 weeks and 41 weeks 7 days","38 weeks and 42 weeks","40 weeks and 44 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Term delivery is between 37 weeks → 41 weeks 7 days.  Why A, C, D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated term window."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"\"Gravida\" is defined as:","options":["The number of pregnancies that reached the period of viability","The total number of times a female becomes pregnant, irrespective of the outcome of pregnancy","Only the number of live births","The number of living children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gravida is the total number of times a female becomes pregnant, irrespective of pregnancy outcome.  Why A wrong: That describes parity, not gravida.  Why C, D wrong: Gravida counts pregnancies, not live births or living children."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"When counting gravida, which of the following is TRUE?","options":["The present (current) pregnancy is excluded","The present pregnancy is included","Abortions are not counted","Ectopic pregnancy is excluded"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In gravida, the present pregnancy is included.  Why A wrong: The current pregnancy IS counted in gravida.  Why C, D wrong: Abortion, ectopic, molar, IUD, and stillbirth are all counted in gravida."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Which of the following outcomes ARE counted toward gravida? (Select all that apply)","options":["Abortion","Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy","Intrauterine death (IUD)","Stillbirth"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Gravida counts every pregnancy irrespective of outcome — abortion, ectopic pregnancy, molar pregnancy, intrauterine death, and stillbirth are all included.  Why each is correct: The notes explicitly list abortion/ectopic/molar/IUD/stillbirth as counted."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"For the purpose of counting gravida, a twin pregnancy is counted as:","options":["Two pregnancies","A single pregnancy","Three pregnancies","Zero pregnancies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In gravida, twins are taken as a single pregnancy.  Why A, C, D wrong: A twin gestation is one pregnancy event, so it counts as one."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A \"nulligravida\" is a female who:","options":["Has been pregnant once","Has never become pregnant","Has had ≥4 pregnancies","Has had one viable pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nulligravida (No + pregnancy) is any female who has never become pregnant.  Why A wrong: That is a primigravida.  Why C wrong: That relates to grand multiparity/multigravida, not nulligravida.  Why D wrong: One viable pregnancy defines primipara, not nulligravida."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A \"primigravida\" is a female who:","options":["Has never conceived","Has conceived for the first time","Has been pregnant more than once","Has had four or more viable pregnancies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Primigravida is a female who has conceived for the first time.  Why A wrong: That is nulligravida.  Why C wrong: That is multigravida.  Why D wrong: That relates to grand multipara."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A \"multigravida\" is a female who:","options":["Has never been pregnant","Is pregnant for the first time","Has become pregnant more than once","Has exactly one viable pregnancy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Multigravida is any female who has become pregnant more than once.  Why A wrong: That is nulligravida.  Why B wrong: That is primigravida.  Why D wrong: One viable pregnancy defines primipara."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"\"Elderly primigravida\" refers to a first-time pregnancy in a female aged:","options":["≥ 30 years","≥ 35 years","≥ 40 years","≥ 25 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Elderly primigravida = age of female ≥ 35 years with 1st-time pregnancy.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated age cut-off is 35 years, not 30, 40, or 25."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"\"Parity\" is defined as the number of pregnancies which have:","options":["Ended in abortion","Gone beyond the period of viability","Been diagnosed in the first trimester","Resulted in cesarean section"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parity is the number of pregnancies that have gone beyond the period of viability.  Why A wrong: Abortions are before viability and are not counted as parity.  Why C, D wrong: Parity is defined by crossing the viability threshold, not by trimester of diagnosis or mode of delivery."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Regarding parity, if a female is currently pregnant, her current pregnancy is:","options":["Always included in parity","Never included in parity","Counted as two","Counted only if she is elderly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a female is currently pregnant, her current pregnancy is never included in parity.  Why A wrong: The current (undelivered) pregnancy is excluded from parity.  Why C, D wrong: These are not stated rules."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In parity, a twin pregnancy is counted as 1, EXCEPT when:","options":["The twins are premature","We are counting the number of living children","One twin has died","The mother is elderly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In parity a twin pregnancy is taken as 1, except when counting the number of living children.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated exception."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A \"nullipara\" is a female in whom:","options":["None of the pregnancies was a viable pregnancy","Exactly one pregnancy was viable","More than one pregnancy was viable","Four or more pregnancies were viable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nullipara = none of the pregnancies was a viable pregnancy.  Why B wrong: That is primipara.  Why C wrong: That is multipara.  Why D wrong: That is grand multipara."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A \"primipara\" is a female who has:","options":["No viable pregnancy","1 viable pregnancy","More than one viable pregnancy","≥ 4 viable pregnancies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Primipara is a female who has 1 viable pregnancy.  Why A wrong: That is nullipara.  Why C wrong: That is multipara.  Why D wrong: That is grand multipara."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A \"multipara\" is a female in whom:","options":["No pregnancy was viable","Exactly 1 pregnancy is viable","More than 1 (>1) pregnancy is viable","≥ 4 pregnancies are viable"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Multipara is a female in whom >1 pregnancy is viable.  Why A wrong: That is nullipara.  Why B wrong: That is primipara.  Why D wrong: That is grand multipara."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A \"grand multipara\" is a female who has:","options":["1 viable pregnancy","2 viable pregnancies","3 viable pregnancies","≥ 4 viable pregnancies"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Grand multipara = female who has ≥ 4 viable pregnancies.  Why A wrong: That is primipara.  Why B, C wrong: These fall under multipara (>1) but do not meet the ≥4 threshold."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"A primigravida (first-time conceive) is referred to as what after her delivery?","options":["Nullipara","Primipara","Multipara","Grand multipara"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A primigravida (1st time conceive) → after delivery becomes a primipara.  Why A wrong: Nullipara has had no viable pregnancy.  Why C, D wrong: These require more than one/≥4 viable pregnancies."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In the GTPAL system, \"G\" stands for:","options":["Gestation weeks","Gravida (number of times female has conceived)","Growth","Gynaecology"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In GTPAL, G = Gravida = the number of times the female has conceived.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not what \"G\" represents in the GTPAL system."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In the GTPAL system, \"T\" (term) refers to the number of term pregnancies, defined as gestation of:","options":["< 37 weeks","37 weeks → 41 weeks + 6 days","≥ 42 weeks","< 20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In GTPAL, T = term pregnancies = 37 weeks → 41 weeks + 6 days.  Why A wrong: < 37 weeks is preterm (P).  Why C wrong: ≥ 42 weeks is post-term.  Why D wrong: < 20 weeks would be an abortion range."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In the GTPAL system, \"P\" (preterm) refers to the number of preterm deliveries, defined as delivery at:","options":["< 37 weeks","37–41 weeks","≥ 42 weeks","Exactly 40 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In GTPAL, P = preterm delivery = < 37 weeks.  Why B wrong: 37–41 weeks is the term (T) range.  Why C wrong: ≥ 42 weeks is post-term.  Why D wrong: 40 weeks is a term delivery."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In the GTPAL system, \"A\" stands for:","options":["Age","Abortion (number of abortions)","Amniotic fluid","Anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In GTPAL, A = Abortion = number of abortions.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not represented by \"A\" in the GTPAL system."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In the GTPAL system, \"L\" stands for:","options":["Labour","Living children","Lactation","Length of gestation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In GTPAL, L = Living children (living children at present).  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not what \"L\" represents."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"When counting the \"L\" (living children) in GTPAL, twins are counted as how many?","options":["1","2","3","0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For living children, twins = 2 (each living child is counted).  Why A wrong: Twins count as 1 for gravida/parity, but as 2 for living children.  Why C wrong: Triplets = 3, not twins.  Why D wrong: A death is counted as 0, but living twins are counted as 2."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"When counting living children in GTPAL, triplets are counted as how many?","options":["1","2","3","0"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For living children, triplets = 3.  Why A, B wrong: Each living child is counted individually, so triplets = 3.  Why D wrong: 0 applies to a child who has died."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"When counting living children in GTPAL, a child who has died is counted as:","options":["1","2","0","0.5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the living-children count, Death = 0 (a deceased child is not a living child).  Why A, B, D wrong: A dead child does not add to the living-children total; it is counted as 0."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Case 1: A 32-weeks-pregnant female has a 5-year-old son delivered vaginally at 38 weeks, twin daughters born at 34 weeks (now 3 years old), and one son born at term 1 year back who died of diarrhea. What is her GTPAL (Gx Pm+n form)?","options":["G4 P2+1+0+3","G4 P1+2+0+3","G3 P2+1+0+3","G4 P2+1+0+4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give G4 P2+1+0+3 — Gravida 4 (current + 3 prior pregnancies), Term = 2 (38-week son and the term son who died), Preterm = 1 (twin gestation at 34 weeks counts as one pregnancy), Abortion = 0, and Living children = 3 (5-year-old son + 2 living twin daughters).  Why B wrong: It swaps the term (2) and preterm (1) counts.  Why C wrong: Gravida is 4 (the current pregnancy is included), not 3.  Why D wrong: Living children = 3, not 4 (the term son died = 0)."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"Case 2: Savita is 39 weeks pregnant with a baby girl born at 39 weeks, a second baby born at 38 weeks, and a history of 1 abortion. What is her GTPAL (Gx Pm+n form)?","options":["G3 P2+0+1+2","G4 P2+0+1+2","G4 P1+1+1+2","G4 P2+1+0+2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give G4 P2+0+1+2 — Gravida 4 (current pregnancy + 2 prior term deliveries + 1 abortion), Term = 2 (39-week and 38-week deliveries, both ≥37 weeks), Preterm = 0, Abortion = 1, and Living children = 2.  Why A wrong: Gravida is 4, not 3 (current pregnancy is included).  Why C wrong: A 38-week birth is term, not preterm, so preterm should be 0 and term 2.  Why D wrong: The abortion (1) must be recorded; preterm is 0, not 1."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In the shorthand \"Gx Pm+n,\" the total parity expression records term, preterm, abortion, and living children as a way of expressing:","options":["Only the number of living children","Gravida and parity together","The expected date of delivery","The Bishop score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe \"an easier way of writing Gx Pm+n\" as expressing GRAVIDA together with parity components (term/preterm/abortion/living).  Why A wrong: It captures more than just living children.  Why C, D wrong: It is unrelated to EDD or the Bishop score."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Fetal haemoglobin switch, fetal vs adult circulation…","question":"In the placental exchange depicted in the fetal circulation figure, oxygen (O2)-rich blood and CO2 exchange occur across the chorionic villi within the:","options":["Intervillous (IV) space of the decidua (mother)","Fetal lungs","Umbilical cord jelly","Amniotic sac"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels O2-rich blood, the decidua (mother), the IV (intervillous) space, and chorionic villi as the site where O2 is delivered and CO2 removed.  Why B wrong: The fetal lungs are not the site of oxygenation in utero.  Why C, D wrong: The cord jelly (Wharton's jelly) and amniotic sac are not gas-exchange sites."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the obstetric formula G(x)P(m+n), what does the symbol \"G\" (Gravida) represent?","options":["Number of times a female has conceived","Number of pregnancies that went beyond the period of viability","Number of abortions","Number of living children"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines Gₓ → \"No. of times a female has conceived.\"  Why B wrong: That defines the Para (Pₘ) component.  Why C wrong: That defines the abortion (n) component.  Why D wrong: Living children is not part of this definition on the page."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the notation G(x)P(m+n), the \"P\" (Para) component refers to:","options":["Number of abortions","Number of pregnancies which have gone beyond the period of viability","Total number of conceptions","Number of ectopic pregnancies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Pₘ → \"No. of pregnancy which has gone beyond period of viability.\"  Why A wrong: Abortions are denoted by \"n.\"  Why C wrong: Total conceptions is Gravida (G).  Why D wrong: Ectopic pregnancies are not defined by this symbol here."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the obstetric notation, the symbol \"n\" denotes the number of:","options":["Normal deliveries","Neonatal deaths","Abortions","Nulliparous events"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines n → \"No. of abortion.\"  Why A wrong: Normal deliveries are not what \"n\" stands for here.  Why B wrong: Neonatal deaths are not defined by this symbol.  Why D wrong: \"n\" specifically means number of abortions."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"A woman is recorded as G₃P₁₊₂. How many times has she conceived?","options":["1","2","3","6"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: G₃ means \"No. of times female has conceived = 3.\"  Why A wrong: 1 is the number of pregnancies beyond viability.  Why B wrong: 2 is the number of abortions.  Why D wrong: The numbers are not summed to give conceptions."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the example G₃P₁₊₂, the \"P₁₊₂\" component indicates the woman has:","options":["1 pregnancy beyond period of viability and 2 abortions","2 pregnancies beyond viability and 1 abortion","1 living child and 2 stillbirths","3 term deliveries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states P₁₊₂ = \"1 Pregnancy has gone beyond period of viability, have 2 abortions.\"  Why B wrong: The numbers are reversed.  Why C wrong: The notation counts viable pregnancies and abortions, not living children/stillbirths.  Why D wrong: It does not indicate 3 term deliveries."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The entire duration of pregnancy is divided into how many trimesters?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states pregnancy is \"Divided into 3 Trimesters.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The page specifies exactly three trimesters."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"According to the notes, the first trimester spans from the:","options":["14th week to 27 weeks + 6 days","1st day of LMP to 13 weeks + 6 days","4th week to 12 weeks","Day of conception to 10 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states 1st TRIMESTER: 1st day of LMP → 13 weeks + 6 days.  Why A wrong: That is the second trimester range.  Why C wrong: These weeks are not the stated boundaries.  Why D wrong: The first trimester is measured from the 1st day of LMP, not conception, ending at 13 weeks + 6 days."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The second trimester of pregnancy is defined as which gestational range?","options":["1st day of LMP to 13 weeks + 6 days","14 weeks to 27 weeks + 6 days","≥28 weeks to 40 weeks","20 weeks to 36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states 2nd TRIMESTER: 14 weeks → 27 weeks + 6 days.  Why A wrong: That is the first trimester.  Why C wrong: That is the third trimester.  Why D wrong: These are not the stated boundaries."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The third trimester of pregnancy extends from:","options":["24 weeks to 38 weeks","14 weeks to 27 weeks + 6 days","≥28 weeks to 40 weeks","30 weeks to 42 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states 3rd TRIMESTER: ≥28 weeks → 40 weeks.  Why A wrong: These are not the stated limits.  Why B wrong: That is the second trimester.  Why D wrong: The stated upper limit is 40 weeks, and it begins at ≥28 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the mnemonic \"U Are My Best Friend\" for first-trimester symptoms, \"U\" stands for:","options":["Uterine cramps","Increased urinary frequency","Umbilical pain","Uterine bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: U = ↑ Urinary frequency, seen in the 1st & 3rd trimester.  Why A/C/D wrong: These symptoms are not part of this mnemonic point."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Increased urinary frequency as a pregnancy symptom is typically seen in which trimesters?","options":["Only the first trimester","Only the second trimester","First and third trimesters","Second and third trimesters"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states urinary frequency is \"seen in pregnant female in 1st & 3rd Trimester.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The notes specifically pair the first and third trimesters."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the first-trimester symptom mnemonic, \"Are\" corresponds to which symptom?","options":["Amenorrhea","Anemia","Anorexia","Ankle edema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Are = Amenorrhea.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: None of these are the mnemonic term."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Morning sickness (nausea and vomiting) in early pregnancy is attributed to which hormone?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin)","Oxytocin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states morning sickness is \"Nausea & vomiting pregnant female due to HCG.\"  Why A wrong: Progesterone is not named as the cause here.  Why B wrong: Estrogen is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin relates to labor/lactation, not morning sickness."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the mnemonic \"U Are My Best Friend,\" \"Best\" refers to which first-trimester symptom?","options":["Backache","Breast discomfort","Bleeding gums","Bloating"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Best = Breast discomfort.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic term used."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the first-trimester symptom mnemonic, \"Friend\" stands for:","options":["Fainting","Flatulence","Fatigue","Fever"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Friend = Fatigue.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not listed in the mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Excessive vomiting during pregnancy leading to weight loss in the female is termed:","options":["Hyperemesis gravidarum","Morning sickness","Ptyalism","Pica"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states excessive vomiting with weight loss → Hyperemesis gravidarum.  Why B wrong: Morning sickness is the milder, self-limiting nausea/vomiting, not with significant weight loss.  Why C wrong: Ptyalism is excessive salivation.  Why D wrong: Pica is craving for non-food items."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"According to the flow chart, hyperemesis gravidarum is linked to which underlying hormonal state?","options":["Low progesterone","Excessive hCG","Low estrogen","Excessive prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows Hyperemesis gravidarum ↓ Excessive hCG.  Why A/C/D wrong: These hormonal states are not implicated in the diagram."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the mnemonic \"High Court Demands Riya's Testimonial\" for causes of excessive hCG, \"High\" stands for which condition?","options":["Hyperthyroidism","Hydatidiform mole (molar pregnancy)","HELLP syndrome","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states High – H. mole (molar pregnancy).  Why A/C/D wrong: These conditions are not listed in the excessive-hCG mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the excessive-hCG mnemonic, \"Court\" corresponds to which malignancy?","options":["Cervical carcinoma","Choriocarcinoma","Colon carcinoma","Cholangiocarcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Court – Choriocarcinoma.  Why A/C/D wrong: These cancers are not part of the mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the excessive-hCG mnemonic, \"Demands\" refers to which chromosomal disorder?","options":["Turner syndrome","Down syndrome (trisomy 21)","Edward syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Demands – Down syndrome (trisomy 21).  Why A/C/D wrong: These syndromes are not listed as causes of excessive hCG here."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the mnemonic for causes of excessive hCG, \"Riya's\" points to:","options":["Rh incompatibility","Rubella infection","Renal failure","Rheumatic heart disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Riya's – Rh incompatibility.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not part of the mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the excessive-hCG mnemonic \"High Court Demands Riya's Testimonial,\" \"Testimonial\" corresponds to:","options":["Twin pregnancy","Term pregnancy","Tubal pregnancy","Threatened abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Testimonial – Twin pregnancy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic term."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Which of the following are causes of excessive hCG in pregnancy according to the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hydatidiform mole","Choriocarcinoma","Down syndrome (trisomy 21)","Placenta previa","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic lists H. mole, choriocarcinoma, Down syndrome, Rh incompatibility, and twin pregnancy as causes of excessive hCG.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is not listed among the causes of excessive hCG."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"What is the earliest sign of pregnancy in the first trimester according to the notes?","options":["Hegar sign","Goodell sign","Chadwick sign","Piskacek sign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Earliest sign: Goodell sign.\"  Why A wrong: Hegar sign becomes positive at 6–10 weeks but is not labelled the earliest sign.  Why C wrong: Chadwick sign is bluish discoloration, not the earliest sign.  Why D wrong: Piskacek sign becomes positive at 8 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Goodell sign refers to which change during pregnancy?","options":["Bluish discoloration of the cervix","Softening of the cervix","Pulsation in the lateral fornix","Unequal growth of the uterus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Goodell sign → \"softening of cervix.\"  Why A wrong: That describes Chadwick/Jacquemier sign.  Why C wrong: That describes Osiander sign.  Why D wrong: That describes Piskacek sign."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"At how many weeks of pregnancy does the Goodell sign appear?","options":["4 weeks","6 weeks","8 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Goodell sign softening of cervix occurs \"at 6 weeks of pregnancy.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In assessing cervical softening (Goodell sign), the consistency of the cervix in a NON-pregnant female is compared to the:","options":["Lips","Tip of the nose","Earlobe","Forehead"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Non pregnant female = Tip of nose.\"  Why A wrong: The lips represent the pregnant female's softer cervix.  Why C/D wrong: These comparisons are not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"During pregnancy, the softened cervix (Goodell sign) is compared in consistency to the:","options":["Tip of the nose","Lips","Cartilage of the ear","Bridge of the nose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Pregnant female = Lips.\"  Why A wrong: The tip of the nose represents the firm non-pregnant cervix.  Why C/D wrong: These are not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"At how many weeks of pregnancy does the Hegar sign become positive?","options":["2–4 weeks","6–10 weeks","12–16 weeks","20–24 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Hegar sign \"Become positive at 6–10 weeks of pregnancy.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated window."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The Hegar sign is based on the fact that in early pregnancy which part of the uterus is empty and soft?","options":["Fundus","Cornu","Lower part of uterus (isthmus)","Cervical canal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states it is based on the fact that \"The lower part of uterus (Isthmus) is empty and soft in early pregnancy.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The notes specifically identify the isthmus (lower uterine segment), not these structures."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"How is the Hegar sign elicited?","options":["Auscultation of the fetal heart with a Doppler","Bimanual palpation with one hand inside the vagina and the other behind the uterus, so fingers of both hands seem to touch","Inspection of the cervix under speculum","Abdominal palpation of fetal parts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes bimanual palpation with one hand inside the vagina and the other behind the uterus, where the fingers of both hands seem to be touching each other because the isthmus is soft/empty.  Why A/C/D wrong: These techniques do not describe the Hegar sign."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The Piskacek sign, which becomes positive at 8 weeks of pregnancy, refers to:","options":["Softening of the cervix","Unequal growth of the uterus in early pregnancy due to lateral implantation","Pulsation felt in the lateral fornix","Bluish discoloration of the vagina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states PISKACEK sign is \"Unequal growth of uterus in early pregnancy... due to lateral implantations.\"  Why A wrong: That is the Goodell sign.  Why C wrong: That is the Osiander sign.  Why D wrong: That is the Chadwick/Jacquemier sign."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The Piskacek sign (unequal uterine growth) is due to:","options":["Fundal implantation","Lateral implantation","Low implantation near the cervix","Cornual implantation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states it \"is due to lateral implantations.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specify lateral implantation as the cause."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The Osiander sign of pregnancy is described as:","options":["Pulsation felt in the lateral fornix of the vagina","Irregular uterine contraction in the first trimester","Softening of the cervix","Unequal uterine growth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states OSIANDER sign: \"Pulsation felt in lateral fornix of vagina.\"  Why B wrong: That is the Palmer sign.  Why C wrong: That is the Goodell sign.  Why D wrong: That is the Piskacek sign."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The Palmer sign of pregnancy refers to:","options":["Bluish discoloration of the cervix","Irregular contraction of the uterus in the first trimester","Pulsation in the lateral fornix","Softening of the isthmus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states PALMAR signs: \"Irregular contraction of uterus in 1st trimester.\"  Why A wrong: That is the Chadwick/Jacquemier sign.  Why C wrong: That is the Osiander sign.  Why D wrong: That relates to the Hegar sign basis."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The Chadwick (Jacquemier) sign of pregnancy is characterized by:","options":["Softening of the cervix","Bluish discoloration of the vagina or cervix","Unequal uterine growth","Pulsation in the lateral fornix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states CHADWICK/JACQUIEMIER sign: \"Bluish discoloration of vagina or cervix during pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: That is the Goodell sign.  Why C wrong: That is the Piskacek sign.  Why D wrong: That is the Osiander sign."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Which of the following first-trimester signs become positive at 8 weeks of pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Piskacek sign","Osiander sign","Palmer sign","Goodell sign","Chadwick/Jacquemier sign"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page groups Piskacek, Osiander, Palmer, and Chadwick/Jacquemier signs under \"Become positive at 8 week of pregnancy.\"  Why D wrong: Goodell sign is the earliest sign, appearing at 6 weeks, not grouped with the 8-week signs."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"At 6 weeks of gestation, the size of the uterus is comparable to a:","options":["Hen's egg","Cricket ball","Fetal head","Grapefruit"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"At 6 weeks: Size of uterus: Hens egg.\"  Why B wrong: Cricket ball corresponds to 8 weeks.  Why C wrong: Fetal head size corresponds to 12 weeks.  Why D wrong: Grapefruit is not the stated comparison."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"At 8 weeks of gestation, the uterus is approximately the size of a:","options":["Hen's egg","Cricket ball","Fetal head","Orange"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"At 8 weeks: Size of uterus: Cricket Ball.\"  Why A wrong: Hen's egg is 6 weeks.  Why C wrong: Fetal head is 12 weeks.  Why D wrong: Orange is not the stated comparison."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"At 12 weeks of gestation, the size of the uterus is comparable to a:","options":["Hen's egg","Cricket ball","Fetal head size","Tennis ball"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"At 12 weeks: Size of uterus: Fetal heads size.\"  Why A wrong: Hen's egg is 6 weeks.  Why B wrong: Cricket ball is 8 weeks.  Why D wrong: Tennis ball is not the stated comparison."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the first trimester, the fetal heart sound can be heard using which instrument?","options":["Fetoscope","Ordinary stethoscope","Doppler","Pinard's stethoscope"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed note states \"In first trimester fetal heart sound can be heard by: Doppler.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stethoscope (and Pinard/fetoscope) can hear the fetal heart later (18–20 weeks), not in the first trimester."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"By Doppler, the fetal heart sound can first be heard at what gestational age?","options":["6 weeks","8 weeks","10 weeks","14 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed note states fetal heart sound can be heard by Doppler \"at 10 weeks of pregnancy.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the early first term, before enlargement, the uterus is described as which type of organ?","options":["Abdominal organ","Intrapelvic organ","Retroperitoneal organ","Thoracic organ"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"In First term = uterus enlarge was intrapelvic organ.\"  Why A wrong: The uterus becomes an abdominal organ later (second term).  Why C/D wrong: These descriptions are not used in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"At 12 weeks, the height of the uterine fundus reaches the level of the:","options":["Umbilicus","Pubic symphysis","Xiphisternum","Midway between umbilicus and pubic symphysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"At 12 weeks = height of uterus is at the level of Pubic Symphysis.\"  Why A wrong: The umbilicus level is reached around 20–22 weeks.  Why C wrong: The xiphisternum is reached at 36 weeks.  Why D wrong: Midway is the 16-week landmark."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the second term of pregnancy, the uterus becomes a/an:","options":["Intrapelvic organ","Abdominal organ","Retroperitoneal organ","Pelvic-only organ"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"In Second term: Uterus becomes abdominal organ.\"  Why A wrong: The uterus is intrapelvic only in the first term.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated descriptions."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"\"Quickening\" in pregnancy is defined as:","options":["The onset of true labour contractions","The perception of fetal movement by the mother for the first time","The engagement of the fetal head into the pelvis","The first fetal heart sound heard on Doppler"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines quickening as \"Perception of fetal movement by the mother for first - time in pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: That describes labour onset.  Why C wrong: That describes lightening/engagement.  Why D wrong: That describes Doppler auscultation, not quickening."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In a primigravida, quickening is typically first perceived at approximately:","options":["12 weeks","16 weeks (16–18)","18 weeks (18–20)","24 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states PRIMIGRAVIDA: At 18 week (18–20).  Why A/D wrong: These are not the stated windows.  Why B wrong: 16 weeks (16–18) is the multigravida timing."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In a multigravida, quickening is typically perceived at about:","options":["18 weeks (18–20)","16 weeks (16–18)","20 weeks","14 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states MULTI GRAVIDA: At 16 weeks (16–18).  Why A wrong: 18 weeks (18–20) is the primigravida timing.  Why C/D wrong: These are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Which sign appearing in the second trimester is characterized as sporadic, non-rhythmic, painless contractions?","options":["True labour contractions","Braxton Hicks contractions","Quickening","Lightening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists Braxton Hicks contractions with 4 characteristics: sporadic/infrequent, non-rhythmic, painless, and don't lead to dilation of cervix.  Why A wrong: True labour contractions are rhythmic, painful, and cause cervical dilation.  Why C wrong: Quickening is fetal movement perception.  Why D wrong: Lightening is descent of the fetal head."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Which of the following are the 4 characteristics of Braxton Hicks contractions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Sporadic/infrequent","Non-rhythmic","Painless","Cause progressive cervical dilation","Do not lead to dilation of the cervix"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists them as sporadic/infrequent, non-rhythmic, painless, and not leading to cervical dilation.  Why D wrong: The notes state Braxton Hicks contractions do NOT lead to dilation of the cervix, which is the opposite of option D."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"During a Braxton Hicks contraction, the intrauterine pressure is usually:","options":["<8 mm Hg (range 5–25 mm Hg)","30–50 mm Hg","60–80 mm Hg",">100 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states intrauterine pressure at the time of Braxton Hicks contraction is \"Usually <8 mm of Hg (5–25 mm Hg).\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These pressures are not the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"After how many weeks of pregnancy can Braxton Hicks contractions be felt?","options":["8 weeks","12 weeks","16 weeks","24 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Braxton Hicks contractions \"can be felt after: 16 weeks of Pregnancy.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Braxton Hicks contractions are NOT felt in which condition?","options":["Twin pregnancy","Abdominal (ectopic) pregnancy","Postdated pregnancy","Molar pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Braxton Hicks are \"Not felt in: Abdominal pregnancy (Ectopic Pregnancy).\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These conditions are not mentioned as excluding Braxton Hicks contractions."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Later in pregnancy, Braxton Hicks contractions are converted into:","options":["True labour pains","False labour pains","Cervical effacement","Placental separation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Later on it becomes: Converted into false labor Pains.\"  Why A wrong: They convert to false, not true, labour pains.  Why C/D wrong: These are not what Braxton Hicks convert into."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"At what gestational age can the fetal heart sound be heard using an ordinary stethoscope?","options":["10 weeks","16–18 weeks","18–20 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"Fetal Heart Sound can be heard by Stethoscope: 18–20 weeks.\"  Why A wrong: 10 weeks is for Doppler.  Why B wrong: 16–18 weeks is when fetal movements are felt per abdomen.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks is when fetal parts are felt per abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"According to the notes, fetal movements can be felt per abdominally at:","options":["10–12 weeks","16–18 weeks","18–20 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"Fetal movements can be felt per abdominally: 16–18 weeks.\"  Why A wrong: Not stated.  Why C wrong: 18–20 weeks is fetal heart sound by stethoscope.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks is fetal parts felt per abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Fetal parts can be felt per abdominally at approximately:","options":["12 weeks","16–18 weeks","18–20 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"Fetal parts can be feel Per abdominally: 20 weeks.\"  Why A wrong: Not stated.  Why B wrong: 16–18 weeks is fetal movements felt per abdomen.  Why C wrong: 18–20 weeks is fetal heart sound by stethoscope."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Which third-trimester symptom is described as urinary frequency returning?","options":["Lightening","Return of urination frequency","Quickening","Engagement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists \"Urination frequency returns\" as a third-trimester symptom.  Why A/D wrong: Lightening/engagement is descent of the head, a separate point.  Why C wrong: Quickening is a second-trimester event."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The \"Lightening\" or \"Welcome sign\" is seen after how many weeks of pregnancy?","options":["28 weeks","32 weeks","36 weeks","40 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Lightening/Welcome sign: Seen after 36 weeks of pregnancy.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"At 36 weeks of gestation, the uterine fundus reaches the level of the:","options":["Umbilicus","Pubic symphysis","Xiphisternum","Midway between umbilicus and pubic symphysis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The fundal-height diagram shows 36 weeks corresponds to the xiphisternum.  Why A wrong: The umbilicus corresponds to ~20–24 weeks.  Why B wrong: The pubic symphysis corresponds to 12 weeks.  Why D wrong: Midway corresponds to 16 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"According to the fundal-height diagram, at 32 weeks the fundus is located at the:","options":["2/3rd distance between umbilicus and xiphisternum","1/3rd distance between umbilicus and xiphisternum","Upper border of umbilicus","Xiphisternum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram maps 32 weeks to \"2/3rd distance between umbilicus & Xiphisternum.\"  Why B wrong: 1/3rd distance corresponds to 28 weeks.  Why C wrong: Upper border of umbilicus corresponds to 24 weeks.  Why D wrong: Xiphisternum corresponds to 36 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the fundal-height diagram, 28 weeks corresponds to the fundus being at:","options":["2/3rd distance between umbilicus and xiphisternum","1/3rd distance between umbilicus and xiphisternum","Lower border of umbilicus","Pubic symphysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram maps 28 weeks to \"1/3rd distance between umbilicus & Xiphisternum.\"  Why A wrong: 2/3rd distance is 32 weeks.  Why C wrong: Lower border of umbilicus is 20 weeks.  Why D wrong: Pubic symphysis is 12 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"At 24 weeks of gestation, the fundal height reaches the:","options":["Upper border of the umbilicus","Lower border of the umbilicus","Xiphisternum","Midway between umbilicus and pubic symphysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram maps 24 weeks to the \"Upper border of umbilicus.\"  Why B wrong: Lower border of umbilicus corresponds to 20 weeks.  Why C wrong: Xiphisternum is 36 weeks.  Why D wrong: Midway is 16 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"According to the diagram, the fundus reaches the lower border of the umbilicus at:","options":["16 weeks","20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram maps 20 weeks to the \"Lower border of umbilicus.\"  Why A wrong: 16 weeks is midway between umbilicus and pubic symphysis.  Why C wrong: 24 weeks is upper border of umbilicus.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is 1/3rd distance toward the xiphisternum."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"At 16 weeks of gestation, the uterine fundus is located:","options":["At the umbilicus","Midway between the umbilicus and pubic symphysis","At the xiphisternum","At the pubic symphysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram maps 16 weeks to \"Midway between umbilicus & Pubic Symphysis.\"  Why A wrong: The umbilicus level is 20–24 weeks.  Why C wrong: Xiphisternum is 36 weeks.  Why D wrong: The pubic symphysis is the 12-week level."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"According to the lightening flow chart, at 36 weeks of pregnancy the mother experiences:","options":["Relief from respiratory discomfort","A lot of respiratory discomfort","Onset of true labour","Decreased fetal movements"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"At 36 weeks of Pregnancy → Mother will have lot of Respiratory discomfort.\"  Why A wrong: Relief comes later, at 40 weeks after the head descends.  Why C/D wrong: These are not part of this flow chart."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"In the lightening process, after 36 weeks the head of the fetus:","options":["Rises out of the pelvis","Goes down into the pelvis","Rotates to a transverse position","Becomes non-engaged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"After 36 weeks head of fetus goes down into pelvis.\"  Why A/D wrong: The head descends and engages, it does not rise or become non-engaged.  Why C wrong: This is not described in the flow chart."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"According to the flow chart, the height of the uterus decreases at 40 weeks and becomes the same as that at:","options":["28 weeks","32 weeks","36 weeks","24 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"Height of Uterus decreases at 40 weeks & become same as 32 weeks.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The comparison stated is with 32 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The relief from respiratory discomfort that occurs when the fetal head descends into the pelvis near term is called:","options":["Quickening","Engagement","Lightening / Welcome sign","Effacement"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart concludes \"Mother will be relieved from Respiratory discomfort → Called as Lightening / Welcome sign.\"  Why A wrong: Quickening is first fetal movement perception.  Why B wrong: Engagement is a related but distinct descriptor; the notes name the relief \"lightening/welcome sign.\"  Why D wrong: Effacement is cervical thinning."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The clinical maneuver shown in the figure where a tape measure is stretched over the pregnant abdomen represents measurement of the:","options":["Abdominal girth","Symphysis-fundal height","Biparietal diameter","Diagonal conjugate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure caption reads \"Symphysis fundal height.\"  Why A wrong: Abdominal girth is a different measurement around the widest part of the abdomen.  Why C wrong: Biparietal diameter is a fetal skull measurement by ultrasound.  Why D wrong: Diagonal conjugate is a pelvic measurement."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"Symphysis-fundal height is measured from the fundus of the uterus to which landmark?","options":["The umbilicus","The xiphisternum","The pubic symphysis","The anterior superior iliac spine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The \"How to measure?\" diagram shows measurement from the fundus of the uterus to the pubic symphysis.  Why A/B/D wrong: These landmarks are not the stated reference point for symphysis-fundal height."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"What is the prerequisite before measuring the symphysis-fundal height?","options":["To fill the bladder","To empty the bladder","To place the woman in lithotomy position","To give an enema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Prerequisite: To empty the bladder.\"  Why A wrong: A full bladder would distort the measurement; the bladder must be emptied.  Why C/D wrong: These are not stated prerequisites."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"After 24 weeks, the symphysis-fundal height in centimeters:","options":["Is always 5 cm more than the gestational age","Corresponds to the gestational age with slight variation","Is unrelated to gestational age","Equals half the gestational age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"After 24 weeks: Symphysis Fundal Height in centimeters (cm) Corresponds to gestational age with slight Variation.\"  Why A/D wrong: No such fixed offset or halving is stated.  Why C wrong: The notes explicitly correlate SFH with gestational age."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"According to the notes, the shape of the uterus at 12 weeks is:","options":["Globular","Ovoid","Spherical","Pyriform"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"12 weeks = globular.\"  Why B wrong: Ovoid is the 28-week shape.  Why C wrong: Spherical is listed for the later/term stage (printed \"12 weeks = spherical,\" likely a typo for term).  Why D wrong: Pyriform (pear-shaped) is the non-pregnant shape, not listed here."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"According to the notes, the shape of the uterus at 28 weeks is:","options":["Globular","Ovoid","Spherical","Cylindrical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"28 weeks = ovoid.\"  Why A wrong: Globular is the 12-week shape.  Why C wrong: Spherical is listed for the later/term stage.  Why D wrong: Cylindrical is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 025 · Gravida/Para notation, three trimesters and their week ranges…","question":"The notes describe the uterus taking on a spherical shape at the later stage of pregnancy. (Note: the page prints \"12 weeks = spherical,\" which is a typographical error since 12 weeks is already assigned \"globular\"; spherical refers to the term/near-term uterus.) The correct shape sequence with advancing gestation is:","options":["Spherical → ovoid → globular","Globular (12 weeks) → ovoid (28 weeks) → spherical (term)","Ovoid → globular → spherical","Globular → spherical → ovoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list globular at 12 weeks and ovoid at 28 weeks, with spherical as the third (term) shape; the repeated \"12 weeks\" label for spherical is a printing error.  Why A/C/D wrong: These orderings do not match the 12-week globular and 28-week ovoid facts stated on the page."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Signs of pregnancy are classified into how many main categories based on who perceives them and their diagnostic certainty?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page classifies signs of pregnancy into three categories — Presumptive, Probable, and Definitive.  Why A wrong: Two is fewer than the three shown.  Why C wrong: Four is more than the three categories listed.  Why D wrong: Five is not correct; only three categories exist."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"A sign of pregnancy that is \"seen and felt by the patient\" is classified as which type of sign?","options":["Probable sign","Definitive sign","Presumptive sign","Positive sign"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Signs seen and felt by the patient herself are termed presumptive signs.  Why A wrong: Probable signs are felt by the examiner but are not 100% diagnostic.  Why B wrong: Definitive signs are felt by the examiner and are 100% diagnostic.  Why D wrong: \"Positive sign\" is a synonym for definitive, which is examiner-detected, not patient-perceived."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"A probable sign of pregnancy is best defined as one that is:","options":["Seen and felt by the patient only","Felt by the examiner but not 100% diagnostic","Felt by the examiner and 100% diagnostic","Only visible on ultrasound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines probable signs as those felt by the examiner but not 100% diagnostic of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: That describes presumptive signs.  Why C wrong: That describes definitive signs.  Why D wrong: USG evidence is a definitive sign, not a probable one."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"A definitive (positive) sign of pregnancy is one that is:","options":["Perceived only by the pregnant woman","Felt by the examiner but not diagnostic","Felt by the examiner and 100% diagnostic that the patient is pregnant","Only a subjective symptom"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Definitive signs are felt by the examiner and are 100% diagnostic that the patient is pregnant.  Why A wrong: Patient-perceived signs are presumptive.  Why B wrong: Examiner-felt but non-diagnostic signs are probable.  Why D wrong: Definitive signs are objective, not subjective symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which of the following is a presumptive sign of pregnancy?","options":["Hegar sign","Amenorrhea","Fetal cardiac activity on USG","Braxton Hicks contraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amenorrhea is listed among presumptive signs (seen and felt by the patient).  Why A wrong: Hegar sign is a probable sign.  Why C wrong: Fetal cardiac activity on USG is a definitive sign.  Why D wrong: Braxton Hicks contraction is a probable sign."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which skin change is listed as a presumptive sign of pregnancy?","options":["Chloasma","Goodell sign","Piskacek sign","Palmar sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chloasma (mask of pregnancy) is listed as a presumptive sign.  Why B wrong: Goodell sign is a probable sign.  Why C wrong: Piskacek sign is a probable sign.  Why D wrong: Palmar sign is a probable sign."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"\"Linea Nigra\" and \"Striae gravidarum\" belong to which category of pregnancy signs?","options":["Probable signs","Definitive signs","Presumptive signs","Radiological signs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Both linea nigra and striae gravidarum are listed under presumptive signs.  Why A wrong: Probable signs are examiner-detected pelvic changes.  Why B wrong: Definitive signs involve USG/fetal heart/fetal parts/X-ray skeleton.  Why D wrong: These are skin changes, not radiological signs."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which of the following presumptive signs relate specifically to the breasts? (Select all that apply)","options":["Breast discomfort","Breast enlargement","Fatigue","Amenorrhea","Chloasma"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Breast discomfort and breast enlargement are the two breast-related presumptive signs listed.  Why C wrong: Fatigue is a presumptive sign but not breast-related.  Why D wrong: Amenorrhea is a presumptive sign but not breast-related.  Why E wrong: Chloasma is a skin (facial pigmentation) change, not breast-related."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which of the following is a probable sign of pregnancy?","options":["Fatigue","Osiander sign","Fetal movement felt per abdomen","Fetal pole on USG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Osiander sign is listed among the probable signs.  Why A wrong: Fatigue is a presumptive sign.  Why C wrong: Fetal movement felt per abdomen is a definitive sign.  Why D wrong: Fetal pole on USG is a definitive sign."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which sign, involving softening of the cervix, is listed among the probable signs of pregnancy?","options":["Goodell sign","Linea nigra","Chloasma","Doppler heart sound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Goodell sign is listed among probable signs on the page.  Why B wrong: Linea nigra is a presumptive sign.  Why C wrong: Chloasma is a presumptive sign.  Why D wrong: Doppler-detected fetal heart sound is a definitive sign."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a probable sign of pregnancy?","options":["Hegar sign","Gestational sac on USG","Striae gravidarum","Fetal skeleton on X-ray"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hegar sign (softening of the lower uterine segment/isthmus) is a listed probable sign.  Why B wrong: Gestational sac on USG is a definitive sign.  Why C wrong: Striae gravidarum is a presumptive sign.  Why D wrong: Fetal skeleton on X-ray is a definitive sign."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Braxton Hicks contractions are classified under which category of pregnancy signs?","options":["Presumptive","Probable","Definitive","Pathological"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Braxton Hicks contraction is listed among the probable signs.  Why A wrong: Presumptive signs are patient-perceived symptoms/skin changes.  Why C wrong: Definitive signs prove pregnancy (USG, FHS, fetal parts, X-ray).  Why D wrong: They are a normal (physiological) probable finding, not pathological."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"All of the following are listed as probable signs of pregnancy EXCEPT:","options":["Piskacek sign","Palmar sign","Braxton Hicks contraction","Amenorrhea"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Amenorrhea is a presumptive sign, not a probable sign.  Why A wrong: Piskacek sign is a listed probable sign.  Why B wrong: Palmar sign is a listed probable sign.  Why C wrong: Braxton Hicks contraction is a listed probable sign."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which of the following is a definitive (positive) sign of pregnancy?","options":["Amenorrhea","Hegar sign","Fetal cardiac activity on ultrasound","Fatigue"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal cardiac activity on USG is listed as evidence of pregnancy under definitive signs.  Why A wrong: Amenorrhea is a presumptive sign.  Why B wrong: Hegar sign is a probable sign.  Why D wrong: Fatigue is a presumptive sign."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Under definitive signs, evidence of pregnancy on ultrasound includes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Cardiac activity","Gestational sac","Fetal pole","Linea nigra"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Linea nigra is a presumptive skin sign, not a USG finding.  Why A wrong: Cardiac activity is a listed USG evidence of pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Gestational sac is a listed USG evidence of pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Fetal pole is a listed USG evidence of pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"According to the page, which instruments are used to hear fetal heart sounds as a definitive sign of pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Doppler","Stethoscope","Sphygmomanometer","Otoscope","Ophthalmoscope"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal heart sounds heard by Doppler and by stethoscope are listed as definitive signs.  Why C wrong: A sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure, not fetal heart sounds.  Why D wrong: An otoscope examines the ear.  Why E wrong: An ophthalmoscope examines the eye."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"On X-ray, at how many weeks of pregnancy can the fetal skeleton be seen (a definitive sign)?","options":["8 weeks","12 weeks","16 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the fetal skeleton can be seen on X-ray at 16 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 8 weeks is too early for skeletal visualization on X-ray.  Why B wrong: 12 weeks is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Regarding X-ray use in pregnancy for detecting the fetal skeleton, the page notes that X-ray is:","options":["The first-line investigation in pregnancy","Contraindicated in pregnancy","Recommended in the first trimester","Safer than ultrasound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page explicitly states \"(x-ray is contraindicated in Pregnancy).\"  Why A wrong: It is contraindicated, not first-line.  Why C wrong: It is contraindicated, especially not recommended in early pregnancy.  Why D wrong: USG is preferred; X-ray is contraindicated due to radiation."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a definitive sign of pregnancy when detected by the examiner?","options":["Fetal parts felt per abdomen / fetal movement","Breast discomfort","Goodell sign","Chloasma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal parts felt per abdomen and fetal movement are listed under definitive signs.  Why B wrong: Breast discomfort is a presumptive sign.  Why C wrong: Goodell sign is a probable sign.  Why D wrong: Chloasma is a presumptive sign."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"A woman firmly believes she is pregnant and even develops symptoms, but she is actually not pregnant. This condition is called:","options":["Ectopic pregnancy","Pseudocyesis (false pregnancy)","Missed abortion","Molar pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pseudocyesis / false pregnancy is when a female assumes she is pregnant but actually she is not.  Why A wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is a real pregnancy implanted outside the uterus.  Why C wrong: Missed abortion is a real (non-viable) intrauterine pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Molar pregnancy is a real abnormal trophoblastic pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which finding definitively distinguishes pseudocyesis (false pregnancy) from a true pregnancy?","options":["Presence of amenorrhea","Presence of breast enlargement","None of the definitive signs of pregnancy are seen","Presence of fatigue"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In pseudocyesis, none of the definitive signs of pregnancy are seen, which distinguishes it from true pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Amenorrhea (a presumptive sign) may be present in pseudocyesis.  Why B wrong: Breast enlargement (presumptive) may occur in pseudocyesis.  Why D wrong: Fatigue (presumptive) can be present and does not exclude false pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"The WHO principle underlying the objectives of antenatal care is best captured by which slogan?","options":["\"Health for All\"","\"Safe Motherhood Only\"","\"Family Planning First\"","\"Immunization for All\""],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states WHO says \"Health for All,\" leading to health during pregnancy and essential obstetric care.  Why B wrong: \"Safe Motherhood Only\" is not the slogan quoted.  Why C wrong: \"Family Planning First\" is not quoted.  Why D wrong: \"Immunization for All\" is not the quoted slogan."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"The need for essential obstetric care during pregnancy is described on the page as involving:","options":["One-time screening only","Continued risk assessment","Care only in the third trimester","Hospitalization throughout pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states essential obstetric care during pregnancy involves continued risk assessment.  Why A wrong: It is continued (ongoing), not one-time.  Why C wrong: It is throughout pregnancy, not only third trimester.  Why D wrong: Hospitalization throughout pregnancy is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which of the following are objectives of antenatal care listed on the page? (Select all that apply)","options":["Identify high-risk female","Identify any pregnancy-related complication","Preventive care","Educate the female regarding body changes, complications, and her fears/concerns","Terminate all high-risk pregnancies"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four listed objectives are identifying high-risk females, identifying pregnancy-related complications, preventive care, and educating the female (body changes, complications, answering fears/concerns).  Why E wrong: Terminating high-risk pregnancies is not an objective listed on the page."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Under \"educate the female,\" antenatal care aims to inform her about all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Changes in the body","Complications","Answering her fears and concerns","How to avoid all future pregnancies"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists educating about body changes, complications, and answering fears/concerns — not avoiding future pregnancies.  Why A wrong: \"Changes in Body\" is listed.  Why B wrong: \"Complication\" is listed.  Why C wrong: \"Answer her fears & concerns\" is listed."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"According to the ideal antenatal visit schedule, up to 28 weeks of gestation the visits should occur:","options":["Once every week","Once every 2 weeks","Once every 4 weeks","Once every 6 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Ideally, up to 28 weeks = 1 visit every 4 weeks.  Why A wrong: Weekly visits apply to ≥36 weeks.  Why B wrong: Every 2 weeks applies to 28-36 weeks.  Why D wrong: Every 6 weeks is not in the schedule."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the ideal antenatal schedule, between 28 and 36 weeks the pregnant woman should be seen:","options":["Once every 4 weeks","Once every 2 weeks","Once weekly","Once every 3 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ideally, 28-36 weeks = 1 visit every 2 weeks.  Why A wrong: Every 4 weeks applies to up to 28 weeks.  Why C wrong: Weekly applies to ≥36 weeks.  Why D wrong: Every 3 weeks is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the ideal antenatal schedule, from 36 weeks onward (≥36 weeks) the visits should be:","options":["Weekly","Every 2 weeks","Every 4 weeks","Every 10 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ideally, ≥36 weeks = 1 visit weekly.  Why B wrong: Every 2 weeks applies to 28-36 weeks.  Why C wrong: Every 4 weeks applies to up to 28 weeks.  Why D wrong: Every 10 days is not in the schedule."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"According to the ideal schedule, the total number of antenatal visits is:","options":["4","8","12-15 visits","20-25 visits"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives the ideal total as 12-15 visits.  Why A wrong: 4 is the GoI minimum, not the ideal total.  Why B wrong: 8 is the recent WHO minimum contacts, not the ideal total.  Why D wrong: 20-25 visits is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"The page notes that the ideal 12-15 antenatal visits are:","options":["Mandatory for every pregnant woman","Not possible for every pregnant female","Only for high-risk women","Only for first pregnancies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the ideal total is \"Not possible for every pregnant female,\" leading to WHO/GoI minimum schedules.  Why A wrong: It is explicitly stated as not possible for everyone.  Why C wrong: The statement applies generally, not just to high-risk women.  Why D wrong: It is not limited to first pregnancies."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"According to WHO, the EARLIER recommended minimum number of antenatal visits was:","options":["3","4","8","12"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states WHO earlier recommended a minimum of 3 antenatal visits.  Why B wrong: 4 is the GoI minimum.  Why C wrong: 8 is the WHO recent (2016) recommendation.  Why D wrong: 12 belongs to the ideal 12-15 range."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"The RECENT (revised) WHO recommendation for the minimum number of antenatal contacts is:","options":["3","4","8","15"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states WHO recently recommends 8 (contacts).  Why A wrong: 3 was the earlier WHO figure.  Why B wrong: 4 is the GoI minimum.  Why D wrong: 15 is the upper end of the ideal range."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"According to the Government of India (GoI), the minimum number of antenatal visits is:","options":["3","4","8","12"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states GoI minimum number of antenatal visits = 4.  Why A wrong: 3 was the earlier WHO minimum.  Why C wrong: 8 is the recent WHO recommendation.  Why D wrong: 12 is the lower ideal count."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Under the GoI 4-visit schedule, the FIRST antenatal visit (registration visit) should occur at:","options":["8-12 weeks","24-26 weeks","32 weeks","36-38 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the GoI 4-visit schedule, the 1st visit (registration visit) is at 8-12 weeks.  Why B wrong: 24-26 weeks is the 2nd visit.  Why C wrong: 32 weeks is the 3rd visit.  Why D wrong: 36-38 weeks is the 4th visit."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the GoI 4-visit antenatal schedule, the SECOND visit is scheduled at:","options":["8-12 weeks","24-26 weeks","32 weeks","36-38 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2nd visit in the GoI schedule is at 24-26 weeks.  Why A wrong: 8-12 weeks is the 1st (registration) visit.  Why C wrong: 32 weeks is the 3rd visit.  Why D wrong: 36-38 weeks is the 4th visit."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the GoI 4-visit antenatal schedule, the THIRD visit is scheduled at:","options":["20 weeks","28 weeks","32 weeks","38 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 3rd visit in the GoI schedule is at 32 weeks.  Why A wrong: 20 weeks is not a GoI visit time.  Why B wrong: 28 weeks is not a GoI visit time.  Why D wrong: 38 weeks falls in the 4th visit range (36-38 weeks)."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the GoI 4-visit antenatal schedule, the FOURTH visit is scheduled at:","options":["8-12 weeks","24-26 weeks","32 weeks","36-38 weeks"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The 4th visit in the GoI schedule is at 36-38 weeks.  Why A wrong: 8-12 weeks is the 1st visit.  Why B wrong: 24-26 weeks is the 2nd visit.  Why C wrong: 32 weeks is the 3rd visit."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"The 2016 modification of WHO antenatal guidelines recommends a minimum of how many contacts?","options":["4 contacts","6 contacts","8 contacts","10 contacts"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 2016 WHO modification recommends 8 minimum-visit/contacts with any health care person.  Why A wrong: 4 is the GoI minimum.  Why B wrong: 6 is not the stated figure.  Why D wrong: 10 is not the stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Under the WHO 2016 model, these 8 contacts are to be made with:","options":["A specialist obstetrician only","Any health care person","A gynaecologist only","A registered midwife only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the 8 contacts are \"with any Health care person.\"  Why A wrong: It is not restricted to an obstetrician.  Why C wrong: It is not restricted to a gynaecologist.  Why D wrong: It is not restricted to a midwife alone."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the WHO 2016 8-contact distribution, how many contacts fall in the FIRST trimester?","options":["One (the 1st contact)","Two (contacts 1-2)","Three (contacts 1-3)","Four (contacts 1-4)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows the 1st contact = 1st trimester (a single first-trimester contact).  Why B wrong: Contacts 2 and 3 are in the 2nd trimester.  Why C wrong: Only the 1st contact is in the first trimester.  Why D wrong: Four contacts are not in the first trimester."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the WHO 2016 8-contact schedule, contacts number 2 and 3 are grouped into which trimester?","options":["1st trimester","2nd trimester","3rd trimester","Postpartum period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page brackets contacts 2 and 3 into the 2nd trimester.  Why A wrong: Only contact 1 is in the 1st trimester.  Why C wrong: Contacts 4-8 are in the 3rd trimester.  Why D wrong: These are antenatal, not postpartum contacts."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the WHO 2016 8-contact schedule, contacts numbered 4, 5, 6, 7, and 8 are grouped into which trimester?","options":["1st trimester","2nd trimester","3rd trimester","Split between 2nd and 3rd"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page brackets contacts 4 through 8 into the 3rd trimester.  Why A wrong: Only contact 1 is first trimester.  Why B wrong: Only contacts 2-3 are second trimester.  Why D wrong: Contacts 4-8 are all in the 3rd trimester, not split."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"During the procedure at the first antenatal visit, the \"obstetric score\" refers to recording:","options":["Height and weight","Gravida and Para","Blood pressure and pulse","Fundal height"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists the obstetric score as Gravida, Para.  Why A wrong: Height/weight is part of \"Build\" under clinical exam.  Why C wrong: BP and pulse are separate clinical exam items.  Why D wrong: Fundal height is part of the per-abdominal (obstetric) examination."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which calculations are performed at the first antenatal visit?","options":["EDD calculation and period of gestation calculation","Only the blood group","Only the Bishop score","Only the APGAR score"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists EDD calculation and period-of-gestation calculation at the first visit.  Why B wrong: Blood group is not listed as a first-visit calculation here.  Why C wrong: Bishop score is a labour assessment, not listed here.  Why D wrong: APGAR is a newborn score, not a first antenatal visit calculation."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"The detailed history at the first antenatal visit includes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["History of present pregnancy","History of past pregnancy","Menstrual history","Newborn APGAR history"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Newborn APGAR history is not part of the antenatal first-visit history listed.  Why A wrong: History of present pregnancy is listed.  Why B wrong: H/O past pregnancy is listed.  Why C wrong: Menstrual history is listed."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which history component at the first antenatal visit specifically captures smoking and alcohol use?","options":["Family history","Menstrual history","Personal history","Past medical history"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists \"Personal History (smoking/alcohol).\"  Why A wrong: Family history covers hereditary/family conditions.  Why B wrong: Menstrual history covers the menstrual cycle.  Why D wrong: Past medical/surgery/drug history covers illnesses and medications."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which of the following are included in the detailed history taken at the first antenatal visit? (Select all that apply)","options":["History of present pregnancy","Menstrual history","Past medical/surgery/drug history","Family history","Personal history (smoking/alcohol)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five — present pregnancy, menstrual, past medical/surgery/drug, family, and personal (smoking/alcohol) histories, along with H/O past pregnancy — are listed.  Why A-E wrong: None are incorrect; each is explicitly listed as part of the detailed history."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"During the first-visit clinical exam, a maternal height/build of \"4 feet 10 inches or less\" raises the suspicion of:","options":["Gestational diabetes","Cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD)","Pre-eclampsia","Anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page links a build of 4 feet 10 inches or less to chances of cephalopelvic disproportion.  Why A wrong: Gestational diabetes is not the linked risk here.  Why C wrong: Pre-eclampsia is assessed via BP/edema, not by short stature here.  Why D wrong: Anemia relates to pallor, not to short stature."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"During the clinical exam, \"specifically pallor\" is checked to screen for which condition?","options":["Jaundice","Anemia","Cyanosis","Dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assessing pallor screens for anemia, a common antenatal concern.  Why A wrong: Jaundice is assessed by icterus/yellow discoloration, not pallor.  Why C wrong: Cyanosis is bluish discoloration, not pallor.  Why D wrong: Dehydration is assessed by skin turgor/mucous membranes, not pallor primarily."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"According to the page, edema during pregnancy is:","options":["Always pathological","Some edema is normal during pregnancy (mild pitting edema)","Never seen in pregnancy","Always a sign of eclampsia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states some edema is normal during pregnancy, presenting as mild pitting edema.  Why A wrong: Not all edema is pathological; mild edema is normal.  Why C wrong: Edema is commonly seen in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Mild physiological edema is not always eclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Normal (physiological) edema of pregnancy is specifically noted to occur in which location?","options":["Face","Ankles","Abdomen","Hands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states mild pitting edema is specifically in the ankles.  Why A wrong: Facial edema is not the normal site described (it suggests pathology).  Why C wrong: The abdomen is not the described edema site.  Why D wrong: The hands are not the normal edema site described here."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Physiological edema of pregnancy is characterized by the fact that it:","options":["Persists despite rest","Subsides by rest","Extends above the ankle","Requires immediate hospitalization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states physiological edema subsides by rest.  Why A wrong: Edema that persists despite rest is pathological.  Why C wrong: Edema above the ankle is a pathological feature.  Why D wrong: Physiological edema is normal and does not need hospitalization."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which of the following are features of PATHOLOGICAL edema in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Does not subside after rest","Occurs above the ankle also","Subsides completely with rest","Limited strictly to the ankles","Is considered normal"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Pathological edema does not subside after rest and occurs above the ankle also.  Why C wrong: Subsiding with rest is a feature of physiological (normal) edema.  Why D wrong: Being limited to the ankles is a feature of normal edema.  Why E wrong: Pathological edema is abnormal, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"During normal pregnancy, the blood pressure tends to be:","options":["Higher than the non-pregnant state","Normal and on the lower side (BP decreases in pregnancy)","Unchanged throughout","Consistently above 140/90 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states during pregnancy BP is normal and on the lower side, and BP decreases in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: BP tends to decrease, not increase.  Why C wrong: BP does change (decreases) during pregnancy.  Why D wrong: BP >140/90 indicates hypertension, not the normal physiological state."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the second trimester, the fall in blood pressure is more pronounced in which component compared to the other?","options":["More in diastolic compared to systolic","More in systolic compared to diastolic","Equal fall in both","No fall in either"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page notes that in the 2nd trimester the change is more in diastolic in comparison to systolic BP.  Why B wrong: The greater fall is in diastolic, not systolic.  Why C wrong: The fall is not equal; diastolic falls more.  Why D wrong: BP does fall in the second trimester."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"The physiological decrease in blood pressure during pregnancy is primarily due to which hormone?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","Oxytocin","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page attributes the BP decrease to progesterone.  Why A wrong: Estrogen is not credited for this effect on the page.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin causes uterine contraction, not BP fall.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is involved in lactation, not this BP effect."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Progesterone lowers blood pressure in pregnancy through which mechanism?","options":["It acts as a smooth muscle relaxant, decreasing peripheral vascular resistance","It increases cardiac output only","It causes vasoconstriction","It increases blood volume acutely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states progesterone is a smooth muscle relaxant that decreases (↓) peripheral vascular resistance.  Why B wrong: The mechanism described is reduced vascular resistance, not merely increased cardiac output.  Why C wrong: It relaxes smooth muscle (vasodilation), not vasoconstriction.  Why D wrong: The described mechanism is decreased peripheral vascular resistance."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"At any time during pregnancy, a blood pressure above which value is defined as hypertension in the pregnant female?","options":[">120/80 mm Hg",">130/85 mm Hg",">140/90 mm Hg",">160/110 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states any time BP >140/90 mm Hg = hypertension in a pregnant female.  Why A wrong: 120/80 is normal.  Why B wrong: 130/85 is not the threshold given.  Why D wrong: 160/110 indicates severe hypertension, but the defining threshold is >140/90."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"During antenatal examination the thyroid is checked for goitre because, in developing countries, hypothyroidism in pregnancy is commonly due to:","options":["Iodine deficiency","Excess iodine","Autoimmune destruction only","Iron deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hypothyroidism is seen in pregnancy due to iodine deficiency in developing countries.  Why B wrong: Excess iodine is not the stated cause.  Why C wrong: Autoimmune destruction is not the cause cited here.  Why D wrong: Iron deficiency causes anemia, not goitre/hypothyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"During antenatal auscultation, which organs/systems are examined?","options":["Heart and lungs","Kidneys and liver","Spleen and stomach","Bladder and bowel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Auscultate = Heart + Lungs.\"  Why B wrong: Kidneys and liver are not auscultated here.  Why C wrong: Spleen and stomach are not the auscultation targets listed.  Why D wrong: Bladder and bowel are not the auscultation targets listed."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"The main purpose of the per-abdominal examination in obstetrics is to:","options":["Check the fundal height of the uterus","Measure blood pressure","Assess the cervix directly","Auscultate the maternal lungs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states per-abdominal examination is to check the fundal height of the uterus.  Why B wrong: BP is measured separately with a sphygmomanometer.  Why C wrong: The cervix is assessed by per speculum/per vaginal examination.  Why D wrong: Lung auscultation is separate from the per-abdominal exam."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Before performing a per-abdominal examination to assess fundal height, the patient's bladder should be:","options":["Full","Empty","Half-full","Catheterized with clamp"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the bladder should be empty.  Why A wrong: A full bladder can alter fundal height assessment.  Why C wrong: Half-full is not what is advised.  Why D wrong: A clamped catheter/full bladder is not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"For per-abdominal examination of fundal height, the patient is positioned:","options":["Standing upright","Lying in the dorsal position with hip and knee flexed","Left lateral with legs extended","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the patient lies in the dorsal position with hip and knee flexed.  Why A wrong: Standing is not the described position.  Why C wrong: Left lateral is not described for this step.  Why D wrong: The prone position is not used."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which border of the examiner's hand is used to assess the fundal height during per-abdominal examination?","options":["Radial border","Ulnar border","Palmar surface only","Fingertips only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to use the ulnar border of the hand to assess fundal height.  Why A wrong: The radial border is not specified.  Why C wrong: The palmar surface alone is not what is described.  Why D wrong: Fingertips alone are not the described technique."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"When assessing fundal height per abdomen, the examiner should start from which point and move in which direction?","options":["Start from the xiphisternum and move downward","Start from the pubic symphysis and go upwards","Start from the umbilicus and move sideways","Start from the flanks and move medially"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to start from the pubic symphysis and then go upwards.  Why A wrong: The described direction is upward from the pubic symphysis, not downward from the xiphisternum.  Why C wrong: The starting point is the pubic symphysis, not the umbilicus.  Why D wrong: Movement is vertical (upwards), not from flanks medially."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"During per-abdominal fundal height assessment, once the top of the uterus (fundus) is felt, the examiner should:","options":["Make an 'X' mark at that point","Apply firm downward pressure","Stop the examination immediately","Reposition the patient prone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Wherever uterus is felt = Make 'X' Mark.\"  Why B wrong: Firm downward pressure is not the instruction.  Why C wrong: The examination continues with marking, not stopping.  Why D wrong: Prone repositioning is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"At approximately 12 weeks of gestation, the uterine fundus is typically palpable at the level of the:","options":["Pubic symphysis","Umbilicus","Xiphisternum","Midway between umbilicus and xiphisternum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The fundal-height landmark diagram places the 12-week fundus at the pubic symphysis level.  Why B wrong: The umbilicus corresponds to about 24 weeks.  Why C wrong: The xiphisternum corresponds to about 36 weeks.  Why D wrong: Midway between umbilicus and xiphisternum corresponds to a later gestation (~32 weeks)."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the fundal-height landmark diagram, the fundus reaches the level of the umbilicus at approximately:","options":["12 weeks","16 weeks","24 weeks","36 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram marks the umbilicus level at about 24 weeks of gestation.  Why A wrong: 12 weeks corresponds to the pubic symphysis.  Why B wrong: 16 weeks is midway between pubic symphysis and umbilicus.  Why D wrong: 36 weeks corresponds to the xiphisternum."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the fundal-height landmark diagram, the fundus lies approximately midway between the pubic symphysis and umbilicus at:","options":["12 weeks","16 weeks","28 weeks","36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram places the 16-week fundus midway between the pubic symphysis and the umbilicus.  Why A wrong: 12 weeks is at the pubic symphysis.  Why C wrong: 28 weeks is above the umbilicus.  Why D wrong: 36 weeks is at the xiphisternum."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"In the fundal-height landmark diagram, the fundus reaches the xiphisternum at approximately:","options":["24 weeks","28 weeks","32 weeks","36 weeks"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram places the fundus at the xiphisternum at about 36 weeks.  Why A wrong: 24 weeks corresponds to the umbilicus.  Why B wrong: 28 weeks is above the umbilicus but below the xiphisternum.  Why C wrong: 32 weeks is between the umbilicus and xiphisternum, not at the xiphisternum."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"The symphysio-fundal height (SFH) is measured using:","options":["A rigid ruler","A measuring tape","A caliper","Ultrasound only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states SFH is measured with a measuring tape.  Why A wrong: A rigid ruler is not used because the abdomen is curved.  Why C wrong: A caliper is not the described tool.  Why D wrong: Ultrasound is a separate modality; SFH uses a measuring tape."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"When measuring symphysio-fundal height, the two ends of the measuring tape are placed at:","options":["Umbilicus and xiphisternum","Pubic symphysis and the 'X' mark (fundus)","Both anterior superior iliac spines","Xiphisternum and pubic symphysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states one end is at the pubic symphysis and the other side is on the 'X' mark (the marked fundal point).  Why A wrong: Umbilicus to xiphisternum is not the SFH measurement.  Why C wrong: The iliac spines are not the SFH landmarks.  Why D wrong: The fundal end is the 'X' mark, not the xiphisternum by default."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Symphysio-fundal height is measured in which unit?","options":["Inches","Centimeters (cm)","Millimeters","Feet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states SFH is measured in cm (centimeters).  Why A wrong: Although the tape has inches, the measurement is recorded in cm.  Why C wrong: Millimeters are not used.  Why D wrong: Feet are not used for SFH."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"During symphysio-fundal height measurement, the patient's legs should be:","options":["Flexed at the hip and knee","Straight (extended)","Crossed","In lithotomy position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the patient's legs should be straight (extended) during SFH measurement.  Why A wrong: Hip/knee flexion is used for palpating fundal height per abdomen, not for the tape measurement of SFH.  Why C wrong: Crossed legs are not appropriate.  Why D wrong: Lithotomy is used for vaginal procedures, not SFH."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"When using the measuring tape for symphysio-fundal height, which side of the tape should face the examiner (to reduce bias)?","options":["The centimeter side should face the examiner","The inches side should be facing the examiner (cm side away)","Both sides face the examiner","Neither side matters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the inches side of the tape should be facing you (examiner), with the cm side away from you — so the reading (cm) is blinded to reduce bias.  Why A wrong: The cm side is kept away from the examiner, not facing.  Why C wrong: Only the inches side faces the examiner.  Why D wrong: The orientation matters to blind the reading."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Symphysio-fundal height measurement becomes especially important after which gestational age?","options":["After 12 weeks","After 16 weeks","After 24 weeks","After 36 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states SFH is very important after 24 weeks.  Why A wrong: 12 weeks is too early for reliable SFH.  Why B wrong: 16 weeks is not the stated threshold.  Why D wrong: 36 weeks is the upper limit of the SFH-week correspondence, not the start of its importance."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"After 24 weeks, the symphysio-fundal height in centimeters corresponds to the number of weeks of gestation up to approximately:","options":["30 weeks","36 weeks","40 weeks","42 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states SFH in cm corresponds to the number of weeks up to 36 weeks.  Why A wrong: The correspondence extends to 36 weeks, not just 30.  Why C wrong: 40 weeks is beyond the stated correspondence limit.  Why D wrong: 42 weeks is beyond the stated limit."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"A pregnant woman at 30 weeks of gestation would be expected to have a symphysio-fundal height of approximately:","options":["20 cm","25 cm","30 cm","40 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After 24 weeks, SFH in cm equals the number of weeks (up to 36 weeks); at 30 weeks it is approximately 30 cm.  Why A wrong: 20 cm would correspond to ~20 weeks.  Why B wrong: 25 cm would correspond to ~25 weeks.  Why D wrong: 40 cm exceeds both the gestational age and the correspondence range (which caps at 36 weeks)."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which device can detect the fetal heart sound as early as 10 weeks of gestation?","options":["Ordinary stethoscope","Hand-held Doppler","Pinard fetoscope","Palpating hand"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a hand-held Doppler can detect FHS as early as 10 weeks.  Why A wrong: A stethoscope detects FHS later (18-20 weeks).  Why C wrong: A fetoscope/stethoscope detects FHS later, not at 10 weeks.  Why D wrong: Palpation does not detect fetal heart sounds."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Using a stethoscope, the fetal heart sound can usually be heard by approximately how many weeks?","options":["10-12 weeks","14-16 weeks","18-20 weeks","24-26 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that by stethoscope FHS can be heard by 18-20 weeks.  Why A wrong: 10-12 weeks is when the Doppler (not stethoscope) can detect FHS.  Why B wrong: 14-16 weeks is earlier than the stethoscope range given.  Why D wrong: 24-26 weeks is later than the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"By stethoscope, the fetal heart sound can be heard DEFINITIVELY by approximately:","options":["18 weeks","20 weeks","22 weeks","24 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that by stethoscope FHS is heard definitively by 22 weeks.  Why A wrong: 18 weeks is the earlier end of the range, not the definitive point.  Why B wrong: 20 weeks is the upper end of the initial range, not the definitive time.  Why D wrong: 24 weeks is later than the stated definitive value."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"A per speculum examination during the antenatal period is done primarily to:","options":["See the cervix","Measure fundal height","Auscultate the fetal heart","Assess maternal blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Do: Per speculum examination → To see the Cervix.\"  Why B wrong: Fundal height is measured by tape/palpation, not speculum.  Why C wrong: The fetal heart is auscultated with Doppler/stethoscope.  Why D wrong: BP is measured separately."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Per vaginal examination can be done in EARLY pregnancy to diagnose pregnancy because at that stage the uterus is:","options":["An abdominal organ","An intrapelvic organ","Located above the umbilicus","Non-palpable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states per vaginal examination can be done in early pregnancy when the uterus is an intrapelvic organ, to diagnose pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The uterus becomes an abdominal organ later in pregnancy, when PV is less useful for this purpose.  Why C wrong: In early pregnancy the uterus is still within the pelvis, not above the umbilicus.  Why D wrong: The uterus is palpable bimanually in early pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"Which of the following are stated purposes of the per vaginal examination during antenatal assessment? (Select all that apply)","options":["To diagnose pregnancy in early pregnancy (intrapelvic uterus)","To assess the size of the uterus","To rule out pelvic pathology","To measure the symphysio-fundal height","To auscultate the fetal heart"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists diagnosing pregnancy (early, intrapelvic uterus), assessing uterine size, and ruling out pelvic pathology as purposes of PV examination.  Why D wrong: SFH is measured with a tape, not by PV examination.  Why E wrong: Fetal heart auscultation uses Doppler/stethoscope, not PV examination."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"The overall sequence of the WHO objective linking population health to antenatal care is best represented as:","options":["Health for All → Health during pregnancy → Need for essential obstetric care (continued risk assessment)","Family planning → Institutional delivery → Postnatal care","Immunization → Nutrition → Delivery","Risk assessment → Discharge → Follow-up"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's flow chart is WHO \"Health for All\" → Health during pregnancy → Need for essential obstetric care during pregnancy (continued risk assessment).  Why B wrong: This sequence is not the one on the page.  Why C wrong: This sequence is not depicted.  Why D wrong: This is not the stated objective chain."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"A pregnant woman at 34 weeks, following the ideal antenatal schedule, should attend antenatal visits at what frequency?","options":["Once every 4 weeks","Once every 2 weeks","Once weekly","Once every 6 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At 34 weeks she is in the 28-36 week band, where the ideal schedule is 1 visit every 2 weeks.  Why A wrong: Every 4 weeks applies up to 28 weeks.  Why C wrong: Weekly visits begin at ≥36 weeks.  Why D wrong: Every 6 weeks is not part of the schedule."},{"section":"Batch 026 · Signs of pregnancy, pseudocyesis, antenatal care objectives…","question":"A woman at 38 weeks of gestation, per the ideal antenatal schedule, should be seen:","options":["Weekly","Every 2 weeks","Every 4 weeks","Only once more before delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At 38 weeks (≥36 weeks) the ideal schedule calls for weekly visits.  Why B wrong: Every 2 weeks applies to 28-36 weeks.  Why C wrong: Every 4 weeks applies up to 28 weeks.  Why D wrong: The schedule specifies weekly, not a single visit."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"In a pregnant woman with antepartum hemorrhage (APH), which of the following procedures is contraindicated?","options":["Per vaginal (internal) examination","Auscultation of fetal heart sounds","Recording maternal blood pressure","Measuring fundal height"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In antepartum hemorrhage, per vaginal examination is contraindicated because it can provoke torrential bleeding, especially if a low-lying placenta (placenta previa) is present.  Why B wrong: Auscultation of fetal heart sounds is a non-invasive, safe assessment.  Why C wrong: Recording maternal BP is essential monitoring and is safe.  Why D wrong: Measuring fundal height is a non-invasive abdominal assessment."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Per vaginal examination should be avoided in a pregnant woman who presents with which of the following?","options":["Any bleeding per vaginum","Mild backache","Nausea","Leg cramps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Any bleeding per vaginum is a listed situation where per vaginal examination is contraindicated (risk of aggravating hemorrhage).  Why B wrong: Backache alone is not a contraindication to vaginal examination.  Why C wrong: Nausea is not related to a contraindication for vaginal examination.  Why D wrong: Leg cramps are a common minor complaint, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"According to the notes, per vaginal examination is contraindicated in a pregnant woman with which of the following histories?","options":["History of previous abortion","History of previous normal delivery","History of appendicectomy","History of migraine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A history (H/O) of previous abortion is listed as a situation contraindicating per vaginal examination.  Why B wrong: A previous normal delivery is not a contraindication.  Why C wrong: Appendicectomy history is unrelated.  Why D wrong: Migraine history is unrelated to vaginal examination."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Per vaginal examination is contraindicated when ultrasonography (USG) shows which finding?","options":["Low lying placenta","Posterior placenta at fundus","Normal amniotic fluid","Single live fetus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A low-lying placenta on USG (placenta previa) contraindicates per vaginal examination because it can precipitate severe hemorrhage.  Why B wrong: A fundal placenta is not a contraindication.  Why C wrong: Normal amniotic fluid is a reassuring finding, not a contraindication.  Why D wrong: A single live fetus is a normal finding."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Per vaginal examination should be avoided if the pregnant patient complains of which of the following?","options":["Leaking of fluid from the vagina","Increased salivation","Frequency of micturition","Heartburn"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient complains of leaking of fluid from the vagina (suggesting rupture of membranes), per vaginal examination is contraindicated to reduce infection risk.  Why B wrong: Increased salivation (ptyalism) is not a contraindication.  Why C wrong: Frequency of micturition is a common minor symptom.  Why D wrong: Heartburn is a common minor symptom, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"How often should hemoglobin (Hb) and hematocrit be checked during pregnancy?","options":["In every trimester","Only in the first visit","Only in the third trimester","Once a year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Hb & hematocrit should be checked in every trimester.  Why B wrong: It is not limited to the first visit; it is repeated every trimester.  Why C wrong: It is not restricted to the third trimester.  Why D wrong: Yearly testing is not appropriate during pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What hemoglobin level defines anemia in pregnancy?","options":["Less than 11 gm%","Less than 13 gm%","Less than 15 gm%","Less than 9 gm%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anemia in pregnancy is defined as Hb <11 gm% (gm/dL) per the notes and WHO.  Why B wrong: 13 gm% is a normal/near-normal value, not the anemia cut-off.  Why C wrong: 15 gm% is above the normal range and not the cut-off.  Why D wrong: <9 gm% indicates moderate anemia, but the defining cut-off for anemia in pregnancy is <11 gm%."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Anemia is the most common ______ cause of maternal mortality.","options":["Indirect","Direct","Iatrogenic","Fortuitous"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state anemia is the most common indirect cause of maternal mortality.  Why B wrong: Hemorrhage is the most common direct cause; anemia is indirect.  Why C wrong: Iatrogenic is not the classification used.  Why D wrong: Fortuitous (incidental) causes are unrelated to pregnancy, whereas anemia is a pregnancy-aggravated indirect cause."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"For the oral glucose tolerance test (GTT) in pregnancy, what glucose load and timing are used per the notes?","options":["75 gm glucose and 2 hours GTT","50 gm glucose and 1 hour GTT","100 gm glucose and 3 hours GTT","25 gm glucose and 30 minutes GTT"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify blood glucose testing with a 75 gm glucose load and a 2-hour GTT.  Why B wrong: 50 gm/1 hour is the older glucose challenge test, not what is stated here.  Why C wrong: 100 gm/3 hours is another older protocol, not the one described.  Why D wrong: 25 gm/30 min is not a recognized GTT protocol."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"In the 75 gm 2-hour GTT, a 2-hour value less than 140 mg/dl is interpreted as:","options":["Normal","Gestational diabetes","Overt diabetes","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A 2-hour value <140 mg/dl is interpreted as normal.  Why B wrong: Gestational diabetes corresponds to a value at/above the diagnostic threshold, not a normal value.  Why C wrong: Overt diabetes corresponds to >200 mg/dl.  Why D wrong: Hypoglycemia is not defined by this cut-off in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"In the 75 gm GTT, a 2-hour value greater than 200 mg/dl indicates:","options":["Overt diabetes","Normal glucose tolerance","Impaired fasting glucose only","Gestational hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A 2-hour value >200 mg/dl indicates overt diabetes per the notes.  Why B wrong: Values >200 mg/dl are far above the normal range.  Why C wrong: This is a 2-hour post-load value, not a fasting glucose measure.  Why D wrong: Gestational hypertension is a blood-pressure disorder, unrelated to glucose values."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"If the initial GTT is negative (normal), when should it be repeated?","options":["At 24–28 weeks","At 12–14 weeks","At 36–40 weeks","Never"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows that if the initial test is negative, it is repeated at 24–28 weeks.  Why B wrong: 12–14 weeks is not the repeat window given.  Why C wrong: 36–40 weeks is late in the third trimester, not the specified repeat time.  Why D wrong: The test is repeated, not omitted."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Which test is used for screening syphilis in antenatal care?","options":["VDRL","VDRL is replaced by GTT","NESTROFF test","Coombs test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Screening for syphilis is done with the VDRL test.  Why B wrong: GTT screens for gestational diabetes, not syphilis.  Why C wrong: NESTROFF test screens for thalassemia trait.  Why D wrong: Coombs test detects antibodies (e.g., in Rh disease), not syphilis."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Which approach is used for HIV testing in antenatal care as described in the notes?","options":["\"Opt out\" approach","\"Opt in\" approach","Mandatory testing by law","Testing only if symptomatic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify HIV testing uses an \"opt out\" approach — testing is offered routinely and the patient is counselled to get it done unless she declines.  Why B wrong: The notes specifically state \"opt out,\" not opt in.  Why C wrong: Testing is not compulsory; the woman can decline.  Why D wrong: It is routine, not restricted to symptomatic women."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"How often should urine routine microscopy be performed during antenatal care?","options":["In every visit","Only in the first visit","Only once in third trimester","Only if the patient reports dysuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state urine routine microscopy should be done in every visit.  Why B wrong: It is repeated at every visit, not only the first.  Why C wrong: It is not limited to the third trimester.  Why D wrong: It is done routinely, not only when symptomatic."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Which of the following are looked for on urine routine microscopy in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pus cells","RBC cast","Sugar","Protein","Platelets"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list pus cells, RBC cast, sugar, and protein as elements assessed on urine routine microscopy (sugar and protein by the dipstick method).  Why E wrong: Platelets are not assessed on urine microscopy; they are a blood parameter."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Sugar and protein in urine are detected by which method during antenatal screening?","options":["Dipstick method","Culture and sensitivity","Gram staining","Enzyme immunoassay"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes bracket sugar and protein as detected by the dipstick method.  Why B wrong: Culture and sensitivity is done when pus cells are seen, to identify infection.  Why C wrong: Gram staining identifies bacterial morphology, not routine sugar/protein.  Why D wrong: Enzyme immunoassay is not used for routine urine sugar/protein testing."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"If urine routine microscopy shows pus cells, what is the next appropriate step?","options":["Do a urine culture and sensitivity","Immediately start IV antibiotics without further tests","Repeat the dipstick only","Ignore it as normal in pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states that when urine microscopy shows pus cells, a culture and sensitivity should be done to identify the organism and appropriate treatment.  Why B wrong: Antibiotics should be guided by culture/sensitivity, not started blindly.  Why C wrong: Repeating the dipstick does not identify the causative organism.  Why D wrong: Pus cells indicate infection that must be evaluated, not ignored."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"A pregnant woman has no symptoms of UTI but urine shows infection (bacterial count ≥10⁵/ml). This condition is termed:","options":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria","Acute cystitis","Pyelonephritis","Sterile pyuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe asymptomatic bacteriuria as common in pregnancy — the patient has no UTI symptoms but has infection (10⁵/ml).  Why B wrong: Acute cystitis is symptomatic bladder infection.  Why C wrong: Pyelonephritis is a kidney infection, a possible consequence, not the description of asymptomatic infection.  Why D wrong: Sterile pyuria means pus cells without bacterial growth, opposite to this scenario."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Untreated asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy can lead to which of the following complications? (Select all that apply)","options":["Preterm labor (PTL)","Pyelonephritis","Gestational diabetes","Neural tube defect"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show asymptomatic bacteriuria can lead to preterm labor (PTL) and pyelonephritis, and therefore has to be treated.  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes is a glucose-metabolism disorder, not caused by bacteriuria.  Why D wrong: Neural tube defects are caused by folate deficiency, not urinary infection."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Because asymptomatic bacteriuria can cause preterm labor and pyelonephritis, what is the appropriate action?","options":["It has to be treated","It should be observed without treatment","It resolves only after delivery","It requires only increased fluid intake"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state the condition \"has to be treated\" to prevent PTL and pyelonephritis.  Why B wrong: Observation alone risks preterm labor and kidney infection.  Why C wrong: Waiting until delivery leaves the mother at risk of complications.  Why D wrong: Fluids alone do not eradicate the bacterial infection."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"In the first trimester, which type of ultrasound scan is performed as routine USG in pregnancy?","options":["Dating scan","Anomaly scan","Doppler scan","Biophysical profile"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state routine USG in the 1st trimester is the dating scan.  Why B wrong: The anomaly scan is performed at 18–20 weeks, not the first trimester.  Why C wrong: Doppler scans assess blood flow and are not the routine first-trimester scan.  Why D wrong: A biophysical profile assesses fetal well-being later in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"The dating scan is best for assessing which of the following?","options":["Gestational age of the fetus","Fetal cardiac anomalies","Placental blood flow","Amniotic fluid infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The dating scan is best for assessing the gestational age of the fetus.  Why B wrong: Cardiac anomalies are assessed on the anomaly scan/TIFFA.  Why C wrong: Placental blood flow is assessed with Doppler.  Why D wrong: Infection is not diagnosed by the dating scan."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Which parameter is used in the dating scan to assess gestational age?","options":["Crown rump length","Biparietal diameter only","Femur length only","Abdominal circumference only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify the parameter used is crown rump length (CRL), the most accurate early measure of gestational age.  Why B wrong: Biparietal diameter is used later, not the parameter named for the dating scan here.  Why C wrong: Femur length is a later biometric parameter.  Why D wrong: Abdominal circumference is used for growth assessment later in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Which of the following is classified as a \"special investigation\" (not part of essential care) in the notes?","options":["Serum TSH","Hemoglobin","Routine urine microscopy","Blood pressure recording"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Serum TSH is listed under special investigations, which are not a part of essential care.  Why B wrong: Hemoglobin is part of essential first-visit investigations.  Why C wrong: Routine urine microscopy is essential, done every visit.  Why D wrong: Blood pressure recording is part of routine subsequent-visit assessment."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Which test is used for screening the thalassemia trait?","options":["NESTROFF test","VDRL test","Coombs test","Dipstick test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the NESTROFF test is used for screening the thalassemia trait.  Why B wrong: VDRL screens for syphilis.  Why C wrong: Coombs test detects antibodies in blood.  Why D wrong: Dipstick test screens urine sugar/protein."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Screening for thalassemia trait (NESTROFF test) is routinely done in medical colleges of which states, per the notes?","options":["UP, Haryana, Punjab","Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka","Assam, Nagaland, Manipur","Goa, Gujarat, Rajasthan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state thalassemia screening is routinely done in medical colleges in UP, Haryana, and Punjab.  Why B wrong: These southern states are not the ones named.  Why C wrong: These northeastern states are not named for thalassemia screening.  Why D wrong: These states are not the ones mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Screening for sickle cell anemia is routinely done in which region of India, according to the notes?","options":["Central India","Coastal South India","Northeast India","Kashmir valley"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state screening for sickle cell anemia is done in Central India, where the disease is prevalent among tribal populations.  Why B wrong: Coastal South India is not the region named.  Why C wrong: Northeast India is not named for sickle cell screening.  Why D wrong: Kashmir valley is not the region named."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"According to the notes, Down syndrome screening is done under what condition?","options":["If the patient can afford it, in all females irrespective of the mother's age","Only in mothers over 35 years","Only in mothers under 20 years","Only if there is a family history"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Down syndrome screening is done if the patient can afford it, in all females irrespective of age of the mother.  Why B wrong: It is not restricted to older mothers; it is offered irrespective of age.  Why C wrong: There is no lower-age-only restriction.  Why D wrong: It is not limited to those with a family history."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Antibody testing for rubella immunity is ideally done during which period?","options":["Preconception period","Third trimester","During active labor","Immediately postpartum only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state antibody for rubella immunity is done in the preconception period.  Why B wrong: The recommended timing is before conception, not the third trimester.  Why C wrong: Testing during labor is too late for immunity planning.  Why D wrong: Postpartum-only testing misses the preconception opportunity emphasized here."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"If rubella antibodies are present (positive) in a female, what does this indicate?","options":["She is immune to rubella infection","She has an active rubella infection now","She must receive the rubella vaccine immediately","She is at high risk of congenital rubella syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if antibodies are present (+ve), the female is immune to rubella infection (due to previous rubella vaccine or previous rubella infection).  Why B wrong: Presence of antibodies indicates immunity, not necessarily active infection.  Why C wrong: An immune female does not need the vaccine.  Why D wrong: An immune female is protected, so risk to fetus is low."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"A non-pregnant woman is found to have absent rubella antibodies. What is the appropriate management?","options":["Give the rubella vaccine and advise avoiding pregnancy for at least 1 month","Give the rubella vaccine and allow immediate pregnancy","Give the rubella vaccine only after conception","No vaccine is required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if antibodies are absent and the patient is not pregnant, give the rubella vaccine; pregnancy is contraindicated for at least 1 month afterward (as it is a live vaccine).  Why B wrong: Pregnancy must be avoided for at least 1 month after the live rubella vaccine.  Why C wrong: The rubella vaccine is contraindicated during pregnancy; it is given before conception.  Why D wrong: An antibody-negative woman needs the vaccine to gain immunity."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"A pregnant woman is found to be non-immune to rubella (antibodies absent). What is the correct advice?","options":["Cannot give rubella vaccine during pregnancy; advise avoiding crowded places and parks in the first trimester","Give the rubella vaccine immediately","Terminate the pregnancy","Give two doses of rubella vaccine before delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the rubella vaccine cannot be given during pregnancy (live vaccine); the woman is advised to avoid crowded places and parks (to reduce exposure) in the first trimester.  Why B wrong: The live rubella vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Termination is not the advised management for non-immunity.  Why D wrong: The vaccine is not given during pregnancy at all."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"During subsequent antenatal visits, which of the following should be assessed? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ask for complaints","Ask about fetal movement","Blood pressure","Urine (routine and microscopy)","Crown rump length dating scan"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Subsequent visits include asking for complaints, asking about fetal movement, checking BP, and doing urine routine & microscopy.  Why E wrong: Crown rump length dating scan is a first-trimester routine USG, not a subsequent-visit assessment."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"In subsequent antenatal visits, hemoglobin should be checked how often, and anemia is defined as Hb below what value?","options":["In each trimester; Hb <11 gm/dL","Only once; Hb <13 gm/dL","Every week; Hb <9 gm/dL","Only at term; Hb <7 gm/dL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Hb should be checked in each trimester, and anemia in pregnancy is Hb <11 gm/dL.  Why B wrong: Hb is checked each trimester, and the cut-off is <11, not <13.  Why C wrong: Weekly Hb testing is not indicated, and <9 is not the defining cut-off.  Why D wrong: Hb is not checked only at term; the cut-off is <11, not <7."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"During subsequent visits, an obstetric examination is performed primarily to assess what?","options":["Fetal growth","Maternal dental health","Placental grade only","Blood group"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state an obstetric examination is done to assess fetal growth.  Why B wrong: Dental health is not the purpose of the obstetric examination.  Why C wrong: Placental grade is a USG finding, not the stated purpose.  Why D wrong: Blood group is a laboratory test, not assessed by obstetric examination."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"The anomaly scan (TIFFA / Level 2 USG) is ideally performed at which gestational age?","options":["18 to 20 weeks","6 to 8 weeks","28 to 30 weeks","36 to 38 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the anomaly scan / TIFFA is done at 18 to 20 weeks.  Why B wrong: 6–8 weeks is too early; that is the dating range.  Why C wrong: 28–30 weeks is later than the anomaly scan window.  Why D wrong: 36–38 weeks is near term, not the anomaly scan time."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What does the abbreviation TIFFA stand for?","options":["Targeted Imaging for Fetal Anomalies","Total Intrauterine Fetal Fluid Assessment","Timed Investigation of Fetal Femur Anatomy","Trans-Fundal Imaging for Fetal Age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TIFFA stands for Targeted Imaging for Fetal Anomalies (a Level 2 USG).  Why B wrong: This is a fabricated expansion; it does not describe TIFFA.  Why C wrong: This is not the correct expansion of TIFFA.  Why D wrong: This is not the correct expansion of TIFFA."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"If only one USG can be done in a pregnancy, which is the best scan and at what gestational age?","options":["Anomaly scan at 18–20 weeks","Dating scan at 6–8 weeks","Doppler scan at 32 weeks","Growth scan at 28 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if only one USG has to be done, the best is the anomaly scan at 18–20 weeks, because organogenesis is complete by then and anomalies can be detected.  Why B wrong: The dating scan gives gestational age but not anomaly detection.  Why C wrong: A Doppler scan assesses blood flow, not anomalies at the ideal window.  Why D wrong: A growth scan is done later and does not replace anomaly screening."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Why is the anomaly scan performed at 18–20 weeks the best single scan?","options":["Because organogenesis is complete by this time","Because the placenta has fully matured","Because the fetus is fully term","Because amniotic fluid is at its lowest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state organogenesis is complete by 18–20 weeks, so structural anomalies can be detected.  Why B wrong: Placental maturation is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: The fetus is not term at 18–20 weeks (term is ~37–40 weeks).  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid levels are not the reason given."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Folic acid supplementation is given to all pregnant females primarily to prevent which condition?","options":["Neural tube defects","Gestational diabetes","Pre-eclampsia","Postpartum hemorrhage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state folic acid is given to all pregnant females to prevent neural tube defects (NTDs).  Why B wrong: Gestational diabetes is not prevented by folic acid.  Why C wrong: Pre-eclampsia prevention involves calcium/aspirin, not folic acid.  Why D wrong: PPH is not prevented by folic acid."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the prophylactic dose of folic acid for all pregnant females?","options":["400 mcg","4 mg","40 mcg","1 gm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the prophylactic dose for all pregnant females as 400 mcg.  Why B wrong: 4 mg is the therapeutic dose for women with a previous NTD baby.  Why C wrong: 40 mcg is far too low.  Why D wrong: 1 gm is an incorrect (excessively high) value."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"For all pregnant females, when should the prophylactic folic acid be started?","options":["1 month before conception (or as soon as pregnancy is diagnosed)","3 months before conception","Only after 12 weeks of pregnancy","Only in the third trimester"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For all pregnant females, prophylactic folic acid is started 1 month before conception, or as soon as pregnancy is diagnosed.  Why B wrong: 3 months before conception applies to the therapeutic (4 mg) dose for previous NTD history.  Why C wrong: Starting at 12 weeks is too late to prevent NTDs (neural tube closes early).  Why D wrong: The third trimester is far too late."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"When is prophylactic folic acid (400 mcg) stopped?","options":["3 months after conception","At delivery","1 month after conception","It is never stopped"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the prophylactic dose is stopped 3 months after conception (the neural tube closes in early pregnancy).  Why B wrong: It is stopped at 3 months after conception, not at delivery.  Why C wrong: The stop time is 3 months, not 1 month, after conception.  Why D wrong: It is stopped after the first trimester (3 months after conception)."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"The 400 mcg folic acid dose given to all pregnant females is called the:","options":["Prophylactic dose","Therapeutic dose","Maintenance dose","Loading dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 400 mcg dose is called the prophylactic dose of folic acid, given to prevent neural tube defect.  Why B wrong: The therapeutic dose is 4 mg for those with a previous NTD baby.  Why C wrong: \"Maintenance dose\" is not the term used.  Why D wrong: \"Loading dose\" is not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the therapeutic dose of folic acid for a woman with a history of a previous baby with a neural tube defect?","options":["4 mg","400 mcg","40 mg","0.4 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For females with a previous history of neural tube defects in any baby, the therapeutic dose is 4 mg.  Why B wrong: 400 mcg (0.4 mg) is the prophylactic dose for all pregnant females.  Why C wrong: 40 mg is far too high.  Why D wrong: 0.4 mg equals the prophylactic dose, not the therapeutic dose."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"For a woman with a previous NTD baby, when is the therapeutic folic acid (4 mg) started and stopped?","options":["Started 3 months before conception, stopped 3 months after conception","Started 1 month before conception, stopped at delivery","Started at 12 weeks, stopped at 28 weeks","Started at conception, stopped at term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The therapeutic 4 mg dose is started 3 months before conception and stopped 3 months after conception.  Why B wrong: These timings correspond to the prophylactic dose start (1 month) and are incorrect for the therapeutic dose.  Why C wrong: These times do not match the notes.  Why D wrong: These times do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"The 4 mg folic acid dose is termed the:","options":["Therapeutic dose to prevent recurrence of neural tube defect","Prophylactic dose to prevent anemia","Maintenance dose for lactation","Loading dose for iron stores"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 4 mg dose is the therapeutic dose of folic acid, given to prevent recurrence of a neural tube defect.  Why B wrong: 400 mcg is the prophylactic dose; 4 mg is therapeutic and it targets NTD recurrence, not anemia.  Why C wrong: It is not a lactation maintenance dose.  Why D wrong: It relates to folic acid/NTD, not iron loading."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the risk of recurrence of a neural tube defect in the next pregnancy?","options":["5%","25%","50%","1%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the risk of recurrence of NTD is 5% in the next pregnancy.  Why B wrong: 25% is not the figure given.  Why C wrong: 50% is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 1% is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"After folic acid supplementation, from which month of pregnancy is the iron + folic acid tablet started (to prevent anemia)?","options":["4th month of pregnancy","1st month of pregnancy","7th month of pregnancy","Immediately after delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that the folic acid regimen is followed by starting Fe + folic acid tablet in the 4th month of pregnancy to prevent anemia.  Why B wrong: Iron is generally started in the 4th month (2nd trimester), not the 1st.  Why C wrong: The 7th month is later than the stated start.  Why D wrong: Fe + folic acid is started during pregnancy, in the 4th month."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the total amount of iron needed during the entire pregnancy?","options":["1000 mg","100 mg","500 mg","2000 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the total amount of iron needed in pregnancy is 1000 mg.  Why B wrong: 100 mg is far too low for the whole pregnancy.  Why C wrong: 500 mg is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 2000 mg is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"According to the notes, what is the duration of pregnancy used to calculate iron requirement per day?","options":["280 days","180 days","365 days","252 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state duration of pregnancy = 280 days.  Why B wrong: 180 days is the duration of Anemia Mukt Bharat iron supplementation, not pregnancy.  Why C wrong: 365 days is a full year, not the pregnancy duration.  Why D wrong: 252 days is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Based on total iron need (1000 mg) over pregnancy, the per-day iron requirement is approximately:","options":["4–6 mg/day","60 mg/day","0.5 mg/day","100 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give per-day iron requirement as 4–6 mg/day (1000 mg over ~280 days).  Why B wrong: 60 mg/day is the elemental iron in supplement tablets, not the daily physiological requirement calculated here.  Why C wrong: 0.5 mg/day is far too low.  Why D wrong: 100 mg/day is far too high."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"According to the notes, the iron requirement in the first term (trimester) is approximately how much per day?","options":["Negligible / not much (marked as \"x\")","6–7 mg/day","10 mg/day","4 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes mark the 1st term requirement as \"(x)\" — i.e., iron requirement is minimal/not much in the first trimester (which is why iron supplementation begins in the 2nd trimester).  Why B wrong: 6–7 mg/day is the 2nd and 3rd term requirement.  Why C wrong: 10 mg/day is not stated for the first term.  Why D wrong: 4 mg/day is not specifically assigned to the first term."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"According to the notes, the iron requirement in the 2nd and 3rd trimester (term) is:","options":["6–7 mg/day","1–2 mg/day","15–20 mg/day","60–70 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 2nd term and 3rd term requirement is 6–7 mg/day.  Why B wrong: 1–2 mg/day is too low for the later trimesters.  Why C wrong: 15–20 mg/day is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 60–70 mg/day is the dietary intake needed, not the physiological requirement."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Only what percentage of dietary iron is actually absorbed?","options":["10%","50%","90%","30%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state only 10% of dietary iron is absorbed.  Why B wrong: 50% overestimates iron absorption.  Why C wrong: 90% is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 30% is incorrect per these notes."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Because only 10% of dietary iron is absorbed, what daily dietary iron intake would be required (which is practically impossible)?","options":["60–70 mg/day","6–7 mg/day","200 mg/day","10 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that to meet needs from diet, 60–70 mg/day of dietary iron would be required, which is impossible — hence supplementation is mandatory.  Why B wrong: 6–7 mg/day is the absorbed physiological requirement, not the required dietary intake.  Why C wrong: 200 mg/day is not the value stated.  Why D wrong: 10 mg/day is not the value stated."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Why is iron supplementation considered mandatory in pregnancy?","options":["Because meeting iron needs through diet alone (60–70 mg/day) is practically impossible","Because dietary iron is fully absorbed","Because pregnancy decreases iron requirement","Because iron is stored in excess before pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes conclude that since achieving 60–70 mg/day dietary iron is impossible (only 10% absorbed), iron supplementation is mandatory in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Only 10% of dietary iron is absorbed, not all of it.  Why C wrong: Pregnancy increases iron requirement.  Why D wrong: Iron stores are usually insufficient, necessitating supplementation."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Under which Government of India programme are iron + folic acid tablets provided free of cost?","options":["Anemia Mukt Bharat Programme","Janani Suraksha Yojana","Mission Indradhanush","Poshan Abhiyaan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Fe + folic acid is given free of cost under the Anemia Mukt Bharat Programme.  Why B wrong: Janani Suraksha Yojana is a maternity benefit/cash-assistance scheme, not the iron-folic acid programme named here.  Why C wrong: Mission Indradhanush is an immunization programme.  Why D wrong: Poshan Abhiyaan is a broader nutrition mission, not the one named here."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"In the Anemia Mukt Bharat tablet, what are the amounts of iron and folic acid per tablet?","options":["Fe = 60 mg and Folic acid = 500 mcg","Fe = 100 mg and Folic acid = 400 mcg","Fe = 30 mg and Folic acid = 1 mg","Fe = 60 mg and Folic acid = 5 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state each tablet contains Fe = 60 mg + Folic acid = 500 mcg, and every pregnant female should take 1 tablet/day.  Why B wrong: The folic acid is 500 mcg and iron is 60 mg, not these values.  Why C wrong: These amounts are incorrect.  Why D wrong: The folic acid content is 500 mcg, not 5 mg."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"How many iron + folic acid tablets should every pregnant female take per day under the programme?","options":["1 tablet/day","2 tablets/day","3 tablets/day","Half a tablet/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state every pregnant female should take 1 tablet/day.  Why B wrong: The stated dose is 1 tablet/day, not 2.  Why C wrong: 3 tablets/day is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Half a tablet is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"When is iron-folic acid supplementation started in pregnancy under the programme?","options":["4th month of pregnancy (2nd trimester)","1st month of pregnancy","7th month of pregnancy","After delivery only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state supplementation is started in the 4th month of pregnancy (2nd trimester).  Why B wrong: It is not started in the first month/first trimester.  Why C wrong: The 7th month is later than the correct start.  Why D wrong: It is started during pregnancy, in the 4th month."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"For how many days is iron-folic acid continued during pregnancy under the Anemia Mukt Bharat schedule?","options":["180 days (6 months)","90 days (3 months)","280 days","100 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state it is continued for 180 days (6 months) throughout pregnancy.  Why B wrong: 90 days is too short.  Why C wrong: 280 days is the duration of pregnancy, not the supplementation days.  Why D wrong: 100 days is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"After delivery, for how many days is iron-folic acid supplementation continued to replenish iron stores?","options":["180 days","42 days","90 days","280 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state iron-folic acid is given for 180 days after delivery to replenish iron stores.  Why B wrong: 42 days (6 weeks) is the puerperium period, not the supplementation duration stated.  Why C wrong: 90 days is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 280 days is the duration of pregnancy, not postpartum supplementation."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the RDA (recommended dietary allowance) of folic acid in pregnancy per the notes?","options":["500 mcg","400 mcg","1000 mcg","250 mcg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the RDA of folic acid in pregnancy as 500 mcg (written \"500 mg\" but referring to the microgram supplement value stated for the tablet).  Why B wrong: 400 mcg is the prophylactic supplementation dose, not the stated RDA figure.  Why C wrong: 1000 mcg is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 250 mcg is the iodine RDA, not folic acid."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the RDA of calcium in pregnancy per the notes (including the newer recommendation)?","options":["1000 mg (new recommendation 1200 mg/day)","500 mg","250 mg","2000 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the RDA of calcium in pregnancy is 1000 mg, with the new recommendation being 1200 mg/day.  Why B wrong: 500 mg is too low.  Why C wrong: 250 mg is the iodine RDA, not calcium.  Why D wrong: 2000 mg is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the RDA of iodine in pregnancy per the notes?","options":["250 mg","150 mcg","1000 mg","500 mcg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the RDA of iodine in pregnancy is 250 mg (as written; standard value is 250 mcg).  Why B wrong: 150 mcg is the non-pregnant iodine requirement, not the value stated here.  Why C wrong: 1000 mg is the calcium value.  Why D wrong: 500 mcg is the folic acid value."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the RDA of vitamin D in pregnancy per the notes?","options":["10 mg (400 IU)","100 mg (4000 IU)","1 mg (40 IU)","25 mg (1000 IU)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the RDA of vitamin D in pregnancy is 10 mg (400 IU) — the standard value is 10 mcg = 400 IU.  Why B wrong: 4000 IU is not the value stated.  Why C wrong: 40 IU is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 1000 IU is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"According to the notes, why should vitamin D not be taken in the 1st trimester?","options":["It can cause congenital malformation","It causes maternal hypertension","It reduces folic acid absorption","It causes gestational diabetes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes remember that vitamin D should not be taken in the 1st trimester as it can lead to congenital malformation.  Why B wrong: Hypertension is not the reason given.  Why C wrong: Reduced folate absorption is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Gestational diabetes is not the reason."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"How many extra calories per day are recommended during pregnancy?","options":["350 kcal/day","500 kcal/day","150 kcal/day","1000 kcal/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state extra calories in pregnancy = 350 kcal/day.  Why B wrong: 500 kcal/day is the extra requirement during lactation, not pregnancy per these notes.  Why C wrong: 150 kcal/day is too low.  Why D wrong: 1000 kcal/day is far too high."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"During which trimesters are the extra 350 kcal/day required?","options":["2nd and 3rd trimester (not in the 1st trimester)","1st trimester only","Throughout all three trimesters equally","Only in the 3rd trimester"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state extra calories are required in the 2nd/3rd trimester and not in the 1st trimester.  Why B wrong: They are not required in the first trimester.  Why C wrong: They are not required in the first trimester, so not all three equally.  Why D wrong: They are required in the 2nd trimester as well, not only the 3rd."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the total daily caloric requirement of a non-pregnant woman versus a pregnant woman per the notes?","options":["Non-pregnant = 2200 kcal/day; Pregnancy = 2550 kcal/day","Non-pregnant = 2000 kcal/day; Pregnancy = 2350 kcal/day","Non-pregnant = 1800 kcal/day; Pregnancy = 2150 kcal/day","Non-pregnant = 2500 kcal/day; Pregnancy = 3000 kcal/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state total calories for a non-pregnant woman = 2200 kcal/day and pregnancy = 2550 kcal/day (a difference of 350 kcal, the extra requirement).  Why B wrong: These values do not match the notes.  Why C wrong: These values do not match the notes.  Why D wrong: These values do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the protein requirement for a non-pregnant woman versus during pregnancy per the notes?","options":["Non-pregnant = 1 gm/kg/day; Pregnancy = 1.2–1.5 gm/kg/day","Non-pregnant = 0.5 gm/kg/day; Pregnancy = 1 gm/kg/day","Non-pregnant = 2 gm/kg/day; Pregnancy = 3 gm/kg/day","Non-pregnant = 1.5 gm/kg/day; Pregnancy = 2 gm/kg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state protein for non-pregnant = 1 gm/kg/day and pregnancy = 1.2–1.5 gm/kg/day.  Why B wrong: These values are too low.  Why C wrong: These values are too high.  Why D wrong: These values do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the general rule regarding live vaccines during pregnancy?","options":["All live vaccines are contraindicated in pregnancy","All live vaccines are safe in pregnancy","Only live vaccines can be given in pregnancy","Live vaccines are given only in the third trimester"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The general rule is that all live vaccines are contraindicated (C/I) in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Live vaccines are contraindicated, not safe, in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Killed vaccines are the ones that can be given, not live ones.  Why D wrong: Live vaccines are contraindicated throughout pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the general rule regarding killed (inactivated) vaccines in pregnancy?","options":["All killed vaccines can be given in pregnancy","All killed vaccines are contraindicated in pregnancy","Killed vaccines are given only postpartum","Killed vaccines cause fetal malformation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The general rule states all killed vaccines can be given in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Killed vaccines are permitted, not contraindicated.  Why C wrong: They can be given during pregnancy, not only postpartum.  Why D wrong: Killed vaccines are safe and do not cause fetal malformation."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Using the mnemonic \"HIT + Rabies,\" which vaccines are considered absolutely safe in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hepatitis","Influenza","Tetanus","Rabies","Measles"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list absolutely safe vaccines as H = Hepatitis, I = Influenza, T = Tetanus, and Rabies.  Why E wrong: Measles is a live vaccine and is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Which of the following vaccines are absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Mumps","Measles","Rubella","Chicken pox","Tetanus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list absolutely contraindicated (live) vaccines as Mumps, Measles, Rubella, Small pox, Chicken pox, and HPV vaccine.  Why E wrong: Tetanus (a toxoid/killed vaccine) is absolutely safe and is given in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Which of the following live vaccines are absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy per the notes list?","options":["Small pox and HPV vaccine","Hepatitis and Influenza","Tetanus and Rabies","Tetanus toxoid and Diphtheria toxoid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Small pox and HPV vaccine are among the absolutely contraindicated vaccines listed.  Why B wrong: Hepatitis and Influenza are absolutely safe.  Why C wrong: Tetanus and Rabies are absolutely safe.  Why D wrong: Tetanus + Diphtheria toxoid are given in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"After receiving a contraindicated (live) vaccine, pregnancy should be avoided for at least how long?","options":["1 month","3 months","1 week","6 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state pregnancy is contraindicated for at least 1 month after these live vaccines.  Why B wrong: The stated interval is 1 month, not 3.  Why C wrong: 1 week is too short.  Why D wrong: 6 months is longer than the stated 1 month."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Which vaccines are generally not given but may be given to a pregnant female in an epidemic or when travelling to an endemic area? (Select all that apply)","options":["Yellow fever","Cholera vaccine","Polio vaccine","Measles vaccine"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list yellow fever, cholera vaccine, and polio vaccine as generally not given but permissible in an epidemic or when a pregnant female travels to an endemic area.  Why D wrong: Measles is a live vaccine that is absolutely contraindicated, not in this \"may be given\" group."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Tetanus immunization in pregnancy consists of which toxoids?","options":["Tetanus toxoid and Diphtheria toxoid","Tetanus toxoid and Pertussis toxoid","Diphtheria toxoid alone","Tetanus toxoid and Rubella"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state tetanus immunization is with Tetanus Toxoid & Diphtheria Toxoid.  Why B wrong: Pertussis toxoid is not the combination stated.  Why C wrong: Diphtheria toxoid alone is not the immunization described.  Why D wrong: Rubella is a contraindicated live vaccine, not part of tetanus immunization."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"When is the 1st dose of tetanus toxoid (Td1) given in pregnancy?","options":["Early in pregnancy (at the first visit itself)","At 28 weeks","At the time of delivery","In the postpartum period"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 1st dose (Td1) is given early in pregnancy, at the first visit itself.  Why B wrong: 28 weeks is not the timing for the first dose.  Why C wrong: It is given at the first visit, not at delivery.  Why D wrong: It is given during pregnancy, not postpartum."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the interval between the 1st (Td1) and 2nd (Td2) doses of tetanus toxoid?","options":["4 weeks","2 weeks","8 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 2nd dose (Td2) is given 4 weeks after the first dose.  Why B wrong: 2 weeks is too short.  Why C wrong: 8 weeks is longer than the stated interval.  Why D wrong: 12 weeks is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"When is a single Td booster (instead of Td1 and Td2) given?","options":["In the 3rd trimester, if pregnancy occurs within 3 years of the last pregnancy and 2 Td doses were previously received","In the 1st trimester of every pregnancy","Only if the woman has never been immunized","At delivery for every woman"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a Td booster is given in the 3rd trimester if pregnancy occurs within 3 years of the last pregnancy and 2 Td doses were already received — then Td1 and Td2 need not be given.  Why B wrong: The booster is given in the 3rd trimester, not the 1st.  Why C wrong: A never-immunized woman needs the full Td1 and Td2 schedule, not just a booster.  Why D wrong: The booster is conditional (within 3 years and 2 prior doses), not for every woman at delivery."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the dose of the tetanus toxoid injection?","options":["0.5 ml IM","1 ml IV","0.1 ml intradermal","2 ml IM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the dose is 0.5 ml IM.  Why B wrong: It is given IM, not IV, and the volume is 0.5 ml.  Why C wrong: 0.1 ml intradermal is the BCG technique, not tetanus toxoid.  Why D wrong: 2 ml is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the site of administration of the tetanus toxoid in pregnancy?","options":["Upper arm","Gluteal region","Anterolateral thigh","Deltoid of both arms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the site is the upper arm.  Why B wrong: The gluteal region is not the site given.  Why C wrong: The anterolateral thigh is used for infants, not the stated adult site here.  Why D wrong: It is given in the upper arm, not both deltoids."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What does WHO now recommend regarding tetanus and diphtheria immunization in pregnant women?","options":["Tetanus + Diphtheria toxoid given together in all pregnant females","Tetanus toxoid alone in all pregnant females","Diphtheria toxoid alone in high-risk women","No routine tetanus immunization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state WHO now recommends Tetanus + Diphtheria Toxoid to be given together in all pregnant females.  Why B wrong: WHO now recommends the combined Td, not tetanus alone.  Why C wrong: It is for all pregnant females, and combined, not diphtheria alone in high-risk women.  Why D wrong: Routine immunization is recommended, not omitted."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"What is the minimum total sleep recommended per day for a pregnant woman as antenatal advice?","options":["10 hours (8 hours at night + 2 hours during the day)","6 hours (all at night)","12 hours (all at night)","8 hours (6 at night + 2 during day)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state minimum sleep = 10 hours, split as 8 hours at night and 2 hours during the day.  Why B wrong: 6 hours is below the recommended minimum.  Why C wrong: 12 hours all at night is not the stated breakdown.  Why D wrong: The night portion is 8 hours, giving a 10-hour total, not 8."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Which position should a pregnant woman avoid, especially in late pregnancy, to prevent supine hypotension syndrome?","options":["Supine (lying flat on the back) position","Left lateral position","Sitting position","Right lateral position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise not lying in the supine position, because the gravid uterus presses on the inferior vena cava (IVC), reducing venous return and maternal BP, causing supine hypotension syndrome.  Why B wrong: The left lateral position is actually recommended as it relieves IVC compression.  Why C wrong: Sitting is not associated with this syndrome.  Why D wrong: While the right lateral is less ideal than left, the specifically contraindicated position named is supine."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"In supine hypotension syndrome, the gravid uterus compresses which vessel, leading to a fall in maternal blood pressure?","options":["Inferior vena cava (IVC)","Aorta only","Renal artery","Pulmonary artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the gravid uterus pressing on the IVC, which decreases venous return, decreases the mother's BP, and causes supine hypotension syndrome.  Why B wrong: The primary vessel compressed in this syndrome is the IVC (a low-pressure venous return vessel), not the aorta alone.  Why C wrong: The renal artery is not the compressed vessel described.  Why D wrong: The pulmonary artery is intrathoracic and not compressed by the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"Arrange the pathophysiology of supine hypotension syndrome correctly.","options":["Supine position → gravid uterus presses on IVC → venous return decreases → mother's BP decreases","Supine position → gravid uterus presses on aorta → cardiac output increases → BP increases","Left lateral position → uterus presses on IVC → BP increases","Sitting position → uterus presses on IVC → venous return increases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show the sequence: lying supine → gravid uterus presses on IVC → venous return ↓ → mother's BP ↓ → supine hypotension syndrome.  Why B wrong: BP decreases (not increases), and the vena cava (not aorta output increase) is central to the mechanism.  Why C wrong: The left lateral position relieves compression and does not cause the syndrome.  Why D wrong: Sitting does not cause IVC compression or increased venous return in this context."},{"section":"Batch 027 · Contraindications in antepartum hemorrhage…","question":"A pregnant woman in her third trimester feels dizzy and her BP drops while lying flat on her back during examination. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Turn her to the left lateral position","Place her flat and elevate both legs only","Ask her to sit up straight quickly","Administer oxygen and keep her supine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These are features of supine hypotension syndrome from IVC compression by the gravid uterus; turning the woman to the left lateral position relieves IVC compression, restoring venous return and BP.  Why B wrong: Keeping her supine (even with legs up) maintains IVC compression by the uterus.  Why C wrong: Sitting up quickly can worsen hypotension and does not directly relieve IVC compression like the lateral position.  Why D wrong: Keeping her supine maintains the compression; repositioning is the priority."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which resting/sleeping position is advised for a pregnant woman?","options":["Supine position","Left lateral or right lateral position","Prone position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise the pregnant woman to adopt a Left lateral or Right lateral position, which optimizes uteroplacental blood flow.  Why A wrong: Supine position can cause aortocaval (IVC) compression and supine hypotension.  Why C wrong: Prone position is impractical and uncomfortable with a gravid uterus.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg is not the advised resting position in normal pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Regarding oral hygiene in pregnancy, an untreated oral infection can lead to which of the following?","options":["Post-term labor","Preterm labor","Gestational diabetes","Polyhydramnios"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"As infection → Preterm Labor,\" so oral infections increase the risk of preterm labor.  Why A wrong: Infection is linked to preterm, not post-term, labor.  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes is not the stated consequence of oral infection.  Why D wrong: Polyhydramnios is unrelated to oral infection in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"As part of oral hygiene advice, a pregnant woman should be told to report immediately if which symptom occurs?","options":["Mild nausea","Fever","Backache","Leg cramps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specifically say \"Report if fever occurs\" in the oral hygiene advice.  Why A wrong: Mild nausea is a normal minor ailment, not the specified reporting sign here.  Why C wrong: Backache is a minor ailment managed conservatively, not an immediate-report sign.  Why D wrong: Leg cramps are a minor ailment, not the specified reporting sign."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Smoking during pregnancy is most directly associated with which fetal complication?","options":["Macrosomia","Intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR)","Post-maturity","Neonatal jaundice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes link smoking (Quit smoking & Alcohol) to IUGR.  Why A wrong: Macrosomia is associated with maternal diabetes, not smoking.  Why C wrong: Post-maturity is not a smoking-related outcome in the notes.  Why D wrong: Neonatal jaundice is not the stated smoking outcome."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Alcohol consumption during pregnancy is most directly associated with which condition?","options":["Fetal Alcohol Syndrome","Gestational hypertension","Rh incompatibility","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes link alcohol use in pregnancy to Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS).  Why B wrong: Gestational hypertension is not the stated alcohol-related outcome.  Why C wrong: Rh incompatibility is due to blood-group antigen mismatch, not alcohol.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa relates to abnormal placental implantation, not alcohol."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which of the following is a recognized feature of Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS)?","options":["Macrosomia","IUGR","Excess amniotic fluid","Increased birth weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: FAS features listed include IUGR, facial dysmorphia, and CNS abnormality.  Why A wrong: FAS causes growth restriction, not macrosomia.  Why C wrong: Excess amniotic fluid is not a listed FAS feature.  Why D wrong: FAS is associated with reduced, not increased, birth weight."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Fetal Alcohol Syndrome characteristically involves abnormality of which body system?","options":["Renal system","Central nervous system (CNS)","Skeletal system only","Endocrine system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"CNS Abnormality\" as one of the three key FAS features.  Why A wrong: Renal abnormality is not listed for FAS.  Why C wrong: While facial features are affected, CNS abnormality is the systemic feature listed, not skeletal alone.  Why D wrong: Endocrine abnormality is not listed for FAS."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which set of facial characteristics is typical of Fetal Alcohol Syndrome?","options":["Small eye openings, smooth philtrum, thin upper lip","Large eyes, deep philtrum, thick lips","Cleft lip, low-set ears, flat nose","Micrognathia, macroglossia, epicanthal folds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The labeled figure lists FAS facial characteristics as small eye openings, smooth philtrum, and thin upper lip.  Why B wrong: These are the opposite of FAS features.  Why C wrong: Cleft lip/low-set ears/flat nose are not the listed FAS facial triad.  Why D wrong: These features describe other syndromes, not the FAS triad shown."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"In an uncomplicated pregnancy, sexual activity (coitus) is:","options":["Absolutely contraindicated throughout pregnancy","Not contraindicated","Contraindicated only in the first trimester","Allowed only after 20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Coitus is not contraindicated\" in normal pregnancy.  Why A wrong: It is not absolutely contraindicated in an uncomplicated pregnancy.  Why C wrong: There is no first-trimester-only restriction in normal pregnancy.  Why D wrong: No 20-week threshold is stated for normal pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"In a woman with a history of bleeding in pregnancy whose USG shows low-lying placenta, sexual activity is:","options":["Encouraged","Contraindicated (C/I)","Allowed with no precautions","Only allowed in the left lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that with a history of bleeding + USG showing low-lying placenta, sexual activity is contraindicated (C/I).  Why A wrong: It should be avoided, not encouraged, in this scenario.  Why C wrong: It is contraindicated, not merely allowed.  Why D wrong: Position modification does not make it safe; it is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Constipation in pregnancy is mainly attributed to the effect of which hormone?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","hCG","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The bowel-habit flowchart shows Progesterone causes constipation via smooth muscle relaxation.  Why A wrong: Estrogen is not the cause cited for constipation.  Why C wrong: hCG is linked to nausea, not constipation, in these notes.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin stimulates uterine contraction and is unrelated to bowel constipation here."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"What is the mechanism by which progesterone causes constipation in pregnancy?","options":["It increases intestinal peristalsis","It relaxes smooth muscle","It increases gastric acid secretion","It causes fluid overload"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone causes smooth muscle relaxation, slowing bowel transit and causing constipation.  Why A wrong: Progesterone reduces, not increases, peristalsis.  Why C wrong: Increased gastric acid is not the mechanism given for constipation.  Why D wrong: Fluid overload is not the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which advice is given to a pregnant woman to relieve constipation?","options":["Reduce fluid intake and reduce fiber","Increase fluids and roughage (fiber)","Take antacids","Bed rest and elevate legs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise (1) Fluids and (2) Roughage to manage constipation.  Why A wrong: This is the opposite of the correct advice.  Why C wrong: Antacids are advised for heartburn, not constipation.  Why D wrong: Leg elevation/bed rest addresses cramps/varicosities, not constipation."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"A pregnant patient should be advised to report immediately if she experiences which of the following warning signs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Leaking of fluid from vagina","Fever (especially high grade)","Bleeding","Painful uterine contraction","Decreased fetal movements"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are listed as warning signs of pregnancy that require immediate reporting: leaking fluid, fever (especially high grade), bleeding, painful uterine contraction, and decreased fetal movements.  Why A/B/C/D/E individually: Each is explicitly enumerated as a danger sign requiring the patient to report immediately."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"A slight increase in body temperature is considered normal during pregnancy. This is due to the effect of which hormone?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","Prolactin","Thyroxine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note explains \"Progestone = Body Temperature ↑\" — progesterone is thermogenic and raises basal body temperature.  Why A wrong: Estrogen is not cited as the thermogenic hormone here.  Why C wrong: Prolactin governs lactation, not the temperature rise noted.  Why D wrong: Thyroxine is not the hormone identified in the note."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Nausea/vomiting (morning sickness) of pregnancy is mainly caused by which hormone?","options":["hCG","Progesterone","Estrogen","Oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state morning sickness is \"Due to hcg.\"  Why B wrong: Progesterone is cited for heartburn, backache, and constipation, not morning sickness.  Why C wrong: Estrogen is not the stated cause of morning sickness here.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is unrelated to nausea in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Morning sickness (nausea/vomiting) of pregnancy typically peaks at which gestational age?","options":["4–6 weeks","8–10 weeks","16–18 weeks","20–24 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state nausea/vomiting peaks at 8–10 weeks.  Why A wrong: 4–6 weeks is earlier than the stated peak.  Why C wrong: By 16 weeks it is subsiding, not peaking.  Why D wrong: 20–24 weeks is well past the peak."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Morning sickness of pregnancy usually subsides within which time frame?","options":["Within 8 weeks","Within 16 weeks","Within 24 weeks","It persists throughout pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state nausea/vomiting subsides \"within 16 weeks.\"  Why A wrong: 8 weeks corresponds to peak, not resolution.  Why C wrong: 24 weeks is later than the stated resolution.  Why D wrong: It typically subsides, not persists throughout."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which dietary advice is appropriate for a pregnant woman with morning sickness? (Select all that apply)","options":["Avoid liquids with food","Eat a biscuit before getting out of bed","Avoid tea and coffee","Avoid spicy food","Take small frequent meals"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are listed advice for morning sickness: avoid liquids with food, eat biscuit before getting out of bed, avoid tea & coffee, avoid spicy food, and small frequent meals.  Why A/B/C/D/E individually: Each is explicitly in the \"Advice\" column for nausea/vomiting management."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which drug combination is recommended (at bedtime) for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy?","options":["Vitamin B6 + Doxylamine","Metoclopramide + Ranitidine","Ondansetron + Antacid","Vitamin D + Calcium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the drug as Vit B6 + Doxylamine, taken at bed time.  Why B wrong: This combination is not the one specified in the notes.  Why C wrong: Ondansetron + antacid is not the listed regimen.  Why D wrong: Vit D + Calcium is used for backache/leg cramps, not nausea."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Heartburn during pregnancy is due to which hormone-mediated effect?","options":["Progesterone causing lower esophageal sphincter relaxation","Estrogen increasing gastric emptying","hCG increasing acid secretion","Oxytocin causing pyloric spasm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state heartburn is due to Progesterone → Lower esophageal sphincter relax, allowing acid reflux.  Why B wrong: Estrogen and gastric emptying are not the stated mechanism.  Why C wrong: hCG is linked to nausea, not heartburn.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin/pyloric spasm is not involved."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which advice is given for a pregnant woman with heartburn? (Select all that apply)","options":["Avoid spicy food","Small frequent meals","Head up position and rest","Take antacids","Drink large amounts of liquid with meals"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Advice for heartburn includes avoid spicy food, small frequent meals, head-up position/rest, and antacids.  Why A/B/C/D correct: Each is explicitly listed as heartburn advice.  Why E wrong: The notes advise avoiding liquids with food, not drinking large amounts with meals."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Backache in pregnancy is most commonly due to which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Lordosis","Faulty posture","Weight gain","Low blood pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list backache causes as lordosis, faulty posture, and weight gain.  Why A/B/C correct: Each is explicitly listed as a cause of backache.  Why D wrong: Low blood pressure is linked to dizziness/syncope, not backache."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Backache in pregnancy is most commonly seen from which point onwards?","options":["From the 1st trimester","From the 2nd term (trimester) onwards","Only in labor","Only postpartum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state backache is MC (most common) from 2nd Term onwards.  Why A wrong: It is not most common in the 1st trimester.  Why C wrong: Backache is a minor ailment of pregnancy, not confined to labor.  Why D wrong: It is an antenatal ailment, most common from the 2nd trimester."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"What is the recommended dose of calcium supplementation for backache/musculoskeletal support in pregnancy?","options":["250 mg tab","500 mg tab (1. OD or 2. BD)","1000 mg tab TDS","100 mg tab OD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify Calcium supplementation 500 mg Tab, dosed once daily (OD) or twice daily (BD).  Why A wrong: 250 mg is not the stated dose.  Why C wrong: 1000 mg TDS is not stated.  Why D wrong: 100 mg OD is not the stated dose."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Vitamin D supplementation in pregnancy should never be given during which period, because it is teratogenic then?","options":["3rd trimester","2nd trimester","1st trimester","During labor"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Vit D supplementation should never be given in the 1st trimester as it is TERATOGENIC.  Why A wrong: The caution applies to the 1st trimester, not the 3rd.  Why B wrong: The restriction is specifically for the 1st trimester.  Why D wrong: The teratogenic caution refers to early pregnancy (organogenesis), not labor."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"What is the advice regarding taking calcium and iron supplements together?","options":["Always take them together for better absorption","Do not take calcium and iron together","Take both only on an empty stomach together","Iron should always be taken at day time with calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise NOT to take calcium and iron together (they impair each other's absorption).  Why A wrong: Taking them together reduces absorption, so it is not advised.  Why C wrong: The notes do not advise taking both together on an empty stomach.  Why D wrong: The notes advise separating them (iron by day, calcium at bedtime), not combining."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"For best absorption, when should iron (Fe) and calcium (Ca) supplements be taken according to the notes?","options":["Iron (Fe) at day time, Calcium (Ca) at bed time","Iron at bed time, Calcium at day time","Both at bed time","Both at day time"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the better schedule is Fe = Day Time and Ca (La) = Bed time.  Why B wrong: This reverses the recommended timing.  Why C wrong: Both at bedtime is not advised (they should be separated).  Why D wrong: Both at day time is not the recommended split."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Besides supplementation, which additional advice is given to a pregnant woman with backache?","options":["Complete bed rest","Postural advice","High-dose analgesics","Iron infusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Postural advice\" as management for backache.  Why A wrong: Complete bed rest is not advised for simple backache.  Why C wrong: High-dose analgesics are not the recommended measure.  Why D wrong: Iron infusion is unrelated to backache management."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Leg cramps in pregnancy may be caused by which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Decreased calcium","Increased lactic acid","Varicose veins","Increased calcium"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list leg-cramp causes as decreased calcium, increased lactic acid, and varicose veins.  Why A/B/C correct: Each is explicitly listed as a cause.  Why D wrong: It is decreased (not increased) calcium that contributes to cramps."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which advice is appropriate to relieve leg cramps in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Calcium supplement","Elastic stocking","Extension of knee and dorsiflexion of foot","Plantar flexion of the foot"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise calcium supplement, elastic stocking, and extension of knee with dorsiflexion of foot for leg cramps.  Why A/B/C correct: Each is explicitly listed as leg-cramp advice.  Why D wrong: Plantar flexion worsens calf cramps; dorsiflexion (not plantar flexion) is advised."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Dizziness and syncope in pregnancy are mainly caused by which mechanism?","options":["IVC (inferior vena cava) compression","Increased cardiac output","Hyperglycemia","Cerebral edema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state dizziness & syncope are mainly due to IVC compression (IVA is a typographical error for IVC), which reduces venous return.  Why B wrong: Increased cardiac output is not the cause of syncope; reduced venous return is.  Why C wrong: Hyperglycemia is not the stated mechanism.  Why D wrong: Cerebral edema is not the cause of positional dizziness here."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"What position should a pregnant woman with dizziness/syncope be advised to lie in?","options":["Supine position","Left lateral position","Prone position","Right Fowler's position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise the left lateral position, which relieves IVC compression and improves venous return.  Why A wrong: Supine worsens IVC compression.  Why C wrong: Prone is impractical and not advised.  Why D wrong: Fowler's position is not the advice given for syncope from IVC compression."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"In evaluating vaginal discharge in pregnancy, which examination is performed?","options":["Per rectal examination","Per speculum examination","Bimanual pelvic examination only","Digital cervical assessment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise to ask about associated symptoms and do a per speculum examination for vaginal discharge.  Why A wrong: Per rectal examination is not the advised assessment for vaginal discharge.  Why C wrong: Bimanual examination alone is not what the notes specify.  Why D wrong: Digital cervical assessment is not the specified step."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"In the first trimester, how should a confirmed vaginal infection preferably be treated?","options":["Systemic oral therapy","Local (topical) treatment","Intravenous antibiotics","No treatment until 2nd trimester"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if infection is positive, treatment is preferred, and in the 1st trimester local treatment is preferred (to avoid systemic teratogenic exposure).  Why A wrong: Systemic therapy is avoided in the 1st trimester in favor of local treatment.  Why C wrong: IV antibiotics are not the preferred first-trimester approach.  Why D wrong: Treatment is preferred, not deferred, using local therapy."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"A pregnant woman with a fungal (candidal) vaginal infection is best treated with which pessary?","options":["Metronidazole pessary","Clotrimazole pessary","Miconazole pessary","Nystatin oral tablet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify Fungal infection → Clotrimazole pessary.  Why A wrong: Metronidazole pessary is listed for bacterial vaginosis.  Why C wrong: Miconazole pessary is listed for Trichomonas in these notes.  Why D wrong: Oral nystatin is not the local pessary specified for fungal infection."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"According to these notes, bacterial vaginosis in pregnancy is treated with which pessary?","options":["Clotrimazole pessary","Metronidazole pessary","Miconazole pessary","Fluconazole pessary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify Bacterial vaginosis → Metronidazole pessary.  Why A wrong: Clotrimazole is listed for fungal infection.  Why C wrong: Miconazole is listed for Trichomonas.  Why D wrong: Fluconazole pessary is not listed in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"According to these notes, Trichomonas vaginal infection is treated with which pessary?","options":["Metronidazole pessary","Clotrimazole pessary","Miconazole pessary","Nystatin pessary"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Trichomonas → Miconazole pessary. (Note: standard evidence-based treatment of trichomoniasis is oral metronidazole; the notes' local pairing is reproduced here per source fidelity.)  Why A wrong: The notes assign metronidazole pessary to bacterial vaginosis.  Why B wrong: Clotrimazole is assigned to fungal infection in the notes.  Why D wrong: Nystatin pessary is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which ultrasound method is commonly used in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters of pregnancy?","options":["Transvaginal scan (TVS)","Transabdominal scan (TAS)","Doppler only","3D reconstruction only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Transabdominal Scan (TAS) is used in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters.  Why A wrong: TVS is commonly used in the 1st trimester.  Why C wrong: Doppler is not the method named for routine 2nd/3rd trimester scanning here.  Why D wrong: 3D reconstruction is not the described routine method."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"What is the probe frequency used for transabdominal scan (TAS)?","options":["3.5 MHz","5–7 MHz","10 MHz","1 MHz"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the TAS probe frequency is 3.5 MHz.  Why B wrong: 5–7 MHz is the TVS probe frequency.  Why C wrong: 10 MHz is not stated.  Why D wrong: 1 MHz is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"When a transabdominal scan (TAS) is used in early pregnancy, what condition of the bladder is required for proper visualization?","options":["Empty bladder","Full bladder","Bladder half full","Bladder irrigation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if TAS is used in early pregnancy, a full bladder is needed for proper visualization (acts as an acoustic window).  Why A wrong: An empty bladder is required for TVS, not early-pregnancy TAS.  Why C wrong: A half-full bladder is not specified.  Why D wrong: Bladder irrigation is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which ultrasound method is commonly used in the 1st trimester?","options":["Transabdominal scan (TAS)","Transvaginal scan (TVS)","Doppler ultrasound","Level II TIFFA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Trans Vaginal Scan (TVS) is commonly used in the 1st trimester.  Why A wrong: TAS is used in the 2nd/3rd trimester.  Why C wrong: Doppler is not the routine first-trimester method here.  Why D wrong: TIFFA (Level II) is done at 18–20 weeks, not routinely in the 1st trimester."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Compared with transabdominal scan, structures on transvaginal scan (TVS) can be seen approximately how much earlier?","options":["1 day earlier","1 week earlier","1 month earlier","There is no difference"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state all structures can be seen 1 week earlier on TVS than on the transabdominal scan.  Why A wrong: The stated difference is 1 week, not 1 day.  Why C wrong: The difference is 1 week, not 1 month.  Why D wrong: There is a difference — TVS visualizes structures about 1 week earlier."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"What is the probe frequency used for transvaginal scan (TVS)?","options":["3.5 MHz","5–7 MHz","2 MHz","12 MHz"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state TVS probe frequency is 5–7 MHz.  Why A wrong: 3.5 MHz is the TAS frequency.  Why C wrong: 2 MHz is not stated.  Why D wrong: 12 MHz is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"What is the required bladder state for a transvaginal scan (TVS)?","options":["Full bladder","Empty bladder","Half-full bladder","Bladder catheterized with saline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the bladder should be empty for TVS.  Why A wrong: A full bladder is needed for early-pregnancy TAS, not TVS.  Why C wrong: A half-full bladder is not specified.  Why D wrong: Saline instillation is not required for TVS."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"A Level I (routine) USG is typically performed between which gestational ages?","options":["6–8 weeks","10–14 weeks","18–20 weeks","28–30 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Level 1/Routine USG is done between 10–14 weeks.  Why A wrong: 6–8 weeks is earlier than the routine scan window.  Why C wrong: 18–20 weeks is the Level II (TIFFA) window.  Why D wrong: 28–30 weeks is not the routine Level I window."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"A Level I (routine) USG is done to determine which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Number of fetuses","To document pregnancy","To know gestational age","To detect all congenital malformations"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Level I USG is done to know the number of fetus, to document pregnancy, and to know gestational age.  Why A/B/C correct: Each is explicitly listed under Level I purposes.  Why D wrong: Detecting all congenital malformations is the role of Level II (TIFFA), not routine Level I."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"What does the abbreviation TIFFA (Level II scan) stand for?","options":["Targeted Imaging for Fetal Anomaly","Total Intrauterine Fetal Fluid Assessment","Trans-Interstitial Fetal Flow Analysis","Timed Investigation for Fetal Activity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define TIFFA as Targeted Imaging for Fetal Anomaly (targeted scan).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the expansion given in the notes for TIFFA."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"The Level II scan (TIFFA) is best able to do which of the following?","options":["Detect all congenital malformations","Only estimate gestational age","Only count the number of fetuses","Confirm pregnancy earliest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state TIFFA (Level II) can detect all congenital malformations.  Why B wrong: Gestational age estimation is a Level I purpose.  Why C wrong: Counting fetuses is a Level I purpose.  Why D wrong: Earliest pregnancy confirmation is not the TIFFA role described."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"At what gestational age is the Level II (TIFFA / targeted) scan performed?","options":["10–14 weeks","18–20 weeks","24–28 weeks","32–36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Level II is done between 18–20 weeks.  Why A wrong: 10–14 weeks is the Level I routine window.  Why C wrong: 24–28 weeks is not the TIFFA window.  Why D wrong: 32–36 weeks is not the TIFFA window."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which anomalies are noted as detectable by a routine (Level I) scan? (Select all that apply)","options":["Anencephaly","Limb reduction defect","Ventricular septal defect","Cleft palate"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state only a few anomalies can be detected on routine scan: anencephaly and limb reduction defect.  Why A/B correct: Both are explicitly listed as detectable on routine scan.  Why C wrong: VSD is not among the few routine-detectable anomalies listed.  Why D wrong: Cleft palate is not listed among the few routine-detectable anomalies."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"According to the schedule USG plan, the initial (booking) scan is done at what gestational age and preferably by which method?","options":["10–14 weeks by TVS (preferred)","18–20 weeks by TAS","6–8 weeks by TAS","24 weeks by TVS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the initial scan at booking (10–14 weeks) is done by TVS (preferred).  Why B wrong: 18–20 weeks is the targeted (Level II) scan.  Why C wrong: 6–8 weeks is not the stated booking window.  Why D wrong: 24 weeks is not the booking window."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"In the schedule of USG in pregnancy, the targeted scan is performed at:","options":["10–14 weeks","18–20 weeks","28–32 weeks","36–40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the targeted scan is done at 18–20 weeks.  Why A wrong: 10–14 weeks is the initial booking scan.  Why C wrong: 28–32 weeks is not the targeted-scan window.  Why D wrong: 36–40 weeks is not the targeted-scan window."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"A 3rd trimester scan (if needed) may be done for which of the following reasons? (Select all that apply)","options":["To localize the placenta","To check fetal growth","High-risk pregnancy","To confirm pregnancy for the first time"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list 3rd trimester scan indications as localize placenta, check fetal growth, and high-risk pregnancy.  Why A/B/C correct: Each is explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Pregnancy is confirmed by the booking scan much earlier, not the 3rd trimester scan."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which is the best investigation to detect congenital malformation?","options":["Level I routine USG","TIFFA / Targeted scan (Level II)","Transabdominal scan at 8 weeks","Doppler study"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the best answer as TIFFA (Targeted scan) / Level II Scan for detecting congenital malformation.  Why A wrong: Level I detects only a few anomalies, not all.  Why C wrong: An early TAS is not the best for anomaly detection.  Why D wrong: Doppler assesses blood flow, not the best for structural anomaly detection."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"If a USG is to be done only once in pregnancy, at what time should it be performed?","options":["10–14 weeks","18–20 weeks","28–30 weeks","36–38 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if USG is done only once, it should be at 18–20 weeks (the anomaly/targeted scan window).  Why A wrong: 10–14 weeks is the booking window, not the single best time given.  Why C wrong: 28–30 weeks is not the answer given.  Why D wrong: 36–38 weeks is not the answer given."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Regarding safety of ultrasound in pregnancy, which statement is correct?","options":["USG is harmful and should be avoided","USG is absolutely safe in pregnancy","USG is only safe after 20 weeks","USG causes fetal malformations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"Usg is absolutely safe in Pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: It is stated to be safe, not harmful.  Why C wrong: It is safe throughout pregnancy, not only after 20 weeks.  Why D wrong: USG does not cause malformations per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Ultrasound can determine the number of fetuses. Which of the following is a possible finding?","options":["Singleton","Twin","Triplet","Quadruplet","All of the above"],"correct":"E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list that USG can tell the number of fetus: singleton, twin, triplet, quadruplet.  Why A/B/C/D wrong individually: Each alone is incomplete; all four are listed possibilities, so \"all of the above\" is correct."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which is described as the best test to confirm pregnancy?","options":["Urine hCG","USG","Serum beta-hCG","Clinical examination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state USG confirms pregnancy and is the \"Best test to confirm pregnancy = USG.\"  Why A wrong: Urine hCG is a screening test, not stated as the best confirmatory test here.  Why C wrong: Serum beta-hCG detects pregnancy hormone but the notes name USG as best.  Why D wrong: Clinical examination is less definitive than USG per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"What is the earliest (1st) sign of pregnancy detectable on USG?","options":["Yolk sac","Gestational sac","Cardiac activity","Fetal pole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the gestational sac is the 1st sign of pregnancy on USG.  Why A wrong: The yolk sac appears after the gestational sac.  Why C wrong: Cardiac activity appears later (around 5–6 weeks).  Why D wrong: The fetal pole is not the first sign listed; the gestational sac is."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"On transvaginal scan (TVS), at what gestational age is the gestational sac first seen?","options":["4 weeks","5 weeks","6 weeks","7 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows the gestational sac is seen at 4 weeks on TVS.  Why B wrong: 5 weeks is when the yolk sac appears on TVS.  Why C wrong: 6 weeks is later than the gestational-sac timing.  Why D wrong: 7 weeks is too late for first gestational-sac appearance."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"On transvaginal scan (TVS), at what gestational age is the yolk sac seen?","options":["4 weeks","5 weeks","6 weeks","7 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows the yolk sac is seen at 5 weeks on TVS.  Why A wrong: 4 weeks corresponds to the gestational sac.  Why C wrong: 6 weeks is later than yolk sac appearance.  Why D wrong: 7 weeks is too late."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"On transvaginal scan (TVS), cardiac activity is seen at which gestational age?","options":["4 weeks","5–6 weeks","8 weeks","10 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows cardiac activity is seen at 5–6 weeks on TVS.  Why A wrong: 4 weeks is gestational sac timing.  Why C wrong: 8 weeks is later than the stated cardiac activity window.  Why D wrong: 10 weeks is too late."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"For each USG sign, how much later does it appear on transabdominal scan (TAS) compared to TVS?","options":["Same time","Approximately 1 week later","Approximately 2 weeks later","Approximately 1 month later"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"+ 1 week\" for TAS versus TVS for each sign (gestational sac, yolk sac, cardiac activity).  Why A wrong: TAS lags TVS, not the same time.  Why C wrong: The lag is 1 week, not 2 weeks.  Why D wrong: The lag is 1 week, not a month."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Cardiac activity on USG appears in which week of gestational age (earliest seen)?","options":["3 weeks","5 weeks","7 weeks","9 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state cardiac activity appears in the 5th week of gestational age and can be earliest seen at 5 weeks.  Why A wrong: 3 weeks is too early for cardiac activity.  Why C wrong: 7 weeks is later than the earliest appearance.  Why D wrong: 9 weeks is much later."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"The gestational sac on USG can be seen in which of the following conditions?","options":["Only intrauterine pregnancy","Only ectopic pregnancy","Both intrauterine and ectopic pregnancy","Neither, it is only seen after delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the gestational sac can be seen in both intrauterine pregnancy and ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: It is not limited to intrauterine pregnancy.  Why B wrong: It is not limited to ectopic pregnancy.  Why D wrong: It is seen in early pregnancy, not after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"In an intrauterine pregnancy, the true gestational sac is located where?","options":["Eccentrically on one side","In the centre of the uterine cavity","Outside the uterus","In the cervix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a true gestational sac (intrauterine pregnancy) is located eccentrically on one side.  Why B wrong: A centrally located sac suggests a pseudo (ectopic-associated) sac.  Why C wrong: An intrauterine sac is within, not outside, the uterus.  Why D wrong: The cervix location is not the described intrauterine finding."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"In ectopic pregnancy, the pseudo gestational sac seen in the uterus is typically located:","options":["Eccentrically on one side","In the centre","In the fundus only","Attached to the cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the pseudo gestational sac (ectopic pregnancy) is located in the centre.  Why A wrong: Eccentric location suggests a true intrauterine sac.  Why C wrong: Fundus-only is not the described location.  Why D wrong: Cervical attachment is not described."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"A mean sac diameter of ≥ 25 mm with no cardiac activity on USG indicates which condition?","options":["Normal early pregnancy","Blighted ovum","Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The very important point states that a mean sac diameter ≥ 25 mm with no cardiac activity indicates a blighted ovum.  Why A wrong: This finding is abnormal, not a normal pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is defined by extrauterine implantation, not this sac-diameter criterion.  Why D wrong: Molar pregnancy has a characteristic \"snowstorm\" appearance, not this criterion."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"A blighted ovum represents a pregnancy that has stopped at which stage?","options":["Stage of fertilized ovum","Stage of viable fetus","Stage of implantation only","Stage of full term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explain a blighted ovum is a pregnancy that stopped at the stage of the fertilized ova (empty gestational sac with no embryo).  Why B wrong: There is no viable fetus in a blighted ovum.  Why C wrong: It is described as arrest at the fertilized ovum stage, not merely implantation.  Why D wrong: A blighted ovum does not progress to full term."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"A pregnant woman reports that eating a biscuit before getting out of bed helps her symptoms. This advice is specifically given for which minor ailment?","options":["Backache","Leg cramps","Morning sickness (nausea/vomiting)","Heartburn"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Eating a biscuit before getting out of bed is advice for nausea/vomiting (morning sickness).  Why A wrong: Backache advice is calcium, Vit D, and postural measures.  Why B wrong: Leg-cramp advice is calcium, stockings, and knee extension/dorsiflexion.  Why D wrong: Heartburn advice includes antacids and head-up rest, not the biscuit measure."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Antenatal advice, Fetal Alcohol Syndrome, warning signs of pregnancy…","question":"Which type of transabdominal scan (TAS) variety is used specifically as a targeted scan for fetal anomaly?","options":["Level I / Routine USG","Level II (TIFFA)","Doppler scan","Nuchal translucency scan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes divide TAS into 2 varieties — Level I (Routine) and Level II (TIFFA), where Level II is the targeted anomaly scan.  Why A wrong: Level I is the routine scan, not the targeted anomaly scan.  Why C wrong: Doppler is not one of the two TAS varieties listed.  Why D wrong: Nuchal translucency is not listed as one of the two TAS varieties here."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"How many methods are used clinically to determine gestational age as per the notes?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state there are 2 methods to know gestational age — Clinical method and USG.  Why A wrong: Only one method is incomplete; two are listed.  Why C wrong: Three methods are not described.  Why D wrong: Four methods are not described."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"At 36 weeks of pregnancy, the fundus of the uterus is at the level of which anatomical landmark?","options":["Umbilicus","Xiphisternum","Pubic symphysis","Midway between pubic symphysis and umbilicus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state at 36 weeks the uterus is at the level of the xiphisternum.  Why A wrong: The umbilicus corresponds to earlier weeks (around 20–24 weeks), not 36.  Why C wrong: The pubic symphysis is the lowest landmark, corresponding to early pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Midway between pubic symphysis and umbilicus corresponds to about 16 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"According to the fundal-height diagram, at which gestational age is the uterus 2/3rd of the way between the umbilicus and the xiphisternum?","options":["28 weeks","32 weeks","36 weeks","40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram maps 32 weeks to \"2/3rd between umbilicus and xiphisternum.\"  Why A wrong: 28 weeks is lower on the abdomen.  Why C wrong: 36 weeks is at the xiphisternum itself.  Why D wrong: 40 weeks fundal height drops back down (equal to ~22 weeks) due to engagement."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"In the fundal-height diagram, which gestational age corresponds to \"1/3rd between umbilicus and xiphisternum\"?","options":["24 weeks","28 weeks","32 weeks","36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 28 weeks maps to 1/3rd of the distance between umbilicus and xiphisternum.  Why A wrong: 24 weeks corresponds to the upper border of the umbilicus.  Why C wrong: 32 weeks is 2/3rd of the way up.  Why D wrong: 36 weeks is at the xiphisternum."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"According to the diagram, the fundus reaches the upper border of the umbilicus at approximately how many weeks?","options":["20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks","32 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram maps 24 weeks to the upper border of the umbilicus.  Why A wrong: 20 weeks corresponds to the lower border of the umbilicus.  Why C wrong: 28 weeks is 1/3rd above the umbilicus.  Why D wrong: 32 weeks is 2/3rd toward the xiphisternum."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"In the fundal-height diagram, which gestational age corresponds to the lower border of the umbilicus?","options":["16 weeks","20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram maps 20 weeks to the lower border of the umbilicus.  Why A wrong: 16 weeks is midway between pubic symphysis and umbilicus.  Why C wrong: 24 weeks is the upper border of the umbilicus.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is above the umbilicus."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"At approximately 16 weeks of pregnancy, the fundus of the uterus is located at which landmark?","options":["Upper border of umbilicus","Xiphisternum","Midway between pubic symphysis and umbilicus","Lower border of umbilicus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram maps 16 weeks to midway between the pubic symphysis and umbilicus.  Why A wrong: The upper border of the umbilicus corresponds to 24 weeks.  Why B wrong: The xiphisternum corresponds to 36 weeks.  Why D wrong: The lower border of the umbilicus corresponds to 20 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"As the uterus rises to the xiphisternum at 36 weeks, what is the immediate mechanical consequence described in the notes?","options":["It pulls the diaphragm down","It pushes the diaphragm up, causing respiratory discomfort","It compresses the bladder only","It has no effect on breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus at the xiphisternum pushes the diaphragm up, so the patient has a lot of respiratory discomfort.  Why A wrong: The diaphragm is pushed up, not down.  Why C wrong: The described effect is on the diaphragm/respiration, not solely the bladder.  Why D wrong: It clearly causes respiratory discomfort."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"After 36 weeks of pregnancy, the head of the fetus descends into the pelvis. This process is called:","options":["Lightening","Engagement","Effacement","Crowning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"After 36 weeks: The head of fetus goes down into the pelvis (Engagement).\"  Why A wrong: Lightening is the mother's sensation of relief that follows, not the descent itself.  Why C wrong: Effacement is thinning of the cervix, not head descent.  Why D wrong: Crowning is appearance of the head at the vulva during delivery."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"At 40 weeks of pregnancy, the height of the uterus becomes approximately equal to its height at which earlier gestational age?","options":["16 weeks","20 weeks","22 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that at 40 weeks the height of the uterus is equal to the height of the uterus at 22 weeks (because the fetal head descends into the pelvis).  Why A wrong: 16 weeks is far lower.  Why B wrong: 20 weeks is the lower border of umbilicus, not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is higher than the 40-week fundal height."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"The sensation of relief a mother feels in late pregnancy when the fetal head descends and respiratory discomfort decreases is known as:","options":["Quickening","Lightening / WELCOME sign","Engagement","Braxton Hicks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label this \"Lightening / WELCOME sign,\" occurring at 38–40 weeks (late third trimester).  Why A wrong: Quickening is the first perception of fetal movements (earlier in pregnancy).  Why C wrong: Engagement is the physical descent of the head, not the maternal sensation of feeling light.  Why D wrong: Braxton Hicks are false/practice contractions, unrelated to this sensation."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"During which period of pregnancy does Lightening (WELCOME sign) occur?","options":["12–16 weeks","20–24 weeks","28–32 weeks","38–40 weeks (late third trimester)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify Lightening occurs at 38–40 weeks, in the late third trimester.  Why A wrong: This is first trimester/early second trimester.  Why B wrong: This is mid-pregnancy.  Why C wrong: This precedes the descent-related lightening."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which USG parameter is used to detect gestational age in the 1st trimester?","options":["Crown Rump Length (CRL)","Biparietal diameter","Femur length","Abdominal circumference"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists CRL as the 1st-trimester USG parameter for gestational age.  Why B wrong: Biparietal diameter (head circumference) is the 2nd-trimester parameter.  Why C wrong: Femur length is the 3rd-trimester parameter.  Why D wrong: Abdominal circumference is used for growth assessment, not primarily gestational age dating."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which USG parameter is used to detect gestational age in the 2nd trimester?","options":["Crown Rump Length","Biparietal diameter (Head circumference)","Femur length","Abdominal circumference"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Biparietal diameter (Head circumference) as the 2nd-trimester parameter.  Why A wrong: CRL is the 1st-trimester parameter.  Why C wrong: Femur length is the 3rd-trimester parameter.  Why D wrong: Abdominal circumference is a growth parameter."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which USG parameter is used to detect gestational age in the 3rd trimester?","options":["Crown Rump Length","Biparietal diameter","Femur length","Nuchal translucency"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Femur length as the 3rd-trimester parameter for gestational age.  Why A wrong: CRL is the 1st-trimester parameter.  Why B wrong: BPD is the 2nd-trimester parameter.  Why D wrong: Nuchal translucency is a marker for chromosomal defects, not routine dating."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Regarding accuracy of USG dating, which statement is correct?","options":["The later the USG, the more accurate the gestational age","The earlier the USG in pregnancy, the more accurately it tells about gestational age","USG timing does not affect dating accuracy","USG can only date pregnancy in the third trimester"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"The earlier you do the USG in Pregnancy – More accurate it tells about gestational age.\"  Why A wrong: Later USG is less accurate for dating.  Why C wrong: Timing strongly affects accuracy.  Why D wrong: USG dates best in the first trimester."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which is the best USG parameter to detect fetal age / gestational age?","options":["Femur length","Abdominal circumference","Crown rump length","Biparietal diameter"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best USG parameter to detect fetal age/gestational age is Crown Rump Length.  Why A wrong: Femur length is a 3rd-trimester dating parameter, not the best overall.  Why B wrong: Abdominal circumference is for growth.  Why D wrong: BPD is a 2nd-trimester parameter, less accurate than CRL."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"The best time in pregnancy to detect gestational age by USG is:","options":["1st Trimester","2nd Trimester","3rd Trimester","During labour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best time to detect gestational age is the 1st Trimester.  Why B wrong: Second trimester is less accurate for dating.  Why C wrong: Third trimester is least accurate for dating.  Why D wrong: Dating is not done during labour."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Crown Rump Length (CRL) is measured from which two points?","options":["Head to heel","Vertex to coccyx (coccyrax)","Crown to knee","Occiput to sacrum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state CRL is measured from Vertex to coccyx (\"corcyrax\").  Why A wrong: Head to heel is the crown-heel length, not CRL.  Why C wrong: Crown to knee is not a defined measurement here.  Why D wrong: Occiput to sacrum is not the CRL landmark given."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which formula converts CRL into weeks of pregnancy according to the notes?","options":["CRL + 6.5 = weeks of pregnancy","CRL + 42 = weeks of pregnancy","CRL – 6.5 = weeks of pregnancy","CRL × 6.5 = weeks of pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"CRL + 6.5 = weeks of pregnancy.\"  Why B wrong: CRL + 42 gives days of pregnancy, not weeks.  Why C wrong: The operation is addition of 6.5, not subtraction.  Why D wrong: The relationship is additive, not multiplicative."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"According to the notes, CRL + 42 equals:","options":["Weeks of pregnancy","Days of pregnancy","Fetal weight in grams","Months of pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"CRL + 42 = Days of pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: CRL + 6.5 gives weeks.  Why C wrong: This formula does not compute weight.  Why D wrong: It gives days, not months."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"A Level 1 USG (routine scan) is characterized by which of the following?","options":["It is a targeted scan detecting all congenital malformations","It is a normal USG that can detect only very few congenital malformations","It can detect only cardiac anomalies","It cannot detect any malformation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe Level 1 USG as a normal USG that can detect very few congenital malformations.  Why A wrong: Detecting all malformations via targeted scan is Level 2 USG.  Why C wrong: It is not limited to cardiac anomalies; it detects a few NTDs.  Why D wrong: It can detect a few malformations, not none."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"A Level 2 USG is also known as a:","options":["Routine scan","Targeted scan that can detect all congenital malformations","Doppler scan","Nuchal scan only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Level 2 USG → Targeted Scan → can detect all congenital malformation.  Why A wrong: Routine scan is Level 1.  Why C wrong: Doppler is not the definition given.  Why D wrong: It is not limited to the nuchal scan; it detects all malformations."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which of the following congenital malformations can a Level 1 USG detect? (Select all that apply)","options":["Anencephaly","Spina Bifida","Limb Reduction defect","Complex congenital heart defect","Diaphragmatic hernia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Anencephaly, Spina Bifida, and Limb Reduction defect (all neural tube defects/gross defects) as detectable on Level 1 USG.  Why D wrong: Detailed cardiac defects require the targeted Level 2 scan.  Why E wrong: Diaphragmatic hernia is not listed among Level 1 detectable defects; detailed anomalies need Level 2."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Anencephaly, Spina Bifida, and Limb Reduction defects listed as detectable on Level 1 USG are grouped as which category of defect?","options":["Neural Tube defects","Cardiac defects","Abdominal wall defects","Chromosomal defects"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes bracket Anencephaly, Spina Bifida and Limb Reduction defect as Neural Tube defects.  Why B wrong: These are not cardiac defects.  Why C wrong: They are not abdominal wall defects.  Why D wrong: They are structural neural tube defects, not chromosomal."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Increased Nuchal Translucency (seen in the 1st term) on USG is associated with which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Down Syndrome","Turner Syndrome","Congenital Heart defect","Spina Bifida","Anencephaly"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Down Syndrome, Turner Syndrome, and Congenital Heart defect against increased nuchal translucency.  Why D wrong: Spina Bifida is associated with lemon/banana signs, not nuchal translucency.  Why E wrong: Anencephaly is associated with frog-eye/Mickey-mouse sign, not nuchal translucency."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"During which trimester is the increased nuchal translucency USG sign classically detected?","options":["1st term (1st trimester)","2nd trimester","3rd trimester","During labour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify \"Increased Nuchal Translucency (1st term).\"  Why B wrong: The absent nasal bone/short femur markers are 2nd-trimester findings.  Why C wrong: It is a first-trimester marker.  Why D wrong: Not a labour finding."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Absent nasal bone on USG is a marker for which congenital malformation?","options":["Spina Bifida","Down syndrome","Anencephaly","Turner syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes match \"Absent Nasal bone\" to Down syndrome.  Why A wrong: Spina Bifida shows lemon and banana signs.  Why C wrong: Anencephaly shows frog-eye/Mickey-mouse signs.  Why D wrong: Turner syndrome is linked with increased nuchal translucency, not specifically absent nasal bone."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which USG findings are grouped as 2nd-trimester markers of Down syndrome in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Short femur (shoot femur)","Short humerus","Increased nuchal skin fold thickness","Lemon sign","Banana sign"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes bracket short femur, short humerus, and increased nuchal skin fold thickness as 2nd-trimester markers under absent nasal bone (Down syndrome).  Why D wrong: Lemon sign indicates Spina Bifida.  Why E wrong: Banana sign indicates Spina Bifida."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"The \"Frog eye sign\" and \"Mickey mouse sign\" on USG are characteristic of which condition?","options":["Spina Bifida","Anencephaly","Down syndrome","Turner syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes and figure (\"Figure: Anencephaly\") link the Frog eye sign / Mickey mouse sign to Anencephaly.  Why A wrong: Spina Bifida shows the lemon and banana signs.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome shows absent nasal bone / short femur / nuchal findings.  Why D wrong: Turner syndrome is associated with increased nuchal translucency."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"The \"Lemon sign\" and \"Banana sign\" on USG are characteristic of which condition?","options":["Anencephaly","Spina Bifida","Down syndrome","Congenital heart defect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly label the lemon sign and banana sign under \"SPINA BIFIDA.\"  Why A wrong: Anencephaly shows frog-eye/Mickey-mouse sign.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome shows absent nasal bone and nuchal markers.  Why D wrong: CHD is linked to increased nuchal translucency, not lemon/banana signs."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"For assessing the GROWTH of the fetus on USG, there is essentially only one parameter, which is:","options":["Femur length","Biparietal diameter","Abdominal circumference (fetal)","Crown rump length"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that for growth of the fetus on USG there is only one parameter — Abdominal circumference (fetal), usable in any trimester.  Why A wrong: Femur length dates the 3rd trimester.  Why B wrong: BPD is a 2nd-trimester dating parameter.  Why D wrong: CRL is the best dating parameter, not growth."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"The fetal abdominal circumference can be used to assess growth during which trimester(s)?","options":["Only 1st trimester","Only 2nd trimester","Only 3rd trimester","Any trimester"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state abdominal circumference can be used \"In any Trimester.\"  Why A/B/C wrong: The notes do not restrict it to a single trimester; it applies to any trimester."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which of the following are formulas used to calculate the weight of the fetus? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hadlock formula","Shephard formula","Johnson formula","Naegele's rule","McDonald's rule"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Hadlock, Shephard, and Johnson formulas for calculating fetal weight.  Why D wrong: Naegele's rule calculates the expected date of delivery, not fetal weight.  Why E wrong: McDonald's rule estimates gestational age from fundal height, not fetal weight."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"What is the best USG parameter to assess fetal growth?","options":["Femur length","Abdominal circumference","Biparietal diameter","Crown rump length"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best USG parameter to assess growth is Abdominal Circumference.  Why A wrong: Femur length is a dating parameter.  Why C wrong: BPD is a 2nd-trimester dating parameter.  Why D wrong: CRL assesses age, not growth."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"What is the best USG parameter to detect fetal macrosomia?","options":["Abdominal circumference","Femur length","Nuchal translucency","Crown rump length"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best USG parameter to detect macrosomia is abdominal circumference.  Why B wrong: Femur length is used for dating, not macrosomia detection.  Why C wrong: Nuchal translucency screens for chromosomal defects.  Why D wrong: CRL assesses gestational age."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which hormone is responsible for fetal growth (per the \"Lets Revise\" point)?","options":["Insulin and insulin-like growth factors","Human placental lactogen only","Thyroxine only","Cortisol only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the hormone responsible for fetal growth is Insulin & Insulin-like growth factors.  Why B wrong: hPL is not the answer given.  Why C wrong: Thyroxine is not the stated hormone.  Why D wrong: Cortisol is not the stated hormone."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Anencephaly is classified as which type of defect?","options":["Abdominal wall defect","Neural Tube Defect","Cardiac defect","Chromosomal defect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes open the Anencephaly section with \"It is a neural Tube Defect.\"  Why A wrong: It is not an abdominal wall defect.  Why C wrong: It is not a cardiac defect.  Why D wrong: It is a structural neural tube defect, not chromosomal."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"What is the MAIN problem in anencephaly?","options":["Absent limbs","Skull/cranial vault of the baby is absent with the brain exposed, so such babies cannot survive","Absent kidneys","Absent heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the main problem is that the skull/cranial vault is absent (brain of baby is exposed) and such babies cannot survive.  Why A wrong: Limb reduction is a different NTD.  Why C wrong: Renal agenesis is not the main problem described.  Why D wrong: Cardiac absence is not the main problem described."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which of the following are the associated problems in anencephaly listed in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Adrenal glands of the fetus are absent","Swallowing defect","Absent liver","Absent lungs","Absent kidneys"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list two associated problems — absent fetal adrenal glands and a swallowing defect.  Why C wrong: Absent liver is not listed.  Why D wrong: Absent lungs is not listed.  Why E wrong: Absent kidneys is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"In anencephaly, the swallowing defect (inability of the fetus to swallow amniotic fluid) leads directly to which of the following?","options":["Oligohydramnios","Polyhydramnios","Anhydramnios","No change in amniotic fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show the swallowing-defect pathway leading to Polyhydramnios (excess amniotic fluid, because the fetus cannot swallow it).  Why A wrong: Reduced fluid (oligohydramnios) is the opposite.  Why C wrong: Anhydramnios (absent fluid) is not what is described.  Why D wrong: The fluid clearly increases."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"According to the anencephaly flowchart, polyhydramnios can lead to which sequence of complications?","options":["Preterm labor → PROM → Postpartum Haemorrhage","Post-term pregnancy only","Gestational diabetes","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flowchart lists 1. Polyhydramnios → 2. Preterm labor → 3. PROM → 4. Post partum → 5. Haemorrhage.  Why B wrong: Post-term pregnancy arises from the separate DHEA/estrogen pathway, not from polyhydramnios.  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes is not in this pathway.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"In anencephaly, absent fetal adrenal glands mean DHEA-S is not formed. What is the downstream consequence per the notes?","options":["Placenta produces excess estrogen","Placenta cannot form estrogen, so estrogen cannot signal the uterus to contract, leading to post-term pregnancy","The uterus contracts prematurely","Progesterone rises abnormally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes trace: DHEA-S not formed → Placenta cannot form estrogen → Estrogen cannot signal the uterus to contract → can lead to Post-term pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Estrogen production falls, it does not rise.  Why C wrong: Lack of estrogen signal delays contractions, leading to post-term (not premature) labour.  Why D wrong: The pathway concerns estrogen deficiency, not progesterone excess."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Because the placenta cannot signal the uterus to contract in anencephaly, the pregnancy can progress to which outcome?","options":["Preterm pregnancy","Post-term pregnancy","Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the estrogen-signalling failure \"Can lead to Post-term pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: Post-term (prolonged), not preterm, results.  Why C wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is unrelated to this mechanism.  Why D wrong: Molar pregnancy is unrelated to this mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Anencephaly is most common in which sex of the fetus?","options":["Male fetus","Female fetus","Equal in both","Only in twins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state anencephaly is \"Most common in female fetus.\"  Why A wrong: It is more common in females, not males.  Why C wrong: There is a stated female predominance.  Why D wrong: There is no twin restriction stated."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"If a woman has had 1 previous neural tube defect (NTD) baby, what is the recurrence risk of NTD stated in the notes?","options":["2%","5%","10%","25%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"If previous 1 NTD baby – 5%.\"  Why A wrong: 2% is not stated.  Why C wrong: 10% is the risk after 2 previous NTD babies.  Why D wrong: 25% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"If a woman has had 2 previous NTD babies, what is the recurrence risk of NTD?","options":["5%","10%","15%","20%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"If previous 2 NTD baby = 10%.\"  Why A wrong: 5% is the risk after 1 previous NTD baby.  Why C wrong: 15% is not stated.  Why D wrong: 20% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Because of the recurrence risk, what is recommended for females with a history of a previous neural tube defect baby?","options":["Reduce the dose of folic acid","Stop folic acid entirely","Increase the dose of folic acid","Give iron only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the dose of folic acid is increased in females who have a history of a previous NTD baby.  Why A wrong: The dose is increased, not reduced.  Why B wrong: Folic acid is continued at a higher dose, not stopped.  Why D wrong: The intervention is folic acid, not iron alone."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"In the current (\"New concept\") approach, screening for anencephaly is done by:","options":["Maternal blood AFP levels","USG (level 1)","Amniocentesis","Chorionic villus sampling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"New concept\" states Screening = USG (level 1).  Why A wrong: Maternal blood AFP screening is the \"Old concept\" (not done now).  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis is a diagnostic test, not first-line screening.  Why D wrong: CVS is not mentioned as the screening tool here."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"In the \"New concept\" approach, diagnosis of anencephaly is confirmed by:","options":["Level 1 USG","Level 2 USG","Maternal AFP","Fundal height"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"New concept\" states Diagnosis = (Level 2) USG.  Why A wrong: Level 1 is used for screening.  Why C wrong: Maternal AFP is the old-concept screening tool.  Why D wrong: Fundal height does not diagnose anencephaly."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"The \"Old concept\" (not done now) for anencephaly screening relied on which measurement?","options":["Maternal blood levels of AFP","Fetal heart rate","Amniotic fluid index","Biparietal diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Old concept (NOT DONE NOW)\" states Screening = Maternal Blood Levels of AFP.  Why B wrong: FHR is not the old-concept screen.  Why C wrong: AFI is not the old-concept screen.  Why D wrong: BPD is a dating parameter, not the old AFP screen."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Why does the old-concept maternal AFP screen detect NTDs?","options":["Because AFP falls in mother if the baby has NTD","Because if the baby has any NTD, the level of AFP will be raised in the mother also","Because AFP is produced only by the mother's liver","Because AFP is unrelated to NTD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explain: \"Because if baby has any NTD, then level of AFP will be raised in Mother also.\"  Why A wrong: AFP rises (not falls) in the mother with an NTD baby.  Why C wrong: AFP is produced by the fetus, not the mother's liver.  Why D wrong: AFP is directly related to NTD detection."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"The maternal AFP test can be done at what gestational age, and what is the best time?","options":["Test ≥ 15 weeks; best time 16–18 weeks","Test ≥ 10 weeks; best time 11–13 weeks","Test ≥ 20 weeks; best time 22–24 weeks","Test at any time; best time 8–10 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the test is done at ≥ 15 weeks with best time 16–18 weeks.  Why B wrong: 10 weeks / 11–13 weeks is not stated.  Why C wrong: 20 weeks / 22–24 weeks is not stated.  Why D wrong: There is a specific ≥15-week threshold, not \"any time.\""},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which is the diagnostic (confirmatory) test noted alongside the old AFP-based approach?","options":["Amniocentesis","Chorionic villus sampling","Cordocentesis","Cell-free DNA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Diagnostic: Amniocentesis.\"  Why B wrong: CVS is not the diagnostic test named here.  Why C wrong: Cordocentesis is not named.  Why D wrong: Cell-free DNA is not named."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Alpha Feto Protein (AFP) is best classified as which type of molecule?","options":["Steroid hormone","Glycoprotein produced by the fetus","Polysaccharide","Fatty acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state AFP is a glycoprotein produced by the fetus.  Why A wrong: It is not a steroid hormone.  Why C wrong: It is a glycoprotein, not a polysaccharide.  Why D wrong: It is not a fatty acid."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which structure produces AFP initially in the fetus?","options":["Liver","Yolk sac","Placenta","Kidney"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state AFP is produced initially by the Yolk sac.  Why A wrong: The liver produces AFP later, not initially.  Why C wrong: The placenta is not the AFP producer described.  Why D wrong: The kidney is not the source."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which structure produces AFP later in fetal development?","options":["Yolk sac","Liver","Adrenal gland","Spleen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state AFP is produced later by the Liver.  Why A wrong: The yolk sac is the initial (earlier) source.  Why C wrong: The adrenal gland is not the source.  Why D wrong: The spleen is not the source."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"In a fetus with a neural tube defect, what happens to AFP levels, and what is the pathway by which it reaches the mother?","options":["AFP falls; passes via placenta directly","AFP increases; passes into fetal urine → amniotic fluid → mother's blood","AFP increases; passes only via umbilical vein","AFP stays normal; no maternal transfer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes trace Fetus = NTD → ↑AFP → Urine = Amniotic fluid → Mother Blood.  Why A wrong: AFP increases, not falls.  Why C wrong: The described route is via urine and amniotic fluid, not solely the umbilical vein.  Why D wrong: AFP rises and is transferred to the mother."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Where are the MAXIMUM levels of AFP seen?","options":["Maternal blood","Amniotic fluid","Fetal blood","Maternal urine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maximum levels of AFP are seen in Fetal Blood.  Why A wrong: Maternal blood has lower levels (detectable only from ≥15 weeks).  Why B wrong: Amniotic fluid is an intermediate, not the maximum.  Why D wrong: Maternal urine is not where maximal AFP is seen."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"From what gestational age can AFP be detected in maternal blood, and what is the best time?","options":["From ≥ 15 weeks; best time 16–18 weeks","From ≥ 8 weeks; best time 9–11 weeks","From ≥ 20 weeks; best time 24–26 weeks","From ≥ 12 weeks; best time 13–14 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state AFP can be detected in maternal blood ≥ 15 weeks, best time 16–18 weeks.  Why B wrong: 8 weeks is too early per the notes.  Why C wrong: 20/24–26 weeks is not the stated window.  Why D wrong: 12/13–14 weeks is not the stated window."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"What is the unit of measurement for AFP as stated in the notes?","options":["Multiple of Median (MOM)","Milligrams per deciliter","International units per liter","Nanograms per milliliter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the unit of measurement is \"Multiple of Meridian = MOM\" (i.e., Multiple of Median, MoM).  Why B wrong: mg/dL is not the stated unit.  Why C wrong: IU/L is not the stated unit.  Why D wrong: ng/mL is not the stated unit."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"What is the normal AFP level in maternal blood, and what value is considered abnormal?","options":["Normal < 2.5 MOM; abnormal ≥ 2.5 MOM","Normal < 1.0 MOM; abnormal ≥ 1.0 MOM","Normal < 5.0 MOM; abnormal ≥ 5.0 MOM","Normal < 0.5 MOM; abnormal ≥ 0.5 MOM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Normal mother Blood = < 2.5 MOM and Abnormal = ≥ 2.5 MOM.  Why B wrong: 1.0 MOM is not the stated cutoff.  Why C wrong: 5.0 MOM is not the stated cutoff.  Why D wrong: 0.5 MOM is not the stated cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"In a neural tube defect / anencephaly, what happens to AFP levels?","options":["Levels of AFP are decreased","Levels of AFP are increased","AFP levels are unchanged","AFP becomes undetectable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"In NTD: levels of AFP are increased.\"  Why A wrong: AFP is increased, not decreased, in NTD.  Why C wrong: Levels change (increase) in NTD.  Why D wrong: AFP is elevated, not undetectable."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"What is the best biochemical marker to diagnose anencephaly (per the notes)?","options":["Alpha feto protein","Acetylcholinesterase","Beta hCG","Estriol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best biochemical marker to diagnose anencephaly is ACETYLCHOLINESTERASE.  Why A wrong: AFP is a screening/associated marker, but the best specific biochemical marker named is acetylcholinesterase.  Why C wrong: Beta hCG is not named for this.  Why D wrong: Estriol is not named for this."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which of the following conditions are associated with INCREASED AFP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Neural Tube defect","Abdominal wall defect","Pilonidal Sinus","Sacrococcygeal Teratoma","Molar pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Neural Tube defect, Abdominal wall defect, Pilonidal Sinus, and Sacrococcygeal Teratoma under \"AFP increased.\"  Why E wrong: Molar pregnancy is listed under AFP decreased."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Which of the following conditions are associated with DECREASED AFP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Molar Pregnancy","Abortion","Down Syndrome","Maternal Diabetes","Neural tube defect"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list under \"AFP decreased\" (mnemonic M-A-D): Molar Pregnancy, Abortion, Down Syndrome, and Diabetes (mother).  Why E wrong: Neural tube defect causes increased AFP, not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"The mnemonic \"MAD\" for conditions with decreased AFP stands for which of the following?","options":["Molar pregnancy, Abortion, Down syndrome (and Diabetes in mother)","Macrosomia, Anencephaly, Diabetes","Molar pregnancy, Anencephaly, Down syndrome","Meningocele, Abortion, Diabetes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give M – Molar Pregnancy, A – Abortion, D – Down Syndrome plus Diabetes (mother) for decreased AFP.  Why B wrong: Anencephaly raises AFP, not lowers it.  Why C wrong: Anencephaly does not lower AFP.  Why D wrong: Meningocele (an NTD) raises AFP."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Anencephaly can be diagnosed by which type of USG?","options":["Only Level 2 (targeted) USG","Level 1 USG / Routine USG","Doppler USG only","Transvaginal USG only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Anencephaly can be diagnosed by Level 1 USG / Routine USG.\"  Why A wrong: Level 1/routine USG is sufficient; Level 2 is not required for anencephaly.  Why C wrong: Doppler is not required.  Why D wrong: A transvaginal scan is not specified as the only means."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"What is the EARLIEST gestational age at which anencephaly can be detected on USG?","options":["10 weeks","12 weeks","14 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state anencephaly can be detected earliest on USG at 14 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 10 weeks is too early per the notes.  Why B wrong: 12 weeks is not the stated earliest time.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks is later than the earliest detection point."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"According to the notes, the best time at which anencephaly can be detected on USG is:","options":["14 weeks of pregnancy","18 weeks of pregnancy","22 weeks of pregnancy","28 weeks of pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Best time Anencephaly can be detected on USG – 14 weeks of pregnancy.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The notes specify 14 weeks, not 18/22/28 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"The USG sign(s) of anencephaly listed in the \"USG for Anencephaly\" section include: (Select all that apply)","options":["Mickey mouse sign","Frog eye sign","Lemon sign","Banana sign","Nuchal translucency"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the USG sign of anencephaly is the Mickey mouse sign and frog eye sign.  Why C wrong: Lemon sign is a Spina Bifida sign.  Why D wrong: Banana sign is a Spina Bifida sign.  Why E wrong: Nuchal translucency is a chromosomal-defect marker."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Prevention of anencephaly / NTD is achieved by giving which supplement to the mother?","options":["Iron","Folic acid","Calcium","Vitamin D"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state prevention of anencephaly/NTD is achieved \"By giving Folic acid to mother.\"  Why A wrong: Iron does not prevent NTDs.  Why C wrong: Calcium does not prevent NTDs.  Why D wrong: Vitamin D is not the preventive agent for NTDs."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"What is the PROPHYLACTIC dose of folic acid to prevent NTD in a pregnant female who has NEVER given birth to an NTD baby?","options":["400 mcg/day","4 mg/day","5 mg/day","1 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the prophylactic dose as 400 mcg/day for a female who has never had an NTD baby.  Why B wrong: 4 mg/day is the therapeutic dose for prevention of recurrence.  Why C wrong: 5 mg/day is not the value stated.  Why D wrong: 1 mg/day is not the value stated."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"What is the THERAPEUTIC dose of folic acid to prevent RECURRENCE of NTD in a woman with a prior NTD baby?","options":["400 mcg/day","4 mg/day","40 mcg/day","400 mg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the therapeutic dose as 4 mg/day to prevent recurrence of NTD.  Why A wrong: 400 mcg/day is the prophylactic (first-time) dose.  Why C wrong: 40 mcg/day is not stated.  Why D wrong: 400 mg/day is an incorrect unit/value."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Ideally, when should folic acid be started and for how long continued relative to conception?","options":["Started 1 month before conception and continued for 3 months after conception","Started at delivery","Started only in the third trimester","Started 6 months before and continued until delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state folic acid should ideally be given 1 month before conception and continued for 3 months after conception.  Why B wrong: Starting at delivery is far too late to prevent NTDs.  Why C wrong: The neural tube closes early, so third-trimester start is too late.  Why D wrong: The stated timing is 1 month before and 3 months after, not 6 months before to delivery."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"If a female does not seek advice before planning pregnancy, when should folic acid be started (per the notes)?","options":["Only after the first trimester","From the day pregnancy is diagnosed","Only if an NTD is detected","It should not be started at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if the female does not come for advice before planning pregnancy, folic acid should be started from the day the pregnancy is diagnosed.  Why A wrong: It should begin immediately at diagnosis, not delayed to after the first trimester.  Why C wrong: It is given routinely, not only if an NTD is found.  Why D wrong: It should be started."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"The figure labelled \"Anoncephaly\" (Anencephaly) on USG is being pointed to alongside which sign?","options":["Lemon sign","Banana sign","Mickey mouse sign and frog eye sign","Nuchal fold sign"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The figure of anencephaly is annotated with \"Mickey mouse sign and frog eye sign.\"  Why A wrong: Lemon sign is shown separately under Spina Bifida.  Why B wrong: Banana sign is shown separately under Spina Bifida.  Why D wrong: The nuchal fold sign belongs to the Down-syndrome markers."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"A 32-week pregnant woman reports increasing shortness of breath. Based on the notes, what is the most likely explanation, and what will relieve it later?","options":["Uterus at xiphisternum pushing the diaphragm up; relieved after engagement/lightening near term","Anemia; relieved by iron","Pneumonia; relieved by antibiotics","Asthma; relieved by inhalers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explain the rising uterus pushes the diaphragm up causing respiratory discomfort, which decreases after 36 weeks when the head engages and the uterus descends (lightening).  Why B wrong: Anemia is not the mechanism described in the notes.  Why C wrong: Pneumonia is not described.  Why D wrong: Asthma is not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"A fetus is diagnosed with anencephaly. Considering the associated absent adrenal glands, which pregnancy complication is the mother at increased risk of due to the estrogen-signalling pathway?","options":["Preterm labour","Post-term (prolonged) pregnancy","Gestational hypertension","Placental abruption"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Absent fetal adrenal glands → no DHEA-S → placenta cannot form estrogen → no signal for the uterus to contract → post-term pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Preterm labour arises from the separate polyhydramnios pathway, not the estrogen pathway.  Why C wrong: Gestational hypertension is not linked to this mechanism in the notes.  Why D wrong: Placental abruption is not the described outcome."},{"section":"Batch 029 · Methods to know gestational age, USG parameters per trimester…","question":"Increased nuchal skin fold thickness detected on second-trimester USG is a marker most associated with which condition?","options":["Spina Bifida","Anencephaly","Down syndrome","Abdominal wall defect"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Increased nuchal skin fold thickness is grouped with the 2nd-trimester Down syndrome markers (with short femur and short humerus).  Why A wrong: Spina Bifida shows lemon/banana signs.  Why B wrong: Anencephaly shows frog-eye/Mickey-mouse signs.  Why D wrong: Abdominal wall defect raises AFP but is not marked by nuchal skin fold thickness."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"What is the total number of chromosomes present in a normal human cell?","options":["23 chromosomes","44 chromosomes","46 chromosomes","48 chromosomes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A normal human cell has 46 chromosomes, arranged as 23 pairs.  Why A wrong: 23 is the number of chromosome pairs (or the haploid count in a gamete), not the total.  Why B wrong: 44 is only the number of autosomes.  Why D wrong: 48 is not the human chromosome number."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Human chromosomes are arranged into how many pairs?","options":["22 pairs","23 pairs","44 pairs","46 pairs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 46 chromosomes are grouped as 23 pairs.  Why A wrong: 22 pairs refers only to the autosome pairs.  Why C wrong: 44 is the count of individual autosomes, not pairs.  Why D wrong: 46 is the total chromosome number, not the number of pairs."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Of the 46 human chromosomes, how many are autosomes and how many are sex chromosomes?","options":["46 autosomes and 0 sex chromosomes","44 autosomes and 2 sex chromosomes","22 autosomes and 2 sex chromosomes","42 autosomes and 4 sex chromosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are 44 autosomes and 2 sex chromosomes (22 pairs of autosomes + 1 pair of sex chromosomes).  Why A wrong: Sex chromosomes are always present (2 of them).  Why C wrong: 22 is the number of autosome pairs, not individual autosomes.  Why D wrong: There are only 2 sex chromosomes, not 4."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"How many pairs of autosomes are present in a human cell?","options":["21 pairs","22 pairs","23 pairs","24 pairs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are 22 pairs of autosomes plus 1 pair of sex chromosomes.  Why A wrong: 21 pairs would give 42 autosomes, which is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 23 pairs is the total chromosome pair count including sex chromosomes.  Why D wrong: 24 pairs is not correct for humans."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"What is the sex chromosome complement of a normal human male?","options":["22 pairs + XX","22 pairs + XY","23 pairs + XY","22 pairs + YY"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Males have 22 pairs of autosomes plus XY sex chromosomes.  Why A wrong: XX is the female complement.  Why C wrong: 23 pairs plus XY would exceed the normal count.  Why D wrong: YY is not a normal human complement."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"What is the sex chromosome complement of a normal human female?","options":["22 pairs + XY","22 pairs + XX","22 pairs + X0","23 pairs + XX"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Females have 22 pairs of autosomes plus XX sex chromosomes.  Why A wrong: XY is the male complement.  Why C wrong: X0 (single X) is Turner syndrome (monosomy X), not a normal female.  Why D wrong: 23 pairs plus XX would exceed the normal count."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"The presence of 46 chromosomes in a human cell is referred to as which number?","options":["Haploid number","Diploid number","Triploid number","Aneuploid number"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 46 is called the diploid number.  Why A wrong: Haploid number is 23 (found in gametes).  Why C wrong: Triploid refers to 69 chromosomes.  Why D wrong: Aneuploid means an abnormal (defective) chromosome number, not the normal 46."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"All human cells are diploid EXCEPT which of the following?","options":["Somatic cells","Skin cells","Gametes","Muscle cells"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Gametes (sperm and ova) are haploid (23 chromosomes); all other body cells are diploid.  Why A wrong: Somatic cells are diploid.  Why B wrong: Skin cells are somatic and diploid.  Why D wrong: Muscle cells are somatic and diploid."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"How many chromosomes are present in a male gamete (sperm)?","options":["46","23","44","22"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The male gamete (sperm) carries 23 chromosomes (haploid).  Why A wrong: 46 is the diploid number of a somatic cell.  Why C wrong: 44 is the number of autosomes in a somatic cell.  Why D wrong: 22 is the autosome number in a gamete, not the total."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"How many chromosomes are present in a female gamete (ovum)?","options":["46","44","23","24"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The female gamete (ovum) carries 23 chromosomes (haploid).  Why A wrong: 46 is the diploid somatic number.  Why B wrong: 44 is the somatic autosome count.  Why D wrong: 24 is not correct for a human ovum."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"A student is asked to identify the diploid cells versus the haploid cells in the body. Which statement is correct?","options":["Sperm and ova are diploid, all other cells are haploid","Sperm and ova are haploid (23), all other body cells are diploid (46)","All human cells including gametes are diploid","Only sex chromosomes are haploid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gametes are haploid (23) while somatic (body) cells are diploid (46).  Why A wrong: It reverses the correct relationship.  Why C wrong: Gametes are the exception and are haploid.  Why D wrong: Ploidy refers to whole-cell chromosome number, not just sex chromosomes."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In a female karyotype, the sex chromosomes appear as which pair?","options":["XY","XX","X0","YY"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The female karyotype figure shows the sex chromosome pair as XX.  Why A wrong: XY is the male sex chromosome pair.  Why C wrong: X0 represents monosomy X (Turner syndrome).  Why D wrong: YY is not a viable human sex chromosome pair."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In a male karyotype figure, the sex chromosomes are labelled as which combination?","options":["XX","X (representing XY)","X0","Two X chromosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The male chromosome figure shows the sex chromosome pair as X (XY), distinguishing it from the female XX.  Why A wrong: XX is female.  Why C wrong: X0 is Turner syndrome monosomy.  Why D wrong: Two X chromosomes indicate a female."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"What does the term \"aneuploidy\" mean?","options":["Normal chromosome number","A defect (abnormality) in chromosome number","A defect in chromosome structure only","Doubling of the entire genome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aneuploidy means a defect in chromosome number.  Why A wrong: Aneuploidy is by definition an abnormal number, not normal.  Why C wrong: Aneuploidy concerns number, not structural rearrangement.  Why D wrong: Doubling of the genome (polyploidy) is a different concept."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Which of the following are recognised TYPES of aneuploidy listed in the notes?","options":["Trisomy, Monosomy, Triploid","Diploid, Haploid, Triploid","Deletion, Duplication, Inversion","Mosaicism, Translocation, Deletion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes classify aneuploidy types as Trisomy, Monosomy, and Triploid.  Why B wrong: Diploid and haploid are normal ploidy states, not aneuploidy types listed.  Why C wrong: These are structural chromosome abnormalities, not the numeric types listed here.  Why D wrong: These are structural/mosaic abnormalities, not the listed types."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"What is the defining feature of \"trisomy\"?","options":["One less chromosome than normal","One extra chromosome (total = 47)","Three complete sets of chromosomes","Absence of a sex chromosome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trisomy means one extra chromosome, so total number = 46 + 1 = 47.  Why A wrong: One less chromosome describes monosomy.  Why C wrong: Three complete sets describes triploidy (69).  Why D wrong: Absence of a sex chromosome describes monosomy X (Turner)."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"What is the total number of chromosomes in a cell with trisomy?","options":["45","46","47","69"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In trisomy there is one extra chromosome, giving 46 + 1 = 47.  Why A wrong: 45 is the count in monosomy (e.g., Turner).  Why B wrong: 46 is the normal diploid number.  Why D wrong: 69 is the triploid number."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"What is the defining feature of \"monosomy\"?","options":["One extra chromosome","One less chromosome","Two extra chromosomes","Triple set of chromosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monosomy means one less chromosome.  Why A wrong: One extra chromosome is trisomy.  Why C wrong: Two extra chromosomes is not monosomy.  Why D wrong: Triple set is triploidy."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Which syndrome is given as the classic example of monosomy (monosomy X)?","options":["Down syndrome","Turner syndrome — 45 (X0)","Edward syndrome","Patau syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Turner syndrome, 45 (X0), monosomy X, is the example of monosomy.  Why A wrong: Down syndrome is trisomy 21.  Why C wrong: Edward syndrome is trisomy 18.  Why D wrong: Patau syndrome is trisomy 13."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"What is the chromosome number in Turner syndrome?","options":["47","46","45","69"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Turner syndrome is monosomy X with 45 chromosomes (X0).  Why A wrong: 47 is a trisomy count.  Why B wrong: 46 is the normal diploid number.  Why D wrong: 69 is the triploid number."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In triploidy, how many chromosomes are present in the cell?","options":["46","47","69","92"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In triploidy the chromosome number is 69 (three haploid sets, 3 × 23).  Why A wrong: 46 is diploid.  Why B wrong: 47 is a trisomy count.  Why D wrong: 92 (tetraploidy) is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"According to the notes, which trisomy is described as the \"most common trisomy\"?","options":["Trisomy 13","Trisomy 18","Trisomy 21","Trisomy 16"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Trisomy 18 (Edward syndrome) is the most common trisomy.  Why A wrong: Trisomy 13 is Patau syndrome.  Why C wrong: Trisomy 21 is Down syndrome.  Why D wrong: Trisomy 16 is not the answer given in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Trisomy 18 is also known by which name?","options":["Patau syndrome","Down syndrome","Edward syndrome","Turner syndrome"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Trisomy 18 is Edward syndrome.  Why A wrong: Patau syndrome is trisomy 13.  Why B wrong: Down syndrome is trisomy 21.  Why D wrong: Turner syndrome is monosomy X (45, X0), not a trisomy."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Trisomy 13 is also known as which syndrome?","options":["Edward syndrome","Patau syndrome","Down syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trisomy 13 is Patau syndrome.  Why A wrong: Edward syndrome is trisomy 18.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is trisomy 21.  Why D wrong: Klinefelter is not listed in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Trisomy 21 is also known as which syndrome?","options":["Edward syndrome","Patau syndrome","Down syndrome","Turner syndrome"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Trisomy 21 is Down syndrome.  Why A wrong: Edward syndrome is trisomy 18.  Why B wrong: Patau syndrome is trisomy 13.  Why D wrong: Turner syndrome is monosomy X."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In Down syndrome (Trisomy 21), the extra chromosome is present on which chromosome pair?","options":["Chromosome 13","Chromosome 18","Chromosome 21","Chromosome 23 (sex chromosome)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Down syndrome has an extra chromosome 21 (three copies of chromosome 21).  Why A wrong: An extra chromosome 13 is Patau syndrome.  Why B wrong: An extra chromosome 18 is Edward syndrome.  Why D wrong: An extra sex chromosome would not give Down syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"A nurse teaches that as maternal age increases above a certain age, the chance of having a baby with Down syndrome also increases. Above what maternal age is this stated?","options":["Above 25 years","Above 30 years","Above 35 years","Above 40 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that as maternal age increases above 30 years, the chances of Down syndrome in the baby also increase.  Why A wrong: 25 years is not the value stated.  Why C wrong: Although 35 is a commonly cited clinical threshold, these notes specifically state above 30 years.  Why D wrong: 40 years is not the value stated here."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"According to the notes, why is screening for Down syndrome important?","options":["Because these babies have increased chances of mental retardation","Because these babies have increased birth weight","Because these babies have increased chance of prematurity only","Because these babies always have heart failure at birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Down syndrome babies have increased chances of mental retardation, which is why screening is done.  Why B wrong: Increased birth weight is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Prematurity is not the reason given.  Why D wrong: The notes do not state heart failure at birth as the screening rationale."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Which pregnant females should be screened for Down syndrome?","options":["Only those above 35 years","Only those with a family history","All pregnant females irrespective of their age (universal screening)","Only primigravida women"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: All pregnant females, irrespective of age, should be screened — this is called universal screening.  Why A wrong: Screening is universal, not restricted to older mothers.  Why B wrong: It is not limited to those with a family history.  Why D wrong: It applies to all pregnant women, not only primigravidae."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"The practice of screening all pregnant women for Down syndrome irrespective of age is termed as:","options":["Selective screening","Targeted screening","Universal screening","Diagnostic screening"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Screening all pregnant females regardless of age is called universal screening.  Why A wrong: Selective screening applies only to selected groups.  Why B wrong: Targeted screening focuses on specific risk groups.  Why D wrong: A diagnostic test is a confirmatory test, not the term for universal screening."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"When a pregnant female first presents for antenatal care, what is the correct sequence for detecting Down syndrome?","options":["Do diagnostic test first, then screening test","Do screening test first; if positive, then do diagnostic test","Do only the diagnostic test in all cases","Do only the screening test and never confirm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: You should first do a screening test; if it is positive, then proceed to a diagnostic test.  Why A wrong: The screening test comes before the diagnostic test.  Why C wrong: Diagnostic testing for everyone is invasive and not the routine approach.  Why D wrong: A positive screen must be confirmed with a diagnostic test."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In which situation should a diagnostic test be done directly, without first doing a screening test?","options":["If the mother is under 20 years","If the pregnant female gives a history of Down syndrome (previous affected child)","If the mother is primigravida","If the mother has gestational diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the pregnant female gives a history of Down syndrome, a diagnostic test is done directly.  Why A wrong: Young age is not the exception described.  Why C wrong: Primigravida status is not the exception.  Why D wrong: Gestational diabetes is not the exception described."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"First-trimester Down syndrome screening covers which gestational age range?","options":["From LMP up to 10 weeks","From 1st day of LMP up to 13 weeks + 6 days","From 14 weeks to 20 weeks","From 20 weeks to 27 weeks + 6 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First-trimester screening is done from the 1st day of LMP up to 13 weeks + 6 days.  Why A wrong: 10 weeks is not the stated upper limit.  Why C wrong: 14–20 weeks falls in the second trimester.  Why D wrong: 20–27 weeks + 6 days is second-trimester range."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Second-trimester Down syndrome screening covers which gestational age range?","options":["LMP to 13 weeks + 6 days","14 weeks to 27 weeks + 6 days","28 weeks to 40 weeks","11 weeks to 13 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Second-trimester screening is done from 14 weeks to 27 weeks + 6 days.  Why A wrong: That is the first-trimester range.  Why C wrong: 28–40 weeks is the third trimester.  Why D wrong: 11–13 weeks is the first trimester (nuchal translucency window)."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In first-trimester screening, the biochemical (blood) test used is known as the:","options":["Triple test","Quadruple test","Dual test","Integrated test"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The first-trimester biochemical test is the Dual test.  Why A wrong: The triple test is a second-trimester test.  Why B wrong: The quadruple test is a second-trimester test.  Why D wrong: Integrated screening combines first and second trimester screening, not the first-trimester blood test itself."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In first-trimester screening, if BOTH the biochemical (Dual) test and the USG are done together, it is called:","options":["Combined test","Integrated test","Triple test","Quadruple test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When both the biochemical test and USG are done in the first trimester, it is called the Combined test.  Why B wrong: Integrated test combines first- AND second-trimester screening.  Why C wrong: Triple test is a second-trimester biochemical test.  Why D wrong: Quadruple test is a second-trimester biochemical test."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"When BOTH first-trimester and second-trimester screening are performed, the combination is called:","options":["Combined screening","Integrated screening","Dual screening","Universal screening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Doing both first-trimester and second-trimester screening is called Integrated screening.  Why A wrong: Combined test refers to biochemical + USG within the first trimester only.  Why C wrong: Dual test is the first-trimester blood test.  Why D wrong: Universal screening means screening all pregnant women, a separate concept."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"The first-trimester Dual test measures which two markers in the mother's blood?","options":["AFP and unconjugated estriol","hCG level and PAPP-A level","Inhibin A and AFP","hCG and Inhibin A"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Dual test measures hCG level and PAPP-A (Pregnancy Associated Plasma Protein A).  Why A wrong: AFP and uE3 belong to the triple test.  Why C wrong: Inhibin A is a quadruple-test marker; AFP is a triple/quadruple marker.  Why D wrong: Inhibin A is not part of the Dual test."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In the Dual test, \"PAPP-A\" stands for:","options":["Placental Alkaline Phosphatase Protein","Pregnancy Associated Plasma Protein A","Prenatal Alpha Protein Antigen","Pregnancy Antenatal Plasma Peptide A"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PAPP-A stands for Pregnancy Associated Plasma Protein A.  Why A wrong: Placental alkaline phosphatase is a different enzyme.  Why C wrong: This is not the correct expansion.  Why D wrong: This is not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In Down syndrome, what happens to biochemical markers in general, and what is the exception noted?","options":["All markers increase except hCG/Inhibin","All biochemical markers decrease except hCG (and Inhibin)","All markers remain unchanged","Only AFP increases; all others decrease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in Down syndrome all biochemical markers decrease except hCG (and Inhibin), which increase.  Why A wrong: The general trend is a decrease, not an increase.  Why C wrong: Markers do change in Down syndrome.  Why D wrong: AFP actually decreases in Down syndrome; hCG and Inhibin are the ones that rise."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"The first-trimester ultrasound in Down syndrome screening primarily measures which parameter?","options":["Nuchal skin fold thickness","Nuchal translucency","Biparietal diameter","Femur length"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first-trimester USG measures nuchal translucency.  Why A wrong: Nuchal skin fold thickness is a second-trimester USG marker.  Why C wrong: Biparietal diameter is not the specified screening parameter here.  Why D wrong: Short femur is a second-trimester soft marker, not the first-trimester measurement."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"How is nuchal translucency described anatomically?","options":["The bright white area in front of the chest","The translucent (black) area below the neck in the region of the cervical spine","The fluid within the fetal abdomen","The thickened skin fold at the fetal forehead"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nuchal translucency is the translucent (black) area below the neck in the region of the cervical spine.  Why A wrong: It is located at the back of the neck, not the chest.  Why C wrong: It is not abdominal fluid.  Why D wrong: It is at the neck/cervical spine, not the forehead."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"At what nuchal translucency measurement is the finding considered abnormal?","options":["Greater than or equal to 2 mm","Greater than or equal to 3 mm","Greater than or equal to 5 mm","Greater than or equal to 6 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A nuchal translucency of ≥3 mm is considered abnormal.  Why A wrong: 2 mm is not the abnormal threshold stated.  Why C wrong: ≥5 mm is the abnormal cutoff for nuchal skin fold thickness (2nd trimester), not translucency.  Why D wrong: 6 mm is not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"An abnormally increased nuchal translucency (≥3 mm) can indicate which of the following conditions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Down syndrome (most common reason for increased NT)","Turner syndrome","Cardiac malformation","Anencephaly","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Increased NT indicates Down syndrome (the most common reason), Turner syndrome, and cardiac malformation.  Why A correct: Down syndrome is explicitly the MC (most common) reason for increased NT.  Why B correct: Turner syndrome is listed.  Why C correct: Cardiac malformation is listed.  Why D wrong: Anencephaly is a neural-tube defect and is not listed here.  Why E wrong: Placenta previa is a placental implantation abnormality, unrelated to NT."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"What is the most common (MC) reason for an increased nuchal translucency?","options":["Turner syndrome","Cardiac malformation","Down syndrome","Patau syndrome"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Down syndrome is stated as the most common (MC) reason for increased nuchal translucency.  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome is a cause but not the most common one.  Why B wrong: Cardiac malformation is listed but not the MC cause.  Why D wrong: Patau syndrome is not the listed MC cause."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"What is the best time to measure nuchal translucency on ultrasound?","options":["8–10 weeks","11–13 weeks","14–18 weeks","20–24 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best time to measure nuchal translucency is 11–13 weeks.  Why A wrong: 8–10 weeks is too early.  Why C wrong: 14–18 weeks is the second-trimester window.  Why D wrong: 20–24 weeks is too late for NT measurement."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In the second trimester, the biochemical (blood) test that measures three markers is known as the:","options":["Dual test","Triple test","Combined test","Integrated test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second-trimester three-marker biochemical test is the Triple test.  Why A wrong: Dual test is a first-trimester test.  Why C wrong: Combined test is first-trimester biochemical + USG.  Why D wrong: Integrated test combines first- and second-trimester screening."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"The second-trimester Triple test measures which three markers in the mother's blood?","options":["hCG, PAPP-A, and Inhibin A","hCG, Alpha Feto Protein (AFP), and Unconjugated Estriol (E3)","AFP, PAPP-A, and Inhibin A","hCG, Inhibin A, and PAPP-A"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Triple test measures hCG, Alpha Feto Protein (AFP), and Unconjugated Estriol (E3).  Why A wrong: PAPP-A is a first-trimester (Dual test) marker; Inhibin A is added only in the quadruple test.  Why C wrong: PAPP-A and Inhibin A are not part of the triple test.  Why D wrong: PAPP-A and Inhibin A are not the triple-test markers."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In a Down syndrome pregnancy, which triple-test marker is INCREASED?","options":["Alpha Feto Protein (AFP)","Unconjugated Estriol (E3)","hCG","All three are increased"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In Down syndrome, hCG is increased while AFP and uE3 are decreased.  Why A wrong: AFP is decreased.  Why B wrong: Unconjugated estriol (E3) is decreased.  Why D wrong: Only hCG rises; the other two fall."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In a Down syndrome pregnancy, what happens to Alpha Feto Protein (AFP) and Unconjugated Estriol (E3) in the triple test?","options":["Both increase","Both decrease","AFP increases, E3 decreases","AFP decreases, E3 increases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Down syndrome both AFP and unconjugated estriol (E3) decrease.  Why A wrong: They do not increase.  Why C wrong: Both decrease, not opposite directions.  Why D wrong: Both decrease, not opposite directions."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Which of the following are OTHER names for the Triple test? (Select all that apply)","options":["BART test","Kettering test","Combined test","Dual test","Integrated test"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The Triple test is also called the BART test and the Kettering test.  Why A correct: BART test is listed as another name.  Why B correct: Kettering test is listed as another name.  Why C wrong: Combined test refers to first-trimester biochemical + USG.  Why D wrong: Dual test is the first-trimester blood test.  Why E wrong: Integrated test combines first- and second-trimester screening."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"When Inhibin A is added to the Triple test markers, the resulting test is called the:","options":["Dual test","Combined test","Quadruple test","Integrated test"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Adding Inhibin A to the triple test makes it the Quadruple test.  Why A wrong: Dual test is a first-trimester two-marker blood test.  Why B wrong: Combined test is first-trimester biochemical + USG.  Why D wrong: Integrated test combines first- and second-trimester screening."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In a Down syndrome pregnancy, the level of Inhibin A:","options":["Decreases","Increases","Remains unchanged","Is not measurable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Down syndrome, Inhibin A is increased.  Why A wrong: It increases, not decreases.  Why C wrong: It changes (rises).  Why D wrong: Inhibin A is measurable and is part of the quadruple test."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In a Down syndrome pregnancy, which quadruple-test markers are DECREASED?","options":["hCG and Inhibin A","AFP and Unconjugated Estriol (E3)","hCG and AFP","Inhibin A and E3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the quadruple test in Down syndrome, AFP and E3 are decreased.  Why A wrong: hCG and Inhibin A are increased, not decreased.  Why C wrong: hCG is increased, not decreased.  Why D wrong: Inhibin A is increased, not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In a Down syndrome pregnancy, which quadruple-test markers are INCREASED?","options":["AFP and E3","hCG and Inhibin A","AFP and Inhibin A","E3 and hCG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the quadruple test in Down syndrome, hCG and Inhibin A are increased.  Why A wrong: AFP and E3 are decreased.  Why C wrong: AFP is decreased, not increased.  Why D wrong: E3 is decreased; only hCG rises among these two."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Match the quadruple-test result pattern seen in Down syndrome. Which option correctly lists the direction of all four markers?","options":["AFP ↑, E3 ↑, hCG ↓, Inhibin A ↓","AFP ↓, E3 ↓, hCG ↑, Inhibin A ↑","AFP ↓, E3 ↑, hCG ↓, Inhibin A ↑","AFP ↑, E3 ↓, hCG ↑, Inhibin A ↓"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Down syndrome the quadruple test shows AFP ↓, E3 ↓, hCG ↑, and Inhibin A ↑.  Why A wrong: This reverses every marker's direction.  Why C wrong: E3 decreases (not increases) and hCG increases (not decreases).  Why D wrong: AFP decreases and Inhibin A increases, opposite to this option."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In second-trimester USG screening, the marker \"nuchal skin fold thickness\" is considered abnormal when it is:","options":["≥2 mm","≥3 mm","≥5 mm","≥6 mm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Nuchal skin fold thickness ≥5 mm is abnormal.  Why A wrong: 2 mm is not the cutoff.  Why B wrong: ≥3 mm is the cutoff for nuchal translucency (first trimester), not skin fold thickness.  Why D wrong: 6 mm is not the stated cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"A nurse must distinguish two ultrasound markers. Which statement correctly separates nuchal translucency from nuchal skin fold thickness?","options":["Both are the same measurement done in the third trimester","Nuchal translucency (1st trimester, abnormal ≥3 mm) is different from nuchal skin fold thickness (2nd trimester, abnormal ≥5 mm)","Nuchal translucency is abnormal at ≥5 mm and skin fold at ≥3 mm","Nuchal skin fold thickness is measured in the first trimester"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NT is a first-trimester marker (abnormal ≥3 mm) while nuchal skin fold thickness is a second-trimester marker (abnormal ≥5 mm); the notes warn not to confuse the two.  Why A wrong: They are different measurements at different times, not the same.  Why C wrong: The cutoffs are swapped.  Why D wrong: Nuchal skin fold thickness is a second-trimester marker."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Which of the following are second-trimester ultrasound findings (soft markers) that suggest the baby may have Down syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Increased nuchal skin fold thickness (≥5 mm)","Absent nasal bone","Short femur","Short humerus","Long femur"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The listed second-trimester USG markers of Down syndrome are increased nuchal skin fold thickness (≥5 mm), absent nasal bone, short femur, and short humerus.  Why A correct: Nuchal skin fold thickness ≥5 mm is a listed marker.  Why B correct: Absent nasal bone is a listed marker.  Why C correct: Short femur is a listed marker.  Why D correct: Short humerus is a listed marker.  Why E wrong: The marker is a SHORT femur, not a long femur."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Which second-trimester USG finding, related to the fetal face, is a marker for Down syndrome?","options":["Cleft lip","Absent nasal bone","Cataract","Micrognathia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Absent nasal bone is a listed second-trimester USG marker for Down syndrome.  Why A wrong: Cleft lip is not listed as the Down syndrome marker here.  Why C wrong: Cataract is not a listed marker.  Why D wrong: Micrognathia is not the marker listed in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Which long-bone USG findings in the second trimester suggest Down syndrome?","options":["Short femur and short humerus","Long femur and long humerus","Short tibia and short radius only","Bowed femur and fractured humerus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Short femur and short humerus are the listed long-bone markers of Down syndrome.  Why B wrong: The markers are SHORT bones, not long.  Why C wrong: The notes specify femur and humerus, not tibia and radius.  Why D wrong: Bowing/fracture are not the listed markers."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"If any screening test for Down syndrome is positive, which confirmatory (diagnostic) test is performed?","options":["Repeat ultrasound","Karyotyping","Another triple test","Maternal serum AFP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A positive screening test is confirmed by a diagnostic (confirmatory) test called karyotyping.  Why A wrong: Repeating ultrasound is still screening, not confirmatory.  Why C wrong: The triple test is a screening test, not confirmatory.  Why D wrong: Maternal serum AFP is a screening marker, not the confirmatory test."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Karyotyping (the confirmatory test for Down syndrome) requires which type of cells?","options":["Maternal red blood cells","Viable fetal cells","Maternal serum only","Placental hormone extracts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For karyotyping, viable fetal cells are needed.  Why A wrong: Maternal RBCs are not used for fetal karyotyping.  Why B correct.  Why C wrong: Karyotyping needs cells, not just serum.  Why D wrong: Hormone extracts cannot be karyotyped."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"Which of the following procedures can be used to obtain fetal cells for karyotyping? (Select all that apply)","options":["Chorionic villus sampling","Amniocentesis","Cordocentesis","Endometrial biopsy","Maternal venipuncture only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal cells can be obtained by chorionic villus sampling, amniocentesis, and cordocentesis.  Why A correct: Chorionic villus sampling is listed.  Why B correct: Amniocentesis is listed.  Why C correct: Cordocentesis is listed.  Why D wrong: Endometrial biopsy does not sample fetal cells.  Why E wrong: Simple maternal venipuncture is not listed as a fetal-cell source here."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"A pregnant woman at 12 weeks gestation is undergoing first-trimester Down syndrome screening. Which combination of tests is appropriate at this stage?","options":["Triple test and nuchal skin fold thickness","Dual test (hCG, PAPP-A) and nuchal translucency USG","Quadruple test and absent nasal bone assessment","Cordocentesis and karyotyping"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First-trimester screening (up to 13 weeks + 6 days) uses the Dual test (hCG, PAPP-A) plus nuchal translucency on USG.  Why A wrong: Triple test and nuchal skin fold thickness are second-trimester components.  Why C wrong: Quadruple test and absent nasal bone are second-trimester components.  Why D wrong: Cordocentesis/karyotyping are diagnostic tests done only after a positive screen."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"A woman had first-trimester combined screening and now, at 16 weeks, is undergoing second-trimester screening as well. What is this overall approach called?","options":["Combined screening","Integrated screening","Universal screening","Diagnostic screening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Performing both first- and second-trimester screening is called Integrated screening.  Why A wrong: Combined test is first-trimester biochemical + USG only.  Why C wrong: Universal screening refers to screening all women irrespective of age.  Why D wrong: This is screening, not a diagnostic test."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"A 32-year-old primigravida has a previous child with Down syndrome. What is the recommended approach in her current pregnancy?","options":["Do only routine screening tests","Directly perform a diagnostic test","No testing is required","Wait until third trimester to test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a pregnant female gives a history of Down syndrome, the diagnostic test is done directly (the exception to the screen-first rule).  Why A wrong: With a prior history, direct diagnostic testing is indicated rather than screening first.  Why C wrong: Testing is required given the risk history.  Why D wrong: Testing should not be delayed to the third trimester."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"In Down syndrome, what is the relationship of the sex chromosomes shown in the karyotype figure?","options":["Always X0 (single X)","Either XX (female) or XY (male), with an extra chromosome 21","Always triploid","Always missing sex chromosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Down syndrome karyotype shows normal sex chromosomes — XX (female) or XY (male) — with the abnormality being an extra chromosome 21.  Why A wrong: X0 is Turner syndrome, not Down syndrome.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is a trisomy, not triploidy.  Why D wrong: Sex chromosomes are present; only chromosome 21 is trisomic."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"The notes make an exception statement \"Except: Trisomy 18\" regarding which fact about trisomies?","options":["Trisomy 18 is the least common trisomy","Trisomy 18 (Edward syndrome) is highlighted as the most common trisomy","Trisomy 18 never survives birth","Trisomy 18 is a monosomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes single out Trisomy 18 (Edward syndrome) as the most common trisomy.  Why A wrong: It is the most common, not the least common, per the notes.  Why C wrong: Survival is not the point being made.  Why D wrong: Trisomy 18 is a trisomy, not a monosomy."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Human chromosome number & diploid/haploid concept, gametes, karyotype…","question":"A student lists chromosome numbers for various genetic conditions. Which pairing is INCORRECT?","options":["Normal cell — 46","Trisomy — 47","Turner syndrome (monosomy X) — 45","Triploidy — 46"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct (as the incorrect pairing): Triploidy has 69 chromosomes, not 46; this pairing is wrong.  Why A wrong (i.e., correct pairing): A normal cell has 46 chromosomes.  Why B wrong (correct pairing): Trisomy = 47.  Why C wrong (correct pairing): Turner syndrome = 45 (X0)."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"In chorionic villus sampling (CVS), what is the sample obtained for testing?","options":["Amniotic fluid","Chorionic villi","Fetal blood","Maternal serum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In CVS the sample taken is chorionic villi.  Why A wrong: Amniotic fluid is the sample in amniocentesis, not CVS.  Why C wrong: Fetal blood is the sample in cordocentesis/PUBS.  Why D wrong: Maternal serum is not the sample used in CVS."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"The study material examined in chorionic villus sampling is derived from which cells?","options":["Trophoblast","Fibroblast amniocytes","Fetal RBCs","Decidual cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The study material in CVS is trophoblast.  Why B wrong: Fibroblast amniocytes are the study material in amniocentesis.  Why C wrong: Fetal RBC is the study material in cordocentesis.  Why D wrong: Decidual (maternal) cells are not the study material in CVS."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"From what gestational age can chorionic villus sampling be done?","options":["≥6 weeks","≥10 weeks","≥15 weeks","≥18 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CVS can be done at ≥10 weeks of gestation.  Why A wrong: 6 weeks is too early; the stated minimum is 10 weeks.  Why C wrong: ≥15 weeks is the minimum for amniocentesis.  Why D wrong: ≥18 weeks is the timing for cordocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the best time (ideal gestational age) to perform chorionic villus sampling?","options":["6–8 weeks","11–13 weeks (first trimester)","16–18 weeks (second trimester)","24–28 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best time to do CVS is 11–13 weeks, i.e. the first trimester.  Why A wrong: 6–8 weeks is earlier than the recommended best window.  Why C wrong: 16–18 weeks (second trimester) is the best time for amniocentesis.  Why D wrong: 24–28 weeks corresponds to peak insulin resistance, not CVS timing."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the main advantage of chorionic villus sampling?","options":["Gives faster result (24–48 hrs)","Lowest chance of fetal loss","Can detect structural defects","Can assess fetal lung maturity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The advantage of CVS is that it gives a faster result (24–48 hrs).  Why B wrong: CVS actually has a higher fetal loss (1%) than amniocentesis.  Why C wrong: Neither CVS nor amniocentesis detects structural defects; USG is used for that.  Why D wrong: Fetal lung maturity is assessed by amniocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the disadvantage of chorionic villus sampling regarding fetal loss?","options":["Chances of fetal loss (1%)","No chance of fetal loss","Fetal loss 1 in 300","Fetal loss 3 in 100"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The disadvantage of CVS is a chance of fetal loss of about 1%.  Why B wrong: There is a definite risk of fetal loss with CVS.  Why C wrong: 1 in 300 is the fetal loss rate for amniocentesis.  Why D wrong: 3 in 100 is the fetal loss rate for cordocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"In amniocentesis, the sample obtained for study is:","options":["Amniotic fluid","Chorionic villi","Fetal blood from umbilical vein","Placental trophoblast"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sample in amniocentesis is amniotic fluid.  Why B wrong: Chorionic villi are sampled in CVS.  Why C wrong: Umbilical vein fetal blood is sampled in cordocentesis.  Why D wrong: Trophoblast is the study material of CVS."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"The study material analyzed in amniocentesis consists of which cells?","options":["Fibroblast amniocytes","Trophoblast","Fetal RBCs","Maternal lymphocytes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The study material in amniocentesis is fibroblast amniocytes.  Why B wrong: Trophoblast is the CVS study material.  Why C wrong: Fetal RBC is the cordocentesis study material.  Why D wrong: Maternal lymphocytes are not analyzed in amniocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"From what minimum gestational age can amniocentesis be performed?","options":["≥10 weeks","≥15 weeks","≥18 weeks","≥24 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amniocentesis can be done from ≥15 weeks.  Why A wrong: ≥10 weeks is the minimum for CVS.  Why C wrong: ≥18 weeks is the timing for cordocentesis.  Why D wrong: ≥24 weeks is not the stated minimum for amniocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the best time to perform amniocentesis?","options":["11–13 weeks (first trimester)","16–18 weeks (second trimester)","6–8 weeks","34–36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best time for amniocentesis is 16–18 weeks, the second trimester.  Why A wrong: 11–13 weeks (first trimester) is the best time for CVS.  Why C wrong: 6–8 weeks is far too early for amniocentesis.  Why D wrong: 34–36 weeks is not the stated ideal window."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the approximate chance of fetal loss with amniocentesis?","options":["1 in 100","1 in 300 procedures","3 in 100 procedures","5 in 100"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chance of fetal loss with amniocentesis is less — about 1 in 300 procedures.  Why A wrong: 1 in 100 is the most common complication rate of CVS.  Why C wrong: 3 in 100 is the fetal loss rate for cordocentesis.  Why D wrong: 5 in 100 is not a rate stated on the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which of the following is an advantage of amniocentesis over CVS?","options":["Faster result within 24–48 hrs","It can be used for knowing fetal lung maturity","It carries higher fetal loss","It detects structural defects"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amniocentesis is safer, has less fetal loss, and can be used for knowing fetal lung maturity.  Why A wrong: Faster result (24–48 hrs) is an advantage of CVS; amniocentesis result comes late.  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis has lower, not higher, fetal loss than CVS.  Why D wrong: Structural defects are detected by USG, not amniocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Amniocentesis done later in pregnancy is used for all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Assessing lung maturity of fetus","Detecting fetal infection","Treatment of polyhydramnios","Karyotyping the trophoblast"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Later-pregnancy amniocentesis is used for lung maturity, fetal infection, and polyhydramnios treatment — not for karyotyping the trophoblast (trophoblast is CVS material).  Why A wrong: Lung maturity of fetus is a stated use.  Why B wrong: Fetal infection detection is a stated use.  Why C wrong: Polyhydramnios treatment is a stated use."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is a disadvantage of amniocentesis?","options":["Result comes late (7–10 days)","High fetal loss (3 in 100)","Cannot detect chromosomal defects","Causes oromandibular limb defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The disadvantage of amniocentesis is that the result comes late (7–10 days).  Why B wrong: 3 in 100 fetal loss belongs to cordocentesis.  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis can detect chromosomal number defects.  Why D wrong: Oromandibular limb defect is a complication of CVS done before 10 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the most common complication of chorionic villus sampling?","options":["Fetal loss/abortion (1 in 100)","Oromandibular limb defect","Rh isoimmunization","Preterm labour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common complication of CVS is fetal loss/abortion (1 in 100).  Why B wrong: Oromandibular limb defect is the complication specifically of CVS done before 10 weeks.  Why C wrong: Rh isoimmunization is not the stated most common CVS complication.  Why D wrong: Preterm labour is not listed as the most common CVS complication."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the most common complication when chorionic villus sampling is done before 10 weeks?","options":["Oromandibular limb defect of fetus","Neural tube defect","Amniotic fluid embolism","Fetal anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CVS done before 10 weeks most commonly causes oromandibular limb defect of the fetus.  Why B wrong: Neural tube defect is not the stated complication of early CVS.  Why C wrong: Amniotic fluid embolism is not associated here.  Why D wrong: Fetal anemia relates to Rh isoimmunization (assessed by cordocentesis), not early CVS."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Both CVS and amniocentesis can detect all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Defect in chromosomal number","Inborn error of metabolism","Hemoglobinopathy in the fetus","Structural defect in the fetus"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Both tests can tell about chromosomal number defects, inborn errors of metabolism, and hemoglobinopathies, but NOT structural defects — USG is done for structural defects.  Why A wrong: Chromosomal number defects are detectable by both.  Why B wrong: Inborn errors of metabolism are detectable by both.  Why C wrong: Hemoglobinopathy in the fetus is detectable by both."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which is an example of an inborn error of metabolism detectable by CVS/amniocentesis?","options":["Cystic fibrosis","Sickle cell anemia","Down syndrome","Spina bifida"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives cystic fibrosis as the example of an inborn error of metabolism.  Why B wrong: Sickle cell anemia is given as the example of a hemoglobinopathy, not an inborn error of metabolism, in this list.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is a chromosomal (number) defect example.  Why D wrong: Spina bifida is a structural defect (detected on USG)."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which fetal hemoglobinopathy is given as an example detectable by CVS/amniocentesis?","options":["Sickle cell anemia","Cystic fibrosis","Cri-du-chat syndrome","Anencephaly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sickle cell anemia is the example of hemoglobinopathy in the fetus.  Why B wrong: Cystic fibrosis is listed as an inborn error of metabolism.  Why C wrong: Cri-du-chat is a chromosomal defect, not the given hemoglobinopathy example.  Why D wrong: Anencephaly is a structural defect detected by USG."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which investigation is done to detect structural defects in the fetus?","options":["USG (ultrasonography)","Chorionic villus sampling","Amniocentesis","Cordocentesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Since CVS and amniocentesis cannot detect structural defects, USG is done for structural defects.  Why B wrong: CVS cannot detect structural defects.  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis cannot detect structural defects.  Why D wrong: Cordocentesis is used for fetal Hb/Rh isoimmunization, not structural defects."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"In cordocentesis (percutaneous umbilical blood sampling), the sample is taken from which vessel, as close to the placenta as possible?","options":["Umbilical vein","Umbilical artery","Maternal uterine artery","Fetal femoral vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In cordocentesis, fetal blood is taken from the umbilical vein as close to the placenta as possible.  Why B wrong: The umbilical vein, not artery, is sampled.  Why C wrong: Maternal uterine artery is not sampled in this fetal test.  Why D wrong: The fetal femoral vein is not used."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the study material in cordocentesis?","options":["Fetal RBC","Trophoblast","Fibroblast amniocytes","Amniotic fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The study material in cordocentesis is fetal RBC.  Why B wrong: Trophoblast is the CVS study material.  Why C wrong: Fibroblast amniocytes are the amniocentesis study material.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid is the amniocentesis sample."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the disadvantage of cordocentesis regarding fetal loss?","options":["Maximum fetal loss (3 in 100 procedures)","Fetal loss 1 in 300","Fetal loss 1 in 100","No fetal loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cordocentesis has the maximum fetal loss — 3 in 100 procedures.  Why B wrong: 1 in 300 is the amniocentesis fetal loss.  Why C wrong: 1 in 100 is the CVS most common complication rate.  Why D wrong: Cordocentesis has a definite (and highest) risk of fetal loss."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Cordocentesis is NOT used for which of the following?","options":["Karyotyping","Knowing fetal Hb","Assessing Rh isoimmunization","Sampling fetal blood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cordocentesis is not used for karyotyping.  Why B wrong: It is used to know the Hb of the fetus.  Why C wrong: It is used in Rh isoimmunization.  Why D wrong: It does obtain a fetal blood sample."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the main clinical use of cordocentesis?","options":["To know the Hb of the fetus (as in Rh isoimmunization)","To assess fetal lung maturity","To detect structural defects","To treat polyhydramnios"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cordocentesis is used when we want to know the Hb of the fetus, e.g. in Rh isoimmunization.  Why B wrong: Fetal lung maturity is assessed by amniocentesis.  Why C wrong: Structural defects are detected by USG.  Why D wrong: Polyhydramnios treatment is a use of amniocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"From what minimum gestational age is cordocentesis performed?","options":["≥18 weeks","≥10 weeks","≥15 weeks","≥24 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cordocentesis is done at ≥18 weeks.  Why B wrong: ≥10 weeks is for CVS.  Why C wrong: ≥15 weeks is for amniocentesis.  Why D wrong: ≥24 weeks is not the stated time for cordocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the total weight gained in a normal pregnancy?","options":["10–12 kg (about 11 kg)","5–6 kg","15–18 kg","24 kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Total weight gained in pregnancy is 10–12 kg (about 11 kg), equal to 24 lb.  Why B wrong: 5–6 kg is the reproductive weight (or net weight) portion, not the total.  Why C wrong: 15–18 kg exceeds the stated normal total.  Why D wrong: 24 is the value in pounds (lb), not kilograms."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"The total weight gained in pregnancy (10–12 kg) is equivalent to how many pounds?","options":["24 lb","11 lb","5 lb","50 lb"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 10–12 kg (11 kg) equals 24 lb, since 1 kg = 2.2 lb.  Why B wrong: 11 is the value in kilograms, not pounds.  Why C wrong: 5 lb is far too low.  Why D wrong: 50 lb is far more than the stated equivalent."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"According to the notes, 1 kg is equal to how many pounds?","options":["2.2 lb","1.2 lb","3.3 lb","0.45 lb"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states 1 kg = 2.2 lb.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated conversion of 2.2 lb per kilogram."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"How much weight (reproductive weight gained) is shed off after delivery?","options":["5–6 kg","10–12 kg","2–3 kg","24 kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The reproductive weight gained, which is shed off after delivery, is 5–6 kg.  Why B wrong: 10–12 kg is the total weight gained.  Why C wrong: 2–3 kg is not the stated amount.  Why D wrong: 24 is the total gain in pounds."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"The net weight gained in pregnancy (retained after delivery) is approximately:","options":["5–6 kg","10–12 kg","1–2 kg","8–9 kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The net weight gained is 5–6 kg.  Why B wrong: 10–12 kg is the total gain, of which net is only part.  Why C wrong: 1–2 kg is too low.  Why D wrong: 8–9 kg is not the stated net weight."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Weight gained during pregnancy depends on all of the following factors EXCEPT:","options":["Socioeconomic status","Parity","Prepregnancy weight","Maternal blood group"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The four listed factors are socioeconomic status, parity, prepregnancy weight, and associated pregnancy conditions — maternal blood group is not listed.  Why A wrong: Socioeconomic status is a listed factor.  Why B wrong: Parity is a listed factor.  Why C wrong: Prepregnancy weight is a listed factor."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Regarding socioeconomic status and pregnancy weight gain, which statement is correct?","options":["Low socioeconomic status → less weight gained; high socioeconomic status → more weight gained","Low socioeconomic status → more weight gained","High socioeconomic status → less weight gained","Socioeconomic status has no effect on weight gain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low socioeconomic status leads to less weight gained; high socioeconomic status leads to more weight gained.  Why B wrong: Low status is associated with less, not more, weight gain.  Why C wrong: High status is associated with more, not less, weight gain.  Why D wrong: Socioeconomic status is a listed factor affecting weight gain."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Regarding parity, weight gained in the first pregnancy compared to subsequent pregnancies is:","options":["More than in subsequent pregnancies","Less than in subsequent pregnancies","Equal in all pregnancies","Unrelated to parity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Weight gained in the 1st pregnancy is more than the weight gained in subsequent pregnancies.  Why B wrong: It is more, not less.  Why C wrong: It differs between first and later pregnancies.  Why D wrong: Parity is explicitly a listed factor."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Regarding prepregnancy weight, obese females tend to:","options":["Gain more weight during pregnancy","Gain less weight during pregnancy","Lose weight during pregnancy","Have no weight change"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An obese female gains more weight during pregnancy.  Why B, C, D wrong: The note states obese women gain more, not less, none, or lose weight."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which associated pregnancy conditions increase weight gain during pregnancy?","options":["Twin, polyhydramnios, diabetic","IUGR, oligohydramnios","Preterm labour, anemia","Hypertension only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Associated pregnancy conditions increasing weight are twin, polyhydramnios, and diabetic.  Why B wrong: IUGR is associated with LESS weight gain, not more.  Why C wrong: Preterm labour and anemia are not listed here.  Why D wrong: Hypertension alone is not the listed group."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which of the following does NOT affect weight gain in pregnancy?","options":["Smoking","Parity","Prepregnancy weight","Socioeconomic status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note explicitly states smoking does not affect weight gain in pregnancy.  Why B, C, D wrong: Parity, prepregnancy weight, and socioeconomic status are all listed factors that DO affect weight gain."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Excessive (more) weight gain in pregnancy can be a marker for all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["IUGR in fetus","Diabetes in pregnancy","Twin pregnancy","Post-term pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IUGR in the fetus is a cause of LESS weight gain, not more.  Why B wrong: Diabetes in pregnancy causes more weight gain.  Why C wrong: Twin pregnancy causes more weight gain.  Why D wrong: Post-term pregnancy is listed under more weight gain."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"In diabetes in pregnancy, more weight gain is due to which fetal/pregnancy effects?","options":["Macrosomia (larger baby) and polyhydramnios","IUGR and oligohydramnios","Preterm delivery","Fetal death"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetes in pregnancy → baby weighs more (macrosomia) and polyhydramnios, leading to more weight gain.  Why B wrong: IUGR/oligohydramnios are opposite effects (less weight).  Why C, D wrong: Preterm delivery and fetal death are not the stated mechanisms of increased weight gain here."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Less weight gain in pregnancy is a marker for which fetal condition?","options":["IUGR (intrauterine growth restriction) in fetus","Macrosomia","Polyhydramnios","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Less weight gain is a marker for IUGR in the fetus.  Why B, C, D wrong: Macrosomia, polyhydramnios, and twin pregnancy are all markers of MORE weight gain."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the normal expected rate of weight gain in pregnancy?","options":["<0.5 kg/week or <2 kg/month",">1 kg/week",">3 kg/month","2 kg/week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normally weight gain should be <0.5 kg/week or <2 kg/month.  Why B, C, D wrong: These exceed the normal rate; gains above 0.5 kg/week or 2 kg/month were earlier used as criteria for diagnosing PIH."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"A rapid weight gain of more than 0.5 kg/week or more than 2 kg/month was earlier used as a criterion for diagnosing which condition?","options":["PIH (pregnancy-induced hypertension)","Gestational diabetes","IUGR","Anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A gain >0.5 kg/week or >2 kg/month was earlier the criterion for diagnosing PIH.  Why B wrong: Rapid weight gain here was linked to PIH, not gestational diabetes.  Why C wrong: IUGR is associated with less weight gain.  Why D wrong: Anemia is not diagnosed by rapid weight gain."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"As a nursing implication, at every antenatal visit the nurse should:","options":["Measure and document the weight of the pregnant female","Perform amniocentesis","Measure only blood pressure","Administer iron injections"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At every antenatal visit, the weight of the pregnant female should be measured and documented.  Why B wrong: Amniocentesis is not a routine antenatal-visit action.  Why C wrong: The stated implication specifically concerns weight (not BP alone).  Why D wrong: Routine iron injections are not the stated nursing implication."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the total amount of water retained during pregnancy?","options":["6.5 L","2 L","10 L","4.5 L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The total amount of water retained in pregnancy is 6.5 L.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 6.5 L."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which statement about edema in pregnancy is correct?","options":["It is physiological and more in the lower limbs","It is always pathological","It worsens on taking rest","It is currently a mandatory criterion to diagnose PIH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Edema in pregnancy is physiological, more in the lower limbs, and subsides on taking rest.  Why B wrong: It is physiological, not always pathological.  Why C wrong: It subsides (not worsens) on taking rest.  Why D wrong: Edema was earlier a criterion for PIH, but now it is not."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Edema in pregnancy subsides with which measure?","options":["Taking rest","Increased salt intake","Standing for long periods","Diuretics only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states edema subsides on taking rest.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated measure; rest is the listed factor that reduces edema."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which was earlier one of the criteria to diagnose PIH but is now no longer used?","options":["Edema","Proteinuria","Blood pressure","Headache"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Earlier, edema was one of the criteria to diagnose PIH; now it is not.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the item that the note states was dropped from PIH criteria."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What happens to Na+ and K+ concentration in pregnancy?","options":["Decreases","Increases","Remains unchanged","Na+ increases, K+ decreases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Na+ and K+ concentration decreases in pregnancy.  Why B, C, D wrong: The note states both decrease, not increase, stay the same, or diverge."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What happens to plasma osmolality during pregnancy?","options":["Decreases","Increases","Remains constant","Fluctuates widely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Plasma osmolality decreases in pregnancy.  Why B, C, D wrong: The note states it decreases, not increases, stays constant, or fluctuates."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"How many extra calories are required per day in pregnancy?","options":["+350 kcal/day","+600 kcal/day","+150 kcal/day","+1000 kcal/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Extra calories required in pregnancy are +350 kcal/day.  Why B wrong: +600 kcal/day is required in lactation.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated pregnancy value."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"How many extra calories are required per day during lactation?","options":["+600 kcal/day","+350 kcal/day","+250 kcal/day","+500 kcal/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Extra calories required in lactation are +600 kcal/day.  Why B wrong: +350 kcal/day is the pregnancy requirement.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated lactation value."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the total iron requirement per day (RDA) in pregnancy?","options":["60 mg/day","30 mg/day","100 mg/day","500 mcg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Total iron required per day (RDA) in pregnancy is 60 mg/day.  Why B, C wrong: These do not match the stated 60 mg/day.  Why D wrong: 500 mcg/day is the folic acid RDA, and the unit (mcg) is different."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the RDA of folic acid in pregnancy?","options":["500 mcg/day","60 mg/day","250 mcg/day","1200 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Folic acid RDA in pregnancy is 500 mcg/day.  Why B wrong: 60 mg/day is the iron RDA.  Why C wrong: 250 mcg/day is the iodine RDA.  Why D wrong: 1200 mg/day is the calcium RDA."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the RDA of vitamin D in pregnancy?","options":["10 mcg/day (40 IU/day)","500 mcg/day","250 mcg/day","60 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin D RDA in pregnancy is 10 mcg/day (40 IU/day).  Why B wrong: 500 mcg/day is the folic acid RDA.  Why C wrong: 250 mcg/day is the iodine RDA.  Why D wrong: 60 mg/day is the iron RDA."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the RDA of iodine in pregnancy?","options":["250 mcg/day","500 mcg/day","60 mg/day","1200 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Iodine RDA in pregnancy is 250 mcg/day.  Why B wrong: 500 mcg/day is folic acid.  Why C wrong: 60 mg/day is iron.  Why D wrong: 1200 mg/day is calcium."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the RDA of calcium in pregnancy?","options":["1200 mg/day","60 mg/day","500 mcg/day","250 mcg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium RDA in pregnancy is 1200 mg/day.  Why B wrong: 60 mg/day is iron.  Why C wrong: 500 mcg/day is folic acid.  Why D wrong: 250 mcg/day is iodine."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"By approximately how much does basal metabolic rate (BMR) increase in pregnancy?","options":["10–20%","30–40%","50%","5%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BMR increases in pregnancy by 10–20%.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 10–20% increase."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which is the main hormone during pregnancy that is responsible for sustaining the pregnancy?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","FSH","LH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone is the main hormone during pregnancy and is responsible for sustaining pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Estrogen is increased but is not the hormone named as sustaining pregnancy here.  Why C, D wrong: FSH and LH are decreased in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"A deficiency (low level) of progesterone in pregnancy can lead to:","options":["Abortion","Macrosomia","Polyhydramnios","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If progesterone is less in pregnancy, it leads to abortion.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated consequence of low progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which hormone is responsible for the amenorrhea of pregnancy?","options":["Progesterone","FSH","LH","Oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone is responsible for the amenorrhea of pregnancy.  Why B, C wrong: FSH and LH are low in pregnancy and not the cause of amenorrhea here.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not stated as causing amenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Progesterone has a negative feedback on which hormone, causing its levels to be low in pregnancy?","options":["LH","FSH","Estrogen","HPL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone has negative feedback on LH, so LH levels are low in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Estrogen (not progesterone) has negative feedback on FSH.  Why C wrong: Estrogen levels are increased in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: HPL is not the target of progesterone's negative feedback here."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Progesterone acts as a smooth muscle relaxant. Its relaxation of the oesophageal sphincter in pregnancy leads to:","options":["Increased GERD (gastroesophageal reflux)","Diarrhea","Hypertension","Reduced acid reflux"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone relaxes the oesophageal sphincter, increasing GERD in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Intestinal relaxation causes constipation, not diarrhea.  Why C wrong: Vasodilation lowers peripheral vascular resistance, not raises BP.  Why D wrong: Sphincter relaxation increases (not reduces) reflux."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Relaxation of intestinal smooth muscle by progesterone in pregnancy commonly leads to:","options":["Constipation","Diarrhea","Vomiting","Malabsorption"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone relaxes the intestine → constipation.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated effect of intestinal relaxation."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Progesterone-induced vasodilation in pregnancy causes what change in peripheral vascular resistance?","options":["Peripheral vascular resistance decreases","Peripheral vascular resistance increases","No change","Peripheral vascular resistance doubles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone causes vasodilation, so peripheral vascular resistance decreases in pregnancy.  Why B, C, D wrong: Vasodilation lowers (not raises, maintains, or doubles) peripheral vascular resistance."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Progesterone relaxes the ureter in pregnancy, which leads to:","options":["Urinary stasis","Increased urine flow","Renal stones dissolution","Anuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone relaxes the ureter → urinary stasis in pregnancy.  Why B, C, D wrong: Ureteric relaxation causes stasis, not increased flow, stone dissolution, or anuria."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What happens to estrogen levels during pregnancy?","options":["Increased","Decreased","Unchanged","Absent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen is increased in pregnancy.  Why B, C, D wrong: The note states estrogen is increased, not decreased, unchanged, or absent."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which is the most common estrogen in pregnancy?","options":["E2 (Estradiol)","E3 (Estriol)","E1 (Estrone)","E4 (Estetrol)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common estrogen in pregnancy is E2 (Estradiol).  Why B wrong: E3 (Estriol) is the most specific estrogen in pregnancy, not the most common.  Why C, D wrong: E1 and E4 are not named as the most common in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which is the most specific estrogen in pregnancy?","options":["E3 (Estriol)","E2 (Estradiol)","E1 (Estrone)","Progesterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most specific estrogen in pregnancy is E3 (Estriol).  Why B wrong: E2 (Estradiol) is the most common, not most specific.  Why C wrong: E1 (Estrone) is not named as most specific.  Why D wrong: Progesterone is not an estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Estrogen has a negative feedback on which hormone, causing it to fall in pregnancy?","options":["FSH","LH","Progesterone","HPL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen has a negative feedback on FSH, so FSH↓ in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Progesterone (not estrogen) has negative feedback on LH.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is increased in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: HPL is not the target of estrogen's negative feedback."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Estrogen increases clotting factors in pregnancy, making pregnancy a:","options":["Hypercoagulable state","Hypocoagulable state","Bleeding disorder","Anticoagulated state"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen increases clotting factors, so pregnancy is a hypercoagulable state.  Why B, C, D wrong: Increased clotting factors make pregnancy hypercoagulable, not hypocoagulable/anticoagulated/bleeding-prone."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Estrogen causes congestion of mucosa in pregnancy. Which mucosal change causes bleeding gums?","options":["Gums congested → bleeding","Nasal mucosa congested","Bladder mucosa congested","Intestinal mucosa congested"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen causes gums to become congested, leading to bleeding.  Why B wrong: Nasal mucosal congestion is a separate change (not bleeding gums).  Why C wrong: Bladder mucosa congestion is a separate listed change.  Why D wrong: Intestinal mucosa is not listed among the congested mucosae."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which of the following mucosae is/are congested in pregnancy due to estrogen? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nasal mucosa","Gums","Bladder mucosa","Corneal mucosa","Bronchial mucosa"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists nasal mucosa, gums, and bladder mucosa as congested in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Corneal mucosa is not listed.  Why E wrong: Bronchial mucosa is not listed as congested here."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"In pregnancy, the hormone pattern is best described as:","options":["Progesterone↑, Estrogen↑, LH↓, FSH↓","Progesterone↓, Estrogen↓, LH↑, FSH↑","Progesterone↑, Estrogen↓, LH↑, FSH↓","All hormones decreased"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In pregnancy: Progesterone↑, Estrogen↑, LH↓, and FSH↓.  Why B wrong: This reverses all the changes.  Why C wrong: Estrogen is increased and LH is decreased, not as stated.  Why D wrong: Progesterone and estrogen are increased, not all decreased."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Which hormone is responsible for the insulin resistance seen in pregnancy?","options":["HPL (Human Placental Lactogen)","Progesterone","FSH","Oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Insulin resistance in pregnancy is due to the hormone HPL (Human Placental Lactogen).  Why B wrong: Progesterone has other effects but is not named as the cause of insulin resistance here.  Why C, D wrong: FSH and oxytocin are not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Insulin resistance in pregnancy is maximum at which gestational age?","options":["24–28 weeks","11–13 weeks","16–18 weeks","34–36 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Insulin resistance is maximum at 24–28 weeks of pregnancy.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated peak of 24–28 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the physiological purpose of insulin resistance in pregnancy?","options":["So that the mother does not utilize glucose, raising glucose that reaches the fetus","To lower maternal blood glucose","To prevent glucose from reaching the fetus","To increase maternal fat storage only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Insulin resistance ensures the mother does not utilize glucose → ↑glucose → it reaches the fetus.  Why B wrong: It raises, not lowers, available glucose.  Why C wrong: The purpose is to allow glucose to reach the fetus, not prevent it.  Why D wrong: The stated purpose is glucose delivery to the fetus, not fat storage."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Because of insulin resistance, pregnancy is described as a:","options":["Diabetogenic state","Hypoglycemic state","Ketogenic state","Hypercalcemic state"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Due to insulin resistance, pregnancy is a diabetogenic state.  Why B, C, D wrong: The note specifically calls pregnancy diabetogenic, not hypoglycemic, ketogenic, or hypercalcemic."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Bluish discoloration of the cervix in pregnancy is known as:","options":["Chadwick sign / Jacquemier sign","Goodell sign","Hegar sign","Osiander sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bluish discoloration of the cervix is the Chadwick sign (also called Jacquemier sign).  Why B wrong: Goodell sign refers to softening of the cervix.  Why C wrong: Hegar sign is softening of the lower uterine segment (not on this page as the answer).  Why D wrong: Osiander sign is not the stated term here."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"At approximately how many weeks does the Chadwick (Jacquemier) sign appear?","options":["8 weeks","6 weeks","12 weeks","4 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Chadwick/Jacquemier sign (bluish discoloration) appears at 8 weeks.  Why B wrong: 6 weeks is when the Goodell sign (soft cervix) appears.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 8 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"Softening of the cervix in pregnancy is termed:","options":["Goodell sign","Chadwick sign","Jacquemier sign","Show"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A soft cervix is called the Goodell sign.  Why B, C wrong: Chadwick/Jacquemier sign refer to bluish discoloration.  Why D wrong: \"Show\" is the release of the mucus plug with blood, not cervical softening."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"At approximately how many weeks does the Goodell sign (soft cervix) appear?","options":["6 weeks","8 weeks","10 weeks","4 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Goodell sign appears at 6 weeks.  Why B wrong: 8 weeks is when the Chadwick sign appears.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 6 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"During pregnancy the cervical canal is closed by a thick mucus plug. When this plug is released along with blood at the time of labor, it is called:","options":["Show","Lochia","Liquor","Caput"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At the onset of labor the cervix dilates and the mucus plug is released along with blood — this is called \"show.\"  Why B wrong: Lochia is postpartum vaginal discharge, not the mucus plug.  Why C wrong: Liquor refers to amniotic fluid.  Why D wrong: Caput refers to scalp swelling in the fetus."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Prenatal diagnostic sampling…","question":"What is the normal length of the cervix during pregnancy as given in the notes?","options":["4–5 cm","2–3 cm","6–7 cm","1–2 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cervix length is stated as 4–5 cm.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 4–5 cm."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Bluish discolouration of the vagina and cervix seen during pregnancy is known as:","options":["Goodell sign","Chadwick sign","Hegar sign","Osiander sign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states bluish discolouration of the vagina → CHADWICK sign, caused by increased vascularity/congestion in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Goodell sign refers to softening of the cervix, not bluish discolouration.  Why C wrong: Hegar sign is softening of the lower uterine segment/isthmus.  Why D wrong: Osiander sign refers to increased pulsation in the lateral fornices, not colour change."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Which organism present in the vagina increases in number during pregnancy and is responsible for maintaining vaginal acidity?","options":["Escherichia coli","Döderlein (Doderlein) bacilli","Staphylococcus aureus","Gardnerella vaginalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the number of Döderlein bacilli (Lactobacilli) increases in the vagina during pregnancy, driving glycogen conversion to lactic acid.  Why A wrong: E. coli is not the acid-producing commensal described.  Why C wrong: Staphylococcus aureus is a pathogen, not the glycogen-fermenting commensal.  Why D wrong: Gardnerella vaginalis is associated with bacterial vaginosis, not physiological acidity."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Döderlein bacilli maintain the acidic pH of the vagina by converting which substance into lactic acid?","options":["Glucose in cervical mucus","Glycogen present in the vaginal epithelium","Protein in vaginal secretions","Urea from urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Döderlein bacilli convert the glycogen present in vaginal epithelium into lactic acid, making the pH of vagina acidic.  Why A wrong: The substrate specified is glycogen in the vaginal epithelium, not glucose in cervical mucus.  Why C wrong: Protein is not the substrate for lactic acid production described.  Why D wrong: Urea from urine is unrelated to vaginal acidity."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"During pregnancy, increased conversion of glycogen into lactic acid by Döderlein bacilli leads to:","options":["Increased lactic acid and increased vaginal acidity","Decreased lactic acid and alkaline pH","No change in vaginal pH","Increased vaginal pH above 7"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows ↑conversion of glycogen into lactic acid → ↑lactic acid → ↑acidity.  Why B wrong: Lactic acid increases, not decreases; the pH becomes more acidic.  Why C wrong: The page explicitly shows an increase in acidity.  Why D wrong: pH becomes more acidic (lower), not alkaline/above 7."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"What is the pH of the vagina in a non-pregnant female according to the notes?","options":["3.5","4.5","5.5","7.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Non pregnant female PH of Vagina = 4.5\".  Why A wrong: 3.5 is the pH in the pregnant female, not non-pregnant.  Why C wrong: 5.5 is not given as any value on the page.  Why D wrong: 7.0 is neutral and not the stated vaginal pH."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"What is the pH of the vagina in a pregnant female according to the notes?","options":["4.5","3.5","6.5","7.4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"PH of vagina in Pregnant female: (3.5)\".  Why A wrong: 4.5 is the non-pregnant vaginal pH.  Why C wrong: 6.5 is not a value given on the page.  Why D wrong: 7.4 is blood pH, not vaginal pH."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Why is the chance of infection reduced in the vagina during pregnancy?","options":["Because increased acidity means pathogenic bacteria cannot survive","Because the vagina becomes alkaline","Because glycogen stops being produced","Because Döderlein bacilli disappear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states because of acidity, pathogenic bacteria cannot survive and so chances of infection are less.  Why B wrong: The vagina becomes more acidic, not alkaline.  Why C wrong: Glycogen production continues and is the substrate for acidity.  Why D wrong: Döderlein bacilli increase, not disappear."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"The breast is anatomically made up of which two main structures that produce and carry milk?","options":["Muscles and cartilage","Ducts and lobules","Bone and fatty tissue","Nerves and lymphatics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the breast is made of ducts and lobules; milk is synthesized in lobules and ducts carry milk from lobules to nipples.  Why A wrong: Muscles and cartilage are not the milk-producing/carrying structures.  Why C wrong: Fatty tissue is present but bone is not part of the breast structure.  Why D wrong: Nerves and lymphatics are not the milk-synthesizing/carrying units."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Where is milk synthesized in the breast?","options":["In the ducts","In the lobules (alveoli)","In the nipple","In the fatty tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states milk is synthesized in the lobules, and ducts carry the milk to the nipples.  Why A wrong: Ducts carry the milk; they do not synthesize it.  Why C wrong: The nipple is the outlet, not the site of synthesis.  Why D wrong: Fatty tissue provides support/bulk, not milk synthesis."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"During pregnancy, the increase in breast lobules is mainly due to which hormone(s)?","options":["Estrogen and progesterone (mainly progesterone)","Estrogen only","Oxytocin only","Prolactin only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states during pregnancy lobules increase due to action of estrogen and progesterone (mainly progesterone).  Why B wrong: Estrogen alone is responsible for ductal increase, not the main lobular growth.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin causes milk ejection, not lobular growth in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Prolactin drives milk production but is not credited here for lobular increase."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"During pregnancy, the increase in the breast ducts is due to which hormone?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states ducts increase due to estrogen.  Why A wrong: Progesterone mainly drives lobular increase, not ductal.  Why C wrong: Prolactin is for milk synthesis, not stated for ductal growth here.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is for milk let-down, not duct growth."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Which of the following changes occurs to the nipple during pregnancy?","options":["Size decreases and it becomes lighter","Size increases and it becomes darker","Size and colour remain unchanged","It retracts and disappears"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states size of nipples increase and becomes darker in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The nipple enlarges and darkens, opposite to shrinking/lightening.  Why C wrong: Clear changes in size and colour do occur.  Why D wrong: The nipple does not disappear."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Which change to the areola is seen during pregnancy?","options":["Becomes lighter and smaller","Becomes more dark, its size increases, and a secondary areola appears","Completely disappears","Becomes white with no size change"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states areola becomes more dark and its size increases during pregnancy, and a secondary areola can be seen.  Why A wrong: The areola darkens and enlarges, not lightens/shrinks.  Why C wrong: The areola does not disappear.  Why D wrong: It becomes darker, not white."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"The modified sebaceous glands on the areola that become prominent during pregnancy are called:","options":["Bartholin glands","Montgomery tubercles","Skene glands","Meibomian glands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states modified sebaceous glands called MONTGOMERY TUBERCLE become very prominent and their number increases during pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Bartholin glands are located at the vaginal introitus, not the areola.  Why C wrong: Skene glands are periurethral glands.  Why D wrong: Meibomian glands are sebaceous glands of the eyelid."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"According to the notes, the number of Montgomery tubercles in each breast increases to approximately:","options":["1–2","5–15","20–30","40–50"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Montgomery tubercle number increases to 5–15 in each breast.  Why A wrong: 1–2 is too few compared with the stated range.  Why C wrong: 20–30 exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 40–50 is far above the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Colostrum is best described as which of the following?","options":["A clear watery fluid","A yellow coloured fluid","A bluish milk","A green mucus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes colostrum as a yellow coloured fluid.  Why A wrong: Colostrum is yellow, not clear/watery.  Why C wrong: It is not bluish.  Why D wrong: It is not green mucus."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"From what gestational age can colostrum be squeezed from the breast?","options":["4 weeks of pregnancy","8 weeks of pregnancy","12 weeks of pregnancy","20 weeks of pregnancy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states colostrum can be squeezed from breast at 12 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 4 weeks is not given for colostrum expression.  Why B wrong: 8 weeks is not the stated timing.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks is later than the stated 12 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Regarding the consistency of colostrum during pregnancy, which statement is correct?","options":["It is initially thick and becomes thin later","It is initially thin and becomes thick & yellow at 16 weeks","It is always thick from the start","It never changes consistency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states colostrum is initially thin and becomes thick & yellow at 16 weeks.  Why A wrong: The progression is thin→thick, not thick→thin.  Why C wrong: It is initially thin, not thick from the start.  Why D wrong: It clearly changes from thin to thick."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Colostrum is especially rich in which of the following?","options":["Fat and carbohydrate","Vitamins and immunoglobulins","Potassium and fat","Water only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states colostrum is rich in vitamins and immunoglobulins.  Why A wrong: Fat and carbohydrate are actually lower in colostrum than in breast milk.  Why C wrong: Potassium and fat are lower in colostrum than breast milk.  Why D wrong: Colostrum is nutrient-rich, not just water."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Colostrum has everything more than breast milk EXCEPT (remembered as \"KFC\") — which three components are less in colostrum compared to breast milk?","options":["Potassium, Fat, Carbohydrate","Protein, Fat, Calcium","Potassium, Folate, Copper","Protein, Fluid, Carbohydrate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's \"KFC\" mnemonic lists K: Potassium, F: Fat, C: Carbohydrate as less in colostrum compared to breast milk.  Why B wrong: Protein and calcium are not the listed exceptions.  Why C wrong: Folate and copper are not the listed exceptions.  Why D wrong: Fluid and protein are not the listed exceptions."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Linea nigra is best described as:","options":["A butterfly-shaped facial pigmentation","A brown-black line extending from the xiphisternum up to the pubic symphysis","Silvery white stretch marks on the thighs","Bluish discolouration of the vagina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states linea nigra is a brown-black line which extends from xiphisternum up to the pubic symphysis.  Why A wrong: Butterfly-shaped facial pigmentation describes chloasma, not linea nigra.  Why C wrong: Silvery white stretch marks describe striae albicans.  Why D wrong: Bluish vaginal discolouration is the Chadwick sign."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Linea nigra during pregnancy is caused by which hormone?","options":["Progesterone","Melanocyte stimulating hormone","Insulin","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states linea nigra is due to melanocyte stimulating hormone.  Why A wrong: Progesterone is not credited for this pigmentation on the page.  Why C wrong: Insulin is unrelated to skin pigmentation here.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is for uterine contraction/milk ejection, not pigmentation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"What happens to linea nigra after delivery?","options":["It becomes permanent","It disappears after delivery","It turns into striae","It spreads to the face"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states linea nigra disappears after delivery.  Why A wrong: It is not permanent.  Why C wrong: It does not convert into striae.  Why D wrong: It does not spread to the face."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Striae gravidarum (stretch marks) are typically seen at which of the following sites?","options":["Below the umbilicus, breast, and thigh","Only on the face","Only on the palms and soles","Only on the back of the neck"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states striae are seen below the umbilicus, breast, and thigh.  Why B wrong: The face is the site of chloasma, not striae.  Why C wrong: Palms and soles are not the listed sites.  Why D wrong: The back of the neck is not a listed site."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"In an ongoing (current) pregnancy, the colour of the stretch marks (striae gravidarum) is:","options":["Silvery white","Pink","Black","Yellow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in ongoing pregnancy the striae are pink in color (STRIAE GRAVIDARUM).  Why A wrong: Silvery white indicates old marks of previous pregnancy (striae albicans).  Why C wrong: Black is not the described colour.  Why D wrong: Yellow is not the described colour."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Stretch marks from a previous pregnancy that appear silvery white are called:","options":["Striae gravidarum","Striae albicans","Linea nigra","Chloasma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states stretch marks of previous pregnancy appear silvery white → STRIAE ALBICANS.  Why A wrong: Striae gravidarum are the pink marks of an ongoing pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Linea nigra is the midline abdominal pigment line.  Why D wrong: Chloasma is facial pigmentation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Apart from pregnancy, stretch marks can also appear in which of the following conditions where the abdomen is stretched?","options":["Obesity and edema","Dehydration","Weight loss","Anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states stretch marks are seen whenever the abdomen is stretched, e.g. obesity, edema.  Why B wrong: Dehydration does not stretch the abdomen.  Why C wrong: Weight loss reduces stretch rather than causing it.  Why D wrong: Anemia is a blood disorder, not a cause of abdominal stretching."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Chloasma gravidarum, also known as the \"pregnancy mask,\" is characterized by:","options":["A midline abdominal line","Butterfly-shaped, diffuse patchy pigmented areas","Silvery white stretch marks","Bluish cervical discolouration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes chloasma gravidarum as butterfly-shaped, diffuse patchy areas.  Why A wrong: A midline abdominal line is linea nigra.  Why C wrong: Silvery white stretch marks are striae albicans.  Why D wrong: Bluish cervical discolouration is the Chadwick sign."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Chloasma gravidarum (pregnancy mask) is typically seen on which part of the body?","options":["Forehead and cheeks","Abdomen below umbilicus","Thighs and breasts","Palms and soles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states chloasma is seen on the forehead and cheeks.  Why B wrong: Abdomen below umbilicus is the site of striae/linea nigra.  Why C wrong: Thighs and breasts are sites of striae.  Why D wrong: Palms and soles are not the described sites."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"What is the fate of chloasma gravidarum after delivery?","options":["It becomes permanent","It disappears after delivery","It spreads over the entire body","It converts into striae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states chloasma disappears after delivery.  Why A wrong: It is not permanent.  Why C wrong: It does not spread over the whole body.  Why D wrong: It does not become striae."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"During pregnancy, the total blood volume increases. By what percentage does the plasma volume increase?","options":["10–20%","20–30%","40–50%","60–70%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states plasma volume increases by 40–50% in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 10–20% is below the stated plasma value.  Why B wrong: 20–30% is the RBC volume increase, not plasma.  Why D wrong: 60–70% exceeds the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"During pregnancy, by what percentage does the RBC volume increase?","options":["20–30%","40–50%","5–10%","60–70%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states RBC volume increases by 20–30% in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: 40–50% is the plasma volume increase, not RBC.  Why C wrong: 5–10% is below the stated RBC value.  Why D wrong: 60–70% exceeds the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"During pregnancy, the liquid (plasma) component of blood increases more than the solid component. This results in:","options":["Hemoconcentration","Hemodilution","Polycythemia","No change in blood composition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the liquid component increases more than the solid, so there is hemodilution during pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Hemoconcentration is the opposite of what occurs.  Why C wrong: Polycythemia (excess RBC) is opposite to the dilutional effect.  Why D wrong: There is a definite change (dilution)."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"As a result of hemodilution during pregnancy, blood viscosity:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Remains constant","Becomes zero"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows hemodilution → blood viscosity ↓ (decreases).  Why A wrong: Viscosity decreases due to dilution, it does not increase.  Why C wrong: It changes (decreases), not remains constant.  Why D wrong: Blood viscosity does not become zero."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Hematocrit (packed cell volume) during pregnancy:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Doubles","Stays exactly the same"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hematocrit/packed cell volume decreases in pregnancy due to hemodilution.  Why A wrong: It decreases, not increases, because plasma expansion dilutes cells.  Why C wrong: It does not double.  Why D wrong: It changes (decreases) rather than staying the same."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"The dilutional fall in hemoglobin concentration seen in all pregnant females is termed:","options":["Pathological anemia","Physiological (dilutional) anemia","Aplastic anemia","Hemolytic anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states there is physiological hemodilution leading to physiological anemia in pregnancy, with Hb concentration decreasing in all pregnant females.  Why A wrong: It is physiological, not pathological.  Why C wrong: Aplastic anemia results from marrow failure, not dilution.  Why D wrong: Hemolytic anemia is due to RBC destruction, not dilution."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"In physiological anemia of pregnancy, the hemoglobin level should never fall below:","options":["7 gm/dL","9 gm/dL","11 gm/dL","13 gm/dL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in physiological anemia, Hb never goes below 11 gm/dL.  Why A wrong: 7 gm/dL indicates severe pathological anemia, not physiological.  Why B wrong: 9 gm/dL is below the physiological cut-off given.  Why D wrong: 13 gm/dL is a normal non-dilutional value, not the lower limit."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Although RBC volume increases in pregnancy raising total Hb mass, the Hb concentration (gm/dL) decreases. What best explains this apparent paradox?","options":["Increased RBC destruction","Plasma volume expands proportionally more than RBC volume (hemodilution)","Iron overload","Decreased total Hb mass"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows RBC volume increase raises total Hb mass, yet plasma volume rises 40–50% versus RBC 20–30%, so the concentration (gm/dL) falls due to dilution.  Why A wrong: The fall is dilutional, not due to increased destruction.  Why C wrong: Iron overload is not the mechanism described.  Why D wrong: Total Hb mass actually increases; only concentration falls."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"During pregnancy, the white blood cell (WBC) count may physiologically rise up to approximately:","options":["11,000/mm³","40,000/mm³","80,000/mm³","1,50,000/mm³"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states WBC count increases up to 80,000/mm³ in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 11,000/mm³ is the upper limit of a normal non-pregnant count, below the stated value.  Why B wrong: 40,000/mm³ is below the stated maximum.  Why D wrong: 1,50,000/mm³ is not stated (and near platelet-count magnitudes)."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"The physiological fall in platelet count during pregnancy is referred to as:","options":["Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura","Benign gestational thrombocytopenia","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the platelet count decrease is called benign gestational thrombocytopenia.  Why A wrong: ITP is an autoimmune disorder, not the physiological pregnancy change described.  Why C wrong: TTP is a serious microangiopathic disorder.  Why D wrong: DIC is a consumptive coagulopathy, not a benign physiological change."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Regarding plasma proteins in pregnancy, which of the following statements is correct?","options":["Total plasma protein (gm) increases and globulin increases","Total plasma protein decreases and globulin decreases","Albumin increases and globulin decreases","Both total protein and globulin decrease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists total plasma protein (gm) and globulin ↑ under the \"increase in pregnancy\" column.  Why B wrong: Total plasma protein (gm) increases, it does not decrease.  Why C wrong: Albumin concentration decreases and globulin increases.  Why D wrong: Total protein and globulin increase, not decrease."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Which plasma protein concentration (gm/dL) DECREASES during pregnancy?","options":["Globulin","Albumin","Fibrinogen","C-reactive protein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists plasma protein concentration (gm/dL) — Albumin ↓ under the decrease column.  Why A wrong: Globulin increases in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Fibrinogen (a clotting factor) increases in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: C-reactive protein, an inflammatory marker, increases."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"What is the normal albumin-to-globulin (A:G) ratio in a non-pregnant person?","options":["1:1","1.7:1","1:1.7","3:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states normally albumin:globulin = 1.7:1.  Why A wrong: 1:1 is the ratio seen during pregnancy, not normally.  Why C wrong: 1:1.7 is the inverse and not the stated normal ratio.  Why D wrong: 3:1 is not a value given on the page."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"During pregnancy, the albumin-to-globulin (A:G) ratio changes to:","options":["1.7:1","1:1","2:1","1:3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states pregnancy albumin:globulin = 1:1 (because albumin falls and globulin rises).  Why A wrong: 1.7:1 is the normal non-pregnant ratio.  Why C wrong: 2:1 is not stated.  Why D wrong: 1:3 is not stated on the page."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Pregnancy is best described hematologically as which type of state with respect to coagulation?","options":["Hypocoagulable state","Hypercoagulable state","Anticoagulated state","Normocoagulable with no change"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states all clotting factors increase in pregnancy and that pregnancy is a hypercoagulable state.  Why A wrong: Clotting factors increase, making it hypercoagulable, not hypocoagulable.  Why C wrong: It is not anticoagulated; clotting potential rises.  Why D wrong: There is a definite pro-coagulant change."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"In pregnancy, all clotting factors increase EXCEPT which factors?","options":["Factor 8 and 9","Factor 11 and 13","Factor 5 and 7","Factor 2 and 10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states all clotting factors increase except Factor 11 and 13.  Why A wrong: Factors 8 and 9 are not the listed exceptions.  Why C wrong: Factors 5 and 7 are not the listed exceptions.  Why D wrong: Factors 2 and 10 are not the listed exceptions."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Which clotting factor (serum protein) is specifically mentioned as increased in pregnancy?","options":["Serum albumin","Serum fibrinogen","Serum ferritin","Serum transferrin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists clotting factor (S. fibrinogen) ↑ as part of the hypercoagulable state.  Why A wrong: Serum albumin decreases in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Serum ferritin is an iron parameter and decreases in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Serum transferrin is an iron transport protein, not the clotting factor referred to here."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"The erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) during pregnancy:","options":["Decreases below normal","Increases","Remains constant at 10 mm","Becomes zero"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows ESR ↑ in pregnancy (linked to increased fibrinogen).  Why A wrong: ESR increases, it does not decrease.  Why C wrong: It rises above the normal 10 mm value.  Why D wrong: ESR does not become zero."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"According to the notes, the ESR that is normally about 10 mm can rise in pregnancy up to approximately:","options":["20 mm","40 mm","60 mm","100 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states normal ESR 10 mm goes up to 40 mm in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 20 mm is below the stated maximum.  Why C wrong: 60 mm exceeds the stated value.  Why D wrong: 100 mm is far above the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Which of the following inflammatory mediators increase during pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["ESR","C-reactive protein","Albumin","Platelet count","Hematocrit"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists all inflammatory mediators increase → ESR and C-reactive protein.  Why C wrong: Albumin decreases in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Platelet count decreases (benign gestational thrombocytopenia).  Why E wrong: Hematocrit decreases due to hemodilution."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Which type of immunity INCREASES during pregnancy?","options":["Cell-mediated immunity","Humoral immunity","Innate immunity only","Neither increases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists humoral immunity under \"increase in pregnancy\".  Why A wrong: Cell-mediated immunity is listed under decrease in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Innate immunity is not the category specified.  Why D wrong: Humoral immunity does increase."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Which type of immunity DECREASES during pregnancy (helping prevent rejection of the fetus)?","options":["Humoral immunity","Cell-mediated immunity","Passive immunity","Mucosal immunity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists cell-mediated immunity under \"decrease in pregnancy\".  Why A wrong: Humoral immunity increases in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Passive immunity is not the category listed.  Why D wrong: Mucosal immunity is not the category listed."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Which of the following parameters remain CONSTANT during pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bleeding time","Clotting time","Plasma volume","ESR","WBC count"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists bleeding time and clotting time as constant in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Plasma volume increases 40–50%.  Why D wrong: ESR increases in pregnancy.  Why E wrong: WBC count increases up to 80,000/mm³."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"After delivery, the level of clotting factors becomes normal within approximately what time period?","options":["24 hours after delivery","1 week after delivery","2 weeks after delivery","6 weeks after delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the level of clotting factors becomes normal 2 weeks after delivery.  Why A wrong: 24 hours is too early per the notes.  Why B wrong: 1 week is not the stated period.  Why D wrong: 6 weeks is longer than the stated 2 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"In the body, iron absorption mainly occurs in which part of the gastrointestinal tract?","options":["Stomach","Duodenum and jejunum","Ileum","Colon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states iron absorption occurs in the duodenum and jejunum.  Why A wrong: The stomach is not the main site of iron absorption.  Why C wrong: The ileum is the site for vitamin B12/bile salt absorption, not the main iron site here.  Why D wrong: The colon absorbs water/electrolytes, not iron."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"In which chemical form is iron absorbed from the gut?","options":["Ferric (Fe³⁺)","Ferrous (Fe²⁺)","Elemental iron","Iron oxide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the form of Fe absorbed is ferrous (Fe²⁺).  Why A wrong: Ferric iron must be reduced to ferrous before absorption; it is not the absorbed form.  Why C wrong: Elemental iron is not the absorbable ionic form.  Why D wrong: Iron oxide is not the absorbed form."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Approximately what percentage of dietary iron is absorbed from the diet?","options":["1%","10%","30%","50%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states percentage of Fe absorbed from diet is 10%.  Why A wrong: 1% is below the stated value.  Why C wrong: 30% exceeds the stated value.  Why D wrong: 50% is far above the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"What is the storage form of iron in the body?","options":["Transferrin","Ferritin","Hemoglobin","Myoglobin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the storage form of Fe in the body is ferritin.  Why A wrong: Transferrin is the transport form, not the storage form.  Why C wrong: Hemoglobin is the functional oxygen-carrying form, not the storage form.  Why D wrong: Myoglobin stores oxygen in muscle, not iron per se in this context."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"What is the transport form of iron in the blood?","options":["Ferritin","Serum transferrin","Hemosiderin","Ceruloplasmin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the transport form of Fe is serum transferrin.  Why A wrong: Ferritin is the storage form, not transport.  Why C wrong: Hemosiderin is a storage/deposit form, not the transport protein.  Why D wrong: Ceruloplasmin transports copper, not the iron transport form named here."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"With respect to iron and the placenta, iron is:","options":["Passively diffused across the placenta","Actively transported across the placenta","Not transferred to the fetus","Excreted through the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Fe is actively transported across the placenta.  Why A wrong: It is active transport, not passive diffusion.  Why C wrong: Iron is transferred to the fetus.  Why D wrong: Iron is delivered to the fetus, not excreted."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"During pregnancy, most parameters of iron metabolism decrease. Which parameters specifically decrease? (Select all that apply)","options":["Serum ferritin","Serum iron (S. Fe)","Serum transferrin","Total iron binding capacity (TIBC)"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states all parameters of Fe metabolism decrease — S. ferritin and S. Fe both ↓.  Why C wrong: Serum transferrin is an exception that increases in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: TIBC is an exception that increases in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Which two iron-related parameters INCREASE in pregnancy (remembered as \"the 2 T's\")?","options":["Serum ferritin and serum iron","Serum transferrin and total iron binding capacity (TIBC)","Hemoglobin and hematocrit","Ferritin and hemosiderin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states except (the 2 'T's): serum transferrin ↑ and total iron binding capacity (TIBC) ↑.  Why A wrong: Serum ferritin and serum iron decrease.  Why C wrong: Hemoglobin and hematocrit fall due to hemodilution.  Why D wrong: Ferritin decreases; these are not the \"2 T's\"."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"What is the total amount of iron needed during pregnancy?","options":["300 mg","500 mg","1000 mg","1500 mg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states total amount of Fe needed in pregnancy is 1000 mg.  Why A wrong: 300 mg is the amount needed specifically by the fetus.  Why B wrong: 500 mg is not a value given on the page.  Why D wrong: 1500 mg exceeds the stated total."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"Out of the total iron needed in pregnancy, how much is needed by the fetus?","options":["100 mg","300 mg","500 mg","1000 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states out of the total amount, 300 mg is needed by the fetus.  Why A wrong: 100 mg is not the stated fetal requirement.  Why C wrong: 500 mg is not stated.  Why D wrong: 1000 mg is the total requirement, not just the fetal share."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"According to the notes (1000 mg over ~280 days), the daily iron requirement in a pregnant female is approximately:","options":["1 mg/day","4–6 mg/day","10 mg/day","15 mg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page calculates daily requirement of Fe in pregnant female = 1000/280 = 4–6 mg/day.  Why A wrong: 1 mg/day is the non-pregnant female requirement.  Why C wrong: 10 mg/day is not the calculated value.  Why D wrong: 15 mg/day is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"What is the daily iron requirement in a non-pregnant female according to the notes?","options":["1 mg/day","2 mg/day","4 mg/day","6 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page notes daily requirement of Fe in non-pregnant female is 1 mg/day.  Why B wrong: 2 mg/day is not the stated non-pregnant value.  Why C wrong: 4 mg/day is within the pregnant female range.  Why D wrong: 6 mg/day is the upper pregnant requirement, not non-pregnant."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"The iron requirement during pregnancy is not constant. In the 1st trimester, the daily iron requirement is approximately:","options":["1–2 mg/day","4–6 mg/day","6–8 mg/day","10 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states this Fe requirement is not constant in pregnancy and that in the 1st trimester it is 1–2 mg/day.  Why B wrong: 4–6 mg/day is the average daily requirement, not the 1st-trimester value.  Why C wrong: 6–8 mg/day is not stated for the 1st trimester.  Why D wrong: 10 mg/day is not stated for the 1st trimester."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Vaginal changes in pregnancy…","question":"A key reason the daily iron requirement in pregnancy (4–6 mg/day) is higher than in a non-pregnant female (1 mg/day) is that in pregnancy iron must additionally supply:","options":["The fetus (300 mg of the total 1000 mg)","The vaginal flora","The Montgomery tubercles","The colostrum only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows total 1000 mg is needed in pregnancy, with 300 mg required by the fetus, explaining the higher daily requirement versus the 1 mg/day non-pregnant need.  Why B wrong: Vaginal flora do not create the iron demand described.  Why C wrong: Montgomery tubercles are breast glands, unrelated to iron demand.  Why D wrong: Colostrum is not listed as the reason for increased iron demand here."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"What is the daily iron requirement during the 2nd trimester of pregnancy?","options":["2-3 mg/day","4-6 mg/day","6-7 mg/day","8-10 mg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the iron requirement in the 2nd trimester is 4-6 mg/day.  Why A wrong: 2-3 mg/day is below the stated 2nd-trimester requirement.  Why C wrong: 6-7 mg/day is the 3rd-trimester requirement, not the 2nd.  Why D wrong: 8-10 mg/day is not given for any trimester."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"What is the daily iron requirement during the 3rd trimester of pregnancy?","options":["4-6 mg/day","6-7 mg/day","10-12 mg/day","1-2 mg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 3rd-trimester iron requirement is 6-7 mg/day.  Why A wrong: 4-6 mg/day is the 2nd-trimester requirement.  Why C wrong: 10-12 mg/day is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: 1-2 mg/day is far below the stated requirement."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"What percentage of dietary iron is actually absorbed by the body?","options":["5%","10%","25%","50%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state only 10% of dietary iron is absorbed.  Why A wrong: 5% understates the stated absorption fraction.  Why C wrong: 25% overstates the absorption fraction.  Why D wrong: 50% is far above the stated 10%."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Because only 10% of dietary iron is absorbed, how much iron must a pregnant woman take in her diet to meet the body's need of 4-6 mg/day?","options":["30 mg","40 mg","60 mg","100 mg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a pregnant female would have to take 60 mg of iron in diet to fulfil the 4-6 mg/day requirement (because absorption is only 10%).  Why A wrong: 30 mg is below the stated dietary amount.  Why B wrong: 40 mg is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 100 mg is above the stated 60 mg."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Why is iron (Fe) supplementation considered mandatory during pregnancy according to the notes?","options":["Because iron is toxic in the diet","Because obtaining 60 mg of iron through diet alone is practically impossible","Because iron cannot be absorbed at all during pregnancy","Because pregnant women should avoid dietary iron"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Since taking 60 mg of iron in diet is practically impossible, Fe supplementation is mandatory during pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The notes do not state dietary iron is toxic.  Why C wrong: Iron is absorbed (about 10%), just inefficiently.  Why D wrong: Dietary iron is not to be avoided; supplementation is added because diet alone is insufficient."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Under which government programme does the Government of India supply Fe + Folic acid tablets free of cost?","options":["Janani Suraksha Yojana","Anemia Mukt Bharat Programme","Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan","Mission Indradhanush"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes name the Anemia Mukt Bharat Programme, under which the Government of India supplies Fe + Folic acid tablets free of cost.  Why A wrong: Janani Suraksha Yojana is a maternal cash-benefit scheme, not named here for free iron-folic acid supply.  Why C wrong: PMSMA provides antenatal check-ups, not named for this tablet supply.  Why D wrong: Mission Indradhanush is an immunization programme."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"What is the prophylactic dose of iron given as ferrous sulphate during pregnancy per the notes?","options":["200 mg Ferrous sulphate","400 mg Ferrous sulphate","600 mg Ferrous sulphate","1000 mg Ferrous sulphate"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the prophylactic dose of iron as 600 mg Ferrous sulphate.  Why A wrong: 200 mg is not the stated dose.  Why B wrong: 400 mg is not the stated dose (400 is the folic acid figure elsewhere, in mcg).  Why D wrong: 1000 mg is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"In the Anemia Mukt Bharat prophylactic combination, what is the dose of folic acid supplied with iron?","options":["400 mcg","500 mcg","4 mg","600 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list 500 mcg as the folic acid dose alongside 600 mg ferrous sulphate.  Why A wrong: 400 mcg is the daily preventive folic acid dose in all pregnant females, not the Anemia Mukt Bharat programme figure.  Why C wrong: 4 mg is the therapeutic dose for women with a prior NTD baby.  Why D wrong: 600 mg is the ferrous sulphate (iron) dose, not folic acid."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"What is the most common type of anemia in pregnancy in India, per the notes?","options":["Pure iron deficiency anemia","Iron + folic acid deficiency anemia (dimorphic anemia)","Sickle cell anemia","Aplastic anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common anemia in pregnancy in India is Fe + folic acid deficiency anemia, called dimorphic anemia — hence both iron and folic acid are supplied.  Why A wrong: It is combined iron AND folic acid deficiency, not pure iron deficiency.  Why C wrong: Sickle cell anemia is not stated as most common.  Why D wrong: Aplastic anemia is not the most common type."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"The combined iron and folic acid deficiency anemia most common in Indian pregnancy is termed:","options":["Microcytic anemia","Macrocytic anemia","Dimorphic anemia","Hemolytic anemia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specifically call the Fe + folic acid deficiency anemia \"Dimorphic Anemia.\"  Why A wrong: Microcytic describes pure iron deficiency, not the combined picture.  Why B wrong: Macrocytic describes pure folate/B12 deficiency, not the combined picture.  Why D wrong: Hemolytic anemia is unrelated to this deficiency description."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"From which month of pregnancy should the Fe + Folic acid tablets ideally be started?","options":["1st month","2nd month","4th month","7th month"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The recommendation states the Fe + folic acid tablets should be taken from the 4th month of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 1st month is not the recommended start for these tablets.  Why B wrong: 2nd month is not stated.  Why D wrong: 7th month is too late compared to the stated 4th month."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"How frequently should the Fe + Folic acid tablets ideally be taken during pregnancy?","options":["Weekly","Daily","Twice weekly","Monthly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The recommendation states the tablets should be taken daily throughout pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Weekly dosing is not the ideal stated schedule.  Why C wrong: Twice weekly is not stated.  Why D wrong: Monthly is far less frequent than the recommended daily intake."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"According to the notes, the Fe + Folic acid tablets should ideally be taken for at least how many days during pregnancy?","options":["90 days","100 days","180 days","270 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the tablets should be taken for at least 180 days during pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 90 days is below the stated minimum.  Why B wrong: 100 days is the older IFA guideline but not the figure given here.  Why D wrong: 270 days is not the stated duration."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"For how many days after delivery should iron-folic acid supplementation be continued, and why?","options":["90 days, to prevent PPH","180 days, to replenish iron stores","42 days, until the puerperium ends","365 days, to prevent osteoporosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes recommend continuing for 180 days after delivery to replenish iron stores.  Why A wrong: The stated purpose is replenishing iron stores, not preventing PPH, and duration is 180 days.  Why C wrong: 42 days is not the stated duration.  Why D wrong: 365 days and osteoporosis prevention are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"What is the primary role of folic acid supplementation in pregnancy?","options":["Prevents postpartum hemorrhage","Prevents Neural Tube Defects","Prevents gestational diabetes","Prevents preeclampsia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the role of folic acid in pregnancy is to prevent Neural Tube Defects (NTDs).  Why A wrong: PPH prevention is not the role of folic acid.  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes prevention is not attributed to folic acid.  Why D wrong: Preeclampsia prevention is not the stated role."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"What is the recommended daily dose of folic acid in all pregnant females (routine prevention of NTDs)?","options":["100 mcg/day","400 mcg/day","4 mg/day","600 mg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the folic acid dose in all pregnant females is 400 mcg/day.  Why A wrong: 100 mcg/day is below the stated dose.  Why C wrong: 4 mg/day is the therapeutic dose for women with a prior NTD baby.  Why D wrong: 600 mg refers to ferrous sulphate iron, not folic acid."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"When should folic acid be started and until when should it be continued for routine NTD prevention?","options":["Start at conception, stop at delivery","Start 1 month before conception and continue until 3 months after conception","Start only in the 3rd trimester","Start 6 months before conception and stop at conception"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise starting folic acid 1 month before conception and continuing until 3 months after conception.  Why A wrong: Ideal timing begins before conception, not at conception.  Why C wrong: The 3rd trimester is too late; the neural tube closes early.  Why D wrong: The stated window is 1 month before to 3 months after conception, not 6 months before."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"A pregnant woman has a history of a previous baby born with a neural tube defect (NTD). What is the recommended (therapeutic) dose of folic acid for her?","options":["400 mcg/day","500 mcg/day","4 mg/day","40 mg/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For a woman with a history of an NTD baby, the therapeutic dose of folic acid is 4 mg/day.  Why A wrong: 400 mcg/day is the routine preventive dose, not the therapeutic dose.  Why B wrong: 500 mcg/day is the Anemia Mukt Bharat programme dose.  Why D wrong: 40 mg/day is ten times the stated therapeutic dose."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"The 4 mg/day dose of folic acid given to a woman with a prior NTD baby is referred to as the:","options":["Prophylactic dose","Maintenance dose","Therapeutic dose","Loading dose"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the 4 mg/day dose as the therapeutic dose of folic acid.  Why A wrong: The prophylactic/preventive dose is 400 mcg/day.  Why B wrong: \"Maintenance dose\" is not the term used.  Why D wrong: \"Loading dose\" is not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Within the Anemia Mukt Bharat programme, folic acid is supplied at what dose and from what point in pregnancy?","options":["400 mcg/day from conception","500 mcg/day from the 4th month and throughout pregnancy","4 mg/day from the 1st month","600 mg/day from the 3rd trimester"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state folic acid is included in Anemia Mukt Bharat at 500 mcg/day, from the 4th month of pregnancy and throughout pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 400 mcg/day is the routine preventive dose, not the programme figure/timing.  Why C wrong: 4 mg/day is the therapeutic dose for a prior NTD baby.  Why D wrong: 600 mg refers to iron, not folic acid."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which formula correctly expresses cardiac output as taught in the notes?","options":["Cardiac Output = Heart Rate ÷ Stroke Volume","Cardiac Output = Heart Rate × Stroke Volume","Cardiac Output = Stroke Volume ÷ Heart Rate","Cardiac Output = Heart Rate + Stroke Volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Output = Heart Rate × Stroke Volume.  Why A wrong: Cardiac output is a product, not a quotient.  Why C wrong: Dividing stroke volume by heart rate is incorrect.  Why D wrong: The two are multiplied, not added."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"At what week of pregnancy does cardiac output start to increase?","options":["3rd week","5th week","12th week","20th week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state cardiac output starts increasing at the 5th week of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 3rd week is earlier than stated.  Why C wrong: 12th week is later than the stated start.  Why D wrong: 20th week is well after the stated onset."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"According to Dutta, cardiac output during pregnancy is maximum at:","options":["20-24 weeks","28-32 weeks","30-34 weeks","36-40 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state per Dutta cardiac output is maximum at 30-34 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 20-24 weeks is not the Dutta figure.  Why B wrong: 28-32 weeks is the Williams figure, not Dutta.  Why D wrong: 36-40 weeks is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"According to Williams, cardiac output during pregnancy is maximum at:","options":["24-28 weeks","28-32 weeks","30-34 weeks","34-38 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state per Williams cardiac output is maximum at 28-32 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 24-28 weeks is not stated.  Why C wrong: 30-34 weeks is the Dutta figure.  Why D wrong: 34-38 weeks is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Overall, when is cardiac output maximum (and chances of heart failure highest) in a pregnant/parturient woman, per Bertan?","options":["During the 1st stage of labour","Immediately after delivery (+70%)","At 28-32 weeks","During the 2nd trimester"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per Bertan, cardiac output is maximum immediately after delivery, rising by +70%, when heart failure risk is highest.  Why A wrong: The 1st stage of labour is not the peak point stated.  Why C wrong: 28-32 weeks is only the 3rd best (antenatal) peak.  Why D wrong: The 2nd trimester is when BP falls most, not when output peaks."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"By approximately what percentage does cardiac output increase during the 2nd stage of labour (the \"2nd best\" peak)?","options":["+20%","+50%","+70%","+100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the 2nd stage of labour as the 2nd best peak with a +50% increase in cardiac output.  Why A wrong: +20% is not the stated value.  Why C wrong: +70% is the increase immediately after delivery.  Why D wrong: +100% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which is listed as the \"3rd best\" period of maximum cardiac output during pregnancy?","options":["5th week","20-24 weeks (both authors)","28-32 weeks (William) or 30-34 weeks (Dutta)","Immediately after delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the 3rd best peak as 28-32 weeks (William) or 30-34 weeks (Dutta).  Why A wrong: The 5th week is when output only begins to rise.  Why B wrong: 20-24 weeks is not stated.  Why D wrong: Immediately after delivery is the overall (1st) peak per Bertan."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which hormone is described as the \"main hormone in pregnancy\" responsible for the fall in blood pressure?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","Oxytocin","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes name progesterone as the main hormone in pregnancy, acting as a smooth muscle relaxant that lowers peripheral vascular resistance and thus BP.  Why A wrong: Estrogen is not the hormone named in this BP-lowering pathway.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin causes uterine contraction, not the BP fall described.  Why D wrong: Prolactin governs lactation, not this vascular effect."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Progesterone lowers blood pressure in pregnancy mainly because it acts as a:","options":["Vasoconstrictor","Smooth muscle relaxant","Diuretic","Positive inotrope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe progesterone as a smooth muscle relaxant, which decreases peripheral vascular resistance and lowers BP.  Why A wrong: A vasoconstrictor would raise, not lower, BP.  Why C wrong: Progesterone's stated effect is smooth-muscle relaxation, not diuresis.  Why D wrong: A positive inotrope increases contractility, not the mechanism described."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"In the progesterone pathway, the fall in blood pressure during pregnancy is directly due to a decrease in:","options":["Blood volume","Peripheral vascular resistance","Heart rate","Stroke volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes trace: progesterone → smooth muscle relaxant → peripheral vascular resistance ↓ → blood pressure ↓.  Why A wrong: Blood volume actually increases in pregnancy; it is not the stated cause of the BP fall here.  Why C wrong: Heart rate rises in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: The pathway cites peripheral vascular resistance, not stroke volume."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"During pregnancy, which of the following blood pressure parameters decrease? (Select all that apply)","options":["Systolic BP","Diastolic BP","Mean arterial pressure","Central venous pressure only","None of them change"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state systolic BP ↓, diastolic BP ↓, and mean arterial pressure ↓ all decrease during pregnancy.  Why D wrong: The notes do not single out central venous pressure as the only decrease; systolic, diastolic, and MAP all fall.  Why E wrong: BP parameters do change (they decrease)."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which formula for Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) is given in the notes?","options":["MAP = (Systolic + Diastolic) / 2","MAP = (Systolic + 2 × Diastolic) / 3","MAP = (2 × Systolic + Diastolic) / 3","MAP = Systolic - Diastolic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give MAP = (Systolic + 2(Diastolic BP)) / 3.  Why A wrong: A simple average of systolic and diastolic is not the formula shown.  Why C wrong: The multiplier of 2 applies to diastolic, not systolic.  Why D wrong: Systolic minus diastolic is the pulse pressure, not MAP."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"In which BP component is the maximum decrease seen during pregnancy?","options":["Systolic BP","Diastolic BP","Pulse pressure","Mean arterial pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maximum decrease in BP is seen in diastolic BP.  Why A wrong: Systolic BP falls, but the maximum fall is in diastolic BP.  Why C wrong: Pulse pressure is not stated as the site of maximum decrease.  Why D wrong: MAP falls but the greatest decrease is specifically in diastolic BP."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"During which trimester is the maximum decrease in blood pressure seen?","options":["First trimester","Second trimester","Third trimester","Immediately postpartum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maximum decrease in BP is seen in the second trimester.  Why A wrong: The first trimester is not the point of maximum BP fall.  Why C wrong: BP tends to rise back toward baseline in the 3rd trimester.  Why D wrong: Immediately postpartum is associated with a rise in cardiac output, not maximum BP fall."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"In which maternal position is the maximum decrease in blood pressure seen?","options":["Left lateral position","Supine position","Standing position","Right lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maximum decrease in BP is seen in the supine position (due to IVC compression).  Why A wrong: Left lateral position relieves IVC compression and is advised.  Why C wrong: Standing is not the stated position of maximum BP fall.  Why D wrong: Right lateral position is not the stated position."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"The figure on the page depicts fetal/uterine compression of which vessel in the supine position?","options":["Aorta","Inferior vena cava (IVC)","Renal artery","Portal vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure is captioned \"Fetal compression IVC in supine position,\" i.e., compression of the inferior vena cava.  Why A wrong: The figure specifies the IVC, not the aorta.  Why C wrong: The renal artery is not the vessel depicted.  Why D wrong: The portal vein is not involved in this mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"In a woman in late 3rd trimester who lies supine, the pregnant uterus presses on the IVC. What is the immediate hemodynamic consequence?","options":["Venous return increases","Venous return decreases, lowering maternal cardiac output","Peripheral resistance rises sharply","Heart rate falls to bradycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows: uterus presses on IVC → venous return decreases → cardiac output of mother decreases.  Why A wrong: Venous return decreases, not increases.  Why C wrong: The described mechanism is reduced venous return, not a rise in peripheral resistance.  Why D wrong: Bradycardia is not the stated consequence."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"The fall in maternal BP from IVC compression in the supine position leads to a clinical condition called:","options":["Supine hypertension syndrome","Supine hypotension syndrome","Orthostatic hypotension","Postural tachycardia syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the resulting BP fall as Supine Hypotension Syndrome.  Why A wrong: It is hypotension (low BP), not hypertension.  Why B wrong (distractor check): correct as stated.  Why C wrong: Orthostatic hypotension relates to standing up, not supine positioning.  Why D wrong: POTS is a distinct tachycardia disorder, not this syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Besides lowering maternal cardiac output, IVC compression in the supine position reduces blood flow to the fetus, resulting in:","options":["Fetal macrosomia","Fetal distress","Polyhydramnios","Fetal tachycardia only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows: volume of blood going to fetus decreases → fetal distress.  Why A wrong: Macrosomia is unrelated to acute IVC compression.  Why C wrong: Polyhydramnios is an amniotic fluid excess, not the stated consequence.  Why D wrong: The notes state fetal distress generally, not isolated fetal tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"To avoid supine hypotension syndrome, in which position should a pregnant woman ideally lie?","options":["Supine position","Right lateral position","Left lateral position","Prone position"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise that the pregnant female should not lie supine and the maximum lie should be in the left lateral position (relieving IVC compression).  Why A wrong: Supine is exactly the position to avoid.  Why B wrong: The notes specify left lateral, not right lateral.  Why D wrong: Prone position is impractical/unsafe in late pregnancy and not advised."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"How does the pulse rate change as a normal clinical indicator of the heart during pregnancy?","options":["Decreases","Increases","Remains exactly the same","Becomes irregular"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Pulse Rate ↑ (increased) as a normal change in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Pulse rate rises, not falls.  Why C wrong: It changes (increases), not stays identical.  Why D wrong: An irregular pulse is not a normal pregnancy finding; it would suggest arrhythmia/heart disease."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"How does blood pressure change as a normal clinical indicator during pregnancy?","options":["Increases","Decreases","Remains unchanged","Rises only in the 2nd trimester"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Blood Pressure ↓ (decreased) as a normal change in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: BP falls, not rises, in normal pregnancy.  Why C wrong: It decreases rather than staying unchanged.  Why D wrong: BP falls most in the 2nd trimester; it does not rise then."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"What is the normal status of jugular venous pressure (JVP) during pregnancy?","options":["Markedly raised","Normal","Markedly decreased","Absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Jugular Venous Pressure as \"Normal\" during pregnancy.  Why A wrong: A raised JVP would suggest heart disease, not a normal pregnancy change.  Why C wrong: JVP is not markedly decreased; it is normal.  Why D wrong: JVP is not absent."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"How is the first heart sound (S1) described in normal pregnancy?","options":["Soft and muffled","Loud with splitting prominent","Absent","Fixed and split only on expiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state S1 = Loud + splitting prominent in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: S1 becomes loud, not soft/muffled.  Why C wrong: S1 is present and accentuated.  Why D wrong: The notes describe prominent splitting, not fixed expiratory splitting."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"How is the second heart sound (S2) described in normal pregnancy per these notes?","options":["Loud and split","Normal in pregnancy","Absent","Replaced by a murmur"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state S2 = Normal in pregnancy (in the \"normal clinical indicators\" list).  Why A wrong: A loud, split S2 is listed under indicators of heart DISEASE, not normal pregnancy.  Why C wrong: S2 is present and normal.  Why D wrong: S2 is not replaced by a murmur."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"In pregnancy, the third heart sound (S3), normally not easily heard, becomes easily audible and is called:","options":["Ejection click","Gallop rhythm","Opening snap","Pericardial rub"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in pregnancy S3 is easily heard and is called the Gallop Rhythm.  Why A wrong: An ejection click is a different sound, not S3.  Why C wrong: An opening snap is associated with mitral stenosis, not physiological S3.  Why D wrong: A pericardial rub is a friction sound, not S3."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"How many murmurs are described as normal (physiological) in pregnancy?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state there are 2 murmurs which are normal in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Two, not one, are listed.  Why C wrong: Three is more than stated.  Why D wrong: Four is more than stated."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which two murmurs are considered normal (physiological) in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Systolic murmur","Continuous murmur (mammary murmur)","Diastolic murmur","Pansystolic murmur of mitral regurgitation","Austin Flint murmur"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list systolic murmur and continuous (mammary) murmur as the two normal murmurs in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: A diastolic murmur is listed as an indicator of heart DISEASE, not a normal finding.  Why D wrong: A pansystolic mitral regurgitation murmur is pathological, not physiological.  Why E wrong: The Austin Flint murmur is pathological and not mentioned in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"The continuous murmur that is normal in pregnancy is also known as the:","options":["Graham Steell murmur","Mammary murmur","Still's murmur","Carey Coombs murmur"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the continuous murmur = Mammary murmur.  Why A wrong: The Graham Steell murmur relates to pulmonary regurgitation, not this.  Why C wrong: Still's murmur is an innocent childhood murmur, not the mammary murmur.  Why D wrong: The Carey Coombs murmur relates to acute rheumatic fever, not pregnancy's mammary murmur."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Why is the mammary (continuous) murmur heard in pregnancy?","options":["Due to valve calcification","Due to increased blood flow in the internal mammary vessels","Due to pericardial effusion","Due to septal defect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the mammary murmur is heard because of increased blood flow in the internal mammary vessels.  Why A wrong: Valve calcification is a pathological cause, not the reason given.  Why C wrong: Pericardial effusion is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: A septal defect is not the stated cause of the mammary murmur."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Where is the mammary (continuous) murmur best heard?","options":["Right 2nd intercostal space","2nd/3rd left intercostal space","5th left intercostal space at the apex","Xiphoid region"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes locate the mammary murmur at the 2nd/3rd left intercostal space.  Why A wrong: The right 2nd space is the aortic area, not where the mammary murmur is located here.  Why C wrong: The 5th left space (apex) is where the apex beat is felt, not the mammary murmur location given.  Why D wrong: The xiphoid region is not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"In pregnancy, the enlarging uterus pushes the diaphragm upward. As a result, the heart rotates:","options":["Downward and to the right","Upward and outward toward the left side","Directly backward","Downward and to the left"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus pushes the diaphragm up, causing the heart to rotate upwards & outwards to the left-hand side.  Why A wrong: The heart moves up and left, not down and right.  Why C wrong: It does not simply move directly backward.  Why D wrong: It rotates upward (not downward) and to the left."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which of the following results from the heart rotating upward and outward in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Palpitations felt in pregnancy","Apex beat shifted to the 4th ICS, 2.5 cm lateral to the mid-clavicular line","ECG showing left axis deviation","Absent heart sounds","Right axis deviation on ECG"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes attribute palpitations, a shifted apex beat (4th ICS, 2.5 cm lateral to mid-clavicular line), and left axis deviation on ECG to the positional change of the heart.  Why D wrong: Heart sounds are not absent; they are accentuated.  Why E wrong: The notes specify left axis deviation, not right."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"In pregnancy, where is the apex beat heard due to the positional shift of the heart?","options":["5th ICS at the mid-clavicular line","4th ICS, 2.5 cm lateral to the mid-clavicular line","3rd ICS at the sternal border","6th ICS in the anterior axillary line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the apex beat is heard in the 4th ICS, 2.5 cm lateral to the mid-clavicular line in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The 5th ICS at the mid-clavicular line is the normal non-pregnant apex location, not the pregnancy shift described.  Why C wrong: The 3rd ICS at the sternal border is not the stated location.  Why D wrong: The 6th ICS anterior axillary line is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"The ECG axis change caused by the physiological rotation of the heart in pregnancy is:","options":["Right axis deviation","Left axis deviation","Extreme axis deviation","No change in axis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state ECG shows Left Axis deviation in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The shift is to the left, not right.  Why C wrong: Extreme axis deviation is not stated.  Why D wrong: The axis does change (leftward)."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"On chest X-ray during normal pregnancy, the heart typically appears as:","options":["Marked cardiomegaly","Mild cardiomegaly","A normal-sized heart with no change","Boot-shaped heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that on X-ray the heart appears as mild cardiomegaly in normal pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Marked cardiomegaly is an indicator of heart DISEASE, not a normal pregnancy finding.  Why C wrong: There is a mild apparent enlargement, not no change.  Why D wrong: A boot-shaped heart suggests Tetralogy of Fallot, not physiological pregnancy change."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which of the following is an indicator of heart DISEASE (not a normal change) in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cyanosis","Hemoptysis","Raised JVP","Diastolic murmur","Mild cardiomegaly on X-ray"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cyanosis, hemoptysis, raised JVP, and a diastolic murmur are all listed as indicators of heart disease in pregnancy.  Why E wrong: Mild cardiomegaly is a normal pregnancy X-ray finding; it is MARKED cardiomegaly that indicates heart disease."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which of the following is listed as an indicator of heart disease in pregnancy relating to the second heart sound?","options":["S2 = normal","S2 = loud & splitting prominent","S2 = absent","S2 = soft single sound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Among the indicators of heart disease, the notes list S2 = loud & splitting prominent.  Why A wrong: A normal S2 is the normal pregnancy finding, not a disease indicator.  Why C wrong: An absent S2 is not the listed indicator.  Why D wrong: A soft single S2 is not listed as an indicator here."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"The appearance of which heart sound is a listed indicator of heart disease in pregnancy?","options":["S1","S2","S3","S4"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"S4 is heard\" as an indicator of heart disease in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: A loud S1 is a normal pregnancy change.  Why B wrong: S2 becoming loud/split is listed, but the specifically appearing new sound indicative of disease is S4.  Why C wrong: An easily heard S3 (gallop) is a normal pregnancy change, not a disease indicator."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"What is the status of jugular venous pressure (JVP) as an indicator of heart disease in pregnancy?","options":["Normal","Raised","Absent","Fluctuating with respiration only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Under indicators of heart disease, JVP is raised (the notes' \"sed.\" = raised), unlike the normal JVP of physiological pregnancy.  Why A wrong: A normal JVP is the physiological pregnancy finding, not the disease indicator.  Why C wrong: JVP is raised, not absent.  Why D wrong: Simple respiratory fluctuation is not the listed disease indicator."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which of the following is listed as an indicator of heart disease in pregnancy involving heart rhythm?","options":["Sinus rhythm","Any arrhythmia","Gallop rhythm","Regular pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Any arrhythmia\" as an indicator of heart disease in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Normal sinus rhythm is not a disease indicator.  Why C wrong: A gallop rhythm (audible S3) is a normal pregnancy finding.  Why D wrong: A regular pulse is normal, not a disease indicator."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"In developed countries, what is the most common cause of heart disease in pregnancy?","options":["Rheumatic heart disease","Congenital heart disease","Ischemic heart disease","Cardiomyopathy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in developed countries the most common cause of heart disease in pregnancy is congenital heart disease.  Why A wrong: Rheumatic heart disease is most common in developing countries, not developed.  Why C wrong: Ischemic heart disease is not the stated most common cause.  Why D wrong: Cardiomyopathy is not the stated most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which specific congenital lesion is cited as the most common cause of heart disease in pregnancy in developed countries?","options":["Ventricular septal defect","Atrial septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus","Tetralogy of Fallot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify atrial septal defect (ASD) under congenital heart disease in developed countries.  Why A wrong: VSD is not the lesion specified in the notes.  Why C wrong: PDA is not the lesion specified.  Why D wrong: Tetralogy of Fallot is not the lesion specified."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"In developing countries, what is the most common cause of heart disease in pregnancy?","options":["Congenital heart disease","Rheumatic heart disease","Peripartum cardiomyopathy","Hypertensive heart disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in developing countries the most common cause of heart disease in pregnancy is rheumatic heart disease.  Why A wrong: Congenital heart disease is most common in developed countries.  Why C wrong: Peripartum cardiomyopathy is not the stated most common cause.  Why D wrong: Hypertensive heart disease is not the stated most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"Which specific valvular lesion is cited as the most common cause of heart disease in pregnancy in developing countries?","options":["Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify mitral stenosis under rheumatic heart disease in developing countries.  Why B wrong: Aortic stenosis is not the lesion specified.  Why C wrong: Mitral regurgitation is not the specified lesion.  Why D wrong: Aortic regurgitation is not the specified lesion."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"This section of the notes (\"Maternal Adaptation in Pregnancy Part-3\") primarily focuses on changes in which body system?","options":["Respiratory system","Cardiovascular system (CVS)","Renal system","Gastrointestinal system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The heading is \"Changes in CVS during Pregnancy,\" i.e., the cardiovascular system.  Why A wrong: The respiratory system is not the focus of this section.  Why C wrong: The renal system is not the focus here.  Why D wrong: The GI system is not the focus here."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"A woman in her second trimester reports feeling faint whenever she lies flat on her back. The most appropriate nursing advice, based on these notes, is to:","options":["Lie flat in the supine position","Turn to the left lateral position","Stand up immediately","Lie in the prone position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because supine positioning causes IVC compression and supine hypotension, the notes advise the left lateral position as the ideal lie.  Why A wrong: Remaining supine perpetuates IVC compression and the faintness.  Why C wrong: Standing suddenly does not address the stated mechanism and may worsen symptoms.  Why D wrong: The prone position is not advised in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 033 · Iron requirement in pregnancy, dietary iron absorption…","question":"A pregnant woman's ECG shows left axis deviation and a chest X-ray shows mild cardiomegaly, but she has no cyanosis, normal JVP, and only a soft systolic murmur. These findings are best interpreted as:","options":["Definite signs of underlying heart disease","Normal physiological adaptations of pregnancy","Signs of rheumatic mitral stenosis","Evidence of an atrial septal defect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Left axis deviation, mild cardiomegaly, normal JVP, and a systolic murmur are all listed as normal physiological changes of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Disease indicators (cyanosis, raised JVP, diastolic murmur, marked cardiomegaly, S4, arrhythmia) are absent.  Why C wrong: Mitral stenosis would present with a diastolic murmur and other disease indicators, which are absent.  Why D wrong: ASD is a disease cause; the described benign findings do not establish it."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the most common heart disease encountered in pregnancy?","options":["Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Coarctation of aorta","Eisenmenger syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common heart disease in pregnancy is Mitral stenosis.  Why B wrong: Aortic stenosis is a cesarean indication, not the most common heart disease in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Coarctation of aorta is a condition where pregnancy is contraindicated, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Eisenmenger syndrome carries the worst prognosis but is not the most common heart disease."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which of the following heart conditions makes pregnancy contraindicated?","options":["Mitral stenosis","Primary pulmonary hypertension","Congenital heart disease with good repair","Mild mitral regurgitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary hypertension (primary, and secondary e.g. Eisenmenger syndrome) is listed as a heart disease in which pregnancy is contraindicated.  Why A wrong: Mitral stenosis is the most common but not listed as an absolute contraindication.  Why C wrong: Congenital heart disease actually has the best prognosis in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Mild mitral regurgitation is not among the contraindicated conditions listed."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Under pulmonary hypertension contraindicating pregnancy, which is given as an example of the SECONDARY type?","options":["Marfan syndrome","Eisenmenger syndrome","Coarctation of aorta","Mitral stenosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list secondary pulmonary hypertension with the example \"Eisenmenger syndrome.\"  Why A wrong: Marfan syndrome (with aorta involved) is a separate contraindicated condition.  Why C wrong: Coarctation of aorta is listed separately, associated with increased maternal mortality.  Why D wrong: Mitral stenosis is the most common heart disease, not an example of pulmonary hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Marfan syndrome contraindicates pregnancy specifically when which structure is involved?","options":["Mitral valve","Aorta","Pulmonary artery","Tricuspid valve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"MARFAN syndrome with Aorta involved\" as a condition where pregnancy is contraindicated.  Why A wrong: The listed involvement is the aorta, not the mitral valve.  Why C wrong: Pulmonary artery involvement is not the criterion stated for Marfan syndrome.  Why D wrong: The tricuspid valve is not mentioned in relation to Marfan syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Coarctation of aorta in pregnancy is associated with which of the following?","options":["Increased chances of maternal mortality","Complete cure of hypertension","No effect on the fetus","Guaranteed vaginal delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state coarctation of aorta leads to \"Increase chances of maternal mortality.\"  Why B wrong: Coarctation does not cure hypertension; it worsens maternal risk.  Why C wrong: The notes emphasize maternal mortality risk, contradicting \"no effect.\"  Why D wrong: Coarctation of aorta is actually a cesarean-section indication, not an assurance of vaginal delivery."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"If pregnancy must be terminated in a heart disease patient, what is the BEST time to perform MTP (medical termination of pregnancy)?","options":["Before 12 weeks","Between 16 and 20 weeks","After 24 weeks","Only at term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best time to do MTP in a heart disease patient is \"Before 12 weeks.\"  Why B wrong: 16–20 weeks is later than the recommended earliest, lower-risk window.  Why C wrong: After 24 weeks is well beyond the recommended MTP period and higher-risk.  Why D wrong: Termination at term is not an MTP; delivery risks would be maximal."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the best method to perform MTP in a heart disease patient?","options":["Suction evacuation","Hysterotomy","Prostaglandin induction","Dilatation and curettage under general anaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best method to do MTP in a heart disease patient is \"Suction Evacuation.\"  Why B wrong: Hysterotomy is a major surgery with greater hemodynamic stress, not preferred.  Why C wrong: Prostaglandin induction is not the method named in the notes.  Why D wrong: Sharp curettage is not the stated preferred method (suction evacuation is)."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which heart disease has the WORST prognosis / highest maternal mortality rate in pregnancy?","options":["Mitral stenosis","Eisenmenger syndrome","Aortic regurgitation","Congenital heart disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Eisenmenger syndrome has the worst prognosis / highest maternal mortality rate.  Why A wrong: Mitral stenosis is the most common but not the highest-mortality condition here.  Why C wrong: Aortic regurgitation is not identified as the worst-prognosis lesion.  Why D wrong: Congenital heart disease actually has the BEST prognosis in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which heart disease has the BEST prognosis in pregnancy?","options":["Congenital heart disease","Eisenmenger syndrome","Mitral stenosis","Coarctation of aorta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes answer \"Which heart disease has best prognosis in pregnancy?\" with \"Congenital Heart disease.\"  Why B wrong: Eisenmenger syndrome has the worst prognosis.  Why C wrong: Mitral stenosis is the most common lesion, not the best-prognosis one named.  Why D wrong: Coarctation of aorta increases maternal mortality."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the best mode of delivery in a heart disease patient?","options":["Elective cesarean section for all","Vaginal delivery using forceps/vacuum","Emergency cesarean at term","Unassisted spontaneous vaginal delivery with prolonged pushing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best mode of delivery in a heart disease patient is \"Vaginal delivery using FORCEP/vacuum\" (assisted second stage to reduce maternal effort/strain).  Why A wrong: Cesarean is reserved for specific indications (aorta involvement), not routine.  Why C wrong: Emergency cesarean is not the default best mode.  Why D wrong: Prolonged maternal pushing increases cardiac strain; assisted delivery is preferred to shorten the second stage."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"In a heart disease patient, cesarean section is specifically indicated in which situation?","options":["When the aorta is involved","Whenever mitral stenosis is present","In all first pregnancies","When congenital heart disease is present"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state cesarean section is done \"When Aorta is involved\" (e.g., coarctation of aorta, aortic stenosis, aortic aneurysm).  Why B wrong: Mitral stenosis does not by itself mandate cesarean; vaginal assisted delivery is preferred.  Why C wrong: First pregnancy alone is not a cesarean indication.  Why D wrong: Congenital heart disease has the best prognosis and does not automatically require cesarean."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which of the following are aorta-involving conditions in which cesarean section is done for a cardiac patient? (Select all that apply)","options":["Coarctation of aorta","Aortic stenosis","Aortic aneurysm","Mitral stenosis","Primary pulmonary hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list coarctation of aorta, aortic stenosis, and aortic aneurysm under \"When Aorta is involved\" as cesarean indications.  Why D wrong: Mitral stenosis involves the mitral valve, not the aorta.  Why E wrong: Primary pulmonary hypertension is a pregnancy contraindication, not listed as an aorta-involvement cesarean indication."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"A heart disease patient develops postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) after delivery. Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED?","options":["Injection Methyl Ergometrine","Injection Oxytocin","Misoprostol","Tranexamic acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed note states \"Heart disease patient... Post Partum Haemorrhage (PPH)... Injection Methyl Ergometrine is Contraindicated\" (ergometrine causes vasoconstriction/raises BP, dangerous in cardiac patients).  Why B wrong: Oxytocin is not named as contraindicated in the note.  Why C wrong: Misoprostol is not the contraindicated agent named.  Why D wrong: Tranexamic acid is not the contraindicated agent named."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Regarding warfarin as an anticoagulant in pregnancy, which statement about its ADVANTAGE is correct?","options":["It is a strong anticoagulant","It does not cross the placenta","It is a weak anticoagulant","It never causes fetal malformation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The comparison table lists warfarin's advantage as being a \"Strong Anticoagulant.\"  Why B wrong: Warfarin CAN cross the placenta; not crossing the placenta is heparin's advantage.  Why C wrong: Being a weak anticoagulant is heparin's disadvantage, not warfarin's advantage.  Why D wrong: Warfarin can cross the placenta and cause fetal malformation."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the main DISADVANTAGE of warfarin in pregnancy?","options":["It can cross the placenta and lead to fetal malformation","It is a weak anticoagulant","It is contraindicated in breastfeeding","It cannot be used at any gestation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists warfarin's disadvantage as \"It can cross the placenta & lead to fetal malformation.\"  Why B wrong: Being weak is heparin's disadvantage, not warfarin's.  Why C wrong: The notes explicitly say warfarin is NOT contraindicated in breastfeeding.  Why D wrong: Warfarin is actually the anticoagulant of choice from 13–36 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the main ADVANTAGE of heparin as an anticoagulant in pregnancy?","options":["It doesn't cross the placenta, so it doesn't lead to fetal malformation","It is a strong anticoagulant","It can be given orally","It stops labour pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists heparin's advantage as \"Doesn't cross placenta → Doesn't lead to fetal malformation.\"  Why B wrong: Being a strong anticoagulant is warfarin's advantage; heparin is weak.  Why C wrong: The notes do not describe heparin as oral; its safety in pregnancy is due to not crossing the placenta.  Why D wrong: Heparin has no role in stopping labour pain."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the main DISADVANTAGE of heparin in pregnancy?","options":["Weak anticoagulant","Crosses the placenta","Causes fetal malformation","Contraindicated in breastfeeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists heparin's disadvantage as being a \"Weak Anticoagulant.\"  Why B wrong: Heparin does NOT cross the placenta (that is its advantage).  Why C wrong: Heparin does not cause fetal malformation; that is warfarin's risk.  Why D wrong: The breastfeeding-safety point in the notes refers to warfarin, not a heparin disadvantage."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"In the first trimester (<12 weeks), which anticoagulant is the drug of choice?","options":["Heparin","Warfarin","Aspirin","Clopidogrel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The gestation table states 1st trimester (<12 weeks) → Heparin (it doesn't cross the placenta, avoiding teratogenicity during organogenesis).  Why B wrong: Warfarin is the choice from 13–36 weeks, not the first trimester.  Why C wrong: Aspirin is not listed as the anticoagulant of choice here.  Why D wrong: Clopidogrel is not part of this gestation-based anticoagulation scheme."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"From 13 weeks to 36 weeks of gestation, which anticoagulant is the drug of choice?","options":["Warfarin","Heparin","Enoxaparin only","No anticoagulant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The gestation table states 13 weeks → 36 weeks → Warfarin.  Why B wrong: Heparin is preferred in the 1st trimester and at ≥36 weeks, not the 13–36 week window.  Why C wrong: Enoxaparin is not the agent named for this window in the notes.  Why D wrong: An anticoagulant (warfarin) is indicated in this period."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"At ≥36 weeks of gestation, which anticoagulant is preferred and why?","options":["Heparin, because it is a weak anticoagulant and the patient can deliver anytime","Warfarin, because it is strong","Heparin, because it crosses the placenta","Warfarin, because it is safe near delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states ≥36 weeks → Heparin, with the reason \"because heparin is a weak anticoagulant & patient can deliver anytime\" (easier to manage bleeding at delivery).  Why B wrong: Warfarin's strength and placental crossing make it unsafe near delivery.  Why C wrong: Heparin does NOT cross the placenta; this is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: The safe-near-delivery agent is heparin, not warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"When a patient on anticoagulation starts having labour pain, what should be done regarding heparin?","options":["Stop heparin","Double the heparin dose","Switch to warfarin","Continue heparin unchanged"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"When patient starts having labor pain → Stop Heparin.\"  Why B wrong: Increasing anticoagulation before delivery raises bleeding risk.  Why C wrong: Warfarin is more hazardous near delivery; switching is not advised.  Why D wrong: Continuing heparin during active labour increases hemorrhage risk."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"After delivery, when is heparin/warfarin restarted?","options":["12 hours after vaginal delivery or 24 hours after cesarean section","Immediately after delivery","48 hours after any delivery","Only after discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"12 hours after vaginal delivery or 24 hours after Cesarean section → Start Heparin/Warfarin.\"  Why B wrong: Immediate restart increases postpartum bleeding risk.  Why C wrong: 48 hours is not the interval given in the notes.  Why D wrong: Restart is timed to hours after delivery, not to discharge."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which statement about warfarin and breastfeeding is correct per the notes?","options":["Warfarin is not contraindicated in breastfeeding","Warfarin is absolutely contraindicated in breastfeeding","Warfarin must be stopped before lactation","Warfarin passes heavily into breast milk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"Warfarin is not contraindicated in breast feeding.\"  Why B wrong: This directly contradicts the note.  Why C wrong: There is no such stopping requirement stated.  Why D wrong: The note's point is that warfarin is safe during breastfeeding."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"If a patient is on warfarin at the time of delivery, what is the recommended mode of delivery?","options":["Cesarean section","Forceps vaginal delivery","Vacuum vaginal delivery","Spontaneous vaginal delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Suppose patient is on warfarin at time of delivery → Do Cesarean section\" (warfarin crosses the placenta, increasing fetal intracranial haemorrhage risk with vaginal delivery).  Why B wrong: Forceps delivery increases fetal trauma/bleeding risk on a warfarinized fetus.  Why C wrong: Vacuum delivery similarly risks fetal haemorrhage.  Why D wrong: Spontaneous vaginal delivery is not advised when the fetus is anticoagulated by transplacental warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"For mitral stenosis requiring intervention during pregnancy, which surgery is done?","options":["Balloon valvuloplasty (balloon valvotomy)","Mitral valve replacement with mechanical valve","Coronary artery bypass grafting","Aortic valve replacement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in-pregnancy treatment/surgery = \"Balloon Valeriloplasty\" (balloon valvuloplasty/valvotomy) for mitral stenosis.  Why B wrong: Valve replacement is not the pregnancy treatment named here.  Why C wrong: CABG addresses coronary disease, not mitral stenosis.  Why D wrong: The lesion is mitral (not aortic) stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"During which period is balloon valvuloplasty ideally performed for mitral stenosis in pregnancy?","options":["In the 2nd trimester, between 14–18 weeks","In the 1st trimester, before 12 weeks","In the 3rd trimester, after 36 weeks","During active labour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify balloon valvuloplasty \"In 2nd Trimester (between 14–18 weeks).\"  Why B wrong: The stated window is the second trimester, not before 12 weeks.  Why C wrong: After 36 weeks is not the recommended timing.  Why D wrong: Active labour is not the timing given for elective valvuloplasty."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"By how much does the size of the kidney increase during pregnancy?","options":["By 1 cm","By 2 cm","By 4 cm","It decreases by 1 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The renal-system diagram states \"Size of the kidney increases by 1 cm.\"  Why B wrong: 2 cm is the increase in the transverse diameter of the chest, not the kidney.  Why C wrong: 4 cm is how much the diaphragm pushes the lungs up, not kidney size.  Why D wrong: The kidney enlarges, it does not shrink."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which of the following is a renal-tract change in pregnancy caused by progesterone acting as a smooth muscle relaxant?","options":["Hydroureter","Reduced GFR","Kidney shrinkage","Increased serum creatinine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram lists hydroureter and urinary stasis as due to progesterone (a smooth muscle relaxant).  Why B wrong: GFR actually increases in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: The kidney enlarges by 1 cm, it does not shrink.  Why D wrong: Serum creatinine decreases in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Hydroureter and urinary stasis in pregnancy are attributed to which hormone?","options":["Progesterone (smooth muscle relaxant)","Estrogen","Oxytocin","Prolactin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly label these as \"Due to progesterone (smooth muscle Relaxant).\"  Why B wrong: Estrogen is not the smooth-muscle relaxant named here.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin stimulates uterine contraction and is not the cause of ureteric dilatation stated.  Why D wrong: Prolactin governs lactation, not ureteric smooth muscle relaxation here."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Urinary stasis during pregnancy increases the chance of which complication?","options":["Urinary tract infection (UTI)","Renal calculi dissolution","Decreased bladder pressure","Hydronephrosis resolution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Due to urinary stasis → ↑chances of UTI.\"  Why B wrong: Stasis promotes infection, not stone dissolution.  Why C wrong: Bladder pressure increases in pregnancy, it does not decrease.  Why D wrong: Urinary stasis and hydroureter reflect dilatation, not resolution."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which of the following bladder changes occur in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bladder mucosa becomes congested","Bladder pressure increases","Intraurethral pressure increases","Bladder capacity permanently disappears","Intraurethral pressure falls to zero"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram lists bladder mucosa congested, bladder pressure increases, and intraurethral pressure increases.  Why D wrong: Bladder capacity is affected but does not \"permanently disappear.\"  Why E wrong: Intraurethral pressure increases, it does not fall to zero."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"During which trimesters is urinary frequency increased in pregnancy?","options":["1st trimester and 3rd trimester","Only the 2nd trimester","Only the 1st trimester","Only the 3rd trimester"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list urinary frequency ↑ in the 1st trimester and the 3rd trimester.  Why B wrong: The 2nd trimester alone is not listed; frequency eases as the uterus rises out of the pelvis.  Why C wrong: Both 1st and 3rd trimesters are listed, not the 1st alone.  Why D wrong: Both 1st and 3rd trimesters are listed, not the 3rd alone."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the sequence of renal haemodynamic changes in pregnancy?","options":["↑Blood volume → ↑Renal blood flow → ↑GFR → ↑filtering capacity","↓Blood volume → ↓Renal blood flow → ↓GFR","↑Blood volume → ↓Renal blood flow → ↓GFR","No change in renal blood flow or GFR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows increased blood volume in pregnancy → ↑renal blood flow → ↑GFR → ↑filtering capacity.  Why B wrong: Both blood volume and GFR increase, not decrease.  Why C wrong: Renal blood flow increases along with blood volume.  Why D wrong: There is a clear increase in renal blood flow and GFR."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Because of the increased GFR in pregnancy, excretion of which substances is INCREASED? (Select all that apply)","options":["Urea","Uric acid","Creatinine","Glucose is never affected","Sodium is fully retained"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list increased excretion of urea, uric acid, and creatinine due to the raised GFR.  Why D wrong: The notes address urea, uric acid, and creatinine; the glucose claim is not the listed content and is stated as an absolute.  Why E wrong: The notes do not state full sodium retention; the increased-excretion list is urea, uric acid, creatinine."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"As a consequence of increased renal excretion in pregnancy, what happens to serum urea, serum uric acid, and serum creatinine?","options":["They decrease","They increase","They remain unchanged","Only serum creatinine increases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show serum urea, serum uric acid, and serum creatinine all \"Decreases (↓)\" because of the raised GFR/excretion.  Why B wrong: These serum values fall, not rise, in normal pregnancy.  Why C wrong: They change (decrease), not remain unchanged.  Why D wrong: All three decrease; creatinine does not increase."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"By how much does the transverse diameter of the chest increase in pregnancy?","options":["2 cm","4 cm","6 cm","1 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"2 cm = Transverse diameter of Chest increases by 2 cm.\"  Why B wrong: 4 cm is how much the diaphragm pushes the lungs up.  Why C wrong: 6 cm is the increase in chest circumference.  Why D wrong: 1 cm is the increase in kidney size, not chest transverse diameter."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"By how much does the diaphragm push the lungs up in pregnancy?","options":["4 cm","2 cm","6 cm","8 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"4 cm = Diaphragm pushes the lungs up by 4 cm.\"  Why B wrong: 2 cm is the increase in the transverse diameter of the chest.  Why C wrong: 6 cm is the increase in chest circumference.  Why D wrong: 8 cm is not a value given."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"By how much does the circumference of the chest increase in pregnancy?","options":["6 cm","2 cm","4 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"6 cm = Circumference of chest Increase by 6 cm.\"  Why B wrong: 2 cm is the transverse-diameter increase.  Why C wrong: 4 cm is the diaphragm elevation.  Why D wrong: 10 cm is not a value given."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the normal subcostal angle, and what does it become in pregnancy?","options":["Normal = 68°, pregnancy = 103°","Normal = 103°, pregnancy = 68°","Normal = 90°, pregnancy = 120°","Normal = 68°, pregnancy = 68°"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the subcostal angle is normally 68° and increases to 103° in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed; the angle widens in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: 90° and 120° are not the values given.  Why D wrong: The angle changes to 103°, it does not stay at 68°."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which respiratory parameters INCREASE during pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Inspiratory capacity (IC)","Tidal volume (TV)","Minute ventilation (MV)","Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV)","Vital capacity (VC)"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list IC (inspiratory capacity), TV (tidal volume), and MV (minute ventilation) as parameters that increase.  Why D wrong: Inspiratory reserve volume remains normal in pregnancy.  Why E wrong: Vital capacity remains normal in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which respiratory parameters REMAIN NORMAL (unchanged) during pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV)","Respiratory rate (RR)","Vital capacity (VC)","Tidal volume (TV)","Minute ventilation (MV)"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list IRV (inspiratory reserve volume), RR (respiratory rate), and VC (vital capacity) as parameters that remain normal.  Why D wrong: Tidal volume increases in pregnancy.  Why E wrong: Minute ventilation increases in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Although the respiratory rate remains normal in pregnancy, what increases and what is the resulting acid–base effect?","options":["Depth of respiration increases → more CO2 flushed out → respiratory alkalosis","Depth of respiration decreases → CO2 retention → respiratory acidosis","Respiratory rate doubles → metabolic acidosis","No change occurs in respiration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state RR remains normal but the depth of respiration increases → more CO2 is flushed out → \"Respiratory Alkalosis during Pregnancy.\"  Why B wrong: Depth increases (not decreases); CO2 is washed out, causing alkalosis not acidosis.  Why C wrong: The respiratory rate remains normal, and the disturbance is respiratory alkalosis, not metabolic acidosis.  Why D wrong: Respiration does change — depth increases."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"The physiological hyperventilation of pregnancy leads to which acid–base state?","options":["Respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Increased depth of respiration flushes out more CO2, producing respiratory alkalosis, as stated in the notes.  Why B wrong: CO2 is lost, not retained, so acidosis does not occur.  Why C wrong: The disturbance is respiratory (from CO2 loss), not metabolic.  Why D wrong: Metabolic acidosis is not produced by hyperventilation here."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which of the following gastrointestinal (SI/GI system) changes occur in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Increased GERD","Constipation","Vomiting (morning sickness)","Increased gastric emptying speed","Complete loss of appetite always"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list GERD ↑, constipation, and vomiting (morning sickness) as GI-system changes.  Why D wrong: Gastric emptying is slowed (contributing to GERD/constipation), not sped up.  Why E wrong: Complete loss of appetite is not listed among the GI changes."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"All liver enzymes decrease in pregnancy EXCEPT which one?","options":["Alkaline phosphatase","ALT (SGPT)","AST (SGOT)","Gamma-glutamyl transferase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state all liver enzymes decrease in pregnancy except alkaline phosphatase, which increases because it is also produced by the placenta.  Why B wrong: ALT decreases in pregnancy per the rule.  Why C wrong: AST decreases in pregnancy per the rule.  Why D wrong: GGT is not the exception named; alkaline phosphatase is."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Why does alkaline phosphatase rise in pregnancy?","options":["It is also produced by the placenta","The liver enlarges markedly","Renal clearance falls","It is released by the fetal kidney"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state alkaline phosphatase increases \"as it is also produced by placenta.\"  Why B wrong: The reason given is placental production, not liver enlargement.  Why C wrong: Renal clearance is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: The source is the placenta, not the fetal kidney."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What happens to adrenal hormones during pregnancy?","options":["They increase","They decrease","They remain unchanged","They are completely suppressed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The endocrine flow chart states adrenal hormones \"↑in Pregnancy.\"  Why B wrong: They rise, not fall.  Why C wrong: They change (increase), not stay the same.  Why D wrong: They are increased, not suppressed."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Regarding the pancreas and insulin in pregnancy, which of the following is correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["Insulin resistance develops in pregnancy","Insulin secretion increases","Insulin secretion completely stops","Insulin resistance decreases below non-pregnant levels","The pancreas is destroyed"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show pancreas → insulin → insulin resistance in pregnancy AND insulin secretion ↑.  Why C wrong: Insulin secretion increases; it does not stop.  Why D wrong: Resistance increases in pregnancy, it does not fall below baseline.  Why E wrong: The pancreas is not destroyed; its insulin secretion rises."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"All pituitary hormones increase in pregnancy EXCEPT which two?","options":["LH and FSH (both decrease)","ACTH and TSH","Prolactin and GH","Oxytocin and ADH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state all pituitary hormones increase in pregnancy except LH (↓) and FSH (↓).  Why B wrong: ACTH/TSH are not listed as the decreasing exceptions.  Why C wrong: Prolactin and GH are not the named exceptions.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin and ADH are not listed as the decreasing exceptions."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which hormone's alpha-subunit is similar to TSH and therefore stimulates the thyroid in pregnancy?","options":["hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin)","hPL (human placental lactogen)","Progesterone","Estrogen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the α-subunit of hCG is similar to TSH and stimulates the thyroid.  Why B wrong: hPL is not described as having a TSH-like alpha-subunit here.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is a steroid hormone without a TSH-like subunit.  Why D wrong: Estrogen is a steroid hormone, not the TSH-mimicking glycoprotein described."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"As a result of hCG stimulating the thyroid, what happens to total T3 and total T4 in pregnancy?","options":["Both total T3 and total T4 increase","Both decrease","Only total T3 increases","Both remain unchanged"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show that hCG stimulation of the thyroid raises Total T3 ↑ and Total T4 ↑.  Why B wrong: Total T3 and T4 rise, they do not fall.  Why C wrong: Both, not just T3, increase.  Why D wrong: They change (increase), not remain unchanged."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Despite rising total T3 and T4, why do pregnant women remain euthyroid?","options":["Thyroid binding globulin also increases, so free T3 and free T4 remain normal","The thyroid gland is removed","hCG blocks all thyroid activity","Free T3 and free T4 both fall to zero"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state thyroid binding globulin also increases, so Free T3 = Normal and Free T4 = Normal → pregnant females are euthyroid.  Why B wrong: The thyroid gland is present and stimulated, not removed.  Why C wrong: hCG stimulates (does not block) the thyroid.  Why D wrong: Free hormone levels stay normal, they do not fall to zero."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the daily iodine requirement during pregnancy?","options":["250 mcg/day","150 mcg/day","500 mcg/day","25 mcg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the iodine requirement in pregnancy is 250 mcg/day.  Why B wrong: 150 mcg/day is the general adult requirement, not the pregnancy value given here.  Why C wrong: 500 mcg/day exceeds the stated requirement.  Why D wrong: 25 mcg/day is far below the stated requirement."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the most common cause of hypothyroidism during pregnancy in DEVELOPING countries?","options":["Iodine deficiency","Hashimoto's thyroiditis","Thyroidectomy","Radioactive iodine therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of hypothyroidism in pregnancy in developing countries is iodine deficiency.  Why B wrong: Hashimoto's thyroiditis is the leading cause in developed countries.  Why C wrong: Thyroidectomy is not the most common cause named.  Why D wrong: Radioactive iodine therapy is not the stated most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"What is the most common cause of hypothyroidism during pregnancy in DEVELOPED countries?","options":["Hashimoto's thyroiditis","Iodine deficiency","Iodine excess","Pituitary failure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause in developed countries is Hashimoto's thyroiditis.  Why B wrong: Iodine deficiency is the leading cause in developing countries.  Why C wrong: Iodine excess is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Pituitary failure is not the named cause."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"By what gestational age does the fetus start producing its own thyroxine?","options":["11–13 weeks of pregnancy","6–8 weeks of pregnancy","20–24 weeks of pregnancy","36–40 weeks of pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetus starts producing thyroxine by 11–13 weeks of pregnancy.  Why B wrong: 6–8 weeks is earlier than the stated onset.  Why C wrong: 20–24 weeks is later than the value given.  Why D wrong: 36–40 weeks is near term, well after the stated onset."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"The instruction note preceding the anticoagulant table indicates that the \"Most common heart disease in pregnancy: Mitral stenosis\" material should be placed where?","options":["Before the topic of anticoagulants in pregnancy","After the thyroid changes","At the end of the renal section","Within the APGAR scoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The starburst note reads \"INCLUDE BEFORE THE TOPIC OF ANTICOAGULANTS IN PREGNANCY,\" referring to the mitral stenosis material.  Why B wrong: The note places it before anticoagulants, not after thyroid content.  Why C wrong: It is tied to the anticoagulant/cardiac topic, not the renal section.  Why D wrong: APGAR scoring is unrelated to this placement note."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"A pregnant cardiac patient at 30 weeks needs anticoagulation. Based on the gestation-based scheme, which agent is appropriate?","options":["Warfarin","Heparin only","No anticoagulant is safe","Aspirin monotherapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At 30 weeks (within 13–36 weeks), warfarin is the anticoagulant of choice per the notes.  Why B wrong: Heparin is preferred in the 1st trimester and at ≥36 weeks, not at 30 weeks.  Why C wrong: Anticoagulation is indicated; warfarin is safe in this window per the notes.  Why D wrong: Aspirin is not the gestation-based anticoagulant named."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"A cardiac patient at 38 weeks is on warfarin and now enters labour. What are the appropriate immediate steps? (Select all that apply)","options":["Because she is on warfarin at delivery, plan cesarean section","At ≥36 weeks the preferred anticoagulant is heparin (not warfarin)","Continue high-dose warfarin through labour","Increase warfarin to speed delivery","Give injection methyl ergometrine prophylactically if cardiac"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a patient on warfarin at delivery should undergo cesarean, and that heparin (not warfarin) is preferred at ≥36 weeks because delivery may occur anytime.  Why C wrong: Warfarin should not be continued through delivery (fetal bleeding risk).  Why D wrong: Increasing anticoagulation raises hemorrhage risk.  Why E wrong: Methyl ergometrine is contraindicated in cardiac patients (and here would be prophylactic misuse)."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Heart disease in pregnancy, Anticoagulants in pregnancy…","question":"Which statement about renal physiology in pregnancy is INCORRECT?","options":["Serum creatinine increases due to reduced GFR","Renal blood flow increases","GFR increases","Excretion of urea, uric acid and creatinine increases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This is the INCORRECT statement — in pregnancy GFR increases and serum creatinine DECREASES; the option falsely claims an increase due to reduced GFR.  Why B wrong: This is true; renal blood flow does increase (so it is not the incorrect one to select).  Why C wrong: This is true; GFR increases.  Why D wrong: This is true; excretion of urea, uric acid, and creatinine increases."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"In a pregnant woman, on what does the fetus majorly depend for its brain development?","options":["Mother's thyroxine","Fetal insulin","Maternal cortisol","Fetal growth hormone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetus majorly depends on the mother's thyroxine for its brain development.  Why B wrong: Insulin does not drive fetal brain development in this context.  Why C wrong: Cortisol is not the hormone cited for fetal brain development.  Why D wrong: Fetal growth hormone is not the dependent factor described."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"What is the consequence in the fetus if a pregnant female has undiagnosed hypothyroidism?","options":["The fetus gets less thyroxine leading to mental retardation","The fetus develops macrosomia","The fetus develops hyperthyroidism","No effect on the fetus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Undiagnosed hypothyroidism → fetus gets less thyroxine → mental retardation in the fetus.  Why B wrong: Macrosomia is linked to maternal diabetes, not hypothyroidism.  Why C wrong: The fetus gets LESS thyroxine, not excess.  Why D wrong: There is a clear adverse effect (mental retardation)."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Mrs Reena, a diagnosed case of hypothyroidism on thyroxine, conceives. What advice regarding her thyroxine dose should be given?","options":["Increase her thyroxine dose by 30–40% from the day of conception","Stop thyroxine during pregnancy","Decrease her thyroxine dose by 30–40%","Continue the same dose without any change"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During pregnancy, from the day of conception, her thyroxine dose should be increased by 30–40%.  Why B wrong: Stopping thyroxine worsens hypothyroidism and endangers fetal brain development.  Why C wrong: The dose must be increased, not decreased, because demand rises in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: The requirement increases in pregnancy so an unchanged dose is inadequate."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"By what percentage is the thyroxine dose increased in a known hypothyroid patient once she becomes pregnant?","options":["30–40%","10–20%","50–60%","70–80%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify an increase of 30–40%.  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures do not match the stated 30–40% increase."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Bleeding in pregnancy occurring after 28 weeks of pregnancy and until delivery is termed:","options":["Antepartum hemorrhage","Abortion","Postpartum hemorrhage","Ectopic bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bleeding after 28 weeks of pregnancy and until delivery is classified as Antepartum Hemorrhage (APH).  Why B wrong: Abortion refers to early pregnancy loss (≤20 weeks).  Why C wrong: Postpartum hemorrhage occurs after delivery, not before.  Why D wrong: Ectopic bleeding is a specific early-pregnancy condition, not the post-28-week category."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which of the following are causes of bleeding in EARLY pregnancy according to the classification chart?","options":["Abortion, ectopic pregnancy, and molar pregnancy","Placenta previa and abruptio placenta","Postpartum atony","Cervical cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In early pregnancy, bleeding causes listed are Abortion, Ectopic Pregnancy, and Molar Pregnancy (all UPT positive).  Why B wrong: Placenta previa and abruptio placenta are causes of APH (after 28 weeks).  Why C wrong: Postpartum atony causes bleeding after delivery.  Why D wrong: Cervical cancer is not part of this early-pregnancy bleeding chart."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"The Urine Pregnancy Test (UPT) result in ectopic pregnancy is:","options":["Positive","Negative","Always false","Not applicable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows Ectopic Pregnancy UPT +ve (positive).  Why B wrong: UPT is positive, not negative, in ectopic pregnancy.  Why C wrong: It is a genuine positive, not false.  Why D wrong: UPT is applicable and positive."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"The Urine Pregnancy Test (UPT) result in molar pregnancy is:","options":["Positive","Negative","Weakly positive only after 28 weeks","Inconclusive"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows Molar Pregnancy UPT +ve (positive).  Why B wrong: UPT is positive in molar pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Molar pregnancy is an early-pregnancy condition; the 28-week reference is irrelevant.  Why D wrong: The result is clearly positive."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which two investigations are listed for evaluating bleeding in early pregnancy (e.g., in abortion)?","options":["hCG levels and USG (TVS)","ECG and chest X-ray","Liver function test and CT scan","Karyotyping and MRI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists (1) hCG levels and (2) USG (TVS — transvaginal sonography).  Why B wrong: ECG and chest X-ray are not the listed investigations.  Why C wrong: LFT and CT are not mentioned here.  Why D wrong: Karyotyping and MRI are not the early-pregnancy bleeding investigations listed."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"\"USG (TVS)\" used in early-pregnancy bleeding evaluation refers to which type of ultrasound?","options":["Transvaginal sonography","Transabdominal only","Doppler of umbilical artery","3D fetal facial scan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TVS stands for Transvaginal Sonography.  Why B wrong: TVS is specifically transvaginal, not transabdominal only.  Why C wrong: Doppler of the umbilical artery is a different study.  Why D wrong: A 3D facial scan is unrelated to TVS abbreviation."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Antepartum hemorrhage (bleeding after 28 weeks) is caused by which two conditions per the chart?","options":["Placenta previa and abruptio placenta","Ectopic and molar pregnancy","Abortion and cervical incompetence","PCOS and fibroids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antepartum Hemorrhage branches into Placenta previa and Abruptio placenta.  Why B wrong: Ectopic and molar are early-pregnancy causes.  Why C wrong: These are early/recurrent-loss causes, not APH.  Why D wrong: PCOS and fibroids are abortion-related causes, not APH."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"As per the definition given, abortion is any pregnancy loss occurring at:","options":["≤ 20 weeks","≤ 28 weeks","≤ 37 weeks","≤ 12 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Abortion is defined as any pregnancy loss at ≤ 20 weeks (or as per WHO).  Why B wrong: 28 weeks is the threshold for APH classification, not abortion.  Why C wrong: 37 weeks marks term; unrelated to abortion definition.  Why D wrong: 12 weeks is only the first-trimester limit, not the abortion definition cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"As per WHO, abortion is also defined as death of a fetus with a weight of:","options":["< 500 gms","< 1000 gms","< 2500 gms","< 250 gms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO defines abortion as death of fetus with weight < 500 gms.  Why B wrong: 1000 gms is not the WHO abortion weight cutoff.  Why C wrong: 2500 gms is the low-birth-weight threshold, not abortion.  Why D wrong: 250 gms is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Abortion is broadly classified into which two types?","options":["Spontaneous abortion and induced abortion","Complete and incomplete only","Threatened and inevitable only","Septic and missed only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart states abortion can be Spontaneous Abortion (Miscarriage) or Induced Abortion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are subtypes/clinical varieties, but the primary classification given here is spontaneous vs induced."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Spontaneous abortion is also known as:","options":["Miscarriage","Termination","Evacuation","Curettage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Spontaneous abortion is labelled \"(Miscarriage)\" in the chart.  Why B wrong: Termination implies an induced procedure.  Why C/D wrong: Evacuation and curettage are procedures, not synonyms for spontaneous abortion."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"A single episode of spontaneous abortion is termed:","options":["Isolated abortion","Recurrent abortion","Habitual abortion","Induced abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A single episode is called Isolated Abortion.  Why B wrong: Recurrent abortion requires ≥3 episodes.  Why C wrong: Habitual abortion is another term for recurrent, not single-episode.  Why D wrong: Induced abortion is a separate branch (not spontaneous)."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"How many episodes of abortion define recurrent abortion per the chart?","options":["≥ 3 times","≥ 2 times","≥ 5 times","Exactly 4 times"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart states ≥3 times abortion → Recurrent Abortion.  Why B wrong: 2 episodes is when investigation begins, not the definition of recurrent.  Why C/D wrong: These figures are not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Both isolated (single-episode) and recurrent abortions in the chart are each subdivided into:","options":["1st trimester and 2nd trimester abortion","Complete and incomplete","Early and late latent","Threatened and missed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Both branches split into 1st trimester abortion and 2nd trimester abortion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other clinical descriptors, not the subdivision shown here."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of isolated (single episode) abortion?","options":["Chromosomal anomaly in fetus (aneuploidy)","Maternal trauma","TORCH infection","Uterine fibroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause is a chromosomal anomaly in the fetus / germplasm defect / aneuploidy.  Why B wrong: Trauma is an \"other cause,\" not the most common.  Why C wrong: TORCH is an other (infectious) cause.  Why D wrong: Fibroid is a structural other cause, not the most common overall."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"The single BEST answer for the most common chromosomal cause of isolated abortion is:","options":["Aneuploidy","Trisomy","Monosomy X","Trisomy 16"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Best Answer\" listed is Aneuploidy.  Why B wrong: Trisomy is the 2nd best answer.  Why C wrong: Monosomy X is the 3rd best answer.  Why D wrong: Trisomy 16 is the 4th best answer."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"In the ranked list of chromosomal causes of isolated abortion, the 2nd best answer is:","options":["Trisomy","Aneuploidy","Monosomy X","Trisomy 16"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 2nd Best Answer = Trisomy.  Why B wrong: Aneuploidy is the 1st/best answer.  Why C wrong: Monosomy X is 3rd.  Why D wrong: Trisomy 16 is 4th."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"In the ranked list of chromosomal causes of isolated abortion, the 3rd best answer is:","options":["Monosomy X","Trisomy","Aneuploidy","Trisomy 16"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 3rd Best Answer = Monosomy X.  Why B wrong: Trisomy is 2nd.  Why C wrong: Aneuploidy is the best answer.  Why D wrong: Trisomy 16 is 4th."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"In the ranked list of chromosomal causes of isolated abortion, the 4th best answer is:","options":["Trisomy 16","Monosomy X","Trisomy","Aneuploidy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 4th Best Answer = Trisomy 16.  Why B wrong: Monosomy X is 3rd.  Why C wrong: Trisomy is 2nd.  Why D wrong: Aneuploidy is 1st/best."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which infection group is stated to cause a single episode of isolated abortion?","options":["TORCH infections","HIV","Tuberculosis","Malaria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Infection: TORCH can lead to a single episode of abortion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These specific infections are not listed as causes of isolated abortion here."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which of the following is listed as an endocrine cause of isolated abortion?","options":["Diabetes","Cushing's disease","Addison's disease","Acromegaly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetes is listed among endocrine causes of isolated abortion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These endocrine disorders are not in the listed causes."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which thyroid conditions are listed as endocrine causes of isolated abortion?","options":["Hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism","Only hypothyroidism","Only hyperthyroidism","Thyroid storm only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Both Hypothyroidism and Hyperthyroidism are listed as endocrine causes of isolated abortion.  Why B wrong: Both, not only hypothyroidism, are listed for isolated abortion.  Why C wrong: Both, not only hyperthyroidism, are listed.  Why D wrong: Thyroid storm is not a listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which of the following is an endocrine cause of isolated abortion related to ovarian dysfunction?","options":["PCOS","Ovarian torsion","Ovarian cyst rupture","Premature ovarian failure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PCOs (PCOS) is listed as an endocrine cause of isolated abortion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These ovarian conditions are not listed as causes."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"A decrease in which hormone is listed as an endocrine cause of isolated abortion?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased progesterone levels are listed as an endocrine cause of isolated abortion.  Why B wrong: Estrogen decrease is not the listed cause.  Why C wrong: Prolactin is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not a listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which two uterine malformations are illustrated as causes of isolated abortion?","options":["Septate uterus and bicornuate uterus","Arcuate uterus and unicornuate uterus","Didelphys and hypoplastic uterus","Retroverted and anteverted uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figures show Septate uterus and Bicornuate uterus as uterine malformations.  Why B/C wrong: These other Müllerian anomalies are not the ones illustrated.  Why D wrong: Retroversion/anteversion are positions, not malformations."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which is the MOST COMMON fibroid leading to abortion?","options":["Submucous fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Intramural fibroid","Cervical fibroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common fibroid leading to abortion is the submucous fibroid.  Why B wrong: Subserous fibroids are less likely to cause abortion.  Why C wrong: Intramural is not the most common cause of abortion here.  Why D wrong: Cervical fibroid is not the stated most common type."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Can TORCH infections lead to an isolated single episode of abortion?","options":["Yes","No","Only in the third trimester","Only with induced abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Q1 answer states TORCH infections CAN lead to isolated single episode of abortion — Yes.  Why B wrong: The answer is explicitly Yes.  Why C wrong: No trimester restriction is given for isolated abortion here.  Why D wrong: This relates to spontaneous isolated abortion, not induced."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Can hypothyroidism lead to isolated (single episode) abortion?","options":["Yes","No","Only if untreated for >5 years","Only in twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Q2 answer confirms hypothyroidism can lead to isolated abortion — Yes.  Why B wrong: The stated answer is Yes.  Why C/D wrong: No such qualifiers are given."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Can hyperthyroidism lead to isolated (single episode) abortion?","options":["Yes","No","Only in the first pregnancy","Never"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Q3 answer confirms hyperthyroidism can lead to isolated abortion — Yes.  Why B/D wrong: The answer is Yes.  Why C wrong: No such restriction is stated."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"The most common uterine malformation leading to abortion / recurrent abortion is:","options":["Septate uterus","Bicornuate uterus","Unicornuate uterus","Arcuate uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Q5 answer states the most common uterine malformation leading to abortion/recurrent abortion is septate uterus.  Why B wrong: Bicornuate is a malformation but not the most common cause stated.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common malformation."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Recurrent Abortion / Recurrent Pregnancy Loss (RPL) is defined as when a female has:","options":["3 or ≥3 episodes of abortion","2 episodes of abortion","1 episode of abortion","5 or more episodes only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RPL is when a female has 3 or ≥3 episodes of abortion.  Why B wrong: 2 episodes is the point at which investigation begins, not the definition.  Why C wrong: A single episode is isolated abortion.  Why D wrong: The threshold is 3, not 5."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of 1st trimester recurrent pregnancy loss (RPL)?","options":["Idiopathic","Cervical incompetence","Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome","Balanced translocation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 1st trimester RPL, the most common cause is Idiopathic.  Why B wrong: Cervical incompetence is a 2nd trimester RPL cause.  Why C wrong: APLA is an immunological cause under 2nd trimester RPL.  Why D wrong: Balanced translocation is the most common chromosomal abnormality of recurrent abortion but not the overall most common cause of 1st trimester RPL (idiopathic)."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Among \"other\" causes of 1st trimester RPL, chromosomal anomaly accounts for approximately what percentage?","options":["3–8%","10–15%","20–25%","30–40%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chromosomal anomaly is listed at 3–8% as an other cause of 1st trimester RPL.  Why B/C/D wrong: These percentages do not match the stated 3–8%."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which endocrine causes are listed as \"other\" causes of 1st trimester RPL?","options":["Diabetes and hypothyroidism","PCOS and hyperthyroidism","Adrenal insufficiency and diabetes","Hyperprolactinemia and hypothyroidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Endocrine cause (diabetes, hypothyroidism) is listed as an other cause of 1st trimester RPL.  Why B/C/D wrong: Only diabetes and hypothyroidism are specified for 1st trimester RPL here."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"2nd trimester RPL is most associated with which of the following?","options":["Uterine anomalies with cervical incompetence","Idiopathic causes","Aneuploidy","Submucous fibroid only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 2nd trimester RPL → Uterine anomalies with cervical incompetence.  Why B wrong: Idiopathic is the most common 1st trimester RPL cause.  Why C wrong: Aneuploidy is linked to isolated (single-episode) abortion.  Why D wrong: Submucous fibroid is a fibroid cause, not the specified 2nd trimester RPL association."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"The immunological cause of 2nd trimester RPL leads to which syndrome?","options":["Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APLA)","Sheehan syndrome","HELLP syndrome","Asherman syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Immunological cause → Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APLA) in 2nd trimester RPL.  Why B wrong: Sheehan syndrome is postpartum pituitary necrosis.  Why C wrong: HELLP is a pre-eclampsia complication.  Why D wrong: Asherman is intrauterine adhesions, not the listed immunological cause."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"What is the most common chromosomal anomaly which leads to a SINGLE episode of abortion?","options":["Aneuploidy","Balanced translocation","Monosomy X","Trisomy 16"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One-liner 1 states aneuploidy is the most common chromosomal anomaly leading to a single episode of abortion.  Why B wrong: Balanced translocation is linked to recurrent abortion, not single-episode.  Why C/D wrong: These are lower-ranked causes, not the most common for single episode."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"What is the most common chromosomal abnormality which leads to RECURRENT abortion?","options":["Balanced translocation of chromosome","Aneuploidy","Monosomy X","Trisomy 21"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One-liner 2 states balanced translocation of chromosome is the most common chromosomal abnormality leading to recurrent abortion.  Why B wrong: Aneuploidy is the most common in single-episode abortion.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common cause of recurrent abortion."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Regarding thyroid disorders and recurrent abortion, which statement is correct?","options":["Hypothyroidism can lead to recurrent abortion, but hyperthyroidism does not","Both hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism lead to recurrent abortion","Only hyperthyroidism leads to recurrent abortion","Neither leads to recurrent abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One-liners state hypothyroidism CAN lead to recurrent abortion, while hyperthyroidism does NOT lead to recurrent abortion.  Why B wrong: Hyperthyroidism does not cause recurrent abortion.  Why C wrong: It is hypothyroidism, not hyperthyroidism, that causes recurrent abortion.  Why D wrong: Hypothyroidism does cause recurrent abortion."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Do TORCH infections lead to recurrent abortion?","options":["No, TORCH infections don't lead to recurrent abortion","Yes, TORCH is the most common cause of recurrent abortion","Only Toxoplasma leads to recurrent abortion","Only Rubella leads to recurrent abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One-liner 5 states infections like TORCH don't lead to recurrent abortion (though they can cause a single/isolated episode).  Why B/C/D wrong: TORCH is not a cause of recurrent abortion per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Investigation for recurrent abortion should begin after how many episodes of abortion?","options":["2 episodes","1 episode","3 episodes","4 episodes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Remember\" note states investigation for recurrent abortion should begin after 2 episodes of abortion.  Why B wrong: One episode does not trigger investigation.  Why C wrong: 3 episodes defines recurrent, but investigation begins after 2.  Why D wrong: 4 is not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which investigation is performed on the parents of a patient with recurrent abortion?","options":["Karyotyping of parents","Torch test of parents","Genetic tattooing","Amniocentesis of parents"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Karyotyping of parents is listed as an investigation that is done in recurrent abortion.  Why B wrong: TORCH test is specifically listed under investigations NOT done.  Why C wrong: Not a real investigation.  Why D wrong: Amniocentesis is a fetal test, not a parental karyotype study listed here."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which blood sugar investigations are done for recurrent abortion workup?","options":["Fasting and post-prandial blood sugar","Oral glucose tolerance test only","HbA1c only","Random blood sugar only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fasting & post-prandial blood sugar is listed as an investigation done.  Why B/C/D wrong: These specific tests are not the ones listed; the notes specify fasting & post-prandial blood sugar."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which thyroid investigations are done in the recurrent abortion workup?","options":["TSH, T3, T4","Only TSH","Thyroid antibodies only","Radioactive iodine uptake"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TSH, T3, T4 are listed as investigations done for recurrent abortion.  Why B wrong: All three (TSH, T3, T4) are listed, not TSH alone.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the tests specified."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which imaging investigation is done in the recurrent abortion workup?","options":["USG (TVS)","CT abdomen","MRI brain","Hysterosalpingography only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: USG = (TVS) is listed as an investigation done.  Why B/C/D wrong: These imaging modalities are not the ones listed in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which antibody test is performed on the mother in the recurrent abortion workup?","options":["Phospholipid antibody testing","Anti-nuclear antibody only","Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody","Rh antibody titre"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phospholipid Ab testing in mother is listed as an investigation done (linked to APLA syndrome).  Why B/C/D wrong: These antibody tests are not the ones specified in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which investigation is specifically listed as NOT done in the recurrent abortion workup?","options":["TORCH test","Karyotyping","TSH, T3, T4","USG (TVS)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TORCH test is listed under \"investigations not done,\" because TORCH does not cause recurrent abortion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are all investigations that ARE done for recurrent abortion."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"The most common uterine malformation leading to recurrent pregnancy loss is:","options":["Septate uterus","Bicornuate uterus","Arcuate uterus","Unicornuate uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common malformation leading to recurrent pregnancy loss = septate uterus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common malformation for RPL."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"The most common fibroid which leads to abortion / recurrent abortion is:","options":["Submucous fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Intramural fibroid","Pedunculated fibroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most common fibroid leading to abortion/recurrent abortion = submucous fibroid.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common fibroid type."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Cervical incompetence is defined as:","options":["Painless, sudden dilatation of the internal os","Painful, gradual dilatation of the external os","Fixed retroversion of the uterus","Painful cervical laceration during delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cervical incompetence is defined as painless, sudden dilatation of the internal os.  Why B wrong: It is painless and sudden, involving the internal os, not painful/gradual external os.  Why C wrong: Retroversion is a position, not cervical incompetence.  Why D wrong: It is not a delivery laceration."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"In cervical incompetence, painless sudden dilatation of the internal os leads to which immediate consequence?","options":["Rupture of membranes","Placental abruption","Uterine rupture","Cord prolapse without membrane rupture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sudden dilatation of the internal os leads to rupture of membranes.  Why B wrong: Abruption is a separate APH condition.  Why C wrong: Uterine rupture is not the described sequence.  Why D wrong: The sequence described is membrane rupture, then expulsion of the fetus."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Following rupture of membranes in cervical incompetence, the final event in the pathophysiology sequence is:","options":["Expulsion of fetus","Spontaneous cervical closure","Fetal descent arrest","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After rupture of membranes, the sequence ends in expulsion of the fetus.  Why B wrong: The cervix does not spontaneously close; it is incompetent.  Why C/D wrong: These are not part of the described sequence."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"In cervical incompetence, the most common time (trimester) of abortion is:","options":["2nd trimester","1st trimester","3rd trimester","Term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common time of abortion in cervical incompetence is the 2nd trimester.  Why B wrong: 1st trimester loss is more typically chromosomal.  Why C/D wrong: These are later than the stated 2nd trimester."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"In cervical incompetence, the most common type of abortion is:","options":["Recurrent abortion","Isolated single-episode abortion","Induced abortion","Missed abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common type of abortion in cervical incompetence is recurrent abortion.  Why B wrong: It is recurrent, not single-episode.  Why C wrong: Induced is elective, not the type described here.  Why D wrong: Missed abortion is not the stated type."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"In normal uterine anatomy (main problem reference), the internal os is:","options":["Closed","Widely open","Absent","Fused with the external os"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"normal anatomy\" diagram shows the internal os is Closed.  Why B wrong: A widely open internal os describes incompetence, not normal anatomy.  Why C/D wrong: The internal os is present and distinct in normal anatomy."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"In cervical incompetence, the internal os is described as:","options":["Not properly closed, leading to sudden dilatation","Fused and rigid","Completely absent","Closed but scarred"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In cervical incompetence the internal os is not properly closed, leading to sudden dilatation.  Why B wrong: It is lax/not closed, not fused and rigid.  Why C wrong: The os is present but incompetent.  Why D wrong: The issue is failure to stay closed, not scarring."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"The surgical management of cervical incompetence involves:","options":["Putting a stitch around the internal os to tighten it (cerclage)","Cesarean hysterectomy","Dilatation and curettage","Endometrial ablation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Management is to put a stitch around the internal os to tighten it, called os cerclage surgery.  Why B wrong: Hysterectomy is not the management of cervical incompetence.  Why C wrong: D&C is an evacuation procedure, not cerclage.  Why D wrong: Endometrial ablation is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"The surgery used to manage cervical incompetence is called:","options":["Cerclage surgery","Cystocele repair","Myomectomy","Salpingectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The surgery is called CERCLAGE surgery.  Why B wrong: Cystocele repair is for prolapse.  Why C wrong: Myomectomy removes fibroids.  Why D wrong: Salpingectomy removes the fallopian tube."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"What is the route of surgery for cervical cerclage as stated in the notes?","options":["Vaginal","Abdominal","Laparoscopic","Robotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The route of surgery for cerclage is stated as Vaginal.  Why B/C/D wrong: The notes specify the vaginal route, not abdominal, laparoscopic, or robotic."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"A patient with a history of three second-trimester painless losses and sudden cervical dilatation is best managed by which procedure?","options":["Cervical cerclage via the vaginal route","Emergency cesarean section","Immediate hysterectomy","Bed rest alone with no intervention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This clinical picture is cervical incompetence (painless, sudden dilatation, recurrent 2nd trimester loss); management is cerclage via the vaginal route.  Why B wrong: Cesarean is not the management of cervical incompetence.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is inappropriate and destroys fertility.  Why D wrong: Bed rest alone does not correct the incompetent os; a stitch is required."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"A woman presents with early pregnancy bleeding, a positive UPT, and is being evaluated with hCG levels and transvaginal ultrasound. Which diagnoses are being considered per the classification? (Select all that apply)","options":["Abortion","Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy","Placenta previa","Abruptio placentae"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Early pregnancy bleeding with positive UPT is evaluated (hCG, USG-TVS) for abortion, ectopic pregnancy, and molar pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is an APH cause (after 28 weeks), not early pregnancy.  Why E wrong: Abruptio placentae is also an APH cause, occurring after 28 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which of the following are listed as endocrine causes of isolated abortion? (Select all that apply)","options":["Diabetes","Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","PCOS","Decreased progesterone levels"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five — diabetes, hypothyroidism, hyperthyroidism, PCOs, and decreased progesterone levels — are listed endocrine causes of isolated abortion.  Why any wrong: None are wrong; all appear in the endocrine-causes list."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"Which of the following investigations ARE performed in a recurrent-abortion workup? (Select all that apply)","options":["Karyotyping of parents","Fasting & post-prandial blood sugar","TSH, T3, T4","USG (TVS)","TORCH test"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Karyotyping of parents, fasting & post-prandial blood sugar, TSH/T3/T4, and USG (TVS) are all investigations done; phospholipid Ab testing is also done.  Why E wrong: TORCH test is specifically listed under investigations NOT done, since TORCH does not cause recurrent abortion."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"A pregnant woman is diagnosed with abruptio placentae at 32 weeks with vaginal bleeding. Under which broad category of bleeding in pregnancy does this fall?","options":["Antepartum hemorrhage (after 28 weeks)","Early pregnancy bleeding","Abortion","Postpartum hemorrhage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Abruptio placentae at 32 weeks is bleeding after 28 weeks = antepartum hemorrhage.  Why B wrong: This is beyond 28 weeks, not early pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Abortion is loss at ≤20 weeks.  Why D wrong: Postpartum hemorrhage occurs after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hypothyroidism in pregnancy & thyroxine dose adjustment…","question":"In the 2nd trimester RPL pathway, the immunological cause specifically progresses to which condition per the flow?","options":["Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APLA)","Idiopathic loss","Balanced translocation","Submucous fibroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 2nd trimester RPL \"Other\" pathway shows Immunological cause → Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APLA).  Why B wrong: Idiopathic is the 1st trimester RPL common cause.  Why C wrong: Balanced translocation is a chromosomal, not immunological, cause.  Why D wrong: Submucous fibroid is a structural cause, not immunological."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"Which of the following are recognised methods of performing cervical cerclage?","options":["McDonald cerclage and Shirodkar cerclage","Fothergill and Manchester repair","Wertheim and Schauta procedures","Fothergill and McCall culdoplasty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list two methods of doing cervical cerclage — McDonald cerclage (purse string suture) and Shirodkar cerclage.  Why B wrong: Fothergill/Manchester repair is a surgery for uterine prolapse, not cerclage.  Why C wrong: Wertheim and Schauta are radical hysterectomy procedures for cervical cancer.  Why D wrong: McCall culdoplasty addresses vault/enterocele repair, not cervical cerclage."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In which type of cervical cerclage is a purse string suture applied?","options":["Shirodkar cerclage","McDonald cerclage","Transabdominal cerclage","Wurm procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify that in McDonald cerclage a purse string suture is applied.  Why A wrong: Shirodkar cerclage is listed as a separate method; the purse-string designation is given to McDonald.  Why C wrong: Transabdominal cerclage is not the method named for the purse-string suture in these notes.  Why D wrong: The Wurm procedure is not mentioned in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"According to the cervical cerclage procedure diagram, what happens to the internal os once the suture is pulled tightly?","options":["The internal os remains open","The internal os is closed and the cervical canal is narrowed","The internal os is completely excised","The internal os prolapses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows that before tying the internal os is open with an open cervical canal; after the suture is pulled tight the internal os closes and the cervical canal is narrowed.  Why A wrong: The os is open only before tying, not after.  Why C wrong: No tissue is excised; a suture merely narrows the canal.  Why D wrong: Prolapse of the os is not part of the procedure."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What is the ideal gestational age (time) to perform cervical cerclage surgery?","options":["8–12 weeks","14–18 weeks","20–24 weeks","28–32 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the time to do cerclage surgery is 14–18 weeks.  Why A wrong: 8–12 weeks is earlier than the stated window.  Why C wrong: 20–24 weeks is later than the stated 14–18 weeks.  Why D wrong: 28–32 weeks is in the third trimester, well past the stated window."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"At what gestational age is the cervical cerclage suture normally removed?","options":["32 weeks","34 weeks","37 weeks of pregnancy","40 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the suture is removed at 37 weeks of pregnancy (or earlier if the patient goes into preterm labour).  Why A wrong: 32 weeks is earlier than routine removal time.  Why B wrong: 34 weeks is earlier than the stated 37 weeks.  Why D wrong: 40 weeks is later than the stated removal time."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"Besides the routine 37-week removal, when else should the cervical cerclage suture be removed early?","options":["If the patient develops mild anaemia","If the patient goes into preterm labour","If the patient gains excess weight","If the patient reports mild backache"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the suture should be removed earlier if the patient goes into preterm labour.  Why A wrong: Anaemia is not a listed indication for early suture removal.  Why C wrong: Weight gain is not a stated indication.  Why D wrong: Mild backache alone is not the stated indication; preterm labour is."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What history is characteristic in the diagnosis of cervical incompetence?","options":["One episode of painful first-trimester abortion","History of ≥3 painless, recurrent second-trimester abortions","A single painful third-trimester bleed","Recurrent painful first-trimester miscarriages"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give HISTORY = ≥3, painless, recurrent 2nd trimester abortions as the diagnostic history.  Why A wrong: A single first-trimester painful abortion is not characteristic of cervical incompetence.  Why C wrong: Third-trimester painful bleeding suggests other pathology, not cervical incompetence.  Why D wrong: Cervical incompetence abortions are painless and second-trimester, not painful first-trimester."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"Which investigation can diagnose cervical incompetence during pregnancy?","options":["Plain X-ray abdomen","Ultrasonography (USG)","Complete blood count","Urine culture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state USG can diagnose cervical incompetence during pregnancy.  Why A wrong: X-ray is not used to assess cervical length/os in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: A blood count does not diagnose cervical incompetence.  Why D wrong: Urine culture detects infection, not cervical incompetence."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What happens to the length of the cervix as it dilates in cervical incompetence?","options":["It becomes longer","It becomes short","It stays exactly the same","It thickens without changing length"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"As cervix dilates it becomes short.\"  Why A wrong: The cervix shortens, not lengthens, as it dilates.  Why C wrong: The length does change — it shortens.  Why D wrong: The notes describe shortening, not thickening."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"On USG, a cervical length of what measurement at <20 weeks suggests cervical incompetence?","options":["≤2.5 cms at <20 weeks","≤4 cms at <30 weeks","≥5 cms at term","≤1 cm at 36 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state cervical length ≤2.5 cms in pregnancy at <20 weeks indicates cervical incompetence.  Why B wrong: The value is ≤2.5 cms at <20 weeks, not ≤4 cms at <30 weeks.  Why C wrong: ≥5 cms at term is not the stated threshold.  Why D wrong: ≤1 cm at 36 weeks is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What degree of internal os dilation on USG is suggestive of cervical incompetence?","options":["If ≥2 cms","If ≥5 cms","If ≤0.5 cm","Any degree of closure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state dilation of internal os \"If ≥ 2 cms\" is a diagnostic finding.  Why B wrong: ≥5 cms is not the stated cut-off; ≥2 cms is.  Why C wrong: ≤0.5 cm indicates a closed/near-closed os, not incompetence.  Why D wrong: Closure of the os is normal, not indicative of incompetence."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"A pregnant woman at <20 weeks has cervical length ≤2.5 cms and internal os dilatation ≥2 cms on USG. According to the notes, this indicates cervical incompetence — what is the management?","options":["Immediate caesarean section","Cerclage surgery","Only bed rest","Termination of pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The USG flow chart shows that at <20 weeks with these findings → cervical incompetence → management is cerclage surgery.  Why A wrong: Caesarean section is not the management of cervical incompetence at <20 weeks.  Why C wrong: Bed rest alone is not the stated management for this diagnosis.  Why D wrong: The aim is to preserve the pregnancy with cerclage, not terminate it."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"According to the USG management flow chart, if a woman at <20 weeks presents in preterm labour, what is the management?","options":["Cerclage surgery","Corticosteroid + Tocolytics","Immediate delivery","Antibiotics only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows that at <20 weeks with preterm labour → management is Corticosteroid + Tocolytics.  Why A wrong: Cerclage is the management for cervical incompetence, not established preterm labour.  Why C wrong: The aim in preterm labour management here is corticosteroid + tocolytics, not immediate delivery.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics alone are not the stated management."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What is the purpose of giving corticosteroids in the management of preterm labour?","options":["To stop uterine contractions","To promote fetal lung maturity (as paired with tocolytics in the notes)","To induce abortion","To treat maternal infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the preterm labour arm, corticosteroids are given (paired with tocolytics); corticosteroids promote fetal lung maturity while tocolytics buy time by suppressing contractions.  Why A wrong: Stopping contractions is the role of tocolytics, not corticosteroids.  Why C wrong: Corticosteroids are not used to induce abortion here.  Why D wrong: Corticosteroids are not antibiotics for infection."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication to cervical cerclage?","options":["Intact membranes","Ruptured membranes","Cervical length of 3 cms","Absence of infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Membranes rupture\" as the first contraindication of cervical cerclage.  Why A wrong: Intact membranes are a prerequisite, not a contraindication.  Why C wrong: A longer cervix (3 cms) is not a contraindication.  Why D wrong: Absence of infection is favourable, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What degree of internal os dilatation is a contraindication to cervical cerclage?","options":["Dilatation of internal os ≥4 cms","Dilatation of internal os ≥2 cms","Dilatation of internal os ≥1 cm","Any dilatation whatsoever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Dilatation of internal os ≥4 cms\" as a contraindication of cerclage.  Why B wrong: ≥2 cms is the diagnostic value for incompetence (and indication for cerclage), not the contraindication threshold.  Why C wrong: ≥1 cm is not the stated contraindication value.  Why D wrong: The contraindication threshold is specifically ≥4 cms, not any dilatation."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"Which infection is specifically listed as a contraindication to cervical cerclage?","options":["Urinary tract infection","Chorioamnionitis","Vaginal candidiasis","Bacterial vaginosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Infection → Chorioamnionitis\" as a contraindication of cervical cerclage.  Why A wrong: UTI is not the specific infection named.  Why C wrong: Candidiasis is not the stated contraindication.  Why D wrong: Bacterial vaginosis is not the specific infection listed."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"Which fetal condition is a contraindication to cervical cerclage?","options":["Breech presentation","Gross congenital anomaly of fetus","Twin pregnancy","Macrosomia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Gross congenital anomaly of fetus\" as a contraindication of cervical cerclage.  Why A wrong: Breech presentation is not the listed contraindication.  Why C wrong: Twin pregnancy is not listed as a contraindication here.  Why D wrong: Macrosomia is not listed as a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"According to the notes, which of the following are the FIVE types of isolated/single episode of abortion? (Select all that apply)","options":["Threatened abortion","Inevitable abortion","Missed abortion","Incomplete abortion","Complete abortion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart lists all five types of isolated/single-episode abortion — threatened, inevitable, missed, incomplete, and complete abortion.  Why A/B/C/D/E each: All five appear in the classification chart, so each is a correct type."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In threatened and inevitable abortion, the patient typically complains of amenorrhoea followed by bleeding (with a positive urine pregnancy test) plus pain abdomen. Notably, in these types there is NO history of what?","options":["Fever","Product of conception coming out","Headache","Nausea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that for these types \"There is no history of: Product of conception coming out.\"  Why A wrong: Fever is not the feature discussed here.  Why C wrong: Headache is not the stated absent feature.  Why D wrong: Nausea is not the feature being distinguished."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What triad of complaints is characteristic of incomplete and complete abortion?","options":["Amenorrhoea + positive urine pregnancy test + pain abdomen + history of product of conception coming out","Amenorrhoea alone","Painless bleeding without pregnancy test change","Fever with vomiting only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in incomplete + complete abortion the patient complains of amenorrhoea (urine pregnancy test +ve) + pain abdomen + history of product of conception coming out.  Why B wrong: Amenorrhoea alone is incomplete; the full set includes pain and POC history.  Why C wrong: The pregnancy test is positive and pain is present.  Why D wrong: Fever with vomiting is not the described triad."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In case of incomplete + complete abortion, the patient ALWAYS complains of which one distinguishing feature?","options":["Product of conception coming out","High-grade fever","Painless spotting","Absence of bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state that in incomplete + complete abortion the patient always complains of product of conception coming out.  Why B wrong: Fever is not the always-present distinguishing feature.  Why C wrong: Bleeding is present with pain; not painless spotting.  Why D wrong: Bleeding is present, not absent."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"How is threatened abortion defined in the notes?","options":["An abortion that cannot be reversed","A type of abortion from which the process can be REVERSED","An abortion where the fetus is already dead","An abortion where all products have been expelled"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines threatened abortion as a type of abortion from which the process can be reversed.  Why A wrong: Irreversibility describes inevitable abortion, not threatened.  Why C wrong: A dead fetus is not part of the threatened abortion definition (USG shows live fetus).  Why D wrong: Expulsion of all products describes complete abortion."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What are the symptoms of threatened abortion according to the table?","options":["Pain + amenorrhoea + bleeding, with no history of product of conception coming out","Painless bleeding only","Fever + amenorrhoea + expulsion of products","Amenorrhoea without any bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists symptoms as pain + amenorrhoea + bleeding, with no history of product of conception coming out.  Why B wrong: Pain and amenorrhoea are present, not painless bleeding only.  Why C wrong: There is no expulsion of products in threatened abortion.  Why D wrong: Bleeding is present, so amenorrhoea without bleeding is wrong."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"On per-abdomen examination in threatened abortion, the height of the uterus is:","options":["Larger than the period of pregnancy","Equal to the period of pregnancy","Smaller than the period of pregnancy","Not palpable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the height of uterus is equal to the period of pregnancy in threatened abortion.  Why A wrong: It is equal, not larger.  Why C wrong: It is equal, not smaller.  Why D wrong: It is palpable and equals the period of pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In threatened abortion, the internal os is:","options":["Open","Closed","Fully dilated to 4 cms","Absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the internal os is closed in threatened abortion.  Why A wrong: The os is closed, not open (open os indicates inevitable abortion).  Why C wrong: The os is not dilated in threatened abortion.  Why D wrong: The os is present and closed."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What does USG show in threatened abortion?","options":["Dead fetus","Live fetus","Empty uterus","Molar changes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states USG shows a live fetus in threatened abortion (consistent with a reversible process).  Why A wrong: A dead fetus is seen in inevitable abortion, not threatened.  Why C wrong: An empty uterus suggests complete abortion.  Why D wrong: Molar changes indicate molar pregnancy, not threatened abortion."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What is the main aim in the treatment of threatened abortion?","options":["To speed up the abortion process","To continue the pregnancy and give rest to the patient","To perform immediate suction evacuation","To induce labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the main aim in threatened abortion is to continue the pregnancy plus give rest to the patient.  Why A wrong: Speeding up abortion is the aim in inevitable abortion, not threatened.  Why C wrong: Suction evacuation is used in inevitable abortion management.  Why D wrong: Inducing labour contradicts the aim of continuing the pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In the management of threatened abortion, for how long should bed rest be advised after bleeding stops?","options":["24 hours","48 hours","72 hours","1 week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Advise bed rest to patient × 48 hours after bleeding stop.\"  Why A wrong: The stated duration is 48 hours, not 24.  Why C wrong: 72 hours is not the stated duration.  Why D wrong: 1 week is not the stated bed rest period."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What is the dose of diazepam advised in the management of threatened abortion?","options":["Tab 5 mg twice daily","Tab 10 mg once daily","Tab 2 mg thrice daily","Injection 5 mg IV stat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Give diazepam: Tab 5 mg twice daily.\"  Why B wrong: The dose is 5 mg twice daily, not 10 mg once daily.  Why C wrong: 2 mg thrice daily is not the stated dose.  Why D wrong: It is an oral tablet twice daily, not an IV stat injection."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In the management of threatened abortion, the nurse is instructed to do which of the following regarding the patient's pads?","options":["Discard the pads immediately","Preserve her pads","Weigh only heavily soaked pads","Reuse the pads"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Preserve her pads\" (to assess bleeding and check for any expelled products).  Why A wrong: The instruction is to preserve, not discard.  Why C wrong: The notes simply say preserve pads, not selectively weigh them.  Why D wrong: Reusing pads is unhygienic and not instructed."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"Once 48 hours have passed in threatened abortion management, the patient is advised to avoid which of the following for 2 weeks? (Select all that apply)","options":["Heavy work","Intercourse","Lifting heavy weight","Drinking water"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that once 48 hrs have passed she should avoid heavy work, avoid intercourse, and avoid lifting heavy weight — all to be done for 2 weeks.  Why A/B/C correct: Each is explicitly listed as an activity to avoid.  Why D wrong: Drinking water is not restricted and is not on the list."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"For how long should the activity restrictions (avoid heavy work, intercourse, lifting) be maintained after threatened abortion?","options":["1 week","2 weeks","3 weeks","4 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state all these restrictions are to be done × 2 weeks.  Why A wrong: The stated duration is 2 weeks, not 1.  Why C wrong: 3 weeks is the timing for repeating the ultrasound, not the activity restriction.  Why D wrong: 4 weeks is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"After how long should the ultrasound be repeated in the management of threatened abortion?","options":["After 1 week","After 2 weeks","After 3 weeks","After 6 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Repeat ultrasound after 3 weeks.\"  Why A wrong: 1 week is not the stated interval.  Why B wrong: 2 weeks is the activity-restriction period, not the repeat USG timing.  Why D wrong: 6 weeks is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"How is inevitable abortion defined in the notes?","options":["An abortion where the process can be reversed","When abortion reaches a stage from where it cannot be reversed","An abortion where products are completely expelled","An abortion diagnosed only on histology"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines inevitable abortion as when abortion reaches a stage from where it cannot be reversed (product of conception do not come out yet).  Why A wrong: Reversibility describes threatened abortion.  Why C wrong: Complete expulsion describes complete abortion.  Why D wrong: The definition is clinical, not histological."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What are the symptoms of inevitable abortion according to the table?","options":["Pain + amenorrhoea, with no history of product of conception coming out","Painless bleeding with product expelled","Fever + expulsion of products","Amenorrhoea with an empty uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists symptoms as pain + amenorrhoea, with no history of product of conception out.  Why B wrong: In inevitable abortion products have not yet come out.  Why C wrong: Fever and expulsion are not the listed symptoms.  Why D wrong: The uterus is not empty; the fetus (dead) is still present."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"On per-abdomen examination in inevitable abortion, the height of the uterus is:","options":["Smaller than the period of pregnancy","Equal to the period of pregnancy","Larger than the period of pregnancy","Not assessed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the height of uterus is equal to the period of pregnancy in inevitable abortion.  Why A wrong: It is equal, not smaller.  Why C wrong: It is equal, not larger.  Why D wrong: It is assessed and is equal to the period of pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In inevitable abortion, the internal os is:","options":["Closed","Open","Narrowed by suture","Absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the internal os is OPEN in inevitable abortion (distinguishing it from threatened abortion, where it is closed).  Why A wrong: A closed os indicates threatened abortion, not inevitable.  Why C wrong: Suture narrowing refers to cerclage, not this scenario.  Why D wrong: The os is present and open."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What does USG show in inevitable abortion?","options":["Live fetus","Dead fetus","Empty uterus","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states USG shows a dead fetus in inevitable abortion.  Why A wrong: A live fetus indicates threatened abortion.  Why C wrong: An empty uterus indicates complete abortion.  Why D wrong: Twin pregnancy is unrelated to this finding."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What is the aim of management in inevitable abortion?","options":["To continue the pregnancy","Since the pregnancy cannot be continued, to speed up the process of abortion","To perform cervical cerclage","To give complete bed rest for 2 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in inevitable abortion the pregnancy cannot be continued, so the aim is to speed up the process of abortion.  Why A wrong: Continuing the pregnancy is the aim in threatened abortion, not inevitable.  Why C wrong: Cerclage is for cervical incompetence, not inevitable abortion.  Why D wrong: Bed rest to continue pregnancy applies to threatened abortion."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In first-trimester inevitable abortion, what is the method used to speed up the abortion process?","options":["Oxytocin drip","Suction evacuation followed by curettage (blunt curettage)","Cervical cerclage","Expectant management for 3 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that for the 1st trimester the method is suction evacuation followed by curettage (blunt curettage).  Why A wrong: Oxytocin drip is used in the 2nd trimester, not the 1st.  Why C wrong: Cerclage is for cervical incompetence.  Why D wrong: Expectant management is not the stated method for inevitable abortion."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In the second-trimester management of inevitable abortion, after admitting the patient, which drug drip is started?","options":["Magnesium sulfate drip","Oxytocin drip","Normal saline only","Diazepam infusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in the 2nd trimester the patient is admitted and an oxytocin drip is started.  Why A wrong: Magnesium sulfate is not the stated drip.  Why C wrong: Normal saline is the carrier fluid; oxytocin is the active drug added.  Why D wrong: Diazepam is used orally in threatened abortion, not as an infusion here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"What is the oxytocin drip dose used in second-trimester inevitable abortion management?","options":["5 IU in 250 ml NS at 20 drops/min","10 IU on 500 ml of NS at 40–60 drops/min","20 IU in 1000 ml NS at 30 drops/min","10 IU IM stat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Dose = 10 IU on 500 ml of NS at 40–60 drops/min.  Why A wrong: The stated dose is 10 IU in 500 ml, not 5 IU in 250 ml.  Why C wrong: The volume is 500 ml and rate 40–60 drops/min, not 1000 ml at 30 drops/min.  Why D wrong: It is an IV drip in NS, not an IM stat dose."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"A curette has two ends. Which end is described in the notes/figure?","options":["Blunt end and sharp curette end","Serrated and toothed ends","Curved and straight ends","Rubber and metal ends"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The curette figure labels a \"Blunt\" end and a \"Sharp curette\" end.  Why B wrong: Serrated/toothed ends are not what the figure shows.  Why C wrong: The figure distinguishes blunt vs sharp, not curved vs straight.  Why D wrong: The instrument is metal; there is no rubber end."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In pregnant females, which type of curette is generally used, and why?","options":["Sharp curette, because the uterus is firm","Blunt curette, because the uterus is very soft (except in molar pregnancy)","Sharp curette, because the uterus is very soft","Either type equally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in pregnant females a blunt curette is used as the uterus is very soft, except in the case of molar pregnancy.  Why A wrong: A blunt (not sharp) curette is used, and the uterus is soft, not firm.  Why C wrong: A sharp curette is not the routine choice in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: The notes specify blunt curette for pregnant females, not either type."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In which condition is the blunt-curette rule for pregnant females an exception (i.e., a different curette approach is used)?","options":["Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy","Twin pregnancy","Post-term pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that a blunt curette is used in pregnant females \"except in case of Molar pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is not the stated exception.  Why C wrong: Twin pregnancy is not the stated exception.  Why D wrong: Post-term pregnancy is not the stated exception."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In non-pregnant females, which type of curettage is used?","options":["Blunt curettage","Sharp curettage","Ovum forceps only","Suction evacuation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes indicate that in non-pregnant females, sharp curettage is used.  Why A wrong: Blunt curette is used in pregnant females (soft uterus), not non-pregnant.  Why C wrong: Ovum forceps are used to complete the process when POC does not expel, not as the routine non-pregnant curettage method.  Why D wrong: Suction evacuation is part of 1st-trimester abortion management, not the non-pregnant curettage rule."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"In the second-trimester scenario, if the entire product of conception (POC) does not expel by itself, how should the process be completed?","options":["By using ovum forceps (do not do curettage)","By performing sharp curettage","By repeat suction evacuation","By expectant management"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if the entire POC does not expel by itself, complete the process by ovum forceps (do not do curettage).  Why B wrong: The notes explicitly say do NOT do curettage in this situation.  Why C wrong: Repeat suction evacuation is not the stated method here.  Why D wrong: The process must be completed with ovum forceps, not left to expectant management."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"According to the notes, in the second-trimester abortion setting, when the product of conception does not expel by itself, which instrument is used instead of doing curettage?","options":["Sharp curette","Ovum forceps","Suction cannula","Blunt curette"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes emphasise using ovum forceps (and explicitly not curettage) when the POC does not expel by itself in the 2nd trimester.  Why A wrong: Sharp curette/curettage is specifically to be avoided in this scenario.  Why C wrong: A suction cannula is used earlier (suction evacuation in 1st trimester), not here.  Why D wrong: A blunt curette is not the instrument specified for completing this process."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Methods of cervical cerclage, timing of cerclage surgery and suture…","question":"A woman with a history of three painless second-trimester losses is now pregnant. USG at 18 weeks shows cervical length of 2.2 cms and internal os dilatation of 2.5 cms with no contractions. What is the most appropriate management?","options":["Corticosteroid + tocolytics","Cervical cerclage surgery","Immediate oxytocin drip","Suction evacuation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Recurrent painless second-trimester losses with cervical length ≤2.5 cms and internal os ≥2 cms at <20 weeks (no labour) indicate cervical incompetence, managed by cerclage surgery per the USG flow chart.  Why A wrong: Corticosteroid + tocolytics is for the preterm-labour arm; this patient is not in preterm labour.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin drip speeds up abortion in inevitable abortion, not applicable to preserving a wanted pregnancy with incompetence.  Why D wrong: Suction evacuation is a first-trimester abortion method, not indicated here."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Which type of abortion is characterised by the patient being unaware that the fetus is dead, with a closed internal os and a dead macerated fetus on USG?","options":["Missed abortion","Incomplete abortion","Complete abortion","Threatened abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Missed abortion is defined as the condition when the patient is unaware that the fetus is dead; the internal os is closed and USG shows a dead macerated fetus.  Why B wrong: In incomplete abortion the os is open and products are partly expelled.  Why C wrong: In complete abortion the uterus is empty and os is closed after all products are expelled.  Why D wrong: In threatened abortion the fetus is alive on USG."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"What is the classic symptom triad of a missed abortion?","options":["Pain + amenorrhea + bleeding (brown discharge)","Pain + fever + purulent discharge","Bleeding + shock + expulsion of products","Painless bright red bleeding only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Missed abortion presents with pain + amenorrhea + bleeding that is a brown discharge, with no history of products of conception coming out.  Why B wrong: Fever + purulent discharge indicates septic abortion.  Why C wrong: Shock with expulsion of products suggests incomplete abortion.  Why D wrong: Painless bright red bleeding is not the described missed-abortion picture."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In a missed abortion, the per-abdomen finding regarding uterine height is:","options":["Height of uterus is less than the period of pregnancy","Height of uterus equals the period of pregnancy","Height of uterus is greater than the period of pregnancy","Uterus is not palpable per abdomen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In missed abortion the height of the uterus is less than the period of pregnancy because the dead fetus does not grow.  Why B wrong: Equal height corresponds to threatened abortion.  Why C wrong: Height greater than period of pregnancy is not a feature of missed abortion.  Why D wrong: The uterus is palpable; height is simply reduced."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In missed abortion, the discharge described from the vagina is characteristically:","options":["Brown discharge","Bright red bleeding","Purulent foul-smelling discharge","Clear watery discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bleeding in missed abortion is described as a brown discharge.  Why B wrong: Bright red active bleeding is not the described feature of missed abortion.  Why C wrong: Purulent foul-smelling discharge indicates septic abortion.  Why D wrong: Clear watery discharge is not a feature."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The overall aim in the management of a missed abortion is to:","options":["Remove the product of conception","Preserve the pregnancy at all costs","Give only reassurance and do nothing","Perform an immediate hysterectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The stated aim of managing missed abortion is to remove the product of conception.  Why B wrong: The fetus is already dead, so preservation is not possible.  Why C wrong: \"Do nothing/reassurance\" applies to complete abortion with an empty uterus.  Why D wrong: Hysterectomy is not the described management."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"For a first-trimester missed abortion, which surgical procedure is used to remove the products of conception?","options":["Suction evacuation followed by curettage","Dilation and evacuation with ovum forceps","Total abdominal hysterectomy","Manual vacuum aspiration of the cervix only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the first trimester, management is suction evacuation followed by curettage.  Why B wrong: Dilation & evacuation with ovum forceps is the second-trimester method.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not indicated.  Why D wrong: The specified method is suction evacuation then curettage."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In first-trimester missed abortion, which drug is given vaginally in the posterior fornix, and at what dose?","options":["Tab. Misoprostol 800 mcg","Tab. Misoprostol 200 mcg","Tab. Mifepristone 600 mcg","Tab. Methotrexate 50 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For first-trimester management, Tab. Misoprostol 800 mcg is placed vaginally in the posterior fornix.  Why B wrong: 200 mcg is the second-trimester dose.  Why C wrong: Mifepristone is not the drug listed.  Why D wrong: Methotrexate is not listed for this indication."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"After giving Misoprostol 800 mcg vaginally for first-trimester missed abortion, the dose can be repeated after:","options":["24 hours","4 hours","6 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the first-trimester Misoprostol dose can be repeated after 24 hours.  Why B wrong: 4-hourly repetition applies to the second-trimester 200 mcg regimen.  Why C wrong: 6 hours is not the stated interval.  Why D wrong: 12 hours is not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In second-trimester management of missed abortion, the surgical technique used is:","options":["Dilation & Evacuation with ovum forceps","Suction evacuation with curettage","Trans-vaginal sonography only","Laparotomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Second-trimester management uses Dilation & Evacuation, using ovum forceps.  Why B wrong: Suction evacuation with curettage is the first-trimester method.  Why C wrong: TVS is a diagnostic step in complete abortion, not the evacuation technique.  Why D wrong: Laparotomy is not the described method."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In second-trimester missed abortion, Tab. Misoprost 200 mcg is given at what frequency?","options":["Every 4 hours","Every 6 hours","Every 8 hours","Every 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the second trimester, Tab. Misoprost 200 mcg is given every 4 hours.  Why B wrong: 6 hours is not the stated interval.  Why C wrong: 8 hours is not the stated interval.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is the repeat interval of the first-trimester regimen."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The maximum number of doses of Misoprost 200 mcg (4-hourly) permitted in second-trimester missed-abortion management is:","options":["5 doses","3 doses","4 doses","6 doses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note specifies a maximum of 5 doses.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated maximum is 5 doses, not 3, 4, or 6."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In incomplete abortion, which statement best defines the process?","options":["Process of abortion has started and products have begun coming out, but the process is incomplete","The patient is unaware the fetus is dead","All products are expelled and bleeding stops","Bleeding occurs but the fetus is still alive with a closed os"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incomplete abortion is defined as the process having started and products of conception starting to come out, but the process remaining incomplete.  Why B wrong: That defines missed abortion.  Why C wrong: That describes complete abortion.  Why D wrong: A live fetus with closed os describes threatened abortion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Which sequence of symptoms is characteristic of incomplete abortion?","options":["Pain + amenorrhea + bleeding, with history of products coming out, then lot of bleeding leading to shock","Pain + amenorrhea + brown discharge with no products coming out","Painless spotting that resolves spontaneously","Fever + purulent discharge + tenderness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incomplete abortion shows pain + amenorrhea + bleeding, a history of products of conception coming out, then a lot of bleeding progressing to shock.  Why B wrong: Brown discharge with no products describes missed abortion.  Why C wrong: Painless self-resolving spotting is not the described picture.  Why D wrong: Fever + purulent discharge describes septic abortion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In incomplete abortion, the internal os on examination is:","options":["Open, and the products of conception can be seen coming out from it","Closed","Open but no products visible","Not assessable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In incomplete abortion the internal os is open and products of conception can be seen coming out from it.  Why B wrong: A closed os occurs in missed and complete abortion.  Why C wrong: Products are visibly coming out, not absent.  Why D wrong: The os is assessable and found open."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The USG finding in incomplete abortion is:","options":["Incomplete fetus / retained products","Empty uterus","Dead macerated fetus","Live fetus with cardiac activity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: USG in incomplete abortion shows an incomplete fetus (retained products).  Why B wrong: Empty uterus is seen in complete abortion.  Why C wrong: Dead macerated fetus is the missed-abortion finding.  Why D wrong: A live fetus indicates threatened abortion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In incomplete abortion, the per-abdomen height of the uterus is:","options":["Less than the period of pregnancy","Equal to the period of pregnancy","More than the period of pregnancy","Always at the umbilicus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In incomplete abortion the height of the uterus is less than the period of pregnancy because products have partly expelled.  Why B wrong: Equal height is seen in threatened abortion.  Why C wrong: Greater height is not described.  Why D wrong: A fixed umbilical level is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The management of incomplete abortion is described as:","options":["Same/similar to management of missed abortion","Completely different, requiring hysterectomy","Only reassurance","Only anti-D injection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states management of incomplete abortion is \"some (same) like missed abortion.\"  Why B wrong: Hysterectomy is not required.  Why C wrong: Reassurance alone applies to complete abortion.  Why D wrong: Anti-D is one component given in all types, not the sole management."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Which definition matches complete abortion?","options":["Entire products of conception come out on their own, followed by stopping of bleeding","Products start coming out but process is incomplete","Fetus is dead but retained with closed os","Bleeding with a live fetus and closed os"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In complete abortion, the entire products of conception come out on their own and are followed by cessation of bleeding.  Why B wrong: That is incomplete abortion.  Why C wrong: That is missed abortion.  Why D wrong: That is threatened abortion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The symptom sequence of complete abortion ends with:","options":["Products of conception come out, then bleeding stops","Continuous bleeding leading to shock","Persistent brown discharge","Fever and purulent discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In complete abortion, pain + amenorrhea + bleeding occurs, products of conception come out, then bleeding stops.  Why B wrong: Bleeding progressing to shock is the incomplete-abortion pattern.  Why C wrong: Persistent brown discharge is a missed-abortion feature.  Why D wrong: Fever/purulent discharge indicate septic abortion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In complete abortion, the internal os and USG findings are respectively:","options":["Closed os with empty uterus","Open os with retained products","Closed os with dead macerated fetus","Open os with empty uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In complete abortion, the internal os is closed and USG shows an empty uterus.  Why B wrong: Open os with retained products is incomplete abortion.  Why C wrong: Dead macerated fetus is missed abortion.  Why D wrong: The os is closed, not open."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In complete abortion, the per-abdomen uterine height is:","options":["Smaller than the period of pregnancy","Equal to the period of pregnancy","Larger than the period of pregnancy","Same as a full-term uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In complete abortion the height of the uterus is smaller than the period of pregnancy because the uterus is now empty.  Why B/C/D wrong: The uterus is emptied and thus smaller than the period of pregnancy, not equal, larger, or full-term."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The first diagnostic step in the management of complete abortion is to:","options":["Do a trans-vaginal sonography (TVS)","Perform suction curettage immediately","Give high-dose Misoprostol","Start IV antibiotics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Management of complete abortion begins with a trans-vaginal sonography (TVS) to check the uterus.  Why B wrong: If the uterus is empty, no evacuation is needed.  Why C wrong: Misoprostol is used to evacuate retained products, not indicated when the uterus is empty.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics are for septic abortion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In complete abortion, if TVS shows the uterus is empty, the appropriate action is to:","options":["Do nothing and give reassurance to the patient","Perform dilation and curettage","Administer ovum forceps evacuation","Repeat Misoprostol every 4 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the uterus is empty on TVS, the management is to do nothing and provide reassurance to the patient.  Why B wrong: No curettage is needed when the uterus is already empty.  Why C wrong: Evacuation is unnecessary for an empty uterus.  Why D wrong: Misoprostol is not needed once the uterus is empty."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In all types of abortion, which injection must be given to Rh-negative females?","options":["Anti-D injection","Anti-tetanus serum","Anti-gas-gangrene serum","Oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In all types of abortion, all Rh-negative females must be given an anti-D injection.  Why B wrong: Anti-tetanus serum is part of septic-abortion management, not routine for Rh-negative status.  Why C wrong: Anti-gas-gangrene serum is for septic abortion.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not the anti-Rh-sensitisation agent."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"For an Rh-negative female with an abortion at <12 weeks, the recommended anti-D dose is:","options":["50 mcg","100 mcg","300 mcg","500 mcg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For abortion at <12 weeks, anti-D dose is 50 mcg.  Why B wrong: 100 mcg is not the stated dose.  Why C wrong: 300 mcg is the dose for ≥12 weeks.  Why D wrong: 500 mcg is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"For an Rh-negative female with an abortion at ≥12 weeks, the recommended anti-D dose is:","options":["300 mcg","50 mcg","150 mcg","600 mcg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For abortion at ≥12 weeks, anti-D dose is 300 mcg.  Why B wrong: 50 mcg is for <12 weeks.  Why C wrong: 150 mcg is not stated.  Why D wrong: 600 mcg is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The route of administration for the anti-D injection given in abortion is:","options":["Intramuscular","Intravenous","Subcutaneous","Oral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page specifies the route of anti-D is intramuscular.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated route is intramuscular, not IV, subcutaneous, or oral."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the clinical algorithm to identify abortion type, the FIRST thing to look for in the question is:","options":["Whether there is a history of products of conception coming out","The internal os status","The height of the uterus","The USG finding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm states the 1st thing to look at is history vs no history of products of conception coming out.  Why B wrong: Internal os is the 2nd thing to check.  Why C wrong: Uterine height is examined later in the closed-os/no-history branch.  Why D wrong: USG is checked after height, later in the algorithm."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the algorithm, if there is a HISTORY of products of conception coming out with an OPEN internal os, the diagnosis is:","options":["Incomplete abortion","Complete abortion","Inevitable abortion","Missed abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: History of products coming out + open os = incomplete abortion.  Why B wrong: History of products + closed os = complete abortion.  Why C wrong: Inevitable abortion is in the \"no history\" + open os branch.  Why D wrong: Missed abortion is closed os with dead fetus in the no-history branch."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the algorithm, HISTORY of products coming out with a CLOSED internal os indicates:","options":["Complete abortion","Incomplete abortion","Threatened abortion","Inevitable abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: History of products + closed os = complete abortion.  Why B wrong: Incomplete abortion has an open os.  Why C wrong: Threatened abortion is in the no-history/closed-os/height-equal/fetus-alive branch.  Why D wrong: Inevitable abortion is no-history + open os."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the algorithm, NO history of products coming out with an OPEN internal os indicates:","options":["Inevitable abortion","Complete abortion","Missed abortion","Threatened abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: No history of products + open os = inevitable abortion.  Why B wrong: Complete abortion requires a history of products with closed os.  Why C wrong: Missed abortion is no-history + closed os + dead fetus.  Why D wrong: Threatened abortion is no-history + closed os + fetus alive."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the no-history/closed-os branch, if the height of uterus equals the period of gestation and USG shows the fetus is alive, the diagnosis is:","options":["Threatened abortion","Missed abortion","Incomplete abortion","Inevitable abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Closed os, uterine height = period of gestation, and a live fetus on USG = threatened abortion.  Why B wrong: Missed abortion has height less than gestation and a dead fetus.  Why C wrong: Incomplete abortion has an open os with history of products.  Why D wrong: Inevitable abortion has an open os."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the no-history/closed-os branch, if the height of uterus is less than the period of gestation and USG shows the fetus is dead, the diagnosis is:","options":["Missed abortion","Threatened abortion","Complete abortion","Inevitable abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Closed os, uterine height less than period of gestation, and a dead fetus on USG = missed abortion.  Why B wrong: Threatened abortion has a live fetus and height equal to gestation.  Why C wrong: Complete abortion has a history of products and an empty uterus.  Why D wrong: Inevitable abortion has an open os."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The \"2nd thing\" to look at in the algorithm (after establishing history/no history) is:","options":["The internal os (open or closed)","The maternal blood pressure","The fetal heart rate","The gestational age by dates"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After history status, the 2nd thing is to look at the internal os.  Why B/C/D wrong: The algorithm's second branch point is the internal os, not BP, FHR, or dates."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"A febrile abortion patient with a temperature of ≥100.4°F for more than 24 hours, lower abdominal pain and tenderness, and purulent/foul-smelling discharge is diagnosed as:","options":["Septic abortion","Missed abortion","Complete abortion","Threatened abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Any abortion with fever ≥100.4°F for >24 hours, lower-abdominal pain and tenderness, and purulent/foul-smelling discharge is called septic abortion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These types are not defined by fever and purulent discharge."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the definition of septic abortion, the fever threshold and duration are:","options":["≥100.4°F for more than 24 hours","≥101°F for more than 48 hours","≥99°F for more than 12 hours","≥102°F for more than 6 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The criterion is fever ≥100.4°F for more than 24 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures do not match the stated criterion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Which of the following is one of the three defining symptoms of septic abortion? (Select the best single option)","options":["Purulent/foul-smelling discharge","Brown vaginal discharge","Painless bleeding","Empty uterus on USG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Purulent/foul-smelling discharge is one of the three defining features (with fever and lower-abdominal pain/tenderness).  Why B wrong: Brown discharge is a missed-abortion feature.  Why C wrong: Painless bleeding is not a defining criterion.  Why D wrong: An empty uterus is a complete-abortion finding."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Septic abortion is MOSTLY seen with which type of abortion, and in what approximate percentage?","options":["Induced (illegal) abortion — 85%","Spontaneous abortion — 85%","Missed abortion — 50%","Complete abortion — 90%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Septic abortion is mostly seen with induced abortion (85%), which are done illegally.  Why B wrong: Spontaneous abortion accounts for only 15% of cases.  Why C wrong: 50% with missed abortion is not stated.  Why D wrong: Complete abortion is not linked to sepsis in this way."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Septic abortion may also be seen with spontaneous abortion in approximately what percentage of cases?","options":["15%","85%","50%","5%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Septic abortion may be seen with spontaneous abortion in about 15% of cases.  Why B wrong: 85% corresponds to induced abortions.  Why C/D wrong: These percentages are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The most common variety of SPONTANEOUS abortion associated with septic abortion is:","options":["Missed abortion","Incomplete abortion","Threatened abortion","Complete abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common variety of spontaneous abortion associated with septic abortion is missed abortion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the most common spontaneous type linked with sepsis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The most common organism responsible for septic abortion is:","options":["Mixed organisms","Escherichia coli alone","Group B Streptococcus alone","Clostridium perfringens alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common organism responsible for septic abortion is mixed organisms.  Why B/C/D wrong: A single organism is not the answer; the source states mixed organisms."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the grading of septic abortion, Grade 1 refers to:","options":["Uterus infection","Pelvic infection involving fallopian tube and ovaries","Infection spread outside the pelvis","Septic shock with jaundice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 1 septic abortion = uterus infection.  Why B wrong: Pelvic infection (fallopian tube, ovaries) is Grade 2.  Why C wrong: Spread outside pelvis is Grade 3.  Why D wrong: Septic shock with jaundice is part of Grade 3."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Grade 2 septic abortion involves pelvic infection of which structures?","options":["Fallopian tubes and ovaries","Uterus only","Peritoneum only","Liver and kidney"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 2 = pelvic infection involving the fallopian tubes and ovaries.  Why B wrong: Uterus-only infection is Grade 1.  Why C wrong: Peritonitis is a Grade 3 feature.  Why D wrong: Liver/kidney involvement is not the Grade 2 definition."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Grade 3 septic abortion is defined by infection spreading outside the pelvis and includes which features?","options":["Peritonitis, shock, and jaundice (septic shock)","Only uterine infection","Only fallopian tube infection","Brown vaginal discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 3 = infection spread outside the pelvis, presenting with peritonitis, shock, and jaundice (septic shock).  Why B wrong: Uterine-only infection is Grade 1.  Why C wrong: Fallopian-tube infection is part of Grade 2.  Why D wrong: Brown discharge is a missed-abortion feature."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"According to the note, Grade 3 septic shock is seen ONLY with which type of abortion?","options":["Illegally induced abortion","Spontaneous complete abortion","Threatened abortion","Missed abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states Grade 3 septic shock is seen only with illegally induced abortion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not linked with Grade 3 septic shock per the note."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The MOST COMMON type of abortion associated with SHOCK (best answer) is:","options":["Septic abortion","Incomplete abortion","Missed abortion","Complete abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For \"most common type of abortion associated with shock,\" the best answer given is septic abortion.  Why B wrong: Incomplete abortion is stated as the 2nd best answer.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the best answer for shock association."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"For \"most common type of abortion associated with shock,\" the 2nd best answer is:","options":["Incomplete abortion","Septic abortion","Complete abortion","Threatened abortion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 2nd best answer for abortion associated with shock is incomplete abortion.  Why B wrong: Septic abortion is the best (1st) answer.  Why C/D wrong: These are not identified as the second-best answer."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Which of the following is the FIRST step in the management of septic abortion?","options":["Hospitalization","Anti-D injection","Anti-gas-gangrene serum","Reassurance and discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first management step listed for septic abortion is hospitalization.  Why B wrong: Anti-D is given later, only if Rh-negative.  Why C wrong: Anti-gas-gangrene serum is a later step.  Why D wrong: A septic patient requires admission, not discharge."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In septic abortion management, hydration is maintained by:","options":["IV fluids","Oral rehydration only","Blood transfusion only","Subcutaneous fluids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hydration in septic abortion is maintained with IV fluids.  Why B wrong: Oral-only rehydration is inadequate for a septic patient.  Why C wrong: Blood transfusion is not the described hydration method.  Why D wrong: Subcutaneous fluids are not specified."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Before starting antibiotics in septic abortion, which specimen is taken and sent for culture?","options":["Vaginal/cervical swab","Blood culture only","Urine culture only","Peritoneal fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A vaginal/cervical swab is taken and sent for culture in septic-abortion management.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies a vaginal/cervical swab, not blood, urine, or peritoneal fluid."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the septic abortion antibiotic regimen, Penicillin G is given at what dose?","options":["5 million units","1 gm IV","1.5 mg/kg","600 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Penicillin G is given as 5 million units.  Why B wrong: 1 gm IV is the Ampicillin dose (alternative).  Why C wrong: 1.5 mg/kg is the Gentamycin dose.  Why D wrong: 600 mg is the Metronidazole dose."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the septic abortion regimen, the alternative to Penicillin G is Ampicillin at what dose?","options":["1 gm IV","5 million units","600 mg IV","1.5 mg/kg IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ampicillin is given as 1 gm IV (as an alternative to Penicillin G).  Why B wrong: 5 million units is Penicillin G's dose.  Why C wrong: 600 mg IV is Metronidazole's dose.  Why D wrong: 1.5 mg/kg IV is Gentamycin's dose."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Penicillin G (5 million units) or Ampicillin (1 gm IV) is administered at what frequency in septic abortion?","options":["6 hourly","8 hourly","4 hourly","12 hourly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Penicillin G / Ampicillin is given 6 hourly.  Why B wrong: 8 hourly applies to Gentamycin + Metronidazole.  Why C/D wrong: These intervals are not specified."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the septic abortion regimen, Gentamycin IV is dosed at:","options":["1.5 mg/kg IV","600 mg IV","1 gm IV","5 million units"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gentamycin is given at 1.5 mg/kg IV.  Why B wrong: 600 mg IV is Metronidazole.  Why C wrong: 1 gm IV is Ampicillin.  Why D wrong: 5 million units is Penicillin G."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the septic abortion regimen, Metronidazole is given at what dose?","options":["600 mg IV","1.5 mg/kg IV","1 gm IV","200 mg IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Metronidazole is given at 600 mg IV.  Why B wrong: 1.5 mg/kg IV is Gentamycin.  Why C wrong: 1 gm IV is Ampicillin.  Why D wrong: 200 mg is not the stated dose."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Gentamycin (1.5 mg/kg IV) plus Metronidazole (600 mg IV) are given at what frequency in septic abortion?","options":["8 hourly","6 hourly","4 hourly","12 hourly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gentamycin + Metronidazole are given 8 hourly.  Why B wrong: 6 hourly applies to Penicillin G / Ampicillin.  Why C/D wrong: These intervals are not specified."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"After starting antibiotics for septic abortion, within what time frame should the process of abortion be completed by evacuation?","options":["Within 24 hours","Within 6 hours","Within 48 hours","Within 72 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Within 24 hours of starting antibiotics, complete the process of abortion by evacuation.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated window is 24 hours, not 6, 48, or 72."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In septic abortion management, the dose of anti-gas-gangrene serum given IM is:","options":["6000 units","3000 units","300 units","5 million units"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-gas-gangrene is given 6000 units IM.  Why B wrong: 3000 units is the anti-tetanus serum dose.  Why C wrong: 300 units is not stated.  Why D wrong: 5 million units is the Penicillin G dose."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In septic abortion management, the dose of anti-tetanus serum given IM is:","options":["3000 units","6000 units","1500 units","300 units"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-tetanus serum is given 3000 units IM.  Why B wrong: 6000 units is the anti-gas-gangrene dose.  Why C/D wrong: These doses are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In septic abortion, if the patient is Rh-negative, which drug is given (with dose based on gestation)?","options":["Anti-D","Anti-gas-gangrene","Anti-tetanus serum","Gentamycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient is Rh-negative, give anti-D (50 mcg at <12 weeks, 300 mcg at ≥12 weeks, route i/m).  Why B/C wrong: These are given routinely in septic abortion regardless of Rh status, not specifically for Rh-negative sensitisation.  Why D wrong: Gentamycin is an antibiotic, not for Rh sensitisation."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Ectopic pregnancy is best defined as implantation occurring:","options":["Anywhere outside the uterine cavity","In the posterior wall of the upper uterus","Only within the cervix","Within the endometrial lining"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ectopic pregnancy is when implantation occurs anywhere outside the uterine cavity.  Why B wrong: The posterior wall of the upper uterus is the normal implantation site.  Why C wrong: Cervical implantation is only one specific ectopic site, not the definition.  Why D wrong: Implantation in the endometrium is normal, not ectopic."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Ectopic pregnancy is fundamentally a disorder of:","options":["Implantation","Ovulation","Fertilization","Menstruation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ectopic pregnancy is a disorder of implantation.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is specifically an implantation disorder, not of ovulation, fertilization, or menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"After fertilization, the morula becomes a blastocyst, which attaches to the uterine endometrium (implantation) on which day?","options":["6th day after fertilization","3rd day after fertilization","10th day after fertilization","14th day after fertilization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On the 6th day after fertilization, the blastocyst attaches to the uterine endometrium, which is called implantation.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated day of implantation is the 6th day, not the 3rd, 10th, or 14th."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The normal site of implantation is:","options":["Posterior wall of the upper part of the uterus","Anterior wall of the lower uterine segment","The cervical canal","The fallopian tube ampulla"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal implantation site is the posterior wall of the upper part of the uterus.  Why B wrong: The lower segment/anterior wall is not the normal site.  Why C wrong: The cervix is an abnormal (ectopic) site.  Why D wrong: The ampulla is the most common ectopic (tubal) site, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The most common site of ectopic pregnancy is the fallopian tube, accounting for approximately what percentage of cases?","options":["90%","50%","70%","15%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of ectopic pregnancy is the fallopian tube (90%).  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated figure is 90%, not 50%, 70%, or 15%."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Because the fallopian tube is the most common site of ectopic pregnancy, ectopic pregnancy is also called:","options":["Tubal pregnancy","Cervical pregnancy","Abdominal pregnancy","Cornual pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Since the fallopian tube (90%) is the most common site, ectopic pregnancy is also called tubal pregnancy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other, less common ectopic sites, not the general alternate name."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Arranged from medial to lateral, the parts of the fallopian tube are:","options":["Interstitium > Isthmus > Ampulla > Infundibulum","Infundibulum > Ampulla > Isthmus > Interstitium","Ampulla > Isthmus > Interstitium > Infundibulum","Isthmus > Interstitium > Infundibulum > Ampulla"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: From medial to lateral the fallopian tube parts are Interstitium > Isthmus > Ampulla > Infundibulum.  Why B wrong: That is the lateral-to-medial order.  Why C/D wrong: These orders are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"The most common site for fertilization is the:","options":["Ampulla","Isthmus","Interstitium","Infundibulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common (M/C) site for fertilization is the ampulla of the fallopian tube.  Why B/C/D wrong: These parts of the tube are not the usual fertilization site."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"Within the fallopian tube, the most common site for ectopic pregnancy is the:","options":["Ampulla","Isthmus","Interstitium","Infundibulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site within the fallopian tube for ectopic pregnancy is the ampulla.  Why B/C/D wrong: While the tube overall accounts for 90%, the specific most common tubal segment is the ampulla, not the isthmus, interstitium, or infundibulum."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the fallopian tube anatomy figure, the cornua is described as:","options":["The uterine angle (part of the uterus)","The finger-like projections at the tube's end","The widest part of the tube","The narrowest part near the ovary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the cornua as the uterine angle, which is part of the uterus.  Why B wrong: The finger-like projections are the fimbriae.  Why C wrong: The widest part is the ampulla.  Why D wrong: The narrow segment near the uterus is the isthmus/interstitium."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In the fallopian tube anatomy, the finger-like projections at the distal (infundibular) end that help pick up the ovum are the:","options":["Fimbriae","Cornua","Isthmus","Interstitium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the fimbriae at the infundibulum end of the tube.  Why B wrong: The cornua is the uterine angle.  Why C wrong: The isthmus is a narrow medial segment.  Why D wrong: The interstitium is the intramural (uterine wall) portion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Types of abortion — definitions, symptoms, per-abdomen findings, internal…","question":"In early development, the structure that becomes the blastocyst before implantation is the:","options":["Morula","Zygote","Gastrula","Fetus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the morula becomes the blastocyst, which then implants on the 6th day.  Why B wrong: The zygote precedes the morula stage.  Why C wrong: The gastrula stage occurs after implantation.  Why D wrong: The fetal stage is much later in development."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Which is the widest and longest part of the Fallopian tube?","options":["Isthmus","Ampulla","Interstitial part","Infundibulum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ampulla is the widest and longest part of the Fallopian tube, and is where fertilization normally occurs.  Why A wrong: The isthmus is the second narrowest part, not the widest.  Why C wrong: The interstitial (intramural) part is the narrowest part of the tube.  Why D wrong: The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped fimbriated end, not the longest part."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Which is the narrowest part of the Fallopian tube?","options":["Ampulla","Isthmus","Interstitium (interstitial part)","Infundibulum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The interstitial (intramural) part, called the interstitium, is the narrowest part of the Fallopian tube.  Why A wrong: The ampulla is the widest and longest part.  Why B wrong: The isthmus is the second narrowest part, not the narrowest.  Why D wrong: The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped distal opening, not the narrowest part."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Which is the second narrowest part of the Fallopian tube?","options":["Isthmus","Ampulla","Interstitium","Infundibulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The isthmus is the second narrowest part of the Fallopian tube (after the interstitium, which is narrowest).  Why B wrong: The ampulla is the widest and longest part.  Why C wrong: The interstitium is the narrowest (first), not second.  Why D wrong: The infundibulum is the fimbriated funnel-shaped end."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"What is the most common site (m/c site) of ectopic pregnancy overall?","options":["Ovary","Cervix","Fallopian tube (Ampulla)","Abdominal cavity"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of ectopic pregnancy overall is the Fallopian tube, specifically the ampulla (M/c site = FT).  Why A wrong: The ovary is the second most common site, not the first.  Why B wrong: The cervix is the least common site.  Why D wrong: Abdominal ectopic is rare, though it survives the longest."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"What is the most common site within the Fallopian tube for ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Isthmus","Ampulla","Interstitium","Infundibulum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ampulla is the most common site in the Fallopian tube for ectopic pregnancy, because fertilization occurs in the ampulla.  Why A wrong: The isthmus is second in the order of incidence.  Why C wrong: The interstitium is the least common tubal site.  Why D wrong: The infundibulum is third in the order of incidence."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Arrange the parts of the Fallopian tube in the correct decreasing order of incidence of ectopic pregnancy.","options":["Interstitium > Infundibulum > Isthmus > Ampulla","Ampulla > Isthmus > Infundibulum > Interstitium","Isthmus > Ampulla > Interstitium > Infundibulum","Infundibulum > Ampulla > Isthmus > Interstitium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The decreasing order of incidence is Ampulla > Isthmus > Infundibulum > Interstitium (mnemonic: Ampulla > Is > in > Interstitium).  Why A wrong: This reverses the correct order.  Why C wrong: The ampulla is the most common, so it must be first.  Why D wrong: The infundibulum is not the most common site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"What is the least common site within the Fallopian tube for ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Ampulla","Isthmus","Infundibulum","Interstitium"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The interstitium is the least common site in the Fallopian tube for ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The ampulla is the most common tubal site.  Why B wrong: The isthmus is second in incidence.  Why C wrong: The infundibulum is third in incidence."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Why does the ampulla have the highest incidence of ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Because it is the narrowest part","Because fertilization occurs in the ampulla","Because it is closest to the uterus","Because it has no cilia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ampulla is the most common site because fertilization occurs there.  Why A wrong: The ampulla is the widest part, not the narrowest.  Why C wrong: The interstitium is closest to the uterus, not the ampulla.  Why D wrong: The tube is lined by ciliated columnar epithelium; loss of cilia is a risk factor, not the reason ampulla is m/c."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"What is the second most common site of ectopic pregnancy overall?","options":["Ovary","Cervix","Abdomen","Isthmus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary is the second most common site of ectopic pregnancy overall.  Why B wrong: The cervix is the least common site overall.  Why C wrong: Abdominal ectopic is rare (though survives longest).  Why D wrong: The isthmus is a tubal subsite, not the overall second most common site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"What is the least common site of ectopic pregnancy overall?","options":["Ampulla","Ovary","Cervical","Abdominal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The cervical (cervix) is the least common site of ectopic pregnancy overall.  Why A wrong: The ampulla is the most common site.  Why B wrong: The ovary is the second most common site.  Why D wrong: Abdominal ectopic, while rare, is not listed as the least common overall."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"By which stage of pregnancy must an ectopic pregnancy end (abort or rupture)?","options":["Third trimester","First trimester or early second trimester","Only at term","Second trimester only, never first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The outcome of ectopic pregnancy has to end in the 1st trimester or early second trimester.  Why A wrong: An ectopic pregnancy cannot continue to the third trimester.  Why C wrong: An ectopic pregnancy cannot reach term.  Why D wrong: It typically ends in the first trimester, not exclusively the second."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"When an ectopic pregnancy aborts (is expelled) from the tube, this outcome is called:","options":["Tubal rupture","Tubal abortion","Heterotopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the ectopic pregnancy aborts, it is called tubal abortion.  Why A wrong: Tubal rupture occurs when the pregnancy continues to grow, not when it aborts.  Why C wrong: Heterotopic pregnancy is a coexisting intrauterine and ectopic twin pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Molar pregnancy is a separate gestational trophoblastic condition."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"When an ectopic pregnancy continues to grow within the tube, it can lead to:","options":["Tubal abortion","Tubal rupture","Live birth","Spontaneous resorption"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the ectopic pregnancy continues to grow, it can lead to tubal rupture.  Why A wrong: Tubal abortion results from the pregnancy being expelled/aborting, not continued growth.  Why C wrong: An ectopic pregnancy cannot result in a live birth.  Why D wrong: Continued growth leads to rupture, not resorption."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"The most common site for tubal abortion is:","options":["Ampulla","Isthmus","Interstitium","Infundibulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site for tubal abortion is the ampulla.  Why B wrong: The isthmus is the most common site for tubal rupture, not abortion.  Why C wrong: The interstitium is where ectopic pregnancy survives longest, not the m/c abortion site.  Why D wrong: The infundibulum's outcome is abortion but the ampulla is the most common overall abortion site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"The most common site for tubal rupture is:","options":["Ampulla","Isthmus","Infundibulum","Interstitium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site for tubal rupture is the isthmus.  Why A wrong: The ampulla's outcome is more commonly abortion.  Why C wrong: The infundibulum's outcome is abortion.  Why D wrong: The interstitium's outcome is rupture but the isthmus is the classic m/c rupture site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"In which part of the Fallopian tube does an ectopic pregnancy end the earliest, and at what gestational age?","options":["Ampulla, at 12–16 weeks","Isthmus, at 6–8 weeks","Interstitium, at 12–16 weeks","Infundibulum, at 10–12 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ectopic pregnancy in the isthmus ends earliest, at 6–8 weeks (because the isthmus is narrow and ruptures early).  Why A wrong: The ampulla is not the earliest to end.  Why C wrong: The interstitium is where pregnancy survives the longest, not ends earliest.  Why D wrong: The infundibulum is not the earliest-ending site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"In which part of the Fallopian tube does an ectopic pregnancy survive for the longest time?","options":["Ampulla","Isthmus","Interstitium","Infundibulum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Ectopic pregnancy survives for the longest time in the interstitium because this part is closest to the uterus, where endometrium and myometrium support the pregnancy (12–16 weeks).  Why A wrong: The ampulla is not the longest-surviving tubal site.  Why B wrong: The isthmus ends earliest (6–8 weeks).  Why D wrong: The infundibulum is not the longest-surviving site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Why does an ectopic pregnancy in the interstitial part of the tube survive for the longest time (12–16 weeks)?","options":["Because it is the narrowest part","Because it is closest to the uterus, where the endometrium and myometrium support the pregnancy","Because fertilization occurs there","Because it has the most cilia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The interstitium is closest to the uterus; the uterine endometrium and myometrium support the ectopic pregnancy here (12–16 weeks).  Why A wrong: Being narrow would favor early rupture, not longest survival.  Why C wrong: Fertilization occurs in the ampulla, not the interstitium.  Why D wrong: Cilia number is not the reason for prolonged survival."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Which is considered the most dangerous site of ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Ampulla","Isthmus","Interstitium","Infundibulum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The interstitium is the most dangerous site of ectopic pregnancy (rich blood supply near the uterus, late rupture with severe hemorrhage).  Why A wrong: The ampulla is the most common but not the most dangerous site.  Why B wrong: The isthmus ends earliest and is not the most dangerous.  Why D wrong: The infundibulum is not the most dangerous site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Overall, at which site does an ectopic pregnancy last the longest / survive for the longest time?","options":["Interstitial ectopic","Ampullary ectopic","Abdominal ectopic","Ovarian ectopic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Overall, ectopic pregnancy survives for the longest time as an abdominal ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The interstitium is the longest-surviving tubal site, but overall the abdominal ectopic lasts longer.  Why B wrong: Ampullary ectopic does not survive the longest.  Why D wrong: Ovarian ectopic is not the longest-surviving site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"What is a \"heterotopic pregnancy\"?","options":["A pregnancy in which the fetus has abnormal chromosomes","A twin pregnancy in which one pregnancy is inside the uterus and the other is ectopic","A pregnancy located only in the abdominal cavity","A molar pregnancy with elevated hCG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heterotopic pregnancy is a twin pregnancy in which one pregnancy is inside the uterus and the other is ectopic.  Why A wrong: It refers to location of gestations, not chromosomal abnormality.  Why C wrong: Abdominal location alone is abdominal ectopic, not heterotopic.  Why D wrong: Molar pregnancy is a distinct trophoblastic condition."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"The Fallopian tube is lined by which type of epithelium?","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Ciliated columnar epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tube is lined by ciliated columnar epithelium, which together with tube peristalsis moves the zygote toward the uterine cavity.  Why A wrong: Stratified squamous epithelium lines the vagina/ectocervix, not the tube.  Why C wrong: Simple cuboidal epithelium is not the tubal lining.  Why D wrong: Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"The ciliated columnar epithelium plus tube peristalsis serve which function?","options":["Produce estrogen","Move the zygote toward the uterine cavity","Prevent fertilization","Implant the zygote in the tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ciliated columnar epithelium + tube peristalsis move the zygote toward the uterine cavity; impairment of this leads to ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Estrogen production is an ovarian function.  Why C wrong: Cilia/peristalsis do not prevent fertilization; they aid zygote transport.  Why D wrong: Their role is transport toward the uterus, not tubal implantation (which is abnormal/ectopic)."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Which of the following is a risk factor for ectopic pregnancy that is RELATED to the genital tract?","options":["Smoking","Low socioeconomic status","Adhesions inside the tube (from salpingitis/PID)","Young age of intercourse"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Adhesions inside the tube (from salpingitis, PID, endometriosis, tubal surgery) are genital-tract-related risk factors for ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Smoking is listed under \"Not Related.\"  Why B wrong: Low socioeconomic status is listed under \"Not Related.\"  Why D wrong: Young age of intercourse is listed under \"Not Related.\""},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Which of the following conditions causes adhesions inside the tube that predispose to ectopic pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Salpingitis (inflammation of tube)","PID (Pelvic Inflammatory Disease)","Endometriosis","Tubal surgery","Smoking"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Salpingitis, PID, endometriosis (endometrial tissue blocks the tube), and tubal surgery all cause adhesions inside the tube, a genital-tract-related risk factor.  Why E wrong: Smoking is listed under \"Not Related\" risk factors, not a cause of tubal adhesions."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"In the context of ectopic pregnancy risk, how does endometriosis contribute to tubal adhesions?","options":["It increases hCG levels","Endometrial tissue blocks the tube","It causes immobile cilia","It increases tube peristalsis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In endometriosis, endometrial tissue blocks the tube, causing adhesions that predispose to ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Endometriosis does not act by raising hCG.  Why C wrong: Immobile cilia is a separate mechanism (Kartagener syndrome).  Why D wrong: Adhesions impair, not increase, effective transport."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Immobile cilia in the Fallopian tube, a risk factor for ectopic pregnancy, is known as:","options":["Turner syndrome","Kartagener syndrome","Asherman syndrome","Meigs syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Immobile cilia is known as Kartagener syndrome, which impairs zygote transport and predisposes to ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome is a chromosomal (45,X) disorder, unrelated to ciliary immobility.  Why C wrong: Asherman syndrome is intrauterine adhesions.  Why D wrong: Meigs syndrome is ovarian tumor with ascites and pleural effusion."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Which assisted-reproduction procedure is listed as a genital-tract-related risk factor for ectopic pregnancy?","options":["IVF (In vitro Fertilization)","Intrauterine device insertion","Oral contraceptive use","Amniocentesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IVF (In vitro Fertilization) is listed as a genital-tract-related risk factor for ectopic pregnancy.  Why B wrong: An IUD is not listed as a risk factor here.  Why C wrong: Contraceptives prevent all types of pregnancy including ectopic.  Why D wrong: Amniocentesis is a diagnostic procedure, not a listed risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a risk factor NOT related to the genital tract for ectopic pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Smoking","Low socioeconomic status","Multiple partners","Young age of intercourse","Tubal surgery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Smoking, low socioeconomic status, multiple partners, and young age of intercourse are listed under risk factors NOT related to the genital tract.  Why E wrong: Tubal surgery is a genital-tract-related risk factor, not a \"Not Related\" factor."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"What is the most common risk factor / most common cause of ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Smoking","Salpingitis or PID","IVF","Multiple partners"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common risk factor / m/c cause of ectopic pregnancy is salpingitis or PID (which causes tubal adhesions).  Why A wrong: Smoking is a \"Not Related\" risk factor.  Why C wrong: IVF is a risk factor but not the most common cause.  Why D wrong: Multiple partners is a \"Not Related\" factor."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"The highest risk of ectopic pregnancy is associated with which single best answer?","options":["Tubal surgery","Smoking","Endometriosis","IVF"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The best answer for the highest risk of ectopic pregnancy is tubal surgery.  Why B wrong: Smoking is a \"Not Related\" factor, not the highest-risk factor.  Why C wrong: Endometriosis is a risk factor but not the single highest-risk answer.  Why D wrong: IVF is a risk factor but tubal surgery is the best answer."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"The second best answer for the condition carrying the highest risk of ectopic pregnancy is:","options":["Previous history of ectopic pregnancy","Smoking","Low socioeconomic status","Young age of intercourse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 2nd best answer for highest risk of ectopic pregnancy is a previous history (H/O) of ectopic pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Smoking is a \"Not Related\" factor.  Why C wrong: Low socioeconomic status is a \"Not Related\" factor.  Why D wrong: Young age of intercourse is a \"Not Related\" factor."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Is the risk of ectopic pregnancy increased after using contraceptives?","options":["Yes, contraceptives increase the risk of all ectopic pregnancy","No — contraceptives prevent all types of pregnancy including ectopic pregnancy","Yes, only oral contraceptives increase the risk","Contraceptives have no effect on any pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: No — contraceptives prevent all types of pregnancy including ectopic pregnancy; however, when a contraceptive agent fails, that failure can lead to ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Contraceptives do not increase ectopic risk; they prevent pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Contraceptives generally prevent pregnancy, not selectively raise ectopic risk.  Why D wrong: Contraceptives do prevent pregnancy, so this is false."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"When failure of a contraceptive agent occurs, what may result?","options":["Molar pregnancy only","Ectopic pregnancy","No pregnancy at all","Guaranteed intrauterine pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When failure of a contraceptive agent occurs, it can lead to ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: The source specifies ectopic pregnancy, not molar pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Contraceptive failure means pregnancy can occur.  Why D wrong: Contraceptive failure does not guarantee an intrauterine location."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Regarding similarity with normal pregnancy, which statement about hCG in ectopic pregnancy is correct?","options":["hCG is absent in ectopic pregnancy","hCG is present in ectopic pregnancy, so the urine pregnancy test is positive (+ve)","hCG is present only after 12 weeks","hCG makes the urine pregnancy test negative"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: hCG is present in ectopic pregnancy also, so the Urine Pregnancy Test is positive (+ve).  Why A wrong: hCG is present in ectopic pregnancy.  Why C wrong: hCG is present early, making UPT positive; not delayed to 12 weeks.  Why D wrong: hCG makes the urine pregnancy test positive, not negative."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Which hormone, besides hCG, is present in ectopic pregnancy (similar to normal pregnancy)?","options":["Progesterone","Prolactin","Oxytocin","Relaxin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone is present in ectopic pregnancy, similar to normal pregnancy (both hCG and progesterone are present).  Why B wrong: Prolactin is not the hormone highlighted for this similarity.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is not the relevant hormone here.  Why D wrong: Relaxin is not the hormone described."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Compared with a normal pregnancy, the quantities of hCG and progesterone in an ectopic pregnancy are:","options":["Higher in ectopic pregnancy","Equal to normal pregnancy","Lesser in quantity than normal pregnancy","Completely absent in ectopic pregnancy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Both hCG and progesterone are present in ectopic pregnancy, but they are lesser in quantity than in normal pregnancy.  Why A wrong: They are lower, not higher (higher hCG suggests molar pregnancy).  Why B wrong: They are lower, not equal.  Why D wrong: They are present, just reduced in quantity."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"In a normal pregnancy, how does hCG behave after every 48 hours (up to 10 weeks)?","options":["Levels decrease","Levels double","Levels increase but do not double","Levels remain unchanged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In normal pregnancy, hCG levels double after every 48 hours until 10 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Decreasing levels suggest abortion.  Why C wrong: Increasing but not doubling suggests ectopic pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Levels change (double) in a normal pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"In an ectopic pregnancy, how do hCG levels behave after every 48 hours?","options":["Levels double","Levels increase but do not double","Levels decrease","Levels stay exactly the same"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In ectopic pregnancy, after every 48 hours hCG levels increase but do not double.  Why A wrong: Doubling occurs in normal pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Decreasing levels suggest abortion.  Why D wrong: Levels do increase (just not doubling), so they don't stay identical."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"In a normal pregnancy, at what hCG level on TVS (transvaginal ultrasound) should a gestational sac always be seen inside the uterus?","options":["≥ 500 IU","≥ 1000 IU","≥ 1500 IU","≥ 5000 IU"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In normal pregnancy, when hCG levels are ≥ 1500 IU on TVS, a gestational sac is always seen inside the uterus.  Why A wrong: 500 IU is below the discriminatory threshold.  Why B wrong: 1000 IU is below the stated threshold of 1500 IU.  Why D wrong: 5000 IU is higher than the discriminatory threshold given."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"In an ectopic pregnancy, when hCG levels are > 1500 IU and TVS is done, what will be found?","options":["Gestational sac inside the uterus","No gestational sac inside the uterus; uterus will be empty","A molar (grape-like) mass in the uterus","Twins inside the uterus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In ectopic pregnancy, when hCG > 1500 IU, TVS shows no gestational sac inside the uterus and the uterus will be empty.  Why A wrong: A sac inside the uterus indicates a normal intrauterine pregnancy.  Why C wrong: A grape-like mass indicates molar pregnancy.  Why D wrong: An empty uterus (not twins) is the ectopic finding."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"What is the best combination for diagnosing ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Urine pregnancy test alone","Progesterone alone","hCG + TVS (transvaginal ultrasound)","Abdominal X-ray"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The best diagnosis of ectopic pregnancy uses hCG + TVS (Best = hCG + TVS).  Why A wrong: A urine pregnancy test only confirms pregnancy, not location.  Why B wrong: Progesterone alone is not the best diagnostic combination.  Why D wrong: X-ray is not used to diagnose ectopic pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"A pregnant woman (UPT positive) presents with bleeding in the 1st trimester. What are the three possible causes to be differentiated?","options":["Abortion, ectopic pregnancy, molar pregnancy","Abortion, PID, endometriosis","Molar pregnancy, salpingitis, IVF failure","Ectopic pregnancy, Kartagener syndrome, PID"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bleeding in the 1st trimester in a UPT-positive woman has 3 causes: abortion, ectopic pregnancy, and molar pregnancy.  Why B wrong: PID and endometriosis are risk factors, not causes of first-trimester bleeding here.  Why C wrong: Salpingitis and IVF failure are not the three listed causes.  Why D wrong: Kartagener syndrome and PID are risk factors, not bleeding causes."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"On what basis are abortion, ectopic pregnancy, and molar pregnancy differentiated in first-trimester bleeding?","options":["On the basis of blood pressure","On the basis of hCG levels","On the basis of hemoglobin","On the basis of gestational age only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All three (abortion, ectopic, molar) can be differentiated based on hCG levels.  Why A wrong: Blood pressure is not the differentiating basis.  Why C wrong: Hemoglobin is not the differentiating basis.  Why D wrong: hCG level behavior (not gestational age alone) differentiates them."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"In first-trimester bleeding, which pattern of hCG values indicates abortion?","options":["hCG values less than normal pregnancy, and after 48 hours values decrease","hCG values less than normal, and after 48 hours values increase but do not double","hCG values higher than normal pregnancy","hCG values that double every 48 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In abortion, hCG values are less than normal pregnancy and after 48 hours the values decrease.  Why B wrong: Increasing but not doubling indicates ectopic pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Higher-than-normal hCG indicates molar pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Doubling every 48 hours is a normal pregnancy pattern."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"In first-trimester bleeding, which hCG pattern indicates ectopic pregnancy?","options":["hCG higher than normal pregnancy","hCG values less than normal, and after 48 hours values increase but do not double","hCG values that decrease after 48 hours","hCG values that double after 48 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In ectopic pregnancy, hCG values are less than normal pregnancy, but after 48 hours values increase (but do not double).  Why A wrong: Higher-than-normal hCG indicates molar pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Decreasing values indicate abortion.  Why D wrong: Doubling values indicate normal pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"In first-trimester bleeding, which hCG pattern indicates molar pregnancy?","options":["hCG values less than normal pregnancy","hCG values that decrease after 48 hours","hCG values higher than normal pregnancy","hCG values that increase but do not double"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In molar pregnancy, hCG values are higher than normal pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Values less than normal indicate abortion or ectopic.  Why B wrong: Decreasing values indicate abortion.  Why D wrong: Increasing but not doubling indicates ectopic pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"According to the differentiation concept, when repeat hCG values after 48 hours DECREASE, the diagnosis is:","options":["Ectopic pregnancy","Abortion","Molar pregnancy","Normal pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If hCG levels decrease on repeat after 48 hours, the diagnosis is abortion.  Why A wrong: Ectopic shows increase but not doubling, not a decrease.  Why C wrong: Molar shows higher-than-normal hCG.  Why D wrong: Normal pregnancy shows doubling, not a decrease."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"According to the differentiation concept, when repeat hCG values after 48 hours INCREASE but do not double, the diagnosis is:","options":["Abortion","Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy","Normal pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If hCG levels increase but do not double on repeat, the diagnosis is ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Abortion shows decreasing levels.  Why C wrong: Molar shows values higher than normal pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Normal pregnancy shows doubling."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"For abortion and ectopic pregnancy (both with hCG less than normal), what is the next diagnostic step in the concept flow?","options":["Immediately perform surgery","Repeat hCG values after 48 hours","Give progesterone","Do an abdominal X-ray"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For abortion and ectopic (both with hCG values less than normal pregnancy), the next step is to repeat hCG values after 48 hours to see whether they decrease (abortion) or increase but not double (ectopic).  Why A wrong: Surgery is not the diagnostic next step in this flow.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is not the differentiating next step here.  Why D wrong: X-ray is not part of this diagnostic concept."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"What is the progesterone level typically found in ectopic pregnancy?","options":["< 5 ng/mL","≥ 25 ng/mL","> 50 ng/mL","100 ng/mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In ectopic pregnancy, progesterone is < 5 ng/mL, which is less than in normal pregnancy.  Why B wrong: ≥ 25 ng/mL is the normal pregnancy progesterone level.  Why C wrong: > 50 ng/mL is not the level given for ectopic pregnancy.  Why D wrong: 100 ng/mL is far higher than the ectopic level."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"What is the normal-pregnancy progesterone level referenced for comparison with ectopic pregnancy?","options":["< 5 ng/mL","≥ 25 ng/mL","≥ 10 ng/mL","< 1 ng/mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal pregnancy progesterone is ≥ 25 ng/mL, whereas in ectopic pregnancy it is < 5 ng/mL.  Why A wrong: < 5 ng/mL is the ectopic-pregnancy level.  Why C wrong: ≥ 10 ng/mL is not the value stated for normal pregnancy.  Why D wrong: < 1 ng/mL is far below the normal-pregnancy level."},{"section":"Batch 038 · Fallopian tube anatomy, ectopic pregnancy sites & order of incidence…","question":"Compared with normal pregnancy, progesterone levels in ectopic pregnancy are:","options":["Higher","The same","Less than normal pregnancy","Undetectable"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In ectopic pregnancy, levels of progesterone are less than in normal pregnancy (< 5 ng/mL vs ≥ 25 ng/mL).  Why A wrong: Progesterone is lower, not higher, in ectopic pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Levels differ; ectopic is lower.  Why D wrong: Progesterone is present (< 5 ng/mL), just reduced, not undetectable."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"How many types of endometrial reactions are described in relation to pregnancy in these notes?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe \"2 Reactions\" of the endometrium — the Decidual reaction and the Arias-Stella reaction.  Why A wrong: There is more than one reaction listed.  Why C wrong: Only two, not three, reactions are described.  Why D wrong: Four is more than the two reactions given."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The decidual reaction refers to which of the following changes?","options":["Converting of endometrium into decidua","Converting of myometrium into decidua","Converting of decidua into endometrium","Converting of cervix into decidua"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define the decidual reaction as \"Converting of endometrium to decidua.\"  Why B wrong: The myometrium (muscle layer) is not the layer that becomes decidua; it is the endometrium.  Why C wrong: The direction is endometrium → decidua, not the reverse.  Why D wrong: The cervix is not involved in the decidual reaction."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The Arias-Stella reaction is characterized by all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Enlarged glands in the endometrium (glandular cystic hyperplasia)","Abundant cytoplasm","Hobnail nucleus","Complete absence of glands"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The Arias-Stella reaction shows enlarged/hyperplastic glands, not absence of glands, so \"complete absence of glands\" is NOT a feature.  Why A wrong: Enlarged glands in the endometrium (glandular cystic hyperplasia) is a listed feature.  Why B wrong: Abundant cytoplasm is a listed feature.  Why C wrong: Hobnail nucleus is a listed feature."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"\"Glandular cystic hyperplasia\" of the endometrium is described as part of which reaction?","options":["Decidual reaction","Arias-Stella reaction","Inflammatory reaction","Allergic reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Enlarged glands in the endometrium (glandular cystic hyperplasia) is a characteristic feature of the Arias-Stella reaction.  Why A wrong: The decidual reaction is defined only as conversion of endometrium to decidua.  Why C wrong: An inflammatory reaction is not described here.  Why D wrong: An allergic reaction is not described here."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The type of nucleus described as characteristic of the Arias-Stella reaction is a:","options":["Hobnail nucleus","Owl-eye nucleus","Signet-ring nucleus","Reed-Sternberg nucleus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Hobnail Nucleus\" as a characteristic feature of the Arias-Stella reaction.  Why B wrong: Owl-eye inclusions are seen in CMV infection, not this reaction.  Why C wrong: Signet-ring cells relate to certain carcinomas, not the Arias-Stella reaction.  Why D wrong: Reed-Sternberg cells relate to Hodgkin lymphoma, not this reaction."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"According to the notes, the decidual reaction is seen in which condition(s)?","options":["Normal pregnancy only","Ectopic pregnancy only","Normal pregnancy and ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the decidual reaction is \"Seen in normal pregnancy + Ectopic pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: It is seen in ectopic pregnancy in addition to normal pregnancy.  Why B wrong: It is also seen in normal pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Molar pregnancy is listed for the Arias-Stella reaction, not specifically the decidual reaction here."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The Arias-Stella reaction is seen in which of the following (Select All That Apply)?","options":["Normal pregnancy","Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy","Menopause","Prepubertal state"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the Arias-Stella reaction is seen in normal pregnancy, ectopic pregnancy, and molar pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Menopause is not listed.  Why E wrong: A prepubertal state is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In an ectopic pregnancy, the endometrium undergoes which change?","options":["It gets converted to decidua","It is completely destroyed","It becomes myometrium","It develops chorionic villi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"In case of ectopic pregnancy: Endometrium gets converted to decidua.\"  Why B wrong: It is converted, not destroyed.  Why C wrong: The endometrium does not turn into myometrium.  Why D wrong: Chorionic villi are absent in the shed decidua because the blastocyst is not implanted in the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"Why does an ectopic pregnancy tend to end, according to the notes?","options":["The levels of progesterone are low","The levels of estrogen are extremely high","The uterus contracts strongly","The placenta grows too fast"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"The levels of progesterone are low & ectopic pregnancy has to end.\"  Why B wrong: High estrogen is not given as the reason.  Why C wrong: Uterine contractions are not cited as the reason.  Why D wrong: Rapid placental growth is not the reason given."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"When an ectopic pregnancy ends, what happens to the decidua and what symptom does the patient develop?","options":["The entire decidua is shed and the patient complains of bleeding","The decidua thickens and the patient becomes amenorrheic permanently","The decidua turns into villi and the patient has no symptoms","The decidua is retained and the patient has constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow states \"When Ectopic pregnancy Ends → this entire decidua is shed → So patient complains of bleeding.\"  Why B wrong: The decidua is shed, not thickened, and bleeding (not permanent amenorrhea) occurs.  Why C wrong: Villi are absent, and the patient does have a symptom (bleeding).  Why D wrong: The decidua is shed, not retained, and the symptom is bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The shedding of decidua in the form of a single cast in ectopic pregnancy is termed:","options":["Decidual cast","Molar cast","Membranous shed","Endometrial polyp"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Some female may have decidua shed in the form of 'DECIDUAL CAST'.\"  Why B wrong: \"Molar cast\" is not the term used.  Why C wrong: \"Membranous shed\" is not a described term.  Why D wrong: An endometrial polyp is a different entity, not the shed decidua."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"Why does the decidual cast shed in ectopic pregnancy NOT contain chorionic villi?","options":["Because the blastocyst is not implanted in the uterus","Because the villi are digested by enzymes","Because the villi migrate to the ovary","Because progesterone destroys the villi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Decidua will not have any villi as blastocyst not implanted in uterus.\"  Why B wrong: Enzymatic digestion of villi is not the reason given.  Why C wrong: Villi migrating to the ovary is not a described mechanism.  Why D wrong: Progesterone destroying villi is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, the classic presenting complaint begins with:","options":["Amenorrhea for 6–8 weeks","High-grade fever","Painless heavy menstrual flow","Sudden weight gain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the patient will complain of \"Amenorrhea (for 6–8 weeks)\" first, followed by vaginal bleeding.  Why B wrong: Fever is not part of the presenting complaint listed.  Why C wrong: The bleeding follows amenorrhea; the initial complaint is amenorrhea, not heavy menstrual flow.  Why D wrong: Weight gain is not a listed complaint."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In ruptured ectopic pregnancy, the amenorrhea typically lasts for how long before symptoms appear?","options":["6–8 weeks","1–2 weeks","12–16 weeks","20–24 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify amenorrhea \"for 6–8 weeks.\"  Why B wrong: 1–2 weeks is too short and not stated.  Why C wrong: 12–16 weeks is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 20–24 weeks is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"After amenorrhea, the next complaint listed in ruptured ectopic pregnancy is:","options":["Vaginal bleeding","Diarrhea","Headache","Cough"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sequence given is amenorrhea \"followed by\" vaginal bleeding.  Why B wrong: Diarrhea is not part of the presentation.  Why C wrong: Headache is not listed.  Why D wrong: Cough is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"According to the notes, which is the MOST COMMON symptom of ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Pain in abdomen","Vaginal bleeding","Amenorrhea","Fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly mark \"Pain in Abdomen\" as the \"Most common symptom of ectopic pregnancy.\"  Why B wrong: Vaginal bleeding is a feature but not labeled as the most common symptom.  Why C wrong: Amenorrhea is the first complaint but not labeled the most common symptom.  Why D wrong: Fever is not among the listed features."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The classic triad of ectopic pregnancy consists of:","options":["Amenorrhea, vaginal bleeding, and pain in abdomen","Fever, vomiting, and headache","Hypertension, proteinuria, and edema","Amenorrhea, breast tenderness, and nausea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Triad of Ectopic Pregnancy\" is depicted as amenorrhea, vaginal bleeding, and pain in abdomen.  Why B wrong: Fever/vomiting/headache is not the triad described.  Why C wrong: Hypertension/proteinuria/edema describes pre-eclampsia, not the ectopic triad.  Why D wrong: Breast tenderness and nausea are not part of the depicted triad."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"Excessive bleeding from a ruptured ectopic pregnancy leads to which type of shock?","options":["Hypovolemic shock","Cardiogenic shock","Neurogenic shock","Septic shock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Due to excessive bleeding → Hypovolemic shock.\"  Why B wrong: Cardiogenic shock is due to pump failure, not blood loss.  Why C wrong: Neurogenic shock is due to loss of vascular tone, not hemorrhage.  Why D wrong: Septic shock is due to infection, not the acute blood loss described."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In the classic \"whenever in question\" picture, the duration of amenorrhea preceding a ruptured ectopic pregnancy is given as:","options":["6–8 (6–10) weeks","2–4 weeks","10–14 weeks","16–20 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Patient with 6–8 (6–10 weeks) of Amenorrhea.\"  Why B wrong: 2–4 weeks is not the stated range.  Why C wrong: 10–14 weeks is not the stated range.  Why D wrong: 16–20 weeks is not the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"A patient presents with 6–10 weeks of amenorrhea, vaginal bleeding, abdominal pain, a positive urine pregnancy test, and is in shock. The MOST LIKELY diagnosis is:","options":["Ruptured ectopic pregnancy","Normal early pregnancy","Urinary tract infection","Appendicitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give exactly this picture (amenorrhea + bleeding + pain + positive UPT + shock) as pointing to \"Ruptured Ectopic pregnancy.\"  Why B wrong: Shock and this bleeding/pain pattern are not features of a normal pregnancy.  Why C wrong: A UTI does not cause amenorrhea with a positive pregnancy test and shock.  Why D wrong: Appendicitis would not give amenorrhea with a positive urine pregnancy test."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The urine pregnancy test in the classic ruptured ectopic pregnancy scenario is:","options":["Positive (+ve)","Negative","Always inconclusive","Not indicated"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes include \"Urine Pregnancy Test (+ve)\" in the diagnostic picture.  Why B wrong: The test is positive, not negative.  Why C wrong: It is described as positive, not inconclusive.  Why D wrong: It is part of the diagnostic picture, so it is indicated."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In the normal course described, the egg travels down through the fallopian tube and implants where?","options":["Into the wall of the uterus","In the ovary","In the cervix","In the peritoneal cavity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure legend states \"Normal course of an egg down through the fallopian tube and into the wall of the uterus.\"  Why B wrong: The ovary is where the egg is released, not where it normally implants.  Why C wrong: Cervical implantation is abnormal (a type of ectopic), not the normal course.  Why D wrong: Peritoneal implantation is abnormal, not the normal course."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In the illustrated ectopic pregnancy, the embryo is implanted in which structure before it reaches the uterus?","options":["Right fallopian tube","Left ovary","Cervical canal","Urinary bladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure states \"The embryo is implanted in the right fallopian tube before it reaches the uterus.\"  Why B wrong: The illustration shows the tube, not the ovary, as the implantation site.  Why C wrong: The cervical canal is not the site shown.  Why D wrong: The urinary bladder is not an implantation site."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"As the ectopic embryo grows within the fallopian tube (step B in the figure), what happens to the tube?","options":["The fallopian tube bulges","The fallopian tube shrinks","The tube calcifies","The tube detaches from the uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure states \"The embryo grows causing the fallopian tube to bulge.\"  Why B wrong: The tube enlarges/bulges, it does not shrink.  Why C wrong: Calcification is not described.  Why D wrong: Detachment is not described in this step."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In step C of the figure, when the ectopic embryo grows larger, the fallopian tube:","options":["Ruptures and hemorrhages","Heals spontaneously","Reabsorbs the embryo silently","Moves the embryo into the uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure states \"As the embryo grows larger, the fallopian tube ruptures and hemorrhages.\"  Why B wrong: The tube ruptures rather than heals.  Why C wrong: Silent reabsorption is not what is depicted.  Why D wrong: The embryo does not relocate into the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"On ultrasound (USG) in a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, free fluid is characteristically seen in the:","options":["Pouch of Douglas (POD)","Pleural cavity","Pericardial sac","Renal pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"On USG: free fluid in POD\" (Pouch of Douglas).  Why B wrong: The pleural cavity is a chest structure, not the site described.  Why C wrong: The pericardial sac surrounds the heart, not relevant here.  Why D wrong: The renal pelvis is part of the kidney, not the POD."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The Pouch of Douglas is also known by which of the following names (Select All That Apply)?","options":["Cul de sac","Recto-uterine pouch","Utero-vesical pouch","Vesico-vaginal pouch","Rectovesical pouch"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the Pouch of Douglas as \"cul de sac\" and \"Recto-uterine pouch.\"  Why C wrong: The utero-vesical pouch is a separate, anterior pouch labeled distinctly in the diagram.  Why D wrong: \"Vesico-vaginal pouch\" is not a label used for the POD.  Why E wrong: The rectovesical pouch is a male anatomical structure, not the female Pouch of Douglas."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In the pelvic diagram, the pouch located anterior to the uterus (between uterus and bladder) is the:","options":["Utero-vesical pouch","Recto-uterine pouch","Pouch of Douglas","Cul de sac"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the anterior pouch (between uterus and urinary bladder) as the \"Utero vesical pouch.\"  Why B wrong: The recto-uterine pouch (Pouch of Douglas) lies posterior, between uterus and rectum.  Why C wrong: The Pouch of Douglas is the posterior pouch, not the anterior one.  Why D wrong: \"Cul de sac\" is another name for the Pouch of Douglas (posterior)."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In the labeled pelvic diagram, \"UB\" refers to which structure?","options":["Urinary bladder","Umbilical band","Uterine base","Upper bowel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the pelvic diagram \"UB\" is labeled adjacent to the urethra and utero-vesical pouch, denoting the urinary bladder.  Why B wrong: \"Umbilical band\" is not an anatomical label in the diagram.  Why C wrong: \"Uterine base\" is not the meaning of UB here.  Why D wrong: \"Upper bowel\" is not represented by UB."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"Following rupture of an ectopic pregnancy, blood collects in the peritoneum, producing which condition?","options":["Hemoperitoneum","Hemothorax","Hemarthrosis","Hematuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow states rupture → bleeding → \"Blood collects in peritoneum → Hemoperitoneum.\"  Why B wrong: Hemothorax is blood in the chest cavity, not the peritoneum.  Why C wrong: Hemarthrosis is blood in a joint.  Why D wrong: Hematuria is blood in the urine."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"Arrange the sequence following rupture of an ectopic pregnancy that leads to hemoperitoneum:","options":["Rupture → bleeding occurs → blood collects in peritoneum → hemoperitoneum","Bleeding → hemoperitoneum → rupture → blood collects","Hemoperitoneum → rupture → bleeding → collection","Blood collects → rupture → hemoperitoneum → bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart order is: rupture of ectopic pregnancy → bleeding occurs → blood collects in peritoneum → hemoperitoneum.  Why B wrong: The order is scrambled; rupture precedes bleeding.  Why C wrong: Hemoperitoneum is the end result, not the first step.  Why D wrong: Blood collection cannot precede the rupture and bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"When blood from a hemoperitoneum irritates the diaphragm, the patient develops:","options":["Referred pain in the shoulder tip","Pain in the knee","Numbness of the face","Toothache"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Irritate Diaphragm → Referred pain in shoulder tip.\"  Why B wrong: Knee pain is not a referred site of diaphragmatic irritation.  Why C wrong: Facial numbness is unrelated to diaphragmatic irritation.  Why D wrong: Toothache is not the referred pain described."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"A bluish-black discoloration around the umbilicus indicating hemoperitoneum is called:","options":["Cullen sign","Grey Turner sign","Chadwick sign","Goodell sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Bluish black area around the umbilicus → Cullen Sign,\" indicating hemoperitoneum.  Why B wrong: Grey Turner sign is flank bruising, not periumbilical.  Why C wrong: Chadwick sign is bluish discoloration of the cervix/vagina in pregnancy, not periumbilical bruising.  Why D wrong: Goodell sign is softening of the cervix, not a bruise."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"Cullen sign indicates which underlying condition?","options":["Hemoperitoneum (blood in peritoneum)","Pulmonary embolism","Deep vein thrombosis","Cerebral hemorrhage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Cullen sign \"Indicates: Hemoperitoneum (Blood in peritoneum).\"  Why B wrong: Pulmonary embolism is not indicated by Cullen sign.  Why C wrong: DVT is not indicated by Cullen sign.  Why D wrong: Cerebral hemorrhage is intracranial, unrelated to Cullen sign."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"Besides ruptured ectopic pregnancy, blood in the peritoneum (hemoperitoneum) can also be due to (Select All That Apply):","options":["Ruptured cyst","Hemorrhagic pancreatitis","Common cold","Migraine","Iron-deficiency anemia"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Blood in peritoneum can be due to: 1. Ruptured cyst, 2. Hemorrhagic pancreatitis.\"  Why C wrong: A common cold does not cause hemoperitoneum.  Why D wrong: Migraine is unrelated to hemoperitoneum.  Why E wrong: Iron-deficiency anemia is a blood disorder, not a cause of blood collecting in the peritoneum."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In a patient with ruptured ectopic pregnancy who is in shock, what should the nurse/clinician NOT do?","options":["Wait for USG to confirm the diagnosis","Establish IV access","Arrange blood","Prepare for surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes stress \"Do NOT wait for USG to confirm the diagnosis\" in a shocked patient — it is a clinical diagnosis.  Why B wrong: Establishing IV access is appropriate resuscitation, not something to avoid.  Why C wrong: Arranging blood is part of correct management.  Why D wrong: Preparing for surgery (laparotomy) is appropriate definitive management."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"A ruptured ectopic pregnancy in a patient who is in shock is primarily a:","options":["Clinical diagnosis","Radiological diagnosis requiring MRI","Histopathological diagnosis","Genetic diagnosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"It is a clinical diagnosis,\" so one should not wait for imaging.  Why B wrong: One should NOT wait for imaging (including USG/MRI) in the shocked patient.  Why C wrong: Histopathology is not needed to make the acute diagnosis.  Why D wrong: It is not a genetic diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"Which bedside diagnostic procedure can be performed to confirm a ruptured ectopic pregnancy at the bedside?","options":["Culdocentesis","Amniocentesis","Chorionic villus sampling","Cordocentesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Can do — CULDOCENTESIS.\"  Why B wrong: Amniocentesis samples amniotic fluid in an intrauterine pregnancy, not relevant here.  Why C wrong: Chorionic villus sampling is a prenatal genetic test, not for ruptured ectopic.  Why D wrong: Cordocentesis samples fetal cord blood, not used here."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In culdocentesis, the syringe/needle is passed through which route?","options":["Through the posterior fornix of the vagina","Through the anterior abdominal wall midline","Through the rectum","Through the urethra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Culdocentesis: Take a syringe through posterior fornix of vagina.\"  Why B wrong: It is a transvaginal, not transabdominal midline, approach.  Why C wrong: The rectum is not the route for culdocentesis.  Why D wrong: The urethra is not the route used."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"During culdocentesis, after passing through the posterior fornix, the needle enters which space?","options":["Pouch of Douglas","Uterine cavity","Urinary bladder","Rectal ampulla"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the needle \"Enter into pouch of Douglas.\"  Why B wrong: The uterine cavity is not the target space.  Why C wrong: The bladder is anterior and is not entered.  Why D wrong: The rectum is not entered during culdocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"On culdocentesis, aspiration of NON-clotting blood indicates:","options":["Ruptured ectopic pregnancy","Normal finding","Needle within an artery/vein","Pelvic abscess"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"If this syringe contains Non clotting blood → Ruptured ectopic pregnancy.\"  Why B wrong: Non-clotting blood in the POD is abnormal and diagnostic of ruptured ectopic.  Why C wrong: Blood that CLOTS suggests the needle is in a vessel; non-clotting blood indicates hemoperitoneum from rupture.  Why D wrong: A pelvic abscess would yield pus, not non-clotting blood."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"During culdocentesis, if the aspirated blood CLOTS, it means:","options":["The syringe/needle was in some artery or vein","The patient definitely has a ruptured ectopic pregnancy","There is no bleeding at all","The patient has an infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed note states \"If you get blood which clots: It means syringe was in some artery/vein.\"  Why B wrong: Clotting blood suggests a vessel puncture, not confirmation of hemoperitoneum.  Why C wrong: Getting blood shows there is bleeding into a vessel; it does not mean no bleeding.  Why D wrong: Clotting relates to vessel puncture, not infection."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In the culdocentesis diagram, which instrument holds the posterior lip of the cervix?","options":["Tenaculum","Bivalve speculum","Forceps","Sound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the \"Tenaculum holding posterior lip of cervix.\"  Why B wrong: The bivalve speculum holds the vaginal walls open; it does not grasp the cervical lip.  Why C wrong: \"Forceps\" is not the labeled instrument.  Why D wrong: A uterine sound measures the cavity; it is not shown grasping the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In the culdocentesis setup, which instrument is used to keep the vagina open for access?","options":["Bivalve speculum","Tenaculum","Long needle","Rectal tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the \"Bivalve speculum,\" which opens the vagina for the procedure.  Why B wrong: The tenaculum grasps the cervix, not opens the vagina.  Why C wrong: The long needle on the syringe performs the aspiration, not vaginal opening.  Why D wrong: A rectal tube is not part of this diagram."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In the management of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, the patient's clinical state that dictates urgency is that the patient is:","options":["In shock","Afebrile and stable","In active labour","Fully conscious with no distress"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The management box states \"Management of ruptured ectopic pregnancy → Patient is in shock.\"  Why B wrong: The patient is unstable/in shock, not stable.  Why C wrong: Active labour is not the described state.  Why D wrong: A patient in shock is not without distress."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The priority management of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy in shock is:","options":["Resuscitation","Discharge home","Oral rehydration only","Reassurance and observation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Priority management: Resuscitation\" as the immediate step.  Why B wrong: A shocked patient must not be discharged.  Why C wrong: Oral rehydration is inadequate for hemorrhagic shock; IV resuscitation is required.  Why D wrong: Mere observation is inappropriate for a shocked, bleeding patient."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"As part of resuscitation for ruptured ectopic pregnancy, how many IV lines are recommended in the notes?","options":["2 IV lines","1 IV line","4 IV lines","No IV line"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The resuscitation list specifies \"2 I/V line.\"  Why B wrong: The notes call for two, not one, IV lines.  Why C wrong: Four IV lines is not what is stated.  Why D wrong: IV access is essential; \"no IV line\" is wrong."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"Which resuscitation measures are included in the priority management of ruptured ectopic pregnancy (Select All That Apply)?","options":["Establish 2 IV lines","Give IV fluids","Arrange blood","Catheterize the patient","Send home with oral antibiotics"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The resuscitation list is 2 IV lines, IV fluid, blood arrangement, catheterization, and blood investigations.  Why E wrong: Discharge with oral antibiotics is not part of resuscitation for a shocked bleeding patient."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In resuscitation for ruptured ectopic pregnancy, which of the following investigations/reports are ordered as listed?","options":["CBC, ABO, Rh, CT, BT","LFT, RFT, TFT only","ECG and echocardiography only","EEG and CSF analysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"CBC, ABO, Rh, CT, BT report\" — complete blood count, ABO grouping, Rh typing, clotting time, and bleeding time.  Why B wrong: LFT/RFT/TFT are not the specific investigations listed.  Why C wrong: ECG/echo are not the listed investigations.  Why D wrong: EEG/CSF analysis are unrelated to this obstetric emergency."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"Why is catheterization performed as part of resuscitation in ruptured ectopic pregnancy?","options":["It is a listed resuscitation measure (to monitor urine output/manage the shocked patient)","To deliver the fetus","To perform culdocentesis","To measure the diagonal conjugate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Catheterize\" is explicitly listed among the resuscitation measures for the shocked patient.  Why B wrong: A catheter does not deliver a fetus.  Why C wrong: Culdocentesis is a separate vaginal procedure, not catheterization.  Why D wrong: The diagonal conjugate is a pelvic measurement, not measured by catheterization."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The definitive management of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy described in the notes is:","options":["Laparotomy (opening the abdomen of the patient)","Bed rest and analgesics","Oral misoprostol","Blood transfusion alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state definitive management is to \"Open the abdomen of patient → called as LAPAROTOMY.\"  Why B wrong: Bed rest/analgesics do not treat life-threatening rupture.  Why C wrong: Misoprostol is not the definitive treatment for a ruptured ectopic.  Why D wrong: Transfusion supports resuscitation but is not the definitive surgical management."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The surgical procedure of opening the abdomen in a ruptured ectopic pregnancy is called:","options":["Laparotomy","Laparoscopy","Hysteroscopy","Colposcopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state opening the abdomen is \"called as LAPAROTOMY.\"  Why B wrong: Laparoscopy is minimally invasive keyhole surgery, not \"opening the abdomen\" as described.  Why C wrong: Hysteroscopy visualizes the uterine cavity, not an open abdominal procedure.  Why D wrong: Colposcopy examines the cervix, not an abdominal surgery."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"According to the management flow, resuscitation and definitive management (laparotomy) are best described as:","options":["Carried out together (resuscitation + definitive management)","Only resuscitation is ever needed","Only laparotomy, with no resuscitation","Neither is required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows priority resuscitation \"+\" definitive management branching together from the shocked patient, i.e., done in combination.  Why B wrong: Definitive surgical management is also required.  Why C wrong: Resuscitation is still needed alongside surgery.  Why D wrong: Both are clearly required."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"In the pelvic anatomy diagram, the structure lying immediately posterior to the vagina and Pouch of Douglas is the:","options":["Rectum","Urinary bladder","Urethra","Ovary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the \"Rectum\" posteriorly, with the Pouch of Douglas (recto-uterine pouch) between the uterus and rectum, and the anus below.  Why B wrong: The urinary bladder lies anteriorly (near the utero-vesical pouch), not posteriorly.  Why C wrong: The urethra is anterior and inferior, not posterior to the vagina.  Why D wrong: The ovary is not the labeled posterior structure in this sagittal diagram."},{"section":"Batch 039 · Endometrial reactions in pregnancy, decidual cast in ectopic pregnancy…","question":"The most common presenting symptom, most important initial complaint duration, and hemodynamic complication of ectopic pregnancy are, respectively:","options":["Abdominal pain; amenorrhea 6–8 weeks; hypovolemic shock","Vaginal bleeding; amenorrhea 2 weeks; septic shock","Fever; amenorrhea 12 weeks; cardiogenic shock","Headache; amenorrhea 20 weeks; neurogenic shock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pain in abdomen is the most common symptom, amenorrhea lasts 6–8 weeks initially, and excessive bleeding causes hypovolemic shock — all stated in the notes.  Why B wrong: The shock type is hypovolemic (not septic) and amenorrhea is 6–8 weeks (not 2).  Why C wrong: Fever is not a listed symptom and shock is hypovolemic, not cardiogenic.  Why D wrong: Headache is not listed; the shock is hypovolemic, not neurogenic."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"According to the notes, there are how many ways in which the abdomen can be opened for surgery?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Always are 2 ways in which abdomen can be opened\" — open surgery (laparotomy) and laparoscopy.  Why A wrong: There are two, not one.  Why C wrong: Only two ways are listed, not three.  Why D wrong: Four is incorrect; only laparotomy and laparoscopy are given."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"The open-surgery method of opening the abdomen is also known as:","options":["Laparoscopy","Laparotomy","Culdocentesis","Hysteroscopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label open surgery as \"Laparotomy,\" where the abdomen is opened between the umbilicus and pubic symphysis.  Why A wrong: Laparoscopy is the minimally invasive keyhole method, not open surgery.  Why C wrong: Culdocentesis is needle aspiration of the pouch of Douglas, not abdominal opening.  Why D wrong: Hysteroscopy visualises the uterine cavity, not the abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In the laparotomy diagram, the vertical open-surgery incision is described as running between which two landmarks?","options":["Xiphisternum and umbilicus","Umbilicus (Umb) and pubic symphysis (Pub symp)","Two anterior superior iliac spines","Umbilicus and xiphisternum only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The laparotomy diagram labels \"Umb\" (umbilicus) above and \"Pub symp\" (pubic symphysis) below, marking the extent of the midline incision.  Why A wrong: The upper landmark shown is the umbilicus, not the xiphisternum.  Why C wrong: These are transverse pelvic landmarks, not the vertical incision shown.  Why D wrong: The lower landmark is the pubic symphysis, not the xiphisternum."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, by which approach is the abdomen opened?","options":["Laparoscopy","Laparotomy","Culdotomy","Mini-laparotomy under local anaesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in ruptured ectopic pregnancy the abdomen is opened by LAPAROTOMY, as in all emergency surgery where vitals are unstable.  Why A wrong: Laparoscopy is never done when the patient is in shock/vitals are unstable.  Why C wrong: Culdotomy is not the described emergency approach.  Why D wrong: A full laparotomy, not a mini-procedure, is required for the emergency."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"The surgery performed in a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, in which the entire ruptured tube is removed, is called:","options":["Salpingostomy","Salpingectomy","Salpingo-oophorectomy","Cornual resection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in ruptured ectopic the entire ruptured tube is removed, which is called SALPINGECTOMY.  Why A wrong: Salpingostomy opens the tube but preserves it (used in unruptured cases with incomplete family).  Why C wrong: Salpingo-oophorectomy removes tube + ovary and is NOT done in ectopic.  Why D wrong: Cornual resection is not the procedure described here."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is the definitive management of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Methotrexate therapy","Expectant management/observation","Laparotomy (surgery)","Laparoscopy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the definitive management of ruptured ectopic is LAPAROTOMY, and best management of ruptured ectopic is surgery.  Why A wrong: Methotrexate is medical management for unruptured, not ruptured, ectopic.  Why B wrong: Expectant management has no role in ruptured ectopic.  Why D wrong: Laparoscopy is never done when vitals are unstable, as in rupture."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, salpingectomy is performed:","options":["Only if the patient is nulliparous","Only if the patient is multiparous","Whether the patient is nulliparous or multiparous","Only if the patient's family is complete"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state salpingectomy is done \"whether patient is nulliparous/multiparous\" in ruptured ectopic.  Why A wrong: Parity does not restrict salpingectomy in the ruptured emergency setting.  Why B wrong: It is not limited to multiparous women.  Why D wrong: In ruptured ectopic, the tube is removed regardless of family completion."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Which of the following has NO role in the management of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Medical management","Expectant management","Laparoscopy","Salpingectomy via laparotomy","Any surgery except salpingectomy"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list four things with no role in ruptured ectopic: medical management, expectant management, laparoscopy (never done in shock/unstable vitals), and any surgery except salpingectomy.  Why D wrong (not selected): Salpingectomy via laparotomy is precisely the treatment that IS done.  Why A/B/C/E correct: All are explicitly listed under \"There is no role of\" for ruptured ectopic."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Why is laparoscopy NOT done in a ruptured ectopic pregnancy?","options":["It is too expensive","It is never done if the patient is in shock/vitals are unstable","It cannot remove the tube","It requires general anaesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify laparoscopy \"is never done if patient is in shock/vitals are unstable,\" which is the case in ruptured ectopic.  Why A wrong: Cost is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Laparoscopy can remove the tube; hemodynamic instability is the reason it is avoided.  Why D wrong: Anaesthesia type is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"A patient with unruptured ectopic pregnancy classically complains of amenorrhea lasting approximately how many weeks?","options":["2–4 weeks","6–10 weeks","12–16 weeks","20–24 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the first complaint of unruptured ectopic as amenorrhea of 6–10 weeks.  Why A wrong: The stated range is 6–10 weeks, not 2–4.  Why C wrong: 12–16 weeks is second trimester, not the classic ectopic amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: 20–24 weeks is far beyond the described window."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Which of the following make up the classic clinical triad of unruptured ectopic pregnancy in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Amenorrhea","Vaginal bleeding","Pain in abdomen","High-grade fever","Projectile vomiting"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the patient's complaints as (1) Amenorrhea 6–10 weeks, (2) Vaginal bleeding, and (3) Pain in abdomen — the classic ectopic triad.  Why D wrong: High-grade fever is not part of the listed triad.  Why E wrong: Projectile vomiting is not a listed feature of ectopic pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"According to the notes, what is the most specific/most common symptom of ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Vaginal bleeding","Pain in abdomen","Amenorrhea","Adnexal mass"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Most specific symptom/most common symptom of ectopic pregnancy = Pain in Abdomen.\"  Why A wrong: Vaginal bleeding is a feature but not the most common/specific per the notes.  Why C wrong: Amenorrhea is a complaint but not identified as the most specific symptom.  Why D wrong: Adnexal mass is a per-vaginal examination finding, not the most common symptom."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"On per-vaginal (bimanual) examination of a patient with ectopic pregnancy, which sign is characteristically elicited?","options":["Cervical movement tenderness (cervical excitation)","Hegar's sign","Chadwick's sign","Goodell's sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that on per vaginal examination there is cervical movement tenderness.  Why B wrong: Hegar's sign (soft isthmus) is a normal early-pregnancy sign, not specific to ectopic.  Why C wrong: Chadwick's sign is bluish discolouration of the vulva/vagina in normal pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Goodell's sign is cervical softening in normal pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"On per-vaginal examination in ectopic pregnancy, the examiner \"may get\" which of the following masses?","options":["Fibroid mass","Adnexal (tubal) mass","Cervical polyp","Ovarian cyst on the contralateral side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the examiner \"May get = Adnexal mass (Tubal)\" on per vaginal examination.  Why A wrong: A fibroid mass is not the described ectopic finding.  Why C wrong: A cervical polyp is unrelated to ectopic examination findings.  Why D wrong: The described mass is an adnexal/tubal mass, not a contralateral ovarian cyst."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is the FIRST investigation of choice for ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Transvaginal sonography (TVS)","Serum β-hCG alone","Laparoscopy","Plain X-ray abdomen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the \"1st Investigation: TVS\" (transvaginal sonography).  Why B wrong: β-hCG is combined with TVS as the best investigation, but TVS is the first-line imaging.  Why C wrong: Laparoscopy is the gold standard/diagnostic, not the first investigation.  Why D wrong: Plain X-ray has no role in diagnosing ectopic pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"According to the notes, what is the single best (1st best answer) investigation for diagnosing ectopic pregnancy?","options":["TVS alone","TVS + β-hCG","β-hCG alone","Serum progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give \"TVS + β hCG\" as the 1st best answer for best investigation of ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: TVS alone is listed as the 2nd best answer, not the 1st.  Why C wrong: β-hCG alone is not the described best combination.  Why D wrong: Serum progesterone is not listed as the best investigation."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Which investigation is listed as the \"2nd best answer\" for ectopic pregnancy?","options":["TVS","β-hCG","Laparoscopy","MRI pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"TVS (2nd best Answer)\" for best investigation.  Why B wrong: β-hCG is combined with TVS for the 1st best answer, not listed alone as 2nd best.  Why C wrong: Laparoscopy is the gold standard, a separate category.  Why D wrong: MRI is not listed in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is the GOLD STANDARD investigation for ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Transvaginal sonography","Laparoscopy","Serum β-hCG","Culdocentesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the gold standard as Laparoscopy (or Laparoscopy + TVS).  Why A wrong: TVS is the first/best imaging but not the gold standard.  Why C wrong: β-hCG is a supportive marker, not the gold standard.  Why D wrong: Culdocentesis is not listed as the gold standard here."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"How many options exist for the management of an unruptured ectopic pregnancy according to the notes?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Management of unruptured Ectopic: There are 2 options\" — medical management and surgical management.  Why A wrong: Two options are listed, not one.  Why C wrong: Only medical and surgical management are described.  Why D wrong: Four is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is the BEST management option for an UNRUPTURED ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Surgery","Medical management","Laparotomy","Salpingectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Unruptured Ectopic Pregnancy = Best Management → Medical Management\" and mark medical management as the \"Best option.\"  Why A wrong: Surgery is the best management for ruptured, not unruptured, ectopic.  Why C wrong: Laparotomy is for the ruptured emergency.  Why D wrong: Salpingectomy is a surgical option, not the best (first) management for unruptured cases."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is the DRUG OF CHOICE for the medical management of unruptured ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Mifepristone","Methotrexate","Misoprostol","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the drug of choice is METHOTREXATE, a folic acid antagonist.  Why A wrong: Mifepristone is an antiprogestin used for medical abortion, not the drug of choice here.  Why C wrong: Misoprostol is a prostaglandin analogue, not used for ectopic medical management.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is a uterotonic, irrelevant to ectopic medical management."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Methotrexate, the drug used in ectopic pregnancy, belongs to which pharmacological class?","options":["Folic acid antagonist","Beta-blocker","Calcium channel blocker","Progesterone agonist"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe methotrexate as a folic acid antagonist.  Why B wrong: It is not a beta-blocker.  Why C wrong: It is not a calcium channel blocker.  Why D wrong: It is an antimetabolite, not a progesterone agonist."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is the dose of methotrexate used in the medical management of ectopic pregnancy?","options":["25 mg/m²","50 mg/m²","100 mg/m²","5 mg/m²"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the dose = 50 mg/m².  Why A wrong: 25 mg/m² is not the stated dose.  Why C wrong: 100 mg/m² is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 5 mg/m² is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Which of the following conditions must be fulfilled before giving methotrexate for ectopic pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Patient should be hemodynamically stable","It should be a case of unruptured ectopic pregnancy","Size of ectopic pregnancy = 4 cm","Appropriate β-hCG value","Patient must be in hypovolemic shock"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list four conditions for methotrexate: hemodynamic stability, unruptured ectopic, ectopic size = 4 cm, and an appropriate β-hCG value.  Why E wrong: Shock is a contraindication; methotrexate requires a hemodynamically STABLE patient.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly listed conditions to be fulfilled."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is the maximum size of the ectopic pregnancy for which methotrexate can be used, per the notes?","options":["2 cm","4 cm","6 cm","8 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the size of ectopic pregnancy = 4 cm as one of the methotrexate criteria.  Why A wrong: 2 cm is not the stated size.  Why C wrong: 6 cm exceeds the criterion.  Why D wrong: 8 cm exceeds the criterion."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"According to Dutta, the β-hCG value below which methotrexate can be given for ectopic pregnancy is:","options":["< 1000 IU","< 3000 IU","< 5000 IU","< 10000 IU"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the Dutta cut-off as β-hCG < 3000 IU.  Why A wrong: < 1000 IU is the value at which observation/expectant management may be chosen, not the Dutta methotrexate cut-off.  Why C wrong: < 5000 IU is the updated value, not Dutta's.  Why D wrong: < 10000 IU is not given."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"The UPDATED β-hCG cut-off value below which methotrexate can be used in ectopic pregnancy is:","options":["< 1000 IU","< 3000 IU","< 5000 IU","< 15000 IU"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the updated value as β-hCG < 5000 IU.  Why A wrong: < 1000 IU relates to observation/expectant management.  Why B wrong: < 3000 IU is the older Dutta value, not the updated one.  Why D wrong: < 15000 IU is not given."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is stated to be the best management of unruptured ectopic pregnancy in the highlighted box?","options":["Multiple-dose oral methotrexate","Methotrexate (i/m) single dose therapy","Laparotomy with salpingectomy","Expectant management"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed statement reads \"Methotrexate (i/m) single dose therapy is best management of Unruptured ectopic pregnancy.\"  Why A wrong: The notes specify single-dose intramuscular therapy, not multiple oral doses.  Why C wrong: Salpingectomy via laparotomy is surgical management for ruptured cases.  Why D wrong: Expectant management is not preferred due to risk of rupture."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"By what route is single-dose methotrexate given for unruptured ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Oral","Intramuscular (i/m)","Intravenous","Intrathecal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Methotrexate is given by the intramuscular (i/m) route as single-dose therapy.  Why A wrong: Oral route is not specified.  Why C wrong: Intravenous route is not specified.  Why D wrong: Intrathecal route is not used here."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"If all conditions for methotrexate are fulfilled AND the β-hCG value is < 1000 IU with values decreasing on their own, what management is chosen?","options":["Immediate laparotomy","Observation (expectant management)","Single-dose methotrexate","Salpingectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when methotrexate conditions are met, hCG < 1000 IU and the values are decreasing themselves, the plan is OBSERVATION (expectant management).  Why A wrong: Laparotomy is for ruptured/unstable cases.  Why C wrong: With spontaneously falling low hCG, observation is chosen over drug therapy.  Why D wrong: Salpingectomy is a surgical intervention, not indicated here."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Below which β-hCG value (with spontaneously decreasing values) is expectant management/observation considered in ectopic pregnancy?","options":["< 1000 IU","< 3000 IU","< 5000 IU","< 500 IU"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify observation when hCG < 1000 IU and values are decreasing by themselves.  Why B wrong: < 3000 IU is the Dutta methotrexate cut-off.  Why C wrong: < 5000 IU is the updated methotrexate cut-off.  Why D wrong: < 500 IU is not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Why is expectant management (observation) NOT preferred in ectopic pregnancy?","options":["Risk of hemorrhage from the ovary","Risk of rupture of the ectopic","Risk of infection","Risk of drug toxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes note observation/expectant management is \"Not preferred → Risk of rupture of ectopic.\"  Why A wrong: The stated risk is rupture of the ectopic, not ovarian hemorrhage.  Why C wrong: Infection is not the reason given.  Why D wrong: Drug toxicity is unrelated to observation (no drug is given)."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is the 2nd option for managing an unruptured ectopic pregnancy (when medical management is not chosen)?","options":["Expectant management","Surgery","Methotrexate","Blood transfusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the \"2nd option in unruptured ectopic pregnancy → Surgery.\"  Why A wrong: Expectant management is described as not preferred.  Why C wrong: Methotrexate is the medical (1st/best) option, not the 2nd surgical option.  Why D wrong: Blood transfusion is supportive, not a definitive 2nd option."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In UNRUPTURED ectopic pregnancy requiring surgery, by which route is the abdomen opened?","options":["Laparotomy","Laparoscopy","Vaginal approach","Culdotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in unruptured ectopic (vitals stable), the abdomen is opened by LAPAROSCOPY.  Why A wrong: Laparotomy is for ruptured ectopic where vitals are unstable.  Why C wrong: A vaginal approach is not described.  Why D wrong: Culdotomy is not the described route."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Why can laparoscopy be used in unruptured ectopic pregnancy (unlike in ruptured cases)?","options":["Because the tube is larger","Because the vitals are stable as it is unruptured ectopic","Because β-hCG is lower","Because the patient is nulliparous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the abdomen is opened by laparoscopy because \"vitals stable as it is unruptured ectopic.\"  Why A wrong: Tube size is not the reason laparoscopy is allowed.  Why C wrong: β-hCG level is not the reason laparoscopy can be used.  Why D wrong: Parity is not the reason."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In surgical management of unruptured ectopic pregnancy, if the patient's FAMILY IS COMPLETE, which procedure is done?","options":["Linear salpingostomy","Remove tube — Salpingectomy","Salpingo-oophorectomy","Only methotrexate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if the family is complete, the tube is removed — SALPINGECTOMY.  Why A wrong: Linear salpingostomy is done when family is NOT complete (tube-conserving).  Why C wrong: Ovaries are never removed in ectopic pregnancy.  Why D wrong: This is a surgical branch, not medical management."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In surgical management of unruptured ectopic pregnancy, if the patient's FAMILY IS NOT COMPLETE, which tube-conserving procedure is performed?","options":["Salpingectomy","Linear salpingostomy","Total abdominal hysterectomy","Salpingo-oophorectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe, for incomplete family, opening the tube above the ectopic, removing the ectopic, and leaving the incision open — LINEAR SALPINGOSTOMY.  Why A wrong: Salpingectomy removes the tube and is done when family is complete.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not indicated for ectopic.  Why D wrong: Ovaries are not removed in ectopic pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Which of the following are steps of LINEAR SALPINGOSTOMY as described in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Open the tube above the ectopic pregnancy","Remove the ectopic pregnancy","Leave the incision site open (don't put stitches)","Remove the entire tube","Remove the ovary"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list three steps of linear salpingostomy: (1) open the tube above the ectopic, (2) remove the ectopic pregnancy, (3) leave the incision site open (don't put stitches).  Why D wrong: Removing the entire tube is salpingectomy, not salpingostomy.  Why E wrong: The ovary is never removed in ectopic pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In linear salpingostomy, what is done with the incision site on the fallopian tube?","options":["Closed with interrupted sutures","Closed with staples","Left open (no stitches placed)","Cauterised completely"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify leaving the incision site open — \"Don't put stitches.\"  Why A wrong: Sutures are specifically not placed.  Why B wrong: Staples are not used; the site is left open.  Why D wrong: Complete cauterisation is not described."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In ectopic pregnancy surgery, are the ovaries removed?","options":["Yes, salpingo-oophorectomy is standard","No, only salpingectomy (tube removal) is done, not salpingo-oophorectomy","Yes, both ovaries are removed","Only the affected ovary is removed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly answer \"No\" — salpingo-oophorectomy is NOT done; salpingectomy is performed in ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Salpingo-oophorectomy is specifically not done.  Why C wrong: Ovaries are preserved.  Why D wrong: Even the affected ovary is preserved; only the tube is dealt with."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"The term \"salpingo-oophorectomy\" refers to removal of:","options":["The fallopian tube only","The ovary only","Both the fallopian tube and the ovary","The uterus and tube"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Salpingo-oophorectomy means removal of both the fallopian tube (salpingo-) and ovary (-oophorectomy); the notes stress this is NOT done in ectopic pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Removal of tube only is salpingectomy.  Why B wrong: Removal of ovary only is oophorectomy.  Why D wrong: That would involve the uterus, not the definition of salpingo-oophorectomy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"\"Molar pregnancy\" is best classified under which umbrella group of diseases?","options":["Sexually transmitted infections","Gestational trophoblastic diseases (GTD)","Ovarian tumours","Endometrial hyperplasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes place molar pregnancy under Gestational Trophoblastic Diseases (GTD), which has 2 categories.  Why A wrong: Molar pregnancy is not an STI.  Why C wrong: It is a trophoblastic (placental) disease, not an ovarian tumour.  Why D wrong: It is not endometrial hyperplasia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Gestational trophoblastic diseases are divided into how many categories?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state GTD has \"2 Categories\" — benign (molar pregnancy) and malignant (gestational trophoblastic neoplasia).  Why B wrong: Only two categories are given.  Why C wrong: Four is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Five is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Which category of gestational trophoblastic disease does molar pregnancy fall under?","options":["Benign","Malignant","Pre-malignant only","Metastatic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list molar pregnancy under the BENIGN category of GTD.  Why B wrong: The malignant category is gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (invasive mole, choriocarcinoma).  Why C wrong: It is classified as benign, though it can become malignant.  Why D wrong: Metastatic disease belongs to the malignant category."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"The malignant category of gestational trophoblastic disease is called:","options":["Molar pregnancy","Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN)","Hydatidiform mole","Endometrial carcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes name the malignant category as GESTATIONAL TROPHOBLASTIC NEOPLASIA.  Why A wrong: Molar pregnancy is the benign category.  Why C wrong: Hydatidiform mole is a form of benign molar pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Endometrial carcinoma is unrelated to trophoblastic neoplasia."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"According to the notes, which are the two types of benign molar pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Partial mole","Complete mole (hydatidiform mole/vesicular mole)","Invasive mole","Choriocarcinoma","Placental site trophoblastic tumour"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Under benign molar pregnancy the notes list partial mole and complete mole (also called hydatidiform mole/vesicular mole).  Why C wrong: Invasive mole is malignant (GTN), not benign.  Why D wrong: Choriocarcinoma is malignant.  Why E wrong: Placental site trophoblastic tumour is not listed in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"\"Hydatidiform mole\" and \"vesicular mole\" are alternative names for which type of molar pregnancy?","options":["Partial mole","Complete mole","Invasive mole","Choriocarcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes equate complete mole with \"hydatidiform mole/vesicular mole.\"  Why A wrong: Partial mole is listed separately with no alternative names here.  Why C wrong: Invasive mole is a malignant entity.  Why D wrong: Choriocarcinoma is malignant, not a complete mole synonym."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON (M/C) type of gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (malignant GTD)?","options":["Choriocarcinoma","Invasive mole","Partial mole","Placental site tumour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list M/C (most common) malignant GTD as invasive mole.  Why A wrong: Choriocarcinoma is listed as the 2nd most common, not the most common.  Why C wrong: Partial mole is benign.  Why D wrong: Placental site tumour is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"What is the 2nd MOST COMMON malignant gestational trophoblastic neoplasia?","options":["Invasive mole","Choriocarcinoma","Complete mole","Partial mole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"2nd M/C = Choriocarcinoma.\"  Why A wrong: Invasive mole is the most common, not the 2nd.  Why C wrong: Complete mole is benign.  Why D wrong: Partial mole is benign."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Molar pregnancy is described as a benign disease of which tissue that can become malignant?","options":["Endometrium","Trophoblast","Myometrium","Ovarian stroma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Molar pregnancy is Benign disease of TROPHOBLAST which can become malignant.\"  Why A wrong: It arises from trophoblast, not endometrium.  Why C wrong: It is not a myometrial disease.  Why D wrong: It is not an ovarian stromal disease."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Molar pregnancy has increased chances of changing into which malignant conditions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Invasive mole","Choriocarcinoma","Endometrial carcinoma","Ovarian teratoma"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state molar pregnancy has increased chances of changing into invasive mole and choriocarcinoma.  Why C wrong: Endometrial carcinoma is not the malignant transformation described.  Why D wrong: Ovarian teratoma is unrelated to molar transformation."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"When molar pregnancy becomes malignant, which malignancy is the BEST answer for the most common outcome?","options":["Choriocarcinoma","Invasive mole","Complete mole","Placental site tumour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give \"Best answer: Invasive Mole\" for the most common malignancy arising from molar pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Choriocarcinoma is the 2nd best answer, not the best.  Why C wrong: Complete mole is benign.  Why D wrong: Placental site tumour is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In the blastocyst diagram, the outer cell layer that gives rise to the placenta (and is problematic in molar pregnancy) is the:","options":["Inner cell mass (embryo)","Trophoblast","Endometrium","Myometrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the outer layer as \"Trophoblast,\" distinct from the inner cell mass (embryo); molar pregnancy is a disease of trophoblast.  Why A wrong: The inner cell mass forms the embryo, not the placental trophoblast.  Why C wrong: The endometrium is the uterine lining shown separately.  Why D wrong: The myometrium is the uterine muscle, not labelled in the blastocyst."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In the blastocyst diagram, the inner cell mass gives rise to which structure?","options":["Placenta","Embryo","Trophoblast","Amnion only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the inner cell mass as \"embryo.\"  Why A wrong: The placenta arises from the trophoblast.  Why C wrong: Trophoblast is the outer layer, separate from the inner cell mass.  Why D wrong: The inner cell mass forms the embryo proper, not the amnion alone."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"The fundamental problem in molar pregnancy is:","options":["Improper excessive proliferation of trophoblast","Failure of implantation","Premature rupture of membranes","Cervical incompetence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the problem is \"Improper excessive proliferation of trophoblast.\"  Why B wrong: Molar pregnancy involves trophoblast overgrowth, not failed implantation.  Why C wrong: PROM is unrelated to the molar pathophysiology.  Why D wrong: Cervical incompetence is a mid-trimester loss cause, not molar disease."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In molar pregnancy, if the proliferating trophoblast GIVES some space for the inner cell mass to grow (so some fetal part is present), it is called:","options":["Complete mole","Partial mole","Invasive mole","Choriocarcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when it gives some space to the inner cell mass to grow, some fetal part will be present — this is called PARTIAL MOLE.  Why A wrong: Complete mole has no fetal part.  Why C wrong: Invasive mole is malignant.  Why D wrong: Choriocarcinoma is malignant."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In molar pregnancy, if the trophoblast does NOT give any space to the inner cell mass (no fetal part grows, entire uterus is full of trophoblast), it is called:","options":["Partial mole","Complete mole","Ectopic pregnancy","Missed abortion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when no space is given, no fetal part grows, and the entire uterus is full of trophoblast — this is COMPLETE MOLE.  Why A wrong: Partial mole has some fetal part present.  Why C wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is implantation outside the uterus, unrelated to mole.  Why D wrong: Missed abortion is retained non-viable pregnancy, not trophoblast overgrowth."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In a COMPLETE mole, which of the following is true regarding fetal tissue?","options":["Some fetal part is present","No fetal part grows; entire uterus is full of trophoblast","A normal fetus is present","Twin fetuses are present"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in complete mole no fetal part grows and the entire uterus is full of trophoblast.  Why A wrong: Presence of fetal parts characterises partial mole.  Why C wrong: A normal fetus is not present in complete mole.  Why D wrong: Twins are not the described feature."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Whether it is a partial or complete mole, the pregnancy has to end in which trimester?","options":["1st trimester","2nd trimester","3rd trimester","It continues to term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that whether partial or complete mole, \"Pregnancy has to end in 1st trimester.\"  Why B wrong: It ends in the first, not the second, trimester.  Why C wrong: It does not reach the third trimester.  Why D wrong: A molar pregnancy does not continue to term."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"A patient with molar pregnancy typically complains of first-trimester bleeding with a positive urine pregnancy test occurring at approximately how many weeks of pregnancy?","options":["6–8 weeks","12–14 weeks","16–18 weeks","20–22 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 1st term bleeding at 6–8 weeks of pregnancy with a positive urine pregnancy test.  Why B wrong: 12–14 weeks is not the stated timing.  Why C wrong: 16–18 weeks is second trimester.  Why D wrong: 20–22 weeks is far beyond the stated window."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"A patient with molar pregnancy typically presents with bleeding at which stage, along with a positive urine pregnancy test?","options":["1st trimester (1st term) bleeding","Antepartum (3rd trimester) hemorrhage","Postpartum bleeding","Intrapartum bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe patient complaints of 1st-trimester bleeding with a positive urine pregnancy test (1st term bleeding).  Why B wrong: Third-trimester APH is not the molar presentation described.  Why C wrong: Postpartum bleeding is unrelated to first-trimester molar presentation.  Why D wrong: Intrapartum bleeding is not the described feature."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In molar pregnancy, on abdominal examination the height of the uterus is characteristically:","options":["Less than the period of gestation","Equal to the period of gestation","More than the period of gestation","Not palpable at all"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Height of uterus more than period of gestation\" as a feature of molar pregnancy (due to trophoblastic overgrowth).  Why A wrong: The uterus is larger, not smaller, than dates.  Why B wrong: It exceeds, rather than matches, the gestational age.  Why D wrong: The uterus is palpable and enlarged."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Which of the following is a characteristic finding on examination in molar pregnancy?","options":["Fetal parts are easily palpable","No fetal part palpable","Multiple fetal poles palpable","Fetal head engaged in pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"No fetal part palpable\" in molar pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Fetal parts are not palpable in molar pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Multiple fetal poles are not palpable.  Why D wrong: There is no fetus to engage in the pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Regarding fetal heart sounds in molar pregnancy, which is true?","options":["Normal fetal heart sound is heard","No fetal heart sound is heard","Fetal heart rate is 160–180 bpm","Two fetal heart sounds are heard"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"No Fetal Heart Sound Heard\" in molar pregnancy.  Why A wrong: No fetal heart sound is heard because there is no viable fetus.  Why C wrong: There is no fetal heart rate to record.  Why D wrong: No fetal heart sounds are heard, let alone two."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"The classic ultrasound (USG) appearance of a complete molar pregnancy is described as:","options":["Snow-storm appearance","Double-bubble sign","Target sign","String of pearls"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"On USG: Snow storm like appearance\" in molar pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Double-bubble is a duodenal atresia sign, unrelated to mole.  Why C wrong: Target/ring sign is associated with ectopic pregnancy/intussusception.  Why D wrong: String of pearls is a polycystic ovary sign."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"In molar pregnancy, besides cytotrophoblast, which trophoblastic layer also proliferates and is responsible for high hormone levels?","options":["Syncytiotrophoblast","Amnion","Decidua basalis","Chorion laeve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the \"Syncytiotrophoblast also proliferates,\" leading to raised hCG.  Why B wrong: The amnion is a fetal membrane, not the hCG-secreting layer.  Why C wrong: Decidua basalis is maternal endometrium, not proliferating trophoblast.  Why D wrong: Chorion laeve is the smooth chorion, not the proliferating hormone-secreting layer described."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Compared to a normal pregnancy, the levels of hCG in molar pregnancy are:","options":["Lower than normal pregnancy","Same as normal pregnancy","Higher than normal pregnancy","Undetectable"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Levels of hCG in Molar Pregnancy are higher than Normal Pregnancy\" (due to syncytiotrophoblast proliferation).  Why A wrong: hCG is elevated, not decreased.  Why B wrong: It is higher, not the same as normal pregnancy.  Why D wrong: hCG is markedly elevated, not undetectable."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Which hormone is markedly elevated and secreted by the proliferating syncytiotrophoblast in molar pregnancy?","options":["Progesterone","Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)","Estriol","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes indicate the syncytiotrophoblast proliferation causes raised hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin).  Why A wrong: Progesterone is not the marker highlighted for molar pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Estriol is not the elevated marker described.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is not related to molar trophoblastic proliferation."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"Which of the following are recognised clinical/investigative features of molar pregnancy in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Height of uterus more than period of gestation","No fetal part palpable","No fetal heart sound heard","Snow-storm appearance on USG","hCG lower than normal pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list five features: uterus larger than dates, no fetal part palpable, no fetal heart sound, snow-storm USG, and syncytiotrophoblast proliferation with raised hCG.  Why E wrong: hCG is HIGHER than normal pregnancy, not lower.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed features of molar pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · Ectopic pregnancy surgical management, ruptured vs unruptured ectopic…","question":"A 28-year-old woman at 8 weeks presents with vaginal bleeding, a uterus larger than dates, no fetal heart sounds, a snow-storm pattern on USG, and very high β-hCG. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":["Complete molar pregnancy","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy","Threatened abortion with a live fetus","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Uterus > dates, absent fetal heart sounds, snow-storm USG, and markedly raised hCG are the classic features of a complete molar pregnancy described in the notes.  Why B wrong: Ruptured ectopic presents with an unstable patient, adnexal mass, and no intrauterine snow-storm.  Why C wrong: A threatened abortion with a live fetus would have audible fetal heart activity and normal-for-dates uterus.  Why D wrong: Twin pregnancy would show two fetal poles and heart sounds, not a snow-storm."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"A patient presents with first-trimester bleeding along with passage of grape-like vesicles per vaginum. This clinical hint is most suggestive of which condition?","options":["Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy (hydatidiform mole)","Threatened abortion","Placenta previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Passage of grape-like vesicles with first-trimester bleeding is a classic hint pointing to molar pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Ectopic pregnancy presents with pain and bleeding but not vesicle passage.  Why C wrong: Threatened abortion causes bleeding without vesicle passage.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa causes painless bleeding in the third trimester, not vesicle passage."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"In molar pregnancy, the height of the uterus characteristically shows which relationship to the period of gestation?","options":["Height of uterus < period of gestation","Height of uterus = period of gestation","Height of uterus > period of gestation","No change in uterine height"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In molar pregnancy the uterus is larger than expected, so height of uterus > period of gestation.  Why A wrong: A smaller-than-dates uterus suggests IUGR/missed abortion, not classic mole.  Why B wrong: Equal height would be a normal pregnancy finding.  Why D wrong: Uterine height is abnormally enlarged, not unchanged."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Compared with a normal pregnancy, the β-hCG values in molar pregnancy are:","options":["Lower than normal pregnancy","Same as normal pregnancy","Higher than normal pregnancy","Undetectable"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: β-hCG values in molar pregnancy are higher than in a normal pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Low hCG suggests non-viable/ectopic pregnancy, not mole.  Why B wrong: Molar hCG is characteristically elevated, not equal.  Why D wrong: hCG is markedly elevated, not undetectable."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The classic ultrasonography (USG) finding in molar pregnancy is described as:","options":["Double bubble appearance","Snow storm like appearance","Ground glass appearance","Halo sign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Molar pregnancy shows a snow storm like appearance on USG.  Why A wrong: Double bubble is seen in duodenal atresia.  Why C wrong: Ground glass is not the classic molar finding.  Why D wrong: Halo sign relates to fetal demise/scalp edema, not mole."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The excessive vomiting (hyperemesis gravidarum) seen in molar pregnancy is primarily due to:","options":["Decreased progesterone","Increased hCG","Increased oxytocin","Decreased estrogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increased hCG leads to vomiting; in molar pregnancy the very high hCG causes hyperemesis gravidarum.  Why A wrong: Decreased progesterone is not the cause of molar vomiting.  Why B is correct as above.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is involved in labour, not molar vomiting.  Why D wrong: Estrogen decrease does not drive hyperemesis in mole."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Arrange the pathophysiology of vomiting in molar pregnancy: Molar pregnancy → ↑hCG → ↑vomiting → ?","options":["Placental abruption","Hyperemesis gravidarum","Preterm labour","Oligohydramnios"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The end result of the cascade (molar pregnancy → ↑hCG → ↑vomiting) is hyperemesis gravidarum.  Why A wrong: Abruption is an APH cause, not a consequence of molar hCG.  Why C wrong: Preterm labour is unrelated to this cascade.  Why D wrong: Oligohydramnios is not a result of high molar hCG."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH) in a normal pregnancy is usually seen after 20 weeks. In molar pregnancy, PIH characteristically appears:","options":["After 20 weeks","Before 20 weeks","Only during labour","Only in the postnatal period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Due to increased hCG, PIH can be seen before 20 weeks in molar pregnancy (normally PIH is seen after 20 weeks).  Why A wrong: After 20 weeks is the normal-pregnancy timing, not the molar hint.  Why C wrong: PIH is not confined to labour.  Why D wrong: Molar PIH appears antenatally and early, not only postnatally."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"In normal pregnancy, pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH) is generally seen after how many weeks of gestation?","options":["12 weeks","16 weeks","20 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normally PIH is seen after 20 weeks of gestation.  Why A/B wrong: These are earlier than the standard 20-week threshold.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is later than the standard threshold for PIH onset."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Increased hCG in molar pregnancy stimulates the ovary, leading to formation of which structure, typically bilaterally?","options":["Corpus luteum cyst","Theca-lutein cyst (B/L)","Dermoid cyst","Endometrioma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: High hCG stimulates the ovary and theca-lutein cysts form, characteristically bilateral (B/L).  Why A wrong: Corpus luteum cyst is a normal single functional cyst, not the hCG-driven bilateral cyst here.  Why C wrong: Dermoid cysts are germ-cell tumours, not hCG-driven.  Why D wrong: Endometriomas arise from endometriosis, not high hCG."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Thyrotoxicosis / thyroid storm may occur in molar pregnancy because the alpha (α) subunit of hCG is structurally similar to the alpha subunit of which hormone?","options":["LH","FSH","TSH","ACTH"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The α-subunit of hCG is similar to the α-subunit of TSH, so high hCG stimulates the thyroid → ↑T3, T4 → thyrotoxicosis/thyroid storm.  Why A/B wrong: Although LH/FSH also share an α-subunit, the notes specifically attribute molar thyrotoxicosis to hCG–TSH cross-reactivity.  Why D wrong: ACTH is a different peptide and is not the basis of molar thyrotoxicosis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"In molar pregnancy, cross-stimulation of the thyroid by hCG results in which laboratory pattern?","options":["Decreased T3 and T4","Increased T3 and T4","Normal T3 and T4","Increased TSH only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: hCG–TSH similarity stimulates the thyroid, raising T3 and T4, producing thyrotoxicosis.  Why A wrong: Levels rise, not fall.  Why C wrong: Levels are abnormally elevated.  Why D wrong: Thyroid hormones (T3/T4) rise; TSH is typically suppressed in thyrotoxicosis, not elevated."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"In a partial mole, the ovum (containing 23 chromosomes) is fertilized by how many sperms?","options":["No sperm","One sperm","Two sperms","Three sperms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Partial mole is dispermic — a normal ovum is fertilized by 2 sperms.  Why A wrong: Fertilization by no sperm does not occur in partial mole.  Why B wrong: Fertilization by a single sperm of an empty ovum produces a complete mole.  Why D wrong: Three sperms are not described; partial mole is dispermic (two sperms)."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The karyotype of a partial mole is:","options":["46 (XX) diploid","45 X monosomy","69 triploid (e.g. 69 XXY)","47 XXY"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Partial mole results from an ovum (23) plus two sperms giving a triploid karyotype such as 69 (XXY).  Why A wrong: 46 (XX) diploid is the complete-mole karyotype.  Why B wrong: 45 X is Turner syndrome, unrelated.  Why D wrong: 47 XXY is Klinefelter, not a molar karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"A partial mole formed from a 23-chromosome ovum fertilized by two sperms is best described as being:","options":["Haploid","Diploid","Triploid","Tetraploid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 69-chromosome karyotype of a partial mole is triploid.  Why A wrong: Haploid is a single set (23).  Why B wrong: Diploid (46) describes the complete mole.  Why D wrong: Tetraploid (92) is not the partial-mole karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"A partial mole in which the ovum is fertilized by two sperms is termed:","options":["Monospermic","Dispermic","Aspermic","Polyspermic (>2)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Partial mole is dispermic (fertilized by 2 sperms).  Why A wrong: Monospermic describes the complete mole (single sperm).  Why C wrong: Aspermic (no sperm) does not occur.  Why D wrong: The notes specify two sperms (dispermic), not >2."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"In a partial mole, the extra genetic material comes from the:","options":["Mother (maternal)","Father (paternal)","Both parents equally","Neither parent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The extra genetic material in a partial mole comes from the father (paternal).  Why A wrong: The extra material is paternal, not maternal.  Why C wrong: It is not shared equally; the excess is paternal.  Why D wrong: The additional genome is paternal in origin."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"A complete mole arises when a sperm fertilizes an ovum that is:","options":["Normal with 23 chromosomes","Empty (no chromosome)","Already fertilized","Triploid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a complete mole an empty ovum (no chromosome) is fertilized by a sperm.  Why A wrong: A normal 23-chromosome ovum fertilized by two sperms gives a partial mole.  Why C wrong: The ovum is not pre-fertilized.  Why D wrong: The ovum is empty (no chromosomes), not triploid."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"In a complete mole, the empty ovum is fertilized by a single sperm carrying 23 chromosomes. What happens next to generate the zygote's chromosome number?","options":["The chromosome number stays at 23","The sperm chromosome number gets doubled","Maternal chromosomes are added","The zygote loses chromosomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After a single sperm (23) fertilizes the empty ovum, the sperm's chromosome number gets doubled, producing the complete mole zygote.  Why A wrong: The number doubles rather than staying at 23.  Why C wrong: The ovum is empty, so no maternal chromosomes are added.  Why D wrong: Chromosomes are doubled, not lost."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The karyotype of a complete mole is:","options":["69 XXY (triploid)","46 XX (diploid)","45 X","23 X (haploid)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The complete mole zygote has a chromosome number of 46 (XX), which is diploid.  Why A wrong: 69 XXY triploid is the partial mole.  Why C wrong: 45 X is Turner syndrome, unrelated.  Why D wrong: 23 X is haploid, not the complete mole."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"A complete mole is described as monospermic because it is:","options":["Fertilized by two sperms","Fertilized by a single sperm","Not fertilized at all","Fertilized by three sperms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Complete mole is monospermic — an empty ovum is fertilized by a single sperm whose chromosomes then double.  Why A wrong: Dispermic (two sperms) describes the partial mole.  Why C wrong: Fertilization does occur, by one sperm.  Why D wrong: Three sperms are not involved."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"In a complete mole, the entire genetic material belongs to the:","options":["Mother","Father","Both equally","Neither"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a complete mole the entire genetic material belongs to the father (paternal), because the ova is empty with no maternal genetic material.  Why A wrong: The mother contributes no genetic material (empty ovum).  Why C wrong: There is no equal contribution; it is entirely paternal.  Why D wrong: The genome is entirely paternal, not absent."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Regarding fetal parts, which statement correctly distinguishes partial from complete mole?","options":["Both have fetal parts","Partial mole has some fetal parts; complete mole has no fetal part","Complete mole has fetal parts; partial mole has none","Neither has fetal parts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Some fetal part is present in partial mole, whereas no fetal part is present in complete mole.  Why A wrong: Complete mole has no fetal parts.  Why C wrong: It is reversed — partial (not complete) has fetal parts.  Why D wrong: Partial mole does contain some fetal parts."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"In a partial mole, the β-hCG level is typically:","options":["≥10⁵ IU/L (≥1 lakh IU/L)","More than normal pregnancy but <10⁵ IU/L (<1 lakh IU/L)","Below normal pregnancy values","Undetectable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In partial mole, hCG levels are more than normal pregnancy but the value is <10⁵ IU/L (<1 lakh IU/L).  Why A wrong: ≥10⁵ IU/L (≥1 lakh) is characteristic of complete mole.  Why C wrong: Values are higher than normal pregnancy, not lower.  Why D wrong: hCG is elevated, not undetectable."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"In a complete mole, the β-hCG level is typically:","options":["Below normal pregnancy","<10⁵ IU/L","≥10⁵ IU/L (≥1 lakh IU/L)","Same as non-pregnant women"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Complete mole shows hCG levels ≥10⁵ IU/L (≥1 lakh IU/L).  Why A wrong: hCG is markedly elevated, not below normal.  Why B wrong: <10⁵ IU/L is characteristic of partial mole.  Why D wrong: Levels are far above non-pregnant values."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"On ultrasonography, a partial mole most often gives an image resembling:","options":["Missed abortion","Snow storm appearance","Twin pregnancy","Complete placenta previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In partial mole the USG image is similar to a missed abortion.  Why B wrong: The snow storm appearance is characteristic of complete mole.  Why C wrong: Twin pregnancy is not the described partial-mole image.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is a placental location issue, not a molar USG image."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The classic snow storm appearance on USG is characteristic of which type of molar pregnancy?","options":["Partial mole","Complete mole","Invasive mole only","Neither"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Snow storm appearance image on USG is seen in complete mole.  Why A wrong: Partial mole resembles a missed abortion, not snow storm.  Why C wrong: The snow storm appearance is described for complete mole USG.  Why D wrong: Complete mole clearly shows it."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"All the following complications of molar pregnancy are described as being MORE common in complete mole (and less in partial mole) EXCEPT:","options":["Hyperemesis gravidarum","Pregnancy-induced hypertension","Bilateral theca-lutein cyst","Deep vein thrombosis unrelated to trophoblast"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The four listed molar complications are hyperemesis gravidarum, PIH, B/L theca-lutein cyst, and thyrotoxicosis; DVT unrelated to trophoblast is not on that list.  Why A/B/C wrong: All three are explicitly listed complications that are more common in complete mole."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"According to the notes, the four listed complications of molar pregnancy (hyperemesis, PIH, theca-lutein cyst, thyrotoxicosis) are:","options":["More in partial mole, less in complete mole","More in complete mole, less in partial mole","Equal in both","Absent in complete mole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These complications are more in complete mole and less in partial mole.  Why A wrong: The reverse is true.  Why C wrong: They are not equal — they cluster in complete mole.  Why D wrong: They are actually more common in complete mole."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Whether it is a partial mole or a complete mole, which statement is true regarding the pregnancy outcome?","options":["Only partial moles can survive","Only complete moles can survive","Neither can survive","Both can be carried to term"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Whether partial or complete mole, none of them can survive.  Why A/B wrong: Neither type produces a viable pregnancy.  Why D wrong: A molar pregnancy cannot be carried to term."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The Investigation of Choice (IOC) for molar pregnancy is:","options":["Histopathological examination","USG (ultrasonography)","Serum electrolytes","Chest X-ray"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IOC in molar pregnancy is USG.  Why A wrong: Histopathological examination is the gold standard investigation, not the IOC.  Why C wrong: Serum electrolytes do not diagnose a mole.  Why D wrong: Chest X-ray is used for suspected lung metastasis, not molar diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The gold standard investigation for confirming molar pregnancy is:","options":["USG","Serum β-hCG","Histopathological examination","MRI pelvis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The gold standard investigation is histopathological examination.  Why A wrong: USG is the IOC, not the gold standard.  Why B wrong: β-hCG supports diagnosis and follow-up but is not the gold standard confirmatory test.  Why D wrong: MRI is not the gold standard for molar diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The management of choice (best answer) for molar pregnancy, whether partial or complete, is:","options":["Medical management with methotrexate","Suction evacuation","Immediate total hysterectomy in all patients","Expectant management"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management of molar pregnancy = Suction evacuation (best answer), regardless of partial or complete.  Why A wrong: Methotrexate is chemotherapy used for gestational trophoblastic neoplasia/choriocarcinoma, not initial mole evacuation.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is reserved for select cases (e.g. older women with completed family), not all patients.  Why D wrong: A mole must be evacuated, not managed expectantly."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"A woman over 40 years of age with a completed family is diagnosed with molar pregnancy. The preferred management would be hysterectomy because:","options":["Suction evacuation is contraindicated over age 40","Molar pregnancy has chances to become malignant","It prevents future hyperemesis","Oxytocin is ineffective in older women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a female >40 years with family complete, hysterectomy is chosen because molar pregnancy has chances to become malignant.  Why A wrong: Suction evacuation is not contraindicated by age alone.  Why C wrong: Preventing hyperemesis is not the reason for hysterectomy.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin efficacy is unrelated to the choice."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The chance of a partial mole progressing to gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) is approximately:","options":["4%","15–20%","<1%","50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Partial mole carries about a 4% risk of gestational trophoblastic neoplasia.  Why B wrong: 15–20% is the GTN risk for complete mole.  Why C wrong: <1% is the choriocarcinoma risk for partial mole.  Why D wrong: 50% is not a quoted figure."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The risk of a partial mole developing into choriocarcinoma is approximately:","options":["4%","<1%","15–20%","25%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Partial mole has a <1% risk of choriocarcinoma.  Why A wrong: 4% is the partial mole GTN figure (and complete-mole choriocarcinoma figure).  Why C wrong: 15–20% is the complete-mole GTN figure.  Why D wrong: 25% is not quoted."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The chance of a complete mole progressing to gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) is approximately:","options":["4%","<1%","15–20%","60%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Complete mole carries a 15–20% risk of gestational trophoblastic neoplasia.  Why A wrong: 4% is the partial mole GTN risk.  Why B wrong: <1% is the partial mole choriocarcinoma risk.  Why D wrong: 60% is not a quoted figure."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The risk of a complete mole developing into choriocarcinoma is approximately:","options":["<1%","4%","15–20%","30%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Complete mole has about a 4% risk of choriocarcinoma.  Why A wrong: <1% is the partial mole choriocarcinoma risk.  Why C wrong: 15–20% is the complete-mole GTN (not choriocarcinoma) risk.  Why D wrong: 30% is not quoted."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"After evacuation or hysterectomy for a molar pregnancy, the removed tissue should always be sent for:","options":["Culture and sensitivity","Histopathological examination","Karyotyping only","Cytology smear only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Always send the tissue for histopathological examination after evacuation/hysterectomy.  Why A wrong: Culture and sensitivity is for infection, not molar confirmation.  Why C wrong: Karyotyping alone is not the routine instruction.  Why D wrong: A cytology smear is not the specified test."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"During post-evacuation follow-up of molar pregnancy, β-hCG levels are initially measured:","options":["Daily","Weekly","Monthly","Once at 6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For follow-up, measure β-hCG levels weekly until three normal values are obtained.  Why A wrong: Daily measurement is not the schedule.  Why C wrong: Monthly measurement begins only after three normal weekly values.  Why D wrong: A single 6-month test is not the follow-up scheme."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"During molar pregnancy follow-up, weekly β-hCG monitoring continues until:","options":["hCG rises again","3 normal values are obtained","6 normal values are obtained","10 weeks pass"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Weekly β-hCG is measured until you get 3 normal values.  Why A wrong: A rise indicates malignancy, not the endpoint for switching to monthly.  Why C wrong: The notes specify 3, not 6, normal values.  Why D wrong: The switch is defined by normal values, not a fixed number of weeks."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"After obtaining 3 normal weekly β-hCG values, the follow-up β-hCG is then measured:","options":["Weekly for 3 months","Monthly till 6 months","Daily for 1 month","Only if symptoms recur"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After 3 normal values, β-hCG is measured monthly till 6 months.  Why A wrong: Weekly monitoring is only until 3 normal values.  Why C wrong: Daily monitoring is not used.  Why D wrong: Follow-up is scheduled, not symptom-triggered only."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"During post-molar follow-up, if hCG levels increase or do not return to normal within 6 months, this indicates:","options":["Successful evacuation","Malignancy has occurred","Normal recovery","Need to repeat USG only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If hCG increases or does not return to normal in 6 months, it means malignancy has occurred.  Why A wrong: Persistent/rising hCG is not a sign of success.  Why C wrong: Failure to normalize is abnormal, not normal recovery.  Why D wrong: The interpretation is malignancy, not merely repeat imaging."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"During the 6-month post-evacuation follow-up period for molar pregnancy, pregnancy is:","options":["Recommended","Contraindicated","Mandatory for hCG monitoring","Encouraged after 1 month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During these 6 months, pregnancy is contraindicated (a new pregnancy would confound β-hCG follow-up).  Why A wrong: Pregnancy is not recommended during follow-up.  Why C wrong: Pregnancy would obscure hCG monitoring, so it is avoided.  Why D wrong: Conception is contraindicated for the full 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The contraceptive of choice recommended during the follow-up period after molar pregnancy is:","options":["Copper intrauterine device","Oral contraceptive pills (estrogen + progesterone containing)","Barrier method only","Tubal ligation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The contraceptive of choice is oral contraceptive pills (estrogen + progesterone containing pills).  Why A wrong: An IUD is not the recommended method here.  Why C wrong: Barrier methods are not stated as the choice.  Why D wrong: Tubal ligation is permanent and not the recommended interim method."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Molar pregnancy is best described as:","options":["A malignant tumour of the ovary","A benign disease of trophoblast with malignant potential","A purely benign condition with no malignant potential","A congenital uterine anomaly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Molar pregnancy is a benign disease of trophoblast with malignant potential.  Why A wrong: It is trophoblastic, not primarily an ovarian malignancy.  Why C wrong: It has malignant potential, so not purely benign.  Why D wrong: It is a gestational trophoblastic disease, not a congenital anomaly."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"A patient with molar pregnancy who has undergone evacuation or hysterectomy can still:","options":["Never develop malignancy","Change into malignancy","Only develop infection","Become permanently infertile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A patient of molar pregnancy after evacuation/hysterectomy can also change into malignancy, hence the need for follow-up.  Why A wrong: Malignant transformation remains possible after treatment.  Why C wrong: Infection is a risk but not the point emphasized here.  Why D wrong: Permanent infertility is not the stated consequence."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The most common malignancy occurring after a hydatidiform mole is:","options":["Choriocarcinoma","Invasive mole","Placental site trophoblastic tumour","Endometrial carcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common malignancy after H. mole is invasive mole.  Why A wrong: Choriocarcinoma is the 2nd most common malignancy after H. mole.  Why C wrong: Placental site trophoblastic tumour is not the most common.  Why D wrong: Endometrial carcinoma is unrelated to molar sequelae."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The second most common malignancy after a hydatidiform mole is:","options":["Invasive mole","Choriocarcinoma","Cervical carcinoma","Ovarian carcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2nd most common malignancy after H. mole is choriocarcinoma.  Why A wrong: Invasive mole is the most common, not the second.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the molar-associated malignancies listed."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"A key nursing intervention BEFORE uterine evacuation in molar pregnancy is:","options":["Start oxytocin infusion immediately","Prepare the client for uterine evacuation","Administer methotrexate","Delay evacuation for 6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing intervention (1) is to prepare the client for uterine evacuation.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin should not be started before evacuation.  Why C wrong: Methotrexate is for GTN/choriocarcinoma, not pre-evacuation care.  Why D wrong: Evacuation should be timely, not delayed 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Why should oxytocin NOT be started before evacuation in molar pregnancy?","options":["It causes hyperemesis","It increases the chances of thromboembolism","It lowers β-hCG too fast","It causes theca-lutein cysts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin should not be started before evacuation because chances of thromboembolism increase.  Why A wrong: Hyperemesis is due to high hCG, not oxytocin.  Why C wrong: Rapid hCG lowering is not the stated risk.  Why D wrong: Theca-lutein cysts arise from high hCG, not oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"In the management of molar pregnancy, oxytocin should be started:","options":["Before evacuation begins","During the first trimester","After evacuation is complete","Never in any circumstance"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Start oxytocin after evacuation is complete.  Why A wrong: Starting before evacuation risks thromboembolism.  Why B wrong: Timing is tied to evacuation, not trimester.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is used, but only after evacuation is complete."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"After evacuation of a molar pregnancy, which procedure is performed to ensure complete removal of tissue?","options":["Sharp curettage","Total hysterectomy","Cervical cerclage","Amniocentesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After evacuation, sharp curettage is done.  Why B wrong: Hysterectomy is not the routine step after every evacuation.  Why C wrong: Cervical cerclage is for cervical incompetence.  Why D wrong: Amniocentesis is a prenatal diagnostic procedure, irrelevant here."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"After evacuation of a molar pregnancy, the nurse should monitor the patient for all of the following complications EXCEPT:","options":["PPH (postpartum haemorrhage)","Infection","Thromboembolism","Gestational diabetes"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The complications to monitor after evacuation are PPH, infection, and thromboembolism; gestational diabetes is not listed.  Why A/B/C wrong: All three are explicitly listed post-evacuation monitoring targets."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"After evacuation of a molar pregnancy, the nurse should instruct the patient not to conceive for:","options":["3 months","6 months","1 year","2 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the patient not to conceive for 6 months after evacuation.  Why A wrong: The interval is 6, not 3, months.  Why C wrong: 1 year is the interval after choriocarcinoma, not after simple molar evacuation.  Why D wrong: 2 years is not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"After evacuation of a molar pregnancy, which laboratory parameter should the nurse specifically monitor?","options":["β-hCG levels","Serum amylase","Blood urea nitrogen","Serum uric acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should monitor her β-hCG levels after evacuation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the specified monitoring parameters for molar follow-up."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"After evacuation of a partial mole, β-hCG levels normally return to normal after approximately:","options":["3 weeks","7 weeks","9 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After evacuation of a partial mole, β-hCG levels come back to normal after 7 weeks.  Why A wrong: 3 weeks is too early per the notes.  Why C wrong: 9 weeks is the interval for complete mole.  Why D wrong: 12 weeks is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"After evacuation of a complete mole, β-hCG levels come back to normal after approximately:","options":["7 weeks","9 weeks","6 weeks","3 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After complete mole evacuation, β-hCG levels come back to normal after 9 weeks.  Why A wrong: 7 weeks is the interval for partial mole.  Why C/D wrong: These are shorter than the stated 9-week interval."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Choriocarcinoma most commonly develops after which condition?","options":["Ectopic pregnancy","H. mole / molar pregnancy","Normal term delivery","Spontaneous abortion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Choriocarcinoma most commonly develops after H. mole/molar pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is not the most common precursor.  Why C wrong: Term delivery is a less common precursor than molar pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Abortion can precede it but is not the most common cause stated."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Choriocarcinoma is characterized in nature as:","options":["Benign","Premalignant only","Malignant","Non-neoplastic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Choriocarcinoma is malignant in nature.  Why A wrong: It is malignant, not benign.  Why B wrong: It is frankly malignant, not merely premalignant.  Why D wrong: It is a neoplasm."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The tumour marker used for choriocarcinoma is:","options":["CA-125","β-hCG","AFP","CEA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tumour marker for choriocarcinoma is β-hCG.  Why A wrong: CA-125 is used for ovarian cancer.  Why C wrong: AFP is used for yolk-sac tumours/hepatocellular carcinoma.  Why D wrong: CEA is used for colorectal cancer."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The most common site of metastasis of choriocarcinoma is:","options":["Liver","Brain","Lungs (followed by vagina)","Bone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of metastasis is the lungs, followed by the vagina.  Why A/B/D wrong: Liver, brain, and bone are not the most common sites per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"After the lungs, the next most common site of metastasis for choriocarcinoma is the:","options":["Liver","Vagina","Brain","Spleen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Metastasis is to lungs followed by vagina.  Why A/C/D wrong: Liver, brain, and spleen are not the second site described."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The treatment of choice (TOC) for choriocarcinoma is:","options":["Hysterectomy","Chemotherapy","Radiotherapy","Suction evacuation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Choriocarcinoma is a chemosensitive tumour and the TOC is chemotherapy (not hysterectomy).  Why A wrong: The notes explicitly state TOC is chemotherapy, not hysterectomy.  Why C wrong: Radiotherapy is not the treatment of choice.  Why D wrong: Suction evacuation is for molar pregnancy, not choriocarcinoma."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Choriocarcinoma is best described as a tumour that is:","options":["Radiosensitive","Chemoresistant","Chemosensitive","Hormone-resistant"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Choriocarcinoma is a chemosensitive tumour, which is why chemotherapy is the treatment of choice.  Why A wrong: The notes emphasize chemosensitivity, not radiosensitivity.  Why B wrong: It is chemosensitive, not chemoresistant.  Why D wrong: Hormone resistance is not described."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"After treatment of choriocarcinoma, pregnancy is contraindicated for:","options":["6 months","1 year","2 years","3 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After choriocarcinoma, pregnancy is contraindicated for 1 year.  Why A wrong: 6 months is the interval after simple molar evacuation, not choriocarcinoma.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 1-year interval."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"On a chest X-ray, choriocarcinoma that has metastasized to the lungs classically produces which appearance?","options":["Miliary mottling","Snow storm appearance","Cavitary lesion","Pleural effusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A chest X-ray showing a snow storm appearance indicates choriocarcinoma metastasized to the lungs.  Why A wrong: Miliary mottling is typical of miliary tuberculosis.  Why C wrong: Cavitary lesions suggest TB/abscess, not this metastatic pattern.  Why D wrong: Pleural effusion is a different finding."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"A G₂P₁ female at 10 weeks of pregnancy complains of excessive nausea and vomiting. Her β-hCG is ≥10⁵ IU/L; USG shows no fetal parts and a snow storm appearance, and she has vaginal bleeding. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":["Partial mole","Complete mole","Ectopic pregnancy","Threatened abortion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: No fetal parts + snow storm appearance + β-hCG ≥10⁵ IU/L + hyperemesis and bleeding is diagnostic of a complete mole.  Why A wrong: Partial mole has some fetal parts, hCG <10⁵ IU/L, and a missed-abortion-like USG.  Why C wrong: Ectopic pregnancy would not show a snow storm intrauterine appearance.  Why D wrong: Threatened abortion does not produce snow storm USG or such high hCG."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Antepartum haemorrhage (APH) is defined as any bleeding from the genital tract occurring at or beyond how many weeks of pregnancy, up till delivery?","options":["20 weeks","24 weeks","28 weeks","37 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: APH is any bleeding in/from the genital tract at or beyond 28 weeks of pregnancy up till delivery.  Why A/B wrong: These thresholds are earlier than the defined 28 weeks.  Why D wrong: 37 weeks marks term, not the APH threshold."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Which of the following are placental causes of antepartum haemorrhage (APH)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placenta","Circumvallate placenta","Vasa previa","Cervical polyps"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Placental causes of APH are placenta previa, abruptio placenta, and circumvallate placenta.  Why D wrong: Vasa previa is a fetal cause, not a placental cause.  Why E wrong: Cervical polyps are a local cause, not a placental cause."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"The fetal cause of antepartum haemorrhage (APH) listed is:","options":["Placenta previa","Vasa previa","Cervix polyps","Circumvallate placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fetal cause of APH is vasa previa.  Why A/D wrong: Placenta previa and circumvallate placenta are placental causes.  Why C wrong: Cervix polyps are a local cause."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Which of the following are local causes of antepartum haemorrhage (APH)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cervix polyps","Cervix trauma","Abruptio placenta","Vasa previa","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Local causes of APH are cervix polyps and cervix trauma.  Why C/E wrong: Abruptio placenta and placenta previa are placental causes.  Why D wrong: Vasa previa is a fetal cause."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"According to the notes, which type of placenta does NOT lead to antepartum haemorrhage (APH)?","options":["Circumvallate placenta","Battledore placenta","Placenta previa","Abruptio placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that battledore placenta does not lead to APH.  Why A wrong: Circumvallate placenta is a placental cause of APH.  Why C/D wrong: Placenta previa and abruptio placenta are placental causes of APH."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"According to the notes, the most common cause of antepartum haemorrhage (APH) is:","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placenta","Vasa previa","Cervix polyps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause is abruptio placenta. (The page writes \"most common cause of PPH: Abruptio Placenta,\" but in the context of the APH chapter this refers to APH; abruptio placenta is a classic APH cause.)  Why A wrong: Placenta previa is a major cause but not listed as the most common here.  Why C wrong: Vasa previa is a fetal cause, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Cervix polyps are a local cause."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Which of the following are recognized other names for abruptio placenta? (Select all that apply)","options":["Accidental hemorrhage","Uterine apoplexy","Snow storm hemorrhage","Concealed previa","Battledore hemorrhage"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Other names of abruptio placenta are accidental hemorrhage and uterine apoplexy.  Why C wrong: Snow storm relates to molar pregnancy, not abruptio.  Why D wrong: Concealed previa is not a listed name.  Why E wrong: Battledore refers to a cord-insertion variant, not abruptio."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Which conditions are causes of bleeding in the 1st trimester (early pregnancy)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Abortion","Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy","Abruptio placenta","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: 1st trimester (early pregnancy) causes of bleeding are abortion, ectopic pregnancy, and molar pregnancy.  Why D/E wrong: Abruptio placenta and placenta previa are 3rd trimester causes of bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"Which conditions are causes of bleeding in the 3rd trimester of pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Abruptio placenta","Placenta previa","Abortion","Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: 3rd trimester causes of bleeding are abruptio placenta and placenta previa.  Why C/D/E wrong: Abortion, ectopic pregnancy, and molar pregnancy are 1st trimester (early pregnancy) causes."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Molar pregnancy — clinical hints…","question":"A patient with molar pregnancy on post-evacuation follow-up wants to become pregnant again. The nurse's most appropriate advice is to:","options":["Conceive immediately to boost recovery","Avoid pregnancy for 6 months and use OCPs","Undergo hysterectomy first","Take methotrexate prophylactically"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pregnancy is contraindicated for 6 months during follow-up, and OCPs (estrogen + progesterone) are the contraceptive of choice.  Why A wrong: Immediate conception is contraindicated and would confound hCG follow-up.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not routinely required just to allow future pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Prophylactic methotrexate is not the advised routine."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In placenta previa, where is the placenta located?","options":["Fundus of the uterus","Lower uterine segment","Cornu of the uterus","Cervical canal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines placenta previa as a condition in which the placenta is located in the lower uterine segment.  Why A wrong: The fundus is the normal upper site; placenta previa is specifically a low-lying placenta.  Why C wrong: The cornu is a normal implantation area, not the definition of previa.  Why D wrong: Implantation in the cervical canal is cervical pregnancy, not placenta previa."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Premature separation of a normally situated placenta before delivery is termed:","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placentae","Placenta accreta","Vasa previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines abruptio placenta as premature separation of the placenta before delivery.  Why A wrong: Placenta previa is a low-lying placenta, not premature separation.  Why C wrong: Placenta accreta is abnormal adherence of the placenta to the myometrium.  Why D wrong: Vasa previa refers to fetal vessels crossing the internal os."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Between placenta previa and abruptio placenta, which is described as the \"more common\" cause of antepartum hemorrhage in the source?","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placenta","Both occur equally","Neither causes APH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source labels \"Abruptio Placenta (More Common)\" and gives it a higher incidence (1 in 200) than previa (1 in 300).  Why A wrong: Placenta previa is less common (1 in 300).  Why C wrong: The incidences differ (200 vs 300).  Why D wrong: Both are classic causes of APH."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"For localization of the placenta, ultrasonography (USG) is best done in which trimester?","options":["1st trimester","2nd trimester","3rd trimester","Any trimester equally"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source note states that USG for localization of placenta is done in the 3rd trimester (a low-lying placenta in early pregnancy often \"migrates\" up as the lower segment forms).  Why A wrong: 1st trimester is too early for reliable localization.  Why B wrong: 2nd trimester localization frequently changes by term.  Why D wrong: The source specifically names the 3rd trimester."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"What is the stated incidence of placenta previa in pregnancy?","options":["1 in 100 pregnancies","1 in 200 pregnancies","1 in 300 pregnancies","1 in 500 pregnancies"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the incidence of placenta previa as 1 in 300 pregnancies.  Why A wrong: 1 in 100 is not the figure given.  Why B wrong: 1 in 200 is the incidence of abruptio, not previa.  Why D wrong: 1 in 500 is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"What is the stated incidence of abruptio placenta in pregnancy?","options":["1 in 100 pregnancies","1 in 200 pregnancies","1 in 300 pregnancies","1 in 1000 pregnancies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the incidence of abruptio placenta as 1 in 200 pregnancies.  Why A wrong: 1 in 100 is not stated.  Why C wrong: 1 in 300 is the incidence of placenta previa.  Why D wrong: 1 in 1000 is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Antepartum hemorrhage (APH) is more common in which type of woman according to the source?","options":["Primigravida (first pregnancy)","Multiparous female","Nulliparous female","Adolescent primi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes that \"APH is more common in multiparous female,\" listed under increased maternal parity.  Why A wrong: Primigravida is not identified as higher risk in the source.  Why C wrong: Nulliparous (never delivered) is the opposite of the risk group.  Why D wrong: Adolescent primi is not the stated risk group."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a common risk factor for placenta previa?","options":["Decreased maternal age","Increased maternal age","Nulliparity","Underweight mother"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists increased maternal age as risk factor #1 for placenta previa.  Why A wrong: Younger age is protective, not a risk factor.  Why C wrong: Increased parity (not nulliparity) is the risk factor.  Why D wrong: Underweight is not listed as a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which of the following are listed as common risk factors for placenta previa? (Select all that apply)","options":["Increased maternal age","Increased maternal parity","Smoking","Twin pregnancy","Alcohol"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists four common risk factors: increased maternal age, increased maternal parity, smoking, and twin pregnancy.  Why E wrong: Alcohol is explicitly stated as NOT a risk factor (for abruptio) and is not listed among previa risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Smoking is listed as a common risk factor for which condition(s) in the source?","options":["Only abruptio placenta","Only placenta previa","Both placenta previa and abruptio placenta","Neither"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Smoking appears in the common risk factors for placenta previa AND under the \"most important\" abruptio risks (Smoking/cocaine/abuse).  Why A wrong: It is also a previa risk factor.  Why B wrong: It is also an abruptio risk factor.  Why D wrong: It is a listed risk for both."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"A \"big\" (large-sized) placenta predisposes to placenta previa. Which of the following causes a big placenta per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Twin pregnancy","Diabetes","Succenturiate lobe","Oligohydramnios","Fibroid"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists twin pregnancy, diabetes, and succenturiate lobe as causes of a big placenta (which increases previa risk).  Why D wrong: Oligohydramnios is not listed as a cause of a big placenta.  Why E wrong: Fibroid is listed under abruptio risk factors, not as a cause of a big placenta."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"A placenta divided into two lobes of roughly equal size, connected by vessels, is termed:","options":["Succenturiate placenta","Bilobate placenta","Circumvallate placenta","Battledore placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source labels the two-lobed placenta illustration as \"Placenta Bilobate.\"  Why A wrong: Succenturiate refers to an accessory (extra small) lobe, mentioned separately.  Why C wrong: Circumvallate is a placenta with a raised edge/membrane fold.  Why D wrong: Battledore describes marginal cord insertion."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"A defective endometrium increases the risk of placenta previa. Which of the following cause a defective endometrium per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Smoking","Cesarean section","Curettage","Endometritis","Folic acid supplementation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists smoke, cesarean section, curettage, and endometritis as causes of a defective endometrium.  Why E wrong: Folic acid supplementation is not a cause of defective endometrium; folic acid deficiency is instead an abruptio risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which of the following is listed as the single \"most important\" specific risk factor for placenta previa?","options":["Smoking","Previous history of placenta previa","Anemia","Grand multiparity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Under \"Most Important,\" the source lists previous history of placenta previa first.  Why A wrong: Smoking is a common (not the most important specific) risk factor.  Why C wrong: Anemia is not listed.  Why D wrong: Increased parity is a common risk factor but not named as \"most important.\""},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Per the source's \"Remember\" note, what are the 2 MOST important risk factors for placenta previa?","options":["Smoking and diabetes","Previous H/O placenta previa and previous H/O cesarean section","Twin pregnancy and increased maternal age","Trauma and PIH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifically remembers the 2 most important risk factors as previous history of placenta previa and previous history of cesarean section.  Why A wrong: These are lesser risk factors, not the top two.  Why C wrong: These are common risk factors, not the \"2 most important.\"  Why D wrong: Trauma and PIH are the most important abruptio risk factors, not previa."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Per the source's \"Remember\" note, which are the 3 MOST important risk factors for abruptio placenta?","options":["Previous H/O abruptio placenta, abdominal trauma, PIH","Smoking, diabetes, twin pregnancy","Cesarean section, curettage, endometritis","Increased age, increased parity, alcohol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the 3 most important abruptio risk factors as previous H/O abruptio placenta, trauma (abdominal), and pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH).  Why B wrong: These relate more to placenta previa / big placenta.  Why C wrong: These cause a defective endometrium (previa risk).  Why D wrong: Alcohol is explicitly NOT a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In the source, abdominal trauma and PIH cause abruptio placenta through which common mechanism?","options":["Uterine decompression","Placental migration","Endometrial atrophy","Cervical incompetence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source brackets trauma (abdominal) and PIH together as leading to \"Uterine Decompression,\" a mechanism of abruptio.  Why B wrong: Placental migration relates to previa localization, not abruptio.  Why C wrong: Endometrial atrophy is not the described mechanism.  Why D wrong: Cervical incompetence relates to preterm loss, not abruptio."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which of the following is explicitly stated to be NOT a risk factor for abruptio placenta?","options":["Trauma","PIH","Alcohol","Cocaine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Not a Risk factor for Abruptio → alcohol.\"  Why A wrong: Trauma is one of the 3 most important abruptio risk factors.  Why B wrong: PIH is one of the 3 most important abruptio risk factors.  Why D wrong: Cocaine/abuse (smoking) is listed as an abruptio risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"most important\" risk factors for abruptio placenta? (Select all that apply)","options":["Folic acid deficiency","Thrombophilia","Increased BP (PIH)","Polyhydramnios","Alcohol intake"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The abruptio figure on p-206 lists folic acid deficiency, thrombophilia, increased BP (PIH), and polyhydramnios among the risk factors.  Why E wrong: Alcohol is explicitly NOT a risk factor for abruptio."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which of these is listed as an abruptio placenta risk factor in the source diagram? (Select all that apply)","options":["PROM (premature rupture of membranes)","Twin pregnancy","Fibroids","Trauma","Cervical previa"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source abruptio figure lists PROM, twin pregnancy, fibroids, and trauma as risk factors.  Why E wrong: \"Cervical previa\" is not a listed term/risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Folic acid deficiency is a risk factor for which condition per the source?","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placenta","Cervical incompetence","Vasa previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Folic acid deficiency is listed among the abruptio placenta risk factors in the figure.  Why A wrong: It is not listed for placenta previa.  Why C wrong: Not associated with cervical incompetence here.  Why D wrong: Not associated with vasa previa here."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In placenta previa classification, Type I is defined as:","options":["Placenta reaches up to the margins of the internal os","Placenta is in the lower uterine segment but does NOT reach the margins of the internal os","Placenta covers the internal os incompletely","Placenta completely covers the internal os"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Type I as placenta in the lower uterine segment that does not reach up to the margins of the internal os (also called lateral placenta previa).  Why A wrong: That describes Type II (marginal).  Why C wrong: That describes Type III (incomplete).  Why D wrong: That describes Type IV (complete/central)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Placenta previa Type I is also known as:","options":["Marginal placenta previa","Lateral placenta previa","Incomplete placenta previa","Central placenta previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source calls Type I \"Lateral Placenta previa.\"  Why A wrong: Marginal is Type II.  Why C wrong: Incomplete is Type III.  Why D wrong: Central (complete) is Type IV."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Placenta previa Type II, in which the placenta reaches up to the margins of the internal os, is known as:","options":["Lateral placenta previa","Marginal placenta previa","Incomplete placenta previa","Complete placenta previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Type II placenta reaches up to the margins of the internal os = marginal placenta previa.  Why A wrong: Lateral is Type I.  Why C wrong: Incomplete is Type III.  Why D wrong: Complete/central is Type IV."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Placenta previa Type III is described as:","options":["Placenta does not reach the internal os","Placenta reaches the margins of the internal os","Placenta covers the internal os incompletely (incomplete placenta previa)","Placenta completely covers the internal os"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Type III placenta covers the internal os incompletely = incomplete placenta previa.  Why A wrong: That is Type I.  Why B wrong: That is Type II (marginal).  Why D wrong: That is Type IV (complete/central)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Placenta previa Type IV, in which the placenta completely covers the internal os, is also called:","options":["Lateral placenta previa","Marginal placenta previa","Incomplete placenta previa","Complete placenta previa or central placenta"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Type IV placenta covers internal os completely = complete placenta previa or central placenta.  Why A wrong: Lateral is Type I.  Why B wrong: Marginal is Type II.  Why C wrong: Incomplete is Type III."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which types of placenta previa are described as having both anterior and posterior sub-varieties in the source classification?","options":["Type I and Type II","Type III and Type IV","Only Type I","Only Type IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Type 1 (Anterior, Posterior) and Type 2 (Anterior, Posterior) sub-varieties.  Why B wrong: Types III and IV are not subdivided into anterior/posterior in the source.  Why C wrong: Type II also has anterior/posterior.  Why D wrong: Type I and II both have the subdivision."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"According to the source's abruptio \"Page/grade classification,\" which grade is diagnosed retrospectively by examining the placenta because the patient has no complaint?","options":["Grade 0","Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 0 is defined as the patient having no complaint; it is a retrospective diagnosis on looking at the placenta.  Why B wrong: Grade 1 has pain abdomen + bleeding with normal fetal heart sound.  Why C wrong: Grade 2 has absent fetal heart sound.  Why D wrong: Grade 3 has absent fetal heart sound plus maternal shock."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In the abruptio grading, Grade 1 is characterized by:","options":["No complaint, retrospective diagnosis","Pain abdomen + bleeding with a NORMAL fetal heart sound","Pain abdomen + bleeding with ABSENT fetal heart sound","Maternal shock with DIC"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 1 = pain abdomen + bleeding, fetal heart sound \"Normal.\"  Why A wrong: That is Grade 0.  Why C wrong: Absent fetal heart sound defines Grade 2.  Why D wrong: Maternal shock ± DIC defines Grade 3."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In the abruptio grading, at which grade does the fetal heart sound first become ABSENT while the mother's vitals are still stable?","options":["Grade 0","Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 2 = pain abdomen + bleeding with fetal heart sound \"Absent\" (maternal shock not yet present).  Why A wrong: Grade 0 has no complaints.  Why B wrong: Grade 1 has a normal fetal heart sound.  Why D wrong: Grade 3 adds maternal shock ± DIC to the absent fetal heart sound."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which features characterize Grade 3 abruptio placenta? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pain abdomen + bleeding","Fetal heart sound absent","Mother in shock (vitals unstable)","May have DIC (± DIC)","Normal fetal heart sound"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 3 = pain abdomen + bleeding, fetal heart sound absent, mother in shock (vitals unstable), ± DIC.  Why E wrong: In Grade 3 the fetal heart sound is absent, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"DIC (disseminated intravascular coagulation) is associated with which grade of abruptio placenta in the source?","options":["Grade 0","Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source shows \"± DIC\" only under Grade 3 (mother in shock).  Why A/B/C wrong: DIC is not listed for Grades 0, 1, or 2."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Abruptio placenta occurs in three varieties. Which variety has externally VISIBLE bleeding?","options":["Concealed variety","Revealed variety","Mixed variety","Silent variety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the revealed variety, the placenta separates such that bleeding is visible externally.  Why A wrong: In the concealed variety, bleeding is not visible.  Why C wrong: Mixed has both revealed and concealed components.  Why D wrong: \"Silent variety\" is not a source term."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In which variety of abruptio placenta is the bleeding NOT visible externally?","options":["Revealed variety","Concealed variety","Marginal variety","Lateral variety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the concealed variety, the placenta separates in a manner in which bleeding is not visible.  Why A wrong: Revealed variety has visible bleeding.  Why C wrong: Marginal is a placenta previa term, not an abruptio variety.  Why D wrong: Lateral is a placenta previa term (Type I)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"How many varieties of abruptio placenta are described, and what are they?","options":["2 — revealed and concealed","3 — revealed, concealed, and mixed","3 — anterior, posterior, central","4 — Type I to Type IV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states abruptio has 3 varieties: revealed, concealed, and mixed.  Why A wrong: Mixed is also included, making three.  Why C wrong: Anterior/posterior/central are placenta previa terms.  Why D wrong: Type I–IV is placenta previa classification."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which is described as the \"dangerous variety\" of placenta previa?","options":["Type 1 Anterior","Type 2 Posterior","Type 3","Type 4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source explicitly states the dangerous variety of placenta previa is Type 2 Posterior.  Why A wrong: Type 1 Anterior is a minor degree.  Why C wrong: Type 3 is a major degree but not named the \"dangerous variety.\"  Why D wrong: Type 4 is a major degree but not the named \"dangerous variety.\""},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"The Stallworthy sign is associated with which varieties of placenta previa?","options":["Anterior varieties","Posterior varieties (Type 1 Posterior and Type 2 Posterior)","Only Type 4 (central)","Only Type 1 Anterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Stallworthy sign is seen in posterior varieties of placenta previa, shown branching to Type 1 Posterior and Type 2 Posterior.  Why A wrong: The sign is with posterior, not anterior, varieties.  Why C wrong: It is not limited to Type 4.  Why D wrong: It is not associated with Type 1 Anterior."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which placenta previa types are classified as MINOR degrees (where vaginal delivery can be tried)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Type 1 Anterior","Type 1 Posterior","Type II Anterior","Type 2 Posterior","Type 4"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Minor degrees include Type 1 (anterior and posterior) and Type II Anterior; these can attempt vaginal delivery.  Why D wrong: Type 2 Posterior (dangerous variety) is a major degree.  Why E wrong: Type 4 is a major degree requiring cesarean."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which placenta previa types are classified as MAJOR degrees requiring cesarean section? (Select all that apply)","options":["Type 2 Posterior (dangerous variety)","Type 3","Type 4","Type 1 Anterior","Type II Anterior"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Major degrees are Type 2 Posterior (dangerous variety), Type 3, and Type 4 — all require cesarean section.  Why D wrong: Type 1 Anterior is a minor degree.  Why E wrong: Type II Anterior is a minor degree (vaginal delivery can be tried)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"A woman with Type II Anterior placenta previa is being counseled about the mode of delivery. Based on the source, which is appropriate?","options":["Mandatory cesarean section","Can try vaginal delivery (minor degree)","Immediate hysterectomy","Induction is contraindicated in all cases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Type II Anterior is grouped with minor degrees where vaginal delivery can be tried.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is mandatory for major degrees (Type 2 Posterior, Type 3, Type 4).  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not indicated for a minor-degree previa.  Why D wrong: The source permits attempting vaginal delivery in minor degrees."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"A woman with Type 4 (central) placenta previa requires which mode of delivery per the source?","options":["Vaginal delivery","Cesarean section","Forceps delivery","Vacuum-assisted delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Type 4 is a major degree; the source states major degrees \"have to do cesarean section.\"  Why A wrong: Vaginal delivery is unsafe when the placenta covers the internal os completely.  Why C wrong: Forceps is a vaginal operative delivery, still contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Vacuum is a vaginal operative delivery, still contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In the concealed variety of abruptio, blood collects behind the placenta and infiltrates the myometrium, giving the uterus a bluish, bruised appearance. This is called:","options":["Bandl's ring","Couvelaire uterus","Cornual uterus","Constriction ring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes this exact sequence (blood collects behind placenta → myometrium → blue/bruised uterus) as Couvelaire uterus.  Why A wrong: Bandl's ring is a sign of obstructed labor.  Why C wrong: \"Cornual uterus\" is not the described entity.  Why D wrong: Constriction ring is a localized myometrial spasm, not myometrial hemorrhage."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Arrange the pathophysiology of Couvelaire uterus in correct order per the source.","options":["Blood collects behind placenta → goes into myometrium → uterus turns blue (bruised) → Couvelaire uterus","Uterus turns blue → blood collects behind placenta → Couvelaire uterus → myometrium","Myometrium bleeds → placenta separates → revealed bleeding","Placenta covers os → bleeding visible → Couvelaire uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source flow is: concealed abruptio → blood collects behind the placenta → goes into the myometrium → uterus appears blue/bruised → called Couvelaire uterus.  Why B wrong: The order of events is reversed.  Why C wrong: This is not the source sequence and mentions revealed bleeding.  Why D wrong: This mixes in placenta previa concepts."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Couvelaire uterus is seen in which variety of abruptio placenta?","options":["Revealed variety","Concealed variety","Mixed variety only","It is unrelated to abruptio"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Couvelaire uterus is seen in the concealed variety of abruptio.  Why A wrong: The revealed variety has visible bleeding, not concealed myometrial infiltration.  Why C wrong: The source specifies the concealed variety.  Why D wrong: It is a recognized complication of abruptio."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"According to the source, Couvelaire uterus is a risk factor for which complication?","options":["Postpartum hemorrhage","Placenta previa","Cervical incompetence","Gestational diabetes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Couvelaire uterus is a risk factor for postpartum hemorrhage (blood in the myometrium impairs contraction/atony).  Why B wrong: It is a consequence of abruptio, not a cause of previa.  Why C wrong: Not related to cervical incompetence.  Why D wrong: Not related to gestational diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which statement about Couvelaire uterus is TRUE per the source?","options":["It is an absolute indication for hysterectomy","It is NOT an indication for hysterectomy","It always requires blood transfusion of 10 units","It only occurs in placenta previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source explicitly states Couvelaire uterus is NOT an indication for hysterectomy.  Why A wrong: This is the opposite of the source statement.  Why C wrong: No such transfusion figure is given.  Why D wrong: It occurs in concealed abruptio, not previa."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"A patient in her third trimester presents with painless, bright-red vaginal bleeding with no obvious cause. This is most typical of:","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placenta","Vasa previa","Uterine rupture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists placenta previa bleeding as bright red, painless, causeless (no reason), and recurrent in the third trimester.  Why B wrong: Abruptio bleeding is dark red and painful.  Why C wrong: Vasa previa bleeding is fetal blood with membrane rupture, not described here.  Why D wrong: Uterine rupture is not described on these pages."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"The vaginal bleeding of placenta previa is classically described as: (Select all that apply)","options":["Bright red in color","Painless","Causeless","Recurrent","Dark red with abdominal pain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists placenta previa bleeding as bright red, painless, causeless, recurrent, and initially less in amount.  Why E wrong: Dark red with pain describes abruptio, not previa."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In placenta previa, an initial small bleed that precedes later heavier bleeding is called:","options":["Sentinel infarct","Warning hemorrhage","Show","Revealed hemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states previa bleeding is initially less in amount (\"warning hemorrhage\"), followed later by excessive bleeding.  Why A wrong: \"Sentinel infarct\" is not a source term.  Why C wrong: \"Show\" is the blood-tinged mucus plug of early labor, not previa bleeding.  Why D wrong: \"Revealed hemorrhage\" refers to an abruptio variety, not the warning bleed."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"The vaginal bleeding of abruptio placenta is typically described as:","options":["Bright red and painless","Dark red with abdominal pain","Bright red with no pain, recurrent","Clear watery discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists abruptio bleeding as dark red in color with pain in the abdomen present.  Why A wrong: Bright red and painless describes placenta previa.  Why C wrong: Recurrent painless bleeding describes previa.  Why D wrong: Clear watery discharge suggests rupture of membranes, not abruptio."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which of the following features are characteristic of abruptio placenta bleeding in the third trimester? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dark red color","Abdominal pain present","History of trauma or PIH","No warning hemorrhage; bleeding not recurrent in the same pregnancy","Painless bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists abruptio as dark red, painful, with a history of trauma/PIH, no warning hemorrhage, and bleeding not recurrent in the same pregnancy.  Why E wrong: Abruptio bleeding is painful; painless bleeding is a feature of placenta previa."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Recurrent bleeding in the same pregnancy is a feature of which condition?","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placenta","Both equally","Neither"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists recurrent bleeding under placenta previa, and states abruptio bleeding is NOT recurrent in the same pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Abruptio bleeding is not recurrent.  Why C wrong: The two differ on this point.  Why D wrong: Previa clearly shows recurrent bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In placenta previa, how does the patient's general condition relate to blood loss?","options":["General condition corresponds to the amount of blood lost","General condition does not correspond to blood loss","Patient is always in shock regardless of bleeding","Vitals are always normal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in previa the general condition corresponds to the amount of blood lost (less bleeding → stable vitals; more bleeding → unstable vitals).  Why B wrong: That describes abruptio (concealed/mixed), not previa.  Why C wrong: Vitals depend on the amount of bleeding.  Why D wrong: Vitals become unstable if bleeding is heavy."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In placenta previa, if the bleeding is less, the patient's vitals will be:","options":["Stable","Unstable","Absent","Always in shock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if bleeding is less → patient's vitals will be stable.  Why B wrong: Unstable vitals occur when bleeding is more.  Why C wrong: Vitals are not absent.  Why D wrong: Shock corresponds to more bleeding, not less."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In placenta previa, if the bleeding is more, the patient's vitals will be:","options":["Stable","Unstable","Unchanged","Improved"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if bleeding is more → patient's vitals will be unstable.  Why A wrong: Stable vitals correspond to less bleeding.  Why C wrong: Vitals change (deteriorate) with more bleeding.  Why D wrong: Vitals worsen, not improve."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"In concealed/mixed abruptio placenta, the relationship between the patient's general condition and visible blood loss is:","options":["They correspond exactly to each other","They do NOT correspond to each other (condition worse than visible loss)","There is never any deterioration","Bleeding is always fully visible"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that in abruptio, the general condition of the patient and the amount of blood loss do NOT correspond to each other (because bleeding is concealed).  Why A wrong: Corresponding condition-to-loss describes placenta previa.  Why C wrong: The patient can deteriorate despite little visible loss.  Why D wrong: In concealed abruptio, bleeding is hidden, not fully visible."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Per the source, what substance is released when the placenta separates in abruptio, contributing to abdominal pain and coagulation problems?","options":["Oxytocin","Thromboplastin","Progesterone","Prostacyclin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source flow states: placenta separates → releases thromboplastin → thromboplastin contracts the uterus → causing pain in the abdomen in abruptio.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin is not the substance described in this mechanism.  Why B correct as above.  Why C wrong: Progesterone maintains pregnancy; it is not released to cause this.  Why D wrong: Prostacyclin is a vasodilator, not the described mediator."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"According to the source, the abdominal pain in abruptio placenta is caused by which sequence?","options":["Placenta separates → releases thromboplastin → thromboplastin contracts the uterus → pain in abdomen","Placenta previa → warning hemorrhage → painless bleeding","Blood collects behind placenta → visible bleeding → no pain","Uterine decompression → placental migration → pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes placenta separation releasing thromboplastin, which contracts the uterus and causes abdominal pain in abruptio.  Why B wrong: That describes painless previa bleeding.  Why C wrong: Concealed abruptio has hidden bleeding and pain, not painless visible bleeding.  Why D wrong: This is not the source's pain mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"A third-trimester woman presents with sudden dark-red vaginal bleeding, a tense painful abdomen, a bluish bruised uterus at surgery, and a history of PIH. This clinical picture best fits:","options":["Placenta previa, minor degree","Abruptio placenta (concealed variety) with Couvelaire uterus","Vasa previa","Type IV central placenta previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dark red painful bleeding, PIH history, and a bruised (blue) uterus indicate concealed abruptio placenta with Couvelaire uterus.  Why A wrong: Previa bleeding is painless and bright red.  Why C wrong: Vasa previa is fetal bleeding on membrane rupture, without a Couvelaire uterus.  Why D wrong: Central previa causes painless bright-red bleeding, not a bruised uterus."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"A multiparous woman with a previous history of placenta previa and a prior cesarean section presents with painless recurrent bright-red bleeding at 34 weeks. Which is the priority initial diagnostic step per the source?","options":["Immediate digital vaginal examination","Ultrasonography (USG) for placental localization","Amniocentesis","Cervical cerclage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states USG is used for localization of the placenta (done in the 3rd trimester); with the two most important previa risk factors present, USG localization is the priority.  Why A wrong: A digital vaginal exam in suspected previa can provoke torrential bleeding and is avoided.  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis does not localize the placenta or diagnose previa.  Why D wrong: Cerclage treats cervical incompetence, not previa bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which condition classically presents with PAINLESS third-trimester bleeding versus PAINFUL third-trimester bleeding?","options":["Painless = abruptio; Painful = previa","Painless = placenta previa; Painful = abruptio placenta","Both are painless","Both are painful"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies placenta previa is painless bleeding and abruptio is associated with abdominal pain.  Why A wrong: This reverses the two.  Why C wrong: Abruptio is painful.  Why D wrong: Previa is painless."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"The color of bleeding helps distinguish the two conditions. Bright red bleeding suggests ___ while dark red bleeding suggests ___.","options":["Abruptio; previa","Previa; abruptio","Vasa previa; uterine rupture","Both suggest previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists bright red bleeding for placenta previa and dark red bleeding for abruptio placenta.  Why A wrong: This reverses the association.  Why C wrong: Those conditions are not the ones compared here.  Why D wrong: Dark red is abruptio, not previa."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which condition shows \"no warning hemorrhage\" before the main bleed?","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placenta","Both","Neither"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states abruptio has \"no warning haemorrhage,\" whereas previa has a warning hemorrhage followed by excessive bleeding.  Why A wrong: Previa characteristically has a warning bleed.  Why C wrong: The two differ on this feature.  Why D wrong: Abruptio clearly lacks a warning bleed."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Which of the following abruptio risk factors is linked to substance abuse in the source?","options":["Alcohol","Smoking (cocaine, abuse)","Folic acid","Thrombophilia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The abruptio figure labels smoking with \"(Cocaine, abuse).\"  Why A wrong: Alcohol is explicitly NOT a risk factor.  Why C wrong: Folic acid deficiency (not abuse) is the listed risk.  Why D wrong: Thrombophilia is a clotting disorder, not substance abuse."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"An accessory small lobe of placenta connected to the main placenta, which increases placental size/risk, is called a:","options":["Succenturiate lobe","Bilobate placenta","Battledore placenta","Circumvallate placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Succenturiate lobe\" under causes of a big placenta associated with placenta previa.  Why B wrong: Bilobate is two roughly equal lobes, listed separately.  Why C wrong: Battledore describes marginal cord insertion.  Why D wrong: Circumvallate has a raised membranous edge."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-2 — Placenta previa vs Abruptio placenta…","question":"Regarding the abruptio grade where the fetal heart sound remains NORMAL despite pain and bleeding, this indicates the fetus is likely:","options":["Alive (Grade 1)","Dead (Grade 2)","Dead with maternal shock (Grade 3)","Not yet formed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In Grade 1, the fetal heart sound is \"Normal,\" indicating the fetus is alive despite abruptio.  Why B wrong: Absent fetal heart sound (fetal death) defines Grade 2.  Why C wrong: Grade 3 adds maternal shock to absent fetal heart sound.  Why D wrong: The fetus is present but has a normal heartbeat."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In a patient with placenta previa, per-abdominal examination classically reveals the uterus to be:","options":["Hard, tense, and tender","Soft, non-tender, and relaxed","Rigid and board-like","Woody and irritable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In placenta previa the uterus is soft, non-tender, and relaxed on per-abdominal examination because the bleeding is revealed (from the lower segment) and not concealed within the myometrium.  Why A wrong: A hard, tense, tender uterus is the hallmark of abruptio placentae, not placenta previa.  Why C wrong: A rigid, board-like uterus suggests concealed abruption, not previa.  Why D wrong: A woody, irritable uterus indicates abruption/couvelaire uterus, not previa."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"On per-abdominal examination of a patient with abruptio placentae, the uterus is typically:","options":["Soft and relaxed","Non-tender and lax","Hard, tensed, and tender","Normal in tone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In abruptio placentae the retroplacental clot irritates the myometrium, making the uterus hard, tensed, and tender.  Why A wrong: A soft, relaxed uterus is a feature of placenta previa.  Why B wrong: A non-tender, lax uterus points to previa, not abruption.  Why D wrong: Uterine tone is markedly increased in abruption, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Regarding the fetal heart sound (FHS) and fetal parts in placenta previa, which is correct?","options":["FHS not easily heard and fetal parts not easily palpable","FHS easily heard and fetal parts easily palpable","FHS absent and fetal parts absent","FHS heard only on Doppler and fetal parts never palpable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In placenta previa the uterus is relaxed, so the fetal heart sound is easily heard and fetal parts are easily palpable.  Why A wrong: FHS not easily heard and fetal parts not easily palpable describe abruptio placentae.  Why C wrong: In previa the FHS is present and regular, not absent.  Why D wrong: Fetal parts are readily palpable in a relaxed previa uterus."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In abruptio placentae, fetal heart sound and fetal parts are characteristically:","options":["Easily heard / easily palpable","Not easily heard / not easily palpable","Heard clearly with normal parts","Loud with a regular rhythm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because the uterus is hard and tense in abruptio, the FHS is not easily heard and fetal parts are not easily palpable.  Why A wrong: Easily heard FHS and easily palpable parts are features of placenta previa.  Why C, D wrong: FHS may be absent (fetal death/distress) in abruption, not clear/loud/regular."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In placenta previa, the relationship between the height of the uterus and the period of pregnancy is:","options":["Height of uterus more than period of pregnancy","Height of uterus equal to period of pregnancy","Height of uterus less than period of pregnancy","No fixed relationship"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In previa the bleeding is revealed and not retained, so fundal height corresponds to (equals) the period of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Height greater than the period of pregnancy is seen in abruptio due to concealed retroplacental blood.  Why C wrong: Reduced height is not a feature of either condition here.  Why D wrong: The source specifies a definite relationship (equal)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In abruptio placentae, the height of the uterus is typically:","options":["Equal to period of pregnancy","Less than period of pregnancy","More than period of pregnancy","Unrelated to gestation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Concealed retroplacental hemorrhage increases uterine size, so fundal height is more than the period of pregnancy in abruption.  Why A wrong: Height equal to period of pregnancy is characteristic of previa.  Why B, D wrong: Reduced or unrelated height is not a described feature of abruption."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"The fetal heart sound in a classic case of placenta previa is:","options":["Present and regular","Always absent","Irregular and faint","Only detectable by ultrasound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states fetal heart sound is present and regular in placenta previa because the fetus is usually not compromised.  Why B wrong: FHS is present, not absent, in previa; absence points to abruption.  Why C, D wrong: It is described as regular and easily heard, not faint/irregular or only detectable on USG."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Why may the fetal heart sound not be heard in abruptio placentae?","options":["The placenta blocks conduction of sound","It may cause death or distress of the fetus","The uterus is too soft to transmit sound","The fetal parts descend into the pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes that in abruptio the fetal heart sound is not heard because it may cause death or distress of the fetus.  Why A wrong: The mechanism is placental separation compromising the fetus, not sound blockage by placenta.  Why C wrong: The uterus is hard in abruption, not soft.  Why D wrong: Fetal descent is unrelated to the absent FHS in abruption."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Per vaginal or per speculum examination in a patient with suspected placenta previa or abruptio placentae is:","options":["Mandatory to confirm the diagnosis","Contraindicated","Recommended only if bleeding is mild","The first step in every case"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per vaginal/per speculum examination is contraindicated in both placenta previa and abruptio placentae because it can provoke torrential hemorrhage.  Why A, C, D wrong: It is never a mandatory, mild-bleeding-only, or first-step maneuver; it is avoided in APH until previa is excluded (except a planned double set-up)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Which complication is common to BOTH placenta previa and abruptio placentae?","options":["Postpartum hemorrhage (PPH)","Disseminated intravascular coagulation as the leading complication","Malpresentation only","No shared complication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PPH is listed as a common complication of both placenta previa and abruptio placentae.  Why B wrong: DIC is listed specifically under abruptio, not as a shared leading complication of both.  Why C wrong: Malpresentation is a feature of previa, not shared.  Why D wrong: PPH is explicitly shared by both."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Fetal death is described as MORE common in which condition?","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placentae","Equal in both","Neither"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states fetal death is more common in abruptio placentae, whereas it is less common in placenta previa.  Why A wrong: Fetal death is less common in previa.  Why C, D wrong: The two conditions differ, with abruption carrying the higher fetal death risk."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Malpresentation such as transverse lie or breech is listed as a complication of:","options":["Abruptio placentae","Placenta previa","Both equally","DIC"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The low-lying placenta in placenta previa prevents engagement of the presenting part, causing malpresentation (transverse lie or breech).  Why A wrong: Malpresentation is listed under previa, whereas DIC is the specific abruptio complication.  Why C wrong: It is specifically a previa complication, not shared equally.  Why D wrong: DIC is a complication, not itself a malpresentation."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is specifically listed as a complication of:","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placentae","Vasa previa","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thromboplastin release from the separating placenta in abruption triggers DIC, which is listed as an abruptio complication.  Why A wrong: DIC is not listed under placenta previa.  Why C, D wrong: Vasa previa and twin pregnancy are not the conditions specified for DIC here."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"As a general rule, whenever a patient of APH presents, she should be:","options":["Immediately taken for per vaginal examination","Always taken as a case of placenta previa unless proved otherwise","Assumed to have abruptio placentae","Discharged after a single ultrasound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that any APH patient is always taken as a case of placenta previa unless proved otherwise, to avoid dangerous vaginal examination.  Why A wrong: Per vaginal examination is exactly what must be avoided.  Why C wrong: The default assumption is previa, not abruption.  Why D wrong: These patients need hospitalization, not discharge."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In a patient presenting with APH, which examination must NEVER be performed?","options":["Per abdominal palpation","Auscultation of fetal heart sound","Per vaginal examination","Vital signs assessment"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note explicitly says never do per vaginal examination in APH, as it may provoke catastrophic bleeding in previa.  Why A, B, D wrong: Abdominal palpation, FHS auscultation, and vitals assessment are safe, essential first-line steps."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Why must patients with placenta previa always be hospitalized?","options":["Because they always need immediate cesarean section","Because in placenta previa bleeding may reoccur","Because they cannot receive outpatient ultrasound","Because vaginal delivery is impossible"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states patients are always hospitalized because in placenta previa the bleeding may reoccur (recurrent, unpredictable hemorrhage).  Why A wrong: Not all previa patients need immediate cesarean; some undergo expectant management.  Why C, D wrong: Hospitalization is for recurrent bleeding risk, not ultrasound access or delivery route."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"The initial ultrasound investigation used for placenta previa is:","options":["Transvaginal ultrasound (TVS)","Transabdominal ultrasound (USG/TAS)","MRI","Doppler only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The initial USG for placenta previa is transabdominal ultrasound (TAS).  Why A wrong: TVS is the investigation of choice (IOC), but the INITIAL scan is transabdominal.  Why C, D wrong: MRI and Doppler are not the described initial investigations."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"The investigation of choice (IOC) for confirming placenta previa is:","options":["Transabdominal ultrasound","Transvaginal sonography (TVS)","Per vaginal examination","Clinical diagnosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states TVS is the IOC for placenta previa.  Why A wrong: Transabdominal USG is the initial, not the IOC, investigation.  Why C wrong: PV examination is contraindicated (unsafe) in previa.  Why D wrong: Clinical diagnosis is used for abruptio, not previa."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"The diagnosis of abruptio placentae is primarily:","options":["By transvaginal ultrasound","Clinical diagnosis","Per speculum examination","By MRI"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists abruptio placentae as a clinical diagnosis.  Why A wrong: TVS is the IOC for previa, not abruptio.  Why C wrong: Speculum examination is contraindicated in APH.  Why D wrong: MRI is not the described diagnostic method for abruption."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In placenta previa, per vaginal (PV) examination is best described as:","options":["The confirmatory test","Contraindicated / unsafe (UI)","The first step","Mandatory before ultrasound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note \"PV exam is UI\" (unsafe/contraindicated) indicates PV examination must be avoided in previa.  Why A, C, D wrong: PV exam is never confirmatory, first-step, or pre-USG mandatory in previa; TVS is used instead."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"The APH chapter section covered on these pages is titled:","options":["Antepartum Hemorrhage (APH) Part-1","Antepartum Hemorrhage (APH) Part-3","Postpartum Hemorrhage","Placental abruption Part-2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The heading reads \"ANTEPARTUM HEMORRHAGE (APH) (PART-3).\"  Why A, C, D wrong: The page is titled Part-3, not Part-1, PPH, or a Part-2."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"When a patient of APH arrives, the immediate care (1st-line management) includes which THREE simultaneous assessments?","options":["Check vitals, assess fetal heart sound, assess blood loss","USG, MRI, and blood culture","PV examination, speculum, and enema","Weight, height, and urine output only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Immediate/basic/first-line care consists of checking vitals, assessing the fetal heart sound, and assessing blood loss.  Why B wrong: Imaging/culture are not the immediate bedside assessments.  Why C wrong: PV/speculum examinations are contraindicated in APH.  Why D wrong: These parameters are not the described first-line APH assessments."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"As part of the simultaneous first-line resuscitation in APH, how many IV cannulas are recommended and of what size?","options":["One small-bore cannula","Two large-bore cannulas","Three medium cannulas","A central line only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The protocol specifies 2 IV cannulas (large bore) to allow rapid fluid and blood administration.  Why A wrong: A single small-bore line is inadequate for hemorrhage resuscitation.  Why C, D wrong: Three cannulas or a central-line-only approach is not what is specified."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Which measures are performed SIMULTANEOUSLY during first-line APH management? (Select all that apply)","options":["Insert 2 large-bore IV cannulas","Start IV fluids","ABO and Rh typing to arrange for blood","Give oxygen (O2) by mask","Perform per vaginal examination"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Simultaneously the protocol lists 2 large-bore IV cannulas, IV fluids, ABO/Rh typing to arrange blood, and O2 by mask.  Why A–D correct: All four are explicit simultaneous first-line steps.  Why E wrong: Per vaginal examination is contraindicated in APH, not part of resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"During APH resuscitation, ABO and Rh typing is done in order to:","options":["Diagnose the cause of bleeding","Arrange for blood","Determine mode of delivery","Assess fetal lung maturity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ABO and Rh typing is performed to arrange for blood (cross-matched transfusion).  Why A wrong: Blood typing does not diagnose the bleeding cause.  Why C wrong: Mode of delivery depends on type/degree of previa, not blood typing.  Why D wrong: Lung maturity is unrelated to blood typing."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In the first-line management of APH, oxygen is administered via:","options":["Nasal prongs only","O2 by mask","Ventilator","Not administered"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The protocol lists O2 by mask as a simultaneous first-line step.  Why A wrong: The source specifies a mask, not nasal prongs.  Why C wrong: Mechanical ventilation is not part of routine first-line APH care.  Why D wrong: Oxygen is indeed administered."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Definitive management of placenta previa is divided into which two broad options?","options":["Terminate pregnancy immediately (active) vs continue pregnancy (expectant)","Vaginal delivery vs induction","Bed rest vs discharge","Blood transfusion vs iron therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Definitive management is either terminate the pregnancy immediately (active management) or continue the pregnancy (expectant management).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the two definitive-management categories described."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Active management (immediate termination of pregnancy) in placenta previa is indicated:","options":["Only when the fetus is preterm","In any condition where the mother's life is at risk","Only if the mother requests it","Only after 40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Active management (terminate the pregnancy immediately) is chosen in any condition where the mother's life is at risk.  Why A wrong: Prematurity favors expectant management, not immediate termination.  Why C, D wrong: Maternal life risk, not patient request or a fixed 40-week rule, drives active management."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"The PURPOSE of expectant management in placenta previa is to:","options":["Let the fetus attain lung maturity (37 weeks)","Reduce maternal blood pressure","Avoid the need for corticosteroids","Deliver immediately regardless of gestation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Expectant (continue pregnancy) management aims to let the fetus attain lung maturity, targeting 37 weeks.  Why B, C wrong: BP control and avoiding steroids are not the stated purpose (steroids are in fact given).  Why D wrong: The goal is to delay delivery to reach maturity, not to deliver immediately."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"The absolute CONDITION that must be respected during expectant management of placenta previa is:","options":["Never putting the mother's life at risk","Always delivering vaginally","Avoiding all hospital admission","Withholding corticosteroids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The condition for expectant management is never putting the mother's life at risk; maternal safety overrides fetal maturity goals.  Why B, C, D wrong: These contradict the described expectant protocol (which includes hospitalization and corticosteroids)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Which is an indication for ACTIVE management of placenta previa?","options":["Patient vitals stable with no bleeding","Patient vitals unstable (patient in shock)","Gestational age under 37 weeks with no distress","No congenital malformation on USG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Unstable vitals / shock is listed as an indication for active management.  Why A, C, D wrong: Stable vitals, preterm without distress, and no malformation are conditions for EXPECTANT management, not active."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Which of the following are indications for ACTIVE management of placenta previa? (Select all that apply)","options":["Unstable vitals / shock","Active or continuous bleeding","Fetal distress","USG showing a malformation incompatible with life (e.g., neural tube defect)","Term/advanced gestational age"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Active management is indicated with unstable vitals/shock, active/continuous bleeding, advanced gestational age, fetal distress, and USG malformation incompatible with life such as neural tube defect.  Why A–E correct: All five are the listed active-management indications.  (No incorrect option here — all five are stated indications.)"},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Under active management, a malformation on USG that justifies terminating the pregnancy is one that is:","options":["Minor and treatable","Incompatible with life, such as neural tube defect","Any congenital heart defect","A cleft lip"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Active management is indicated when USG shows a malformation incompatible with life, e.g., neural tube defect.  Why A wrong: Minor, treatable defects favor expectant management.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the \"incompatible with life\" example given."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Which set of conditions must ALL be fulfilled to choose expectant management of placenta previa?","options":["Stable vitals, no active bleeding, gestation <37 weeks, no fetal distress, no/minor congenital malformation on USG","Unstable vitals, active bleeding, term gestation","Fetal distress plus lethal malformation","Any single one of the criteria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Expectant management requires ALL five: stable vitals, no active bleeding, gestation <37 weeks, no fetal distress, and no congenital malformation / only minor defect on USG.  Why B, C wrong: These describe active-management indications.  Why D wrong: All five conditions must be fulfilled, not just one."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"For expectant management, the gestational-age criterion is that the pregnancy is:","options":["≥ 40 weeks","Less than 37 weeks (preterm)","Exactly 37 weeks","Any gestation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Expectant management applies when gestational age is under 37 weeks (i.e., preterm, so we aim to reach lung maturity). (The source's \"<37 hours\" is a typographical error for <37 weeks.)  Why A, C wrong: At or beyond 37 weeks the fetus is mature and we can deliver, not continue expectant care.  Why D wrong: There is a specific preterm threshold (<37 weeks), not any gestation."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"On USG, expectant management of placenta previa is appropriate when there is:","options":["A lethal neural tube defect","No congenital malformation, or only a minor defect","Any major malformation","Multiple congenital anomalies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Expectant management requires no congenital malformation or only a minor defect on USG.  Why A, C, D wrong: Lethal or major/multiple malformations favor active management (termination)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"The regime followed during expectant management of placenta previa is known as:","options":["McDonald's regime","Macafee-Johnson's regime","Shirodkar regime","Misgav-Ladach regime"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The steps of expectant management constitute the Macafee-Johnson's regime.  Why A wrong: McDonald relates to cervical cerclage/gestational dating, not expectant previa management.  Why C wrong: Shirodkar is a cerclage technique.  Why D wrong: Misgav-Ladach is a cesarean technique."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Which of the following are steps of the Macafee-Johnson expectant regime? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hospitalization","Bed rest","Injection corticosteroid to mature fetal lungs","Inj. Anti-D if the patient is Rh negative","Arrange for blood"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The five steps are hospitalization, bed rest, corticosteroid injection for fetal lung maturity, Anti-D if Rh negative, and arranging for blood.  Why A–E correct: All are explicitly listed steps of the regime.  (No incorrect option — all five are stated.)"},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In the expectant management regime, corticosteroid injection is given to:","options":["Control maternal bleeding","Mature the lungs of the fetus","Prevent Rh sensitization","Reduce uterine contractions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Injection corticosteroid is given so that the lungs of the fetus mature (accelerating surfactant production).  Why A wrong: Steroids do not control maternal hemorrhage.  Why C wrong: Rh sensitization is prevented by Anti-D, not steroids.  Why D wrong: Tocolysis, not steroids, reduces contractions."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"During expectant management, if the patient is Rh negative, she should receive:","options":["Injection Anti-D","Injection oxytocin","Injection corticosteroid only","No additional treatment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The regime states that if the patient is Rh negative, give Inj. Anti-D to prevent isoimmunization.  Why B wrong: Oxytocin is not indicated here.  Why C wrong: Corticosteroid is a separate step and does not address Rh status.  Why D wrong: An Rh-negative patient specifically requires Anti-D."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"During expectant management, the \"daily check\" of the patient should include: (Select all that apply)","options":["Vitals","Fetal heart sound","Ask about bleeding","Daily per vaginal examination","Daily cesarean readiness drill"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The daily check comprises vitals, fetal heart sound, and asking about bleeding.  Why A–C correct: All three are the listed daily-check items.  Why D wrong: PV examination is contraindicated in APH/previa.  Why E wrong: A daily cesarean drill is not part of the described daily check."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Expectant management of placenta previa is continued up until how many weeks, at which point the patient is delivered?","options":["34 weeks","37 weeks","40 weeks","42 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Expectant management continues until 37 weeks, then the patient is delivered (fetal lungs are mature).  Why A wrong: 34 weeks is earlier than the stated target.  Why C, D wrong: 40/42 weeks exceed the 37-week target for previa delivery."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"During expectant management, if on any day bleeding starts or fetal distress appears, the correct action is to:","options":["Continue waiting until 37 weeks","Not wait till 37 weeks and immediately terminate","Increase bed rest only","Repeat the ultrasound and observe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If bleeding starts or fetal distress is present, do NOT wait till 37 weeks — immediately terminate (deliver).  Why A wrong: Waiting endangers mother/fetus once bleeding or distress occurs.  Why C, D wrong: Bed rest or observation is unsafe once active bleeding/distress develops."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In deciding the mode of delivery in placenta previa, minor degrees include which types?","options":["Type III and Type IV","Type I (anterior/posterior) and Type II anterior","Type II posterior only","Only Type IV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Minor degrees are Type I (anterior and posterior) and Type II-anterior, for which vaginal delivery can be attempted.  Why A wrong: Type III and IV are major degrees.  Why C wrong: Type II posterior is the \"dangerous variety\" grouped with major degrees.  Why D wrong: Type IV is a major degree."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"For minor degrees of placenta previa (Type I and Type II-anterior), the preferred mode of delivery is to:","options":["Perform immediate cesarean section","Try vaginal delivery","Perform double set-up examination only","Delay delivery indefinitely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For minor degrees, one may try vaginal delivery.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is reserved for major degrees / heavy bleeding.  Why C wrong: Double set-up examination is a diagnostic step, not the delivery mode.  Why D wrong: Delivery is not delayed indefinitely."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Which are classified as MAJOR degrees of placenta previa requiring cesarean section? (Select all that apply)","options":["Type III and Type IV","Dangerous variety of placenta previa (Type II posterior)","Excessive bleeding irrespective of type of placenta previa","Type I anterior","Type II anterior"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Major degrees are Type III & IV, the dangerous variety (Type II posterior), and any patient with excessive bleeding regardless of previa type — all needing cesarean section.  Why A–C correct: All are listed under major degrees.  Why D, E wrong: Type I anterior and Type II anterior are minor degrees (attempt vaginal delivery)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"The \"dangerous variety of placenta previa\" refers to:","options":["Type I anterior","Type II posterior","Type II anterior","Type III only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Type II posterior is termed the dangerous variety of placenta previa and is managed as a major degree (cesarean).  Why A, C wrong: Type I anterior and Type II anterior are minor degrees.  Why D wrong: Type III is a major degree but is not the specific \"dangerous variety\" label used here."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"A patient with placenta previa who has excessive bleeding, irrespective of the type of previa, should be delivered by:","options":["Vaginal delivery","Cesarean section","Expectant management","Double set-up only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Excessive bleeding irrespective of the type of placenta previa mandates cesarean section (grouped under major degrees).  Why A wrong: Vaginal delivery is unsafe with excessive bleeding.  Why C wrong: Expectant management is inappropriate with active heavy bleeding.  Why D wrong: Double set-up is diagnostic; the delivery route here is cesarean."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Before deciding the mode of delivery in placenta previa, it is necessary to:","options":["Perform amniocentesis","Know the type of placenta previa","Do a per vaginal examination in the ward","Wait until labor begins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For mode of delivery in placenta previa, it is necessary to know the type of placenta previa.  Why A wrong: Amniocentesis is not required to decide delivery mode.  Why C wrong: Ward PV examination is contraindicated (only a planned double set-up in OT).  Why D wrong: The type must be known regardless of labor onset."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"The preferred method to determine the type of placenta previa is USG; the second method used if USG is not available is:","options":["MRI","Double set-up examination","Amniocentesis","Per abdominal palpation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The two methods are (1) USG and, if not available, (2) double set-up examination.  Why A, C, D wrong: MRI, amniocentesis, and abdominal palpation are not the described backup methods."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"A \"double set-up examination\" is best described as:","options":["A per vaginal examination done in the ward","A per vaginal examination performed in the OT after all arrangements for cesarean section are made","Two consecutive ultrasounds","Examination by two doctors on the ward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a double set-up examination, in the OT after doing all arrangements for cesarean section, a pervaginal examination is done; hence \"double set-up.\"  Why A wrong: It is never done casually in the ward; full cesarean readiness must be in place.  Why C, D wrong: It is not two ultrasounds or two examiners; it is a controlled PV exam with cesarean backup."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In the pathophysiology of abruptio placentae, when the placenta separates, there is release of:","options":["Oxytocin","Thromboplastin","Surfactant","Progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the placenta separates in abruption, thromboplastin is released, which can trigger the coagulation cascade.  Why A, C, D wrong: Oxytocin, surfactant, and progesterone are not the substance released by placental separation in this mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Thromboplastin release following placental separation in abruptio placentae can lead to two outcomes. One is uterine contraction (progressing to stage of delivery); the other is:","options":["Placenta previa","Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)","Amniotic fluid embolism","Gestational diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thromboplastin release can lead to DIC, in addition to causing uterine contraction that advances the stage of delivery.  Why A wrong: Previa is a separate placental-location disorder, not a consequence of abruption thromboplastin.  Why C, D wrong: AFE and GDM are not the outcomes described here."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In abruptio placentae, thromboplastin release causing uterine contraction leads to:","options":["Cessation of labor","The stage of delivery","Placental implantation","Cervical incompetence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Uterine contraction from thromboplastin release progresses the patient to the stage of delivery.  Why A wrong: Contraction promotes, not stops, labor.  Why C, D wrong: Implantation and cervical incompetence are unrelated to this acute process."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In Case 1 (a patient of APH coming to Emergency), what is the correct sequence of next steps?","options":["USG → resuscitate → deliver","Patient vitals check (BP, PR) → auscultate FHS → ask about blood loss → resuscitate","Per vaginal exam → cesarean → transfuse","Discharge → follow-up USG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Case 1 steps are: (1) check vitals (BP, PR), (2) auscultate fetal heart sound, (3) ask about blood loss, (4) resuscitate.  Why A wrong: USG is not the first step in the emergency Case 1 sequence.  Why C wrong: PV exam is contraindicated in APH.  Why D wrong: These patients require assessment and resuscitation, not discharge."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In Case 1 of APH, resuscitation of the patient includes which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["IV line","Blood cross match","IV fluid","Oral rehydration only","Per vaginal examination"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Resuscitation in Case 1 comprises IV line, blood cross match, and fluid.  Why A–C correct: All are listed under \"Resuscitate.\"  Why D wrong: Oral rehydration is inadequate for hemorrhagic resuscitation.  Why E wrong: PV examination is contraindicated in APH."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In Case 2 (a patient of APH with STABLE vitals in Emergency), the next step is:","options":["Immediate cesarean section","USG (initial USG = transabdominal sonography/TAS)","Per vaginal examination","Discharge home"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a stable APH patient, the next step is USG — the initial USG is transabdominal sonography (TAS).  Why A wrong: Immediate cesarean is for unstable patients (Case 3).  Why C wrong: PV exam is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: A stable APH patient still needs evaluation, not discharge."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In Case 2, if the initial transabdominal ultrasound suggests placenta previa, the next imaging step is:","options":["MRI","Transvaginal ultrasound","Repeat transabdominal scan in one week","Per speculum examination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If placenta previa is suspected on TAS, the flow proceeds to transvaginal ultrasound (the IOC for confirming previa).  Why A wrong: MRI is not the described next step.  Why C wrong: A delayed repeat scan is not indicated when previa is suspected.  Why D wrong: Speculum examination is contraindicated in APH."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"In Case 3 (a patient of APH with UNSTABLE vitals in Emergency), the correct sequence of next steps is:","options":["Observe → repeat USG → discharge","Resuscitate patient → immediate delivery → cesarean section","Expectant management → 37 weeks → deliver","Per vaginal exam → vaginal delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For unstable vitals, the steps are resuscitate the patient, then immediate delivery by cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Observation/discharge is unsafe with unstable vitals.  Why C wrong: Expectant management requires stable vitals, which are absent here.  Why D wrong: PV exam is contraindicated, and cesarean (not vaginal) is indicated for unstable APH."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"Which of the following correctly differentiates placenta previa from abruptio placentae on per-abdominal examination?","options":["Previa: hard/tender uterus; Abruptio: soft/relaxed uterus","Previa: soft/relaxed uterus with easily heard FHS; Abruptio: hard/tender uterus with FHS not easily heard","Both have a hard, tender uterus","Both have an absent fetal heart sound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Previa = soft, relaxed, non-tender uterus with easily heard FHS; abruptio = hard, tensed, tender uterus with FHS not easily heard.  Why A wrong: It reverses the two conditions.  Why C wrong: Only abruptio has a hard, tender uterus.  Why D wrong: FHS is present/regular in previa, not absent in both."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"A patient with APH has an unstable pulse and BP with active bleeding. According to definitive management principles, she requires:","options":["Expectant management","Active management (immediate termination/delivery)","Discharge with outpatient USG","Bed rest and corticosteroids only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Unstable vitals with active bleeding are indications for active management (immediate termination/delivery) because the mother's life is at risk.  Why A wrong: Expectant management requires stable vitals and no active bleeding.  Why C wrong: An unstable, bleeding patient cannot be safely discharged.  Why D wrong: Bed rest/steroids belong to expectant management, inappropriate when unstable."},{"section":"Batch 043 · Antepartum Hemorrhage Part-3 — Management of Placenta Previa, mode of…","question":"A stable APH patient at 32 weeks with no active bleeding, no fetal distress, and no congenital malformation on USG is best managed by:","options":["Immediate cesarean section","Expectant (Macafee-Johnson) management","Double set-up examination immediately","Anti-D only and discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All expectant-management conditions are met (stable vitals, no bleeding, <37 weeks, no distress, no malformation), so expectant Macafee-Johnson management is appropriate to reach lung maturity.  Why A wrong: Immediate cesarean is for unstable/active-bleeding/term cases.  Why C wrong: Double set-up is a diagnostic step for mode of delivery, not routine here.  Why D wrong: Anti-D is only one component and only if Rh negative; discharge is unsafe (hospitalization is required)."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In a patient with abruptio placentae, immediate delivery should be done primarily to prevent which major complication?","options":["Amniotic fluid embolism","Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)","Uterine inversion","Rh isoimmunisation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in abruptio patients immediate delivery should be done to prevent DIC, and lists DIC as the major complication of abruptio.  Why A wrong: Amniotic fluid embolism is not the stated indication for immediate delivery in abruptio.  Why C wrong: Uterine inversion is a third-stage complication, not the reason for immediate delivery here.  Why D wrong: Rh isoimmunisation is unrelated to the urgency of delivery in abruptio."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Which of the following is a \"NEVER do\" rule in the management of abruptio placentae according to the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Never prolong delivery","Never do expectant management","Never give drugs to decrease uterine contractions (tocolytics)","Never perform resuscitation","Never terminate the pregnancy immediately"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists three \"never\" points — never prolong delivery, never do expectant management, and never give drugs to decrease uterine contractions (tocolytics).  Why D wrong: Resuscitation is the initial management, so it must be done, not avoided.  Why E wrong: Immediate termination of pregnancy IS recommended, so it is not a \"never\"."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Why should tocolytics (drugs that decrease uterine contractions) be avoided in abruptio placentae?","options":["They cause maternal hypertension","Delivery must not be prolonged in abruptio","They cause fetal macrosomia","They increase amniotic fluid volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Since immediate/prompt delivery is required and delivery must never be prolonged, tocolytics (which delay labour) are contraindicated.  Why A wrong: Maternal hypertension is not the reason cited for avoiding tocolytics here.  Why C wrong: Tocolytics do not cause macrosomia.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid volume is not the concern."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"What is the initial management of a patient with abruptio placentae?","options":["Expectant management with bed rest","Resuscitation","Tocolytic therapy","Immediate hysterectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states initial management = Resuscitation (followed by immediate termination of pregnancy).  Why A wrong: Expectant management is explicitly to be avoided in abruptio.  Why C wrong: Tocolytics are contraindicated in abruptio.  Why D wrong: Hysterectomy is not the initial step; vaginal delivery is preferred."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"After resuscitation, what is the next component of management in abruptio placentae?","options":["Immediate termination of pregnancy","Continue pregnancy to term","Start oral iron therapy","Wait for spontaneous labour at 40 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The management equation is Resuscitation + Immediate Termination of Pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Continuing/prolonging pregnancy is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Oral iron therapy is not the management focus in acute abruptio.  Why D wrong: Waiting to term contradicts the \"never prolong delivery\" rule."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"What is the preferred mode of delivery in abruptio placentae (when no contraindication is present)?","options":["Elective cesarean section","Vaginal delivery","Instrumental forceps delivery only","Delivery is always by hysterotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Mode of delivery => vaginal delivery (preferred)\".  Why A wrong: Cesarean is reserved for specific indications, not the default preferred mode.  Why C wrong: Forceps is not stated as the preferred mode.  Why D wrong: Hysterotomy is not the routine mode of delivery."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Which of the following is an indication for cesarean section in abruptio placentae? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fetal distress present","Patient's vitals unstable","Gestational age < 32 weeks","Maternal age > 30 years","Gestational age > 37 weeks"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The three listed cesarean indications in abruptio are fetal distress present, patient's vitals unstable, and gestational age < 32 weeks.  Why D wrong: Maternal age > 30 years is not a listed indication.  Why E wrong: Gestational age > 37 weeks is not listed; the age criterion is < 32 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In abruptio placentae, cesarean section is indicated when gestational age is:","options":["Less than 32 weeks","Greater than 37 weeks","Exactly 40 weeks","Between 34 and 36 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists gestational age < 32 weeks as a cesarean indication in abruptio.  Why B, C, D wrong: These gestational ranges are not the stated cesarean cut-off; only < 32 weeks is listed."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In a patient with abruptio placentae complicated by DIC, the coagulation profile characteristically shows:","options":["Increased fibrinogen and decreased D-Dimer","Decreased fibrinogen and increased D-Dimer","Normal fibrinogen and normal D-Dimer","Increased platelets and increased fibrinogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The case describes an abnormal coagulation profile with fibrinogen decreased (↓) and D-Dimer increased in DIC.  Why A wrong: Fibrinogen falls (not rises) and D-Dimer rises (not falls) in DIC.  Why C wrong: The profile is abnormal, not normal.  Why D wrong: Platelets and fibrinogen typically fall in DIC."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In a patient of abruptio with fetal distress and DIC, what is the FIRST step in management?","options":["Immediate vaginal delivery","Correct the DIC (give Fresh Frozen Plasma / Cryoprecipitate)","Perform emergency hysterectomy","Start tocolytics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The case states \"1st correct DIC → (Fresh Frozen Plasma / Cryoprecipitate given)\", then blood transfusion, followed by vaginal delivery.  Why A wrong: Delivery follows AFTER correcting DIC and blood transfusion.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not the first step in this scenario.  Why D wrong: Tocolytics are contraindicated in abruptio."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Which blood products are used to correct DIC in a patient with abruptio placentae?","options":["Packed red cells only","Fresh Frozen Plasma / Cryoprecipitate","Platelet concentrate only","Albumin infusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page specifies Fresh Frozen Plasma / Cryoprecipitate to correct DIC.  Why A wrong: Packed red cells (blood transfusion) follow DIC correction but are not the DIC-correcting product.  Why C wrong: Platelet concentrate alone is not the stated agent.  Why D wrong: Albumin does not correct DIC."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In the abruptio + DIC case, what is the correct sequence of management?","options":["Vaginal delivery → correct DIC → blood transfusion","Correct DIC (FFP/Cryoprecipitate) → blood transfusion → vaginal delivery","Blood transfusion → vaginal delivery → correct DIC","Cesarean section → correct DIC → discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow is correct DIC (FFP/Cryoprecipitate) → blood transfusion → followed by vaginal delivery.  Why A, C wrong: Delivery is the last step, after DIC correction and transfusion.  Why D wrong: Vaginal delivery (not cesarean) is preferred and DIC correction precedes it."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"A patient with a previous cesarean section now presents with placenta previa in this pregnancy. Which complication should be anticipated?","options":["Increased chances of placenta accreta","Increased chances of gestational diabetes","Increased chances of oligohydramnios","Increased chances of macrosomia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The case states previous cesarean + current placenta previa increases chances of placenta accreta.  Why B wrong: Gestational diabetes is not the anticipated complication of this combination.  Why C wrong: Oligohydramnios is unrelated to this scenario.  Why D wrong: Macrosomia is a diabetes-related complication, not tied to previous cesarean + previa."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In a patient with previous cesarean, placenta previa, and anticipated placenta accreta, what is the recommended delivery plan?","options":["Vaginal delivery","Cesarean section followed by hysterectomy","Expectant management to term","Induction of labour with oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states delivery is always by cesarean section followed by hysterectomy.  Why A wrong: Vaginal delivery is not recommended when placenta accreta is anticipated with previa.  Why C wrong: Expectant management is not the plan for anticipated accreta.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin induction is not the recommended approach here."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Which is the main hormone that regulates blood sugar levels?","options":["Glucagon","Insulin","Cortisol","Human Placental Lactogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states insulin is the main hormone which regulates blood sugar levels.  Why A wrong: Glucagon raises blood sugar but is not listed as the main regulating hormone here.  Why C wrong: Cortisol is a counter-regulatory hormone, not the main regulator described.  Why D wrong: HPL causes insulin resistance but is not the main sugar-regulating hormone."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Insulin is secreted by which cells?","options":["Alpha cells of the pancreas","Beta (β) cells of the pancreas","Delta cells of the pancreas","Acinar cells of the pancreas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states insulin is secreted by the pancreas (β cells).  Why A wrong: Alpha cells secrete glucagon, not insulin.  Why C wrong: Delta cells secrete somatostatin.  Why D wrong: Acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes, not insulin."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"By approximately what gestational age does the fetus start producing its own insulin?","options":["6 weeks of pregnancy","12 weeks of pregnancy (11–12 weeks)","20 weeks of pregnancy","28 weeks of pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the fetus starts producing insulin by 12 weeks of pregnancy (11–12 weeks).  Why A, C, D wrong: These gestational ages do not match the stated 11–12 week onset of fetal insulin production."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"What is the role of insulin in blood glucose regulation?","options":["It moves glucose from the cells into the blood, raising blood glucose","It helps move glucose from the blood into the cells, decreasing blood glucose","It converts glucose to protein in the liver","It has no effect on blood glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states insulin helps move glucose from blood to the cells, so blood glucose level decreases, regulating blood sugar levels.  Why A wrong: Insulin lowers blood glucose by moving it into cells, not the reverse.  Why C wrong: The described role is glucose transport into cells, not conversion to protein.  Why D wrong: Insulin clearly affects (lowers) blood glucose."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"If insulin is present in NORMAL quantities, the blood sugar level will be:","options":["Increased","Normal","Zero","Fluctuating wildly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in normal quantities of insulin, blood sugar levels are normal.  Why A wrong: Increase occurs with insulin deficiency/resistance, not normal quantities.  Why C wrong: Blood sugar does not fall to zero with normal insulin.  Why D wrong: Normal insulin keeps sugar normal, not wildly fluctuating."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"If insulin is present in EXCESS, what happens to the blood sugar level?","options":["It increases","It decreases (↓)","It remains unchanged","It becomes undetectable permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in excess insulin, blood sugar level decreases (↓).  Why A wrong: Excess insulin lowers, not raises, blood sugar.  Why C wrong: Excess insulin does change (lowers) blood sugar.  Why D wrong: A transient decrease is described, not permanent undetectability."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"When insulin is present in LESS quantity or there is resistance to insulin, the resulting sequence is:","options":["Blood sugar increase → Hyperglycemia → Diabetes","Blood sugar decrease → Hypoglycemia → coma","Blood sugar normal → no effect","Blood sugar increase → Hypoglycemia → Diabetes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows: less/resistance to insulin → Blood Sugar Level Increase → Hyperglycemia → Diabetes.  Why B wrong: Insulin deficiency/resistance raises sugar (hyperglycemia), not hypoglycemia.  Why C wrong: There is a definite effect — hyperglycemia.  Why D wrong: The raised sugar leads to hyperglycemia, not hypoglycemia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"During pregnancy, what happens to insulin sensitivity in ALL pregnant females?","options":["Increased insulin sensitivity","Insulin resistance develops","Complete absence of insulin","Insulin becomes toxic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states there is insulin resistance in all pregnant females.  Why A wrong: Sensitivity decreases (resistance), it does not increase.  Why C wrong: Insulin is still produced; there is resistance, not absence.  Why D wrong: Insulin does not become toxic."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"As a consequence of insulin resistance in pregnancy, the sequence of events is:","options":["Glucose does not enter the cells → Hyperglycemia → ↑Insulin secretion","Glucose enters cells rapidly → Hypoglycemia → ↓Insulin secretion","Glucose is destroyed in placenta → Normoglycemia","Glucose converts to fat → weight loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows insulin resistance → glucose does not enter cells → Hyperglycemia → ↑Insulin secretion.  Why B wrong: Resistance prevents glucose entry causing hyperglycemia, not hypoglycemia.  Why C wrong: Glucose is not destroyed in the placenta.  Why D wrong: Weight loss is not the described consequence."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Insulin resistance in pregnancy is due to which hormone?","options":["Estrogen","Human Placental Lactogen","Progesterone","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page (Q.1) states insulin resistance is due to the hormone Human Placental Lactogen.  Why A wrong: Estrogen is not the specified cause of insulin resistance here.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is a labour/lactation hormone, not the cause of insulin resistance."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Maximum insulin resistance in pregnancy is seen between which gestational weeks?","options":["12–16 weeks","18–22 weeks","24–28 weeks (starting from 24 weeks)","32–36 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page (Q.2) states maximum insulin resistance is seen between 24–28 weeks, starting from 24 weeks.  Why A, B, D wrong: These week ranges do not match the stated 24–28 week window of maximum resistance."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Pregnancy is described as which type of metabolic state?","options":["Ketogenic state","Diabetogenic state","Hypoglycemic state","Alkalotic state"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states pregnancy is a diabetogenic state (owing to insulin resistance predisposing to diabetes).  Why A wrong: Pregnancy is termed diabetogenic, not specifically ketogenic.  Why C wrong: It predisposes to hyperglycemia, not hypoglycemia.  Why D wrong: \"Alkalotic state\" is not the described term."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"What is the main source of energy for the fetus, which it depends on the mother for?","options":["Protein","Glucose","Fatty acids","Ketone bodies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the fetus depends on the mother for glucose, and glucose is the main source of energy for the fetus.  Why A wrong: Protein is not the stated main energy source.  Why C wrong: Fatty acids are not the stated main fetal energy source.  Why D wrong: Ketone bodies are not the primary fetal energy source described."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"When there is maternal hyperglycemia (↑glucose), what happens across the placenta?","options":["Glucose transport from mother to fetus increases, causing fetal hyperglycemia","Glucose transport stops completely","Maternal insulin crosses to lower fetal glucose","Fetal glucose decreases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows maternal hyperglycemia → increased glucose transport from mother to fetus via placenta → fetal hyperglycemia.  Why B wrong: Transport increases, it does not stop.  Why C wrong: Maternal insulin does not cross the placenta.  Why D wrong: Fetal glucose rises (fetal hyperglycemia), it does not decrease."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In response to fetal hyperglycemia, the fetal pancreas will:","options":["Stop producing insulin","Produce more insulin","Produce glucagon only","Undergo atrophy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states fetal hyperglycemia leads the fetal pancreas to produce more insulin.  Why A wrong: The fetal pancreas increases insulin production, not stops it.  Why C wrong: Increased insulin (not glucagon) is described.  Why D wrong: There is no mention of pancreatic atrophy."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Which hormone is responsible for the growth of the fetus?","options":["Growth hormone","Insulin","Thyroxine","Cortisol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the hormone responsible for growth of the fetus is insulin.  Why A wrong: The notes specify insulin, not maternal/fetal growth hormone.  Why C wrong: Thyroxine is not the stated growth hormone here.  Why D wrong: Cortisol is not identified as responsible for fetal growth."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Excess fetal insulin driven by maternal hyperglycemia causes excessive fetal growth. A fetal weight ≥ 4 kg is termed:","options":["Macrosomia","Microsomia","Intrauterine growth restriction","Oligohydramnios"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states ↑growth of fetus with weight ≥ 4 kg is called MACROSOMIA.  Why B wrong: Microsomia means abnormally small size, the opposite.  Why C wrong: IUGR is restricted growth, not excessive growth.  Why D wrong: Oligohydramnios refers to low amniotic fluid, not fetal weight."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"A fetus is considered macrosomic when its weight is:","options":["≥ 2.5 kg","≥ 3 kg","≥ 4 kg","≥ 5.5 kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines macrosomia as fetal weight ≥ 4 kg.  Why A, B, D wrong: These weight thresholds do not match the stated ≥ 4 kg cut-off for macrosomia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Regarding placental transfer in pregnancy, which statement is TRUE?","options":["Glucose is transported from mother to fetus, but maternal insulin does not cross the placenta","Both glucose and maternal insulin cross the placenta freely","Neither glucose nor insulin crosses the placenta","Maternal insulin crosses but glucose does not"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states glucose is transported from mother to fetus via placenta, while maternal insulin does not cross the placenta.  Why B wrong: Maternal insulin does NOT cross the placenta.  Why C wrong: Glucose does cross the placenta.  Why D wrong: It is the reverse — glucose crosses, insulin does not."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"What is the drug of choice (DOC) for diabetes in pregnancy?","options":["Metformin","Glyburide","Insulin","Glibenclamide"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the DOC for diabetes in pregnancy is insulin, because it cannot cross the placenta.  Why A, B, D wrong: These are oral hypoglycemic drugs; although metformin and glyburide can be used, insulin remains the DOC."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Why is insulin the preferred drug for diabetes in pregnancy?","options":["It is cheaper than oral drugs","It cannot cross the placenta (so it does not affect the fetus)","It crosses the placenta and controls fetal sugar directly","It prevents insulin resistance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states insulin is the DOC because it cannot cross the placenta.  Why A wrong: Cost is not the reason cited.  Why C wrong: Insulin does NOT cross the placenta — that is precisely why it is safe.  Why D wrong: Preventing insulin resistance is not the reason given."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Why are most oral hypoglycemic drugs generally NOT used in pregnancy?","options":["They can cross the placenta and cause fetal hypoglycemia","They cause maternal hyperglycemia","They increase amniotic fluid","They cannot be absorbed by pregnant women"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states oral hypoglycemic drugs can cross the placenta and can cause fetal hypoglycemia, therefore they are not used in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: The concern is fetal hypoglycemia, not maternal hyperglycemia.  Why C wrong: Amniotic fluid change is not the reason cited.  Why D wrong: Poor maternal absorption is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Which TWO oral hypoglycemic drugs can be used in pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Metformin","Glyburide","Glimepiride","Chlorpropamide","Pioglitazone"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states only 2 oral hypoglycemic drugs can be used in pregnancy — Metformin and Glyburide.  Why C, D, E wrong: Glimepiride, chlorpropamide, and pioglitazone are not among the two permitted oral agents listed."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"How many oral hypoglycemic drugs are stated to be usable in pregnancy?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states only 2 oral hypoglycemic drugs can be used in pregnancy (metformin and glyburide).  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated number is exactly 2, not 1, 3, or 4."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"The fasting blood sugar (FBS) test requires an overnight fast of how many hours?","options":["2–4 hours","8–10 hours","12–14 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states FBS requires an 8–10 hours overnight fast.  Why A, C, D wrong: These fasting durations do not match the stated 8–10 hour requirement for FBS."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"The post-prandial blood sugar level is measured at what time after a meal?","options":["30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours (2 hours pp value)","4 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states post-prandial blood sugar level is the 2 hours pp value.  Why A, B, D wrong: These timings do not match the stated 2-hour post-prandial value."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Random blood sugar level is measured:","options":["Only after 8 hours fasting","Irrespective of meals","Only 2 hours after breakfast","Only at midnight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states random blood sugar levels are measured irrespective of meals.  Why A wrong: Fasting is FBS, not random blood sugar.  Why C wrong: 2 hours after a meal is the post-prandial test.  Why D wrong: A fixed midnight timing is not required for random blood sugar."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Glycosylated hemoglobin (HbA1C) reflects the glucose state over approximately what period?","options":["Past 24 hours","Past 3 weeks","Past 6 months","Past 1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states HbA1C reflects the past 3 weeks glucose state.  Why A, C, D wrong: These durations do not match the stated \"past 3 weeks\" written in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"If glucose is present in the urine, the condition is termed:","options":["Ketonuria","Glycosuria","Proteinuria","Hematuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states if glucose is present in urine ⇒ glycosuria.  Why A wrong: Ketonuria is the presence of ketones, not glucose.  Why C wrong: Proteinuria is protein in urine.  Why D wrong: Hematuria is blood in urine."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Which of the following is a listed investigation done for diabetes in general? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fasting Blood Sugar (FBS)","Post-prandial blood sugar (2 hours pp)","Random blood sugar","Glucose tolerance test","Serum bilirubin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists FBS, post-prandial blood sugar, random blood sugar, glucose tolerance test, HbA1C, and urine glucose as diabetes investigations.  Why E wrong: Serum bilirubin is a liver test, not one of the listed diabetes investigations."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In a NON-pregnant female, child, or male, glycosuria is a/an:","options":["Normal finding","Abnormal finding that indicates diabetes","Sign of pregnancy","Harmless variant of no concern"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states glycosuria is an abnormal finding in non-pregnant females, children, and males, and indicates diabetes.  Why A wrong: It is normal only in pregnancy, not in these groups.  Why C wrong: Glycosuria is not a sign of pregnancy.  Why D wrong: In these groups it is significant (indicates diabetes), not harmless."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In a PREGNANT female, glycosuria is:","options":["Always indicative of diabetes","A normal finding that does not indicate diabetes","An indication for immediate delivery","A sign of macrosomia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in pregnancy glycosuria is a normal finding and it does not indicate diabetes.  Why A wrong: In pregnancy it does NOT indicate diabetes.  Why C wrong: It is not an indication for delivery.  Why D wrong: Glycosuria is not a sign of macrosomia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"To detect diabetes in a pregnant female, which test should be done?","options":["Urine glucose test","Blood test (never do urine glucose test)","Ketone strip test only","Ultrasound only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in a pregnant female, always do a blood test to detect diabetes and never do a urine glucose test (because glycosuria is normal in pregnancy).  Why A wrong: Urine glucose test is explicitly to be avoided in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: A ketone strip does not diagnose diabetes here.  Why D wrong: Ultrasound does not diagnose diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"There are 2 situations of diabetes in pregnancy. When a woman who is ALREADY diabetic conceives, this is termed:","options":["Gestational Diabetes Mellitus","Overt Diabetes","Type 2 Diabetes only","Transient neonatal diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a diabetic female who conceives has Overt Diabetes.  Why A wrong: GDM occurs when a non-diabetic woman becomes diabetic during pregnancy.  Why C wrong: The notes classify it as overt diabetes, not specifically type 2.  Why D wrong: Transient neonatal diabetes is a fetal condition, not this maternal category."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"When a woman WITHOUT prior diabetes conceives but becomes diabetic during pregnancy due to insulin resistance, this is called:","options":["Overt Diabetes","Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM)","Pre-gestational diabetes","Type 1 Diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a female without diabetes who becomes diabetic in pregnancy due to insulin resistance has Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM).  Why A wrong: Overt diabetes means diabetes existed before pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Pre-gestational diabetes describes overt diabetes, not GDM.  Why D wrong: Type 1 diabetes is not the category described for pregnancy-acquired diabetes here."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In overt diabetic patients (before pregnancy), what is the fasting blood sugar (FBS) criterion?","options":["≥ 100 mg%","≥ 126 mg%","≥ 140 mg%","≥ 200 mg%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in overt diabetic patients FBS is ≥ 126 mg%.  Why A, C wrong: These values do not match the stated FBS cut-off of ≥ 126 mg%.  Why D wrong: ≥ 200 mg% is the random blood sugar criterion, not FBS."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In overt diabetic patients (before pregnancy), the random blood sugar criterion is:","options":["≥ 126 mg%","≥ 140 mg%","≥ 200 mg%","≥ 250 mg%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states random blood sugar ≥ 200 mg% in overt diabetic patients.  Why A wrong: ≥ 126 mg% is the FBS criterion.  Why B wrong: 140 relates to GTT gestational cut-off, not random sugar for overt diabetes.  Why D wrong: ≥ 250 mg% is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In overt diabetic patients (before pregnancy), the HbA1C criterion is:","options":["≥ 5.0%","≥ 6.5%","≥ 7.5%","≥ 8.0%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states HbA1C ≥ 6.5% in overt diabetic patients.  Why A, C, D wrong: These percentages do not match the stated ≥ 6.5% HbA1C cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In a gestational diabetic patient, what are the FBS, RBS, and HbA1C values BEFORE pregnancy?","options":["All are raised","All are normal","Only HbA1C is raised","Only FBS is raised"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in a gestational diabetic patient before pregnancy, FBS, RBS, and HbA1C are all normal (she was not diabetic before pregnancy).  Why A wrong: They are normal, not raised, before pregnancy in GDM.  Why C, D wrong: None of these is raised before pregnancy in GDM; all are normal."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Regarding history of diabetes before pregnancy, which is correct?","options":["Present in overt diabetes; absent in gestational diabetes","Absent in overt diabetes; present in gestational diabetes","Present in both","Absent in both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The concepts table shows history of diabetes before pregnancy is present in overt diabetes and absent in gestational diabetes.  Why B wrong: It is the reverse — overt has the prior history, not GDM.  Why C, D wrong: History differs between the two; it is not present/absent in both."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In gestational diabetes, blood sugar levels are raised (due to insulin resistance) from approximately which gestational age onwards?","options":["12 weeks onwards","20 weeks onwards","24 weeks onwards (≥ 24 weeks)","36 weeks onwards"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in gestational diabetes, blood sugar levels are raised when there is insulin resistance, from ≥ 24 weeks / 24 weeks onwards.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the stated 24-weeks-onwards timing for GDM hyperglycemia."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In OVERT diabetes, blood sugar levels during pregnancy are:","options":["Normal in the 1st trimester, raised only in the 3rd","Raised throughout pregnancy (all trimesters and after delivery)","Raised only after delivery","Normal throughout pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The concepts table shows in overt diabetes, blood sugar is raised (↑) before pregnancy, day one, 1st, 2nd, 3rd trimester and after delivery — i.e., raised throughout pregnancy.  Why A wrong: In overt diabetes it is raised even in the 1st trimester.  Why C wrong: It is raised throughout, not only after delivery.  Why D wrong: It is raised, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In the concepts table, what is the blood sugar status in gestational diabetes during the 2nd trimester?","options":["Always raised","Normal or raised (↑)","Always normal","Undetectable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows gestational diabetes 2nd trimester = \"Normal or ↑\".  Why A wrong: It may be normal or raised, not always raised.  Why C wrong: It can be raised, so not always normal.  Why D wrong: \"Undetectable\" is not a table value."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In gestational diabetes, what is the blood sugar status AFTER delivery?","options":["Raised (↑)","Normal","Undetectable","Higher than in pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The concepts table shows gestational diabetes returns to Normal after delivery (once the insulin resistance of pregnancy resolves).  Why A wrong: In overt diabetes it stays raised after delivery, but in GDM it returns to normal.  Why C wrong: It is measurable and normal, not undetectable.  Why D wrong: It normalises rather than rising further."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In gestational diabetes, what is the blood sugar level in the 1st trimester?","options":["Raised","Normal","Undetectable","Always ketotic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The concepts table shows gestational diabetes 1st trimester = Normal (hyperglycemia only appears from ~24 weeks).  Why A wrong: It is normal in the 1st trimester in GDM (unlike overt where it is raised).  Why C, D wrong: These are not the table values."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In gestational diabetes, what is the blood sugar status in the 3rd trimester?","options":["Normal","Raised (↑)","Undetectable","Lower than before pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The concepts table shows gestational diabetes 3rd trimester = ↑ (raised), consistent with hyperglycemia from 24 weeks onward.  Why A wrong: By the 3rd trimester GDM sugars are raised, not normal.  Why C, D wrong: These are not table values."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Why does congenital malformation and 1st-trimester abortion occur in the fetus of an OVERT diabetic mother?","options":["Raised 1st-trimester blood sugar → free radical formation → congenital malformation / abortion","Low blood sugar in 3rd trimester","Excess insulin crossing the placenta","Normal 1st-trimester sugars protecting the fetus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows in overt diabetes, raised 1st-trimester blood sugar → increased glucose → free radicals → congenital malformation in fetus and 1st-trimester abortion.  Why B wrong: The mechanism is raised (not low) 1st-trimester sugar.  Why C wrong: The mechanism described is free radicals from hyperglycemia, not insulin crossing the placenta.  Why D wrong: Sugars are raised (abnormal) in the 1st trimester in overt diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Why does gestational diabetes NOT cause 1st-trimester congenital malformation or abortion?","options":["Because blood sugar is normal in the 1st trimester → no free radicals → no malformation/abortion","Because glucose does not cross the placenta","Because the fetus produces no insulin","Because maternal insulin protects the fetus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows in gestational diabetes, blood sugar is normal in the 1st trimester → no free radicals → no congenital malformation and no 1st-trimester abortion.  Why B wrong: Glucose does cross the placenta; the protective factor is normal 1st-trimester sugar.  Why C wrong: The fetus does produce insulin; that is not the reason given.  Why D wrong: Maternal insulin does not cross the placenta; it is not the protective mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"As per the national guidelines, at what point should the 75 gm 2-hour GTT be done in ALL pregnant females (Universal Test)?","options":["At the first antenatal visit","Only at 36 weeks","Only during labour","Only after delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in all pregnant females (Universal Test), do the 75 gm 2-hour GTT at the first antenatal visit as per national guidelines.  Why B, C, D wrong: The stated timing is the first antenatal visit, not 36 weeks, labour, or postpartum."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"For the 75 gm 2-hour glucose tolerance test used in pregnancy (national guidelines), is overnight fasting needed?","options":["Yes, 8–10 hours fasting is required","No, overnight fasting is not needed","Yes, 12 hours fasting is required","Only water fasting for 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page explicitly states overnight fasting is not needed; the test is done irrespective of previous meals.  Why A, C, D wrong: No fasting is required for this test, contrary to these options."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In the 75 gm 2-hour GTT (national guidelines), how much glucose is given and in how much water?","options":["50 gm glucose in 200 ml water","75 gm glucose in 300 ml of water","100 gm glucose in 250 ml water","75 gm glucose in 100 ml water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states give 75 gm glucose in 300 ml of water, then check blood glucose after 2 hours.  Why A, C, D wrong: These glucose amounts/water volumes do not match the stated 75 gm in 300 ml."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"After giving 75 gm glucose, blood glucose is checked after how many hours?","options":["1 hour","2 hours","3 hours","30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states after 2 hours check the blood glucose levels.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated timing for checking is 2 hours after the glucose load."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In the 75 gm 2-hour GTT, a 2-hour blood sugar value of < 140 is interpreted as:","options":["Overt diabetes","Gestational diabetes","Normal (repeat the test between 24–28 weeks)","Impaired fasting glucose"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows < 140 → Normal → repeat the test between 24–28 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Overt diabetes corresponds to ≥ 200.  Why B wrong: Gestational diabetes corresponds to 140–200.  Why D wrong: The notes classify < 140 simply as normal, not impaired fasting glucose."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"If the first-visit GTT is normal (< 140), when should the test be repeated?","options":["Between 24–28 weeks of pregnancy","Immediately after delivery","At 36–40 weeks","Never — one normal test is enough"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states if < 140 (normal), repeat the test between 24–28 weeks of pregnancy.  Why B, C wrong: These timings do not match the stated 24–28 week repeat window.  Why D wrong: A repeat is required because GDM typically appears around 24 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In the 75 gm 2-hour GTT, a 2-hour value of 140–200 indicates:","options":["Normal","Gestational Diabetes","Overt Diabetes","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows 140–200 → Gestational Diabetes.  Why A wrong: Normal is < 140.  Why C wrong: Overt diabetes is ≥ 200.  Why D wrong: 140–200 is hyperglycemic, not hypoglycemic."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In the 75 gm 2-hour GTT, a 2-hour value of ≥ 200 indicates:","options":["Normal","Gestational Diabetes","Overt Diabetes","Impaired glucose tolerance"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows ≥ 200 → Overt Diabetes.  Why A wrong: Normal is < 140.  Why B wrong: Gestational diabetes is 140–200.  Why D wrong: The notes label ≥ 200 as overt diabetes, not impaired glucose tolerance."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Overt diabetes in pregnancy means:","options":["Diabetes that develops for the first time after 24 weeks","The patient had diabetes before pregnancy (history present)","Diabetes that resolves after delivery","Glycosuria without hyperglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states overt diabetes means the patient has diabetes before pregnancy, with a history of diabetes before pregnancy present.  Why A wrong: Developing after 24 weeks describes gestational diabetes.  Why C wrong: Resolution after delivery is characteristic of gestational diabetes.  Why D wrong: Overt diabetes involves true hyperglycemia, not merely glycosuria."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"For overt diabetes, the 75 gm 2-hour GTT (post-prandial) value is:","options":["< 140","140–200","≥ 200","Normal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states for overt diabetes, the 75 gm 2 hrs GTT 2-hour post-prandial value is ≥ 200.  Why A wrong: < 140 is normal.  Why B wrong: 140–200 is gestational diabetes.  Why D wrong: Overt diabetes has an abnormal (≥ 200) value, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"Congenital malformation in the fetus is seen in which condition, and its chances depend on what?","options":["Seen in fetus of diabetic mother; chances depend on mother's blood glucose in the 1st trimester","Seen only in gestational diabetes; depends on 3rd-trimester sugar","Seen in all pregnancies; depends on maternal age","Seen in fetus of diabetic mother; depends on paternal glucose levels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states congenital malformation is seen in the fetus of a diabetic mother, and chances depend on the mother's blood glucose of the 1st trimester.  Why B wrong: Malformation relates to raised 1st-trimester glucose (overt diabetes), depending on 1st-trimester sugar, not 3rd-trimester.  Why C wrong: It is specific to the diabetic mother's fetus, not all pregnancies, and depends on maternal glucose, not age.  Why D wrong: It depends on the mother's (not father's) 1st-trimester blood glucose."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"The 75 gm 2-hour GTT screening for diabetes in all pregnant females is described as which type of test?","options":["Selective/high-risk test","Universal Test (done in all pregnant females)","Confirmatory test only after symptoms","Optional test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page labels it a Universal Test — done in all pregnant females as per national guidelines.  Why A wrong: It is universal, not limited to high-risk women.  Why C wrong: It is a screening test done regardless of symptoms.  Why D wrong: It is a recommended universal test, not optional."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"According to the concepts table, in overt diabetes the blood sugar level \"before pregnancy\" and on \"day one of pregnancy\" is:","options":["Normal in both","Raised (↑) in both","Normal before, raised on day one","Raised before, normal on day one"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The concepts table shows overt diabetes blood sugar is raised (↑) both before pregnancy and on day one of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: In overt diabetes it is raised, not normal.  Why C, D wrong: It is raised in both time points, not mixed."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"According to the concepts table, in overt diabetes the 1st trimester blood sugar level is:","options":["Normal","Raised (↑)","Undetectable","Lower than gestational diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows overt diabetes 1st trimester = ↑ (raised), which is why free radicals and congenital malformation occur.  Why A wrong: It is raised in the 1st trimester in overt diabetes (unlike GDM which is normal).  Why C, D wrong: These are not the table values."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"A pregnant woman at her first antenatal visit has a 75 gm 2-hour GTT result of 170 mg%. What is the diagnosis?","options":["Normal","Gestational Diabetes","Overt Diabetes","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 2-hour value of 170 falls in the 140–200 range → Gestational Diabetes.  Why A wrong: Normal requires < 140.  Why C wrong: Overt diabetes requires ≥ 200.  Why D wrong: 170 is hyperglycemic, not hypoglycemic."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"A pregnant woman at her first antenatal visit has a 75 gm 2-hour GTT value of 130 mg%. What should be the next step?","options":["Diagnose overt diabetes and start insulin","Consider it normal and repeat the test between 24–28 weeks","Diagnose gestational diabetes","Repeat the test after delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A value < 140 is normal; per the flowchart, repeat the test between 24–28 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 130 is normal, not overt diabetes (which is ≥ 200).  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes needs 140–200.  Why D wrong: The repeat is scheduled at 24–28 weeks, not after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"According to the notes, glucose transported to the fetus in maternal hyperglycemia ultimately leads to which fetal complication (excess growth)?","options":["Intrauterine growth restriction","Macrosomia (fetal weight ≥ 4 kg)","Anencephaly","Oligohydramnios"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows raised maternal glucose → fetal hyperglycemia → ↑fetal insulin → ↑growth → macrosomia (weight ≥ 4 kg).  Why A wrong: The result is excess growth (macrosomia), not restriction.  Why C wrong: Anencephaly is a congenital malformation, not the excess-growth outcome described here.  Why D wrong: Oligohydramnios is unrelated to this growth pathway."},{"section":"Batch 044 · Abruptio placentae management…","question":"In gestational diabetes, blood sugar levels rise specifically because of which underlying mechanism appearing around 24 weeks?","options":["Insulin resistance","Loss of pancreatic beta cells","Reduced Human Placental Lactogen","Fetal insulin crossing to the mother"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The concepts table states gestational diabetic patients' blood sugar levels are raised when there is insulin resistance, from ≥ 24 weeks onwards.  Why B wrong: The mechanism described is insulin resistance, not beta-cell loss.  Why C wrong: HPL (which causes resistance) rises in pregnancy; reduced HPL is not the mechanism.  Why D wrong: Fetal insulin does not cross to the mother (maternal insulin does not cross the placenta either)."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"A diabetic female who has conceived comes for antenatal advice and wants to know the chances of congenital malformation in her fetus. Which test best predicts this risk?","options":["Random blood sugar","HbA1c","Fasting blood sugar","Oral glucose tolerance test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HbA1c reflects average glucose control; more glucose → more HbA1c → more chances of congenital malformation, so HbA1c is the best predictive test.  Why A wrong: Random blood sugar gives only a single point value, not average control.  Why C wrong: Fasting blood sugar reflects only fasting state, not longer-term control.  Why D wrong: OGTT diagnoses diabetes/GDM but does not predict malformation risk best."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"On checking HbA1c in a pregnant diabetic woman, which value indicates that chances of congenital malformation are LESS?","options":["HbA1c > 6.5%","HbA1c < 6.5%","HbA1c > 7.5%","HbA1c = 8%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If HbA1c < 6.5%, chances of congenital malformation are less; > 6.5% chances are more.  Why A wrong: > 6.5% indicates more chances of malformation.  Why C wrong: > 7.5% is high, indicating more chances.  Why D wrong: 8% is high (> 6.5%), indicating more chances."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the relationship between glucose, HbA1c and congenital malformation in a diabetic mother?","options":["More glucose → more HbA1c → more chances of congenital malformation","More glucose → less HbA1c → more malformation","Less glucose → more HbA1c → more malformation","Glucose has no relation to HbA1c"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"More is the glucose ~ more is HbA1c ~ more are chances of congenital malformation.\"  Why B wrong: Higher glucose raises (not lowers) HbA1c.  Why C wrong: Less glucose lowers HbA1c.  Why D wrong: Glucose directly determines HbA1c."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Which is the best investigation to DETECT congenital malformation in the fetus of a diabetic mother?","options":["HbA1c","Level 2 USG / Targeted scan / Anomaly scan (TIFFA)","Non-stress test","Amniocentesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Best test to detect congenital malformation is Level 2 USG/Targeted Scan/Anomaly scan/TIFFA (Targeted Imaging For fetal Anomaly).  Why A wrong: HbA1c predicts risk, does not detect the malformation.  Why C wrong: NST assesses fetal well-being, not structural anomalies.  Why D wrong: Amniocentesis is used for karyotyping/genetic testing, not the best anomaly-detection scan here."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"At what gestational age is the Level 2 USG / anomaly scan (TIFFA) done to detect congenital malformation?","options":["11–13 weeks","18–20 weeks","24–28 weeks","32–36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The anomaly scan/TIFFA is done between 18–20 weeks.  Why A wrong: 11–13 weeks is the NT scan window, not the anomaly scan.  Why C, D wrong: These are later windows, not the standard anomaly-scan timing."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Which body system is MOST commonly involved in congenital malformation of a diabetic mother's fetus?","options":["CNS > CVS","CVS > CNS","Renal system","Gastrointestinal system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most common system involved is CVS > CNS.  Why A wrong: Order is reversed; CVS is more common than CNS.  Why C, D wrong: Renal and GI are not the most common systems involved."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the MOST common congenital malformation in the fetus of a diabetic mother?","options":["Neural tube defect","Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","Sacral agenesis","Cleft palate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most common congenital malformation is Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD) > Neural Tube Defect (NTD).  Why A wrong: NTD is second most common, not the most common.  Why C wrong: Sacral agenesis is the most specific, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Cleft palate is not listed as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Which is the MOST SPECIFIC congenital malformation associated with maternal diabetes?","options":["Ventricular septal defect","Neural tube defect","Sacral agenesis (caudal regression syndrome)","Polycoria"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Most specific congenital malformation is Sacral Agenesis (Caudal Regression Syndrome).  Why A wrong: VSD is the most common, not most specific.  Why B wrong: NTD is common but not the most specific marker of diabetic embryopathy.  Why D wrong: Polycoria is a fetal effect of hyperglycemia, not a specific structural malformation."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Sacral agenesis in a diabetic mother's fetus is also known as:","options":["Caudal regression syndrome","VACTERL syndrome","Potter syndrome","Pierre Robin syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sacral agenesis is the Caudal Regression Syndrome.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are unrelated syndromes not equated with sacral agenesis in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Which measure helps PREVENT congenital malformation in babies of diabetic mothers?","options":["Strict control over glucose (keep glucose levels normal)","Starting oral hypoglycemic drugs","Bed rest throughout pregnancy","Restricting fluids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Strict control over glucose (keeping glucose levels normal) is a listed prevention strategy.  Why B wrong: Oral hypoglycemics have no role in overt diabetes management here.  Why C, D wrong: Bed rest and fluid restriction are not prevention measures for malformation."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the recommended folic acid dose to prevent congenital malformation in babies of diabetic mothers?","options":["100 mcg/day","200 mcg/day","400 mcg/day","5 mg/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Folic acid supplementation = 400 mcg/day is recommended.  Why A, B wrong: Doses lower than the stated 400 mcg/day.  Why D wrong: 5 mg is a higher therapeutic dose, not the value in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"When should folic acid supplementation be started and continued for prevention of congenital malformation in a diabetic mother?","options":["Start 1 month before pregnancy and continue till 1st trimester","Start after confirming pregnancy till delivery","Start only in 2nd trimester","Start at 24 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Folic acid should be started 1 month before pregnancy and continued till the 1st trimester.  Why B wrong: It must begin before conception, not after confirmation.  Why C, D wrong: Starting later misses the critical neural-tube closure period."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the treatment of overt diabetes in pregnancy?","options":["Diet modification only","Oral hypoglycemic drugs only","Only INSULIN","Exercise only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In overt diabetes there is no role of diet modification and no role of oral hypoglycemic drugs; give only INSULIN (treatment of choice / drug of choice).  Why A wrong: Diet modification has no role in overt diabetes.  Why B wrong: Oral hypoglycemic drugs have no role in overt diabetes in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Exercise alone has no role in overt diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In an overt diabetic already on insulin who conceives, what happens to the insulin requirement during the second and third trimesters?","options":["Decreases","Increases","Remains unchanged","Insulin is stopped"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The dose of insulin increases in the second and third trimester.  Why A wrong: Requirement rises, not falls, due to increasing insulin resistance.  Why C wrong: It does not remain unchanged across these trimesters.  Why D wrong: Insulin is continued and increased, not stopped."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Why is the insulin dose NOT increased in the 1st trimester of pregnancy?","options":["Because insulin resistance is highest then","Because increasing the dose in the mother increases the chances of hypoglycemia","Because the fetus produces its own insulin","Because glucose is not absorbed in the 1st trimester"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the 1st trimester if the dose is increased in the mother, chances of hypoglycemia are increased, so it is not increased.  Why A wrong: Insulin resistance rises later (2nd/3rd trimester), not the 1st.  Why C wrong: Not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Glucose absorption is normal; not the reason given."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), the patient's blood glucose levels are normal at which time?","options":["Before pregnancy and in the 1st trimester","From 24 weeks onwards","Throughout pregnancy","Only in the 3rd trimester"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In GDM, blood glucose is normal before pregnancy and in the 1st trimester.  Why B wrong: Sugar levels rise from 24 weeks onwards, so they are not normal then.  Why C wrong: They become abnormal later in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Levels are abnormal (raised), not normal, in the 3rd trimester."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"From which gestational age do sugar levels typically increase in gestational diabetes mellitus?","options":["12 weeks","20 weeks","24 weeks onwards","32 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In GDM, sugar levels increase from 24 weeks onwards.  Why A, B wrong: Earlier than the stated onset.  Why D wrong: Later than the stated onset of 24 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In the 75 gm glucose tolerance test used for GDM, the diagnostic 2-hour post-prandial value lies between:","options":["100 – 120 mg%","120 – 140 mg%","140 – 200 mg%","200 – 250 mg%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: With the 75 gm glucose tolerance test, the 2-hour PP value is between 140 – 200 mg%.  Why A, B wrong: Below the stated diagnostic range.  Why D wrong: Above the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Regarding congenital malformation in babies born to mothers with gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), which statement is correct?","options":["There is high risk of congenital malformation","There is no risk of congenital malformation in babies of GDM","Risk is same as overt diabetes","Only cardiac malformations occur"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state there is NO risk of congenital malformation in babies of GDM (because glucose is normal in the 1st trimester when organogenesis occurs).  Why A wrong: There is no risk, not high risk.  Why C wrong: Overt diabetes carries the malformation risk; GDM does not.  Why D wrong: There is no malformation risk of any kind in GDM babies per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the FIRST step in the management of gestational diabetes mellitus?","options":["Start insulin immediately","Diet modification + lifestyle modification","Oral metformin","Cesarean section"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 1st step in GDM management is diet modification + lifestyle modification.  Why A wrong: Insulin is started only if diet fails to achieve targets.  Why C wrong: Metformin is given only if the patient cannot take insulin after diet failure.  Why D wrong: Delivery mode is not part of initial glycemic management."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In the management of GDM, what is the target fasting blood sugar (FBS)?","options":["< 95 mg%","< 120 mg%","< 140 mg%","< 6%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The aim for FBS is < 95 mg%.  Why B wrong: < 120 mg% is the 2-hour PP target, not FBS.  Why C wrong: 140 mg% is a diagnostic GTT threshold, not the FBS aim.  Why D wrong: < 6% is the HbA1c target, not FBS."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In GDM management, what is the target 2-hour post-prandial glucose value?","options":["< 95 mg%","< 120 mg%","< 140 mg%","< 200 mg%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The aim for 2-hour PP value is < 120 mg%.  Why A wrong: < 95 mg% is the FBS target.  Why C, D wrong: These are higher than the stated post-prandial target."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the target HbA1c in the management of GDM?","options":["< 6%","< 6.5%","< 7%","< 8%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The aim for HbA1c in GDM management is < 6%.  Why B, C, D wrong: All are higher than the stated target of < 6%."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"For how long is diet modification tried in GDM before deciding on insulin, and what happens if the aim is achieved?","options":["2 weeks; if aim achieved, continue on diet (no need of insulin)","6 weeks; then always start insulin","24 hours; then start insulin","3 months; then perform cesarean"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diet modification is done for 2 weeks; if the aim is achieved, continue the patient on diet with no need for insulin.  Why B wrong: The trial period is 2 weeks, and insulin is not mandatory if targets are met.  Why C, D wrong: Neither the duration nor the action matches the notes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In a GDM patient, if the glycemic aim is NOT achieved after 2 weeks of diet modification, the next step is to:","options":["Continue diet for another 2 weeks","Put the patient on insulin","Start oral hypoglycemic drugs first","Deliver immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the aim is not achieved in 2 weeks, put the patient on insulin.  Why A wrong: Diet is not simply prolonged once it has failed.  Why C wrong: Insulin (not oral drugs) is the next step; metformin is reserved for those who cannot take insulin.  Why D wrong: Delivery is not the response to failed glycemic targets."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Which statement about OVERT diabetes in pregnancy is TRUE (per the \"Remember\" comparison)?","options":["Role of diet is present","Only treatment is insulin","Exercise plays a major role","Oral hypoglycemic drugs are the mainstay"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In overt diabetes there is no role of diet, no role of exercise, and no role of oral hypoglycemic drugs — only treatment is insulin.  Why A wrong: Diet has no role in overt diabetes.  Why C wrong: Exercise has no role in overt diabetes.  Why D wrong: Oral hypoglycemic drugs have no role in overt diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Which statement about GESTATIONAL diabetes is TRUE (per the \"Remember\" comparison)?","options":["No role of diet","No role of exercise","Insulin is given only if diet fails","Insulin is the first-line therapy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In gestational diabetes, role of diet and exercise are present, and insulin is given only if diet fails.  Why A, B wrong: Diet and exercise both have a role in GDM.  Why D wrong: Insulin is second-line (after diet fails), not first-line."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In a GDM patient in whom diet has failed and who says she cannot take insulin, which oral drug is given?","options":["Glibenclamide","Metformin","Sitagliptin","Pioglitazone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient says she cannot take insulin, give oral metformin.  Why A, C, D wrong: These oral agents are not the one specified in the notes; metformin is the named option."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the drug of choice (DOC) for diabetes in pregnancy — including overt diabetes and GDM?","options":["Metformin","Insulin","Glyburide","Acarbose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The DOC for diabetes in pregnancy, for overt diabetes in pregnancy, and for GDM in pregnancy is INSULIN.  Why A wrong: Metformin is only a fallback when a GDM patient cannot take insulin.  Why C, D wrong: Not the drug of choice named in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In diet modification for a GDM patient, carbohydrates should constitute what percentage of the diet?","options":["20%","30 – 40%","40 – 50%","< 10%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Carbohydrates = 40 – 50% of the diet.  Why A wrong: 20% is the protein proportion.  Why B wrong: 30–40% is the fat proportion.  Why D wrong: < 10% is the saturated fat limit."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What proportion of a GDM patient's diet should be protein?","options":["10%","20%","40%","50%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protein = 20% of the diet.  Why A, C, D wrong: Do not match the stated 20% protein value."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In the recommended GDM diet, fat should constitute what percentage?","options":["20%","30 – 40%","40 – 50%","< 10%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fat = 30 – 40% of the diet.  Why A wrong: 20% is protein.  Why C wrong: 40–50% is carbohydrate.  Why D wrong: < 10% is the saturated-fat limit."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In the GDM diet, saturated fats should be limited to:","options":["< 10%","20%","30%","40%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Saturated fats should be < 10% of the diet.  Why B, C, D wrong: All exceed the < 10% limit specified."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"How should meals be distributed in a GDM patient's diet?","options":["3 major meals + 3 minor meals","2 major meals only","6 equal large meals","1 major meal + snacks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diet consists of 3 major meals + 3 minor meals.  Why B, C, D wrong: None match the specified 3 major + 3 minor pattern."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the general caloric requirement for a diabetic pregnant woman?","options":["15 – 20 Kcal/kg/day","25 – 30 Kcal/kg/day","35 – 40 Kcal/kg/day","45 – 50 Kcal/kg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Caloric requirement for a diabetic (pregnant) = 25 – 30 Kcal/kg/day.  Why A, C, D wrong: Do not match the stated 25–30 Kcal/kg/day."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"For an OBESE diabetic pregnant woman, the recommended daily calorie intake is:","options":["800 – 1000 kcal/day","1200 – 1800 kcal/day","2000 – 2500 kcal/day","2500 – 3000 kcal/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For obese patients, 1200 – 1800 kcal/day is recommended.  Why A wrong: Below the stated range.  Why C wrong: 2000–2500 kcal/day is for non-obese patients.  Why D wrong: Above the stated ranges."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"For a NON-OBESE diabetic pregnant woman, the recommended daily calorie intake is:","options":["1200 – 1800 kcal/day","2000 – 2500 kcal/day","1000 – 1500 kcal/day","3000 – 3500 kcal/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For non-obese patients, 2000 – 2500 kcal/day is recommended.  Why A wrong: 1200–1800 kcal/day is for obese patients.  Why C, D wrong: Do not match the stated non-obese range."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the role of exercise in a GDM patient (nursing implication)?","options":["Exercise is contraindicated","Exercise is essential","Exercise only after delivery","Exercise increases risk of malformation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing implication #2 states exercise is essential.  Why A wrong: Exercise is not contraindicated; it is essential.  Why C, D wrong: Not supported by the notes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"How is self-monitoring of glucose advised for a GDM patient, and how often?","options":["By glucometer, 4–5 times a day","By lab sampling once a week","By urine dipstick twice a day","By HbA1c daily"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Self-monitoring of glucose is done by glucometer, 4–5 times a day.  Why B, C wrong: Not the method/frequency stated.  Why D wrong: HbA1c is not measured daily; it reflects long-term control."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"When is HbA1c measured in a GDM patient during antenatal monitoring?","options":["At the end of the 1st trimester and 3 months afterwards","Every week","Only at delivery","Daily by glucometer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HbA1c measurement is done at the end of the 1st trimester and 3 months afterwards.  Why B, C, D wrong: These frequencies/timings do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Diabetic patients have an increased chance of which infections, which can lead to preterm labor?","options":["UTI / Vaginitis","Meningitis","Osteomyelitis","Endocarditis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In diabetic patients there are increased chances of infections (UTI/Vaginitis), which can lead to preterm labor.  Why B, C, D wrong: These infections are not the ones associated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Why is daily fetal movement count especially emphasized in diabetic patients?","options":["Increased chances of intrauterine death of the fetus","Increased chances of hyperglycemia in mother","To detect congenital malformation","To confirm the due date"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In diabetic patients there are increased chances of intrauterine death of the fetus, so daily fetal movement count is stressed.  Why B wrong: Movement count monitors fetal well-being, not maternal glucose.  Why C wrong: Malformation is detected by anomaly scan, not movement count.  Why D wrong: Movement count does not confirm dates."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In which position should a diabetic pregnant woman lie to count fetal movements?","options":["Supine","Left lateral position","Right lateral position","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: She should lie in the left lateral position for fetal movement counting.  Why A wrong: Supine may cause aortocaval compression; not advised.  Why C, D wrong: Not the specified position (left lateral is stated)."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"When should a diabetic pregnant woman count fetal movements according to the daily fetal movement count instructions?","options":["After breakfast, after lunch and after dinner","Only at night","Only in the morning","Every hour continuously"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Count fetal movements after breakfast, after lunch and after dinner.  Why B, C wrong: Counting is tied to the three main meals, not a single time.  Why D wrong: Continuous hourly counting is not the instruction."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"For how long at a time should fetal movements be counted, and what should be done with the record?","options":["Count for 1 hour and maintain a chart","Count for 10 minutes only","Count for 24 hours continuously","Count for 5 minutes without recording"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Count fetal movement for 1 hour and maintain a chart.  Why B, C, D wrong: Neither the duration nor the charting matches the notes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In ALL pregnant females, including overt diabetes and GDM, at what gestation is a Level 2 USG done?","options":["11–13 weeks","18–20 weeks","24–28 weeks","32–36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In all pregnant females (overt + GDM), Level 2 USG is done at 18–20 weeks.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 18–20 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Because diabetes is a high-risk pregnancy, antepartum fetal monitoring is started from which gestational age?","options":["20 – 24 weeks","28 – 32 weeks","34 – 36 weeks","37 – 40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetes is high risk in pregnancy, so antepartum fetal monitoring begins from 28–32 weeks.  Why A, C, D wrong: Do not match the stated 28–32 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Which tests make up the weekly antepartum fetal monitoring in a diabetic pregnancy?","options":["NST and biophysical score, done weekly","HbA1c and FBS, done weekly","Anomaly scan, done weekly","Amniocentesis, done weekly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antepartum fetal monitoring includes NST and biophysical score, done weekly.  Why B wrong: These are glycemic tests, not fetal-monitoring tests.  Why C, D wrong: Not the weekly monitoring tools specified."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"At what gestational age are diabetic patients generally delivered (labor induced)?","options":["34 – 36 weeks","37 – 38 weeks","39 – 40 weeks","41 – 42 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetic patients are delivered at 39–40 weeks by inducing labor.  Why A, B wrong: Earlier than the stated timing.  Why D wrong: Later than the stated 39–40 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the BEST mode of delivery in a diabetic pregnancy?","options":["Elective cesarean section","Vaginal delivery","Forceps delivery","Vacuum extraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best mode of delivery is vaginal delivery.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is reserved for specific indications (e.g., macrosomia), not routine.  Why C, D wrong: Instrumental deliveries are not the stated best mode."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In a diabetic female, what estimated fetal weight is an indication for cesarean section?","options":["≥ 3.0 kg","≥ 3.5 kg","≥ 4.0 kg","≥ 4.5 kg"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Cesarean section is indicated if in a diabetic female the weight of the fetus is ≥ 4.5 kg.  Why A, B wrong: Below the cesarean threshold.  Why C wrong: ≥ 4 kg defines macrosomia but the cesarean indication here is ≥ 4.5 kg."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Macrosomia is defined as a fetal weight of:","options":["≥ 2.5 kg","≥ 3 kg","≥ 4 kg","≥ 5 kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Macrosomia = fetal weight ≥ 4 kg.  Why A, B wrong: Below the macrosomia definition.  Why D wrong: Higher than the stated ≥ 4 kg threshold."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"After delivery, a diabetic mother must be watched carefully for which increased risk?","options":["Postpartum hemorrhage","Deep vein thrombosis","Puerperal psychosis","Mastitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After delivery there are increased chances of postpartum hemorrhage, so careful monitoring is needed.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the complication emphasized in the notes after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In a woman with gestational diabetes whose blood glucose becomes normal after delivery, what follow-up test should be done at term after 6 weeks?","options":["HbA1c only","Glucose tolerance test (GTT)","Fasting insulin","Random blood sugar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In gestational diabetes, glucose normalizes after delivery, so a GTT is done at term after 6 weeks of delivery.  Why A wrong: The notes specify a GTT, not HbA1c alone.  Why C, D wrong: Not the specified follow-up test."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Maternal hyperglycemia in diabetes increases the chances of which maternal complication?","options":["Infection (UTI, Vaginitis)","Hypothyroidism","Anemia","Rh isoimmunization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal hyperglycemia = increased chances of infection (UTI, Vaginitis).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not listed as maternal complications of diabetes here."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Maternal hyperglycemia leads to fetal hyperglycemia, then polycoria (excess fetal urination), which results in which maternal complication?","options":["Oligohydramnios","Polyhydramnios (increased amniotic fluid)","Placenta previa","Abruptio placentae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal hyperglycemia → polycoria → increased amniotic fluid = polyhydramnios.  Why A wrong: Amniotic fluid increases (polyhydramnios), it is not decreased (oligohydramnios).  Why C, D wrong: These placental disorders are not the pathway described."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Polyhydramnios in a diabetic pregnancy can lead to which two complications?","options":["Preterm labor and PROM","Post-term pregnancy and IUGR","Anemia and hypotension","Hypothyroidism and goiter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Polyhydramnios can lead to preterm labor and PROM (premature rupture of membranes).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the complications listed for polyhydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Diabetes in pregnancy increases the chances of which hypertensive complication?","options":["Pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH)","Portal hypertension","Pulmonary hypertension","Renal artery stenosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There are increased chances of PIH (Pregnancy-Induced Hypertension).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the hypertensive complications listed for diabetic pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"After delivery, the increased chance of postpartum hemorrhage in a diabetic mother is due to:","options":["Overdistended uterus","Retained placenta only","Coagulation factor deficiency","Cervical laceration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After delivery, due to an overdistended uterus (from polyhydramnios/macrosomia), there are increased chances of PPH.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the mechanism given in the notes for PPH here."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What percentage of women with GDM may develop overt diabetes in the future?","options":["10%","25%","50%","75%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In 50% of females, in the future they can have overt diabetes (after GDM).  Why A, B, D wrong: Do not match the stated 50%."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the sequence leading to macrosomia in the fetus of a diabetic mother?","options":["Fetal hyperglycemia → fetal pancreas produces more insulin → more growth of fetus → macrosomia","Fetal hypoglycemia → decreased insulin → macrosomia","Maternal hypoglycemia → fetal growth restriction","Decreased insulin → increased fat breakdown → macrosomia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal hyperglycemia → fetal pancreas produces more insulin → more growth of fetus → macrosomia.  Why B wrong: The fetus is hyperglycemic with increased (not decreased) insulin.  Why C wrong: The mother is hyperglycemic, causing fetal overgrowth, not restriction.  Why D wrong: Insulin (an anabolic/growth hormone) is increased, driving growth."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In a macrosomic baby of a diabetic mother, excessive weight deposited around the shoulders can result in:","options":["Shoulder dystocia","Erb's palsy only","Cleft lip","Talipes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In macrosomic babies excessive weight is deposited around the shoulders, so it can result in shoulder dystocia.  Why B wrong: Erb's palsy may follow shoulder dystocia but is not the direct point stated.  Why C, D wrong: Not related to macrosomic shoulder weight deposition."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Shoulder dystocia is best defined as:","options":["Difficulty in delivery of the shoulders","Difficulty in delivery of the head","Obstruction of the umbilical cord","Rupture of the uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Shoulder dystocia is difficulty in delivery of the shoulders; macrosomia can lead to it.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe different obstetric problems."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Diabetes in pregnancy increases the chances of which fetal outcomes?","options":["Stillbirth and intrauterine death","Anemia and mental retardation","Post-maturity and IUGR","Cleft palate only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There are increased chances of stillbirth and intrauterine death in the fetus.  Why B wrong: Diabetic mother babies do NOT get anemia or mental retardation.  Why C, D wrong: Not the fetal complications listed."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Increased chance of 1st trimester abortion in diabetes occurs in which type?","options":["Only in gestational diabetes","Only in overt diabetes","In both equally","In neither"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Only in overt diabetes is there increased chance of 1st trimester abortion (because glucose is abnormal early in overt diabetes but normal early in GDM).  Why A wrong: GDM has normal 1st-trimester glucose, so no such increased risk.  Why C, D wrong: The risk is specific to overt diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Both gestational and overt diabetes increase the chance of intrauterine death of the fetus. Intrauterine death is defined as death of the fetus:","options":["Before 12 weeks","After 20 weeks of pregnancy","During the 1st trimester","Within 7 days of birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intrauterine death = death of fetus after 20 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A, C wrong: Death before 20 weeks would be abortion, not intrauterine death here.  Why D wrong: That describes early neonatal death, not intrauterine death."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"As described in the notes, stillbirth refers to death of the fetus:","options":["During delivery of the fetus","Before 20 weeks","Within 28 days of birth","At implantation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define increased chances of still birth as death during delivery of the fetus.  Why B, C, D wrong: Do not match the notes' description of stillbirth as death during delivery."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"A neonate born to a diabetic mother is at risk of respiratory distress syndrome mainly because of:","options":["Preterm labor leading to premature immature lungs","Excess surfactant production","Meconium aspiration","Congenital heart disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Due to preterm labor the baby is premature → lungs will not be mature → respiratory distress syndrome.  Why B wrong: Immature lungs have deficient (not excess) surfactant.  Why C, D wrong: Not the mechanism described for RDS here."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"What is the classic metabolic complication seen in a neonate born to a diabetic mother?","options":["Hyperglycemia","Hypoglycemia","Ketoacidosis","Hypernatremia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the neonate the complication is HYPOGLYCEMIA — maternal glucose↑ → fetal glucose↑ → fetal pancreas produces ↑insulin; at birth the glucose source is gone but the increased insulin remains, causing hypoglycemia.  Why A wrong: The neonate becomes hypoglycemic after birth despite in-utero hyperglycemia.  Why C, D wrong: Not the classic described complication."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Why does a neonate of a diabetic mother become hypoglycemic at the time of birth?","options":["The connection between mother and fetus is gone, so the source of increased glucose is gone, but the increased insulin remains in the baby's body","The neonatal pancreas stops producing insulin","The baby has decreased insulin at birth","Maternal glucose continues to rise after birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At birth the mother–fetus connection is gone → source of increased glucose gone → what remains in the baby's body is increased insulin → hypoglycemia.  Why B, C wrong: The baby has high (not stopped/decreased) insulin.  Why D wrong: The maternal glucose supply is cut off at birth."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Which electrolyte abnormalities are seen in the neonate of a diabetic mother?","options":["Hypokalemia, hypocalcemia and hypomagnesemia","Hyperkalemia, hypercalcemia and hypermagnesemia","Hypernatremia and hyperkalemia","Hyperphosphatemia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The neonate shows decreased K+ (hypokalemia), decreased Ca++ (hypocalcemia) and decreased Mg (hypomagnesemia).  Why B, C, D wrong: The levels are decreased, giving hypo- states, not the elevated states listed."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Decreased potassium levels in the neonate of a diabetic mother is termed:","options":["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypomagnesemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ↓K+ levels = hypokalemia.  Why A wrong: Hyperkalemia is high potassium.  Why C wrong: Hypocalcemia is low calcium.  Why D wrong: Hypomagnesemia is low magnesium."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Decreased calcium levels in the neonate of a diabetic mother is termed:","options":["Hypocalcemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypokalemia","Hypomagnesemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ↓Ca++ levels = hypocalcemia.  Why B wrong: Hypercalcemia is high calcium.  Why C, D wrong: Refer to potassium and magnesium, not calcium."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Decreased magnesium levels in the neonate of a diabetic mother is termed:","options":["Hypermagnesemia","Hypomagnesemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypokalemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ↓Mg levels = hypomagnesemia.  Why A wrong: Hypermagnesemia is high magnesium.  Why C, D wrong: Refer to calcium and potassium."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Hyperviscosity syndrome in the neonate of a diabetic mother includes which findings?","options":["Polycythemia (↑Hb) and hyperbilirubinemia","Anemia and thrombocytopenia","Leukopenia and hypoglycemia","Hypocoagulability and bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperviscosity syndrome features polycythemia (↑Hb) and hyperbilirubinemia.  Why B wrong: There is polycythemia (raised Hb), not anemia.  Why C, D wrong: Not the components of hyperviscosity syndrome described."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"Which of the following do we NOT get in babies of diabetic mothers? (Select all that apply)","options":["Anemia","Mental retardation","Hyperglycemia","Hypoglycemia","Polycythemia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: In diabetic mother babies we do NOT get anemia, mental retardation, or hyperglycemia (the neonate develops hypoglycemia, not hyperglycemia, and has polycythemia not anemia).  Why D wrong: Hypoglycemia IS seen in the neonate, so it is not an answer.  Why E wrong: Polycythemia IS seen (hyperviscosity syndrome), so it is not an answer."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"During pregnancy, the diabetic mother and her fetus are both in which glycemic state?","options":["Hyperglycemia","Hypoglycemia","Normoglycemia","Ketoacidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetic mother = hyperglycemia and fetus = hyperglycemia (maternal glucose crosses to the fetus). The neonate later develops hypoglycemia after birth.  Why B wrong: Hypoglycemia occurs in the neonate after birth, not in the mother/fetus during pregnancy.  Why C, D wrong: Not the states described."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"The classification used for gestational diabetes is called:","options":["White classification","Priscilla White score","Modified Bishop score","Bethesda classification"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gestational diabetes classification is called the WHITE classification.  Why B wrong: Not the term used in the notes.  Why C wrong: Bishop score assesses cervical readiness for induction.  Why D wrong: Bethesda is a cytology (Pap smear) classification."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In the White classification of gestational diabetes, GDM controlled on DIET belongs to which category?","options":["A1 category","A2 category","B category","C category"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GDM controlled on diet = A1 category.  Why B wrong: A2 is GDM controlled on insulin.  Why C, D wrong: Not the categories specified for GDM control in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 045 · Diabetes in pregnancy — congenital malformation risk & HbA1c…","question":"In the White classification of gestational diabetes, GDM controlled on INSULIN belongs to which category?","options":["A1 category","A2 category","B category","F category"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GDM controlled on insulin = A2 category.  Why A wrong: A1 is GDM controlled on diet.  Why C, D wrong: Not the categories specified for GDM control in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In overt (pre-gestational) diabetes, what happens to blood sugar during the 1st trimester of pregnancy?","options":["Blood sugar is decreased","Blood sugar is increased","Blood sugar remains normal","Blood sugar is unaffected by trimester"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In overt diabetes the diabetes predates pregnancy, so blood sugar is already increased in the 1st trimester.  Why A wrong: Blood sugar is not decreased in overt diabetes.  Why C wrong: Normal 1st-trimester blood sugar is characteristic of gestational, not overt, diabetes.  Why D wrong: The distinction between overt and gestational diabetes is precisely defined by the 1st-trimester blood sugar level."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In gestational diabetes, blood sugar during the 1st trimester is typically:","options":["Increased","Normal","Undetectable","Dangerously low"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gestational diabetes develops later in pregnancy, so blood sugar is normal in the 1st trimester.  Why A wrong: Increased 1st-trimester sugar indicates overt diabetes.  Why C wrong: Blood sugar is measurable and simply normal.  Why D wrong: Gestational diabetes causes hyperglycaemia later, not first-trimester hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Congenital malformation in babies is a feature of which condition?","options":["Gestational diabetes only","Overt diabetes","Neither overt nor gestational diabetes","Only well-controlled diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In overt diabetes, high blood sugar is present during early organogenesis (1st trimester), so congenital malformation in babies is present.  Why A wrong: In gestational diabetes congenital malformation is absent because 1st-trimester sugar is normal.  Why C wrong: Overt diabetes does cause malformations.  Why D wrong: The determinant is the timing of hyperglycaemia (1st trimester), not merely control status as stated on the page."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Congenital malformation in babies is ABSENT in which condition?","options":["Overt diabetes","Gestational diabetes","Type 1 diabetes before conception","Diabetes with 1st-trimester hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In gestational diabetes blood sugar is normal during 1st-trimester organogenesis, so congenital malformation in babies is absent.  Why A wrong: Overt diabetes has malformations present.  Why C wrong: Pre-conception (overt) diabetes carries malformation risk.  Why D wrong: First-trimester hyperglycaemia (overt pattern) causes malformations."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which type of diabetes in pregnancy can lead to a 1st trimester abortion?","options":["Gestational diabetes","Overt diabetes","Both equally","Neither"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Overt diabetes (raised sugar from the 1st trimester) can lead to 1st trimester abortion.  Why A wrong: Gestational diabetes does not lead to 1st trimester abortion.  Why C wrong: The two differ in this outcome.  Why D wrong: Overt diabetes does carry this risk."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Regarding 1st trimester abortion, gestational diabetes:","options":["Commonly causes it","Does not lead to 1st trimester abortion","Always causes it","Causes it only with insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because 1st-trimester sugar is normal in gestational diabetes, it does not lead to 1st trimester abortion.  Why A wrong: Overt, not gestational, diabetes is linked to 1st-trimester abortion.  Why C/D wrong: Gestational diabetes is not associated with 1st trimester abortion."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"What is the ONLY treatment for overt diabetes in pregnancy per the notes?","options":["Diet for 2 weeks","Oral hypoglycaemic agents","Insulin","Exercise alone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the only treatment for overt diabetes is insulin.  Why A wrong: A 2-week diet trial is the first step in gestational, not overt, diabetes.  Why B wrong: The page specifies insulin, not oral hypoglycaemics.  Why D wrong: Exercise alone is not listed as treatment for overt diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"What is the FIRST-line treatment for gestational diabetes according to the notes?","options":["Insulin immediately","Diet for 2 weeks","Oral metformin","Bed rest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 1st treatment is diet × 2 weeks; if not controlled, then insulin.  Why A wrong: Insulin is used only if diet fails to control sugars.  Why C wrong: Metformin is not listed; the page specifies diet then insulin.  Why D wrong: Bed rest is not the diabetes treatment."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In gestational diabetes, if diet for 2 weeks does not control blood sugar, the next step is:","options":["Repeat diet for another month","Start insulin","Terminate pregnancy","No further action"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows diet × 2 weeks → if not controlled → then insulin.  Why A wrong: The notes do not recommend repeating diet indefinitely.  Why C wrong: Termination is not indicated.  Why D wrong: Uncontrolled sugar requires escalation to insulin."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"On the 2-hour 75 gm GTT, which value indicates OVERT diabetes?","options":["Value between 140–200 mg%","Value ≥200 mg%","Value <140 mg%","Value <100 mg%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For overt diabetes, the 2-hour 75 gm GTT value is ≥200 mg%.  Why A wrong: 140–200 mg% indicates gestational diabetes.  Why C wrong: <140 mg% is not diagnostic of diabetes.  Why D wrong: <100 mg% is normal."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"On the 2-hour 75 gm GTT, which value range indicates GESTATIONAL diabetes?","options":["≥200 mg%","140–200 mg%","100–140 mg%","<90 mg%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gestational diabetes has a 2-hour 75 gm GTT value between 140–200 mg%.  Why A wrong: ≥200 mg% indicates overt diabetes.  Why C/D wrong: These lower ranges are not the values specified for gestational diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"How is hypertension defined in pregnancy?","options":["BP ≥120/80 mm Hg on 1 occasion","BP ≥140/90 mm Hg on 2 occasions, 4 hours apart","BP ≥160/110 mm Hg on 1 occasion","BP ≥130/85 mm Hg on 3 occasions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension in pregnancy is defined as BP ≥140/90 mm Hg on 2 occasions, 4 hours apart.  Why A wrong: 120/80 is normal, not hypertensive.  Why C wrong: 160/110 is the threshold for severe preeclampsia, not the basic definition.  Why D wrong: These values/occasions do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In pregnancy, the diastolic BP is taken at the point of:","options":["First appearance of sound (Korotkoff I)","Muffling of sounds (Korotkoff sound IV)","Disappearance of sound (Korotkoff V)","Peak sound intensity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in pregnancy, diastolic BP is where there is muffling of sounds (Korotkoff sound IV).  Why A wrong: Korotkoff I marks systolic BP.  Why C wrong: Korotkoff V (disappearance) is used outside pregnancy; the notes specify muffling (IV) in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Peak intensity is not a Korotkoff diastolic landmark."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Hypertension in pregnancy is divided into how many broad categories?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state there can be 2 categories (chronic hypertension in pregnancy and PIH).  Why A/C/D wrong: The page specifies exactly 2 categories."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"A hypertensive female who then conceives, i.e., already had high BP before pregnancy, is classified as having:","options":["Pregnancy-induced hypertension","Chronic hypertension in pregnancy","Gestational hypertension","Preeclampsia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A hypertensive female who has conceived falls under chronic hypertension in pregnancy.  Why A wrong: PIH occurs when BP was normal before pregnancy and rises after 20 weeks.  Why C wrong: Gestational hypertension is a subtype of PIH (BP normal before pregnancy).  Why D wrong: Preeclampsia also requires BP normal before pregnancy plus proteinuria/end organ damage."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"A woman with no raised BP before pregnancy who develops high BP due to a placental problem at 20 weeks has:","options":["Chronic hypertension in pregnancy","Pregnancy Induced Hypertension (PIH)","Essential hypertension","White-coat hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A female who did not have raised BP before pregnancy but develops high BP during pregnancy (around 20 weeks) due to a placental problem has PIH.  Why A wrong: Chronic HTN implies raised BP before pregnancy.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the classifications used in the notes for this scenario."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In chronic hypertension in pregnancy, the patient's history of raised BP is:","options":["Absent before pregnancy","Present before pregnancy","Only after delivery","Only during labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In chronic hypertension, the patient gives a history of increased BP before pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Absence of pre-pregnancy raised BP points to PIH.  Why C/D wrong: Chronic HTN is defined by pre-existing raised BP, not post-delivery or intrapartum onset."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In chronic hypertension in pregnancy, BP is raised:","options":["Only after 20 weeks","Throughout pregnancy, even before 20 weeks","Only during the 3rd trimester","Only in labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In chronic hypertension, BP is raised throughout pregnancy (even before 20 weeks).  Why A wrong: BP rising only after 20 weeks describes PIH.  Why C/D wrong: Chronic HTN raises BP throughout, not only in a specific stage."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In chronic hypertension in pregnancy, after delivery the BP:","options":["Comes back to normal within 12 weeks","Does not come back to normal within 12 weeks of delivery","Normalises within 24 hours","Normalises within 6 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In chronic hypertension, BP does not come back to normal within 12 weeks of delivery.  Why A wrong: Return to normal within 12 weeks characterises PIH.  Why C/D wrong: These timelines are not stated; chronic HTN persists beyond 12 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH), BP before pregnancy is:","options":["Raised","Normal","Low","Fluctuating"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In PIH, BP is normal before pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Raised pre-pregnancy BP indicates chronic HTN.  Why C/D wrong: PIH is defined by normal pre-pregnancy BP."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In PIH, BP becomes raised:","options":["Before 20 weeks","After 20 weeks of pregnancy","Only in the 1st trimester","Only after delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In PIH, BP is raised after 20 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: BP raised before 20 weeks suggests chronic HTN.  Why C/D wrong: PIH onset is after 20 weeks, not 1st trimester or postpartum."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In PIH, the BP returns to normal:","options":["Within 12 weeks of delivery","Never","Within 1 week of delivery","Only with lifelong medication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In PIH, BP comes back to normal within 12 weeks of delivery.  Why B wrong: PIH resolves; failure to resolve suggests chronic HTN.  Why C wrong: The specified timeframe is within 12 weeks, not 1 week.  Why D wrong: PIH does not require lifelong medication."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"PIH is further divided into which two types?","options":["Mild and severe eclampsia","Gestational hypertension and preeclampsia","Chronic and acute hypertension","Systolic and diastolic hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state PIH is further divided into 2 types: gestational hypertension and preeclampsia.  Why A wrong: Mild/severe describes preeclampsia grading, not PIH subtypes.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the PIH subtypes given."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which feature is COMMON to both gestational hypertension and preeclampsia?","options":["Proteinuria present","BP normal before pregnancy, increases after 20 weeks, and returns to normal within 12 weeks of delivery","End organ damage present","Raised BP before pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Both share: patient BP normal before pregnancy, BP increases after 20 weeks, BP returns to normal within 12 weeks of delivery.  Why A/C wrong: Proteinuria/end organ damage present distinguishes preeclampsia from gestational HTN.  Why D wrong: Raised BP before pregnancy is chronic HTN, not PIH."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Gestational hypertension is characterised by which of the following?","options":["Proteinuria present or end organ damage present","Proteinuria absent or end organ damage absent","Generalized tonic-clonic seizures","BP ≥160/110 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In gestational hypertension, proteinuria is absent OR end organ damage is absent.  Why A wrong: Presence of proteinuria/end organ damage defines preeclampsia.  Why C wrong: Seizures define eclampsia.  Why D wrong: ≥160/110 defines severe preeclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Preeclampsia (compared to gestational hypertension) is distinguished by:","options":["Proteinuria absent","Proteinuria present or end organ damage present","End organ damage absent","BP normalising before 20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Preeclampsia is defined by proteinuria present OR end organ damage present.  Why A/C wrong: Absence of proteinuria/end organ damage is gestational hypertension.  Why D wrong: BP rises after 20 weeks in PIH; it does not normalise before 20 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Proteinuria is defined as:","options":["Excretion of glucose in urine","Excretion of protein in urine","Excretion of ketones in urine","Excretion of blood in urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Proteinuria is the excretion of protein in urine.  Why A wrong: Glucose in urine is glycosuria.  Why C wrong: Ketones in urine is ketonuria.  Why D wrong: Blood in urine is haematuria."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"On a 24-hour urine sample, the threshold value diagnosing proteinuria is:","options":["≥300 mg (≥0.3 gm)","≥30 mg","≥3 gm","≥1 gm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a 24-hour urine sample, proteinuria is ≥300 mg (≥0.3 gm).  Why B wrong: 30 mg/dL is the random sample threshold, not 24-hour.  Why C/D wrong: These values are higher than the stated 300 mg threshold."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"On a random urine sample (measured in mg/dL), what protein level indicates proteinuria?","options":["≥3 mg/dL","≥30 mg/dL","≥300 mg/dL","≥130 mg/dL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A random urine sample with ≥30 mg/dL protein indicates proteinuria.  Why A wrong: 3 mg/dL is below the threshold.  Why C wrong: 300 mg is the 24-hour total, not the random mg/dL cutoff.  Why D wrong: 130 mg/dL is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"The dipstick method for detecting proteinuria is typically performed in which setting?","options":["ICU only","OPD","Operation theatre","Home only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the dipstick method is done in the OPD.  Why A/C/D wrong: The page specifies OPD as the setting for the dipstick method."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"On a urine dipstick, which color/result indicates that protein (≥ +1 proteinuria) is present?","options":["Green color","Red color","Blue color","Nil/Tracer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On the dipstick, a green color indicates protein and ≥ +1 proteinuria present.  Why B/C wrong: The notes specify green as the protein indicator color.  Why D wrong: Nil/Tracer indicates no proteinuria is present."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"On a urine dipstick, a result of Nil/Tracer indicates:","options":["Severe proteinuria","No proteinuria is present","+2 proteinuria","Glycosuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the dipstick shows Nil/Tracer, no proteinuria is present.  Why A/C wrong: These indicate significant protein, contradicting a Nil/Tracer result.  Why D wrong: Glycosuria is glucose, not protein."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Hypertension in pregnancy PLUS proteinuria together indicate:","options":["Gestational diabetes","Pre-eclampsia","Chronic hypertension","Eclampsia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension in pregnancy + proteinuria = pre-eclampsia.  Why A wrong: Diabetes involves blood sugar, not this combination.  Why C wrong: Chronic HTN is defined by pre-pregnancy raised BP, not proteinuria.  Why D wrong: Eclampsia requires seizures in addition to severe preeclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"During pregnancy, proteinuria is:","options":["Physiological (normal)","Not physiological","Always benign","Same as glycosuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Proteinuria is NOT physiological during pregnancy, so it is always a warning sign.  Why A/C wrong: It is not a normal/benign finding in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Proteinuria is protein loss; glycosuria is glucose loss."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which of the following is considered PHYSIOLOGICAL (normal) during pregnancy?","options":["Proteinuria","Glycosuria","Haematuria","Ketonuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glycosuria is physiological during pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Proteinuria is not physiological during pregnancy.  Why C/D wrong: Haematuria and ketonuria are not described as physiological here."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which liver enzyme finding is a sign of end organ damage in preeclampsia?","options":["Liver enzymes raised to ≥2 times the normal value","Liver enzymes decreased by half","Liver enzymes exactly normal","Liver enzymes raised to ≥10 times normal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A sign of end organ damage is liver enzymes raised to ≥2 times the normal value.  Why B/C wrong: Decreased or normal enzymes do not indicate damage.  Why D wrong: The stated threshold is ≥2 times, not ≥10 times."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which serum creatinine value is a sign of end organ damage in preeclampsia?","options":["Serum creatinine ≥1.1","Serum creatinine <0.5","Serum creatinine ≥0.1","Serum creatinine ≥11"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Serum creatinine ≥1.1 is a listed sign of end organ damage.  Why B wrong: Low creatinine is not a damage sign.  Why C/D wrong: These values (0.1, 11) are not the stated cutoff of 1.1."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which platelet count is a sign of end organ damage in preeclampsia?","options":["Platelet count <1 lakh (<100,000)","Platelet count >4 lakh","Platelet count 3 lakh","Platelet count >5 lakh"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A platelet count <1 lakh is a sign of end organ damage (thrombocytopenia).  Why B/C/D wrong: Normal or high platelet counts are not signs of damage."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a sign of end organ damage in preeclampsia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Liver enzymes raised ≥2 times normal","Serum creatinine ≥1.1","Platelet count <1 lakh","Visual symptoms/cerebral edema","Pulmonary edema"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are listed signs of end organ damage: raised liver enzymes (≥2× normal), serum creatinine ≥1.1, platelet count <1 lakh, visual symptoms/cerebral edema, and pulmonary edema.  Why not exclude any: Each option is explicitly enumerated as a sign of end organ damage on the page."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which neurological/ocular finding is a sign of end organ damage in preeclampsia?","options":["Visual symptoms/cerebral edema","Increased appetite","Ankle swelling only","Muscle cramps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Visual symptoms/cerebral edema is a listed sign of end organ damage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed among the end-organ-damage signs."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which pulmonary finding is a sign of end organ damage in preeclampsia?","options":["Pulmonary embolism","Pulmonary edema","Pneumothorax","Pleuritic pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary edema is listed as a sign of end organ damage.  Why A/C/D wrong: These conditions are not the listed sign; the page specifies pulmonary edema."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In a pregnant female, if any ONE sign of end organ damage/proteinuria is present PLUS hypertension, the diagnosis is:","options":["Gestational hypertension","Preeclampsia","Chronic hypertension","Eclampsia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Any one of these signs present + hypertension in a pregnant female = preeclampsia.  Why A wrong: Gestational HTN has no proteinuria/end organ damage.  Why C wrong: Chronic HTN is defined by pre-pregnancy raised BP.  Why D wrong: Eclampsia additionally requires seizures."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In MILD preeclampsia, the blood pressure range is:","options":["BP ≥140/90 mm Hg but <160/110 mm Hg","BP ≥160/110 mm Hg","BP <120/80 mm Hg","BP ≥180/120 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mild preeclampsia is BP ≥140/90 mm Hg but <160/110 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: ≥160/110 defines severe preeclampsia.  Why C wrong: This is normal BP.  Why D wrong: This exceeds the severe threshold and is not the mild range."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In mild preeclampsia, signs of end organ damage are:","options":["Present","Absent","Always severe","Required for diagnosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In mild preeclampsia, signs of end organ damage are absent.  Why A/C/D wrong: End organ damage present indicates severe preeclampsia, not mild."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In SEVERE preeclampsia, the blood pressure is:","options":["≥140/90 but <160/110 mm Hg","≥160/110 mm Hg","<140/90 mm Hg","Normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severe preeclampsia is BP ≥160/110 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: That range defines mild preeclampsia.  Why C/D wrong: These are below the severe threshold."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In severe preeclampsia, signs of end organ damage are:","options":["Absent","Present","Irrelevant","Normalised"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In severe preeclampsia, signs of end organ damage are present.  Why A wrong: Absence of end organ damage indicates mild preeclampsia.  Why C/D wrong: End organ damage is a defining feature of severe preeclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Proteinuria is seen in which grade(s) of preeclampsia?","options":["Only mild","Only severe","Both mild and severe","Neither"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state proteinuria is seen in both mild and severe preeclampsia.  Why A/B wrong: It is not restricted to one grade.  Why D wrong: Proteinuria is present in preeclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Eclampsia is defined as:","options":["Mild preeclampsia + edema","Severe preeclampsia + generalized tonic-clonic seizure","Chronic hypertension + proteinuria","Gestational hypertension + headache"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eclampsia = severe preeclampsia + generalized tonic clonic seizure.  Why A wrong: Mild preeclampsia does not progress to eclampsia.  Why C/D wrong: These combinations do not define eclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Eclampsia is a complication of:","options":["Mild preeclampsia","Severe preeclampsia","Gestational hypertension","Chronic hypertension only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eclampsia is a complication of severe preeclampsia (it is NOT a complication of mild preeclampsia).  Why A wrong: The notes explicitly state it is not a complication of mild preeclampsia.  Why C/D wrong: Eclampsia arises specifically from severe preeclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In all patients with severe preeclampsia, the nurse should specifically ask about:","options":["Signs of impending eclampsia","Family planning","Dietary preferences","Menstrual history"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nursing implication is to ask about symptoms indicating the patient may throw a convulsion at any time — the signs of impending eclampsia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the priority assessment for imminent convulsion in severe preeclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In severe preeclampsia, which urine output indicates a sign of impending eclampsia (decreased output)?","options":["Output below the normal of ≥30 ml/hr","Output of ≥50 ml/hr","Output of ≥100 ml/hr","Output of ≥60 ml/hr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased urine output is a sign of impending eclampsia; the normal is ≥30 ml/hr, so output below this is concerning.  Why B/C/D wrong: These meet or exceed the normal ≥30 ml/hr and are not decreased output."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which type of headache is a sign of impending eclampsia?","options":["Headache relieved by regular medication","Headache which is NOT caused/relieved by regular medication","Mild occasional headache","Sinus headache"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A headache which is not caused (relieved) by regular medication is a sign of impending eclampsia.  Why A/C/D wrong: A medication-responsive, mild, or sinus headache is not the concerning impending sign."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which abdominal symptom is a sign of impending eclampsia?","options":["Right shoulder pain","Epigastric pain","Lower abdominal cramps","Umbilical pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epigastric pain is a listed sign of impending eclampsia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These specific locations are not the listed impending-eclampsia sign."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which visual symptoms indicate impending eclampsia?","options":["Blurring of vision or diplopia","Improved night vision","Colour blindness only","Photophobia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Visual symptoms such as blurring of vision or diplopia are signs of impending eclampsia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed impending-eclampsia visual symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which of the following are signs of impending eclampsia in a severe preeclampsia patient? (Select all that apply)","options":["Decreased urine output (below ≥30 ml/hr normal)","Headache not caused by regular medication","Epigastric pain","Visual symptoms (blurring of vision or diplopia)","Increased urine output"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four listed signs of impending eclampsia are decreased urine output, headache not relieved by regular medication, epigastric pain, and visual symptoms (blurring/diplopia).  Why E wrong: Increased urine output is not a sign; decreased output is the concern."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In the pathophysiology of eclampsia, when BP increases, the volume of blood going to all organs:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Stays the same","Doubles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When pressure (BP) is increased, the volume of blood going to all organs decreases.  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes state blood volume to organs decreases with raised BP."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In eclampsia, decreased blood flow to the mother's brain leads to:","options":["Cerebral hyperperfusion","Cerebral hypoxia","Increased cerebral oxygenation","Cerebral edema resolution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reduced blood volume to the mother's brain causes cerebral hypoxia.  Why A/C wrong: Blood flow decreases, causing hypoxia, not hyperperfusion/increased oxygenation.  Why D wrong: Hypoxia contributes to injury, not resolution of edema."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Cerebral hypoxia in eclampsia ultimately results in:","options":["Convulsions","Improved consciousness","Bradycardia only","Hypertension resolution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cerebral hypoxia leads to convulsions in eclampsia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Hypoxia produces seizures, not improved consciousness or BP resolution."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Based on the pathophysiology of eclamptic convulsions, the treatment principle involves:","options":["Vasoconstriction of brain vessels","Vasodilation of brain vessels","Diuresis of brain tissue","Cooling of the brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that treatment involves vasodilation of brain vessels to restore blood flow and relieve cerebral hypoxia.  Why A wrong: Vasoconstriction would worsen ischemia.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated treatment principle."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"The term \"chronic hypertension with superimposed preeclampsia\" refers to a female who:","options":["Was normotensive and develops HTN after 20 weeks","Has chronically increased BP, conceives, and suddenly at 20 weeks develops complications","Only has proteinuria without HTN","Has seizures without hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It means a female with chronically increased BP who conceived and suddenly at 20 weeks develops complications (uncontrollable BP, proteinuria, or end organ damage).  Why A wrong: A previously normotensive woman describes plain PIH/preeclampsia.  Why C/D wrong: The condition builds on pre-existing chronic hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In chronic hypertension with superimposed preeclampsia, which change may occur suddenly at 20 weeks? (Select all that apply)","options":["Her BP becomes uncontrollable","She starts having proteinuria","She starts having signs of end organ damage","Her BP returns to normal"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: At 20 weeks the woman with chronic HTN may develop uncontrollable BP, new proteinuria, or signs of end organ damage — any one signals superimposed preeclampsia.  Why D wrong: BP returning to normal is not a feature of superimposed preeclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"The initiating event in the pathophysiology of PIH is:","options":["A problem in the placenta at 20 weeks (≥20 weeks) of pregnancy","Maternal kidney failure","A brain tumour","Excess amniotic fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PIH occurs due to some problem in the placenta at 20 weeks (≥20 weeks) of pregnancy.  Why B/C/D wrong: The trigger is placental, occurring around 20 weeks, not renal, cerebral, or fluid-related."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In PIH, the blood supply to the placenta is decreased and the size of the placenta is:","options":["Large","Small","Normal","Absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In PIH there is decreased blood supply in the placenta and the size of the placenta is small.  Why A/C wrong: The placenta is small, not large or normal.  Why D wrong: The placenta is small, not absent."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In the PIH cascade, decreased placental blood supply directly leads to:","options":["Placental ischemia","Placental hypertrophy","Amniotic fluid embolism","Placenta praevia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased blood supply leads to placental ischemia.  Why B wrong: The placenta becomes ischaemic and small, not hypertrophied.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the PIH ischemia cascade described."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Placental ischemia in PIH triggers the release of:","options":["Inflammatory mediators","Insulin","Surfactant","Oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Placental ischemia causes inflammatory mediators to be released.  Why B/C/D wrong: These substances are not the mediators released in the PIH cascade."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"The release of inflammatory mediators in PIH causes which vascular change in the mother?","options":["Vasoconstriction of the aorta only","Leakage in the blood capillaries of the mother","Rupture of large arteries","Bone marrow suppression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammatory mediators cause leakage in the blood capillaries of the mother.  Why A/C/D wrong: The key effect is increased capillary permeability/leakage, not aortic or large-artery or marrow effects."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"When plasma (liquid component of blood) leaks under the skin in PIH, it causes:","options":["Ascites","Edema","Pleural effusion","Haemoconcentration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Plasma collecting under the skin causes edema.  Why A wrong: Fluid in the peritoneal cavity causes ascites.  Why C wrong: Fluid in the pleural cavity causes pleural effusion.  Why D wrong: Haemoconcentration results from the solid component staying inside, not from plasma leakage under the skin."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"When plasma leaks into the peritoneal cavity in PIH, it causes:","options":["Edema","Ascites","Pleural effusion","Pulmonary embolism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Plasma collecting in the peritoneal cavity causes ascites.  Why A wrong: Under-the-skin collection causes edema.  Why C wrong: Pleural cavity collection causes pleural effusion.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary embolism is not the result of plasma leakage into the peritoneum."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"When plasma leaks into the pleural cavity in PIH, it causes:","options":["Ascites","Pleural effusion","Edema","Pericarditis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Plasma collecting in the pleural cavity causes pleural effusion.  Why A wrong: Peritoneal collection causes ascites.  Why C wrong: Subcutaneous collection causes edema.  Why D wrong: Pericarditis is not described here."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In PIH, when plasma leaks out and the solid components (RBC, WBC, platelets) stay inside the vessels, the blood becomes thick, causing:","options":["Anaemia","Haemoconcentration","Haemodilution","Leukopenia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The solid components staying inside make the blood thick, resulting in haemoconcentration.  Why A/D wrong: There is no anaemia or leukopenia; cells are retained.  Why C wrong: Haemodilution is the opposite; here blood becomes concentrated."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Haemoconcentration in PIH increases the chances of:","options":["Bleeding","Thrombosis","Anaemia","Hypotension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemoconcentration increases the chances of thrombosis (thick blood clots more easily).  Why A wrong: Thick blood promotes clotting, not bleeding.  Why C wrong: Cells are retained, so anaemia is not the result.  Why D wrong: PIH involves hypertension, not hypotension."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In the PIH pathway, haemoconcentration and increased thrombosis ultimately lead to:","options":["End organ damage","Placental regeneration","Improved perfusion","Resolution of hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The solid-component/haemoconcentration/thrombosis pathway leads to end organ damage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are contrary to the ischaemic, thrombotic outcome described."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Which criteria were EARLIER used to diagnose preeclampsia but are NO LONGER used? (Select all that apply)","options":["Edema + ↑BP","Rapid weight gain (≥0.5 kg/week) + ↑BP","↑BP + proteinuria","↑BP + signs of end organ damage"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The older, now-discarded criteria were edema + ↑BP and rapid weight gain (≥0.5 kg/week) + ↑BP.  Why C/D wrong: ↑BP + proteinuria and ↑BP + signs of end organ damage are the CURRENT criteria, not the obsolete ones."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"What rate of rapid weight gain was part of the OLD preeclampsia criteria (with ↑BP)?","options":["≥0.5 kg/week","≥5 kg/week","≥0.05 kg/week","≥2 kg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The old criterion was rapid weight gain of ≥0.5 kg/week combined with ↑BP.  Why B/C/D wrong: These rates do not match the stated ≥0.5 kg/week."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"According to the CURRENT criteria, preeclampsia is diagnosed by ↑BP plus which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Proteinuria","Signs of end organ damage","Edema","Rapid weight gain"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The current criteria are ↑BP + proteinuria OR ↑BP + signs of end organ damage.  Why C/D wrong: Edema and rapid weight gain are the outdated criteria, no longer used."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"The type of edema seen in preeclampsia is:","options":["Non-pitting edema","Pitting edema","Lymphedema","Angioedema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The edema seen in preeclampsia is pitting edema.  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specify pitting edema, not non-pitting, lymphedema, or angioedema."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"Regarding edema in PIH/preeclampsia, which statement is correct?","options":["Edema is a required criterion to diagnose preeclampsia","Edema is seen in PIH/preeclampsia but is NOT a criterion to diagnose preeclampsia","Edema rules out preeclampsia","Edema is never seen in preeclampsia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Edema is seen in PIH/preeclampsia but it is not a criterion to diagnose preeclampsia.  Why A wrong: It is explicitly not a diagnostic criterion.  Why C/D wrong: Edema is commonly seen and does not rule out the condition."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In PIH there is multi-organ dysfunction. Which statement is correct?","options":["Only the maternal organs are affected; the fetus is spared","All maternal organs get less blood and are affected, and the fetus also gets less blood","Only the fetus is affected","No organs are affected"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In PIH all maternal organs get less blood and are affected, and the fetus also gets less blood.  Why A wrong: The fetus is also affected by reduced blood supply.  Why C wrong: Both mother and fetus are affected.  Why D wrong: Multi-organ dysfunction means organs are affected."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"In PIH, the MOST COMMON maternal organ affected is the:","options":["Liver","Kidney","Brain","Heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common maternal organ affected in PIH is the kidney.  Why A/C/D wrong: While other organs can be involved (end organ damage), the most common one specified is the kidney."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"A pregnant woman at 24 weeks with no history of hypertension is found to have BP 150/95 mm Hg on two readings 4 hours apart, with no proteinuria and no end organ damage. This best fits:","options":["Chronic hypertension","Gestational hypertension","Severe preeclampsia","Eclampsia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal BP before pregnancy with BP rising after 20 weeks and NO proteinuria/end organ damage defines gestational hypertension (a PIH subtype).  Why A wrong: No history of pre-pregnancy hypertension excludes chronic HTN.  Why C wrong: Severe preeclampsia requires BP ≥160/110 and/or end organ damage.  Why D wrong: Eclampsia requires seizures."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"A woman at 30 weeks with BP 165/115 mm Hg, proteinuria, and platelet count of 80,000 (<1 lakh) most likely has:","options":["Mild preeclampsia","Severe preeclampsia","Gestational hypertension","Chronic hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BP ≥160/110 with signs of end organ damage (thrombocytopenia <1 lakh) indicates severe preeclampsia.  Why A wrong: Mild is BP <160/110 with no end organ damage.  Why C wrong: Gestational HTN has no proteinuria/end organ damage.  Why D wrong: No pre-pregnancy HTN history is given."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Overt vs gestational diabetes…","question":"A severe preeclampsia patient develops a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. This progression is termed:","options":["Gestational hypertension","Eclampsia","Chronic hypertension","Mild preeclampsia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severe preeclampsia + generalized tonic-clonic seizure = eclampsia.  Why A/C/D wrong: None of these involve seizures superimposed on severe preeclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In pregnancy-induced hypertension, when the kidneys receive less blood, which of the following is the FIRST change to occur?","options":["Increased glomerular filtration rate","Decreased renal blood flow","Increased filtering capacity","Increased urine output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source cascade begins with \"When kidneys get less blood → ↓Renal Blood flow,\" which then reduces the kidney's function capacity, GFR, and filtering capacity.  Why A wrong: GFR decreases, not increases, in this cascade.  Why C wrong: Filtering capacity decreases (↓), it does not increase.  Why D wrong: Reduced renal perfusion decreases urine production, it does not increase output."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Arrange the renal cascade in PIH from reduced kidney perfusion. Which sequence is correct?","options":["↓Renal blood flow → ↓function capacity → ↓GFR → ↓filtering capacity","↓GFR → ↓renal blood flow → ↓filtering capacity → ↓function capacity","↓Filtering capacity → ↓GFR → ↓renal blood flow → ↓function capacity","↓Function capacity → ↓filtering capacity → ↓renal blood flow → ↓GFR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows ↓Renal Blood flow → its function capacity ↓ → Glomerular filtration Rate ↓ → filtering capacity ↓ in that order.  Why B wrong: Renal blood flow falls before GFR, not after.  Why C wrong: Filtering capacity fall is the last step, not the first.  Why D wrong: The cascade starts with renal blood flow reduction, not function capacity."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Which of the following serum values INCREASE when kidney function is impaired in preeclampsia?","options":["Serum urea, serum uric acid, and serum creatinine","Serum sodium and serum potassium only","Serum albumin and serum protein","Serum calcium and serum magnesium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that with reduced renal function, serum urea, serum uric acid, and serum creatinine all increase.  Why B wrong: The page names urea, uric acid, and creatinine, not sodium/potassium.  Why C wrong: Albumin/protein are not listed; proteinuria (loss) is a separate feature.  Why D wrong: Calcium and magnesium are not the listed rising values."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"A serum creatinine value greater than which threshold is considered a sign of end-organ damage in preeclampsia?","options":[">0.5",">1.1",">2.5",">3.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify \"(>1.1 → Sign of End organ damage)\" for serum creatinine.  Why A wrong: 0.5 is well within normal and not the stated cut-off.  Why C wrong: 2.5 is higher than the source-stated threshold of 1.1.  Why D wrong: 3.0 is not the value given for end-organ damage."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"When the fetus receives less blood in preeclampsia and all parts of the fetus are underperfused, the result is:","options":["Macrosomia","Intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR)","Polyhydramnios","Fetal hydrops"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows \"All parts of fetus will get less blood → Less growth of fetus → IUGR (Intrauterine Growth Restriction).\"  Why A wrong: Reduced perfusion causes restricted growth, the opposite of macrosomia.  Why C wrong: Polyhydramnios is excess fluid; the fetal branch here leads to reduced fluid.  Why D wrong: Hydrops is not the outcome described in this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In preeclampsia, reduced blood flow to the fetal kidney ultimately leads to which of the following?","options":["Oligohydramnios","Polyhydramnios","Increased fetal urine output","Fetal ascites"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pathway is Fetal kidney gets less blood → fetal renal blood flow↓ → GFR↓ → fetus produces less urine → ↓Amniotic fluid → OLIGOHYDRAMNIOS.  Why B wrong: Fluid decreases, giving oligo- not polyhydramnios.  Why C wrong: Fetal urine output decreases, not increases.  Why D wrong: Ascites is not the described end result."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Which mechanism explains the oligohydramnios seen in preeclampsia?","options":["Increased fetal swallowing of amniotic fluid","Reduced fetal renal blood flow leading to decreased fetal urine production","Rupture of membranes","Increased placental fluid secretion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source shows ↓fetal renal blood flow → ↓GFR → less fetal urine → ↓amniotic fluid → oligohydramnios; fetal urine is the major source of amniotic fluid.  Why A wrong: Increased swallowing is not the mechanism described.  Why C wrong: The notes attribute it to reduced fetal urine, not membrane rupture.  Why D wrong: The placenta does not secrete the fluid in this described pathway."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"The complications listed for preeclampsia are seen with which severity of the disease?","options":["Only mild preeclampsia","Only severe preeclampsia (not with mild)","Both mild and severe equally","Gestational hypertension without proteinuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"All these complications are seen with severe preeclampsia (Not with mild).\"  Why A wrong: The complications are NOT seen with mild disease.  Why C wrong: They are specific to severe, not equal in both.  Why D wrong: These are complications of severe preeclampsia, not gestational hypertension alone."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In the HELLP syndrome acronym, the letter \"H\" stands for:","options":["Hypertension","Hemolysis (RBC destruction)","Hyperglycemia","Hepatomegaly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define H → Hemolysis (RBC destruction = Fetal Anemia).  Why A wrong: Hypertension is the underlying disorder, not the \"H\" of HELLP.  Why C wrong: Hyperglycemia is unrelated to the HELLP acronym.  Why D wrong: Hepatomegaly is not the meaning of \"H\" here."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In HELLP syndrome, hemolysis (RBC destruction) is associated with which fetal consequence per the notes?","options":["Fetal anemia","Fetal polycythemia","Fetal jaundice only","Fetal thrombocytosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes annotate hemolysis as \"RBC destruction = Fetal Anemia.\"  Why B wrong: Polycythemia (excess RBC) is the opposite of hemolysis-related anemia.  Why C wrong: The source specifically links it to fetal anemia, not jaundice alone.  Why D wrong: Thrombocytosis is not the stated consequence."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In HELLP syndrome, the letters \"EL\" represent:","options":["Elevated Liver enzymes","Elevated Lipase","Extra Lymphocytes","Elevated Leukocytes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define E + L → Elevated Liver Enzymes ↑.  Why B wrong: Lipase is not part of the HELLP acronym.  Why C wrong: Lymphocytes are not represented in HELLP.  Why D wrong: Leukocytes are not part of the acronym."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In HELLP syndrome, the letters \"LP\" stand for:","options":["Low Platelet count","Low Protein","Liver Pain","Low Pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define L + P → Low Platelet Count ↓.  Why B wrong: Protein is not the \"LP\" meaning.  Why C wrong: Liver pain is not the acronym component.  Why D wrong: Low pressure is not part of HELLP."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"HELLP syndrome is a complication of which condition?","options":["Severe preeclampsia","Gestational diabetes","Placenta previa","Hyperemesis gravidarum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HELLP is listed among the complications of severe preeclampsia.  Why B wrong: Gestational diabetes is a separate disorder, not the parent of HELLP here.  Why C wrong: Placenta previa is an antepartum hemorrhage cause, not HELLP's parent.  Why D wrong: Hyperemesis is unrelated to HELLP."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"\"Premature separation of a placenta that is normally located\" describes which complication of severe preeclampsia?","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placenta","Placenta accreta","Vasa previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define abruption placenta as \"Premature separation which is normally located.\"  Why A wrong: Placenta previa is a low-lying/abnormally located placenta, not premature separation.  Why C wrong: Accreta is abnormal adherence, not premature separation.  Why D wrong: Vasa previa involves fetal vessels over the os, not placental separation."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Which severe preeclampsia-spectrum conditions can lead to DIC (disseminated intravascular coagulation)?","options":["Severe pre-eclampsia, eclampsia, or HELLP syndrome","Mild preeclampsia only","Gestational hypertension only","Chronic hypertension only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state DIC results from \"Severe Pre-eclampsia, Eclampsia or HELLP syndrome — Can lead to DIC.\"  Why B wrong: Mild preeclampsia is not listed as a DIC cause.  Why C wrong: Gestational hypertension alone is not the listed trigger.  Why D wrong: Chronic hypertension alone is not the listed trigger."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"IUGR as a complication of preeclampsia is more commonly seen in which severity?","options":["Mild preeclampsia","Severe preeclampsia","Gestational hypertension","Normal pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"IUGR (more common in severe Preeclampsia).\"  Why A wrong: It is more common in severe, not mild.  Why C wrong: Gestational hypertension is not the stated setting.  Why D wrong: IUGR is a pathological complication, not a normal-pregnancy finding here."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"IUD (intrauterine death) of the fetus as a complication of preeclampsia is more common in:","options":["Severe preeclampsia","Mild preeclampsia","Chronic hypertension","Gestational hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"IUD (Intra uterine death) of fetus → more common in severe Preeclampsia.\"  Why B wrong: Mild disease is not the stated setting.  Why C wrong: Chronic hypertension is not listed here.  Why D wrong: Gestational hypertension is not the stated setting."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"\"Thrombocytopenia\" as a complication of preeclampsia refers to:","options":["Decreased platelet count","Increased platelet count","Decreased white cell count","Increased red cell count"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define thrombocytopenia as \"Decreased Platelet count.\"  Why B wrong: That is thrombocytosis, the opposite.  Why C wrong: That would be leukopenia.  Why D wrong: That would be polycythemia."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following are listed complications of severe preeclampsia?","options":["Eclampsia","HELLP syndrome","Abruptio placenta / DIC","Placenta previa","IUGR and IUD"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The complications list includes eclampsia, HELLP syndrome, abruptio placenta, DIC, IUGR, IUD, and thrombocytopenia.  Why A/B/C/E correct: Each appears explicitly in the numbered complications list.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is a cause of antepartum hemorrhage, not listed as a preeclampsia complication."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"As a nursing implication, which three assessments should be done at EVERY antenatal visit in all pregnant females?","options":["Check BP, check proteinuria, check weight","Check hemoglobin, check blood group, check height","Check pulse, check temperature, check reflexes","Check blood sugar, check thyroid, check ECG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"At every visit in all pregnant females: Check her BP, Check proteinuria, Check weight of patient.\"  Why B wrong: These are not the three per-visit checks stated.  Why C wrong: These are not the listed routine per-visit assessments.  Why D wrong: These are not the listed routine per-visit assessments."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In a pregnant patient with increased blood pressure, the nurse should specifically ask about signs of:","options":["Impending eclampsia","Gestational diabetes","Preterm labor","Anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"In patients with increased BP, ask about signs of Impending Eclampsia.\"  Why B wrong: Diabetes signs are not the stated focus for raised BP.  Why C wrong: Preterm labor is not the listed concern here.  Why D wrong: Anemia is not the stated concern for elevated BP."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"(SATA) When assessing for impending eclampsia, the nurse should ask the patient about which of the following?","options":["Urine output","Headache","Epigastric pain","Visual symptoms","Ankle swelling only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Ask about: Urine output, Headache, Epigastric pain, Visual symptoms.\"  Why A/B/C/D correct: Each is explicitly listed as a symptom to ask about.  Why E wrong: \"Ankle swelling only\" is not among the four listed items to ask about."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"The nurse should explain to the preeclamptic patient the importance of which daily self-monitoring activity?","options":["Daily fetal movement count and charting","Daily temperature charting","Daily weight loss diet","Daily blood glucose monitoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Explain: The importance of daily fetal movement count & charting.\"  Why B wrong: Temperature charting is not the stated teaching point.  Why C wrong: A weight-loss diet is not advised in pregnancy here.  Why D wrong: Glucose monitoring is not the listed teaching point."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Which gravidity status is a risk factor for PIH/preeclampsia?","options":["Primigravida","Grand multipara","Multigravida","Nulligravida (never pregnant)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Primigravida\" as a risk factor for PIH/preeclampsia.  Why B wrong: Grand multipara is not the listed risk group for PIH.  Why C wrong: Multigravida is associated with antepartum hemorrhage, not PIH per these notes.  Why D wrong: A never-pregnant woman is not pregnant and cannot develop PIH."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"\"Big placenta\" as a risk factor for preeclampsia is associated with which conditions?","options":["Diabetes or twins","Anemia or infection","Oligohydramnios","Post-term pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Big Placenta (Diabetes or Twins)\" as a risk factor.  Why B wrong: Anemia/infection are not the listed causes of big placenta.  Why C wrong: Oligohydramnios is a consequence, not a cause of big placenta.  Why D wrong: Post-term pregnancy is not the stated association."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"(SATA) Which \"two kinds of syndromes\" are listed as risk factors for preeclampsia?","options":["Metabolic X syndrome","Anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome (APLA)","Down syndrome","Turner syndrome","Marfan syndrome"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"2 kinds of syndromes: Metabolic X syndrome and Anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome (APLA Syndrome).\"  Why A/B correct: Both are explicitly named risk-factor syndromes.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is not listed here.  Why D wrong: Turner syndrome is not listed.  Why E wrong: Marfan syndrome is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"(SATA) Which \"3 types of pregnancy\" are listed as risk factors for PIH/preeclampsia?","options":["Twin pregnancy","Molar pregnancy","Rh negative pregnancy","Ectopic pregnancy","Post-term pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"3 Types of Pregnancy: Twin pregnancy, Molar pregnancy, Rh negative pregnancy.\"  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly named.  Why D wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is not listed as a PIH risk type here.  Why E wrong: Post-term pregnancy is not among the three listed types."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"(SATA) From the second box of risk factors, which are listed as risk factors for preeclampsia?","options":["Previous history of preeclampsia","Family history of increased BP","Kidney disease","Diabetes","Smoking"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The second box lists previous H/O preeclampsia, family H/O ↑BP, kidney disease, and diabetes.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: Smoking is a PROTECTIVE factor against PIH, not a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Which factor is described as PROTECTIVE against PIH?","options":["Smoking","Obesity","Multiple gestation","Kidney disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"Protective factor: Smoking protects against PIH.\"  Why B wrong: Obesity is not listed as protective.  Why C wrong: Multiple (twin) gestation is a risk factor, not protective.  Why D wrong: Kidney disease is a risk factor, not protective."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In antepartum hemorrhage (placenta previa & abruptio), the risk of occurrence is more common in which patient?","options":["Multigravida female","Primigravida female","Nulligravida female","Adolescent primigravida"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Remember\" box states antepartum hemorrhage is \"More common in Multigravida female.\"  Why B wrong: Primigravida is linked with PIH, not with antepartum hemorrhage risk here.  Why C wrong: Nulligravida (never pregnant) cannot have antepartum hemorrhage.  Why D wrong: The notes specify multigravida generally, not adolescent primigravida."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Regarding smoking, which statement correctly contrasts antepartum hemorrhage (APH) with PIH per the notes?","options":["Smoking is a risk factor for APH but protective in PIH","Smoking is protective in APH but a risk factor in PIH","Smoking is a risk factor for both","Smoking is protective in both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for APH \"Smoking is a risk factor,\" and for PIH \"Smoking is protective.\"  Why B wrong: This reverses the correct relationship.  Why C wrong: Smoking is protective in PIH, not a risk factor.  Why D wrong: Smoking is a risk factor in APH, not protective."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"PIH is more common in which type of gravida?","options":["Primigravida female","Multigravida female","Grand multipara","Second gravida only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"PIH: More common in primigravida female.\"  Why B wrong: Multigravida is associated with antepartum hemorrhage, not PIH.  Why C wrong: Grand multipara is not the stated group for PIH.  Why D wrong: The notes say primigravida, not specifically second gravida."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"According to the notes, eclampsia means:","options":["When a severe preeclampsia throws convulsions","Mild rise in blood pressure without proteinuria","Chronic hypertension before pregnancy","Proteinuria without hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define eclampsia as \"When a severe Preeclampsia throws convulsions.\"  Why B wrong: That describes gestational hypertension, not eclampsia.  Why C wrong: That is chronic hypertension, not eclampsia.  Why D wrong: Isolated proteinuria is not eclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Eclampsia is a complication of which condition?","options":["Severe preeclampsia","Mild preeclampsia","Gestational diabetes","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state eclampsia is a \"Complication of: Severe Preeclampsia.\"  Why B wrong: It is a complication of severe, not mild, preeclampsia.  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes does not cause eclampsia.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is a hemorrhage cause, not the parent of eclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"The convulsions in eclampsia occur because of:","options":["Cerebral anoxia","Hypoglycemia","Hypercalcemia","Electrolyte overload"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Convulsions occur because of: Cerebral anoxia.\"  Why B wrong: Hypoglycemia is not the stated cause.  Why C wrong: Hypercalcemia is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Electrolyte overload is not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Convulsions occurring during the antenatal period (during pregnancy) are classified as:","options":["Antepartum eclampsia","Intrapartum eclampsia","Postpartum eclampsia","Pre-eclampsia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes classify convulsions in the antenatal period (during pregnancy) as \"Antepartum eclampsia.\"  Why B wrong: Intrapartum eclampsia occurs during labor.  Why C wrong: Postpartum eclampsia occurs after delivery.  Why D wrong: Pre-eclampsia has no convulsions by definition."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Convulsions occurring during labor are classified as which type of eclampsia?","options":["Intrapartum eclampsia","Antepartum eclampsia","Postpartum eclampsia","Chronic eclampsia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state convulsions \"During labor → Intrapartum eclampsia.\"  Why B wrong: Antepartum is during pregnancy, before labor.  Why C wrong: Postpartum is after delivery.  Why D wrong: \"Chronic eclampsia\" is not a category in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Convulsions occurring within 48 hours of delivery are classified as:","options":["Postpartum eclampsia","Intrapartum eclampsia","Antepartum eclampsia","Late gestational hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state convulsions \"Within 48 hours of delivery → Post partum eclampsia.\"  Why B wrong: Intrapartum is during labor, not after delivery.  Why C wrong: Antepartum is before delivery.  Why D wrong: This is not a classification used in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Within how many hours of delivery can postpartum eclampsia occur, per the notes?","options":["Within 24 hours","Within 48 hours","Within 72 hours","Within 1 week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify \"Within 48 hours of delivery → Post partum eclampsia.\"  Why A wrong: The stated window is 48 hours, not 24.  Why C wrong: 72 hours is not the stated window.  Why D wrong: 1 week is not the stated window."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Which is the MOST COMMON type of eclampsia?","options":["Antepartum eclampsia","Intrapartum eclampsia","Postpartum eclampsia","All are equally common"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Most Common type of Eclampsia → Antepartum Eclampsia.\"  Why B wrong: Intrapartum is not the most common type.  Why C wrong: Postpartum is not the most common type.  Why D wrong: They are not equally common; antepartum is most common."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Which type of eclampsia carries the WORST prognosis?","options":["Antepartum eclampsia","Intrapartum eclampsia","Postpartum eclampsia","All have equal prognosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Eclampsia with worst prognosis → Antepartum Eclampsia.\"  Why B wrong: Intrapartum is not stated as worst prognosis.  Why C wrong: Postpartum is not stated as worst prognosis.  Why D wrong: Antepartum specifically has the worst prognosis."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Because PIH is due to a problem in the placenta and the patient's BP was normal before pregnancy, what is the DEFINITIVE management of PIH?","options":["Delivery (termination of pregnancy)","Lifelong antihypertensives","Bed rest only","Salt restriction only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Definitive management of PIH always is: Delivery = Termination of Pregnancy,\" because removing the placenta returns BP to normal.  Why B wrong: Antihypertensives control BP but are not the definitive cure.  Why C wrong: Bed rest is supportive, not definitive.  Why D wrong: Salt restriction is not the definitive management."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In the context of PIH management, \"termination of pregnancy\" means:","options":["Abortion / killing the fetus","Inducing the labor process (delivery of patient)","Emergency hysterectomy","Permanent sterilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes clarify termination of pregnancy \"Does not mean abortion/kill fetus\" but \"Means: Labor process induced (delivery of patient).\"  Why A wrong: The notes explicitly say it does NOT mean abortion/killing the fetus.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not what is meant.  Why D wrong: Sterilization is not the meaning of termination here."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"According to the notes, mild preeclampsia is defined by which blood pressure range?","options":["≥140/90 but <160/110","≥160/110","≥120/80 but <140/90","≥150/100 only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The termination table defines \"Mild Pre-eclampsia (≥140/90 but <160/110).\"  Why B wrong: ≥160/110 defines severe preeclampsia.  Why C wrong: That range is below the diagnostic threshold for preeclampsia.  Why D wrong: 150/100 is a treatment trigger, not the definition of mild preeclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In mild preeclampsia, termination of pregnancy (delivery) is done at:","options":["≥37 weeks","≥34 weeks","Immediately, irrespective of gestational age","≥28 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states mild preeclampsia → termination \"≥37 weeks (delivery).\"  Why B wrong: ≥34 weeks is for severe preeclampsia.  Why C wrong: Immediate delivery applies to eclampsia/HELLP/abruptio/fetal distress.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is not the stated timing for mild disease."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Severe preeclampsia is defined in the notes by which BP threshold?","options":["≥160/110 mm Hg","≥140/90 mm Hg","≥150/100 mm Hg","≥130/80 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines \"Severe Pre-eclampsia (≥160/110 mm Hg).\"  Why B wrong: ≥140/90 is the threshold for mild preeclampsia.  Why C wrong: 150/100 is a treatment trigger, not the definition of severe.  Why D wrong: 130/80 is below any preeclampsia threshold given."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In severe preeclampsia, delivery (termination of pregnancy) is done at:","options":["≥34 weeks","≥37 weeks","≥40 weeks","≥28 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states severe preeclampsia → termination \"≥34 weeks.\"  Why B wrong: ≥37 weeks applies to mild preeclampsia.  Why C wrong: 40 weeks is not the stated timing.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is not the stated timing for severe preeclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"For eclampsia, when is delivery performed?","options":["Immediate delivery irrespective of gestational age","At ≥37 weeks","At ≥34 weeks","Only after 40 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states eclampsia → \"Immediate delivery irrespective of gestational age.\"  Why B wrong: 37 weeks is for mild preeclampsia.  Why C wrong: 34 weeks is for severe preeclampsia.  Why D wrong: Delivery is immediate, not delayed to 40 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"For HELLP syndrome, when is delivery performed?","options":["Immediate delivery irrespective of gestational age","At ≥34 weeks","At ≥37 weeks","After spontaneous labor only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states HELLP syndrome → \"Immediate delivery irrespective of gestational age.\"  Why B wrong: 34 weeks is for severe preeclampsia, not HELLP.  Why C wrong: 37 weeks is for mild preeclampsia.  Why D wrong: Delivery is immediate, not awaited."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In PIH complicated by placental abruption (PIH with Abruptio), the management is:","options":["Immediate delivery","Wait until 37 weeks","Wait until 34 weeks","Conservative bed rest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"PIH with Abruptio → Immediate delivery.\"  Why B wrong: Waiting to 37 weeks applies to uncomplicated mild disease.  Why C wrong: 34 weeks applies to severe preeclampsia without these complications.  Why D wrong: Conservative management is inappropriate with abruption."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In PIH with fetal distress, the appropriate management is:","options":["Immediate delivery","Delivery at ≥37 weeks","Delivery at ≥34 weeks","Await spontaneous labor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"PIH with fetal distress → Immediate delivery.\"  Why B wrong: 37 weeks is for uncomplicated mild disease.  Why C wrong: 34 weeks is for uncomplicated severe disease.  Why D wrong: Fetal distress mandates immediate delivery, not waiting."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Antihypertensive therapy is definitely given when the BP reaches which level (severe preeclampsia)?","options":["BP ≥160/110 mm Hg","BP ≥140/90 mm Hg","BP ≥130/80 mm Hg","BP ≥120/80 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state antihypertensive is \"Given when BP ≥160/110 mm Hg (severe Pre-eclampsia).\"  Why B wrong: 140/90 defines mild disease and does not automatically warrant treatment.  Why C wrong: 130/80 is below the treatment threshold.  Why D wrong: 120/80 is normal BP."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In mild pre-eclampsia, antihypertensive is given if the BP is continuously raised OR if BP is ≥150/100 mm Hg on how many occasions?","options":["On 2 occasions","On 1 occasion","On 3 occasions","On 4 occasions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for mild pre-eclampsia: \"BP continuously ↑ or BP ≥150/100 mm Hg on 2 occasions.\"  Why B wrong: A single reading is not the stated criterion.  Why C wrong: 3 occasions is not stated.  Why D wrong: 4 occasions is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In mild pre-eclampsia, when antihypertensive is indicated, which route is used per the notes?","options":["Oral antihypertensive","Intravenous antihypertensive","Intramuscular antihypertensive","Subcutaneous antihypertensive"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state mild pre-eclampsia treatment uses \"ORAL Anti-hypertensive\" (≥150/100 mm Hg on 2 occasions).  Why B wrong: IV is reserved for severe pre-eclampsia/eclampsia/crisis.  Why C wrong: IM route is not mentioned for this.  Why D wrong: Subcutaneous route is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In severe pre-eclampsia and eclampsia, antihypertensive must always be given by which route?","options":["Intravenous (I/V)","Oral","Rectal","Intramuscular"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for severe pre-eclampsia and eclampsia \"Always have to give antihypertensive (I/V).\"  Why B wrong: Oral is used for mild pre-eclampsia, not severe/eclampsia.  Why C wrong: Rectal route is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: IM route is not the stated route."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"(SATA) Which antihypertensives CAN be given in pregnancy per the notes?","options":["Labetalol","Methyldopa","Hydralazine","Nifedipine","Enalapril"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Can be given in pregnancy\" list includes labetalol, α-methyldopa, hydralazine, nifedipine, nitroglycerine, and sodium nitroprusside.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four appear on the safe list.  Why E wrong: Enalapril is an ACE inhibitor, listed under Contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Labetalol acts as which type of agent?","options":["Combined alpha + beta blocker","Pure alpha agonist","Calcium channel blocker","ACE inhibitor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe labetalol as \"(α + β blocks),\" i.e., a combined alpha and beta blocker.  Why B wrong: It is a blocker, not an alpha agonist (that describes methyldopa's action differently).  Why C wrong: Nifedipine is the calcium channel blocker, not labetalol.  Why D wrong: ACE inhibitors are contraindicated and are a different class."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following antihypertensives are CONTRAINDICATED in pregnancy?","options":["ACE inhibitors (enalapril, captopril)","Losartan","Diuretics","Diazoxide","Hydralazine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The contraindicated list includes ACE inhibitors (enalapril, captopril), losartan, diuretics, diazoxide, and β-blockers only.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four appear in the contraindicated column.  Why E wrong: Hydralazine is on the \"can be given in pregnancy\" (safe) list."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Which ACE inhibitors are specifically named as contraindicated in pregnancy?","options":["Enalapril and captopril","Labetalol and methyldopa","Nifedipine and hydralazine","Nitroglycerine and nitroprusside"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes name \"Ace inhibitors = Enalapril, captopril\" as contraindicated.  Why B wrong: Labetalol and methyldopa are permitted, not contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Nifedipine and hydralazine are on the safe list.  Why D wrong: Nitroglycerine and nitroprusside are on the safe list."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Regarding beta-blockers in pregnancy, which statement matches the notes?","options":["\"β-blockers only\" (pure beta-blockers) are contraindicated, but labetalol (α+β) can be given","All beta-blockers including labetalol are contraindicated","All beta-blockers are safe in pregnancy","Only pure beta-blockers are the drug of choice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The contraindicated list includes \"β-blockers only,\" while labetalol (a combined α+β blocker) is listed as safe.  Why B wrong: Labetalol is explicitly safe, so not all beta-blockers are contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Pure (\"only\") beta-blockers are contraindicated, so not all are safe.  Why D wrong: Pure beta-blockers are contraindicated, not the drug of choice."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"What is the drug of choice (DOC) for PIH in pregnancy?","options":["Labetalol","Enalapril","Losartan","Diazoxide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"DOC for PIH in Pregnancy - LABETALOL.\"  Why B wrong: Enalapril (ACE inhibitor) is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Losartan is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Diazoxide is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"What is the drug of choice for pre-eclampsia in pregnancy?","options":["Labetalol","Captopril","Furosemide","Losartan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Pre-eclampsia in Pregnancy - LABETALOL.\"  Why B wrong: Captopril is a contraindicated ACE inhibitor.  Why C wrong: Furosemide is a diuretic, contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Losartan is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"For chronic hypertension in pregnancy, what is the BEST answer drug of choice?","options":["Labetalol","Methyldopa","Nifedipine","Hydralazine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state chronic hypertension in pregnancy \"Best Answer: LABETALOL.\"  Why B wrong: Methyldopa is the 2nd best answer, not the best.  Why C wrong: Nifedipine is not listed as the best answer for chronic HTN.  Why D wrong: Hydralazine is not the best answer for chronic HTN."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"For chronic hypertension in pregnancy, what is the 2nd BEST answer drug?","options":["α-Methyldopa","Labetalol","Sodium nitroprusside","Losartan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"2nd Best Answer: α-METHYLDOPA\" for chronic hypertension.  Why B wrong: Labetalol is the best answer, not the 2nd best.  Why C wrong: Sodium nitroprusside is not listed for chronic HTN.  Why D wrong: Losartan is contraindicated in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In a hypertensive crisis in pregnancy (sudden BP ≥160/110 mm Hg), what is the BEST answer drug of choice?","options":["Labetalol I/V","Oral methyldopa","Oral nifedipine","Enalapril I/V"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state hypertensive crisis \"Best Answer: Labetalol I/V.\"  Why B wrong: Methyldopa is slow-acting and cannot be used in a crisis.  Why C wrong: Oral routes are not used in a crisis (IV is required).  Why D wrong: Enalapril is contraindicated in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In a hypertensive crisis in pregnancy, what is the 2nd BEST answer drug?","options":["Hydralazine I/V","Sodium nitroprusside","Methyldopa oral","Losartan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state hypertensive crisis \"2nd Best Answer: hydralazine I/V.\"  Why B wrong: Nitroprusside is not listed as the 2nd best for crisis here.  Why C wrong: Methyldopa is slow-acting and unsuitable for a crisis.  Why D wrong: Losartan is contraindicated in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"A hypertensive crisis in pregnancy is defined as a sudden BP of:","options":["≥160/110 mm Hg","≥140/90 mm Hg","≥150/100 mm Hg","≥130/85 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define hypertensive crisis as \"Suddenly BP ≥160/110 mm of Hg.\"  Why B wrong: 140/90 is the mild-preeclampsia threshold.  Why C wrong: 150/100 is an oral-treatment trigger in mild disease.  Why D wrong: 130/85 is below the crisis threshold."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In a hypertensive crisis, which route of antihypertensive is used?","options":["I/V (intravenous), not oral","Oral only","Rectal","Transdermal patch"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Use: I/V Antihypertensive (not oral)\" for hypertensive crisis.  Why B wrong: Oral is specifically excluded in a crisis.  Why C wrong: Rectal route is not indicated.  Why D wrong: A transdermal patch is not used for acute crisis."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In the IV labetalol regimen for hypertensive crisis, what is the starting dose?","options":["20 mg","40 mg","80 mg","10 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The regimen starts at \"20 mg\" IV labetalol.  Why B wrong: 40 mg is the second dose, not the starting dose.  Why C wrong: 80 mg is a later dose in the escalation.  Why D wrong: 10 mg is the time interval (10 min), not a dose."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"In the IV labetalol regimen, at what time interval is the dose escalated?","options":["Every 10 minutes","Every 5 minutes","Every 20 minutes","Every 30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The regimen shows escalation \"↓10 min\" between each dose (20 → 40 → 80 → 80 mg).  Why B wrong: 5 minutes is not the stated interval.  Why C wrong: 20 minutes is not the stated interval.  Why D wrong: 30 minutes is not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"The IV labetalol dose escalation for hypertensive crisis follows which sequence?","options":["20 mg → 40 mg → 80 mg → 80 mg (each 10 min apart)","10 mg → 20 mg → 40 mg → 80 mg","40 mg → 80 mg → 160 mg → 220 mg","20 mg → 40 mg → 60 mg → 80 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The regimen escalates 20 mg → 40 mg → 80 → 80 mg, each 10 minutes apart.  Why B wrong: The starting dose is 20 mg, not 10 mg.  Why C wrong: These doses do not match the stated schedule.  Why D wrong: The third and fourth doses are 80 mg, not 60 mg then 80 mg."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"What is the MAXIMUM total IV labetalol dose in a hypertensive crisis?","options":["220 mg I/V","100 mg I/V","300 mg I/V","80 mg I/V"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Maximum: 220 mg I/V.\"  Why B wrong: 100 mg is not the stated maximum.  Why C wrong: 300 mg exceeds the stated maximum.  Why D wrong: 80 mg is a single dose in the schedule, not the maximum total."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"(SATA) Besides IV labetalol, which other drugs can be used in a hypertensive crisis per the notes?","options":["I/V Hydralazine","Sustained release nifedipine","I/V Nitroglycerine","Oral methyldopa","Enalapril"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list I/V hydralazine, sustained-release nifedipine, and I/V nitroglycerine as other usable drugs.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed as alternatives.  Why D wrong: Methyldopa is slow-acting and cannot be used in a crisis.  Why E wrong: Enalapril (ACE inhibitor) is contraindicated in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"Why can methyldopa NOT be used in a hypertensive crisis?","options":["It is a slow-acting drug","It is contraindicated in pregnancy","It causes fetal death","It is only available orally-once daily"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Methyldopa is a slow acting drug → cannot be used in Hypertensive crisis.\"  Why B wrong: Methyldopa is actually permitted (safe) in pregnancy; the issue is its slow onset.  Why C wrong: The reason given is slow action, not fetal death.  Why D wrong: The stated reason is slow action, not its dosing frequency."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"A patient with severe preeclampsia at 35 weeks develops sudden BP of 170/115 mm Hg. Which is the priority pharmacologic action?","options":["Give I/V labetalol (best answer for hypertensive crisis)","Start oral methyldopa","Give an ACE inhibitor","Give a diuretic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BP ≥160/110 suddenly is a hypertensive crisis; the best answer is IV labetalol.  Why B wrong: Methyldopa is slow-acting and unsuitable for a crisis.  Why C wrong: ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Diuretics are contraindicated in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"A 28-week pregnant woman with severe preeclampsia is found to have low platelets, elevated liver enzymes, and hemolysis. This clinical picture indicates:","options":["HELLP syndrome","Gestational diabetes","Placenta previa","Hyperemesis gravidarum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hemolysis + Elevated Liver enzymes + Low Platelets = HELLP syndrome, a complication of severe preeclampsia.  Why B wrong: These findings are not diabetes features.  Why C wrong: Placenta previa presents with painless bleeding, not this triad.  Why D wrong: Hyperemesis is severe vomiting, unrelated to this triad."},{"section":"Batch 047 · Renal effects of PIH…","question":"A woman with HELLP syndrome at 32 weeks is being managed. According to the termination table, when should delivery occur?","options":["Immediately, irrespective of gestational age","At 34 weeks","At 37 weeks","After spontaneous labor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HELLP syndrome mandates immediate delivery irrespective of gestational age.  Why B wrong: 34 weeks applies to uncomplicated severe preeclampsia.  Why C wrong: 37 weeks applies to mild preeclampsia.  Why D wrong: Delivery must be immediate, not awaited."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which is the safest anti-hypertensive drug used in pregnancy?","options":["Labetalol","Methyldopa","Sodium nitroprusside","Nifedipine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the safest anti-hypertensive in pregnancy is Methyldopa (a centrally acting agent with a long safety record in pregnancy).  Why A wrong: Labetalol is used but is listed as the oral DOC in mild pre-eclampsia, not labelled the \"safest\".  Why C wrong: Sodium nitroprusside is the last resort/refractory-hypertension drug, not the safest.  Why D wrong: Nifedipine is used in pregnancy but is not named as the safest in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"If all anti-hypertensives fail to control blood pressure in pregnancy, what is the last resort drug?","options":["Methyldopa","Hydralazine","Sodium nitroprusside","Labetalol"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"If all Anti-Hypertensives fail, then last Resort = Sodium Nitroprusside\".  Why A wrong: Methyldopa is the safest first-line, not the last resort.  Why B wrong: Hydralazine is not mentioned as the last resort here.  Why D wrong: Labetalol is a routine agent, not the last resort."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What is the drug of choice (DOC) for refractory hypertension in pregnancy?","options":["Sodium nitroprusside","Methyldopa","Magnesium sulfate","Nifedipine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the DOC for refractory hypertension in pregnancy is Sodium Nitroprusside.  Why B wrong: Methyldopa is the safest routine agent, not for refractory disease.  Why C wrong: MgSO4 is for convulsion prevention/treatment, not a routine anti-hypertensive.  Why D wrong: Nifedipine is not named as the refractory-hypertension DOC."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Mild pre-eclampsia is defined by which blood pressure range?","options":["BP < 140/90","BP ≥ 140/90 but < 160/110","BP ≥ 160/110","BP ≥ 180/120"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The heading \"Management of Mild Pre-eclampsia\" specifies BP ≥ 140/90 but < 160/110.  Why A wrong: BP < 140/90 is normotensive, not pre-eclampsia.  Why C wrong: BP ≥ 160/110 defines severe pre-eclampsia.  Why D wrong: 180/120 is a hypertensive crisis level, beyond the mild definition."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In the management of mild pre-eclampsia, which of the following has NO role?","options":["Monitoring fetal movements","Bed rest and dietary salt restriction","Frequent rest in left lateral position","OPD-based follow up"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly say \"No role of: Bed Rest, Dietary salt restriction\" in mild pre-eclampsia.  Why A wrong: Monitoring fetal movements is advised.  Why C wrong: Frequent rest in the left lateral position is advised.  Why D wrong: Mild pre-eclampsia is managed on an OPD basis, which is recommended."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"On what basis is mild pre-eclampsia generally managed?","options":["ICU admission","Inpatient (hospitalized)","OPD basis","Home delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state mild pre-eclampsia is \"Managed on: OPD Basis\".  Why A wrong: ICU is not required for mild disease.  Why B wrong: Inpatient/hospitalized management is for severe pre-eclampsia.  Why D wrong: Home delivery is not the management basis described."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"A woman with mild pre-eclampsia is advised to take frequent rest in which position?","options":["Supine position","Right lateral position","Left lateral position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise \"Take frequent rest = in left lateral position\" (improves uteroplacental blood flow by relieving aortocaval compression).  Why A wrong: Supine position can cause aortocaval compression and is not advised.  Why B wrong: The notes specify left, not right, lateral.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg is not the advised rest position."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In mild pre-eclampsia, an anti-hypertensive is started if BP is continuously rising OR BP is at least what value on at least 2 occasions?","options":["≥ 140/90","≥ 150/100","≥ 160/110","≥ 130/80"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state anti-hypertensive is started \"If BP is continuously rising (or) BP ≥ 150/100 on at least 2 occasions\".  Why A wrong: 140/90 is the threshold for diagnosis, not for starting a drug here.  Why C wrong: 160/110 defines severe pre-eclampsia (IV therapy).  Why D wrong: 130/80 is below the stated trigger."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"When an oral anti-hypertensive is required in mild pre-eclampsia, which is the drug of choice?","options":["Methyldopa","Labetalol","Sodium nitroprusside","Magnesium sulfate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the oral DOC in mild pre-eclampsia is Labetalol.  Why A wrong: Methyldopa is the \"safest\" agent but the notes specify Labetalol as the oral DOC here.  Why C wrong: Sodium nitroprusside is the refractory/last-resort drug.  Why D wrong: MgSO4 is for seizure prevention, not blood pressure control."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What is the definitive management of pre-eclampsia?","options":["Lifelong anti-hypertensives","Termination of pregnancy (delivery)","Bed rest until term","Magnesium sulfate infusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state definitive management is Termination of Pregnancy, i.e. delivery.  Why A wrong: Anti-hypertensives control BP but do not cure pre-eclampsia.  Why C wrong: Bed rest has no definitive role.  Why D wrong: MgSO4 prevents/treats convulsion but is not the definitive cure."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In mild pre-eclampsia, at what gestational age is delivery (termination of pregnancy) planned as definitive management?","options":["34 weeks","37 weeks","40 weeks","Irrespective of gestational age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state definitive management in mild pre-eclampsia is delivery at 37 weeks.  Why A wrong: 34 weeks is the delivery timing for severe pre-eclampsia.  Why C wrong: 40 weeks is not specified; delivery is planned earlier at 37 weeks.  Why D wrong: \"Irrespective of gestational age\" applies to eclampsia, not mild pre-eclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What is the preferred mode of delivery in mild pre-eclampsia?","options":["Elective caesarean section","Vaginal delivery","Forceps delivery only","Vacuum extraction only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Mode of delivery = VAGINAL delivery\".  Why A wrong: Caesarean is not the default; vaginal delivery is preferred.  Why C wrong: Forceps is an instrument, not the routine mode specified.  Why D wrong: Vacuum extraction is not specified as the mode."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"How is severe pre-eclampsia generally managed?","options":["On OPD basis with follow up","As inpatient (hospitalized) with best rest advised","Home management only","No treatment until convulsion occurs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state severe pre-eclampsia is \"Generally managed as inpatient (Hospitalized)\" with best rest advised.  Why A wrong: OPD management is for mild pre-eclampsia.  Why C wrong: Home management is inadequate for severe disease.  Why D wrong: Active management is required immediately, not only after convulsion."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In severe pre-eclampsia, how frequently should blood pressure be checked?","options":["Once daily","Every 12 hours","At least 6 hourly","Every hour continuously"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"BP = at least 6 hourly\".  Why A wrong: Once daily is too infrequent for severe disease.  Why B wrong: The notes specify at least 6 hourly, not 12 hourly.  Why D wrong: Hourly is not the interval given in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which of the following is part of the monitoring checklist in severe pre-eclampsia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Weight","Input-output chart","Proteinuria","Coagulation profile","Platelet count"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Check items: BP 6 hourly, Weight, Input-output chart, Proteinuria, Coagulation profile, LFT/KFT, and Platelet count — all listed options are monitored.  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each appears explicitly in the \"Check\" list for severe pre-eclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which liver and kidney function investigations are checked in severe pre-eclampsia?","options":["LFT/KFT","Only complete blood count","Only thyroid function tests","Only blood glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"LFT/KFT\" (liver and kidney function tests) among the checks.  Why B wrong: CBC alone is not what is specified for organ function here.  Why C wrong: Thyroid tests are not listed.  Why D wrong: Blood glucose is not the specified organ-function check."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In severe pre-eclampsia, fetal monitoring is done with NST or biophysical score at what frequency?","options":["Once a month","Once weekly","Twice weekly","Daily"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Fetal monitoring: NST or Biophysical score (Twice weekly)\".  Why A wrong: Monthly is too infrequent.  Why B wrong: The notes specify twice weekly, not once weekly.  Why D wrong: Daily is not stated for NST/BPP."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In severe pre-eclampsia, fundus (ophthalmic) examination is performed to detect what?","options":["Fetal position","Visual symptoms","Placental location","Cervical dilatation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Fundus Examination: Visual symptom\" — fundoscopy assesses retinal changes/visual disturbance.  Why A wrong: Fetal position is assessed by abdominal/ultrasound examination, not fundoscopy.  Why C wrong: Placental location is assessed by ultrasound.  Why D wrong: Cervical dilatation is assessed by pelvic examination."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which sign of impending eclampsia should be asked about daily in severe pre-eclampsia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Decreased urine output","Headache","Visual symptoms (blurring of vision/diplopia/blindness)","Epigastric pain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list signs of impending eclampsia to ask about daily: ↓urine output, headache, visual symptoms (blurring of vision/diplopia/blindness), and epigastric pain.  Why all correct: Each is explicitly listed among the impending-eclampsia signs."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"The visual symptoms of impending eclampsia include which of the following?","options":["Blurring of vision, diplopia, and blindness","Night blindness only","Color blindness only","Tunnel vision only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state visual symptoms = blurring of vision / DIPLOPIA / BLINDNESS.  Why B wrong: Night blindness is not listed.  Why C wrong: Color blindness is not listed.  Why D wrong: Tunnel vision is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Epigastric pain in a pre-eclamptic woman is significant because it is a sign of what?","options":["Normal pregnancy discomfort","Impending eclampsia","Labour onset","Gestational diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list epigastric pain among the signs of impending eclampsia (reflects hepatic capsule stretch/liver involvement).  Why A wrong: It is a warning sign, not benign discomfort.  Why C wrong: It is not a sign of labour onset.  Why D wrong: It is not linked to gestational diabetes here."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In addition to daily assessment of impending eclampsia signs, what fetal assessment should be performed in severe pre-eclampsia?","options":["Fetal heart sound should be auscultated","Amniocentesis daily","Chorionic villus sampling","Fetal blood transfusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Fetal heart sound should be auscultated\".  Why B wrong: Daily amniocentesis is not indicated.  Why C wrong: CVS is a first-trimester genetic test, not relevant here.  Why D wrong: Fetal transfusion is not part of routine monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Severe pre-eclampsia (requiring IV anti-hypertensive) is defined by which blood pressure threshold?","options":["≥ 140/90 mm Hg","≥ 150/100 mm Hg","≥ 160/110 mm Hg","≥ 130/80 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in severe pre-eclampsia (≥ 160/110 mm Hg) IV anti-hypertensive must be given.  Why A wrong: 140/90 is the diagnostic threshold for pre-eclampsia (mild).  Why B wrong: 150/100 triggers oral therapy in mild disease.  Why D wrong: 130/80 is below all stated thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which IV anti-hypertensive is given in severe pre-eclampsia?","options":["IV Labetalol","IV Methyldopa","IV Sodium nitroprusside","IV Nifedipine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify IV Labetalol for severe pre-eclampsia.  Why B wrong: Methyldopa is oral (safest agent) and is not given IV here.  Why C wrong: Sodium nitroprusside is reserved for refractory/last-resort cases.  Why D wrong: Nifedipine is not the IV agent named."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which drug is given to prevent seizures in a hypertensive pregnant patient and is the DOC to prevent AND treat convulsion?","options":["Diazepam","Phenytoin","Magnesium sulfate (MgSO4)","Labetalol"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the drug to prevent seizure is MgSO4, which is the DOC to prevent AND treat convulsion in hypertensive pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Diazepam is not the DOC described here.  Why B wrong: Phenytoin (an antiepileptic) is not used; the notes stress \"No antiepileptic drug\".  Why D wrong: Labetalol controls BP, not seizures."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In severe pre-eclampsia, at what gestational age is delivery (definitive management) planned?","options":["≥ 34 weeks","≥ 37 weeks","≥ 40 weeks","≥ 28 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state definitive management of severe pre-eclampsia is termination of pregnancy at ≥ 34 weeks (mode = vaginal).  Why B wrong: 37 weeks is the timing for mild pre-eclampsia.  Why C wrong: 40 weeks is not the target for severe disease.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Eclampsia is best defined as:","options":["Mild pre-eclampsia with proteinuria","Severe pre-eclampsia plus convulsion","Gestational hypertension without proteinuria","Chronic hypertension in pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define eclampsia as \"Severe Pre-Eclampsia + Convulsion\".  Why A wrong: Pre-eclampsia with proteinuria alone is not eclampsia.  Why C wrong: Gestational hypertension without proteinuria is not eclampsia.  Why D wrong: Chronic hypertension precedes pregnancy and is not eclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"During an eclamptic convulsion, what is the very first (1st step) in management?","options":["Give O2 by mask","Airway management and turn patient to left lateral position","Start MgSO4","Prepare patient for delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the 1st step as airway management and turning the patient to the left lateral position (protects airway, prevents aspiration).  Why A wrong: Oxygen follows airway management, not before it.  Why C wrong: MgSO4 is started after airway/oxygen (step 4).  Why D wrong: Delivery preparation is the last step."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In the immediate management of eclampsia, before starting drugs the nurse should first:","options":["Leave the patient to call the doctor","Stay near the patient and call for help","Administer oral fluids","Perform vaginal examination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the first action as \"Stay near the patient & call for help\".  Why A wrong: The nurse should not leave the convulsing patient unattended.  Why C wrong: Oral fluids are dangerous during a convulsion (aspiration risk).  Why D wrong: Vaginal examination is not the immediate priority during a seizure."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"During an eclamptic fit, oxygen is administered by which route?","options":["Nasal cannula at 1 L/min","O2 by mask","Endotracheal tube routinely","Oxygen is contraindicated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Give O2 by mask\".  Why A wrong: Low-flow nasal cannula is not what is specified.  Why C wrong: Routine intubation is not listed as the immediate step.  Why D wrong: Oxygen is indicated, not contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In eclampsia management, MgSO4 is started because it plays the role of DOC for:","options":["Blood pressure control only","Treatment and prevention of convulsion","Pain relief","Uterine contraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Start MgSO4: Role DOC for treatment & prevention of convulsion\".  Why A wrong: BP control is provided by labetalol, not MgSO4.  Why C wrong: MgSO4 is not given for pain relief here.  Why D wrong: MgSO4 is not used for uterine contraction in this context."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which anti-hypertensive is given during eclampsia management?","options":["IV Labetalol","Oral methyldopa","IV magnesium sulfate","Sublingual nifedipine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Give Antihypertensive: I/V Labetalol\".  Why B wrong: Oral methyldopa is inappropriate during an acute eclamptic emergency.  Why C wrong: MgSO4 is for convulsion control, not blood pressure.  Why D wrong: Sublingual nifedipine is not the agent named here."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"After stabilizing an eclamptic patient (airway, oxygen, MgSO4, anti-hypertensive), what should be checked next according to the sequence?","options":["Fetal heart rate","Blood glucose","Maternal weight","Urine protein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Check fetal heart rate\" (step 6) after the stabilizing measures.  Why B wrong: Blood glucose is not the next step listed.  Why C wrong: Weight is a monitoring parameter, not the immediate next step in eclampsia.  Why D wrong: Urine protein is monitored in pre-eclampsia care, not the acute eclampsia step here."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What is the final step in the immediate management of eclampsia?","options":["Discharge the patient","Prepare patient for delivery","Start oral anti-hypertensives","Begin physiotherapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Prepare patient for delivery\" as the last (7th) step.  Why A wrong: Discharge is inappropriate; delivery is the definitive treatment.  Why C wrong: Oral anti-hypertensives are not the acute final step.  Why D wrong: Physiotherapy is irrelevant to the acute management."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In eclampsia, the definitive management is immediate delivery that is performed:","options":["Only after 37 weeks","Only after 34 weeks","Irrespective of gestational age","Only after fetal lung maturity is confirmed"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state definitive management of eclampsia is immediate delivery \"Irrespective of gestational age\".  Why A wrong: 37 weeks applies to mild pre-eclampsia, not eclampsia.  Why B wrong: 34 weeks applies to severe pre-eclampsia.  Why D wrong: Delivery is not delayed for lung maturity in eclampsia."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In PIH/pre-eclampsia/eclampsia/gestational hypertension, what is the best mode of delivery?","options":["Elective caesarean section","Vaginal delivery","Instrumental delivery only","Depends solely on maternal choice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in PIH/pre-eclampsia/eclampsia/gestational hypertension, the best mode of delivery is vaginal delivery.  Why A wrong: Caesarean is not the default best mode.  Why C wrong: Instrumental delivery is not routinely the best mode.  Why D wrong: The mode is clinically determined (vaginal preferred), not solely maternal choice."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"MgSO4 is the drug of choice for what purpose in a hypertensive pregnant female?","options":["Lowering blood pressure","Preventing and treating convulsions","Inducing labour","Treating anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state MgSO4 is the DOC for preventing and treating convulsions in a hypertensive pregnant female.  Why A wrong: BP is lowered by labetalol/other anti-hypertensives.  Why C wrong: MgSO4 is not a labour-inducing agent.  Why D wrong: MgSO4 does not treat anemia."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Regarding the classification of MgSO4, which statement is correct?","options":["It is an antiepileptic drug","It is NOT an antiepileptic drug","It is a beta-blocker","It is a diuretic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Remember: No antiepileptic drug\" — MgSO4 is not classed as an antiepileptic.  Why A wrong: The notes explicitly deny it is an antiepileptic.  Why C wrong: It is not a beta-blocker (that describes labetalol).  Why D wrong: It is not a diuretic."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"MgSO4 is described as which type of drug by its mode of action?","options":["Peripherally acting drug","Centrally acting drug","Locally acting drug","Topical agent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe MgSO4 as a \"Centrally acting drug\" acting on the brain.  Why A wrong: It is centrally, not peripherally, acting per the notes.  Why C wrong: It is not a locally acting drug.  Why D wrong: It is not a topical agent."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Arrange the mode of action of MgSO4: it acts on the brain, then causes which of the following that ultimately stops convulsion?","options":["Vasoconstriction of cerebral vessels → decreased blood flow","Vasodilation of cerebral brain vessels → increased blood flow in brain","No change in cerebral blood flow","Direct muscle paralysis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe: acts on brain → vasodilation of cerebral brain vessels → ↑blood flow in brain → stopping of convulsion.  Why A wrong: It causes vasodilation and increased (not decreased) flow.  Why C wrong: Cerebral blood flow increases per the mechanism.  Why D wrong: The described central mechanism is via cerebral vasodilation, not simple muscle paralysis."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which regimen is followed for administering MgSO4 in eclampsia/pre-eclampsia per the notes?","options":["Zuspan regime","Pritchard regime","Sibai regime","Collaborative regime"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"We follow: PRITCHARD Regime\".  Why A wrong: The Zuspan regime is a different (IV-only) protocol not named in these notes.  Why C wrong: Sibai regime is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: \"Collaborative regime\" is not the named protocol."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In the Pritchard regime, what is the IV component of the MgSO4 loading dose?","options":["4 gm of 20% MgSO4 (slow IV)","5 gm of 50% MgSO4 IV","10 gm of 50% MgSO4 IV","14 gm of 20% MgSO4 IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the loading dose IV = 4 gm of 20% (slow IV) MgSO4.  Why B wrong: 5 gm of 50% is the maintenance/IM component, not the IV loading part.  Why C wrong: 10 gm of 50% is the total IM loading component, not IV.  Why D wrong: 14 gm is the total loading dose (IV + IM), not the IV part alone."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In the Pritchard regime loading dose, what is the IM component of MgSO4?","options":["4 gm of 20% IM","10 gm of 50% (5 gm in each buttock)","5 gm of 50% in one buttock","14 gm of 50% IM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the loading dose IM = 10 gm of 50% (5 gm in each buttock).  Why A wrong: 4 gm of 20% is the IV component.  Why C wrong: 5 gm in one buttock is the maintenance dose, not the loading IM.  Why D wrong: 14 gm is the grand total loading dose, not the IM component."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What is the total loading dose of MgSO4 in the Pritchard regime (IV + IM)?","options":["10 gm","12 gm","14 gm","20 gm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 4 gm IV + 10 gm IM = 14 gm total loading dose, as stated in the notes.  Why A wrong: 10 gm is only the IM portion.  Why B wrong: 12 gm is the volume of NS used in dilution, not the total dose.  Why D wrong: 20 gm is the total volume in mL of the diluted IV syringe, not the drug dose."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What is the maintenance dose of MgSO4 in the Pritchard regime?","options":["4 gm of 20% IV","5 gm of 50% MgSO4 IM (on alternate buttock)","10 gm of 50% IM","14 gm total"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maintenance dose = 5 gm of 50% MgSO4 IM on alternate buttock.  Why A wrong: 4 gm of 20% IV is the loading IV dose.  Why C wrong: 10 gm of 50% IM is the loading IM dose.  Why D wrong: 14 gm is the total loading dose."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"According to the vial label, what is the concentration and strength of the Magnesium Sulfate Injection USP shown?","options":["50% (5 grams/10 mL)","20% (4 grams/10 mL)","10% (1 gram/10 mL)","25% (2.5 grams/10 mL)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vial reads \"Magnesium Sulfate Injection, USP 50% (5 grams/10 mL)\".  Why B wrong: 20% is the diluted IV concentration prepared from the vial, not the vial label.  Why C wrong: 10% describes calcium gluconate antidote strength, not this vial.  Why D wrong: 25% is not the labelled concentration."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"The MgSO4 vial states it must be handled how before IV use?","options":["Given undiluted","Must be diluted before IV use","Warmed to body temperature","Frozen before use"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The vial label states \"Must be Diluted before IV Use\".  Why A wrong: It must NOT be given undiluted IV.  Why C wrong: Warming is not the labelled instruction.  Why D wrong: Freezing is not required."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"For the IM loading dose of 10 gm of 50% MgSO4, how is it distributed?","options":["10 gm in one buttock","5 gm in left buttock and 5 gm in right buttock","8 gm left, 2 gm right","Given as a single IV bolus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes/diagram show IM = 10 gm of 50% solution → 5 gm left buttock + 5 gm right buttock.  Why A wrong: The dose is split between both buttocks, not one.  Why C wrong: The split is equal (5 + 5), not 8 + 2.  Why D wrong: This is an IM dose, not an IV bolus."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"To prepare the IV loading dose (4 gm in 20% solution), what size empty syringe is used?","options":["10 mL syringe","20 mL empty syringe","5 mL syringe","50 mL syringe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Take a 20 ml empty syringe\" for the IV dose preparation.  Why A wrong: 10 mL is the vial volume, not the syringe used.  Why C wrong: 5 mL is too small for the 20 mL final volume.  Why D wrong: 50 mL is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"When preparing the IV MgSO4 loading dose in a 20 mL syringe, how much normal saline (NS) is drawn up?","options":["8 mL of NS","12 mL of NS","20 mL of NS","4 mL of NS"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows 12 mL of NS added to 8 mL of MgSO4 to make 20 mL.  Why A wrong: 8 mL is the volume of MgSO4, not NS.  Why C wrong: 20 mL is the total volume, not the NS alone.  Why D wrong: 4 mL refers to grams of drug, not NS volume."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In preparing the 20 mL IV MgSO4 dose, how much of the 50% MgSO4 solution is drawn up, and how many grams does it contain?","options":["12 mL containing 6 gm","8 mL containing 4 gm of MgSO4","10 mL containing 5 gm","4 mL containing 2 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows 8 mL of MgSO4 = 4 gm of MgSO4 (since 50% = 5 g/10 mL, 8 mL = 4 g).  Why A wrong: 12 mL is the NS volume, not MgSO4.  Why C wrong: 10 mL/5 gm is the full undiluted vial, not the drawn amount for IV.  Why D wrong: 4 mL/2 gm is incorrect for this preparation."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"After mixing 12 mL NS with 8 mL of 50% MgSO4 in a 20 mL syringe, what is the resulting dose and concentration?","options":["Total 20 mL MgSO4 = 4 gm (i.e. 20% solution)","Total 20 mL = 10 gm (50% solution)","Total 10 mL = 4 gm (40% solution)","Total 20 mL = 2 gm (10% solution)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Total 20 mL MgSO4 = 4 gm (i.e. ~20%), converting the 50% concentrate into a 20% IV solution.  Why B wrong: 10 gm/50% is the IM component, not this diluted IV mix.  Why C wrong: The total volume is 20 mL, not 10 mL.  Why D wrong: The concentration is 20%, not 10%."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"How is the MgSO4 maintenance dose (5 gm, 50%) administered and at what interval?","options":["IV push every hour","IM in alternate buttock every 4 hourly","IM in the same buttock every 2 hours","Oral every 6 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maintenance = IM 5 gm in alternate buttock (50%, from 10 mL vial) → every 4 hourly.  Why A wrong: Maintenance is IM, not IV push, and not hourly.  Why C wrong: It is given in alternate (not the same) buttock, every 4 hours.  Why D wrong: It is IM, not oral."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"MgSO4 is described as a drug with what kind of therapeutic index?","options":["Very high therapeutic index","Very low therapeutic index","No therapeutic index","Infinite therapeutic index"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"MgSO4 is a drug which has a very low Therapeutic Index\" (narrow safety margin requiring monitoring).  Why A wrong: It is low, not high.  Why C wrong: It has a defined therapeutic index (4–7 mEq/L).  Why D wrong: Not infinite; it is narrow."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What is the therapeutic index (therapeutic range) of magnesium for MgSO4 therapy?","options":["1–3 mEq/L","4–7 mEq/L","8–10 mEq/L","10–15 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Therapeutic Index = 4–7 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: <4 mEq/L is subtherapeutic (convulsion risk).  Why C wrong: >7 mEq/L is in the toxicity range.  Why D wrong: 10–15 mEq/L is toxic, not therapeutic."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"If the magnesium level in the body falls below 4 mEq/L, what is the likely consequence?","options":["Toxicity symptoms","Patient convulsion","No effect","Cardiac arrest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Mg in body < 4 mEq/L → patient convulsion (subtherapeutic level).  Why A wrong: Toxicity occurs when Mg > 7 mEq/L, not below 4.  Why C wrong: Below 4 the patient can convulse; there is an effect.  Why D wrong: Cardiac arrest occurs at very high toxic levels (≥30 mEq/L), not below 4."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"A magnesium concentration greater than 7 mEq/L in the body indicates what?","options":["Subtherapeutic level with convulsion risk","Toxicity symptoms","Ideal therapeutic level","Need to increase the dose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Mg > 7 mEq/L → toxicity symptom.  Why A wrong: Convulsion risk is at levels < 4 mEq/L.  Why C wrong: The ideal therapeutic level is 4–7 mEq/L.  Why D wrong: The dose should be reduced/withheld, not increased, in toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Before giving each MgSO4 maintenance dose, which three parameters must be checked for signs of toxicity? (Select all that apply)","options":["Urine output ≥ 30 mL/hr","Respiratory rate ≥ 12/min","Deep tendon reflexes/knee jerk present","Blood pressure < 120/80"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the three pre-maintenance-dose checks: urine output ≥ 30 mL/hr, respiratory rate ≥ 12/min, and deep tendon reflexes/knee jerk should be present.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed toxicity-screening parameters.  Why D wrong: A specific BP target is not one of the three MgSO4 toxicity-monitoring parameters."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What is the minimum acceptable urine output before administering a MgSO4 maintenance dose?","options":["≥ 10 mL/hr","≥ 20 mL/hr","≥ 30 mL/hr","≥ 50 mL/hr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes require urine output ≥ 30 mL/hr before giving the maintenance dose.  Why A/B wrong: These are below the stated safe threshold of 30 mL/hr.  Why D wrong: 50 mL/hr exceeds the stated minimum requirement."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What minimum respiratory rate must the patient have before a MgSO4 maintenance dose is given?","options":["≥ 8/min","≥ 10/min","≥ 12/min","≥ 16/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes require respiratory rate ≥ 12/min before giving the maintenance dose.  Why A/B wrong: These are below the stated safe threshold of 12/min.  Why D wrong: 16/min exceeds the stated minimum."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"If any of the three monitoring parameters is abnormal before a scheduled MgSO4 maintenance dose, what should the nurse do?","options":["Give the dose anyway","Do not give the maintenance dose at that time; recheck after 4 hours and give only if normal","Double the next dose","Give the antidote immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if any parameter is abnormal, do not give the maintenance dose at that time; go after 4 hours, check parameters again, and only if normal give the maintenance dose.  Why A wrong: Giving the dose despite abnormal parameters risks toxicity.  Why C wrong: Doubling the dose is dangerous and not indicated.  Why D wrong: The antidote is for established toxicity, not for a single abnormal parameter before a dose."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"The MgSO4 maintenance dose is given only if which condition is met?","options":["Only if all 3 monitoring parameters are normal","Regardless of parameters","Only if BP is elevated","Only during a convulsion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Give maintenance dose only if above 3 parameters are normal\".  Why B wrong: Parameters must be checked first.  Why C wrong: The trigger is normal toxicity parameters, not elevated BP.  Why D wrong: Maintenance dosing is scheduled, not tied to an active convulsion."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"For how long should the MgSO4 maintenance dose be continued?","options":["12 hours after delivery","24 hours after delivery OR 24 hours after the last convulsion","48 hours after delivery","Until discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maintenance dose should be given till 24 hours after delivery OR 24 hours after the last convulsion.  Why A wrong: 12 hours is too short per the notes.  Why C wrong: 48 hours is longer than stated.  Why D wrong: The endpoint is defined by delivery/last convulsion, not discharge."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What is the earliest sign of MgSO4 toxicity?","options":["Cardiac arrest","Loss of knee jerk/loss of deep tendon reflexes","Excessive sweating","Respiratory arrest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the earliest sign is loss of knee jerk/loss of deep tendon reflexes (occurring at Mg ≥ 10 mEq/L).  Why A wrong: Cardiac arrest is a very late sign (≥ 30 mEq/L).  Why C wrong: Diaphoresis is listed but is not the earliest sign.  Why D wrong: Respiratory arrest is a late sign."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Loss of deep tendon reflexes (earliest MgSO4 toxicity sign) corresponds to what magnesium concentration?","options":["≥ 4 mEq/L","≥ 7 mEq/L","≥ 10 mEq/L","≥ 30 mEq/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state loss of knee jerk/deep tendon reflexes occurs at Mg concentration ≥ 10 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: 4 mEq/L is the lower therapeutic limit.  Why B wrong: 7 mEq/L is the upper therapeutic limit.  Why D wrong: ≥ 30 mEq/L corresponds to cardiac arrest."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a sign/symptom of MgSO4 toxicity?","options":["Excessive sweating (diaphoresis)","Increased urine output","Hypertension","Hyperreflexia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list excessive sweating (diaphoresis) as a sign of MgSO4 toxicity.  Why B wrong: Toxicity is associated with decreased, not increased, urine output; also decreased urine output is specifically noted NOT to be a toxicity sign.  Why C wrong: Hypertension is not a listed toxicity sign.  Why D wrong: Toxicity causes loss (not increase) of reflexes."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Decreased respiratory rate (respiratory depression) as a sign of MgSO4 toxicity occurs at what magnesium concentration?","options":["≥ 10 mEq/L","≥ 15 mEq/L","≥ 7 mEq/L","≥ 30 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state decreased respiratory rate (respiratory depression) = Mg concentration ≥ 15 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: ≥ 10 mEq/L corresponds to loss of reflexes.  Why C wrong: 7 mEq/L is the upper therapeutic limit.  Why D wrong: ≥ 30 mEq/L corresponds to cardiac arrest."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which is a late/severe sign of MgSO4 toxicity beyond respiratory depression?","options":["Respiratory arrest","Increased knee jerk","Polyuria","Tachypnea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list respiratory arrest (item 4) as a severe MgSO4 toxicity sign following respiratory depression.  Why B wrong: Reflexes are lost, not increased.  Why C wrong: Polyuria is not a toxicity sign.  Why D wrong: Toxicity causes respiratory depression, not tachypnea."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Cardiac arrest from MgSO4 toxicity occurs at what magnesium concentration?","options":["≥ 10 mEq/L","≥ 15 mEq/L","≥ 30 mEq/L","≥ 7 mEq/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state cardiac arrest = Mg concentration ≥ 30 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: ≥ 10 mEq/L is loss of reflexes.  Why B wrong: ≥ 15 mEq/L is respiratory depression.  Why D wrong: 7 mEq/L is the upper therapeutic limit."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"What is the antidote for MgSO4 toxicity?","options":["10% calcium gluconate, 10 mL","Naloxone","Vitamin K","Protamine sulfate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the antidote for MgSO4 toxicity is 10% calcium gluconate, 10 mL.  Why B wrong: Naloxone reverses opioids, not magnesium.  Why C wrong: Vitamin K reverses warfarin.  Why D wrong: Protamine sulfate reverses heparin."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Which condition is a contraindication for MgSO4 administration?","options":["Hypertension","Renal failure","Diabetes","Anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state renal failure is a contraindication for MgSO4 (magnesium is renally excreted, so it accumulates in renal failure).  Why A wrong: Hypertensive pregnancy is an indication, not a contraindication.  Why C wrong: Diabetes is not listed as a contraindication.  Why D wrong: Anemia is not a listed contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Regarding decreased urine output and MgSO4, which statement is correct?","options":["Decreased urine output IS a direct sign of MgSO4 toxicity","Decreased urine output is NOT a sign of MgSO4 toxicity","Urine output need not be monitored","Increased urine output indicates toxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"Decreased Urine output is not sign of MgSO4 toxicity\" (it is monitored because low output causes Mg accumulation, not because it is a toxicity symptom itself).  Why A wrong: The notes state the opposite.  Why C wrong: Urine output must be measured before every dose.  Why D wrong: Increased urine output is not the toxicity indicator."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"Why is urine output measured every time before giving MgSO4?","options":["Because MgSO4 raises blood glucose","Because MgSO4 is excreted by the kidney, so if urine output is low, magnesium will accumulate in the body","Because MgSO4 causes hypertension","Because urine output predicts fetal wellbeing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state MgSO4 is excreted by the kidney; if urine output is less, Mg will accumulate in the body (risking toxicity).  Why A wrong: The rationale is renal excretion, not glucose effects.  Why C wrong: The reason is accumulation risk, not hypertension.  Why D wrong: Urine output monitoring here is for maternal drug safety, not fetal assessment."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"In which conditions should MgSO4 be given? (Select all that apply)","options":["Eclampsia patient","Severe preeclampsia","HELLP syndrome / impending eclampsia","Impending eclampsia","Renal failure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list MgSO4 indications as eclampsia, severe preeclampsia, HELLP syndrome/impending eclampsia, and impending eclampsia.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly listed indications.  Why E wrong: Renal failure is a contraindication, not an indication."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"A pre-eclamptic patient on MgSO4 develops loss of the knee-jerk reflex. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Continue the infusion as scheduled","Withhold MgSO4 and prepare 10% calcium gluconate 10 mL (the antidote)","Increase the MgSO4 dose","Give an extra dose of labetalol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Loss of knee jerk is the earliest sign of MgSO4 toxicity (Mg ≥ 10 mEq/L); the nurse must withhold MgSO4 and have the antidote (10% calcium gluconate 10 mL) ready.  Why A wrong: Continuing the drug during toxicity is dangerous.  Why C wrong: Increasing the dose worsens toxicity.  Why D wrong: Labetalol treats BP, not magnesium toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Anti-hypertensives in pregnancy, management of mild pre-eclampsia…","question":"A woman with severe pre-eclampsia at 35 weeks is stabilized. According to the notes, what is the definitive management?","options":["Continue conservative management to 40 weeks","Deliver (termination of pregnancy) at ≥ 34 weeks by vaginal route","Immediate caesarean regardless of gestation","Discharge on oral labetalol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For severe pre-eclampsia, definitive management is termination of pregnancy at ≥ 34 weeks with vaginal delivery as the preferred mode; at 35 weeks delivery is appropriate.  Why A wrong: Prolonging to 40 weeks is not advised in severe disease.  Why C wrong: Caesarean is not the default; vaginal delivery is preferred.  Why D wrong: Discharge without delivery is inappropriate for severe pre-eclampsia at this gestation."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"A multifetal pregnancy is best defined as a pregnancy in which the female carries:","options":["Exactly 2 fetuses only","More than 1 fetus","A fetus weighing more than average","A post-term fetus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Multifetal pregnancy is defined as a pregnancy in which the female carries more than one (>1) fetus.  Why A wrong: Two fetuses specifically is a twin pregnancy, a subtype, not the whole definition.  Why C wrong: Fetal weight is unrelated to the definition of multifetal pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Post-term refers to gestational duration, not fetal number."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"A pregnancy carrying 2 fetuses is termed:","options":["Twins","Triplets","Quadruplets","Singleton"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Twins refers to a pregnancy with 2 fetuses.  Why B wrong: Triplets = 3 fetuses.  Why C wrong: Quadruplets = 4 fetuses.  Why D wrong: A singleton is a single-fetus pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"A pregnancy carrying 3 fetuses is called:","options":["Twins","Triplets","Quadruplets","Quintuplets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Triplets = 3 fetuses.  Why A wrong: Twins = 2 fetuses.  Why C wrong: Quadruplets = 4 fetuses.  Why D wrong: Quintuplets (5) is not listed on the page."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"A pregnancy carrying 4 fetuses is called:","options":["Triplets","Twins","Quadruplets","Sextuplets"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Quadruplets = 4 fetuses.  Why A wrong: Triplets = 3 fetuses.  Why B wrong: Twins = 2 fetuses.  Why D wrong: Sextuplets (6) is not on the page."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"According to Hellen's rule, the incidence of twin pregnancy is:","options":["1 in 20 pregnancies","1 in 80 pregnancies","1 in 200 pregnancies","1 in 250 pregnancies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hellen's rule states the incidence of twin pregnancy = 1 in 80 pregnancies.  Why A wrong: 1 in 20 is the highest (dizygotic) incidence, seen in Nigeria.  Why C wrong: 1 in 200 is the lowest dizygotic incidence (Japan).  Why D wrong: 1 in 250 is the constant worldwide monozygotic incidence."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"According to Hellen's rule, the incidence of triplets is:","options":["1 in 80 pregnancies","1 in (80)² pregnancies","1 in 160 pregnancies","1 in (80)³ pregnancies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hellen's rule gives the incidence of triplets = 1 in (80)² pregnancies.  Why A wrong: 1 in 80 is the incidence of twins.  Why C wrong: 160 is not part of Hellen's rule.  Why D wrong: (80)³ would apply to quadruplets, not triplets."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which of the following is a risk factor for twin/multifetal pregnancy?","options":["Maternal family history of twinning","Low maternal age","First pregnancy (primigravida)","Absence of ovulation-inducing drugs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal family history of twinning is listed as a risk factor.  Why B wrong: Increased (not low) maternal age is the risk factor.  Why C wrong: Increased parity, not first pregnancy, raises the risk.  Why D wrong: Use of ovulation-inducing drugs (their presence) increases the risk."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Regarding maternal age as a risk factor for twinning, the maximum risk is at an age of:","options":["20–25 years","25–30 years","30–35 years","40–45 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states increased maternal age (maximum = 30–35 years) is a risk factor.  Why A/B wrong: These are below the maximum-risk age range given.  Why D wrong: 40–45 years is not the range specified."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Increased parity as a risk factor for twinning refers to a female who is more than:","options":["2nd gravida","3rd gravida","4th gravida","5th gravida"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The page specifies increased parity as female >5th gravida.  Why A/B/C wrong: The threshold given on the page is greater than the 5th gravida, not lower."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which ovulation-inducing drug is named as a risk factor for multifetal pregnancy?","options":["Clomiphene citrate","Methotrexate","Misoprostol","Oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes name clomiphene citrate as an ovulation-inducing drug that increases twinning risk.  Why B wrong: Methotrexate is used for medical abortion/ectopic, not ovulation induction.  Why C wrong: Misoprostol is a prostaglandin used for induction/abortion.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is a uterotonic for labour, not ovulation induction."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The chance of multifetal pregnancy with ovulation-inducing drugs is quoted on the page as:","options":["2.5%","5.8%","8.5%","12%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the chances with ovulation-inducing drugs are 5.8%.  Why A/C/D wrong: These figures do not match the value given."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which assisted-reproduction procedure is listed as a risk factor for multifetal pregnancy?","options":["In vitro fertilization (IVF)","Hysterosalpingography","Amniocentesis","Cervical cerclage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In vitro fertilization is listed among the procedures increasing multifetal pregnancy risk.  Why B wrong: HSG is a diagnostic tubal patency test, not a fertility-conception procedure listed here.  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis is a prenatal diagnostic procedure.  Why D wrong: Cervical cerclage treats cervical incompetence."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The purpose of fetal reduction in a multifetal pregnancy is to reduce it to a:","options":["Single pregnancy or twin pregnancy","Triplet pregnancy only","Quadruplet pregnancy","Ectopic pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal reduction aims to reduce a multifetal pregnancy to a single pregnancy or a twin pregnancy.  Why B/C wrong: These retain higher fetal numbers, contrary to the goal of reduction.  Why D wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is a pathological implantation, not a goal of reduction."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which drug is used for fetal reduction, given as an intracardiac injection?","options":["Potassium chloride","Magnesium sulphate","Calcium gluconate","Sodium bicarbonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states potassium chloride intracardiac injection is used for fetal reduction.  Why B wrong: Magnesium sulphate is used for eclampsia/tocolysis.  Why C wrong: Calcium gluconate is the antidote for magnesium toxicity.  Why D wrong: Sodium bicarbonate is used for acidosis correction."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Fetal reduction is typically performed at which gestational time?","options":["6–8 weeks","11–13 weeks","16–18 weeks","20–22 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the time for fetal reduction is 11–13 weeks.  Why A/C/D wrong: These gestational ranges are not the ones specified for fetal reduction."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Twins are broadly classified into which two types?","options":["Dizygotic and Monozygotic","Conjoined and Fraternal","Diamniotic and Monoamniotic","Chorionic and Amniotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Twins are classified as dizygotic and monozygotic (based on zygosity/conception).  Why B wrong: Conjoined and fraternal are subtypes, not the two broad classes.  Why C wrong: Diamniotic/monoamniotic describes membranes, not the primary classification.  Why D wrong: Chorion/amnion are membrane structures, not the two twin types."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Dizygotic twins are also known as:","options":["Fraternal twins","Identical twins","Conjoined twins","Siamese twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dizygotic twins are also called fraternal (non-identical) twins.  Why B wrong: Identical twins are monozygotic.  Why C/D wrong: Conjoined/Siamese twins are a subtype of monozygotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"How are dizygotic twins formed?","options":["Two ova each fertilized by a separate sperm forming two zygotes","One ovum and one sperm forming one zygote that divides","One ovum fertilized by two sperm","Two ova fertilized by one sperm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dizygotic twins arise from two ova (Ova 1 and Ova 2) each fertilized by its own sperm, producing Zygote 1 and Zygote 2.  Why B wrong: That describes monozygotic twinning.  Why C/D wrong: These are not physiologically the mechanism shown for dizygotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which type of twin is more common?","options":["Monozygotic","Dizygotic","Conjoined","Monoamniotic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states dizygotic twins are more common.  Why A wrong: Monozygotic twins are less common (30%).  Why C/D wrong: These are relatively rare subtypes."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Regarding the sex of dizygotic twins, they can be:","options":["Same or different sex","Always the same sex","Always different sex","Always female"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dizygotic twins may be of the same or different sex.  Why B wrong: Being always the same sex applies to monozygotic twins.  Why C wrong: They are not always different sex; they can be the same.  Why D wrong: Sex is not restricted to female."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The looks (appearance) of dizygotic twins are:","options":["Not the same (non-identical)","Always identical","Always mirror images","Always conjoined"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dizygotic twins do not look the same — they are non-identical/fraternal twins.  Why B wrong: Identical looks characterize monozygotic twins.  Why C wrong: Mirror-image looks are a monozygotic phenomenon.  Why D wrong: Conjoined is a monozygotic subtype."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The incidence of dizygotic twins:","options":["Varies from country to country","Is constant worldwide","Is zero in Africa","Is highest in Japan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dizygotic twin incidence varies from country to country.  Why B wrong: Constant worldwide incidence is a feature of monozygotic twins.  Why C wrong: The highest incidence is actually in Nigeria (Africa).  Why D wrong: Japan has the lowest dizygotic incidence, not the highest."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The highest incidence of dizygotic twins is found in which country?","options":["Nigeria","Japan","India","USA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The highest incidence of twins/dizygotic is in Nigeria (1 in 20 pregnancy).  Why B wrong: Japan has the lowest dizygotic incidence.  Why C wrong: India's incidence is 1 in 80.  Why D wrong: USA is not cited as having the highest incidence."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The incidence of dizygotic twins in Nigeria is:","options":["1 in 20 pregnancies","1 in 80 pregnancies","1 in 200 pregnancies","1 in 250 pregnancies"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nigeria has the highest incidence, 1 in 20 pregnancies.  Why B wrong: 1 in 80 is India's incidence.  Why C wrong: 1 in 200 is Japan's (lowest dizygotic) incidence.  Why D wrong: 1 in 250 is the worldwide monozygotic incidence."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The lowest incidence of dizygotic twins is found in which country?","options":["Japan","Nigeria","India","China"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The lowest incidence of dizygotic twins is in Japan (1 in 200 pregnancy).  Why B wrong: Nigeria has the highest incidence.  Why C wrong: India's figure is 1 in 80.  Why D wrong: China is not mentioned on the page."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The incidence of dizygotic twins in Japan is:","options":["1 in 20 pregnancies","1 in 80 pregnancies","1 in 200 pregnancies","1 in 250 pregnancies"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Japan has the lowest dizygotic incidence, 1 in 200 pregnancies.  Why A wrong: 1 in 20 is Nigeria's highest incidence.  Why B wrong: 1 in 80 is India's incidence.  Why D wrong: 1 in 250 is the worldwide monozygotic figure."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The incidence of twins in India is:","options":["1 in 20 pregnancies","1 in 80 pregnancies","1 in 200 pregnancies","1 in 250 pregnancies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The incidence of twins in India is 1 in 80 pregnancies.  Why A wrong: 1 in 20 is Nigeria.  Why C wrong: 1 in 200 is Japan.  Why D wrong: 1 in 250 is worldwide monozygotic."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"How are monozygotic twins formed?","options":["A single ovum fertilized by a single sperm forming a single zygote that divides into two","Two ova each fertilized by a different sperm","One ovum fertilized by two sperm","Two zygotes fusing into one"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monozygotic twins arise when a single ovum and single sperm form a single zygote that divides into two (A and B).  Why B wrong: Two ova and two sperm produce dizygotic twins.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the mechanism shown for monozygotic twinning."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which of the following is the SAME in monozygotic twins? (Select all that apply)","options":["Sex","Genotype","Looks/Phenotype","Blood group","Fingerprints"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Monozygotic twins share the same sex, genotype, looks/phenotype, and blood group (also HLA typing).  Why E wrong: Fingerprints are DIFFERENT in monozygotic twins, so E is not selected."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Regarding HLA typing in monozygotic twins:","options":["It is the same in both twins","It is always different","It cannot be determined","It is same only in males"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HLA typing is listed among features that are the same in monozygotic twins.  Why B wrong: HLA typing is identical, not different.  Why C wrong: HLA typing is determinable and is used to establish monozygosity.  Why D wrong: There is no sex restriction for HLA identity."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which feature is DIFFERENT between monozygotic twins despite their genetic identity?","options":["Fingerprints","Blood group","Genotype","Sex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fingerprints are different between monozygotic twins.  Why B/C/D wrong: Blood group, genotype, and sex are all the same in monozygotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which statement about the relative frequency of monozygotic twins is correct?","options":["They are less common than dizygotic twins","They are more common than dizygotic twins","They occur equally with dizygotic twins","They never occur naturally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monozygotic twins are less common than dizygotic twins.  Why B wrong: Dizygotic twins are more common.  Why C wrong: The proportions are 30% vs 70%, not equal.  Why D wrong: Monozygotic twinning occurs naturally."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"What percentage of twins are monozygotic?","options":["30%","50%","70%","90%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives monozygotic = 30%.  Why B wrong: 50% is not the figure.  Why C wrong: 70% is the dizygotic proportion.  Why D wrong: 90% is not cited."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"What percentage of twins are dizygotic?","options":["30%","50%","70%","100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives dizygotic = 70%.  Why A wrong: 30% is the monozygotic proportion.  Why B/D wrong: These figures are not given."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The incidence of monozygotic twins throughout the world:","options":["Remains constant at 1 in 250 pregnancies","Varies from country to country","Is highest in Nigeria","Is 1 in 80 pregnancies"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monozygotic twin incidence remains constant worldwide at 1 in 250 pregnancies.  Why B wrong: Varying incidence is a dizygotic feature.  Why C wrong: Nigeria's high incidence refers to dizygotic twins.  Why D wrong: 1 in 80 is the twin/India incidence, not the monozygotic constant."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"(Q1 on page) The most common type of twin pregnancy is:","options":["Monozygotic twins","Dizygotic twins","Conjoined twins","Monochorionic monoamniotic twins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common type of twin pregnancy is dizygotic twins.  Why A wrong: Monozygotic twins are less common.  Why C/D wrong: These are rare varieties."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"All twin pregnancies always have how many fetuses (per the page's feature list)?","options":["2 fetuses","3 fetuses","1 fetus","Variable, up to 4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Twins always have 2 fetuses.  Why B wrong: 3 fetuses = triplets.  Why C wrong: 1 fetus is a singleton.  Why D wrong: The defining feature is exactly 2 fetuses."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"How many umbilical cords are always present in a twin pregnancy?","options":["1 umbilical cord shared","2 separate umbilical cords","3 umbilical cords","It depends on chorionicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Twins always have separate cords — i.e., 2 umbilical cords.  Why A wrong: The cords are separate, not shared.  Why C wrong: There are 2, not 3.  Why D wrong: The number of cords (2) is always fixed for twins, unlike placenta/membranes."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"In a twin pregnancy, the number of placentas depends on:","options":["Whether the twins are dizygotic or monozygotic","Maternal age","The number of umbilical cords","Fetal sex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The number of placentas depends on whether the twins are dizygotic or monozygotic.  Why B/D wrong: Maternal age and fetal sex do not determine placenta number.  Why C wrong: The cord number is always 2 and does not dictate placenta count."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The two fetal membranes referred to when counting membranes in twins are:","options":["Chorion and amnion","Decidua and myometrium","Endometrium and perimetrium","Chorion and decidua"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The number of fetal membranes involves the chorion and amnion.  Why B/C wrong: These are uterine layers, not fetal membranes here.  Why D wrong: Decidua is maternal endometrial tissue, not the counted fetal membrane."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The number of fetal membranes (chorion & amnion) in twins depends on:","options":["Whether twins are dizygotic or monozygotic","The blood group of the mother","Parity of the mother","Fetal weight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The number of fetal membranes depends on whether the twins are dizygotic or monozygotic.  Why B/C/D wrong: These maternal/fetal factors do not determine membrane number."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The term \"zygosity\" in twin pregnancy refers to the:","options":["Type of conception","Type of placentation","Number of placentas","Number of membranes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Zygosity (dizygotic vs monozygotic) refers to the type of conception.  Why B wrong: Type of placentation is chorionicity.  Why C/D wrong: Number of placentas/membranes are aspects of chorionicity, not zygosity."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The term \"chorionicity\" in twin pregnancy refers to the:","options":["Type of placentation","Type of conception","Fetal sex","Zygote count"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chorionicity = type of placentation, based on number of placenta and membranes.  Why B wrong: Type of conception is zygosity.  Why C/D wrong: These are not what chorionicity describes."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Chorionicity is classified into monochorionic and dichorionic on the basis of:","options":["Number of placenta","Number of umbilical cords","Fetal genotype","Maternal HLA typing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monochorionic vs dichorionic is decided on the basis of the number of placenta.  Why B wrong: Cord number is always 2 in twins and does not classify chorionicity.  Why C/D wrong: Genotype/HLA relate to zygosity, not chorionicity."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"In twins, the prognosis always depends on:","options":["Chorionicity, not zygosity","Zygosity, not chorionicity","Maternal age","Number of umbilical cords"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In twins, prognosis always depends on chorionicity and not on zygosity.  Why B wrong: This reverses the correct relationship.  Why C/D wrong: These do not determine twin prognosis."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which type of twins always has a worse (bad) prognosis?","options":["Monochorionic twins","Dichorionic twins","Dizygotic twins","Fraternal twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monochorionic twins always have a bad prognosis compared to dichorionic twins.  Why B wrong: Dichorionic twins have a better prognosis.  Why C/D wrong: Dizygotic (fraternal) twins are dichorionic and have good prognosis."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Dizygotic twins are always which type of chorionicity/amnionicity?","options":["Dichorionic and diamniotic (DCDA) with 2 placentas","Monochorionic and monoamniotic","Monochorionic and diamniotic","Dichorionic and monoamniotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dizygotic twins are always dichorionic and diamniotic (DCDA) with 2 placentas.  Why B/C/D wrong: These membrane patterns occur only in monozygotic twins depending on timing of division."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The most common variety of twins is dizygotic, which is always:","options":["Dichorionic and diamniotic","Monochorionic and monoamniotic","Conjoined","Monochorionic and diamniotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The MC variety of twins = dizygotic twins, which are always dichorionic and diamniotic.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are monozygotic patterns, not applicable to dizygotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"In monozygotic twinning, the number of chorion, amnion, and placenta depends on:","options":["The time (day) of division of the zygote","The maternal blood group","The number of sperm","Fetal sex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In monozygotic twins the number of chorion, amnion, and placenta depends on the time of division.  Why B/C/D wrong: These factors do not determine the membrane pattern in monozygotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The chorion is formed roughly by which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 4","Day 8","Day 10","Day 12"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chorion is formed roughly by Day 8 after fertilization.  Why A wrong: Day 4 is not the chorion formation day.  Why C wrong: Day 10 is when the amnion forms.  Why D wrong: Day 12 is associated with conjoined twinning threshold."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The amnion is formed roughly by which day after fertilization?","options":["Day 4","Day 8","Day 10","Day 14"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The amnion is formed roughly by Day 10 after fertilization.  Why A/B wrong: Day 4 and Day 8 (chorion) are earlier.  Why D wrong: Day 14 is not the amnion formation day given."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"If a monozygotic zygote divides less than 4 days after fertilization, the twins are:","options":["Dichorionic diamniotic","Monochorionic diamniotic","Monochorionic monoamniotic","Conjoined"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Division at <4 days gives dichorionic diamniotic twins.  Why B wrong: Monochorionic diamniotic results from 4–8 day division.  Why C wrong: Monochorionic monoamniotic results from >8 (8–12) day division.  Why D wrong: Conjoined twins result from >12 day division."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"If a monozygotic zygote divides between 4 and 8 days after fertilization, the twins are:","options":["Dichorionic diamniotic","Monochorionic diamniotic","Monochorionic monoamniotic","Conjoined"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Division at 4–8 days gives monochorionic diamniotic (MCDA) twins.  Why A wrong: DCDA results from <4 day division.  Why C wrong: MCMA results from >8 (8–12) day division.  Why D wrong: Conjoined results from >12 day division."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"If a monozygotic zygote divides at >8 days (8–12 days), the resulting twins are:","options":["Dichorionic diamniotic","Monochorionic diamniotic","Monochorionic monoamniotic","Conjoined"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Division at >8 days (8–12 days) gives monochorionic monoamniotic (MCMA) twins.  Why A wrong: DCDA is <4 days.  Why B wrong: MCDA is 4–8 days.  Why D wrong: Conjoined twins are >12 days."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"If a monozygotic zygote divides at >12 days after fertilization, the result is:","options":["Dichorionic diamniotic twins","Monochorionic diamniotic twins","Monochorionic monoamniotic twins","Conjoined (Siamese) twins"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Division at >12 days results in conjoined (Siamese) twins.  Why A/B/C wrong: These result from earlier division times (<4, 4–8, and 8–12 days respectively)."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Conjoined twins are also known as:","options":["Siamese twins","Fraternal twins","Dizygotic twins","Vanishing twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Conjoined twins are also called Siamese twins.  Why B/C wrong: Fraternal/dizygotic twins are non-identical and separate.  Why D wrong: Vanishing twin is a different phenomenon not on this page."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which division-time category of monozygotic twins carries a GOOD prognosis?","options":["<4 days (Dichorionic Diamniotic)","4–8 days (Monochorionic Diamniotic)","≥8 days (Monochorionic Monoamniotic)","≥12 days (Conjoined)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The <4 day dichorionic diamniotic group is labelled good prognosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: The 4–8 day, ≥8 day, and ≥12 day groups (all monochorionic or conjoined) carry a bad prognosis."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Which of the following monozygotic twin types carry a BAD prognosis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dichorionic Diamniotic (<4 days)","Monochorionic Diamniotic (4–8 days)","Monochorionic Monoamniotic (≥8 days)","Conjoined/Siamese twins (≥12 days)"],"correct":"B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table marks MCDA, MCMA, and conjoined twins as bad prognosis (all monochorionic or conjoined).  Why A wrong: Dichorionic diamniotic twins have a good prognosis, so A is not selected."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The abbreviation MCDA stands for which twin type?","options":["Monochorionic Diamniotic","Monochorionic Monoamniotic","Dichorionic Diamniotic","Dichorionic Monoamniotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MCDA = Monochorionic Diamniotic.  Why B wrong: MCMA = Monochorionic Monoamniotic.  Why C wrong: DCDA = Dichorionic Diamniotic.  Why D wrong: Dichorionic Monoamniotic is not a listed category."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The abbreviation MCMA stands for which twin type?","options":["Monochorionic Monoamniotic","Monochorionic Diamniotic","Dichorionic Diamniotic","Monochorionic Multiamniotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MCMA = Monochorionic Monoamniotic (division at ≥8 [8–12] days).  Why B wrong: MCDA = Monochorionic Diamniotic.  Why C wrong: DCDA = Dichorionic Diamniotic.  Why D wrong: \"Multiamniotic\" is not a real category."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The most common variety of monozygotic twins is:","options":["Monochorionic Diamniotic","Dichorionic Diamniotic","Monochorionic Monoamniotic","Conjoined"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common variety of monozygotic twins is monochorionic diamniotic.  Why B wrong: Dichorionic diamniotic is the most common variety of all twins (via dizygotic).  Why C/D wrong: MCMA and conjoined are rarer varieties."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"The most common variety of twins overall is:","options":["Dizygotic twins (dichorionic diamniotic)","Monochorionic monoamniotic twins","Conjoined twins","Monochorionic diamniotic twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common variety of twins is dizygotic twins → dichorionic diamniotic.  Why B/C wrong: These are rare varieties.  Why D wrong: Monochorionic diamniotic is the most common monozygotic variety, not of all twins."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"According to the stated rule, which twins have a better prognosis?","options":["Dichorionic twins","Monochorionic twins","Monoamniotic twins","Conjoined twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The rule states dichorionic twins have a better prognosis than monochorionic twins.  Why B wrong: Monochorionic twins have the worse prognosis.  Why C/D wrong: These are associated with worse outcomes, not better."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"Because dichorionic twins have a better prognosis than monochorionic twins, it is clinically important to determine which of the following in twins?","options":["Chorionicity","Maternal blood group","Fetal fingerprints","Number of umbilical cords"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page concludes it is important to know the chorionicity in twins because it determines prognosis.  Why B/C wrong: Blood group and fingerprints relate to zygosity, which does not determine prognosis.  Why D wrong: Cord number is always 2 and is not prognostically decisive."},{"section":"Batch 049 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy definitions (twins, triplets, quadruplets)…","question":"A monozygotic twin pregnancy is diagnosed as monochorionic monoamniotic. Based on the timing rule, the zygote most likely divided at:","options":["<4 days after fertilization","4–8 days after fertilization","≥8 days (8–12 days) after fertilization",">12 days after fertilization"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Monochorionic monoamniotic twins result from division at ≥8 days (8–12 days), after both chorion (Day 8) and amnion (Day 10) have formed.  Why A wrong: <4 days yields dichorionic diamniotic.  Why B wrong: 4–8 days yields monochorionic diamniotic.  Why D wrong: >12 days yields conjoined twins."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Which type of twin pregnancy carries the BEST prognosis?","options":["Dichorionic diamniotic twins","Monochorionic diamniotic twins","Monochorionic monoamniotic twins","Conjoined twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Best Prognosis in Twins: Dichorionic Diamniotic Twins.\"  Why B wrong: Monochorionic diamniotic is intermediate, not best.  Why C wrong: Monoamniotic twins have a poor prognosis (cord entanglement).  Why D wrong: Conjoined twins have the worst prognosis."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Which type of twin pregnancy carries the WORST prognosis?","options":["Dichorionic diamniotic twins","Monochorionic diamniotic twins","Monochorionic monoamniotic twins","Conjoined twins"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Worst Prognosis in Twins: Conjoined Twins.\"  Why A wrong: Dichorionic diamniotic has the best prognosis.  Why B wrong: Monochorionic diamniotic is not the worst.  Why C wrong: Monoamniotic is poor but not the worst; conjoined is worst."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Arrange twin types from BEST to WORST prognosis. Which sequence is correct?","options":["Dichorionic diamniotic > Monochorionic diamniotic > Monochorionic > Monoamniotic > Conjoined","Conjoined > Monoamniotic > Monochorionic > Monochorionic diamniotic > Dichorionic diamniotic","Monochorionic diamniotic > Dichorionic diamniotic > Conjoined > Monoamniotic","Monoamniotic > Dichorionic diamniotic > Conjoined"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes rank prognosis best to worst as Dichorionic Diamniotic > Monochorionic Diamniotic > Monochorionic > Monoamniotic > Conjoined Twins.  Why B wrong: This is worst-to-best (reversed).  Why C wrong: Order is scrambled and incorrect.  Why D wrong: Order is incomplete and incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In which twin conditions is a cesarean section mandatory? (Select all that apply)","options":["Monochorionic monoamniotic","Conjoined twins","Dichorionic diamniotic","Monochorionic diamniotic","Vanishing twin"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list cesarean section has to be done for (1) Monochorionic monoamniotic and (2) Conjoined twins.  Why C wrong: DCDA can be delivered vaginally depending on the lie of the twins.  Why D wrong: MCDA can also be delivered vaginally depending on the lie of the fetuses.  Why E wrong: Vanishing twin is a fetal-death phenomenon, not an indication for mandatory cesarean."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Conjoined twins are also known by which name?","options":["Siamese twins","Vanishing twins","Acardiac twins","Papyraceus twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state conjoined twins are \"Also called: Siamese twin (some body part of the twins is fused with each other).\"  Why B wrong: Vanishing twin refers to a fetus dying and disappearing before 14 weeks.  Why C wrong: Acardiac twin is a twin without a heart.  Why D wrong: Papyraceus refers to a compressed dead twin."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Conjoined twins are classified based on which criterion?","options":["The part of the twins that is fused","The number of placentas","The sex of the twins","Maternal age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state classification is \"Depending upon the part of twins fused.\"  Why B wrong: Number of placentas defines chorionicity, not conjoined type.  Why C wrong: Sex is not the classification basis for conjoined types.  Why D wrong: Maternal age is unrelated to conjoined twin nomenclature."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In conjoined twins, \"Thoracopagus\" refers to twins fused at the:","options":["Thorax (chest)","Abdomen","Buttocks","Head"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Thoracophagus = Thorax fused twins.\"  Why B wrong: Abdomen fusion is Omphalopagus.  Why C wrong: Buttock fusion is Pygopagus.  Why D wrong: Head fusion is Craniopagus."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In conjoined twins, \"Omphalopagus\" means the twins are fused at the:","options":["Abdomen","Thorax","Pelvic bone","Head"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Omphalophagus = Abdomen fused.\"  Why B wrong: Thorax fusion is Thoracopagus.  Why C wrong: Pelvic bone fusion is Ischiopagus.  Why D wrong: Head fusion is Craniopagus."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In conjoined twins, \"Pygopagus\" refers to twins fused at the:","options":["Buttocks","Abdomen","Thorax","Pelvic bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Pyophagus = Buttocks fused\" (pygopagus).  Why B wrong: Abdomen fusion is Omphalopagus.  Why C wrong: Thorax fusion is Thoracopagus.  Why D wrong: Pelvic bone fusion is Ischiopagus."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In conjoined twins, \"Ischiopagus\" means the twins are fused at the:","options":["Pelvic bone","Buttocks","Head","Abdomen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Ischiophagus = Pelvic bone fused.\"  Why B wrong: Buttock fusion is Pygopagus.  Why C wrong: Head fusion is Craniopagus.  Why D wrong: Abdomen fusion is Omphalopagus."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In conjoined twins, \"Craniopagus\" refers to twins in whom the ______ are fused.","options":["Heads","Chests","Abdomens","Buttocks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Craniophagus = Heads are fused.\"  Why B wrong: Chest fusion is Thoracopagus.  Why C wrong: Abdomen fusion is Omphalopagus.  Why D wrong: Buttock fusion is Pygopagus."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"The mnemonic \"TOPIC\" for conjoined twins stands for which correct sequence of fused parts?","options":["Thorax, Abdomen, Buttocks, Pelvic bone, Head","Thorax, Head, Buttocks, Pelvic bone, Abdomen","Head, Abdomen, Buttocks, Pelvic bone, Thorax","Thorax, Abdomen, Head, Pelvic bone, Buttocks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: T = Thoracopagus (thorax), O = Omphalopagus (abdomen), P = Pygopagus (buttocks), I = Ischiopagus (pelvic bone), C = Craniopagus (head).  Why B wrong: Head and abdomen positions are swapped.  Why C wrong: Thorax and head positions are swapped.  Why D wrong: Head and buttocks positions are swapped."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In conjoined twins, \"Rachipagus\" refers to twins that are:","options":["Fused from head to buttocks (all parts fused)","Fused only at the chest","Fused only at the pelvic bone","Twins without a heart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Rachiphagus: Head to buttocks all parts fused.\"  Why B wrong: Chest-only fusion is Thoracopagus.  Why C wrong: Pelvic bone fusion is Ischiopagus.  Why D wrong: A heartless twin is an acardiac twin."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON type of conjoined (compound) twin?","options":["Thoracopagus","Craniopagus","Rachipagus","Ischiopagus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Most common type of compound Twin: Thoracophagus.\"  Why B wrong: Craniopagus is the second-best answer for least common, not most common.  Why C wrong: Rachipagus is the least common type.  Why D wrong: Ischiopagus is not stated as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"What is the LEAST common type of conjoined twin (best answer)?","options":["Rachipagus","Craniopagus","Thoracopagus","Omphalopagus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the least common type of conjoined twin: \"Best answer = Rachiphagus.\"  Why B wrong: Craniopagus is listed as the 2nd best answer for least common, not the best.  Why C wrong: Thoracopagus is the most common, not least.  Why D wrong: Omphalopagus is not identified as least common."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"For the \"least common type of conjoined twin,\" which is the SECOND-best answer per the notes?","options":["Craniopagus","Thoracopagus","Ischiopagus","Pygopagus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 2nd best answer for least common conjoined twin is Craniopagus.  Why B wrong: Thoracopagus is most common.  Why C wrong: Ischiopagus is not listed as second best.  Why D wrong: Pygopagus is not listed as second best."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"How are conjoined twins delivered?","options":["By cesarean section","Normal vaginal delivery","Vacuum-assisted vaginal delivery","Forceps delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state conjoined twins are \"Delivered by Cesarean section.\"  Why B wrong: Vaginal delivery is not appropriate due to fusion.  Why C wrong: Vacuum assistance cannot deliver fused twins.  Why D wrong: Forceps cannot deliver fused twins."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"The prognosis of a twin pregnancy primarily depends on which factor?","options":["Chorionicity","Maternal blood group","Fetal sex","Amniotic fluid colour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Prognosis depends on: Chorionicity.\"  Why B wrong: Maternal blood group is not the determinant of twin prognosis here.  Why C wrong: Fetal sex does not determine twin prognosis.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid colour is not the stated determinant."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Which statement about twin prognosis by chorionicity is TRUE?","options":["Dichorionic twins have better prognosis than monochorionic twins","Monochorionic twins have better prognosis than dichorionic twins","Chorionicity has no effect on prognosis","Monoamniotic twins have the best prognosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Dichorionic twins have better prognosis than monochorionic twins.\"  Why B wrong: This is the reverse of the true statement.  Why C wrong: Prognosis depends on chorionicity.  Why D wrong: Monoamniotic twins have poor prognosis."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"What is the BEST investigation to determine chorionicity in a twin pregnancy?","options":["USG (TVS — transvaginal sonography)","Maternal serum AFP","Amniocentesis","Fetal MRI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Best Investigation to know Chorionicity = USG (TVS).\"  Why B wrong: Serum AFP does not determine chorionicity.  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis is not the best chorionicity test.  Why D wrong: MRI is not the stated best investigation."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"What is the BEST time (gestational age) to determine chorionicity by USG?","options":["11–13 weeks","4–8 days","20–24 weeks","32–34 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Time: 11–13 weeks\" for determining chorionicity.  Why B wrong: 4–8 days is when MCDA (monochorionic) splitting occurs, not the imaging time.  Why C wrong: 20–24 weeks is not the stated optimal window.  Why D wrong: 32–34 weeks is the cesarean timing for monoamniotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In dichorionic twins, the sex of the twins can be:","options":["Same or different","Always the same","Always different","Always male"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for dichorionic twins \"Sex = Same/Difference.\"  Why B wrong: They can also be different (dizygotic possible).  Why C wrong: They can also be the same.  Why D wrong: Sex is not restricted to male."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In monochorionic twins, the sex of the twins is:","options":["Always the same","Same or different","Always different","Cannot be determined"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for monochorionic twins \"Sex = same\" (they are monozygotic).  Why B wrong: Monochorionic twins are always the same sex.  Why C wrong: They cannot be different sexes.  Why D wrong: Sex is determinable and always the same."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"How many placentas are present in a dichorionic twin pregnancy?","options":["2","1","3","0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Number of Placenta: 2\" for dichorionic twins.  Why B wrong: 1 placenta is seen in monochorionic twins.  Why C wrong: 3 placentas is not correct.  Why D wrong: There is always a placenta in a viable pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"How many placentas are present in a monochorionic twin pregnancy?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Number of Placenta – 1\" for monochorionic twins.  Why B wrong: 2 placentas is dichorionic.  Why C wrong: 3 is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 4 is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"How many layers of membrane are present between the twins in a DICHORIONIC twin pregnancy?","options":["4","2","3","0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Number of layers of membrane between Twins – 4\" for dichorionic twins.  Why B wrong: 2 layers is seen in monochorionic diamniotic twins.  Why C wrong: 3 layers is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 0 layers occurs in monoamniotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"How many layers of membrane are present between the twins in a MONOCHORIONIC (diamniotic) twin pregnancy?","options":["2","4","3","0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"No. of layers of membrane between Twins 2\" for monochorionic twins.  Why B wrong: 4 layers is dichorionic.  Why C wrong: 3 layers is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 0 layers occurs in monochorionic monoamniotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"The thickness of the inter-twin membrane in a dichorionic twin pregnancy is:","options":[">2 mm thick","<2 mm thick","Exactly 1 mm","No membrane present"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Thickness of membrane >2 mm Thick\" for dichorionic twins.  Why B wrong: <2 mm is seen in monochorionic twins.  Why C wrong: An exact 1 mm value is not stated.  Why D wrong: A membrane is present in diamniotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"The thickness of the inter-twin membrane in a monochorionic twin pregnancy is:","options":["<2 mm in thickness",">2 mm in thickness",">5 mm in thickness","No membrane present"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Thickness of membrane <2 mm in Thickness\" for monochorionic twins.  Why B wrong: >2 mm is dichorionic.  Why C wrong: >5 mm is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Membrane is present in diamniotic monochorionic twins."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"On ultrasound, which sign is POSITIVE in dichorionic twins?","options":["Twin peak sign (Lambda sign)","Absent membrane","T sign only","No sign is seen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for dichorionic twins the sign is \"Twin peak sign OR Lambda sign (Positive).\"  Why B wrong: A membrane is present, and its absence is not the sign.  Why C wrong: The T-sign is associated with monochorionic twins, not the lambda/twin-peak sign.  Why D wrong: A specific positive sign is present."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"The twin peak sign (Lambda sign) in a MONOCHORIONIC twin pregnancy is:","options":["Negative","Positive","Always strongly positive","Not assessable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for monochorionic twins \"Twin peak sign or Lambda sign (Negative).\"  Why B wrong: A positive twin peak sign indicates dichorionicity.  Why C wrong: It is negative, not strongly positive.  Why D wrong: It is assessable and found negative."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"On USG, the twin peak sign is POSITIVE (+ve) in which type of twin pregnancy?","options":["DCDA (Dichorionic Diamniotic)","MCDA (Monochorionic Diamniotic)","Monochorionic monoamniotic","Conjoined twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The comment table states for DCDA \"On USG → Twin peak sign is +ve.\"  Why B wrong: In monochorionic twins the twin peak/lambda sign is negative.  Why C wrong: Monoamniotic twins do not show the +ve twin peak sign of dichorionicity.  Why D wrong: Conjoined twins are not characterized by the +ve twin peak sign."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"What is the best type of twin pregnancy according to the diagnostic table?","options":["DCDA (Dichorionic Diamniotic)","MCDA (Monochorionic Diamniotic)","Monochorionic monoamniotic","Conjoined twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The comment for DCDA states \"Best type of Twin pregnancy.\"  Why B wrong: MCDA has bad prognosis and TTTS risk.  Why C wrong: Monoamniotic twins risk cord entanglement.  Why D wrong: Conjoined twins have the worst prognosis."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"How many layers of membrane are seen between the fetuses in DCDA on the diagnostic diagram, and what is their character?","options":["4 layers, thick membranes","2 layers, thin membranes","0 layers, no membrane","3 layers, thin membranes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The DCDA diagnosis states \"4 layers of membrane between the fetuses\" and \"Thick membranes.\"  Why B wrong: 2 thin layers describes MCDA.  Why C wrong: 0 layers describes monoamniotic twins.  Why D wrong: 3 layers is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Can DCDA (dichorionic diamniotic) twins be delivered vaginally?","options":["Yes, depending on the lie of the twins","No, always require cesarean","Only if both are cephalic and under 2 kg","Never before 34 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The DCDA comment states \"Can be delivered vaginally depending on the lie of twins.\"  Why B wrong: Cesarean is not mandatory for DCDA.  Why C wrong: This restriction is not stated.  Why D wrong: This constraint is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In MCDA twins, the diagnosis shows how many layers of membrane between the twins?","options":["2 layers, thin membranes (<2 mm thick)","4 layers, thick membranes","0 layers","3 layers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The MCDA diagnosis states \"2 layers of membranes between twins\" and \"Thin membranes (<2 mm thick).\"  Why B wrong: 4 thick layers describes DCDA.  Why C wrong: 0 layers describes monoamniotic twins.  Why D wrong: 3 layers is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"According to the diagnostic table, monochorionic twins are associated with which prognosis and characteristic complication?","options":["Bad prognosis; complication e.g. Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome","Best prognosis; no complications","Bad prognosis; complication is cord entanglement only","Best prognosis; complication is prematurity only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The MCDA comment reads \"Monochorionic twins → Bad prognosis → Complication e.g. Twin to Twin Transfusion syndrome.\"  Why B wrong: Monochorionic twins have bad prognosis, not best.  Why C wrong: Cord entanglement is specific to monoamniotic twins.  Why D wrong: DCDA is best; TTTS is the specific MCDA complication."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Can MCDA (monochorionic diamniotic) twins be delivered vaginally?","options":["Yes, depending on the lie of the fetuses","No, cesarean is always mandatory","Only at 32–34 weeks","Never"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The MCDA comment states \"Can be delivered vaginally depending on the lie of the fetuses.\"  Why B wrong: Cesarean is not always mandatory for MCDA.  Why C wrong: 32–34 weeks cesarean timing applies to monoamniotic twins.  Why D wrong: Vaginal delivery is possible depending on lie."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In monochorionic monoamniotic twins, the number of layers of membrane between the fetuses is:","options":["Zero (\"0\")","2","4","1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for monochorionic monoamniotic \"Number of layers of membranes between the fetus = '0'.\"  Why B wrong: 2 layers describes MCDA.  Why C wrong: 4 layers describes DCDA.  Why D wrong: There is no dividing membrane (0), not 1."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Which complication is SPECIFIC to monochorionic monoamniotic twins?","options":["Cord entanglement","Twin peak sign","Vanishing twin","Congenital rubella"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The comment for monochorionic monoamniotic twins reads \"Complication specific → CORD ENTANGLEMENT.\"  Why B wrong: The twin peak sign is a diagnostic feature of dichorionicity, not a complication.  Why C wrong: Vanishing twin is a fetal-death phenomenon, not specific to monoamniotic twins.  Why D wrong: Congenital rubella is unrelated to chorionicity."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Because of the risk of cord entanglement, monochorionic monoamniotic twins should be delivered by cesarean section at what gestational age?","options":["Between 32–34 weeks","Between 11–13 weeks","Between 37–40 weeks","Between 24–28 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The comment states \"Do a cesarean section always between 32–34 weeks.\"  Why B wrong: 11–13 weeks is the chorionicity assessment window.  Why C wrong: Term (37–40 weeks) is too late given cord entanglement risk.  Why D wrong: 24–28 weeks is too early and not stated."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Regarding Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome (TTTS) in conjoined twins, the notes state that it:","options":["Can be seen but is less common","Never occurs","Is the most common complication","Only occurs in dichorionic twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The comment for conjoined twins reads \"Twin to Twin Transfusion syndrome can be seen but it is less common.\"  Why B wrong: It can be seen (not never).  Why C wrong: It is less common in conjoined twins, not most common.  Why D wrong: TTTS is a monochorionic complication, not dichorionic."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Which complications are seen ONLY in monochorionic twins (MCDA/MCMA)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome","Acardius fetus","Prematurity","Congenital malformation","Cord entanglement (monoamniotic)"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Complications which are seen in Monochorionic Twins Only (MCDA/MCMA)\" as Twin-to-Twin Transfusion syndrome and Acardius fetus.  Why C wrong: Prematurity is common to both monochorionic and dichorionic twins.  Why D wrong: Congenital malformation is common to both types.  Why E wrong: Cord entanglement is specific to monoamniotic twins but is not one of the two \"monochorionic only\" complications listed here (TTTS and acardius)."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Why do dichorionic twins have a better pregnancy outcome than monochorionic twins?","options":["Complications like TTTS and acardius fetus are not seen in dichorionic twins","Dichorionic twins are always the same sex","Dichorionic twins share one placenta","Dichorionic twins have a single amniotic sac"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state these complications \"are not seen in Dichorionic twins. That is why dichorionic twins have better pregnancy.\"  Why B wrong: Dichorionic twins can be same or different sex.  Why C wrong: Dichorionic twins have 2 placentas.  Why D wrong: Dichorionic diamniotic twins have separate sacs."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome (TTTS), what is the underlying vascular abnormality?","options":["A passage/connection between the blood vessels of the twins so blood flows in one direction","Complete absence of any placental vessels","Two entirely separate circulations with no connection","Reversed umbilical arteries in both twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"There is a passage/connection between the blood vessels of twins such that blood flows in one direction.\"  Why B wrong: There is a vascular connection, not an absence of vessels.  Why C wrong: The circulations are connected, not separate, in TTTS.  Why D wrong: The mechanism is a unidirectional shunt, not reversed arteries in both."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In TTTS, the twin that GIVES blood is called the:","options":["Donor twin","Recipient twin","Acardiac twin","Papyraceus twin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"The Twin which gives blood is Donor twin (Twin A).\"  Why B wrong: The recipient twin receives blood.  Why C wrong: Acardiac twin refers to a heartless twin.  Why D wrong: Papyraceus refers to a compressed dead twin."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In TTTS, the twin that RECEIVES blood is called the:","options":["Recipient twin","Donor twin","Vanishing twin","Acardiac twin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"The twin which receives the blood is Recipient twin (Twin B).\"  Why B wrong: The donor twin gives blood.  Why C wrong: Vanishing twin refers to early fetal loss.  Why D wrong: Acardiac twin refers to a heartless twin."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In TTTS, the donor twin typically develops which hematological change?","options":["Anemia","Polycythemia","Leukemia","Thrombocytosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Anemia in Donor Twin.\"  Why B wrong: Polycythemia occurs in the recipient twin.  Why C wrong: Leukemia is not a feature of TTTS.  Why D wrong: Thrombocytosis is not the stated change."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Which set of features describes the DONOR twin in TTTS?","options":["Pale look, less growth, decreased renal blood flow, oligohydramnios","Pink colour, more growth, increased renal blood flow, polyhydramnios","Polycythemia and congestive heart failure","Increased urine output and increased amniotic fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the donor twin has anemia → pale look, less growth, renal blood flow ↓ → GFR ↓ → decreased urine output → oligohydramnios.  Why B wrong: These pink/more-growth/polyhydramnios features belong to the recipient twin.  Why C wrong: Polycythemia and CHF are recipient-twin features.  Why D wrong: Increased urine and fluid are recipient-twin features."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In the donor twin of TTTS, decreased renal blood flow leads to a fall in which parameter, reducing urine output?","options":["GFR (glomerular filtration rate)","Serum sodium","Hematocrit","Blood pressure only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow diagram shows \"Renal blood flow ↓ → GFR ↓ → Urine output ↓.\"  Why B wrong: Serum sodium change is not stated in the pathway.  Why C wrong: Hematocrit is affected by anemia but is not the step reducing urine output.  Why D wrong: The stated pathway is via GFR, not BP alone."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Decreased urine output in the donor twin results in which amniotic fluid abnormality?","options":["Oligohydramnios","Polyhydramnios","Normal fluid","Meconium-stained fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state reduced urine output leads to \"(OLIGOHYDRAMNIOS) — Donor twin.\"  Why B wrong: Polyhydramnios occurs in the recipient twin.  Why C wrong: Fluid is abnormal (reduced) in the donor.  Why D wrong: Meconium staining is not the stated finding."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"The donor twin in TTTS has an increased risk of which organ failure?","options":["Renal failure","Congestive heart failure","Liver failure","Respiratory failure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the donor twin has \"↑Chances of renal failure.\"  Why B wrong: Congestive heart failure is the recipient twin's risk.  Why C wrong: Liver failure is not stated.  Why D wrong: Respiratory failure is not stated for the donor."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In TTTS, the recipient twin develops which hematological change, increasing the risk of thrombosis?","options":["Polycythemia","Anemia","Thrombocytopenia","Neutropenia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Polycythemia in Recipient Twin (Increased chances of Thrombosis).\"  Why B wrong: Anemia occurs in the donor twin.  Why C wrong: Thrombocytopenia is not the stated change.  Why D wrong: Neutropenia is not the stated change."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Which set of features describes the RECIPIENT twin in TTTS?","options":["Pink colour, more growth, increased renal blood flow, increased urine output, polyhydramnios","Pale look, less growth, oligohydramnios","Anemia and renal failure","Decreased urine output and oligohydramnios"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the recipient twin has polycythemia → pink colour, more growth, increased renal blood flow, increased urine output → polyhydramnios.  Why B wrong: Pale/less-growth/oligohydramnios are donor-twin features.  Why C wrong: Anemia and renal failure are donor-twin features.  Why D wrong: Decreased urine and oligohydramnios are donor-twin features."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Increased urine output in the recipient twin of TTTS leads to which amniotic fluid finding?","options":["Polyhydramnios","Oligohydramnios","Anhydramnios","Normal fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state increased urine output leads to \"POLYHYDRAMNIOS — Recipient Twin.\"  Why B wrong: Oligohydramnios occurs in the donor twin.  Why C wrong: Anhydramnios is not the stated finding.  Why D wrong: The fluid is increased (polyhydramnios), not normal."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In the recipient twin of TTTS, the increased blood volume ultimately leads to which cardiac complication?","options":["Congestive heart failure","Renal failure","Anemia","Pericarditis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the recipient twin's ↑Blood leads to \"Congestive heart failure.\"  Why B wrong: Renal failure is the donor twin's risk.  Why C wrong: Anemia is the donor twin's problem.  Why D wrong: Pericarditis is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"What is the diagnostic criterion of Twin Transfusion Syndrome regarding amniotic fluid?","options":["One fetus has oligohydramnios and the other has polyhydramnios","Both fetuses have oligohydramnios","Both fetuses have polyhydramnios","Both fetuses have normal fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the diagnostic criteria: \"One fetus: OLIGOHYDRAMNIOS\" and \"One fetus: POLYHYDRAMNIOS.\"  Why B wrong: Only one fetus (donor) has oligohydramnios.  Why C wrong: Only one fetus (recipient) has polyhydramnios.  Why D wrong: The fluid is abnormal in both, in opposite directions."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Twin Transfusion Syndrome is MOST common in which type of twin, and MORE common in which type?","options":["Most common in Monochorionic Diamniotic; more common in Monochorionic Monoamniotic","Most common in Dichorionic Diamniotic; more common in conjoined","Most common in conjoined; more common in dichorionic","Most common in monoamniotic; never in diamniotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state TTTS is \"Most common in Monochorionic Diamniotic twins & more common in Monochorionic Monoamniotic twin.\"  Why B wrong: TTTS is a monochorionic complication, not dichorionic.  Why C wrong: This reverses the stated frequencies.  Why D wrong: It is most common in monochorionic diamniotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"An acardius (acardiac) twin is defined as:","options":["One twin has a heart, the other twin does not have a heart","Both twins share one heart","One twin has two hearts","Both twins lack hearts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"ACARDIUS TWIN: One twin has heart, Other twin doesn't have heart.\"  Why B wrong: The twins do not share one heart in this definition.  Why C wrong: No twin has two hearts.  Why D wrong: One twin does have a heart."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In an acardiac twin (twin B without a heart), why does the lower part of the body develop while the upper part does not?","options":["Due to gravity blood reaches the lower extremities, but with no heart it cannot reach the upper body, so the upper body is not formed","Because the upper body has no bones","Because the placenta only supplies the legs","Because the umbilical cord attaches to the head"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in twin B, due to gravity blood reaches lower extremities → lower body develops; but because there is no heart, blood cannot reach the upper body → upper body is not formed.  Why B wrong: Absence of bones is not the stated mechanism.  Why C wrong: Selective placental supply to legs is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Cord attachment to the head is not the mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"When an acardiac twin has a developed lower body but an absent/undeveloped upper body (no head), it is called:","options":["Acardius acephalus","Acardius amorphus","Fetus papyraceus","Vanishing twin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state when the upper part of body is not formed it is \"Called as ACARDIUS ACEPHALUS.\"  Why B wrong: Acardius amorphus is when no body part can be identified.  Why C wrong: Fetus papyraceus is a compressed dead twin.  Why D wrong: Vanishing twin disappears before 14 weeks on USG."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"When no part of the body of an acardiac twin can be identified, it is called:","options":["Acardius amorphus","Acardius acephalus","Thoracopagus","Papyraceus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Sometimes in Acardiac twin → No part of body can be identified → Called as ACARDIUS AMORPHUS.\"  Why B wrong: Acardius acephalus has a developed lower body but no head.  Why C wrong: Thoracopagus is a conjoined twin type.  Why D wrong: Papyraceus is a compressed dead twin."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Which fetal complications are common to BOTH monochorionic and dichorionic twins? (Select all that apply)","options":["Prematurity","Congenital malformation","Abortion (if both fetuses die)","Death of fetus","Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list fetal complications common to both types: prematurity, congenital malformation, abortion (if both fetus die), and death of fetus.  Why B wrong: (It is correct — congenital malformation is listed.)  Why C wrong: (It is correct — abortion is listed.)  Why E wrong: TTTS is a complication seen in monochorionic twins ONLY, not common to both."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON fetal complication of twins?","options":["Prematurity","Congenital malformation","Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome","Cord entanglement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Most common fetal complication of Twins: Prematurity.\"  Why B wrong: Congenital malformation is listed but not the most common.  Why C wrong: TTTS is a monochorionic-only complication, not the most common overall.  Why D wrong: Cord entanglement is specific to monoamniotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In twins, \"Abortion\" as a fetal complication is said to occur when:","options":["Both fetuses die","Only one fetus dies","One fetus vanishes","The placenta detaches"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Abortions: if Both fetus die.\"  Why B wrong: Death of only one fetus is described separately (vanishing twin / fetus compresses).  Why C wrong: Vanishing refers to one twin disappearing, listed under death of fetus.  Why D wrong: Placental detachment is not the stated definition of abortion here."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"When one twin dies in the 1st trimester, the resulting phenomenon is termed:","options":["Vanishing twin","Fetus compresses (papyraceus)","Acardius twin","Conjoined twin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"1st Trimester death: VANISHING TWIN.\"  Why B wrong: Fetus compresses/papyraceus occurs with death after the 1st trimester (2nd trimester).  Why C wrong: Acardius refers to a heartless twin.  Why D wrong: Conjoined twin is a fusion abnormality, unrelated to fetal death timing."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"When one twin dies in the 2nd trimester, the resulting phenomenon is termed:","options":["Fetus compresses (fetus papyraceus)","Vanishing twin","Acardius acephalus","Twin peak sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"2nd Trimester death: FETUS COMPRESSES.\"  Why B wrong: Vanishing twin is 1st trimester death.  Why C wrong: Acardius acephalus is a heartless twin variant.  Why D wrong: Twin peak sign is a USG chorionicity sign."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In a vanishing twin, the initial ultrasound shows a twin pregnancy but one fetus dies and disappears before how many weeks?","options":["Before 14 weeks","Before 20 weeks","Before 24 weeks","Before 28 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"One fetus dies before 14 weeks & simply disappears on USG.\"  Why B wrong: 20 weeks is not the stated cut-off.  Why C wrong: 24 weeks is not the stated cut-off.  Why D wrong: 28 weeks is not the stated cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In a vanishing twin, what does a later (follow-up) USG show?","options":["Only 1 fetus is visible, the other has vanished","Both fetuses visible and healthy","A calcified conjoined mass","Three fetuses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Loteron USG = only 1 fetus visible, other vanishes\" (a later/follow-up scan).  Why B wrong: One fetus has vanished, so not both visible.  Why C wrong: A calcified mass describes fetus papyraceus, not vanishing twin.  Why D wrong: Three fetuses are not described."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"The vanishing twin sequence begins with which finding on the initial USG?","options":["Initial USG shows twin pregnancy","Initial USG shows a single fetus","Initial USG shows no fetus","Initial USG shows a molar pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Initial USG shows Twin Pregnancy\" before one fetus dies and vanishes.  Why B wrong: Initially a twin pregnancy (two fetuses) is seen.  Why C wrong: A fetus is present initially.  Why D wrong: A molar pregnancy is not the initial finding."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"Fetus compresses (fetus papyraceus) is defined as:","options":["One twin dies after the 1st trimester and the living twin grows and compresses the dead twin against the uterine walls","One twin vanishes completely before 14 weeks","Two twins fused at the thorax","One twin with no heart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Fetus compresses/papyraceus: One twin dies after 1st trimester, other twin which is alive grows & compresses the dead twin against the walls of uterus.\"  Why B wrong: Complete disappearance before 14 weeks is a vanishing twin.  Why C wrong: Thorax fusion is thoracopagus (conjoined).  Why D wrong: A heartless twin is an acardiac twin."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In fetus papyraceus, the surviving twin compresses the dead twin against the:","options":["Walls of the uterus","Maternal spine","Placenta only","Cervix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the living twin compresses the dead twin \"against the walls of uterus.\"  Why B wrong: The maternal spine is not the stated site.  Why C wrong: Compression is against uterine walls, not the placenta.  Why D wrong: The cervix is not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"IUGR (intrauterine growth restriction) as a twin complication may affect:","options":["One twin or both twins","Only the male twin","Only monochorionic twins","Only the placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list IUGR affecting \"One Twin\" and \"Both Twin.\"  Why B wrong: Sex-specific restriction is not stated.  Why C wrong: IUGR is not restricted to monochorionic twins here.  Why D wrong: IUGR affects fetal growth, listed as one or both twins."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"\"Discordant growth\" between twins is defined by which criteria?","options":["Growth difference ≥20% and/or weight difference ≥20% (one twin grows less, the other grows more)","Growth difference ≥5%","Weight difference ≥50%","Any difference in fetal sex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define discordant growth as one twin grows less and the other grows more, with growth difference ≥20% and weight difference ≥20%.  Why B wrong: The stated threshold is ≥20%, not ≥5%.  Why C wrong: The stated threshold is ≥20%, not ≥50%.  Why D wrong: Discordance is about growth/weight, not sex."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"In discordant twin growth, the growth pattern is best described as:","options":["One twin grows less while the other twin grows more","Both twins grow equally","Both twins stop growing","One twin grows and the other vanishes before 14 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state discordant growth = \"One twin grows less\" and \"Other twin grows more.\"  Why B wrong: Equal growth is concordant, not discordant.  Why C wrong: Growth arrest of both is not the definition.  Why D wrong: Disappearance before 14 weeks describes a vanishing twin."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"A monochorionic diamniotic (MCDA) twin arises from a monozygotic splitting occurring at which time?","options":["4–8 days","11–13 weeks","32–34 weeks","Before 14 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The dichorionic/monochorionic comparison diagram labels MCDA as \"MZ (4–8 Days),\" indicating monozygotic splitting at 4–8 days.  Why B wrong: 11–13 weeks is the USG chorionicity assessment window.  Why C wrong: 32–34 weeks is the cesarean timing for monoamniotic twins.  Why D wrong: Before 14 weeks refers to vanishing twin timing."},{"section":"Batch 050 · Multifetal/twin pregnancy — prognosis ranking of twin types…","question":"A dichorionic diamniotic (DCDA) twin pregnancy can arise from which zygosity?","options":["Either monozygotic (MZ) or dizygotic (DZ)","Only dizygotic","Only monozygotic","Neither"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels DCDA with both \"MZ\" and \"DZ,\" indicating DCDA can be monozygotic or dizygotic.  Why B wrong: DCDA is not exclusively dizygotic.  Why C wrong: DCDA is not exclusively monozygotic.  Why D wrong: DCDA does arise from zygotic pregnancies."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Which of the following is a recognised maternal complication of twin pregnancy?","options":["Decreased weight gain","Increased weight gain","Reduced iron requirement","Height of uterus less than period of gestation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increased weight gain is listed as a maternal complication of twin pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Weight gain is increased, not decreased, in twin pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Iron requirement is increased; mothers need extra iron.  Why D wrong: In twins the height of the uterus is MORE than the period of gestation, not less."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In a twin pregnancy, the height of the uterus (fundal height) compared to the period of gestation is typically:","options":["Equal to the period of gestation","Less than the period of gestation","More than the period of gestation","Unrelated to the period of gestation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A characteristic finding in twin pregnancy is that the height of the uterus is more than the period of gestation.  Why A wrong: In single pregnancy fundal height roughly equals gestation; in twins it exceeds it.  Why B wrong: A smaller-than-dates uterus suggests IUGR/oligohydramnios, not twins.  Why D wrong: There is a definite relationship — the uterus is larger than expected for dates."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Twin pregnancy increases the risk of which haematological complication in the mother?","options":["Polycythaemia","Anemia","Thrombocytosis","Leukopenia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list increased (↑) anemia as a maternal complication of twin pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Twins cause anemia, not increased red cell mass (polycythaemia).  Why C wrong: Thrombocytosis is not a listed twin complication.  Why D wrong: Leukopenia is not a listed twin complication."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The maternal complications of twin pregnancy are said to include \"all complications of pregnancy which start with the letter 'P'.\" Which of the following is that list's stated EXCEPTION (not increased in twins)?","options":["Preterm labor","Polyhydramnios","Post-term pregnancy","Placenta previa"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"[Except = Post-term Pregnancy]\" — twins are not associated with post-term pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Preterm labor is one of the increased 'P' complications.  Why B wrong: Polyhydramnios is one of the increased 'P' complications.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is one of the increased 'P' complications."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Which of the following \"P\" complications is INCREASED in twin pregnancy according to the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Placenta previa","Abruptio placentae","PIH (Pregnancy-induced hypertension)","Polyhydramnios","Post-term pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Placenta previa, abruptio placentae, PIH and polyhydramnios are all on the list of increased 'P' complications (along with PPH, preterm labor and PROM).  Why E wrong: Post-term pregnancy is the explicit EXCEPTION — it is NOT increased in twins."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"\"PROM\" listed as a complication of twin pregnancy stands for:","options":["Premature Rupture Of Membranes","Prolonged Rupture Of Membranes","Post Rupture Of Myometrium","Preterm Rupture Of Muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PROM = Premature Rupture Of Membranes, listed among the increased 'P' complications in twins.  Why B wrong: Prolonged ROM refers to time since rupture, not the abbreviation intended here.  Why C/D wrong: These are not recognised obstetric terms."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Twin pregnancy is classified as which type of pregnancy requiring extra care?","options":["Low risk pregnancy","High risk pregnancy","Normal risk pregnancy","No additional risk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state twin pregnancy is a high risk pregnancy which needs extra care.  Why A, C, D wrong: They understate the risk; twin pregnancy carries clearly increased maternal and fetal risk."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"A mother with twin pregnancy needs which of the following extra requirements? (Select all that apply)","options":["Extra calories","Extra rest","Extra iron and vitamins","More frequent antenatal visits","Complete bed rest with no antenatal visits"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the mother needs extra calories, extra rest, extra iron, vitamins and more frequent antenatal visits.  Why E wrong: Antenatal visits should be MORE frequent, not stopped; this option contradicts the note."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"What is the most common cause of perinatal mortality in twins?","options":["PPH","Prematurity","Congenital anomaly","Birth asphyxia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of perinatal mortality in twins is prematurity.  Why A wrong: PPH is the most common cause of MATERNAL mortality, not perinatal.  Why C/D wrong: These are not identified as the most common perinatal cause in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"What is the most common cause of maternal mortality in twins?","options":["Prematurity","PPH (Postpartum haemorrhage)","Eclampsia","Sepsis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of maternal mortality in twins is PPH.  Why A wrong: Prematurity is the most common cause of perinatal (fetal/newborn) mortality, not maternal.  Why C/D wrong: Not identified as the most common maternal cause in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"What is the most common complication of twin pregnancy?","options":["Placenta previa","Preterm labor","Post-term pregnancy","Polyhydramnios"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common complication of twin pregnancy is preterm labor.  Why A/D wrong: These are complications but not identified as the single most common.  Why C wrong: Post-term pregnancy is the exception — not associated with twins."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In all twin pregnancies, an injection of Dexamethasone (corticosteroid) is given to the mother between which gestational weeks?","options":["24–26 weeks","28–30 weeks","32–34 weeks","36–38 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state to give inj. Dexamethasone (corticosteroids) between 32–34 weeks in all twin pregnancies.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the stated 32–34 week window."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Which drug (a corticosteroid) is given to the mother in all twin pregnancies to mature the fetal lungs?","options":["Dexamethasone","Methyl ergometrine","Oxytocin","Magnesium sulfate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Inj. Dexamethasone (corticosteroid) is given to mature the fetal lungs.  Why B wrong: Methyl ergometrine is a uterotonic given after delivery, not for lung maturity.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin augments labour; it does not mature lungs.  Why D wrong: Magnesium sulfate is used for eclampsia/neuroprotection, not stated here for lung maturity."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The primary purpose of giving Dexamethasone to a mother with twin pregnancy at 32–34 weeks is:","options":["To induce labour","So that the lungs of the fetus mature","To control maternal blood pressure","To prevent postpartum haemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The stated purpose is so that the lungs of the fetus mature.  Why A wrong: Corticosteroids do not induce labour.  Why C wrong: BP control is not the purpose of antenatal corticosteroids.  Why D wrong: PPH prevention is done with uterotonics, not corticosteroids."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"By maturing the fetal lungs, antenatal Dexamethasone in twins primarily helps to prevent which neonatal complication?","options":["Respiratory Distress Syndrome","Neonatal jaundice","Hypoglycaemia","Necrotising enterocolitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow states mature lungs → prevent Respiratory Distress Syndrome → decrease chances of death of fetus/newborn.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated outcome prevented by corticosteroid-induced lung maturity."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The ultimate benefit of antenatal corticosteroids (Dexamethasone) in twin pregnancy, per the flow, is to:","options":["Decrease chances of death of the fetus/newborn","Shorten labour","Increase birth weight","Reduce maternal anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow ends with \"Decrease chances of death of fetus/newborn.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated end benefit of the corticosteroid."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The two broad modes of delivery of twins described are:","options":["Vaginal delivery and Cesarean section","Forceps and vacuum","Induction and augmentation","Home birth and hospital birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart lists Vaginal delivery and Cesarean section as the modes of delivery of twins.  Why B wrong: Forceps/vacuum are instruments, not the two broad modes shown.  Why C wrong: Induction/augmentation are labour methods, not the delivery modes shown.  Why D wrong: Not a clinical mode-of-delivery classification."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Vaginal delivery of twins can be done in which chorionicity/amnionicity types? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dichorionic diamniotic","Monochorionic diamniotic (uncomplicated)","Monochorionic monoamniotic","Conjoined (congenital/conjoined) twins"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Vaginal delivery can be done in dichorionic diamniotic and uncomplicated monochorionic diamniotic twins.  Why C wrong: Monochorionic monoamniotic twins require cesarean section.  Why D wrong: Congenital/conjoined twins require cesarean section."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The decision for vaginal delivery in twins depends primarily on the presentation of which twin?","options":["The 1st twin","The 2nd twin","Both twins equally","Neither; it depends only on chorionicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state vaginal delivery depends on the presentation of the 1st twin.  Why B wrong: The 2nd twin's presentation is managed after the first is delivered; the initial decision hinges on the 1st.  Why C wrong: It is specifically the 1st twin that determines the initial route.  Why D wrong: Presentation of the 1st twin is a key determinant, not chorionicity alone."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Vaginal delivery of twins is possible only if the 1st twin is in which presentation?","options":["Breech","Transverse lie","Cephalic (Vertex)","Oblique lie"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state vaginal delivery is possible only if the 1st twin is cephalic (VERTEX).  Why A/B/D wrong: If the 1st twin is breech, transverse or non-cephalic, cesarean section is done."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"If the 1st twin is in a breech or transverse lie, the recommended mode of delivery is:","options":["Vaginal delivery","Cesarean section","Forceps delivery","Vacuum extraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if the 1st twin is breech or transverse lie, do cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Vaginal delivery requires the 1st twin to be cephalic.  Why C/D wrong: Instrumental delivery is not indicated for a non-cephalic first twin."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Which of the following is an indication for cesarean section in twin pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Congenital (conjoined) twins","Monochorionic monoamniotic twins","TTTS (Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome) or IUGR","1st twin in breech or transverse lie","Uncomplicated dichorionic diamniotic twins with cephalic 1st twin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cesarean section is indicated for congenital (conjoined) twins, monochorionic monoamniotic twins, any complication such as TTTS or IUGR, and when the 1st twin is breech or transverse lie.  Why E wrong: Uncomplicated dichorionic diamniotic twins with a cephalic 1st twin can be delivered vaginally."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"\"TTTS,\" listed as a complication requiring cesarean section in twins, stands for:","options":["Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome","Total Twin Transfer Situation","Transverse Twin Transverse Sign","Twin Term Transfusion Syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TTTS = Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome, a complication of monochorionic twins listed as an indication for cesarean.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not recognised obstetric terms."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"\"IUGR,\" listed as a twin complication indicating cesarean, stands for:","options":["Intrauterine Growth Restriction","Intrauterine General Rupture","Increased Uterine Growth Rate","Internal Uterine Genital Rupture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IUGR = Intrauterine Growth Restriction.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not correct expansions of IUGR."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"When the 1st twin is cephalic (vertex), how many possible conditions/situations for the 2nd twin are described?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe 3 conditions/situations when the first twin is cephalic (vertex).  Why A, C, D wrong: The text explicitly states there are 3 situations."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In the 1st situation (both twins cephalic), the presentation is:","options":["1st twin cephalic, 2nd twin cephalic","1st twin cephalic, 2nd twin breech","1st twin cephalic, 2nd twin transverse lie","1st twin breech, 2nd twin cephalic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the 1st situation, both the 1st and 2nd twins are cephalic.  Why B wrong: That describes the 2nd situation.  Why C wrong: That describes the 3rd situation.  Why D wrong: A breech 1st twin is not one of the three cephalic-first situations."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In the 2nd situation, the presentations of the twins are:","options":["1st cephalic, 2nd cephalic","1st cephalic, 2nd breech","1st cephalic, 2nd transverse","1st breech, 2nd breech"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the 2nd situation the 1st twin is cephalic and the 2nd twin is breech.  Why A wrong: That is the 1st situation.  Why C wrong: That is the 3rd situation.  Why D wrong: Not one of the described cephalic-first situations."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In the 3rd situation, the presentations of the twins are:","options":["1st cephalic, 2nd cephalic","1st cephalic, 2nd breech","1st cephalic, 2nd transverse lie","1st transverse, 2nd cephalic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the 3rd situation the 1st twin is cephalic and the 2nd twin is in transverse lie.  Why A wrong: That is the 1st situation.  Why B wrong: That is the 2nd situation.  Why D wrong: The 1st twin is cephalic in all three situations."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In the 1st condition (both twins cephalic), after the vaginal delivery of the 1st twin, which injection must NOT be given to the mother?","options":["Methyl ergometrine","Dexamethasone","Magnesium sulfate","Vitamin K"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Remember\" note states after delivery of the 1st twin, do NOT give injection Methyl ergometrine to the mother (a 2nd baby is still in utero).  Why B wrong: Dexamethasone is an antenatal corticosteroid, not relevant here.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the drug specifically prohibited after the 1st twin."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Why should methyl ergometrine not be given after delivery of the 1st twin?","options":["Because the 2nd twin is still in the uterus and the uterus must not contract down yet","Because it causes maternal hypotension","Because it prevents lactation","Because it induces the 1st twin's respiration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Methyl ergometrine is a uterotonic that contracts the uterus; giving it before the 2nd twin is delivered would compromise the undelivered 2nd twin. It is only given once the 2nd baby is delivered.  Why B wrong: Ergometrine causes hypertension, not the reason cited here.  Why C wrong: Lactation is unrelated.  Why D wrong: It does not affect neonatal respiration."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Regarding cord clamping in the 1st twin's delivery, the correct instruction is:","options":["Do not clamp the cord until the 2nd twin is delivered","Cord clamp the 1st twin using clamps and cut in between them","Leave the cord attached to the placenta unclamped","Milk the cord toward the 2nd twin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"CORD CLAMP: 1st twin; Use clamps & cut in between them.\"  Why A wrong: The 1st twin's cord IS clamped after its delivery.  Why C/D wrong: These contradict the instruction to clamp and cut in between the clamps."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"After delivering the 1st twin, which assessments should be done regarding the second baby? (Select all that apply)","options":["Check the Fetal Heart Sound of the 2nd baby","Check the lie of the 2nd baby","Immediately give methyl ergometrine","Wait indefinitely with no monitoring"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes direct to \"now check Fetal Heart Sound, lie of Second Baby.\"  Why C wrong: Methyl ergometrine is only given after the 2nd baby is delivered.  Why D wrong: There is a defined waiting time and active monitoring, not indefinite waiting."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"What is the usual time of delivery of the 2nd baby after the first in a twin vaginal delivery?","options":["15 minutes","30 minutes","45 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the usual time of delivery of the 2nd baby is 15 minutes.  Why B wrong: 30 minutes is the MAXIMUM wait, not the usual time.  Why C/D wrong: These exceed both the usual and maximum stated times."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"What is the maximum time one should wait for delivery of the 2nd twin?","options":["15 minutes","20 minutes","30 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maximum wait for the 2nd baby is until 30 minutes.  Why A wrong: 15 minutes is the usual delivery time, not the maximum.  Why B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 30-minute maximum."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In the 1st condition, once the 2nd baby is delivered vaginally, the nurse/midwife can now:","options":["Give inj. methyl ergometrine to the mother","Withhold all uterotonics permanently","Give a second dose of Dexamethasone","Delay cord clamping of the 1st twin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state after the 2nd baby's vaginal delivery, \"Now you can give inj. Methyl ergometrine to the mother.\"  Why B wrong: Uterotonics are appropriate after the last twin is delivered to prevent PPH.  Why C wrong: Dexamethasone is antenatal, not given postpartum.  Why D wrong: The 1st twin's cord was already clamped."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In the 2nd condition (1st cephalic, 2nd breech), the mode of delivery for each twin is:","options":["1st twin vaginal delivery; 2nd twin vaginal breech delivery","Both twins by cesarean section","1st twin cesarean; 2nd twin vaginal","1st twin vaginal; 2nd twin cesarean"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 1st twin = vaginal delivery and 2nd twin = vaginal breech delivery.  Why B, C, D wrong: The 2nd condition is managed by vaginal delivery of both twins (2nd as vaginal breech), not by cesarean."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In the 3rd condition, the 1st twin is cephalic and the 2nd twin is in transverse lie. After vaginal delivery of the 1st twin, the next step for the transverse 2nd twin is to:","options":["Shift the patient to OT","Give methyl ergometrine immediately","Discharge the patient","Perform another dose of corticosteroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow is 1st twin vaginal delivery → shift the patient to OT → give general anaesthesia → internal podalic version.  Why B wrong: Methyl ergometrine is not given while a twin remains undelivered.  Why C wrong: The 2nd twin still needs to be delivered.  Why D wrong: Corticosteroid is not part of this intrapartum sequence."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In the 3rd condition, after shifting to the OT for the transverse 2nd twin, what is given to the mother?","options":["General anaesthesia","Local anaesthesia only","No anaesthesia","Epidural top-up only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow states \"Give General Anesthesia to mother\" before performing internal podalic version.  Why B, C, D wrong: The procedure (internal podalic version) is done under general anaesthesia, per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The manoeuvre in which the operator takes a hand inside the uterus, holds the feet of the baby and brings it down is called:","options":["Internal podalic version","External cephalic version","Brandt-Andrews manoeuvre","McRoberts manoeuvre"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes name this manoeuvre \"internal podalic version.\"  Why B wrong: External cephalic version turns the fetus externally to cephalic.  Why C wrong: Brandt-Andrews is for placental delivery.  Why D wrong: McRoberts is for shoulder dystocia."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Internal podalic version is done under which type of anaesthesia?","options":["General anaesthesia","Spinal anaesthesia","Local infiltration","No anaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly list internal podalic version as done under general anaesthesia.  Why B, C, D wrong: The notes specify general anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The only indication for internal podalic version, per the notes, is:","options":["If a twin is in transverse lie","If the 1st twin is breech","Post-term pregnancy","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the only indication is if a twin is in transverse lie.  Why B wrong: A breech 1st twin is delivered by cesarean, not internal podalic version.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the internal podalic version indication."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"A serious complication associated with internal podalic version is:","options":["Uterine rupture","Placenta previa","Gestational diabetes","Anencephaly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"chances of uterine rupture\" as a risk of internal podalic version (hand inside uterus manipulating the fetus).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not listed as complications of internal podalic version."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Internal podalic version is contraindicated (C/I) in which patients?","options":["Patients with a previous cesarean section","Primigravida patients","Patients over 30 years old","Patients with anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state internal podalic version is contraindicated in previous cesarean section patients (risk of scar/uterine rupture).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the listed contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Regarding prognosis, which twins have the better (good) prognosis when comparing monochorionic versus dichorionic?","options":["Monochorionic twins","Dichorionic twins","Both are equal","Prognosis cannot be compared"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state good prognosis is in dichorionic twins (each with own chorion, fewer shared-placenta complications).  Why A wrong: Monochorionic twins have a poorer prognosis due to shared circulation (e.g. TTTS).  Why C/D wrong: A clear difference favouring dichorionic is stated."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Dizygotic (two-ova) twins are always which chorionicity, and therefore carry a good prognosis?","options":["Always monochorionic","Always dichorionic","Always monoamniotic","Always conjoined"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state dizygotic twins are always dichorionic, giving a good prognosis.  Why A wrong: Dizygotic twins are never monochorionic; each has its own placenta/chorion.  Why C wrong: They are diamniotic and dichorionic, not monoamniotic.  Why D wrong: Conjoined twins are a monochorionic monoamniotic phenomenon, not dizygotic."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"When comparing delivery outcomes of the 1st twin versus the 2nd twin, which has the better prognosis?","options":["1st twin","2nd twin","Both equal","Neither"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state good prognosis is in the 1st twin (delivered first, less risk of hypoxia/trauma during delivery of the second).  Why B wrong: The 2nd twin faces higher intrapartum risk.  Why C/D wrong: A clear difference favouring the 1st twin is stated."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"In superfecundation/superfetation discussion, dizygotic twins arise from how many ova?","options":["1 ovum","2 ova","3 ova","4 ova"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram indicates dizygotic twins arise from 2 ova (as opposed to monozygotic twins from 1 ovum).  Why A wrong: 1 ovum produces monozygotic twins.  Why C/D wrong: Twins arise from 1 or 2 ova, not 3 or 4."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The phenomenon in which the 2nd ovum is released in the same cycle but fertilized at a different act of coitus is called:","options":["Superfecundation","Superfetation","Monozygosity","Vanishing twin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define superfecundation as the 2nd ovum released in the same cycle but at different acts of coitus and getting fertilized.  Why B wrong: Superfetation involves ova released in DIFFERENT cycles.  Why C wrong: Monozygosity is from a single fertilized ovum splitting.  Why D wrong: Vanishing twin refers to loss of one twin, not this fertilization phenomenon."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The phenomenon in which the 2nd ovum is released in a different cycle (fertilization of ova from different menstrual cycles) is called:","options":["Superfecundation","Superfetation","Dizygosity","Conjoined twinning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define superfetation as the 2nd ovum released in different cycles.  Why A wrong: Superfecundation is same cycle, different acts of coitus.  Why C wrong: Dizygosity simply means two ova/zygotes, not the cyclic timing described.  Why D wrong: Conjoined twinning is unrelated to ovulation timing."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The key difference between superfecundation and superfetation relates to:","options":["The number of ova (1 vs 2)","The timing of the 2nd ovum's release — same cycle (different coitus) vs different cycles","The chorionicity of the placenta","Whether the twins are conjoined"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Superfecundation = 2nd ovum released in the same cycle but fertilized at different acts of coitus; superfetation = 2nd ovum released in a different cycle. The distinction is the timing/cycle of release.  Why A wrong: Both involve 2 ova (dizygotic).  Why C/D wrong: Chorionicity and conjoining are not the defining difference here."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"What is the normal duration of a pregnancy?","options":["8 months / 36 weeks / 240 days","9 months + 7 days / 40 weeks / 280 days","10 months / 44 weeks / 300 days","9 months / 38 weeks / 266 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state normal duration of pregnancy = 9 months +7 days OR 40 weeks OR 280 days.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 40 weeks / 280 days figure."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The normal duration of pregnancy in days is:","options":["260 days","270 days","280 days","300 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal pregnancy duration is 280 days (equal to 40 weeks).  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not equal the stated 280 days."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Which formula is used to calculate the Expected Date of Delivery (EDD)?","options":["Naegele's formula","Johnson's formula","McDonald's rule","Bishop's score"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes use Naegele's Formula: 1st day of LMP + 9 months + 7 days = EDD.  Why B wrong: Johnson's formula estimates fetal weight.  Why C wrong: McDonald's rule relates fundal height to gestational age.  Why D wrong: Bishop score assesses cervical readiness for induction."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"According to Naegele's formula, the EDD is calculated by taking the 1st day of the LMP and adding:","options":["9 months and 7 days","7 months and 9 days","9 months only","280 days to the last day of LMP"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Naegele's formula = 1st day of LMP + 9 months + 7 days.  Why B wrong: The order/values are reversed and incorrect.  Why C wrong: The 7-day addition is omitted.  Why D wrong: The formula uses the FIRST day of the LMP, not the last day."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Using Naegele's formula, if the LMP is 10th August 2020, the calculated EDD is approximately:","options":["17th May 2021","17th August 2021","10th May 2021","3rd August 2021"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The worked example gives LMP 10th August 2020 + 9 months + 7 days = 17th May 2021 (note the source printed \"202\" for the year, i.e. 2021).  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match adding 9 months and 7 days to 10 August 2020."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"What percentage of females deliver on their exact EDD?","options":["4%","10%","25%","50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state only 4% of females deliver on their exact EDD.  Why B, C wrong: These figures are not stated.  Why D wrong: 50% is the figure for delivering within 1 week before or after the EDD, not exactly on it."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"According to the notes, what percentage of females deliver within 1 week before or after their EDD?","options":["4%","25%","50%","90%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 50% of females deliver 1 week before or after their EDD.  Why A wrong: 4% is the figure for delivering exactly on the EDD.  Why B, D wrong: These figures are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"If a woman is not delivered by her EDD, the maximum period she should be allowed to wait is until:","options":["40 weeks","41 weeks","42 weeks","44 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if not delivered by EDD, wait — maximum wait = until 42 weeks.  Why A wrong: 40 weeks is the EDD itself, not the maximum wait.  Why B/D wrong: The stated maximum is 42 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Any pregnancy which continues to or beyond how many weeks is called post-term pregnancy (post maturity)?","options":["≥40 weeks","≥41 weeks","≥42 weeks","≥44 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define post-term pregnancy (post maturity) as any pregnancy that continues ≥42 weeks.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the stated ≥42-week threshold."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"\"Post maturity\" is another name for which condition?","options":["Preterm pregnancy","Post-term pregnancy","Term pregnancy","Molar pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The heading and text equate post-term pregnancy with post maturity (≥42 weeks).  Why A wrong: Preterm is before 37 weeks.  Why C wrong: Term is 37–42 weeks.  Why D wrong: Molar pregnancy is a gestational trophoblastic disease, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"According to the notes, the two important reasons/causes of post-term pregnancy are:","options":["Wrong dates and Anencephaly","Diabetes and Hypertension","Twins and Polyhydramnios","Anemia and Preeclampsia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list two important reasons: Wrong dates and Anencephaly.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the two stated causes of post-term pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"The 1st step in assessing a patient with suspected post-term pregnancy is to consider \"wrong dates\" and to:","options":["Review the menstrual history","Perform an immediate cesarean section","Give corticosteroids","Start oxytocin infusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 1st step in assessing a post-term patient is to review the menstrual history (to rule out wrong dates).  Why B wrong: Immediate cesarean is not the first assessment step.  Why C wrong: Corticosteroids are for preterm lung maturity, not post-term assessment.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is a management option later, not the first assessment step."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"Anencephaly, a fetal cause of post-term pregnancy, refers to:","options":["A neural tube defect with absence of a major portion of the brain/skull","Absence of the heart","Absence of kidneys","Absence of limbs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anencephaly is a neural tube defect with absence of a major part of the brain and skull; it is listed as a fetal cause of post-term pregnancy (impaired fetal-pituitary/adrenal signalling delays labour onset).  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe other anomalies, not anencephaly."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"A woman with a twin pregnancy at 33 weeks is admitted. Based on the notes, which of the following management actions is appropriate at this gestation?","options":["Administer inj. Dexamethasone to promote fetal lung maturity","Give methyl ergometrine to prevent PPH","Perform internal podalic version","Withhold all antenatal care"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In all twin pregnancies, Dexamethasone is given between 32–34 weeks to mature fetal lungs; 33 weeks is within this window.  Why B wrong: Methyl ergometrine is a postpartum uterotonic, not for antenatal use.  Why C wrong: Internal podalic version is an intrapartum manoeuvre for a transverse 2nd twin, not indicated antenatally.  Why D wrong: Twin pregnancy is high risk and needs more frequent antenatal care."},{"section":"Batch 051 · Maternal complications of twin pregnancy, corticosteroid use in twins…","question":"A twin pregnancy is diagnosed as monochorionic monoamniotic. Based on the notes, the recommended mode of delivery is:","options":["Vaginal delivery if 1st twin is cephalic","Cesarean section","Internal podalic version","Vaginal breech delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monochorionic monoamniotic twins are an indication for cesarean section per the notes.  Why A wrong: Vaginal delivery is reserved for dichorionic diamniotic or uncomplicated monochorionic diamniotic twins.  Why C/D wrong: These are intrapartum manoeuvres/routes not indicated for monoamniotic twins."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Post-term pregnancy, associated with the problems shown on the page, is defined as pregnancy continuing up to or beyond:","options":["40 weeks","41 weeks","42 weeks","38 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page titles the problems box \"Problems with Post Term Pregnancy (≥42 weeks)\"; post-term is 42 weeks or more.  Why A wrong: 40 weeks is the expected due date (term).  Why B wrong: 41 weeks is late-term, not yet post-term.  Why D wrong: 38 weeks is early term."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In post-term pregnancy, when the weight of the fetus increases to ≥ 4 kg, the resulting condition is called:","options":["Macrosomia","Oligohydramnios","Placental aging","Growth restriction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows \"Weight of fetus ↑ ≥ 4 kg → MACROSOMIA.\"  Why B wrong: Oligohydramnios is decreased amniotic fluid, a separate problem.  Why C wrong: Placental aging is calcification/infarction of placenta, a separate branch.  Why D wrong: Growth restriction is a small (not large) fetus, opposite of macrosomia."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In fetal macrosomia, excessive fat is deposited mainly in which area, leading to delivery difficulty?","options":["Shoulder and trunk area","Head and neck only","Lower limbs only","Face and scalp"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Excessive fat gets deposited in shoulder & trunk area.\"  Why B wrong: The notes specify shoulder and trunk, not head/neck.  Why C wrong: Lower limbs are not the described deposition site.  Why D wrong: Face and scalp are not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"During vaginal delivery of a macrosomic baby, the excessive shoulder fat causes:","options":["Difficulty in delivery of shoulders (shoulder dystocia)","Rapid precipitate delivery","Face presentation","Cord prolapse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows \"At time of vaginal delivery → Difficulty in delivery of shoulders → 1. Shoulder dystocia.\"  Why B wrong: Macrosomia causes obstruction, not precipitate delivery.  Why C wrong: Face presentation is unrelated to shoulder fat.  Why D wrong: Cord prolapse is associated with oligohydramnios/malpresentation, not shoulder fat."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Which of the following is an INCREASED complication of macrosomia in post-term pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Shoulder dystocia","Increased chances of cesarean section","Increased chances of intracranial haemorrhage","Oligohydramnios","Placental calcification"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The macrosomia branch lists 1) Shoulder dystocia, 2) ↑ chances of cesarean section, 3) ↑ chances of intracranial haemorrhage.  Why D wrong: Oligohydramnios is a separate branch (decreased amniotic fluid), not a consequence of macrosomia.  Why E wrong: Placental calcification belongs to the placental aging branch."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In post-term pregnancy, oligohydramnios refers to:","options":["Decreased amniotic fluid","Increased amniotic fluid","Bloody amniotic fluid","Absent placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"OLIGOHYDRAMNIOS → Amniotic fluid is decreased.\"  Why B wrong: Increased fluid is polyhydramnios.  Why C wrong: Bloody fluid is not the definition of oligohydramnios.  Why D wrong: Absent placenta is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In oligohydramnios, decreased amniotic fluid causes less space in the uterus, which most directly leads to:","options":["Compression of the baby's cord","Increased fetal weight","Placental calcification","Macrosomia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows \"Less space in uterus → Cord of baby gets compressed.\"  Why B wrong: Increased weight relates to macrosomia branch.  Why C wrong: Calcification is under placental aging.  Why D wrong: Macrosomia is a different branch."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In oligohydramnios, cord compression of the baby directly results in:","options":["Fetal distress","Increased amniotic fluid","Reduced fetal weight","Improved oxygenation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows \"Cord of baby gets compressed → Fetus distress.\"  Why B wrong: Fluid is decreased, not increased.  Why C wrong: Cord compression causes distress, not weight change.  Why D wrong: Compression reduces (does not improve) oxygenation."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In the oligohydramnios pathway, once the fetus is distressed, it will:","options":["Pass meconium into the amniotic fluid","Gain weight rapidly","Increase amniotic fluid production","Stop moving permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow states \"Fetus distress → Fetal will pass meconium in amniotic fluid.\"  Why B wrong: Weight gain is not part of this pathway.  Why C wrong: The fluid is already decreased in oligohydramnios.  Why D wrong: The notes describe meconium passage, not cessation of movement."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"When the distressed fetus swallows the meconium-stained amniotic fluid, it causes:","options":["Meconium aspiration syndrome","Respiratory distress syndrome from surfactant deficiency","Neonatal jaundice","Erythroblastosis fetalis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow ends with \"Fetus will swallow the meconium stained amniotic fluid → Causing Meconium Aspiration Syndrome.\"  Why B wrong: RDS is from surfactant deficiency in preterm babies, not meconium.  Why C wrong: Jaundice is unrelated to this pathway.  Why D wrong: Erythroblastosis fetalis is Rh incompatibility, not meconium aspiration."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In post-term pregnancy, \"placental aging\" is characterised by which changes in the placenta?","options":["Calcification and infarction","Increased vascularity and growth","Enlargement with cyst formation","Increased amniotic fluid production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Placental Aging → Placenta will have calcification & infarction.\"  Why B wrong: Aging reduces, not increases, placental function.  Why C wrong: Cystic enlargement is not described.  Why D wrong: The placenta does not produce amniotic fluid in this context."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In placental aging, less blood going to the fetus produces a state called:","options":["Utero-placental insufficiency","Placenta previa","Placental abruption","Vasa previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow states \"Less blood goes to fetus → UTERO PLACENTAL insufficiency.\"  Why B wrong: Placenta previa is low implantation, not aging insufficiency.  Why C wrong: Abruption is premature separation, not aging.  Why D wrong: Vasa previa is fetal vessels over the os, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Utero-placental insufficiency from placental aging leads to fetal distress and an increased chance of: (Select all that apply)","options":["Still birth","Intrauterine death","Macrosomia","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Increased amniotic fluid"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The placental aging branch ends with \"Fetal distress → ↑ chance of still Birth → ↑ chance of Intrauterine Death.\"  Why C wrong: Macrosomia is the macrosomia branch, from increased weight.  Why D wrong: Meconium aspiration is the endpoint of the oligohydramnios branch.  Why E wrong: No increase in amniotic fluid is described."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"On examination of a post-term pregnancy, maternal weight is typically found to be:","options":["Stationary or decreased (due to decreased amniotic fluid)","Rapidly increasing","Doubling every week","Fluctuating unpredictably by 5 kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examination point 1 states \"Weight = stationary/ decrease (Amniotic fluid ↓).\"  Why B wrong: Weight does not rapidly increase; fluid is falling.  Why C wrong: No doubling is described.  Why D wrong: Large 5 kg fluctuations are not described."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"On per-abdomen examination of a post-term pregnancy, the finding is:","options":["Uterus is full of fetus","Uterus is empty","Uterus smaller than dates","Multiple gestation confirmed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examination point 2 states \"Per Abdomen = Uterus is full of fetus.\"  Why B wrong: The uterus contains the term/post-term fetus, not empty.  Why C wrong: \"Full of fetus\" is described, not smaller.  Why D wrong: Multiple gestation is not a finding described here."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In post-term pregnancy, the patient's GIRTH (abdominal circumference) shows what change, and why?","options":["Decreases, because amniotic fluid decreases","Increases, because amniotic fluid increases","Stays exactly the same","Decreases, because the fetus shrinks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examination point 3 states \"GIRTH (Patient's Abdominal Circumference) → Decrease (Amniotic fluid ↓).\"  Why B wrong: Amniotic fluid is decreased, so girth falls.  Why C wrong: The notes specify a decrease.  Why D wrong: The decrease is due to reduced fluid, not fetal shrinkage."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In post-term pregnancy, while the patient's abdominal circumference decreases, the fetus's own abdominal circumference:","options":["Increases, due to macrosomia","Decreases, due to starvation","Remains unchanged","Cannot be measured"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"(Fetus: Abdominal Circumference ↑ due to macrosomia).\"  Why B wrong: The fetal abdomen enlarges from fat deposition, not starvation.  Why C wrong: The notes indicate an increase.  Why D wrong: Fetal AC is a standard USG measurement."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Which methods are used to confirm post-term pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Menstrual history","USG (ultrasonography)","Random urine glucose","Skull X-ray of mother","Serum bilirubin"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Under \"How to confirm Post term Pregnancy?\" the notes list 1. Menstrual History and 2. USG.  Why C wrong: Urine glucose is not a listed confirmatory method.  Why D wrong: Maternal skull X-ray is not used.  Why E wrong: Serum bilirubin is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"According to the notes, the best USG to confirm gestational age / post-term pregnancy is one done at which time?","options":["First trimester (early / 1st USG of pregnancy)","Third trimester only","During labour","Postnatal period"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note asks the best USG to confirm gestational age and answers \"1st use of pregnancy\" (the earliest/first-trimester scan is most accurate for dating).  Why B wrong: Third-trimester dating is least accurate.  Why C wrong: USG during labour does not date the pregnancy accurately.  Why D wrong: Postnatal scan cannot date the pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The management of post-term pregnancy described on the page is to:","options":["Start labour pain and induce labour","Wait until 45 weeks","Give tocolytics to stop contractions","Perform immediate cesarean in all cases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under Management the flow is \"Start Labour Pain → Induce labor.\"  Why B wrong: Prolonging beyond post-term increases risk; the plan is to induce.  Why C wrong: Tocolytics stop labour — the opposite of the intended induction.  Why D wrong: Induction (not universal cesarean) is the management shown."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The pelvis (pelvic girdle) is composed of how many bones?","options":["4 bones","2 bones","3 bones","6 bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Pelvis is composed of 4 Bones.\"  Why B wrong: Two is the number of innominate bones only.  Why C wrong: Three is the number of bones fusing to form one innominate bone.  Why D wrong: Six is not the stated total."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Which bones make up the pelvis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Sacrum","Coccyx","Two innominate bones","Femur","Lumbar vertebrae"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists the 4 pelvic bones as sacrum, coccyx, and 2 innominate bones.  Why D wrong: The femur is a leg bone, not part of the pelvic girdle (its head only attaches at the acetabulum).  Why E wrong: Lumbar vertebrae are part of the spine, not the pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Each innominate bone (hip bone) is formed by the fusion of how many bones?","options":["3 bones","2 bones","4 bones","5 bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Each innominate bone (hip Bone) is formed by the fusion of 3 Bones.\"  Why B wrong: Two is incorrect; three bones fuse.  Why C wrong: Four is the total pelvic bone count, not the innominate fusion count.  Why D wrong: Five is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The three bones that fuse to form each innominate (hip) bone are: (Select all that apply)","options":["Ilium","Ischium","Pubis","Sacrum","Coccyx"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists ilium, ischium, and pubis as the three fusing bones.  Why D wrong: Sacrum is a separate pelvic bone, not part of the innominate fusion.  Why E wrong: Coccyx is a separate pelvic bone."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In the innominate bone, the site where the ilium, ischium and pubis meet/fuse (as labelled in the figure) is the:","options":["Triradiate cartilage","Pubic symphysis","Sacroiliac joint","Acetabulum rim"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Innominate Bone figure labels the junction of the three bones as \"Triradiate cartilage.\"  Why B wrong: The pubic symphysis is where the two pubic bones of opposite sides meet.  Why C wrong: The sacroiliac joint connects ilium to sacrum.  Why D wrong: The acetabulum is the hip socket; the fusion cartilage is the triradiate cartilage."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Which part of the hip bone does the ILIUM form?","options":["The superior part of the hip bone","The inferior part of the hip bone","The anterior socket only","The tailbone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"ILIUM: Forms the superior part of hip bone.\"  Why B wrong: The inferior parts are formed by ischium and pubis.  Why C wrong: The ilium is the broad superior part, not just a socket.  Why D wrong: The tailbone is the coccyx."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Which of the following are bony landmarks/features of the iliac bone as labelled in the figure? (Select all that apply)","options":["Iliac crest","Anterior superior iliac spine","Greater sciatic notch","Ischial tuberosity","Pubic tubercle"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The iliac bone figure labels iliac crest, anterior superior iliac spine, posterior superior iliac spine, posterior inferior iliac spine, anterior inferior iliac spine, and greater sciatic notch.  Why D wrong: Ischial tuberosity is a feature of the ischium, not the ilium.  Why E wrong: Pubic tubercle is a feature of the pubic bone."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In the iliac bone figure, which spines are labelled on the POSTERIOR aspect? (Select all that apply)","options":["Posterior superior iliac spine","Posterior inferior iliac spine","Anterior superior iliac spine","Anterior inferior iliac spine"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the posterior superior iliac spine and posterior inferior iliac spine on the posterior side.  Why C wrong: Anterior superior iliac spine is on the anterior aspect.  Why D wrong: Anterior inferior iliac spine is on the anterior aspect."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The rough posterior part of the iliac bone is called the:","options":["Iliac tuberosity","Iliac fossa","Iliac crest","Greater sciatic notch"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Posterior part of Iliac bone is Rough, called as Iliac tuberosity.\"  Why B wrong: The iliac fossa is the anterior depressed part.  Why C wrong: The iliac crest is the upper margin.  Why D wrong: The greater sciatic notch is a separate posterior indentation."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The anterior depressed (concave) part of the iliac bone is called the:","options":["Iliac fossa","Iliac tuberosity","Acetabulum","Obturator foramen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Anterior Part has depressed part called Iliac fossa.\"  Why B wrong: The iliac tuberosity is the rough posterior part.  Why C wrong: The acetabulum is the hip socket.  Why D wrong: The obturator foramen is the large opening in the ischium/pubis region."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The ilium bone is attached to the sacrum at which joint?","options":["Sacroiliac joint","Pubic symphysis","Hip (acetabular) joint","Sacrococcygeal joint"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Ilium Bone is attached to sacrum at sacroiliac joint.\"  Why B wrong: The pubic symphysis joins the two pubic bones.  Why C wrong: The hip joint is between the acetabulum and femoral head.  Why D wrong: The sacrococcygeal joint is between sacrum and coccyx."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The head of the femur attaches to the innominate bone at which structure?","options":["Acetabulum","Obturator foramen","Ischial spine","Triradiate cartilage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the acetabulum as \"(head of femur attaches here).\"  Why B wrong: The obturator foramen is a large opening, not the femoral socket.  Why C wrong: The ischial spine is a pointed landmark, not the femoral joint.  Why D wrong: The triradiate cartilage is the fusion site of the three bones."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Which of the following are labelled parts of the ischium in the figure? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ischial body","Ischial spine","Ischial tuberosity","Ischial ramus","Iliac crest"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels ischial body, ischial spine, ischial tuberosity, and ischial ramus (plus lesser sciatic notch, obturator foramen, ischiopubic ramus).  Why E wrong: The iliac crest is a feature of the ilium, not the ischium."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"According to the notes, the three main parts of the ischium are: (Select all that apply)","options":["Ischial body","Ischial tuberosity","Ischial ramus","Ischial fossa","Ischial crest"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Parts of Ischium\" lists 1. Ischial Body, 2. Ischial Tuberosity, 3. Ischial ramus.  Why D wrong: \"Ischial fossa\" is not a listed part.  Why E wrong: \"Ischial crest\" is not a listed part."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The important pointed structure present posteriorly on the ischium, described as a \"very important landmark,\" is the:","options":["Ischial spine","Ischial tuberosity","Ischial body","Obturator foramen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Posteriorly an important pointed structure is present called ISCHIAL spine (very important Landmark).\"  Why B wrong: The ischial tuberosity is the sitting bone, not the pointed landmark described.  Why C wrong: The ischial body is the main mass, not the pointed spine.  Why D wrong: The obturator foramen is an opening, not a spine."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The station of the fetal head during labour is described with respect to which pelvic landmark?","options":["Ischial spine","Iliac crest","Pubic symphysis","Sacral promontory"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under \"Importance of Ischial Spine,\" point 1 states \"Station of Fetal head is described with respect to Ischial spine.\"  Why B wrong: The iliac crest is not the reference for station.  Why C wrong: The pubic symphysis is not the station reference.  Why D wrong: The sacral promontory is used for pelvic diameters, not station."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"When the lowest part of the fetal head is felt exactly at the level of the ischial spine, the station is called:","options":["0 (zero) station","+5 station","−5 station","Floating"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"If lowest part of head of fetus is felt at level of Ischial spine we call it 'O' station.\"  Why B wrong: +5 means the head is 5 cm below the ischial spine.  Why C wrong: −5 means the head is 5 cm above the ischial spine.  Why D wrong: \"Floating\" is not the zero-station term used here."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"If the fetal head is ABOVE the level of the ischial spine, the station is denoted by which sign?","options":["Minus (−) sign","Plus (+) sign","Zero (0)","Equals (=) sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"If head is above the level of Ischial spine denoted by '−' sign.\"  Why B wrong: The plus sign is for the head below the ischial spine.  Why C wrong: Zero indicates exactly at the spine.  Why D wrong: The equals sign is not used for station."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"If the fetal head is BELOW the level of the ischial spine, the station is denoted by which sign?","options":["Plus (+) sign","Minus (−) sign","Zero (0)","Star (*)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"If head is below the level of Ischial spine denoted by '+' sign.\"  Why B wrong: The minus sign is used for the head above the ischial spine.  Why C wrong: Zero indicates exactly at the spine.  Why D wrong: A star is not used for station."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In the station scale, the number (e.g. −3 or +2) represents:","options":["The distance in centimetres from the ischial spine","The number of contractions per minute","The fetal heart rate in tens","The cervical dilation in centimetres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"The number represents distance in cms from the ischial spine.\"  Why B wrong: Station is not a contraction count.  Why C wrong: It is not related to fetal heart rate.  Why D wrong: Cervical dilation is a separate measurement."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The station scale illustrated on the page ranges from which negative value to which positive value?","options":["−5 to +5","−3 to +3","−10 to +10","0 to +5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows the station scale marked from −5, −4, −3, −2, −1, 0 (ischial spine), +1, +2, +3, +4, +5.  Why B wrong: The scale extends to ±5, not ±3.  Why C wrong: The scale shown is ±5, not ±10.  Why D wrong: The scale includes negative values, not just 0 to +5."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"A station of \"−3\" means the fetal head is:","options":["3 cm above the ischial spine","3 cm below the ischial spine","At the ischial spine","3 cm from the pubic symphysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page example states \"'−3' = Head is 3 cms, above ischial spine.\"  Why B wrong: Below would be a plus sign (+3).  Why C wrong: At the spine is 0 station.  Why D wrong: Station is measured from the ischial spine, not the pubic symphysis."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"A station of \"+2\" means the fetal head is:","options":["2 cm below the ischial spine","2 cm above the ischial spine","At the ischial spine","2 cm above the pubic symphysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page example states \"'+2' = Head is 2 cms, below ischial spine.\"  Why B wrong: Above would be a minus sign (−2).  Why C wrong: At the spine is 0 station.  Why D wrong: Station is measured from the ischial spine."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Which important muscle is attached at the level of the ischial spine?","options":["Levator ani muscle","Rectus abdominis muscle","Psoas major muscle","Gluteus maximus muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"At level of Ischial spine (Important muscle) = Levator ani muscle is attached.\"  Why B wrong: The rectus abdominis is an abdominal wall muscle.  Why C wrong: The psoas major is a hip flexor of the posterior abdominal wall.  Why D wrong: The gluteus maximus is a buttock muscle, not attached at the ischial spine level as described."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The ischial spine is the site for giving which nerve block?","options":["Pudendal nerve block","Sciatic nerve block","Femoral nerve block","Obturator nerve block"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"It is the site for giving Pudendal Nerve Block.\"  Why B wrong: Sciatic nerve block is not the block referenced here.  Why C wrong: Femoral nerve block is not associated with the ischial spine landmark.  Why D wrong: Obturator nerve block is not the described procedure."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"During labour, internal rotation of the fetal head occurs at the level of which structure?","options":["Ischial spine","Pubic symphysis","Sacral promontory","Iliac crest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"During Labor Internal Rotation occurs at the level of ischial spine.\"  Why B wrong: The pubic symphysis is not the site of internal rotation.  Why C wrong: The sacral promontory is not where internal rotation occurs.  Why D wrong: The iliac crest is not related to internal rotation."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Which of the following are labelled parts/features of the pubic bone in the figure? (Select all that apply)","options":["Superior ramus of pubis","Pubic tubercle","Pubic body","Inferior ramus of pubis","Ischial tuberosity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The pubic bone figure labels superior ramus of pubis, pubic tubercle, pubic crest, pubic body, and inferior ramus of pubis (plus ischiopubic ramus).  Why E wrong: The ischial tuberosity is a feature of the ischium, not the pubis."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The point where both pubic bones (of opposite sides) meet is called the:","options":["Pubic symphysis","Sacroiliac joint","Triradiate cartilage","Acetabulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Where both sides of pubic bones meet is called Pubic Symphysis.\"  Why B wrong: The sacroiliac joint is between ilium and sacrum.  Why C wrong: The triradiate cartilage is inside one innominate bone.  Why D wrong: The acetabulum is the hip socket."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The subpubic angle is defined as the angle between the inferior rami of both pubic bones and the:","options":["Pubic symphysis","Sacral promontory","Ischial tuberosities","Iliac crests"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines \"Subpubic angle is the angle between the inferior Ramii of both pubic bones and Pubic symphysis.\"  Why B wrong: The sacral promontory is not part of the subpubic angle.  Why C wrong: The ischial tuberosities are not part of this definition.  Why D wrong: The iliac crests are not part of the subpubic angle."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In the female (gynaecoid) pelvis, the subpubic angle is:","options":["> 90° (obtuse)","< 90° (acute)","Exactly 45°","0° (closed)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"In Female pelvis (gynaecoid pelvis) subpubic angle is >90° (obtuse).\"  Why B wrong: < 90° (acute) describes the male/android pelvis.  Why C wrong: 45° is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: The angle is open (obtuse), not closed."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"In the male (android) pelvis, the subpubic angle is:","options":["< 90° (acute)","> 90° (obtuse)","Exactly 90°","180° (straight)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"In Male pelvis (android pelvis) subpubic angle is <90° (acute).\"  Why B wrong: > 90° (obtuse) describes the female/gynaecoid pelvis.  Why C wrong: Exactly 90° is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 180° is not correct."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The gynaecoid pelvis (with subpubic angle > 90°) is characteristically the pelvis of which sex?","options":["Female","Male","Neither","Both equally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note pairs \"Female pelvis (gynaecoid pelvis).\"  Why B wrong: The android pelvis is the male type.  Why C wrong: The gynaecoid type is specifically the female pelvis.  Why D wrong: It is characteristic of the female pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Which parts of the sacrum region are labelled in the figure? (Select all that apply)","options":["Sacral promontory","Ala of sacral bone","Foramen","Coccyx","Pubic tubercle"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The sacrum figure labels sacral promontory, ala of sacral bone, foramen, and coccyx (tailbone).  Why E wrong: The pubic tubercle is a feature of the pubic bone, not the sacrum."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The coccyx (tailbone), as noted in the figure, is composed of how many fused vertebrae?","options":["4 fused vertebrae","5 fused vertebrae","3 fused vertebrae","2 fused vertebrae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels \"Coccyx (tailbone) (4 fused vertebrae).\"  Why B wrong: Five fused vertebrae describes the sacrum, not the coccyx (per this note).  Why C wrong: Three is not the stated number.  Why D wrong: Two is not the stated number."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"According to the figure, the coccyx (tailbone) is:","options":["Movable","Completely fixed and immovable","Fused to the pubis","Absent in females"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the coccyx as \"Movable.\"  Why B wrong: The note describes it as movable, not fixed.  Why C wrong: The coccyx is attached to the sacrum, not fused to the pubis.  Why D wrong: The coccyx is present in both sexes."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"If ONE ala of the sacral bone is absent, the resulting pelvis is called:","options":["Naegele pelvis","Robert pelvis","Gynaecoid pelvis","Android pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"If one ala of sacral bone is absent = pelvis is called Naegele pelvis.\"  Why B wrong: Robert pelvis is when BOTH alae are absent.  Why C wrong: Gynaecoid is the normal female pelvis.  Why D wrong: Android is the male-type pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"If BOTH alae of the sacral bone are absent, the resulting pelvis is called:","options":["Robert pelvis","Naegele pelvis","Gynaecoid pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"If both ala of sacral bone are absent = Pelvis is called ROBERT pelvis.\"  Why B wrong: Naegele pelvis is when only ONE ala is absent.  Why C wrong: Gynaecoid is the normal female pelvis.  Why D wrong: Anthropoid is not mentioned as the both-alae-absent type."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Naegele pelvis and Robert pelvis are both varieties of:","options":["Contracted pelvis","Normal gynaecoid pelvis","Roomy pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Naegele pelvis & ROBERT pelvis are varieties of contracted pelvis.\"  Why B wrong: They are abnormal (contracted), not normal.  Why C wrong: They are contracted (narrow), not roomy.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid is a separate pelvic shape, not the category given here."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"If a female with a contracted pelvis (Naegele or Robert type) conceives, what is the management according to the notes?","options":["Cesarean section must always be done; normal delivery is not possible","Normal vaginal delivery is always possible","Only forceps delivery is needed","Induction of labour is preferred over cesarean"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow states \"If a female having any of these pelvis conceives → Always have to do Cesarean Section; Normal delivery is not possible.\"  Why B wrong: The notes explicitly state normal delivery is NOT possible.  Why C wrong: Cesarean, not forceps, is the required management.  Why D wrong: Cesarean is mandated, not induction."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"The forward-projecting anterior upper margin of the first sacral vertebra, an important landmark for pelvic measurement, is labelled in the figure as the:","options":["Sacral promontory","Ala of sacral bone","Sacral foramen","Coccyx"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sacrum figure specifically labels the \"sacral promontory.\"  Why B wrong: The ala is the wing/lateral part of the sacrum.  Why C wrong: The foramen is the opening for nerve passage.  Why D wrong: The coccyx is the tailbone below the sacrum."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"Which additional bony features are labelled on the ischium figure besides its three main parts? (Select all that apply)","options":["Lesser sciatic notch","Obturator foramen","Ischiopubic ramus","Acetabulum","Sacral promontory"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The ischium figure labels lesser sciatic notch, obturator foramen, ischiopubic ramus, and acetabulum (head of femur attaches here).  Why E wrong: The sacral promontory is a sacrum landmark shown on a later page, not on the ischium figure."},{"section":"Batch 052 · Post-term pregnancy, Anatomy of the pelvic girdle…","question":"A term pregnant woman at 42+ weeks presents with a fundal height that is stationary, decreased abdominal girth, and a uterus \"full of fetus.\" An early-pregnancy USG had confirmed her dates. What is the most appropriate next management step?","options":["Induce labour","Wait another 3 weeks","Start tocolytics","Reassure and discharge with no follow-up"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These findings confirm post-term pregnancy; the management given is to start labour pain and induce labour.  Why B wrong: Further waiting increases fetal risk (stillbirth, meconium aspiration).  Why C wrong: Tocolytics inhibit labour, the opposite of what is needed.  Why D wrong: Post-term pregnancy requires active management (induction), not discharge."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The maternal pelvis is divided anatomically into false pelvis and true pelvis by which structure?","options":["Pelvic brim","Ischial spine","Ischial tuberosity","Sacral promontory"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pelvis brim divides the maternal pelvis into the false pelvis (above) and the true pelvis (below).  Why B wrong: The ischial spine marks the level of the pelvic cavity/midpelvis, not the false–true division.  Why C wrong: The ischial tuberosity marks the level of the pelvic outlet.  Why D wrong: The sacral promontory is one landmark of the brim, not the divider itself."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"Which is the correct anterior-to-posterior order of structures forming the pelvic brim?","options":["Sacral promontory → sacroiliac joint → pubic symphysis","Pubic symphysis → pubic crest → pubic tubercle → superior pubic ramii → iliopectineal eminence → iliopectineal line → sacroiliac joint → ala of sacrum → sacral promontory","Pubic tubercle → pubic symphysis → iliopectineal line → sacral promontory","Iliopectineal eminence → pubic crest → sacroiliac joint → pubic symphysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: From anterior to posterior the pelvic brim is formed by pubic symphysis, pubic crest, pubic tubercle, upper border of superior pubic ramii, iliopectineal eminence, iliopectineal line, sacroiliac joint, ala of sacral bone, and sacral promontory.  Why A wrong: It reverses the sequence and omits multiple structures.  Why C wrong: The order and structures are incorrect and incomplete.  Why D wrong: The sequence is scrambled and not anterior-to-posterior."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"In the pelvic brim, which structure lies immediately posterior to the pubic tubercle?","options":["Pubic crest","Upper border of the superior pubic ramii","Sacroiliac joint","Sacral promontory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Following the pubic tubercle, the brim is formed by the upper border of the superior pubic ramii, then the iliopectineal eminence.  Why A wrong: The pubic crest lies anterior to the pubic tubercle.  Why C wrong: The sacroiliac joint is much further posterior in the sequence.  Why D wrong: The sacral promontory is the most posterior landmark of the brim."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The most posterior structure forming the pelvic brim is the:","options":["Iliopectineal line","Ala of sacral bone","Sacral promontory","Sacroiliac joint"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The sacral promontory is the last (most posterior) landmark of the pelvic brim.  Why A wrong: The iliopectineal line lies more anteriorly in the sequence.  Why B wrong: The ala of the sacral bone precedes the sacral promontory.  Why D wrong: The sacroiliac joint precedes the ala of the sacrum."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The false pelvis, lying above the pelvic brim, has which primary function?","options":["Takes active part in labor","Only supports the gravid uterus","Forms the birth canal","Contains the ischial spines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The false pelvis lies above the pelvic brim and only supports the gravid uterus.  Why A wrong: It is the true pelvis (below the brim) that takes part in labor.  Why C wrong: The true pelvis forms the birth canal.  Why D wrong: The ischial spines are part of the true pelvis (midpelvis level)."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"Which part of the maternal pelvis takes part in labor?","options":["False pelvis","True pelvis","Greater pelvis","Pelvic brim only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The true pelvis lies below the pelvic brim and takes part in labor.  Why A wrong: The false pelvis only supports the gravid uterus.  Why C wrong: \"Greater pelvis\" is another name for the false pelvis, which does not take part in labor.  Why D wrong: The pelvic brim is only a dividing landmark, not the labor canal."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The true pelvis is divided into how many parts?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The true pelvis is divided into three parts: pelvic inlet, pelvic cavity/midpelvis, and pelvic outlet.  Why A wrong: There are three, not two, divisions.  Why C/D wrong: The number is three, not four or five."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The pelvic inlet lies at the level of the:","options":["Ischial spine","Ischial tuberosity","Pelvic brim","Sacral promontory only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The pelvic inlet lies at the level of the pelvic brim.  Why A wrong: The ischial spine level corresponds to the pelvic cavity/midpelvis.  Why B wrong: The ischial tuberosity level corresponds to the pelvic outlet.  Why D wrong: The sacral promontory is only one point of the brim, not the whole inlet level."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The pelvic cavity (midpelvis) lies at the level of the:","options":["Pelvic brim","Ischial spine","Ischial tuberosity","Pubic symphysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pelvic cavity or midpelvis lies at the level of the ischial spine.  Why A wrong: The pelvic brim corresponds to the inlet.  Why C wrong: The ischial tuberosity corresponds to the outlet.  Why D wrong: The pubic symphysis is an anterior brim landmark, not the cavity level."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The pelvic outlet lies at the level of the:","options":["Ischial spine","Pelvic brim","Ischial tuberosity","Sacral ala"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The pelvic outlet lies at the level of the ischial tuberosity.  Why A wrong: The ischial spine level corresponds to the midpelvis/cavity.  Why B wrong: The pelvic brim corresponds to the inlet.  Why D wrong: The sacral ala is a brim landmark, not the outlet level."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"How many antero-posterior (AP) diameters does the pelvic inlet have?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The AP diameter of the inlet is 3 in number — true conjugate, obstetrical conjugate, and diagonal conjugate.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies exactly 3 AP diameters of the inlet."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The TRUE conjugate is measured from the:","options":["Mid of the pubic symphysis to sacral promontory","Upper border of pubic symphysis to sacral promontory","Lower border of pubic symphysis to sacral promontory","Tip of coccyx to lower border of pubic symphysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The true conjugate runs from the upper border of the pubic symphysis to the sacral promontory and measures 11 cms.  Why A wrong: That describes the obstetrical conjugate.  Why C wrong: That describes the diagonal conjugate.  Why D wrong: That describes the AP diameter of the outlet."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"What is the measurement of the true conjugate?","options":["10 cms","10–10.5 cms","11 cms","12 cms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The true conjugate measures 11 cms.  Why A wrong: 10 cms is the critical obstetric conjugate value.  Why B wrong: 10–10.5 cms is the obstetrical conjugate.  Why D wrong: 12 cms is the diagonal conjugate."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The obstetrical conjugate is measured from the:","options":["Upper border of pubic symphysis to sacral promontory","Mid of the pubic symphysis to sacral promontory","Lower border of pubic symphysis to sacral promontory","Two farthest points on the iliopectineal line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The obstetrical conjugate runs from the mid of the pubic symphysis to the sacral promontory and measures 10–10.5 cms.  Why A wrong: That is the true conjugate.  Why C wrong: That is the diagonal conjugate.  Why D wrong: That defines the transverse diameter of the inlet."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"What is the measurement of the obstetrical conjugate?","options":["10–10.5 cms","11 cms","12 cms","13 cms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The obstetrical conjugate measures 10–10.5 cms.  Why B wrong: 11 cms is the true conjugate.  Why C wrong: 12 cms is the diagonal conjugate.  Why D wrong: 13 cms is the transverse diameter of the inlet."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The diagonal conjugate is measured from the:","options":["Upper border of pubic symphysis to sacral promontory","Mid of pubic symphysis to sacral promontory","Lower border of pubic symphysis to sacral promontory","Between two ischial tuberosities"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagonal conjugate runs from the lower border of the pubic symphysis to the sacral promontory and measures 12 cms.  Why A wrong: That is the true conjugate.  Why B wrong: That is the obstetrical conjugate.  Why D wrong: That is the bituberous (transverse outlet) diameter."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"What is the measurement of the diagonal conjugate?","options":["10 cms","10.5 cms","11 cms","12 cms"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The diagonal conjugate measures 12 cms.  Why A/B wrong: These correspond to the obstetric conjugate range.  Why C wrong: 11 cms is the true conjugate."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"During clinical assessment of the pelvic inlet, if the diagonal conjugate is normal (12 cm, ideal), then:","options":["The fingers cannot touch the sacral promontory","The fingers easily touch the sacral promontory","The pelvis is contracted","Vaginal delivery is impossible"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When the diagonal conjugate is normal or 12 cm (ideal), the examining fingers cannot reach/touch the sacral promontory.  Why B wrong: Touching the promontory indicates a shorter (possibly contracted) inlet.  Why C wrong: A normal 12 cm diagonal conjugate indicates an adequate, not contracted, pelvis.  Why D wrong: An ideal diagonal conjugate favors vaginal delivery."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"If, on clinical assessment, the sacral promontory CAN be touched, the obstetrical conjugate is estimated by subtracting how much from the diagonal conjugate?","options":["0.5 cm","1 cm","1.5 to 2 cm","3 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When the promontory can be touched, obstetrical conjugate = diagonal conjugate − 1.5 to 2 cm.  Why A wrong: 0.5 cm is the decrease that defines a contracted pelvis, not this subtraction.  Why B wrong: Diagonal conjugate − 1 cm gives the transverse conjugate, not the obstetrical conjugate.  Why D wrong: 3 cm is not the value used."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The transverse conjugate is obtained by subtracting how much from the diagonal conjugate?","options":["1 cm","1.5 cm","2 cm","2.5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Transverse conjugate = diagonal conjugate − 1 cm.  Why B/C/D wrong: 1.5–2 cm subtraction gives the obstetrical conjugate; the transverse conjugate uses only 1 cm."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"What is the value of the critical obstetric conjugate below which vaginal delivery is not possible?","options":["8 cm","9 cm","10 cm","12 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The critical obstetric conjugate is 10 cm; if the obstetric conjugate is less than 10 cm, vaginal delivery is not possible.  Why A/B wrong: These are below the stated critical value.  Why D wrong: 12 cm is the diagonal conjugate value, not the critical obstetric conjugate."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The transverse diameter of the pelvic inlet is defined as the distance between:","options":["The two ischial spines","The two farthest points on the iliopectineal line","The two ischial tuberosities","The sacroiliac joint and iliopectineal eminence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The transverse diameter of the inlet is the distance between the 2 farthest points on the iliopectineal line.  Why A wrong: The distance between the ischial spines is the interischial/bispinous diameter of the midpelvis.  Why C wrong: The distance between ischial tuberosities is the bituberous (outlet) diameter.  Why D wrong: That describes the oblique diameter of the inlet."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"What is the value of the transverse diameter of the pelvic inlet?","options":["11 cms","12 cms","13 cms","10 cms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The transverse diameter of the inlet measures 13 cms.  Why A wrong: 11 cms is the bituberous (outlet transverse) or true conjugate value.  Why B wrong: 12 cms is the oblique/diagonal conjugate value.  Why D wrong: 10 cms is the interischial diameter."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The oblique diameter of the pelvic inlet is measured from:","options":["One sacroiliac joint to the opposite iliopectineal eminence","Between two ischial spines","Upper border of pubic symphysis to sacral promontory","Between two ischial tuberosities"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The oblique diameter runs from one side's sacroiliac joint to the opposite side's iliopectineal eminence (e.g., left oblique = left sacroiliac joint to right iliopectineal eminence, and vice versa).  Why B wrong: That is the interischial/bispinous diameter.  Why C wrong: That is the true conjugate.  Why D wrong: That is the bituberous diameter."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"What is the value of the oblique diameter of the pelvic inlet?","options":["10 cms","11 cms","12 cms","13 cms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The oblique diameter of the inlet measures 12 cms.  Why A wrong: 10 cms is the interischial diameter.  Why B wrong: 11 cms is the bituberous/true conjugate value.  Why D wrong: 13 cms is the transverse diameter of the inlet."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The RIGHT oblique diameter of the inlet runs from the:","options":["Right sacroiliac joint to the left iliopectineal eminence","Left sacroiliac joint to the right iliopectineal eminence","Right ischial spine to the left ischial spine","Right ischial tuberosity to left ischial tuberosity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The right oblique diameter runs from the right sacroiliac joint to the left (opposite) iliopectineal eminence.  Why B wrong: That describes the left oblique diameter.  Why C wrong: That describes the interischial/bispinous diameter.  Why D wrong: That describes the bituberous diameter."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"Which is the SMALLEST antero-posterior diameter of the pelvis?","options":["True conjugate","Diagonal conjugate","Obstetric conjugate","Transverse conjugate"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The obstetric conjugate is the smallest AP diameter of the pelvis.  Why A wrong: The true conjugate (11 cm) is larger.  Why B wrong: The diagonal conjugate (12 cm) is the largest AP diameter of the inlet.  Why D wrong: The transverse conjugate is not the designated smallest AP diameter here."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"Which is the LARGEST antero-posterior diameter of the pelvic inlet?","options":["Obstetric conjugate","True conjugate","Diagonal conjugate","Interischial diameter"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagonal conjugate (12 cm) is the largest AP diameter of the inlet.  Why A wrong: The obstetric conjugate is the smallest AP diameter.  Why B wrong: The true conjugate (11 cm) is smaller than the diagonal conjugate.  Why D wrong: The interischial diameter is a transverse midpelvic diameter, not an AP diameter."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"Out of the 3 AP diameters of the pelvic inlet, which one can be measured clinically?","options":["True conjugate","Obstetric conjugate","Diagonal conjugate","All three"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Only the diagonal conjugate can be measured clinically (per vaginal examination); the others are derived from it.  Why A/B wrong: The true and obstetric conjugates cannot be directly measured clinically.  Why D wrong: Only one (diagonal conjugate) is clinically measurable."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"If the diagonal conjugate is 'x' cm in a female, the obstetric conjugate will be:","options":["(x + 2) cm","(x − 2) cm","(x − 1) cm","(x − 0.5) cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Obstetric conjugate = diagonal conjugate − 2 cm, i.e., (x − 2) cm.  Why A wrong: The obstetric conjugate is smaller, not larger, than the diagonal conjugate.  Why C wrong: Subtracting 1 cm gives the transverse conjugate.  Why D wrong: 0.5 cm is the contracted-pelvis decrease value, not this relationship."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"Which is the SHORTEST major diameter of the pelvis, making it the most important diameter during delivery?","options":["Interspinous (interischial) diameter","Diagonal conjugate","Bituberous diameter","Transverse diameter of inlet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The interspinous (interischial/bispinous) diameter is the shortest major diameter of the pelvis, which is why it is the most important during delivery.  Why B wrong: The diagonal conjugate (12 cm) is larger.  Why C wrong: The bituberous diameter (11 cm) is an outlet diameter, not the shortest major diameter.  Why D wrong: The transverse diameter of the inlet (13 cm) is the largest, not shortest."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The transverse diameter of the midpelvis (distance between the two ischial spines) is also called the:","options":["Diagonal conjugate","Interischial or bispinous diameter","Bituberous diameter","Oblique diameter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The transverse diameter of the midpelvis is the distance between the two ischial spines, also called the \"interischial\" or \"bispinous\" diameter.  Why A wrong: The diagonal conjugate is an AP inlet diameter.  Why C wrong: The bituberous diameter is between the two ischial tuberosities (outlet).  Why D wrong: The oblique diameter is an inlet diameter between sacroiliac joint and iliopectineal eminence."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"What is the value of the interischial (bispinous) diameter of the midpelvis?","options":["8 cms","10 cms","11 cms","13 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The interischial/bispinous diameter measures 10 cms.  Why A wrong: <8 cm is the contracted-pelvis threshold for interischial diameter.  Why C wrong: 11 cms is the bituberous (outlet) diameter.  Why D wrong: 13 cms is the transverse diameter of the inlet."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The interischial (bispinous) diameter is described as the:","options":["Largest diameter of the pelvis","Smallest and most important diameter of the entire pelvis","Only oblique diameter","AP diameter of the outlet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The interischial diameter (10 cm) is the smallest and most important diameter of the entire pelvis.  Why A wrong: It is the smallest, not the largest.  Why C wrong: It is a transverse (not oblique) diameter.  Why D wrong: It is a transverse midpelvic diameter, not an AP outlet diameter."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"Because the pelvic cavity lies at the level of the ischial spine, the plane of the cavity is also known as the:","options":["Plane of greatest pelvic dimension","Plane of least pelvic dimension","Plane of inlet","Angle of inclination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Since the cavity lies at the ischial spine level, its plane is the smallest and is called the plane of least pelvic dimension.  Why A wrong: It is the plane of least (smallest), not greatest, dimension.  Why C wrong: The plane of inlet is at the pelvic brim, a different level.  Why D wrong: The angle of inclination refers to the inlet's 55° tilt, not this plane."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"In the male pelvis (android pelvis), the ischial spines are:","options":["Blunt and rounded","Prominent and pointed","Absent","Widely separated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the male (android) pelvis, the ischial spines are prominent and pointed.  Why A wrong: Blunt/rounded spines are characteristic of the gynaecoid (female) pelvis, not the android pelvis.  Why C wrong: The spines are present, indeed prominent.  Why D wrong: The note describes their shape (prominent/pointed), not the distance between them."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The transverse diameter of the pelvic outlet is the outer distance between the two ischial tuberosities and is called the:","options":["Bispinous diameter","Bituberous diameter","Oblique diameter","Diagonal conjugate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The transverse diameter of the outlet is the outer distance between the two ischial tuberosities, called the bituberous diameter.  Why A wrong: The bispinous diameter is between the ischial spines (midpelvis).  Why C wrong: The oblique diameter is an inlet diameter.  Why D wrong: The diagonal conjugate is an AP inlet diameter."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"What is the value of the bituberous (transverse outlet) diameter?","options":["10 cms","11 cms","12 cms","13 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The bituberous (transverse outlet) diameter measures 11 cms.  Why A wrong: 10 cms is the interischial diameter.  Why C wrong: 12 cms is the oblique/diagonal conjugate.  Why D wrong: 13 cms is the transverse diameter of the inlet."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The AP diameter of the pelvic outlet is the distance between the:","options":["Tip of the coccyx and the lower border of the pubic symphysis","Upper border of pubic symphysis and sacral promontory","Two ischial tuberosities","Two farthest points on the iliopectineal line"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The AP diameter of the outlet is the distance between the tip of the coccyx and the lower border of the pubic symphysis.  Why B wrong: That is the true conjugate (an inlet AP diameter).  Why C wrong: That is the bituberous (transverse outlet) diameter.  Why D wrong: That is the transverse diameter of the inlet."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"What is the value of the AP diameter of the pelvic outlet?","options":["10–10.5 cms","11 cms","11.5 to 13 cms (13 cm at the time of labor)","8 cms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The AP diameter of the outlet is 11.5 to 13 cms, becoming 13 cm at the time of labor (due to coccygeal mobility).  Why A wrong: 10–10.5 cms is the obstetric conjugate.  Why B wrong: 11 cms is the bituberous diameter/true conjugate.  Why D wrong: 8 cms relates to contracted-pelvis thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"The inlet of the pelvis makes an angle of 55° with the horizontal. This angle is called the:","options":["Angle of progression","Angle of inclination","Angle of insertion","Subpubic angle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The inlet makes a 55° angle with the horizontal, which is the angle of inclination.  Why A wrong: \"Angle of progression\" is an ultrasound labor-progress term not used here.  Why C wrong: \"Angle of insertion\" is not the term for the inlet's tilt.  Why D wrong: The subpubic angle is the angle at the pubic arch, not the inlet's inclination."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"According to the summary table, what is the AP diameter (true conjugate) value at the pelvic inlet?","options":["11 cm","12 cm","13 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the inlet AP true conjugate as 11 cm (obstetrical conjugate 10–10.5 cm, diagonal conjugate 12 cm).  Why B wrong: 12 cm is the diagonal conjugate and the cavity AP diameter.  Why C wrong: 13 cm is the transverse diameter of the inlet.  Why D wrong: 10 cm is the critical/interischial value, not the true conjugate."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"According to the summary table, the AP diameter of the pelvic cavity/midpelvis is:","options":["10 cms","11 cms","12 cms","13 cms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives the cavity/midpelvis AP diameter as 12 cms.  Why A wrong: 10 cms is the cavity transverse (interischial) diameter.  Why B wrong: 11 cms is the inlet true conjugate/outlet transverse.  Why D wrong: 13 cms is the inlet transverse diameter."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"In the summary table, the transverse diameter of the pelvic cavity (10 cms, interischial diameter) is noted as the:","options":["Largest diameter","Most important diameter","Oblique diameter","Angle of inclination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table labels the 10 cm interischial diameter of the cavity as the \"most important diameter.\"  Why A wrong: It is the smallest, not the largest.  Why C wrong: It is a transverse, not oblique, diameter.  Why D wrong: The angle of inclination is unrelated to this diameter's label."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"A pelvis is called a contracted pelvis when any of its major diameters is decreased by at least:","options":["0.25 cm","0.5 cm","1 cm","2 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If any major diameter of the pelvis is decreased by 0.5 cms, the pelvis is called a contracted pelvis.  Why A wrong: The threshold stated is 0.5 cm, not 0.25 cm.  Why C/D wrong: A decrease of only 0.5 cm is sufficient to define a contracted pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"In a contracted pelvis, the obstetric conjugate value is:","options":["< 10 cms","< 12 cms","> 11 cms","= 13 cms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a contracted pelvis, the obstetric conjugate is less than 10 cms.  Why B wrong: <12 cm is not the stated criterion for the obstetric conjugate in the contracted-pelvis list.  Why C/D wrong: These are larger values that indicate an adequate pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"In a contracted pelvis, the interischial diameter is:","options":["< 6 cms","< 8 cms","< 10 cms","< 12 cms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a contracted pelvis, the interischial diameter is less than 8 cms.  Why A wrong: The stated threshold is <8 cm, not <6 cm.  Why C wrong: 10 cm is the normal interischial value.  Why D wrong: <12 cm is not the interischial contracted criterion."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"In a contracted pelvis, the intertuberous diameter is:","options":["< 8 cms","< 10 cms","< 11 cms","< 13 cms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a contracted pelvis, the intertuberous diameter is less than 8 cms.  Why B wrong: 10 cm relates to the interischial normal value.  Why C wrong: 11 cm is the normal bituberous/intertuberous value.  Why D wrong: 13 cm relates to the inlet transverse diameter."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"During clinical assessment for a contracted pelvis, the obstetric conjugate (OC) is derived from the diagonal conjugate (DC) by which relationship?","options":["OC = DC − 2 cms","OC = DC − 1 cm","OC = DC + 2 cms","OC = DC × 2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states DC − 2 cms = OC (and DC − 1 cm = TC).  Why B wrong: DC − 1 cm gives the transverse conjugate (TC), not the OC.  Why C wrong: The OC is smaller than the DC, so 2 cm is subtracted, not added.  Why D wrong: There is no multiplication relationship."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"On clinical inlet assessment, if the diagonal conjugate is normal or 12 cm (ideal), the examining fingers:","options":["Can easily touch the sacral promontory","Cannot touch the sacral promontory","Reach the ischial tuberosity","Palpate the pubic crest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With a normal/ideal 12 cm diagonal conjugate, the fingers cannot touch the sacral promontory.  Why A wrong: Touching the promontory suggests a shortened conjugate (possible contraction).  Why C wrong: The ischial tuberosity is an outlet landmark, not assessed this way for the inlet.  Why D wrong: The pubic crest is not the target of this assessment."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"Per the clinical-assessment box, if the derived conjugate value is less than 10 cm, the pelvis is said to be:","options":["Normal (gynaecoid)","Contracted pelvis","Android pelvis","Justo-minor pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The box notes that if the conjugate is <10 cm, the pelvis is said to be a contracted pelvis (consistent with obstetric conjugate <10 cm criterion).  Why A wrong: A value <10 cm indicates contraction, not a normal pelvis.  Why C wrong: Android refers to a pelvic shape/type, not this conjugate cut-off.  Why D wrong: \"Justo-minor\" is not the term used in the source."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"At what gestational age is pelvic assessment done in a primigravida?","options":["32 weeks","34 weeks","37 weeks","At the onset of labor"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In a primigravida, pelvic assessment is done at 37 weeks.  Why A/B wrong: These earlier weeks are not the stated timing.  Why D wrong: \"At the onset of labor\" is the timing for a multigravida, not a primigravida."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"When is pelvic assessment performed in a multigravida?","options":["At 37 weeks","At the onset of labor","At 34 weeks","At 20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a multigravida, pelvic assessment is done at the onset of labor.  Why A wrong: 37 weeks is the timing for a primigravida.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated timings for a multigravida."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following correctly match a pelvic diameter with its value?","options":["True conjugate = 11 cm","Diagonal conjugate = 12 cm","Transverse diameter of inlet = 13 cm","Bituberous diameter = 15 cm","Interischial (bispinous) diameter = 10 cm"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: True conjugate 11 cm, diagonal conjugate 12 cm, transverse diameter of inlet 13 cm, and interischial/bispinous diameter 10 cm are all correct source values.  Why D wrong: The bituberous diameter is 11 cm, not 15 cm."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Maternal pelvis, pelvic brim anatomy, divisions of true pelvis…","question":"A primigravida at 37 weeks is found on vaginal examination to have a diagonal conjugate of 11 cm with the sacral promontory reachable. What is her estimated obstetric conjugate, and is vaginal delivery favorable?","options":["9 cm — unfavorable (below critical 10 cm)","13 cm — favorable","11 cm — favorable","8 cm — favorable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Obstetric conjugate = diagonal conjugate − 2 cm = 11 − 2 = 9 cm; since this is below the critical obstetric conjugate of 10 cm, vaginal delivery is not favorable/possible.  Why B wrong: Subtracting (not adding) applies, and 13 cm is not derivable here.  Why C wrong: 11 cm ignores the required 2 cm subtraction.  Why D wrong: 9 cm is the correct value and it is unfavorable, not favorable."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"During clinical assessment of the pelvic cavity, the examiner tries to touch both ischial spines simultaneously with two fingers of the same hand. What does the inability to touch both ischial spines at the same time indicate?","options":["The cavity is narrow (contracted pelvis)","The cavity is normal","The outlet is contracted","The inlet is contracted"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If you cannot touch both ischial spines at the same time, the cavity is normal (adequate space between the spines).  Why A wrong: Being able to touch both spines simultaneously indicates a narrow/contracted cavity, the opposite finding.  Why C wrong: This test assesses the cavity, not the outlet.  Why D wrong: This test assesses the cavity, not the inlet."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"On clinical assessment of the pelvic cavity, the examiner CAN touch both ischial spines simultaneously with two fingers of the same hand. This finding indicates:","options":["Normal cavity","Narrow cavity (contracted pelvis)","Adequate outlet","Wide subpubic angle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If both ischial spines can be touched at the same time, the cavity is narrow, i.e., contracted pelvis.  Why A wrong: A normal cavity is one in which both spines CANNOT be touched simultaneously.  Why C wrong: This assesses the cavity, not the outlet.  Why D wrong: Subpubic angle is not assessed by this manoeuvre."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The clinical assessment of the pelvic cavity is performed by trying to touch both ischial spines simultaneously using:","options":["Two fingers of the same hand","One finger of each hand","The whole palm","A pelvimeter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The technique described uses two fingers of the same hand to try to touch both ischial spines simultaneously.  Why B wrong: The source specifies two fingers of the same hand, not one from each hand.  Why C wrong: The whole palm is not used.  Why D wrong: This is a clinical (digital) assessment, not an instrumental pelvimetry."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Clinical measurement of the bituberous diameter assesses the distance between which two structures?","options":["The two ischial tuberosities","The two ischial spines","The two iliac crests","The sacral promontory and symphysis pubis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bituberous diameter is the diameter measured between the two ischial tuberosities and is assessed during clinical assessment of the pelvic outlet.  Why B wrong: The distance between ischial spines is the interspinous diameter (cavity assessment), not bituberous.  Why C wrong: Iliac crests are not part of the outlet measurement.  Why D wrong: The sacral promontory–symphysis distance relates to the inlet (conjugates), not the bituberous diameter."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"During clinical measurement of the bituberous diameter, how many knuckles should ideally pass between the two ischial tuberosities?","options":["4 knuckles","2 knuckles","3 knuckles","5 knuckles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ideally, 4 knuckles should pass between the two ischial tuberosities, indicating an adequate outlet.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies 4 knuckles as the ideal standard, not 2, 3, or 5."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The bony landmarks used to assess the pelvic outlet during clinical measurement of the bituberous diameter include the ischial tuberosity and which anterior structure shown in the figure?","options":["Symphysis pubis","Sacral promontory","Coccyx","Iliac crest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the ischial tuberosity and the symphysis pubis as the landmarks of the pelvic outlet.  Why B wrong: The sacral promontory is a landmark of the pelvic inlet, not the outlet figure shown.  Why C wrong: The coccyx is not the labelled anterior landmark in the figure.  Why D wrong: The iliac crest is not part of the outlet figure."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The bituberous diameter (transverse diameter of the outlet) shown being measured with a ruler in the figure is approximately:","options":["10 cm","5 cm","15 cm","8 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ruler in the figure reads 10 cm, representing the bituberous (transverse outlet) diameter.  Why B, C, D wrong: The figure explicitly shows 10 cm, not 5, 15, or 8 cm."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"If 4 knuckles do NOT pass between the two ischial tuberosities during outlet assessment, the condition is called:","options":["Contracted outlet (contracted pelvis)","Normal outlet","Android pelvis","Cephalopelvic disproportion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If 4 knuckles do not pass between the two ischial tuberosities, it is called a contracted outlet, which is a form of contracted pelvis.  Why B wrong: A normal outlet allows 4 knuckles to pass.  Why C wrong: Android is a pelvis shape variety, not the term for a contracted outlet.  Why D wrong: CPD relates to a big fetus against a normal pelvis; this finding specifically defines contracted outlet."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"What is the definitive management for a patient diagnosed with contracted pelvis, and at what gestational age is it performed?","options":["Elective cesarean section at 39 weeks of pregnancy","Emergency cesarean section at 37 weeks","Induction of labour at 40 weeks","Vacuum-assisted vaginal delivery at 38 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Whenever contracted pelvis is present, the management is elective cesarean section performed at 39 weeks of pregnancy.  Why B wrong: It is elective (planned), performed at 39 weeks, not emergency at 37 weeks.  Why C wrong: Induction/vaginal delivery risks obstructed labour in a contracted pelvis.  Why D wrong: Instrumental vaginal delivery is inappropriate for a contracted pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"If vaginal delivery is attempted in a case of contracted pelvis, what is the likely outcome?","options":["Obstructed labour","Precipitate labour","Preterm labour","Spontaneous normal delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a case of contracted pelvis, if vaginal delivery is tried, it results in obstructed labour.  Why B wrong: Precipitate labour is rapid labour, not the result of contracted pelvis.  Why C wrong: Preterm labour is not the specific consequence described.  Why D wrong: A contracted pelvis prevents normal delivery; it leads to obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Obstructed labour is best defined as a condition in which:","options":["The uterus contracts and tries to push the baby out, but delivery does not happen due to obstruction in the passage","The uterus fails to contract at any point","The baby is delivered too rapidly through a wide pelvis","Labour occurs before 37 weeks of gestation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Obstructed labour is defined as a condition in which the uterus contracts and tries to push the baby out but, due to obstruction in the passage, delivery does not happen.  Why B wrong: In obstructed labour the uterus IS contracting; the problem is the obstruction.  Why C wrong: Rapid delivery through a wide pelvis is precipitate labour, not obstruction.  Why D wrong: That describes preterm labour."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which of the following is a listed cause of obstructed labour?","options":["Contracted pelvis","Placenta previa","Gestational diabetes","Anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Contracted pelvis is the first listed cause of obstructed labour.  Why B wrong: Placenta previa causes antepartum haemorrhage, not listed as a cause of obstructed labour here.  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes is not listed as a cause.  Why D wrong: Anemia is not a listed cause of obstructed labour."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"All of the following are listed causes of obstructed labour EXCEPT:","options":["Oligohydramnios","Tumor in pelvis","Hydrocephalus","Macrosomia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oligohydramnios is NOT listed among the causes of obstructed labour.  Why B wrong: Tumor in pelvis is a listed cause.  Why C wrong: Hydrocephalus is a listed cause.  Why D wrong: Macrosomia (≥ 4 kg) is a listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"In the causes of obstructed labour, macrosomia is defined by a fetal weight of:","options":["≥ 4 kg","≥ 2.5 kg","≥ 3 kg","≥ 5 kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Macrosomia is defined in the notes as a fetal weight ≥ 4 kg.  Why B wrong: 2.5 kg is the lower limit of normal birth weight, not macrosomia.  Why C wrong: 3 kg is an average birth weight, not macrosomia.  Why D wrong: The source specifies ≥ 4 kg, not ≥ 5 kg."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which fetal condition is listed as a cause of obstructed labour due to enlargement of the fetal head?","options":["Hydrocephalus","Anencephaly","Spina bifida","Microcephaly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrocephalus (excess CSF enlarging the head) is a listed cause of obstructed labour.  Why B wrong: Anencephaly is not listed as a cause here.  Why C wrong: Spina bifida is not listed.  Why D wrong: Microcephaly (small head) would not obstruct labour and is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"On general examination of a mother in obstructed labour, which set of findings is expected?","options":["Exhausted, dehydrated, and tachypnea","Bradycardia, hypothermia, and drowsiness","Hypertension, edema, and proteinuria","Polyuria, weight gain, and bradypnea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On examination the mother is exhausted, dehydrated, and shows tachypnea.  Why B wrong: These are not the described maternal findings in obstructed labour.  Why C wrong: Hypertension/edema/proteinuria describe pre-eclampsia, not obstructed labour.  Why D wrong: Polyuria and bradypnea are not features; the mother is dehydrated with tachypnea."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"A mother in obstructed labour is found to be dehydrated. Which of the following is the direct examination finding associated with this dehydrated, exhausted state?","options":["Tachypnea","Bradycardia","Hypothermia","Hypotension only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The three maternal findings listed are exhausted, dehydrated, and tachypnea; tachypnea accompanies the exhausted/dehydrated state.  Why B wrong: Bradycardia is not listed.  Why C wrong: Hypothermia is not listed.  Why D wrong: Hypotension is not the listed finding; tachypnea is."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"On per abdominal examination in obstructed labour, a groove or depression is seen or felt between the upper and lower parts of the uterus. This is called:","options":["Bandl's ring","Braxton Hicks contraction","Constriction ring","Ferguson reflex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The groove/depression between the upper and lower uterine segments is called the \"BANDL ring\".  Why B wrong: Braxton Hicks are painless false-labour contractions, not a groove sign.  Why C wrong: A constriction ring is a different pathological ring; the source names Bandl's ring.  Why D wrong: Ferguson reflex is a neuroendocrine reflex, not an abdominal sign."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Bandl's ring is described as which of the following in obstructed labour?","options":["The most important sign of obstructed labour","A normal finding in all labours","A sign of placental separation","A sign of full cervical dilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Bandl's ring is the most important sign of obstructed labour.  Why B wrong: It is a pathological sign, not a normal finding.  Why C wrong: It is not a sign of placental separation.  Why D wrong: It is not a marker of cervical dilation."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"In obstructed labour, the fetal heart sound findings may progress in which sequence?","options":["Fetal distress → FHS absent (fetal death)","Normal FHS → tachycardia only","Bradycardia → resolution to normal","FHS absent → return of FHS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes indicate fetal heart sound shows fetal distress, progressing to FHS absent (fetal death).  Why B wrong: The progression leads to distress and death, not just tachycardia.  Why C wrong: FHS does not resolve to normal in obstructed labour; it worsens.  Why D wrong: Once FHS is absent (fetal death), it does not return."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Absence of fetal heart sound (FHS) in obstructed labour indicates:","options":["Fetal death","Fetal distress","Cord compression that is reversible","Normal second stage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FHS absent signifies fetal death in obstructed labour.  Why B wrong: Fetal distress is when FHS is present but abnormal; absence indicates death.  Why C wrong: Absent FHS represents death, not reversible compression.  Why D wrong: Absent FHS is never a normal finding."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"On per vaginal examination in obstructed labour, the vagina is found to be dry and hot. What is the cause of this finding?","options":["Amniotic fluid has drained","Excessive vaginal secretions","Retained placenta","Full bladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vagina is dry and hot because the amniotic fluid is drained.  Why B wrong: The vagina is dry, not with excessive secretions.  Why C wrong: Retained placenta does not explain a dry, hot vagina.  Why D wrong: A full bladder is unrelated to the dry, hot vagina finding."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which of the following is a per vaginal examination finding in obstructed labour?","options":["Bleeding from vagina","Copious clear liquor","Cool, moist vaginal walls","Intact bulging membranes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bleeding from the vagina is listed as a per vaginal finding in obstructed labour.  Why B wrong: Liquor is drained; the vagina is dry, not with copious liquor.  Why C wrong: The vagina is dry and hot, not cool and moist.  Why D wrong: Membranes are ruptured (fluid drained), not intact and bulging."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The swelling that develops on the fetal head, detected on per vaginal examination in obstructed labour, is termed:","options":["Caput succedaneum","Cephalhematoma","Subgaleal hemorrhage","Chignon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Swelling on the fetal head is caput succedaneum.  Why B wrong: Cephalhematoma is a subperiosteal collection limited by suture lines, not the swelling described here.  Why C wrong: Subgaleal hemorrhage is a different bleed, not the term the notes use.  Why D wrong: Chignon is caused by vacuum extraction, not the described obstructed-labour swelling."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"In obstructed labour there is alteration in the shape of the baby's head where the skull bones overlap each other. This process is called:","options":["Moulding","Engagement","Flexion","Restitution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Overlapping of the skull bones altering the head shape is called moulding.  Why B wrong: Engagement is descent of the presenting part into the pelvis, not bone overlap.  Why C wrong: Flexion is a movement of the fetal head, not skull-bone overlap.  Why D wrong: Restitution is realignment of the head after delivery, not bone overlap."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which per vaginal finding in obstructed labour reflects trauma to the maternal urinary tract from prolonged obstruction?","options":["Hematuria","Proteinuria","Glycosuria","Ketonuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hematuria is listed as a per vaginal examination finding in obstructed labour, reflecting bladder/urinary trauma.  Why B wrong: Proteinuria relates to pre-eclampsia, not listed here.  Why C wrong: Glycosuria relates to diabetes, not listed.  Why D wrong: Ketonuria relates to starvation/DKA, not the listed finding."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"In the management of obstructed labour, which drug must NEVER be given, and why?","options":["Oxytocin — because increased uterine contraction can cause uterine rupture","Magnesium sulfate — because it stops contractions","Nifedipine — because it lowers blood pressure","Misoprostol — because it ripens the cervix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Never give oxytocin in obstructed labour, because it increases uterine contraction, which can lead to uterine rupture.  Why B wrong: Magnesium sulfate is not the contraindicated drug named in this context.  Why C wrong: Nifedipine is not the named contraindicated drug.  Why D wrong: Misoprostol is not the drug specified as prohibited here."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Giving oxytocin in obstructed labour is dangerous because increased uterine contraction can lead to:","options":["Uterine rupture","Placenta previa","Cord prolapse","Amniotic fluid embolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin → increased uterine contraction → uterine rupture in an obstructed uterus.  Why B wrong: Placenta previa is a placental implantation abnormality, not caused by oxytocin here.  Why C wrong: Cord prolapse is not the consequence described.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid embolism is not the mechanism specified."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"What is the definitive management of obstructed labour, regardless of whether the baby is alive or dead?","options":["Immediate cesarean section","Trial of forceps delivery","Oxytocin augmentation","Expectant management"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state always perform immediate cesarean section whether the baby is alive or dead.  Why B wrong: Forceps delivery is inappropriate/dangerous in obstructed labour.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is contraindicated as it risks uterine rupture.  Why D wrong: Expectant management risks maternal death; immediate cesarean is required."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which of the following is a maternal complication of obstructed labour?","options":["Uterine rupture","Fetal death","Neonatal jaundice","Caput succedaneum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Uterine rupture is listed as a maternal complication of obstructed labour.  Why B wrong: Fetal death is a fetal complication, not maternal.  Why C wrong: Neonatal jaundice is not listed as a complication here.  Why D wrong: Caput succedaneum is an examination finding on the fetus, not a listed maternal complication."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Prolonged obstructed labour can lead to a vesicovaginal fistula. The classic patient complaint associated with a vesicovaginal fistula is:","options":["Continuous dribbling of urine from the vagina","Absent urine output","Painful urination only","Bloody vaginal discharge without incontinence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A vesicovaginal fistula leads to continuous dribbling of urine from the vagina.  Why B wrong: Urine is continuously leaking, not absent.  Why C wrong: The hallmark is continuous leakage, not merely painful urination.  Why D wrong: The classic complaint is urine dribbling, not bloody discharge."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"In obstructed labour, the abnormal communication that forms between the bladder and the vagina is called:","options":["Vesicovaginal fistula","Rectovaginal fistula","Ureterovaginal fistula","Enterovaginal fistula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Uterine rupture/prolonged obstruction leads to a vesicovaginal fistula (bladder-to-vagina communication).  Why B wrong: Rectovaginal fistula connects rectum to vagina, not the one described.  Why C wrong: The notes specify vesicovaginal (bladder), not ureterovaginal.  Why D wrong: Enterovaginal connects intestine to vagina, not described."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The fetal complication of obstructed labour is:","options":["Fetal death","Neonatal hypoglycemia","Erb's palsy","Transient tachypnea of newborn"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list fetal death as the fetal complication of obstructed labour.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the fetal complication specified in the source."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"In the type of contracted pelvis known as Naegele's pelvis, which structure is abnormal?","options":["One ala of the sacral bone is absent","Both alae of the sacral bone are absent","The pubic symphysis is fused","The coccyx is duplicated"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Naegele's pelvis is defined by absence of one ala (wing) of the sacral bone.  Why B wrong: Absence of both alae describes Robert's pelvis, not Naegele's.  Why C wrong: The defect is in the sacral ala, not the pubic symphysis.  Why D wrong: The coccyx is not involved."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Robert's pelvis, a type of contracted pelvis, is characterized by:","options":["Both alae of the sacral bone are absent","One ala of the sacral bone is absent","Absence of the sacral promontory","A fused symphysis pubis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Robert's pelvis is defined by absence of both alae of the sacral bone.  Why B wrong: Absence of one ala is Naegele's pelvis.  Why C wrong: The defect involves the alae, not the promontory.  Why D wrong: The symphysis pubis is not the involved structure."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"A patient has an established diagnosis of contracted pelvis. According to the notes, what is the recommended mode of delivery each time she conceives?","options":["Caesarean section always has to be done (recurrent cesarean section)","Trial of vaginal delivery each time","Vaginal delivery if the baby is small","Induction of labour with prostaglandins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a case of contracted pelvis, whenever the female conceives, cesarean always has to be done — a case of recurrent cesarean section.  Why B wrong: Vaginal delivery risks obstructed labour in a fixed anatomical contracture.  Why C wrong: With contracted pelvis the bony passage is always inadequate regardless of fetal size; cesarean is always needed.  Why D wrong: Induction is dangerous with contracted pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"According to the notes, the most common indication for recurrent (repeat) cesarean section is:","options":["Contracted pelvis","Cephalopelvic disproportion","Previous scar dehiscence","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common indication for recurrent cesarean section is contracted pelvis (a permanent bony problem requiring cesarean each time).  Why B wrong: In CPD, vaginal delivery may be possible in a later pregnancy if the fetus is normal size, so it is not the most common recurrent indication.  Why C wrong: Scar dehiscence is not the stated answer.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is not the stated most common indication for recurrent cesarean."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"In cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD), the underlying problem is that:","options":["The pelvis is normal but the fetus is big","The pelvis is contracted and the fetus is small","Both the pelvis and fetus are abnormal","The uterus fails to contract"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In cephalopelvic disproportion, the pelvis is normal but the fetus is big.  Why B wrong: The pelvis is normal in CPD, not contracted; the issue is a large fetus.  Why C wrong: Only the fetal size (big) is the problem; the pelvis is normal.  Why D wrong: Uterine contraction failure is not CPD."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"What is the management of cephalopelvic disproportion in the current pregnancy where the fetus is big?","options":["Cesarean section in this pregnancy","Elective vaginal delivery","Oxytocin augmentation","Forceps delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The management of CPD in the current pregnancy is cesarean section.  Why B wrong: With a big fetus and CPD, vaginal delivery is not appropriate in this pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is not the management for disproportion.  Why D wrong: Forceps is not the management for a big fetus causing disproportion."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"A woman had a cesarean for cephalopelvic disproportion. In her next pregnancy the fetus is a normal size. What is the likely plan?","options":["Vaginal delivery can happen (trial of vaginal delivery)","Mandatory repeat cesarean because of contracted pelvis","Mandatory repeat cesarean regardless of fetal size","Termination of pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In CPD, if next time the fetus is a normal size, then vaginal delivery can happen — it is not necessary to do cesarean every time.  Why B wrong: CPD is not a contracted pelvis; the pelvis is normal, so repeat cesarean is not mandatory.  Why C wrong: The whole point is that with a normal-size fetus, cesarean is not mandatory.  Why D wrong: Termination is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which statement correctly contrasts cephalopelvic disproportion with contracted pelvis regarding repeat delivery?","options":["In CPD it is not necessary to do a cesarean every time; vaginal delivery may be tried, whereas contracted pelvis needs cesarean each time","Both require cesarean every time","Both can be delivered vaginally every time","CPD needs cesarean every time; contracted pelvis can be delivered vaginally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In CPD (normal pelvis, big fetus) vaginal delivery can be tried if the next fetus is a normal size; contracted pelvis is a fixed bony problem requiring cesarean each time.  Why B wrong: CPD does not require cesarean every time.  Why C wrong: Contracted pelvis cannot be delivered vaginally.  Why D wrong: This reverses the correct relationship."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The classification of the pelvis into 4 types on the basis of the shape of the inlet is known as:","options":["Caldwell & Moloy classification","Bishop classification","Naegele classification","Robert classification"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The four pelvic types based on inlet shape are the Caldwell & Moloy classification.  Why B wrong: Bishop classification/score assesses cervical favourability for induction, not pelvic shape.  Why C wrong: Naegele refers to a type of contracted pelvis, not the 4-type classification.  Why D wrong: Robert refers to a contracted-pelvis type, not the classification system."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"According to the Caldwell & Moloy classification, which pelvis type is the most common (female pelvis) and its percentage?","options":["Gynecoid — 50%","Android — 20%","Anthropoid — 25%","Platypelloid — 5%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gynecoid pelvis is the female pelvis, most common, occurring in 50%.  Why B wrong: Android is 20% (male pelvis), not the most common female type.  Why C wrong: Anthropoid is 25%, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid is 5%, the least common."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"What percentage of pelvises are of the anthropoid type according to the Caldwell & Moloy classification?","options":["25%","50%","20%","5%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anthropoid pelvis occurs in 25%.  Why B wrong: 50% is gynecoid.  Why C wrong: 20% is android.  Why D wrong: 5% is platypelloid."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The android pelvis, described as the male pelvis, occurs in what percentage of the population per the Caldwell & Moloy classification?","options":["20%","50%","25%","5%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Android (male) pelvis occurs in 20%.  Why B wrong: 50% is gynecoid.  Why C wrong: 25% is anthropoid.  Why D wrong: 5% is platypelloid."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which pelvic type is described as the least common, occurring in only 5%?","options":["Platypelloid","Gynecoid","Anthropoid","Android"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Platypelloid is the least common variety at 5%.  Why B wrong: Gynecoid is the most common (50%).  Why C wrong: Anthropoid is 25%.  Why D wrong: Android is 20%."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"What is the shape of the inlet in a gynecoid pelvis?","options":["Transverse oval","Heart shaped","Long oval","Flat bowl like"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The gynecoid pelvis has a transverse oval inlet shape.  Why B wrong: Heart shaped is the android inlet.  Why C wrong: Long oval is the anthropoid inlet.  Why D wrong: Flat bowl like is the platypelloid inlet."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which pelvic type has a heart-shaped inlet?","options":["Android pelvis","Gynecoid pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The android pelvis has a heart-shaped inlet.  Why B wrong: Gynecoid inlet is transverse oval.  Why C wrong: Anthropoid inlet is long oval.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid inlet is flat bowl like."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The inlet shape of the anthropoid pelvis is best described as:","options":["Long oval","Transverse oval","Heart shaped","Flat bowl like"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anthropoid pelvis has a long oval inlet.  Why B wrong: Transverse oval is gynecoid.  Why C wrong: Heart shaped is android.  Why D wrong: Flat bowl like is platypelloid."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The platypelloid pelvis has an inlet that is described as:","options":["Flat bowl like","Transverse oval","Long oval","Heart shaped"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The platypelloid pelvis has a flat bowl-like inlet.  Why B wrong: Transverse oval is gynecoid.  Why C wrong: Long oval is anthropoid.  Why D wrong: Heart shaped is android."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"In the gynecoid pelvis, the relationship between the transverse and AP diameters of the inlet is:","options":["Transverse diameter > AP diameter","AP diameter > transverse diameter","Transverse diameter >>> AP diameter","AP diameter = transverse diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In gynecoid, the transverse diameter is greater than the AP diameter.  Why B wrong: AP > transverse describes anthropoid.  Why C wrong: Transverse >>> AP (markedly greater) describes platypelloid.  Why D wrong: The diameters are not equal in gynecoid."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which pelvic type is the ONLY one in which the AP diameter of the inlet is greater than the transverse diameter?","options":["Anthropoid pelvis","Gynecoid pelvis","Android pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the anthropoid pelvis, AP diameter > transverse diameter (noted as \"only pelvis\" with this feature).  Why B wrong: Gynecoid has transverse > AP.  Why C wrong: Android has transverse > AP.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid has transverse >>> AP."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"In the platypelloid pelvis, the inlet diameter relationship is:","options":["Transverse diameter >>> AP diameter","AP diameter > transverse diameter","Transverse diameter slightly > AP diameter","AP diameter = transverse diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In platypelloid, transverse diameter is markedly greater than AP diameter (>>>).  Why B wrong: AP > transverse is anthropoid.  Why C wrong: A slight transverse predominance describes gynecoid, not the marked flat platypelloid.  Why D wrong: The diameters are not equal."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"In the android pelvis, the inlet diameter relationship is:","options":["Transverse diameter > AP diameter","AP diameter > transverse diameter","Transverse diameter >>> AP diameter","AP diameter = transverse diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In android pelvis, transverse diameter > AP diameter.  Why B wrong: AP > transverse is anthropoid.  Why C wrong: The markedly greater (>>>) transverse describes platypelloid.  Why D wrong: The diameters are not equal."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The side walls of the gynecoid pelvis are described as:","options":["Parallel and broad","Convergent","Parallel and narrow","Divergent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The gynecoid pelvis has parallel broad side walls.  Why B wrong: Convergent side walls describe android.  Why C wrong: Parallel narrow side walls describe anthropoid.  Why D wrong: Divergent side walls describe platypelloid."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which pelvic type has convergent side walls?","options":["Android pelvis","Gynecoid pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The android pelvis has convergent side walls (contributing to its unfavourable, funnel shape).  Why B wrong: Gynecoid has parallel broad walls.  Why C wrong: Anthropoid has parallel narrow walls.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid has divergent walls."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The side walls of the anthropoid pelvis are:","options":["Parallel and narrow","Parallel and broad","Convergent","Divergent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anthropoid pelvis has parallel narrow side walls.  Why B wrong: Parallel broad describes gynecoid.  Why C wrong: Convergent describes android.  Why D wrong: Divergent describes platypelloid."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The side walls of the platypelloid pelvis are described as:","options":["Divergent","Parallel broad","Convergent","Parallel narrow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The platypelloid pelvis has divergent side walls.  Why B wrong: Parallel broad describes gynecoid.  Why C wrong: Convergent describes android.  Why D wrong: Parallel narrow describes anthropoid."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"A prominent ischial spine is a characteristic feature of which pelvic type?","options":["Android pelvis","Gynecoid pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The android pelvis has a prominent ischial spine.  Why B, C, D wrong: The table specifies prominent ischial spine only for android; it is not listed for gynecoid, anthropoid, or platypelloid."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The subpubic angle in a female (gynecoid) pelvis is:","options":["Greater than 90°","Less than 90°","Exactly 60°","Exactly 45°"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the female/gynecoid pelvis the subpubic angle is > 90°.  Why B wrong: A subpubic angle < 90° is the male/android pelvis.  Why C wrong: 60° is not specified.  Why D wrong: 45° is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"A subpubic angle of less than 90° is characteristic of which pelvis?","options":["Male (android) pelvis","Female (gynecoid) pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A subpubic angle < 90° corresponds to the male (android) pelvis.  Why B wrong: The female/gynecoid pelvis has a subpubic angle > 90°.  Why C wrong: The table gives < 90° for the android/male, not anthropoid.  Why D wrong: The table gives < 90° for android, not platypelloid."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which pelvis is the most common variety AND the most favourable for delivery?","options":["Gynecoid pelvis","Android pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The gynecoid pelvis is both the most common variety and the most favourable for delivery.  Why B wrong: Android is the least favourable for delivery.  Why C wrong: Anthropoid is neither most common nor most favourable.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid is the least common."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which pelvis type is the LEAST favourable for vaginal delivery?","options":["Android pelvis","Gynecoid pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The android pelvis is the least favourable for delivery (converging walls, prominent spines, narrow forepelvis).  Why B wrong: Gynecoid is the most favourable.  Why C wrong: Anthropoid is not identified as the least favourable.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid is the least common but android is stated as least favourable."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"A pelvis in which the AP diameter is greater than the transverse diameter is which type?","options":["Anthropoid pelvis","Gynecoid pelvis","Android pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pelvis with AP diameter > transverse diameter is the anthropoid pelvis.  Why B wrong: Gynecoid has transverse > AP.  Why C wrong: Android has transverse > AP.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid has transverse >>> AP."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The heading of the anatomical topic depicted on the fetal skull page includes sutures and bones. Which of the following is a suture of the fetal skull?","options":["Sagittal suture","Parietal bone","Occipital bone","Frontal bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sagittal suture is one of the fetal skull sutures labelled in the diagram.  Why B wrong: Parietal is a bone, not a suture.  Why C wrong: Occipital is a bone, not a suture.  Why D wrong: Frontal is a bone (the frontal SUTURE is the suture; frontal BONE is a bone)."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which of the following is one of the sutures of the fetal skull labelled in the diagram?","options":["Coronal suture","Parietal suture","Occipital suture","Temporal suture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The coronal suture is one of the labelled sutures (between frontal and parietal bones).  Why B wrong: \"Parietal suture\" is not among the labelled sutures.  Why C wrong: \"Occipital suture\" is not a labelled fetal skull suture here.  Why D wrong: \"Temporal suture\" is not labelled in the fetal skull diagram."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The suture that lies between the parietal bones and the occipital bone in the fetal skull is the:","options":["Lambdoid suture","Coronal suture","Frontal suture","Sagittal suture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The lambdoid suture is labelled at the posterior part of the skull near the occipital bone.  Why B wrong: The coronal suture is anterior, between frontal and parietal bones.  Why C wrong: The frontal suture lies between the two frontal bones.  Why D wrong: The sagittal suture lies between the two parietal bones."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which bone of the fetal skull is labelled at the posterior (back) of the skull in the diagram?","options":["Occipital bone","Frontal bone","Parietal bone","Temporal bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The occipital bone is labelled at the posterior part of the fetal skull.  Why B wrong: The frontal bone is anterior.  Why C wrong: The parietal bones are the lateral/superior bones.  Why D wrong: The temporal bone is not the posterior labelled bone in this diagram."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Fontanelles of the fetal skull are best defined as:","options":["Gaps between the suture lines","Overlapping of the skull bones","Thickened areas of bone","Cartilage joints of the vertebrae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fontanelles are the gaps between the suture lines.  Why B wrong: Overlapping of skull bones is moulding, not a fontanelle.  Why C wrong: Fontanelles are membranous gaps, not thickened bone.  Why D wrong: They relate to the skull, not vertebral cartilage."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"How many fontanelles are present in the fetal skull at the time of birth?","options":["6","2","4","8"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At the time of birth there are 6 fontanelles in the fetal skull.  Why B wrong: 2 are the most important fontanelles (anterior and posterior), but the total is 6.  Why C wrong: The total is 6, not 4.  Why D wrong: The total is 6, not 8."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The two MOST important fontanelles of the fetal skull are the:","options":["Anterior and posterior fontanelles","Left and right sphenoidal fontanelles","Two mastoid fontanelles","Anterior and mastoid fontanelles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 2 most important fontanelles are the anterior and posterior fontanelles.  Why B, C, D wrong: While other fontanelles exist (total 6), the notes identify the anterior and posterior as the 2 most important."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The anterior fontanelle of the fetal skull is also known as the:","options":["Bregma","Lambda","Pterion","Asterion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior fontanelle is also called the BREGMA.  Why B wrong: Lambda is the posterior fontanelle.  Why C wrong: Pterion is not a term used for the anterior fontanelle here.  Why D wrong: Asterion is not used in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The shape of the anterior fontanelle (bregma) is:","options":["Diamond shaped","Triangular","Oval","Circular"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior fontanelle is diamond shaped.  Why B wrong: Triangular is the shape of the posterior fontanelle.  Why C wrong: It is not oval.  Why D wrong: It is not circular."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"What are the dimensions of the anterior fontanelle?","options":["3 cms","1.2 cms","2 cms","5 cms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The dimensions of the anterior fontanelle are 3 cms.  Why B wrong: 1.2 cms is the posterior fontanelle dimension.  Why C wrong: 2 cms is not stated.  Why D wrong: 5 cms is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The anterior fontanelle (bregma) is formed by which sutures?","options":["Frontal suture, coronal suture (left & right), and sagittal suture","Sagittal suture and left & right lambdoid sutures","Coronal and lambdoid sutures only","Frontal and lambdoid sutures only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior fontanelle is formed by the frontal suture, coronal suture (left & right), and sagittal suture.  Why B wrong: That combination forms the posterior fontanelle.  Why C wrong: The anterior fontanelle involves the frontal and sagittal sutures too, not only coronal/lambdoid.  Why D wrong: The lambdoid suture is part of the posterior fontanelle, not the anterior."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"At what age does the anterior fontanelle normally close?","options":["By 1½ years (18 months) of age","At the time of birth","By 6 months of age","By 3 years of age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior fontanelle closes by 1½ years of age (18 months).  Why B wrong: The posterior fontanelle closes at/immediately after birth.  Why C wrong: The notes specify 18 months, not 6 months.  Why D wrong: The notes specify 18 months, not 3 years."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The posterior fontanelle of the fetal skull is also known as the:","options":["Lambda","Bregma","Glabella","Sinciput"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior fontanelle is also called LAMBDA.  Why B wrong: Bregma is the anterior fontanelle.  Why C wrong: Glabella is a facial landmark, not the posterior fontanelle.  Why D wrong: Sinciput refers to the brow region, not the posterior fontanelle."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The shape of the posterior fontanelle (lambda) is:","options":["Triangular","Diamond shaped","Circular","Rectangular"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior fontanelle is triangular in shape.  Why B wrong: Diamond shape is the anterior fontanelle.  Why C wrong: It is not circular.  Why D wrong: It is not rectangular."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"What are the dimensions of the posterior fontanelle?","options":["1.2 cms","3 cms","2 cms","0.5 cms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior fontanelle dimensions are 1.2 cms.  Why B wrong: 3 cms is the anterior fontanelle dimension.  Why C wrong: 2 cms is not stated.  Why D wrong: 0.5 cms is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The posterior fontanelle (lambda) is formed by which sutures?","options":["Sagittal suture and left & right lambdoid sutures","Frontal, coronal, and sagittal sutures","Coronal suture only","Frontal suture and sagittal suture only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior fontanelle is formed by the sagittal suture and the left & right lambdoid sutures.  Why B wrong: That combination forms the anterior fontanelle.  Why C wrong: The coronal suture is part of the anterior fontanelle.  Why D wrong: The lambdoid sutures (not just frontal + sagittal) form the posterior fontanelle."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"When does the posterior fontanelle normally close?","options":["At the time of birth or immediately after birth","By 18 months of age","By 6 years of age","By 6 months of age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior fontanelle closes at the time of birth or immediately after birth.  Why B wrong: 18 months is when the anterior fontanelle closes.  Why C wrong: 6 years is not the stated time.  Why D wrong: 6 months is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which of the following statements correctly differentiates the anterior from the posterior fontanelle?","options":["The anterior fontanelle is diamond shaped (3 cms) and closes at 18 months, while the posterior is triangular (1.2 cms) and closes at birth","The anterior fontanelle is triangular and the posterior is diamond shaped","Both close at 18 months","The anterior is 1.2 cms and the posterior is 3 cms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anterior = bregma, diamond, 3 cms, closes at 18 months; posterior = lambda, triangular, 1.2 cms, closes at birth.  Why B wrong: The shapes are reversed here.  Why C wrong: Only the anterior closes at 18 months; the posterior closes at birth.  Why D wrong: The dimensions are reversed here."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which bone forms the anterior part of the fetal skull, labelled next to the frontal suture in the diagram?","options":["Frontal bone","Occipital bone","Parietal bone","Sphenoid bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The frontal bone (marked \"F\") forms the anterior part of the fetal skull, adjacent to the frontal suture.  Why B wrong: The occipital bone is posterior.  Why C wrong: The parietal bones (marked \"P\") are lateral/superior.  Why D wrong: The sphenoid bone is not labelled in this diagram."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The suture lying between the two frontal bones of the fetal skull is the:","options":["Frontal suture","Sagittal suture","Coronal suture","Lambdoid suture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The frontal suture lies between the two frontal bones (labelled at the front of the diagram).  Why B wrong: The sagittal suture lies between the two parietal bones.  Why C wrong: The coronal suture lies between the frontal and parietal bones.  Why D wrong: The lambdoid suture lies between the parietal and occipital bones."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"The paired bones marked \"P\" on either side of the top of the fetal skull are the:","options":["Parietal bones","Frontal bones","Occipital bones","Temporal bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bones marked \"P\" are the parietal bones, separated by the sagittal suture.  Why B wrong: The frontal bones are marked \"F\" anteriorly.  Why C wrong: The occipital bone is marked \"O\" posteriorly and is single.  Why D wrong: The temporal bones are not the labelled \"P\" bones here."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"A woman with a contracted pelvis is scheduled for delivery. Which of the following delivery methods is appropriate and at what timing?","options":["Elective cesarean section at 39 weeks","Vaginal delivery with epidural at 40 weeks","Induction of labour at 38 weeks","Instrumental delivery at term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For contracted pelvis, elective cesarean section at 39 weeks is the recommended management.  Why B wrong: Vaginal delivery in contracted pelvis leads to obstructed labour.  Why C wrong: Induction risks obstructed labour with a contracted pelvis.  Why D wrong: Instrumental vaginal delivery cannot overcome a fixed bony contracture."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"A labouring woman shows a visible/palpable groove between the upper and lower uterine segments, dry hot vagina, hematuria, and absent fetal heart sounds. What is the MOST likely diagnosis?","options":["Obstructed labour","Placental abruption","Precipitate labour","Cord prolapse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bandl's ring (groove), dry hot vagina from drained liquor, hematuria, and absent FHS are classic features of obstructed labour.  Why B wrong: Placental abruption presents with painful bleeding and a rigid uterus, not Bandl's ring with dry vagina.  Why C wrong: Precipitate labour is very rapid delivery, not obstruction.  Why D wrong: Cord prolapse presents with a palpable cord and acute fetal bradycardia, not this cluster of signs."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"A multiparous woman with obstructed labour and a big baby is in distress. The junior nurse suggests starting oxytocin to speed delivery. What is the priority correct action?","options":["Do not give oxytocin; prepare for immediate cesarean section","Start oxytocin infusion as suggested","Apply fundal pressure","Wait for spontaneous delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is contraindicated in obstructed labour (risk of uterine rupture); immediate cesarean section is required whether the baby is alive or dead.  Why B wrong: Oxytocin increases contractions and risks uterine rupture.  Why C wrong: Fundal pressure worsens the risk of rupture.  Why D wrong: Waiting risks maternal death; immediate cesarean is indicated."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"A postpartum woman who had a prolonged obstructed labour now complains of continuous involuntary dribbling of urine from the vagina. This is due to:","options":["Vesicovaginal fistula","Stress incontinence","Urinary tract infection","Overflow incontinence from a full bladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged obstructed labour causes pressure necrosis leading to a vesicovaginal fistula, producing continuous dribbling of urine from the vagina.  Why B wrong: Stress incontinence occurs with exertion, not continuous dribbling.  Why C wrong: UTI causes dysuria/frequency, not continuous leakage per vagina.  Why D wrong: Overflow incontinence is intermittent and not per vagina."},{"section":"Batch 054 · Clinical assessment of pelvic cavity and outlet, bituberous diameter…","question":"Which single pelvis type is described as both the least favourable for delivery and having a prominent ischial spine?","options":["Android pelvis","Gynecoid pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The android pelvis is the least favourable for delivery and has a prominent ischial spine.  Why B wrong: Gynecoid is the most favourable and does not have the prominent spine feature.  Why C wrong: Anthropoid is characterized by AP > transverse, not the prominent spine.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid is the least common, not the one with prominent ischial spine."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The sinciput of the fetal skull is defined as the part which lies:","options":["Anterior to the anterior fontanelle","Posterior to the posterior fontanelle","Between the anterior and posterior fontanelles","Between the root of nose and chin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define sinciput as the part of the fetal skull which lies anterior to the anterior fontanelle.  Why B wrong: That location defines the occiput, not the sinciput.  Why C wrong: The region between the two fontanelles is the vertex.  Why D wrong: That describes the face."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The occiput of the fetal skull is the part which lies:","options":["Anterior to the anterior fontanelle","Posterior to the posterior fontanelle","Between anterior fontanelle and root of nose","At the supraorbital ridges"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define occiput as the part of the fetal skull which lies posterior to the posterior fontanelle.  Why A wrong: That is the sinciput.  Why C wrong: That corresponds to the brow region.  Why D wrong: Supraorbital ridges belong to the brow/face boundary, not the occiput."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The vertex of the fetal skull is defined as the part of the skull which lies between:","options":["Anterior fontanelle and root of nose","Anterior fontanelle and posterior fontanelle","Root of nose, supraorbital ridges and chin","Posterior fontanelle and occiput"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines vertex as the part of skull lying between the anterior fontanelle and the posterior fontanelle.  Why A wrong: The region between anterior fontanelle and root of nose (+ supraorbital ridges) is the brow.  Why C wrong: That defines the face.  Why D wrong: This is not a defined skull part in the table."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The brow (eyebrow) of the fetal skull is the part lying between the anterior fontanelle and:","options":["The posterior fontanelle","The chin (mentum)","The root of nose and supraorbital ridges","The occiput"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines brow as the part between the anterior fontanelle and the root of nose plus supraorbital ridges.  Why A wrong: Between the two fontanelles is the vertex.  Why B wrong: Down to the chin defines the face.  Why D wrong: The occiput lies posterior to the posterior fontanelle."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The face of the fetal skull is defined as the part lying between the root of nose, supraorbital ridges and:","options":["Anterior fontanelle","Posterior fontanelle","Chin (mentum)","Bregma"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines face as the part between the root of nose, supraorbital ridges and the chin (mentum).  Why A/B wrong: The fontanelles bound the vertex/brow, not the lower boundary of the face.  Why D wrong: Bregma is the anterior fontanelle region, not the chin."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The vertex is seen as the presenting part when the fetal head is:","options":["Fully flexed or deflexed","Partially extended","Fully extended","Rotated transversely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states vertex is seen when the head is fully flexed and also when the head is deflexed.  Why B wrong: Partial extension gives brow.  Why C wrong: Full extension gives face.  Why D wrong: Transverse rotation is not the attitude referenced for vertex."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The brow presentation is seen when the fetal head is:","options":["Fully flexed","Deflexed","Partially extended","Fully extended"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the brow is seen when the head is partially extended.  Why A/B wrong: Full flexion and deflexion give vertex.  Why D wrong: Full extension gives face."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The face presentation is seen when the fetal head is:","options":["Fully flexed","Deflexed","Partially extended","Fully extended"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the face is seen when the head of baby is fully extended.  Why A/B wrong: Flexion/deflexion give vertex.  Why C wrong: Partial extension gives brow."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Match the fetal head attitude with its presentation. When the head is fully flexed, the presentation is:","options":["VERTEX","BROW","FACE","Shoulder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram key states \"Fully flexed = VERTEX\".  Why B wrong: Brow = partially extended.  Why C wrong: Face = fully extended.  Why D wrong: Shoulder relates to transverse lie, not head attitude."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"According to the fetal head attitude key, a deflexed head gives which presentation?","options":["FACE","BROW","VERTEX","Compound"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The key states \"Deflexed = VERTEX\".  Why A wrong: Face is fully extended.  Why B wrong: Brow is partially extended.  Why D wrong: Compound presentation involves 2 parts, unrelated to attitude here."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"A partially extended fetal head produces which presentation?","options":["VERTEX","BROW","FACE","Breech"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key states \"Partially extended = BROW\".  Why A wrong: Vertex = fully flexed or deflexed.  Why C wrong: Face = fully extended.  Why D wrong: Breech is a longitudinal-lie presentation, not a head attitude."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"A fully extended fetal head produces which presentation?","options":["VERTEX","BROW","FACE","Vertex-deflexed"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The key states \"Full extended = FACE\".  Why A/B/D wrong: These correspond to flexed/deflexed (vertex) and partially extended (brow) attitudes."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In cephalic presentation with the head fully flexed (vertex), the engaging diameter is the suboccipitobregmatic diameter, which measures:","options":["9.5 cm","11.5 cm","14 cm","13.5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vertex flow chart states the suboccipito bregmatic diameter is 9.5 cms when the head is fully flexed.  Why B wrong: 11.5 cm is the occipitofrontal diameter.  Why C wrong: 14 cm is the mentovertical diameter (brow).  Why D wrong: 13.5 cm is not a value given on these pages."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In vertex presentation with a deflexed head, the presenting diameter is the:","options":["Suboccipitobregmatic diameter","Suboccipitofrontal (occipitofrontal) diameter","Mentovertical diameter","Submentobregmatic diameter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The vertex flow chart states that with a deflexed head the diameter is suboccipito frontal, or occipito frontal diameter.  Why A wrong: Suboccipitobregmatic corresponds to the fully flexed vertex.  Why C wrong: Mentovertical is for brow.  Why D wrong: Submentobregmatic is for face."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In brow presentation (head partially extended), the engaging diameter is the:","options":["Suboccipitobregmatic diameter","Mentovertical diameter","Biparietal diameter","Submentovertical diameter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The BROW flow chart states the mento vertical diameter is the presenting diameter and it is the longest diameter (14 cms).  Why A wrong: Suboccipitobregmatic is the vertex (flexed) diameter.  Why C wrong: Biparietal is a transverse diameter.  Why D wrong: Submentovertical is a face diameter."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The mentovertical diameter of the fetal skull (seen in brow presentation) is the longest diameter and measures:","options":["9.5 cm","11.5 cm","14 cm","12.5 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The brow flow chart and p-272 both state the mento vertical (longest) diameter = 14 cms.  Why A wrong: 9.5 cm is suboccipitobregmatic/biparietal.  Why B wrong: 11.5 cm is occipitofrontal/submentovertical.  Why D wrong: 12.5 cm is not given."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Because the brow presentation presents the longest diameter (mentovertical, 14 cms), the recommended management of labor is to:","options":["Allow normal vaginal delivery","Apply forceps","Always do cesarean section","Perform vacuum extraction"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The BROW flow chart ends with \"Always do cesarean section\" because the 14 cm mentovertical diameter cannot deliver vaginally.  Why A wrong: The oversized diameter prevents normal vaginal delivery.  Why B/D wrong: Instrumental vaginal delivery is not indicated for the largest (14 cm) diameter."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In face presentation (head fully extended), the engaging diameter is the submentovertical diameter OR the:","options":["Submentobregmatic diameter","Suboccipitobregmatic diameter","Mentovertical diameter","Biparietal diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The FACE flow chart states the submento vertical diameter or submento bregmatic diameter is presented in face presentation.  Why B wrong: Suboccipitobregmatic is the vertex diameter.  Why C wrong: Mentovertical is the brow diameter.  Why D wrong: Biparietal is a transverse diameter."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Regarding the anteroposterior (AP) versus transverse diameters of the fetal skull, which statement is correct?","options":["AP diameters are always shorter than transverse diameters","AP diameters are always longer than transverse diameters","They are always equal","Transverse diameters are always longer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 states AP diameters of the fetal skull are always longer than the transverse diameters.  Why A/D wrong: These reverse the correct relationship.  Why C wrong: They are not equal; AP is longer."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The longest AP diameter of the fetal skull is the:","options":["Occipitofrontal diameter","Suboccipitobregmatic diameter","Mentovertical diameter (seen in brow)","Submentovertical diameter"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 states the longest AP diameter is the MENTO VERTICAL diameter (seen in brow) = 14 cms.  Why A wrong: Occipitofrontal is 11.5 cm (a shorter AP diameter).  Why B wrong: Suboccipitobregmatic is 9.5 cm.  Why D wrong: Submentovertical is a shorter AP diameter."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"According to p-272, the second longest AP diameter of the fetal skull equals the submentovertical diameter and the occipitofrontal diameter, each measuring:","options":["9.5 cm","11.5 cm","14 cm","10.5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 states the second longest AP diameter = submentovertical = occipitofrontal diameter = 11.5 cm each.  Why A wrong: 9.5 cm is the suboccipitobregmatic/biparietal diameter.  Why C wrong: 14 cm is the mentovertical diameter.  Why D wrong: 10.5 cm is not given."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Which statement about the transverse diameters of the fetal skull is correct?","options":["They are always larger than the AP diameters","They are always smaller than the AP diameters","They equal the AP diameters","They are the longest of all skull diameters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 states the transverse diameters are always smaller than the AP diameter.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the source point that AP diameters are always longer."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In the mnemonic \"Mis, Tina, So, Pretty\" for transverse diameters of the fetal skull, the SMALLEST transverse diameter is the:","options":["Bitemporal diameter","Bimastoid diameter","Super subparietal diameter","Biparietal diameter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 lists \"Mis (Smallest) = BIMASTOID diameter\".  Why A wrong: Bitemporal (\"Tina\") is the next diameter, not the smallest.  Why C wrong: Super subparietal (\"So\") is larger than bimastoid.  Why D wrong: Biparietal is the largest transverse diameter."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In the transverse diameter mnemonic, \"Tina\" represents which diameter?","options":["Bimastoid diameter","Bitemporal diameter","Super subparietal diameter","Biparietal diameter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 lists \"Tina = Bitemporal diameter\".  Why A wrong: \"Mis\" = bimastoid (smallest).  Why C wrong: \"So\" = super subparietal.  Why D wrong: \"Pretty\" = biparietal (largest)."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In the transverse diameter mnemonic, \"So\" represents which diameter?","options":["Bimastoid diameter","Bitemporal diameter","Super subparietal diameter","Biparietal diameter"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 lists \"So = Super subparietal diameter\".  Why A/B wrong: These are the two smaller transverse diameters (bimastoid, bitemporal).  Why D wrong: Biparietal (\"Pretty\") is the largest."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The LARGEST transverse diameter of the fetal skull (\"Pretty\" in the mnemonic) is the biparietal diameter, which measures:","options":["9.5 cm","11.5 cm","14 cm","8.5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 lists \"Pretty (LARGEST) = Biparietal diameter = 9.5 cms\".  Why B wrong: 11.5 cm is occipitofrontal/submentovertical.  Why C wrong: 14 cm is mentovertical.  Why D wrong: 8.5 cm is not given."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Moulding of the fetal head is best defined as:","options":["The collection of fluid above the periosteum","The alteration of the shape of the forecoming head while passing through the resistant birth passage during labor","Overlapping of fetal ribs during delivery","The rotation of the head to occipito-anterior position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 defines moulding as the alteration of the shape of the forecoming (coming) head while passing through the resistant birth passage during labor.  Why A wrong: Fluid above periosteum describes caput succedaneum.  Why C wrong: Moulding involves skull bones, not ribs.  Why D wrong: That describes internal rotation, not moulding."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"How many grades of moulding are described in the notes?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 states there are 3 gradings of moulding (Grade 1, 2, 3).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies exactly 3 grades."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Grade 1 moulding is characterized by:","options":["Bones touch but do not overlap (+)","Overlap but easily separated (++)","Fixed overlapping (+++)","No contact between bones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 states Grade 1: bones touch but not overlap (+).  Why B wrong: That is Grade 2 (++).  Why C wrong: That is Grade 3 (+++).  Why D wrong: In Grade 1 bones do touch."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Grade 2 moulding is characterized by:","options":["Bones touch but do not overlap (+)","Overlap but easily separated (++)","Fixed overlapping (+++)","Bones widely separated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 states Grade 2: overlap but easily separated (++).  Why A wrong: Bones touching without overlap is Grade 1.  Why C wrong: Fixed overlapping is Grade 3.  Why D wrong: Not a described grade."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Grade 3 moulding is characterized by:","options":["Bones touch but do not overlap (+)","Overlap but easily separated (++)","Fixed overlapping (+++)","Reversible mild overlap"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 states Grade 3: fixed overlapping (+++).  Why A wrong: That is Grade 1.  Why B wrong: That is Grade 2.  Why D wrong: Not a described grade."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Which statement about the significance of moulding during labor is correct?","options":["Any moulding indicates cephalopelvic disproportion","Some degree of moulding is normal, but ++ or +++ moulding means cephalopelvic disproportion","Moulding never occurs in normal labor","Only Grade 1 moulding indicates disproportion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 states some degree of moulding is normal during labor, but if there is ++ or +++ moulding it means there is cephalopelvic disproportion.  Why A wrong: A small degree of moulding is normal.  Why C wrong: Some moulding is normal in labor.  Why D wrong: It is ++/+++ (Grade 2/3), not Grade 1, that signals disproportion."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The swellings on the fetal head are broadly classified into caput succedaneum and:","options":["Cephalhematoma","Subgaleal hemorrhage","Craniosynostosis","Encephalocele"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 flow chart divides swellings on fetal head into caput succedaneum and cephalhematoma.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the two categories shown in the source flow chart."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Caput succedaneum is a collection of fluid located:","options":["Below the periosteum","Above the periosteum","Inside the skull cavity","Within the brain ventricles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states caput succedaneum is a collection of fluid above the periosteum.  Why A wrong: Blood below the periosteum describes cephalhematoma.  Why C/D wrong: It is a scalp swelling, not intracranial."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The swelling in caput succedaneum is described as:","options":["Localized","Generalized","Fixed and pulsatile","Confined to one suture line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states caput succedaneum is a generalized swelling.  Why A wrong: Localized swelling describes cephalhematoma.  Why C/D wrong: Not features attributed to caput succedaneum."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"With respect to suture lines, caput succedaneum:","options":["Cannot cross suture lines","Can cross suture lines","Is always confined below the periosteum","Follows only the sagittal suture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states caput succedaneum can cross suture lines.  Why A wrong: Cephalhematoma cannot cross suture lines.  Why C/D wrong: Caput lies above periosteum and is generalized, not suture-limited."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Caput succedaneum is formed because:","options":["Of traumatic instrumental delivery","The head of the baby stays in one position for a very long time during labor","Of a bleeding disorder","Of intracranial hemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states caput succedaneum is formed because the head of baby stays in one position for a very long time during labor.  Why A wrong: Traumatic instrumental delivery causes cephalhematoma.  Why C/D wrong: Not the mechanism described."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Caput succedaneum is edema (fluid); therefore, on applying pressure it:","options":["Does not pit","Pits on giving pressure","Pulsates","Increases in size"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states caput succedaneum is edema (fluid) and pits on giving pressure.  Why A wrong: Not pitting on pressure is a feature of cephalhematoma (blood).  Why C/D wrong: Not described features."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In relation to timing, caput succedaneum is:","options":["Not seen at birth and appears within a few hours","Seen at the time of birth and disappears automatically within a few hours of birth","Seen only after several days","Permanent and requires surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states caput succedaneum is seen at the time of birth and disappears automatically within a few hours of birth.  Why A wrong: Appearing after birth describes cephalhematoma.  Why C/D wrong: It resolves spontaneously within hours, not days, and needs no surgery."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Cephalhematoma is a collection of blood located:","options":["Above the periosteum","Below the periosteum","In the subarachnoid space","Within the scalp only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states cephalhematoma is a collection of blood below the periosteum.  Why A wrong: Fluid above periosteum is caput succedaneum.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described location."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The swelling in cephalhematoma is described as:","options":["Generalized","Localized","Diffuse crossing sutures","Bilaterally symmetric"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states cephalhematoma is a localized swelling.  Why A wrong: Generalized swelling is caput succedaneum.  Why C wrong: Cephalhematoma cannot cross suture lines.  Why D wrong: Not a described feature."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"With respect to suture lines, cephalhematoma:","options":["Can cross suture lines","Cannot cross suture lines (because it is below the periosteum)","Always crosses the sagittal suture only","Crosses all sutures freely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states cephalhematoma cannot cross suture lines (below periosteum).  Why A wrong: Caput succedaneum can cross suture lines.  Why C/D wrong: It is limited by the periosteal attachment at sutures."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Cephalhematoma is formed due to:","options":["The head staying in one position for a long time","Traumatic instrumental delivery","Normal spontaneous vaginal delivery","Prolonged edema of the scalp"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states cephalhematoma is formed due to traumatic instrumental delivery.  Why A wrong: That mechanism forms caput succedaneum.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described cause."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Because cephalhematoma is due to blood, on applying pressure it:","options":["Pits on giving pressure","Does not pit on giving pressure","Always ruptures","Disappears instantly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states cephalhematoma is due to blood and does not pit on giving pressure.  Why A wrong: Pitting is a feature of caput succedaneum (edema).  Why C/D wrong: Not described features."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Which of the following can be associated with cephalhematoma?","options":["Feature of underlying bone and jaundice","Generalized scalp edema","Crossing of suture lines","Immediate resolution within hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states cephalhematoma can be associated with feature of underlying bone and can be associated with jaundice.  Why B/C wrong: Generalized swelling and crossing sutures are caput succedaneum features.  Why D wrong: Cephalhematoma resolves over days, not hours."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Regarding timing, cephalhematoma:","options":["Is seen at birth and disappears within hours","Is not seen at birth, appears within a few hours of birth, and disappears automatically in a few days","Never resolves without surgery","Appears only after one month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-273 states cephalhematoma is not seen at birth, appears within a few hours of birth, and disappears automatically in a few days.  Why A wrong: Being present at birth and resolving in hours describes caput succedaneum.  Why C/D wrong: It resolves spontaneously in days, not needing surgery, and appears within hours."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"A newborn has a scalp swelling that was present at birth, is generalized, crosses suture lines, and pits on pressure. This is most likely:","options":["Cephalhematoma","Caput succedaneum","Subdural hematoma","Craniosynostosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Present at birth, generalized, crosses sutures, and pits on pressure are all defining features of caput succedaneum (fluid above periosteum).  Why A wrong: Cephalhematoma appears after birth, is localized, does not cross sutures, and does not pit.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the scalp swellings described on the page."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"A newborn delivered by forceps develops a localized scalp swelling a few hours after birth that does not cross suture lines, does not pit on pressure, and is later associated with jaundice. This is most likely:","options":["Caput succedaneum","Cephalhematoma","Molding","Bruising only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Traumatic instrumental (forceps) delivery, localized, appears after birth, does not cross sutures, does not pit, and associated jaundice are all cephalhematoma features.  Why A wrong: Caput succedaneum is present at birth, generalized, crosses sutures, and pits.  Why C/D wrong: Not consistent with the described localized blood collection below periosteum."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In obstetrics, \"lie\" is defined as the relationship between the:","options":["Fetal head and the maternal pelvis","Longitudinal axis of the fetus and the longitudinal axis of the mother","Presenting part and the cervix","Fetal spine and the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-274 defines lie as the relationship between the longitudinal axis of the fetus and the longitudinal axis of the mother.  Why A wrong: That relationship relates to engagement/presentation, not the definition of lie.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the source definition."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three types of lie described?","options":["Longitudinal lie","Transverse lie","Oblique lie","Compound lie"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: p-274 shows three types of lie: longitudinal, transverse, and oblique; \"compound\" is not a type of lie (compound presentation is a separate concept).  Why A/B/C wrong: These are the three lies illustrated."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"For a transverse lie, the recommended management is to always do a cesarean section:","options":["Only if the baby is alive","Only if the baby is dead","Whether the baby is alive or dead","Only after a trial of labor"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: p-274 states for transverse lie: always do cesarean section, whether the baby is alive or dead.  Why A/B wrong: Cesarean is indicated regardless of fetal viability.  Why D wrong: A trial of labor is not appropriate for transverse lie."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"According to the source, both transverse lie and oblique lie require:","options":["Normal vaginal delivery","Always cesarean section","Vacuum-assisted delivery","External cephalic version only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-274 groups transverse and oblique lie under \"Always cesarean section\".  Why A wrong: These abnormal lies cannot be delivered vaginally.  Why C wrong: Vacuum is not indicated.  Why D wrong: The source specifies cesarean section, not version, as management."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"\"Presentation\" in obstetrics is defined as:","options":["The relationship of the fetal axis to the maternal axis","The part of the fetus which occupies the lower part of the uterus","The degree of flexion of the fetal head","The station of the presenting part relative to the ischial spines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-274 defines presentation as the part of the fetus which occupies the lower part of the uterus.  Why A wrong: That defines lie.  Why C wrong: That describes attitude.  Why D wrong: That describes station."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In a longitudinal lie, the possible presentations include cephalic presentation and:","options":["Shoulder presentation","Breech presentation","Oblique presentation","Transverse presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-275 shows that in longitudinal lie the presentation could be cephalic or breech.  Why A/D wrong: Shoulder/transverse presentations occur in transverse lie.  Why C wrong: Oblique is a lie, not a longitudinal-lie presentation."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The most common presentation of the fetus is:","options":["Breech presentation","Cephalic presentation","Shoulder presentation","Face presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-275 states the most common presentation is cephalic presentation.  Why A wrong: Breech is the most common malpresentation, not the most common presentation overall.  Why C/D wrong: These are uncommon presentations."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"According to the notes, apart from cephalic presentation, all other presentations are termed:","options":["Normal presentations","Malpresentations","Compound presentations","Vertex presentations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-275 states apart from cephalic presentation, the rest of all presentations are malpresentations.  Why A wrong: Only cephalic is considered the normal presentation.  Why C wrong: Compound presentation is a specific separate entity.  Why D wrong: Vertex is a subtype of cephalic."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The most common malpresentation is:","options":["Face","Brow","Breech","Shoulder"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: p-275 states the most common malpresentation is breech.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are malpresentations but not the most common one."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In a transverse lie, the presenting part is the:","options":["Vertex","Shoulder","Breech","Face"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-275 states that in transverse lie the presentation is shoulder presentation.  Why A/D wrong: Vertex and face are cephalic presentations (longitudinal lie).  Why C wrong: Breech is a longitudinal-lie presentation."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"The management of labor for a shoulder presentation (transverse lie) is:","options":["Normal vaginal delivery","Cesarean section","Forceps delivery","Vacuum extraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-275 states the management of labor for shoulder presentation (transverse lie) is cesarean section.  Why A/C/D wrong: Vaginal or instrumental vaginal delivery is not possible/appropriate for a shoulder presentation."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"A shoulder presentation (transverse lie) with a dead baby is specifically called:","options":["Compound presentation","Neglected shoulder presentation","Complete breech","Face presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-275 states shoulder presentation (transverse lie) with a dead baby is called neglected shoulder presentation (managed by cesarean section).  Why A wrong: Compound presentation is when 2 parts occupy the lower uterus.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated presentations."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Maximum chances of cord prolapse are seen with which lie? (PYQ)","options":["Longitudinal lie","Transverse lie","Cephalic vertex","Oblique lie only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-275 Q1 states maximum chances of cord prolapse are with transverse lie.  Why A/C wrong: Longitudinal/cephalic lie has lower risk due to a well-applied presenting part.  Why D wrong: The source specifically names transverse lie."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Hand prolapse is characteristically seen in which lie? (PYQ)","options":["Longitudinal lie","Transverse lie","Breech presentation","Face presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-275 Q1 states hand prolapse is seen in transverse lie.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the answer given; hand prolapse is associated with transverse lie/shoulder presentation."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Compound presentation is defined as:","options":["When the fetal head is fully extended","When 2 parts of the baby occupy the lower part of the uterus","When the cord prolapses before the presenting part","When two fetuses share one placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-275 defines compound presentation as when 2 parts of baby occupy the lower part of the uterus.  Why A wrong: Full extension of the head defines face presentation.  Why C wrong: That describes cord prolapse.  Why D wrong: That describes monochorionic twins, unrelated to the definition."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"A longitudinal lie means the longitudinal axis of the fetus is oriented how relative to the mother's longitudinal axis?","options":["Perpendicular (90°)","Parallel (along the same axis)","At 45° (oblique)","Reversed only in breech"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In longitudinal lie the fetal long axis lies parallel to the mother's long axis, consistent with the vertical-line diagram on p-274 and the definition of lie.  Why A wrong: A perpendicular (90°) orientation is a transverse lie.  Why C wrong: A 45° orientation is an oblique lie.  Why D wrong: Breech is still a longitudinal lie (parallel), just with the buttocks presenting."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"In the fetal skull attitude diagrams, a fully flexed head presents the suboccipitobregmatic diameter. This is favorable for vaginal delivery because it is:","options":["The largest AP diameter","The smallest engaging AP diameter (9.5 cm)","Equal to the mentovertical diameter","A transverse diameter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The suboccipitobregmatic diameter (9.5 cm) presented in a fully flexed vertex is the smallest of the presenting AP diameters listed, favoring vaginal delivery.  Why A wrong: The largest AP diameter is the mentovertical (14 cm) seen in brow.  Why C wrong: Mentovertical is 14 cm, not equal to 9.5 cm.  Why D wrong: Suboccipitobregmatic is an AP diameter, not transverse."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Fetal skull terms, parts of fetal skull and their attitude/presentation…","question":"Which pairing of fetal skull AP diameters both measure 11.5 cm each?","options":["Suboccipitobregmatic and biparietal","Submentovertical and occipitofrontal","Mentovertical and biparietal","Occipitofrontal and mentovertical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: p-272 states the second longest AP diameter = submentovertical = occipitofrontal = 11.5 cm each.  Why A wrong: Suboccipitobregmatic and biparietal are 9.5 cm.  Why C/D wrong: The mentovertical diameter is 14 cm, not 11.5 cm."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"A compound presentation is said to occur when:","options":["Only the head occupies the lower part of the uterus","Two parts of the baby occupy the lower part of the uterus","The umbilical cord presents ahead of the fetus","The placenta covers the internal os"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define compound presentation as the condition in which 2 parts of the baby occupy the lower part of the uterus.  Why A wrong: A single (head) part occupying the lower uterus is a normal cephalic presentation, not compound.  Why C wrong: Cord presenting ahead of the fetus is cord presentation/prolapse, not compound presentation.  Why D wrong: The placenta covering the internal os is placenta previa."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The \"presenting part\" is best defined as the part of presentation which:","options":["Lies at the fundus of the uterus","Lies directly over the internal os","Is delivered last","Faces the maternal spine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define presenting part as the part of presentation which lies directly over the internal os.  Why A wrong: The fundus is the upper part of the uterus, not where the presenting part lies.  Why C wrong: The presenting part is felt first and generally delivered first, not last.  Why D wrong: Facing the maternal spine describes position/orientation, not the definition of presenting part."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"On per vaginal examination, the presenting part is:","options":["The last part felt","The first part felt","Never palpable","Always the fetal buttock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the presenting part is the first part felt on per vaginal examination.  Why A wrong: It is the first, not the last, part felt.  Why C wrong: It is palpable on vaginal examination; that is how it is identified.  Why D wrong: The buttock is the presenting part only in breech; in cephalic it is the head."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"According to the notes, the presenting part in labour depends on whether the fetal head is:","options":["Large or small","Flexed or extended","Ossified or not","Anterior or posterior only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the presenting part depends on whether the head of the baby is flexed or extended.  Why A wrong: Head size is not the determinant listed for the presenting part.  Why C wrong: Degree of ossification is not mentioned as determining the presenting part.  Why D wrong: Anterior/posterior describes position, not the flexion–extension determinant of presenting part."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"When the fetal head is fully flexed, the presenting part is the:","options":["Brow","Face","Vertex","Sinciput"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a fully flexed head presents by the vertex.  Why A wrong: Brow presentation results from partial extension, not full flexion.  Why B wrong: Face presentation results from complete extension, not flexion.  Why D wrong: Sinciput/brow relate to extension; full flexion gives vertex."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"Partial extension of the fetal head results in which presentation?","options":["Vertex","Brow","Face","Breech"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state partial extension of the head gives a brow presentation.  Why A wrong: Vertex results from full flexion, not partial extension.  Why C wrong: Face results from complete extension, not partial extension.  Why D wrong: Breech is a non-cephalic presentation, unrelated to head flexion/extension."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The engaging diameter in a brow presentation is the mento-vertical diameter, which measures:","options":["9.5 cm","11.5 cm","14 cm","12.5 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the engaging diameter in brow presentation is the mento-vertical diameter = 14 cm.  Why A wrong: 9.5 cm is the suboccipitobregmatic (well-flexed vertex) diameter, not mento-vertical.  Why B wrong: 11.5 cm is the occipitofrontal diameter, not the mento-vertical.  Why D wrong: 12.5 cm is the occipitofrontal-range value, not the mento-vertical (14 cm)."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"According to the notes, a persistent brow presentation is managed by:","options":["Normal vaginal delivery","Forceps delivery","Always Cesarean section","Vacuum extraction"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a brow presentation is always managed by Cesarean section (because of the large 14 cm mento-vertical engaging diameter).  Why A wrong: The notes specifically say vaginal delivery is not the route; Cesarean is required.  Why B wrong: Forceps is not indicated for brow; the notes specify Cesarean section.  Why D wrong: Vacuum extraction is not the indicated management; Cesarean section is."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"When the fetal head is completely extended, the presenting part is the:","options":["Vertex","Brow","Face","Occiput"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a completely extended head presents by the face.  Why A wrong: Vertex is the presenting part with full flexion.  Why B wrong: Brow results from partial extension, not complete extension.  Why D wrong: The occiput is the denominator of vertex presentation, not the presenting part in complete extension."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The \"attitude of the fetus\" refers to the relationship of:","options":["The fetus to the maternal pelvis","Different parts of the fetus to each other","The fetal long axis to the maternal long axis","The presenting part to the internal os"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define attitude of the fetus as the relationship of different parts of the fetus to each other.  Why A wrong: The relationship of the fetus/presenting part to the maternal pelvis is position, not attitude.  Why C wrong: The relationship of fetal to maternal long axis is lie, not attitude.  Why D wrong: The part over the internal os is the presenting part, not attitude."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The most common attitude of the fetus is:","options":["Extension","Flexion","Deflexion","Neutral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common attitude of the fetus is flexion.  Why A wrong: Extension is an abnormal, less common attitude.  Why C wrong: Deflexion is a less-flexed variant, not the most common attitude.  Why D wrong: \"Neutral\" is not the term used; flexion is the most common attitude."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The presenting part of the fetus depends on the:","options":["Maternal blood pressure","Attitude of the fetus","Placental position","Amount of liquor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the presenting part depends on the attitude of the fetus.  Why A wrong: Maternal blood pressure does not determine the presenting part.  Why C wrong: Placental position relates to previa, not the presenting part determined by attitude.  Why D wrong: Liquor volume is not listed as the determinant of the presenting part."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the diagram of fetal attitude, position \"A\" (complete flexion) and \"B\" (deflexed head) both have which presenting part?","options":["Face","Brow","Vertex","Sacrum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in both A (complete flexion) and B (deflexed head), the presenting part is the vertex.  Why A wrong: Face presentation occurs with complete extension, not in either A or B.  Why B wrong: Brow presentation occurs with partial extension, not in A or B.  Why D wrong: Sacrum is the denominator of breech, not the presenting part of these flexed heads."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the fetal attitude diagram, \"B\" represents which head position?","options":["Complete flexion","Deflexed head","Fully extended head","Partially extended head"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label B as the deflexed head.  Why A wrong: Complete flexion is labelled A, not B.  Why C wrong: A fully extended head gives a face presentation and is not what B depicts.  Why D wrong: Partial extension gives brow; B is specifically a deflexed head still presenting by vertex."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"Under the \"Flexed\" category, the notes list the two subtypes as:","options":["Complete flexion and deflexed head","Brow and face","Vertex and sinciput","Occiput anterior and occiput posterior"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the flexed subtypes as (a) complete flexion and (b) deflexed head.  Why B wrong: Brow and face are extension presentations, not the flexed subtypes.  Why C wrong: Vertex/sinciput are presenting parts/regions, not the two flexed subtypes listed.  Why D wrong: Occiput anterior/posterior are positions, not flexion subtypes."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"When the fetal head is only slightly extended, the presenting part is the:","options":["Face","Vertex","Brow","Occiput"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the presenting part in a slightly extended head is the brow.  Why A wrong: Face is the presenting part when the head is fully extended.  Why B wrong: Vertex presents with flexion, not extension.  Why D wrong: Occiput is a bony landmark (denominator), not the presenting part with slight extension."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"When the fetal head is fully extended, the presenting part is the:","options":["Brow","Face","Vertex","Sinciput"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the presenting part in a fully extended head is the face.  Why A wrong: Brow presents with slight/partial extension, not full extension.  Why B is correct as above.  Why C wrong: Vertex presents with flexion.  Why D wrong: Sinciput is not the presenting part of a fully extended head; the face is."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The \"denominator\" in obstetrics is defined as:","options":["The soft tissue over the presenting part","A bony point of reference on the presenting part used to describe fetal position relative to the maternal pelvis","The lowest point of the birth canal","The line joining the two ischial spines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define denominator as a bony point of reference on the presenting part used to describe the position of the fetus and comes in relationship to the maternal pelvis.  Why A wrong: The denominator is a bony point, not soft tissue.  Why C wrong: The lowest point of the birth canal is not the definition of denominator.  Why D wrong: The interspinous line relates to station/pelvic measurement, not the denominator."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The denominator for a vertex presentation is the:","options":["Sacrum","Occiput","Mentum","Frontal eminence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table pairs vertex presentation with the occiput as its denominator.  Why A wrong: Sacrum is the denominator for breech.  Why C wrong: Mentum (chin) is the denominator for face.  Why D wrong: Frontal eminence/bone is the denominator for brow."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The denominator for a breech presentation is the:","options":["Occiput","Sacrum","Mentum","Frontal bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table pairs breech presentation with the sacrum as its denominator.  Why A wrong: Occiput is the denominator for vertex.  Why C wrong: Mentum is the denominator for face.  Why D wrong: Frontal bone is the denominator for brow."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The denominator for a brow presentation is the:","options":["Mentum","Occiput","Frontal eminence / frontal bone","Sacrum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table pairs brow presentation with the frontal eminence/bone as denominator.  Why A wrong: Mentum is the denominator for face.  Why B wrong: Occiput is the denominator for vertex.  Why D wrong: Sacrum is the denominator for breech."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The denominator for a face presentation is the:","options":["Frontal eminence","Mentum (chin)","Occiput","Sacrum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table pairs face presentation with the mentum (chin) as its denominator.  Why A wrong: Frontal eminence is the denominator for brow.  Why C wrong: Occiput is the denominator for vertex.  Why D wrong: Sacrum is the denominator for breech."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The denominator is used in relationship to which structure to describe fetal position?","options":["Maternal pelvis","Fetal spine","Placenta","Amniotic sac"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the denominator comes in relationship to the maternal pelvis to describe fetal position.  Why B wrong: The fetal spine relates to lie, not the reference for the denominator's position.  Why C wrong: The placenta is not the reference used for the denominator.  Why D wrong: The amniotic sac is not the reference for describing position by denominator."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the vertex-position clock diagram, position (1) LOT stands for:","options":["Left occipito transverse","Left occipito anterior","Right occipito transverse","Left occipito posterior"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label position 1 as LOT = left occipito transverse.  Why B wrong: Left occipito anterior is LOA (position 2).  Why C wrong: Right occipito transverse is ROT (position 5).  Why D wrong: Left occipito posterior is LOP (position 8)."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the position clock diagram, position (2) LOA stands for:","options":["Left occipito transverse","Left occipito anterior","Right occipito anterior","Left occipito posterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label position 2 as LOA = left occipito anterior.  Why A wrong: LOT (left occipito transverse) is position 1.  Why C wrong: Right occipito anterior is ROA (position 4).  Why D wrong: LOP (left occipito posterior) is position 8."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the position clock diagram, position (4) ROA stands for:","options":["Right occipito anterior","Left occipito anterior","Right occipito transverse","Right occipito posterior"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label position 4 as ROA (right occipito anterior).  Why B wrong: Left occipito anterior is LOA (position 2).  Why C wrong: Right occipito transverse is ROT (position 5).  Why D wrong: Right occipito posterior is ROP (position 6)."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the position clock diagram, position (5) ROT stands for:","options":["Right occipito anterior","Right occipito transverse","Left occipito transverse","Right occipito posterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label position 5 as ROT (right occipito transverse).  Why A wrong: Right occipito anterior is ROA (position 4).  Why C wrong: Left occipito transverse is LOT (position 1).  Why D wrong: Right occipito posterior is ROP (position 6)."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the position clock diagram, position (6) ROP stands for:","options":["Right occipito posterior","Left occipito posterior","Right occipito transverse","Right occipito anterior"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label position 6 as ROP (right occipito posterior).  Why B wrong: Left occipito posterior is LOP (position 8).  Why C wrong: Right occipito transverse is ROT (position 5).  Why D wrong: Right occipito anterior is ROA (position 4)."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the position clock diagram, position (8) LOP stands for:","options":["Left occipito posterior","Right occipito posterior","Left occipito transverse","Left occipito anterior"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label position 8 as LOP (left occipito posterior).  Why B wrong: Right occipito posterior is ROP (position 6).  Why C wrong: Left occipito transverse is LOT (position 1).  Why D wrong: Left occipito anterior is LOA (position 2)."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the position clock diagram, position (7) at the top is labelled:","options":["Occiput anterior","Occiput posterior","Left occipito transverse","Right occipito anterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label position 7 (top of the diagram) as occiput posterior.  Why A wrong: Occiput anterior is position 3 (bottom of the diagram).  Why C wrong: LOT is position 1.  Why D wrong: ROA is position 4."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the position clock diagram, position (3) is labelled:","options":["Occiput posterior","Occiput anterior","Right occipito anterior","Left occipito anterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label position 3 (bottom) as occiput anterior.  Why A wrong: Occiput posterior is position 7 (top).  Why C wrong: ROA is position 4.  Why D wrong: LOA is position 2."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"On the position wheel, the direction facing the pubic symphysis (anterior) corresponds to which reference?","options":["Anterior (pubic symphysis)","Posterior (sacral promontory)","Left transverse","Right transverse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the pubic symphysis side as \"Ant (pubic symphysis).\"  Why B wrong: The sacral promontory marks the posterior reference, not anterior.  Why C/D wrong: Transverse refers to the side directions, not the anterior pubic symphysis reference."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"On the position wheel, the posterior reference point is the:","options":["Pubic symphysis","Sacral promontory","Ischial spine","Iliac crest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the posterior side as \"Post (sacral promontory).\"  Why A wrong: The pubic symphysis is the anterior reference.  Why C wrong: The ischial spine is used for station, not as the posterior wheel reference.  Why D wrong: The iliac crest is not the labelled posterior reference."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"According to the notes, when the fetus is in any position from 1 to 5, the delivery is called:","options":["Occipito posterior delivery","Normal delivery","Cesarean delivery","Instrumental delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when the fetus is in any position 1 to 5, it is called a normal delivery.  Why A wrong: Occipito posterior delivery corresponds to positions 6 to 8.  Why C wrong: Cesarean is not the classification given for positions 1–5.  Why D wrong: Instrumental delivery is not the term used for positions 1–5."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"For a \"normal delivery\" (positions 1 to 5), the lie of the fetus is:","options":["Transverse","Oblique","Longitudinal","Unstable"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state normal delivery means lie = longitudinal.  Why A wrong: A transverse lie cannot deliver vaginally as a normal delivery.  Why B wrong: Oblique lie is unstable and not the lie for normal delivery.  Why D wrong: \"Unstable\" lie is not the described lie; it is longitudinal."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"For a normal delivery (positions 1 to 5), the presentation is:","options":["Breech","Cephalic","Shoulder","Compound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state normal delivery means presentation = cephalic.  Why A wrong: Breech is not the presentation for the described normal delivery.  Why C wrong: Shoulder presentation is abnormal and not deliverable normally.  Why D wrong: Compound presentation is not the normal delivery presentation described."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"For a normal delivery (positions 1 to 5), the presenting part is the:","options":["Vertex","Brow","Face","Sacrum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state normal delivery means presenting part = vertex.  Why B wrong: Brow presents in partial extension and requires Cesarean, not normal delivery.  Why C wrong: Face presentation is a fully extended abnormal presentation.  Why D wrong: Sacrum is the denominator of breech, not the normal presenting part."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"When vaginal delivery occurs with the fetus in positions 6 to 8, it is termed:","options":["Normal delivery","Occipito posterior delivery","Face-to-pubis delivery","Breech delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that vaginal delivery in positions 6 to 8 is an occipito posterior delivery.  Why A wrong: Normal delivery applies to positions 1 to 5.  Why C wrong: \"Face-to-pubis\" is not the term used in the notes for positions 6–8.  Why D wrong: Breech is a non-vertex presentation; these positions are still vertex/occipital."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In an occipito posterior delivery, the position of the fetus corresponds to which numbers?","options":["1, 2, 3","4, 5, 6","6, 7, 8","1 to 5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state occipito posterior delivery corresponds to positions 6, 7, 8.  Why A wrong: Positions 1–3 are within the normal-delivery range (1 to 5).  Why B wrong: Only 6 (of 4,5,6) is in the posterior group; 4 and 5 are in the normal 1–5 group.  Why D wrong: Positions 1 to 5 correspond to normal delivery, not occipito posterior."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In an occipito posterior delivery, the lie, presentation and presenting part are respectively:","options":["Transverse, breech, sacrum","Longitudinal, cephalic, vertex","Oblique, cephalic, face","Longitudinal, breech, vertex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state occipito posterior delivery means lie = longitudinal, presentation = cephalic, presenting part = vertex.  Why A wrong: These are the features of breech, not occipito posterior vertex.  Why C wrong: The lie is longitudinal (not oblique) and presenting part is vertex (not face).  Why D wrong: The presentation is cephalic, not breech."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The most common fetal position during labour is:","options":["Right occipito posterior","Left occipito transverse","Left occipito anterior","Direct occipito anterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common fetal position during labour is left occipito transverse.  Why A wrong: Right occipito posterior is the most common occipito-posterior position, not the overall most common.  Why C wrong: Left occipito anterior is the \"2nd best,\" not the most common.  Why D wrong: Direct occipito anterior is not listed as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"According to the notes, the \"2nd best\" (second most common) fetal position during labour is:","options":["Right occipito posterior","Left occipito anterior","Left occipito transverse","Right occipito transverse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list left occipito anterior as the 2nd best position.  Why A wrong: Right occipito posterior is the commonest OP position, not the 2nd best overall.  Why C wrong: Left occipito transverse is the most common (1st), not 2nd.  Why D wrong: Right occipito transverse is not listed as 2nd best."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The most common occipito-anterior position is:","options":["Right occipito anterior","Left occipito anterior","Direct occipito anterior","Left occipito transverse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common occipito-anterior position is left occipito anterior.  Why A wrong: Right occipito anterior is not the most common OA position.  Why C wrong: Direct occipito anterior is not the commonest OA position named.  Why D wrong: Left occipito transverse is a transverse position, not an occipito-anterior one."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The most common occipito-posterior position is:","options":["Left occipito posterior","Right occipito posterior","Direct occipito posterior","Right occipito transverse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common occipito-posterior position is right occipito posterior.  Why A wrong: Left occipito posterior is not the commonest OP position.  Why C wrong: Direct occipito posterior is not named as the most common OP position.  Why D wrong: Right occipito transverse is a transverse, not a posterior, position."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"When recognising fetal position on an image, the first step is to check whether the occiput is facing towards the pubic symphysis or towards the:","options":["Ischial spine","Sacral promontory","Iliac crest","Coccyx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state step 1 is to check if the occiput faces the pubic symphysis (OA) or the sacral promontory (OP).  Why A wrong: The ischial spine is a station landmark, not the first-step reference here.  Why C wrong: The iliac crest is not used in this recognition step.  Why D wrong: The coccyx is not the posterior reference in this step; the sacral promontory is."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"If the occiput is facing towards the pubic symphysis, the position is:","options":["Occipito posterior","Occipito anterior","Occipito transverse","Mento anterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if the occiput faces the pubic symphysis, it is an occipito anterior position.  Why A wrong: Occipito posterior occurs when the occiput faces the sacral promontory.  Why C wrong: Transverse occurs when the occiput points to the side, not toward the symphysis.  Why D wrong: Mento anterior is a face-presentation position, not relevant here."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"If the occiput is facing towards the sacral promontory, the position is:","options":["Occipito anterior","Occipito posterior","Occipito transverse","Direct occipito anterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the occiput faces the sacral promontory (posteriorly), it is an occipito posterior position. (The source text mistakenly repeats \"occipito anterior\" here; the factually correct answer, consistent with step 1 and the \"Remember\" points on the same page, is occipito posterior.)  Why A wrong: Occipito anterior is when the occiput faces the pubic symphysis, not the sacral promontory.  Why C wrong: Transverse is a lateral-facing occiput, not sacral-facing.  Why D wrong: Direct occipito anterior again means the occiput faces the symphysis, not the sacral promontory."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the image-recognition rule, if the occiput points towards your (examiner's) right hand side, the occiput is on which side of the mother?","options":["Right side","Left side","Anterior","Posterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if the occiput is towards your right hand side, it will be on the left side of the mother.  Why A wrong: The examiner's right corresponds to the mother's left, not right (mirror-image).  Why C/D wrong: Anterior/posterior are determined by symphysis/sacrum, not by the examiner's right/left hand."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"In the image-recognition rule, if the occiput points towards your (examiner's) left side, the occiput is on which side of the mother?","options":["Left side","Right side","Anterior","Midline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if the occiput is towards your left side, it will be on the right side of the mother.  Why A wrong: The examiner's left corresponds to the mother's right, not left.  Why C wrong: Anterior is determined by the pubic symphysis reference, not left/right hand.  Why D wrong: There is no midline position described; it is the mother's right."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The posterior fontanelle is described as being what shape?","options":["Diamond (◇, four-sided)","Triangular (Δ)","Circular","Oval"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the posterior fontanelle is 'Δ' (triangular) in shape.  Why A wrong: The diamond/four-sided shape describes the anterior fontanelle, not the posterior.  Why C wrong: The posterior fontanelle is not circular.  Why D wrong: The posterior fontanelle is triangular, not oval."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The posterior fontanelle lies closer to which bone?","options":["Frontal bone","Occipital bone","Parietal bone","Temporal bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the posterior fontanelle lies closer to the occipital bone.  Why A wrong: The anterior fontanelle lies near the frontal bones, not the posterior fontanelle.  Why C wrong: While parietal bones border it, the notes specifically say it lies closer to the occipital bone.  Why D wrong: The temporal bone is not the landmark for the posterior fontanelle."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"According to the rules, wherever the triangular (Δ) posterior fontanelle is facing, the occiput is:","options":["On the opposite maternal side","Towards that maternal side","Always anterior","Always posterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that wherever 'Δ' is facing, the occiput is towards that maternal side.  Why A wrong: The occiput is toward, not opposite to, the direction the Δ faces.  Why C wrong: It is not always anterior; direction depends on where the Δ faces.  Why D wrong: It is not always posterior; it follows the Δ's facing."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"If the triangular posterior fontanelle (Δ) is facing anteriorly, the position is:","options":["Occipito posterior","Occipito anterior","Occipito transverse","Mento posterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if 'Δ' is facing anteriorly, it is occipito anterior.  Why A wrong: Occipito posterior is when the Δ faces posteriorly.  Why C wrong: Transverse is when the Δ faces laterally.  Why D wrong: Mento posterior applies to a face presentation, not this rule."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"If the triangular posterior fontanelle (Δ) is facing posteriorly, the position is:","options":["Occipito anterior","Occipito posterior","Occipito transverse","Direct occipito anterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if the 'Δ' is facing posteriorly, it is occipito posterior.  Why A wrong: Occipito anterior is when the Δ faces anteriorly.  Why C wrong: Transverse is a lateral-facing Δ.  Why D wrong: Direct occipito anterior again means anterior facing, not posterior."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"If the triangular fontanelle (Δ) is toward the examiner's left hand side, the occiput/position is on which maternal side?","options":["Mother's left hand side","Mother's right hand side","Midline anterior","Midline posterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if 'Δ' is toward your left hand side = mother's right hand side.  Why A wrong: The examiner's left corresponds to the mother's right (mirror), not the mother's left.  Why C/D wrong: Midline anterior/posterior are not determined by the left/right hand rule."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"If the triangular fontanelle (Δ) is toward the examiner's right hand side, the position is on which maternal side?","options":["Mother's right hand side","Mother's left hand side","Anterior","Posterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if 'Δ' is toward your right hand side = mother's left hand side.  Why A wrong: The examiner's right corresponds to the mother's left (mirror), not the mother's right.  Why C/D wrong: Anterior/posterior are set by symphysis/sacrum facing, not by the right/left hand rule."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"The Leopold maneuver is performed by which route of examination?","options":["Per vaginally","Per abdominally","Per rectally","By ultrasound only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the Leopold maneuver is done per abdominally.  Why A wrong: It is an abdominal palpation technique, not a vaginal examination.  Why C wrong: It is not a rectal examination.  Why D wrong: It is a manual abdominal palpation, not an ultrasound procedure."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"Before performing the Leopold maneuver, the patient's bladder should be:","options":["Full","Empty","Half-full","Catheterised and clamped"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the patient's bladder should be empty for the Leopold maneuver.  Why A wrong: A full bladder interferes with palpation and is not recommended.  Why C wrong: The notes specify empty, not half-full.  Why D wrong: The requirement is an empty bladder, not a clamped catheter."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"During the Leopold maneuver, the recommended patient position is:","options":["Left lateral position","Dorsal position with knees flexed","Standing upright","Knee-chest position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the patient should be lying in the dorsal position with knees flexed.  Why A wrong: Left lateral is not the position specified for the Leopold maneuver.  Why C wrong: Standing is not appropriate for abdominal palpation of fetal parts.  Why D wrong: Knee-chest position is used for other purposes, not the Leopold maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 056 · Compound presentation…","question":"A fetus is in the LOA position. Based on the terminology, the denominator (occiput) is located in the mother's:","options":["Left anterior quadrant of the pelvis","Right anterior quadrant of the pelvis","Left posterior quadrant of the pelvis","Directly posterior"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: LOA = left occipito anterior, meaning the occiput lies in the left anterior quadrant of the maternal pelvis.  Why B wrong: Right anterior would be ROA, not LOA.  Why C wrong: Left posterior would be LOP, not LOA.  Why D wrong: Directly posterior is direct occipito posterior, not LOA."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"While performing Leopold's maneuvers, on which side of the patient should the examiner always stand?","options":["Left hand side","Right hand side","At the head end","At the foot end"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the examiner should always stand on the right hand side of the patient.  Why A wrong: The left side is not the standard examining position stated.  Why C wrong: Standing at the head end is not described for Leopold's maneuvers.  Why D wrong: The examiner stands at the side, not the foot end, though facing direction changes by maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"For the first three Leopold maneuvers (1, 2, 3), the examiner should face:","options":["Towards the feet of the patient","Towards the face of the patient","Away from the patient","Towards the left side of the patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the 1st three maneuvers the examiner should face towards the face of the patient.  Why A wrong: Facing the feet is only for the 4th maneuver.  Why C wrong: The examiner faces a defined direction, not away.  Why D wrong: The reference direction is head/feet of patient, not a side."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"For the fourth Leopold maneuver, the examiner faces:","options":["Towards the face of the patient","Towards the feet of the patient","Towards the right side","Towards the head end"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the 4th maneuver the examiner faces towards the feet of the patient.  Why A wrong: Facing the face is for maneuvers 1–3.  Why C/D wrong: The described reference is the feet of the patient, not a side or the head."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The first Leopold maneuver is also called:","options":["Lateral grip","Pawlik grip","Fundal grip","Deep pelvic grip"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The first maneuver is called the \"FUNDAL GRIP\".  Why A wrong: Lateral grip is the second maneuver.  Why B wrong: Pawlik grip is the third maneuver.  Why D wrong: Deep pelvic grip is the fourth maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The first (fundal) grip primarily tells the examiner about which two findings?","options":["Position and engagement","Lie and presentation","Attitude and station","Cervical dilatation and effacement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fundal grip tells about the lie and the presentation.  Why A wrong: Position is determined by the lateral grip; engagement by the 3rd/4th grips.  Why C wrong: Attitude is assessed by the 4th maneuver; station is a vaginal-exam finding.  Why D wrong: Cervical dilatation/effacement are assessed on vaginal examination, not by fundal palpation."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"During the fundal grip, if the examiner feels something (a fetal pole) in the fundus, the lie is:","options":["Transverse lie","Oblique lie","Longitudinal lie","Unstable lie"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If something is felt in the first grip, it is a longitudinal lie.  Why A wrong: Transverse lie is indicated when the 1st grip (fundus) is empty.  Why B/D wrong: Oblique and unstable lie are not the findings described for a palpable fundal pole."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"On the fundal grip, if the first grip (fundus) is found empty, the lie is:","options":["Longitudinal lie","Transverse lie","Cephalic","Breech"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the 1st grip is empty, it indicates a transverse lie.  Why A wrong: A longitudinal lie has a palpable pole in the fundus.  Why C/D wrong: Cephalic and breech are presentations, not lies."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"On the fundal grip, palpating a soft, broad and irregular part in the fundus indicates the fundal pole is the:","options":["Head","Buttocks","Shoulder","Feet only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A soft, broad and irregular part felt in the fundus indicates the buttocks.  Why A wrong: The head is hard, round and globular, not soft/broad/irregular.  Why C/D wrong: Shoulder or feet are not the parts identified by these characteristics in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"If the fundus contains the soft, broad, irregular buttocks, the presentation is:","options":["Breech presentation","Cephalic presentation","Shoulder presentation","Face presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When buttocks are felt in the fundus, it means a cephalic presentation (the head lies in the lower pole/pelvis).  Why A wrong: Buttocks in the fundus indicate the head is presenting, i.e., cephalic, not breech.  Why C/D wrong: Shoulder and face presentations are not indicated by fundal buttocks."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The second Leopold maneuver is also called:","options":["Fundal grip","Lateral grip","Pawlik grip","Deep pelvic grip"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second maneuver is called the lateral grip.  Why A wrong: Fundal grip is the first maneuver.  Why C wrong: Pawlik grip is the third maneuver.  Why D wrong: Deep pelvic grip is the fourth maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The second (lateral) grip primarily tells the examiner about:","options":["Lie of the fetus","Position of the fetus","Engagement of the head","Attitude of the fetus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lateral grip tells us the position of the fetus.  Why A wrong: Lie is determined by the fundal grip.  Why C wrong: Engagement is assessed by the 3rd/4th grips.  Why D wrong: Attitude is assessed by the 4th maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"On the lateral grip, the fetal back is felt as:","options":["Small multiple knob-like structures","A board-like feeling that is smooth, regular and curved","A hard, round, globular structure","Soft, broad and irregular"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The back of the fetus gives a board-like feeling that is smooth, regular and curved.  Why A wrong: Small multiple knob-like structures are the limbs.  Why C wrong: Hard, round, globular is the head (felt on Pawlik grip).  Why D wrong: Soft, broad, irregular describes the buttocks."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"On the lateral grip, small multiple knob-like structures are felt on one side; these represent the:","options":["Fetal back","Fetal head","Fetal limbs","Fetal buttocks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The limbs are felt as small multiple knob-like structures.  Why A wrong: The back gives a smooth, board-like, curved feeling.  Why B wrong: The head is hard, round and globular.  Why D wrong: The buttocks are soft, broad and irregular."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"On the lateral grip, if the fetal back is felt on the left side, the position is one of:","options":["ROA, ROT, ROP","LOA, LOT, LOP","LSA, RSA","LMA, RMA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the back is on the left side, the position is LOA, LOT or LOP (left occipito-anterior/transverse/posterior).  Why A wrong: ROA/ROT/ROP occur when the back is on the right side.  Why C wrong: LSA/RSA are sacral (breech) positions not described here.  Why D wrong: LMA/RMA are mento (face) positions not described here."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"On the lateral grip, if the fetal back is felt on the right side, the position is one of:","options":["LOA, LOT, LOP","ROA, ROT, ROP","Transverse lie","Breech presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the back is on the right side, the position is ROA, ROT or ROP.  Why A wrong: LOA/LOT/LOP occur when the back is on the left side.  Why C/D wrong: These describe lie and presentation, not position derived from the lateral grip."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The third Leopold maneuver is also called:","options":["Fundal grip","Lateral grip","Pawlik grip","Deep pelvic grip"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The third maneuver is called the Pawlik grip.  Why A wrong: Fundal grip is the first maneuver.  Why B wrong: Lateral grip is the second maneuver.  Why D wrong: Deep pelvic grip is the fourth maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The Pawlik grip (third maneuver) is performed using:","options":["Both hands facing the feet","One hand","Two hands parallel to the inguinal ligament","The fingertips of both hands in the fundus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Pawlik grip is done with one hand.  Why A/C wrong: Using both hands (parallel to the inguinal ligament, facing the feet) describes the fourth/deep pelvic grip.  Why D wrong: Fundal palpation with both hands is the first maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"On the Pawlik grip, a cephalic presentation is felt as a structure that is:","options":["Soft, broad and irregular","Board-like, smooth and curved","Hard, round and globular","Multiple knob-like"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In a cephalic presentation the presenting head is felt as a hard, round, globular structure on the Pawlik grip.  Why A wrong: Soft, broad, irregular describes the buttocks.  Why B wrong: Board-like, smooth, curved describes the fetal back.  Why D wrong: Multiple knob-like structures are the limbs."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"On the Pawlik grip, if the baby's head can be moved from side to side, it is described as:","options":["Ballotable","Not ballotable","Fixed","Engaged"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the head can be moved from side to side, it is ballotable.  Why B/C wrong: A head that cannot be moved is \"not ballotable\" / fixed.  Why D wrong: Engaged corresponds to a fixed, non-ballotable head that has entered the pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"If the fetal head is ballotable (can be moved from side to side) on the Pawlik grip, it indicates the head:","options":["Has entered the pelvis","Has not entered the pelvis","Is fixed","Is deeply engaged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If it can be moved, it has not entered the pelvis.  Why A/C/D wrong: A head that has entered the pelvis is fixed/engaged and is not ballotable."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"On the Pawlik grip, a head that cannot be moved from side to side (not ballotable) means the head:","options":["Has entered the pelvis (fixed)","Has not entered the pelvis","Is in transverse lie","Is presenting as buttocks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Not ballotable means the head has entered the pelvis (fixed).  Why B wrong: A ballotable/movable head has not entered the pelvis.  Why C/D wrong: These describe lie and presentation, not engagement."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The fourth Leopold maneuver is also called:","options":["Pawlik grip","Lateral grip","Deep pelvic grip","Fundal grip"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The fourth maneuver is called the deep pelvic grip.  Why A wrong: Pawlik grip is the third maneuver.  Why B wrong: Lateral grip is the second maneuver.  Why D wrong: Fundal grip is the first maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"While performing the deep pelvic grip (4th maneuver), the examiner should be:","options":["Facing the face of the patient","Facing the feet of the patient","Standing at the head end","Standing on the left side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The deep pelvic grip is done by facing the feet of the patient.  Why A wrong: Facing the face is done for maneuvers 1–3.  Why C/D wrong: Position is at the side facing the feet, not the head end or a fixed left side."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In the deep pelvic grip, how should the examiner's hands be placed?","options":["Cupped over the fundus","Parallel to the inguinal ligament","On one side only","Perpendicular to the midline over the umbilicus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the 4th maneuver the hands are kept parallel to the inguinal ligament.  Why A wrong: Cupping the fundus is the first maneuver.  Why C wrong: One-hand palpation is the Pawlik grip.  Why D wrong: This hand placement is not described."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In the deep pelvic grip, if the hands converge below the head of the baby, it means:","options":["Head has entered the pelvis","Head has not entered the pelvis","The lie is transverse","Breech presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the hands are converging below the head, the head has not entered the pelvis.  Why A wrong: Diverging hands indicate the head has entered the pelvis.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated lie/presentation findings."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In the deep pelvic grip, if the examiner's hands diverge, it indicates the head:","options":["Has not entered the pelvis","Has entered the pelvis","Is ballotable","Is in the fundus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If hands diverge, the head has entered the pelvis.  Why A wrong: Converging hands indicate the head has not entered the pelvis.  Why C wrong: A ballotable head has not entered the pelvis.  Why D wrong: The head being palpated in the lower pole indicates a cephalic presenting part, not one in the fundus."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The Leopold 4th maneuver confirms the finding of which grip?","options":["Fundal grip","Lateral grip","Pawlik grip","None of the maneuvers"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 4th maneuver confirms the finding of the Pawlik grip (3rd maneuver).  Why A wrong: The fundal grip finding (lie/presentation) is not what the 4th confirms.  Why B wrong: The lateral grip gives position, not what the 4th confirms.  Why D wrong: It specifically confirms the Pawlik grip."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"Besides confirming presentation and engagement, the Leopold 4th maneuver also tells about:","options":["The lie of the fetus","The attitude of the fetus","The position of the fetal back","Cervical effacement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 4th maneuver also tells about the attitude of the fetus.  Why A wrong: Lie is from the fundal grip.  Why C wrong: Position of the back is from the lateral grip.  Why D wrong: Cervical effacement is a vaginal-examination finding."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In the 4th maneuver, \"hands converging\" corresponds to which conclusion?","options":["Head has gone into the pelvis","Head has not gone into the pelvis","Head is deeply engaged","Transverse lie confirmed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hands converging means the head has not gone into the pelvis.  Why A/C wrong: Head gone in/engaged corresponds to diverging hands.  Why D wrong: Convergence relates to engagement, not lie."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In the 4th maneuver, \"hands diverging\" corresponds to which conclusion?","options":["Head has entered the pelvis","Head has not entered the pelvis","Fetus is in breech presentation","Head is ballotable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diverging hands mean the head has entered the pelvis.  Why B/D wrong: A head not in the pelvis is ballotable and gives converging hands.  Why C wrong: This finding concerns engagement, not presentation type."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"During pregnancy, the uterus grows in size due to which two processes?","options":["Metaplasia and dysplasia","Hypertrophy and hyperplasia","Atrophy and involution","Necrosis and apoptosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The uterus grows in size by hypertrophy (enlargement of cells) and hyperplasia (increase in cell number).  Why A wrong: Metaplasia/dysplasia are not the described growth mechanisms.  Why C wrong: Atrophy and involution are shrinkage processes (involution occurs postpartum).  Why D wrong: Necrosis and apoptosis are cell-death processes, not growth."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The resting/quiescent state of the uterus during pregnancy is mainly due to which hormone?","options":["Estrogen","Oxytocin","Progesterone","Prostaglandin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The uterus is kept quiescent (resting) due to progesterone.  Why A wrong: Estrogen predominates in labour and stimulates contraction.  Why B wrong: Oxytocin stimulates uterine contraction, not quiescence.  Why D wrong: Prostaglandins promote contraction/cervical ripening."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"Progesterone maintains uterine quiescence because it acts as a:","options":["Smooth muscle stimulant","Smooth muscle relaxant","Vasoconstrictor","Prostaglandin synthase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone is a smooth muscle relaxant, keeping the uterus at rest.  Why A wrong: It relaxes rather than stimulates smooth muscle.  Why C wrong: Vasoconstriction is not its described action here.  Why D wrong: It is not described as an enzyme."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"Pregnancy is described as a hormone-predominant condition dominated by:","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","Oxytocin","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pregnancy is a progesterone-predominant condition.  Why A wrong: Estrogen predominance characterizes labour.  Why C/D wrong: Oxytocin and prolactin are not the predominant hormones described for maintaining pregnancy here."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"Labour is described as a hormone-predominant condition dominated by:","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Relaxin","hCG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Labour is an estrogen-predominant condition.  Why A wrong: Progesterone predominance is for pregnancy maintenance.  Why C/D wrong: Relaxin and hCG are not the predominant hormones described for labour."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"According to the physiology of labour, what matures at term to initiate the labour cascade?","options":["Maternal hypothalamic-pituitary axis","Fetal hypothalamic-pituitary axis","Placental villi","Fetal renal system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At term the fetal hypothalamic-pituitary axis matures, initiating labour physiology.  Why A wrong: It is the fetal, not maternal, axis that matures to trigger labour.  Why C/D wrong: These are not described as the maturing trigger."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In the labour cascade, the fetal hypothalamus secretes which hormone?","options":["ACTH","Corticotropin releasing hormone (CRH)","DHEAS","Estrogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fetal hypothalamus produces corticotropin releasing hormone (CRH). (The notes write \"Corticotropin Relaxing hormone\" but the correct term is corticotropin-releasing hormone.)  Why A wrong: ACTH is released by the fetal pituitary, downstream of CRH.  Why C wrong: DHEAS is produced by the fetal adrenal.  Why D wrong: Estrogen is produced by the placenta from DHEAS."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In the labour cascade, fetal CRH stimulates the fetal pituitary to secrete:","options":["DHEAS","ACTH","Progesterone","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fetal pituitary secretes ACTH in response to CRH.  Why A wrong: DHEAS comes from the fetal adrenal.  Why C wrong: Progesterone predominance is a pregnancy state, not this pituitary product.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is maternal/posterior pituitary, not part of this described chain."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"Fetal ACTH acts on which fetal organ in the labour cascade?","options":["Fetal liver","Fetal adrenal gland","Fetal thyroid","Fetal kidney"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ACTH acts on the fetal adrenal gland.  Why A/C/D wrong: The cascade specifically involves the fetal adrenal, not liver, thyroid, or kidney."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The main product of the fetal adrenal gland in the labour cascade is:","options":["Cortisol","DHEAS","Aldosterone","Estriol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The main product of the fetal adrenal is DHEAS (dehydroepiandrosterone sulphate).  Why A/C wrong: Cortisol and aldosterone are not the main product described in this cascade.  Why D wrong: Estriol is produced by the placenta from DHEAS, not by the adrenal directly."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In the labour cascade, DHEAS from the fetal adrenal is utilized by the placenta to produce:","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Prolactin","Relaxin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DHEAS is utilized by the placenta to produce estrogen.  Why A wrong: Progesterone dominates pregnancy, not this cascade endpoint.  Why C/D wrong: Prolactin and relaxin are not the described placental products of DHEAS."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The rise in estrogen at term produces which final effect in the labour cascade?","options":["Relaxes the uterus","Stimulates the uterus to contract → labour pains begin","Softens fetal bones","Suppresses oxytocin receptors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen stimulates the uterus to contract, and labour pains begin.  Why A wrong: Estrogen stimulates contraction; progesterone relaxes the uterus.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the described effects of estrogen at term."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In anencephaly, which structure fails to form, disrupting the labour cascade?","options":["Fetal adrenal glands","Fetal kidneys","Placenta","Fetal pituitary only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In anencephaly the fetal adrenal glands are not formed.  Why B/C wrong: Kidneys and placenta are not the missing structure described.  Why D wrong: The notes specify the fetal adrenal glands are not formed."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In anencephaly, absence of fetal adrenal glands leads to a decrease in DHEA-S, which in turn causes:","options":["Increased estrogen and preterm labour","Decreased estrogen and post-term pregnancy","Increased progesterone and abortion","No hormonal change"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In anencephaly, DHEA-S falls, estrogen falls, and this results in post-term pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Estrogen decreases (not increases), leading to post-term (not preterm).  Why C wrong: The described chain is a fall in DHEA-S and estrogen, not increased progesterone/abortion.  Why D wrong: There is a clear hormonal change (decreased DHEA-S and estrogen)."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"(PYQ Q1) To initiate labour, which hormone is required?","options":["Maternal oxytocin","Fetal CRH","Progesterone","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes' Q1 states fetal CRH is required to initiate labour.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin augments contractions but the initiating hormone stated is fetal CRH.  Why C wrong: Progesterone maintains quiescence, not initiation.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is for lactation, not labour initiation."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"(PYQ Q2) The main estrogen in pregnancy is:","options":["Estrone (E1)","Estradiol (E2)","Estriol (E3)","Estetrol (E4)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes' Q2 states the main estrogen in pregnancy is estradiol (E2).  Why A wrong: Estrone (E1) is not identified as the main estrogen here.  Why C wrong: Estriol (E3) is the most specific estrogen produced by the placenta, not the \"main\" one.  Why D wrong: Estetrol (E4) is not the answer given."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"(PYQ Q3) The most specific estrogen produced by the placenta is:","options":["Estradiol (E2)","Estrone (E1)","Estriol (E3)","Progesterone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes' Q3 states the most specific estrogen produced by the placenta is estriol (E3).  Why A wrong: Estradiol (E2) is the main estrogen of pregnancy, not the most specific placental one.  Why B wrong: Estrone (E1) is not the answer given.  Why D wrong: Progesterone is not an estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"Apart from estrogen, the placenta also produces which hormone that acts on the uterus and fetal membranes?","options":["Placental corticotropin releasing hormone (CRH)","Placental oxytocin","Placental prolactin","Placental insulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The placenta also produces placental corticotropin releasing hormone (CRH), which acts on the uterus and fetal membranes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the placental products described in this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In the decidua, placental CRH promotes production of which prostaglandin that increases uterine contractions?","options":["PGE2","PGF-2α","PGI2","Thromboxane A2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the decidua, PGF-2α is produced, which increases uterine contractions.  Why A wrong: PGE2 is released by fetal membranes and mainly softens (ripens) the cervix.  Why C/D wrong: PGI2 and thromboxane A2 are not the prostaglandins described here."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"During the onset of labour, which of the following receptors INCREASE on the uterus? (Select all that apply)","options":["Oxytocin receptors","Estrogen receptors","Stretch receptors","Progesterone receptors","Insulin receptors"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list increased oxytocin receptors, estrogen receptors and stretch receptors.  Why D wrong: Progesterone receptors DECREASE (down-regulated) at labour onset.  Why E wrong: Insulin receptors are not mentioned in this receptor change."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"At the onset of labour, which uterine receptors DECREASE (down-regulate)?","options":["Oxytocin receptors","Estrogen receptors","Progesterone receptors","Stretch receptors"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone receptors decrease at labour onset (progesterone withdrawal effect).  Why A/B/D wrong: Oxytocin, estrogen and stretch receptors increase, not decrease."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The fetal membranes, under the influence of placental CRH, release which prostaglandin?","options":["PGF-2α","PGE2","PGD2","PGI2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fetal membranes release PGE2.  Why A wrong: PGF-2α is produced by the decidua and increases contractions.  Why C/D wrong: PGD2 and PGI2 are not described in this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The main action of PGE2 released by the fetal membranes is to:","options":["Increase uterine contractions","Soften (ripen) the cervix","Relax the uterus","Stimulate milk let-down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PGE2 mainly softens the cervix (cervical ripening).  Why A wrong: Increasing contractions is chiefly the action of PGF-2α.  Why C wrong: PGE2 ripens the cervix rather than relaxing the uterus.  Why D wrong: Milk let-down is an oxytocin effect."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The PGE2-mediated softening of the cervix is specifically termed:","options":["Effacement","Ripening of cervix","Dilatation","Involution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The softening of the cervix by PGE2 is called ripening of the cervix.  Why A wrong: Effacement is the thinning/shortening of the cervix.  Why C wrong: Dilatation is the opening of the cervical os.  Why D wrong: Involution is the postpartum shrinking of the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"Ripening of the cervix is accompanied by which change in cervical water content?","options":["Increased water content of cervix","Decreased water content of cervix","No change in water content","Loss of all cervical fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ripening is associated with an increased water content of the cervix.  Why B/C/D wrong: The notes describe an increase, not a decrease, no change, or total loss."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"Ripening of the cervix (softening with increased water content) happens during which stage?","options":["Active labour","Pre-labour stage","Second stage of labour","Puerperium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cervical ripening happens in the pre-labour stage.  Why A/C wrong: It precedes active/second-stage labour.  Why D wrong: Puerperium is the postpartum period."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"Which of the following is the FIRST listed event of prelabor?","options":["Lightening","Ripening of the cervix (increased water content, cervix becomes soft)","Sudden burst of energy (nesting)","Weight loss of 1–3 pounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first prelabor event listed is ripening of the cervix with increased water content and softening.  Why A/C/D wrong: Lightening, nesting, and weight loss are later-listed prelabor events."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The prelabor event in which the fetal head descends down into the pelvis is called:","options":["Nesting","Lightening / welcome sign","Crowning","Engagement fixation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lightening (the \"welcome sign\") is when the head of the fetus goes down into the pelvis.  Why A wrong: Nesting is the sudden burst of energy before labour.  Why C wrong: Crowning is the appearance of the head at the vulva during delivery.  Why D wrong: This term is not used in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"As a result of lightening, the mother experiences relief from:","options":["Urinary frequency","Respiratory discomfort","Leg cramps","Backache"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With lightening the mother is relieved of respiratory discomfort (as the head descends, pressure on the diaphragm decreases).  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specifically state relief of respiratory discomfort, not these symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"Which type of labour pain does the patient feel during prelabor?","options":["True labour pain","False labour pain","Referred renal pain","No pain at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During prelabor the patient will feel false labour pain.  Why A wrong: True labour pain marks the onset of active labour, not prelabor.  Why C/D wrong: Renal pain and total absence of pain are not described."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The sudden burst of energy a mother experiences in prelabor is called:","options":["Nesting (Nestling)","Lightening","Quickening","Ripening"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sudden burst of energy is called nesting (the notes spell it \"NESTLING\"; the accepted term is \"nesting\").  Why B wrong: Lightening is descent of the head into the pelvis.  Why C wrong: Quickening is the first perception of fetal movements in mid-pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Ripening refers to cervical softening."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The sudden burst of energy (nesting) typically occurs how long before labour?","options":["1–2 hours before labour","12–24 hours before labour","24–48 hours before labour","1 week before labour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Nesting occurs 24–48 hours before labour.  Why A/B/D wrong: These time frames do not match the stated 24–48 hours."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"In prelabor, changes in progesterone and estrogen levels lead to fluid shifts, which cause:","options":["Weight gain of 1–3 pounds","Weight loss of 1–3 pounds","Weight loss of 5–10 pounds","No change in weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fluid shifts from changing progesterone and estrogen levels cause a weight loss of 1 to 3 pounds.  Why A wrong: The change is weight loss, not gain.  Why C wrong: The amount stated is 1–3 pounds, not 5–10.  Why D wrong: There is a defined 1–3 pound weight loss."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"The prelabor weight loss and fluid shifts are due to a change in the levels of which hormones?","options":["Oxytocin and prolactin","Progesterone and estrogen","Cortisol and aldosterone","Insulin and glucagon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fluid shifts (and resulting weight loss) are due to change in progesterone and estrogen level.  Why A/C/D wrong: These hormone pairs are not the cause described."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"A nurse palpates a term gravida: the fundus is empty (nothing felt) on the first grip. This most likely indicates:","options":["Longitudinal lie with cephalic presentation","Transverse lie","Breech presentation","Engaged fetal head"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An empty first (fundal) grip indicates a transverse lie.  Why A wrong: A longitudinal lie would have a palpable pole in the fundus.  Why C wrong: Breech presentation would have buttocks palpable in the fundus (longitudinal lie).  Why D wrong: Engagement is assessed by the 3rd/4th grips, not the empty-fundus finding."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"On lateral palpation a nurse feels a smooth, curved, board-like structure on the maternal right side and knob-like structures on the left. The fetal position is best described as one of:","options":["LOA / LOT / LOP","ROA / ROT / ROP","Transverse lie","Face presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A smooth board-like back on the right side indicates the back is on the right → ROA/ROT/ROP.  Why A wrong: LOA/LOT/LOP occur when the back is on the left.  Why C wrong: This describes position, not lie.  Why D wrong: Face presentation is not indicated by these palpation findings."},{"section":"Batch 057 · Leopold's maneuvers — examiner positioning…","question":"A term patient's fetal head cannot be balloted on the Pawlik grip, and on the 4th maneuver the examiner's hands diverge. The correct interpretation is that the head is:","options":["Floating and not engaged","Fixed and has entered the pelvis","In transverse lie","In the fundus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A non-ballotable head plus diverging hands both indicate the head has entered (engaged) the pelvis and is fixed.  Why A wrong: A floating, non-engaged head would be ballotable with converging hands.  Why C/D wrong: These findings relate to engagement, not lie or a fundal location."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"For normal labor to occur, how many \"P's\" are essential?","options":["2 P's","3 P's","4 P's","5 P's"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that for normal labor, 4 P's are needed — Pelvis/passage, Passenger, Push, and Psyche.  Why A wrong: Two P's is incomplete and not stated.  Why B wrong: Three P's is the reduced set used only if the question specifically asks for 3, not the full requirement.  Why D wrong: Five P's is not the number given in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Which of the following is the correct list of the 4 P's needed for normal labor?","options":["Pelvis, Passenger, Push, Psyche","Power, Passage, Position, Placenta","Pelvis, Placenta, Power, Psyche","Passenger, Position, Push, Pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the 4 P's as 1. Pelvis/passage (Normal), 2. Passenger (Proper Fetus), 3. Push (Uterine contraction), 4. Psyche (Mindset of Mother).  Why B wrong: Placenta is not one of the listed P's.  Why C wrong: Placenta and Power are not part of this specific list.  Why D wrong: Position is not one of the four listed P's."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"In the 4 P's of labor, \"Passenger\" refers to which of the following?","options":["Normal pelvis","Proper fetus","Uterine contraction","Mindset of mother"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define Passenger as the \"Proper Fetus.\"  Why A wrong: Normal pelvis is the \"Pelvis/passage\" P.  Why C wrong: Uterine contraction is the \"Push\" P.  Why D wrong: Mindset of mother is the \"Psyche\" P."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"In the 4 P's of labor, \"Push\" refers to which factor?","options":["Maternal bearing-down effort only","Uterine contraction","Pelvic passage","Psyche of mother"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly define Push as \"Uterine contraction.\"  Why A wrong: The notes equate Push specifically with uterine contraction, not maternal effort alone.  Why C wrong: Pelvic passage is the \"Pelvis\" P.  Why D wrong: Psyche is a separate P."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"\"Psyche,\" one of the 4 P's of labor, refers to which of the following?","options":["Fetal position","Uterine tone","Mindset of the mother","Pelvic diameters"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define Psyche as the \"Mindset of Mother.\"  Why A wrong: Fetal position is not the definition given.  Why B wrong: Uterine tone relates to the Push/contraction component.  Why D wrong: Pelvic diameters relate to the Pelvis/passage P."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"If a question asks for only 3 P's of labor (instead of 4), which are considered?","options":["The last three P's","The first three P's (Pelvis, Passenger, Push)","Only Push, Psyche, and Passage","Passenger, Psyche, and Pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"If 3 P's are asked = Then 1st (3),\" meaning the first three: Pelvis, Passenger, Push.  Why A wrong: It is the first three, not the last three.  Why C wrong: This combination excludes Passenger and includes Psyche, which is the 4th P.  Why D wrong: This set includes Psyche, which is dropped when only 3 P's are asked."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Where does uterine contraction during labor begin?","options":["At the fundus","At the cornua (angle of uterus)","At the lower uterine segment","At the cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state uterine contraction begins at the CORNUA of the uterus (angle of uterus).  Why A wrong: The fundus is where contraction is maximum, not where it begins.  Why C wrong: The lower uterine segment simply dilates and is not the origin.  Why D wrong: The cervix dilates; it is not the origin of contraction."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"At what speed does the uterine contraction spread downwards during labor?","options":["1 cm/sec","2 cm/sec","5 cm/sec","10 cm/sec"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the contraction spreads downwards at a speed of 2 cm/sec.  Why A wrong: 1 cm/sec understates the stated value.  Why C wrong: 5 cm/sec is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 10 cm/sec is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Uterine contraction during labor is maximum at which site?","options":["Cornua","Fundus","Lower uterine segment","Cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the contraction is maximum \"At FUNDUS.\"  Why A wrong: The cornua is where contraction begins, not where it is maximum.  Why C wrong: The lower uterine segment only dilates.  Why D wrong: The cervix dilates; it is not where contraction is maximal."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Which of the following are characteristics of normal uterine contraction? (Select all that apply)","options":["Coordinated manner (regular & rhythmic)","Leads to descent of fetal head","Leads to dilatation of cervix","Polarity","Continuous non-rhythmic pattern"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the characteristics as coordinated manner (regular & rhythmic), leading to descent of fetal head, leading to dilatation of cervix, and polarity.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly listed under \"Characteristic.\"  Why E wrong: A continuous non-rhythmic pattern is a feature of false labor, not a characteristic of normal coordinated uterine contraction."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"A \"coordinated manner\" of uterine contraction implies that contractions are:","options":["Irregular and continuous","Regular and rhythmic","Absent between the fundus and cervix","Only at the lower uterine segment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state coordinated manner leads to a \"Regular & Rhythmic\" pattern.  Why A wrong: Irregular/continuous describes false labor, not coordinated contraction.  Why C wrong: Contraction is coordinated across the uterus, not absent.  Why D wrong: Coordinated contraction is not confined to the lower segment."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"The concept of \"polarity\" of uterine action means that during a contraction the:","options":["Upper uterine segment contracts and relaxes while the lower uterine segment simply dilates","Lower uterine segment contracts while the upper segment dilates","Whole uterus dilates equally","Cervix contracts while the fundus relaxes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state polarity means the upper part of the uterus (upper uterine segment) contracts & relaxes, while the lower part (lower uterine segment) simply dilates.  Why B wrong: This reverses the correct roles of the segments.  Why C wrong: The two segments behave differently, not equally.  Why D wrong: This does not match the described polarity."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"During retraction of uterine muscles, after each contraction and relaxation the length of the uterine muscle fibers:","options":["Returns fully to its original length","Increases permanently","Does not come back to the original length and permanently decreases slightly","Remains unchanged throughout labor"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that at the time of relaxation the length of uterine muscles does not come back to original length — it permanently decreases slightly (retraction).  Why A wrong: The muscle does NOT return fully to its original length.  Why B wrong: The length decreases, it does not increase.  Why D wrong: The length changes progressively due to retraction."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"When the uterus relaxes in between contractions during labor, the pressure recorded is about 8–10 mmHg. This is termed:","options":["Peak pressure","Basal tone","Intrauterine peak","Tachysystole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when the uterus relaxes between contractions the pressure is 8–10 mmHg, called Basal Tone.  Why A wrong: Peak pressure is measured during contraction, not between them.  Why C wrong: This is not the term used in the notes.  Why D wrong: Tachysystole refers to excessive contraction frequency, not the resting pressure."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"What is the peak intrauterine pressure at the time of contraction during the FIRST stage of labor?","options":["8–10 mmHg","40–50 mmHg","65–75 mmHg","100–120 mmHg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state peak pressure in the 1st stage of labor is 40–50 mmHg.  Why A wrong: 8–10 mmHg is the basal tone between contractions.  Why C wrong: 65–75 mmHg is the intrauterine pressure of an adequate contraction parameter.  Why D wrong: 100–120 mmHg is the peak pressure in the 2nd stage of labor."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"The peak intrauterine pressure at the time of contraction during the SECOND stage of labor is:","options":["40–50 mmHg","65–75 mmHg","100–120 mmHg","8–10 mmHg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state peak pressure in the 2nd stage of labor is 100–120 mmHg.  Why A wrong: 40–50 mmHg is the 1st-stage peak pressure.  Why B wrong: 65–75 mmHg is the adequate-contraction intrauterine pressure.  Why D wrong: 8–10 mmHg is the basal tone."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"In labor, the normal sequence of a uterine cycle is best described as:","options":["Continuous contraction without relaxation","Contraction followed by relaxation","Relaxation only","Simultaneous contraction and relaxation of the same fibers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"There is uterus contraction followed by relaxation.\"  Why A wrong: There is always relaxation between contractions; continuous contraction is abnormal.  Why C wrong: Relaxation alone would not progress labor.  Why D wrong: The phases occur sequentially, not simultaneously."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Why is uterine relaxation between contractions important for the fetus?","options":["Because blood flow to the fetus decreases during contraction and is restored during relaxation","Because it increases fetal heart rate","Because it stops cervical dilatation","Because it prevents the uterus from contracting again"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that at the time of contraction blood to the fetus decreases, and at the time of relaxation blood flow is restored.  Why B wrong: The notes do not attribute relaxation to raising fetal heart rate directly.  Why C wrong: Relaxation does not stop cervical dilatation.  Why D wrong: Relaxation is followed by further contractions; it does not prevent them."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"According to the parameters of an adequate uterine contraction, how many contractions should occur in a 10-minute period?","options":["1 contraction","2 contractions","3 contractions","5 contractions"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state adequate uterine contraction = 3 contractions in a period of 10 minutes.  Why A wrong: One contraction in 10 minutes is inadequate.  Why B wrong: Two contractions is fewer than the stated adequate number.  Why D wrong: 5 or more contractions in 10 minutes is tachysystole, which is excessive."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"For an adequate uterine contraction, each contraction should last approximately:","options":["15 seconds","30 seconds","45 seconds","90 seconds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state each contraction should last for 45 seconds.  Why A wrong: 15 seconds is too short and not stated.  Why B wrong: 30 seconds is not the stated duration.  Why D wrong: 90 seconds is not the stated duration."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"What is the intrauterine pressure during an adequate uterine contraction?","options":["8–10 mm Hg","40–50 mm Hg","65–75 mm Hg","100–120 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state an adequate contraction has an intrauterine pressure of 65–75 mm of Hg.  Why A wrong: 8–10 mmHg is the basal tone between contractions.  Why B wrong: 40–50 mmHg is the 1st-stage peak pressure.  Why D wrong: 100–120 mmHg is the 2nd-stage peak pressure."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Tachysystole is defined as:","options":["Excessive uterine contraction with ≥5 contractions in 10 minutes","3 contractions in 10 minutes","Absence of uterine contraction","Basal tone of 8–10 mmHg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define tachysystole as excessive uterine contraction, contraction ≥5 in 10 minutes.  Why B wrong: 3 contractions in 10 minutes is the adequate/normal number.  Why C wrong: Absence of contraction is not tachysystole.  Why D wrong: 8–10 mmHg is basal tone, unrelated to contraction frequency."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Tachysystole causing fetal distress is specifically called:","options":["Basal tone","Hyperstimulation","Polarity","Retraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state tachysystole causing fetal distress is called HYPER STIMULATION.  Why A wrong: Basal tone is the resting uterine pressure.  Why C wrong: Polarity is a normal characteristic of contraction.  Why D wrong: Retraction refers to the permanent shortening of muscle fibers."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"How is the descent of the fetal head assessed clinically?","options":["By measuring the fundal height","By the station of the fetal head","By the biparietal diameter","By the cervical effacement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state descent of the fetal head is known with the help of the \"Station of fetal head.\"  Why A wrong: Fundal height assesses uterine size/gestation, not head descent.  Why C wrong: Biparietal diameter is a fetal skull measurement, not the descent assessment method here.  Why D wrong: Cervical effacement relates to cervical thinning, not descent."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"The station of the fetal head is defined as the relationship between the lowermost part of the fetal skull and which bony landmark?","options":["Pubic symphysis","Ischial spine","Sacral promontory","Coccyx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state station is the relationship between the lowermost part of the fetal skull and the ischial spine.  Why A wrong: The pubic symphysis is not the reference point for station.  Why C wrong: The sacral promontory is used for conjugate measurements, not station.  Why D wrong: The coccyx is not the reference landmark for station."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"When the fetal head is exactly at the level of the ischial spine, the station is:","options":["-5 station","Zero (0) station","+5 station","-2 station"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when the head is at the level of the ischial spine it is the zero (0) station.  Why A wrong: -5 station corresponds to the pelvic inlet level, above the spines.  Why C wrong: +5 station corresponds to the outlet, below the spines.  Why D wrong: -2 indicates the head is 2 cm above the ischial spine."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"If the fetal head is ABOVE the level of the ischial spines, the station is expressed as:","options":["Positive (+ve) numbers","Negative (-ve) numbers","Zero only","Cannot be expressed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes indicate that above the ischial spine the station is negative (-ve), ranging from -1 to -5 up to the pelvic inlet.  Why A wrong: Positive numbers indicate the head is below the ischial spines.  Why C wrong: Zero is only when the head is exactly at the spines.  Why D wrong: It is clearly expressed as negative values."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"If the fetal head is BELOW the level of the ischial spines, the station is expressed as:","options":["Negative (-ve) numbers","Positive (+ve) numbers","Zero","-5 to -1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes indicate that below the ischial spine the station is positive (+ve), from +1 to +5 down to the outlet.  Why A wrong: Negative numbers indicate the head is above the spines.  Why C wrong: Zero indicates the head is exactly at the spines.  Why D wrong: -5 to -1 corresponds to positions above the spines."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"The number in a station reading (e.g., -3 or +2) represents:","options":["The dilation of the cervix in cm","The distance of the head from the ischial spine in cm","The fundal height in cm","The gestational age in weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the number represents the distance of the head from the ischial spine in cms.  Why A wrong: Cervical dilation is measured separately, not by station number.  Why C wrong: Fundal height is unrelated to station number.  Why D wrong: Gestational age is not represented by station."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"A station of \"-3\" indicates that the fetal head is:","options":["3 cm below the ischial spine","3 cm above the ischial spine","At the ischial spine","3 cm above the pubic symphysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state (−)3 = 3 cms above the ischial spine.  Why A wrong: Below the spine would be a positive value.  Why C wrong: At the spine is 0 station.  Why D wrong: The reference landmark is the ischial spine, not the pubic symphysis."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"A station of \"+2\" indicates that the fetal head is:","options":["2 cm above the ischial spine","2 cm below the level of the ischial spine","At the level of the pelvic inlet","At zero station"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state ⊕2 = 2 cms below the level of the ischial spine.  Why A wrong: Above the spine would be a negative value.  Why C wrong: The pelvic inlet corresponds to -5.  Why D wrong: Zero station is exactly at the spines, not +2."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Engagement of the fetal head means that the station is:","options":["-5 or above","\"0\" (zero) or below it","Only +5","Above the ischial spines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state engagement means the station of the fetal head is \"0\" or below it.  Why A wrong: -5 (above the spines) is not engaged.  Why C wrong: Engagement is not restricted to +5; it is 0 or any positive value.  Why D wrong: Above the ischial spines (negative station) is not engaged."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"According to current practice noted, forceps or vacuum are applied when the fetal head is at which station or below?","options":["0 station","+1 station","+2 station","-2 station"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state these days forceps or vacuum are applied if the head is at '+2' station or below it.  Why A wrong: 0 station is engagement but not the stated threshold for instrumental delivery.  Why B wrong: +1 is above the stated +2 threshold.  Why D wrong: -2 is above the spines, unsuitable for instrumental delivery."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"In the later stages of labor, pain due to dilation of the cervix is carried by which nerve supply?","options":["T10–L1","S2–S4","T4","Pudendal nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cervix nerve supply is S2–S4, responsible for pain due to cervical dilation.  Why A wrong: T10–L1 is the uterine nerve supply (pain from uterine contraction).  Why C wrong: T4 is the level for spinal anesthesia in cesarean section.  Why D wrong: The pudendal nerve supplies the perineum, not the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Pain due to uterine contraction during labor is carried by which nerve supply?","options":["S2–S4","T10–L1","Pudendal nerve","T4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus nerve supply is T10–L1, responsible for pain from uterine contraction.  Why A wrong: S2–S4 is the cervical nerve supply.  Why C wrong: The pudendal nerve supplies the perineum.  Why D wrong: T4 is the level for cesarean spinal anesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"For painless labor, which type of analgesia is preferred because it produces no motor blockage, only sensory blockage?","options":["Spinal anesthesia","Epidural analgesia","General anesthesia","Pudendal nerve block"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state epidural analgesia is preferred for painless labor because there is no motor blockage, only sensory blockage.  Why A wrong: Spinal anesthesia produces both sensory and motor block and is preferred for cesarean, not painless labor.  Why C wrong: General anesthesia is not the preferred method for painless labor here.  Why D wrong: Pudendal nerve block is used for instrumental delivery, not routine painless labor."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"At which spinal segment is epidural analgesia given for painless labor?","options":["T4","T10","S2","L4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the segment for painless-labor epidural is T10.  Why A wrong: T4 is the level for cesarean spinal anesthesia.  Why C wrong: S2 relates to cervical/perineal nerve supply, not the epidural segment given.  Why D wrong: L4 is not the stated segment."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"What is the drug of choice (DOC) for epidural analgesia in painless labor?","options":["Lignocaine","Bupivacaine","Ropivacaine","Fentanyl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the DOC is Bupivacaine.  Why A wrong: Lignocaine is not listed as the DOC here.  Why C wrong: Ropivacaine is not the drug stated in the notes.  Why D wrong: Fentanyl is an opioid adjunct, not the stated DOC."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"When full anesthesia (sensory + motor block) is given, what happens to the uterus?","options":["Uterus keeps contracting","Uterus stops contracting","Uterus contracts faster","No effect on the uterus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that giving anesthesia produces sensory + motor block, and the uterus stops contracting.  Why A wrong: The uterus keeps contracting only with analgesia (no motor block).  Why C wrong: Anesthesia does not speed up contractions; it stops them.  Why D wrong: There is a definite effect — contractions stop."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"With analgesia (no motor block), what happens to the uterus?","options":["Uterus stops contracting","Uterus keeps contracting","Uterus ruptures","Uterus relaxes permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state analgesia means no motor block, so the uterus keeps contracting.  Why A wrong: The uterus stops contracting only with full anesthesia (motor block).  Why C wrong: The notes do not state uterine rupture with analgesia.  Why D wrong: The uterus continues to contract, it does not relax permanently."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"What is the preferred anesthesia for a cesarean section?","options":["Epidural analgesia","Spinal anesthesia","Local infiltration","Pudendal nerve block"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the preferred anesthesia for C-section is spinal anesthesia.  Why A wrong: Epidural analgesia is preferred for painless labor, not cesarean.  Why C wrong: Local infiltration is inadequate for cesarean.  Why D wrong: Pudendal nerve block is for instrumental vaginal delivery."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"To what spinal level is the block extended for cesarean section, and why?","options":["T4, to block pain sensation from the peritoneum and abdominal wall","T10, to block only the uterus","S2–S4, to block the cervix","L1, to block the lower limbs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the level is the T4 segment because pain sensation from the peritoneum and abdominal wall must also be blocked.  Why B wrong: T10 blocks the uterus alone, insufficient for abdominal surgery.  Why C wrong: S2–S4 covers the cervix, not the abdominal wall/peritoneum.  Why D wrong: L1 does not reach the abdominal wall level needed."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"During instrumental delivery (forceps/vacuum), the pain at the time of instrument insertion is due to:","options":["Dilation of the cervix","Uterine contraction","Stretching of the perineum","Descent of the fetal head"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state pain at the time of instrument insertion is due to stretching of the perineum.  Why A wrong: Cervical dilation pain is via S2–S4 and is not the mechanism for instrument-insertion pain.  Why B wrong: Uterine contraction pain (T10–L1) is separate.  Why D wrong: Head descent is not the stated cause of the insertion pain."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"The perineum receives its nerve supply from which nerve?","options":["Obturator nerve","Pudendal nerve","Genitofemoral nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the perineum nerve supply is the pudendal nerve.  Why A wrong: The obturator nerve is not the stated perineal supply.  Why C wrong: The genitofemoral nerve is not the stated perineal supply.  Why D wrong: The ilioinguinal nerve is not the stated perineal supply."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"For instrumental delivery, which nerve block is performed to relieve perineal pain?","options":["Spinal block","Epidural block","Pudendal nerve block","Paracervical block"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that for instrumental delivery, a pudendal nerve block is done.  Why A wrong: Spinal block is for cesarean.  Why B wrong: Epidural block is for painless labor.  Why D wrong: Paracervical block is not the method stated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"What is the anatomical site for administering a pudendal nerve block?","options":["Sacral promontory","Ischial spine","Pubic symphysis","Ischial tuberosity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the site for the pudendal nerve block is the ischial spine.  Why A wrong: The sacral promontory is a pelvic measurement landmark, not the block site.  Why C wrong: The pubic symphysis is not the block site.  Why D wrong: The ischial tuberosity is not the stated site; the ischial spine is."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"The pudendal nerve block is classified as which type of block?","options":["Central neuraxial block","Regional block (1%)","General anesthesia","Field block of the abdomen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the pudendal nerve block is a regional block = 1%.  Why A wrong: It is a regional peripheral block, not a central neuraxial one.  Why C wrong: It is not general anesthesia.  Why D wrong: It targets the perineum via the pudendal nerve, not an abdominal field block."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Which ligament is pierced while performing a pudendal nerve block?","options":["Sacrotuberous ligament","Sacrospinous ligament","Broad ligament","Round ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the ligament pierced in a pudendal nerve block is the sacrospinous ligament.  Why A wrong: The sacrotuberous ligament is not the one stated.  Why C wrong: The broad ligament supports the uterus, not the pudendal block route.  Why D wrong: The round ligament is not pierced in this block."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"The onset of labor begins with:","options":["Rupture of membranes","True labor pain","Show (bloody mucus)","Full cervical dilatation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state onset of labor begins with true labor pain.  Why A wrong: Rupture of membranes may occur but is not the defining onset stated.  Why C wrong: Show is a sign but not the stated onset marker.  Why D wrong: Full dilatation marks the end of the first stage, not onset."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Where is TRUE labor pain typically felt?","options":["In the lower abdomen only","In the lower abdomen with radiation to the back and thigh","Only in the upper abdomen","Only in the perineum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state true labor pain is felt in the lower abdomen and radiates to the back and thigh.  Why A wrong: Pain confined to the lower abdomen only is a feature of false labor.  Why C wrong: The upper abdomen is not the stated site.  Why D wrong: The perineum-only location is not described for true labor pain."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"In FALSE labor pain, where is the pain typically felt?","options":["Lower abdomen only","Lower abdomen with radiation to back and thigh","Upper back only","Perineum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state false labor pain is felt in the lower abdomen only.  Why B wrong: Radiation to back and thigh is a feature of true labor pain.  Why C wrong: Upper back only is not the stated location.  Why D wrong: The perineum is not the stated location for false labor pain."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"The rhythm of TRUE labor pain contractions is described as:","options":["Continuous or irregular","Regular and rhythmic (off and on)","Absent","Continuous without relief"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state true labor contractions are regular and rhythmic (off and on).  Why A wrong: Continuous or irregular pain describes false labor.  Why C wrong: Contractions are present in true labor.  Why D wrong: True labor pain is intermittent (off and on), not continuous."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"The rhythm of FALSE labor pain is characterized by:","options":["Regular and rhythmic contractions","Continuous pain or irregular contractions","Off-and-on rhythmic contractions","Contractions increasing in intensity with time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state false labor pain has continuous pain or irregular rhythm.  Why A wrong: Regular and rhythmic describes true labor.  Why C wrong: Off-and-on rhythmic contractions describe true labor.  Why D wrong: Increasing intensity over time is a true-labor feature."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"How does the intensity of TRUE labor pain change over time?","options":["It decreases in intensity, duration, and frequency","It remains constant","It increases in intensity, duration, and frequency with time","It disappears completely"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state true labor pain increases in intensity, duration, and frequency with time.  Why A wrong: It increases, not decreases.  Why B wrong: It does not remain constant.  Why D wrong: It does not disappear; false labor pain is the one relieved."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Regarding dilation of the cervix, in TRUE versus FALSE labor pain:","options":["Dilation is seen in true labor and not seen in false labor","Dilation is seen in false labor and not seen in true labor","Dilation is seen in both","Dilation is seen in neither"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state dilation of the cervix is \"Seen\" in true labor and \"Not seen\" in false labor.  Why B wrong: This reverses the correct finding.  Why C wrong: Dilation does not occur in false labor.  Why D wrong: Dilation does occur in true labor."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"In TRUE labor, \"show\" refers to:","options":["Clear watery discharge","Blood mixed mucus discharge","Purulent discharge","No discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in true labor the patient complains of blood mixed mucus discharge (show).  Why A wrong: Clear watery discharge suggests membrane rupture, not show.  Why C wrong: Purulent discharge suggests infection, not show.  Why D wrong: Show is present in true labor; false labor has no show."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"On per vaginal examination in TRUE labor, the bag of membranes:","options":["Cannot be felt","Is felt (membranes with amniotic fluid)","Is always ruptured","Is replaced by the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in true labor, on per vaginal exam one feels the membranes with amniotic fluid — the bag of membranes is felt.  Why A wrong: The bag of membranes IS felt in true labor.  Why C wrong: The membranes are felt intact, not necessarily ruptured.  Why D wrong: The placenta does not replace the membranes at this point."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"Regarding relief with sedation/enema, TRUE labor pain is:","options":["Relieved with sedation/enema","Not relieved with sedation/enema","Worsened only by sedation","Unaffected by enema but relieved by sedation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state true labor pain is NOT relieved with sedation/enema.  Why A wrong: Relief with sedation/enema is a feature of FALSE labor.  Why C wrong: The notes do not state true labor pain worsens with sedation.  Why D wrong: True labor is not relieved by either."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"FALSE labor pain is characteristically:","options":["Not relieved with sedation/enema","Relieved with sedation/enema","Associated with cervical dilation","Associated with blood mixed mucus discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state false labor pain is relieved with sedation/enema.  Why A wrong: Non-relief with sedation/enema describes true labor.  Why C wrong: Cervical dilation is a true-labor feature.  Why D wrong: Blood mixed mucus discharge (show) is a true-labor feature."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"What is the most important / best sign of true labor pain?","options":["Regular and rhythmic contractions","Presence of cervical dilation","Radiation of pain to the back and thigh","Blood mixed mucus discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes emphasize (in green) that the most important/best sign of true labor pain is dilation of the cervix present.  Why A wrong: Regular rhythmic contractions support true labor but are not called the best sign.  Why C wrong: Radiation of pain is a supporting feature, not the best sign.  Why D wrong: Show is a supporting feature but cervical dilation is the single best sign."},{"section":"Batch 058 · Four P's of normal labor, uterine contraction…","question":"A pregnant female at 35 weeks complains of pain in the lower abdomen with progressive dilation of the cervix and blood mixed mucus discharge. This clinical picture indicates:","options":["False labor","True labor","Braxton Hicks contractions","Cervical incompetence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give this exact scenario and conclude it is True Labor, because progressive cervical dilation plus blood mixed mucus discharge (show) are true-labor signs.  Why A wrong: False labor has no cervical dilation and no show.  Why B correct as above.  Why C wrong: Braxton Hicks contractions do not cause progressive dilation or show.  Why D wrong: Cervical incompetence is painless dilation and does not fit this painful, show-positive picture."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In normal labour, what is the fetal lie?","options":["Transverse","Oblique","Longitudinal","Unstable"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In normal labour the lie is longitudinal (long axis of fetus parallel to long axis of the uterus).  Why A wrong: Transverse lie is abnormal and precludes vaginal delivery.  Why B wrong: Oblique lie is an abnormal/unstable lie, not normal labour.  Why D wrong: Unstable lie is abnormal and is not the feature of normal labour."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In normal labour, the presentation is:","options":["Breech","Cephalic","Shoulder","Compound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal labour has a cephalic (head-down) presentation.  Why A wrong: Breech is a malpresentation.  Why C wrong: Shoulder presentation occurs with transverse lie and is abnormal.  Why D wrong: Compound presentation (a limb alongside the head) is abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In normal cephalic labour, the presenting part is the:","options":["Brow","Face","Vertex","Sinciput"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The vertex is the presenting part in normal labour (well-flexed cephalic presentation).  Why A wrong: Brow presentation is a deflexed/extended abnormal presentation.  Why B wrong: Face presentation results from full extension and is abnormal.  Why D wrong: Sinciput (brow region) presentation reflects a deflexed head, not normal labour."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"According to the notes, which fetal position occurs most frequently at the onset of normal labour (order given LOT > LOA > DOA > ROA > ROT)?","options":["ROT (Right Occipito Transverse)","LOA (Left Occipito Anterior)","LOT (Left Occipito Transverse)","DOA (Direct Occipito Anterior)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The frequency order given is LOT > LOA > DOA > ROA > ROT, so LOT is the most common.  Why A wrong: ROT is listed as the least common in this order.  Why B wrong: LOA is second most common, not the most common.  Why D wrong: DOA is third in the order."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In a vertex (cephalic) presentation, the denominator used to describe fetal position is the:","options":["Sacrum","Occiput","Mentum","Acromion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In vertex presentation the denominator is the occiput.  Why A wrong: The sacrum is the denominator in breech presentation.  Why C wrong: The mentum (chin) is the denominator in face presentation.  Why D wrong: The acromion (shoulder) is the denominator in shoulder presentation."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"The series of movements which occur while the fetus is traversing the maternal pelvis is called:","options":["Restitution","Cardinal movements","Crowning","Molding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The movements the fetus undergoes while passing through the maternal pelvis are called the cardinal movements of labour.  Why A wrong: Restitution is only one of these movements (untwisting of the neck).  Why C wrong: Crowning is a clinical event, not the whole series of movements.  Why D wrong: Molding is overlapping of skull bones, not the sequence of positional movements."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"According to the notes, how many cardinal movements of labour are there?","options":["6","7","8","5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state there are 8 movements.  Why A wrong: 6 is fewer than the number stated.  Why B wrong: 7 is the classic count in some texts, but this source lists 8.  Why D wrong: 5 is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"What is the FIRST cardinal movement of labour?","options":["Descent","Flexion","Engagement","Internal rotation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Engagement is listed as the 1st movement.  Why A wrong: Descent follows engagement.  Why B wrong: Flexion occurs after descent brings the head onto the levator ani.  Why D wrong: Internal rotation is a later movement."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"Which definition best describes \"engagement\" of the fetal head?","options":["When the head is permanently visible at the vaginal opening","When the greatest transverse diameter of the presenting part has passed through the pelvic inlet / crosses the pelvic brim","When the occiput lies directly behind the pubic symphysis","When the head rotates 2/8 of a circle at the ischial spine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Engagement is defined as the greatest transverse diameter of the presenting part passing through the pelvic inlet or crossing the pelvic brim.  Why A wrong: That describes crowning.  Why C wrong: That describes the position after internal rotation.  Why D wrong: That describes internal rotation."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In all cephalic presentations, the greatest transverse diameter of the fetal head is the:","options":["Occipitofrontal diameter","Suboccipitobregmatic diameter","Biparietal diameter","Mentovertical diameter"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The greatest transverse diameter of the fetal head is the biparietal diameter.  Why A wrong: The occipitofrontal is an anteroposterior (engaging) diameter of a deflexed head, not the transverse diameter.  Why B wrong: The suboccipitobregmatic is the AP engaging diameter of a well-flexed head.  Why D wrong: The mentovertical is the AP diameter of a brow (slightly extended) head."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"What is the measurement of the biparietal diameter of the fetal head?","options":["8.5 cm","9.5 cm","11.5 cm","14 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The biparietal diameter measures 9.5 cm.  Why A wrong: 8.5 cm is the bitemporal diameter, not biparietal.  Why C wrong: 11.5 cm corresponds to the occipitofrontal diameter.  Why D wrong: 14 cm is the mentovertical diameter of a brow presentation."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In a primigravida, engagement of the fetal head usually occurs at:","options":["At onset of labour","38 weeks / before the onset of labour","42 weeks","During the second stage of labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a primigravida engagement occurs around 38 weeks, i.e., before the onset of labour.  Why A wrong: Engagement at the onset of labour is typical of a multigravida.  Why C wrong: 42 weeks is post-term and not the stated time.  Why D wrong: Engagement precedes second stage."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In a multigravida, engagement of the fetal head typically occurs:","options":["At 34 weeks","At 38 weeks","At onset of labour","Two weeks after delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In a multigravida engagement usually occurs at the onset of labour.  Why A wrong: 34 weeks is too early and not stated.  Why B wrong: 38 weeks / before labour is the primigravida timing.  Why D wrong: Engagement occurs before, not after, delivery."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"Clinically, engagement of the fetal head is assessed by which two methods?","options":["Abdominal method and vaginal method","Ultrasound and radiography only","Auscultation and percussion","Speculum and colposcopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe an abdominal method and a vaginal method to know if engagement has occurred.  Why B wrong: Imaging is not the clinical method described.  Why C wrong: Auscultation/percussion are not used to detect engagement.  Why D wrong: Speculum/colposcopy are not used for engagement."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"On abdominal palpation using the \"fifths\" method, which finding means the fetal head is COMPLETELY above the pelvic brim?","options":["0/5","2/5","5/5","1/5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 5/5 palpable means the head is completely above the pelvic brim.  Why A wrong: 0/5 means none of the head is palpable (deeply engaged).  Why B wrong: 2/5 means only the sinciput is felt and the occiput is just felt.  Why D wrong: 1/5 means the sinciput is felt and the occiput is not felt."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"On abdominal (fifths) palpation, \"4/5\" of the head palpable corresponds to which description?","options":["Sinciput felt, occiput not felt","Sinciput high, occiput easily felt","None of head palpable","Completely above the pelvic brim"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 4/5 corresponds to \"sinciput high, occiput easily felt.\"  Why A wrong: That description matches 1/5.  Why C wrong: None palpable is 0/5.  Why D wrong: Completely above the brim is 5/5."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"On abdominal (fifths) palpation, \"3/5\" of the head palpable corresponds to:","options":["Sinciput easily felt, occiput felt","None of head palpable","Sinciput felt, occiput just felt","Head completely above the pelvic brim"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 3/5 corresponds to \"sinciput easily felt, occiput felt.\"  Why B wrong: None palpable is 0/5.  Why C wrong: Sinciput felt, occiput just felt is 2/5.  Why D wrong: Completely above the brim is 5/5."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"On abdominal (fifths) palpation, \"2/5\" of the head palpable corresponds to:","options":["Sinciput high, occiput easily felt","Sinciput felt, occiput just felt","Sinciput felt, occiput not felt","None of head palpable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 2/5 corresponds to \"sinciput felt, occiput just felt.\"  Why A wrong: That is 4/5.  Why C wrong: That is 1/5.  Why D wrong: That is 0/5."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"On abdominal (fifths) palpation, \"1/5\" of the head palpable corresponds to:","options":["Sinciput felt, occiput not felt","Sinciput easily felt, occiput felt","None of head palpable","Completely above pelvic brim"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1/5 corresponds to \"sinciput felt, occiput not felt.\"  Why B wrong: That is 3/5.  Why C wrong: None palpable is 0/5.  Why D wrong: Completely above the brim is 5/5."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"On abdominal (fifths) palpation, \"0/5\" of the head palpable means:","options":["Sinciput felt, occiput just felt","Head completely above the pelvic brim","None of the head is palpable","Sinciput high, occiput easily felt"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 0/5 means none of the head is palpable (head deeply within the pelvis).  Why A wrong: That is 2/5.  Why B wrong: Completely above is 5/5.  Why D wrong: That is 4/5."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"On per-abdominal examination, the fetal head is said to be ENGAGED when:","options":["More than 4/5 of the head is palpable","2/5th or less of the fetal head is palpable per abdomen","Exactly 5/5 of the head is palpable","The sinciput is high and the occiput easily felt"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The head is engaged when 2/5th or less is palpable abdominally.  Why A wrong: More than 2/5 palpable means the head is not yet engaged.  Why C wrong: 5/5 means the head is completely above the brim (not engaged).  Why D wrong: That describes 4/5, i.e., not engaged."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"On per-vaginal examination, the fetal head is said to be engaged when the station of the fetal head is at:","options":["+2 station","-3 station","\"0\" station","-1 station"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: On PV examination, the head is engaged when it is at the \"0\" station (at the level of the ischial spines).  Why A wrong: +2 is below the spines (already descending past engagement).  Why B wrong: -3 is high above the spines, not engaged.  Why D wrong: -1 is above the spines, not yet engaged."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In the Crichton method, when the fetal head is mobile above the symphysis pubis, it corresponds to:","options":["2/5","5/5","0/5","1/5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the Crichton method, a head mobile above the symphysis pubis = 5/5.  Why A wrong: 2/5 corresponds to the head being 2/5 above the symphysis pubis.  Why C wrong: 0/5 means none palpable, not a freely mobile head.  Why D wrong: 1/5 is a lower value, not the freely mobile head."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In the Crichton method, the head \"accommodates the full width of five fingers above the symphysis pubis\" corresponds to which finding?","options":["5/5 fifths of head palpable","2/5 fifths of head palpable","0/5 fifths of head palpable","Head is engaged"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Accommodating the full width of five fingers above the symphysis pubis corresponds to a 5/5 (non-engaged, high) head.  Why B wrong: 2/5 corresponds to the head accommodating two fingers / being 2/5 above.  Why C wrong: 0/5 means none palpable.  Why D wrong: This is a high, non-engaged head."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In the Crichton method, when the head accommodates only TWO fingers above the symphysis pubis, it indicates the head is:","options":["Completely above the pelvic brim","Low / almost engaged (about 2/5 above)","Not descended at all","Freely mobile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Accommodating only two fingers above the symphysis pubis corresponds to the head being about 2/5 above the symphysis pubis (low/engaging).  Why A wrong: Completely above corresponds to 5/5 (five fingers / mobile head).  Why C wrong: The head has clearly descended if only two fingers fit.  Why D wrong: A freely mobile head is the 5/5 finding."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"The engaging (transverse) diameter of the fetal head at the pelvic brim is the biparietal diameter measuring:","options":["11.5 cm","9.5 cm","14 cm","7.5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The transverse engaging diameter is the biparietal diameter = 9.5 cm.  Why A wrong: 11.5 cm is the occipitofrontal diameter.  Why C wrong: 14 cm is the mentovertical diameter.  Why D wrong: 7.5 cm is not the biparietal measurement given."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In a WELL-FLEXED head, the anteroposterior engaging diameter of the fetal head is the:","options":["Occipitofrontal diameter","Suboccipitobregmatic diameter","Mentovertical diameter","Submentobregmatic diameter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A well-flexed head engages with the suboccipitobregmatic diameter (spelled \"sub occipito bragmatic\" in the notes).  Why A wrong: Occipitofrontal is the diameter of a deflexed head.  Why C wrong: Mentovertical is the diameter of a brow (slightly extended) head.  Why D wrong: Submentobregmatic is the diameter of a fully extended (face) head."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In a DEFLEXED head, the engaging diameter is the:","options":["Suboccipitobregmatic diameter","Occipitofrontal or suboccipitofrontal diameter","Mentovertical diameter","Submentovertical diameter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A deflexed head engages with the occipitofrontal or suboccipitofrontal diameter.  Why A wrong: Suboccipitobregmatic is for a well-flexed head.  Why C wrong: Mentovertical is for a brow (slightly extended) head.  Why D wrong: Submentovertical is for a face (fully extended) head."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In a SLIGHTLY EXTENDED head (brow presentation), the engaging diameter is the mentovertical diameter, which measures:","options":["9.5 cm","11.5 cm","14 cm","8.5 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The mentovertical (brow) diameter measures 14 cm — the largest AP diameter of the fetal head, which is why brow presentation obstructs labour.  Why A wrong: 9.5 cm is the biparietal (transverse) diameter.  Why B wrong: 11.5 cm is the occipitofrontal diameter.  Why D wrong: 8.5 cm is the bitemporal diameter."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In a FULLY EXTENDED head (face presentation), the engaging diameter is the:","options":["Suboccipitobregmatic diameter","Occipitofrontal diameter","Submentovertical or submentobregmatic diameter","Mentovertical diameter"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A fully extended (face) head engages with the submentovertical or submentobregmatic diameter.  Why A wrong: Suboccipitobregmatic is for a well-flexed head.  Why B wrong: Occipitofrontal is for a deflexed head.  Why D wrong: Mentovertical is for a slightly extended (brow) head."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of non-engagement of the head (free floating head) in a primigravida at term?","options":["Cephalopelvic disproportion","Placenta previa","Deflexed head / occipitoposterior position","Polyhydramnios"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list deflexed head / occipitoposterior position as the most common (m/c) cause of non-engagement.  Why A wrong: Cephalopelvic disproportion is listed as the 2nd most common cause.  Why B wrong: Placenta previa is listed 3rd.  Why D wrong: Polyhydramnios is not listed among the causes on this page."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"Which is the SECOND most common cause of non-engagement of the head in a primigravida at term?","options":["Occipitoposterior position","Cephalopelvic disproportion","Placenta previa","Fibroid uterus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cephalopelvic disproportion is listed as the 2nd most common cause.  Why A wrong: Occipitoposterior/deflexed head is the most common (1st).  Why C wrong: Placenta previa is listed 3rd.  Why D wrong: Fibroid uterus is not listed on this page."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of non-engagement (free floating head) in a primigravida at term?","options":["Placenta previa","Oligohydramnios","Gestational diabetes","Preterm labour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Placenta previa is listed as the 3rd cause of non-engagement.  Why B wrong: Oligohydramnios is not listed.  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes is not listed as a cause here.  Why D wrong: Preterm labour is not the listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"At the onset of labour, when the occiput is in the LOT position, this abbreviation stands for:","options":["Left Occipito Transverse","Left Occipito Anterior","Lateral Occiput Tilt","Low Occiput Transverse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: LOT = Left Occipito Transverse position, the most common position at onset of labour.  Why B wrong: LOA is Left Occipito Anterior, a different position.  Why C wrong: \"Lateral occiput tilt\" is not an obstetric position term.  Why D wrong: LOT is \"Left,\" not \"Low,\" occipito transverse."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"When the occiput is in the LOT position at onset of labour, the fetal shoulders lie at what angle to the occiput?","options":["45°","90°","180°","30°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The shoulders lie at 90° to the occiput (occupying the anterior-posterior positions relative to the occiput's transverse orientation).  Why A wrong: 45° is not the stated angle.  Why C wrong: 180° would place the shoulders opposite, not perpendicular.  Why D wrong: 30° is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"Which cardinal movement is the SECOND movement, driven by uterine contractions and maternal pushing?","options":["Engagement","Descent of the fetal head","Flexion","Extension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Descent of the fetal head is the 2nd cardinal movement, caused by uterine contraction and the mother pushing the fetus down.  Why A wrong: Engagement is the 1st movement.  Why C wrong: Flexion follows descent (3rd movement).  Why D wrong: Extension is a later movement (delivery of the head)."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"According to the notes, what are the two reasons for descent of the fetal head?","options":["Fetal kicking and amniotic fluid pressure","Uterine contraction and the mother pushing down the fetus","Placental separation and cord traction","Gravity and maternal position only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The two listed reasons are uterine contraction and the mother pushing down the fetus.  Why A wrong: Fetal kicking is not a listed reason.  Why C wrong: Placental separation occurs in the third stage, not as a cause of descent.  Why D wrong: Gravity/position alone are not the reasons listed."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"During labour, flexion of the fetal head occurs when the head touches which structure and recoils?","options":["Ischial spine","Pubic symphysis","Levator ani muscles","Sacral promontory"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When the head touches the levator ani muscles, muscle recoil causes flexion of the head.  Why A wrong: The ischial spine is where internal rotation occurs, not flexion.  Why B wrong: The pubic symphysis is where the occiput lies after internal rotation.  Why D wrong: The sacral promontory is not the structure causing flexion here."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"In a well-flexed head, which part of the fetal head touches the levator ani first?","options":["Sinciput","Occiput","Mentum","Brow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a well-flexed head, the occiput touches the levator ani first.  Why A wrong: The sinciput touches first in a deflexed head, not a well-flexed head.  Why C wrong: The mentum leads only in a face presentation.  Why D wrong: The brow leads in a brow presentation, not a well-flexed head."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"The levator ani muscle fibers are arranged so that their slope directs the presenting part toward the midline of the fetal head. This principle — \"whatever part of the fetal head touches the levator ani first is rotated towards the midline\" — is called:","options":["Naegele's rule","HART rule","Leopold's maneuver","Ritgen maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This principle is named the HART rule in the notes.  Why A wrong: Naegele's rule is for calculating the expected date of delivery.  Why C wrong: Leopold's maneuvers are abdominal palpation techniques.  Why D wrong: The Ritgen maneuver aids delivery of the head, not this rotation principle."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"Internal rotation of the fetal head, driven by the levator ani slope (HART rule), is which cardinal movement?","options":["2nd movement","3rd movement","4th movement","5th movement"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Internal rotation of the fetal head is listed as the 4th cardinal movement.  Why A wrong: The 2nd movement is descent.  Why B wrong: The 3rd movement leads to flexion (head touching levator ani).  Why D wrong: The 5th movement is extension/delivery of the head."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"At what level of the pelvis does internal rotation of the fetal head occur?","options":["Pelvic inlet","Ischial spine","Pelvic outlet","Sacral promontory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Internal rotation occurs at the level of the ischial spine (Q2).  Why A wrong: Engagement occurs at the pelvic inlet, not internal rotation.  Why C wrong: The pelvic outlet is where the head is finally delivered.  Why D wrong: The sacral promontory is a bony landmark of the inlet, not the site of internal rotation."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"During internal rotation, by how much of a circle does the occiput rotate?","options":["1/8 of a circle","2/8 of a circle","3/8 of a circle","4/8 of a circle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The occiput rotates by 2/8 of a circle during internal rotation.  Why A wrong: 1/8 of a circle is the amount the shoulders rotate anteriorly.  Why C wrong: 3/8 is not the stated value for internal rotation of the occiput.  Why D wrong: 4/8 (half circle) is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"After internal rotation is complete, where does the occiput of the fetal head lie?","options":["Directly behind the pubic symphysis","In the hollow of the sacrum","At the ischial spine","Against the left acetabulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After internal rotation the occiput lies directly behind the pubic symphysis.  Why B wrong: Lying in the hollow of the sacrum would be an occipitoposterior position.  Why C wrong: The ischial spine is where rotation occurs, not the final position of the occiput.  Why D wrong: The acetabulum is a lateral hip landmark, not where the occiput lies."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"After the fetal head undergoes internal rotation, if the shoulders do NOT rotate at all, what happens?","options":["Labour proceeds normally without strain","There will be a lot of strain on the neck of the fetus","The head immediately delivers","The placenta separates"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the shoulders do not rotate, a lot of strain is placed on the fetal neck (because the head has rotated 2/8 of a circle).  Why A wrong: Absence of shoulder rotation causes strain, not normal progress.  Why C wrong: Head delivery (extension) is a separate later step, not the consequence of unrotated shoulders.  Why D wrong: Placental separation is a third-stage event, unrelated here."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"To reduce strain on the fetal neck after the head's internal rotation, the shoulders rotate anteriorly by how much of a circle?","options":["2/8 of a circle","1/8 of a circle","3/8 of a circle","Half a circle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The shoulders rotate by 1/8th of a circle anteriorly.  Why A wrong: 2/8 is how much the occiput (head) rotates during internal rotation.  Why C wrong: 3/8 is not stated.  Why D wrong: Half a circle is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"After the head rotates 2/8 and the shoulders rotate 1/8 of a circle, how much strain remains on the fetal neck?","options":["No strain remains","A strain of 1/8 remains, but it can be tolerated","A strain of 2/8 remains and is intolerable","A strain of 3/8 remains"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a strain of 1/8 is still left on the neck but it can be tolerated (2/8 head rotation minus 1/8 shoulder rotation = 1/8 residual).  Why A wrong: A residual strain does remain.  Why C wrong: The residual strain is 1/8, not 2/8, and is tolerable.  Why D wrong: 3/8 is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"During labour, when the head further descends and becomes permanently visible at the mother's vaginal opening, this stage is called:","options":["Engagement","Restitution","Crowning","Extension"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When the head is permanently visible at the vaginal opening, the stage is called crowning.  Why A wrong: Engagement is passage of the biparietal diameter through the pelvic inlet.  Why B wrong: Restitution is untwisting of the neck after the head delivers.  Why D wrong: Extension is the mechanism of delivery of the head, not this visibility stage."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"Which statement about crowning is CORRECT according to the notes?","options":["Crowning is a separate cardinal movement","Crowning is NOT a separate cardinal movement","Crowning occurs before engagement","Crowning is the delivery of the shoulders"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state crowning is not a separate cardinal movement.  Why A wrong: This is the opposite of what the notes say.  Why C wrong: Crowning occurs late in the mechanism, not before engagement.  Why D wrong: Crowning refers to the head being visible, not shoulder delivery."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"According to the notes, when is an episiotomy given?","options":["Before engagement","After crowning occurs","During internal rotation","Before descent begins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Episiotomy is given after crowning occurs.  Why A wrong: Engagement is far earlier and unrelated to episiotomy timing.  Why C wrong: Internal rotation is deep in the pelvis, well before crowning.  Why D wrong: Descent precedes crowning; episiotomy is not given before descent."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"Delivery of the fetal head occurs by which cardinal movement?","options":["Flexion","Internal rotation","Extension","Descent"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Delivery of the fetal head occurs by extension (the head extends as it passes under the pubic symphysis).  Why A wrong: Flexion occurs earlier, when the head meets the levator ani.  Why B wrong: Internal rotation positions the occiput behind the symphysis before delivery.  Why D wrong: Descent brings the head down but is not the mechanism of delivery of the head."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"After the fetal head is delivered, the untwisting of the neck (reversing the 1/8 torsion that was present) is called:","options":["Restitution","External rotation","Internal rotation","Crowning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The untwisting of the neck after delivery of the head is called restitution.  Why B wrong: External rotation is a further movement caused by the shoulders returning to the AP diameter.  Why C wrong: Internal rotation is the earlier deep-pelvis rotation of the head.  Why D wrong: Crowning is when the head is visible at the vaginal opening."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"After delivery of the head, the shoulders move back by 1/8 of a circle to lie in the AP (anteroposterior) diameter. This internal movement of the fetus is visible externally as:","options":["Restitution","Internal rotation","External rotation of the fetal head","Crowning"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The shoulders returning to the AP diameter (going back 1/8 of a circle) is visible externally as external rotation of the fetal head.  Why A wrong: Restitution is the untwisting of the 1/8 torsion; external rotation is the additional movement driven by the shoulders.  Why B wrong: Internal rotation occurs deep in the pelvis before delivery of the head.  Why D wrong: Crowning is head visibility, not the external rotation movement."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"By the end of restitution and external rotation, the occiput has moved back a total of how much, and the shoulders lie in which diameter?","options":["Occiput moves back 1/8 by restitution + 1/8 by shoulder; shoulders lie in the AP diameter","Occiput moves back 2/8 by restitution only; shoulders lie in the transverse diameter","Occiput moves back 1/8 total; shoulders lie in the oblique diameter","Occiput does not move; shoulders lie in the transverse diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The occiput moves back 1/8 of a circle during restitution and a further 1/8 with the shoulders (total 2/8), and the shoulders come to lie in the AP diameter.  Why B wrong: The movement is split 1/8 (restitution) + 1/8 (shoulder), and the shoulders end in the AP, not transverse, diameter.  Why C wrong: The shoulders end in the AP diameter, not oblique.  Why D wrong: The occiput does move (via restitution and external rotation)."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Normal labour — lie/presentation/position/denominator of normal labour…","question":"After the head is delivered by extension, what must deliver next in the mechanism of normal labour?","options":["The placenta","The shoulders","The buttocks","The umbilical cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the head delivers, the shoulders have to deliver next (following external rotation).  Why A wrong: The placenta delivers in the third stage, after the whole baby.  Why B is correct as above.  Why C wrong: In a cephalic delivery the buttocks are the last part, not the next after the head.  Why D wrong: The umbilical cord is not the next structure to deliver in the mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In the cardinal movements of labour, expulsion of the baby's body finally occurs by which mechanism?","options":["Extension","Lateral flexion","Internal rotation","Restitution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Expulsion of baby: By lateral flexion\" — the trunk/body is delivered by lateral flexion of the fetus.  Why A wrong: Extension is the mechanism by which the head is born, not the body.  Why C wrong: Internal rotation aligns the head with the pelvic outlet; it does not expel the body.  Why D wrong: Restitution is untwisting of the head after delivery, not body expulsion."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"How many cardinal movements of labour are described in the current (updated) classification given in the notes?","options":["6","7","8","9"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"8 Cardinal movements\" (Engagement, Descent, Flexion, Internal rotation, Extension, Restitution, External rotation, Expulsion of body).  Why A/B wrong: Fewer than the 8 listed movements.  Why D wrong: 9 movements was the earlier classification (when crowning was included as a separate movement)."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"According to the note, the earlier classification described 9 cardinal movements of labour instead of 8 because which additional step was included?","options":["Restitution","Crowning","Engagement","External rotation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source explicitly notes \"(Earlier: 9 movements: as crowning was included)\".  Why A wrong: Restitution is retained in the 8-movement list.  Why C wrong: Engagement remains the first cardinal movement.  Why D wrong: External rotation remains in the 8-movement list."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which of the following is the correct sequence of the first four cardinal movements of labour?","options":["Descent → Engagement → Flexion → Internal rotation","Engagement → Descent → Flexion → Internal rotation","Flexion → Engagement → Descent → Extension","Engagement → Flexion → Descent → Restitution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The listed order is 1. Engagement, 2. Descent, 3. Flexion, 4. Internal rotation.  Why A wrong: Engagement precedes descent in the list.  Why C wrong: Engagement is first, not flexion; extension comes much later.  Why D wrong: Descent (2) comes before flexion (3), and restitution is 6th, not 4th."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In the 8 cardinal movements, which movement immediately follows extension?","options":["Internal rotation","Restitution","External rotation","Expulsion of body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Order is 5. Extension → 6. Restitution.  Why A wrong: Internal rotation is 4th, before extension.  Why C wrong: External rotation is 7th, after restitution.  Why D wrong: Expulsion of body is the 8th and last movement."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"After external rotation of the fetal head, where does the face of the baby lie?","options":["Towards mother's left thigh","Towards mother's right thigh","Towards mother's abdomen","Towards mother's sacrum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"After External Rotation: Face of baby: lie towards mother's right thigh.\"  Why A wrong: The source specifies the right thigh, not left.  Why C/D wrong: These positions are not stated for the face after external rotation."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"After external rotation of the fetal head, the occiput of the baby lies in which position?","options":["At the same position from which delivery started","Opposite to the starting position","Directly anterior","Towards the mother's right thigh"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Occiput of baby: lies at same position from which delivery started.\"  Why B wrong: The source says the same position, not the opposite.  Why C wrong: No such statement is made.  Why D wrong: It is the face (not occiput) that lies towards the right thigh."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"As a measure to prevent perineal tear, applying warm perineal compresses acts primarily by which mechanism?","options":["Contracting the levator ani muscle","Relaxing the levator ani (levatory ani) muscle","Increasing perineal blood pressure","Numbing the perineal nerves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow diagram shows warm perineal compresses → relaxes levator ani → prevents perineal tear.  Why A wrong: The compresses relax, not contract, the muscle.  Why C wrong: Increasing blood pressure is not the mechanism described.  Why D wrong: Warm compresses relax muscle; they are not an anaesthetic."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a technique to prevent perineal tear during labour management?","options":["Routine episiotomy","Perineal massage","Routine fundal pressure","Rapid uncontrolled delivery of head"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Perineal massage\" is point 2 of the measures to prevent perineal tear.  Why A wrong: Routine episiotomy is listed under \"Not Recommended / Not done.\"  Why C wrong: Routine fundal pressure is listed under \"Not Recommended / Not done.\"  Why D wrong: The source recommends controlled delivery of head, not rapid uncontrolled delivery."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"To prevent perineal tear during delivery of the head, the nurse/midwife should do which of the following?","options":["Keep the head extended and deliver rapidly","Maintain the head in flexion and perform controlled delivery of the head","Push the head out with fundal pressure","Avoid touching the perineum entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Point 3 states \"Maintain head in flexion & controlled delivery of head.\"  Why A wrong: The head should be kept in flexion with a controlled (not rapid) delivery.  Why C wrong: Fundal pressure is \"not done.\"  Why D wrong: Perineal support with one hand is recommended (point 4)."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"During controlled delivery of the head to prevent perineal tear, perineal support should be given with:","options":["Both hands","One hand","A sterile drape only","Forceps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Point 4 states \"Give perineal support with one hand.\"  Why A wrong: Support is with one hand, per the note.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated method of perineal support."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which measures to prevent perineal tear are specifically recommended by WHO according to the notes?","options":["Warm perineal compresses and perineal massage","Maintaining head in flexion/controlled delivery and giving perineal support with one hand","Routine episiotomy and fundal pressure","Enema and bladder emptying"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"(3), (4) recommended by WHO, to prevent perineal tear\" — i.e., maintaining head in flexion/controlled delivery (3) and perineal support with one hand (4).  Why A wrong: While useful, points 1 and 2 are not the ones tagged as WHO-recommended.  Why C wrong: These are \"Not Recommended / Not done.\"  Why D wrong: Not part of the perineal-tear prevention WHO recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which of the following practices are listed as \"Not Recommended / Not done\" during management of labour to prevent perineal tear? (Select all that apply)","options":["Routine episiotomy","Routine fundal pressure","Perineal massage","Warm perineal compresses","Controlled delivery of head"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists under \"Not Recommended / Not done\": 1. Routine episiotomy, 2. Routine fundal pressure.  Why C/D/E wrong: Perineal massage, warm compresses and controlled delivery are recommended measures to prevent perineal tear, not prohibited."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"What is the definition of an episiotomy?","options":["A spontaneous tear of the vaginal wall during delivery","A planned surgical incision given on the perineum","An incision of the cervix to hasten delivery","Removal of the perineal body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define episiotomy as \"Planned surgical incision given on PERINEUM.\"  Why A wrong: A spontaneous tear is a laceration, not a planned incision.  Why C wrong: An incision on the cervix is not an episiotomy.  Why D wrong: Episiotomy is an incision, not removal of the perineal body."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"The two main types of episiotomy described are:","options":["Median (midline) and Mediolateral","Anterior and Posterior","Vertical and Horizontal","Superficial and Deep"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe \"Median episiotomy or midline\" and \"Mediolateral episiotomy.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the episiotomy types described."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"A mediolateral episiotomy makes an angle of how many degrees with the midline when the perineum is stretched?","options":["30°","45°","60°","90°"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the mediolateral episiotomy \"makes an angle of 60° with the midline when perineum is stretched.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated stretched-perineum angle is 60°, not 30/45/90°."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"When the perineum is NOT stretched (relaxed), the mediolateral episiotomy is oriented at an angle in the range of:","options":["15–30°","45–60°","60–75°","90–100°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note \"when(45–60°) perineum is stretched\" indicates the incision is angled 45–60° (the angle becomes 60° once the perineum is fully stretched).  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges are not given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Compared to a mediolateral episiotomy, a median (midline) episiotomy cuts:","options":["More muscle fibers","Fewer muscle fibers","Only the anal sphincter","Only skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states for median episiotomy \"Less muscle fibers are cut.\"  Why A wrong: More muscle fibers are cut in the mediolateral type.  Why C wrong: The anal sphincter is not routinely cut; it may be involved only if a median incision extends.  Why D wrong: Muscle fibers are cut, not only skin."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which of the following are advantages of a MEDIAN (midline) episiotomy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Less bleeding","Easy repair","Fast healing","Less dyspareunia","Can never involve the anal sphincter"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists median episiotomy advantages as less bleeding → easy repair → healing fast → dyspareunia is less.  Why E wrong: \"Can never involve anal sphincter\" is an advantage of the MEDIOLATERAL episiotomy; a median episiotomy CAN involve the anal sphincter if it extends."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"What is the main disadvantage of a MEDIAN (midline) episiotomy?","options":["More bleeding","It can involve the anal sphincter if it extends","Slow healing","Difficult repair"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the median episiotomy disadvantage: \"It can involve anal sphincter if it extends.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: More bleeding, slow healing and difficult repair are disadvantages of the mediolateral type, not the median type."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which statement is an ADVANTAGE of a MEDIOLATERAL episiotomy?","options":["It causes less bleeding","It can never involve the anal sphincter no matter how much it is extended","It heals faster than a median episiotomy","Repair is easier than a median episiotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the mediolateral advantage: \"Can never involve anal sphincter no matter how much it is extended,\" and it is marked \"Preferred.\"  Why A wrong: The mediolateral type causes MORE bleeding.  Why C wrong: Healing is SLOW with mediolateral.  Why D wrong: Repair is DIFFICULT with mediolateral."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which of the following are DISADVANTAGES of a MEDIOLATERAL episiotomy? (Select all that apply)","options":["More muscle fibers are cut","More bleeding","Repair is difficult","Healing is slow","More pain / dyspareunia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the mediolateral disadvantage chain: more muscle fibers cut → more bleeding → repair difficult → healing slow → more pain/dyspareunia.  Why (none wrong): All five options are explicitly listed as disadvantages of the mediolateral episiotomy."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which type of episiotomy is \"always preferred\" according to the notes?","options":["Median (midline) episiotomy","Mediolateral episiotomy","J-shaped episiotomy","Anterior episiotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Always: Medio-lateral episiotomy is preferred\" — mainly because it can never involve the anal sphincter.  Why A wrong: Median episiotomy risks anal sphincter involvement if it extends.  Why C/D wrong: These types are not described."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"What is the WHO recommendation regarding routine episiotomy for women undergoing delivery?","options":["Give episiotomy to all women undergoing delivery","Does NOT recommend giving episiotomy to all females undergoing delivery; give only if indicated","Never perform episiotomy under any circumstances","Give episiotomy only to multigravida women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state WHO \"Does not recommend to give episiotomy in all female undergoing delivery\" and \"WHO says give episiotomy only if it is indicated.\"  Why A wrong: WHO opposes routine episiotomy for all.  Why C wrong: WHO allows it when indicated; it is not absolutely banned.  Why D wrong: No such restriction to multigravida is stated (indications favour primigravida/rigid perineum)."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which of the following is an indication for episiotomy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Shoulder dystocia","After-coming head of breech","Instrumental delivery","Macrosomia","Rigid perineum (e.g., primigravida)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list all five as indications of episiotomy: 1. Shoulder dystocia, 2. After coming head of breech, 3. Instrumental delivery, 4. Macrosomia, 5. Rigid perineum (eg Primigravida).  Why (none wrong): Every option is a listed indication."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"A midwife performs an episiotomy for the after-coming head of a breech delivery. This falls under which category of episiotomy use?","options":["Routine episiotomy","Indicated (selective) episiotomy","Contraindicated procedure","Cosmetic incision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"After coming head of breech\" is listed as an indication, so the episiotomy is being given because it is indicated (WHO: give only if indicated).  Why A wrong: Routine episiotomy for all women is not recommended.  Why C wrong: It is indicated here, not contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Episiotomy is a functional obstetric incision, not cosmetic."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"\"Rigid perineum\" as an indication for episiotomy is most commonly seen in which patient?","options":["Grand multipara","Primigravida","Woman in preterm labour","Woman with previous vaginal deliveries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note gives \"Rigid perineum (eg Primigravida)\" as an example.  Why A/D wrong: Multiparous women/those with prior vaginal deliveries usually have a more distensible perineum.  Why C wrong: Preterm labour is not the cited example of rigid perineum."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Episiotomy scissors are characterized by which special design feature?","options":["Sharp pointed blades","Blunt blades with an angle","Serrated cutting edges","Curved hook tips"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The labelled figure of episiotomy scissors identifies \"Blunt blades\" and an \"Angle.\"  Why A wrong: The blades are blunt, not sharp/pointed, to avoid injuring the fetus.  Why C/D wrong: These features are not shown for episiotomy scissors."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Under which type of anaesthesia is an episiotomy typically given?","options":["General anaesthesia","Spinal anaesthesia","Local anaesthesia (LA)","No anaesthesia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state episiotomy is \"Given under ↓ LA\" i.e., \"Given under local anesthesia.\"  Why A/B wrong: The source specifies local anaesthesia, not general or spinal.  Why D wrong: It is performed under local anaesthesia, not without anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In the mnemonic \"Please Bring Live Vaccine For Tetanus\" for structures cut during episiotomy, \"Please\" stands for:","options":["Perineal skin","Pudendal nerve & vessels","Posterior vaginal wall","Pubococcygeus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic maps \"Please : Pudendal nerve & vessels.\"  Why A wrong: Skin is not the \"Please\" element.  Why C wrong: Posterior vaginal wall is \"Vaccine.\"  Why D wrong: Pubococcygeus is a subdivision of levator ani (\"Live\")."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In the episiotomy structures mnemonic, \"Bring\" refers to which muscle that is cut?","options":["Bulbospongiosus muscle","Levator ani muscle","Transverse perineum muscle","Ischiocavernosus muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic maps \"Bring : Bulbospongiosus muscle.\"  Why B wrong: Levator ani corresponds to \"Live.\"  Why C wrong: Transverse perineum muscle corresponds to \"Tetanus.\"  Why D wrong: Ischiocavernosus is listed among structures NEVER cut."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"The \"Live\" in the episiotomy mnemonic refers to which muscle, which includes the pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus?","options":["Levator ani muscle","Bulbospongiosus muscle","Transverse perineum muscle","Coccygeus muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Live : Levator Ani muscle\" with subdivisions \"Pubococcygeus\" and \"Iliococcygeus.\"  Why B wrong: Bulbospongiosus corresponds to \"Bring.\"  Why C wrong: Transverse perineum muscle corresponds to \"Tetanus.\"  Why D wrong: Coccygeus (ischiococcygeus) is listed among structures never cut."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which two subdivisions of the levator ani muscle are cut during an episiotomy?","options":["Pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus","Ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus","Superficial and deep transverse perineal","External and internal anal sphincter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under \"Live: Levator Ani muscle,\" the notes list the arrows → Pubococcygeus and → Iliococcygeus.  Why B wrong: Ischiocavernosus is never cut; bulbospongiosus is a separate (\"Bring\") structure.  Why C wrong: Those are subdivisions of the transverse perineum muscle (\"Tetanus\").  Why D wrong: The anal sphincter is listed among structures never cut."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In the episiotomy mnemonic, \"Vaccine\" and \"For\" together indicate that the incision is made through the posterior vaginal wall towards which structure?","options":["Anterior vaginal wall","Fourchette","Clitoris","Anal canal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic maps \"Vaccine : Posterior vaginal wall\" and \"For : Fourchette.\"  Why A wrong: It is the posterior (not anterior) vaginal wall.  Why C wrong: The clitoris is anterior and not cut.  Why D wrong: The anal canal/sphincter is a structure that should not be cut."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"\"Tetanus\" in the episiotomy structures mnemonic refers to which muscle, which has superficial and deep parts?","options":["Transverse perineum muscle","Bulbospongiosus muscle","Levator ani muscle","Ischiocavernosus muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Tetanus : Transverse perineum muscle → Superficial → Deep.\"  Why B wrong: Bulbospongiosus is \"Bring.\"  Why C wrong: Levator ani is \"Live.\"  Why D wrong: Ischiocavernosus is never cut."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"The transverse perineum muscle that is cut during episiotomy is divided into which two components?","options":["Superficial and deep","Anterior and posterior","Medial and lateral","Internal and external"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the transverse perineum muscle with arrows → Superficial and → Deep.  Why B/C/D wrong: These divisions are not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which of the following structures are NEVER cut during an episiotomy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ischiocavernosus","Ischiococcygeus","Anal sphincter","Bulbospongiosus","Transverse perineum muscle"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Structure which are never cut during episiotomy: 1. Ischiocavernous 2. Ischiococcygeus 3. Anal sphincter.\"  Why D wrong: Bulbospongiosus (\"Bring\") IS cut.  Why E wrong: Transverse perineum muscle (\"Tetanus\") IS cut."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"During a correctly performed episiotomy, which structure should NOT be cut?","options":["Anal sphincter","Bulbospongiosus muscle","Posterior vaginal wall","Transverse perineum muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anal sphincter is explicitly listed as a structure that is never cut during episiotomy.  Why B/C/D wrong: Bulbospongiosus, posterior vaginal wall and transverse perineum muscle are all in the \"structures cut\" mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which suture material is used to repair an episiotomy?","options":["Silk (non-absorbable)","Chromic catgut \"0\" (absorbable suture)","Stainless steel wire","Polypropylene (non-absorbable)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Suture: Use chromic catgut '0' ↓ Absorbable suture.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are non-absorbable materials; the source specifies an absorbable suture (chromic catgut \"0\")."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Chromic catgut \"0\" used for episiotomy repair is classified as which type of suture?","options":["Absorbable suture","Non-absorbable suture","Monofilament synthetic suture","Permanent suture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly label chromic catgut \"0\" as an \"Absorbable suture.\"  Why B/D wrong: It is absorbable, not permanent/non-absorbable.  Why C wrong: Catgut is a natural (not synthetic monofilament) material and, more importantly, is absorbable."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"What is the correct sequence of layers repaired during episiotomy suturing?","options":["Skin → perineal muscles → vaginal mucosa","Vaginal mucosa → perineal muscles → skin","Perineal muscles → skin → vaginal mucosa","Vaginal mucosa → skin → perineal muscles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the repair \"Sequence: Vaginal mucosa ↓ Perineal muscles ↓ Skin.\"  Why A wrong: Repair begins with vaginal mucosa, not skin.  Why C/D wrong: These orderings do not match the stated inside-to-outside sequence."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"When repairing an episiotomy, which layer is sutured first?","options":["Skin","Perineal muscles","Vaginal mucosa","Subcutaneous fat"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The repair sequence begins with vaginal mucosa.  Why A/B wrong: Skin is last and perineal muscles are in the middle of the sequence.  Why D wrong: Subcutaneous fat is not part of the stated sequence."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"During management of labour, which general principle regarding asepsis is emphasized?","options":["Aseptic precautions are optional in low-risk deliveries","All aseptic precautions should be taken","Sterility is required only during suturing","Clean technique replaces aseptic technique"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"General → all aseptic precautions should be taken.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The source requires full aseptic precautions throughout labour management, without exceptions."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"According to the management of labour, what type of support should be provided to the labouring woman?","options":["Continuous emotional support and encouragement","Support only during the second stage","Support only if the woman requests it","No support to avoid interference"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Continuous emotional support, encouragement to be given.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Support is to be continuous, not restricted or withheld."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"As part of continuous supervision during labour, what fetal parameter is assessed?","options":["Fetal heart sound is auscultated","Fetal weight is measured","Fetal length is measured","Fetal position is fixed permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under \"Continuous supervision needed,\" the note lists \"Fetal heart sound auscultated.\"  Why B/C wrong: Fetal weight/length measurement is not the supervision parameter listed.  Why D wrong: Fetal position changes through cardinal movements; it is not \"fixed.\""},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In a LOW-RISK woman, how often should the fetal heart sound be auscultated during the FIRST stage of labour?","options":["Every 5 minutes","Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every 60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives low-risk female, 1st stage = 30 mins.  Why A wrong: 5 mins is the high-risk 2nd-stage interval.  Why B wrong: 15 mins is the low-risk 2nd stage / high-risk 1st stage interval.  Why D wrong: 60 minutes is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In a LOW-RISK woman, how often should the fetal heart be auscultated during the SECOND stage of labour?","options":["5 mins","15 mins","30 mins","45 mins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives low-risk female, 2nd stage = 15 mins.  Why A wrong: 5 mins is the high-risk 2nd-stage interval.  Why C wrong: 30 mins is the low-risk 1st stage interval.  Why D wrong: 45 mins is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In a HIGH-RISK woman, how often should the fetal heart be auscultated during the FIRST stage of labour?","options":["5 mins","15 mins","30 mins","60 mins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives high-risk female, 1st stage = 15 mins.  Why A wrong: 5 mins is the high-risk 2nd-stage interval.  Why C wrong: 30 mins is the low-risk 1st-stage interval.  Why D wrong: 60 mins is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In a HIGH-RISK woman, how often should the fetal heart be auscultated during the SECOND stage of labour?","options":["5 mins","15 mins","30 mins","45 mins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives high-risk female, 2nd stage = 5 mins.  Why B wrong: 15 mins is the low-risk 2nd stage / high-risk 1st stage interval.  Why C wrong: 30 mins is the low-risk 1st-stage interval.  Why D wrong: 45 mins is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which continuous electronic method is used to monitor the fetal condition during labour?","options":["Continuous CTG (cardiotocography) monitoring","Continuous ultrasound Doppler of the umbilical artery","Continuous fetal scalp pH sampling","Continuous MRI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists \"Continuous CTG monitoring.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the continuous monitoring method listed for labour in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which tool is used for monitoring the progress of labour according to the notes?","options":["Partograph plotting","Bishop score chart","APGAR chart","Growth chart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Partograph plotting for monitoring progress of labor.\"  Why B wrong: Bishop score assesses cervical readiness for induction, not labour progress plotting.  Why C wrong: APGAR assesses newborn condition at birth.  Why D wrong: Growth charts monitor child growth, not labour."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"During EARLY normal labour, what advice regarding maternal position/activity is given?","options":["The woman should be confined to bed at all times","The woman should NOT be confined to bed","The woman must remain in supine position","The woman must remain seated throughout"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In \"Early 'Normal' labor,\" the note states \"Not be confined to bed.\"  Why A wrong: This is the opposite of the recommendation.  Why C wrong: The note specifically says not to lie in supine position.  Why D wrong: No such restriction is stated."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"During the ACTIVE phase of labour, what is recommended regarding maternal position?","options":["Ambulation only","Bed (rest in bed)","Standing only","Squatting only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Active phase = Bed.\"  Why A wrong: Non-confinement to bed applies to early labour, not the active phase.  Why C/D wrong: These specific positions are not indicated for the active phase."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which maternal position should be AVOIDED during labour?","options":["Left lateral position","Supine position","Semi-Fowler's position","Upright position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note explicitly states \"Not to lie in supine position.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are not prohibited; supine is the one specifically to be avoided (risk of aortocaval compression)."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Why should the bowel not be loaded during labour (and an enema may be given)?","options":["A loaded bowel inhibits uterine contractions","A loaded bowel increases fetal heart rate","A loaded bowel causes hypertension","A loaded bowel accelerates cervical dilatation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow note states \"Bowel: Should not be loaded. Give enema ↓ Loaded bowel inhibits uterine contractions.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These effects are not stated; a loaded bowel impairs (inhibits) contractions."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In which conditions is an enema CONTRAINDICATED during labour management? (Select all that apply)","options":["Kidney failure","Intestinal inflammation","Intestinal blockage (obstruction)","Rigid perineum","Low-risk primigravida"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Enema is contraindicated → Kidney failure → Intestinal inflammation or blockage.\"  Why D wrong: Rigid perineum is an indication for episiotomy, not an enema contraindication.  Why E wrong: Low-risk primigravida is not a contraindication to enema."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"What is the recommendation for bladder care during labour?","options":["Encourage the mother to pass urine frequently","Insert an indwelling catheter routinely for all women","Restrict fluids to avoid urination","Keep the bladder full to aid descent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Bladder care: Encourage mother to pass urine frequently.\"  Why B wrong: Routine catheterization for all is not recommended.  Why C wrong: Fluids are not restricted to prevent urination.  Why D wrong: A full bladder obstructs descent; it should be emptied frequently."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Regarding diet in labour, why is solid food NOT allowed and only liquid food given?","options":["Because of delayed gastric emptying at the time of labour","Because solids cause hyperglycaemia","Because liquids speed up cervical dilatation","Because solids trigger uterine rupture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Diet: Delayed gastric emptying at time of labor so solid food not allowed. Liquid food given.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These reasons are not given; the concern is delayed gastric emptying (aspiration risk)."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which method of pain relief can be given during labour according to the notes?","options":["Epidural anaesthesia","General anaesthesia","No analgesia is permitted","Only oral opioids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Pain relief: Can give epidural anesthesia.\"  Why B wrong: General anaesthesia is not the labour analgesia mentioned.  Why C wrong: Pain relief is permitted (epidural).  Why D wrong: Oral opioids are not the method mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Immediately after delivery of the fetal head, what is the FIRST priority action?","options":["Suction blood and mucus from the mouth and pharynx","Clamp the cord immediately","Deliver the shoulders forcefully","Give an injection of oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Point 1 states \"After delivery of head = blood & mucus in mouth & pharynx should be suctioned.\"  Why B wrong: Cord clamping is not the immediate action after head delivery.  Why C wrong: Shoulders are delivered gently, not forcefully.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin timing is not the point emphasized here."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"When cleaning the newborn's eyelids after delivery of the head, in which direction should the sterile cotton swab be wiped?","options":["From outer canthus to inner canthus","From inner canthus to outer canthus","In a circular motion","From the upper lid to the lower lid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Point 2 states \"Clean eyelids with sterile cotton swab from inner canthus to outer canthus.\"  Why A wrong: The direction is inner → outer, not outer → inner.  Why C/D wrong: These directions are not specified in the source."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"During delivery of the head, the newborn's eyelids should be cleaned with:","options":["A sterile cotton swab","Plain tap water","Antibiotic ointment first","A dry gauze piece rubbed vigorously"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note specifies \"Clean eyelids with sterile cotton swab.\"  Why B wrong: Plain tap water is not specified and is not sterile.  Why C wrong: Antibiotic ointment is not mentioned as the cleaning method here.  Why D wrong: A sterile cotton swab wiped inner-to-outer is used, not vigorous dry rubbing."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which of the following is a point to be kept in mind during delivery of the head?","options":["Steps to prevent perineal laceration/tear should be taken","Fundal pressure should be applied routinely","The head should be delivered by extension only, ignoring the perineum","The mother should be placed supine and flat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Point 3 states \"Steps to prevent perineal laceration/tear should be taken.\"  Why B wrong: Routine fundal pressure is \"not done.\"  Why C wrong: Perineal protection is essential during head delivery.  Why D wrong: Supine position is to be avoided in labour."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"As part of episiotomy care, when should the patient be instructed to clean the episiotomy wound?","options":["Only once at bedtime","After every urination and defecation","Only during the daily bath","Only when pain is present"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Point 1 states maintenance of perineal hygiene: \"Instruct patient to clean episiotomy wound after every urination & defecation.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The instruction is after every urination and defecation, not just once, at bath, or only when painful."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"How often should perineal care be given to a patient with an episiotomy?","options":["Once daily","Twice daily","Every hour","Only on discharge day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Point 2 states \"Give perineal care: Twice daily to patient.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies twice daily."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"When cleaning an episiotomy wound with antiseptic solution, in which direction should the cotton swab be moved?","options":["From down to up","From up to down (front to back)","In a circular motion","Side to side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Clean episiotomy with antiseptic solution (Cotton swab = wound = from up to down).\"  Why A wrong: The direction is from up to down (to avoid contaminating the wound with rectal flora), not the reverse.  Why C/D wrong: These directions are not specified."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"With what should the episiotomy wound be cleaned during perineal care?","options":["Plain water","Antiseptic solution","Normal saline only","Hydrogen peroxide only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Clean episiotomy with antiseptic solution.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies an antiseptic solution."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which scoring system is used to assess an episiotomy wound for signs of healing/infection?","options":["Bishop score","REEDA score","APGAR score","Glasgow Coma Scale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Assess the wound using: Reeda score\" (source printed \"Abscess\" is a typo for \"Assess\").  Why A wrong: Bishop score assesses cervical readiness for induction.  Why C wrong: APGAR assesses newborn condition.  Why D wrong: GCS assesses level of consciousness."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In the REEDA score for episiotomy/perineal wound assessment, the first \"R\" stands for:","options":["Redness","Rigidity","Rupture","Retraction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists \"R – Redness.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the REEDA components listed."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In the REEDA score, the two \"E\" components stand for:","options":["Edema and Ecchymosis","Erythema and Exudate","Edema and Erosion","Ecchymosis and Erythema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"E – Edema\" and \"E – Ecchymosis.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated E-components are Edema and Ecchymosis."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"In the REEDA score, \"D\" and \"A\" stand for:","options":["Discharge and Approximation","Dehiscence and Abscess","Drainage and Adhesion","Discharge and Adhesion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"D – Discharge\" and \"A – Approximation (Appoximation).\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the D and A components in the source (Discharge, Approximation)."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"The REEDA score used to assess an episiotomy wound stands for which set of parameters?","options":["Redness, Edema, Ecchymosis, Discharge, Approximation","Redness, Erythema, Exudate, Drainage, Adhesion","Rigidity, Edema, Erosion, Dehiscence, Abscess","Redness, Edema, Erythema, Discharge, Adhesion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes spell out R–Redness, E–Edema, E–Ecchymosis, D–Discharge, A–Approximation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These lists contain terms not matching the source REEDA components."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which instruction should be given to a mother caring for her episiotomy wound?","options":["Sit with legs crossed to reduce pain","Do NOT sit with crossed legs","Remain on strict bed rest and avoid ambulation","Resume sexual activity after 1 week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists instructions to mother: \"Not sit crossed leg.\"  Why A wrong: This is the opposite of the instruction.  Why C wrong: Ambulation is actually encouraged.  Why D wrong: Abstinence is advised for 6 weeks, not 1 week."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Regarding mobility, what instruction is given to a mother with an episiotomy?","options":["Strict bed rest for 6 weeks","Ambulate","Avoid all movement","Only lie in supine position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists \"Ambulate\" as an instruction to the mother.  Why A/C/D wrong: Immobility/bed rest/supine positioning are not advised; ambulation is encouraged."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"For how long after delivery should sexual abstinence be advised to a mother with an episiotomy?","options":["2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Abstinence for 6 weeks after delivery.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies 6 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"A mother 3 days post-episiotomy asks the nurse for advice. Which set of instructions is correct?","options":["Sit cross-legged, stay in bed, and abstain from intercourse for 2 weeks","Do not sit cross-legged, ambulate, and abstain from intercourse for 6 weeks","Cross the legs, avoid walking, resume intercourse immediately","Remain supine, avoid ambulation, and clean the wound once weekly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The instructions to mother are: not sit crossed leg, ambulate, and abstinence for 6 weeks after delivery.  Why A/C/D wrong: They contradict one or more of these instructions (cross-legged sitting, bed rest/no ambulation, or wrong abstinence duration)."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"A midwife wants to reduce the risk of the episiotomy extending into the anal sphincter. Which type of episiotomy should she choose, and why?","options":["Median, because it involves fewer muscle fibers","Mediolateral, because it can never involve the anal sphincter no matter how much it is extended","Median, because it heals faster","Mediolateral, because it causes less bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the mediolateral episiotomy \"can never involve anal sphincter no matter how much it is extended\" and is therefore preferred.  Why A/C wrong: The median type risks anal sphincter involvement if it extends, despite cutting fewer fibers and healing faster.  Why D wrong: The mediolateral type actually causes MORE bleeding; the correct reason is sphincter safety."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"A woman in the second stage of labour is classified as high-risk. The fetal heart should be auscultated at which interval?","options":["Every 30 minutes","Every 15 minutes","Every 5 minutes","Every 60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For a high-risk female in the 2nd stage, the auscultation interval is 5 mins (most frequent monitoring for the highest-risk situation).  Why A wrong: 30 mins is the low-risk 1st-stage interval.  Why B wrong: 15 mins is the low-risk 2nd stage / high-risk 1st stage.  Why D wrong: 60 minutes is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Which statement correctly compares median and mediolateral episiotomy healing and repair?","options":["Median heals fast and is easy to repair; mediolateral heals slow and is difficult to repair","Median heals slow; mediolateral heals fast","Both heal at the same rate","Mediolateral is easier to repair than median"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows median → easy repair, healing fast; mediolateral → repair difficult, healing slow.  Why B wrong: This reverses the correct healing comparison.  Why C wrong: They differ in healing rate.  Why D wrong: Mediolateral repair is more difficult, not easier."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"Why does a mediolateral episiotomy cause more bleeding and more post-delivery pain than a median episiotomy?","options":["Because more muscle fibers are cut","Because it is closer to the clitoris","Because it uses non-absorbable sutures","Because it is performed without anaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The disadvantage chain begins \"More muscle fibers are cut → more bleeding → repair difficult → healing slow → more pain/dyspareunia.\"  Why B wrong: Proximity to the clitoris is not the reason given.  Why C wrong: Chromic catgut (absorbable) is used for both.  Why D wrong: Both are done under local anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Cardinal movements of labour…","question":"A woman in early normal labour asks if she must stay in bed. Based on the notes, the nurse should tell her that in early labour she:","options":["Must stay confined to bed","Should not be confined to bed and may move about","Must remain supine","Must remain seated at all times"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In early normal labour the note says \"Not be confined to bed\"; bed rest is reserved for the active phase.  Why A wrong: Confinement to bed applies to the active phase, not early labour.  Why C wrong: Supine position should be avoided.  Why D wrong: No such restriction is stated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The first stage of labor begins with true labor pain and ends with which of the following?","options":["Delivery of the baby","Full dilatation of cervix (10 cm)","Delivery of the placenta","Rupture of membranes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 1st stage begins with true labor pain and ends with full dilatation of the cervix = 10 cms.  Why A wrong: Delivery of baby marks the end of the 2nd stage.  Why C wrong: Delivery of placenta marks the end of the 3rd stage.  Why D wrong: Rupture of membranes is not the defining end-point of the 1st stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Full dilatation of the cervix corresponds to how many centimeters?","options":["6 cm","8 cm","10 cm","12 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Full dilatation = 10 cms dilated cervix.  Why A wrong: 6 cm is the start of the active phase (recent guideline).  Why B wrong: 8 cm is not full dilatation.  Why D wrong: The cervix does not dilate beyond 10 cm; full is 10 cm."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The second stage of labor begins with full dilatation of cervix and ends with which event?","options":["Delivery of placenta","Delivery of the baby","Onset of true labor pain","Retraction of the uterus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 2nd stage begins with full dilatation of cervix and ends with delivery of baby (fetus).  Why A wrong: Delivery of placenta ends the 3rd stage.  Why C wrong: True labor pain begins the 1st stage.  Why D wrong: Retraction is not the defined end-point of the 2nd stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The third stage of labor begins with delivery of the baby and ends with which of the following?","options":["Full dilatation of cervix","Delivery of the placenta","Onset of true labor pain","1 hour observation period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 3rd stage begins with delivery of baby and ends with delivery of placenta.  Why A wrong: Full dilatation ends the 1st stage.  Why C wrong: True labor pain starts the 1st stage.  Why D wrong: The 1 hour observation period is the 4th stage, after placental delivery."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The fourth stage of labor is defined as which of the following?","options":["From full dilatation to delivery of baby","1 hour observation period after delivery of placenta","From true labor pain to full dilatation","Delivery of baby to delivery of placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 4th stage = 1 hour observation period after delivery of placenta.  Why A wrong: That describes the 2nd stage.  Why C wrong: That describes the 1st stage.  Why D wrong: That describes the 3rd stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"According to recent guidelines, the latent phase of the first stage extends up to how many centimeters of cervical dilatation?","options":["Up to 3 cm","Up to 4 cm","Up to 5 cm","Up to 6 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Recent guidelines: latent phase = up to 5 cms dilatation of cervix.  Why A wrong: Up to 3 cm is the WHO/partogram definition, not the recent guideline.  Why B wrong: 4 cm is the WHO/partogram start of active phase, not the latent limit.  Why D wrong: 6 cm is where the active phase begins per recent guideline."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"As per recent guidelines, the active phase of the first stage is defined by cervical dilatation of:","options":["4–10 cm","6–10 cm","3–8 cm","5–10 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Recent guideline: active phase = 6–10 cms dilatation of cervix.  Why A wrong: 4–10 cm is the WHO/partogram definition.  Why C wrong: Not a stated range.  Why D wrong: Not the recent-guideline range."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"As per WHO/Partogram, the latent phase extends up to what dilatation and the active phase is defined as what range?","options":["Latent up to 5 cm; active 6–10 cm","Latent up to 3 cm; active 4–10 cm","Latent up to 4 cm; active 5–10 cm","Latent up to 2 cm; active 3–10 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO/Partogram: latent phase up to 3 cm; active phase 4–10 cm dilated.  Why A wrong: That is the recent-guideline set of values.  Why C wrong: Incorrect cut-offs.  Why D wrong: Incorrect cut-offs."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The normal duration of the latent phase of the first stage of labor in a nulliparous woman is:","options":["8 hours","12 hours","20 hours","6 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal latent-phase duration in nulliparous = 12 hours.  Why A wrong: 8 hours is the multiparous value.  Why C wrong: ≥ 20 hours defines a prolonged latent phase in nulliparous.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The normal duration of the latent phase of the first stage of labor in a multiparous woman is:","options":["12 hours","10 hours","8 hours","14 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal latent-phase duration in multiparous = 8 hours.  Why A wrong: 12 hours is the nulliparous value.  Why B wrong: Not stated.  Why D wrong: ≥ 14 hours defines a prolonged latent phase in multiparous."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"A prolonged first-stage latent phase in a nulliparous woman is defined as a duration of:","options":["≥ 8 hours","≥ 12 hours","≥ 20 hours","≥ 14 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged latent phase, nulliparous = ≥ 20 hours.  Why A wrong: ≥ 8 hours relates to multiparous 1st stage prolongation figures, not the ≥20 h latent criterion.  Why B wrong: ≥ 12 hours is the general 1st-stage prolonged figure for nulliparous in the table, not the latent-phase ≥20 h criterion.  Why D wrong: ≥ 14 hours is the multiparous prolonged latent-phase value."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"A prolonged first-stage latent phase in a multiparous woman is defined as a duration of:","options":["≥ 20 hours","≥ 14 hours","≥ 8 hours","≥ 12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged latent phase, multiparous = ≥ 14 hours.  Why A wrong: ≥ 20 hours is the nulliparous value.  Why C wrong: 8 hours is the normal (not prolonged) multiparous latent duration.  Why D wrong: ≥ 12 hours is a nulliparous figure."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"According to the stages table, a prolonged first stage in a nulliparous woman is:","options":["≥ 8 hours","≥ 12 hours","≥ 20 hours","≥ 2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Table lists prolonged 1st-stage latent phase, nulliparous = ≥ 12 hours.  Why A wrong: ≥ 8 hours is the multiparous value in the same table.  Why C wrong: ≥ 20 hours is the separately-stated prolonged latent-phase criterion.  Why D wrong: ≥ 2 hours relates to the prolonged 2nd stage (nulliparous)."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"According to the stages table, a prolonged first stage in a multiparous woman is:","options":["≥ 12 hours","≥ 8 hours","≥ 1 hour","≥ 14 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Table lists prolonged 1st-stage, multiparous = ≥ 8 hours.  Why A wrong: ≥ 12 hours is the nulliparous value.  Why C wrong: ≥ 1 hour is the prolonged 2nd stage (multiparous).  Why D wrong: ≥ 14 hours is the separately-stated prolonged latent criterion."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The normal duration of the second stage of labor in a nulliparous woman is:","options":["30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours","15 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 2nd stage normal duration, nulliparous = 1 hour.  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is the multiparous value.  Why C wrong: 2 hours is prolonged 2nd stage (nulliparous).  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The normal (average) duration of the second stage of labor in a multiparous woman is:","options":["1 hour","30 minutes","2 hours","45 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 2nd stage average duration, multiparous = 30 minutes.  Why A wrong: 1 hour is the nulliparous value.  Why C wrong: 2 hours is prolonged nulliparous.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"A prolonged second stage of labor in a nulliparous woman is defined as:","options":["≥ 1 hour","≥ 2 hours","≥ 30 minutes","≥ 12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged 2nd stage, nulliparous = ≥ 2 hours.  Why A wrong: ≥ 1 hour is the multiparous prolonged value.  Why C wrong: 30 minutes is normal multiparous duration.  Why D wrong: ≥ 12 hours relates to the 1st stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"A prolonged second stage of labor in a multiparous woman is defined as:","options":["≥ 2 hours","≥ 1 hour","≥ 30 minutes","≥ 8 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged 2nd stage, multiparous = ≥ 1 hour.  Why A wrong: ≥ 2 hours is the nulliparous value.  Why C wrong: 30 minutes is the normal (not prolonged) multiparous duration.  Why D wrong: ≥ 8 hours relates to the 1st stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In passive management, the normal duration of the third stage of labor is:","options":["5–10 minutes","15–25 minutes","30–45 minutes","1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 3rd stage passively = 15–25 mins.  Why A wrong: 5–10 mins is with active management.  Why C wrong: Not stated.  Why D wrong: 1 hour is the 4th stage observation."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"With active management, the duration of the third stage of labor is:","options":["15–25 minutes","5–10 minutes","30 minutes","1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 3rd stage active management = 5–10 mins.  Why A wrong: 15–25 mins is passive management.  Why C wrong: > 30 minutes defines prolonged 3rd stage.  Why D wrong: 1 hour is the 4th stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"A prolonged third stage of labor (retained placenta) is defined as a duration of:","options":["> 15 minutes","> 30 minutes","> 1 hour","> 10 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged 3rd stage = more than 30 minutes, which leads to retained placenta.  Why A wrong: 15 min is within passive-management range.  Why C wrong: 1 hour is the 4th stage observation period.  Why D wrong: 10 min is within active-management range."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The normal duration of the fourth stage of labor is:","options":["30 minutes","15–25 minutes","1 hour","2 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 4th stage = 1 hour observation period after delivery of placenta.  Why A wrong: Not the 4th-stage value.  Why B wrong: That is 3rd-stage passive management.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Which finding/complaint is characteristically experienced by the mother during the fourth stage of labor?","options":["Physiological chills","Bandl's ring","Foul-smelling discharge","Hematuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table and notes state physiological chills are experienced by the mother/patient in the 4th stage.  Why B wrong: Bandl's ring is a sign of obstructed labor.  Why C wrong: Foul-smelling discharge is a per-vaginal finding of obstructed labor.  Why D wrong: Hematuria is a per-vaginal finding of obstructed labor."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The first stage of labor brings about which primary change?","options":["Delivery of the placenta","Dilatation of the cervix","Cardinal movements of the fetus","Involution of the uterus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 1st stage begins with onset of true labor pains and brings about dilatation of the cervix, ending with full dilatation (10 cm).  Why A wrong: Placental delivery is the 3rd stage.  Why C wrong: Cardinal movements are the main event of the 2nd stage.  Why D wrong: Involution is a postpartum process, not the 1st stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The two important events of the latent phase of the first stage of labor are dilatation of the cervix and:","options":["Descent of fetal head","Effacement (shortening) of the cervix","Rotation of the shoulders","Rupture of membranes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Latent-phase important events = 1. Dilatation of cervix, 2. Effacement of cervix (shortening of cervix).  Why A wrong: Descent of fetal head is a main event of the active phase.  Why C wrong: Not listed as a latent-phase event.  Why D wrong: Not listed as a latent-phase event."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Effacement of the cervix refers to which of the following?","options":["Dilatation (widening) of the cervix","Shortening of the cervix","Lengthening of the cervix","Rupture of the cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Effacement = shortening of the cervix.  Why A wrong: Dilatation is widening, a separate event.  Why C wrong: The cervix shortens, not lengthens.  Why D wrong: Not the meaning of effacement."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The average duration of the first stage of labor in a nulliparous/primigravida woman is:","options":["8 hours","12 hours","6 hours","20 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Average duration nulliparous/primigravida = 12 hours.  Why A wrong: 8 hours is the multiparous/multigravida value.  Why C wrong: Not stated.  Why D wrong: ≥ 20 hours is prolonged latent phase."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The average duration of the first stage of labor in a multiparous/multigravida woman is:","options":["12 hours","8 hours","14 hours","4 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Average duration multiparous/multigravida = 8 hours.  Why A wrong: 12 hours is the nulliparous value.  Why C wrong: ≥ 14 hours is prolonged latent phase, multiparous.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"What is the differential diagnosis (D/D) of a prolonged latent phase?","options":["Obstructed labor","False labor pain","Placenta previa","Uterine rupture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D/D of prolonged latent phase = false labor pain.  Why A wrong: Obstructed labor is a distinct condition, not the D/D of prolonged latent phase.  Why C wrong: Placenta previa is an antepartum hemorrhage cause, not stated here.  Why D wrong: Uterine rupture is a complication of obstructed labor, not the D/D."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Which of the following is the first management step for a prolonged latent phase?","options":["Immediate cesarean section","Therapeutic rest to the patient","Instrumental delivery","Manual removal of placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management of prolonged latent phase begins with therapeutic rest to the patient.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is not the first step for prolonged latent phase.  Why C wrong: Instrumental delivery applies to prolonged 2nd stage.  Why D wrong: Manual removal of placenta applies to retained placenta (3rd stage)."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The two key events occurring during the first stage of labor (per the Events flow chart) are:","options":["Dilatation of cervix and shortening (effacement) of cervix","Descent of head and internal rotation","Delivery of baby and placenta","Crowning and restitution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Events flow chart lists 1. Dilatation of cervix and 2. Shortening of cervix (→ effacement).  Why B wrong: These are fetal descent/rotation events, not the two listed 1st-stage cervical events.  Why C wrong: These are delivery outcomes of later stages.  Why D wrong: Crowning/restitution are cardinal movements, not the listed events."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The length of the cervix during pregnancy is approximately:","options":["2 cm","4 cm","5 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Length of cervix in pregnancy = 4 cms.  Why A wrong: Not the stated pregnancy cervical length.  Why C wrong: 5 cm is the lower uterine segment length in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: 10 cm relates to full cervical dilatation, not cervical length."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"As the cervix shortens during labor, it becomes part of which structure?","options":["Upper uterine segment","Lower uterine segment","Vaginal canal","Fundus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the cervix shortens, it becomes a part of the lower uterine segment.  Why A wrong: The upper uterine segment is the contractile/retracting portion, not formed by the shortened cervix.  Why C wrong: The cervix does not become part of the vagina.  Why D wrong: The fundus is the top of the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The lower uterine segment is formed from which anatomical structure?","options":["Fundus","Isthmus","Cervical canal","Body of uterus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lower uterine segment is formed by the isthmus.  Why A wrong: The fundus is the upper portion of the uterus.  Why C wrong: The cervical canal is not the origin of the LUS.  Why D wrong: The body forms the upper uterine segment."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The isthmus is described as which part of the uterus?","options":["Uppermost part of the uterus","Lowermost part of the uterus (0.5 cm)","The fundal region","The external os"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isthmus is the lowermost part of the uterus, measuring 0.5 cms.  Why A wrong: It is the lowermost, not uppermost part.  Why C wrong: The fundus is uppermost.  Why D wrong: The external os is the vaginal opening of the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The upper boundary of the isthmus is the:","options":["Histological internal os","Anatomical internal os","External os","Cervical canal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Boundary of isthmus — above = anatomical internal os.  Why A wrong: The histological internal os is the lower boundary.  Why C wrong: The external os is not a boundary of the isthmus.  Why D wrong: The cervical canal is not the isthmus boundary."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The lower boundary of the isthmus is the:","options":["Anatomical internal os","Histological internal os","External os","Fundus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Boundary of isthmus — below = histological internal os.  Why A wrong: The anatomical internal os is the upper boundary.  Why C wrong: The external os is not the isthmus boundary.  Why D wrong: The fundus is the uppermost part of the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The length of the isthmus in a non-pregnant female is:","options":["4 cm","5 cm","0.5 cm","1 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Length of isthmus in non-pregnant female = 0.5 cms.  Why A wrong: 4 cm is cervical length in pregnancy.  Why B wrong: 5 cm is LUS length in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The length of the lower uterine segment during pregnancy is:","options":["0.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Lower uterine segment length in pregnancy = 5 cms.  Why A wrong: 0.5 cm is the non-pregnant isthmus length.  Why B wrong: 4 cm is cervical length in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: 10 cm is the total LUS + cervix length at labor."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"At the time of labor, the total length of the lower uterine segment (10 cms) is made up of which components?","options":["LUS (5 cm) + Cervix (4–5 cm)","LUS (4 cm) + Cervix (6 cm)","LUS (10 cm) alone","Isthmus (0.5 cm) + Cervix (9.5 cm)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At labor, LUS length (10 cms) = LUS (5 cm) + Cervix (4–5 cms).  Why B wrong: Incorrect breakdown.  Why C wrong: The 10 cm is a composite, not LUS alone.  Why D wrong: Incorrect breakdown."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In the active phase, the rate of cervical dilatation in a nulliparous woman is:","options":["1 cm/hr","1.2 cm/hr","1.5 cm/hr","2 cm/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rate of dilatation in active phase, nulliparous = 1.2 cm/hr.  Why A wrong: 1 cm/hr is the WHO minimum dilatation.  Why C wrong: 1.5 cm/hr is the multiparous rate.  Why D wrong: 2 cm/hr is the descent rate in multiparous."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In the active phase, the rate of cervical dilatation in a multiparous woman is:","options":["1 cm/hr","1.2 cm/hr","1.5 cm/hr","2 cm/hr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Rate of dilatation in active phase, multiparous = 1.5 cms/hr.  Why A wrong: 1 cm/hr is the WHO minimum.  Why B wrong: 1.2 cm/hr is the nulliparous rate.  Why D wrong: 2 cm/hr is the multiparous descent rate."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"As per WHO, the minimum rate of cervical dilatation (for nulliparous or multiparous) in the active phase is:","options":["0.5 cm/hr","1 cm/hr","1.2 cm/hr","2 cm/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Minimum dilatation (nulliparous or multiparous) per WHO = 1 cm/hr.  Why A wrong: Not stated.  Why C wrong: 1.2 cm/hr is the nulliparous active-phase rate.  Why D wrong: 2 cm/hr is the multiparous descent rate."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The two main events of the active phase of the first stage of labor are dilatation of cervix and:","options":["Effacement of cervix","Descent of fetal head","Delivery of placenta","Rupture of membranes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Main events of the active phase = 1. Dilatation of cervix, 2. Descent of fetal head.  Why A wrong: Effacement is a latent-phase event.  Why C wrong: Placental delivery is the 3rd stage.  Why D wrong: Not listed as a main active-phase event."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The rate of descent of the fetal head in the active phase for a nulliparous woman is:","options":["1 cm/hr","1.2 cm/hr","2 cm/hr","1.5 cm/hr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Descent of fetal head, nulliparous = 1 cm/hr.  Why B wrong: 1.2 cm/hr is the nulliparous dilatation rate, not descent.  Why C wrong: 2 cm/hr is the multiparous descent rate.  Why D wrong: 1.5 cm/hr is the multiparous dilatation rate."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The rate of descent of the fetal head in the active phase for a multiparous woman is:","options":["1 cm/hr","1.2 cm/hr","2 cm/hr","1.5 cm/hr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Descent of fetal head, multiparous = 2 cm/hr.  Why A wrong: 1 cm/hr is the nulliparous descent rate.  Why B wrong: 1.2 cm/hr is the nulliparous dilatation rate.  Why D wrong: 1.5 cm/hr is the multiparous dilatation rate."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"A protracted active phase means the active phase is progressing slowly, with the cervix dilating at a rate of:","options":["> 2 cm/hr","< 1 cm/hr","Exactly 1.2 cm/hr","1.5 cm/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protracted active phase = cervix dilates at rate < 1 cm/hr (slow progress).  Why A wrong: > 2 cm/hr is faster than normal.  Why C wrong: 1.2 cm/hr is the normal nulliparous rate.  Why D wrong: 1.5 cm/hr is the normal multiparous rate."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The first management step in a protracted active phase is to:","options":["Perform cesarean section immediately","Rule out cephalopelvic disproportion","Apply forceps","Give therapeutic rest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management of protracted active phase — 1. Rule out cephalopelvic disproportion.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is not the first step here.  Why C wrong: Forceps apply to prolonged 2nd stage.  Why D wrong: Therapeutic rest is for prolonged latent phase."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Augmentation (acceleration) of labor in a protracted active phase is achieved by:","options":["Bed rest and hydration only","Artificial rupture of membrane + oxytocin","Cesarean section","Forceps delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Augmentation of labor (accelerate labor) = Artificial rupture of membrane + oxytocin.  Why A wrong: This does not accelerate labor.  Why C wrong: Cesarean is not augmentation of labor.  Why D wrong: Forceps is an instrumental delivery method, not augmentation."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Active phase arrest is defined as:","options":["≥ 2 hours have passed with no descent","≥ 4 hours have passed but there is no dilatation of cervix","≥ 1 hour of no contractions","≥ 30 minutes of no placental delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Active phase arrest = ≥ 4 hours have passed but there is no dilatation of cervix.  Why A wrong: Incorrect definition.  Why C wrong: Not the definition of arrest.  Why D wrong: That relates to retained placenta."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The management of active phase arrest is:","options":["Therapeutic rest","Cesarean section","Oxytocin augmentation","Vacuum delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management of active phase arrest = cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Therapeutic rest is for prolonged latent phase.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin augmentation is for protracted active phase.  Why D wrong: Vacuum is for prolonged 2nd stage (not arrest)."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The main event of the second stage of labor is:","options":["Dilatation of the cervix","Cardinal movements of the fetus","Delivery of the placenta","Effacement of the cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Main event of the 2nd stage = cardinal movement of fetus.  Why A wrong: Dilatation is the 1st-stage event.  Why C wrong: Placental delivery is the 3rd stage.  Why D wrong: Effacement is a latent-phase (1st stage) event."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The prolonged duration of the second stage in a nulliparous/primigravida woman is:","options":["1 hour","2 hours","30 minutes","3 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 2nd stage — prolonged, nulliparous/primigravida = 2 hours (average = 1 hour).  Why A wrong: 1 hour is the average, not prolonged.  Why C wrong: 30 minutes is the multiparous average.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The prolonged duration of the second stage in a multiparous woman is:","options":["30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours","45 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 2nd stage — prolonged, multiparous = 1 hour (average = 30 minutes).  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is the average, not prolonged.  Why C wrong: 2 hours is the nulliparous prolonged value.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The guiding principle in the management of a prolonged second stage of labor is to:","options":["Give therapeutic rest","Deliver the baby fast","Wait and watch","Perform manual removal of placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Principle of managing prolonged 2nd stage — we want to deliver the baby fast.  Why A wrong: Rest is for prolonged latent phase.  Why C wrong: Wait & watch is never done in obstructed labor and is inappropriate for prolonged 2nd stage.  Why D wrong: That is a 3rd-stage (retained placenta) intervention."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The two methods used to deliver the baby fast in a prolonged second stage are cesarean section and:","options":["Oxytocin augmentation","Instrumental delivery (forceps/vacuum)","Therapeutic rest","Manual placental removal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 2 methods = 1. Cesarean section, 2. Instrumental delivery (forceps and vacuum).  Why A wrong: Oxytocin augmentation is for protracted active phase.  Why C wrong: Rest is for prolonged latent phase.  Why D wrong: That is a 3rd-stage intervention."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The two types of instrumental delivery for a prolonged second stage are:","options":["Forceps and vacuum","Forceps and cesarean","Vacuum and oxytocin","Symphysiotomy and vacuum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Instrumental delivery methods = forceps and vacuum.  Why B wrong: Cesarean is a separate method, not instrumental.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is a drug for augmentation, not instrumental delivery.  Why D wrong: Symphysiotomy is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Forceps and vacuum can be applied only when the station of the fetal head is at which level?","options":["\"0 station\" or above","\"+2 station\" or below it","\"-2 station\"","Above \"+2 station\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Forceps & vacuum can be applied only when station of fetal head is at \"+2 station\" or below it.  Why A wrong: Not the stated criterion.  Why C wrong: -2 is above the required level.  Why D wrong: If station is above +2, cesarean is done instead."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In the management of a prolonged second stage, if the fetal head station is at \"+2 or below it,\" the appropriate delivery is:","options":["Cesarean section","Forceps/vacuum delivery","Therapeutic rest","Wait and watch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Station +2 or below it → forceps/vacuum delivery.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is done when the station is above +2.  Why C wrong: Rest is not appropriate for prolonged 2nd stage.  Why D wrong: Wait and watch is inappropriate."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In the management of a prolonged second stage, if the fetal head station is above \"+2,\" the appropriate management is:","options":["Forceps delivery","Vacuum delivery","Cesarean section","Oxytocin infusion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Station above +2 → cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Forceps requires +2 or below.  Why B wrong: Vacuum requires +2 or below.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin does not resolve an above-+2 head in a prolonged 2nd stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In the presence of fetal distress during labor, which is TRUE regarding instrumental delivery?","options":["Vacuum is preferred over forceps","Cannot apply vacuum/forceps; do cesarean section","Both forceps and vacuum are contraindicated always","Only oxytocin is given"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In fetal distress = cannot apply vacuum/forceps → do cesarean section (as a rule when distress precludes instrumental use).  Why A wrong: Vacuum is never used in fetal distress.  Why C wrong: Forceps may still be used in the 2nd stage if station is +2 or below (see distress flow chart); it is not always contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not the answer for fetal distress."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Bandl's ring is described as which of the following?","options":["A ring of scar tissue in the cervix","Per-abdomen depression between the upper uterine segment & lower uterine segment","A fetal skull suture line","A vaginal fornix landmark"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bandl's ring = per-abdomen depression between upper uterine segment & lower uterine segment on the patient's abdomen.  Why A wrong: It is not cervical scar tissue.  Why C wrong: It is a uterine finding, not a fetal skull suture.  Why D wrong: It is not a vaginal fornix landmark."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The appearance of Bandl's ring indicates which of the following?","options":["Normal labor progress","Obstructed labor","Placenta previa","Retained placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bandl's ring indicates obstructed labor, and the management is to do a cesarean section.  Why A wrong: It is an abnormal, ominous sign.  Why C wrong: Placenta previa is a separate condition.  Why D wrong: Retained placenta is a 3rd-stage complication."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"When Bandl's ring appears (indicating obstructed labor), the management is:","options":["Oxytocin augmentation","Cesarean section","Vacuum delivery","Therapeutic rest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bandl's ring → indicates obstructed labor → do cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin increases risk of rupture in obstructed labor.  Why C wrong: Vacuum is not used.  Why D wrong: Rest/wait-and-watch is contraindicated in obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In the management of fetal distress during the FIRST stage of labor (cervix not fully dilated), the appropriate action is:","options":["Forceps delivery","Vacuum delivery","Cesarean section","Oxytocin infusion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal distress in 1st stage → cervix not fully dilated → cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Forceps requires full dilatation and appropriate station.  Why B wrong: Vacuum is never used in fetal distress.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin worsens distress."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In fetal distress during the SECOND stage of labor with the fetal head station at \"+2 or below it,\" the appropriate delivery is:","options":["Cesarean section","Vacuum delivery","Forceps","Wait and watch"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal distress in 2nd stage, station +2 or below → FORCEPS.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is for station above +2.  Why B wrong: Vacuum is never used in fetal distress.  Why D wrong: Wait and watch is inappropriate in distress."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In fetal distress during the second stage with the fetal head station above \"+2,\" the appropriate management is:","options":["Forceps","Cesarean section","Vacuum delivery","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal distress in 2nd stage, station above +2 → cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Forceps needs +2 or below.  Why C wrong: Vacuum is never used in fetal distress.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin worsens distress."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Which instrumental method should NEVER be used in the presence of fetal distress?","options":["Forceps","Vacuum","Both forceps and vacuum","Neither; both are safe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vacuum is never used in fetal distress.  Why A wrong: Forceps may be used in the 2nd stage at +2 or below.  Why C wrong: Only vacuum is prohibited; forceps may still be used at appropriate station.  Why D wrong: Vacuum is explicitly prohibited in distress."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Obstructed labor is defined by which combination of features?","options":["Uterus not contracting + cervix closed + intact membranes","Uterus contracting + cervix fully dilated + membranes ruptured, but delivery does not happen due to obstruction","Cervix dilating < 1 cm/hr with no contractions","Placenta retained beyond 30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Obstructed labor = uterus is contracting + cervix is fully dilated + membranes are ruptured, but delivery of fetus does not happen due to some obstruction in labor.  Why A wrong: The uterus IS contracting and the cervix IS fully dilated in obstructed labor.  Why C wrong: That describes protracted active phase.  Why D wrong: That describes retained placenta."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Which of the following are GENERAL symptoms of obstructed labor in the mother? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dehydrated","Exhausted","Tachypneic","Bradycardic","Hypothermic"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: General symptoms of obstructed labor: mother = dehydrated, exhausted, tachypneic.  Why D wrong: Bradycardia is not listed as a general symptom.  Why E wrong: Hypothermia is not listed as a general symptom."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"On per-abdomen examination in obstructed labor, a ring/depression felt and seen between the upper and lower uterine segments is called:","options":["Retraction ring of pregnancy","Bandl's ring","Constriction ring","Braun's ring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per abdomen — ring/depression felt & seen between upper uterine segment & lower uterine segment is called Bandl's ring.  Why A wrong: Not the term used in the notes.  Why C wrong: Not the term used here.  Why D wrong: Not a recognized term in this context."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"On per-abdomen examination in obstructed labor, the fetal heart sound is characteristically:","options":["Regular and normal","Absent/distress","Accelerated","Unchanged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per abdomen finding — fetal heart sound → absent/distress.  Why A wrong: FHS is not normal in obstructed labor.  Why C wrong: It shows distress, not acceleration.  Why D wrong: It is abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Which of the following are per-vaginal findings in obstructed labor? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bleeding per vagina","Hot vagina","Hematuria","Caput on fetal head + moulding of fetal head","Foul-smelling discharge"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Per-vaginal findings of obstructed labor: 1. Bleeding per vagina, 2. Hot vagina, 3. Hematuria, 4. Caput on fetal head + moulding of fetal head, 5. Foul-smelling discharge.  Why D wrong: (It is correct — caput + moulding is listed.)  Why E wrong: (It is correct — foul-smelling discharge is listed.)"},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Hematuria in a woman with obstructed labor most directly reflects:","options":["A urinary tract infection unrelated to labor","Bladder trauma/pressure from the obstruction","Normal labor findings","Placental separation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hematuria is listed as a per-vaginal finding of obstructed labor, reflecting pressure/trauma to the bladder from the obstruction.  Why A wrong: It is a feature of obstructed labor here, not an unrelated UTI.  Why C wrong: Hematuria is not a normal labor finding.  Why D wrong: It is not related to placental separation."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In the management of obstructed labor, which of the following should NEVER be done? (Select all that apply)","options":["Wait & watch","Give oxytocin","Instrumental delivery","Cesarean section","Immediate delivery of the baby"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: In obstructed labor, NEVER: wait & watch, give oxytocin (increases chances of rupture), or perform instrumental delivery.  Why D wrong: Cesarean section is exactly what should ALWAYS be done.  Why E wrong: This is not one of the listed \"never\" items."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Why is oxytocin contraindicated (never given) in obstructed labor?","options":["It reduces uterine contractions","It increases the chances of uterine rupture","It causes fetal bradycardia only","It delays cervical dilatation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Giving oxytocin in obstructed labor increases chances of (uterine) rupture.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin increases, not reduces, contractions — worsening the obstruction.  Why C wrong: The stated reason is increased rupture risk.  Why D wrong: Not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In obstructed labor, the management that should ALWAYS be done is:","options":["Instrumental delivery","Cesarean section","Oxytocin augmentation","Wait and watch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In obstructed labor — Always do: cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Instrumental delivery is in the \"never\" list.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is in the \"never\" list (increases rupture risk).  Why D wrong: Wait and watch is in the \"never\" list."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Which of the following are complications of obstructed labor? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fetal distress/fetal death","Uterine rupture","Vesicovaginal fistula (long-term)","Gestational diabetes","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Complications of obstructed labor = 1. Fetal distress/fetal death, 2. Uterine rupture; long-term complication = vesicovaginal fistula.  Why D wrong: Gestational diabetes is not a complication of obstructed labor.  Why E wrong: Placenta previa is a separate antepartum condition."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The long-term complication of obstructed labor is:","options":["Uterine rupture","Fetal death","Vesicovaginal fistula","Postpartum hemorrhage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Long-term complication of obstructed labor = vesicovaginal fistula.  Why A wrong: Uterine rupture is an acute complication.  Why B wrong: Fetal death is an acute complication.  Why D wrong: PPH is not listed as the long-term complication here."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In developing countries, the most common cause of vesicovaginal fistula is:","options":["Pelvic surgery","Obstructed labor","Radiation therapy","Malignancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Q1 states the most common cause of vesicovaginal fistula in developing countries → obstructed labor.  Why A wrong: Surgery is a cause more typical of developed countries, not the answer here.  Why C wrong: Radiation is not the stated common cause.  Why D wrong: Malignancy is not the stated common cause."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The third stage of labor begins with delivery of the baby and ends with:","options":["Full dilatation of cervix","Delivery of the placenta","The 1-hour observation period","Cardinal movements of the fetus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Third stage begins with delivery of baby and ends with delivery of placenta.  Why A wrong: Full dilatation ends the 1st stage.  Why C wrong: The 1-hour observation is the 4th stage.  Why D wrong: Cardinal movements are the main event of the 2nd stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The main event of the third stage of labor is:","options":["Delivery of the baby","Delivery of the placenta","Dilatation of the cervix","Cardinal movement of the fetus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Main event of the 3rd stage = delivery of placenta.  Why A wrong: Delivery of baby begins (not the main event of) the 3rd stage.  Why C wrong: Dilatation is the 1st-stage event.  Why D wrong: Cardinal movement is the 2nd-stage main event."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Prolonged third stage / retained placenta is defined as:","options":["≥ 30 mins have passed after delivery of baby but placenta hasn't delivered","≥ 15 mins after delivery of baby","≥ 1 hour after delivery of baby","≥ 4 hours of no cervical dilatation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged third stage/retained placenta = ≥ 30 mins have passed after delivery of baby but placenta hasn't delivered.  Why B wrong: 15 min is within the passive-management range.  Why C wrong: 1 hour is the 4th-stage observation period.  Why D wrong: That describes active phase arrest."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In active management of the third stage of labor, the expected duration is:","options":["15–25 min","5–10 mins","30 min","1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Third-stage active management = 5–10 mins.  Why A wrong: 15–25 min is passive management.  Why C wrong: > 30 min indicates prolonged/retained placenta.  Why D wrong: 1 hour is the 4th stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"In passive management of the third stage of labor, the expected duration is:","options":["5–10 mins","15–25 min","1 hour","30–45 min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Third-stage passive management = 15–25 min.  Why A wrong: 5–10 min is active management.  Why C wrong: 1 hour is the 4th stage.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"The fourth stage of labor is best described as:","options":["A 1-hour observation period after delivery of the placenta","From full dilatation to delivery of baby","From delivery of baby to delivery of placenta","Onset of true labor pain to full dilatation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 4th stage of labor = 1 hour observation period after delivery of placenta.  Why B wrong: That is the 2nd stage.  Why C wrong: That is the 3rd stage.  Why D wrong: That is the 1st stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"Physiological chills are typically experienced by the patient during which stage of labor?","options":["First stage (latent phase)","Second stage","Third stage","Fourth stage"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Physiological chills are experienced by the patient in the 4th stage (1-hour observation after placental delivery).  Why A wrong: Not associated with the latent phase.  Why B wrong: Not the 2nd stage.  Why C wrong: Not the 3rd stage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"A nulliparous woman in active labor has her cervix dilating at 0.8 cm/hr. This finding is best classified as:","options":["Normal active-phase progress","Protracted active phase","Active phase arrest","Prolonged latent phase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protracted active phase = cervix dilating at < 1 cm/hr (0.8 cm/hr is below the WHO minimum of 1 cm/hr and the nulliparous norm of 1.2 cm/hr).  Why A wrong: Normal nulliparous rate is 1.2 cm/hr; 0.8 is subnormal.  Why C wrong: Arrest requires ≥ 4 hours with NO dilatation at all.  Why D wrong: This is the active phase, not the latent phase."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"A multiparous woman reaches full dilatation and has been pushing for 70 minutes with the fetal head at +3 station and reassuring FHR. Based on the notes, which is TRUE?","options":["This is a normal second stage; continue","This is a prolonged second stage; forceps/vacuum can be applied since station is below +2","Immediate cesarean is required regardless of station","Oxytocin augmentation is the priority"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Multiparous prolonged 2nd stage = ≥ 1 hour (70 min qualifies); since the head is at +3 (below +2) with no distress, forceps/vacuum delivery is appropriate.  Why A wrong: 70 min exceeds the ≥ 1 hour multiparous prolonged threshold.  Why C wrong: Cesarean is reserved for station above +2 or fetal distress.  Why D wrong: The priority in prolonged 2nd stage is fast delivery via instrumental means at this station, not augmentation."},{"section":"Batch 061 · Stages of labor, definitions/durations/prolonged criteria of each stage…","question":"A laboring woman shows a visible/palpable depression across the abdomen between the upper and lower uterine segments, foul-smelling discharge, and hematuria. The priority management is:","options":["Give oxytocin to speed delivery","Wait and watch for spontaneous delivery","Perform cesarean section","Apply vacuum extraction"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: These are signs of obstructed labor (Bandl's ring, foul discharge, hematuria); the management is ALWAYS cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin is never given — it increases uterine rupture risk.  Why B wrong: Wait and watch is never done in obstructed labor.  Why D wrong: Instrumental delivery (vacuum) is in the \"never\" list for obstructed labor."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"When does the third stage of labor begin?","options":["When cervix is fully dilated","After delivery of the baby","At onset of true labor pains","When placenta is completely delivered"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The third stage of labor begins after delivery of the baby.  Why A wrong: Full cervical dilatation marks the start of the second stage, not the third.  Why C wrong: Onset of true labor pains marks the first stage of labor.  Why D wrong: Complete delivery of the placenta marks the END of the third stage, not its beginning."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"The third stage of labor ends at which point?","options":["Delivery of the baby's head","Complete dilatation of the cervix","Until the delivery of the placenta","One hour after placental delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The third stage ends with (until) the delivery of the placenta.  Why A wrong: Delivery of the head occurs during the second stage.  Why B wrong: Complete cervical dilatation ends the first stage.  Why D wrong: The hour after placental delivery is the fourth stage / immediate postpartum, not the third stage."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the main event of the third stage of labor?","options":["Delivery of the baby","Cervical effacement","Delivery of the placenta","Uterine involution"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The main event of the third stage of labor is delivery of the placenta.  Why A wrong: Delivery of the baby is the main event of the second stage.  Why B wrong: Cervical effacement occurs in the first stage.  Why D wrong: Uterine involution occurs during the puerperium, not the third stage."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"The human placenta is best described as which type of tissue that has to shed after delivery?","options":["Endometrium","Deciduae","Myometrium","Perimetrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The human placenta is deciduae and therefore has to shed (be delivered) after birth.  Why A wrong: Endometrium is the general uterine lining; the decidua is the specialized pregnancy endometrium that sheds.  Why C wrong: Myometrium is the muscular layer of the uterus, not the placenta.  Why D wrong: Perimetrium is the outer serosal covering of the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"The line of separation of the placenta is along which layer?","options":["Zona basalis","Zona compacta","Zona spongiosa","Zona functionalis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The line of separation of the placenta is along the Zona spongiosa.  Why A wrong: The zona basalis is the deep basal layer retained by the mother, not the plane of separation.  Why B wrong: The zona compacta is superficial and not the described plane of cleavage.  Why D wrong: Zona functionalis is a general term and not the specific answer given in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"The most common method of placental separation, occurring from the centre of the placenta, is called:","options":["Matthews Duncan method","Schultz method","Brandt-Andrews method","Credé method"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common method of placental separation is from the centre of the placenta, called the Schultz method.  Why A wrong: Matthews Duncan separation begins at the margin/edge, not the centre, and is less common.  Why C wrong: Brandt-Andrews is a technique for placental delivery (controlled cord traction), not a mode of separation.  Why D wrong: Credé maneuver is fundal expression, not a mode of spontaneous separation."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Which is the main factor responsible for separation of the placenta?","options":["Maternal pushing effort","Uterine contraction","Cord traction","Gravity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Uterine contraction is the main factor responsible for placental separation (it reduces the placental site area, causing shearing).  Why A wrong: Maternal pushing helps expulsion but is not the main separating factor.  Why C wrong: Cord traction assists delivery but is not the primary cause of separation.  Why D wrong: Gravity plays only a minor assisting role."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is termed the \"LIVING LIGATURE\" of the uterus?","options":["Round ligament","Broad ligament","Middle layer of myometrium","Uterosacral ligament"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The middle (criss-cross muscle) layer of the myometrium is called the living ligature, as its contraction compresses blood vessels to control bleeding.  Why A wrong: The round ligament supports uterine position, not hemostasis.  Why B wrong: The broad ligament is a peritoneal fold, not a contractile ligature.  Why D wrong: The uterosacral ligament is a supporting structure, not the living ligature."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"In passive (physiological) management of the third stage, the obstetrician does what after delivery of the baby?","options":["Immediately gives an injectable uterotonic","Relaxes and does nothing to deliver the placenta","Performs controlled cord traction","Manually removes the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In passive management, the obstetrician relaxes and does nothing to actively deliver the placenta, allowing spontaneous separation and expulsion.  Why A wrong: Giving a uterotonic is part of ACTIVE management, not passive.  Why C wrong: Controlled cord traction is part of active management.  Why D wrong: Manual removal is an intervention, not passive management."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"In active management of the third stage, the obstetrician:","options":["Waits passively for spontaneous expulsion","Takes a few steps to actively deliver the placenta","Delays all intervention for 30 minutes","Avoids uterotonic drugs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In active management, the obstetrician takes a few active steps to deliver the placenta (active management of third stage of labor).  Why A wrong: Passive waiting characterizes passive management.  Why C wrong: Active management intervenes early, not after a 30-minute delay.  Why D wrong: Uterotonic administration is a key step of active management."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"According to the notes' comparison table, the time taken for placental delivery in PASSIVE management is:","options":["5–10 minutes","15–30 minutes","30–45 minutes","1–3 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Passive management takes 15–30 minutes for placental delivery.  Why A wrong: 5–10 minutes is the time for ACTIVE management.  Why C wrong: 30–45 minutes is not stated in the notes.  Why D wrong: 1–3 minutes refers to cord clamping timing, not placental delivery."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"In ACTIVE management of the third stage, the time taken for placental delivery is approximately:","options":["15–30 minutes","5–10 minutes","20–40 minutes","45–60 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Active management takes 5–10 minutes for placental delivery.  Why A wrong: 15–30 minutes applies to passive management.  Why C wrong: 20–40 minutes is not stated.  Why D wrong: 45–60 minutes is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Compared with active management, passive management of the third stage of labor is associated with:","options":["Decreased blood loss","Increased blood loss, increased chances of PPH, and increased maternal mortality","No change in maternal mortality","Decreased chances of PPH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Passive management causes ↑ blood loss → ↑ chances of PPH → ↑ maternal mortality.  Why A wrong: Blood loss is increased, not decreased, in passive management.  Why C wrong: Maternal mortality is increased with passive management.  Why D wrong: Chances of PPH are increased, not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Which management of the third stage of labor is preferred and reduces blood loss, PPH, and maternal mortality?","options":["Passive management","Active management","Manual removal only","Delayed management"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Active management is preferred; it decreases blood loss, chances of PPH, and maternal mortality.  Why A wrong: Passive management increases these risks.  Why C wrong: Manual removal is reserved for retained placenta, not routine preferred management.  Why D wrong: \"Delayed management\" is not a recognized preferred strategy."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"The third stage of labor is said to be prolonged when it lasts:","options":["≥ 15 minutes","≥ 20 minutes","≥ 30 minutes","≥ 60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The third stage is said to be prolonged when it is ≥ 30 minutes.  Why A wrong: 15 minutes is within normal passive-management duration.  Why B wrong: 20 minutes is still within normal range.  Why D wrong: 60 minutes exceeds the threshold, but the defining cut-off is ≥ 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"A third stage of labor prolonged ≥ 30 minutes indicates which condition?","options":["Uterine atony","Retained placenta","Uterine rupture","Cervical laceration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the third stage is ≥ 30 minutes, it is termed prolonged and signifies retained placenta.  Why A wrong: Uterine atony causes hemorrhage but is not the definition of prolonged third stage.  Why C wrong: Uterine rupture is a separate obstetric emergency.  Why D wrong: Cervical laceration is a genital tract trauma, not defined by third-stage duration."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the best method to prevent postpartum hemorrhage (PPH)?","options":["Passive management of the third stage","Active management of the third stage of labor (AMTSL)","Uterine massage alone","Early cord clamping"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best method to prevent PPH is Active Management of Third Stage of Labor (AMTSL).  Why A wrong: Passive management increases the risk of PPH.  Why C wrong: Uterine massage is only one component and was the earlier 4th step; not the best overall method.  Why D wrong: Early cord clamping is NOT part of AMTSL and does not prevent PPH."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"According to the duration list, passive management of the third stage lasts:","options":["15–20 mins","5–10 mins","30–45 mins","1–3 mins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The duration list states passive management = 15–20 mins.  Why B wrong: 5–10 mins is the active management duration.  Why C wrong: 30–45 mins is not stated.  Why D wrong: 1–3 mins refers to cord clamping."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"According to the duration list, active management of the third stage lasts:","options":["15–20 mins","5–10 mins","20–30 mins","10–15 mins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The duration list states active management = 5–10 mins.  Why A wrong: 15–20 mins is the passive management duration.  Why C wrong: 20–30 mins is not stated.  Why D wrong: 10–15 mins is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"How many steps are included in the Active Management of Third Stage of Labor (AMTSL)?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: AMTSL has 4 steps: (1) injectable uterotonic, (2) delayed cord clamping, (3) delivery of placenta by controlled cord traction, (4) intermittent assessment of uterine tone.  Why A wrong: There are more than 2 steps.  Why B wrong: There are 4, not 3, steps.  Why D wrong: There are 4, not 5, steps."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Which is the 1st and MOST IMPORTANT step of AMTSL?","options":["Delayed cord clamping","Injectable uterotonic after delivery of the baby","Controlled cord traction","Uterine massage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 1st and most important step of AMTSL is giving an injectable (Inj.) uterotonic after delivery of the baby.  Why A wrong: Delayed cord clamping is the 2nd step.  Why C wrong: Controlled cord traction is the 3rd step.  Why D wrong: Uterine massage was the earlier 4th step, now replaced by intermittent assessment of tone."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"A uterotonic drug used in AMTSL acts by doing what to the uterus?","options":["Relaxes the uterus","Increases the tone of the uterus","Softens the cervix","Dilates the cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A uterotonic is a drug which increases the tone of the uterus, promoting contraction and hemostasis.  Why A wrong: Relaxing the uterus (tocolysis) is the opposite effect.  Why C wrong: Cervical softening is caused by cervical ripening agents, not the described uterotonic action.  Why D wrong: Cervical dilatation is not the mechanism of uterotonics in this context."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the 2nd step of AMTSL?","options":["Injectable uterotonic","Delayed cord clamping","Controlled cord traction","Uterine massage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2nd step of AMTSL is delayed cord clamping.  Why A wrong: Injectable uterotonic is the 1st step.  Why C wrong: Controlled cord traction is the 3rd step.  Why D wrong: Uterine massage/intermittent tone assessment is the 4th step."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the 3rd step of AMTSL?","options":["Injectable uterotonic","Delayed cord clamping","Delivery of placenta by controlled cord traction","Intermittent assessment of uterine tone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 3rd step of AMTSL is delivery of the placenta by controlled cord traction.  Why A wrong: Injectable uterotonic is the 1st step.  Why B wrong: Delayed cord clamping is the 2nd step.  Why D wrong: Intermittent assessment of uterine tone is the 4th step."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the 4th (current) step of AMTSL?","options":["Uterine massage","Intermittent assessment of uterine tone","Manual removal of placenta","Injectable uterotonic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The current 4th step of AMTSL is intermittent assessment of uterine tone (assessing tone off and on after the placenta delivers).  Why A wrong: Uterine massage was the EARLIER 4th step, now replaced.  Why C wrong: Manual removal is only for retained placenta.  Why D wrong: Injectable uterotonic is the 1st step."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What was the EARLIER 4th step of AMTSL before it was updated?","options":["Delayed cord clamping","Uterine massage","Controlled cord traction","Injectable uterotonic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The earlier 4th step of AMTSL was uterine massage.  Why A wrong: Delayed cord clamping is the 2nd step, unchanged.  Why C wrong: Controlled cord traction is the 3rd step.  Why D wrong: Injectable uterotonic is the 1st step."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Which drug does WHO recommend as the FIRST-LINE uterotonic to prevent/treat PPH?","options":["Methylergometrine","Oxytocin","Misoprostol","Carboprost"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO recommends oxytocin as the 1st line drug (uterotonic).  Why A wrong: Methylergometrine is listed as a WHO alternative drug, not first-line.  Why C wrong: Misoprostol (PGE1) is an alternative.  Why D wrong: Carboprost is an alternative prostaglandin, not first-line."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Which of the following is listed by WHO as an ALTERNATIVE uterotonic drug (not first-line)?","options":["Oxytocin","Methylergometrine","Normal saline","Ringer lactate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO lists methylergometrine, syntometrine, prostaglandins, and carboprost as alternative drugs; oxytocin is first-line.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin is the first-line drug, not the alternative.  Why C wrong: Normal saline is an IV fluid, not a uterotonic.  Why D wrong: Ringer lactate is an IV fluid used to dissolve oxytocin, not a uterotonic itself."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"In the prostaglandin abbreviations, PGE1 corresponds to which drug?","options":["Carboprost","Misoprost","Dinoprostone","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PGE1 = Misoprost (misoprostol).  Why A wrong: Carboprost corresponds to PGF2α (PGF-2d).  Why C wrong: Dinoprostone corresponds to PGE2.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not a prostaglandin."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"In the prostaglandin abbreviations, PGF2α (PGF 2d) corresponds to which drug?","options":["Misoprost","Carboprost","Dinoprostone","Syntometrine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PGF2α (written PGF 2d) = Carboprost.  Why A wrong: Misoprost is PGE1.  Why C wrong: Dinoprostone is PGE2.  Why D wrong: Syntometrine is a fixed combination of oxytocin + methylergometrine, not a prostaglandin."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Natural oxytocin is produced (made) in which structure?","options":["Anterior pituitary","Paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus","Adrenal cortex","Ovary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Natural oxytocin is made in the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus (the notes write \"Periventricular nucleus of hypothalamus\").  Why A wrong: The anterior pituitary produces hormones like FSH/LH/prolactin, not oxytocin.  Why C wrong: The adrenal cortex produces steroid hormones.  Why D wrong: The ovary produces estrogen and progesterone, not oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Natural oxytocin, after being made in the hypothalamus, is stored and released by which gland?","options":["Anterior pituitary","Posterior pituitary","Thyroid gland","Pineal gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is stored and released by the posterior pituitary.  Why A wrong: The anterior pituitary does not store oxytocin.  Why C wrong: The thyroid gland secretes thyroid hormones.  Why D wrong: The pineal gland secretes melatonin."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Natural oxytocin is chemically classified as a:","options":["Octapeptide (8 amino acids)","Nonapeptide (9 amino acids)","Decapeptide (10 amino acids)","Steroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Natural oxytocin is a NONA-peptide (9 amino acids).  Why A wrong: The OCTAPEPTIDE (8) description in the notes refers to synthetic oxytocin.  Why C wrong: Decapeptide (10) is not correct for oxytocin.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is a peptide hormone, not a steroid."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Synthetic oxytocin (made in a factory) is described as which type of peptide?","options":["Nonapeptide (9)","Octapeptide (8)","Heptapeptide (7)","Dipeptide (2)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Synthetic oxytocin is described as an OCTAPEPTIDE (8) and is made in a factory.  Why A wrong: Nonapeptide (9) refers to natural oxytocin.  Why C wrong: Heptapeptide (7) is not correct.  Why D wrong: Dipeptide (2) is not correct."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"For intramuscular (IM) oxytocin, the onset of action is approximately:","options":["1 minute","3 minutes","10 minutes","30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM oxytocin has an onset of action of 3 minutes.  Why A wrong: 1 minute is the onset for IV infusion.  Why C wrong: 10 minutes is not stated.  Why D wrong: 30 minutes is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"For intramuscular (IM) oxytocin, the duration of action is approximately:","options":["1 hour","3 hours","30 minutes","6 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM oxytocin has a duration of action of 3 hours.  Why A wrong: 1 hour is the duration for IV infusion.  Why C wrong: 30 minutes is not stated.  Why D wrong: 6 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"For IV infusion of oxytocin, the onset of action is approximately:","options":["3 minutes","1 minute","5 minutes","10 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IV infusion oxytocin has an onset of action of 1 minute.  Why A wrong: 3 minutes is the onset for IM route.  Why C wrong: 5 minutes is not stated.  Why D wrong: 10 minutes is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"For IV infusion of oxytocin, the duration of action is approximately:","options":["3 hours","1 hour","30 minutes","2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IV infusion oxytocin has a duration of action of 1 hour.  Why A wrong: 3 hours is the duration for the IM route.  Why C wrong: 30 minutes is not stated.  Why D wrong: 2 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Why should oxytocin NEVER be given as an IV bolus?","options":["It causes hypertension and stroke","It leads to hypotension and cardiac arrest","It causes immediate uterine rupture","It causes hyperglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin given as an IV bolus can lead to hypotension → cardiac arrest.  Why A wrong: The described effect is hypotension, not hypertension/stroke.  Why C wrong: Uterine rupture is not the stated reason for avoiding IV bolus.  Why D wrong: Hyperglycemia is not the stated adverse effect."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What are the trade (brand) names of oxytocin mentioned in the notes?","options":["Methergin and Hemabate","Pitocin and Syntocinon","Prostodin and Cerviprime","Cytotec and Misoprost"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The trade names for oxytocin are Pitocin and Syntocinon.  Why A wrong: Methergin is the trade name for methylergometrine; Hemabate is carboprost.  Why C wrong: These are prostaglandin brand names, not oxytocin.  Why D wrong: Cytotec/Misoprost are misoprostol brands, not oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the dose of oxytocin used in AMTSL to prevent PPH?","options":["5 IU IM","10 IU in electrolyte-rich media (RL, NS) as IV infusion","20 IU in dextrose 5%","40 IU orally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The AMTSL dose is 10 IU of oxytocin in electrolyte-rich media (Ringer lactate, normal saline) given as an IV infusion.  Why A wrong: 5 IU IM is not the stated AMTSL dose.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin must NOT be dissolved in dextrose 5%; 20 IU is not the stated dose.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not given orally (it is a peptide)."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Oxytocin should NOT be dissolved in which solution because it can lead to water intoxication?","options":["Ringer lactate","Normal saline","Dextrose 5% solution","Hartmann's solution"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin should not be dissolved in dextrose 5% solution because it can lead to water intoxication (oxytocin has an antidiuretic effect).  Why A wrong: Ringer lactate is an electrolyte-rich medium recommended for oxytocin.  Why B wrong: Normal saline is an electrolyte-rich medium recommended for oxytocin.  Why D wrong: Hartmann's is an electrolyte solution similar to RL and is not the contraindicated diluent."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"The plasma half-life (T1/2) of oxytocin is approximately:","options":["1–2 minutes","3–5 minutes","10–15 minutes","30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The half-life (T1/2) of oxytocin is 3–5 minutes.  Why A wrong: 1–2 minutes is not the stated half-life.  Why C wrong: 10–15 minutes is not stated.  Why D wrong: 30 minutes is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Which hormone is responsible for the milk ejection (let-down) reflex?","options":["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is the hormone responsible for milk ejection (let-down reflex).  Why A wrong: Prolactin is responsible for milk production/synthesis, not ejection.  Why C wrong: Estrogen prepares breast tissue but does not cause milk ejection.  Why D wrong: Progesterone inhibits milk production during pregnancy; it is not the ejection hormone."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the trade name of methylergometrine mentioned in the notes?","options":["Pitocin","Methergin inj.","Hemabate","Cytotec"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Methylergometrine's trade name is Methergin injection.  Why A wrong: Pitocin is a trade name for oxytocin.  Why C wrong: Hemabate is carboprost.  Why D wrong: Cytotec is misoprostol."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the dose and route of methylergometrine used in AMTSL?","options":["0.2 mg IM","0.2 mg IV","0.5 mg IM","10 IU IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Methylergometrine is given 0.2 mg IM.  Why B wrong: It must NEVER be given IV (risk of severe hypertension).  Why C wrong: 0.5 mg is the amount of methylergometrine in the syntometrine combination, not the standalone dose.  Why D wrong: 10 IU IV refers to oxytocin, not methylergometrine."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Methylergometrine causes which type of uterine contraction?","options":["Rhythmic contractions with relaxation in between","Tetanic (sustained) contraction with no relaxation between contractions","No effect on uterine tone","Uterine relaxation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Methylergometrine causes tetanic contraction of the uterus with no relaxation in between contractions.  Why A wrong: Rhythmic contractions with relaxation describe oxytocin's action, not methylergometrine's.  Why C wrong: It strongly affects uterine tone.  Why D wrong: It contracts, not relaxes, the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Methylergometrine can cause which cardiovascular effect, and therefore should never be given IV?","options":["Hypotension","Increased blood pressure (hypertension)","Bradycardia","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Methylergometrine can cause ↑ BP (hypertension) and should never be given IV.  Why A wrong: It causes hypertension, not hypotension (hypotension is an oxytocin bolus effect).  Why C wrong: Bradycardia is not the stated effect.  Why D wrong: Hypoglycemia is not the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"The contraindications to methylergometrine are remembered by the mnemonic \"TOPER.\" What does the \"O\" stand for?","options":["Oxytocin sensitivity","Organic heart disease in mother","Obesity","Oligohydramnios"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the TOPER mnemonic, O = Organic heart disease in mother.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin sensitivity is not a listed contraindication.  Why C wrong: Obesity is not listed.  Why D wrong: Oligohydramnios is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"In the methylergometrine contraindication mnemonic \"TOPER,\" the \"T\" stands for administration after delivery of the:","options":["Placenta","1st twin (in a twin pregnancy)","Anterior shoulder","Cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: T = After delivery of 1st twin; giving methylergometrine after the first twin would cause tetanic contraction and endanger the undelivered second twin.  Why A wrong: It is given AFTER placental delivery in some protocols; the contraindication is after the 1st twin.  Why C wrong: Anterior shoulder is not part of the mnemonic.  Why D wrong: Cord is not part of the mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication to methylergometrine per the TOPER mnemonic?","options":["Rh-positive pregnancy","Pre-eclampsia / PIH","Gestational diabetes","Anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: P = Pre-eclampsia / PIH is a contraindication because methylergometrine raises blood pressure.  Why A wrong: It is Rh-NEGATIVE pregnancy (R), not Rh-positive, that is listed.  Why C wrong: Gestational diabetes is not listed.  Why D wrong: Anemia is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"In the TOPER mnemonic for methylergometrine contraindications, \"E\" stands for:","options":["Epilepsy","Eclampsia","Edema","Endometritis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: E = Eclampsia is a contraindication to methylergometrine.  Why A wrong: Epilepsy is not listed.  Why C wrong: Edema alone is not listed.  Why D wrong: Endometritis is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"In the TOPER mnemonic for methylergometrine contraindications, \"R\" stands for:","options":["Rh negative pregnancy","Renal failure","Rheumatic fever","Retained placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: R = Rh negative pregnancy is a contraindication to methylergometrine.  Why B wrong: Renal failure is not listed.  Why C wrong: Rheumatic fever is not listed.  Why D wrong: Retained placenta is not listed as a contraindication in the mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Syntometrine is a fixed-dose combination of which two drugs?","options":["Oxytocin + misoprostol","Oxytocin + methylergometrine (methergins)","Carboprost + oxytocin","Methylergometrine + carboprost"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Syntometrine is a fixed-dose combination of oxytocin + methylergometrine (methergins).  Why A wrong: It does not contain misoprostol.  Why C wrong: It does not contain carboprost.  Why D wrong: It does not combine methylergometrine with carboprost."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"In the fixed-dose syntometrine combination, what are the amounts of each component?","options":["Oxytocin 10 IU + methylergometrine 0.2 mg","Oxytocin 5 U + methylergometrine 0.5 mg","Oxytocin 5 U + methylergometrine 0.2 mg","Oxytocin 10 U + methylergometrine 0.5 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Syntometrine = oxytocin 5 U + methylergometrine 0.5 mg (fixed dose combination).  Why A wrong: The stated amounts are 5 U and 0.5 mg, not 10 IU and 0.2 mg.  Why C wrong: Methylergometrine amount is 0.5 mg, not 0.2 mg, in the combination.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin amount is 5 U, not 10 U."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the WHO-recommended dose and route of misoprostol (PGE1) to prevent PPH?","options":["200 mcg IM","600 mcg orally","250 mcg IM","100 mcg IV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Misoprost (PGE1) is given 600 mcg orally (WHO recommended).  Why A wrong: 200 mcg IM is not the stated dose/route.  Why C wrong: 250 mcg IM is the dose of carboprost (PGF2α).  Why D wrong: 100 mcg IV is the dose of carbetocin."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"A dose-dependent side effect of misoprostol (PGE1) is:","options":["Diarrhea","Fever + shivering","Hypertension","Constipation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Misoprostol's dose-dependent side effect is fever + shivering.  Why A wrong: Diarrhea is the stated side effect of carboprost (PGF2α), not misoprostol.  Why C wrong: Hypertension is a methylergometrine effect.  Why D wrong: Constipation is not a listed effect."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Which uterotonic can be safely given to asthmatic patients?","options":["Carboprost (PGF2α)","Misoprost (PGE1)","Methylergometrine","Dinoprostone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Misoprost (PGE1) can be given to asthmatic patients.  Why A wrong: Carboprost (PGF2α) is contraindicated in asthmatic patients (causes bronchospasm).  Why C wrong: Methylergometrine's issue is hypertension, but misoprost is the drug specifically noted as safe in asthma.  Why D wrong: Dinoprostone is used for labor induction, not the noted PPH drug safe in asthma."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Misoprostol (PGE1) is contraindicated in patients with which history?","options":["Diabetes","Previous cesarean section","Hypertension","Anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Misoprost is contraindicated in previous cesarean section (risk of uterine rupture/scar dehiscence).  Why A wrong: Diabetes is not a stated contraindication.  Why C wrong: Hypertension is a methylergometrine concern, not the stated misoprost contraindication.  Why D wrong: Anemia is not a stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Carboprost (PGF2α) is also known by which names in the notes?","options":["Pitocin / Syntocinon","Hemabate / dinoprost","Methergin / syntometrine","Cytotec / misoprost"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carboprost (PGF-2d) is also called Hemabate dinoprost.  Why A wrong: Pitocin/Syntocinon are oxytocin trade names.  Why C wrong: Methergin is methylergometrine; syntometrine is a combination.  Why D wrong: Cytotec/misoprost are misoprostol names."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the dose and route of carboprost (PGF2α) for PPH?","options":["600 mcg orally","250 mcg IM","100 mcg IV","0.2 mg IM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carboprost is given 250 mcg IM.  Why A wrong: 600 mcg orally is the misoprostol dose.  Why C wrong: 100 mcg IV is carbetocin.  Why D wrong: 0.2 mg IM is methylergometrine."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Carboprost (PGF2α) is contraindicated in which patients, and its main side effect is:","options":["Contraindicated in hypertensives; side effect fever","Contraindicated in asthmatic patients; side effect diarrhea","Contraindicated in diabetics; side effect constipation","Contraindicated in cardiac patients; side effect hypotension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carboprost is contraindicated in asthmatic patients (bronchoconstriction) and its side effect is diarrhea.  Why A wrong: Fever/shivering is the misoprostol side effect.  Why C wrong: Diabetes/constipation are not stated.  Why D wrong: Cardiac/hypotension are not the stated carboprost profile."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"PGE2 (dinoprostone) is:","options":["The first-line drug for PPH","NOT used in PPH/AMTSL; it is used in induction of labor","Given 250 mcg IM for PPH","Contraindicated in labor induction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PGE2 (dinoprostone) is NOT used in PPH/AMTSL; it is used in induction of labor (cervical ripening).  Why A wrong: Oxytocin is first-line for PPH, not dinoprostone.  Why C wrong: 250 mcg IM is the carboprost dose; dinoprostone is not given this way for PPH.  Why D wrong: Dinoprostone is USED for labor induction, not contraindicated for it."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the dose and route of carbetocin?","options":["10 IU IV infusion","100 mcg IV","250 mcg IM","600 mcg orally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carbetocin is given 100 mcg IV.  Why A wrong: 10 IU IV infusion is the oxytocin AMTSL dose.  Why C wrong: 250 mcg IM is the carboprost dose.  Why D wrong: 600 mcg orally is the misoprostol dose."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Carbetocin is best described as:","options":["A prostaglandin analogue","A synthetic oxytocin with a longer half-life","An ergot alkaloid","A tocolytic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carbetocin is a synthetic oxytocin with a longer half-life (longer t½).  Why A wrong: It is an oxytocin analogue, not a prostaglandin.  Why C wrong: Ergot alkaloids are methylergometrine-type drugs.  Why D wrong: Tocolytics relax the uterus; carbetocin is a uterotonic."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Regarding cord clamping, why is immediate clamping avoided (delayed cord clamping recommended)?","options":["Because the cord has 80 mL of blood that should go to the baby to prevent anemia","Because immediate clamping causes maternal hemorrhage","Because the cord has no blood","Because it prevents jaundice in the baby"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cord contains 80 mL of blood; delaying clamping lets this blood transfer to the baby, preventing anemia.  Why B wrong: Cord clamping timing relates to neonatal blood volume, not maternal hemorrhage.  Why C wrong: The cord does contain blood (80 mL).  Why D wrong: The stated benefit is prevention of anemia, not jaundice (delayed clamping may actually slightly increase jaundice risk)."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Approximately how much blood is contained in the umbilical cord/placenta that transfers to the baby with delayed clamping?","options":["30 mL","50 mL","80 mL","120 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The cord contains about 80 mL of blood that transfers to the baby with delayed clamping.  Why A wrong: 30 mL is understated.  Why B wrong: 50 mL is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 120 mL is overstated."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Early cord clamping is defined as clamping the cord within what time after delivery of the baby?","options":["Within 1 minute of delivery","Between 1–3 minutes of delivery","Within 5 minutes of delivery","Immediately at crowning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Early cord clamping = clamping the cord within 1 minute of delivery of the baby.  Why B wrong: 1–3 minutes defines DELAYED cord clamping.  Why C wrong: 5 minutes is not the stated cut-off.  Why D wrong: Clamping at crowning is not defined; the baby must be delivered first."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Delayed cord clamping is defined as clamping the cord at what time after delivery?","options":["Within 1 minute","Between 1–3 minutes of delivery","After 5 minutes","After 10 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delayed cord clamping = clamping the cord between 1–3 minutes of delivery of the baby.  Why A wrong: Within 1 minute is EARLY cord clamping.  Why C wrong: After 5 minutes is not the stated definition.  Why D wrong: After 10 minutes is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Early cord clamping is preferred in all of the following conditions EXCEPT:","options":["Neonatal resuscitation is needed","Birth asphyxia in the baby","Mother is Rh-negative","Healthy term baby with no complications"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: A healthy term baby with no complications benefits from DELAYED clamping, not early clamping.  Why A wrong: If neonatal resuscitation is needed, early clamping is preferred.  Why B wrong: Birth asphyxia is a stated indication for early clamping.  Why C wrong: Rh-negative mother is a stated indication for early clamping."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Early cord clamping is preferred when the baby is a known case of heart disease because:","options":["It reduces jaundice","The extra 80 mL of blood can cause heart failure","It prevents infection","It improves oxygenation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a baby with known heart disease, the extra 80 mL of blood from delayed clamping can cause heart failure (volume overload), so early clamping is preferred.  Why A wrong: The reason given is heart failure risk, not jaundice reduction.  Why C wrong: Infection prevention is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Oxygenation improvement is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"For an HIV-positive mother, what does WHO recommend regarding cord clamping?","options":["Delayed cord clamping","Early cord clamping","No clamping until placenta delivers","Immediate cord milking"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO recommends early cord clamping if the mother is HIV positive.  Why A wrong: Delayed cord clamping is the National (NACO) guideline for HIV positive mothers, not the WHO recommendation.  Why C wrong: Not clamping until placenta delivery is not recommended.  Why D wrong: Cord milking is not the stated WHO recommendation here."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"For an HIV-positive mother, what does the National guideline (NACO) recommend regarding cord clamping?","options":["Early cord clamping","Delayed cord clamping","Cord milking","Manual removal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The National guideline (NACO) recommends delayed cord clamping for HIV positive mothers.  Why A wrong: Early cord clamping is the WHO recommendation, not the NACO/national one.  Why C wrong: Cord milking is not the stated NACO recommendation.  Why D wrong: Manual removal is unrelated to cord clamping timing."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"The 3rd step of AMTSL, delivery of the placenta by controlled cord traction, is also known as which technique?","options":["Credé maneuver","Modified Brandt-Andrews technique","McRoberts maneuver","Ritgen maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Controlled cord traction is also called the Modified Brandt-Andrews technique (notes write \"Modified brand Andrew's technique\").  Why A wrong: The Credé maneuver is fundal expression, a different technique.  Why C wrong: McRoberts maneuver is for shoulder dystocia.  Why D wrong: Ritgen maneuver is used to control delivery of the fetal head."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Before delivering the placenta by controlled cord traction, what must the operator ensure?","options":["That the cord is clamped early","That the placenta is separated from the uterus","That the mother is anesthetized","That an oxytocin bolus is given"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Before delivering the placenta, one must make sure that the placenta is separated from the uterus, to avoid uterine inversion.  Why A wrong: Cord clamping timing is a separate step; early clamping is not a prerequisite.  Why C wrong: General anesthesia is not required for controlled cord traction.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin should never be given as an IV bolus, and it is not the prerequisite for traction."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the BEST sign of placental separation?","options":["Lengthening of the cord","Placenta lying in the vagina","Gush of blood","Rising of the fundus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best sign of placental separation is the placenta lying in the vagina.  Why A wrong: Lengthening of the cord is the 2nd best sign, not the best.  Why C wrong: A gush of blood is a sign but not identified as the best sign here.  Why D wrong: Rising of the fundus is a classical sign but not the best sign per these notes."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"What is the 2nd best sign of placental separation?","options":["Lengthening of the cord","Placenta lying in the vagina","Uterus becoming globular","Fundus rising above umbilicus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 2nd best sign of placental separation is lengthening of the cord.  Why B wrong: Placenta lying in the vagina is the BEST (1st) sign.  Why C wrong: A globular uterus is a classical sign but not the 2nd best per these notes.  Why D wrong: Fundus rising is a classical sign but not identified as 2nd best here."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"If the placenta has NOT separated from the uterus and the cord is pulled, what dangerous complication can occur?","options":["Cord prolapse","Uterine inversion (uterus comes out with the placenta) leading to shock","Placenta previa","Uterine atony only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulling the cord before separation causes the uterus to come out along with the placenta → uterine inversion → patient immediately goes into shock.  Why A wrong: Cord prolapse occurs before delivery of the baby, not during placental delivery.  Why C wrong: Placenta previa is an antepartum condition of placental location.  Why D wrong: Uterine atony can cause bleeding but is not the described inversion sequence."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Immediately following acute uterine inversion, the patient characteristically:","options":["Develops hypertension","Goes into shock","Becomes febrile","Has no symptoms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With uterine inversion, the patient immediately goes into shock (neurogenic and hemorrhagic).  Why A wrong: Hypertension is not the response; shock (hypotension) occurs.  Why C wrong: Fever is not the immediate response.  Why D wrong: The patient is symptomatic and goes into shock."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"The current 4th step of AMTSL, intermittent assessment of uterine tone, is performed:","options":["Before delivery of the baby","After the placenta delivers, assessing tone off and on","Only if PPH occurs","During the first stage of labor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the placenta delivers, the uterine tone is assessed off and on (intermittently) — this is the 4th step of AMTSL.  Why A wrong: It is done after placental delivery, not before the baby is born.  Why C wrong: It is a routine intermittent assessment, not only reactive to PPH.  Why D wrong: It occurs in the third stage/immediate postpartum, not the first stage."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"(PYQ – Q2) Which of the following is NOT included in AMTSL?","options":["Injectable uterotonic to mother","Early cord clamping","Uterine massage","Delivery of placenta by controlled cord traction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Early cord clamping is NOT included in AMTSL; delayed cord clamping is the AMTSL component.  Why A wrong: Injectable uterotonic is the 1st step of AMTSL.  Why C wrong: Uterine massage (earlier 4th step) is/was part of AMTSL.  Why D wrong: Controlled cord traction is the 3rd step of AMTSL."},{"section":"Batch 062 · Third stage of labor, placental separation…","question":"Which cord clamping practice is actually included in AMTSL?","options":["Early cord clamping","Delayed cord clamping","No cord clamping","Immediate cord milking only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delayed cord clamping is the practice included in AMTSL (it is the 2nd step).  Why A wrong: Early cord clamping is NOT part of AMTSL.  Why C wrong: The cord is clamped (in a delayed fashion), not left unclamped.  Why D wrong: Cord milking alone is not the defined AMTSL step; delayed clamping is."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Induction of labor means:","options":["To initiate the process of labor in a quiescent uterus","To accelerate the process of labor in a uterus already contracting","To stop premature uterine contractions","To perform an emergency cesarean section"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Induction of labor is defined as initiating the process of labor in a quiescent (non-contracting) uterus.  Why B wrong: Accelerating labor in an already-contracting uterus is augmentation, not induction.  Why C wrong: Stopping premature contractions is tocolysis, not induction.  Why D wrong: Cesarean section is a surgical delivery, not induction."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Augmentation of labor is defined as:","options":["Initiating labor in a quiescent uterus","To accelerate the process of labor in a uterus which is already contracting","Delivery by forceps","Ripening of the cervix before term"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Augmentation of labor accelerates the process of labor in a uterus that is already contracting.  Why A wrong: That describes induction of labor.  Why C wrong: Forceps delivery is an instrumental delivery, not augmentation.  Why D wrong: Cervical ripening is a preparatory step, not augmentation."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Before induction of labor, it is necessary to check the status of the:","options":["Placenta","Cervix","Umbilical cord","Amniotic sac only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is necessary to check the status of the cervix before induction of labor.  Why A wrong: Placental status is not the check used to decide readiness for induction.  Why C wrong: The umbilical cord is not the parameter assessed prior to induction.  Why D wrong: Amniotic sac alone is not the assessed status; the cervix is."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"The status of the cervix before induction of labor is assessed using which scoring system?","options":["Apgar score","Bishop score","Glasgow coma scale","Silverman score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cervix is checked by the Bishop score before induction of labor.  Why A wrong: Apgar score assesses newborn condition at birth.  Why C wrong: Glasgow coma scale assesses level of consciousness.  Why D wrong: Silverman score assesses neonatal respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"How many parameters are assessed in the Bishop score?","options":["3","4","5","6"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Bishop score has 5 parameters.  Why A/B/D wrong: The Bishop score uses exactly 5 parameters, not 3, 4, or 6."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the five parameters of the Bishop score?","options":["Dilatation of cervix","Position of cervix","Effacement of cervix","Fetal heart rate"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal heart rate is not part of the Bishop score; the five parameters are dilatation, position, effacement, station of fetal head, and consistency of cervix.  Why A/B/C wrong: Dilatation, position, and effacement of the cervix are all Bishop score parameters."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"In the Bishop score, \"Station of fetal head\" corresponds to which mnemonic word (as given in the notes)?","options":["Delhi","Police","Special","Commandos"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the notes' mnemonic, \"Special\" → Station of fetal head.  Why A wrong: \"Delhi\" → Dilatation of cervix.  Why B wrong: \"Police\" → Position of cervix.  Why D wrong: \"Commandos\" → Consistency of cervix."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"In the Bishop score mnemonic, \"Employed\" stands for which parameter?","options":["Effacement of cervix","Dilatation of cervix","Consistency of cervix","Station of fetal head"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Employed\" → Effacement of cervix in the notes' mnemonic.  Why B wrong: Dilatation corresponds to \"Delhi.\"  Why C wrong: Consistency corresponds to \"Commandos.\"  Why D wrong: Station corresponds to \"Special.\""},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"In the Bishop score mnemonic given in the notes, \"Commandos\" refers to:","options":["Consistency of cervix","Contraction of uterus","Cephalopelvic disproportion","Cord position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Commandos\" → Consistency of cervix.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the parameter represented by the mnemonic word."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"According to the notes, which two Bishop score parameters are scored on a scale of 0 to 2 (rather than 0 to 3)?","options":["Dilatation and Effacement","Position and Consistency","Station and Dilatation","Effacement and Station"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Position (score 2) and Consistency (score 2) are scored 0 to 2; the notes indicate \"(Position consistency)\" as the 0 to 2 parameters.  Why A wrong: Dilatation and Effacement each carry a maximum of 3.  Why C wrong: Station carries 3 and Dilatation carries 3.  Why D wrong: Effacement and Station each carry a maximum of 3."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Each Bishop score parameter is scored ranging from:","options":["0 to 3 or 0 to 2","0 to 5","1 to 10","0 to 1 only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Each parameter scores from 0 to 3 or 0 to 2.  Why B/C/D wrong: These ranges are not used for Bishop scoring."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"What is the maximum possible Bishop score?","options":["10","11","13","15"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The maximum Bishop score is 13 (3+2+3+3+2).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the sum of the parameter maxima."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"If the Bishop score is ≥9, induction of labor is likely to be:","options":["Unsuccessful","Successful","Contraindicated","Delayed by 6 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A Bishop score ≥9 indicates induction of labor will be successful.  Why A wrong: An unsuccessful outcome is associated with a low score (<6).  Why C wrong: A high score is favorable, not contraindicating.  Why D wrong: No such delay is indicated by a high score."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"When the Bishop score is <6, what is the recommended approach?","options":["Proceed directly with oxytocin induction","Induction will not be successful; first ripen the cervix, then induce labor","Perform immediate cesarean section","Wait 6 weeks and reassess"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With a score <6, induction of labor will not be successful, so the cervix should first be ripened and then labor induced.  Why A wrong: Direct induction without ripening is not advised with an unfavorable cervix.  Why C wrong: A low Bishop score does not mandate cesarean.  Why D wrong: No 6-week wait is indicated."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"In the Modified Bishop score, effacement is replaced by:","options":["Cervical length","Fetal weight","Amniotic fluid index","Position of placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the Modified Bishop score, effacement is replaced by cervical length.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the substitution made in the modified score."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Because it measures cervical length, the Modified Bishop score requires which investigation?","options":["X-ray","USG (ultrasonography)","CT scan","MRI"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Modified Bishop score needs USG to measure cervical length.  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specify USG, not X-ray, CT, or MRI."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"In the mnemonic on page 1, \"Left\" stands for:","options":["Length of cervix","Level of station","Lie of fetus","Location of placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Left: Length of cervix\" (used in the modified score).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not what \"Left\" denotes in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"The Modified Bishop score is done with the help of USG, whereas the standard Bishop score is done:","options":["Clinically","By MRI","By blood test","By amniocentesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Bishop score is done clinically; the modified version uses USG.  Why B/C/D wrong: The standard Bishop score is a clinical assessment, not imaging or lab-based."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which cervical assessment method is most commonly done?","options":["Modified Bishop score","Bishop score","Amniotic fluid index","Cervical smear"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Bishop score is most commonly done.  Why A wrong: Modified Bishop is more accurate but not the most commonly performed.  Why C/D wrong: These are not cervical readiness scoring methods."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which cervical assessment method is more accurate?","options":["Bishop score","Modified Bishop score","Apgar score","Partograph"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Modified Bishop score is more accurate.  Why A wrong: The standard Bishop score is common but less accurate than the modified one.  Why C/D wrong: These do not assess cervical readiness."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which prostaglandin analogue is PGE1, recently approved for induction of labor?","options":["Misoprostol","Dinoprostone","Carboprost","Mifepristone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PGE1 is Misoprost (Misoprostol), recently approved for induction of labor.  Why B wrong: Dinoprostone is PGE2.  Why C wrong: Carboprost is 15-methyl PGF2α.  Why D wrong: Mifepristone (RU486) is an antiprogestogen, not a prostaglandin."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"What is the dose and route of Misoprostol (PGE1) for induction of labor as given in the notes?","options":["25 mcg per vaginally, 4 hourly","50 mcg orally, 2 hourly","0.5 mcg intracervically, 6 hourly","200 mg per vaginally, once"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The best dose of Misoprost is 25 mcg per vaginally, given 4 hourly.  Why B wrong: 50 mcg orally 2 hourly is not the dose stated.  Why C wrong: 0.5 mcg intracervically 6 hourly is the PGE2 (Dinoprostone) regimen.  Why D wrong: 200 mg per vaginally is the Mifepristone dose."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Misoprostol (PGE1) for induction of labor should NOT be used in patients with:","options":["Previous LSCS (lower segment cesarean section)","Asthma","Hypertension","Diabetes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PGE1/Misoprost is not used in patients with previous LSCS.  Why B wrong: PGE1 is NOT contraindicated in asthmatics.  Why C/D wrong: The notes specify previous LSCS as the contraindication, not hypertension or diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"According to the notes, the dose-dependent side effect of Misoprostol (PGE1) is:","options":["Fever + shivering","Diarrhea","Hypotension","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The side effect of Misoprost is fever + shivering (a dose-dependent side effect).  Why B wrong: Diarrhea is the main side effect of 15-methyl PGF2α (Carboprost).  Why C/D wrong: These are not listed as PGE1 side effects."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Misoprostol (PGE1) is contraindicated in which patient?","options":["Cesarean section patient","Asthmatic patient","Diabetic patient","Anemic patient"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Misoprost is contraindicated in a cesarean section patient.  Why B wrong: It is explicitly \"Not contraindicated in Asthmatic.\"  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"A key exam point about Misoprostol (PGE1) is that it is:","options":["Not contraindicated in asthmatic patients","Contraindicated in asthmatic patients","Only given intravenously","Used only for tocolysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PGE1 (Misoprost) is NOT contraindicated in asthmatic patients (unlike PGF2α/Carboprost).  Why B wrong: It is not contraindicated in asthma; that applies to Carboprost.  Why C wrong: The stated route is per vaginally.  Why D wrong: It is used for induction, PPH, and medical abortion."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Misoprost (PGE1) acts on which structures?","options":["Cervix + uterus","Cervix only","Uterus only","Placenta only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PGE1 acts on the cervix + uterus.  Why B/C wrong: It acts on both cervix and uterus, not one alone.  Why D wrong: The placenta is not its site of action."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which of the following are uses of Misoprost (PGE1)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Induction of labor (ripens cervix)","Prevention and treatment of PPH","Medical abortion","Tocolysis to stop preterm labor","Anticoagulation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: PGE1 is used for induction of labor (ripens cervix), PPH (prevention + treatment), and medical abortion.  Why D wrong: PGE1 stimulates contractions; it is not a tocolytic.  Why E wrong: It has no anticoagulant use."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"In what form is Misoprost available?","options":["Tablet","Intravenous infusion only","Suppository only","Transdermal patch"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Misoprost is available in tablet form.  Why B/C/D wrong: The notes state tablet form is available."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"PGE2 used for cervical ripening is also known as:","options":["Dinoprostone (Cerviprime gel)","Misoprostol","Carboprost","Oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PGE2 is Dinoprostone, available as Cerviprime gel.  Why B wrong: Misoprostol is PGE1.  Why C wrong: Carboprost is 15-methyl PGF2α.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is a hormone, not a prostaglandin."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"What is the dose and route of Dinoprostone (PGE2) cerviprime gel?","options":["0.5 mcg intracervically, 6 hourly, 4 doses","25 mcg per vaginally, 4 hourly","200 mg per vaginally","10 mg posterior vaginal fornix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dinoprostone gel is 0.5 mcg intracervically, 6 hourly, 4 doses.  Why B wrong: 25 mcg per vaginally 4 hourly is the Misoprost dose.  Why C wrong: 200 mg per vaginally is Mifepristone.  Why D wrong: 10 mg in the posterior vaginal fornix is Cervidil (Dinoprostone insert)."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"PGE2 (Dinoprostone gel) acts on the cervix but NOT on the:","options":["Uterus","Cervix","Vagina","Fetus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PGE2 acts on the cervix, \"Not on uterus.\"  Why B wrong: It does act on the cervix.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated distinction; the point is that it does not act on the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Because PGE2 (Dinoprostone) acts on the cervix and not the uterus, it is NOT used for:","options":["Prevention and treatment of PPH","Induction of labor","Ripening of cervix","Cervical softening"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Since PGE2 does not act on the uterus, it is not used in prevention and treatment of PPH.  Why B/C/D wrong: PGE2 IS used for induction of labor and ripening/softening of the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which prostaglandin is known as 15-methyl PGF2α?","options":["Carboprost / Dinoprost / Hemabate","Misoprostol","Dinoprostone","Mifepristone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 15-methyl PGF2α is Carboprost / Dinoprost / Hemabate.  Why B wrong: Misoprostol is PGE1.  Why C wrong: Dinoprostone is PGE2.  Why D wrong: Mifepristone is an antiprogestogen."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"The main side effect of 15-methyl PGF2α (Carboprost) is:","options":["Diarrhea","Fever + shivering","Hypertension","Constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The main side effect of Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α) is diarrhea.  Why B wrong: Fever + shivering is the side effect of Misoprost (PGE1).  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated main side effect."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"15-methyl PGF2α (Carboprost) is contraindicated in patients with:","options":["Asthma","Diabetes","Hypertension","Previous LSCS only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α) is contraindicated in asthmatic patients.  Why B/C wrong: These are not the stated contraindication.  Why D wrong: Previous LSCS is the caution for Misoprost, not the asthma contraindication of Carboprost."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"15-methyl PGF2α (Carboprost) acts on the:","options":["Uterus, not on cervix","Cervix, not on uterus","Cervix + uterus","Placenta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carboprost acts on the uterus, not on the cervix.  Why B wrong: That describes PGE2 (Dinoprostone).  Why C wrong: Cervix + uterus describes PGE1 (Misoprost).  Why D wrong: It does not act on the placenta."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"What are the uses of 15-methyl PGF2α (Carboprost)?","options":["Prevention and treatment of PPH; NOT used in induction of labor or cervical ripening","Induction of labor and cervical ripening only","Tocolysis","Medical abortion only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carboprost is used for prevention + treatment of PPH and is NOT used in induction of labor or ripening of cervix (as it acts on uterus, not cervix).  Why B wrong: It is specifically NOT used for induction/ripening.  Why C wrong: It stimulates the uterus; it is not a tocolytic.  Why D wrong: Its stated use is PPH prevention/treatment, not solely medical abortion."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Cervidil is a vaginal insert containing which drug?","options":["Dinoprostone","Misoprostol","Oxytocin","Mifepristone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cervidil contains Dinoprostone.  Why B/C/D wrong: Cervidil's active drug is Dinoprostone, not misoprostol, oxytocin, or mifepristone."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"What is the dose of Dinoprostone contained in a Cervidil insert?","options":["10 mg","0.5 mg","25 mcg","200 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cervidil (Dinoprostone) contains 10 mg.  Why B wrong: 0.5 mcg is the Cerviprime gel dose.  Why C wrong: 25 mcg is the Misoprost dose.  Why D wrong: 200 mg is the Mifepristone dose."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Where is the Cervidil insert placed?","options":["Posterior vaginal fornix","Intracervically","Inside the uterine cavity","Anterior abdominal wall"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cervidil is kept in the posterior vaginal fornix, which allows slow release of the drug.  Why B wrong: Intracervical placement is used for Cerviprime gel, not the Cervidil insert.  Why C wrong: It is not placed inside the uterine cavity.  Why D wrong: It is a vaginal device, not abdominal."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Cervidil is designed for:","options":["Cervical ripening","Postpartum hemorrhage control","Contraception","Tocolysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cervidil is designed for cervical ripening (as labelled on the device).  Why B/C/D wrong: Its stated purpose is cervical ripening."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"When should a Cervidil insert be removed?","options":["After 12 hours, or earlier if contractions begin","After 6 weeks","After 4 doses","Only after delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cervidil should be removed after 12 hours, or earlier if contraction begins.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated removal criterion."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Mifepristone is also known as:","options":["RU486 / Antiprogestogen","PGE1","Hemabate","Cerviprime"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mifepristone is RU486, an antiprogestogen.  Why B wrong: PGE1 is Misoprostol.  Why C wrong: Hemabate is Carboprost.  Why D wrong: Cerviprime is Dinoprostone gel."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"What is the dose and route of Mifepristone given in the notes?","options":["200 mg per vaginally","25 mcg per vaginally","10 mg vaginal insert","0.5 mcg intracervically"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mifepristone dose is 200 mg per vaginally.  Why B wrong: 25 mcg is the Misoprost dose.  Why C wrong: 10 mg is Cervidil.  Why D wrong: 0.5 mcg intracervically is Cerviprime gel."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which drug is used for BOTH inducing and augmentation of labor?","options":["Oxytocin","Misoprostol","Carboprost","Mifepristone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is used both for inducing and augmentation of labor.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not described as used for both induction and augmentation in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Foley's induction (Foley catheter) is a mechanical method best used for patients with:","options":["Previous LSCS","Active herpes infection","Placenta previa","Fetal distress"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Foley's induction is best used for patients with previous LSCS (avoiding prostaglandins that are contraindicated after cesarean).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are contraindications for induction generally, not indications for Foley's induction."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"In Foley's mechanical induction, the balloon catheter is placed in the uterus and filled with normal saline. What volume is used?","options":["30–50 mL","100–150 mL","5–10 mL","200 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Foley balloon is filled with normal saline 30–50 mL.  Why B/C/D wrong: These volumes are not the stated fill volume."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"After filling the Foley balloon with saline, what is the next step?","options":["Pull it down to the level of the internal os","Rupture the membranes immediately","Remove it after 6 hours","Inflate a second balloon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The catheter is pulled down to the level of the internal os to apply pressure and promote cervical ripening.  Why B wrong: Membrane rupture is not the described step.  Why C wrong: No 6-hour removal is described here.  Why D wrong: A second balloon is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"The Foley/transcervical balloon catheter shown is placed with the balloon between which structures, holding extra-amniotic saline?","options":["Between the amnion/chorion and the uterine wall (extra-amniotic space)","Inside the amniotic sac","In the vagina","In the rectum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The transcervical balloon sits in the extra-amniotic space (extraamniotic saline), outside the amnion and chorion.  Why B wrong: It is extra-amniotic, not inside the amniotic sac.  Why C/D wrong: It is a transcervical/uterine placement, not vaginal or rectal."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"If saline is infused even after filling the balloon bulb of the transcervical catheter, this technique is called:","options":["Extra-amniotic saline infusion","Amnioinfusion","Intra-amniotic instillation","Hydramnios induction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Infusing saline beyond filling the bulb is termed extra-amniotic saline infusion.  Why B wrong: Amnioinfusion instills fluid into the amniotic cavity, which is different.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the technique described."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Stripping of membranes for induction is described as which type of procedure?","options":["OPD (outpatient) procedure","Major surgical procedure","Intensive care procedure","Laparoscopic procedure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stripping of membranes is an OPD procedure.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is a simple outpatient procedure, not a major/ICU/laparoscopic one."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Sweeping and stripping of the membranes acts by:","options":["Releasing prostaglandins (PGs)","Increasing oxytocin secretion directly","Rupturing the membranes","Constricting cervical vessels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sweeping and stripping releases prostaglandins (PGs), promoting cervical changes/contractions.  Why B wrong: The mechanism cited is prostaglandin release, not direct oxytocin secretion.  Why C wrong: The note explicitly says don't rupture the membrane.  Why D wrong: It does not act by vasoconstriction."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"An important precaution during stripping of membranes is:","options":["Don't rupture the membrane","Rupture the membrane fully","Infuse saline into the uterus","Give IV oxytocin first"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states: \"Don't rupture the membrane\" during stripping.  Why B wrong: Rupturing the membrane is exactly what must be avoided.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the precaution stated for stripping."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Hygroscopic dilators, such as laminaria, are:","options":["Generally not used in term pregnancy; used in abortion to dilate the cervix","The first choice for term induction","Used to stop contractions","Given intravenously"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hygroscopic dilators (like laminaria) are generally not used in term pregnancy and are used in abortion to dilate the cervix.  Why B wrong: They are generally NOT used in term pregnancy.  Why C wrong: They dilate the cervix; they do not stop contractions.  Why D wrong: They are mechanical cervical devices, not IV drugs."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"The general rule for contraindications to induction of labor is:","options":["Wherever vaginal delivery is contraindicated, there induction of labor is contraindicated","Induction is contraindicated only in first pregnancies","Induction is contraindicated after 40 weeks","Induction is contraindicated in all multiparous women"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The rule is that wherever vaginal delivery is contraindicated, induction of labor is contraindicated (i.e., conditions requiring cesarean).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated rule."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication for induction of labor? (Select all that apply)","options":["Contracted pelvis","Cephalopelvic disproportion","Transverse lie / Brow presentation / Face (mentoposterior) position","Major degree of placenta previa","Bishop score of 10"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contracted pelvis, cephalopelvic disproportion, malpresentations (transverse lie/brow/mentoposterior face), and major degree of placenta previa are all contraindications to induction.  Why E wrong: A high Bishop score (≥9) favors successful induction; it is not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which fetal condition is a contraindication for induction of labor?","options":["Fetal distress","Normal fetal heart rate","Vertex presentation","Adequate liquor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal distress is a contraindication for induction of labor.  Why B/C/D wrong: Normal FHR, vertex presentation, and adequate liquor are reassuring findings, not contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Active genital tract herpes in the mother is a contraindication for induction because:","options":["Vaginal delivery is contraindicated (risk of neonatal transmission)","It causes fetal macrosomia","It shortens the cervix","It increases amniotic fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Active genital tract herpes contraindicates vaginal delivery (and therefore induction) due to risk of transmission to the neonate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the reason and are not stated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Induction of labor is contraindicated in a patient with a previous history of: (Select all that apply)","options":["2 LSCS (two prior cesarean sections)","Myomectomy (removal of fibroid)","Normal vaginal delivery","One episiotomy","Elective forceps delivery"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: A history of 2 LSCS and a history of myomectomy (removal of fibroid) are contraindications to induction due to uterine scar rupture risk.  Why C wrong: Prior normal vaginal delivery is not a contraindication.  Why D wrong: A prior episiotomy is not a contraindication.  Why E wrong: Prior forceps delivery is not listed as a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Augmentation of labor is done by which two methods?","options":["Artificial rupture of membranes (amniotomy) and oxytocin","Cesarean section and forceps","Cervidil and Foley catheter","Mifepristone and misoprostol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Augmentation is done by artificial rupture of membranes/amniotomy and oxytocin.  Why B wrong: These are delivery methods, not augmentation.  Why C/D wrong: These are cervical ripening/induction agents/devices, not the augmentation methods listed."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Augmentation of labor is indicated when there is a protracted active phase, defined as cervical dilatation of:","options":["<1 cm/hr","<3 cm/hr",">5 cm/hr",">2 cm/hr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A protracted active phase is dilatation of cervix <1 cm/hr, which prompts augmentation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These rates do not match the stated definition."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"What is the first step in augmentation of labor?","options":["Artificial rupture of membranes / amniotomy with Kocher's forceps","Immediate oxytocin bolus","Cesarean section","Insertion of Cervidil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 1st step is artificial rupture of membranes/amniotomy with Kocher's forceps.  Why B wrong: Oxytocin is started after 30 min, not first.  Why C wrong: Cesarean is not part of augmentation.  Why D wrong: Cervidil is a ripening device, not the first augmentation step."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"In augmentation of labor, oxytocin is started how long after amniotomy?","options":["After 30 minutes","Immediately","After 6 hours","After 12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After amniotomy, oxytocin is started after 30 min.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated interval is 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which instrument is used to perform artificial rupture of membranes (ARM/amniotomy)?","options":["Kocher's forceps","Cusco's speculum","Doyen's retractor","Allis forceps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ARM/amniotomy is done with Kocher's forceps.  Why B wrong: Cusco's speculum is used for visualizing the cervix, not rupturing membranes.  Why C wrong: Doyen's retractor is used in abdominal surgery.  Why D wrong: Allis forceps is a tissue-grasping instrument, not used for amniotomy here."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"During amniotomy, an \"amino cot\" (finger cot) is used on the examining finger primarily to:","options":["Aid in rupturing the membranes / protect the finger","Measure cervical dilatation","Deliver medication","Monitor fetal heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An amnio/finger cot is applied to the examining finger to assist safe rupture of the membranes.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not used for measuring dilatation, drug delivery, or fetal monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"ARM (amniotomy) is also known as:","options":["Artificial rupture of membrane","Augmentation with medication","Anterior repair of membrane","Assisted reproductive method"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ARM = Artificial Rupture of Membrane (amniotomy).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the abbreviation ARM."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which of the following is an advantage of artificial rupture of membrane (ARM)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Release of PGE2 → increased uterine contractions","Allows seeing the colour of amniotic fluid → detect fetal distress","Decreases bleeding in placental abruption","Prevents cord prolapse","Eliminates need for consent"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: ARM releases PGE2 (increasing uterine contractions), lets you see amniotic fluid colour to detect fetal distress, and decreases bleeding in placental abruption (leading to uterine contraction → decreased bleeding).  Why D wrong: ARM increases the risk of cord prolapse (especially with a free-floating head); it does not prevent it.  Why E wrong: Consent is still required for induction/ARM."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"How does ARM help reduce bleeding in placental abruption?","options":["It leads to uterine contraction, which decreases bleeding","It compresses the placenta directly","It increases amniotic fluid pressure","It stops prostaglandin release"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ARM decreases bleeding in placental abruption by leading to uterine contraction → decreased bleeding.  Why B/C/D wrong: The mechanism given is uterine contraction, not direct compression, increased fluid pressure, or stopping prostaglandins."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Seeing the colour of the amniotic fluid after ARM primarily helps detect:","options":["Fetal distress (e.g., meconium staining)","Placenta previa","Maternal anemia","Gestational diabetes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Observing amniotic fluid colour helps detect fetal distress (e.g., meconium-stained liquor).  Why B/C/D wrong: Fluid colour does not diagnose placenta previa, maternal anemia, or gestational diabetes."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which of the following are risks of ARM? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cord prolapse","Infection","Trauma","Cervical ripening","Placenta previa"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The risks of ARM are cord prolapse, infection, and trauma.  Why D wrong: Cervical ripening is a goal/benefit, not a risk.  Why E wrong: Placenta previa is a pre-existing condition, not a risk caused by ARM."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"The risk of cord prolapse during ARM is increased if the procedure is done when the head is:","options":["Free floating (not engaged)","Deeply engaged","On the perineum","Crowning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cord prolapse risk increases if ARM is done with a free floating head.  Why B/C/D wrong: An engaged, low, or crowning head reduces cord prolapse risk; the danger is with a free-floating (unengaged) head."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication of ARM? (Select all that apply)","options":["Free floating head","Intrauterine death of fetus","HIV positive females","Active genital herpes infection","Engaged fetal head"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications of ARM are free floating head (can lead to cord prolapse), intrauterine death of fetus, HIV positive females, and active genital herpes infection.  Why E wrong: An engaged fetal head is a favorable condition, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"ARM is contraindicated in HIV-positive and active genital herpes patients mainly because:","options":["Membrane rupture increases risk of vertical transmission to the fetus","It causes maternal bleeding","It reduces uterine contractions","It shortens the cervix excessively"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In HIV-positive and active genital herpes patients, rupturing membranes increases the risk of transmitting infection to the fetus, so ARM is contraindicated.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the reason for the contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"In polyhydramnios, membrane rupture should be performed as:","options":["Controlled rupture of membranes","Rapid full rupture","It should never be done","Only with oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In polyhydramnios, do controlled rupture of membranes to avoid sudden decompression.  Why B wrong: Rapid full rupture causes sudden gush and complications.  Why C wrong: Controlled ARM can be done; it is not absolutely forbidden.  Why D wrong: The point is controlled rupture, not that oxytocin is required."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Why is uncontrolled ARM dangerous in polyhydramnios? (Best sequence)","options":["Uterus is over-distended → sudden gush of amniotic fluid → uterus shrinks suddenly → placenta separates → abruptio","Cervix ripens → contractions stop → hemorrhage","Fetus descends → cord prolapse → infection","Fluid decreases → fetal distress → cesarean"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In polyhydramnios the uterus is over-distended; if ARM is uncontrolled, the entire amniotic fluid comes out suddenly, the uterus shrinks suddenly, the placenta separates, and abruptio results — so controlled ARM (or not doing it) is advised.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not reflect the pathophysiologic sequence described in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which of the following are nursing implications in Induction of Labor (IOL)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Obtain informed consent","Rule out any contraindication; review history of the patient","Maintain a partograph to assess the progress of labor","Use aseptic precautions","Withhold all monitoring during induction"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing implications in IOL include obtaining informed consent, ruling out contraindications/reviewing history, maintaining a partograph to assess labor progress, and using aseptic precautions.  Why E wrong: Monitoring must be maintained during induction, not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"During induction of labor, which tool is maintained to assess the progress of labor?","options":["Partograph","Bishop score chart only","Apgar chart","Growth chart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A partograph is maintained to assess the progress of labor.  Why B wrong: The Bishop score is used before induction to assess cervix, not to track ongoing labor progress.  Why C wrong: The Apgar chart assesses the newborn after birth.  Why D wrong: A growth chart tracks child growth, not labor progress."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Before starting induction of labor, the nurse should rule out contraindications by:","options":["Reviewing the patient's history","Skipping history if the cervix is favorable","Only checking the fetal weight","Obtaining consent after induction has begun"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should rule out any contraindication by reviewing the patient's history.  Why B wrong: History review is essential regardless of cervical status.  Why C wrong: Ruling out contraindications requires more than fetal weight.  Why D wrong: Informed consent must be obtained before induction, not after."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Which structures are labelled surrounding the transcervical balloon catheter in the Foley induction diagram?","options":["Amnion and chorion","Myometrium and endometrium only","Bladder and rectum","Ovary and fallopian tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the amnion and chorion, with the balloon in the extra-amniotic (extraamniotic saline) space.  Why B/C/D wrong: These structures are not the ones labelled around the transcervical balloon in the figure."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"Regarding the balloon volume shown in the Foley induction diagram, the balloon is depicted filled with approximately:","options":["30 mL of extra-amniotic saline","100 mL","5 mL","250 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows the balloon filled with 30 mL of extra-amniotic saline (within the stated 30–50 mL range).  Why B/C/D wrong: These volumes are not shown in the figure."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"A woman with a previous LSCS requires induction. Which agent should be AVOIDED per these notes?","options":["Misoprostol (PGE1)","Foley catheter","Mechanical dilation","Extra-amniotic saline infusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Misoprostol (PGE1) is not used in patients with previous LSCS (uterine rupture risk); Foley's induction is preferred.  Why B/C/D wrong: Foley's induction and mechanical/balloon methods are the preferred (mechanical) approaches for previous LSCS patients."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"A patient with severe asthma needs a uterotonic for PPH. Which prostaglandin is CONTRAINDICATED?","options":["15-methyl PGF2α (Carboprost)","PGE1 (Misoprostol)","Oxytocin","Dinoprostone gel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α) is contraindicated in asthmatic patients.  Why B wrong: PGE1/Misoprost is explicitly NOT contraindicated in asthmatics.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is not a prostaglandin and is not the asthma contraindication here.  Why D wrong: Dinoprostone gel acts on cervix and is not the asthma contraindication highlighted."},{"section":"Batch 063 · Induction of labor, augmentation of labor, Bishop score…","question":"A quiescent (non-contracting) uterus at term needs labor started. This intervention is termed:","options":["Induction of labor","Augmentation of labor","Tocolysis","Cervical cerclage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Initiating labor in a quiescent uterus is induction of labor.  Why B wrong: Augmentation applies to a uterus already contracting.  Why C wrong: Tocolysis suppresses contractions.  Why D wrong: Cervical cerclage is a stitch to keep the cervix closed."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"Before instilling Cerviprime (dinoprostone) or Misoprostol for cervical ripening/induction, the nurse should instruct the patient to:","options":["Empty the bladder (pass urine) before the drug is placed","Drink 2 litres of water","Lie in the knee-chest position","Take a hot water bath"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Instruct patient to pass urine before installing cerviprime/misoprostol\" so the bladder is empty during the procedure and subsequent rest.  Why B wrong: Fluid loading is not indicated and risks bladder distension.  Why C wrong: Knee-chest position is not the required position for prostaglandin administration.  Why D wrong: A hot bath is not part of the pre-administration instruction."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"After a prostaglandin (cerviprime/misoprostol) is administered for induction, the mother should be instructed to remain in the lateral position for how long?","options":["5–10 minutes","15–20 minutes","30–60 minutes","2–3 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify the mother should remain in the lateral position for 30–60 minutes after augmentation of the drug (to help retention/absorption).  Why A wrong: 5–10 minutes is too short as per the note.  Why B wrong: 15–20 minutes is not the stated duration.  Why D wrong: 2–3 hours is longer than the specified 30–60 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"While a patient is on prostaglandin (PG) induction, an important nursing responsibility is to:","options":["Monitor for side effects of prostaglandins (PG's)","Stop all monitoring once the drug is given","Encourage ambulation immediately","Avoid checking the cervix at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Monitor side effects of PG's\" as a nursing action during administration.  Why B wrong: Monitoring must continue, not stop.  Why C wrong: The patient is kept in the lateral position for 30–60 minutes, not ambulated immediately.  Why D wrong: Cervical dilation must be assessed later (after 4–6 hours), not avoided entirely."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"After starting prostaglandin induction, cervical dilation should be assessed after how many hours?","options":["1–2 hours","4–6 hours","8–10 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Assess cervical dilation after 4–6 hours of induction.\"  Why A wrong: 1–2 hours is earlier than the stated re-assessment window.  Why C wrong: 8–10 hours exceeds the stated 4–6 hours.  Why D wrong: 12 hours is not the specified assessment time."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"When using prostaglandin for cervical ripening, a repeat dose should NOT be given before:","options":["30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours","6 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes clearly state \"Do not repeat dose before 2 hours.\"  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is too short and risks hyperstimulation.  Why B wrong: 1 hour is less than the stated minimum interval.  Why D wrong: 6 hours is longer than the minimum required 2-hour interval."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"During an oxytocin infusion, which parameters should the nurse routinely check?","options":["Respiratory rate, blood pressure, contractions, and fetal heart rate","Only maternal temperature","Only urine output","Only maternal weight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Check RR, BP, contraction, fetal heart rate\" during oxytocin infusion.  Why B wrong: Temperature alone is insufficient monitoring.  Why C wrong: Urine output alone does not cover the required parameters.  Why D wrong: Maternal weight is not a monitored parameter during infusion."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"During oxytocin infusion, the nurse must watch for signs of water intoxication, which include:","options":["Confusion, drowsiness, and headache","Fever and rash","Constipation and dry mouth","Hypertension and bradycardia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state signs of water intoxication include \"confusion, drowsiness, headache\" (oxytocin has an antidiuretic effect causing water retention/hyponatraemia).  Why B wrong: Fever and rash are not signs of water intoxication.  Why C wrong: Constipation and dry mouth are not the listed signs.  Why D wrong: These are not the specific water-intoxication signs listed."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"A patient on oxytocin infusion develops uterine hyperstimulation with fetal distress. What is the FIRST/immediate nursing action?","options":["Increase the oxytocin rate","Stop the oxytocin infusion immediately","Give an enema","Ambulate the patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the first step when uterine hyperstimulation/fetal distress occurs as \"Stop oxytocin immediately.\"  Why A wrong: Increasing oxytocin worsens hyperstimulation and fetal distress.  Why C wrong: An enema is unrelated to managing hyperstimulation.  Why D wrong: The patient should be positioned in left lateral, not ambulated."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"When uterine hyperstimulation/fetal distress occurs during oxytocin infusion, in which position should the patient be placed?","options":["Right lateral position","Supine flat","Left lateral position","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Keep patient in left lateral position\" to improve uteroplacental perfusion.  Why A wrong: Right lateral is not the recommended position here.  Why B wrong: Supine flat can worsen aortocaval compression and fetal distress.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg is not the listed intervention."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"For a patient with uterine hyperstimulation/fetal distress on oxytocin, oxygen should be administered by face mask at what flow rate?","options":["2–4 L/min","6–10 L/min","12–15 L/min","1 L/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify \"O₂ inhalation (6–10 L/min) face mask.\"  Why A wrong: 2–4 L/min is below the stated range.  Why C wrong: 12–15 L/min exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 1 L/min is far below the stated 6–10 L/min."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"After stopping oxytocin, positioning the patient in left lateral, and starting oxygen for hyperstimulation/fetal distress, the nurse should next:","options":["Discharge the patient","Notify the doctor","Remove the IV line","Offer oral fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Notify doctor\" as an action after the immediate corrective measures for hyperstimulation/fetal distress.  Why A wrong: Discharge is inappropriate during an intrapartum emergency.  Why C wrong: IV access should be maintained, not removed.  Why D wrong: Oral fluids are not the priority in this emergency."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"Immediately after an Artificial Rupture of Membrane (ARM/amniotomy), the priority nursing assessment is to:","options":["Exclude cord prolapse","Measure maternal height","Give an enema","Start oral feeds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the first action after ARM is \"Exclude cord prolapse,\" a life-threatening obstetric emergency (umbilical cord may slip down with the gush of fluid).  Why B wrong: Maternal height is irrelevant after ARM.  Why C wrong: An enema is not indicated after ARM.  Why D wrong: Starting oral feeds is not the priority assessment."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"After ARM, the nurse should observe the amniotic fluid for its:","options":["Color and quantity","pH only","Glucose content","Specific gravity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states to observe \"Color and quantity of amniotic fluid\" (e.g., meconium staining suggests fetal distress).  Why B wrong: pH testing of liquor is not the noted observation.  Why C wrong: Glucose content is not assessed in liquor here.  Why D wrong: Specific gravity is not the noted parameter."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"After ARM, to detect a developing intrauterine infection, the nurse should check the patient's temperature:","options":["Every 30 minutes","Every 2 hours (2 hourly)","Once a day","Only if the patient complains"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state to check \"temperature 2 hourly to detect developing infection\" after ARM, because rupture of membranes increases the infection risk.  Why A wrong: 30-minutely temperature is more frequent than the stated 2-hourly.  Why C wrong: Once daily is inadequate for detecting early infection after ARM.  Why D wrong: Monitoring must be scheduled 2-hourly, not symptom-driven only."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"After ARM, besides amniotic fluid, the nurse should also assess the fetal head for its:","options":["Position and station","Circumference only","Bone density","Colour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists \"Position, station of fetal head\" as an assessment after ARM.  Why B wrong: Head circumference is not the intrapartum assessment noted here.  Why C wrong: Bone density is not assessed intrapartum.  Why D wrong: Colour of the head is not the noted parameter (colour applies to amniotic fluid)."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"After ARM, the time of the procedure should be documented/recorded on the:","options":["Discharge summary","Histogram (partograph)","Consent form only","Referral letter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state to record the \"Time of ARM on histogram\" (partograph), so labour progress and events are charted.  Why A wrong: Discharge summary is not where intrapartum ARM time is charted.  Why C wrong: The consent form is not the charting document for procedure timing.  Why D wrong: A referral letter is not the routine record for ARM time."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"What advice should the nurse give the patient regarding position after ARM?","options":["Rest in the left lateral position","Stand and walk continuously","Rest in the prone position","Sit upright at all times"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note advises \"Rest after ARM in left lateral position.\"  Why B wrong: Continuous walking is not advised after ARM.  Why C wrong: Prone position is not appropriate for a pregnant labouring woman.  Why D wrong: Sitting upright at all times is not the noted advice."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"Why is a single shot of antibiotic given after ARM?","options":["To reduce labour pain","Because there are increased chances of infection after membrane rupture","To speed cervical dilatation","To lower blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Give single shot of antibiotic (as ↑ chances of infection)\" because rupturing the membranes removes a barrier and increases ascending-infection risk.  Why A wrong: The antibiotic is not an analgesic.  Why C wrong: Antibiotics do not accelerate cervical dilatation.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics are not given to lower blood pressure."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"A partogram (partograph) is best defined as:","options":["A graphical representation of cervical dilatation with respect to time","A blood pressure record","A record of maternal weight gain","An ultrasound report of the fetus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define the partogram as \"a graphical representation of cervical dilatation with respect to time.\"  Why B wrong: It is not a blood-pressure record (though BP is one plotted parameter).  Why C wrong: It does not record maternal weight gain.  Why D wrong: It is not an ultrasound report."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"The composite partograph was given by which organisation, as part of which programme?","options":["UNICEF — as part of the \"Reproductive Health\" programme","WHO — as part of the \"Safe Motherhood\" programme","FIGO — as part of the \"Maternal Nutrition\" programme","ICMR — as part of the \"Family Planning\" programme"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"WHO gave composite partograph as a part of 'Safe Motherhood' programme.\"  Why A wrong: The source attributes it to WHO under Safe Motherhood, not UNICEF.  Why C wrong: FIGO is not credited in the notes.  Why D wrong: ICMR/Family Planning is not the stated source."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"In the original WHO composite partograph, which phase(s) of labour was/were plotted?","options":["Only the active phase","Only the latent phase","Both the latent phase and the active phase","Only the second stage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in the composite partograph \"Both latent phase & active phase were plotted.\"  Why A wrong: Only the active phase is plotted in the modified (current) partograph, not the composite.  Why B wrong: The latent phase was not plotted alone.  Why D wrong: The second stage alone was not the basis of the composite partograph."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"Regarding the WHO composite partograph, which statement is currently TRUE?","options":["It is the recommended partograph worldwide","It is no longer used","It plots only the second stage","It is used only for twins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes emphasise \"But now, this partograph is no longer used.\"  Why A wrong: The Modified WHO partograph is now used instead.  Why C wrong: It plotted both latent and active phases, not only the second stage.  Why D wrong: It was not restricted to twin pregnancies."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"Which partograph is used in current obstetric practice?","options":["Composite WHO partograph","Modified WHO partograph","Friedman's original curve only","No partograph is used now"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Now we use: Modified WHO partograph.\"  Why A wrong: The composite partograph is no longer used.  Why C wrong: The source names the Modified WHO partograph, not Friedman's curve.  Why D wrong: A partograph is still very much used intrapartum."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"According to the principles of the Modified WHO partograph, which phase is NOT plotted?","options":["Latent phase","Active phase","First stage","Descent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Principle 1 states \"No latent phase to be plotted\" on the modified partograph.  Why B wrong: The active phase IS plotted (plotting begins with the active phase).  Why C wrong: The active first stage is charted.  Why D wrong: Descent of the head is one of the plotted parameters."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the Modified WHO partograph, plotting begins with which phase of labour?","options":["Latent phase","Active phase","Second stage","Third stage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Principle 2 states \"Plotting begins with 'Active phase'.\"  Why A wrong: The latent phase is not plotted.  Why C wrong: The second stage is not where partograph plotting begins.  Why D wrong: The third stage (placental) is not where plotting begins."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"As per WHO, on the modified partograph, plotting/active phase begins at what cervical dilatation?","options":["2 cm","3 cm","4 cm","6 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"As per WHO/Partogram begins at 4 cm dilation\" and \"Plotting begins from 4 cms.\"  Why A wrong: 2 cm is within the latent phase, which is not plotted.  Why B wrong: 3 cm is still latent phase.  Why D wrong: 6 cm is not the stated starting point in this source (the source uses 4 cm)."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the WHO modified partograph, time is plotted on which axis?","options":["X-axis (horizontal)","Y-axis (vertical)","Both axes","It is not plotted"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Principle 4 states \"Time is plotted on: X-axis.\"  Why B wrong: The Y-axis is used for cervical dilatation.  Why C wrong: Time is on X-axis only, not both.  Why D wrong: Time is a key plotted parameter (X-axis)."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the WHO modified partograph, cervical dilatation is plotted on which axis?","options":["X-axis","Y-axis","Neither axis","A separate third axis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Principle 5 states \"Cervix dilatation is plotted on: Y-axis.\"  Why A wrong: The X-axis is used for time.  Why C wrong: Cervical dilatation is plotted (on the Y-axis).  Why D wrong: There is no separate third axis for dilatation."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"How many parallel lines are present on the (modified) partogram to monitor labour progress?","options":["1 line","2 parallel lines","3 lines","4 lines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Principle 6 states \"There are 2 lines (parallel lines) on partogram\" — the Alert line and the Action line.  Why A wrong: There are two, not one.  Why C wrong: There are two parallel lines, not three.  Why D wrong: There are two parallel lines, not four."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"The two parallel lines drawn on the partogram are called the:","options":["Alert line and Action line","Baseline and Reference line","Latent line and Active line","Descent line and Ascent line"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The partogram diagram labels the two parallel lines as the \"Alert line\" and \"Action line.\"  Why B wrong: These are not the named lines on the partogram.  Why C wrong: There are no lines called latent/active lines.  Why D wrong: There is no descent/ascent line pair."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"As per WHO, the minimum expected rate of cervical dilatation in the active phase of labour is:","options":["0.5 cm/hour","1 cm/hour","2 cm/hour","3 cm/hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram annotation (\"WHO says: rate of dilatation minimum 1 cm/hr\") indicates the alert line is drawn at a minimum dilatation rate of 1 cm per hour.  Why A wrong: 0.5 cm/hr is below the stated minimum.  Why C wrong: 2 cm/hr is faster than the stated minimum.  Why D wrong: 3 cm/hr is not the stated minimum rate."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the partograph, the progress of the patient (cervical dilatation) is plotted using which symbol?","options":["\"O\"","\"X\"","\"•\" (dot)","\"▲\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"The progress of patient is plotted on partograph = by 'X'\" (cervix is Plot X).  Why A wrong: \"O\" is used to plot descent of the head, not cervical dilatation.  Why C wrong: A dot is not the noted symbol.  Why D wrong: A triangle is not used."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the partograph, labour is said to be progressing NORMALLY when the plotting lies:","options":["To the left of the alert line","To the right of the action line","On the action line","Below the X-axis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Labour is said to be progressing NORMALLY → if it lies to left of Alert line.\"  Why B wrong: Lying to the right of the action line means action must be taken (abnormal).  Why C wrong: Touching the action line indicates action is required.  Why D wrong: Plotting below the X-axis is not meaningful."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the partograph, the time duration between the alert line and the action line is:","options":["2 hours","4 hours","6 hours","8 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram is labelled \"Time duration = 4 hrs,\" meaning the action line is drawn 4 hours to the right of the alert line.  Why A wrong: 2 hours is not the labelled interval.  Why C wrong: 6 hours is not the labelled interval.  Why D wrong: 8 hours is not the labelled interval."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"If the patient's progress (cervical dilatation) crosses to the RIGHT of the alert line, what should happen?","options":["Nothing; this is normal","The obstetrician should be alerted and the patient should be referred to a higher centre","The oxytocin should be doubled immediately","Discharge the patient home"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if progress lies to the right of the alert line, \"Obstetrician should get alerted\" and \"this is the time when patient should be referred to higher centre.\"  Why A wrong: Crossing to the right of the alert line is NOT normal.  Why C wrong: Doubling oxytocin is not the stated response at the alert line.  Why D wrong: Discharge is inappropriate during abnormal labour progress."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"According to the partograph interpretation, what does it signify when the plotting touches the action line or lies to the right of the action line?","options":["Labour is normal","Action has to be taken now","The patient can be discharged","Plotting should stop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if progress \"touches the action or lies to the right of action line → Now action has to be taken.\"  Why A wrong: Reaching the action line is abnormal, not normal.  Why B is correct as above.  Why C wrong: Discharge is inappropriate; intervention is required.  Why D wrong: Plotting/monitoring continues; it does not stop."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"Regarding the \"action\" required when the partograph reaches the action line, which statement is TRUE?","options":["Action always means immediate cesarean section","Action does not always mean cesarean section; it depends on the diagnosis","Action always means forceps delivery","Action always means stopping labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Remember\" box states \"Action does not always mean cesarean section\" and \"Action depends on diagnosis.\"  Why A wrong: The notes explicitly say action is NOT always cesarean.  Why C wrong: Forceps is not the universal action.  Why D wrong: Stopping labour is not the universal action."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"According to the notes, the appropriate management (action) for a PROLONGED LATENT PHASE is:","options":["Immediate cesarean section","Therapeutic rest","Artificial rupture of membrane + oxytocin","Forceps delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Prolonged latent phase = Therapeutic Rest.\"  Why A wrong: Cesarean is not the noted action for prolonged latent phase.  Why C wrong: ARM + oxytocin is the action for protracted active phase, not prolonged latent phase.  Why D wrong: Forceps is not indicated for a prolonged latent phase."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"According to the notes, the appropriate management (action) for a PROTRACTED ACTIVE PHASE is:","options":["Therapeutic rest","Artificial rupture of membrane (ARM) + oxytocin","Bed rest only","Immediate cesarean section"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Protracted active phase = Artificial rupture of membrane + Oxytocin\" (augmentation of labour).  Why A wrong: Therapeutic rest is the action for prolonged latent phase, not protracted active phase.  Why C wrong: Bed rest alone does not augment protracted active-phase labour.  Why D wrong: Cesarean is not the automatic action; augmentation is tried first."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On a partograph showing good progress, the cervical (dilatation) line should:","options":["Cross to the right of the alert line","NOT cross the alert line (stays to its left)","Follow the action line exactly","Go below zero"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The example graph (a) is annotated \"The cervical line does not cross the Alert line\" and \"Pt progress is towards left of alert line,\" indicating good/normal progress.  Why A wrong: Crossing right of the alert line indicates abnormal progress.  Why C wrong: Good progress stays left of the alert line, not on the action line.  Why D wrong: Dilatation cannot go below zero."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the partograph, descent of the fetal head is plotted using which symbol?","options":["\"X\"","\"O\"","\"△\"","\"*\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The example graph labels \"Descent of head [Plot O],\" i.e., descent is charted with the symbol \"O.\"  Why A wrong: \"X\" is used for cervical dilatation (Plot X).  Why C wrong: A triangle is not used.  Why D wrong: An asterisk is not the noted symbol."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"In the partograph example graph, the \"Good progress of descent\" is demonstrated by the descent plot (O) that shows the fetal head:","options":["Remaining at the same station throughout","Descending (station value falling) over time","Rising higher over time","Not being recorded"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Graph (a) annotates \"Good progress of descent,\" shown by the descent plot moving downward (head descending) over successive hours.  Why A wrong: A static head station indicates arrest/poor descent, not good progress.  Why C wrong: The head rising higher indicates poor progress.  Why D wrong: Descent is actively plotted with \"O.\""},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the partograph example, the X-axis for the mid (cervicograph) section shows two rows: \"Hours\" and \"Time.\" If Hours read 1–8, the corresponding \"Time\" row starts at 10 and reads:","options":["10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17 (clock hours)","1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8","20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80, 90","0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5, 3, 3.5, 4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The example graph shows Hours 1–8 aligned with Time 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17 (actual clock time of the observations).  Why B wrong: This repeats the \"Hours\" row, not the \"Time\" (clock) row.  Why C wrong: These are not the values shown.  Why D wrong: These fractional values are not on the axis."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"The complete WHO modified partograph is divided into how many parts?","options":["2 parts","3 parts","4 parts","5 parts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"Complete Partograph → 3 Parts\" (Top, Middle, Bottom).  Why A wrong: There are three, not two, parts.  Why C wrong: There are three parts, not four.  Why D wrong: There are three parts, not five."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the complete WHO modified partograph, the TOP part records the:","options":["Fetal condition","Cervical dilatation across time","Maternal condition","Drug schedule only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"TOP part → Fetal condition\" (fetal heart rate, liquor, moulding).  Why B wrong: Cervical dilatation across time is the middle part.  Why C wrong: Maternal condition is the bottom part.  Why D wrong: Drug schedule is part of the maternal (bottom) section, not the whole top part."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the complete WHO modified partograph, the MIDDLE part records the:","options":["Fetal heart rate","Cervical dilatation across time","Maternal blood pressure","Urine output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"Middle part → Cervical dilatation across time.\"  Why A wrong: Fetal heart rate is in the top part.  Why C wrong: Blood pressure is in the bottom (maternal) part.  Why D wrong: Urine output is in the bottom (maternal) part."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the complete WHO modified partograph, the BOTTOM part records the:","options":["Fetal condition","Cervical dilatation","Maternal condition","Amniotic fluid colour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"Bottom Part → Maternal condition\" (contractions, oxytocin, drugs/IV fluids, BP & pulse, temperature, urine).  Why A wrong: Fetal condition is the top part.  Why B wrong: Cervical dilatation is the middle part.  Why D wrong: Amniotic fluid (liquor) is charted in the top (fetal) part."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the WHO modified partograph form, the fetal heart rate scale ranges from:","options":["60 to 120","80 to 200","100 to 160","40 to 180"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The partograph form shows the Fetal heart rate scale printed from 80 up to 200.  Why A wrong: The scale starts at 80, not 60, and goes to 200.  Why C wrong: 100–160 is only the normal range, not the printed scale extent.  Why D wrong: The printed scale is 80–200, not 40–180."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"Which of the following parameters is recorded in the UPPER part (fetal condition) of the WHO modified partograph? (Select the best group)","options":["Fetal heart rate, liquor, and moulding","Blood pressure, pulse, and temperature","Contractions per 10 minutes and oxytocin","Urine amount, protein, and acetone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The upper part of the form lists Fetal heart rate, Liquor, and Moulding — all fetal condition parameters.  Why B wrong: BP, pulse, and temperature are maternal (lower part) parameters.  Why C wrong: Contractions and oxytocin are lower-part (maternal) parameters.  Why D wrong: Urine amount/protein/acetone are lower-part (maternal) parameters."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the WHO modified partograph, uterine contractions are recorded as the number of contractions per:","options":["5 minutes","10 minutes","15 minutes","30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The form labels this row \"Contractions per 10 mints\" (per 10 minutes), scaled 1–5.  Why A wrong: The form uses a 10-minute window, not 5.  Why C wrong: Not 15 minutes.  Why D wrong: Not 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the WHO modified partograph, the row that records oxytocin is labelled:","options":["Oxytocin U/L, drops/minute","Oxytocin mg/kg","Oxytocin tablets/day","Oxytocin mL/hour only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The form's lower part has a row labelled \"Oxytocin U/L drops/Minute.\"  Why B wrong: The form does not use mg/kg for oxytocin.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is given IV, not as tablets/day on this chart.  Why D wrong: The chart specifically records units/litre and drops/minute."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"On the WHO modified partograph form, the blood pressure and pulse section is scaled from approximately:","options":["40 to 100","80 to 180","100 to 200","60 to 140"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The form's \"Blood pressure and pulse\" scale is printed from 80 up to 180.  Why A wrong: The scale starts at 80, not 40.  Why C wrong: The upper limit shown is 180, not 200.  Why D wrong: The printed range is 80–180, not 60–140."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"In the maternal (lower) section of the WHO modified partograph, urine is assessed for which three components?","options":["Amount, protein, and acetone","Colour, odour, and pH","Glucose, ketone, and bilirubin","Volume, sodium, and potassium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The form lists Urine — \"Amount, Protein, Acetone.\"  Why B wrong: Colour/odour/pH are not the charted components.  Why C wrong: Bilirubin is not charted here (acetone/ketones and protein are).  Why D wrong: Sodium and potassium are not on the urine row."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"Besides fetal heart rate, the fetal-condition (top) part of the partograph also records liquor and:","options":["Moulding","Maternal temperature","Oxytocin drops","Blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The top part lists FHR, Liquor, and \"moulding\" (degree of overlap of fetal skull bones).  Why B wrong: Temperature is a maternal (bottom) parameter.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is charted in the maternal (bottom) part.  Why D wrong: Blood pressure is a maternal (bottom) parameter."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"Which identifying/demographic details are entered at the top of the WHO modified partograph form?","options":["Reg. No., Name, Age, Date, Labour duration (Hrs), and Faculty Name","Blood group and Rh only","Occupation and address only","Insurance number only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The form header has fields for Reg. No., Name, Age, Date, Labour duration (Hrs), and Faculty Name.  Why B wrong: Blood group/Rh fields are not the labelled header on this form.  Why C wrong: Occupation/address are not the labelled fields.  Why D wrong: Insurance number is not on the form header."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"In the completed partograph example (Mrs. J), the recorded obstetric history was:","options":["Gravida 1, Para 0+0","Gravida 3, Para 2+1","Gravida 2, Para 1+0","Gravida 4, Para 3+1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The worked example shows \"Name Mrs. J, Gravida 1, Para 0+0.\"  Why B wrong: The example is a primigravida (Gravida 1, Para 0+0), not multipara.  Why C wrong: Not Gravida 2, Para 1+0.  Why D wrong: Not Gravida 4, Para 3+1."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"In the completed partograph example (Mrs. J), what was the hospital number and the time membranes ruptured?","options":["Hospital number 1443; membranes ruptured at 13:30 hours","Hospital number 1234; membranes ruptured at 10:00 hours","Hospital number 1443; membranes ruptured at 08:00 hours","Hospital number 2000; membranes ruptured at 13:30 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The example records Hospital number 1443 and \"Ruptured membranes 13:30 hours.\"  Why B wrong: The hospital number is 1443, and 10:00 A.M. was the time of admission, not rupture.  Why C wrong: Membrane rupture was at 13:30, not 08:00.  Why D wrong: The hospital number is 1443, not 2000 (2.5.2000 was the date of admission)."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"In the completed partograph example (Mrs. J), the date and time of admission were:","options":["2.5.2000 at 10:00 A.M.","5.2.2000 at 1:00 P.M.","2.5.2000 at 13:30 hours","2.5.2010 at 10:00 A.M."],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The example records \"Date of admission 2.5.2000\" and \"Time of admission 10:00 A.M.\"  Why B wrong: The date/time do not match the example.  Why C wrong: 13:30 hours was the time of membrane rupture, not admission.  Why D wrong: The year is 2000, not 2010."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"A labouring woman's partograph shows the cervical dilatation plot has moved between the alert and action lines. The obstetric team is alerted and considers referral. This corresponds to the plot being:","options":["To the left of the alert line","Between the alert and action lines (right of alert line)","Far right of the action line","Below the descent line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crossing to the right of the alert line (toward the action line) is when \"the obstetrician should get alerted\" and referral to a higher centre is considered.  Why A wrong: Left of the alert line is normal progress; no alert needed.  Why C wrong: Far right of the action line means action must already be taken.  Why D wrong: \"Below the descent line\" is not a partograph concept."},{"section":"Batch 064 · Nursing care during Cerviprime/Misoprostol administration…","question":"A primigravida in the active phase should dilate at a minimum of 1 cm/hr. Over 4 hours she dilates from 4 cm to only 5 cm, and her plot now touches the action line. According to the notes, the nurse anticipates that:","options":["No intervention is needed","Action must now be taken (which may be ARM + oxytocin or other, depending on diagnosis — not necessarily cesarean)","Immediate cesarean section is the only option","Oxytocin must be stopped"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reaching the action line means \"action has to be taken now,\" and per the \"Remember\" box, action depends on the diagnosis and does not always mean cesarean (e.g., a protracted active phase is managed with ARM + oxytocin).  Why A wrong: Reaching the action line requires intervention.  Why C wrong: Cesarean is not the only/automatic action.  Why D wrong: Stopping oxytocin is done for hyperstimulation, not for slow progress at the action line."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the upper part of the partograph, how frequently should the fetal heart rate (FHR) be monitored and recorded?","options":["Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every 1 hour","Every 2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The partograph specifies that fetal heart rate is monitored every 30 minutes in the upper part of the chart.  Why A wrong: 15 minutes is more frequent than the stated partograph interval.  Why C wrong: 1 hour is too infrequent for FHR monitoring on a partograph.  Why D wrong: 2 hours is the interval for maternal BP, not FHR."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, how is the fetal heart rate represented?","options":["With a cross (X)","With a dot","With a shaded box","With a triangle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state FHR is represented with a dot on the partograph.  Why A wrong: A cross/X is not used for FHR representation here.  Why C wrong: Shaded boxes are used to represent uterine contractions, not FHR.  Why D wrong: A triangle is not the symbol used for FHR."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"What is the normal range of fetal heart rate (FHR) recorded on the partograph?","options":["100–140 bpm","110–160 bpm","120–180 bpm","80–120 bpm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give normal FHR = 110–160 bpm.  Why A wrong: The upper limit (140) is below the stated 160 bpm.  Why C wrong: 180 bpm exceeds the normal upper limit of 160.  Why D wrong: 80–120 bpm is below the normal range and indicates bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the fetal heart rate section of the partograph, each square represents how much time?","options":["15 minutes","30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state each square represents 30 minutes for FHR recording.  Why A wrong: 15 minutes is not the value assigned to each square.  Why C wrong: 1 hour corresponds to a big square on the cervical dilatation section, not the FHR square described here.  Why D wrong: 2 hours is not represented by a single square."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, the letter \"I\" used to record the status of amniotic fluid stands for:","options":["Inadequate liquor","Intact (membranes are intact)","Increased liquor","Infected liquor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the partograph amniotic fluid coding, I = Intact (membranes are intact).  Why A wrong: \"Inadequate liquor\" is not the meaning of I; reduced liquor is not coded this way.  Why C wrong: \"Increased liquor\" is not part of the coding system.  Why D wrong: \"Infected liquor\" is not a partograph amniotic fluid code."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph amniotic fluid coding, \"A\" stands for:","options":["Adequate liquor","Absent liquor","Amber liquor","Abnormal liquor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A = Absent liquor in the partograph amniotic fluid coding.  Why A wrong: \"Adequate liquor\" is not the meaning of A.  Why C wrong: \"Amber\" is a colour term not used in this coding.  Why D wrong: \"Abnormal\" is not a defined amniotic fluid code."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, the amniotic fluid code \"C\" indicates:","options":["Clotted liquor","Clear liquor","Coloured liquor","Concentrated liquor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: C = Clear liquor in partograph amniotic fluid coding.  Why A wrong: \"Clotted\" is not part of the coding.  Why C wrong: \"Coloured\" is not a defined code.  Why D wrong: \"Concentrated\" is not used."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, the amniotic fluid code \"B\" indicates:","options":["Bile stained amniotic fluid","Blood stained amniotic fluid","Brown amniotic fluid","Bloody show"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: B = Blood stained amniotic fluid in the partograph coding.  Why A wrong: Bile staining is not a partograph amniotic fluid code.  Why C wrong: \"Brown\" is not the defined meaning of B.  Why D wrong: \"Bloody show\" is a sign of labour onset, not the amniotic-fluid code B."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, the amniotic fluid code \"M\" indicates:","options":["Mild liquor","Meconium stained amniotic fluid","Mucous stained amniotic fluid","Moderate liquor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: M = Meconium stained amniotic fluid in the partograph coding.  Why A wrong: \"Mild\" is not the defined meaning of M.  Why C wrong: \"Mucous stained\" is not the code.  Why D wrong: \"Moderate\" is not used in this coding."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"When recording the status of amniotic fluid on the partograph, at what time point is it specifically noted?","options":["At the onset of the first contraction","When membranes rupture / at the time of artificial rupture of membranes","Only after full dilatation","Every 4 hours regardless of membrane status"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states to record time when membranes rupture / artificial rupture of membranes.  Why A wrong: Recording is tied to membrane rupture, not the first contraction.  Why C wrong: It is noted at membrane rupture, which can occur before full dilatation.  Why D wrong: The note ties it to rupture of membranes, not a fixed 4-hourly schedule."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, moulding of grade 0 is recorded as:","options":["+ (plus one)","(–) / minus sign","++","+++"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 0 moulding = (–), i.e., no moulding.  Why A wrong: \"+\" is used for Grade +1.  Why C wrong: \"++\" is used for Grade +2.  Why D wrong: \"+++\" is used for Grade +3."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, moulding grade +1 is recorded as:","options":["(–)","+","++","+++"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grade +1 moulding is recorded as a single plus (+).  Why A wrong: (–) denotes grade 0 (no moulding).  Why C wrong: ++ denotes grade +2.  Why D wrong: +++ denotes grade +3."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, moulding grade +2 is recorded as:","options":["+","++","+++","(–)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grade +2 moulding is recorded as ++.  Why A wrong: + denotes grade +1.  Why C wrong: +++ denotes grade +3.  Why D wrong: (–) denotes grade 0."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, moulding grade +3 is recorded as:","options":["+","++","+++","++++"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Grade +3 moulding is recorded as +++.  Why A wrong: + denotes grade +1.  Why B wrong: ++ denotes grade +2.  Why D wrong: ++++ (four plus) is not one of the listed grades; the maximum shown is +++."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, increasing moulding is an indicator of:","options":["Rapid, uncomplicated progress of labour","Slow progress of labour","Normal fetal descent only","Adequate liquor volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moulding indicates less/slow progress of labour.  Why A wrong: Moulding signals slow, not rapid uncomplicated progress.  Why C wrong: Moulding relates to skull-bone overlap and slow progress, not simply normal descent.  Why D wrong: Moulding does not indicate liquor volume."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"The combination of moulding plus slow progress of labour is suggestive of:","options":["Cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD)","Precipitate labour","Placenta previa","Cord prolapse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state moulding + slow progress of labour = cephalopelvic disproportion.  Why B wrong: Precipitate labour is excessively rapid, the opposite of slow progress.  Why C wrong: Placenta previa is an abnormal placental site, unrelated to moulding.  Why D wrong: Cord prolapse is a cord emergency, not indicated by moulding + slow progress."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"Regarding moulding during labour, which statement is TRUE?","options":["Any degree of moulding is always pathological","Some amount of moulding is normal during labour","Moulding never occurs in normal labour","Moulding only occurs after delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state some amount of moulding is normal during labour.  Why A wrong: A small amount of moulding is a normal finding, not always pathological.  Why C wrong: Moulding does occur in normal labour.  Why D wrong: Moulding occurs during labour as the head passes through the pelvis, not after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"The middle part of the partograph is used to plot:","options":["Fetal heart rate against time","Dilatation of cervix against time","Maternal blood pressure against time","Uterine contractions against time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The middle part records dilatation of cervix against time.  Why A wrong: FHR is plotted in the upper part.  Why C wrong: Maternal BP is recorded in the bottom part.  Why D wrong: Uterine contractions are recorded in the bottom part."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the middle part of the partograph, each big square represents:","options":["30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours","4 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each big square on the partograph = 1 hour.  Why A wrong: 30 minutes corresponds to each small square.  Why C wrong: 2 hours is not the value of a big square.  Why D wrong: 4 hours is the interval between alert and action lines, not a single big square."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, the X-axis represents:","options":["Cervical dilatation","1 hour (time)","Fetal heart rate","Descent of head"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The X-axis represents time, with each unit = 1 hour.  Why A wrong: Cervical dilatation is plotted on the Y-axis.  Why C wrong: FHR is a separate section, not the X-axis of the cervicograph.  Why D wrong: Descent of head is plotted separately, not as the X-axis."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the middle part of the partograph, each small square represents:","options":["15 minutes","30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each small square = 30 minutes.  Why A wrong: 15 minutes is not the stated value.  Why C wrong: 1 hour corresponds to a big square.  Why D wrong: 2 hours is not represented by a small square."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, each square on the Y-axis represents how much cervical dilatation?","options":["0.5 cm","1 cm","2 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On the Y-axis, each square represents 1 cm.  Why A wrong: 0.5 cm is not the stated value.  Why C wrong: 2 cm is not the value per square.  Why D wrong: 5 cm is not represented by a single square."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, what is the time interval between the alert line and the action line?","options":["2 hours","3 hours","4 hours","6 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The time duration between the alert and action lines = 4 hours.  Why A wrong: 2 hours is the maternal BP recording interval, not the alert-to-action gap.  Why B wrong: 3 hours is not the stated interval.  Why D wrong: 6 hours exceeds the stated 4-hour interval."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"The alert line on the partograph is plotted on the basis of cervical dilatation at a rate of:","options":["0.5 cm/hour","1 cm/hour (in active phase)","2 cm/hour","1 cm/2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The alert line is plotted on the basis of cervical dilatation = 1 cm/hour in the active phase.  Why A wrong: 0.5 cm/hour is slower than the expected alert-line rate.  Why C wrong: 2 cm/hour is faster than the standard.  Why D wrong: 1 cm per 2 hours is slower than the alert-line basis."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"During labour monitoring on the partograph, how often is a per-vaginal examination performed?","options":["Every 1 hour","Every 2 hours","Every 4 hours","Every 6 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per-vaginal examination is done every 4 hours.  Why A wrong: 1 hour is more frequent than the stated interval.  Why B wrong: 2 hours is the BP interval, not the PV exam interval.  Why D wrong: 6 hours is less frequent than the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the bottom part of the partograph, uterine contractions are recorded. On this section, each square represents:","options":["Duration of one contraction","One contraction","30 minutes","1 cm dilatation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the contraction recording, each square represents 1 contraction.  Why A wrong: Duration is shown by the type of shading, not by counting squares.  Why C wrong: 30 minutes applies to time squares elsewhere, not to the number-of-contractions squares.  Why D wrong: 1 cm dilatation refers to the Y-axis of the cervicograph, not the contraction section."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph contraction chart, the number of contractions is counted over what time period?","options":["5 minutes","10 minutes","15 minutes","30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart records the number of contractions in 10 minutes.  Why A wrong: 5 minutes is not the stated counting window.  Why C wrong: 15 minutes is not used.  Why D wrong: 30 minutes is how often contractions are assessed by palpation, not the counting window itself."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"How are uterine contractions measured/assessed during labour according to the partograph notes?","options":["By continuous electronic tocography only","Every 30 minutes by placing your hand on the uterus","Every 2 hours by ultrasound","Only when the mother reports pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Contractions are measured every 30 minutes by placing your hand on the uterus (palpation).  Why A wrong: The notes describe manual palpation, not mandatory electronic tocography.  Why C wrong: Ultrasound is not the method or interval described.  Why D wrong: Assessment is scheduled every 30 minutes, not only when pain is reported."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, how is a uterine contraction lasting less than 20 seconds represented?","options":["Dotted / stippled squares","Diagonal-line (hatched) squares","Completely shaded (solid black) squares","Empty squares"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Contractions of duration < 20 seconds are shown by dotted/stippled shading.  Why B wrong: Diagonal (hatched) lines represent 20–40 second contractions.  Why C wrong: Solid black squares represent contractions > 40 seconds.  Why D wrong: Empty squares do not denote a contraction duration category."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, a uterine contraction lasting 20–40 seconds is represented by:","options":["Dotted / stippled squares","Diagonal-line (hatched) squares","Solid black squares","Blank squares"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 20–40 second contractions are shown by diagonal (hatched) line shading.  Why A wrong: Dotted squares represent < 20 seconds.  Why C wrong: Solid black squares represent > 40 seconds.  Why D wrong: Blank squares are not a defined duration category."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, a uterine contraction lasting more than 40 seconds is represented by:","options":["Dotted / stippled squares","Diagonal-line (hatched) squares","Completely shaded (solid black) squares","Cross-marked squares"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Contractions > 40 seconds are shown by completely shaded (solid black) squares.  Why A wrong: Dotted squares represent < 20 seconds.  Why B wrong: Hatched squares represent 20–40 seconds.  Why D wrong: Cross-marked squares are not a defined category."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the \"Recording contractions\" diagram, \"3 contractions each lasting less than 20 seconds\" would be represented by how many dotted squares in the 10-minute column?","options":["1 dotted square","2 dotted squares","3 dotted squares","4 dotted squares"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Since each square = 1 contraction and duration < 20 s uses dotted shading, 3 such contractions = 3 dotted squares.  Why A wrong: 1 square would represent only 1 contraction.  Why B wrong: 2 squares would represent only 2 contractions.  Why D wrong: 4 squares would represent 4 contractions."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"According to the recording-contractions diagram, \"4 contractions each lasting 20–40 seconds\" is plotted as:","options":["4 dotted squares","4 diagonal-hatched squares","4 solid black squares","2 hatched squares"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 20–40 second contractions use diagonal hatching, and 4 contractions = 4 squares filled with hatching.  Why A wrong: Dotted shading denotes < 20 seconds.  Why C wrong: Solid black denotes > 40 seconds.  Why D wrong: 2 squares would represent only 2 contractions, not 4."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the recording-contractions diagram, \"5 contractions each lasting more than 40 seconds\" is depicted as:","options":["5 dotted squares","5 diagonal-hatched squares","5 completely shaded (solid black) squares","5 blank squares"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Contractions > 40 seconds use solid black shading; 5 such contractions = 5 solid black squares.  Why A wrong: Dotted shading is for < 20 seconds.  Why B wrong: Hatching is for 20–40 seconds.  Why D wrong: Blank squares indicate no recorded contraction."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"The bottom part of the partograph also records information about oxytocin administration. What does \"Oxytocin U/L drops/min\" refer to on the chart?","options":["The fetal heart rate","The concentration and drip rate of oxytocin infusion","The maternal temperature","The urine output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The bottom part records oxytocin dose in units per litre and the infusion rate in drops per minute.  Why A wrong: FHR is recorded in the upper part.  Why C wrong: Temperature is a separate row on the chart.  Why D wrong: Urine output is recorded separately."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"According to the partograph notes, the maternal pulse rate should be checked how often?","options":["Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every 1 hour","Every 2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse rate of the mother is checked every 30 minutes.  Why A wrong: 15 minutes is more frequent than stated.  Why C wrong: 1 hour is not the stated interval.  Why D wrong: 2 hours is the BP interval, not the pulse interval."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, the maternal blood pressure (BP) is recorded how often?","options":["Every 30 minutes","Every 1 hour","Every 2 hours","Every 4 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: BP of the mother is recorded every 2 hours.  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is the pulse interval.  Why B wrong: 1 hour is not the stated BP interval.  Why D wrong: 4 hours is the temperature interval."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, maternal temperature should be checked how often?","options":["Every 30 minutes","Every 1 hour","Every 2 hours","Every 4 hours"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Temperature is checked every 4 hours.  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is the pulse interval.  Why B wrong: 1 hour is not the temperature interval.  Why C wrong: 2 hours is the BP interval."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In addition to contractions, oxytocin, pulse, BP and temperature, the partograph also monitors which of the following?","options":["Urine output","Serum electrolytes","Blood glucose","Fetal weight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list urine output among the parameters the partograph tells about.  Why B wrong: Serum electrolytes are not listed on the partograph.  Why C wrong: Blood glucose is not a listed partograph parameter (though urine acetone/protein are recorded).  Why D wrong: Fetal weight is not monitored on the partograph."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the partograph, in addition to oxytocin, the administration of which of the following is charted in the \"Drugs given and fluids\" section?","options":["IV fluid","Oral antibiotics","Antenatal steroids","Iron tablets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list IV fluid among items recorded on the partograph.  Why B wrong: Oral antibiotics are not the specified charted item.  Why C wrong: Antenatal steroids are not listed on this partograph section.  Why D wrong: Iron tablets are not charted on the partograph."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"How does the WHO simplified partogram compare to the modified WHO partogram?","options":["It includes detailed latent phase plotting","It is similar to the modified WHO partogram but has no latent phase plotting","It plots only the fetal heart rate","It replaces the alert and action lines with a single line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO simplified partogram is similar to the modified WHO partogram but has no latent phase plotting.  Why A wrong: It specifically omits latent phase plotting.  Why C wrong: It records more than FHR.  Why D wrong: The notes describe absence of latent phase plotting, not a change to the alert/action lines."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"The WHO (simplified) partogram divides progress into 3 colour-coded areas. What does the GREEN area indicate?","options":["Alert / refer","Normal progress","Action needed","Fetal distress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The green area indicates normal progress.  Why A wrong: Alert/refer corresponds to the amber area.  Why C wrong: Action corresponds to the red area.  Why D wrong: Colour zones indicate progress of labour, not specifically fetal distress."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the WHO partogram colour-coded areas, the AMBER area indicates:","options":["Normal progress","Alert / refer","Action / intervention required","Full dilatation reached"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The amber area indicates alert/refer.  Why A wrong: Normal progress is the green area.  Why C wrong: Action is the red area.  Why D wrong: The colour zones track progress, not specifically full dilatation."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"On the WHO partogram colour-coded areas, the RED area indicates:","options":["Normal progress","Alert / refer","Action (intervention required)","Discharge the patient"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The red area indicates action is required.  Why A wrong: Green area = normal progress.  Why B wrong: Amber area = alert/refer.  Why D wrong: The red area signals intervention, not discharge."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the WHO partogram, a contraction described as \"Mild\" typically corresponds to a duration of:","options":["Less than 20 seconds","20–40 seconds","More than 40 seconds","More than 60 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On this page \"Mild\" is aligned with the < 20 seconds duration category (dotted symbol).  Why B wrong: 20–40 seconds is a moderate contraction (hatched symbol).  Why C wrong: > 40 seconds is a strong contraction (solid symbol).  Why D wrong: > 60 seconds is not one of the listed duration categories."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"Preterm labour is defined as labour that starts at what gestational age?","options":["Less than 34 weeks","Less than 37 weeks","Less than 40 weeks","Less than 42 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The definition states preterm labour is when labour starts at < 37 weeks.  Why A wrong: 34 weeks is when fetal lungs start maturing, not the definition of preterm labour.  Why C wrong: < 40 weeks would include most term labours.  Why D wrong: > 42 weeks would be post-term, not preterm."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"Because the lungs of a preterm fetus are not mature, the preterm baby is at risk of developing which condition?","options":["Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS)","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Transient tachypnoea due to caesarean","Bronchiectasis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state immature fetal lungs can lead to Respiratory Distress Syndrome.  Why B wrong: Meconium aspiration relates to meconium-stained fluid, not lung immaturity per se.  Why C wrong: Transient tachypnoea is not the complication highlighted here.  Why D wrong: Bronchiectasis is a chronic airway disease, not the neonatal complication described."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"According to the notes, in addition to immature lungs, what is the other major problem of prematurity in a preterm baby?","options":["The brain of the baby is not fully developed","The kidneys are enlarged","The heart has closed too early","The liver is oversized"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"brain of baby is not fully developed\" as a problem of prematurity.  Why B wrong: Enlarged kidneys are not the stated problem.  Why C wrong: Early heart closure is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: An oversized liver is not the stated problem."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"According to the notes, fetal lungs start maturing by which gestational age?","options":["28 weeks","32 weeks","34 weeks","37 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetal lungs start maturing by 34 weeks.  Why A wrong: 28 weeks is earlier than the stated start.  Why B wrong: 32 weeks is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 37 weeks is when maturation is complete, not when it starts."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"According to the notes, fetal lung maturation is complete by which gestational age?","options":["34 weeks","36 weeks","37 weeks","40 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maturation is complete by 37 weeks.  Why A wrong: 34 weeks is when maturation begins.  Why B wrong: 36 weeks is not the stated completion age.  Why D wrong: 40 weeks is term but not the stated lung-maturation completion point."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"To determine whether the lungs of the fetus are mature or not, which investigation is performed?","options":["Fetal Lung Maturity tests","Non-stress test","Biophysical profile only","Doppler ultrasound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetal lung maturity tests are done to know whether fetal lungs are mature.  Why B wrong: A non-stress test assesses fetal well-being, not lung maturity.  Why C wrong: The biophysical profile assesses overall fetal well-being, not surfactant-based lung maturity.  Why D wrong: Doppler assesses blood flow, not lung maturity."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"Fetal lung maturity testing is based on the presence of which substance produced by the lungs?","options":["Bilirubin","Surfactant (soapy material)","Amniotic albumin","Alpha-fetoprotein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal lung cells secrete a soapy material called surfactant, which is the basis of the lung maturity test.  Why A wrong: Bilirubin relates to haemolysis/jaundice, not lung maturity.  Why C wrong: \"Amniotic albumin\" is not the tested substance.  Why D wrong: Alpha-fetoprotein relates to neural tube defects, not lung maturity."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"Which cells of the fetal lung secrete surfactant?","options":["Type I pneumocytes","Type II pneumocytes","Goblet cells","Clara/club cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Type II pneumocytes secrete surfactant (soapy material).  Why A wrong: Type I pneumocytes are involved in gas exchange, not surfactant secretion.  Why C wrong: Goblet cells secrete mucus, not surfactant.  Why D wrong: Although Clara/club cells contribute to airway secretions, the notes specify Type II pneumocytes."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"What is the role of surfactant in the fetal lungs?","options":["Increases surface tension of the lungs","Decreases surface tension of the lungs","Produces mucus in the airways","Transports oxygen in blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the role of surfactant is to decrease the surface tension of the lungs (preventing alveolar collapse).  Why A wrong: Surfactant decreases, not increases, surface tension.  Why C wrong: Surfactant is not a mucus producer.  Why D wrong: Oxygen transport is a function of haemoglobin, not surfactant."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"Surfactant is a soapy material formed by which structure?","options":["Fetal kidneys","Fetal lungs","Maternal placenta","Fetal liver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state surfactant is formed by fetal lungs.  Why A wrong: The kidneys produce urine, not surfactant.  Why C wrong: The placenta does not form surfactant.  Why D wrong: The liver does not produce surfactant."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"After surfactant is formed by the fetal lungs, where is it ultimately secreted?","options":["Into the maternal bloodstream","Into the amniotic fluid","Into the fetal urine","Into the umbilical vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surfactant is ultimately secreted into the amniotic fluid, which is why lung maturity is assessed via amniotic fluid.  Why A wrong: It is not secreted into maternal blood.  Why C wrong: It is not secreted into fetal urine.  Why D wrong: It is not secreted into the umbilical vein."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"Which procedure is used to obtain amniotic fluid for fetal lung maturity testing?","options":["Chorionic villus sampling","Amniocentesis","Cordocentesis","Fetoscopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state amniotic fluid is taken out by doing amniocentesis to perform lung maturity tests.  Why A wrong: Chorionic villus sampling obtains placental tissue, not amniotic fluid.  Why C wrong: Cordocentesis samples umbilical cord blood.  Why D wrong: Fetoscopy is direct fetal visualization, not amniotic fluid aspiration."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"The most commonly done fetal lung maturity test measures the ratio of which two substances in amniotic fluid?","options":["Lecithin and sphingomyelin","Albumin and globulin","Bilirubin and biliverdin","Cholesterol and triglycerides"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most commonly done test measures the ratio of lecithin and sphingomyelin (L:S ratio) in amniotic fluid.  Why B wrong: Albumin/globulin ratio is not a lung maturity test.  Why C wrong: Bilirubin/biliverdin relate to haemolysis, not lung maturity.  Why D wrong: Cholesterol/triglycerides are not the L:S ratio components."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the lecithin:sphingomyelin (L:S) ratio test, fetal lungs are considered MATURE when the ratio is:","options":["Less than or equal to 2","Greater than or equal to 2","Equal to 1","Less than 0.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal lungs are mature when Lecithin:Sphingomyelin ≥ 2.  Why A wrong: L:S ≤ 2 indicates immature lungs.  Why C wrong: A ratio of exactly 1 is below the maturity threshold.  Why D wrong: < 0.5 is far below the maturity threshold."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the L:S ratio test, fetal lungs are considered NOT mature when the ratio is:","options":["Greater than or equal to 2","Less than or equal to 2","Greater than 3","Exactly 2.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal lungs are not mature when Lecithin:Sphingomyelin ≤ 2.  Why A wrong: L:S ≥ 2 indicates mature lungs.  Why C wrong: > 3 would indicate mature lungs.  Why D wrong: 2.5 (>2) would indicate maturity."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"The \"best test\" for fetal lung maturity described in the notes checks for the presence of which substance in amniotic fluid?","options":["Lecithin","Sphingomyelin","Phosphatidylglycerol","Meconium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The best test checks for the presence of phosphatidylglycerol in amniotic fluid.  Why A wrong: Lecithin is used in the L:S ratio (most commonly done), not described as the best test.  Why B wrong: Sphingomyelin is the denominator of the L:S ratio, not the best-test marker.  Why D wrong: Meconium is fetal bowel content, not a lung maturity marker."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the phosphatidylglycerol test, fetal lungs are MATURE when:","options":["Phosphatidylglycerol is absent in amniotic fluid","Phosphatidylglycerol is present in amniotic fluid","Lecithin is absent","Sphingomyelin is present"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Presence of phosphatidylglycerol in amniotic fluid indicates mature fetal lungs.  Why A wrong: Absence indicates immature lungs.  Why C wrong: The test is based on phosphatidylglycerol, not absence of lecithin.  Why D wrong: The test is based on phosphatidylglycerol, not sphingomyelin presence."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the phosphatidylglycerol test, fetal lungs are NOT mature when:","options":["Phosphatidylglycerol is present in amniotic fluid","Phosphatidylglycerol is absent in amniotic fluid","The L:S ratio is above 2","Surfactant bubbles are seen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Absence of phosphatidylglycerol in amniotic fluid indicates immature fetal lungs.  Why A wrong: Presence indicates mature lungs.  Why C wrong: An L:S ratio above 2 indicates maturity, not immaturity.  Why D wrong: Visible bubbles on shaking indicate maturity, not immaturity."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"Which is the best test to detect lung maturity of the fetus in diabetic mothers?","options":["Lecithin:sphingomyelin ratio","Phosphatidylglycerol in amniotic fluid","Shake/bubble test","Nile blue sulphate test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify that phosphatidylglycerol in amniotic fluid is the best test to detect lung maturity in diabetic females.  Why A wrong: The L:S ratio can be unreliable in diabetics, so it is not the best test here.  Why C wrong: The shake/bubble test is a bedside screening test, not the best test in diabetics.  Why D wrong: The Nile blue sulphate test is not designated as best for diabetic mothers."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"The bedside fetal lung maturity test that involves taking amniotic fluid in a test tube and shaking it is known as the:","options":["Shake test / bubble test","Coombs test","Kleihauer-Betke test","Apt test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bedside test is the shake test/bubble test, done by taking amniotic fluid in a test tube and shaking it.  Why B wrong: The Coombs test detects antibodies on RBCs.  Why C wrong: The Kleihauer-Betke test detects fetal cells in maternal blood.  Why D wrong: The Apt test differentiates fetal from maternal blood."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the shake (bubble) test, if the fetal lungs are MATURE, what is observed after shaking the test tube?","options":["No bubbles are seen","Bubbles can be seen (soapy material = surfactant)","The fluid turns blue","A clot forms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If fetal lungs are mature, on shaking the tube, bubbles (soapy material = surfactant) can be seen.  Why A wrong: Absence of bubbles indicates immature lungs.  Why C wrong: Colour change relates to the Nile blue test, not the shake test.  Why D wrong: Clot formation is not part of the shake test."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"In the shake (bubble) test, if the fetal lungs are NOT mature, what is observed after shaking the test tube?","options":["Bubbles will not be seen","Excessive persistent bubbles form","The fluid turns pink","The fluid separates into two layers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If fetal lungs are not mature, bubbles will not be seen on shaking (little/no surfactant).  Why B wrong: Persistent bubbles indicate mature lungs, not immature.  Why C wrong: Colour change is not a feature of the shake test.  Why D wrong: Layer separation is not the described result."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"The Nile blue sulphate test for fetal lung maturity is based on the principle that fetal skin cells contain:","options":["Phospholipids similar to surfactant","Excess melanin","Keratin only","Haemoglobin F"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Nile blue sulphate test is based on the principle that fetal skin cells have phospholipids similar to surfactant.  Why B wrong: Melanin content is not the basis of this test.  Why C wrong: Keratin alone is not the basis.  Why D wrong: Haemoglobin F relates to fetal blood, not this skin-cell principle."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"According to the Nile blue sulphate test principle, if fetal skin cells contain phospholipids, this indicates that:","options":["Surfactant is present in the lungs of the fetus, so the lungs are mature","The lungs are immature","The fetus has a skin infection","The amniotic fluid is meconium stained"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If skin cells have phospholipids, it means surfactant is present in the fetal lungs, so the lungs are mature.  Why B wrong: Presence of phospholipids indicates maturity, not immaturity.  Why C wrong: The test does not diagnose skin infection.  Why D wrong: The test is unrelated to meconium staining."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"A woman is being monitored on a partograph. Her cervical dilatation plot has crossed to the RIGHT of the alert line. Based on the WHO colour zones described, into which zone has she likely moved and what does it prompt?","options":["Green zone — continue routine care","Amber zone — alert / refer","Grey zone — discharge home","Blue zone — no action required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crossing the alert line reflects slower-than-expected progress, corresponding to the amber (alert/refer) zone in the WHO colour scheme.  Why A wrong: The green zone represents normal progress to the left of the alert line.  Why C wrong: There is no grey \"discharge home\" zone; crossing the alert line signals concern.  Why D wrong: There is no blue zone in the described 3-area scheme."},{"section":"Batch 065 · Partograph — fetal heart rate recording, status of amniotic fluid…","question":"A diabetic pregnant woman at 35 weeks needs assessment of fetal lung maturity before delivery. Which test would be MOST appropriate?","options":["Lecithin:sphingomyelin ratio","Phosphatidylglycerol in amniotic fluid","Non-stress test","Fundal height measurement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phosphatidylglycerol in amniotic fluid is the best test for lung maturity in diabetic females.  Why A wrong: The L:S ratio is less reliable in diabetics, so it is not the most appropriate here.  Why C wrong: The non-stress test evaluates fetal well-being, not lung maturity.  Why D wrong: Fundal height estimates fetal growth, not lung maturity."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"The fetal lung maturity test using Nile Blue sulphate begins by obtaining amniotic fluid through which procedure?","options":["Amniocentesis","Chorionic villus sampling","Cordocentesis","Percutaneous umbilical blood sampling"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure states amniotic fluid is taken out by amniocentesis, which contains fetal skin cells.  Why B wrong: Chorionic villus sampling obtains placental tissue, not amniotic fluid.  Why C wrong: Cordocentesis samples umbilical cord blood.  Why D wrong: Percutaneous umbilical blood sampling samples fetal blood, not amniotic fluid."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Amniotic fluid used in the Nile Blue sulphate test contains which fetal component that the test evaluates?","options":["Fetal red blood cells","Fetal skin cells","Fetal hair follicles","Fetal cartilage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state amniotic fluid has fetal skin cells, which are stained by the dye.  Why A wrong: The test is not based on fetal red blood cells.  Why C wrong: Hair follicles are not the component evaluated.  Why D wrong: Cartilage is not involved in this test."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which dye is added to amniotic fluid in the fetal lung maturity test described in the notes?","options":["Methylene blue","Nile Blue sulphate (Nile Blue SO4)","Congo red","Sudan black"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify adding Nile Blue SO4 (Nile Blue sulphate) dye to the amniotic fluid.  Why A wrong: Methylene blue is not used in this test.  Why C wrong: Congo red is used for amyloid staining, not this test.  Why D wrong: Sudan black stains lipids in blood films, not this test."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"After adding Nile Blue sulphate dye, what colour do the fetal skin cells appear when examined under the microscope?","options":["Blue","Green","Orange","Red"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state it is an \"orange color skin test\" and stem/skin cells appear orange in colour.  Why A/B/D wrong: The test specifically produces an orange colour, not blue, green, or red."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"In the Nile Blue sulphate test, what percentage of stem/skin cells appearing orange indicates fetal lung maturity?","options":["≥10%","≥25%","≥50%","≥90%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if stem cells appear orange in colour ≥50%, it means the lungs of the fetus are mature.  Why A/B/D wrong: The cut-off given in the notes is ≥50%, not 10%, 25%, or 90%."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"A positive Nile Blue sulphate test (≥50% orange cells) indicates which of the following about the fetus?","options":["Fetal skin is mature","Fetal lungs are mature","Fetal kidneys are mature","Fetal brain is mature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"It means lungs of fetus are Mature,\" and Q1 clarifies Nile Blue sulphate tells about lung maturity.  Why A wrong: Q1 clarifies it does NOT tell about skin maturity despite staining skin cells.  Why C/D wrong: The test does not assess kidney or brain maturity."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"According to the notes (Q1), the Nile Blue sulphate test does NOT indicate skin maturity; it actually tells about:","options":["Skin maturity","Lung maturity","Liver maturity","Cardiac maturity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Q1 states \"Nile Blue sulphate does not tell about skin maturity. It tells about – lung maturity.\"  Why A wrong: The notes specifically say it does NOT tell about skin maturity.  Why C/D wrong: It does not assess liver or cardiac maturity."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which class of drug is used to accelerate fetal lung maturity?","options":["Corticosteroid","Antibiotic","Beta-agonist","Calcium channel blocker"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the drug which can accelerate fetal lung maturity is a corticosteroid.  Why B wrong: Antibiotics have no role in lung maturity (noted as having no role in preterm labor).  Why C wrong: Beta-agonists are tocolytics, not for lung maturity.  Why D wrong: Calcium channel blockers (nifedipine) are tocolytics, not for lung maturity."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which two corticosteroids are used to accelerate fetal lung maturity as shown in the notes?","options":["Dexamethasone and Betamethasone","Hydrocortisone and Prednisolone","Methylprednisolone and Cortisone","Fludrocortisone and Triamcinolone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows Corticosteroid branching into Dexamethasone and Betamethasone.  Why B/C/D wrong: These corticosteroids are not the ones listed for fetal lung maturity in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the correct dexamethasone regimen for accelerating fetal lung maturity per the notes?","options":["2 injections, 12 mg each, 24 hours apart, i/m","4 injections, 6 mg each, 12 hours apart, i/m","4 injections, 12 mg each, 24 hours apart, i/m","2 injections, 6 mg each, 12 hours apart, i/m"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state dexamethasone = 4 injections, 6 mg each, 12 hours apart, i/m.  Why A wrong: That is the betamethasone regimen.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated dexamethasone dose/frequency."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the correct betamethasone regimen for accelerating fetal lung maturity per the notes?","options":["4 injections, 6 mg each, 12 hours apart, i/m","2 injections, 6 mg each, 24 hours apart, i/m","2 injections, 12 mg each, 24 hours apart, i/m","4 injections, 12 mg each, 12 hours apart, i/m"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state betamethasone = 2 injections, 12 mg each, 24 hours apart, i/m.  Why A wrong: That is the dexamethasone regimen.  Why B/D wrong: These do not match the stated betamethasone dose/frequency."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"How many total injections of dexamethasone are given in the fetal lung maturity regimen?","options":["2","3","4","6"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dexamethasone is given as 4 injections.  Why A wrong: 2 injections is the betamethasone count.  Why B/D wrong: Not the stated number for dexamethasone."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"After giving the last corticosteroid injection, how long does the corticosteroid need to act (achieve its effect)?","options":["At least 12 hours","At least 24 hours","At least 48 hours","At least 72 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Note states corticosteroid needs at least 48 hours to act after giving the last injection.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated latency is 48 hours, not 12, 24, or 72 hours."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the contraindication for administering corticosteroids in the setting of preterm labor?","options":["Chorioamnionitis","Diabetes mellitus","Twin pregnancy","Polyhydramnios"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state contraindication of corticosteroid = chorioamnionitis (a uterine/amniotic infection where steroids could worsen infection).  Why B wrong: Diabetes is not listed as the corticosteroid contraindication here (it is a contraindication for β-agonist tocolytics).  Why C/D wrong: Twin pregnancy and polyhydramnios are causes of preterm labor, not corticosteroid contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which corticosteroid is the \"corticosteroid of choice in India\" for accelerating fetal lung maturity?","options":["Betamethasone","Dexamethasone","Hydrocortisone","Prednisolone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state corticosteroid of choice in India = dexamethasone.  Why A wrong: Betamethasone is an option but the notes specify dexamethasone as the choice in India.  Why C/D wrong: These are not used for fetal lung maturity in this context."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity should be given in all patients who deliver or are about to deliver at what gestational age?","options":["<28 weeks","<34 weeks","<37 weeks","<40 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in all patients who deliver or are about to deliver at <37 weeks of pregnancy, give corticosteroid.  Why A/B wrong: The stated cut-off for giving corticosteroid is <37 weeks (though tocolytics/MgSO4 use <34 weeks).  Why D wrong: 40 weeks is term; corticosteroids are for preterm (<37 weeks)."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the most common cause of preterm labor?","options":["Infection","Idiopathic","Polyhydramnios","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of preterm labor is idiopathic (unknown).  Why A/C/D wrong: Infection, polyhydramnios, and twin pregnancy are listed causes but not the MOST common cause."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which infections are listed as causes of preterm labor?","options":["Bacterial vaginosis or UTI","Hepatitis or HIV","Malaria or dengue","Tuberculosis or typhoid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Infection like bacterial vaginosis or UTI\" as a cause of preterm labor.  Why B/C/D wrong: These infections are not the ones cited in the notes as preterm labor causes."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Over-distension of the uterus is a cause of preterm labor. Which of the following causes over-distension? (Select all that apply)","options":["Polyhydramnios","Twin pregnancy","Macrosomia","Oligohydramnios","Intrauterine growth restriction"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list polyhydramnios, twin pregnancy, and macrosomia (written \"LMACROSOMIA\") under over-distension of uterus.  Why D wrong: Oligohydramnios is reduced fluid, causing under-distension, not over-distension.  Why E wrong: IUGR produces a small fetus and would not over-distend the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the most important risk factor for preterm labor?","options":["Maternal age","Previous history of preterm labour","Anemia","Primigravida status"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most important risk factor is previous history of preterm labour.  Why A/C wrong: These are not identified as the most important risk factor in the notes.  Why D wrong: Primigravida is associated with OP position, not the key preterm labor risk factor here."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"To prevent preterm labor in a patient with a previous history of preterm labor, which hormone is given?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","Oxytocin","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state give progesterone to the pregnant female (it acts as a smooth muscle relaxant).  Why A wrong: Estrogen is not used for this preventive purpose.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin stimulates uterine contractions and would worsen preterm labor.  Why D wrong: Prolactin has no role here."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Progesterone prevents preterm labor because it acts as a:","options":["Smooth muscle relaxant","Vasoconstrictor","Uterine stimulant","Diuretic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone acts as a smooth muscle relaxant, thereby relaxing the uterus.  Why B/C/D wrong: Progesterone is not described as a vasoconstrictor, uterine stimulant, or diuretic."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"The surgical procedure used to prevent preterm labor by reinforcing the cervix is called:","options":["Cervical cerclage (circlage) surgery","Cesarean section","Dilatation and curettage","Hysterectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe cerclage (circlage) surgery placed at the internal os of the cervix to prevent preterm labor.  Why B wrong: Cesarean section is a delivery method, not a preventive cervical procedure.  Why C wrong: D&C evacuates uterine contents; it does not prevent preterm labor.  Why D wrong: Hysterectomy removes the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Cervical cerclage is placed at which anatomical part of the cervix as shown in the notes?","options":["External os","Internal os","Cervical canal midpoint","Vaginal fornix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the cerclage at the internal os (between uterus and cervix).  Why A wrong: The cerclage is depicted at the internal os, not the external os.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the labeled sites in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which are the two types of cervical cerclage surgery mentioned in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["McDonald cerclage","Shirodkar cerclage","Wurm cerclage","Lash cerclage","Benson cerclage"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list McDonald cerclage and Shirodkar cerclage as the two types.  Why C/D/E wrong: These types are not mentioned in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"For a diagnosis of preterm labor, uterine contractions must be present before which gestational age?","options":["<34 weeks","<37 weeks","<40 weeks","<28 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagnosis box states uterine contractions should be present <37 weeks plus any one additional criterion.  Why A wrong: <34 weeks is the threshold for tocolytics/MgSO4, not the diagnostic contraction criterion.  Why C wrong: 40 weeks is term.  Why D wrong: <28 weeks is not the stated diagnostic threshold."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the normal length of the cervix in pregnancy per the notes?","options":["2 cm","2.5 cm","4 cm","6 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state normal length of cervix in pregnancy = 4 cms.  Why A/B wrong: 2 cm / 2.5 cm are abnormal (shortened) lengths indicating preterm labor.  Why D wrong: 6 cm is not the stated normal length."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"As the cervix dilates during preterm labor, the length of the cervix:","options":["Increases","Shortens","Remains unchanged","Doubles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state as the cervix dilates, the length of the cervix shortens.  Why A/C/D wrong: The length decreases (shortens), it does not increase, stay the same, or double."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"On USG, what cervical length supports a diagnosis of preterm labor?","options":["Length <2.5 cms","Length >4 cms","Length 4–5 cms","Length >6 cms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state on USG, length of cervix = <2.5 cms supports preterm labor (Q1: cut-off length to predict preterm labor = 2.5 cms).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are normal or longer lengths, not indicative of preterm labor."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What degree of cervical dilation on USG (as an alternative to shortened length) supports a diagnosis of preterm labor?","options":["Dilation ≥ 2 cms","Dilation ≥ 5 cms","Dilation ≥ 8 cms","Dilation ≥ 10 cms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"OR Dilation of cervix ≥ 2 cms\" as an alternate USG criterion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These larger dilations are seen in active/advanced labor, not the stated preterm labor threshold."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"The cut-off length of the cervix to predict preterm labor is:","options":["4 cms","3.5 cms","2.5 cms","1 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Q1 states the cut-off length of cervix to predict preterm labor = 2.5 cms.  Why A wrong: 4 cm is the normal cervical length.  Why B/D wrong: These are not the stated cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the shape of the cervix seen on USG in preterm labor?","options":["\"V\" shaped","\"U\" shaped","\"T\" shaped","\"Y\" shaped"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes (Q1) state the shape of cervix on USG in preterm labor = \"U\" shaped cervix.  Why A/C/D wrong: These shapes are not the answer given in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Fetal fibronectin, a diagnostic marker for preterm labor, is a protein found in:","options":["Maternal serum only","Amniotic fluid or vaginal secretions at <37 weeks","Fetal urine","Cord blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetal fibronectin is a protein in amniotic fluid or vaginal secretions at <37 weeks.  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specify amniotic fluid or vaginal secretions, not serum, fetal urine, or cord blood."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"A positive fetal fibronectin test at <37 weeks indicates which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Preterm labor","PROM (premature rupture of membranes)","Term delivery","Post-term pregnancy","Normal pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetal fibronectin \"It indicates: 1. Preterm Labor 2. PROM.\"  Why C/D/E wrong: A positive fetal fibronectin does not indicate term delivery, post-term pregnancy, or normal pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"For diagnosis of preterm labor, uterine contractions before 37 weeks must be accompanied by:","options":["Any one of USG cervical findings or positive fetal fibronectin","Both USG findings AND positive fetal fibronectin together","Elevated maternal temperature","Rupture of membranes only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state uterine contractions <37 weeks PLUS \"Any 1 of the following\" (USG finding or fetal fibronectin).  Why B wrong: Only one of the additional criteria is required, not both.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the listed additional diagnostic criteria."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"In the management of preterm labor, if the lungs of the fetus are not mature, the drug of choice (DOC) is:","options":["Corticosteroid","Nifedipine","MgSO4","Oxytocin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The management flow chart states for immature fetal lungs, DOC = corticosteroid.  Why B wrong: Nifedipine is a tocolytic, not for lung maturity.  Why C wrong: MgSO4 is for neuroprotection, not lung maturity.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin stimulates contractions and is contraindicated in preterm labor management."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Since corticosteroids need 48 hours to act, which class of drugs is given to decrease uterine contractions for those 48 hours?","options":["Tocolytics","Uterotonics","Antibiotics","Antipyretics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state we must give drugs that decrease uterine contractions for 48 hours = tocolytics (to buy time for steroids to act).  Why B wrong: Uterotonics increase contractions, the opposite of what is needed.  Why C/D wrong: Antibiotics and antipyretics do not decrease uterine contractions."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"In preterm labor management, when the brain of the baby is not mature, which drug is given for neuroprotection?","options":["Corticosteroid","MgSO4 (magnesium sulphate)","Nifedipine","Indomethacin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for neuroprotection (immature fetal brain) we give MgSO4.  Why A wrong: Corticosteroid is for lung maturity, not neuroprotection.  Why C/D wrong: These are tocolytics, not neuroprotective agents."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"MgSO4 for neuroprotection is given in all cases of preterm labor occurring before which gestational age?","options":["<28 weeks","<32 weeks","<34 weeks","<37 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state MgSO4 for neuroprotection is given in all preterm labor <34 weeks.  Why A/B wrong: The stated threshold is <34 weeks.  Why D wrong: <37 weeks is the corticosteroid threshold, not the MgSO4/neuroprotection threshold."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Tocolytics are best defined as drugs which:","options":["Increase uterine contractions","Decrease or stop uterine contractions","Mature the fetal lungs","Induce labor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define tocolytics as drugs which decrease or stop uterine contractions.  Why A wrong: That describes uterotonics/oxytocin, the opposite.  Why C wrong: Lung maturation is the role of corticosteroids.  Why D wrong: Tocolytics delay, not induce, labor."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Tocolytics are given for what duration in preterm labor?","options":["Short duration","Continuously until term","Minimum 2 weeks","Until delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state tocolytics are given for a short duration (to buy time for corticosteroids to act).  Why B/C/D wrong: They are not given continuously to term, for 2 weeks, or until delivery."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Tocolytics are indicated when preterm labor occurs before which gestational age?","options":["≤28 weeks","≤34 weeks","≤37 weeks","≤40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state tocolytics are given when preterm labor occurs before ≤34 weeks.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated cut-off for tocolytics is ≤34 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which is the best tocolytic drug, belonging to the calcium channel blocker class?","options":["Nifedipine","Atosiban","Indomethacin","Terbutaline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best tocolytic is nifedipine (a calcium channel blocker).  Why B wrong: Atosiban is best in heart disease patients, not the overall best.  Why C wrong: Indomethacin has maximum fetal side effects.  Why D wrong: Terbutaline is a β-agonist tocolytic, not the best."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the best tocolytic to use in a diabetic mother?","options":["β-agonist","Nifedipine","Indomethacin","MgSO4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best tocolytic in a diabetic mother is nifedipine.  Why A wrong: β-agonists cause hyperglycemia and are contraindicated in diabetics.  Why C/D wrong: These are not identified as best for diabetic mothers."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the best tocolytic to use in a patient with heart disease?","options":["Nifedipine","Terbutaline","Atosiban","Ritodrine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best tocolytic in a heart disease patient is atosiban (an oxytocin antagonist).  Why A wrong: Nifedipine is best overall/in diabetics, not specified for heart disease here.  Why B/D wrong: These β-agonists have cardiac side effects and are not preferred in heart disease."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Atosiban, used as a tocolytic, exerts its effect by acting as a(n):","options":["Calcium channel blocker","Antagonist of oxytocin","Beta-agonist","Prostaglandin synthase inhibitor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state atosiban is an antagonist of oxytocin.  Why A wrong: Nifedipine is the calcium channel blocker.  Why C wrong: Ritodrine/terbutaline are β-agonists.  Why D wrong: Indomethacin is the prostaglandin synthase inhibitor."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which tocolytic is associated with the maximum fetal side effects?","options":["Nifedipine","Atosiban","Indomethacin","Terbutaline"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the tocolytic with maximum fetal side effect is indomethacin.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not identified as having the maximum fetal side effects."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Indomethacin as a tocolytic can lead to which fetal complications? (Select all that apply)","options":["Oligohydramnios","Premature closure of ductus arteriosus","Hyperglycemia","Hypokalemia","Pulmonary edema"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state indomethacin leads to oligohydramnios and premature closure of the ductus arteriosus.  Why C/D/E wrong: Hyperglycemia, hypokalemia, and pulmonary edema are β-agonist side effects, not indomethacin's."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which class of tocolytic is associated with the maximum maternal side effects?","options":["β-agonist","Calcium channel blocker","Oxytocin antagonist","Prostaglandin inhibitor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the tocolytic with maternal side effects is the β-agonist.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the tocolytics with maximum maternal side effects."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which of the following are β-agonists used as tocolytics? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ritodrine","Isoxsuprine (Isosuprine)","Terbutaline","Nifedipine","Atosiban"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list ritodrine, isoxsuprine, and terbutaline as β-agonists used as tocolytics.  Why D wrong: Nifedipine is a calcium channel blocker.  Why E wrong: Atosiban is an oxytocin antagonist."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the most common side effect of β-agonist tocolytics?","options":["Tremors","Constipation","Bradycardia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common side effect of β-agonist is tremors.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed as the most common β-agonist side effect (β-agonists cause tachycardia, not bradycardia)."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which of the following are other side effects of β-agonist tocolytics? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hyperglycemia","Hypokalemia (↓K+)","Pulmonary edema","Oligohydramnios","Premature closure of ductus arteriosus"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list hyperglycemia, hypokalemia (↓K+), and pulmonary edema as other β-agonist side effects.  Why D/E wrong: Oligohydramnios and premature ductus closure are indomethacin fetal effects, not β-agonist effects."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Because β-agonist tocolytics cause hyperglycemia, they are contraindicated in which patients?","options":["Diabetic patients","Hypertensive patients","Asthmatic patients","Anemic patients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the tocolytic contraindicated in diabetic patients is the β-agonist (due to hyperglycemia side effect).  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated contraindication is diabetes, not hypertension, asthma, or anemia."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Regarding MgSO4 as a tocolytic, which statement from the notes is correct?","options":["It is the first-line tocolytic","It can act as a tocolytic, but the level at which its tocolytic effect is seen is also where toxicity occurs, so it is not commonly used as a tocolytic","It has no tocolytic effect at any dose","It is the best tocolytic in heart disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Note states MgSO4 can act as a tocolytic, but the level at which its tocolytic effect is seen is also where toxicity occurs, so it is not commonly used as a tocolytic.  Why A wrong: Nifedipine is the best/first-line tocolytic, not MgSO4.  Why C wrong: MgSO4 does have a tocolytic effect, just at toxic levels.  Why D wrong: Atosiban is best in heart disease."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which of the following is a complete list of tocolytic drugs per the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nifedipine","Atosiban","Indomethacin","β-agonists (isoxsuprine, ritodrine, terbutaline)","MgSO4"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The tocolytic drugs list includes: 1. Nifedipine, 2. Atosiban, 3. Indomethacin, 4. β-agonist (isosuprine, ritodrine, terbutaline), 5. MgSO4.  All options listed are the exact tocolytic drugs enumerated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"In the review table, corticosteroid (dexamethasone) plays which role and is used in which situation?","options":["Matures lungs of fetus; used in preterm labor (up to 37 weeks)","Decreases uterine contractions; used <34 weeks","Neuroprotection; used <34 weeks","Induces labor; used at term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The review table lists corticosteroid (DEXA) — role: mature lungs of fetus; to be used: preterm labor 37 weeks.  Why B wrong: That describes the tocolytic row.  Why C wrong: That describes the MgSO4 row.  Why D wrong: Corticosteroid does not induce labor."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"In the review table, what is the role of tocolytics?","options":["Mature the lungs of the fetus","Decrease or make uterine contractions absent","Neuroprotection of fetal brain","Increase amniotic fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The review table states tocolytic role = it decreases or makes uterine contraction absent; used in preterm labor <34 weeks.  Why A wrong: That is the corticosteroid role.  Why C wrong: That is the MgSO4 role.  Why D wrong: Not a stated tocolytic role."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"In the review table, low-dose MgSO4 is used for what role and at what gestational age?","options":["Neuroprotection; preterm labor <34 weeks","Lung maturity; 37 weeks","Decrease uterine contractions; <34 weeks","Induction of labor; term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The review table lists MgSO4 (low dose) — role: for neuroprotection; to be used: preterm labor <34 weeks.  Why B wrong: That is the corticosteroid role.  Why C wrong: That is the tocolytic role.  Why D wrong: MgSO4 does not induce labor."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which of the following drugs/interventions have NO role in the treatment of preterm labor? (Select all that apply)","options":["Antibiotics","Progesterone","Induction of labor","Nifedipine","Corticosteroid"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list drugs with no role in preterm labor: 1. Antibiotics, 2. Progesterone, 3. No role of induction of labor.  Why D wrong: Nifedipine is the best tocolytic, which has a definite role.  Why E wrong: Corticosteroid matures fetal lungs, an important role."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Which statement about progesterone in preterm labor is correct per the notes?","options":["It can prevent preterm labor but cannot treat preterm labor and is not a tocolytic drug","It is a first-line tocolytic","It both prevents and treats preterm labor","It matures the fetal lungs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone can prevent preterm labor, cannot treat preterm labor, and is NOT a tocolytic drug.  Why B wrong: Progesterone is explicitly not a tocolytic.  Why C wrong: It prevents but cannot treat preterm labor.  Why D wrong: Corticosteroid matures the lungs, not progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Case 1: A pregnant female at 35 weeks of gestation has uterine contractions and a cervical length of 2 cms on USG. What is the next step in management?","options":["Give corticosteroid (dexamethasone)","Give tocolytic","Give MgSO4","Induce labor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state at 35 weeks (>34 weeks), give corticosteroid (dexamethasone); there is no role of tocolytic or MgSO4 at more than 34 weeks.  Why B/C wrong: The notes explicitly state no role of tocolytic and no role of MgSO4 at >34 weeks.  Why D wrong: There is no role of induction of labor in preterm labor management."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Why are tocolytics and MgSO4 NOT given in Case 1 (35 weeks gestation)?","options":["Because the patient is at more than 34 weeks","Because the cervix is too short","Because there is no fetal fibronectin","Because the fetus is already mature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes bracket \"No Role of Tocolytic / No Role of MgSO4\" with the annotation \"at more than 34 weeks.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated reason is the gestational age being >34 weeks, not cervical length, fibronectin, or fetal maturity."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"When preterm labor occurs between 24–37 weeks, which drug should be given (per the \"Remember\" note)?","options":["Only corticosteroid","Only tocolytic","Corticosteroid + tocolytic + MgSO4","Only MgSO4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Remember note states when preterm labor occurs between 24–37 weeks, give only corticosteroid.  Why B/D wrong: Tocolytic-only or MgSO4-only is not correct for this range.  Why C wrong: The triple combination is for <34 weeks, not the 24–37 week statement given here (which applies once >34 weeks per Case 1)."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Case 2: A pregnant female at 32 weeks of gestation has uterine contractions and cervical length of 2 cms on USG. Which are the appropriate management steps? (Select all that apply)","options":["Corticosteroid","Tocolytic","MgSO4","Induction of labor","Antibiotics"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: At 32 weeks (<34 weeks), the notes give corticosteroid, tocolytic, and MgSO4; the Remember note confirms preterm labor <34 weeks → corticosteroid + tocolytic + MgSO4.  Why D wrong: The notes state no role of induction of labor.  Why E wrong: Antibiotics have no role in preterm labor."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Per the \"Remember\" note, when preterm labor occurs at <34 weeks, the correct management is:","options":["Corticosteroid + Tocolytic + MgSO4","Only corticosteroid","Only tocolytic","Induction of labor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state when preterm labor occurs at <34 weeks, give corticosteroid + tocolytic & MgSO4.  Why B wrong: Corticosteroid alone is for 24–37 weeks (once >34 weeks).  Why C/D wrong: Tocolytic alone or induction is not the recommended <34 weeks management."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Regarding occipito-posterior (OP), which classification is correct?","options":["It is a malposition, not a malpresentation, because the presentation is cephalic and presenting part is vertex","It is a malpresentation because the presenting part is breech","It is a malpresentation because presentation is transverse","It is a normal position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state OP is a malposition and NOT a malpresentation, as the presentation is cephalic and the presenting part is vertex.  Why B wrong: The presenting part is vertex (cephalic), not breech.  Why C wrong: The presentation is cephalic, not transverse.  Why D wrong: OP is abnormal (a malposition), not a normal position."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"At the onset of labor, what percentage of fetuses are in the occipito-posterior (OP) position?","options":["2%","10%","50%","80%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state at the onset of labor, 10% of fetuses are in OP position.  Why A wrong: 2% is the figure during the late stage of labor.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated onset figures."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"During the late stage of labor, only what percentage of fetuses remain in the OP position?","options":["2%","10%","20%","50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state during the late stage of labor, only 2% remain OP, because OP positions rotate in 80–90% of cases and become occipitoanterior.  Why B wrong: 10% is the onset-of-labor figure.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated late-stage figures."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"In what percentage of cases do OP positions rotate to become occipitoanterior during labor?","options":["20–30%","50–60%","80–90%","100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state OP positions rotate in 80–90% of cases and become occipitoanterior.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated rotation rate is 80–90%."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the most common cause of occipito-posterior (OP) position?","options":["Android pelvis","Gynecoid pelvis","Deflexed head","Twin pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of OP position is android pelvis.  Why B wrong: Gynecoid pelvis is the ideal/normal female pelvis, favoring occipitoanterior.  Why C wrong: Deflexed head is the SECOND most common cause, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Twin pregnancy is not listed as an OP cause."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the second most common cause of occipito-posterior (OP) position?","options":["Android pelvis","Deflexed head of baby","Polyhydramnios","Placenta previa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the second most common cause of OP position is a deflexed head of the baby.  Why A wrong: Android pelvis is the MOST common cause.  Why C/D wrong: These are not listed as OP causes."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"Occipito-posterior (OP) position is more common in which type of female?","options":["Multigravida","Primigravida","Grand multipara","Elderly gravida"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state OP is more common in primigravida females.  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specify primigravida, not multigravida, grand multipara, or elderly gravida."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the most common occipito-posterior (OP) position?","options":["Right occipito-posterior position","Left occipito-posterior position","Direct occipito-posterior position","Right occipito-transverse position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common OP position is the right occipito-posterior (ROP) position.  Why B/C/D wrong: The notes specify right occipito-posterior as the most common OP position."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What is the most common occipito-anterior (OA) position?","options":["Right occipito-anterior position","Left occipito-anterior position","Direct occipito-anterior position","Left occipito-transverse position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common occipito-anterior position is the left occipito-anterior (LOA) position.  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specify left occipito-anterior as the most common OA position."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"In the OP position, the fetal head is typically:","options":["Fully flexed","Deflexed","Hyperextended","Rotated 90 degrees"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The engaging diameter diagram labels the head as \"deflexed :- OP position,\" and deflexed head is the second most common cause of OP.  Why A wrong: A fully flexed head gives a vertex (OA) presentation, not OP.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated head attitude in OP."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"When the fetal head is fully flexed, the presentation is:","options":["Vertex (occipito-anterior)","Brow","Face","Occipito-posterior"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram notes \"Fully flexed = vx (vertex), OA\" — full flexion gives a vertex/occipito-anterior presentation.  Why B/C wrong: Brow and face presentations occur with deflexion/extension, not full flexion.  Why D wrong: OP is associated with a deflexed head, not full flexion."},{"section":"Batch 066 · Fetal lung maturity test, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity…","question":"What happens to engagement of the fetal head in the occipito-posterior (OP) position?","options":["Engagement occurs early","Engagement is delayed","Engagement is unaffected","Engagement never occurs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The engaging diameter diagram states \"Engagement is delayed\" in the deflexed (OP) head position (larger presenting diameter).  Why A/C wrong: A deflexed head presents a larger diameter, delaying engagement, not making it early or unaffected.  Why D wrong: Engagement is delayed, not entirely prevented."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The suboccipitobregmatic diameter of the fetal head measures:","options":["9.5 cm","10 cm","11.5 cm","13.5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state suboccipitobregmatic (diameter A) = 9.5 cm, the engaging diameter in a well-flexed vertex.  Why B wrong: 10 cm is the suboccipitofrontal diameter.  Why C wrong: 11.5 cm is the occipitofrontal diameter.  Why D wrong: 13.5 cm is the mento-vertical diameter (not mentioned here)."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The occipitofrontal diameter of the fetal head measures:","options":["9.5 cm","10 cm","11.5 cm","12 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list occipitofrontal = 11.5 cm.  Why A wrong: 9.5 cm is the suboccipitobregmatic diameter.  Why B wrong: 10 cm is the suboccipitofrontal diameter.  Why D wrong: 12 cm is not the value given for occipitofrontal."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The suboccipitofrontal diameter of the fetal head measures:","options":["9.5 cm","10 cm","11.5 cm","13 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state suboccipitofrontal = 10 cm.  Why A wrong: 9.5 cm is the suboccipitobregmatic diameter.  Why C wrong: 11.5 cm is the occipitofrontal diameter.  Why D wrong: 13 cm is not given."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"When the presenting diameter is the occipitofrontal (11.5 cm) or suboccipitofrontal (10 cm) rather than the suboccipitobregmatic, what is the expected consequence on labor?","options":["Engagement occurs early","Engagement is delayed","Rapid descent","Precipitate labor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that with occipitofrontal/suboccipitofrontal presenting diameters, \"Engagement is delayed\" because larger diameters engage.  Why A wrong: Larger diameters delay, not hasten, engagement.  Why C wrong: Descent is impeded, not rapid.  Why D wrong: Precipitate labor is unrelated to a deflexed/larger presenting diameter."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In the mechanism of labor with occipitoposterior position, complete forward rotation occurs when the head moves through what fraction of a circle?","options":["1/8th of circle","2/8th of circle","3/8th of circle","Full circle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In complete forward rotation the head moves through 3/8th of a circle, and in ~90% of cases this is how abnormal (OP) delivery occurs.  Why A wrong: 1/8th of a circle is incomplete forward rotation.  Why B wrong: 2/8th is not the described value.  Why D wrong: A full circle is not involved in forward rotation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In occipitoposterior position, in approximately what percentage of cases does the head undergo complete forward rotation (3/8th of circle)?","options":["50%","65%","90%","100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in 90% of cases abnormal delivery occurs as the head moves through 3/8th of the circle (complete forward rotation).  Why A wrong: 50% understates the figure.  Why B wrong: 65% is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: Not all cases (100%) rotate completely forward."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"What is the best management of occipitoposterior position when it is diagnosed during labor?","options":["Immediate cesarean section","Wait and watch","Forceps delivery immediately","Manual rotation immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because ~90% of cases undergo complete forward rotation, the best management of OP position diagnosed during labor is \"wait and watch.\"  Why A wrong: Cesarean is not the first-line management given the high spontaneous rotation rate.  Why C wrong: Immediate forceps is not indicated when spontaneous rotation is likely.  Why D wrong: Manual rotation is not the best initial approach; watchful waiting is."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In incomplete forward rotation, the baby's head rotates by only what fraction of a circle?","options":["1/8th of circle","2/8th of circle","3/8th of circle","4/8th of circle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In incomplete forward rotation the baby rotates only 1/8th of a circle.  Why B, C, D wrong: These larger fractions are not the value given for incomplete forward rotation (3/8th describes complete rotation)."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In incomplete forward rotation, from which position does the head become ROT (right occipitotransverse) where further rotation does not occur?","options":["LOA","ROP","LOP","OA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that from ROP it becomes ROT and further rotation does not occur — this is incomplete forward rotation.  Why A wrong: LOA is a favorable anterior position, not the described starting point.  Why C wrong: LOP is not the position specified in this pathway.  Why D wrong: OA is the desired end position, not the origin of incomplete rotation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which of the following conditions favor incomplete forward rotation (deep transverse arrest)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Android pelvis (prominent ischial spines)","Big baby","Less liquor","Gynaecoid pelvis","Polyhydramnios"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Incomplete forward rotation occurs in android pelvis (most common; ischial spines prominent), big baby, and less liquor.  Why D wrong: Gynaecoid pelvis is the ideal pelvis and favors normal rotation.  Why E wrong: Polyhydramnios (excess liquor) is not listed; \"less liquor\" is the risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The most common pelvis associated with incomplete forward rotation / deep transverse arrest is:","options":["Gynaecoid pelvis","Android pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common pelvis in incomplete forward rotation/deep transverse arrest is the android pelvis, in which the ischial spines are prominent.  Why A wrong: Gynaecoid is the normal favorable pelvis.  Why C wrong: Anthropoid is associated with deep sacral (canal) arrest/persistent OP.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid is associated with face presentation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"When the baby's head stops in the transverse position despite good uterine contractions (due to incomplete forward rotation), the condition is called:","options":["Deep sacral arrest","Deep transverse arrest","Persistent OP position","Face presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the head arrests in the transverse position in spite of good uterine contraction, it is called deep transverse arrest.  Why A wrong: Deep sacral arrest results from posterior rotation (persistent OP).  Why C wrong: Persistent OP is posterior rotation, not transverse arrest.  Why D wrong: Face presentation is unrelated to this arrest of rotation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"At what level of the pelvis does deep transverse arrest occur?","options":["Pelvic inlet","Ischial spine level","Pelvic outlet","Sacral promontory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows deep transverse arrest occurs at the level of the ischial spine.  Why A wrong: It is not at the inlet.  Why C wrong: It is at the spines (midpelvis), not the outlet.  Why D wrong: The sacral promontory is related to posterior rotation, not deep transverse arrest."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In deep transverse arrest occurring in an android pelvis, the recommended management is:","options":["Vacuum delivery","Manual rotation followed by forceps","Cesarean section","Kielland forceps"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart specifies that in android pelvis, deep transverse arrest is managed by cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Vacuum is an option in \"other pelvic\" types, not android pelvis.  Why B wrong: Manual rotation followed by forceps applies to other pelvic types.  Why D wrong: Kielland forceps is outdated and belongs to the \"other pelvic\" options, not android pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"For deep transverse arrest in pelvic types other than android, which of the following are management options? (Select all that apply)","options":["Vacuum delivery","Manual rotation followed by forceps","Kielland forceps (outdated)","Cesarean section (best)","Symphysiotomy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: For \"other pelvic\" types, options listed are vacuum delivery, manual rotation followed by forceps, Kielland forceps (outdated), and cesarean section (best).  Why E wrong: Symphysiotomy is not listed among the management options."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Among the management options for deep transverse arrest in \"other pelvic\" types, which is considered the best?","options":["Vacuum delivery","Manual rotation followed by forceps","Kielland forceps","Cesarean section"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly mark cesarean section as \"Best\" among the other-pelvic management options.  Why A wrong: Vacuum is an option but not marked best.  Why B wrong: Manual rotation + forceps is an option but not the best.  Why C wrong: Kielland forceps is explicitly noted as outdated."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which instrumental delivery method can rotate the head of the fetus?","options":["Only forceps","Vacuum","Neither vacuum nor forceps","Ventouse cannot but forceps can (any type)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Vacuum can rotate the head of fetus, forceps cannot\" — vacuum permits autorotation of the head.  Why A wrong: Ordinary forceps cannot rotate the head.  Why C wrong: Vacuum can rotate; the statement is not that neither can.  Why D wrong: This reverses the correct relationship."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which is the only type of forceps that can rotate the fetal head (though now outdated)?","options":["Wrigley's forceps","Simpson forceps","Kielland forceps","Neville-Barnes forceps"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the only forceps that can rotate the head is the Kielland forceps (now outdated).  Why A, B, D wrong: These forceps types do not rotate the head; only Kielland forceps was designed for rotation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In posterior rotation of an occipitoposterior position, the occiput rotates posteriorly and comes to lie directly anterior to which structure?","options":["Symphysis pubis","Sacral promontory","Ischial tuberosity","Coccyx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In posterior rotation, instead of rotating anteriorly the occiput rotates posteriorly and lies directly anterior to the sacral promontory.  Why A wrong: Anterior rotation (not posterior) brings the occiput toward the symphysis pubis.  Why C wrong: The ischial tuberosity is not the structure described.  Why D wrong: The coccyx is not the reference point given."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Posterior rotation of the occiput results in which position?","options":["Occipito-anterior position","Direct OP position / Persistent OP position","Occipitotransverse position","Mentoanterior position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Posterior rotation leads to direct OP position, also called persistent OP position (DOP).  Why A wrong: Occipito-anterior is the result of forward rotation, not posterior rotation.  Why C wrong: Occipitotransverse arrest is deep transverse arrest, a different entity.  Why D wrong: Mentoanterior refers to face presentation, not OP posterior rotation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Posterior rotation (persistent OP) is most common in which type of pelvis?","options":["Gynaecoid pelvis","Android pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state posterior rotation is most common in the anthropoid pelvis, where the occiput rotates posteriorly.  Why A wrong: Gynaecoid is the normal pelvis favoring anterior rotation.  Why B wrong: Android pelvis is associated with deep transverse arrest.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid is associated with face presentation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"When persistent OP (DOP) stays in position for a very long time despite good uterine contractions, the resulting arrest is called:","options":["Deep transverse arrest","Deep sacral arrest","Shoulder dystocia","Cephalopelvic disproportion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When DOP remains for a long time despite good uterine contraction, it becomes deep sacral arrest.  Why A wrong: Deep transverse arrest is arrest in the transverse position (android pelvis).  Why C wrong: Shoulder dystocia is impaction of the shoulders, a different problem.  Why D wrong: CPD is a size mismatch, not the specific arrest described."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The management of deep sacral arrest / persistent OP that fails to deliver is:","options":["Vacuum extraction","Kielland forceps","Cesarean section","Manual rotation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state management of this condition is cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Vacuum is not the stated management here.  Why B wrong: Kielland forceps is outdated and not the management given.  Why D wrong: Manual rotation is not the stated management for deep sacral arrest."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Deep transverse arrest is most commonly associated with which pelvis? (PYQ-style)","options":["Android pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Gynaecoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Q1 in the notes states deep transverse arrest is most common in the android pelvis.  Why B wrong: Anthropoid is linked to deep canal (sacral) arrest.  Why C wrong: Gynaecoid is the normal pelvis.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid relates to face presentation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Deep canal (sacral) arrest is most commonly associated with which pelvis? (PYQ-style)","options":["Android pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Gynaecoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Q2 in the notes states deep canal arrest is most common in the anthropoid pelvis.  Why A wrong: Android pelvis is associated with deep transverse arrest.  Why C wrong: Gynaecoid is the normal pelvis.  Why D wrong: Platypelloid relates to face presentation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In a transverse lie, what is the lie of the fetus described as?","options":["Longitudinal","Oblique","Transverse","Unstable"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In transverse lie the fetal lie is, by definition, transverse.  Why A wrong: Longitudinal lie is cephalic or breech, not transverse.  Why B wrong: Oblique lie is a separate malpresentation.  Why D wrong: Unstable lie changes repeatedly; it is not the defined transverse lie."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In a transverse lie, the presenting part is:","options":["Vertex","Shoulder","Face","Breech"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In transverse lie the presentation is shoulder.  Why A wrong: Vertex presents in a cephalic longitudinal lie.  Why C wrong: Face is a cephalic malpresentation.  Why D wrong: Breech presents in a longitudinal lie."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In shoulder presentation (transverse lie), the denominator is:","options":["Occiput","Sacrum","Mentum","Acromion process"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The denominator in transverse lie/shoulder presentation is the acromion process.  Why A wrong: Occiput is the denominator in vertex presentation.  Why B wrong: Sacrum is the denominator in breech.  Why C wrong: Mentum is the denominator in face presentation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The most common position in a transverse lie is:","options":["Dorsoanterior position","Dorsoposterior position","Dorsosuperior position","Dorsoinferior position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common position in transverse lie is the dorsoanterior position.  Why B, C, D wrong: These other dorso- positions are not the most common one stated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The most common cause of transverse lie overall is:","options":["Placenta previa","Prematurity / preterm delivery","Fibroids","Contracted pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of transverse lie is prematurity/preterm delivery.  Why A wrong: Placenta previa is the most common cause at term, not overall.  Why C wrong: Fibroids can cause it but are not the most common cause.  Why D wrong: Contracted pelvis is not the stated most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The most common cause of transverse lie at term is:","options":["Prematurity","Placenta previa","Polyhydramnios","Multiple pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of transverse lie at term is placenta previa.  Why A wrong: Prematurity is the most common cause overall, not specifically at term.  Why C, D wrong: Polyhydramnios and multiple pregnancy can contribute but are not the stated most common cause at term."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"What usually happens to most fetuses with a transverse lie during pregnancy?","options":["They remain transverse until labor","They spontaneously rotate and become cephalic/breech","They always require cesarean section","They develop into a compound presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state most fetuses with transverse lie spontaneously rotate during pregnancy and become cephalic/breech.  Why A wrong: Most do not remain transverse; they spontaneously convert.  Why C wrong: Spontaneous rotation often avoids the need for cesarean if converted before labor.  Why D wrong: Compound presentation is not the described outcome."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"On examination of a transverse lie, the height of the uterus is:","options":["More than the period of gestation","Equal to the period of gestation","Less than the period of gestation","Unrelated to the period of gestation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In transverse lie the fetus lies across, so fundal height is less than the period of gestation.  Why A wrong: Height greater than dates suggests polyhydramnios/multiple pregnancy, not classic transverse lie finding.  Why B wrong: The notes specify it is less, not equal.  Why D wrong: Fundal height is clinically related to gestation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"On Leopold maneuver in a transverse lie, which grips are found empty?","options":["Fundal grip (1st) and deep pelvic grip (4th)","Lateral grips (2nd) only","Pelvic grip (3rd) only","All four grips"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In transverse lie, the fundal grip (1st grip) and the deep pelvic grip (4th grip) are empty, since neither head nor breech occupies the fundus or the pelvis.  Why B wrong: The lateral grips actually detect the fetal poles at the sides.  Why C wrong: The pelvic grip alone is not what is described.  Why D wrong: Not all four grips are empty; the poles are palpable at the sides."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which malpresentation carries the highest chance of cord prolapse?","options":["Vertex presentation","Transverse lie","Face presentation","Frank breech"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the highest chances of cord prolapse are with transverse lie.  Why A wrong: A well-applied vertex fits the pelvis and rarely allows cord prolapse.  Why C wrong: Face presentation is not the highest-risk stated.  Why D wrong: Frank breech (extended legs) has relatively lower cord prolapse risk than transverse lie."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In the management of transverse lie, if the patient comes at term (≥36 weeks), the first-line management attempted is:","options":["Immediate cesarean section","External cephalic version","Internal podalic version","Forceps delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient comes at term (≥36 weeks), external cephalic version is attempted first.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is reserved for when the patient comes in labor or ECV fails/contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Internal podalic version is not the first-line stated approach.  Why D wrong: Forceps has no role in a transverse lie."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In transverse lie, when should cesarean section be performed even if ECV was the initial plan?","options":["If external cephalic version fails twice or is contraindicated","Only if the baby is dead","Only in primigravida","Never"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states cesarean is done if ECV fails twice OR if ECV is contraindicated.  Why B wrong: Cesarean is done whether the baby is alive or dead if the patient is in labor.  Why C wrong: This applies regardless of parity.  Why D wrong: Cesarean is definitely indicated in these situations."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"If a patient with transverse lie presents in labor, the management is:","options":["Trial of vaginal delivery","External cephalic version","Cesarean section whether the baby is alive or dead","Await spontaneous rotation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient comes in labor, cesarean section is done whether the baby is alive or dead, because there is no mechanism of labor in transverse lie.  Why A wrong: Transverse lie babies cannot be delivered vaginally.  Why B wrong: ECV is done at term before labor, not once in labor.  Why D wrong: Once in labor, awaiting rotation is unsafe; delivery is by cesarean."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which statement about labor mechanism in transverse lie is TRUE?","options":["There is a normal mechanism of labor","There is no mechanism of labor, and babies cannot be delivered vaginally","Vaginal delivery is possible with forceps","The head engages normally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state there is no mechanism of labor in transverse lie and transverse lie babies cannot be delivered vaginally.  Why A wrong: There is no mechanism of labor in transverse lie.  Why C wrong: Vaginal/forceps delivery is not possible.  Why D wrong: The head does not engage in a transverse lie."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"If a transverse lie is recognized during pregnancy (before labor), which procedure can be tried?","options":["Internal podalic version","External cephalic version","Immediate cesarean","Symphysiotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If transverse lie is recognized in pregnancy, external cephalic version can be tried.  Why A wrong: Internal podalic version is not the stated antenatal option.  Why C wrong: Immediate cesarean is not indicated antenatally if ECV can be tried.  Why D wrong: Symphysiotomy is unrelated to converting a transverse lie."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"External cephalic version (ECV) is performed by which route?","options":["Per vaginally","Per abdominally","Per rectally","Via laparoscopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ECV \"is done per abdominally,\" manipulating the fetus through the abdominal wall.  Why A wrong: It is not a vaginal procedure.  Why C, D wrong: It is neither rectal nor laparoscopic."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"What are the indications for external cephalic version (ECV)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Transverse lie","Breech","Occipitoposterior position","Vertex presentation","Face presentation"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The indications for ECV listed are transverse lie and breech, aiming to convert to cephalic.  Why C wrong: OP position is managed differently, not by ECV.  Why D wrong: Vertex/cephalic is already the desired presentation.  Why E wrong: Face presentation is not an indication for ECV."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The goal of external cephalic version is to:","options":["Make the fetus breech","Make the fetus cephalic","Rotate the shoulders","Deliver the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the aim is to \"try to make it cephalic.\"  Why A wrong: The goal is to convert away from breech to cephalic.  Why C wrong: It repositions the whole fetus, not just shoulders.  Why D wrong: It has nothing to do with placental delivery."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which investigation must be done before performing an external cephalic version?","options":["Amniocentesis","Ultrasonography (USG)","X-ray pelvimetry","Cordocentesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Do an USG → before doing ECV\" to confirm presentation, liquor, placenta, etc.  Why A wrong: Amniocentesis is not a prerequisite for ECV.  Why C wrong: X-ray pelvimetry is not the stated pre-ECV investigation.  Why D wrong: Cordocentesis is unrelated to ECV."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"External cephalic version is typically performed as which type of procedure?","options":["Emergency inpatient surgery","OPD (outpatient) procedure","Intrapartum operative procedure","ICU procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label ECV an \"OPD Procedure.\"  Why A, C, D wrong: It is not an emergency surgery, intrapartum operation, or ICU procedure; it is an outpatient department procedure."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Regarding anesthesia for external cephalic version:","options":["General anesthesia is mandatory","No anesthesia is needed, but tocolytics are used to relax the uterus","Spinal anesthesia is required","Local infiltration of the abdomen is required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state no anesthesia is needed, but tocolytics are used so that the uterus is relaxed.  Why A wrong: GA is not required for ECV.  Why C wrong: Spinal anesthesia is not required.  Why D wrong: Local infiltration is not used; tocolytics are used instead."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which tocolytic is the drug of choice (DOC) for uterine relaxation before ECV, according to the notes?","options":["Nifedipine","Terbutaline","Magnesium sulfate","Indomethacin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Terbutaline as the DOC tocolytic for ECV.  Why A, C, D wrong: Although these are tocolytics, the notes specifically name Terbutaline as the drug of choice."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"According to the notes, terbutaline for ECV is given at what dose and route?","options":["25 mg S/C","250 mcg IV","10 mg oral","5 mg IM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify terbutaline \"25 mg S/C\" (subcutaneous). [Note: the usual clinical dose is 250 micrograms subcutaneously; the value is reproduced as written in the source.]  Why B, C, D wrong: These doses/routes are not what the source states."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"According to the notes, how long before the ECV procedure should terbutaline be administered?","options":["Immediately at the time of procedure","30 minutes before the procedure","2 hours before the procedure","24 hours before the procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state terbutaline is given 30 minutes before the procedure.  Why A, C, D wrong: These timings do not match the stated 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"During external cephalic version, monitoring should be:","options":["Intermittent auscultation only","Continuous fetal monitoring","No monitoring required","Maternal ECG only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state ECV should be done under continuous fetal monitoring.  Why A wrong: Intermittent auscultation is inadequate during this risky procedure.  Why C wrong: Monitoring is required because fetal distress can occur.  Why D wrong: Maternal ECG is not the focus; fetal monitoring is."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"For Rh-negative females undergoing external cephalic version, which prophylaxis should be given?","options":["Anti-D injection","Corticosteroids","Rh-positive transfusion","Broad-spectrum antibiotics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if Rh-negative females, give Anti-D injection (to prevent isoimmunization from any feto-maternal hemorrhage during manipulation).  Why B wrong: Corticosteroids are for fetal lung maturity, not this indication.  Why C wrong: Transfusion is not indicated for ECV.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics are not the stated prophylaxis."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which complication can occur while performing an external cephalic version?","options":["Uterine rupture always","Fetal distress / fetal death","Placental abruption is impossible","Cord prolapse is prevented"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that while doing the procedure, fetal distress/death can happen — hence continuous monitoring is required.  Why A wrong: Uterine rupture is not stated as an \"always\" outcome.  Why C wrong: Manipulation can, in fact, provoke complications; it is not risk-free.  Why D wrong: ECV does not prevent cord prolapse; it can provoke fetal compromise."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"What is the minimum period of gestation (prerequisite) for performing an ECV, usually done at 37 weeks?","options":["≥28 weeks","≥32 weeks","≥36 weeks","≥40 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The prerequisite is a period of gestation ≥36 weeks, usually done at 37 weeks (as chances of reversion are less at that stage).  Why A, B wrong: Earlier gestations carry a higher chance of the fetus reverting.  Why D wrong: ≥40 weeks is not the stated minimum; ECV is done around 37 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Why is ECV usually performed at 37 weeks rather than earlier?","options":["The fetus is too small earlier","Chances of reversion (back to malpresentation) are less at 37 weeks","The placenta is not formed earlier","Anesthesia is safer at 37 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ECV is usually done at 37 weeks because chances of reversion are less at that gestation.  Why A wrong: Fetal size is not the reason given.  Why C wrong: The placenta is fully formed well before 37 weeks.  Why D wrong: Anesthesia is not used for ECV; this is not the reason."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which of the following are prerequisites for performing an external cephalic version? (Select all that apply)","options":["Liquor should be adequate","Membranes should be intact","Singleton pregnancy","Fetal heart rate normal on CTG","Ruptured membranes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Prerequisites include adequate liquor, intact membranes, singleton pregnancy (not done in twins/multifetal), and a normal FHR on CTG.  Why E wrong: Membranes must be intact, so ruptured membranes is a contraindication, not a prerequisite."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"ECV is contraindicated in twin/multifetal pregnancy because a prerequisite is:","options":["Multiple pregnancy","Singleton pregnancy","Post-term pregnancy","Preterm pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A prerequisite is a singleton pregnancy; ECV is not done in twins/multifetal pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Multiple pregnancy is exactly what precludes ECV.  Why C, D wrong: Term/gestation criteria are separate; the prerequisite here is singleton status."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Before ECV, the fetal heart rate should be assessed and found to be:","options":["Absent","Normal on CTG","Bradycardic","Irrelevant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A prerequisite is that the fetal heart rate should be normal on CTG.  Why A wrong: An absent FHR (fetal death) contraindicates the procedure's intent.  Why C wrong: Bradycardia (distress) is not acceptable before ECV.  Why D wrong: FHR assessment is essential, not irrelevant."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"ECV should NOT be attempted if which of the following contraindications for vaginal delivery is present?","options":["Placenta previa or contracted pelvis","Adequate liquor","Normal CTG","Intact membranes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state there should be no contraindication for vaginal delivery — no placenta previa/contracted pelvis — before ECV.  Why B, C, D wrong: Adequate liquor, normal CTG, and intact membranes are prerequisites (favorable), not contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"ECV should not be done if the uterus or fetus are:","options":["Grossly anomalous","Normal","Singleton","Term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state ECV should not be done if the uterus/fetus are grossly anomalous.  Why B, C, D wrong: Normal, singleton, and term status are favorable/required conditions, not contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which of the following are relative contraindications of external cephalic version? (Select all that apply)","options":["PIH (pregnancy-induced hypertension)","Heart disease in mother","IUGR","Previous LSCS","Adequate liquor"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four relative contraindications listed are PIH, heart disease in mother, IUGR, and previous LSCS.  Why E wrong: Adequate liquor is a prerequisite (favorable condition), not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In the ECV flow chart, external cephalic version in transverse lie/breech is first attempted:","options":["Multiple times consecutively","Once (1st time)","Only after labor begins","Only under general anesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows ECV is attempted once (1st time) initially.  Why A wrong: It is attempted once at first, then a second attempt on another day if unsuccessful.  Why C wrong: ECV is done antenatally, not after labor begins.  Why D wrong: No anesthesia (GA) is used."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"If the first ECV attempt is successful, what is the next step?","options":["Immediate cesarean section","Send the patient home and tell her to come when she gets labor pain, then vaginal delivery","Repeat ECV the next day","Start oxytocin immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states if successful, send the patient home and tell her to come when she gets labor pain, then proceed to vaginal delivery.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is not needed after a successful version.  Why C wrong: Repeat ECV is for unsuccessful attempts.  Why D wrong: Immediate oxytocin is used only after a successful 2nd attempt, not after the 1st successful attempt."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"If the first ECV attempt is unsuccessful, the flow chart recommends:","options":["Immediate cesarean section","Send patient home, call her another day, and try ECV a 2nd time","Start oxytocin","Do internal podalic version"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If unsuccessful, send the patient home and call her another day to try external cephalic version a 2nd time.  Why A wrong: Cesarean follows only after the 2nd attempt also fails.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is not the next step after a failed 1st attempt.  Why D wrong: Internal podalic version is not in this flow chart."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"If the 2nd ECV attempt is successful, the flow chart advises:","options":["Wait for spontaneous labor","Immediately do vaginal delivery by starting oxytocin","Elective cesarean at 39 weeks","Repeat ECV a 3rd time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the 2nd attempt succeeds, immediately do vaginal delivery by starting oxytocin.  Why A wrong: The flow chart advises immediate induction, not awaiting spontaneous labor.  Why C wrong: Elective cesarean at 39 weeks is for the unsuccessful 2nd attempt.  Why D wrong: There is no 3rd attempt."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"If the 2nd ECV attempt is also unsuccessful, the recommended management is:","options":["Try a 3rd ECV","Elective cesarean section at 39 weeks","Await spontaneous version","Emergency cesarean immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the 2nd attempt is unsuccessful, elective cesarean section at 39 weeks is planned.  Why A wrong: There is no 3rd attempt in the flow chart.  Why C wrong: Spontaneous version is not relied upon after two failures.  Why D wrong: The plan is an elective (not emergency) cesarean at 39 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"A neglected shoulder presentation with hand prolapse shows all features of which condition?","options":["Precipitate labor","Obstructed labor","Preterm labor","Normal labor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In hand prolapse/neglected shoulder presentation, all features of obstructed labor are seen (including Bandl's ring).  Why A wrong: Precipitate labor is rapid delivery, the opposite scenario.  Why C wrong: Preterm labor is unrelated to this obstruction picture.  Why D wrong: This is a pathological, obstructed labor, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Which clinical sign of obstructed labor is specifically noted in neglected shoulder presentation?","options":["Bandl's ring","Chadwick's sign","Goodell's sign","Hegar's sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Bandl's ring is seen — a pathological retraction ring indicating imminent uterine rupture in obstructed labor.  Why B wrong: Chadwick's sign is bluish discoloration of the vagina in early pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Goodell's sign is softening of the cervix.  Why D wrong: Hegar's sign is softening of the lower uterine segment in early pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The management of a neglected shoulder presentation (obstructed labor) is:","options":["External cephalic version","Cesarean section, whether the baby is dead or alive","Forceps delivery","Vacuum extraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management is cesarean section whether the baby is dead or alive.  Why A wrong: ECV is for antenatal transverse lie, not neglected obstructed shoulder presentation.  Why C, D wrong: Instrumental vaginal delivery has no role in a neglected shoulder presentation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In the modern management of neglected shoulder presentation, what role do destructive procedures (decapitation/evisceration) play?","options":["They are first-line","There is no role for destructive procedures","They are used only for a live baby","They replace cesarean"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state there is no role of destructive procedures such as decapitation/evisceration; cesarean section is done instead.  Why A, C, D wrong: Destructive procedures are not first-line, not used for live babies, and do not replace cesarean in modern practice."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In a face presentation, the presenting part is:","options":["Vertex","Face","Brow","Shoulder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In face presentation the presenting part is the face.  Why A wrong: Vertex presents in a well-flexed cephalic presentation.  Why C wrong: Brow is a separate deflexed presentation.  Why D wrong: Shoulder presents in a transverse lie."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"What abnormal attitude of the fetal head characterizes face presentation?","options":["Complete flexion","Complete extension of the fetal head","Slight deflexion only","Lateral flexion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In normal attitude there is flexion of the fetal head, but in face presentation there is complete extension.  Why A wrong: Complete flexion is the normal (vertex) attitude.  Why C wrong: Slight deflexion describes brow/OP, not the complete extension of face presentation.  Why D wrong: Lateral flexion is not the described attitude."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The denominator in face presentation is:","options":["Occiput","Sacrum","Mentum (chin)","Acromion process"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In face presentation the denominator is the mentum (chin).  Why A wrong: Occiput is the denominator in vertex presentation.  Why B wrong: Sacrum is the denominator in breech.  Why D wrong: Acromion process is the denominator in shoulder presentation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The commonest position in face presentation is:","options":["Right mentoposterior (RMP)","Left mentoanterior (LMA)","Right mentoanterior (RMA)","Left mentoposterior (LMP)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The commonest position in face presentation is left mentoanterior (LMA).  Why A, C, D wrong: These positions are not the commonest one stated; LMA is the most common."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Face presentation is most common in which parity group?","options":["Primigravida","Multiparous females","Grand multipara only","Nulliparous only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state face presentation is most common in multiparous females.  Why A wrong: Primigravida is not the stated most common group.  Why C wrong: Grand multipara is a subset; the notes specify multiparous females broadly.  Why D wrong: Nulliparous is not the stated group."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"Face presentation is most commonly seen in which type of pelvis?","options":["Gynaecoid pelvis","Android pelvis","Anthropoid pelvis","Platypelloid pelvis"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The most common pelvis in which face presentation is seen is the platypelloid pelvis.  Why A wrong: Gynaecoid is the normal pelvis.  Why B wrong: Android is associated with deep transverse arrest.  Why C wrong: Anthropoid is associated with persistent OP/deep sacral arrest."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The most common cause of face presentation is:","options":["Anencephaly","Placenta previa","Contracted pelvis","Multiple pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of face presentation is anencephaly (absence of the cranial vault causes the head to extend).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated most common cause of face presentation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The most common presenting part in anencephaly is:","options":["Vertex","Face","Breech","Shoulder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common presenting part in anencephaly is the face (leading to face presentation).  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated most common presenting part in anencephaly."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In face presentation, when the mentum (chin) faces anteriorly (mentoanterior position), the outcome is:","options":["Cesarean section is mandatory","Vaginal delivery is possible","Destructive procedure needed","Immediate forceps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows when the mentum faces anteriorly (mentoanterior position), vaginal delivery is possible.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is reserved for mentoposterior position.  Why C wrong: Destructive procedures are not used.  Why D wrong: Immediate forceps is not the general rule for mentoanterior."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In face presentation, when the mentum (chin) faces posteriorly (mentoposterior position), the management is:","options":["Vaginal delivery","Cesarean section","Vacuum extraction","External cephalic version"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows mentum posteriorly (mentoposterior position) requires cesarean section, as vaginal delivery is not possible.  Why A wrong: Vaginal delivery is possible only in mentoanterior, not mentoposterior.  Why C wrong: Vacuum is contraindicated in face presentation.  Why D wrong: ECV is not used in labor for face presentation."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"In face presentation, which is more common?","options":["Mentoposterior","Mentoanterior","They are equally common","Mentotransverse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state mento-anterior is more common than mentoposterior.  Why A wrong: Mentoposterior is less common.  Why C wrong: They are not equally common.  Why D wrong: Mentotransverse is not the stated comparison."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"The most common position specifically in face presentation is:","options":["Right mentoanterior","Left mento anterior","Left mentoposterior","Right mentoposterior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes reiterate the most common position is left mento anterior.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated most common position."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"A woman with a transverse lie is diagnosed at 37 weeks with adequate liquor, intact membranes, singleton pregnancy, and a reassuring CTG. What is the most appropriate first step in management?","options":["Elective cesarean section","External cephalic version","Await spontaneous labor","Internal podalic version"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With all prerequisites met (≥36 weeks, adequate liquor, intact membranes, singleton, normal CTG), external cephalic version is the appropriate first step to convert to cephalic.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is reserved for when ECV fails/is contraindicated or the patient is in labor.  Why C wrong: A transverse lie at 37 weeks warrants active intervention (ECV), not passive waiting.  Why D wrong: Internal podalic version is not the antenatal approach described."},{"section":"Batch 067 · Fetal head diameters…","question":"A multiparous woman in labor is found to have a face presentation with the chin (mentum) directed posteriorly. What is the priority management?","options":["Allow vaginal delivery","Cesarean section","Apply vacuum","Manual rotation with forceps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mentoposterior face presentation cannot deliver vaginally, so cesarean section is the priority.  Why A wrong: Vaginal delivery is possible only in the mentoanterior position.  Why C wrong: Vacuum is contraindicated in face presentation.  Why D wrong: Forceps rotation is not appropriate for mentoposterior face presentation."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"In brow presentation, the degree of deflexion of the fetal head is best described as:","options":["Complete flexion","Partial extension","Complete extension","Military attitude"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Brow presentation occurs due to partial extension (deflexion) of the fetal head, midway between full flexion and full extension.  Why A wrong: Complete flexion produces vertex presentation, not brow.  Why C wrong: Complete extension produces face presentation, not brow.  Why D wrong: Military attitude (head neither flexed nor extended) gives occipito-frontal/sinciput presentation, not brow."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"What is the engaging diameter (the longest one) in a brow presentation?","options":["Suboccipitobregmatic diameter","Occipitofrontal diameter","Mentovertical diameter","Submentobregmatic diameter"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In brow presentation the engaging diameter is the longest diameter, the mentovertical diameter, measuring 14 cm.  Why A wrong: Suboccipitobregmatic (9.5 cm) is the engaging diameter of a well-flexed vertex presentation.  Why B wrong: Occipitofrontal (11.5 cm) is the diameter in deflexed/occipitoposterior vertex.  Why D wrong: Submentobregmatic (9.5 cm) is the engaging diameter of face presentation (mento-anterior)."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The mentovertical diameter engaging in brow presentation measures:","options":["9.5 cm","11.5 cm","12 cm","14 cm"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The mentovertical diameter, the engaging diameter of brow presentation, measures 14 cm — the longest antero-posterior diameter of the fetal skull.  Why A wrong: 9.5 cm is the suboccipitobregmatic/submentobregmatic diameter.  Why B wrong: 11.5 cm is the occipitofrontal diameter.  Why C wrong: 12 cm is not the mentovertical value quoted."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"What is the denominator in a brow presentation?","options":["Occiput","Mentum (chin)","Frontal bones","Sacrum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The denominator in brow presentation is the frontal bones (the brow/sinciput).  Why A wrong: Occiput is the denominator of vertex presentation.  Why B wrong: Mentum (chin) is the denominator of face presentation.  Why D wrong: Sacrum is the denominator of breech presentation."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Regarding the mechanism of labour in a persistent brow presentation, which statement is correct?","options":["It follows the same mechanism as vertex","There is no mechanism of labour","Rapid spontaneous vaginal delivery occurs","It converts to a compound presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In persistent brow presentation there is no mechanism of labour because the large mentovertical diameter (14 cm) cannot negotiate the pelvis.  Why A wrong: The vertex mechanism does not apply as the head is deflexed with the largest diameter presenting.  Why C wrong: Spontaneous vaginal delivery is not possible with a persistent brow.  Why D wrong: The notes state no mechanism of labour, not conversion to compound presentation."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"What is the definitive mode of delivery for a persistent brow presentation?","options":["Vacuum extraction","Forceps delivery","Only cesarean section","Spontaneous vaginal delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A persistent brow presentation can only be delivered by cesarean section because there is no mechanism of labour.  Why A wrong: Vacuum is used only in vertex presentation, not brow.  Why B wrong: Forceps cannot be applied where cesarean is mandatory (brow is one such condition).  Why D wrong: The large mentovertical diameter prevents spontaneous vaginal delivery."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Which of the following presentations/lies mandates a cesarean section? (Select all that apply)","options":["Transverse lie","Brow presentation","Occipito-anterior vertex","Face presentation with mento-posterior","Face presentation with mento-anterior"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list three conditions requiring cesarean section — transverse lie, brow, and mento-posterior face presentation.  Why C wrong: Occipito-anterior vertex delivers vaginally and is not an indication for cesarean.  Why E wrong: Mento-anterior face can deliver vaginally (and forceps may be applied); only mento-posterior face needs cesarean."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"A face presentation requires cesarean section when the position is:","options":["Mento-anterior","Mento-posterior","Mento-lateral (transverse)","Any face position always"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Persistent mento-posterior face presentation must be delivered by cesarean section as the head cannot be born vaginally.  Why A wrong: Mento-anterior face can be delivered vaginally (forceps may even be applied).  Why C wrong: Mento-lateral usually rotates; the listed cesarean indication is specifically mento-posterior.  Why D wrong: Not every face position needs cesarean — mento-anterior can deliver vaginally."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The general principle governing the use of obstetric forceps is that forceps:","options":["Can be applied in any presentation regardless of feasibility of vaginal delivery","Can be applied in all conditions where vaginal delivery is possible, but not where cesarean section is mandatory","Are used only when cesarean section is contraindicated","Replace the need for consent and episiotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states forceps can be applied in all conditions where vaginal delivery is possible but cannot be applied where cesarean section has to be done.  Why A wrong: Forceps cannot be used where cesarean is mandatory (e.g., brow, transverse lie).  Why C wrong: Forceps are for assisting vaginal delivery, not a substitute for contraindicated cesarean.  Why D wrong: Consent and episiotomy remain required for instrumental delivery."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"In which of the following can forceps be used? (Select all that apply)","options":["Occipito-anterior position / vertex presentation","Face – mento-anterior","Delivery of the after-coming head in breech","Occipito-posterior after manual rotation","Transverse lie"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list four conditions where forceps can be used — occipito-anterior/vertex, face mento-anterior, delivery of head in breech, and occipito-posterior after manual rotation.  Why E wrong: Transverse lie mandates cesarean section; forceps cannot be applied."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Forceps may be applied in an occipito-posterior position only after:","options":["The bladder is filled","Manual rotation to a favourable position","The cervix is 6 cm dilated","Maximum vacuum pressure is reached"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists \"Occipito posterior after manual rotation\" as a condition for forceps use.  Why A wrong: The bladder should be empty before instrumental delivery, not filled.  Why C wrong: For forceps the os must be fully (10 cm) dilated; 6 cm is a vacuum criterion.  Why D wrong: Vacuum pressure is unrelated to forceps application in OP position."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Forceps can be used to assist delivery of the after-coming head in which presentation?","options":["Face presentation","Brow presentation","Breech presentation","Transverse lie"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Delivery of head in breech\" as a valid forceps indication (forceps to the after-coming head).  Why A wrong: Vacuum is not used in face; forceps use in face is limited to mento-anterior head, not \"after-coming head\".  Why B wrong: Brow presentation requires cesarean section, not forceps.  Why D wrong: Transverse lie requires cesarean section."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The vacuum extractor (ventouse) can be applied only in which presentation?","options":["Face presentation","Brow presentation","Vertex presentation","Breech presentation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state vacuum can be applied only in vertex presentation (occipito-anterior and occipito-posterior).  Why A wrong: Vacuum is not used in face presentation.  Why B wrong: Brow presentation needs cesarean section.  Why D wrong: Vacuum is not used for delivery of the head in breech."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Why can the vacuum extractor be applied in an occipito-posterior vertex position?","options":["Because it compresses the fetal head strongly","Because vacuum can rotate the head of the baby","Because it does not require consent","Because it works at negative station"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explain vacuum can be used in OP position \"because vacuum can rotate head of baby.\"  Why A wrong: Vacuum is chosen for less trauma/compression, not strong compression.  Why C wrong: Consent is always required for instrumental delivery.  Why D wrong: Instrumental delivery is done at +2 station or below, not at negative station."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"In which of the following conditions is the vacuum extractor NOT used? (Select all that apply)","options":["Face presentation (mento-anterior/mento-posterior)","Delivery of the after-coming head in breech","Occipito-anterior vertex","Occipito-posterior vertex"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state vacuum is not used in face presentation and in delivery of the head in breech.  Why C wrong: Occipito-anterior vertex is a valid indication for vacuum.  Why D wrong: Occipito-posterior vertex is a valid indication because vacuum can rotate the head."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The two main types of instrumental (assisted) vaginal delivery devices are:","options":["Forceps and cesarean scalpel","Forceps and ventouse (vacuum)","Vacuum and dilator","Speculum and forceps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instrumental delivery is classified into forceps and ventouse/vacuum.  Why A wrong: A cesarean scalpel is not an instrumental vaginal delivery device.  Why C wrong: A dilator is not one of the two instrumental delivery devices.  Why D wrong: A speculum is a diagnostic instrument, not an assisted-delivery device."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three main parts of an obstetric forceps as labelled in the diagram?","options":["Blade","Shank","Handle","Cup"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The forceps diagram labels the blade, shank, and handle; the \"cup\" belongs to the vacuum (ventouse), not the forceps.  Why A wrong: Blade is a labelled forceps part.  Why B wrong: Shank is a labelled forceps part.  Why C wrong: Handle is a labelled forceps part."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The main components of a vacuum extractor (ventouse) shown in the diagram include the:","options":["Cup, tube, and suction mechanism","Blade, shank, and lock","Toe, heel, and cephalic curve","English lock and sliding lock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ventouse/vacuum diagram labels the cup, tube, and suction.  Why B wrong: Blade, shank, and lock are forceps parts.  Why C wrong: Toe, heel, and cephalic curve are parts of the forceps blade.  Why D wrong: English and sliding locks are types of forceps locks."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Which nursing action must always be performed before an instrumental delivery?","options":["Fill the bladder","Take informed consent","Delay episiotomy","Keep the cervix undilated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Always take consent\" before instrumental delivery.  Why A wrong: The bladder should be emptied, not filled.  Why C wrong: Episiotomy should be given, not delayed.  Why D wrong: For forceps the cervix should be fully dilated."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Which procedure is routinely performed to facilitate an instrumental delivery and reduce perineal trauma?","options":["Cervical cerclage","Episiotomy","Amniocentesis","Symphysiotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note directs to \"Give episiotomy\" during instrumental delivery.  Why A wrong: Cervical cerclage is a procedure for cervical incompetence in pregnancy, not for delivery.  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis is a prenatal diagnostic procedure.  Why D wrong: Symphysiotomy divides the pubic symphysis and is not the routine step described."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Instrumental (forceps/vacuum) delivery is done only when the fetal head is at which station?","options":["Above the ischial spines (negative station)","At 0 station","At +2 station or below it","At −2 station"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states instrumental delivery is done only if the head of the fetus is at +2 station or below it (+3, +4, +5).  Why A wrong: At negative station instrumental delivery is NOT done.  Why B wrong: 0 station is above the required +2 threshold.  Why D wrong: −2 is a negative (high) station where instrumental delivery is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"At which of the following stations is instrumental delivery NOT performed?","options":["+2","+3","+4","−ve (above the spines)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows instrumental delivery is \"not done\" at negative stations (head above the ischial spines).  Why A wrong: +2 is the minimum station at which instrumental delivery may be done.  Why B wrong: +3 is below +2, so instrumental delivery is permitted.  Why C wrong: +4 is below +2, so instrumental delivery is permitted."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"In the fetal station system, a positive station (+) indicates the presenting part is:","options":["Above the level of the ischial spines","At the level of the ischial spines","Below the level of the ischial spines","At the pelvic brim"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Positive stations (+1 to +5) mean the presenting part is below the ischial spines; instrumental delivery is done at +2 or below (i.e., +3, +4, +5).  Why A wrong: Above the spines corresponds to negative stations.  Why B wrong: The level of the ischial spines is station 0.  Why D wrong: The pelvic brim corresponds to a high negative station, not a positive one."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The rounded tip at the end of the forceps blade is called the:","options":["Heel","Toe","Shank","Lock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The labelled forceps diagram identifies the tip of the blade as the \"toe\".  Why A wrong: The heel is the part of the blade nearest the shank, not the tip.  Why C wrong: The shank connects the blade to the handle.  Why D wrong: The lock is the articulation between the two blades."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The part of the forceps blade that lies nearest to the shank (opposite the toe) is the:","options":["Toe","Cephalic curve","Heel","Finger guide"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the base of the blade near the shank as the \"heel\".  Why A wrong: The toe is the far tip of the blade.  Why B wrong: The cephalic curve is the curvature of the blade that fits the fetal head.  Why D wrong: The finger guide projects from the handle region, not the blade base."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Which of the following are labelled parts of an obstetric forceps? (Select all that apply)","options":["Blade","Shanks","Lock","Finger guide","Handle"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The parts-of-forceps diagram labels the blade, shanks, lock, finger guide, and handle (along with toe, heel, and cephalic curve).  Why A–E all correct: Each of blade, shanks, lock, finger guide, and handle is explicitly labelled in the diagram."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The blade of a modern obstetric forceps is fenestrated (has an opening). This design serves all of the following purposes EXCEPT:","options":["Makes the instrument light","Causes less trauma","Causes less compression on the fetal head","Increases the weight for a firmer grip"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: A fenestrated blade is designed to be lighter, not heavier; increasing weight is not a purpose.  Why A wrong: Fenestration does make the instrument light — a true benefit.  Why B wrong: It causes less trauma — a true benefit.  Why C wrong: It causes less compression on the fetal head — a true benefit."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"How many curves are present in an obstetric forceps blade?","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are 2 curves in forceps — the cephalic curve and the pelvic curve.  Why A wrong: A single curve does not accommodate both the fetal head and the maternal pelvis.  Why C wrong: Only two curves are described, not three.  Why D wrong: Only two curves are described, not four."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The cephalic curve of the obstetric forceps is described as:","options":["The edge of the forceps directed upward","The inside part of the forceps that fits around the fetal head","The articulation of the two blades","The projection that guards the fingers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cephalic curve is the inside part of the forceps that fits around the fetal head.  Why A wrong: That describes the pelvic curve (edge of the forcep, directed upward).  Why C wrong: The articulation is the lock.  Why D wrong: The finger guard is a separate projection."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The pelvic curve of the obstetric forceps corresponds to which part and orientation?","options":["Inside part; directed downward","Edge of the forceps; always directed upward","Inside part; directed upward","Lock; directed laterally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pelvic curve is the edge of the forceps and is always directed upward during correct application.  Why A wrong: The pelvic curve is the edge, not the inside part, and is directed upward.  Why C wrong: The inside part is the cephalic curve.  Why D wrong: The lock is the articulation, not the pelvic curve."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"During correct forceps application, the pelvic curve should always be directed:","options":["Upward","Downward","Laterally toward the maternal thigh","Posteriorly toward the sacrum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes emphasize the pelvic curve is \"always directed upward.\"  Why B wrong: Downward orientation is incorrect application.  Why C wrong: Lateral orientation does not match the pelvic axis.  Why D wrong: Posterior/sacral orientation is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Which additional forceps part, projecting near the handle to allow a proper grip, is labelled in the diagram?","options":["Cephalic curve","Fingers guards","Toe","Fenestration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels \"fingers guards\" (finger guides) projecting near the handle/shank region.  Why A wrong: The cephalic curve is part of the blade curvature.  Why C wrong: The toe is the tip of the blade.  Why D wrong: The fenestration is the opening within the blade."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The two recognized types of forceps lock are:","options":["English lock and Sliding lock","French lock and German lock","Fixed lock and Rotating lock","Upper lock and Lower lock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The lock flowchart divides forceps locks into English lock and Sliding lock.  Why B wrong: French/German locks are not the classification given.  Why C wrong: Fixed/rotating locks are not the terms used.  Why D wrong: Upper/lower locks are not the classification given."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The English lock of obstetric forceps is characterized by:","options":["A single socket on any one handle","Two sockets, one in each handle (more common type)","No socket at all","Sockets located on the blades"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The English lock has 2 sockets, one in each handle, and is the more common type.  Why A wrong: A single socket on one handle describes the sliding lock.  Why C wrong: The English lock has two sockets, not none.  Why D wrong: The sockets are in the handles, not the blades."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The sliding lock of obstetric forceps has:","options":["Two sockets, one in each handle","A single socket on any one handle","Locks on both blades","No articulation mechanism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sliding lock has a single socket on any one handle.  Why A wrong: Two sockets (one per handle) describe the English lock.  Why C wrong: The lock is on the handle, not on both blades.  Why D wrong: The sliding lock still provides an articulation."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"When applying an English-lock forceps, which blade is always introduced first?","options":["Right blade","Left blade","Whichever is closer to the fetal occiput","Both blades simultaneously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With the English lock, the left blade is always introduced first (and the right blade is then slid over it).  Why A wrong: The right blade is applied second, not first.  Why C wrong: The order is fixed (left first), not determined by occiput position.  Why D wrong: Blades are applied sequentially, not simultaneously."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The very first point to check when identifying/orienting a forceps blade before application is that the:","options":["Lock is closed","Pelvic curve should face upward","Socket is placed below","Blades are equidistant from the sagittal suture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"1st Point\" states the pelvic curve should be faced upward when identifying/orienting the blade.  Why A wrong: Lock closing is a criterion for confirming correct application, not the first orientation point.  Why C wrong: Socket below applies specifically to the right forceps in the 2nd point.  Why D wrong: Equidistance from the sagittal suture is a confirmation of correct application, not the first identification point."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Regarding the left forceps blade (2nd Point), which statements are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["It goes into the left maternal side","Its socket is upwards when held correctly","Its socket is placed below","It goes into the right maternal side"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: For the left forceps, it goes into the left maternal side and its socket is upwards when held correctly.  Why C wrong: Socket placed below describes the right forceps.  Why D wrong: The left forceps goes into the left maternal side, not the right."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"For the right forceps blade, when held correctly the socket is:","options":["Upwards","Placed below","On the fetal head","Absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the right forceps, the socket is placed below.  Why A wrong: The socket being upward describes the left forceps.  Why C wrong: Sockets are in the handles, not on the fetal head.  Why D wrong: The right forceps has a socket; it is simply below."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"During bilateral forceps application, the correct sequence is:","options":["Right blade first, then left blade slides over it","Left blade first, then right blade slides over it","Both blades locked before insertion","Right blade only, no left blade needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The left forceps is introduced first and the right blade is slid over it.  Why A wrong: The order is left first, then right — not the reverse.  Why C wrong: Blades are inserted individually and then locked.  Why D wrong: Both blades are required."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Which findings confirm that the forceps have been correctly applied? (Select all that apply)","options":["The lock closes properly","The two blades are equidistant from the sagittal suture","The lock will not close","The blades overlap the fetal face"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Correct application is confirmed when the lock closes properly and the two blades lie equidistant from the sagittal suture.  Why C wrong: A lock that will not close signals INCORRECT application.  Why D wrong: Blades should lie symmetrically over the sides of the head, not overlap the face."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"After correct forceps application, the two blades should be positioned in what relationship to the fetal sagittal suture?","options":["Overlapping the suture","Equidistant from the sagittal suture","Both on one side of the suture","Perpendicular to the suture at the occiput"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Correctly applied blades lie equidistant from the sagittal suture, confirming symmetric bi-parietal placement.  Why A wrong: Blades should not overlap/cross the sagittal suture.  Why C wrong: Both on one side indicates asymmetric, incorrect application.  Why D wrong: The relationship is equidistance, not perpendicular placement."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Historically the vacuum extractor cup was made of metal; modern cups are made of:","options":["Stainless steel","Silastic (silicone) material","Latex rubber","Polyvinyl chloride"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cup was earlier metal but is now made of silastic material.  Why A wrong: Stainless steel is a metal, representing the older design.  Why C wrong: Latex rubber is not the material named.  Why D wrong: PVC is not the material named."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The advantages of the modern silastic vacuum cup include: (Select all that apply)","options":["It is soft","It causes less trauma to the head","It applies maximum compression","It requires no consent"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The silastic cup is soft and causes less trauma to the head.  Why C wrong: Vacuum is chosen for less trauma; it is not intended to maximize compression.  Why D wrong: Consent is always required for instrumental delivery."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"What is the initial (starting) negative pressure applied in vacuum extraction?","options":["0.1 kg/cm²","0.2 kg/cm²","0.5 kg/cm²","0.8 kg/cm²"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The initial pressure in vacuum is 0.2 kg/cm².  Why A wrong: 0.1 kg/cm² is the increment, not the starting pressure.  Why C wrong: 0.5 kg/cm² is an intermediate value, not the initial pressure.  Why D wrong: 0.8 kg/cm² is the maximum pressure, not the initial pressure."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"During vacuum extraction, the negative pressure is increased in steps of:","options":["0.1 kg/cm²","0.2 kg/cm²","0.3 kg/cm²","0.5 kg/cm²"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pressure is increased by 0.1 kg/cm² increments.  Why B wrong: 0.2 kg/cm² is the initial pressure, not the increment.  Why C wrong: 0.3 kg/cm² is not the stated increment.  Why D wrong: 0.5 kg/cm² is not the stated increment."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"What is the maximum negative pressure applied during vacuum extraction?","options":["0.2 kg/cm²","0.5 kg/cm²","0.8 kg/cm²","1.0 kg/cm²"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The maximum pressure in vacuum extraction is 0.8 kg/cm².  Why A wrong: 0.2 kg/cm² is the initial pressure.  Why B wrong: 0.5 kg/cm² is an intermediate value.  Why D wrong: 1.0 kg/cm² exceeds the stated maximum."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Reaching the maximum vacuum pressure of 0.8 kg/cm² by stepwise increments takes approximately:","options":["1–2 minutes","5–10 minutes","15–20 minutes","30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Building up to the maximum pressure of 0.8 kg/cm² takes about 5–10 minutes.  Why A wrong: 1–2 minutes is too short per the notes.  Why C wrong: 15–20 minutes exceeds the stated time.  Why D wrong: 30 minutes exceeds the stated time."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"For correct vacuum application, the centre of the cup should be placed at the:","options":["Anterior fontanelle","Posterior fontanelle","Flexion point","Occipital protuberance"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The vacuum cup is applied such that the centre of the cup lies over the 'flexion point'.  Why A wrong: The anterior fontanelle is 6 cm away from the flexion point, not the site of cup centre.  Why B wrong: The posterior fontanelle is 3 cm from the flexion point, not the exact centre.  Why D wrong: The occipital protuberance is not the described landmark."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The flexion point (for vacuum cup placement) is located how far from the posterior fontanelle?","options":["3 cm anterior to the posterior fontanelle","6 cm anterior to the posterior fontanelle","3 cm posterior to the posterior fontanelle","6 cm posterior to the posterior fontanelle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flexion point is 3 cm anterior to the posterior fontanelle.  Why B wrong: 6 cm relates to the distance from the anterior fontanelle, not posterior.  Why C/D wrong: The flexion point is anterior to, not posterior to, the posterior fontanelle."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The flexion point for vacuum cup placement is located how far from the anterior fontanelle?","options":["3 cm posterior to the anterior fontanelle","6 cm posterior to the anterior fontanelle","3 cm anterior to the anterior fontanelle","6 cm anterior to the anterior fontanelle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flexion point is 6 cm posterior to the anterior fontanelle.  Why A wrong: 3 cm relates to the posterior fontanelle distance, not anterior.  Why C/D wrong: The flexion point is posterior to, not anterior to, the anterior fontanelle."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The flexion point over which the vacuum cup is centred is defined as the point that is:","options":["6 cm anterior to posterior fontanelle and 3 cm posterior to anterior fontanelle","3 cm anterior to posterior fontanelle and 6 cm posterior to anterior fontanelle","Equidistant (5 cm) from both fontanelles","At the anterior fontanelle exactly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flexion point is 3 cm anterior to the posterior fontanelle and 6 cm posterior to the anterior fontanelle.  Why A wrong: The distances are reversed in this option.  Why C wrong: The distances are unequal (3 cm vs 6 cm), not equidistant.  Why D wrong: It is not located at the anterior fontanelle."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"The prerequisites for forceps application are commonly recalled by the mnemonic \"FORCEP\". What does the \"F\" stand for?","options":["Fetal distress","Favourable station (≥ +2)","Full bladder","Fetal weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the FORCEP mnemonic, F = favourable station (≥ +2).  Why A wrong: Fetal distress may be an indication but is not the \"F\" of this prerequisite mnemonic.  Why C wrong: The bladder should be empty (that is \"E\"), not full.  Why D wrong: Fetal weight is not the \"F\" prerequisite listed."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"In the FORCEP prerequisites, what does the \"O\" require regarding the cervix?","options":["Os should be closed","Os should be fully dilated (10 cm)","Os should be at least 6 cm dilated","Os need not be assessed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: O = Os should be fully dilated, i.e., the cervix must be 10 cm dilated for forceps.  Why A wrong: A closed os precludes vaginal delivery entirely.  Why C wrong: 6 cm (partial) dilation is a vacuum criterion, not the forceps requirement.  Why D wrong: Cervical dilation is a critical prerequisite that must be assessed."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"For forceps delivery the cervix (os) must be dilated to:","options":["6 cm","8 cm","10 cm (fully dilated)","4 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The forceps prerequisite requires the os to be fully dilated at 10 cm.  Why A wrong: 6 cm is the minimum for vacuum, not forceps.  Why B wrong: 8 cm is not full dilation.  Why D wrong: 4 cm is early labour, far from full dilation."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"In the FORCEP mnemonic, the \"R\" prerequisite includes which requirements?","options":["Membranes should be ruptured and the head should be rotated","Rectum should be empty","Reflexes should be intact","Regional anaesthesia is mandatory"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: R = membranes should be ruptured and the head should be rotated (to a favourable position).  Why B wrong: An empty rectum is not the stated \"R\" requirement.  Why C wrong: Reflexes are not part of this mnemonic.  Why D wrong: Regional anaesthesia is not the \"R\" prerequisite listed."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"In the FORCEP prerequisites, the \"C\" stands for requirements that include:","options":["Cervix should be closed","Uterus should be contracting and consent should be taken","Cesarean set-up must be avoided","Catheter should remain in place"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: C = the uterus should be contracting and consent should be taken.  Why A wrong: A closed cervix would prevent delivery; the os must be fully dilated.  Why C wrong: A cesarean backup is prudent, but that is not the meaning of \"C\" here.  Why D wrong: The bladder should be emptied (E); a retained catheter is not the \"C\" requirement."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"In the FORCEP prerequisites, the \"E\" requires that:","options":["Bladder should be empty and the head of the baby should be engaged","Episiotomy must be avoided","Epidural must be given","Estrogen should be administered"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: E = the bladder should be empty and the head of the baby should be engaged.  Why B wrong: Episiotomy is given, not avoided, in instrumental delivery.  Why C wrong: Epidural is not this mnemonic component.  Why D wrong: Estrogen administration is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"In the FORCEP prerequisites, the \"P\" requires that the pelvis should be:","options":["Contracted","Normal, with no cephalopelvic disproportion","Anthropoid only","Android only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: P = the pelvis should be normal with no cephalopelvic disproportion (CPD).  Why A wrong: A contracted pelvis with CPD contraindicates forceps.  Why C/D wrong: Specific pelvis types (anthropoid/android) are not the requirement; a normal pelvis without CPD is."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"A key advantage of the vacuum extractor over forceps regarding cervical dilatation is that vacuum:","options":["Requires the cervix to be closed","Can be applied even when the cervix is only partially dilated (≥ 6 cm)","Requires 12 cm dilatation","Can be applied before rupture of membranes only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vacuum shares the forceps prerequisites except that it can be applied even when the cervix is partially dilated (≥ 6 cm).  Why A wrong: A closed cervix precludes delivery.  Why C wrong: 12 cm dilatation is not physiological (full dilatation is 10 cm).  Why D wrong: Membranes should be ruptured (a shared prerequisite), and this option misstates the vacuum advantage."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Which of the following is a distinct advantage of vacuum over forceps regarding the fetal head?","options":["Vacuum can rotate the head of the fetus","Vacuum compresses the head more","Vacuum cannot rotate the head","Vacuum requires the head to be at negative station"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An advantage of vacuum is that it can rotate the head of the fetus (autorotation), useful in occipito-posterior positions.  Why B wrong: Vacuum causes less compression/trauma, not more.  Why C wrong: Vacuum's ability to rotate the head is precisely its advantage.  Why D wrong: Instrumental delivery is done at +2 or below, not at negative station."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"Compared to forceps, the vacuum extractor differs in its prerequisites by allowing use with partial cervical dilatation and by being able to:","options":["Ignore rupture of membranes","Rotate the fetal head","Be applied above the ischial spines","Bypass the need for consent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The two vacuum-specific differences listed are: application with partial dilatation (≥6 cm) and the ability to rotate the fetal head.  Why A wrong: Rupture of membranes is a shared prerequisite; it is not bypassed.  Why C wrong: The favourable-station requirement (+2 or below) still applies.  Why D wrong: Consent is always required."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"A woman in second stage has the fetal vertex in an occipito-posterior position at +2 station, cervix fully dilated, membranes ruptured, empty bladder, and a normal pelvis. Which instrument can rotate the head and is appropriate here?","options":["Vacuum extractor","Cesarean section is mandatory","Symphysiotomy","Destructive operation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vacuum can be applied in occipito-posterior vertex and can rotate the head; all prerequisites (station, dilatation, membranes, bladder, pelvis) are met.  Why B wrong: OP vertex is not an automatic indication for cesarean when prerequisites are met.  Why C wrong: Symphysiotomy is not indicated with a normal pelvis and no CPD.  Why D wrong: A destructive operation is reserved for a dead fetus with obstructed labour, not this scenario."},{"section":"Batch 068 · Brow presentation, indications for cesarean section…","question":"A fetus is in a persistent brow presentation at term with the mentovertical diameter (14 cm) engaging. What is the most appropriate management?","options":["Apply forceps","Apply vacuum","Perform cesarean section","Await spontaneous delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Brow presentation has no mechanism of labour and requires cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Forceps cannot be applied where cesarean is mandatory (brow is such a condition).  Why B wrong: Vacuum is used only in vertex, not brow.  Why D wrong: Spontaneous delivery is impossible with the 14 cm mentovertical diameter engaging."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"In which fetal head position CANNOT a vacuum (ventouse) be used, though forceps can?","options":["Occipito-anterior position","Occipito-posterior position","After-coming head of breech","Left occipito-anterior"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The comparison table shows vacuum marked \"×\" for after-coming head of breech (and for face presentation), whereas forceps can be used there.  Why A wrong: Vacuum is marked \"✓\" for occipito-anterior position.  Why B wrong: Vacuum is marked \"✓\" for occipito-posterior position.  Why D wrong: Left occipito-anterior is an occipito-anterior variant where vacuum is applicable."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Forceps can be applied for delivery of the after-coming head of a breech, but vacuum cannot. This is best reflected in which comparison?","options":["Both can be used equally","Forceps ✓, Vacuum ×","Forceps ×, Vacuum ✓","Neither can be used"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the table, \"after coming head of breech\" is listed under forceps use, while vacuum is marked \"×\".  Why A wrong: They are not equal for this indication.  Why C wrong: It is forceps (not vacuum) that can be used.  Why D wrong: Forceps can be used."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"In which of the following presentations can forceps be used but vacuum is contraindicated (marked ×)?","options":["Occipito-anterior","Occipito-posterior","Face (mento-anterior) presentation","Vertex presentation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Face (mento-anterior) position shows vacuum \"×\"; forceps can be used in this position.  Why A wrong: Vacuum is ✓ for occipito-anterior.  Why B wrong: Vacuum is ✓ for occipito-posterior.  Why D wrong: Vertex is the standard position where both can generally be used."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which instrument has the ADVANTAGE of being usable in preterm delivery and in fetal distress?","options":["Vacuum (ventouse)","Forceps","Both equally","Neither"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Advantage\" row lists preterm delivery use and fetal distress use under forceps; vacuum is marked \"×\" for both.  Why A wrong: Vacuum is marked \"×\" for preterm and fetal distress advantage.  Why C wrong: Only forceps holds this advantage.  Why D wrong: Forceps does hold this advantage."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which is NOT an indication for using forceps as listed in the notes?","options":["Maternal exhaustion","Fetal distress","Prolonged 2nd stage of labor","Cephalopelvic disproportion"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Cephalopelvic disproportion is an absolute CONTRAINDICATION for instrumental delivery, not an indication.  Why A wrong: Maternal exhaustion is a listed indication for forceps.  Why B wrong: Fetal distress is a listed indication for forceps.  Why C wrong: Prolonged 2nd stage of labor is a listed indication for forceps."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"As per the notes, a prolonged second stage of labor indicating instrumental (forceps) delivery is defined for a PRIMIGRAVIDA as a second stage lasting:","options":["≥ 30 minutes","≥ 1 hour","≥ 2 hours","≥ 4 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state prolonged 2nd stage = Primigravida ≥2 hrs.  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is not the stated threshold.  Why B wrong: ≥1 hr is the multigravida threshold, not primigravida.  Why D wrong: 4 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"For a MULTIGRAVIDA, the second stage of labor is considered prolonged (indication for forceps) when it lasts:","options":["≥ 1 hour","≥ 2 hours","≥ 3 hours","≥ 30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give Multigravida ≥1 hr as the prolonged 2nd stage threshold.  Why B wrong: ≥2 hrs is the primigravida threshold.  Why C wrong: 3 hours is not stated.  Why D wrong: 30 minutes is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which instrument is specifically indicated to deliver the fetal head in a mother with heart disease (to shorten the second stage / avoid maternal pushing)?","options":["Vacuum","Forceps","Neither","Cesarean only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Forceps indications include \"In heart disease patients to deliver the head of fetus\"; vacuum is also marked ✓ for heart disease but forceps is the classically listed instrument to relieve maternal effort.  Why A wrong: Though vacuum is ✓, the notes list this under forceps indications.  Why C wrong: An instrument is indicated to reduce maternal cardiac effort.  Why D wrong: The point relates to instrumental delivery, not automatic cesarean."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"The complication of forceps delivery is MOST COMMONLY:","options":["Fetal complication","Maternal complication (perineal tear)","Intracranial hemorrhage","Cephalhematoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state forceps most commonly leads to maternal complication (perineal tear).  Why A wrong: Fetal complication is the most common problem with vacuum, not forceps.  Why C wrong: Intracranial hemorrhage is a vacuum-related fetal complication.  Why D wrong: Cephalhematoma is a vacuum-related fetal complication."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Forceps delivery is classically associated with which nerve injury to the neonate?","options":["Facial nerve palsy","6th (abducens) nerve palsy","Brachial plexus palsy","Radial nerve palsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Forceps → Facial nerve palsy.\"  Why B wrong: 6th nerve palsy is listed under vacuum complications.  Why C wrong: Brachial plexus palsy is not listed here for forceps.  Why D wrong: Radial nerve palsy is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Vacuum (ventouse) delivery MOST COMMONLY leads to which type of complication?","options":["Maternal complication","Fetal complication","Perineal tear","Facial nerve palsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state vacuum most commonly leads to fetal complication.  Why A wrong: Maternal complication is most common with forceps.  Why C wrong: Perineal tear is a maternal (forceps) complication.  Why D wrong: Facial nerve palsy is a forceps complication."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which of the following is a fetal complication listed under VACUUM delivery? (Select all that apply)","options":["Intracranial hemorrhage","6th nerve palsy","Cephalhematoma","Facial nerve palsy","Perineal tear"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list intracranial hemorrhage, 6th nerve palsy, and cephalhematoma as vacuum fetal complications.  Why D wrong: Facial nerve palsy is a forceps complication.  Why E wrong: Perineal tear is a maternal forceps complication."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"What is the maximum number of attempts recommended for a forceps (or vacuum) delivery before it is declared failed?","options":["2 attempts","3 attempts","4 attempts","5 attempts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Both forceps and vacuum flow charts state \"Maximum attempt → 3 attempts.\"  Why A, C, D wrong: The notes specify exactly 3 attempts as the maximum."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"After a failed forceps delivery (baby not delivered after 3 attempts), the recommended management is:","options":["Repeat forceps with more traction","Switch to vacuum","Cesarean section","Wait for spontaneous delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state failed forceps → management → cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Repeating forceps after failure is unsafe and not recommended.  Why B wrong: Sequential use of instruments increases fetal trauma; the notes direct to cesarean.  Why D wrong: Failed instrumental delivery mandates operative delivery, not waiting."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"After a failed vacuum delivery, the management of choice is:","options":["Cesarean section","Forceps trial","Manual rotation","Oxytocin augmentation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state failed vacuum → management → cesarean section.  Why B wrong: Switching to forceps is not the directed management per notes.  Why C wrong: Manual rotation is not the failed-vacuum protocol.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not appropriate after failed instrumental delivery."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which of the following is an ABSOLUTE contraindication for instrumental (vacuum/forceps) delivery?","options":["Cephalopelvic disproportion","Maternal exhaustion","Fetal distress","Prolonged second stage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cephalopelvic disproportion is listed as absolute contraindication #1.  Why B wrong: Maternal exhaustion is an indication, not a contraindication.  Why C wrong: Fetal distress is an indication.  Why D wrong: Prolonged second stage is an indication."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which fetal bony deformity is given as an example of an absolute contraindication to instrumental delivery?","options":["Osteogenesis Imperfecta","Spina bifida","Achondroplasia","Cleft palate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Known bony deformities of fetus, e.g. Osteogenesis Imperfecta\" as an absolute contraindication.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the example given for the bony-deformity contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Instrumental delivery is contraindicated in a fetus with a known coagulopathy because of which of the following?","options":["Bleeding disorder","Cardiac defect","Renal failure","Neural tube defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Known coagulopathy in fetus – Bleeding disorder\" as an absolute contraindication.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the reason cited; coagulopathy refers to a bleeding disorder."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"According to the notes, instrumental delivery should be AVOIDED in which maternal condition?","options":["HIV positive females","Diabetic females","Hypertensive females","Rh-negative females"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Avoid in HIV +ve females\" (to reduce risk of vertical transmission via fetal scalp trauma).  Why B, C, D wrong: These conditions are not the ones flagged to avoid instrumental delivery in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which of the following are absolute contraindications for instrumental delivery? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cephalopelvic disproportion","Inadequate pelvis","Known bony deformities of fetus (e.g. Osteogenesis Imperfecta)","Known coagulopathy in fetus (bleeding disorder)","Maternal exhaustion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: All four are explicitly listed as absolute contraindications.  Why E wrong: Maternal exhaustion is an indication for instrumental delivery, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"As the first nursing implication before an instrumental (forceps/vacuum) delivery, the nurse should:","options":["Take consent","Start oxytocin","Give general anesthesia","Perform amniotomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing implications list #1 is \"Take consent.\"  Why B wrong: Oxytocin is not the listed first step.  Why C wrong: General anesthesia is not the listed nursing implication.  Why D wrong: Amniotomy is not listed as a nursing implication."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"When explaining an instrumental delivery to the patient, the nurse should explain both the procedure and:","options":["The complications","The cost","The hospital policy","The anesthetist's name"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing implication #2 states \"Explain to patient = Procedure + complications.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not part of the stated explanation content."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which nursing action must be checked BEFORE proceeding with an instrumental delivery?","options":["That prerequisites are fulfilled and no contraindication exists","That the baby weighs less than 3 kg","That the mother is nil per oral for 12 hours","That labor has been induced"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing implications #3 and #4 state check that prerequisites are fulfilled and check no contraindication for instrumental delivery.  Why B wrong: A specific 3-kg limit is not stated.  Why C wrong: A 12-hour NPO rule is not part of these notes.  Why D wrong: Induction is not a listed prerequisite."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"During instrumental delivery, the patient should be informed that after a maximum of 3 attempts and failure, the plan is to proceed to:","options":["Cesarean section","Craniotomy","Symphysiotomy","Manual removal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing implication #5 states \"maximum 3 attempts → fail → cesarean section.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated plan after failed attempts."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"After an instrumental delivery is completed, the nurse must check the mother AND the fetus for:","options":["Any complication","Blood group compatibility","Weight gain","Immunization status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing implications #6 and #7 state check mother for any complication and check fetus for any complication.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the listed post-procedure checks."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"The modern (\"These days\") vacuum device that has an inbuilt pump to create vacuum, is smaller, disposable, and portable is the:","options":["Kiwi (Omnicup) device","Malmstrom cup","Bird's cup","Silastic cup"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the \"Never\" (newer) vacuum device — the Kiwi/Omnicup — with inbuilt pump, smaller size, disposable, portable.  Why B, C, D wrong: These older ventouse cups are not the device described in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which of the following are features of the modern Kiwi Omnicup vacuum device? (Select all that apply)","options":["Inbuilt pump to create vacuum","Smaller in size","Disposable","Can be used for both occipito-anterior/occipito-posterior (OA/OP) deliveries","Portable"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five features are explicitly listed for the Omnicup device.  Why (none) wrong: Every option is a listed feature."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"\"High forceps\" application (now outdated) refers to forceps applied when the fetal head is:","options":["Above the ischial spine","At the level of the ischial spine","At +2 station","Visible at the introitus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: High forceps (outdated) = forceps applied when head of fetus is above ischial spine.  Why B wrong: Head at the level of ischial spine defines mid-cavity forceps.  Why C wrong: +2 station or below defines low forceps.  Why D wrong: Head visible at the introitus defines outlet forceps."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"\"Mid-cavity forceps\" (outdated) is defined as forceps applied when the fetal head is at:","options":["The level of the ischial spine ('O' station or '+' station)","Above the ischial spine","+2 station or below","At the perineum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mid-cavity forceps (outdated) = head at level of ischial spine ('O' station or '+' station).  Why B wrong: Above ischial spine = high forceps.  Why C wrong: +2 or below = low forceps.  Why D wrong: At perineum = outlet forceps."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"\"Low forceps\" application is defined as forceps applied when the fetal head is at:","options":["+2 station or below, but has not reached the perineum","Above the ischial spine","At the ischial spine ('O' station)","Visible through the introitus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low forceps = head at +2 station or below it but has not reached the perineum.  Why B wrong: Above ischial spine = high forceps.  Why C wrong: At the ischial spine = mid-cavity forceps.  Why D wrong: Visible through introitus = outlet forceps."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"\"Outlet forceps\" application refers to forceps applied when:","options":["The fetal head is at the level of the perineum and the scalp is visible through the mother's introitus","The fetal head is above the ischial spine","The fetal head is at the ischial spine","The fetal head is at +2 station but not at the perineum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Outlet forceps = head at level of perineum, i.e., scalp of fetus visible through mother's introitus.  Why B wrong: That is high forceps.  Why C wrong: That is mid-cavity forceps.  Why D wrong: That is low forceps."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which forceps is used specifically for OUTLET forceps delivery and is described as a short & light forceps?","options":["Wrigley forceps","Kielland forceps","Piper forceps","Simpson forceps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Wrigley forceps is described as the outlet forcep — short & light forcep, with only one forceps used at the level of the outlet.  Why B wrong: Kielland forceps is the rotational forceps.  Why C wrong: Piper forceps is for the after-coming head of breech.  Why D wrong: Simpson is not the forceps named in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"At the level of the outlet, which forceps requires only ONE forceps (blade set) to be used?","options":["WRIGLEY forceps","Kielland forceps","Piper forceps","Long forceps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state \"Only one forcep is used at the level of outlet = WRIGLEY forceps.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the outlet forceps described as needing only one forceps."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Regarding rotation of the fetal head, which statement is correct?","options":["Forceps cannot rotate the head of the fetus; vacuum can rotate the head","Vacuum cannot rotate the head; forceps can rotate the head","Both can rotate the head equally","Neither can rotate the head"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state forceps cannot rotate the fetal head, while vacuum can rotate the fetal head.  Why B wrong: This reverses the true statement.  Why C wrong: They differ in rotational ability.  Why D wrong: Vacuum (and the exception Kielland forceps) can rotate the head."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which is the ONLY forceps that can rotate the head of the fetus (the exception to the \"forceps cannot rotate\" rule)?","options":["Kielland forceps","Wrigley forceps","Piper forceps","Simpson forceps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state Kielland forceps (outdated) is the only forceps that can rotate the fetal head — the rotational forceps.  Why B wrong: Wrigley is the outlet forceps.  Why C wrong: Piper is for the after-coming breech head.  Why D wrong: Simpson is not named as rotational here."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which features characterize the Kielland forceps? (Select all that apply)","options":["Long forceps","Sliding lock","Only forceps that can rotate the fetal head (rotational forceps)","Now considered outdated","Short & light forceps"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Kielland forceps is described as long forceps with a sliding lock, the only rotational forceps, and outdated.  Why E wrong: Short & light forceps describes the Wrigley (outlet) forceps, not Kielland."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"The Kielland forceps is fitted with which type of lock?","options":["Sliding lock","English lock","French lock","Pivot lock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the Kielland forceps with a sliding lock.  Why B wrong: English lock is shown on the Piper forceps.  Why C wrong: French lock is not mentioned in these notes.  Why D wrong: Pivot lock is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"The Piper forceps is used specifically to deliver:","options":["The after-coming head of a breech","The occipito-posterior head","A face presentation","Twin B"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state \"PIPER forcep → To deliver the after coming head of breech.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the specific use listed for Piper forceps."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which structural features are labelled on the Piper forceps in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Long forceps","Perineal curve","English lock","Sliding lock","Short & light blades"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The Piper forceps diagram labels a long forceps, a perineal curve, and an English lock.  Why D wrong: Sliding lock is a Kielland feature.  Why E wrong: Short & light blades describe the Wrigley forceps."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which type of lock is labelled on the Piper forceps?","options":["English lock","Sliding lock","Screw lock","German lock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Piper forceps diagram labels an \"English lock.\"  Why B wrong: Sliding lock belongs to the Kielland forceps.  Why C, D wrong: These lock types are not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Before an instrumental delivery, adequate analgesia can be provided by which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Epidural block","Pudendal nerve block","General anesthesia only","Spinal-only block","No analgesia needed"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list epidural block and pudendal nerve block under \"Give adequate analgesia.\"  Why C wrong: General anesthesia only is not the listed method.  Why D wrong: Spinal-only block is not the listed method.  Why E wrong: Adequate analgesia is explicitly recommended."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Which procedure is recommended (under \"Also know\") to enlarge the vaginal outlet during instrumental delivery?","options":["Episiotomy","Symphysiotomy","Cervical cerclage","Fundal pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Give episiotomy\" as a step.  Why B wrong: Symphysiotomy is not listed.  Why C wrong: Cervical cerclage is a pregnancy-maintenance procedure, unrelated here.  Why D wrong: Fundal pressure is not the listed step."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"In forceps delivery, \"traction\" refers to which action?","options":["Force = pull on the forceps","Rotation of the blades","Compression of the head","Pushing the head upward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define Traction: (Force = pull forceps).  Why B wrong: Rotation is a separate action (and forceps generally cannot rotate).  Why C wrong: Traction is pulling, not compression.  Why D wrong: Traction pulls the head down/out, not upward."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"What is the recommended traction force for forceps delivery in a PRIMIGRAVIDA?","options":["16–18 kg","12–13 kg","20–25 kg","8–10 kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Primigravida = 16–18 kg traction force.  Why B wrong: 12–13 kg is the multigravida value.  Why C, D wrong: These values are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"What is the recommended traction force for forceps delivery in a MULTIGRAVIDA?","options":["12–13 kg","16–18 kg","20 kg","10 kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Multigravida = 12–13 kg traction force.  Why B wrong: 16–18 kg is the primigravida value.  Why C, D wrong: These values are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Along which diameter of the fetal head is the forceps applied?","options":["Occipito-mental diameter","Biparietal diameter","Suboccipito-bregmatic diameter","Occipito-frontal diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state \"Forceps is applied along occipital mental diameter of fetal head.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are other fetal head diameters, not the one along which forceps is applied per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"When a vacuum is applied on the fetal scalp, an artificial swelling is formed which is called:","options":["Chignon","Caput succedaneum","Cephalhematoma","Molding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state vacuum applied on scalp → swelling formed → Chignon.  Why B wrong: Caput succedaneum forms subsequently/naturally; the vacuum-specific swelling is the chignon (which then becomes caput per the notes' arrow chain).  Why C wrong: Cephalhematoma is a subperiosteal blood collection, a separate complication.  Why D wrong: Molding is overlapping of skull bones, not a scalp swelling."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"According to the notes, the vacuum-induced scalp swelling (chignon) subsequently forms/becomes a:","options":["Caput","Cephalhematoma","Subgaleal hemorrhage","Molding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The arrow chain in the notes shows Chignon → Caput.  Why B, C, D wrong: The notes specify caput, not cephalhematoma, subgaleal hemorrhage, or molding, as the next step."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Normally, blood loss after a NORMAL VAGINAL delivery is defined as:","options":["Less than 500 ml","Less than 1000 ml","Less than 250 ml","Less than 1500 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state normal blood loss after vaginal delivery = <500 ml.  Why B wrong: <1000 ml is the threshold for cesarean section.  Why C, D wrong: These values are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Normal blood loss after a CESAREAN SECTION is defined as:","options":["Less than 1000 ml","Less than 500 ml","Less than 750 ml","Less than 1500 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state normal blood loss after cesarean section = <1000 ml.  Why B wrong: <500 ml is for vaginal delivery.  Why C, D wrong: These values are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Postpartum hemorrhage (PPH) after a VAGINAL delivery is defined as a blood loss of:","options":["≥ 500 mL","≥ 1000 mL","≥ 250 mL","≥ 1500 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes define PPH as ≥500 mL after vaginal delivery.  Why B wrong: ≥1000 mL is the threshold after c-section.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the stated thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Postpartum hemorrhage after a CESAREAN SECTION is defined as a blood loss of:","options":["≥ 1000 mL","≥ 500 mL","≥ 750 mL","≥ 2000 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes define PPH as ≥1000 mL after c-section.  Why B wrong: ≥500 mL is for vaginal delivery.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the stated thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"Postpartum hemorrhage is defined as excessive bleeding occurring after delivery and up until:","options":["12 weeks of delivery","6 weeks of delivery","24 hours of delivery","42 days of delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state PPH is bleeding after delivery and \"uptil 12 weeks of delivery.\"  Why B wrong: 6 weeks is the traditional puerperium, but the notes specify 12 weeks.  Why C wrong: 24 hours only separates primary from secondary PPH.  Why D wrong: 42 days (6 weeks) is not the stated cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"PRIMARY postpartum hemorrhage occurs within what time period after delivery?","options":["Within 24 hours of delivery","After 24 hours up to 12 weeks","Within 1 hour","Within 7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state primary PPH occurs within 24 hours of delivery.  Why B wrong: That describes secondary PPH.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the stated definition."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of primary PPH?","options":["Atonic uterus","Retained placental tissue","Trauma (perineal tear)","Bleeding disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state most common cause of primary PPH → atonic uterus.  Why B wrong: Retained placental tissue is the most common cause of secondary PPH.  Why C wrong: Trauma is the 2nd most common cause of PPH overall.  Why D wrong: Bleeding disorder (↓thromboplastin) is the 4th \"T,\" not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"SECONDARY postpartum hemorrhage is defined as PPH occurring:","options":["After 24 hours of delivery and up until 12 weeks after delivery","Within 24 hours of delivery","Within 1 hour of delivery","After 12 weeks postpartum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state secondary PPH occurs after 24 hours of delivery and up until 12 weeks after delivery.  Why B wrong: Within 24 hours is primary PPH.  Why C wrong: Within 1 hour is not the definition.  Why D wrong: The window ends at 12 weeks, it does not begin after."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of secondary PPH?","options":["Retained placental tissue","Atonic uterus","Perineal trauma","Coagulopathy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state most common cause of secondary PPH → retained placental tissue.  Why B wrong: Atonic uterus is the most common cause of primary PPH.  Why C wrong: Perineal trauma is the 2nd most common cause overall.  Why D wrong: Coagulopathy is the 4th \"T.\""},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"The causes of postpartum hemorrhage are remembered as the \"4 T's.\" Which of the following are the 4 T's? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tone (atonic uterus)","Trauma","Tissue (retained placental tissue)","Thrombin/Thromboplastin (bleeding disorder)","Temperature"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The 4 T's listed are ↓Tone (atonic uterus), Trauma, retained placental Tissue, and ↓Thromboplastin (bleeding disorder).  Why E wrong: Temperature is not one of the 4 T's."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"In the \"4 T's\" of PPH, the first \"T\" (1T) refers to:","options":["Decreased tone of uterus (atonic uterus)","Trauma","Retained tissue","Thromboplastin deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1T = ↓ tone of uterus (atonic uterus).  Why B wrong: Trauma is 2T.  Why C wrong: Retained tissue is 3T.  Why D wrong: ↓Thromboplastin is 4T."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"In the \"4 T's\" of PPH, the fourth \"T\" (4T) refers to:","options":["Decreased thromboplastin (bleeding disorder)","Trauma","Tone","Tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 4T = ↓ Thromboplastin (bleeding disorder).  Why B wrong: Trauma is 2T.  Why C wrong: Tone is 1T.  Why D wrong: Tissue is 3T."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of postpartum hemorrhage overall?","options":["Atonic uterus (↓ tone of uterus)","Trauma","Retained placental tissue","Bleeding disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state the most common cause of PPH is ↓ tone of uterus, called atonic uterus.  Why B wrong: Trauma is the 2nd most common cause.  Why C wrong: Retained placental tissue is the most common cause of secondary PPH specifically.  Why D wrong: Bleeding disorder is the 4th \"T.\""},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"What is the SECOND most common cause of postpartum hemorrhage, with perineal tear as an example?","options":["Trauma","Atonic uterus","Retained tissue","Coagulopathy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Notes state the 2nd most common cause of PPH is trauma (e.g. perineal tear).  Why B wrong: Atonic uterus is the most common cause.  Why C wrong: Retained tissue is the 3rd \"T\"/most common secondary cause.  Why D wrong: Coagulopathy is the 4th \"T.\""},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"A vacuum (ventouse) can be used in which of the following situations per the comparison table? (Select all that apply)","options":["Occipito-anterior position","Occipito-posterior position","Maternal exhaustion","Prolonged 2nd stage of labor","Fetal distress"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Vacuum is marked ✓ for occipito-anterior, occipito-posterior, maternal exhaustion, and prolonged 2nd stage of labor.  Why E wrong: Vacuum is marked \"×\" for fetal distress (fetal distress is a forceps indication/advantage)."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"A nurse is caring for a woman who delivered vaginally and has lost an estimated 600 mL of blood. This finding is best classified as:","options":["Postpartum hemorrhage (exceeds the ≥500 mL threshold)","Normal blood loss","Secondary PPH by definition","Insignificant, requires no action"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PPH after vaginal delivery is defined as ≥500 mL; 600 mL exceeds this, so it is PPH.  Why B wrong: Normal loss after vaginal delivery is <500 mL, so 600 mL is abnormal.  Why C wrong: Timing (not just volume) determines primary vs secondary; volume alone does not make it \"secondary.\"  Why D wrong: PPH requires prompt assessment and intervention."},{"section":"Batch 069 · Forceps vs Vacuum comparison…","question":"A woman develops heavy vaginal bleeding on postpartum day 10. Ultrasound shows retained products of conception. This is best classified as:","options":["Secondary PPH due to retained placental tissue","Primary PPH due to atonic uterus","Trauma-related PPH","Normal lochia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bleeding after 24 hours (day 10) is secondary PPH, and its most common cause is retained placental tissue.  Why B wrong: Primary PPH occurs within 24 hours; day 10 is beyond that.  Why C wrong: The cause here is retained tissue, not trauma.  Why D wrong: Heavy bleeding with retained products is pathological, not normal lochia."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a MATERNAL risk factor for postpartum haemorrhage (PPH)?","options":["Prolonged labour","Anaemia","Succenturiate placental lobe","Placenta accreta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anaemia is listed among the maternal factors that predispose to PPH.  Why A wrong: Prolonged labour is a labour-related factor, not a maternal factor.  Why C wrong: A big placenta with a succenturiate lobe is a placenta-related factor.  Why D wrong: Placenta accreta is a placenta-related factor."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"\"Over-distended uterus\" is a maternal risk factor for PPH. Which of the following causes an over-distended uterus as listed in the notes?","options":["Oligohydramnios","Twin pregnancy","Fetal growth restriction","Placenta praevia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list twin (multiple) pregnancy, polyhydramnios, and macrosomia as causes of an over-distended uterus.  Why A wrong: Oligohydramnios (reduced liquor) does not distend the uterus.  Why C wrong: Fetal growth restriction gives a small-for-dates, not distended, uterus.  Why D wrong: Placenta praevia is not the listed cause of over-distension."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"According to the notes, which three conditions produce an over-distended uterus that raises PPH risk? (Select all that apply)","options":["Twin pregnancy","Polyhydramnios","Macrosomia","Anaemia","Antepartum haemorrhage"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Twin, polyhydramnios, and macrosomia are the three listed causes of over-distended uterus.  Why D wrong: Anaemia is a separate maternal factor, not a cause of over-distension.  Why E wrong: Antepartum haemorrhage in this pregnancy is a separate maternal factor."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"A woman with pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH) in the current pregnancy is at increased risk of PPH because PIH is classified as which type of risk factor?","options":["Placenta-related factor","Labour-related factor","Maternal factor","Fetal factor"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: PIH in this pregnancy is listed under maternal factors for PPH.  Why A wrong: Placenta-related factors are big placenta/succenturiate lobe and placenta accreta.  Why B wrong: Labour-related factors are prolonged labour, excess oxytocin, precipitate labour.  Why D wrong: \"Fetal factor\" is not a category used in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Which two maternal history/pregnancy findings (besides anaemia, PIH and over-distension) are listed as maternal PPH risk factors? (Select all that apply)","options":["Antepartum haemorrhage in this pregnancy","History of PPH in previous pregnancy","Precipitate labour","Excessive oxytocin use"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Antepartum haemorrhage in this pregnancy and a history of PPH in a previous pregnancy are listed maternal factors.  Why C wrong: Precipitate labour is a labour-related factor.  Why D wrong: Excessive oxytocin use is a labour-related factor."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Which of the following is a LABOUR-related risk factor for PPH?","options":["Anaemia","Placenta accreta","Excessive use of oxytocin","History of PPH in previous pregnancy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Excessive use of oxytocin is listed among labour-related factors.  Why A wrong: Anaemia is a maternal factor.  Why B wrong: Placenta accreta is a placenta-related factor.  Why D wrong: Previous PPH history is a maternal factor."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Precipitate labour, a labour-related PPH risk factor, is defined in the notes as an entire delivery process occurring within:","options":["1 hour","3 hours","6 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define precipitate labour as the entire process of delivery occurring in 3 hours.  Why A, C, D wrong: These durations do not match the notes' 3-hour definition."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Which of the following is a PLACENTA-related risk factor for PPH?","options":["Prolonged labour","Placenta accreta","Twin pregnancy","Anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Placenta accreta is listed among placenta-related factors (along with a big placenta/succenturiate lobe).  Why A wrong: Prolonged labour is labour-related.  Why C wrong: Twin pregnancy causes over-distension (maternal factor).  Why D wrong: Anaemia is a maternal factor."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"A \"big placenta\" is given as a placenta-related PPH risk factor. Which example of a big placenta is cited in the notes?","options":["Battledore placenta","Succenturiate lobe","Circumvallate placenta","Velamentous cord insertion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give a succenturiate lobe as an example of a big placenta.  Why A, C, D wrong: These placental variants are not the example cited in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"In the resuscitation (non-specific) measures for PPH, how many large IV cannulas should be secured?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify starting 2 large IV cannulas as the first resuscitation step.  Why A, C, D wrong: The notes state 2 cannulas, not 1, 3, or 4."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Which IV fluid should be started during PPH resuscitation, and which should NOT be used?","options":["Start Dextrose 5%; avoid RL/NS","Start RL/NS; do not start Dextrose 5%","Start colloid only; avoid all crystalloids","Start Dextrose 25%; avoid NS"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes say start RL/NS and specifically \"Don't start Dextrose 5%\".  Why A wrong: This reverses the correct recommendation.  Why C wrong: Crystalloids (RL/NS) are recommended, not banned.  Why D wrong: Dextrose is to be avoided, not started."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"During PPH resuscitation a blood sample is taken. Which investigations are specified in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Haemoglobin (Hb)","ABO grouping","Rh typing","Complete blood count (CBC)","Liver function tests"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Hb, ABO, Rh, and CBC as the blood sample tests.  Why E wrong: Liver function tests are not listed among the resuscitation blood samples."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"As part of non-specific/resuscitation measures in PPH, besides arranging blood, which nursing action is listed?","options":["Insert a nasogastric tube","Catheterize the bladder","Give oral fluids","Elevate the head of the bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list catheterizing the bladder as a resuscitation measure (an empty bladder aids uterine contraction).  Why A wrong: Nasogastric tube is not listed.  Why C wrong: Oral fluids are inappropriate; IV RL/NS is used.  Why D wrong: Head elevation is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"According to the notes, what is the FIRST step of the specific measures in managing PPH?","options":["Bimanual compression","Uterine massage","Hysterectomy","B-Lynch sutures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The specific-measures flow begins with \"1st step: Uterine Massage\" (plus Inj. Tranexa).  Why A wrong: Bimanual compression is a mechanical method used later.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is the last surgical resort.  Why D wrong: B-Lynch sutures are part of surgical management, done later."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"In the PPH specific-measures flow, uterine massage is given simultaneously with which injection?","options":["Inj. Tranexamic acid (Tranexa)","Inj. Diazepam","Inj. Furosemide","Inj. Magnesium sulfate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow states \"Uterine Massage + Inj. Tranexa\" as the first step.  Why B, C, D wrong: These drugs are not part of the first specific-measure step for PPH."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"In the stepwise PPH flow, medical management (uterine massage + drugs) is attempted for how long before moving to mechanical methods?","options":["10 minutes","20 minutes","30 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state medical management is tried for 30 minutes; if it fails, proceed to mechanical method.  Why A, B, D wrong: The specified duration is 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the correct escalation sequence for PPH management once specific measures begin?","options":["Surgical → Mechanical → Medical","Medical management → Mechanical method → Surgical method (last)","Mechanical → Medical → Surgical","Medical → Surgical → Mechanical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow is medical management (30 min) → if fail, mechanical method → if fails, surgical method as the last resort.  Why A, C, D wrong: These orders contradict the notes' medical → mechanical → surgical sequence."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Which drug does the WHO recommend as the FIRST-LINE drug to treat PPH?","options":["Methergine","Carboprost","Oxytocin","Misoprostol"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"WHO recommends: Oxytocin as 1st line drug.\"  Why A wrong: Methergine is a second-line drug in the notes.  Why B wrong: Carboprost is the most potent drug but not the WHO first-line choice.  Why D wrong: Misoprostol is a prostaglandin second-line option."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"If bleeding is not controlled by the first-line drug, which of the following are listed as SECOND-line drugs for PPH? (Select all that apply)","options":["Syntometrine","Methergine","Prostaglandins (Misoprost/Carboprost)","Oxytocin","Dextrose 5%"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list syntometrine, methergine, and prostaglandins (misoprost, carboprost) as second-line drugs.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is the first-line drug, not second-line.  Why E wrong: Dextrose 5% is a fluid to be avoided, not a drug."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"The two prostaglandins listed as second-line PPH drugs in the notes are:","options":["Misoprostol and Carboprost","Dinoprostone and Oxytocin","Methergine and Syntometrine","Latanoprost and Bimatoprost"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Misoprost and Carboprost under prostaglandins.  Why B wrong: Oxytocin is not a prostaglandin.  Why C wrong: These are separate second-line agents, not prostaglandins.  Why D wrong: These are ophthalmic prostaglandin analogues, not PPH drugs."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Alongside uterotonic drugs, which agent should be given \"simultaneously\" to help control PPH bleeding?","options":["Tranexamic acid","Heparin","Warfarin","Aspirin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Simultaneously give: Tranexamic acid.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: Heparin, warfarin, and aspirin are anticoagulants/antiplatelets that would worsen bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the dose of oxytocin used to treat PPH according to the drug table?","options":["10 IU","20 IU","40 IU","5 IU"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists oxytocin dose as 40 IU (IM or IV infusion).  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the 40 IU dose given."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"By which routes is oxytocin given for PPH per the notes?","options":["IV bolus only","IM or IV infusion","Sublingual only","Per rectal only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states oxytocin is given IM or as IV infusion.  Why A wrong: The notes explicitly warn \"Never give it as IV bolus.\"  Why C, D wrong: Sublingual/rectal routes are used for misoprostol, not oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Which important precaution about oxytocin administration is emphasized in the notes?","options":["It may be given as a rapid IV bolus","Never give it as an IV bolus","It must always be diluted in Dextrose 5%","It is contraindicated in PPH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state oxytocin should never be given as an IV bolus.  Why A wrong: This directly contradicts the warning.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin is given with NS or RL, not dextrose 5%.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is the WHO first-line drug for PPH."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"With which fluids should oxytocin be given, and with which should it NOT be given?","options":["Given with dextrose 5%; not with NS/RL","Given with NS or RL; not with dextrose 5%","Given with dextrose 25%; not with NS","Given only with plain water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state oxytocin is given with NS or RL, not with dextrose 5%.  Why A wrong: This reverses the recommendation.  Why C, D wrong: These fluids are not the recommended diluents."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"The notes describe oxytocin as the drug of choice / first drug recommended by WHO for which purpose(s)?","options":["To prevent PPH only","To treat PPH only","To prevent and treat PPH","To induce abortion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states oxytocin is the drug of choice/1st drug recommended by WHO to prevent AND treat PPH.  Why A, B wrong: Both prevention and treatment are covered, not just one.  Why D wrong: The context is PPH, not abortion induction."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the dose and route of methylergometrine used for PPH?","options":["0.2 mg IM","0.2 mg IV","2 mg IM","20 mg oral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists methylergometrine as 0.2 mg IM.  Why B wrong: The notes warn it must never be given IV.  Why C, D wrong: These doses/routes do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the maximum number of doses of methylergometrine that may be repeated for PPH?","options":["3 doses","5 doses","8 doses","10 doses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states a maximum of 5 doses of methylergometrine.  Why A, D wrong: These do not match the stated maximum.  Why C wrong: 8 doses is the maximum for carboprost, not methylergometrine."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the dosing interval for repeat methylergometrine injections?","options":["5–10 minutes","15–90 minutes","20–40 minutes","60–120 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the repeat interval is 20–40 minutes.  Why A, D wrong: These intervals are not given.  Why B wrong: 15–90 minutes is the carboprost interval."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Why should methylergometrine never be given intravenously?","options":["It causes hypotension","It leads to hypertension","It causes bronchospasm","It causes hypoglycaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state methylergometrine is never given IV because it leads to hypertension.  Why A wrong: It causes hypertension, not hypotension.  Why C wrong: Bronchospasm is the concern with carboprost (asthma), not IV methergine.  Why D wrong: Hypoglycaemia is not the listed adverse effect."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Syntometrine is described in the notes as a fixed-dose combination of which two drugs?","options":["Oxytocin + Methylergometrine (methergin)","Oxytocin + Misoprostol","Carboprost + Tranexamic acid","Methergine + Misoprostol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines syntometrine as a fixed-dose combination of oxytocin + methergin.  Why B, C, D wrong: These combinations are not what syntometrine contains per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"PGE1 (misoprostol) has a special advantage over ergometrine because it:","options":["Can be given in an asthmatic patient","Must be given IV bolus","Causes hypertension","Is contraindicated in PPH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state misoprost (PGE1) can be given in an asthmatic patient.  Why B wrong: Misoprostol is given sublingually/orally/rectally, not IV bolus.  Why C wrong: Hypertension is the methylergometrine concern.  Why D wrong: Misoprostol is used to prevent and treat PPH."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the dose and route of misoprostol recommended by WHO to TREAT PPH?","options":["600 mcg oral","800 mcg sublingual","1000 mcg per rectal","250 mcg IM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states 800 mcg sublingual is used to treat PPH and is recommended by WHO.  Why A wrong: 600 mcg oral is used to PREVENT PPH (AMTSL).  Why C wrong: 1000 mcg per rectal is for treating PPH but is NOT recommended by WHO.  Why D wrong: 250 mcg IM is the carboprost dose."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What dose and route of misoprostol is used to PREVENT PPH (active management of third stage of labour, AMTSL)?","options":["800 mcg sublingual","600 mcg oral","1000 mcg rectal","40 IU IV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists 600 mcg oral (route: oral) to prevent PPH as part of AMTSL.  Why A wrong: 800 mcg sublingual is the treatment dose.  Why C wrong: 1000 mcg rectal is a treatment dose not recommended by WHO.  Why D wrong: 40 IU IV is the oxytocin dose."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"For treating PPH, the notes state misoprostol can be given per rectally at what dose, which is NOT recommended by WHO?","options":["250 mcg","600 mcg","800 mcg","1000 mcg"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state per-rectal misoprostol 1000 mcg can be used for treating PPH but is not recommended by WHO.  Why A wrong: 250 mcg is the carboprost dose.  Why B wrong: 600 mcg oral is the prevention dose.  Why C wrong: 800 mcg sublingual is the WHO-recommended treatment dose."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"In the drug table, PGF2α is represented by which drug (also called Dinoprost or Hemabate)?","options":["Misoprostol","Carboprost","Oxytocin","Methylergometrine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PGF2α = Carboprost (also called Dinoprost or Hemabate) per the notes.  Why A wrong: Misoprostol is PGE1.  Why C, D wrong: These are not prostaglandins."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the dose and route of carboprost (PGF2α) for PPH?","options":["250 mcg IM","800 mcg sublingual","40 IU IV","0.2 mg IM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives carboprost 250 mcg IM (total up to 2 mg).  Why B wrong: 800 mcg sublingual is misoprostol.  Why C wrong: 40 IU IV is oxytocin.  Why D wrong: 0.2 mg IM is methylergometrine."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the repeat interval and maximum number of doses for carboprost injections?","options":["Every 20–40 minutes, max 5 doses","Every 15–90 minutes, max 8 doses","Every 60 minutes, max 3 doses","Every 5 minutes, max 10 doses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states carboprost is repeated every 15–90 minutes to a maximum of 8 doses (total 2 mg).  Why A wrong: 20–40 min/5 doses is methylergometrine.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"According to the notes, which drug is described as the \"most potent drug / best drug to treat PPH\"?","options":["Oxytocin","Methylergometrine","Carboprost","Tranexamic acid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Important Points\" column labels carboprost as the most potent/best drug to treat PPH.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin is the WHO first-line drug, but carboprost is labelled the most potent.  Why B wrong: Methylergometrine is a second-line ergot.  Why D wrong: Tranexamic acid is not a specific PPH drug."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the dose and route of tranexamic acid used in PPH per the notes?","options":["1 gm IV","250 mcg IM","40 IU IV","0.2 mg IM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists tranexamic acid as 1 gm IV.  Why B wrong: 250 mcg IM is carboprost.  Why C wrong: 40 IU IV is oxytocin.  Why D wrong: 0.2 mg IM is methylergometrine."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"How does the notes characterize tranexamic acid in relation to PPH?","options":["It is the specific first-line drug for PPH","It is not a specific drug for PPH; it is used for controlling any kind of bleeding","It is a uterotonic that contracts the uterus","It is contraindicated in obstetric haemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state tranexamic acid is not a specific PPH drug and is used to control any kind of bleeding (antifibrinolytic).  Why A wrong: Oxytocin, not tranexamic acid, is the first-line PPH drug.  Why C wrong: It is an antifibrinolytic, not a uterotonic.  Why D wrong: It is used adjunctively in PPH, not contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"After 30 minutes of medical management fails to control PPH bleeding, what is the AIM of the mechanical method?","options":["To dilate the cervix","To press the uterus so that bleeding stops","To remove the uterus","To transfuse blood faster"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the aim of the mechanical method is to press the uterus so bleeding stops.  Why A wrong: Cervical dilation is not the goal.  Why C wrong: Uterus removal (hysterectomy) is the surgical last resort.  Why D wrong: Transfusion is part of resuscitation, not the mechanical method's aim."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"The notes describe how many \"ways\" of performing the mechanical method for PPH?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"2 ways\": (1) bimanual compression and (2) balloon tamponade (Bakri/Sengstaken-Blakemore).  Why A, C, D wrong: The notes specify 2 ways."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"In bimanual compression of the uterus for PPH, where are the two hands placed?","options":["Both hands per abdominally","One hand on the uterus per abdominally and the other hand inside the uterus (vagina)","Both hands inside the vagina","One hand on the fundus and the other on the cervix externally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state one hand is on the uterus per abdominally and the other hand is inside the uterus, then the uterus is pressed between the two hands.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the described bimanual technique."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"The second mechanical method for PPH uses a Sengstaken-Blakemore oesophageal tube or which specific catheter?","options":["Foley catheter","Bakri balloon catheter","Malecot catheter","Condom catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify use of a Sengstaken-Blakemore oesophageal tube or a Bakri balloon catheter, placed transvaginally.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the devices named in the notes for this step."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"By which route is the Bakri balloon catheter placed for PPH?","options":["Transabdominal","Transvaginal","Transcervical laparoscopic","Percutaneous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the Bakri balloon catheter placement as \"Transvaginal placement.\"  Why A, C, D wrong: These routes are not described for the Bakri balloon."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"With what and how much fluid is the Bakri balloon bulb inflated inside the uterus?","options":["100–200 ml of cold saline","300–500 ml of warm normal saline","1000 ml of dextrose","50 ml of air"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the bulb is inflated with 300–500 ml of warm normal saline once inside the uterus.  Why A, C, D wrong: These volumes/fluids do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"How does the inflated Bakri balloon stop PPH bleeding?","options":["By sealing the cervix chemically","The bulb presses the uterus, so bleeding can stop (tamponade)","By absorbing the blood","By constricting the ovarian arteries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the bulb presses the uterus, and bleeding can stop (uterine tamponade).  Why A, C, D wrong: These mechanisms are not described."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the maximum capacity of the Bakri balloon catheter (Q1 highlighted point)?","options":["300 mL","500 mL","800 mL","1000 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The highlighted point states the maximum capacity of the Bakri balloon catheter = 500 mL.  Why A wrong: 300 mL is the lower end of the inflation range, not the maximum.  Why C, D wrong: These exceed the stated maximum capacity."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"If bleeding does not stop despite mechanical methods, the next step is surgical management. This choice DEPENDS mainly on:","options":["Maternal age only","Whether the patient desires future pregnancy (family completion)","The mother's blood group","The size of the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state surgical management depends on whether the patient wants future pregnancy vs. has a complete family.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the deciding factors described."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"For a PPH patient who WANTS future pregnancy, which uterus-preserving surgical procedure is applied?","options":["Hysterectomy","B-Lynch sutures (B-Lynch procedure)","Salpingectomy","Oophorectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if the patient wants future pregnancy, apply stitches on the uterus — B-Lynch sutures (B-Lynch procedure).  Why A wrong: Hysterectomy removes the uterus, ending fertility; used when family is complete.  Why C, D wrong: These remove tubes/ovaries and are not the described uterus-preserving step."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"For a PPH patient whose family is complete, the surgical management is to:","options":["Apply B-Lynch sutures","Remove the uterus (hysterectomy)","Insert a Bakri balloon","Give more oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if the patient's family is complete, remove the uterus (hysterectomy).  Why A wrong: B-Lynch sutures preserve the uterus, used when future pregnancy is desired.  Why C wrong: Bakri balloon is a mechanical, not surgical, step and is tried before surgery.  Why D wrong: More oxytocin is medical management, already tried."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"B-Lynch sutures for PPH are applied along the:","options":["Cervix only","Entire length of the uterus","Fallopian tubes","Vaginal wall"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state B-Lynch sutures are applied along the entire length of the uterus (compression suture).  Why A, C, D wrong: These sites are not where B-Lynch sutures are placed."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"If bleeding continues after B-Lynch sutures, the next surgical step is to stop the blood supply of the uterus by ligating which vessels first? (Select all that apply)","options":["Uterine artery","Ovarian artery","Femoral artery","Carotid artery"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list ligation of the uterine artery and the ovarian artery to stop uterine blood supply.  Why C, D wrong: The femoral and carotid arteries do not supply the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"The uterine artery is described in the notes as a branch of the anterior division of which artery, ligation of which is the next step if bleeding persists?","options":["External iliac artery","Internal iliac artery","Common iliac artery","Abdominal aorta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterine artery is a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery; ligate the anterior division of the internal iliac artery.  Why A, C, D wrong: The uterine artery arises from the internal iliac, not these vessels."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"After arterial ligation, if the uterus still bleeds, what is the LAST resort for PPH?","options":["B-Lynch sutures","Bimanual compression","Hysterectomy","Bakri balloon"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state hysterectomy is the last resort if bleeding persists after arterial ligation.  Why A, B, D wrong: These are earlier steps in the escalation, not the last resort."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"A woman who sustained a perineal tear during delivery develops PPH. According to the case in the notes, the management is to:","options":["Give more oxytocin","Repair the tear","Perform hysterectomy","Apply a Bakri balloon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The case states a perineal tear causing PPH is managed by repairing the tear (traumatic PPH from genital tract laceration).  Why A wrong: Uterotonics treat atonic PPH, not a bleeding perineal laceration.  Why C, D wrong: These target the uterus, not the perineal tear."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"How is a perineal tear defined in the notes?","options":["Tearing of the cervix during labour","Tearing of the perineum during delivery","Rupture of the uterus","Separation of the pubic symphysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define a perineal tear as tearing of the perineum during delivery.  Why A wrong: That describes a cervical tear.  Why C wrong: That is uterine rupture.  Why D wrong: That is symphysis pubis diastasis."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"A FIRST-degree perineal tear involves tearing of:","options":["Vaginal skin + mucosa only","Vaginal skin + mucosa + vaginal muscles","Anal sphincter","Rectal mucosa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a 1st-degree tear involves vaginal skin + mucosa only.  Why B wrong: Involvement of vaginal muscles indicates a 2nd-degree tear.  Why C wrong: Anal sphincter involvement indicates a 3rd-degree tear.  Why D wrong: Rectal mucosa tearing indicates a 4th-degree tear."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"A SECOND-degree perineal tear additionally involves which structure compared to a first-degree tear?","options":["External anal sphincter","Vaginal muscles","Rectal mucosa","Internal anal sphincter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a 2nd-degree tear involves vaginal skin + vaginal mucosa + vaginal muscles.  Why A, D wrong: Anal sphincter involvement is 3rd degree.  Why C wrong: Rectal mucosa involvement is 4th degree."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"According to the notes, a THIRD-degree perineal tear involves vaginal skin, mucosa, vaginal muscles, and additionally:","options":["Only the vaginal skin","External or internal anal sphincter (or both) torn","Rectal mucosa torn","The cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a 3rd-degree tear extends to the external or internal anal sphincter (or both).  Why A wrong: Skin alone is 1st degree.  Why C wrong: Rectal mucosa tearing is the additional feature of 4th degree.  Why D wrong: The cervix is not part of the perineal-tear grading."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What distinguishes a FOURTH-degree perineal tear from a third-degree tear in the notes?","options":["Involvement of vaginal muscles","Involvement of the anal sphincter","The rectal mucosa is also torn","Involvement of vaginal skin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 4th-degree tear adds tearing of the rectal mucosa on top of skin/muscles/anal sphincter involvement.  Why A, B, D wrong: These features are already present in lower-grade tears; rectal mucosa involvement is what makes it 4th degree."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Which grades of perineal tear are collectively called a \"complete perineal tear\"?","options":["1st and 2nd degree","2nd and 3rd degree","3rd and 4th degree","1st degree only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 3rd-degree and 4th-degree perineal tears are called complete perineal tears (anal sphincter involvement).  Why A, B, D wrong: These combinations are not termed \"complete perineal tear.\""},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Where and under what anaesthesia are 1st- and 2nd-degree perineal tears repaired?","options":["In the operation theatre under general anaesthesia","In the labour room under local anaesthesia","In the ward without anaesthesia","In the ICU under spinal anaesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 1st- and 2nd-degree tears are repaired in the labour room under local anaesthesia.  Why A wrong: OT/general anaesthesia is for 3rd- and 4th-degree tears.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Where and under what anaesthesia are 3rd- and 4th-degree perineal tears repaired?","options":["In the labour room under local anaesthesia","In the operation theatre under general anaesthesia","At the bedside without anaesthesia","In the OPD under local anaesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 3rd- and 4th-degree tears are repaired in the operation theatre under general anaesthesia.  Why A wrong: Labour room/local anaesthesia is for 1st- and 2nd-degree tears.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"At the time of delivery of the fetal head, which measures are recommended to PREVENT a perineal tear? (Select all that apply)","options":["Give perineal support","Deliver the head slowly by maintaining flexion","Warm compress at the perineum","Routine episiotomy for all","Rapid forceful delivery of the head"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list perineal support, slow delivery of the head with maintained flexion, and warm compress at the perineum as ways to prevent perineal tears.  Why D wrong: WHO does not recommend routine episiotomy to prevent tears.  Why E wrong: Rapid forceful delivery increases tear risk; slow controlled delivery is advised."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Which two perineal-tear prevention measures are specifically highlighted as \"Recommended by WHO\"?","options":["Give perineal support and deliver the head slowly by maintaining flexion","Routine episiotomy and forceps use","Warm compress and routine episiotomy","Rapid delivery and fundal pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes bracket perineal support and slow delivery of the head (maintaining flexion) as \"Recommended by WHO.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These include measures not endorsed (routine episiotomy, forceps, fundal pressure) by the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the WHO's stance on routine episiotomy for preventing perineal tears, per the \"Remember\" note?","options":["WHO recommends routine episiotomy for all deliveries","WHO does NOT recommend routine episiotomy to prevent perineal tears","WHO recommends episiotomy only in twins","WHO has no opinion on episiotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Remember\" note states WHO does not recommend routine episiotomy to prevent perineal tears.  Why A wrong: This is the opposite of the WHO stance.  Why C, D wrong: These are not stated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Puerperium is defined as the period following childbirth up to how long after delivery?","options":["2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define puerperium as the period following childbirth up until 6 weeks after delivery.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the 6-week definition."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"In the classification of puerperium, the \"immediate\" period is defined as:","options":["Within 24 hours after delivery","24 hours to 1 week after delivery","1 week to 6 weeks after delivery","6 to 12 weeks after delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes classify the immediate puerperium as within 24 hours of delivery.  Why B wrong: 24 hours to 1 week is the \"early\" period.  Why C wrong: 1 week to 6 weeks is the \"late\" period.  Why D wrong: This falls outside the puerperium."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"The \"early\" puerperium period is defined in the notes as:","options":["Within 24 hours after delivery","24 hours to 1 week after delivery","1 week to 6 weeks after delivery","6 weeks to 3 months after delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The early puerperium is from 24 hours to 1 week after delivery.  Why A wrong: That is the immediate period.  Why C wrong: That is the late period.  Why D wrong: That is beyond the puerperium."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"The \"late\" puerperium period is defined in the notes as:","options":["Within 24 hours after delivery","24 hours to 1 week after delivery","1 week to 6 weeks after delivery","Beyond 6 weeks after delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The late puerperium is from 1 week to 6 weeks after delivery.  Why A wrong: That is the immediate period.  Why B wrong: That is the early period.  Why D wrong: That falls outside the puerperium."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Immediately after delivery, at what level is the fundus of the uterus positioned?","options":["At the level of the xiphisternum","At the level of the umbilicus (about 13 cm above the pubic symphysis)","Below the pubic symphysis","At mid-thigh level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus is at the level of the umbilicus, i.e. about 13 cm above the pubic symphysis, immediately after delivery.  Why A, C, D wrong: These positions do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Immediately after delivery, the size of the uterus corresponds to that of how many weeks of pregnancy?","options":["12 weeks","16 weeks","22 weeks","28 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that immediately after delivery the uterus is the size of a 22-week pregnancy.  Why A, B, D wrong: These weeks do not match the notes' value of 22 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What is the weight of the uterus immediately after delivery?","options":["500 gm","1 kg","300 gm","80 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus weighs 1 kg immediately after delivery.  Why A wrong: 500 gm is the weight after 1 week.  Why C wrong: 300 gm is the weight at 2 weeks.  Why D wrong: 80 gm is the weight at the end of the 6th week."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"During involution, what is the approximate weight of the uterus after 1 week?","options":["1 kg","500 gm","300 gm","100 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus weighs 500 gm after 1 week.  Why A wrong: 1 kg is the immediate post-delivery weight.  Why C wrong: 300 gm is the 2-week weight.  Why D wrong: 100 gm is the 3–5 week weight."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"During uterine involution, what is the approximate weight of the uterus at 2 weeks?","options":["500 gm","300 gm","100 gm","80 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus weighs 300 gm at 2 weeks.  Why A wrong: 500 gm is the 1-week weight.  Why C wrong: 100 gm is the 3–5 week weight.  Why D wrong: 80 gm is the end-of-6th-week weight."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"During involution, the uterus weighs approximately 100 gm at which time?","options":["Immediately after delivery","1 week","3–5 weeks","End of 6th week"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus weighs 100 gm at 3–5 weeks.  Why A wrong: Immediately after delivery it is 1 kg.  Why B wrong: At 1 week it is 500 gm.  Why D wrong: At the end of the 6th week it is 80 gm."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"By the end of the 6th week (completed involution), what is the approximate weight of the uterus?","options":["300 gm","100 gm","80 gm","500 gm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus weighs 80 gm at the end of the 6th week.  Why A wrong: 300 gm is the 2-week weight.  Why B wrong: 100 gm is the 3–5 week weight.  Why D wrong: 500 gm is the 1-week weight."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"During involution, at what rate does the height of the uterus (fundal height) descend?","options":["1.25 cm/day","2.5 cm/day","1 cm/week","5 cm/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fundal height descends by 1.25 cm/day.  Why B, C, D wrong: These rates do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"After how many days does the uterus lie at the level of the pubic symphysis and become no longer palpable per abdominally?","options":["3–5 days","6–8 days","10–12 days","20–22 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that after 10–12 days the uterus lies at the level of the pubic symphysis and is not palpable per abdominally.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the notes' 10–12 days."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"By what time does the uterus return to its original (pre-pregnancy) position/size after delivery?","options":["2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterus returns to its original position by 6 weeks.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the 6-week timeframe."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"When involution of the uterus does not occur at the normal rate and is slower than expected, the condition is called:","options":["Subinvolution","Hyperinvolution","Prolapse","Retroversion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if involution is slower than the normal rate, it is called subinvolution.  Why B wrong: \"Hyperinvolution\" is not the described condition.  Why C wrong: Prolapse is descent of the uterus, a different entity.  Why D wrong: Retroversion is an abnormal position, not delayed involution."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"What are the TWO most common causes of subinvolution of the uterus? (Select all that apply)","options":["Retained placental tissue in the uterus","Infection","Twin pregnancy","Anaemia"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list retained placental tissue and infection as the two most common causes of subinvolution.  Why C, D wrong: Twins and anaemia are PPH risk factors, not the listed causes of subinvolution."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"For subinvolution due to retained placental tissue, what is the recommended management per the notes?","options":["Give antibiotics only","Do an ultrasound (USG), then gentle curettage to remove the tissue","Immediate hysterectomy","Bed rest only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state management for retained placental tissue is to do an USG and then gentle curettage to remove it.  Why A wrong: Antibiotics are the management for the infection cause, not retained tissue.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not the described step for retained tissue.  Why D wrong: Bed rest alone would not remove retained tissue."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"For subinvolution caused by infection, the management is:","options":["Curettage","Antibiotics","Hysterectomy","Balloon tamponade"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state infection-related subinvolution is managed with antibiotics.  Why A wrong: Curettage is for retained placental tissue.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the described management for infective subinvolution."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"According to the note, curettage performed in the postpartum period can lead to which complication(s)?","options":["Asherman syndrome (intrauterine adhesions)","Placenta praevia","Ectopic pregnancy","Hydatidiform mole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes warn that postpartum curettage can lead to Asherman syndrome / intrauterine adhesions.  Why B, C, D wrong: These conditions are not the complication described for postpartum curettage."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"Asherman syndrome, a complication of postpartum curettage, refers to:","options":["Intrauterine adhesions","Uterine prolapse","Ovarian cysts","Cervical incompetence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes equate Asherman syndrome with intrauterine adhesions.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are unrelated to the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"If a woman develops uterine prolapse after delivery, the important teaching point states surgery should NOT be done until:","options":["The next menstrual period","The uterus returns to its original size and position (till 6 weeks after delivery)","Breastfeeding is stopped","6 months have passed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that for postpartum prolapse, do not do any surgery until the uterus returns to its original size and position, i.e. till 6 weeks after delivery.  Why A, C, D wrong: These timeframes are not stated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"A postpartum woman is found to have a poorly contracted, boggy uterus that is larger and higher than expected for her postpartum day, with delayed descent. This clinical picture most likely represents:","options":["Normal involution","Subinvolution of the uterus","Complete perineal tear","Hyperinvolution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delayed/slower-than-normal involution (uterus larger/higher than expected) defines subinvolution per the notes.  Why A wrong: Normal involution shows the expected 1.25 cm/day descent and weight loss.  Why C wrong: A perineal tear is a genital tract laceration, not delayed uterine involution.  Why D wrong: \"Hyperinvolution\" is not the described entity."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"A woman with PPH is asthmatic. Which uterotonic listed in the notes is preferred because it can be safely given to asthmatic patients?","options":["Carboprost (PGF2α)","Misoprostol (PGE1)","Methylergometrine","Ergometrine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specifically note misoprostol (PGE1) can be given in an asthmatic patient.  Why A wrong: Carboprost (PGF2α) can cause bronchospasm and is best avoided in asthmatics.  Why C, D wrong: These are ergot alkaloids, not the agent flagged as asthma-safe in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 070 · Postpartum haemorrhage risk factors, PPH management…","question":"A postpartum patient with PPH has failed 30 minutes of medical management and bimanual compression. Family is complete and no further children desired; B-Lynch and arterial ligation have failed. What is the most appropriate final intervention?","options":["Repeat oxytocin infusion","Insert a Bakri balloon","Hysterectomy","Warm compress"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the escalation, hysterectomy is the last resort when all prior mechanical and surgical steps fail.  Why A wrong: Medical management has already failed.  Why B wrong: The mechanical balloon step has been surpassed.  Why D wrong: Warm compress relates to perineal-tear prevention, not refractory PPH."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the management of choice for uterine prolapse occurring in the puerperium?","options":["Vaginal hysterectomy","Ring pessary","Manchester repair","Sacrospinous fixation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the management of prolapse in puerperium is a ring pessary, a conservative supportive device appropriate in the postpartum period.  Why A wrong: Hysterectomy is not the puerperal management and is definitive surgery, not first-line here.  Why C wrong: Manchester repair is a surgical procedure, not the puerperal management indicated.  Why D wrong: Sacrospinous fixation is a surgical suspension, not the puerperal management indicated."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to the notes, prolapse of the uterus seen after menopause is chiefly attributed to which mechanism?","options":["Increased estrogen","Weakening of supports of uterus","Uterine fibroids","Ovarian cysts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes attribute prolapse in the puerperium/after menopause to weakening of the supports of the uterus (after menopause and due to birth trauma).  Why A wrong: Estrogen falls after menopause; it is not the cause given.  Why C wrong: Fibroids are not listed as the cause of prolapse here.  Why D wrong: Ovarian cysts are unrelated to the support-weakening mechanism described."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"The notes list two contributing causes for weakening of uterine supports leading to prolapse. Which pair is correct?","options":["After menopause and due to birth trauma","Obesity and diabetes","Smoking and hypertension","Anemia and malnutrition"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows weakening of supports occurs \"after menopause\" and \"due to birth trauma.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These factors are not the two causes given in the notes for support weakening."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is described as the best way to prevent uterine prolapse?","options":["Bed rest","Perineal (Kegel's) exercises","High-fiber diet","Ring pessary insertion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best way to prevent prolapse is perineal exercises (Kegel's exercise), which strengthen the pelvic floor.  Why A wrong: Bed rest is not the preventive measure listed.  Why C wrong: A high-fiber diet helps constipation, not prevention of prolapse per the notes.  Why D wrong: A ring pessary is a management/treatment device, not the prevention method described."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"When a nurse/doctor palpates the puerperal uterus and it feels \"soft and boggy,\" what is the immediate action indicated?","options":["Administer antibiotics","Do a uterine massage","Give a blood transfusion","Insert a ring pessary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when the uterus feels soft and boggy, the action is to do a uterine massage (and any clot is passed out).  Why A wrong: Antibiotics treat infection, not a boggy (atonic) uterus.  Why C wrong: Transfusion is not the indicated step for a boggy uterus in these notes.  Why D wrong: A ring pessary treats prolapse, not uterine atony."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"In assessing the puerperal uterus, the nurse/doctor is directed to first check which of the following?","options":["Fundal height of the uterus","Cervical dilatation","Blood pressure only","Fetal heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list checking fundal height of the uterus and palpating the uterus as the nurse/doctor's steps.  Why B wrong: Cervical dilatation is an intrapartum assessment, not the puerperal fundal check listed.  Why C wrong: BP is not the specific uterine assessment named here.  Why D wrong: Fetal heart rate is irrelevant after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"The endometrium (decidua) is described as having two layers. Which layer sheds after delivery and forms the lochia?","options":["Deep layer","Superficial layer","Myometrial layer","Serosal layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The superficial layer sheds after delivery as progesterone falls, support is lost, and shedding of decidua produces lochia.  Why A wrong: The deep layer regenerates the new endometrium; it does not shed as lochia.  Why C wrong: Myometrium is muscle, not the shed decidual layer.  Why D wrong: The serosa is the outer covering, not the shed decidua."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Per the endometrial diagram, shedding of the superficial decidual layer occurs because of which hormonal change after delivery?","options":["Progesterone decreases","Estrogen increases","Oxytocin decreases","Prolactin increases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show shedding occurs \"as progesterone ↓, so support is lost.\"  Why B wrong: An estrogen rise is not the trigger described.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin change is not cited for shedding of the superficial layer.  Why D wrong: Prolactin governs lactation, not decidual shedding here."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to the notes, regeneration of the deep layer of the endometrium starts on which postpartum day?","options":["Day 3","Day 7","Day 10","Day 16"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the deep layer \"Starts: 7 days\" to regenerate.  Why A wrong: Day 3 is not the stated start.  Why C wrong: Day 10 is when regeneration is completed, not started.  Why D wrong: Day 16 is when the entire endometrium is restored."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"By which postpartum day is regeneration of the deep endometrial layer said to be completed?","options":["7 days","10 days","16 days","6 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state regeneration is \"Completed: 10 days.\"  Why A wrong: 7 days is when regeneration starts.  Why C wrong: 16 days is when the entire endometrium is restored.  Why D wrong: 6 weeks is when the placental site regenerates."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to the notes, the entire endometrium is restored by how many days postpartum?","options":["10 days","14 days","16 days","21 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Entire endometrium is restored = 16 days.\"  Why A wrong: 10 days is completion of deep-layer regeneration, not full restoration.  Why B/D wrong: 14 and 21 days are not the value given for full endometrial restoration."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"The entire endometrium is restored by 16 days EXCEPT at which site?","options":["The internal cervical os","The place where the placenta was attached","The fundus of the uterus","The cornua"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state restoration occurs \"Except at place where Placenta was attached.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These sites are not the exception named; the placental site is the exception."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"The placental attachment site of the endometrium regenerates by which time after delivery?","options":["10 days","16 days","3 weeks","6 weeks"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the placental site \"Regenerate by 6 weeks after delivery.\"  Why A/B wrong: 10 and 16 days apply to the rest of the endometrium, not the placental site.  Why C wrong: 3 weeks is not the placental-site value."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to the notes, which is the most common site of postpartum infection in the uterus?","options":["Superficial decidual layer","Placental attachment site (deep layer)","Cervix","Vaginal wall"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the deep layer / placental site as the \"Most common site of infection.\"  Why A wrong: The superficial layer sheds as lochia; the placental site is the infection site.  Why C/D wrong: Cervix and vagina are routes of spread, not the most common site named."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Lochia is best defined as which of the following?","options":["Postpartum shedding of decidua","Amniotic fluid leakage","Menstrual bleeding after delivery","Cervical mucus plug"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define lochia as postpartum shedding of decidua.  Why B wrong: Amniotic fluid is not lochia.  Why C wrong: Lochia is not a menstrual period.  Why D wrong: The mucus plug (operculum) is not lochia."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to Dutta, physiological lochia is normally seen for how many days?","options":["15–21 days","24–38 days","10 days","6 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state lochia is seen for 15–21 days per Dutta.  Why B wrong: 24–38 days is the range given by Williams, not Dutta.  Why C/D wrong: 10 days and 6 weeks are not the Dutta range."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to Williams, physiological lochia may be seen for how many days?","options":["15–21 days","24–38 days","10–14 days","40–45 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes cite Williams as 24–38 days.  Why A wrong: 15–21 days is the Dutta figure.  Why C/D wrong: These ranges are not given for Williams."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the normal pH of physiological lochia?","options":["Acidic","Neutral","Alkaline","Strongly acidic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state lochia has an alkaline pH.  Why A/D wrong: Lochia is alkaline, not acidic.  Why B wrong: It is specifically alkaline, not neutral."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"An offensive/foul-smelling lochia is a warning sign that may indicate which condition?","options":["Puerperal sepsis","Normal involution","Breast engorgement","Constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show offensive/foul-smelling lochia points toward puerperal sepsis.  Why B wrong: Normal lochia is not foul-smelling; foul smell is abnormal.  Why C/D wrong: Engorgement and constipation are unrelated to foul lochia."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"In the sequence of lochia, lochia rubra (red) is seen until which day and consists of what?","options":["Until 4th day; blood + products of conception","Until 10th day; pus cells","From day 10 onward; epithelial cells","Until 14th day; serum only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state lochia rubra is red, seen until the 4th day, and is blood + product of conception.  Why B wrong: That describes lochia serosa (until 10th day, pus cells).  Why C wrong: That describes lochia alba (≥10 days, epithelial cells).  Why D wrong: Serum-only until 14 days is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Lochia serosa is described as which color, seen until which day, and containing what?","options":["Red, until 4th day, blood","Yellow, until 10th day, pus cells","White, ≥10 days, epithelial cells","Brown, until 6 weeks, mucus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state lochia serosa is yellow, seen until the 10th day, containing pus cells.  Why A wrong: That is lochia rubra.  Why C wrong: That is lochia alba.  Why D wrong: Not described in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Lochia alba is characterized by which color, timing, and cell content?","options":["Red, 4th day, blood","Yellow, 10th day, pus cells","White, ≥10 days, epithelial cells","Green, 3 weeks, bacteria"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state lochia alba is white, seen ≥10 days, containing epithelial cells.  Why A wrong: That is lochia rubra.  Why B wrong: That is lochia serosa.  Why D wrong: Not described in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the correct chronological sequence of lochia after delivery?","options":["Alba → serosa → rubra","Rubra → serosa → alba","Serosa → rubra → alba","Rubra → alba → serosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the sequence: lochia rubra → lochia serosa → lochia alba.  Why A/C/D wrong: These orders do not match the rubra-serosa-alba progression in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Persistent red bleeding (lochia rubra) continuing beyond the normal period is significant because it indicates which of the following?","options":["Subinvolution of the uterus","Normal healing","Breast engorgement","Constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state persistent red bleeding beyond normal continues to indicate subinvolution.  Why B wrong: Persistent red bleeding is abnormal, not normal healing.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to persistent lochia rubra."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of MORE (increased) amount of lochia? (Select all that apply)","options":["After cesarean","Twin delivery","Hydramnios","Following preterm labor","Foul smell from infection/retained cotton plug"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list increased lochia after cesarean, twin delivery, hydramnios, and with foul smell from infection/retained cotton plug.  Why D wrong: \"Following preterm labor\" is listed under SCANTY (reduced) lochia, not more."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Scanty lochia is associated with which of the following per the notes?","options":["Cesarean delivery","Following preterm labor","Twin delivery","Hydramnios"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list scanty lochia \"following preterm labor.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Cesarean, twin delivery, and hydramnios are all listed under MORE (increased) lochia."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"A foul smell in the lochia along with increased amount most likely indicates which two possibilities per the notes?","options":["Infection or retained cotton plug","Dehydration or anemia","Constipation or urinary retention","Breast engorgement or mastitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list foul smell as due to infection or a retained cotton plug.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not linked to foul-smelling lochia in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"When assessing lochia by checking the female's pad, a stain of less than 1 inch is graded as which amount?","options":["Scanty","Light","Moderate","Heavy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pad chart shows a stain <1 inch = scanty.  Why B wrong: <4 inch = light.  Why C wrong: <6 inch = moderate.  Why D wrong: Heavy = saturated in 1 hour."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"A lochia stain of less than 4 inches on the pad is graded as which of the following?","options":["Scanty","Light","Moderate","Excessive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pad chart shows <4 inch = light.  Why A wrong: <1 inch = scanty.  Why C wrong: <6 inch = moderate.  Why D wrong: Excessive = pad saturated in 15 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"A lochia stain of less than 6 inches on the pad corresponds to which grading?","options":["Scanty","Light","Moderate","Heavy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The pad chart shows <6 inch = moderate.  Why A/B wrong: These are smaller stains (scanty/light).  Why D wrong: Heavy = saturation within 1 hour."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to the pad-assessment chart, bleeding that saturates the pad within 1 hour is classified as which amount?","options":["Moderate","Heavy","Excessive","Light"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows a pad saturated \"in 1 hour\" = heavy.  Why A/D wrong: These are lesser amounts based on stain size.  Why C wrong: Excessive = pad saturated in 15 minutes, faster than heavy."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Per the pad-assessment chart, saturation of a pad within 15 minutes is graded as which of the following?","options":["Heavy","Moderate","Excessive","Scanty"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows a pad saturated \"in 15 minutes\" = excessive.  Why A wrong: Heavy is saturation in 1 hour, not 15 minutes.  Why B/D wrong: These are much lesser amounts."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the recommended method to assess the amount of lochia in a postpartum woman?","options":["Weigh the woman daily","Check the pad of the female","Measure abdominal girth","Perform an ultrasound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"How to assess lochia? Check pad of female\" using stain size/saturation time.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the pad-based assessment method described."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"After delivery, how long does the cervix take to regain its normal shape and size?","options":["1 week","3 weeks","6 weeks","10 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cervix takes 6 weeks to regain normal shape and size.  Why A wrong: 1 week is when 1 finger can be admitted through the internal os.  Why B/D wrong: 3 weeks and 10 days are not the cervical value."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"After how long postpartum can one finger be admitted through the internal cervical os?","options":["After 24 hours","After 1 week of delivery","After 3 weeks","After 6 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"After 1 week of delivery: 1 finger can be admitted through internal os.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These timings are not the ones given for admitting one finger."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Regarding the external cervical os after childbirth, which statement is correct?","options":["It returns fully to the pre-pregnant state within 6 weeks","It never comes back to the pre-pregnant state","It closes completely within 24 hours","It becomes pinpoint again after delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the external os never comes back to the pre-pregnant state.  Why A/C wrong: These contradict the \"never returns\" point.  Why D wrong: A pinpoint/circular os describes the nulliparous (pre-pregnancy) shape, which is not regained."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"The external cervical os of a nulliparous woman typically has which shape?","options":["Transverse (slit-like)","Pinpoint or circular","Star-shaped","Triangular"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the nulliparous os is pinpoint or circular.  Why A wrong: A transverse (slit) os is the multiparous shape.  Why C/D wrong: Star-shaped and triangular are not the shapes given."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"The external cervical os of a multiparous woman characteristically appears as which shape?","options":["Pinpoint","Circular","Transverse (slit-like)","Y-shaped"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show the multiparous os is transverse (a slit).  Why A/B wrong: Pinpoint/circular describes the nulliparous os.  Why D wrong: Y-shaped is not the described multiparous appearance."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"After delivery, vaginal rugosities (rugae) reappear by approximately what time?","options":["1 week","3 weeks","6 weeks","10 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Rugosities appear = 3 weeks.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These times are not the value given for rugae reappearance."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Which statement about the vagina after delivery is correct per the notes?","options":["Elasticity fully returns to normal after delivery","Elasticity never comes to 'normal' after delivery, so chances of vaginal prolapse increase","Rugae disappear permanently","The vagina shortens permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state vaginal elasticity never returns to normal after delivery, so chances of vaginal prolapse increase.  Why A wrong: Elasticity does not fully return; that is the opposite of the note.  Why C wrong: Rugae reappear at 3 weeks; they do not disappear permanently.  Why D wrong: Permanent shortening is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"In the normal puerperium, body temperature may rise slightly by how much during the first 24 hours after delivery?","options":["0.5°F","1.0°F","2°F","1.5°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state temperature rises slightly by 0.5°F for the first 24 hours after delivery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values do not match the 0.5°F stated."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"The slight rise in temperature during the first 24 hours postpartum is primarily attributed to which cause?","options":["Dehydrating effects of labor","Bacterial infection","Breast engorgement","Anesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes attribute the rise to the dehydrating effects of labor.  Why B wrong: Infection causes pyrexia after 24 hours, not the physiological first-day rise.  Why C wrong: Engorgement causes fever by the 4th day, not this initial rise.  Why D wrong: Anesthesia is not cited as the cause."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the recommended management for the mild temperature rise in the early puerperium?","options":["Broad-spectrum antibiotics","Increase hydration by encouraging oral fluid","Cold sponging only","Immediate blood cultures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state management is to increase hydration by encouraging oral fluid (since the rise is due to dehydration).  Why A/D wrong: Antibiotics/cultures are for infection, not the physiological dehydration rise.  Why C wrong: Cold sponging alone is not the management named."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"In the early puerperium, temperature should be monitored at what interval until the woman is fully awake?","options":["Every 2 hours","Every 4 hours","Every 6 hours","Every 8 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Monitor temperature 4 hourly till time she is awake.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals do not match the 4-hourly frequency stated."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"If a postpartum woman's temperature rises after the first 24 hours and appears around the 4th day postpartum, which cause should be considered per the notes?","options":["Breast engorgement","Normal involution","Diaphoresis","After-pains"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if temperature rises after that time, breast engorgement (by the 4th day postpartum) is a consideration.  Why B wrong: Normal involution does not cause a late fever.  Why C/D wrong: Diaphoresis and after-pains are separate puerperal changes, not causes of the 4th-day fever."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"A late postpartum temperature rise due to infection may signify which conditions per the notes?","options":["Puerperal pyrexia / puerperal sepsis","Diaphoresis","After-pains","Constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list infection causing puerperal pyrexia / puerperal sepsis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to an infective temperature rise."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"How does the pulse rate normally behave immediately after delivery per the notes?","options":["Rises slightly after delivery","Falls markedly","Remains exactly unchanged","Becomes irregular"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the pulse rate rises (↑) slightly after delivery in the normal puerperium.  Why B/C wrong: It does not fall markedly nor stay exactly unchanged.  Why D wrong: Irregularity is not described."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"A persistently rising pulse rate in the puerperium may be an important sign of which condition?","options":["Infection (perpetual pyrexia / puerperal sepsis)","Normal recovery","Breast engorgement","Constipation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state, for pulse rate, \"Infection → Perpetual pyrexia puerperal sepsis.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not explain a persistently elevated pulse per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Puerperal pyrexia is defined as a temperature of how much, occurring on how many occasions, and separated by what interval, after delivery?","options":[">38°C or 100.4°F on 2 occasions, 24 hours apart",">37°C on 1 occasion within 24 hours",">39°C on 3 occasions, 12 hours apart",">38.5°C once within 48 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define puerperal pyrexia as a temperature of >38°C or 100.4°F on 2 occasions, 24 hours apart, after delivery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These thresholds/occasions/intervals do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Puerperal pyrexia typically manifests within which time frame after delivery?","options":["First 10 days","First 24 hours only","After 6 weeks","First 3 days only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state puerperal pyrexia manifests in the first 10 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These time frames do not match the \"first 10 days\" given."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the most common cause of puerperal pyrexia?","options":["Urinary tract infection","Puerperal sepsis","Breast engorgement","Deep vein thrombosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of puerperal pyrexia is puerperal sepsis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These may cause fever but are not the most common cause given."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the most common site of puerperal sepsis (endometritis)?","options":["Cervix","Endometrium at the site of placental attachment","Vagina","Fallopian tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the M/C site is the endometrium (endometritis) at the site of placental attachment.  Why A/C wrong: Cervix and vagina are routes of spread, not the M/C site.  Why D wrong: The fallopian tube is not the most common site."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the most common causative organism of puerperal sepsis?","options":["Group A streptococci","Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus aureus","Chlamydia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the M/C cause is group A streptococci.  Why B wrong: E. coli is not the M/C organism given.  Why C wrong: Staph aureus is the M/C cause of mastitis, not puerperal sepsis.  Why D wrong: Chlamydia is the M/C organism in late-onset endometritis, not overall."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the most common mode of spread of puerperal sepsis?","options":["Hematogenous spread","Direct spread from cervix and vagina","Lymphatic spread only","Airborne"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the M/C mode of spread is direct spread from the cervix and vagina.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the mode of spread named in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Which mode of delivery is most commonly associated with puerperal sepsis and is the most common risk factor for endometritis?","options":["Normal vaginal delivery","C-section (cesarean)","Forceps delivery","Vacuum extraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the M/C mode of delivery associated with puerperal sepsis is C-section, which is the most common risk factor for endometritis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These modes are not identified as the most common association/risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the most common organism causing late-onset endometritis?","options":["Group A streptococci","Chlamydia","Staph aureus","E. coli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common organism in late-onset endometritis is Chlamydia.  Why A wrong: Group A streptococci is the M/C cause of puerperal sepsis overall.  Why C wrong: Staph aureus is the M/C cause of mastitis.  Why D wrong: E. coli is not the organism named for late-onset endometritis."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the most common cause of mastitis?","options":["Chlamydia","Group A streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus","E. coli"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cause of mastitis is Staph aureus.  Why A wrong: Chlamydia is for late-onset endometritis.  Why B wrong: Group A streptococci is for puerperal sepsis.  Why D wrong: E. coli is not the mastitis cause named."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"other risk factors\" for endometritis/puerperal sepsis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Iron deficiency anemia","Prolonged labor","PROM (premature rupture of membranes)","Bacterial vaginosis","Twin delivery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list iron deficiency anemia, prolonged labor, PROM, and bacterial vaginosis as other risk factors.  Why E wrong: Twin delivery is listed under increased lochia, not as an endometritis risk factor here."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What is the drug regimen recommended for management of puerperal sepsis?","options":["Amoxicillin alone","Clindamycin + Gentamicin","Metronidazole + Doxycycline","Ceftriaxone alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state management of puerperal sepsis is Clindamycin + Gentamicin.  Why A/C/D wrong: These regimens are not the combination named in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Which of the following are listed as signs/symptoms of endometritis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Temperature rise","Uterine tenderness","Rise in all inflammatory markers","Subinvolution of the uterus","Hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list temperature ↑, uterine tenderness, all inflammatory markers ↑, and subinvolution of the uterus as signs/symptoms of endometritis.  Why E wrong: Hypothermia is not listed; endometritis causes fever, not low temperature."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to the notes on puerperal weight changes, how much weight is lost at the time of delivery?","options":["2 kg","5 kg","10 kg","1 kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state weight loss of 5 kg at the time of delivery.  Why A wrong: 2 kg is lost during the puerperium, not at delivery.  Why C/D wrong: These values are not given."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"How much additional weight is lost during the puerperium (after delivery) per the notes?","options":["5 kg","2 kg","7 kg","0.5 kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 2 kg is lost at the time of the puerperium.  Why A wrong: 5 kg is lost at delivery.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Excessive sweating in the puerperium is also known by which term?","options":["Diaphoresis","Diuresis","Dyspnea","Diplopia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list excessive sweating and label it (Diaphoresis).  Why B wrong: Diuresis is increased urination, not sweating.  Why C wrong: Dyspnea is difficulty breathing.  Why D wrong: Diplopia is double vision."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to the notes, urinary retention in the puerperium is due to which of the following causes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pain","Laxity of the abdominal wall","Overhydration","Uterine massage"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list urinary retention as due to pain and laxity of the abdominal wall.  Why C/D wrong: Overhydration and uterine massage are not listed as causes of retention."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"To prevent postpartum urinary retention, patients should be encouraged to pass urine at what interval?","options":["Every 2–3 hours","Every 6 hours","Once a day","Only when the bladder feels full"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Encourage patient to pass urine every 2–3 hours.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals do not match the 2–3 hourly instruction."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Constipation in the puerperium is attributed to which of the following mechanisms? (Select all that apply)","options":["Atony of the intestine","Reflex inhibition","Excess fiber intake","Hyperactive bowel"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list constipation as due to atony of the intestine and reflex inhibition.  Why C/D wrong: Excess fiber and a hyperactive bowel are not listed; roughage/fluid is the remedy."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What dietary measure is recommended for postpartum constipation?","options":["Sufficient roughage and fluid","Low-fiber diet","Fluid restriction","Protein-only diet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes recommend sufficient roughage and fluid for constipation.  Why B/C wrong: Low fiber and fluid restriction would worsen constipation.  Why D wrong: A protein-only diet is not advised."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to the notes, why is something to eat commonly given to women soon after labor?","options":["Because females are hungry after labor","To prevent hypoglycemia only","To promote lactation","To reduce nausea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state females are hungry after labor, so something to eat is given.  Why B/C/D wrong: These specific reasons are not the ones stated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"\"After-pains\" in the puerperium are best described as which of the following?","options":["Spasmodic colicky pain in the lower abdomen in early postpartum days due to uterine contraction","Constant sharp perineal pain","Burning epigastric pain","Headache due to spinal anesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define after-pains as spasmodic colicky pain in the lower abdomen in early postpartum days due to uterine contraction.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the definition of after-pains given."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"After-pains are most commonly (MC) seen in which of the following situations? (Select all that apply)","options":["Multiparous women","Multiple pregnancy","Polyhydramnios","Large sized baby","Primigravida with a small baby"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list after-pains as most common in multiparous women, multiple pregnancy, polyhydramnios, and large sized baby (all cause overdistension).  Why E wrong: A primigravida with a small baby is not listed; after-pains are less prominent in primiparas."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"After-pains can be precipitated by which of the following factors?","options":["Retained placental tissue and suckling of the breast by the baby","Early ambulation","Adequate hydration","Analgesic use"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state after-pains can be precipitated by retained placental tissue and by suckling of the breast by the baby (which releases oxytocin causing contractions).  Why B/C wrong: Ambulation and hydration are not precipitants of after-pains.  Why D wrong: Analgesics relieve, not precipitate, after-pains."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Which of the following are components of the management of after-pains? (Select all that apply)","options":["Encourage frequent urine passing (a full bladder causes discomfort)","Massage of uterus to expel any clot","Analgesic / antispasmodic","Fluid restriction"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list management as encouraging frequent voiding (full bladder causes discomfort), uterine massage to expel clots, and analgesic/antispasmodic.  Why D wrong: Fluid restriction is not part of after-pain management."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Why is a postpartum woman encouraged to pass urine frequently as part of after-pain management?","options":["A full bladder can cause discomfort","To prevent dehydration","To increase milk supply","To lower blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a full bladder can cause discomfort, so frequent voiding is encouraged.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reasons are not the one given in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"In the puerperium there is an increase in fibrinogen, Factor VIII, and platelets for about 1 week after delivery. What is the clinical consequence of this?","options":["Increased risk of thrombosis","Increased risk of hemorrhage","Anemia","Hypotension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the rise in fibrinogen + Factor VIII + platelets for 1 week after delivery puts the female at increased risk of THROMBOSIS.  Why B wrong: These clotting factors increase clotting risk, not bleeding.  Why C/D wrong: Anemia and hypotension are not the consequence described."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"According to the notes, the increased levels of clotting components (fibrinogen, Factor VIII, platelets) predisposing to thrombosis persist for approximately how long after delivery?","options":["1 week","3 weeks","6 weeks","24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state these elevations last \"for 1 week after delivery.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations are not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"What general advice regarding activity is recommended for the postpartum woman to reduce complications such as thrombosis?","options":["Complete bed rest for 6 weeks","Ambulate early","Avoid all movement for 1 week","Restrict fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes advise \"Ambulate early.\"  Why A/C wrong: Prolonged immobility increases thrombosis risk; the note advises early ambulation.  Why D wrong: Fluid restriction is not the advice given."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Which of the following is an aim of postnatal assessment? (Select all that apply)","options":["General health status check","Detect gynae/obstetric problems","Guidance on self-care and baby care","Family planning method advice","Diagnose fetal anomalies"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the aims of postnatal assessment as general health status check, detecting gynae/obstetric problems, guidance on self-care and baby care, and family planning method advice.  Why E wrong: Diagnosing fetal anomalies is antenatal, not a postnatal assessment aim."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"At which of the following times is postnatal assessment carried out? (Select all that apply)","options":["First 0–24 hours of delivery","At the time of discharge","At the end of puerperium","During labor"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the three assessment times as first 0–24 hours of delivery, at the time of discharge, and at the end of the puerperium.  Why D wrong: During labor is intrapartum, not a postnatal assessment time."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"The postnatal assessment mnemonic \"BUBBLEHE\" is used to remember what to check. What does the first \"B\" stand for?","options":["Bladder","Breast","Bowel","Blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the notes' BUBBLEHE list, the first item is Breast (B).  Why A wrong: Bladder is a later \"B\" in the sequence.  Why C wrong: Bowel is a later \"B.\"  Why D wrong: Blood pressure is not part of the BUBBLEHE list given."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"In the BUBBLEHE postnatal assessment mnemonic, which components are included? (Select all that apply)","options":["Breast, Uterus","Bowel, Bladder","Lochia, Episiotomy","Homan's sign, Emotional status","Blood glucose"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes' BUBBLEHE list is Breast, Uterus, Bowel, Bladder, Lochia, Episiotomy, Homan's sign, and Emotional status.  Why E wrong: Blood glucose is not part of the BUBBLEHE list."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"In the BUBBLEHE mnemonic, the \"E\" and \"H\" toward the end of the sequence stand for which components?","options":["Episiotomy and Homan's sign","Emotional and Head","Edema and Heart","Elimination and Hydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the notes, after Lochia comes Episiotomy (E) and Homan's sign (H), with Emotional status at the end.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the components listed in BUBBLEHE."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Homan's sign is used to detect which condition?","options":["Thrombophlebitis (deep vein thrombosis)","Endometritis","Breast engorgement","Uterine atony"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes place Homan's sign under a box labeled \"Thrombophlebitis,\" used to detect leg vein thrombosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Homan's sign does not assess these conditions."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"How is Homan's sign elicited?","options":["Gently press on the knee and grasp and dorsiflex the foot","Palpate the calf muscles only","Percuss the patellar tendon","Apply a blood pressure cuff to the thigh"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes/figure show gently pressing on the knee and grasping and dorsiflexing the foot to elicit the sign.  Why B/C/D wrong: These maneuvers are not how Homan's sign is performed."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Homan's sign is considered POSITIVE when the patient reports which of the following on dorsiflexion of the foot?","options":["Pain or discomfort in the calf","Pain in the knee joint","Numbness in the toes","Foot cramps only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Homan's sign is positive if she has pain or discomfort in her calf.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the criterion for a positive Homan's sign given."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"Which of the following are described as clinical features associated with thrombophlebitis in the Homan's sign box? (Select all that apply)","options":["Acute onset of unilateral leg pain","Localized area of edema","Erythema, heat, and tenderness over a superficial vein","Bilateral facial swelling"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list acute onset of unilateral leg pain and a localized area of edema, erythema, heat, and tenderness over a superficial vein.  Why D wrong: Bilateral facial swelling is not a feature listed."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"After the uterine massage removes a boggy uterus's clots, what is the expected effect described in the notes?","options":["If there is any clot, it is passed out","Bleeding stops permanently without further care","The uterus prolapses","Lochia turns alba immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that with uterine massage, if there is any clot it is passed out.  Why B/C/D wrong: These outcomes are not described for uterine massage in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"A postpartum woman on day 3 has bright red vaginal discharge. Based on the lochia sequence, this finding is:","options":["Abnormal — lochia rubra should have stopped by day 1","Normal — lochia rubra is seen until the 4th day","A sign of subinvolution requiring immediate surgery","Lochia alba"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lochia rubra (red) is normally seen until the 4th day, so red discharge on day 3 is expected.  Why A wrong: Lochia rubra normally persists to day 4, not day 1.  Why C wrong: Subinvolution is indicated by red bleeding persisting BEYOND the normal period, not on day 3.  Why D wrong: Lochia alba is white and seen ≥10 days."},{"section":"Batch 071 · Management of uterine prolapse in puerperium, Kegel exercises…","question":"A nurse assessing a postpartum woman finds a peri-pad saturated within 15 minutes. What is the priority interpretation?","options":["Scanty flow, reassure","Light flow, routine care","Excessive bleeding — a critical finding requiring urgent action","Normal moderate flow"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the pad chart, saturation within 15 minutes = excessive, the most severe category, which requires urgent attention.  Why A/B/D wrong: These underestimate the severity; 15-minute saturation is excessive, not scanty/light/moderate."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Which hormone is responsible for the milk ejection (milk let-down / galactotenesis) reflex?","options":["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The milk ejection / milk let-down reflex (galactotenesis) is mediated by oxytocin.  Why A wrong: Prolactin is responsible for milk production (galactopoesis), not ejection.  Why C wrong: Estrogen inhibits the action of prolactin during pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Progesterone also inhibits prolactin action and does not eject milk."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Oxytocin causes milk ejection by producing contraction of which structure in the breast?","options":["Lactiferous ducts","Myoepithelial cells of the alveoli","Nipple erectile tissue","Cooper's ligaments"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin causes contraction of the myoepithelial cells of the alveoli, squeezing milk out.  Why A wrong: Ducts carry the milk but are not the contractile element driving ejection.  Why C wrong: Nipple tissue is not the site of the ejection reflex.  Why D wrong: Cooper's ligaments are suspensory support structures, not contractile."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Which hormone is chiefly responsible for continuous milk production (galactopoesis)?","options":["Oxytocin","Prolactin","FSH","LH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prolactin is responsible for continuous milk production (galactopoesis).  Why A wrong: Oxytocin causes milk ejection/let-down, not production.  Why C/D wrong: FSH and LH are gonadotropins governing ovarian function, not milk production."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Besides prolactin, which stimulus maintains continuous milk production?","options":["Suckling of the breast by the baby","Maternal exercise","High estrogen levels","Progesterone surge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Suckling of the breast by the baby, along with prolactin, maintains continuous milk production.  Why B wrong: Exercise is not listed as a stimulus.  Why C/D wrong: High estrogen and progesterone inhibit prolactin action."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Prolactin levels reach their maximum during which period?","options":["First trimester","Second trimester","Third trimester of pregnancy","Immediately before menstruation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Levels of prolactin are maximum in pregnancy, specifically the 3rd trimester.  Why A/B wrong: Prolactin rises progressively and peaks in the 3rd trimester, not earlier.  Why D wrong: The peak is related to pregnancy, not the menstrual cycle."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Why does milk production NOT occur during pregnancy despite high prolactin levels?","options":["Prolactin is not yet produced","High estrogen and progesterone inhibit the action of prolactin","Oxytocin is absent","The alveoli are undeveloped"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen levels are high in pregnancy and progesterone inhibits the action of prolactin, so milk production does not occur.  Why A wrong: Prolactin is present and in fact maximal during pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin governs ejection, not the block on production.  Why D wrong: The block is hormonal inhibition, not undeveloped alveoli."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"What hormonal change after delivery allows milk production to begin?","options":["Rise in estrogen and progesterone","Fall in the levels of estrogen and progesterone, removing inhibition of prolactin","Fall in prolactin","Rise in oxytocin only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After delivery, levels of estrogen and progesterone fall, so their inhibition of prolactin decreases and milk production occurs.  Why A wrong: These hormones fall, not rise, after delivery.  Why C wrong: Falling prolactin would reduce milk, not enable it.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin drives ejection, not the initiation of production."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Amenorrhea during pregnancy is primarily due to increased levels of which hormone?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amenorrhea in pregnancy is due to progesterone (increased).  Why A wrong: Estrogen is elevated too but the note attributes the amenorrhea to progesterone.  Why C wrong: Prolactin relates to lactational amenorrhea, not pregnancy amenorrhea per this note.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not the cause of amenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"After delivery, lochia typically lasts for how many days?","options":["5–7 days","10–14 days","15–21 days","30–40 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states lochia lasts 15–21 days after delivery.  Why A/B/D wrong: These durations do not match the stated 15–21 days."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"The absence of menstruation in a woman who is breastfeeding is called:","options":["Primary amenorrhea","Lactational amenorrhea","Secondary amenorrhea","Cryptomenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient is breastfeeding there is no menstruation — this is called lactational amenorrhea.  Why A wrong: Primary amenorrhea is failure to ever menstruate by expected age.  Why C wrong: Secondary amenorrhea is a general term; the specific breastfeeding-related term is lactational amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Cryptomenorrhea is hidden/obstructed menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In lactational amenorrhea, prolactin exerts its effect by inhibiting the release of which hypothalamic hormone?","options":["GnRH (Gonadotropin releasing hormone)","TRH","CRH","ADH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the axis shown, prolactin INHIBITS GnRH release from the hypothalamus, suppressing FSH/LH and thus ovulation/menstruation.  Why B/C/D wrong: TRH, CRH, and ADH are not the hormone inhibited by prolactin in this reproductive axis."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis shown, the pituitary secretes which two gonadotropins acting on the ovary?","options":["TSH and ACTH","FSH and LH","GH and prolactin","Oxytocin and ADH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pituitary secretes FSH and LH, which act on the ovary in the diagrammed axis.  Why A wrong: TSH and ACTH act on thyroid and adrenal, not ovary.  Why C wrong: GH and prolactin are not the gonadotropins depicted acting on the ovary.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin and ADH are posterior pituitary hormones, not ovarian gonadotropins."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In the diagrammed reproductive axis, the ovary acts on the uterus via which hormones?","options":["FSH and LH","Estrogen and progesterone","Oxytocin and prolactin","GnRH and dopamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ovary → uterus arrow releases estrogen and progesterone.  Why A wrong: FSH and LH act on the ovary, not from ovary to uterus.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin and prolactin are not the ovarian outputs shown.  Why D wrong: GnRH is hypothalamic; dopamine is a prolactin inhibitor."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"What is the normal level of prolactin in a non-pregnant female?","options":["5–25 ng/ml","10–200 ng/ml","50–100 ng/ml","1–5 ng/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal levels of prolactin in a female are 5–25 ng/ml.  Why B wrong: 10–200 is the value quoted for a pregnant female.  Why C/D wrong: These ranges are not given."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"As per the notes, the normal level of prolactin in a pregnant female is stated as:","options":["5–25 ng/ml","10–200 mg/mL","1–2 ng/ml","300–500 mg/mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states 10–200 mg/mL for a pregnant female (conventionally this is ng/mL; the printed unit \"mg/mL\" is reproduced as written but the value 10–200 is the key figure).  Why A wrong: 5–25 ng/ml is for the non-pregnant female.  Why C/D wrong: These values are not given."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"What is the drug of choice (DOC) for elevated prolactin (hyperprolactinemia)?","options":["Metoclopramide","Cabergoline","Pyridoxine","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The DOC for increased prolactin is Cabergoline (a dopamine agonist).  Why A wrong: Metoclopramide is antidopaminergic and increases prolactin — used to boost milk.  Why C wrong: Pyridoxine increases dopamine and is used to stop lactation, not the DOC.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin governs milk ejection."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Which drug is the second choice for lowering elevated prolactin?","options":["Cabergoline","Bromocriptine","Estrogen","Metoclopramide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bromocriptine is the 2nd choice for elevated prolactin.  Why A wrong: Cabergoline is the first choice/DOC.  Why C wrong: Estrogen inhibits prolactin action but is not listed as the 2nd choice drug here.  Why D wrong: Metoclopramide raises prolactin."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"A notable side effect of Bromocriptine is:","options":["Decreased blood pressure","Increased blood pressure (↑BP)","Hyperglycemia","Bradycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note lists the side effect of Bromocriptine as increased BP (↑BP).  Why A wrong: The stated effect is increased, not decreased, BP.  Why C/D wrong: Hyperglycemia and bradycardia are not the noted side effect."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Suckling leads to continuous milk production because it causes:","options":["An increase in dopamine","A decrease in dopamine","An increase in estrogen","A decrease in oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suckling → dopamine decreases; since dopamine is the prolactin-inhibiting factor, prolactin then increases.  Why A wrong: Suckling decreases (not increases) dopamine.  Why C wrong: Estrogen is not the mediator here.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not decreased by suckling — suckling triggers oxytocin release."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Dopamine is best described as a:","options":["Prolactin-releasing factor","Prolactin-inhibiting factor","Milk ejection factor","Gonadotropin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dopamine is the prolactin-inhibiting factor; less dopamine means more prolactin.  Why A wrong: It inhibits, not releases, prolactin.  Why C wrong: Milk ejection is by oxytocin.  Why D wrong: Dopamine is not a gonadotropin."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"To increase milk production, dopamine levels should be:","options":["Increased","Decreased","Kept unchanged","Replaced by pyridoxine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Whenever milk production has to be increased → decrease dopamine (which raises prolactin).  Why A wrong: Increasing dopamine would suppress prolactin and lower milk.  Why C wrong: A change (decrease) is needed.  Why D wrong: Pyridoxine increases dopamine and is used to stop lactation."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"A woman after delivery has decreased milk production. Which drug is appropriate to stimulate lactation?","options":["Cabergoline","Bromocriptine","Metoclopramide (antidopaminergic)","Estrogen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For a female with decreased milk production after delivery, an antidopaminergic such as Metoclopramide is given (it lowers dopamine → raises prolactin → more milk).  Why A/B wrong: Cabergoline and bromocriptine are dopamine agonists that reduce prolactin/milk.  Why D wrong: Estrogen inhibits prolactin action and reduces lactation."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In a female with intrauterine death, the goal is to stop lactation. Which is a correct pharmacological strategy?","options":["Give Metoclopramide","Reduce prolactin using Cabergoline or Bromocriptine","Decrease dopamine","Increase suckling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To stop lactation, prolactin is decreased using Cabergoline or Bromocriptine (dopamine agonists).  Why A wrong: Metoclopramide increases prolactin/milk, opposite of the goal.  Why C wrong: Decreasing dopamine would raise prolactin and increase milk.  Why D wrong: Suckling increases milk production."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"To stop lactation after intrauterine death, which hormone can be given to inhibit the action of prolactin?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Oxytocin","GnRH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen inhibits the action of prolactin and is used to stop lactation.  Why A wrong: Progesterone is not listed as the agent here for inhibiting prolactin action.  Why C wrong: Oxytocin promotes ejection.  Why D wrong: GnRH governs gonadotropins, not prolactin inhibition here."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In the strategy to stop lactation, which agent is used to INCREASE dopamine?","options":["Metoclopramide","Pyridoxine","Cabergoline","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pyridoxine increases dopamine, which suppresses prolactin and helps stop lactation.  Why A wrong: Metoclopramide is antidopaminergic (decreases dopamine).  Why C wrong: Cabergoline is a dopamine agonist but the note lists it under \"↓ Prolactin,\" while pyridoxine is listed specifically under \"↑ Dopamine.\"  Why D wrong: Oxytocin does not raise dopamine."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"The yellow-coloured breast secretion that comes out during the first 48–72 hours of birth is called:","options":["Mature milk","Transitional milk","Colostrum","Foremilk"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For the first 48–72 hours of birth, yellow-coloured secretion (colostrum) comes out.  Why A/B wrong: Mature and transitional milk appear later.  Why D wrong: Foremilk is the initial watery milk of a mature feed, not the first-days colostrum."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Colostrum should be given to the baby primarily because it is:","options":["High in fat and calories","Rich in immunoglobulins and nutrients","Sterile water","Rich in carbohydrate only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colostrum is rich in immunoglobulins and nutrients and should be given to the baby.  Why A wrong: Colostrum is actually lower in some components (like fat) compared with mature breast milk.  Why C wrong: It is a nutrient/immunoglobulin-rich secretion, not water.  Why D wrong: Its key value is immunoglobulins/nutrients, not carbohydrate alone."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Compared with mature breast milk, colostrum is LOWER in which components (remember K, F, C)?","options":["Potassium, Fat, Carbohydrate","Protein, Immunoglobulins, Antibodies","Sodium, Iron, Water","Calcium, Phosphate, Vitamin D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states colostrum has less compared to breast milk in K = potassium, F = fat, C = carbohydrate.  Why B wrong: Colostrum is actually higher in immunoglobulins/protein.  Why C/D wrong: These components are not the K/F/C set given."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Breast engorgement typically occurs on which postpartum day, and in which women?","options":["Day 2, only in breastfeeding women","Day 4, in both breastfeeding and non-breastfeeding women","Day 7, only in non-breastfeeding women","Day 10, only in primigravida"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Breast engorgement occurs on day 4 in both breastfeeding and non-breastfeeding females.  Why A/C/D wrong: The day and the population (both groups) do not match these options."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Which is an appropriate breast-care measure for engorgement?","options":["Stimulate the nipple frequently","Avoid nipple stimulation","Sleep without any bra","Apply ice before breastfeeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Care of breast includes avoiding nipple stimulation.  Why A wrong: Nipple stimulation should be avoided, not encouraged.  Why C wrong: A tight-fitting bra is advised in the evening during sleeping.  Why D wrong: Warm compress (not ice) is used before breastfeeding; ice is used between feedings."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"As part of breast care for engorgement, when should a tight-fitting bra be worn?","options":["All day only","In the evening during sleeping","Never","Only during feeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note advises wearing a tight-fitting bra in the evening during sleeping.  Why A/C/D wrong: These timings are not what the note specifies."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"With appropriate care, breast engorgement relieves within approximately:","options":["6–12 hours","24–36 hours","48–72 hours","5–7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Engorgement relieves by 24–36 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the stated 24–36 hours."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"For breast engorgement, when should a warm compress be applied?","options":["Between feedings","Before breastfeeding","Only at night","It should never be used"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use warm compress before breastfeeding (to aid milk flow/let-down).  Why A wrong: Ice pack is used between feedings, not warm compress.  Why C/D wrong: The note specifies use before breastfeeding."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"When should an ice pack be applied for breast engorgement?","options":["Before breastfeeding","Between feedings","During active feeding","Only after weaning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use ice pack between feedings (to reduce swelling/pain).  Why A wrong: Warm compress, not ice, is used before feeding.  Why C/D wrong: The note specifies between feedings."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In addition to compresses, which measure is recommended for engorgement discomfort?","options":["Give analgesics","Restrict fluids","Stop breastfeeding entirely","Apply pressure bandage all day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Giving analgesics is listed as part of breast care for engorgement.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not measures listed in the note."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Mastitis is defined as infection of the:","options":["Milk ducts only","Breast parenchyma","Nipple skin","Axillary lymph nodes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mastitis is infection of the breast parenchyma.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the definition given for mastitis."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"The most common organism causing mastitis is:","options":["Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pyogenes","Candida albicans"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The MC (most common) organism in mastitis is Staphylococcus aureus.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the most common causative organism per the note."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"The most common source of infection in mastitis is:","options":["Contaminated breast pump","Nose and mouth of the baby","Maternal bloodstream","Hospital environment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The MC source of infection in mastitis is the nose and mouth of the baby.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common source."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Symptoms of mastitis are typically seen at approximately:","options":["3–4 days postpartum","3–4 weeks postpartum","3–4 months postpartum","Within 24 hours of delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Symptoms of mastitis are seen in 3–4 weeks.  Why A/C/D wrong: These timings do not match the stated 3–4 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Mastitis is characteristically:","options":["Bilateral","Unilateral","Always bilateral in primigravida","Confined to the nipple"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mastitis is unilateral.  Why A/C wrong: It is not typically bilateral.  Why D wrong: It involves the breast parenchyma, not just the nipple."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Regarding breastfeeding during mastitis, which statement is correct?","options":["Breastfeeding must be stopped immediately","Breastfeeding is not contraindicated","Only formula feeding is allowed","Feeding is allowed only from the affected breast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In mastitis, breastfeeding is NOT contraindicated (continued feeding/milk ejection aids resolution).  Why A/C wrong: Feeding need not be stopped or switched to formula.  Why D wrong: The note does not restrict feeding to the affected breast only."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"A breastfeeding woman with mastitis most commonly complains of:","options":["Fever and pain in the breast","Painless lump only","Nipple discharge without symptoms","Weight gain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Patient complains of fever and pain in the breast.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed complaints."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Which antibiotics are used in the management of mastitis?","options":["Penicillin / Dicloxacillin","Metronidazole alone","Ciprofloxacin only","No antibiotics needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Management includes Penicillin/Dicloxacillin (anti-staphylococcal), plus continued milk ejection.  Why B/C wrong: These agents are not the ones specified.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics are part of management."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Besides antibiotics, which measure is part of mastitis management?","options":["Milk ejection (continued emptying of the breast)","Complete cessation of feeding","Cold compress only","Breast binding tightly for 2 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Management is Penicillin/Dicloxacillin PLUS milk ejection (keeping the breast emptied).  Why B wrong: Feeding/milk ejection continues; it is not stopped.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the listed management measures."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In a breastfeeding woman, menstruation typically returns within:","options":["3–6 months","7–9 weeks","4 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In breastfeeding women, return of menstruation is at 3–6 months (maximum: till she breastfeeds).  Why B/D wrong: 7–9 weeks / 12 weeks apply to non-breastfeeding women.  Why C wrong: 4 weeks is the ovulation return time in non-breastfeeding women."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"For a breastfeeding woman, the maximum time up to which menstruation may remain absent is:","options":["Till she continues to breastfeed","Exactly 12 weeks","Exactly 6 weeks","Exactly 4 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In breastfeeding women the maximum is \"till she breast feeds.\"  Why B wrong: 12 weeks is the maximum for non-breastfeeding women.  Why C/D wrong: These fixed durations are not the breastfeeding maximum."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In a non-breastfeeding woman, menstruation typically returns by:","options":["3–6 months","7–9 weeks (maximum by 12 weeks)","4 weeks","Till she breastfeeds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In non-breastfeeding women, menstruation returns at 7–9 weeks, maximum by 12 weeks.  Why A wrong: 3–6 months is for breastfeeding women.  Why C wrong: 4 weeks is the ovulation return time in non-breastfeeding women.  Why D wrong: This applies only to breastfeeding women."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In a breastfeeding woman, ovulation returns by approximately:","options":["4 weeks","10 weeks","12 weeks","3–6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In breastfeeding women, ovulation returns by 10 weeks.  Why A wrong: 4 weeks is for non-breastfeeding women.  Why C wrong: 12 weeks is the maximum for return of menstruation in non-breastfeeding women.  Why D wrong: 3–6 months is return of menstruation in breastfeeding women, not ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In a non-breastfeeding woman, ovulation returns by approximately:","options":["4 weeks","10 weeks","3 months","12 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In non-breastfeeding women, ovulation returns by 4 weeks.  Why B wrong: 10 weeks is for breastfeeding women.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match ovulation return in non-breastfeeding women."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"The \"Golden rule of 3\" for contraception in a breastfeeding woman states that contraception should be started latest by:","options":["3 weeks after delivery","3 months after delivery","3 days after delivery","3 years after delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For breastfeeding women (Golden rule of 3), use contraception latest by 3 months after delivery.  Why A wrong: 3 weeks is for non-breastfeeding women.  Why C/D wrong: These durations are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"In a non-breastfeeding woman, contraception should be started latest by:","options":["3 months after delivery","3 weeks after delivery","6 weeks after delivery","12 weeks after delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In non-breastfeeding women, use contraception latest by 3 weeks after delivery (because ovulation returns earlier, by 4 weeks).  Why A wrong: 3 months applies to breastfeeding women.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated timings."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"The contraceptive of choice for a lactating (breastfeeding) female is:","options":["Combined oral contraceptive pills","IUCD (Intrauterine contraceptive device)","Estrogen patch","Depot estrogen injection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The contraceptive of choice for a lactating female is the IUCD.  Why A/C/D wrong: Estrogen-containing methods are avoided in lactation as they can inhibit prolactin/milk; the note specifies IUCD."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"IUCD insertion within 10 minutes of delivery of the placenta is termed:","options":["Interval IUCD insertion","Post-placental IUCD insertion","Post-partum IUCD insertion","Emergency IUCD insertion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Insertion within 10 minutes of delivery of the placenta is post-placental IUCD insertion.  Why A wrong: Interval insertion is after 6 weeks.  Why C wrong: Post-partum insertion is after 10 min but within 24–48 hours.  Why D wrong: \"Emergency\" is not the classification used."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"For a lactating woman, if an IUCD is not chosen at the post-placental stage, the 2nd choice contraceptive is:","options":["Combined OC pills","Progesterone only pills","Estrogen patch","Depot medroxyprogesterone with estrogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2nd choice noted (under post-placental section) is progesterone only pills (safe in lactation, do not suppress milk).  Why A/C/D wrong: Estrogen-containing methods are avoided; progesterone-only is the stated 2nd choice."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"IUCD insertion after 10 minutes but within 24–48 hours after delivery is called:","options":["Post-placental IUCD insertion","Post-partum IUCD insertion","Interval IUCD insertion","Immediate IUCD insertion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Insertion after 10 minutes but 24–48 hours after delivery is post-partum IUCD insertion.  Why A wrong: Post-placental is within 10 minutes.  Why C wrong: Interval insertion is after 6 weeks.  Why D wrong: \"Immediate\" is not the classification used."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"If an IUCD is not inserted within 48 hours of delivery, it should NOT be inserted until:","options":["6 weeks of delivery","2 weeks of delivery","3 months of delivery","10 days of delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After 48 hours of delivery, the IUCD is not inserted until 6 weeks of delivery (to reduce expulsion/perforation risk).  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the stated 6 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"IUCD insertion after 6 weeks of delivery is termed:","options":["Post-placental IUCD insertion","Post-partum IUCD insertion","Interval IUCD insertion","Emergency IUCD insertion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Insertion after 6 weeks of delivery is interval IUCD insertion.  Why A wrong: Post-placental is within 10 minutes.  Why B wrong: Post-partum is within 24–48 hours.  Why D wrong: \"Emergency\" is not the classification used."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Antepartum fetal monitoring refers to monitoring the well-being of the fetus in the antenatal period, especially in:","options":["Low-risk pregnancies","High-risk pregnancies","Post-term pregnancies only","Twin pregnancies only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antepartum fetal monitoring means monitoring fetal well-being in the antenatal period in \"HIGH RISK PREGNANCIES.\"  Why A wrong: It is emphasized in high-risk, not low-risk, pregnancies.  Why C/D wrong: It is not restricted to post-term or twin pregnancies alone."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the listed investigations for antepartum fetal monitoring?","options":["Daily fetal movement count","Nonstress test","Amniocentesis for karyotyping","Biophysical score"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The listed investigations are daily fetal movement count, listening to FHS by stethoscope, Doppler USG, nonstress test, biophysical score, and USG — amniocentesis for karyotyping is not listed.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are all explicitly listed investigations."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Which instrument is listed for listening to the fetal heart sounds (FHS) in antepartum monitoring?","options":["Stethoscope","Sphygmomanometer","Speculum","Amnioscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The investigations list includes \"Listening to FHS by stethoscope.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These instruments are not used for auscultating fetal heart sounds here."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Which imaging modality is listed among antepartum fetal monitoring investigations?","options":["Doppler USG","CT scan","MRI","X-ray abdomen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Doppler USG (and USG) are listed investigations.  Why B/C/D wrong: CT, MRI, and X-ray are not listed investigations."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"The Daily Fetal Movement Count is performed by:","options":["The obstetrician only","The pregnant female herself","A radiologist","The staff nurse using Doppler"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The daily fetal movement count is done by the pregnant female.  Why A/C/D wrong: The count is self-monitored by the mother, not done by these providers."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"\"Quickening\" is defined as:","options":["The onset of labour pains","When a pregnant female feels fetal movements for the first time","The moment of ovulation","The first fetal heart sound heard"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Quickening means when a pregnant female feels fetal movements for the first time.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated events, not the definition of quickening."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Quickening is felt by a primigravida at approximately:","options":["16 weeks","18 weeks of pregnancy","20 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a primigravida, quickening is felt at 18 weeks of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: 16 weeks is for a multigravida.  Why C/D wrong: These weeks are not the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Quickening is felt by a multigravida at approximately:","options":["18 weeks","16 weeks of pregnancy","20 weeks","14 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a multigravida, quickening is felt at 16 weeks of pregnancy (earlier than primigravida because she recognizes the movements).  Why A wrong: 18 weeks is for a primigravida.  Why C/D wrong: These weeks are not the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Formal daily fetal movement counting is advised from which point in pregnancy?","options":["From the 1st trimester","From the 3rd trimester onwards (≥28 weeks)","From 20 weeks","Only after 40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: From the 3rd trimester onwards (≥28 weeks of pregnancy), all pregnant females are advised to count fetal movements.  Why A/C/D wrong: These gestational periods do not match the stated ≥28 weeks / 3rd trimester."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"For daily fetal movement counting, in which position and for how long is the pregnant female advised to lie after meals?","options":["Right lateral position for 30 minutes","Left lateral position for 1 hour","Supine position for 2 hours","Sitting upright for 15 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The female is advised to lie in the left lateral position for 1 hour after breakfast, lunch, and dinner, and count movements.  Why A/C/D wrong: The correct position/duration is left lateral for 1 hour."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"After which meals is the pregnant female advised to count fetal movements?","options":["Only after breakfast","After breakfast, lunch and dinner","Only after dinner","Only when she feels movements"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: She counts fetal movements after breakfast, lunch and dinner (three times daily) and maintains a chart.  Why A/C wrong: It is after all three meals, not just one.  Why D wrong: A structured count after meals is advised, not only when movements are felt."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"After counting her fetal movements, the pregnant female should:","options":["Report to hospital daily","Maintain a chart","Repeat an ultrasound","Take medication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note ends the sequence with \"Maintain a Chart.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These steps are not part of the movement-counting instruction."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"According to the normal criterion, an adequate daily fetal movement count is:","options":["10 fetal movements in 2 hours of rest, or 10 movements in 12 hours of activity","5 movements in 1 hour of rest","20 movements in 6 hours","2 movements in 30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal = 10 fetal movements in 2 hours of rest OR 10 fetal movements in 12 hours of activity.  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures do not match the stated normal criterion."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"The criterion of 10 fetal movements (in 2 hours of rest or 12 hours of activity) is known as the:","options":["Manning score","Cardiff count of 10","Apgar count","Bishop score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This criterion is called the Cardiff count of 10.  Why A wrong: The Manning score is the biophysical profile.  Why C wrong: Apgar assesses the newborn at birth.  Why D wrong: Bishop score assesses cervical readiness for labour."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Decreased fetal movement should be regarded as a sign of:","options":["Normal fetal sleep, ignore it","Fetal distress, to be taken seriously","Maternal dehydration only","Impending labour only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased fetal movement is a sign of fetal distress and should be taken seriously.  Why A wrong: It should not be ignored.  Why C/D wrong: The note specifically flags it as a fetal distress sign."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"When a pregnant female complains of decreased fetal movements, the next step is to:","options":["Perform a cesarean section immediately","Do a Nonstress Test (NST)","Start oxytocin","Reassure and discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The next step for decreased fetal movement is to do an NST (nonstress test).  Why A/C wrong: These interventions are not the immediate next step per the note.  Why D wrong: Decreased movement must be evaluated, not simply reassured."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"The Nonstress Test (NST) is best described as:","options":["A confirmatory diagnostic test","Only a screening test","An invasive procedure","A treatment for fetal distress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The NST is described as only a screening test.  Why A wrong: It screens; it is not confirmatory.  Why C wrong: NST is non-invasive.  Why D wrong: It is a monitoring/screening test, not a treatment."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"If the NST is abnormal/nonreactive, the next step in evaluation is to perform a:","options":["Repeat NST after 24 hours","Biophysical profile / score","Contraction stress test only","Immediate forceps delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An abnormal/nonreactive NST is followed by the Biophysical Profile/Score.  Why A/C/D wrong: The note specifies progression to the biophysical profile/score after an abnormal NST."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"A woman with intrauterine death is advised measures to stop lactation. Select ALL correct strategies mentioned in the notes.","options":["Give Cabergoline (decrease prolactin)","Give Bromocriptine (decrease prolactin)","Give Estrogen (inhibit action of prolactin)","Give Pyridoxine (increase dopamine)","Give Metoclopramide (antidopaminergic)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: To stop lactation the note lists decreasing prolactin (Cabergoline, Bromocriptine), inhibiting prolactin action (Estrogen), and increasing dopamine (Pyridoxine).  Why E wrong: Metoclopramide is antidopaminergic; it raises prolactin and INCREASES milk, so it is used to stimulate (not stop) lactation."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"Select ALL correct breast-care measures for engorgement from the notes.","options":["Avoid nipple stimulation","Wear a tight-fitting bra in the evening during sleeping","Use warm compress before breastfeeding","Use ice pack between feedings","Give analgesics"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are listed care-of-breast measures: avoid nipple stimulation, wear tight-fitting bra during sleeping, warm compress before feeding, ice pack between feedings, and give analgesics.  Why (none) wrong: Every option is a stated measure; engorgement also relieves by 24–36 hours."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"A postpartum non-breastfeeding woman asks when she must begin contraception. The best advice is to start it latest by:","options":["3 weeks after delivery, because ovulation returns by about 4 weeks","3 months after delivery","6 months after delivery","Only when menstruation resumes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In non-breastfeeding women ovulation returns by ~4 weeks, so contraception should be started latest by 3 weeks after delivery.  Why B wrong: 3 months is the rule for breastfeeding women (Golden rule of 3).  Why C/D wrong: Waiting that long risks unintended pregnancy since ovulation returns early."},{"section":"Batch 072 · Lactation physiology…","question":"A woman delivered 30 minutes ago (placenta already out) and wants an IUCD. Based on timing classification, which insertion type now applies if done within 24–48 hours?","options":["Post-placental IUCD insertion","Post-partum IUCD insertion","Interval IUCD insertion","IUCD is contraindicated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Post-placental insertion is only within 10 minutes of placental delivery; after that, insertion done within 24–48 hours is post-partum IUCD insertion.  Why A wrong: The 10-minute window for post-placental has passed.  Why C wrong: Interval insertion is after 6 weeks.  Why D wrong: IUCD is the contraceptive of choice in lactating women; it is not contraindicated within this window."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"The Nonstress Test (NST) is compared to which of the following diagnostic recordings, as it monitors the fetal heart rate and its characteristics on graph paper?","options":["EEG","ECG","EMG","X-ray"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe NST as being \"Like 'ECG'\" — it monitors the fetal Heart Rate and its characteristics on a graph paper.  Why A wrong: EEG records brain electrical activity, not heart rate.  Why C wrong: EMG records muscle electrical activity.  Why D wrong: X-ray is an imaging modality, not a continuous rate tracing."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the Nonstress Test, what type of lead/electrode is attached to the mother's abdomen?","options":["Multiple electrodes","A single transducer","Chest leads","Scalp electrode"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Leads = \"TRANSDUCER\" (single), which is attached to the mother's abdomen.  Why A wrong: A single transducer, not multiple electrodes, is used.  Why C wrong: Chest leads are used in maternal ECG, not fetal NST.  Why D wrong: A scalp electrode is used in internal (invasive) fetal monitoring, not the external NST transducer."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Which maneuver is used to feel the fetal back so the transducer can be correctly attached during NST?","options":["Ritgen maneuver","Leopold maneuver","McRoberts maneuver","Valsalva maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Leopold maneuver: Feel the fetal back → Attach the Transducer.\"  Why A wrong: Ritgen maneuver is used to control delivery of the fetal head.  Why C wrong: McRoberts maneuver is used to relieve shoulder dystocia.  Why D wrong: Valsalva maneuver relates to bearing-down/pushing, not locating the fetal back."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the Leopold maneuver during NST, the transducer is attached over which part of the fetus?","options":["Fetal head","Fetal limbs","Fetal back","Fetal buttocks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The transducer is attached after feeling the fetal back, because the fetal heart is best heard through the back.  Why A wrong: The head is not where the transducer is placed for FHR in this technique.  Why B wrong: Limbs are not used for transducer placement.  Why D wrong: The buttocks are not the site described."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"What is the NORMAL fetal heart rate range?","options":["60–100 beats/min","100–120 beats/min","110–160 beats/min","160–200 beats/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state NORMAL FHR = 110–160 beats/min.  Why A wrong: 60–100 is the adult resting heart rate, not fetal.  Why B wrong: 100–120 is not the stated normal range.  Why D wrong: 160–200 is above the normal fetal range."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"A fetal heart rate of less than 110 beats/min is termed:","options":["Tachycardia","Bradycardia","Variability","Acceleration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state ABNORMAL → <110 = BRADYCARDIA.  Why A wrong: Tachycardia is FHR >160 beats/min.  Why C wrong: Variability refers to beat-to-beat changes, not a rate abnormality.  Why D wrong: Acceleration is a transient rise in FHR."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"A fetal heart rate greater than 160 beats/min is termed:","options":["Bradycardia","Tachycardia","Normal","Sinusoidal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state >160 = TACHYCARDIA.  Why A wrong: Bradycardia is FHR <110 beats/min.  Why C wrong: >160 is abnormal, not normal.  Why D wrong: Sinusoidal refers to a wave-like variability pattern, not a rate value."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"According to the notes, between fetal bradycardia and tachycardia, which is considered MORE dangerous?","options":["Tachycardia","Bradycardia","Both equally dangerous","Neither is dangerous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes mark Bradycardia (<110) as \"more dangerous\" because it leads to fetal hypoxia.  Why A wrong: Tachycardia is abnormal but noted as less dangerous than bradycardia here.  Why C wrong: The notes specifically single out bradycardia as more dangerous.  Why D wrong: Both are abnormal; bradycardia especially is dangerous."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Fetal bradycardia is most directly associated with which of the following consequences?","options":["Fetal hypoxia","Fetal macrosomia","Maternal hypertension","Neonatal jaundice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show Bradycardia (more dangerous) → Fetal Hypoxia.  Why B wrong: Macrosomia relates to fetal overgrowth, not bradycardia in this context.  Why C wrong: Maternal hypertension is a maternal condition, not the direct consequence shown.  Why D wrong: Neonatal jaundice is unrelated to the bradycardia arrow diagram."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"When a nurse detects fetal bradycardia or tachycardia during monitoring, which is the FIRST/priority nursing action listed?","options":["Give O2 by mask","Inform the doctor/senior","Check the mother's vitals","Stop any oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the nurse's role in order beginning with \"Inform the doctor/senior.\"  Why A wrong: Giving O2 by mask is step 3, not first.  Why C wrong: Checking mother's vitals is step 4.  Why D wrong: Stopping oxytocin is step 2."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the nurse's role for fetal bradycardia/tachycardia, which medication should be stopped?","options":["Magnesium sulfate","Oxytocin","Betamethasone","Insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Stop any oxytocin\" as the second action.  Why A wrong: Magnesium sulfate is not listed for stopping here.  Why C wrong: Betamethasone is for fetal lung maturity, not stopped in this protocol.  Why D wrong: Insulin is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the management of fetal bradycardia/tachycardia, oxygen is administered to the mother by which route?","options":["Nasal cannula","By mask","Endotracheal tube","Venturi mask only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Give O2 by mask.\"  Why A wrong: The notes specify mask, not nasal cannula.  Why C wrong: Endotracheal tube is invasive ventilation, not indicated here.  Why D wrong: The notes simply say \"by mask,\" not specifically Venturi."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Which of the following is the LAST listed nursing action for fetal bradycardia/tachycardia?","options":["Inform the doctor/senior","Stop any oxytocin","Give O2 by mask","Check mother's vitals"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Check mother's vital\" as the 4th (last) action.  Why A wrong: Informing the doctor is first.  Why B wrong: Stopping oxytocin is second.  Why C wrong: Giving O2 is third."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"The continual normal changing of the fetal heart rate (within normal limits) is called:","options":["Fetal acceleration","Beat-to-beat variability","Sinusoidal pattern","Fetal deceleration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state FHR keeps on changing (until the normal limit); this is called \"Beat to Beat Variability.\"  Why A wrong: Acceleration is a transient FHR rise with movement.  Why C wrong: Sinusoidal pattern is an abnormal wave-like pattern.  Why D wrong: Deceleration is a drop in FHR, not the normal changing described."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Presence of beat-to-beat variability generally indicates that the fetus is:","options":["Hypoxic","Acidotic","Healthy","Anemic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state VARIABILITY → \"Means fetus is healthy.\"  Why B wrong: Acidosis is indicated by absent/decreased variability, not its presence.  Why A wrong: Hypoxia is linked with absent variability.  Why D wrong: Anemia is linked with the sinusoidal pattern, not normal variability."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"The normal range of beat-to-beat variability is:","options":["1–5 beats/min","5–25 beats/min","25–40 beats/min","40–60 beats/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Beat to Beat Variability Ranges between 5 and 25 beats/min.\"  Why A wrong: 1–5 is below the stated normal.  Why C wrong: 25–40 exceeds the stated upper limit.  Why D wrong: 40–60 is far above the normal range."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"When beat-to-beat variability is absent, the FHR is described as:","options":["Increased/accelerated","Fixed/decreased variability","Highly reactive","Sinusoidal only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show Absent Beat to Beat Variability → \"FHR becomes fixed/decreased variability.\"  Why A wrong: Absent variability makes the tracing flat/fixed, not accelerated.  Why C wrong: Reactivity relates to accelerations, opposite of absent variability.  Why D wrong: Sinusoidal is a separate abnormal wave-like pattern."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"A question states the FHR is between 118–120 bpm for 30 minutes with no variability. This finding best represents which of the following?","options":["Normal reactive tracing","Absent/fixed (decreased) variability — abnormal","Fetal tachycardia","Fetal acceleration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes use exactly this example (FHR 118–120 bpm × 30 minutes) to illustrate fixed/decreased variability, which is abnormal.  Why A wrong: A flat, fixed tracing is not reactive/normal.  Why C wrong: The rate 118–120 is within normal limits; the problem is the lack of variability, not tachycardia.  Why D wrong: Acceleration is a rise in FHR, not shown here."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Absent or fixed/decreased beat-to-beat variability is abnormal and indicates which two conditions?","options":["Fetal hypoxia and fetal acidosis","Fetal anemia and hyperglycemia","Maternal fever and dehydration","Fetal macrosomia and polyhydramnios"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state ABNORMAL fixed/decreased variability = Indicate Fetal Hypoxia, Fetal Acidosis.  Why B wrong: Fetal anemia is linked to the sinusoidal pattern, not absent variability.  Why C wrong: Maternal fever/dehydration is not the indicated finding.  Why D wrong: Macrosomia/polyhydramnios are unrelated to this variability finding."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"A wave-like FHR pattern seen on the tracing is called a:","options":["Saltatory pattern","Sinusoidal heart rate pattern","Reactive pattern","Variable deceleration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe a \"Sinusoidal Heart Rate Pattern\" as a wave-like pattern.  Why A wrong: Saltatory pattern is not mentioned in these notes.  Why C wrong: Reactive refers to accelerations, not a wave-like pattern.  Why D wrong: Variable deceleration is a drop pattern, not the wave-like sinusoidal."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"The sinusoidal heart rate pattern is classified as:","options":["Normal and reassuring","Abnormal, indicating fetal anemia","A sign of a healthy fetus","Normal variability"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the sinusoidal pattern is Abnormal and \"Means: Fetal Anemia.\"  Why A wrong: It is abnormal, not reassuring.  Why C wrong: It indicates a compromised (anemic) fetus.  Why D wrong: It is a distinct abnormal wave-like pattern, not normal variability."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"The sinusoidal (wave-like) FHR pattern most classically indicates:","options":["Fetal anemia","Fetal hyperglycemia","Maternal hypotension","Fetal polycythemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the sinusoidal pattern \"Indicates Fetal Anemia.\"  Why B wrong: Hyperglycemia is not the indicated condition.  Why C wrong: Maternal hypotension is not the cause described.  Why D wrong: Polycythemia (opposite of anemia) is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Which of the following is a cause of a sinusoidal FHR pattern (fetal anemia) listed in the notes?","options":["Placenta previa","Vasa previa","Placental abruption","Uterine rupture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Vasa Previa as cause #1 of the sinusoidal pattern/fetal anemia.  Why A wrong: Placenta previa is not listed among the sinusoidal causes.  Why C wrong: Placental abruption is not listed here.  Why D wrong: Uterine rupture is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Which of the following blood-group–related condition can cause a sinusoidal FHR pattern due to fetal anemia?","options":["ABO transfusion reaction","Rh incompatibility","G6PD deficiency","Sickle cell trait"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Rh Incompatibility as cause #2 of the sinusoidal pattern/fetal anemia.  Why A wrong: ABO transfusion reaction is not the listed cause.  Why C wrong: G6PD deficiency is not listed.  Why D wrong: Sickle cell trait is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In twin pregnancies, which condition can produce a sinusoidal FHR pattern from fetal anemia?","options":["Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome","Conjoined twins","Vanishing twin","Locked twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Twin-Twin Transfusion Syndrome as cause #3 of the sinusoidal pattern/fetal anemia.  Why B wrong: Conjoined twins is not the listed cause.  Why C wrong: Vanishing twin is not listed.  Why D wrong: Locked twins is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome, which twin classically develops anemia (associated with the sinusoidal pattern)?","options":["Recipient twin","Donor twin","Both twins equally","Neither twin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify \"Twin to Twin Transfusion Syndrome (Donor twin)\" — the donor twin loses blood and becomes anemic.  Why A wrong: The recipient twin becomes polycythemic/volume-overloaded, not anemic.  Why C wrong: The two twins are affected differently.  Why D wrong: The donor twin is specifically affected."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Absent/decreased beat-to-beat variability on the tracing is classically \"seen in\" which conditions?","options":["Fetal hypoxia and fetal acidosis","Fetal anemia only","Normal healthy fetus","Maternal anxiety"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state absent/decreased beat-to-beat variability is \"seen in Fetal Hypoxia, Fetal acidosis.\"  Why B wrong: Fetal anemia is linked to the sinusoidal pattern, not absent variability.  Why C wrong: A healthy fetus shows present variability.  Why D wrong: Maternal anxiety is not the listed association."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"During NST, a fetal heart rate acceleration occurs in response to:","options":["Uterine contractions","Fetal movement","Maternal position change","Oxytocin infusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state FHR Acceleration occurs \"Whenever Fetal movement occurs.\"  Why A wrong: Contractions relate to the contraction stress test, not the acceleration definition here.  Why C wrong: Maternal position change is not the described trigger.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin infusion is not the described trigger for acceleration."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"A fetal heart rate acceleration is defined as an increase in FHR by:","options":["10 beats/min for 10 sec","15 beats/min for 15 sec","20 beats/min for 20 sec","5 beats/min for 5 sec"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state FHR \"Increase by 15 beats/min × 15 sec\" = FHR acceleration.  Why A wrong: 10 beats/min for 10 sec is not the stated criterion.  Why C wrong: 20 beats/min for 20 sec is not stated.  Why D wrong: 5 beats/min for 5 sec is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"The presence of fetal heart rate accelerations indicates:","options":["A healthy fetus","Fetal hypoxia","Fetal acidosis","Fetal anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state FHR acceleration \"Indicates: HEALTHY FETUS.\"  Why B wrong: Hypoxia is indicated by absent variability, not accelerations.  Why C wrong: Acidosis is linked to absent variability, not accelerations.  Why D wrong: Anemia is linked to the sinusoidal pattern."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"An NST is typically indicated when a pregnant woman presents with which complaint?","options":["Increased fetal movement","Decreased fetal movement","Frequent hiccups","Excess weight gain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Whenever a pregnant female complains of decreased fetal movement → Do Nonstress Test (NST).\"  Why A wrong: Increased movement is not the trigger described.  Why C wrong: Hiccups are not the trigger.  Why D wrong: Weight gain is not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"During NST, in which position(s) can the pregnant woman be asked to lie? (Select all that apply)","options":["Semi-Fowler position","Recliner position","Left lateral position","Prone position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state to ask the pregnant female to lie in SEMI-FOWLER position / Recliner Position / Left Lateral Position.  Why D wrong: Prone position is not listed and is unsafe in pregnancy.  Why E wrong: Trendelenburg position is not listed for NST."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"During NST, the belt of the NST machine is tied to the mother's abdomen such that the transducer lies over the:","options":["Fetal head","Fetal back","Maternal umbilicus","Fundus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state to tie the belt such that the \"transducer lies on the fetal back.\"  Why A wrong: The head is not the placement site.  Why C wrong: The umbilicus is not the placement target.  Why D wrong: The fundus is not where the transducer is placed for FHR."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"For how long is the Nonstress Test performed (initial period)?","options":["10 minutes","20 minutes","30 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"[Test is done for 20 minutes].\"  Why A wrong: 10 minutes is not stated.  Why C wrong: 30 minutes is the biophysical/Manning duration, not the initial NST.  Why D wrong: 60 minutes is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"During the NST, whenever the mother feels fetal movement she is instructed to:","options":["Change position","Press a button","Cough","Hold her breath"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in the 20 minutes, whenever she feels fetal movement \"She will press a button on left.\"  Why A wrong: Changing position is not the instruction.  Why C wrong: Coughing is not the instruction.  Why D wrong: Holding breath is not the instruction."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"When the mother presses the button during NST, what appears on the graph?","options":["A dot","An arrow representing fetal movement","A red line","Nothing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"An arrow will come on the graph (Represent fetal movement).\"  Why A wrong: The notes specify an arrow, not a dot.  Why C wrong: A red line is not described.  Why D wrong: Something (an arrow) does appear."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In a normal fetus, whenever there is fetal movement, the FHR should:","options":["Decrease by 15 beats/min","Increase (accelerate) by 15 beats/min × 15 sec","Stay exactly the same","Drop below 110 beats/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in a NORMAL fetus, FHR should increase (FHR acceleration) by 15 beats/min × 15 sec.  Why A wrong: A decrease would be abnormal.  Why C wrong: FHR should change (accelerate), not stay flat.  Why D wrong: Dropping below 110 is bradycardia, abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"During a 20-minute NST, obtaining 2 or more accelerations is termed:","options":["Nonreactive NST","Reactive (Normal) NST","Abnormal NST","Positive NST"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state getting 2 or more accelerations within 20 minutes is \"Called Normal NST/Reactive NST.\"  Why A wrong: Nonreactive is when accelerations are inadequate.  Why C wrong: This finding is normal, not abnormal.  Why D wrong: \"Positive NST\" is not the term used here."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"After obtaining a reactive (normal) NST, what is the appropriate next step?","options":["Repeat NST for 40 minutes","Send the patient back and reassure her everything is normal","Perform an emergency cesarean","Start oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Next stop: \"Send the patient back. Tell her everything is NORMAL.\"  Why A wrong: Repeating for 40 minutes is done for a nonreactive tracing.  Why C wrong: Emergency cesarean is not indicated for a normal NST.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not indicated after a reactive NST."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"If fewer than 2 accelerations occur in the first 20 minutes of NST, what is the immediate next step?","options":["Declare the NST nonreactive immediately","Repeat the NST for 20 more minutes","Do a biophysical profile immediately","Deliver the baby"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if <2 accelerations in 20 minutes → NEXT STEP → \"Repeat NST for 20 more minutes.\"  Why A wrong: Nonreactive is declared only after the full 40 minutes.  Why C wrong: Biophysical profile comes only after a nonreactive result.  Why D wrong: Delivery is not the immediate next step here."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"If fewer than 2 accelerations are obtained over a total of 40 minutes of NST, the test is called:","options":["Reactive NST","Nonreactive/Abnormal NST","Normal NST","Negative NST"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state total NST for 40 minutes with <2 accelerations is \"Called: Nonreactive NST/Abnormal NST.\"  Why A wrong: Reactive requires ≥2 accelerations.  Why C wrong: This finding is abnormal, not normal.  Why D wrong: \"Negative NST\" is not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"What is the total duration of NST after which persistent <2 accelerations is labeled nonreactive?","options":["20 minutes","30 minutes","40 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Total NST = 40 minutes; <2 accelerations in 40 minutes = nonreactive.  Why A wrong: 20 minutes is only the first phase.  Why B wrong: 30 minutes is the biophysical duration, not NST total.  Why D wrong: 60 minutes is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"After a nonreactive (abnormal) NST, which confirmatory test should be performed next?","options":["Contraction stress test","Biophysical score (Biophysical Profile)","Amniocentesis","Repeat NST after 1 week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state after nonreactive NST → NEXT STEP → CONFIRMATORY TEST: \"DO BIOPHYSICAL SCORE.\"  Why A wrong: Contraction stress test is not the stated confirmatory step here.  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis is not the next step described.  Why D wrong: Repeating in 1 week applies to a normal biophysical score, not a nonreactive NST."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Which of the following is an abnormal/non-reassuring finding on NST? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bradycardia (<110) or tachycardia (≥160)","Absent/decreased beat-to-beat variability","Sinusoidal heart wave pattern","<2 accelerations in 20 minutes (and total 40 minutes)","2 or more accelerations in 20 minutes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes' \"Abnormal findings on NST (non-reassuring)\" box lists bradycardia/tachycardia, absent/decreased variability, sinusoidal pattern, and <2 accelerations in 20 (then 40) minutes.  Why E wrong: 2 or more accelerations in 20 minutes is the normal/reactive finding, not abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the NST abnormal-findings note, the values quoted for bradycardia and tachycardia respectively are:","options":["<100 and >150","<110 and ≥160","<120 and ≥170","<90 and ≥180"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Bradycardia/Tachycardia (<110) (≥160).\"  Why A wrong: <100 and >150 are not the stated cutoffs.  Why C wrong: <120 and ≥170 are incorrect.  Why D wrong: <90 and ≥180 are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"The single MOST important parameter assessed on NST is:","options":["Amniotic fluid volume","Fetal heart rate parameters (FHR)","Maternal blood pressure","Fetal breathing movements"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes emphasize \"Most important: Parameter on NST = Parameter\" referring to the fetal heart rate parameters that the NST evaluates.  Why A wrong: Amniotic fluid is a biophysical profile parameter, not the NST parameter.  Why C wrong: Maternal BP is not the NST's key parameter.  Why D wrong: Fetal breathing movements belong to the biophysical profile, not NST."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"NST should be done in which of the following situations? (Select all that apply)","options":["All pregnant women who complain of decreased fetal movement","All high-risk pregnancies","Only in labor","Only postnatally"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state NST should be done in (1) all pregnant females who complain of decreased fetal movement and (2) all high-risk pregnancies.  Why C wrong: The notes do not restrict NST to labor.  Why D wrong: NST is antenatal, not a postnatal test."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"At what gestational age does one begin performing routine NST?","options":["≥28 weeks","≥32 weeks","≥36 weeks","≥24 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Time to begin: ≥32 weeks: have to do NST.\"  Why A wrong: ≥28 weeks is not the stated threshold.  Why C wrong: ≥36 weeks is too late per the notes.  Why D wrong: ≥24 weeks is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In a routine high-risk pregnancy, what is the interval between NSTs?","options":["Daily","Weekly (every week)","Monthly","Every 6 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Interval between NST: Weekly (every week do NST in high Risk Pregnancy).\"  Why A wrong: Daily is not the standard interval.  Why C wrong: Monthly is too infrequent.  Why D wrong: Every 6 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In a very high-risk pregnancy (e.g., severe preeclampsia, eclampsia), how often can NST be done?","options":["Once a month","Twice weekly (every 72 hours)","Once daily","Only at delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for very high-risk pregnancy (severe preeclampsia, eclampsia) \"You can also do NST twice weekly (every 72 hours).\"  Why A wrong: Monthly is too infrequent for very high risk.  Why C wrong: Once daily is not what the notes state.  Why D wrong: NST is not restricted to delivery."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Which conditions are given as examples of \"very high risk pregnancy\" requiring more frequent NST? (Select all that apply)","options":["Severe preeclampsia","Eclampsia","Common cold","Mild first-trimester nausea"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give severe preeclampsia and eclampsia as examples of very high-risk pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Common cold is not a listed high-risk condition.  Why D wrong: Mild first-trimester nausea is not high-risk."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"The Biophysical Score is also known as the:","options":["Bishop score","Manning score","Apgar score","Silverman score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes title the section \"Bio Physical Score/Manning Score.\"  Why A wrong: Bishop score assesses cervical ripeness for induction.  Why C wrong: Apgar score assesses the newborn at birth.  Why D wrong: Silverman score assesses neonatal respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"The biophysical profile (Manning score) is performed using which modality?","options":["CTG only","USG (ultrasound)","MRI","X-ray"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Biophysical Score is \"Done by: USG.\"  Why A wrong: CTG (NST) is only one component; the biophysical profile itself is done by USG.  Why C wrong: MRI is not used.  Why D wrong: X-ray is not used."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"For how long is the biophysical profile (Manning score) performed?","options":["20 minutes","30 minutes","40 minutes","10 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the biophysical score is \"Done for: 30 minutes.\"  Why A wrong: 20 minutes is the initial NST duration.  Why C wrong: 40 minutes is the total NST duration.  Why D wrong: 10 minutes is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"How many parameters are assessed in the biophysical profile/Manning score?","options":["3 parameters","4 parameters","5 parameters","6 parameters"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the biophysical score looks at \"5 parameters.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: 3, 4, and 6 do not match; the notes specify 5 parameters."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the biophysical profile, each parameter is scored as which of the following?","options":["0 (abnormal) or 2 (normal)","0 (abnormal) or 1 (normal)","1 (abnormal) or 5 (normal)","−1 or +1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state each parameter is given a score of either (2) if NORMAL or '0' if ABNORMAL.  Why B wrong: The normal score is 2, not 1.  Why C wrong: The scoring is 0/2, not 1/5.  Why D wrong: Negative scores are not used."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"What is the total maximum score of the biophysical profile (Manning score)?","options":["8/10","10/10","5/5","12/12"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With 5 parameters scored 0–2 each, total maximum = 10/10 as stated in the notes.  Why A wrong: 8/10 is a possible score, not the maximum.  Why C wrong: 5/5 does not match the 0–2 scoring of 5 parameters.  Why D wrong: 12/12 is not the maximum."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Which of the following are the 5 parameters of the biophysical profile (per the \"Toppers Always Make Brief Notes\" mnemonic)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fetal tone","Amniotic fluid (USG measure)","Fetal movement","Fetal breathing movements","NST (reactivity)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic gives Toppers=Fetal tone, Always=Amniotic fluid, Make=Fetal Movement, Brief=Breathing movements, Notes=NST — all five parameters.  Why no distractor: All five listed options are the correct parameters; none is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the biophysical profile mnemonic, \"Toppers\" stands for which parameter?","options":["Fetal tone","Fetal movement","Amniotic fluid","NST"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Toppers = Fetal tone.\"  Why B wrong: Fetal movement corresponds to \"Make.\"  Why C wrong: Amniotic fluid corresponds to \"Always.\"  Why D wrong: NST corresponds to \"Notes.\""},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"How is amniotic fluid measured for the biophysical profile per the mnemonic (\"Always\")?","options":["By USG measure — single largest vertical pocket","By palpation","By fundal height","By maternal weight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Always = Amniotic fluid = USG measure (Single largest vertical Pocket).\"  Why B wrong: Palpation is not the method.  Why C wrong: Fundal height is a clinical estimate, not the biophysical measure.  Why D wrong: Maternal weight is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the biophysical profile, what number of fetal movements is considered NORMAL?","options":["≥1","≥2","≥3","≥5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Make = Fetal Movement (≥3 = Normal).\"  Why A wrong: ≥1 is the criterion for breathing movements, not fetal movement.  Why B wrong: ≥2 is not the stated criterion.  Why D wrong: ≥5 is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the biophysical profile, what number of fetal breathing movements is considered NORMAL?","options":["≥1","≥3","≥5","≥2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Brief = Breathing movements (≥1 = NORMAL).\"  Why B wrong: ≥3 is the fetal movement criterion, not breathing.  Why C wrong: ≥5 is not stated.  Why D wrong: ≥2 is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the biophysical profile mnemonic, \"Notes = NST\" is scored as normal when the NST is:","options":["Reactive","Nonreactive","Absent","Sinusoidal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Notes = NST (Reactive = NORMAL).\"  Why B wrong: Nonreactive is abnormal.  Why C wrong: Absent is not the normal criterion.  Why D wrong: Sinusoidal is an abnormal FHR pattern."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"How many methods are described for measuring amniotic fluid with USG?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"To measure Amniotic fluid with USG there are 2 ways\" — AFI and single largest vertical pocket.  Why A/C/D wrong: Only 2 methods are listed."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"What is the normal (adequate) range of the Amniotic Fluid Index (AFI)?","options":["2–8 cm","5–24 cm","8–18 cm","10–30 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state AFI Normal: 5–24 cm (adequate).  Why A wrong: 2–8 cm is the normal range for the single largest vertical pocket, not AFI.  Why C wrong: 8–18 cm is not the stated AFI range.  Why D wrong: 10–30 cm is not the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Using the Amniotic Fluid Index (AFI), oligohydramnios is diagnosed when AFI is:","options":["≤5 cm","≥25 cm","5–24 cm","8–18 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Oligohydramnios = AFI ≤5 (amniotic fluid decreased).  Why B wrong: ≥25 cm is polyhydramnios.  Why C wrong: 5–24 cm is the normal range.  Why D wrong: 8–18 cm is not a listed cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Using the Amniotic Fluid Index (AFI), polyhydramnios is diagnosed when AFI is:","options":["≤5 cm","5–24 cm","≥25 cm","2–8 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Polyhydramnios = AFI ≥25 (amniotic fluid increased).  Why A wrong: ≤5 cm is oligohydramnios.  Why B wrong: 5–24 cm is normal.  Why D wrong: 2–8 cm is the single largest vertical pocket normal range."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Oligohydramnios reflects which change in amniotic fluid volume?","options":["Increased amniotic fluid","Decreased amniotic fluid","Normal amniotic fluid","Blood-stained fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes indicate Oligohydramnios (AFI ≤5) = amniotic fluid decreased (↓).  Why A wrong: Increased fluid is polyhydramnios.  Why C wrong: Normal fluid is AFI 5–24 cm.  Why D wrong: Fluid color is not the definition here."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"What is the normal (adequate) range of the single largest vertical pocket of amniotic fluid?","options":["5–24 cm","2–8 cm","<2 cm","≥8 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Single largest Vertical pocket Normal = 2–8 cm (adequate).  Why A wrong: 5–24 cm is the AFI normal range.  Why C wrong: <2 cm indicates oligohydramnios.  Why D wrong: ≥8 cm indicates polyhydramnios."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Using the single largest vertical pocket method, oligohydramnios is diagnosed when the pocket is:","options":["<2 cm","2–8 cm","≥8 cm","≤5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state If <2 cm = oligohydramnios (single largest vertical pocket).  Why B wrong: 2–8 cm is normal.  Why C wrong: ≥8 cm is polyhydramnios.  Why D wrong: ≤5 cm is the AFI oligohydramnios cutoff, not the vertical pocket cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"Using the single largest vertical pocket method, polyhydramnios is diagnosed when the pocket is:","options":["<2 cm","2–8 cm","≥8 cm","≥25 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state If ≥8 cm = POLYHYDRAMNIOS (single largest vertical pocket).  Why A wrong: <2 cm is oligohydramnios.  Why B wrong: 2–8 cm is normal.  Why D wrong: ≥25 cm is the AFI polyhydramnios cutoff, not the vertical pocket cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"A biophysical profile score of 10/10 or 8/10 is interpreted as normal. What is the appropriate action?","options":["Deliver immediately","Repeat the biophysical score after 1 week","Repeat the biophysical score on the same day","Take immediate action"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Score 10/10 or 8/10 = NORMAL = Repeat Biophysical score after 1 week.  Why A wrong: Immediate delivery is for very low scores (4/10 or 2/10).  Why C wrong: Same-day repeat is for a score of 6/10.  Why D wrong: Immediate action is for scores of 4/10 or 2/10."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"A biophysical profile score of 6/10 warrants which action?","options":["Repeat after 1 week","Repeat the biophysical score on the same day","Immediate delivery","No further testing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Score 6/10 = Repeat Biophysical score on the same day.  Why A wrong: Repeat after 1 week is for a normal score (8–10/10).  Why C wrong: Immediate delivery is for scores of 4/10 or 2/10.  Why D wrong: A 6/10 is equivocal and does require repeat testing."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"A biophysical profile score of 4/10 or 2/10 requires which action?","options":["Repeat after 1 week","Repeat on the same day","Take immediate action → deliver the patient","Discharge home"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Score 4/10 or 2/10 = Take immediate action → Deliver the patient.  Why A wrong: 1-week repeat is for a normal score.  Why B wrong: Same-day repeat is for a score of 6/10.  Why D wrong: Discharge is inappropriate for such low scores."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"For sinusoidal wave pattern/decreased variability, which management step regarding maternal positioning is recommended?","options":["Supine position and give O2","Left lateral position and give O2","Trendelenburg position","Right lateral position only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state to \"Let the mother lie in left lateral Position & give O2.\"  Why A wrong: Supine position can worsen aortocaval compression; the notes specify left lateral.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg is not the stated position.  Why D wrong: The notes specify left lateral, not right lateral."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"For sinusoidal wave pattern/decreased variability management, which of the following steps are recommended? (Select all that apply)","options":["Inform doctor","Stop all oxytocin","Left lateral position and give O2","Increase oxytocin infusion","Discharge the patient"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list management as: 1. Inform doctor, 2. Stop all oxytocin, 3. Left lateral position & give O2.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin should be stopped, not increased.  Why E wrong: Discharge is inappropriate for this abnormal finding."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"In the NST tracing, the upward arrows shown at the bottom of the graph represent:","options":["Uterine contractions","Fetal movements","Maternal heartbeats","Decelerations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tracing labels the upward arrows as \"Fetal Movements,\" corresponding to the button presses by the mother.  Why A wrong: Contractions are recorded separately (tocotransducer), not by these arrows.  Why C wrong: The arrows mark fetal movement, not maternal heartbeats.  Why D wrong: Decelerations are dips in the FHR line, not the movement arrows."},{"section":"Batch 073 · Nonstress Test — principle, transducer, Leopold maneuver…","question":"A biophysical profile score of 8/10 falls into which management category?","options":["Normal — repeat after 1 week","Equivocal — repeat same day","Abnormal — deliver immediately","Contraindicated"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes group 10/10 and 8/10 together as NORMAL, managed by repeating the biophysical score after 1 week.  Why B wrong: Same-day repeat applies to a score of 6/10.  Why C wrong: Immediate delivery applies to 4/10 or 2/10.  Why D wrong: There is no \"contraindicated\" category."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"The Biophysical Score (BPS) is used as a diagnostic test for which of the following conditions?","options":["Decreased fetal movement, fetal hypoxia, and uteroplacental insufficiency","Gestational diabetes and pre-eclampsia","Cervical incompetence and placenta previa","Rh incompatibility and hydrops fetalis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists the diagnostic indications for the Biophysical Score as (1) decreased fetal movement, (2) fetal hypoxia, and (3) uteroplacental insufficiency.  Why B wrong: Gestational diabetes and pre-eclampsia are not the listed BPS indications.  Why C wrong: Cervical incompetence and placenta previa are structural conditions, not BPS indications.  Why D wrong: Rh incompatibility/hydrops are assessed differently and are not the listed indications."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"The Modified Biophysical Score (Modified BPS) assesses how many parameters?","options":["5 parameters","4 parameters","2 parameters","3 parameters"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in the Modified BPS \"we see only 2 Parameters (Quick).\"  Why A wrong: The full/complete Biophysical Profile has 5 parameters, not the modified version.  Why B/D wrong: The modified version uses exactly 2 parameters, not 4 or 3."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"Which two parameters make up the Modified Biophysical Score?","options":["Fetal breathing and fetal tone","Nonstress Test and measurement of amniotic fluid by USG (Amniotic Fluid Index)","Fetal movement and fetal heart tone","Contraction stress test and Doppler"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists the two parameters as (1) Nonstress Test and (2) Measure amniotic fluid by USG: Amniotic Fluid Index.  Why A wrong: Fetal breathing and tone are part of the full biophysical profile, not the modified 2-parameter version.  Why C wrong: Fetal movement and heart tone are not the two listed modified BPS parameters.  Why D wrong: Contraction stress test and Doppler are not part of the Modified BPS."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"In the Modified Biophysical Score, amniotic fluid is measured by ultrasound using which index?","options":["Ponderal Index","Amniotic Fluid Index (AFI)","Cephalic Index","Bishop Index"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amniotic fluid is measured by USG using the Amniotic Fluid Index (AFI).  Why A wrong: The Ponderal Index relates to newborn body proportion, not amniotic fluid.  Why C wrong: The Cephalic Index relates to head shape, not amniotic fluid.  Why D wrong: The Bishop score/index assesses cervical readiness for labour, not amniotic fluid."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"The Modified Biophysical Score is best classified as which type of test?","options":["A diagnostic test","A screening test (not a diagnostic test)","A confirmatory test","A therapeutic test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note explicitly states the Modified BPS is also a screening test and NOT a diagnostic test.  Why A wrong: The full Biophysical Score is the diagnostic test; the modified version is only screening.  Why C wrong: It does not confirm a diagnosis; abnormal results prompt further diagnostic testing.  Why D wrong: It is an assessment tool, not a treatment."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"A pregnant woman complains of decreased fetal movement. According to the notes, what is the correct sequence of testing?","options":["Biophysical Score first, then NST","Screening test (NST or NST + AFI = Modified BPS) → if abnormal → diagnostic test (Biophysical Score)","Ultrasound only","Immediate cesarean section"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows: decreased fetal movement → 2 screening tests [NST or NST + AFI] = Modified BPS → if Abnormal → Diagnostic test: Biophysical Score.  Why A wrong: The order is reversed; screening comes first, diagnostic BPS follows an abnormal screen.  Why C wrong: Ultrasound (AFI) is part of the screening step, not the standalone endpoint.  Why D wrong: Cesarean is not the immediate step for decreased fetal movement per this flow."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"When a Nonstress Test is performed during labour with uterine contractions, the graph displays two lines representing which parameters?","options":["Maternal heart rate and maternal BP","Fetal heart rate (FHR) and uterine contractions","Fetal movement and amniotic fluid","Maternal temperature and fetal weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes/diagram show the two lines as FHR and Uterine contractions.  Why A wrong: Maternal heart rate/BP are not the two tracings shown.  Why C wrong: Fetal movement and amniotic fluid are not the two lines on this tracing.  Why D wrong: Temperature and fetal weight are not plotted on this graph."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"When the nonstress test is done during labour and records both fetal heart rate and uterine contractions, it is called:","options":["Cardiotocograph (CTA/CTG)","Electrocardiograph","Echocardiograph","Ultrasonograph"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"This is called CTA (Cardiotocograph).\"  Why B wrong: An ECG records the heart's electrical activity, not fetal heart rate with contractions.  Why C wrong: Echocardiography images cardiac structures, not this labour tracing.  Why D wrong: Ultrasonography images tissues; it is not this specific dual-tracing test."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"How many transducers are used in cardiotocography (CTG)?","options":["1 transducer","2 transducers","3 transducers","4 transducers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"In CTG: We use 2 Transducers.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: CTG uses exactly two transducers—one for fetal heart rate and one for uterine contractions."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"During CTG, where should the transducer used to record the fetal heart rate be placed?","options":["Near the fundus of the uterus","Near the fetal back","Over the maternal umbilicus","Over the symphysis pubis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fetal heart rate transducer is placed near the fetal back.  Why A wrong: Near the fundus is where the uterine contraction transducer is placed.  Why C/D wrong: These sites are not specified for the FHR transducer."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"During CTG, where should the transducer used to record uterine contractions be placed?","options":["Near the fetal back","Near the fundus of the uterus (where contractions are maximum)","Over the cervix","Over the lower uterine segment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the uterine contraction transducer is placed near the fundus of the uterus, where maximum contractions occur.  Why A wrong: Near the fetal back is for the fetal heart rate transducer.  Why C/D wrong: The specified site is the fundus, not the cervix or lower segment."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"What is the full form of CTG?","options":["Cardiotocogram","Cardiotachogram","Cerebrotocogram","Cardiotomograph"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the full form of CTG is Cardiotocogram.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansions of CTG."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the notes, when is CTG ideally done?","options":["During the first trimester in all patients","During labour (in all patients ideally)","Only before conception","Only during the postpartum period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states CTG is done during labour, in all patients ideally.  Why A wrong: It is a labour/intrapartum test, not a first-trimester test.  Why C/D wrong: It is not done before conception or postpartum."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"The procedure of CTG is essentially the same as which test?","options":["Contraction stress test","Nonstress Test","Amniocentesis","Doppler velocimetry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Procedure is same like Non stress test.\"  Why A wrong: While related, the notes specify the procedure resembles the NST, not the CST.  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis is an invasive sampling procedure, unlike CTG.  Why D wrong: Doppler velocimetry measures blood flow and is a different procedure."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"The parameters of CTG are the same as the NST plus which additional parameter that may be present?","options":["Acceleration","Deceleration","Variability","Baseline rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state CTG parameters are same as NST plus one additional parameter which can be present: Deceleration.  Why A wrong: Acceleration is already a part of the standard NST assessment.  Why C/D wrong: Variability and baseline rate are components of the tracing but the notes specifically highlight deceleration as the added parameter."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the physiology in the notes, when the uterus contracts, what happens to the pressure inside the uterus and the volume of blood reaching the fetus?","options":["Pressure decreases and blood volume increases","Pressure increases and volume of blood coming to the fetus decreases","Both pressure and blood volume increase","Both pressure and blood volume decrease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows: uterus contracts → pressure inside uterus ↑ → volume of blood coming to fetus ↓.  Why A wrong: Pressure rises (not falls) and blood volume falls (not rises).  Why C wrong: Blood volume to the fetus decreases, not increases.  Why D wrong: Pressure increases, it does not decrease."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"When the uterus contracts and blood flow to the fetus decreases, a normal (healthy) fetus responds how?","options":["FHR increases sharply","Can tolerate the decrease; FHR remains normal","FHR drops (deceleration)","Fetus stops moving permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a normal fetus can tolerate the decrease in blood and the FHR remains normal (N).  Why A wrong: The FHR remains normal, it does not spike.  Why C wrong: A drop in FHR (deceleration) is the response of an unhealthy fetus, not a normal one.  Why D wrong: Cessation of movement is not the described response."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"When blood flow to the fetus decreases during a contraction, an unhealthy fetus responds how?","options":["FHR remains normal","FHR will decrease — this is called deceleration","FHR accelerates","No change occurs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state an unhealthy fetus cannot tolerate the decrease, so the FHR will drop, which is called deceleration.  Why A wrong: A normal fetus keeps a normal FHR; the unhealthy fetus decelerates.  Why C wrong: The unhealthy fetus decelerates, it does not accelerate.  Why D wrong: There is a change—the FHR falls."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"By what magnitude and duration is fetal heart rate deceleration defined in the notes?","options":["FHR decreases by 15 bpm × 15 seconds with uterine contraction","FHR decreases by 10 bpm × 10 seconds","FHR decreases by 20 bpm × 20 seconds","FHR decreases by 5 bpm × 30 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define FHR deceleration as a decrease of 15 bpm × 15 secs occurring with uterine contraction.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated values of 15 bpm and 15 seconds."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"The presence of FHR deceleration during uterine contractions indicates what?","options":["The fetus is thriving","The fetus has some problem","Normal labour progress","Maternal dehydration only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state deceleration \"indicates: the fetus has some problem.\"  Why A wrong: Deceleration is a bad sign, not a sign of thriving.  Why C wrong: It signals a fetal problem, not normal progress.  Why D wrong: The notes attribute it to a fetal problem, not maternal dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the notes, which statement about FHR acceleration and deceleration is correct?","options":["Acceleration is a bad thing; deceleration is a good thing","FHR acceleration is a good thing; FHR deceleration is a bad thing","Both are bad things","Both are good things"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note explicitly states (1) FHR acceleration is a good thing and (2) FHR deceleration is a bad thing.  Why A wrong: The statement is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: The two have opposite significance—one good, one bad."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"What is described as the most important parameter on CTG?","options":["Baseline variability","FHR deceleration (present = abnormal)","Uterine contraction frequency","Fetal movement count"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most important parameter on CTG is FHR deceleration → present = abnormal.  Why A wrong: Variability is important but the notes single out deceleration as most important.  Why C wrong: Contraction frequency is recorded but is not named the most important parameter.  Why D wrong: Fetal movement count is not the CTG parameter emphasized here."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"Which of the following are listed as abnormal CTG findings? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bradycardia/Tachycardia","Absent or decreased beat-to-beat variability","Sinusoidal heart rate pattern","Absent acceleration","Late deceleration / variable deceleration"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list all five as abnormal CTG findings: bradycardia/tachycardia, absent/decreased beat-to-beat variability, sinusoidal heart rate pattern, absent acceleration, and late/variable deceleration.  Why not incorrect: There is no wrong option here—every listed finding appears on the page as abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"A sinusoidal fetal heart rate pattern on CTG is best interpreted as:","options":["A normal reassuring finding","An abnormal CTG finding","A sign of good fetal oxygenation","An artifact requiring no action"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A sinusoidal heart rate pattern is listed among the abnormal CTG findings.  Why A/C wrong: It is abnormal and not reassuring, and does not indicate good oxygenation.  Why D wrong: It is a genuine abnormal finding, not a dismissible artifact."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"How many types of deceleration are described in the notes?","options":["2 types","3 types","4 types","5 types"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table is titled \"Deceleration: 3 types\" — early, late, and variable.  Why A/C/D wrong: Exactly three types are described."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"Early deceleration is caused by (seen in) which mechanism?","options":["Head compression","Uteroplacental insufficiency","Cord compression","Maternal fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists early deceleration as seen in \"Compression\" (head compression), which is physiological.  Why B wrong: Uteroplacental insufficiency causes late deceleration.  Why C wrong: Cord compression/prolapse causes variable deceleration.  Why D wrong: Maternal fever is not the listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the notes, how should early deceleration be regarded clinically?","options":["Worst kind of deceleration","It can be seen during labour, is physiological, and is nothing to worry about","Bad kind of deceleration requiring cesarean","Always indicates cord prolapse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The importance column states early deceleration can be seen during labour, is physiological, and is nothing to worry about.  Why A wrong: Late deceleration is the worst kind, not early.  Why C wrong: Early deceleration is benign, not a cesarean indication.  Why D wrong: Cord prolapse relates to variable deceleration."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"Late deceleration is seen in (caused by) which condition?","options":["Head compression","Uteroplacental insufficiency (decreased blood supply to the fetus)","Cord compression","Maternal hypertension only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists late deceleration as seen in uteroplacental insufficiency with decreased blood supply to the fetus.  Why A wrong: Head compression causes early deceleration.  Why C wrong: Cord compression causes variable deceleration.  Why D wrong: The listed cause is uteroplacental insufficiency, not isolated maternal hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"Which type of deceleration is described as the \"worst kind of deceleration\"?","options":["Early deceleration","Late deceleration","Variable deceleration","Prolonged deceleration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The importance column marks late deceleration as the worst kind of deceleration.  Why A wrong: Early deceleration is physiological and benign.  Why C wrong: Variable deceleration is described as a \"bad\" kind but not the worst.  Why D wrong: Prolonged deceleration is not one of the three listed types."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"Variable deceleration is seen in (caused by) which condition?","options":["Head compression","Uteroplacental insufficiency","Cord compression or cord prolapse","Placental abruption only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists variable deceleration as seen in cord compression or cord prolapse.  Why A wrong: Head compression causes early deceleration.  Why B wrong: Uteroplacental insufficiency causes late deceleration.  Why D wrong: The listed cause is cord compression/prolapse, not abruption alone."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the notes, variable deceleration is regarded as:","options":["Physiological and reassuring","A bad kind of deceleration","The worst kind of deceleration","A normal variant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The importance column labels variable deceleration as a \"bad kind of deceleration.\"  Why A/D wrong: It is abnormal, not physiological/normal.  Why C wrong: Late deceleration—not variable—is the worst kind."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"For a pregnant woman with a nonreassuring FHR pattern, what is the immediate \"next step\" identified in the notes?","options":["Perform cesarean immediately","Inform the doctor/senior","Discharge the patient","Increase oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart lists the \"Next step\" as inform doctor/senior.  Why A wrong: Cesarean preparation is later in the priority steps, not the first \"next step.\"  Why C wrong: Discharge is inappropriate with a nonreassuring pattern.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin should be stopped, not increased."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"In the management (priority steps) of a nonreassuring FHR pattern, what should be done with oxytocin?","options":["Increase the oxytocin infusion","Stop the oxytocin","Start oxytocin","Replace oxytocin with prostaglandin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Priority step 2 states \"Stop oxytocin.\"  Why A/C wrong: Oxytocin causes contractions that worsen fetal compromise; it should be stopped, not started/increased.  Why D wrong: Substituting a uterotonic would worsen the situation; the note says stop oxytocin."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"In managing a nonreassuring FHR pattern, oxygen is given by mask at what flow rate?","options":["2–4 L","4–6 L","8–10 L","12–15 L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Priority step 3 states \"Give O2 by mask (8–10 L).\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 8–10 L rate."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"When repositioning a patient with a nonreassuring FHR pattern, which position is recommended?","options":["Supine position","Left lateral position","Prone position","Fowler's position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Priority step 4 states to reposition the patient into the left lateral position.  Why A wrong: Supine can worsen aortocaval compression and fetal perfusion.  Why C/D wrong: Prone and Fowler's positions are not the recommended repositioning here."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"In the setting of cord prolapse, which position should the patient be placed in?","options":["Left lateral position","Trendelenburg position","Semi-Fowler's position","Lithotomy position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify for cord prolapse = Trendelenburg position.  Why A wrong: Left lateral is the general repositioning; Trendelenburg is specifically for cord prolapse.  Why C/D wrong: These are not indicated for cord prolapse in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"Which of the following are part of the priority steps in managing a nonreassuring FHR pattern? (Select all that apply)","options":["Check the cause of the condition","Stop oxytocin","Give O2 by mask (8–10 L) and IV fluid","Reposition the patient (left lateral / Trendelenburg for cord prolapse)","Prepare the patient for cesarean"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The priority-step list contains all six actions: check cause, stop oxytocin, O2 by mask 8–10 L, reposition, IV fluid, and prepare for cesarean.  Why not incorrect: Every option reflects a listed priority step, so all are correct."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"In the priority steps for a nonreassuring FHR pattern, which intervention comes first?","options":["Prepare for cesarean","Check the cause of the condition","Give IV fluid","Give oxygen by mask"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Priority step 1 is to check the cause of the condition.  Why A wrong: Cesarean preparation is the last (step 6).  Why C wrong: IV fluid is step 5.  Why D wrong: Oxygen is step 3, not first."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"Which of the following CTG findings are listed as indications for cesarean section? (Select all that apply)","options":["Sinusoidal heart rate pattern","Variable decelerations","Late deceleration","Bradycardia","Early deceleration"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The cesarean list contains sinusoidal HR pattern, variable decelerations, late deceleration, and bradycardia.  Why E wrong: Early deceleration is physiological and benign, and is NOT listed as a cesarean indication."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"During labour, fetal distress can be detected by which two approaches according to the notes?","options":["Either CTG should be done OR FHS should be heard by stethoscope","Only continuous CTG","Only ultrasound biophysical profile","Maternal pulse and BP monitoring alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states fetal distress in labour is detected by either CTG or hearing the FHS with a stethoscope.  Why B wrong: The notes offer an alternative (stethoscope), not CTG only.  Why C wrong: The intrapartum methods listed are CTG or auscultation, not the BPP.  Why D wrong: Maternal pulse/BP does not detect fetal distress here."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"For a LOW-RISK patient in the FIRST stage of labour, how often should the fetal heart be auscultated/monitored?","options":["Every 5 minutes","Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every 60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives 1st stage labour, low-risk = after every 30 minutes.  Why A wrong: Every 5 minutes is 2nd stage, high-risk.  Why B wrong: Every 15 minutes is 1st stage high-risk (or 2nd stage low-risk).  Why D wrong: 60 minutes is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"For a HIGH-RISK patient in the FIRST stage of labour, how often should fetal monitoring be done?","options":["Every 5 minutes","Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every 45 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives 1st stage labour, high-risk = after every 15 minutes.  Why A wrong: Every 5 minutes is 2nd stage, high-risk.  Why C wrong: Every 30 minutes is 1st stage low-risk.  Why D wrong: 45 minutes is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"For a LOW-RISK patient in the SECOND stage of labour, how often should fetal monitoring be done?","options":["Every 5 minutes","Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every 20 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives 2nd stage labour, low-risk = after every 15 minutes.  Why A wrong: Every 5 minutes is 2nd stage high-risk.  Why C wrong: Every 30 minutes is 1st stage low-risk.  Why D wrong: 20 minutes is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"For a HIGH-RISK patient in the SECOND stage of labour, how often should fetal monitoring be done?","options":["Every 5 minutes","Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every 10 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives 2nd stage labour, high-risk = after every 5 minutes.  Why B wrong: Every 15 minutes is 2nd stage low-risk or 1st stage high-risk.  Why C wrong: Every 30 minutes is 1st stage low-risk.  Why D wrong: 10 minutes is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"When NST or CTG is performed by placing the transducer on the mother's abdomen, this is termed:","options":["Internal fetal monitoring","External fetal monitoring","Invasive fetal monitoring","Scalp electrode monitoring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state placing the transducer on the mother's abdomen (NST or CTG) is external fetal monitoring.  Why A/C/D wrong: Internal/invasive/scalp electrode monitoring requires rupturing membranes and placing a transducer on the fetal scalp."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"During labour, if the membranes are ruptured and the transducer is placed directly on the fetal scalp, this is termed:","options":["External fetal monitoring","Internal fetal monitoring","Non-invasive monitoring","Doppler monitoring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state rupturing membranes and putting the transducer on the fetal scalp is internal fetal monitoring.  Why A/C wrong: Placing the transducer on the maternal abdomen is external/non-invasive monitoring.  Why D wrong: This describes a scalp electrode, not Doppler."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the \"Older Concept,\" the total duration of pregnancy is measured as:","options":["38 weeks","40 weeks","42 weeks","36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The older concept diagram states total duration of pregnancy = 40 weeks (from 1st day of LMP to 40 weeks).  Why A/C/D wrong: The page specifies 40 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the Older Concept, death of the fetus BEFORE 20 weeks of pregnancy is termed:","options":["Abortion","Intrauterine death of fetus","Stillbirth","Late fetal death"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels fetal death before 20 weeks as abortion.  Why B wrong: Intrauterine death of fetus applies after 20 weeks.  Why C wrong: Stillbirth applies when the fetus dies during delivery.  Why D wrong: \"Late fetal death\" belongs to the new-concept classification, not the older-concept before-20-week category."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the Older Concept, death of the fetus AFTER 20 weeks of pregnancy is termed:","options":["Abortion","Intrauterine death of fetus (IUD)","Miscarriage","Ectopic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels fetal death after 20 weeks as intrauterine death of the fetus.  Why A/C wrong: Death before 20 weeks is called abortion/miscarriage.  Why D wrong: Ectopic is an implantation location issue, unrelated to this classification."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the notes, the 20-week point separates abortion from intrauterine fetal death. This threshold is measured from:","options":["The day of ovulation","The 1st day of the LMP","The date of conception","The date of implantation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pregnancy timeline in both concepts is measured from the 1st day of the LMP.  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes reference the 1st day of the LMP as the starting point, not ovulation, conception, or implantation."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the notes, if the fetus dies during delivery, it is termed:","options":["Abortion","Stillbirth","Intrauterine death","Neonatal death"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"If fetus dies during delivery → STILL BIRTH.\"  Why A wrong: Abortion is death before 20 weeks.  Why C wrong: Intrauterine death is death in utero after 20 weeks, not during delivery.  Why D wrong: Neonatal death occurs after live birth, not during delivery."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"In the \"New Concept\" classification, fetal death occurring earliest in gestation (from the 1st day of LMP up to ~20 weeks) is called:","options":["Early fetal death/demise","Intermediate death of fetus","Late death of fetus","Stillbirth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The new concept diagram labels the earliest interval as early fetal death/demise.  Why B wrong: Intermediate death of fetus is the middle interval.  Why C wrong: Late death of fetus is the interval nearest to 40 weeks.  Why D wrong: Stillbirth refers to death during delivery."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"In the \"New Concept\" classification of intrauterine fetal death, the three categories are:","options":["Early, intermediate, and late death of the fetus","Abortion, IUD, and stillbirth","Threatened, inevitable, and complete","Acute, subacute, and chronic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The new concept lists early fetal death/demise, intermediate death of fetus, and late death of fetus.  Why B wrong: Those are older/general terms, not the new-concept three categories.  Why C wrong: Those describe types of abortion.  Why D wrong: Those are generic time descriptors not used here."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"In a case of suspected intrauterine fetal death, what is the typical presenting complaint of the patient?","options":["Increased fetal movements","Absent fetal movements","Vaginal bleeding only","Severe headache"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the patient complains of absent fetal movements.  Why A wrong: Movements are absent, not increased.  Why C wrong: Bleeding is not the presenting complaint listed.  Why D wrong: Headache is not listed as the presentation of IUD."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"On per-abdomen examination in intrauterine fetal death, the fundal height is typically:","options":["Greater than the period of gestation","Less than the period of gestation","Equal to the period of gestation","Not assessable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fundal height = less than the period of gestation in IUD.  Why A/C wrong: The fundal height is reduced, not increased or equal.  Why D wrong: It is assessable and found to be less than expected."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"On examination in intrauterine fetal death, fetal movement and fetal heart sound (FHS) are:","options":["Both present","Both absent","Movement present, FHS absent","Movement absent, FHS present"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state on examination fetal movement = absent and FHS = absent.  Why A/C/D wrong: Both fetal movement and FHS are absent in IUD."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"Which components are assessed in the O/E (on examination) of a suspected intrauterine fetal death? (Select all that apply)","options":["Weight of the patient","Per abdomen: fundal height and fetal movement","FHS (fetal heart sound)","Ultrasound for cardiac activity","Bishop score"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list O/E as: (1) weight of patient, (2) per abdomen (fundal height less than gestation, fetal movement absent), (3) FHS absent, and ultrasound showing absent cardiac activity.  Why E wrong: Bishop score (cervical assessment for labour induction) is not part of the IUD examination listed here."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"What is described as the diagnostic (confirmatory) sign of intrauterine fetal death on ultrasound?","options":["Absent fetal movements","Absent cardiac activity on ultrasound","Reduced fundal height","Absent fetal heart sound on stethoscope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state on ultrasound, cardiac activity is absent, which is the diagnostic sign of death of the fetus.  Why A wrong: Absent movement is a symptom, not the diagnostic sign.  Why C wrong: Reduced fundal height is a clinical finding, not the diagnostic (confirmatory) sign.  Why D wrong: Absent FHS on stethoscope is suggestive but ultrasound confirming absent cardiac activity is the diagnostic sign."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"The chapter heading on page 370 links intrauterine fetal death (IUD) with which serious coagulation complication?","options":["Deep vein thrombosis (DVT)","Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC)","Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)","Haemophilia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chapter title is \"Intrauterine Fetal Death (IUD) and DIC,\" linking retained dead fetus with disseminated intravascular coagulation.  Why A wrong: DVT is a venous thrombosis, not the coagulopathy paired with IUD here.  Why C wrong: ITP is an immune platelet disorder, not the heading.  Why D wrong: Haemophilia is an inherited bleeding disorder, not associated in this heading."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"A labouring patient develops late decelerations on CTG. Based on the notes, what is the most likely underlying mechanism?","options":["Fetal head compression","Uteroplacental insufficiency","Umbilical cord compression","Maternal anxiety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Late decelerations are seen in uteroplacental insufficiency (reduced blood supply to the fetus) per the deceleration table.  Why A wrong: Head compression causes early deceleration.  Why C wrong: Cord compression/prolapse causes variable deceleration.  Why D wrong: Maternal anxiety is not a listed cause of late deceleration."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"A woman in labour shows variable decelerations with a suspicion of cord prolapse. After informing the senior, which positioning is most appropriate?","options":["Left lateral position","Trendelenburg position","Supine position","Prone position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify cord prolapse = Trendelenburg position (variable deceleration is caused by cord compression/prolapse).  Why A wrong: Left lateral is the general repositioning; cord prolapse specifically calls for Trendelenburg.  Why C/D wrong: Supine and prone are not indicated for cord prolapse."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"Which single deceleration type is physiological and requires no worry, per the notes?","options":["Late deceleration","Variable deceleration","Early deceleration","Prolonged deceleration"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Early deceleration is described as physiological and \"nothing to worry.\"  Why A wrong: Late deceleration is the worst kind.  Why B wrong: Variable deceleration is a bad kind.  Why D wrong: Prolonged deceleration is not one of the three listed types."},{"section":"Batch 074 · Antepartum fetal monitoring…","question":"According to the notes, an abnormal Modified Biophysical Score in a woman with decreased fetal movement should be followed by which definitive test?","options":["Repeat Modified BPS","Full Biophysical Score (diagnostic test)","Contraction stress test","Amniocentesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows abnormal Modified BPS → diagnostic test: Biophysical Score.  Why A wrong: Repeating a screening test is not the escalation step shown.  Why C/D wrong: The notes escalate to the full Biophysical Score, not CST or amniocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"On ultrasonography (USG), which sign of fetal death is seen when at least 7 days have passed since the death of the fetus?","options":["Robert sign","Spalding sign","Ball sign","Halo sign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if death of the fetus occurred and ≥7 days have passed, the SPALDING sign can be seen on USG, representing overlapping of the cranial bones of the fetus.  Why A wrong: Robert sign is an X-ray finding (gas in great vessels), not the ≥7 day USG sign.  Why C wrong: Ball sign relates to hyperflexion/hyperextension of the fetal spine seen ≥3–4 weeks after death.  Why D wrong: Halo sign (scalp edema) is not the sign described in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"The Spalding sign of fetal death represents which of the following?","options":["Gas in the fetal great vessels","Overlapping of the cranial (skull) bones of the fetus","Hyperextension of the fetal spine","Absence of fetal cardiac activity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spalding sign is defined as overlapping of the cranial/skull bones of the fetus, seen on USG (≥7 days) and X-ray (≥7 days after fetal death).  Why A wrong: Gas in great vessels is Robert sign.  Why C wrong: Hyperextension/hyperflexion of fetal spine is Ball sign.  Why D wrong: Absent cardiac activity confirms death but is not the Spalding sign specifically."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Which is the FIRST sign of fetal death seen on X-ray?","options":["Spalding sign","Ball sign","Robert sign","Overlapping of skull bones"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Robert sign is the 1st sign of fetal death seen on X-ray and appears within 12 hours of fetal death.  Why A wrong: Spalding sign appears later, ≥7 days after fetal death.  Why B wrong: Ball sign appears ≥3–4 weeks after fetal death.  Why D wrong: Overlapping of skull bones is the description of Spalding sign, which is not the first X-ray sign."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Robert sign of fetal death on X-ray is described as:","options":["Overlapping of skull bones","Presence of gas in the great vessels of the fetus (Aorta, IVC)","Hyperflexion of the fetal spine","Collapse of the fetal thorax"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define Robert sign as presence of gas in the great vessels of the fetus (Aorta, IVC).  Why A wrong: Overlapping of skull bones is Spalding sign.  Why C wrong: Hyperflexion of the spine is Ball sign.  Why D wrong: Thoracic collapse is not the described feature."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Robert sign is typically seen within what time period after fetal death?","options":["Within 12 hours","Within 7 days","Within 3–4 weeks","Within 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Robert sign is seen within 12 hrs of fetal death and is the first X-ray sign.  Why B wrong: 7 days is the timing of Spalding sign.  Why C wrong: 3–4 weeks is the timing of Ball sign.  Why D wrong: The notes specify 12 hours, not 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"The Ball sign of fetal death on X-ray refers to:","options":["Gas in the great vessels","Overlapping of skull bones","Hyperflexion (or) hyperextension of the fetal spine","Rounded gas-filled abdomen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes Ball sign as hyperflexion (OR) hyperextension of the fetal spine.  Why A wrong: Gas in great vessels is Robert sign.  Why B wrong: Overlapping of skull bones is Spalding sign.  Why D wrong: This is not the described feature."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"The Ball sign of fetal death is typically seen at what interval after fetal death?","options":["Within 12 hrs","≥7 days","≥3–4 weeks","Within 48 hrs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Ball sign is seen ≥3–4 weeks of fetal death.  Why A wrong: 12 hrs corresponds to Robert sign.  Why B wrong: ≥7 days corresponds to Spalding sign.  Why D wrong: 48 hrs is not a value given in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"On X-ray, the Spalding sign (overlapping of skull bones of the fetus) is seen at approximately what interval after fetal death?","options":["Within 12 hrs","≥7 days","≥3–4 weeks","Within 24 hrs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Spalding sign as seen ≥7 days after fetal death.  Why A wrong: 12 hrs is Robert sign.  Why C wrong: 3–4 weeks is Ball sign.  Why D wrong: 24 hrs is not the given value."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Arrange the chronological (earliest to latest) appearance of the three X-ray signs of fetal death.","options":["Spalding → Robert → Ball","Robert (12 hrs) → Spalding (≥7 days) → Ball (≥3–4 weeks)","Ball → Spalding → Robert","Robert → Ball → Spalding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the timings given: Robert within 12 hrs, Spalding ≥7 days, Ball ≥3–4 weeks.  Why A wrong: Robert appears earliest, not Spalding.  Why C wrong: This reverses the correct order.  Why D wrong: Ball is the latest, not before Spalding."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"After fetal death, the amniotic fluid classically takes on which color?","options":["Green (meconium-stained)","Tobacco juice color","Golden yellow","Port-wine red"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also know\" note states colors of amniotic fluid on fetal death is tobacco juice color.  Why A wrong: Green/meconium staining relates to fetal distress in a live fetus, not the described change.  Why C wrong: Golden yellow is associated with Rh isoimmunization, not this note.  Why D wrong: Port-wine is not the described color."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"If a dead fetus (after intrauterine death) remains retained in the uterus for about 3–4 weeks, which serious complication may develop?","options":["Ectopic pregnancy","Disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC)","Placenta previa","Gestational diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that a complication of intrauterine death — if the dead fetus stays in the uterus for 3–4 weeks — can lead to DIC.  Why A wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is an implantation problem, unrelated to a retained dead fetus.  Why C wrong: Placenta previa is abnormal placental location, not caused by retained dead fetus.  Why D wrong: Gestational diabetes is a metabolic disorder unrelated to fetal retention."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In grief management of intrauterine death (IUD) when adopting a \"wait and watch\" approach, what naturally occurs?","options":["The uterus ruptures spontaneously","The body automatically expels the fetus and the labor process starts","The fetus is reabsorbed completely","Immediate cesarean is required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management point 1 states that with grief management (wait and watch), the body automatically expels the fetus and the labor process starts.  Why A wrong: Uterine rupture is not the described natural outcome.  Why C wrong: Complete reabsorption is not stated.  Why D wrong: Cesarean is not the default wait-and-watch outcome."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In the management of intrauterine death, if the patient wants delivery, the appropriate nursing/medical response is to:","options":["Refuse and insist on waiting","Respect her decision and start induction of labor","Perform an emergency hysterectomy","Discharge the patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management point 2 states that if the patient wants delivery, respect her decision and start induction of labor.  Why A wrong: Refusing her decision contradicts respecting patient autonomy.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not the appropriate management for IUD delivery.  Why D wrong: Discharge is inappropriate when delivery is requested."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In intrauterine death, if the time interval between IUD and expulsion reaches 3–4 weeks, what should be done?","options":["Continue observation indefinitely","Induction of labor (otherwise it will lead to DIC)","Immediate cesarean section","Prescribe antibiotics only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management point 3 states that if the interval between IUD and expulsion is 3–4 weeks, induction of labor should be done, otherwise it will lead to DIC.  Why A wrong: Indefinite observation risks DIC.  Why C wrong: Vaginal delivery via induction is preferred; cesarean is not indicated here.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics alone do not address the retained fetus."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"What is the preferred mode of delivery in a routine intrauterine fetal death?","options":["Cesarean section","Vaginal delivery","Forceps delivery only","Vacuum extraction only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management point 4 states the mode of delivery is vaginal delivery.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is generally avoided in IUD unless another indication exists.  Why C wrong: Forceps is an instrumental assist, not the described general mode.  Why D wrong: Vacuum extraction is not the described general mode."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"A pregnant female at 32 weeks complains of loss of fetal movement; USG shows no cardiac activity and the fetus is in transverse lie. What is the next step in management?","options":["Vaginal delivery","Cesarean section","Wait and watch indefinitely","External cephalic version then vaginal delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The case teaches that in transverse lie vaginal delivery is not possible whether the baby is alive or dead, so the mode of delivery is cesarean section.  Why A wrong: Vaginal delivery is impossible in transverse lie.  Why C wrong: Indefinite waiting risks DIC and does not resolve the transverse lie.  Why D wrong: External version is not indicated for a dead fetus and is unreliable at term/transverse; cesarean is the answer."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In transverse lie, vaginal delivery is:","options":["Possible only if the fetus is alive","Possible only if the fetus is dead","Not possible whether the baby is alive or dead","Always the preferred route"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state vaginal delivery is not possible whether the baby is alive or dead in transverse lie.  Why A wrong: It is impossible even if alive.  Why B wrong: It is impossible even if dead.  Why D wrong: Cesarean is required, not vaginal delivery."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"What does the abbreviation DIC stand for in obstetric practice?","options":["Diffuse Intrauterine Contraction","Disseminated Intravascular Coagulopathy","Delayed Intrapartum Complication","Direct Internal Compression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The heading defines DIC as Disseminated Intravascular Coagulopathy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are invented expansions not matching the standard term used in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In the pathophysiology of DIC, what is the first event that begins the cascade?","options":["Bleeding occurs","Clotting mechanism is activated","Thromboembolism","Maternal death"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart begins with \"Clotting mechanism is activated,\" leading to clot formation in circulation.  Why A wrong: Bleeding occurs later after clotting factors are consumed.  Why C wrong: Thromboembolism is a downstream event.  Why D wrong: Maternal death is the terminal consequence."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In DIC, because clots are formed very rapidly in the circulation, the clotting factors become consumed. This is why DIC is also called:","options":["Consumptive coagulopathy","Constrictive coagulopathy","Congenital coagulopathy","Compensatory coagulopathy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"So, CLOTTING factors are consumed → Also called Consumptive coagulopathy.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the alternative name given in the notes; the correct term is consumptive coagulopathy."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In the DIC cascade, after clotting factors are consumed and bleeding occurs, the combination of bleeding + clotting leads to which event just before increased chances of maternal death?","options":["Amniotic fluid embolism","Thromboembolism","Placental abruption","Uterine atony"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows Bleeding + clotting → Thromboembolism → increased chances of maternal death.  Why A wrong: Amniotic fluid embolism is a cause of DIC, not this downstream step.  Why C wrong: Placental abruption is a cause of DIC, not a step in this cascade.  Why D wrong: Uterine atony is not part of this described cascade."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"DIC in obstetrics is best described as a condition that is:","options":["Harmless and self-limiting","Life threatening for the mother","Beneficial for clot control","Limited to the placenta only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart ends by stating DIC is life threatening for the mother, with increased chances of maternal death.  Why A wrong: DIC is dangerous, not self-limiting.  Why C wrong: It causes pathological clotting and bleeding, not beneficial clot control.  Why D wrong: It is a systemic (disseminated) intravascular process, not placenta-limited."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON obstetrical cause of DIC?","options":["Amniotic fluid embolism","Abruptio placentae (accidental haemorrhage)","Septic abortion","HELLP syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The list marks M/C (most common) cause as Abruptio Placentae/Accidental haemorrhage.  Why A wrong: Amniotic fluid embolism is a cause but not the most common per the notes.  Why C wrong: Septic abortion/shock is listed but not the most common.  Why D wrong: Severe preeclampsia/eclampsia/HELLP is listed but not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Which of the following is listed as an obstetrical cause of DIC? (Select all that apply)","options":["Abruptio placentae / accidental haemorrhage","Intrauterine death of fetus","Septic shock / septic abortion","Amniotic fluid embolism","Severe preeclampsia / eclampsia / HELLP syndrome"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are explicitly listed as obstetrical causes of DIC (points 1–5).  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each appears verbatim in the \"Obstetrical Causes of DIC\" list."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Which blood disorder of pregnancy is listed as an obstetric cause of DIC that involves hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets?","options":["HELLP syndrome","ITP","Thalassemia","Von Willebrand disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The list includes severe preeclampsia/eclampsia/HELLP syndrome as a cause of DIC.  Why B wrong: ITP is not listed as an obstetric cause of DIC here.  Why C wrong: Thalassemia is not listed.  Why D wrong: Von Willebrand disease is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"During assessment of DIC, which of the following is a key clinical finding?","options":["Controlled minimal bleeding","Uncontrolled bleeding","Absence of any bleeding","Localized bruising only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assessment point 1 lists uncontrolled bleeding as a key finding in DIC.  Why A wrong: Bleeding is uncontrolled, not controlled/minimal.  Why C wrong: Bleeding is present and significant.  Why D wrong: DIC bleeding is widespread, not merely localized bruising."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Presence of hematemesis, hemoptysis, and occult blood in stool in a peripartum patient should raise suspicion of which condition?","options":["Gestational diabetes","Disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC)","Hyperemesis gravidarum","Preeclampsia without bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assessment point 2 lists hematemesis, hemoptysis, and occult blood in stool as signs of DIC (widespread bleeding).  Why A wrong: Gestational diabetes does not cause these bleeding manifestations.  Why C wrong: Hyperemesis is vomiting, not GI/pulmonary bleeding.  Why D wrong: These bleeding signs point to a coagulopathy, i.e., DIC."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Hemoptysis, as an assessment finding in DIC, refers to:","options":["Vomiting of blood","Coughing up of blood","Blood in the urine","Blood in the stool"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hemoptysis is coughing up of blood, listed as a bleeding manifestation of DIC.  Why A wrong: Vomiting of blood is hematemesis.  Why C wrong: Blood in urine is hematuria (not listed).  Why D wrong: Blood in stool is occult blood/melena, a separate listed finding."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Besides bleeding manifestations, which additional clinical finding is listed in the assessment of DIC?","options":["Signs of shock","Hypertension crisis","Hyperglycemia","Fever with rash"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Assessment point 3 lists signs of shock in DIC (due to uncontrolled blood loss).  Why B wrong: Hypertensive crisis is not the listed finding.  Why C wrong: Hyperglycemia is not part of DIC assessment here.  Why D wrong: Fever with rash is not a listed DIC finding."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In the laboratory investigation of DIC, what happens to the platelet count?","options":["It increases","It decreases","It remains unchanged","It doubles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lab findings list platelet count ↓ (decreased) in DIC because platelets are consumed.  Why A wrong: Platelets decrease, not increase.  Why C wrong: The count changes (falls) in DIC.  Why D wrong: There is no doubling; platelets fall."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In DIC, the fibrinogen level typically:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Stays normal","Becomes undetectable within minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lab list states fibrinogen levels ↓ (decreased) as clotting factors are consumed.  Why A wrong: Fibrinogen falls, not rises.  Why C wrong: It is reduced, not normal.  Why D wrong: While reduced, \"undetectable within minutes\" is not the stated finding."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In DIC, the prothrombin time (PT), clotting time, and partial thromboplastin time (PTT) are:","options":["All prolonged (increased)","All shortened (decreased)","Unchanged","Only PT is prolonged"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The lab list shows prothrombin time ↑, clotting time ↑, and partial thromboplastin time ↑ (all prolonged) in DIC.  Why B wrong: They are prolonged, not shortened.  Why C wrong: They change (prolong) in DIC.  Why D wrong: All three are prolonged, not just PT."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In DIC, the level of fibrin degradation products (FDPs) is:","options":["Decreased","Increased","Absent","Normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lab list states fibrin degradation product ↑ (increased) in DIC, reflecting excess fibrinolysis.  Why A wrong: FDPs are elevated, not decreased.  Why C wrong: FDPs are present and increased.  Why D wrong: They are abnormally elevated, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Regarding clotting factors in DIC, the notes state that:","options":["All clotting factors are increased","All clotting factors are decreased (consumed)","Only factor VIII is affected","Clotting factors remain unchanged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lab list begins with \"All clotting factors ↓\" (decreased) due to consumption.  Why A wrong: They are decreased, not increased.  Why C wrong: All factors are affected, not only factor VIII.  Why D wrong: They fall in DIC."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In the management of DIC, the resuscitation step includes securing how many IV lines and giving oxygen?","options":["One (1) IV line","Two (2) IV lines and O2","Three (3) IV lines","No IV line needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The management diagram shows \"Resuscitate patient: (2) IV line, O2.\"  Why A wrong: Two IV lines are specified, not one.  Why C wrong: The notes specify two, not three.  Why D wrong: IV access is essential in resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"To replenish clotting factors in DIC, the notes recommend giving:","options":["Only normal saline","Fresh frozen plasma / cryoprecipitate","Only intravenous antibiotics","Only oral iron"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The management diagram states \"Replenish clotting factors → Give fresh frozen plasma/cryoprecipitate.\"  Why A wrong: Saline is a crystalloid; it does not replace clotting factors.  Why C wrong: Antibiotics do not replenish clotting factors.  Why D wrong: Oral iron treats anemia, not acute clotting factor deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In DIC management, if fresh frozen plasma / cryoprecipitate is NOT available, what alternative is given to replace clotting factors?","options":["Blood transfusion","Oral vitamin K","Intramuscular iron","Aspirin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram notes that if FFP/cryoprecipitate is \"not available,\" blood transfusion is given.  Why B wrong: Oral vitamin K is not the stated alternative for acute factor replacement.  Why C wrong: IM iron treats anemia, not acute clotting factor loss.  Why D wrong: Aspirin is an antiplatelet agent and would worsen bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In a patient with DIC, once resuscitation and clotting factor replacement are done, the recommended mode of delivery is:","options":["Always cesarean section","Always vaginal delivery (never do cesarean section)","Immediate hysterectomy","Delayed delivery for 4 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Remember\" note states: whenever any patient has DIC, always do vaginal delivery (never do cesarean section), and do delivery once resuscitation is done.  Why A wrong: Cesarean is explicitly to be avoided in DIC because surgical incision worsens uncontrolled bleeding.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not the recommended routine step here.  Why D wrong: Delivery should proceed after stabilization, not be delayed weeks."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Why is cesarean section avoided in a patient with DIC?","options":["It is faster than vaginal delivery","A surgical incision would worsen the uncontrolled bleeding in a coagulopathic patient","It requires no anesthesia","It prevents thromboembolism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes emphasize always vaginal delivery and never cesarean in DIC; conceptually a surgical incision creates further bleeding surfaces in a patient who cannot clot.  Why A wrong: Speed is not the reason; the concern is bleeding risk.  Why C wrong: Cesarean does require anesthesia; irrelevant here.  Why D wrong: Cesarean does not prevent thromboembolism; it increases bleeding risk."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Which two conditions are named as the most important neural tube defects (NTDs)?","options":["Anencephaly and Spina bifida","Encephalocele and Hydrocephalus","Microcephaly and Macrocephaly","Cleft lip and Cleft palate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the most important NTDs as (1) Anencephaly and (2) Spina bifida.  Why B wrong: Encephalocele/hydrocephalus are not the two named most important ones.  Why C wrong: Micro/macrocephaly are not neural tube defects in this list.  Why D wrong: Cleft lip/palate are facial clefts, not NTDs."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In anencephaly, what is the main structural problem?","options":["Absence of the vertebral column","Absence of the skull / cranial vault","Absence of the limbs","Absence of the heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the main problem in anencephaly is that the skull/cranial vault is absent.  Why A wrong: Absent vertebral column relates to spina bifida-type defects, not the cranial vault.  Why C wrong: Limb absence is not the defining feature of anencephaly.  Why D wrong: The heart is not the primary structure absent in anencephaly."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Anencephaly is described as a condition that is:","options":["Fully treatable after birth","Incompatible with life (babies die when born)","Compatible with normal life expectancy","Only a cosmetic defect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that skull features are incompatible with life, so affected babies have to die when they are born.  Why A wrong: It is not treatable; it is incompatible with life.  Why C wrong: It is not compatible with a normal lifespan.  Why D wrong: It is a lethal defect, not merely cosmetic."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"During development, the neural plate rolls into which structure that later closes at its ends (neuropores)?","options":["Notochord","Neural tube","Yolk sac","Amnion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows Neural Plate → Neural Tube, with cranial and caudal neuropores.  Why A wrong: The notochord is a separate midline structure, not the tube that forms from the neural plate.  Why C wrong: The yolk sac is an extraembryonic structure.  Why D wrong: The amnion is a fetal membrane, not derived from the neural plate."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Failure of the cranial (anterior) neuropore to fuse/close results in which defect?","options":["Spina bifida","Anencephaly","Encephalocele","Hydrocephalus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when the cranial neuropore fails to fuse/close, it results in anencephaly (also described as non-closure of the anterior neuropore).  Why A wrong: Spina bifida results from failure of the caudal/posterior neuropore.  Why C wrong: Encephalocele is not the defect specified for cranial neuropore failure here.  Why D wrong: Hydrocephalus is CSF accumulation, not neuropore closure failure."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Failure of the posterior (caudal) neuropore to close results in which defect?","options":["Anencephaly","Spina bifida","Cleft palate","Omphalocele"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when the posterior/caudal neuropore fails to close, it results in spina bifida.  Why A wrong: Anencephaly results from cranial (anterior) neuropore failure.  Why C wrong: Cleft palate is a facial defect, not caused by caudal neuropore failure.  Why D wrong: Omphalocele is an abdominal wall defect, not a neuropore closure failure."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In anencephaly, non-closure of which neuropore is responsible, leaving the brain exposed?","options":["Anterior (cranial) neuropore","Posterior (caudal) neuropore","Both neuropores equally","Neither neuropore"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anencephaly point 2 states it results from non-closure of the anterior neuropore; point 3–4 note the skull is absent so the brain is exposed and incompatible with life.  Why B wrong: The posterior neuropore relates to spina bifida.  Why C wrong: It is specifically the anterior neuropore, not both.  Why D wrong: A neuropore is definitely involved."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In anencephaly, the fetus has a defect in swallowing. This leads to an increase in amniotic fluid, producing which condition?","options":["Oligohydramnios","Polyhydramnios","Anhydramnios","Normal fluid volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The associated-problems flow chart shows: defect in swallowing → amount of amniotic fluid increases → POLYHYDRAMNIOS.  Why A wrong: Oligohydramnios is decreased fluid, the opposite of what occurs here.  Why C wrong: Anhydramnios is near-absence of fluid, not the described increase.  Why D wrong: The fluid volume is abnormally increased, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Why does a fetus with anencephaly develop polyhydramnios?","options":["Increased fetal urine output","Defect in swallowing (fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid)","Placental hyperfunction","Maternal diabetes only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart attributes polyhydramnios to a defect in swallowing, so amniotic fluid accumulates.  Why A wrong: The mechanism described is impaired swallowing, not increased urine output.  Why C wrong: Placental hyperfunction is not the described cause.  Why D wrong: Maternal diabetes can cause polyhydramnios generally, but the notes attribute this case to the swallowing defect."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In anencephaly, polyhydramnios leads to over distension of the uterus, which in turn can precipitate: (Select all that apply)","options":["Preterm labor","PROM (premature rupture of membranes)","Post-maturity syndrome","Placenta previa","Uterine fibroids"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows over distension of uterus branching to PRETERM LABOR and PROM.  Why A/B correct: Both are the two listed downstream outcomes of uterine over distension.  Why C wrong: Post-maturity is a separate consequence (from adrenal underdevelopment), not from over distension.  Why D wrong: Placenta previa is not a listed outcome here.  Why E wrong: Uterine fibroids are not a listed outcome."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In anencephaly, the fetal adrenal glands are not properly developed. Why is this significant for labor?","options":["Adrenal glands produce amniotic fluid","Fetal adrenal glands play a very important role in the initiation of labor","Adrenal glands control fetal swallowing","Adrenal glands form the fetal skull"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fetal adrenal glands play a very important role in initiation of labor; in anencephaly they are undeveloped, which can delay labor onset.  Why A wrong: Adrenal glands do not produce amniotic fluid.  Why C wrong: Swallowing is a neurological function, not adrenal.  Why D wrong: Adrenal glands do not form the skull."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Because fetal adrenal glands are undeveloped in anencephaly (impairing labor initiation), the pregnancy may become:","options":["Post-term pregnancy (>42 weeks)","Molar pregnancy","Ectopic pregnancy","Multiple pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that with undeveloped adrenal glands, labor is not initiated, leading to post-term pregnancy >42 weeks.  Why B wrong: Molar pregnancy is a trophoblastic disease, unrelated.  Why C wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is abnormal implantation, unrelated.  Why D wrong: Multiple pregnancy is unrelated to adrenal development."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"A post-term pregnancy is defined by the notes as gestation beyond how many weeks?","options":[">37 weeks",">40 weeks",">42 weeks",">44 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define post-term pregnancy as >42 weeks.  Why A wrong: >37 weeks is term, not post-term.  Why B wrong: 40 weeks is the expected due date, not post-term.  Why D wrong: The threshold given is 42, not 44 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Regarding onset of labor in anencephaly, which statement is TRUE?","options":["It always results in preterm labor only","It always results in post-term pregnancy only","It can result in EITHER preterm labor OR post-term pregnancy","Labor never occurs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in anencephaly you can get either PRETERM labor OR POST-TERM pregnancy (preterm from polyhydramnios/over distension; post-term from undeveloped adrenals).  Why A wrong: It is not only preterm.  Why B wrong: It is not only post-term.  Why D wrong: Labor does occur (though timing varies)."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"In anencephaly, which fetal part remains normal/preserved?","options":["The skull","The face","The cranial vault","The brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the part which is normal in anencephaly is the face.  Why A wrong: The skull is absent in anencephaly.  Why C wrong: The cranial vault is absent.  Why D wrong: The brain is exposed/abnormal in anencephaly."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Because the face is the normal (well-formed) part in anencephaly, what is the most common presentation at labor?","options":["Breech presentation","Face presentation","Shoulder presentation","Brow presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common presentation in anencephaly at labor is face presentation (because the face is the normal part).  Why A wrong: Breech is not the described presentation for anencephaly.  Why C wrong: Shoulder presentation is associated with transverse lie, not anencephaly specifically.  Why D wrong: Brow presentation is not the answer given in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Which congenital anomaly most commonly leads to face presentation?","options":["Spina bifida","Anencephaly","Hydrocephalus","Cleft palate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes directly state that the congenital anomaly most commonly leading to face presentation is anencephaly.  Why A wrong: Spina bifida is not linked to face presentation here.  Why C wrong: Hydrocephalus is not the anomaly given.  Why D wrong: Cleft palate does not cause face presentation."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Neural tube defects (NTDs) are most common in which fetus?","options":["Male fetus","Female fetus","Equally in both sexes","Only in twins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Other Important points on NTD\" state NTDs are most common in the female fetus.  Why A wrong: The notes specify female, not male.  Why C wrong: There is a stated female predominance.  Why D wrong: There is no restriction to twins."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"What is the approximate recurrence risk of a neural tube defect in a subsequent pregnancy according to the notes?","options":["1%","5%","25%","50%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state NTD can recur in the next pregnancy with about 5% chances.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 5% figure given in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Which supplement is recommended to PREVENT the recurrence of neural tube defects?","options":["Iron","Folic acid","Calcium","Vitamin D"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that to prevent recurrence of NTD, folic acid supplementation is given.  Why A wrong: Iron prevents anemia, not NTD.  Why C wrong: Calcium is for bone/hypertension prophylaxis, not NTD prevention.  Why D wrong: Vitamin D is not the NTD-preventing supplement described."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"For ALL pregnant females (general prophylaxis to prevent NTD), what is the recommended prophylactic folic acid dose per the notes?","options":["400 mg/day (prophylactic dose)","40 mg/day","4 g/day","4000 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the prophylactic dose as 400 mg/day for all pregnant females to prevent NTD. (Note: the standard prophylactic folic acid dose is actually 400 micrograms/day (0.4 mg); the \"mg\" in the source is a printing error for \"mcg,\" but the numeric value 400 is keyed as printed.)  Why B wrong: 40 mg/day is not the stated value.  Why C wrong: 4 g/day is far too high and not stated.  Why D wrong: 4000 mg/day is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"According to the notes, prophylactic folic acid for all pregnant females should ideally begin:","options":["At 20 weeks of pregnancy","1 month before conception","At the time of delivery","Only in the third trimester"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the prophylactic folic acid should begin 1 month before conception.  Why A wrong: 20 weeks is too late to prevent NTD (neural tube closes early).  Why C wrong: At delivery is far too late.  Why D wrong: Third trimester is too late for NTD prevention."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"For a pregnant female with a history of a previous neural tube defect baby OR who is on an antiepileptic drug like valproic acid, what is the recommended THERAPEUTIC folic acid dose?","options":["400 mcg","4 mg","40 mg","0.4 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the therapeutic dose as 4 mg for high-risk women (previous NTD baby or on valproic acid) to prevent recurrence.  Why A wrong: 400 mcg is the prophylactic (general) dose, not the therapeutic dose.  Why C wrong: 40 mg is not the stated dose.  Why D wrong: 0.4 mg equals the prophylactic dose, not the therapeutic 4 mg."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"For high-risk pregnant females (previous NTD baby or on valproic acid), when should the therapeutic (4 mg) folic acid be started?","options":["1 month before conception","3 months before conception","At 12 weeks of pregnancy","After delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the therapeutic dose (4 mg) should be given 3 months before conception in high-risk women.  Why A wrong: 1 month before conception is the timing for the general prophylactic dose.  Why C wrong: 12 weeks is too late for neural tube closure.  Why D wrong: After delivery is too late."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Which antiepileptic drug is specifically named as an indication for the higher (therapeutic 4 mg) folic acid dose because of increased NTD risk?","options":["Valproic acid","Paracetamol","Metformin","Amoxicillin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes name valproic acid as the antiepileptic drug requiring the therapeutic 4 mg folic acid dose.  Why B wrong: Paracetamol is an analgesic, not an antiepileptic linked to NTD.  Why C wrong: Metformin is an antidiabetic, not the named drug.  Why D wrong: Amoxicillin is an antibiotic, not linked to NTD here."},{"section":"Batch 075 · Signs of fetal death on USG and X-ray with timings…","question":"Which statement correctly contrasts the prophylactic versus therapeutic folic acid regimens for NTD prevention?","options":["Prophylactic: 4 mg starting 1 month before conception; Therapeutic: 400 mcg starting 3 months before","Prophylactic: 400 mcg (0.4 mg) starting 1 month before conception for all women; Therapeutic: 4 mg starting 3 months before conception for high-risk women","Both use 4 mg starting at 12 weeks","Both use 400 mcg starting at delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This matches the notes — all pregnant females get the prophylactic dose (400; standard mcg) beginning 1 month before conception, while high-risk women get the therapeutic 4 mg beginning 3 months before conception.  Why A wrong: It swaps the doses and timings.  Why C wrong: Doses and timing are wrong; 12 weeks is too late.  Why D wrong: Delivery timing is too late and dosing/regimen is wrong."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What is the prophylactic dose of folic acid recommended for all pregnant females?","options":["4 mg/day","400 mcg/day","500 mcg/day","60 mg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The prophylactic dose of folic acid given to all pregnant females is 400 mcg/day.  Why A wrong: 4 mg/day is the therapeutic dose for women with a previous history of an NTD baby, not the routine prophylactic dose.  Why C wrong: 500 mcg is the dose of folic acid in government (Anemia Mukt Bharat) tablets, not the prophylactic recommendation.  Why D wrong: 60 mg is the dose of iron, not folic acid."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"A pregnant woman with a previous history of a baby with neural tube defect (NTD) should receive what therapeutic dose of folic acid?","options":["400 mcg/day","500 mcg/day","4 mg/day","40 mg/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The therapeutic dose of folic acid for a pregnant female with a previous history of an NTD baby is 4 mg/day.  Why A wrong: 400 mcg/day is the routine prophylactic dose, insufficient for high-risk women.  Why B wrong: 500 mcg is the government tablet dose.  Why D wrong: 40 mg/day is not a recommended folic acid dose."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What dose of folic acid is given to a diabetic pregnant female (without previous NTD history)?","options":["400 mcg","4 mg","500 mcg","5 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The dose of folic acid given to diabetic pregnant females is 400 mcg (same as the standard prophylactic dose).  Why B wrong: 4 mg is reserved for a diabetic woman with a previous NTD history.  Why C wrong: 500 mcg is the Anemia Mukt Bharat tablet dose.  Why D wrong: 5 mg is not the stated dose."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What dose of folic acid is given to a diabetic pregnant female with a previous history of NTD?","options":["400 mcg/day","4 mg/day","500 mcg/day","60 mg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A diabetic pregnant female with previous history of NTD is given 4 mg/day of folic acid.  Why A wrong: 400 mcg is for a diabetic without previous NTD history.  Why C wrong: 500 mcg is the government-supplied tablet dose.  Why D wrong: 60 mg is the iron dose."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What is the dose of folic acid in the tablets distributed by the Government of India under the Anemia Mukt Bharat Programme?","options":["400 mcg","4 mg","500 mcg","60 mg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The folic acid dose in government tablets under the Anemia Mukt Bharat Programme is 500 mcg.  Why A wrong: 400 mcg is the general prophylactic dose, not the tablet content.  Why B wrong: 4 mg is the therapeutic dose for high-risk women.  Why D wrong: 60 mg is the iron dose in the tablet."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Under the Anemia Mukt Bharat Programme, what is the dose of iron in each government-distributed tablet?","options":["30 mg","60 mg","100 mg","500 mcg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The dose of iron in the Anemia Mukt Bharat tablet is 60 mg.  Why A wrong: 30 mg is not the stated dose.  Why C wrong: 100 mg is not the stated dose.  Why D wrong: 500 mcg refers to folic acid, not iron."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which government programme distributes iron and folic acid tablets free of cost to pregnant women in India?","options":["Janani Suraksha Yojana","Anemia Mukt Bharat Programme","Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana","Reproductive and Child Health Programme"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Anemia Mukt Bharat Programme is the Government of India initiative distributing iron & folic acid tablets free of cost.  Why A wrong: JSY is a cash-incentive scheme promoting institutional delivery.  Why C wrong: PMMVY is a maternity benefit cash transfer scheme.  Why D wrong: RCH is a broader programme, not the specific IFA distribution scheme named here."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"From which month of pregnancy should the iron and folic acid tablet regimen ideally be started?","options":["1st month","2nd month","4th month","6th month"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: One tablet of iron plus one tablet of folic acid should be taken by all pregnant females starting from the 4th month of pregnancy.  Why A/B wrong: In the first trimester (1st–2nd month) folic acid alone is used; combined iron-folic acid starts from the 4th month.  Why D wrong: The regimen starts at the 4th month, not the 6th."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"For a minimum of how many days should iron and folic acid tablets ideally be continued during pregnancy?","options":["90 days","120 days","180 days","270 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: IFA tablets should be continued for a minimum of 180 days throughout pregnancy.  Why A/B wrong: 90 and 120 days are shorter than the recommended minimum.  Why D wrong: 270 days is longer than the stated minimum requirement."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"For how many days after delivery should iron and folic acid tablets be continued to replenish iron stores?","options":["42 days","90 days","180 days","365 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: IFA tablets should also be continued for 180 days after delivery to replenish the iron stores.  Why A wrong: 42 days is the traditional puerperium/postpartum period, not the IFA continuation period stated.  Why B/D wrong: 90 and 365 days are not the stated durations."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What is the purpose of continuing iron and folic acid tablets for 180 days after delivery?","options":["To increase breast milk production","To replenish the iron stores","To prevent postpartum depression","To promote uterine involution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The stated purpose of continuing IFA for 180 days postpartum is to replenish the iron stores.  Why A wrong: IFA is not given to increase milk production.  Why C wrong: It does not target postpartum depression.  Why D wrong: It is not aimed at uterine involution."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"When should routine folic acid supplementation ideally be stopped in pregnancy?","options":["At conception","3 months after conception","At the end of the first month","At delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the flow diagram, folic acid supplementation is stopped 3 months after conception, after which (from the 4th month onwards) combined iron & folic acid tablets are given.  Why A wrong: Folic acid is continued after conception, not stopped at conception.  Why C wrong: It is stopped at 3 months, not at the end of the first month.  Why D wrong: It is stopped at 3 months, well before delivery."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which screening test is done in the mother's blood to screen for neural tube defects?","options":["Maternal serum acetylcholinesterase","Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)","Maternal serum hCG","Maternal serum estriol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The screening test for NTD checks the level of alpha-fetoprotein in the mother's blood — Maternal Serum Alpha Feto Protein (AFP).  Why A wrong: Acetylcholinesterase is the best biochemical marker (confirmatory in amniotic fluid), not the maternal blood screening test.  Why C/D wrong: hCG and estriol are part of Down syndrome screening, not the specific NTD blood screen described."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"At what gestational age is maternal serum AFP screening for neural tube defects performed?","options":["8–10 weeks","11–14 weeks","15–20 weeks (ideally 16–18 weeks)","24–28 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal serum AFP is checked at 15–20 weeks, ideally 16–18 weeks.  Why A wrong: 8–10 weeks is too early for this screen.  Why B wrong: 11–14 weeks is the nuchal translucency window, not the AFP screen.  Why D wrong: 24–28 weeks is later than the stated screening period."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In a neural tube defect, how does the maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein level change?","options":["Decreased","Raised (increased)","Unchanged","Absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In NTD, AFP is raised (↑).  Why A wrong: AFP is decreased in conditions such as maternal diabetes and Down syndrome, not NTD.  Why C/D wrong: AFP is clearly elevated, not unchanged or absent, in NTD."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"If the maternal serum AFP level is raised, which diagnostic test is done to confirm a neural tube defect?","options":["Repeat maternal serum AFP","Level 2 USG / Targeted scan / TIFFA / Anomaly scan","Chorionic villus sampling","Non-stress test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If AFP is raised, the diagnostic (confirmatory) test is a Level 2 USG, also called targeted scan / TIFFA / anomaly scan.  Why A wrong: A repeat AFP is a screening measure, not the confirmatory diagnostic test.  Why C wrong: CVS is used for chromosomal/genetic diagnosis, not the stated NTD anatomy scan.  Why D wrong: NST assesses fetal wellbeing in later pregnancy, not NTD diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Is maternal serum AFP considered the best biochemical marker for neural tube defect, and why?","options":["Yes, because it is highly specific for NTD","No, because it can be raised in many other conditions","Yes, because it is only produced in NTD","No, because it is never elevated in NTD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AFP is NOT the best biochemical marker for NTD because it can be raised in many other conditions (abdominal wall defects, etc.).  Why A/C wrong: AFP is not specific; it rises in several other conditions.  Why D wrong: AFP IS elevated in NTD; the issue is lack of specificity, not lack of elevation."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What is the best (most specific) biochemical marker for neural tube defect?","options":["Alpha-fetoprotein","Acetylcholinesterase","Human chorionic gonadotropin","Estriol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best biochemical marker for NTD is acetylcholinesterase.  Why A wrong: AFP is a screening marker but is not the best because it can rise in many conditions.  Why C/D wrong: hCG and estriol are markers used in Down syndrome/triple screen, not the best NTD marker."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"At what gestational age can anencephaly be diagnosed earliest on ultrasound?","options":["8 weeks","10 weeks","14 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anencephaly can be diagnosed earliest on USG at 10 weeks.  Why A wrong: 8 weeks is too early for the earliest diagnosis stated.  Why C wrong: 14 weeks is when it can be BEST diagnosed, not the earliest.  Why D wrong: 20 weeks is later than the earliest diagnosis point."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"At what gestational age can anencephaly be BEST diagnosed on ultrasound?","options":["10 weeks","12 weeks","14 weeks","18 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Anencephaly can be best diagnosed on USG at 14 weeks.  Why A wrong: 10 weeks is the earliest possible diagnosis, not the best.  Why B/D wrong: 12 and 18 weeks are not the stated best-diagnosis time."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which of the following are ultrasound signs of anencephaly? (Select all that apply)","options":["Mickey mouse sign","FROG eye sign","Lemon sign","Banana sign","Turtle sign"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The USG signs of anencephaly are the Mickey mouse sign and the FROG eye sign.  Why C/D wrong: Lemon sign and banana sign are USG signs of spina bifida, not anencephaly.  Why E wrong: Turtle sign is a clinical sign of shoulder dystocia, not a USG sign of anencephaly."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"On ultrasound, scalloping of the frontal bone in a fetus is described as which sign of spina bifida?","options":["Banana sign","Lemon sign","Mickey mouse sign","Frog eye sign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Scalloping of the frontal bone = LEMON sign, a USG sign of spina bifida.  Why A wrong: The banana sign refers to the cerebellum appearance, not the frontal bone.  Why C/D wrong: Mickey mouse and frog eye signs are seen in anencephaly."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"The \"banana sign\" seen on ultrasound in spina bifida refers to the abnormal appearance of which structure?","options":["Frontal bone","Cerebellum","Orbits","Spine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the banana sign, the cerebellum looks like a banana — a USG sign of spina bifida.  Why A wrong: The frontal bone produces the lemon sign.  Why C wrong: The orbits are involved in anencephaly signs (frog eye).  Why D wrong: The banana sign describes cerebellar shape, not the spine directly."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What is the management of anencephaly if it is diagnosed in a pregnant female?","options":["Continue pregnancy with close monitoring","Termination of pregnancy","Intrauterine surgery","Elective cesarean at term"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The management of anencephaly diagnosed in pregnancy is termination of pregnancy (anencephaly is incompatible with life).  Why A wrong: The condition is lethal, so pregnancy is not continued to term.  Why C wrong: No intrauterine surgery corrects anencephaly.  Why D wrong: Cesarean at term is not the management for a lethal anomaly."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is primarily a protein synthesized by which of the following?","options":["The placenta","The fetus","The maternal liver","The corpus luteum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AFP is a protein synthesized by the fetus.  Why A wrong: The placenta synthesizes hCG and other hormones, not AFP as the primary source.  Why C wrong: It is fetal, not maternal, liver that produces AFP.  Why D wrong: The corpus luteum produces progesterone, not AFP."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In the early weeks of pregnancy, alpha-fetoprotein is synthesized mainly by which fetal structure?","options":["Fetal GIT","Fetal liver","Fetal yolk sac","Fetal kidney"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In early weeks of pregnancy, AFP is synthesised by the fetal yolk sac.  Why A/B wrong: The fetal GIT and liver produce AFP in the later weeks, not early.  Why D wrong: The kidney is not a stated source of AFP."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In the later weeks of pregnancy, alpha-fetoprotein is synthesized mainly by which fetal structures?","options":["Fetal yolk sac","Fetal GIT and liver","Placenta and amnion","Fetal brain and spinal cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In later weeks of pregnancy, AFP is synthesised by the fetal GIT and liver.  Why A wrong: The yolk sac is the early source, not the later one.  Why C/D wrong: Placenta, amnion, brain and spinal cord are not the stated later-week sources."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Why can maternal serum AFP levels be used as a screening test for neural tube defects?","options":["AFP is produced only in the maternal liver","Fetal AFP enters the amniotic fluid and then passes into the mother's blood","AFP crosses from mother to fetus","AFP is produced by the placenta and secreted into maternal blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal AFP comes into the amniotic fluid and then goes into the mother's blood; that is why maternal serum AFP is used as a screening test for NTD.  Why A wrong: AFP is of fetal origin, not maternal liver.  Why C wrong: The flow is fetus → amniotic fluid → mother, not mother to fetus.  Why D wrong: AFP is fetal, not placental, in origin."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which of the following conditions cause an INCREASE in AFP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Neural tube defect","Abdominal wall defect (gastroschisis/omphalocele)","Pilonidal sinus","Down syndrome","Maternal diabetes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: AFP is raised in NTD, abdominal wall defects (gastroschisis, omphalocele), and pilonidal sinus.  Why D wrong: In Down syndrome baby, AFP is decreased, not increased.  Why E wrong: In a diabetic mother, AFP is decreased."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"An elevated AFP due to an abdominal wall defect can be caused by which of the following?","options":["Gastroschisis and omphalocele","Pilonidal sinus","Down syndrome","Molar pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Abdominal wall defects that raise AFP include gastroschisis and omphalocele.  Why B wrong: Pilonidal sinus is listed separately (also raises AFP) but is not an abdominal wall defect.  Why C/D wrong: Down syndrome and molar pregnancy are associated with decreased AFP."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which of the following conditions are associated with a DECREASED AFP level? (Select all that apply)","options":["Diabetic mother","Down syndrome baby","Abortion","Molar pregnancy","Neural tube defect"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: AFP is decreased in a diabetic mother, a Down syndrome baby, abortion, and molar pregnancy.  Why E wrong: NTD is associated with raised, not decreased, AFP."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"A low maternal serum AFP is most classically associated with which chromosomal condition in the fetus?","options":["Neural tube defect","Down syndrome","Gastroschisis","Omphalocele"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AFP is decreased when the baby has Down syndrome.  Why A/C/D wrong: NTD, gastroschisis, and omphalocele all cause raised AFP."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"According to the notes, macrosomia is defined as a fetal weight of:","options":["≥2.5 kg or >50th percentile","≥3 kg or >75th percentile","≥4 kg or >90th percentile","≥4.5 kg or >95th percentile"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Macrosomia is defined as fetal weight ≥4 kg or >90th percentile.  Why A/B wrong: These thresholds are below the stated definition.  Why D wrong: 4.5 kg is the cesarean threshold in a diabetic mother, not the definition of macrosomia."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Macrosomia is more commonly seen in which fetuses?","options":["Female fetuses","Male fetuses","Twin pregnancies","Preterm fetuses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Macrosomia is more common in male fetuses.  Why A wrong: It is not more common in female fetuses.  Why C/D wrong: Twins and preterm fetuses are not the stated predisposition."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which pregnancy condition increases the chances of macrosomia due to prolonged intrauterine growth?","options":["Preterm pregnancy","Post-term pregnancy","Ectopic pregnancy","Molar pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Macrosomia is seen if pregnancy continues beyond term (post-term pregnancy = increased chances). (Note: the source writes \"beyond 72 weeks,\" which appears to be a typographical error for 42 weeks, the standard post-term threshold; the intended concept is post-term pregnancy.)  Why A wrong: Preterm pregnancy is associated with lower birth weight, not macrosomia.  Why C/D wrong: Ectopic and molar pregnancies do not proceed to a large-for-gestational-age fetus."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Maternal which condition is a major risk factor for fetal macrosomia?","options":["Anemia","Diabetes","Hypertension","Hypothyroidism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Macrosomia is seen if the mother is diabetic.  Why A wrong: Maternal anemia is not the stated cause of macrosomia.  Why C/D wrong: Hypertension and hypothyroidism are not the stated risk factors here."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What is the correct pathophysiological sequence leading to macrosomia in a diabetic mother?","options":["Maternal hypoglycemia → decreased fetal insulin → decreased growth","Maternal hyperglycemia → fetal hyperglycemia → fetal pancreas secretes more insulin → increased growth → macrosomia","Maternal hyperglycemia → decreased fetal insulin → decreased growth","Maternal hypoglycemia → fetal hyperglycemia → macrosomia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mother has hyperglycemia → fetus also hyperglycemic → fetal pancreas secretes more insulin → increased growth → macrosomia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the direction of glucose/insulin changes described in the flow chart."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which hormone is needed for fetal growth and drives macrosomia in a hyperglycemic fetus?","options":["Growth hormone","Insulin","Thyroxine","Cortisol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hormone needed for fetal growth is insulin; excess fetal insulin promotes macrosomia.  Why A wrong: Growth hormone is the main postnatal growth driver, not the key fetal-growth hormone described here.  Why C/D wrong: Thyroxine and cortisol are not identified as the fetal-growth hormone in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In macrosomia, the excess fetal weight is deposited mainly in which regions, leading to shoulder dystocia?","options":["Head and neck","Shoulder and trunk area","Legs and feet","Arms and hands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In macrosomia, excess weight gets deposited in the shoulder and trunk area, causing difficulty delivering the shoulders (shoulder dystocia).  Why A wrong: The head is not the primary site of fat deposition described.  Why C/D wrong: Legs, feet, arms, and hands are not the stated deposition areas."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Difficulty delivering the shoulders during vaginal delivery of a macrosomic baby is called:","options":["Cephalopelvic disproportion","Shoulder dystocia","Breech presentation","Cord prolapse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Difficulty in delivering the shoulders during vaginal delivery is called shoulder dystocia.  Why A wrong: CPD refers to a size mismatch between fetal head and maternal pelvis.  Why C wrong: Breech is a malpresentation, not shoulder difficulty.  Why D wrong: Cord prolapse is umbilical cord descent, unrelated to shoulder difficulty."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In fetal growth disturbances (IUGR and macrosomia), which organ is the FIRST organ affected?","options":["Brain","Abdomen (liver)","Kidney","Heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first organ affected in both IUGR and macrosomia is the abdomen (liver).  Why A wrong: The brain is the LAST organ affected.  Why C/D wrong: Kidney and heart are not identified as first or last affected organs."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In IUGR and macrosomia, which organ is the LAST organ affected (relatively spared)?","options":["Liver","Abdomen","Brain","Lungs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The last organ affected in IUGR/macrosomia is the brain (brain-sparing effect).  Why A/B wrong: The abdomen/liver is the first organ affected, not the last.  Why D wrong: Lungs are not identified as the last organ affected."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which ultrasound parameter is checked to assess fetal growth and is the best parameter to detect both macrosomia and IUGR?","options":["Biparietal diameter","Femur length","Abdominal circumference","Head circumference"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The abdominal circumference is the USG parameter used to assess fetal growth and is the best parameter to detect both macrosomia and IUGR (as the liver/abdomen is affected first).  Why A/B/D wrong: Biparietal diameter, femur length, and head circumference are not the best growth-detection parameter stated here."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What is the usual (preferred) mode of delivery in macrosomia?","options":["Elective cesarean section","Vaginal delivery","Forceps delivery","Vacuum extraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The usual mode of delivery in macrosomia is vaginal delivery (cesarean is reserved for specific weight thresholds).  Why A wrong: Cesarean is only for defined high weights, not routine.  Why C/D wrong: Forceps and vacuum are not the stated default mode of delivery."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In a DIABETIC mother, cesarean section is indicated for macrosomia when the estimated fetal weight is at least:","options":["4 kg","4.5 kg","5 kg","5.5 kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cesarean section is indicated only if fetal weight is ≥4.5 kg in a diabetic mother.  Why A wrong: 4 kg is the definition of macrosomia but not the cesarean threshold.  Why C wrong: 5 kg is the threshold for a nondiabetic mother.  Why D wrong: 5.5 kg is not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In a NON-DIABETIC mother, cesarean section is indicated for macrosomia when the estimated fetal weight is at least:","options":["4 kg","4.5 kg","5 kg","6 kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Cesarean section is indicated if fetal weight is ≥5 kg in a nondiabetic mother.  Why A wrong: 4 kg defines macrosomia but is not the cesarean threshold.  Why B wrong: 4.5 kg is the diabetic-mother threshold.  Why D wrong: 6 kg is higher than the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"How is shoulder dystocia defined in terms of the delay after delivery of the head?","options":["Delay of ≥30 seconds in delivering the shoulder","Delay of ≥1 minute in delivering the shoulder after delivery of the head","Delay of ≥5 minutes in delivering the shoulder","Delay of ≥10 minutes in delivering the shoulder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Shoulder dystocia is difficulty in delivery of shoulders, defined as a delay of ≥1 minute in delivering the shoulder after delivery of the head.  Why A/C/D wrong: These time intervals do not match the stated ≥1 minute criterion."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which clinical sign, where the fetal head retracts back against the perineum, is characteristic of shoulder dystocia?","options":["Turtle sign","Lemon sign","Banana sign","Mickey mouse sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The turtle sign (head tries to go back inside) is characteristic of shoulder dystocia.  Why B/C wrong: Lemon and banana signs are USG signs of spina bifida.  Why D wrong: Mickey mouse sign is a USG sign of anencephaly."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which of the following are predisposing factors for shoulder dystocia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Macrosomia","Male fetus","Post-term pregnancy","Diabetic mother","Preterm delivery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Predisposing factors are macrosomia and its associations — male fetus, post-term pregnancy, and diabetic mother.  Why E wrong: Preterm delivery (smaller baby) is not a predisposing factor for shoulder dystocia."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In the HELPERR mnemonic for shoulder dystocia, what does the first \"H\" stand for?","options":["Hyperextend the head","Call for help","Hydrate the mother","Halt pushing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: H = call for help — the first step when shoulder dystocia occurs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated meaning of H."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In the HELPERR mnemonic for shoulder dystocia, what does the first \"E\" stand for?","options":["Enter your hands","Episiotomy","Evacuate the bladder","Extend the legs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: E = episiotomy (the notes write \"liver episiotomy,\" an OCR/typographical rendering; the intended step is episiotomy to enlarge the outlet).  Why A wrong: \"Enter your hands\" corresponds to the later E (internal rotation maneuvers), not this first E.  Why C/D wrong: Bladder emptying and leg extension are not the stated meaning of this E."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In the HELPERR mnemonic, the leg maneuver (L) refers to which named maneuver?","options":["Zavanelli maneuver","McRoberts maneuver","Wood's corkscrew maneuver","Gaskin maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: L = leg maneuver = McRoberts maneuver.  Why A wrong: Zavanelli is the last-resort maneuver (pushing the head back and doing cesarean).  Why C wrong: Wood's corkscrew is the \"Enter/rotate\" (E) maneuver.  Why D wrong: Gaskin (all-fours) maneuver is the \"Roll over\" (R) step."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"How many assistants are required to perform the McRoberts maneuver?","options":["1 assistant","2 assistants","3 assistants","4 assistants"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The McRoberts maneuver requires 2 assistants.  Why A/C/D wrong: These numbers do not match the stated requirement of 2 assistants."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"During the McRoberts maneuver, how should the assistants position the mother's legs?","options":["Extend the legs straight and abduct them","Flex the legs against her abdomen and abduct them","Cross the legs at the ankles","Elevate the legs in lithotomy without flexion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each assistant flexes the legs of the mother against her abdomen and abducts them.  Why A wrong: The legs are flexed, not extended straight.  Why C/D wrong: These do not describe the McRoberts positioning."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which maneuver is the FIRST, best, and most effective maneuver performed in shoulder dystocia?","options":["Wood's corkscrew maneuver","McRoberts maneuver","Gaskin maneuver","Zavanelli maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The McRoberts maneuver is the first maneuver done in shoulder dystocia and is the best/most effective maneuver.  Why A/C wrong: Wood's corkscrew and Gaskin are later steps, not the first/best.  Why D wrong: Zavanelli is the last-resort maneuver when everything else fails."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In the HELPERR mnemonic, what does \"P\" stand for in the management of shoulder dystocia?","options":["Push on the fundus","Give suprapubic pressure and again perform McRoberts maneuver","Perform cesarean section","Pull the fetal head firmly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: P = give suprapubic pressure and again perform the McRoberts maneuver.  Why A wrong: Fundal pressure is NEVER given in shoulder dystocia.  Why C wrong: Cesarean is not the P step.  Why D wrong: Forceful traction on the head is not the P step."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which type of pressure is CORRECT to apply in shoulder dystocia, and which is contraindicated?","options":["Fundal pressure is given; suprapubic pressure is avoided","Suprapubic pressure is given; fundal pressure is never given","Both suprapubic and fundal pressure are given","Neither suprapubic nor fundal pressure is given"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suprapubic pressure is given in shoulder dystocia; fundal pressure is never given (it worsens impaction).  Why A wrong: This reverses the correct and contraindicated pressures.  Why C wrong: Fundal pressure is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Suprapubic pressure is indeed used."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In the HELPERR mnemonic, \"Enter your hands and rotate the shoulder of the baby\" corresponds to which named maneuver(s)?","options":["Wood's corkscrew maneuver / Rubin maneuver","McRoberts maneuver","Gaskin maneuver","Zavanelli maneuver"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: E (Enter hands and rotate the baby's shoulder) = Wood's corkscrew maneuver / Rubin maneuver.  Why B wrong: McRoberts is the leg maneuver (L).  Why C wrong: Gaskin is the roll-over (all-fours) maneuver (R).  Why D wrong: Zavanelli is the last-resort maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In the HELPERR mnemonic, the first \"R\" stands for which action?","options":["Roll over the patient","Removal of the arm of the baby","Rotate the head","Repeat McRoberts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: R = Removal of the (posterior) arm of the baby.  Why A wrong: Roll over the patient is the second R (Gaskin maneuver).  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated meaning of the first R."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"In the HELPERR mnemonic, \"Roll over the patient onto all four limbs\" is known as which maneuver?","options":["Rubin maneuver","Wood's corkscrew maneuver","Gaskin maneuver","McRoberts maneuver"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Rolling the patient onto all four limbs (all-fours) is the Gaskin maneuver (the second R).  Why A/B wrong: Rubin and Wood's corkscrew are the internal rotation (E) maneuvers.  Why D wrong: McRoberts is the leg maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"If all standard maneuvers fail in shoulder dystocia, which is the last-resort maneuver?","options":["McRoberts maneuver","Gaskin maneuver","Zavanelli maneuver","Wood's corkscrew maneuver"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If everything fails, the last step is the Zavanelli maneuver.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are earlier maneuvers in the sequence, not the last resort."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What does the Zavanelli maneuver involve?","options":["Rotating the anterior shoulder 180 degrees","Pushing the delivered head back into the uterus and then performing cesarean section","Fracturing the clavicle to reduce shoulder width","Applying fundal pressure with McRoberts positioning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Zavanelli maneuver involves pushing the delivered head back into the uterus and then doing a cesarean section.  Why A wrong: That describes the Wood's/Rubin rotation maneuvers.  Why C wrong: Cleidotomy (clavicle fracture) is a destructive procedure not done nowadays.  Why D wrong: Fundal pressure is contraindicated in shoulder dystocia."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which of the following steps are NEVER done in shoulder dystocia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Giving fundal pressure","Fracturing the clavicle of the baby (cleidotomy)","Giving suprapubic pressure","Performing McRoberts maneuver","Rotating the baby's shoulder (Wood's corkscrew)"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Never give fundal pressure and never fracture the clavicle of the baby (cleidotomy) — destructive procedures are not done nowadays.  Why C/D/E wrong: Suprapubic pressure, McRoberts, and Wood's corkscrew are all appropriate maneuvers used in shoulder dystocia."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Intentional fracturing of the fetal clavicle to relieve shoulder dystocia is known by which term?","options":["Symphysiotomy","Cleidotomy","Craniotomy","Decapitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deliberate fracture of the clavicle is called cleidotomy, a destructive procedure that is never done nowadays.  Why A wrong: Symphysiotomy is division of the pubic symphysis.  Why C wrong: Craniotomy is perforation of the fetal skull.  Why D wrong: Decapitation is a different destructive procedure."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What is the most common MATERNAL complication of shoulder dystocia?","options":["Uterine rupture","Postpartum hemorrhage (PPH)","Amniotic fluid embolism","Puerperal sepsis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common maternal complication of shoulder dystocia is PPH (postpartum hemorrhage).  Why A/C/D wrong: While these are obstetric emergencies, they are not the stated most common maternal complication of shoulder dystocia."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"What is the most common FETAL complication of shoulder dystocia?","options":["Brachial plexus injury","Intracranial hemorrhage","Facial nerve palsy","Cephalohematoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common fetal complication of shoulder dystocia is brachial plexus injury.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most common fetal complication."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Brachial plexus injury from shoulder dystocia most commonly leads to which condition?","options":["Klumpke's paralysis","Erb's paralysis","Total plexus palsy","Facial palsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Brachial plexus injury in shoulder dystocia most commonly leads to Erb's paralysis.  Why A wrong: Klumpke's paralysis (lower plexus) is less common in this context.  Why C wrong: Total plexus palsy is not the stated typical outcome.  Why D wrong: Facial palsy is unrelated to the brachial plexus."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"Which nerve is damaged when the McRoberts maneuver is performed?","options":["Femoral nerve","Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The McRoberts maneuver can damage the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (from extreme hip flexion).  Why A/C/D wrong: The femoral, sciatic, and obturator nerves are not the stated injured nerve."},{"section":"Batch 076 · Folic acid prophylactic/therapeutic doses…","question":"During a normal delivery, which fetal bone is most commonly fractured?","options":["Femur","Humerus","Clavicle","Skull"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: During normal delivery, the bone of the baby most commonly fractured is the clavicle.  Why A/B wrong: Femur and humerus fractures are far less common in normal delivery.  Why D wrong: Skull fracture is not the most common delivery-related fracture."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"How many chromosomes are present in a normal human somatic (body) cell?","options":["23","44","46","47"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A normal human cell contains 46 chromosomes (the diploid number).  Why A wrong: 23 is the haploid number found only in gametes (sperm/ova).  Why B wrong: 44 refers only to the autosomes, excluding the 2 sex chromosomes.  Why D wrong: 47 chromosomes represents trisomy (one extra chromosome), an abnormal count."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Of the 46 chromosomes in a human cell, how many are autosomes?","options":["22","23","44","46"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: There are 44 autosomes, arranged as 22 pairs, which are the same in males and females.  Why A wrong: 22 is the number of autosome pairs, not the total number of autosomes.  Why B wrong: 23 is the haploid gamete number, not the autosome count.  Why D wrong: 46 is the total chromosome count including the 2 sex chromosomes."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"How many pairs do the 44 autosomes form?","options":["11 pairs","22 pairs","23 pairs","46 pairs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 44 autosomes are arranged as 22 pairs of chromosomes.  Why A wrong: 11 pairs would be only 22 chromosomes.  Why C wrong: 23 pairs (46 chromosomes) includes the sex-chromosome pair as well.  Why D wrong: 46 pairs would be 92 chromosomes, which is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"How many sex chromosomes are present in a normal human cell?","options":["1","2","22","44"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A normal human cell has 2 sex chromosomes (XY in males, XX in females).  Why A wrong: Gametes carry 1 sex chromosome; body cells carry 2.  Why C wrong: 22 is the number of autosome pairs.  Why D wrong: 44 is the number of autosomes."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"What is the correct chromosomal genotype (karyotype) of a normal human male?","options":["46 (XX)","44 + XX","44 + XY or 46 (XY)","45 (XO)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A male has 44 autosomes plus XY sex chromosomes, written as 44 + XY or 46(XY).  Why A wrong: 46(XX) is the female karyotype.  Why B wrong: 44 + XX is the female autosome + sex chromosome pattern.  Why D wrong: 45(XO) is Turner syndrome (monosomy), an abnormal karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"What is the correct chromosomal genotype (karyotype) of a normal human female?","options":["44 + XY","46 (XX) / 44 autosomes + XX","47 (XXY)","45 (XO)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A female has 44 autosomes plus XX sex chromosomes = 46(XX).  Why A wrong: 44 + XY is the male karyotype.  Why C wrong: 47(XXY) is Klinefelter syndrome, an abnormal male karyotype.  Why D wrong: 45(XO) is Turner syndrome, an abnormal female karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The complete genetic makeup of an individual is referred to as the:","options":["Phenotype","Genotype","Karyotype","Haplotype"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Genotype is defined on the page as the genetic makeup of the individual.  Why A wrong: Phenotype is the observable physical expression, not the genetic makeup.  Why C wrong: Karyotype is the photographic arrangement of all chromosomes, not the definition of genetic makeup.  Why D wrong: Haplotype refers to a set of linked alleles, not the overall genetic makeup."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"All human cells contain 46 chromosomes EXCEPT which cells?","options":["Nerve cells","Male and female gametes (sperm and ova)","Skin cells","Liver cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All human cells have the diploid number 46 except the male and female gametes (sperm & ova), which have 23.  Why A wrong: Nerve cells are somatic cells and contain 46 chromosomes.  Why C wrong: Skin cells are somatic and diploid (46).  Why D wrong: Liver cells are somatic and diploid (46)."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"How many chromosomes are present in a human gamete (sperm or ova)?","options":["46","44","23","22"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sperm and ova each contain 23 chromosomes, which is the haploid number.  Why A wrong: 46 is the diploid number of somatic cells.  Why B wrong: 44 is the number of autosomes in a somatic cell.  Why D wrong: 22 is the number of autosomes in a gamete (excluding its single sex chromosome)."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The chromosome number 46 found in body cells is termed the:","options":["Haploid number","Diploid number","Triploid number","Aneuploid number"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 46 chromosomes represents the diploid number (full set of pairs).  Why A wrong: Haploid (23) is half the diploid number, seen in gametes.  Why C wrong: Triploid is 69 chromosomes, an abnormal count.  Why D wrong: Aneuploid is any deviation from the normal number."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Half of the diploid chromosome number is called the:","options":["Diploid number","Haploid number","Triploid number","Tetraploid number"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Half of the diploid number is called the haploid number (23 chromosomes).  Why A wrong: Diploid is the full number (46).  Why C wrong: Triploid is three sets (69).  Why D wrong: Tetraploid is four sets, not half of diploid."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In summary, all human cells (except sperm/ova) contain how many pairs of chromosomes?","options":["22 pairs","23 pairs","44 pairs","46 pairs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 46 chromosomes = 23 pairs of chromosomes (the diploid number).  Why A wrong: 22 pairs counts only the autosomes.  Why C wrong: 44 pairs would be 88 chromosomes.  Why D wrong: 46 pairs would be 92 chromosomes."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Any deviation from the normal chromosome number of 46 (either more or less than 46) is called:","options":["Euploidy","Aneuploidy","Polyploidy","Monoploidy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aneuploidy is any deviation (more or less) from the normal number of 46 chromosomes.  Why A wrong: Euploidy refers to a normal, balanced set of chromosomes.  Why C wrong: Polyploidy is a general term for extra whole sets, but the page specifically names aneuploidy for any deviation.  Why D wrong: Monoploidy refers specifically to a single set, not any deviation."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"During McRobert's maneuver, which nerve is most likely to get damaged?","options":["Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh","Sciatic nerve"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh is the nerve that gets damaged during McRobert's maneuver.  Why A wrong: The femoral nerve is not the nerve identified on the page.  Why B wrong: The obturator nerve is not implicated in McRobert's maneuver here.  Why D wrong: The sciatic nerve is not the answer given."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In shoulder dystocia, which is considered the first, best, and most effective maneuver?","options":["Wood's screw maneuver","McRobert's maneuver","Zavanelli maneuver","Rubin maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: McRobert's maneuver is the first, best, and most effective maneuver in shoulder dystocia.  Why A wrong: Wood's screw is a secondary rotational maneuver, not the first choice.  Why C wrong: Zavanelli (cephalic replacement) is a last-resort maneuver.  Why D wrong: Rubin maneuver is another rotational technique, not the first/best/most effective."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"During a normal (vaginal) delivery, which bone of the baby is most commonly fractured?","options":["Femur","Humerus","Clavicle","Skull"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The clavicle is the bone most commonly fractured in the baby during normal delivery.  Why A wrong: The femur is not the most commonly fractured bone during delivery.  Why B wrong: The humerus may fracture but is less common than the clavicle.  Why D wrong: Skull fractures are uncommon in normal vaginal delivery."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In aneuploidy, the \"haploid number\" condition corresponds to how many chromosomes?","options":["23","45","47","69"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The haploid number under the aneuploidy chart is chromosome no. = 23.  Why B wrong: 45 corresponds to monosomy (one less chromosome).  Why C wrong: 47 corresponds to trisomy (one extra chromosome).  Why D wrong: 69 corresponds to triploidy."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Triploidy (triploid number) is characterized by a chromosome number of:","options":["23","45","47","69"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Triploidy has a chromosome number of 69 (three complete sets of 23).  Why A wrong: 23 is the haploid number.  Why B wrong: 45 is monosomy.  Why C wrong: 47 is trisomy."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Trisomy is defined as the presence of one extra chromosome, giving a total chromosome number of:","options":["45","46","47","69"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Trisomy = 1 extra chromosome, total number = 47.  Why A wrong: 45 (one less) is monosomy.  Why B wrong: 46 is the normal count.  Why D wrong: 69 is triploidy."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Monosomy is characterized by one less chromosome, giving a total chromosome number of:","options":["45","46","47","23"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monosomy = 1 less chromosome, total number = 45.  Why B wrong: 46 is the normal count.  Why C wrong: 47 is trisomy (one extra).  Why D wrong: 23 is the haploid number."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Chromosome number 23 is normally seen only in sperm and ova. If it is seen in any other cell, it indicates:","options":["Normal karyotyping","Abnormal karyotyping","Triploidy","Trisomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 23 chromosomes in any cell other than sperm/ova is abnormal karyotyping.  Why A wrong: It is abnormal, not normal, in a somatic cell.  Why C wrong: Triploidy is 69, not 23.  Why D wrong: Trisomy is 47, not 23."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Karyotyping is best defined as:","options":["Measurement of hormone levels","A photograph of all chromosomes arranged together","Counting only the sex chromosomes","A study of gene sequences"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Karyotyping is defined on the page as a photo of all chromosomes together.  Why A wrong: Hormone measurement is not karyotyping.  Why C wrong: Karyotyping arranges all chromosomes, not just the sex chromosomes.  Why D wrong: Gene sequencing is a different molecular technique."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In a normal male karyotype, which sex chromosomes are present?","options":["XX","XY","XO","XXY"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal male karyotype shows X and Y sex chromosomes.  Why A wrong: XX is the normal female karyotype.  Why C wrong: XO is Turner syndrome (monosomy).  Why D wrong: XXY is Klinefelter syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In a normal female karyotype, which sex chromosomes are present?","options":["XY","XX","XO","XYY"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal female karyotype shows two X chromosomes (XX).  Why A wrong: XY is the male karyotype.  Why C wrong: XO is Turner syndrome.  Why D wrong: XYY is an abnormal male karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In a normal karyotype, chromosomes are numbered from pair 1 to pair 22 for the autosomes plus the sex-chromosome pair. What is the highest numbered autosome pair?","options":["20","21","22","23"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The autosome pairs are numbered 1 through 22 in the karyotype, followed by the sex chromosomes (X, Y).  Why A wrong: The numbering continues beyond 20 to 22.  Why B wrong: 21 is not the highest autosome number; 22 is.  Why D wrong: There is no numbered autosome pair 23; the 23rd pair is the sex chromosomes."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"What is the investigation of choice (IOC) for detecting aneuploidy?","options":["Ultrasonography","Karyotyping","Amniocentesis alone","Serum beta-hCG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Karyotyping is the IOC for detecting aneuploidy.  Why A wrong: Ultrasonography can suggest markers but is not the IOC for aneuploidy.  Why C wrong: Amniocentesis is a sampling method; the analysis (karyotyping) is the IOC.  Why D wrong: Serum beta-hCG is a screening marker, not confirmatory."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Down syndrome is caused by trisomy of which chromosome, and what is its investigation of choice?","options":["Trisomy 18; ultrasound","Trisomy 13; amniocentesis","Trisomy 21; karyotyping","Monosomy X; karyotyping"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Down syndrome is Trisomy 21, and the IOC/best investigation for diagnosing it is karyotyping.  Why A wrong: Trisomy 18 is Edward syndrome, not Down; ultrasound is not the IOC.  Why B wrong: Trisomy 13 is Patau syndrome, not Down.  Why D wrong: Monosomy X is Turner syndrome, not Down syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"A sperm carries 23 chromosomes. This is made up of:","options":["23 autosomes + 0 sex chromosome","22 autosomes + 1 sex chromosome","21 autosomes + 2 sex chromosomes","44 autosomes + 1 sex chromosome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A sperm has 22 autosomes plus 1 sex chromosome = 23.  Why A wrong: A gamete always carries one sex chromosome, not zero.  Why C wrong: A gamete carries only one sex chromosome, not two.  Why D wrong: 44 autosomes is the diploid autosome count, not the haploid gamete count."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The two types of sperm produced (with respect to sex chromosome) are:","options":["22 + X and 22 + Y","44 + XX and 44 + XY","23 + X and 23 + Y","46 + X and 46 + Y"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A sperm is either 22 + X or 22 + Y (22 autosomes plus one sex chromosome).  Why B wrong: These are diploid patterns, not the haploid sperm composition.  Why C wrong: A sperm has a total of 23 (22 autosomes + 1 sex), not 23 autosomes plus a sex chromosome.  Why D wrong: 46 is the diploid number, not the sperm's chromosome count."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"An ovum (ova) always carries which sex chromosome?","options":["Only Y","Only X","Either X or Y","XX"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ovum is always 22 + X; it carries only the X sex chromosome.  Why A wrong: The ovum never carries a Y chromosome.  Why C wrong: Only the sperm can carry either X or Y; the ovum carries only X.  Why D wrong: XX (two X's) is the diploid female pattern, not a single haploid ovum."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The sex of the baby is determined by which parent's gamete?","options":["The ovum from the mother","The sperm from the father","Both equally","Neither; it is random after birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Since the ovum always carries X, the sperm (carrying X or Y) determines the sex; a Y-bearing sperm gives a male and an X-bearing sperm gives a female.  Why A wrong: The ovum always provides X and cannot vary the outcome.  Why C wrong: The father's sperm is the deciding factor, not both equally.  Why D wrong: Sex is determined at fertilization, not randomly after birth."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"A sperm carrying 22 + Y fertilizes an ovum (22 + X). What is the sex and karyotype of the resulting zygote?","options":["Female, 44 + XX","Male, 44 + XY","Female, 44 + XY","Male, 44 + XX"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 22 + Y sperm + 22 + X ovum = 44 + XY, which is a male zygote.  Why A wrong: 44 + XX would result from an X-bearing sperm and would be female.  Why C wrong: A 44 + XY karyotype is male, not female.  Why D wrong: A Y-bearing sperm produces XY, not XX."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"A sperm carrying 22 + X fertilizes an ovum (22 + X). The resulting zygote is:","options":["Male, 44 + XY","Female, 44 + XX","Male, 46 + XX","Female, 44 + XY"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An X-bearing sperm plus an X-bearing ovum = 44 + XX, a female zygote.  Why A wrong: 44 + XY (male) requires a Y-bearing sperm.  Why C wrong: A male is XY, and 46 + XX is not a valid combination here.  Why D wrong: 44 + XY is male, not female."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Sperm and ova are collectively known as:","options":["Somatic cells","Gametes","Zygotes","Blastomeres"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sperm and ova are the gametes (male gamete and female gamete).  Why A wrong: Somatic cells are body cells, not reproductive gametes.  Why C wrong: A zygote is the cell formed after fertilization, not a gamete.  Why D wrong: Blastomeres are cells of the early dividing embryo."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Gametes are derived from which type of cells?","options":["Somatic cells","Germ cells","Ectodermal cells","Mesodermal cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gametes are derived from germ cells.  Why A wrong: Somatic cells give rise to body tissues, not gametes.  Why C wrong: The page specifies germ cells arise from endoderm of the yolk sac, not ectoderm.  Why D wrong: Mesoderm is not the stated origin of germ cells."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Germ cells are derived from which embryonic layer/structure?","options":["Ectoderm of neural tube","Endoderm of yolk sac","Mesoderm of somites","Trophoblast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Germ cells are derived from the endoderm of the yolk sac.  Why A wrong: The neural tube ectoderm gives rise to the nervous system.  Why C wrong: Mesodermal somites form muscle and skeleton, not germ cells.  Why D wrong: The trophoblast forms placental tissue, not germ cells."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The process by which a germ cell is transformed into a gamete is called:","options":["Fertilization","Gametogenesis","Implantation","Capacitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The germ-cell-to-gamete process is called gametogenesis.  Why A wrong: Fertilization is the fusion of sperm and ovum.  Why C wrong: Implantation is embedding of the blastocyst in the endometrium.  Why D wrong: Capacitation is the functional maturation of sperm in the female tract."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The male germ cell is called:","options":["Oogonia","Spermatogonia","Spermatid","Spermatozoa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The male germ cell is called spermatogonia.  Why A wrong: Oogonia is the female germ cell.  Why C wrong: A spermatid is a later stage produced during spermatogenesis.  Why D wrong: Spermatozoa are the mature sperm, not the germ cell."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The female germ cell is called:","options":["Spermatogonia","Oogonia","Ovum","Oocyte"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The female germ cell is called oogonia.  Why A wrong: Spermatogonia is the male germ cell.  Why C wrong: The ovum is the mature female gamete, not the germ cell.  Why D wrong: An oocyte is an intermediate developmental stage, not the base germ cell named here."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The process by which sperm are formed from the male germ cell is called:","options":["Oogenesis","Spermatogenesis","Spermiogenesis","Gametogenesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogenesis is the process forming sperm from the male germ cell.  Why A wrong: Oogenesis is the female equivalent forming ova.  Why C wrong: Spermiogenesis is only the transformation of spermatids to sperm, a sub-step.  Why D wrong: Gametogenesis is the general term for gamete formation."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In spermatogenesis, spermatogonia (46, XY) divide by mitosis to form which cell?","options":["Secondary spermatocyte","Primary spermatocyte","Spermatid","Sperm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogonia undergo mitosis to form the primary spermatocyte (46, XY or 44 + XY).  Why A wrong: Secondary spermatocytes are formed later, after Meiosis 1.  Why C wrong: Spermatids form after Meiosis 2.  Why D wrong: Sperm are the final product after spermiogenesis."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"What is the chromosome number of the primary spermatocyte?","options":["23 (X)","23 (Y)","46 (XY) or 44 + XY","69"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The primary spermatocyte is diploid, 46(XY) or 44 + XY.  Why A wrong: 23(X) is a haploid secondary spermatocyte/spermatid, not the primary spermatocyte.  Why B wrong: 23(Y) is likewise a haploid later stage.  Why D wrong: 69 is a triploid abnormal number."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The primary spermatocyte undergoes which division to form secondary spermatocytes?","options":["Mitosis","Meiosis 1","Meiosis 2","Amitosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Meiosis 1 converts the primary spermatocyte into two secondary spermatocytes.  Why A wrong: Mitosis forms the primary spermatocyte from spermatogonia, not the secondary spermatocyte.  Why C wrong: Meiosis 2 acts on secondary spermatocytes to form spermatids.  Why D wrong: Amitosis is direct division, not the process here."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"What is the chromosome number of a secondary spermatocyte?","options":["46 (XY)","23 (X) or 23 (Y) [i.e., 22 + x or 22 + y]","44 + XY","69"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary spermatocytes are haploid: 23(X)/22 + x or 23(Y)/22 + y.  Why A wrong: 46(XY) is the diploid primary spermatocyte.  Why C wrong: 44 + XY is the diploid pattern.  Why D wrong: 69 is triploid."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Secondary spermatocytes undergo which division to form spermatids?","options":["Mitosis","Meiosis 1","Meiosis 2","Fertilization"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Meiosis 2 converts secondary spermatocytes into spermatids.  Why A wrong: Mitosis does not reduce or produce spermatids here.  Why B wrong: Meiosis 1 produced the secondary spermatocytes.  Why D wrong: Fertilization is unrelated to spermatid formation."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The four cells (23x, 23x, 23y, 23y) formed after Meiosis 2 in spermatogenesis are called:","options":["Primary spermatocytes","Secondary spermatocytes","Spermatids","Spermatogonia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The four haploid cells after Meiosis 2 are spermatids.  Why A wrong: Primary spermatocytes are the diploid precursors.  Why B wrong: Secondary spermatocytes are the two intermediate cells after Meiosis 1.  Why D wrong: Spermatogonia are the original germ cells."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Spermiogenesis is best defined as:","options":["Formation of spermatocytes from spermatogonia","Transformation of spermatids into sperms","The mitotic division of germ cells","The fusion of sperm and ovum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spermiogenesis is the transformation of spermatids into sperms.  Why A wrong: That describes an earlier phase of spermatogenesis, not spermiogenesis.  Why C wrong: Mitosis of germ cells is not spermiogenesis.  Why D wrong: Fusion of gametes is fertilization."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In mitosis, what happens to the chromosome number of the daughter cells?","options":["It becomes half","It remains the same","It doubles","It becomes one-fourth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In mitosis the chromosome number remains the same as the parent cell.  Why A wrong: Halving of chromosome number occurs in meiosis, not mitosis.  Why C wrong: The number does not double in mitotic daughter cells.  Why D wrong: It is not reduced to one-fourth."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In meiosis, what happens to the chromosome number?","options":["It remains the same","It becomes half","It doubles","It triples"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In meiosis the chromosome number becomes half (diploid to haploid).  Why A wrong: The number remaining the same describes mitosis.  Why C wrong: The number does not double.  Why D wrong: The number does not triple."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Meiosis consists of how many types of division?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Meiosis has 2 types of division — Meiosis 1 and Meiosis 2.  Why A wrong: There is more than one meiotic division.  Why C/D wrong: There are only two meiotic divisions, not three or four."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"During which meiotic division does the chromosome number actually become half?","options":["Meiosis 1","Meiosis 2","Both equally","Neither"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In Meiosis 1 the chromosome number becomes half; in Meiosis 2 it remains the same.  Why B wrong: In Meiosis 2 the chromosome number remains the same (sister chromatids separate).  Why C wrong: Only Meiosis 1 halves the number.  Why D wrong: Meiosis 1 does reduce the number."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In spermatogenesis, chromosome number becomes half when the primary spermatocyte changes into which cell?","options":["Spermatogonia","Secondary spermatocyte","Spermatid","Mature sperm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chromosome number becomes half when the primary spermatocyte changes into the secondary spermatocyte (Meiosis 1).  Why A wrong: Spermatogonia are the diploid precursors, formed before this step.  Why C wrong: Spermatids form later in Meiosis 2, after the halving has already occurred.  Why D wrong: Mature sperm are the end product, well after the reduction."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"One primary spermatocyte gives rise to how many secondary spermatocytes?","options":["1","2","4","8"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One primary spermatocyte produces 2 secondary spermatocytes.  Why A wrong: It produces two, not one.  Why C wrong: 4 is the number of spermatids/sperms formed, not secondary spermatocytes.  Why D wrong: 8 is incorrect at every stage of spermatogenesis."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"One primary spermatocyte ultimately gives rise to how many spermatids and how many sperms?","options":["2 spermatids and 2 sperms","4 spermatids and 4 sperms","8 spermatids and 8 sperms","4 spermatids and 2 sperms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One primary spermatocyte forms 4 spermatids and ultimately 4 sperms.  Why A wrong: The count is four, not two.  Why C wrong: Eight is more than the actual four.  Why D wrong: Each spermatid becomes one sperm, so 4 spermatids yield 4 sperms, not 2."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"According to the comparison table, spermatogenesis is defined as:","options":["The transformation of spermatid to sperm","The process by which sperms are formed from the germ cell (spermatogonia)","The formation of the primary spermatocyte only","The fusion of sperm and ovum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogenesis is the process by which sperms are formed from the germ cell (spermatogonia).  Why A wrong: That is the definition of spermiogenesis.  Why C wrong: Forming the primary spermatocyte is only one step, not the whole process.  Why D wrong: Fusion of gametes is fertilization."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"When does spermatogenesis begin, and how long does it continue?","options":["Begins at birth and stops at puberty","Begins at puberty and continues throughout life","Begins in the fetus and stops at birth","Begins at puberty and stops at age 50"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogenesis begins at puberty and continues throughout life.  Why A wrong: It begins at puberty, not birth, and does not stop at puberty.  Why C wrong: It is not restricted to fetal life.  Why D wrong: It continues throughout life, not stopping at a fixed age of 50."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"How much time is taken for spermatogenesis (formation of sperm)?","options":["28 days","48–72 hours","70–74 days","90–120 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The time taken for spermatogenesis is 70–74 days.  Why A wrong: 28 days is not the stated spermatogenesis duration.  Why B wrong: 48–72 hours is the fertilizable span of sperm, not the formation time.  Why D wrong: 90–120 days is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"What is the size of a mature sperm?","options":["20–30 microns","50–60 microns","70–74 microns","100–120 microns"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The size of a sperm is 50–60 microns.  Why A wrong: 20–30 microns is not the stated value.  Why C wrong: 70–74 refers to the days for spermatogenesis, not sperm size.  Why D wrong: 100–120 microns is too large and not given."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"What is the fertilizable span (lifespan for fertilization) of sperm?","options":["24 hours","48–72 hours","70–74 hours","6–8 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fertilizable span of sperm is 48–72 hours.  Why A wrong: 24 hours is closer to the ovum's fertilizable span, not the sperm's.  Why C wrong: 70–74 refers to days for spermatogenesis, not the fertilizable span.  Why D wrong: 6–8 hours is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Spermatogenesis occurs at which specific site?","options":["Epididymis","Testis → Seminiferous tubule","Vas deferens","Prostate gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogenesis occurs in the testis, specifically in the seminiferous tubules.  Why A wrong: The epididymis is where sperm mature and are stored, not where spermatogenesis occurs.  Why C wrong: The vas deferens transports sperm.  Why D wrong: The prostate contributes seminal fluid, not sperm production."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In the structure of a sperm, the head is capped by which structure that contains enzymes to penetrate the ovum?","options":["Centriole","Acrosome","Axial filament","Terminal disc"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The acrosome caps the sperm head and contains enzymes for penetration.  Why A wrong: The centriole lies in the connecting/mid piece.  Why C wrong: The axial filament runs through the tail.  Why D wrong: The terminal disc is located at the end of the mid piece."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Which four regions make up the structure of a sperm from front to back?","options":["Acrosome, nucleus, mitochondria, centriole","Head, mid (connecting) piece, tail, end piece","Head, neck, body, tail","Acrosome, head, tail, flagellum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sperm is divided into head, mid (connecting) piece, tail, and end piece.  Why A wrong: These are internal components, not the four gross regions.  Why C wrong: \"Neck and body\" is not the labeling used on the page.  Why D wrong: This mixes components and regions incorrectly."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In the sperm, which structures are located in the head region?","options":["Mitochondria and axial filament","Acrosome, nucleus, and plasma membrane","Terminal disc and centriole","End piece only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The head contains the acrosome, nucleus, and is covered by the plasma membrane.  Why A wrong: Mitochondria are in the mid piece and the axial filament in the tail.  Why C wrong: The terminal disc and centriole are in the mid/connecting piece.  Why D wrong: The end piece is the terminal tail segment, not the head."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Which structures are found in the mid (connecting) piece of the sperm?","options":["Acrosome and nucleus","Mitochondria, centriole, and terminal disc","Axial filament only","Plasma membrane only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mid/connecting piece contains the centriole, mitochondria, and terminal disc.  Why A wrong: Acrosome and nucleus are in the head.  Why C wrong: The axial filament runs into the tail region.  Why D wrong: The plasma membrane covers the whole sperm, and is not the specific mid-piece structure named."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"The axial filament of the sperm is located in which region?","options":["Head","Mid piece","Tail","Acrosome"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The axial filament is located in the tail of the sperm.  Why A wrong: The head holds the acrosome and nucleus.  Why B wrong: The mid piece contains the mitochondria and centriole.  Why D wrong: The acrosome is a head structure."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In the detailed (front/side view) sperm-head structure, the space between the acrosome and the nuclear envelope on the anterior aspect is called the:","options":["Post-acrosomal region","Peri-acrosomal / Sub-acrosomal space","Equatorial segment","Posterior ring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The labels show the peri-acrosomal space (front view) and sub-acrosomal space (side view) around the acrosome.  Why A wrong: The post-acrosomal region lies behind the acrosome.  Why C wrong: The equatorial segment is the mid-portion of the acrosomal cap.  Why D wrong: The posterior ring is at the junction with the connecting piece."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"Which of the following are labeled structures of the detailed sperm head/neck in the front and side views? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nuclear vacuoles","Post-acrosomal sheath / Posterior ring","Mitochondrial sheath","Central pair / Outer dense fibers","Axoneme"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed structures — nuclear vacuoles, post-acrosomal sheath, posterior ring, mitochondrial sheath, central pair, outer dense fibers, and axoneme — are labeled in the detailed sperm figure.  Why A right: Nuclear vacuoles are shown within the nucleus.  Why B right: Post-acrosomal sheath and posterior ring are labeled at the head-neck junction.  Why C right: The mitochondrial sheath is labeled in the connecting piece.  Why D right: Central pair and outer dense fibers are labeled in the tail's axoneme complex.  Why E right: The axoneme is labeled at the base in both front and side views."},{"section":"Batch 077 · Fundamentals of Gynaecology — human chromosome number…","question":"In the detailed sperm structure, the \"connecting piece\" contains the centriole and connects the head to the tail. Which structure surrounds the axoneme in the connecting/mid piece to provide energy for motility?","options":["Nuclear envelope","Mitochondrial sheath","Acrosome","Equatorial segment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mitochondrial sheath surrounds the axoneme in the connecting piece and provides energy (ATP) for sperm motility.  Why A wrong: The nuclear envelope surrounds the nucleus in the head.  Why C wrong: The acrosome caps the head for ovum penetration.  Why D wrong: The equatorial segment is part of the acrosomal region of the head."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Sperm (spermatozoa) are formed in which organ of the male reproductive system?","options":["Epididymis","Testis","Seminal vesicle","Prostate gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Sperm is formed in Testis\" — the testis is the site of spermatogenesis.  Why A wrong: The epididymis is where sperm mature and become motile, not where they are formed.  Why C wrong: The seminal vesicle adds fructose to seminal fluid; it does not produce sperm.  Why D wrong: The prostate gland contributes to semen but does not form sperm."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"In the epididymis, sperm undergo which two processes?","options":["Become mature and become motile","Are produced and stored permanently","Acquire fructose and gelatinous coating","Undergo meiosis and become haploid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show the epididymis is where \"Sperm become mature\" and \"Sperm become motile.\"  Why B wrong: Sperm are produced in the testis, not the epididymis.  Why C wrong: Fructose and gelatinous material are added by the seminal vesicle.  Why D wrong: Sperm complete meiosis during spermatogenesis in the testis, not in the epididymis."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Which accessory gland of the male reproductive tract adds fructose to the seminal fluid?","options":["Prostate gland","Bulbourethral gland","Seminal vesicle","Epididymis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the seminal vesicle \"Add Fructose\" to the fluid.  Why A wrong: The prostate contributes to semen but is not noted as the source of fructose.  Why B wrong: The bulbourethral gland is a separate contributor, not the fructose source described.  Why D wrong: The epididymis matures sperm; it does not add fructose."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The seminal fluid, a gelatinous material, is collectively called:","options":["Plasma","Semen","Lymph","Colostrum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Seminal fluid = called as SEMEN, gelatinous material.\"  Why A wrong: Plasma is the liquid component of blood.  Why C wrong: Lymph is the fluid of the lymphatic system.  Why D wrong: Colostrum is the first breast milk, unrelated to male reproduction."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Which gland, in addition to the seminal vesicle, contributes to semen as shown in the sperm pathway?","options":["Prostate gland","Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pituitary gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label \"PROSTATE gland (also contribute to semen).\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The adrenal, thyroid, and pituitary are endocrine glands unrelated to the direct production of semen described here."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Arrange the correct order of the sperm pathway shown in the notes: Testis → ? → Vas deferens → ... → Penis.","options":["Prostate → Epididymis","Epididymis","Seminal vesicle → Epididymis","Ejaculatory duct → Epididymis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pathway diagram runs Testis → Epididymis → Vas deferens → (seminal vesicle/prostate) → Ejaculatory duct → Penis; the epididymis immediately follows the testis.  Why A/C/D wrong: The prostate, seminal vesicle, and ejaculatory duct come after the epididymis and vas deferens, not before the epididymis."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"In the male reproductive pathway, the duct that carries sperm from the epididymis onward is the:","options":["Vas deferens","Ureter","Urethra only","Fallopian tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label \"Vas deferens\" as the duct following the epididymis in the sperm pathway.  Why B wrong: The ureter carries urine from kidney to bladder.  Why C wrong: The urethra is the terminal common passage; the vas deferens transports sperm earlier in the path.  Why D wrong: The fallopian tube is a female structure."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Which duct is formed by the union that then opens to deliver semen toward the penis (after prostate) in the pathway?","options":["Ejaculatory duct","Common bile duct","Thoracic duct","Nasolacrimal duct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Ejaculatory DUCT\" in the pathway after the prostate gland, before the penis.  Why B/C/D wrong: The bile, thoracic, and nasolacrimal ducts belong to the digestive, lymphatic, and lacrimal systems respectively."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"In the male reproductive diagram, which structure lies posterior to the bladder and is labeled along with the vas deferens?","options":["Seminal vesicle","Scrotum","Penis","Epididymis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anatomy figure labels the \"Seminal vesicle\" near the bladder along with the ureter and vas deferens.  Why B wrong: The scrotum is the external sac containing the testes.  Why C wrong: The penis is the external copulatory organ.  Why D wrong: The epididymis sits on the testis, lower in the diagram."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"In the male reproductive figure, which structure is labeled as continuous with the scrotum's contents and produces sperm?","options":["Testis","Prostate gland","Urethra","Erectile tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the \"Testis\" within the scrotum, the sperm-producing organ.  Why B wrong: The prostate is labeled near the bladder base.  Why C wrong: The urethra is the tube carrying urine/semen.  Why D wrong: Erectile tissue is the spongy tissue of the penis."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"According to the notes, semen is formed by the contribution of all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Seminal vesicle","Prostate gland","Bulbourethral gland","Epididymis"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The listed contributors to semen are (a) seminal vesicle, (b) prostate gland, and (c) bulbourethral gland; the epididymis is not listed as a semen-contributing gland.  Why A/B/C wrong: These three are the exact contributors named in the question \"Semen is formed by contribution from.\""},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The MAJOR contributor to semen is:","options":["Prostate gland","Seminal vesicle","Bulbourethral gland","Epididymis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"Major contributor to semen is: Seminal vesicle.\"  Why A wrong: The prostate contributes but is not the major contributor per the notes.  Why C wrong: The bulbourethral gland is a minor contributor.  Why D wrong: The epididymis is not a semen-forming gland."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The process by which the female germ cell (oogonia) develops into ova is called:","options":["Spermatogenesis","Oogenesis","Folliculogenesis","Capacitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Oogonia (Female germ cell) → ova; Process is called as oogenesis.\"  Why A wrong: Spermatogenesis is formation of sperm in males.  Why C wrong: Folliculogenesis is follicle development, not the term used here for germ-cell maturation.  Why D wrong: Capacitation is a change sperm undergo in the female tract."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"When does oogenesis begin?","options":["At puberty","In intrauterine (fetal) life","At menarche","At the time of fertilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes emphasize \"Oogenesis begins in intrauterine life.\"  Why A wrong: Meiosis I is completed at puberty, but oogenesis itself begins earlier, in intrauterine life.  Why C wrong: Menarche is the first menstruation; oogenesis begins before this.  Why D wrong: Fertilization triggers completion of meiosis II, not the beginning of oogenesis."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The chromosome number of oogonia (female germ cell) is:","options":["23 (X)","46 (XX) or 44 + XX","47 (XXY)","45 (XO)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label oogonia as \"46 (XX) or 44 + XX\" (diploid).  Why A wrong: 23 (X) is the haploid number of the secondary oocyte/ova.  Why C wrong: 47 (XXY) is the Klinefelter genotype.  Why D wrong: 45 (XO) is the Turner genotype."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Oogonia divide by which process to form the primary oocyte?","options":["Meiosis I","Mitosis","Meiosis II","Binary fission"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The oogenesis diagram shows oogonia undergoing \"Mitosis\" to form the primary oocyte.  Why A wrong: Meiosis I is the next division, from primary oocyte to secondary oocyte.  Why C wrong: Meiosis II occurs later, in the secondary oocyte.  Why D wrong: Binary fission is a form of asexual reproduction in prokaryotes."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The chromosome content of the primary oocyte is:","options":["23 (X)","46 (XX) or 44 + XX","23 (Y)","22 + X"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the primary oocyte as \"46 (XX) or 44 + XX\" (diploid).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are haploid or partial counts; the primary oocyte remains diploid until meiosis I completes."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Meiosis I in the primary oocyte gets arrested in which stage?","options":["Metaphase","Anaphase","Prophase","Telophase"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Meiosis 1 (But this division gets arrested in PROPHASE).\"  Why A wrong: Metaphase is where meiosis II is arrested, not meiosis I.  Why B/D wrong: Anaphase and telophase are not the arrest points described."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The arrested meiosis I division of the primary oocyte is completed at what time of life?","options":["Intrauterine life","At the time of birth","At puberty","At menopause"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"The division is completed at time of 'Puberty'.\"  Why A wrong: Meiosis I begins/arrests in intrauterine life but completes only at puberty.  Why B wrong: At birth the primary oocyte is still arrested.  Why D wrong: Menopause marks the cessation of reproductive cycles, not completion of meiosis I."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Completion of meiosis I in the primary oocyte produces which two structures?","options":["Two secondary oocytes","Secondary oocyte (23 X) and first polar body (23 X)","Ovum and second polar body","Two ova"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows meiosis I yielding a \"Secondary oocyte 23 (X)\" and a \"First Polar Body 23 (X).\"  Why A wrong: Only one secondary oocyte forms, plus a polar body.  Why C wrong: The ovum and second polar body arise from meiosis II.  Why D wrong: Only one ovum ultimately forms per oogenesis."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The chromosome number of the secondary oocyte is:","options":["46 (XX)","23 (X)","44 + XX","47 (XXY)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the secondary oocyte as \"23 (X)\" — haploid.  Why A/C wrong: These are diploid counts of oogonia/primary oocyte.  Why D wrong: 47 (XXY) is Klinefelter genotype, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The secondary oocyte starts meiosis II, but this division gets arrested in which phase?","options":["Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the secondary oocyte \"Starts undergoing Meiosis II but meiosis II gets arrested in Metaphase.\"  Why A wrong: Prophase is the arrest point of meiosis I.  Why C/D wrong: Anaphase and telophase are not the arrest phases described."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Meiosis II of the secondary oocyte is completed only at what event?","options":["At puberty","At ovulation","At the time of fertilization","At birth"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Meiosis II is completed only at time of fertilization.\"  Why A wrong: Puberty completes meiosis I, not meiosis II.  Why B wrong: Ovulation releases the secondary oocyte with meiosis II still arrested.  Why D wrong: At birth the cell is arrested in meiosis I."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Completion of meiosis II at fertilization produces the ovum/female pronucleus with what chromosome number and which additional structure?","options":["46 (XX) and first polar body","23 (X) ova/female pronucleus and 2nd polar body","23 (Y) and secondary oocyte","44 + XX and primordial follicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows meiosis II producing \"23 (X) ova/female pronucleus\" and the \"2nd Polar Body.\"  Why A wrong: 46 (XX) is diploid and the first polar body forms in meiosis I.  Why C/D wrong: These structures/counts do not match the meiosis II product."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Ovulation is best defined as the release of:","options":["Primary oocyte from the ovary every month","Secondary oocyte from the ovary every month","Mature ovum from the fallopian tube","First polar body from the follicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed note states \"Ovulation is the release of secondary oocyte from primary oocyte every month\" — the cell released is the secondary oocyte.  Why A wrong: The primary oocyte is not released; it remains in the ovary and divides first.  Why C wrong: A mature ovum forms only after fertilization completes meiosis II.  Why D wrong: The polar body is a byproduct, not the released reproductive cell."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"In a newborn female ovary, which cell type is present?","options":["Only secondary oocytes","Primary oocyte but no secondary oocyte","Mature ova only","Oogonia only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"In a newborn female ovary ... We will get primary oocyte but no secondary oocyte.\"  Why A wrong: Secondary oocytes appear only after meiosis I completes at puberty.  Why C wrong: Mature ova form only at fertilization.  Why D wrong: Oogonia have already divided by mitosis into primary oocytes before birth."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"A primordial follicle consists of a primary oocyte surrounded by:","options":["Myometrial cells","Follicular (granulosa) cells of the ovary","Sertoli cells","Endometrial cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define the primordial follicle as \"Primary oocyte + Follicular cells of ovary.\"  Why A wrong: Myometrium is uterine smooth muscle, not part of a follicle.  Why C wrong: Sertoli cells are found in the male testis.  Why D wrong: The endometrium lines the uterus, not the ovarian follicle."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"According to the follicle-count table, the number of follicles in the ovary at the 5th month of intrauterine life (20 weeks) is:","options":["1–2 million","4–5 lakh","6–7 million (maximum = 7 million)","400–500"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"5th month of Intrauterine life (20 weeks) → 6–7 million follicles (maximum = 7 million).\"  Why A wrong: 1–2 million is the number at birth.  Why B wrong: 4–5 lakh is the number at puberty.  Why D wrong: 400–500 is the number that mature in the entire lifetime."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The maximum number of ovarian follicles (7 million) is reached at:","options":["Time of birth","Time of puberty","5th month of intrauterine life / 20th week of intrauterine life","Menopause"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Maximum no. of follicles are seen at: 5th month of Intrauterine life or 20th week of Intrauterine life.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: Follicle numbers decline after the 20-week peak, so birth, puberty, and menopause have fewer."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The number of ovarian follicles present at the time of birth is:","options":["6–7 million","1–2 million","4–5 lakh","400–500"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"At time of birth → 1–2 million follicles.\"  Why A wrong: 6–7 million is the intrauterine peak at 20 weeks.  Why C wrong: 4–5 lakh is the puberty count.  Why D wrong: 400–500 is the number maturing over a lifetime."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The number of ovarian follicles at the time of puberty is approximately:","options":["7 million","1–2 million","4–5 lakh","400–500"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"At time of puberty → 4–5 lakh follicles.\"  Why A/B wrong: These are the intrauterine and birth counts, respectively.  Why D wrong: 400–500 is the lifetime number that actually mature."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Out of the millions of follicles present, how many actually mature during a woman's entire lifetime?","options":["1–2 million","4–5 lakh","400–500","7 million"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table notes \"Only 400–500 follicles mature in entire life time.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These are total follicle counts at various life stages, not the number that mature."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The size of the follicle just before ovulation is:","options":["10–12 mm","18–20 mm","25–30 mm","5–8 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Size of follicle first before ovulation = 18–20 mm.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These sizes do not match the value given for a pre-ovulatory (Graafian) follicle."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"At the time of ovulation, which polar body is released?","options":["1st polar body","2nd polar body","Both polar bodies","No polar body"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"When is 1st polar body released: at the time of ovulation.\"  Why B wrong: The 2nd polar body is released at fertilization.  Why C wrong: Only the first polar body is released at ovulation.  Why D wrong: A polar body is released — the first one."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The 2nd polar body is released at the time of:","options":["Ovulation","Fertilization","Puberty","Menstruation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"When is 2nd polar body released: At the time of fertilization.\"  Why A wrong: The 1st polar body is released at ovulation.  Why C/D wrong: Puberty and menstruation are not when the 2nd polar body is extruded."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The size of the mature ova is:","options":["60 microns","120 microns","200 microns","30 microns"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Size of ova = 120 microns.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 120-micron size of the ovum."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Which of the following is the largest cell in the human body?","options":["Neuron","Ova","Spermatozoon","Hepatocyte"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Largest cell = ova.\"  Why A wrong: The neuron is the longest cell, not the largest.  Why C wrong: The sperm is one of the smallest cells.  Why D wrong: The hepatocyte (liver cell) is not the largest."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Which of the following is the longest cell in the human body?","options":["Ova","Neuron","Muscle fiber","Sperm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Longest cell = NEURON.\"  Why A wrong: The ovum is the largest cell, not the longest.  Why C wrong: Muscle fibers are long but the notes specify the neuron.  Why D wrong: Sperm are among the smallest cells."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The fertilization span (lifespan for fertilization) of the ova is:","options":["6–12 hours","12–24 hours","24–48 hours","48–72 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Fertilization span of ova = 12–24 hours.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the stated 12–24 hour fertilization window of the ovum."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"In oogenesis (as summarized on p-388), at puberty meiosis completes to yield a secondary oocyte and which polar body, and this release event is called:","options":["Menstruation","Ovulation","Implantation","Capacitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The p-388 diagram shows \"At Puberty Meiosis complete\" giving a secondary oocyte and 1st polar body, and labels \"This is called OVULATION.\"  Why A wrong: Menstruation is the shedding of the endometrium.  Why C wrong: Implantation is embedding of the blastocyst.  Why D wrong: Capacitation is a sperm-activation process."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The sex of the baby is determined by which chromosome?","options":["X chromosome","Y chromosome","Autosome 21","X-linked recessive gene"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Sex of baby is determined by: 'Y chromosome'.\"  Why A wrong: The X chromosome is present in both sexes; presence of Y determines maleness.  Why C wrong: Autosome 21 relates to Down syndrome, not sex determination.  Why D wrong: A single X-linked recessive gene does not determine sex."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"If the Y chromosome is ABSENT, the baby's sex will be:","options":["Male","Female","Ambiguous","Cannot be determined"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Y Chromosome absent → Female.\"  Why A wrong: A male results when the Y chromosome is present.  Why C/D wrong: Absence of Y specifically yields a female phenotype in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"If the Y chromosome is PRESENT, the baby's sex will be:","options":["Female","Male","Intersex","Depends on X count"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Y Chromosome Present → Male.\"  Why A wrong: Absence of Y gives a female.  Why C/D wrong: The notes indicate presence of Y directly yields a male."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The genotype of Turner syndrome is:","options":["47 (XXY)","45 (XO)","47 (XYY)","46 (XX)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Turner syndrome → Genotype = 45 (XO).\"  Why A wrong: 47 (XXY) is Klinefelter syndrome.  Why C wrong: 47 (XYY) is a different condition, not Turner.  Why D wrong: 46 (XX) is a normal female karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"In Turner syndrome (45, XO), the Y chromosome is absent; therefore the individual's sex is:","options":["Male","Female","Ambiguous","Intersex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in Turner syndrome \"Y chromosome is absent ↓ Female.\"  Why A wrong: Absence of Y precludes a male phenotype.  Why C/D wrong: The notes assign a female sex to Turner syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The genotype of Klinefelter syndrome is:","options":["45 (XO)","47 (XXY)","46 (XY)","47 (XXX)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Klinefelter syndrome ↓ Genotype = 47 (XXY).\"  Why A wrong: 45 (XO) is Turner syndrome.  Why C wrong: 46 (XY) is a normal male karyotype.  Why D wrong: 47 (XXX) is triple-X syndrome, not Klinefelter."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"In Klinefelter syndrome (47, XXY), the Y chromosome is present; therefore the individual's sex is:","options":["Female","Male","Ambiguous","Neither"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in Klinefelter \"Y chromosome is present ↓ Male.\"  Why A wrong: Presence of Y determines a male sex.  Why C/D wrong: The notes assign a male sex to Klinefelter syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The MOST COMMON method used for sex determination is:","options":["Karyotyping","Looking at the external genitalia","Amniocentesis","Chorionic villus sampling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Most common method used for sex determination ↓ By looking at external genitalia.\"  Why A wrong: Karyotyping is the best method, not the most common one per the notes.  Why C/D wrong: These are prenatal diagnostic tests, not the commonest sex-determination method described."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"When the sex of the baby cannot be determined by looking at the genitalia, this condition is called:","options":["Cryptorchidism","Ambiguous genitalia","Hypospadias","Hermaphroditism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"When by looking at the genitalia, the sex of baby cannot be determined is called as Ambiguous genitalia.\"  Why A wrong: Cryptorchidism is undescended testis.  Why C wrong: Hypospadias is abnormal urethral opening.  Why D wrong: Hermaphroditism refers to presence of both gonadal tissues; the notes specifically use \"ambiguous genitalia.\""},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"The BEST method for sex determination is:","options":["External genitalia examination","Karyotyping","Ultrasonography","Hormone assay"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Best method for sex determination → Karyotyping.\"  Why A wrong: External genitalia examination is the most common, not the best method.  Why C/D wrong: Ultrasonography and hormone assays are not named as the best method here."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Fibroid is described as the most common ______ in females.","options":["Malignant tumor","Benign tumor","Infectious lesion","Cystic swelling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"It is the most common benign tumor in females.\"  Why A wrong: A fibroid is benign, not malignant.  Why C/D wrong: A fibroid is a solid benign neoplasm, not an infection or simple cyst."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"Fibroid is also described as the most common ______ in females.","options":["Pelvic tumor","Ovarian cyst","Breast tumor","Cervical cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"It is the most common Pelvic tumor in female.\"  Why B wrong: An ovarian cyst is a different entity.  Why C wrong: The notes specify pelvic tumor, not breast tumor.  Why D wrong: Cervical cancer is a malignancy, not a benign fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"A fibroid is composed of smooth muscle plus connective tissue and is therefore also called:","options":["Leiomyoma","Adenoma","Lipoma","Fibroadenoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"FIBROID = smooth muscle + connective tissue → LEIOMYOMA.\"  Why B wrong: An adenoma arises from glandular epithelium.  Why C wrong: A lipoma is a tumor of fat (adipose) tissue.  Why D wrong: A fibroadenoma is a benign breast tumor, not a uterine fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"A fibroid (leiomyoma) is composed of which two tissue components?","options":["Smooth muscle and connective tissue","Skeletal muscle and fat","Epithelium and cartilage","Nerve and vascular tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state fibroid is \"Composed of smooth muscle + Connective tissue.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These tissue combinations do not match the composition of a fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"According to the notes, the most common SITE for a fibroid is the:","options":["Cervix","Uterus (uterine fibroid)","Ovary","Fallopian tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sites diagram labels \"Uterus (Uterine fibroid) — Most common.\"  Why A wrong: Cervical fibroid is an example of the less-common extra-uterine type.  Why C/D wrong: Ovary and fallopian tube are not listed as the most common fibroid site."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"A fibroid present at a site other than the uterus (e.g., cervical fibroid) is classified as:","options":["Uterine fibroid, most common","Extra-uterine fibroid, less common","Submucous fibroid","Intramural fibroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Extra uterine fibroid — Less common — Present at sites other than the uterus e.g. cervical fibroid.\"  Why A wrong: Uterine fibroids are the most common type, located in the uterus.  Why C/D wrong: Submucous and intramural are subtypes of uterine fibroids, located within the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"A fibroid located within the myometrium (wall of the uterus) is called:","options":["Submucous fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Intramural / interstitial fibroid","Pedunculated fibroid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels a fibroid in the uterine wall as \"Intramural fibroid / Interstitial fibroid.\"  Why A wrong: A submucous fibroid lies beneath the endometrium, projecting into the uterine cavity.  Why B wrong: A subserous fibroid lies beneath the serosa on the outer surface.  Why D wrong: Pedunculated fibroids hang on a stalk from the surface or cavity."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"A fibroid lying just beneath the serosa (outer covering) of the uterus is called a:","options":["Submucous fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Intramural fibroid","Cervical fibroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels a fibroid beneath the serosa as \"Subserous fibroid.\"  Why A wrong: A submucous fibroid lies beneath the endometrium/mucosa.  Why C wrong: An intramural fibroid is within the muscle wall.  Why D wrong: A cervical fibroid is an extra-uterine example."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"A fibroid located just beneath the endometrium, projecting into the uterine cavity, is called a:","options":["Subserous fibroid","Submucous (submucosal) fibroid","Intramural fibroid","Extra-uterine fibroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the fibroid projecting into the uterine cavity beneath the mucosa as \"Submucous fibroid\" / \"Submucosal.\"  Why A wrong: Subserous fibroids lie beneath the outer serosa.  Why C wrong: Intramural fibroids sit within the muscle wall.  Why D wrong: Extra-uterine fibroids are outside the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"A subserous fibroid that hangs from the outer uterine surface on a stalk is termed:","options":["Pedunculated subserous fibroid","Non-pedunculated subserous fibroid","Intramural fibroid","Submucous fibroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram distinguishes \"Pedunculated subserous fibroids\" (on a stalk) from \"Non pedunculated subserous\" (broad-based).  Why B wrong: Non-pedunculated subserous fibroids sit on a broad base without a stalk.  Why C wrong: An intramural fibroid is within the wall.  Why D wrong: A submucous fibroid projects into the cavity, not the outer surface."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"In the fibroid figure, which layer is labeled as the innermost lining of the uterus?","options":["Myometrium","Endometrium","Serosa","Peritoneal cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the \"Endometrium\" as the inner lining, with myometrium and serosa external to it.  Why A wrong: The myometrium is the muscular layer outside the endometrium.  Why C wrong: The serosa is the outermost covering.  Why D wrong: The peritoneal cavity is the space outside the serosa, not a uterine layer."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"In the fibroid figure, the muscular layer of the uterus is labeled as the:","options":["Endometrium","Myometrium","Serosa","Uterine cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the \"Myometrium\" as the muscle layer between the endometrium and serosa.  Why A wrong: The endometrium is the inner lining.  Why C wrong: The serosa is the outer covering.  Why D wrong: The uterine cavity is the space, not a tissue layer."},{"section":"Batch 078 · Male reproductive anatomy & sperm pathway, Oogenesis…","question":"A pedunculated fibroid that projects into the uterine cavity on a stalk beneath the mucosa is best described as:","options":["Pedunculated submucosal fibroid","Pedunculated subserous fibroid","Intramural fibroid","Cervical fibroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Types of fibroid\" figure labels a stalked fibroid within the cavity as \"Pedunculated submucosal.\"  Why B wrong: Pedunculated subserous fibroids hang from the outer serosal surface.  Why C wrong: Intramural fibroids are embedded in the wall without a stalk.  Why D wrong: Cervical fibroids are located in the cervix (extra-uterine example)."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Uterine fibroid limited to the myometrium is classified as which type?","options":["Submucous fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Interstitial / Intramural fibroid","Pedunculated fibroid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An interstitial (intramural) fibroid is limited to the myometrium, as shown in the classification chart.  Why A wrong: Submucous fibroids grow towards the uterine cavity.  Why B wrong: Subserous fibroids grow towards the peritoneal cavity.  Why D wrong: \"Pedunculated\" describes a subserous fibroid with a stalk, not one limited to the myometrium."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A submucous fibroid grows towards which structure?","options":["Peritoneal cavity","Uterine cavity","Broad ligament","Myometrium only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Submucous fibroids grow towards the uterine cavity.  Why A wrong: Growth towards the peritoneal cavity describes a subserous fibroid.  Why C wrong: Broad ligament extension is not the defining feature of submucous fibroid.  Why D wrong: Being limited to the myometrium describes an intramural fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A subserous fibroid grows towards which cavity?","options":["Uterine cavity","Endometrial cavity","Peritoneal cavity","Cervical canal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Subserous fibroids grow towards the peritoneal cavity.  Why A/B wrong: Growth towards the uterine/endometrial cavity describes a submucous fibroid.  Why D wrong: The cervical canal is not the direction of subserous fibroid growth."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A subserous fibroid that has a stalk is termed:","options":["Unpedunculated","Pedunculated","Intramural","Interstitial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A subserous fibroid \"with stalk\" is called pedunculated.  Why A wrong: \"Without stalk\" subserous fibroid is unpedunculated.  Why C/D wrong: Intramural/interstitial fibroids are limited to the myometrium, not defined by a stalk."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A subserous fibroid without a stalk is termed:","options":["Pedunculated","Unpedunculated","Submucous","Cervical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A subserous fibroid \"without stalk\" is called unpedunculated.  Why A wrong: \"With stalk\" makes it pedunculated.  Why C/D wrong: These are separate fibroid categories, not defined by stalk presence."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"What is the most common site of fibroid?","options":["Cervix","Ovary","Uterus","Fallopian tube"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of fibroid is the uterus.  Why A wrong: Cervical fibroids exist but are not the most common site.  Why B wrong: The ovary is the most common site of endometriosis, not fibroid.  Why D wrong: The fallopian tube is not a common fibroid site."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The most common variety of uterine fibroid is:","options":["Submucous fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Intramural fibroid","Cervical fibroid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common variety of uterine fibroid is intramural fibroid.  Why A wrong: Submucous fibroid has maximum symptoms/complications but is not the most common variety.  Why B wrong: Subserous fibroid is not the most common variety.  Why D wrong: Cervical fibroid is uncommon overall."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"\"To begin with, all fibroids are ______ fibroid.\" Fill in the blank.","options":["Submucous","Intramural","Subserous","Pedunculated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To begin with, all fibroids are intramural fibroids (they then grow inward or outward).  Why A/C/D wrong: Submucous, subserous and pedunculated forms develop later as the fibroid grows in a particular direction."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The typical gross appearance (colour) of a fibroid is:","options":["Red / salmon pink","Pale / yellow","Black","Blue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A fibroid's appearance is pale/yellow.  Why A wrong: Red/salmon pink appearance is seen specifically in red degeneration.  Why C/D wrong: These are not described appearances of a fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The consistency of a uterine fibroid is described as:","options":["Soft","Cystic","Firm","Fluctuant"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The consistency of a fibroid is FIRM.  Why A/B/D wrong: Fibroids are firm, not soft, cystic, or fluctuant."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A uterine fibroid is covered by a:","options":["True capsule","Pseudocapsule","Serous membrane only","Mucous layer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A fibroid is covered by a pseudocapsule.  Why A wrong: It has a pseudocapsule, not a true capsule.  Why C/D wrong: These do not describe the fibroid covering."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"In a fibroid, the blood vessels supplying it are found in which region?","options":["Centre of the fibroid","Periphery of the fibroid","Evenly throughout the fibroid","Only at the base"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The blood vessels supplying a fibroid are found in the periphery of the fibroid.  Why A wrong: The centre is the least vascular part.  Why C/D wrong: Vascularity is concentrated at the periphery, not uniform or basal."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The most vascular part of a fibroid is the:","options":["Centre","Periphery (pseudocapsule)","Core","Stalk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most vascular part of a fibroid is the periphery (pseudocapsule).  Why A/C wrong: The centre/core is the least vascular part.  Why D wrong: The stalk is not identified as the most vascular part."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The least vascular part of a fibroid is the:","options":["Periphery","Pseudocapsule","Centre","Surface"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The least vascular part of a fibroid is the centre.  Why A/B wrong: The periphery/pseudocapsule is the most vascular part.  Why D wrong: The surface is not identified as least vascular."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Degeneration in a fibroid begins from which part?","options":["Periphery","Pseudocapsule","Centre","Stalk"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Degeneration in a fibroid begins from the centre (as it is the least vascular part).  Why A/B wrong: The periphery/pseudocapsule is the most vascular and not where degeneration begins.  Why D wrong: The stalk is not the origin of degeneration."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Uterine fibroids are more common in which age group?","options":["15–25 years","25–35 years","35–45 years","50–60 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fibroids are more common at 35–45 years of age.  Why A/B/D wrong: These age ranges are not stated as the peak age for fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A uterine fibroid is a hormone-dependent tumour. Which hormone is it dependent on?","options":["Progesterone","Estrogen","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fibroid is an estrogen-dependent tumour.  Why A/C/D wrong: These hormones are not the ones fibroids depend on for growth."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"What happens to a fibroid at the time of menopause?","options":["It grows rapidly","It shrinks","It becomes malignant","It calcifies immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because fibroid is estrogen dependent, at menopause (when estrogen falls) the fibroid shrinks.  Why A wrong: Fibroids grow with estrogen, and estrogen drops at menopause.  Why C wrong: Malignancy is a rare complication, not the expected menopausal change.  Why D wrong: Immediate calcification is not the stated menopausal outcome."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Which of the following conditions is NOT associated with increased estrogen as listed in the notes?","options":["Fibroid","Endometriosis","Endometrial/uterine cancer","Cervical cancer"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The four estrogen-linked conditions listed are fibroid, endometriosis, endometrial/uterine cancer, and ovarian cancer — cervical cancer is not in the list.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are all listed as conditions increasing with raised estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Increased estrogen is associated with all of the following EXCEPT (per the notes list): fibroid, endometriosis, endometrial cancer, and ______.","options":["Ovarian cancer","Breast fibroadenoma","Vaginal cancer","Vulval cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ovarian cancer is the fourth condition listed as increasing with raised estrogen (so it belongs in the list, completing the four).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not part of the four estrogen-associated conditions listed."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The four estrogen-related conditions (fibroid, endometriosis, endometrial cancer, ovarian cancer) are all MORE common in which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Obese female","Nulliparous female","Smoking patients","Multiparous female","Underweight female"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: These four conditions are more common in obese females and nulliparous females.  Why C wrong: Smoking is noted to act against all these four conditions (protective/opposite), not increasing them.  Why D wrong: Multiparity is not listed as a risk factor (adenomyosis, by contrast, is more common in multiparous females).  Why E wrong: Obesity, not being underweight, is the associated risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A nulliparous female is defined as one who:","options":["Has given birth to one child","Has never given birth to a child","Has multiple pregnancies","Is currently pregnant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nulliparous means a female who has never given birth to a child.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe parous, multiparous, or gravid states, not nulliparity."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"According to the notes, smoking acts in what way with respect to the four estrogen-related conditions (fibroid, endometriosis, endometrial cancer, ovarian cancer)?","options":["Increases their risk","Acts against (protective toward) all these 4 conditions","Has no relation","Only worsens fibroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smoking patients are noted to be \"against all these 4 conditions\" (i.e., it acts opposite to raised estrogen).  Why A wrong: The notes state smoking acts against, not in favour of, these conditions.  Why C/D wrong: A relationship exists and it applies to all four, not just fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Degeneration in a fibroid begins from the centre because the centre is:","options":["Most vascular","Least vascular","Covered by pseudocapsule","Rich in estrogen receptors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Degeneration begins from the centre because it is the least vascular part.  Why A wrong: The periphery is the most vascular part.  Why C wrong: The pseudocapsule covers the whole fibroid; it is not why the centre degenerates.  Why D wrong: Estrogen-receptor density is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The most common type of degeneration seen in a fibroid is:","options":["Red degeneration","Hyaline degeneration","Atrophy","Calcific degeneration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common degeneration seen in a fibroid is hyaline degeneration.  Why A wrong: Red degeneration is specific to pregnancy, not the most common overall.  Why C wrong: Atrophy is the most common degeneration in menopausal females, not overall.  Why D wrong: Calcific degeneration is not listed as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"In a menopausal female, the most common degeneration seen in a fibroid is:","options":["Hyaline degeneration","Red degeneration","Atrophy","Cystic degeneration"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common degeneration in a menopausal female's fibroid is atrophy (as estrogen falls, the fibroid atrophies).  Why A wrong: Hyaline degeneration is the most common overall, not specifically in menopause.  Why B wrong: Red degeneration is specific to pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Cystic degeneration is not the stated answer."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Which degeneration of a fibroid is specific to pregnancy?","options":["Hyaline degeneration","Atrophy","Red degeneration","Fatty degeneration"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Red degeneration is the degeneration specific to pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Hyaline degeneration is the most common overall, not pregnancy-specific.  Why B wrong: Atrophy is characteristic of menopause.  Why D wrong: Fatty degeneration is not the pregnancy-specific type."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Red degeneration of a fibroid is most commonly seen during which trimester of pregnancy?","options":["1st trimester","2nd trimester","3rd trimester","Puerperium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Red degeneration is seen in pregnancy only, most commonly in the 2nd trimester.  Why A/C wrong: The notes specify the 2nd trimester as most common, not the 1st or 3rd.  Why D wrong: It occurs during pregnancy, not the postpartum period."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"What is the underlying cause (pathophysiology) of red degeneration of a fibroid?","options":["Bacterial infection","Thrombosis in the blood vessels supplying the fibroid","Excessive estrogen","Calcium deposition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Red degeneration is caused by thrombosis in the blood vessels which supply the fibroid.  Why A wrong: It is not due to bacterial infection.  Why C wrong: Estrogen dependence causes growth, not the acute red degeneration event.  Why D wrong: Calcium deposition is not the cause of red degeneration."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A pregnant woman in her 2nd trimester with a known fibroid presents with abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":["Hyaline degeneration","Red degeneration of fibroid","Fibroid atrophy","Torsion of ovary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Red degeneration (2nd trimester) presents with pain in the abdomen and nausea/vomiting due to thrombosis of fibroid vessels.  Why A wrong: Hyaline degeneration is usually asymptomatic and not pregnancy-specific.  Why C wrong: Atrophy is a menopausal phenomenon.  Why D wrong: The clinical scenario with a known fibroid in pregnancy points to red degeneration."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Why is red degeneration of a fibroid called \"red\"?","options":["Due to increased blood flow","The fibroid appears red/salmon pink due to haemolysed RBCs","Due to fresh haemorrhage","Due to melanin pigment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is called red degeneration because the fibroid appears red/salmon pink due to haemolysed RBCs.  Why A wrong: There is actually thrombosis reducing flow, not increased blood flow.  Why C wrong: The colour is from haemolysed RBCs, not fresh haemorrhage.  Why D wrong: Melanin has no role."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"On examination, a fibroid undergoing red degeneration classically emits:","options":["A foul purulent odour","A fishy odour","No odour","A sweet fruity odour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: We get a fishy odour from a fibroid with red degeneration.  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specifically describe a fishy odour, not purulent, absent, or fruity."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"What is the recommended management approach for red degeneration of a fibroid?","options":["Emergency hysterectomy","Myomectomy","Conservative management (no surgery)","Chemotherapy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Red degeneration is managed conservatively — NO surgery (no myomectomy/hysterectomy).  Why A/B wrong: Surgery (hysterectomy/myomectomy) is specifically avoided in red degeneration.  Why D wrong: Chemotherapy is not indicated; red degeneration is not malignancy."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Conservative management of red degeneration includes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Bed rest","Analgesics for pain","Antiemetics for vomiting","Immediate myomectomy"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Surgery such as myomectomy is avoided; conservative management is bed rest, analgesics, and antiemetics.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are all part of conservative management of red degeneration."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"In red degeneration management, antiemetics are given specifically to control:","options":["Pain","Vomiting","Fever","Bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antiemetics are given for vomiting.  Why A wrong: Analgesics are given for pain.  Why C/D wrong: Antiemetics do not treat fever or bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"In the conservative management of red degeneration, analgesics are given for:","options":["Vomiting","Pain","Nausea","Bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Analgesics are given for pain.  Why A/C wrong: Antiemetics manage vomiting/nausea.  Why D wrong: Analgesics do not control bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"What is the least common complication of a fibroid?","options":["Menorrhagia","Pressure symptoms","Malignancy","Degeneration"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The least common complication of fibroid is malignancy (rarely < 0.5%).  Why A/B/D wrong: Menorrhagia, pressure symptoms, and degeneration are far more common than malignancy."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The approximate risk of malignancy developing in a fibroid is:","options":["< 0.5%","5%","10%","25%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Malignancy in a fibroid is rare, occurring in < 0.5%.  Why B/C/D wrong: These overestimate the very low malignant transformation risk."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Maximum chances of malignancy (malignant transformation) are seen in which type of fibroid?","options":["Intramural fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Submucous fibroid","Cervical fibroid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Maximum chances of malignancy are seen in submucous fibroid.  Why A wrong: Intramural is the most common variety but not the one with maximum malignancy risk.  Why B/D wrong: Subserous and cervical fibroids do not carry the maximum malignancy risk."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"What is the most common presentation of a fibroid?","options":["Menorrhagia","Asymptomatic","Pain","Urinary retention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mostly fibroids are asymptomatic; the most common presentation of fibroid is asymptomatic.  Why A wrong: Menorrhagia is the most common symptom when symptoms occur, but most fibroids are asymptomatic overall.  Why C/D wrong: Pain and urinary retention are less common presentations."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"When a fibroid is symptomatic, the most common symptom is related to:","options":["Bowel habits","Menstrual symptoms","Urinary system","Skin changes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common symptom of fibroid is menstrual symptoms.  Why A/C wrong: Bowel (constipation) and urinary symptoms are pressure effects, not the most common symptom.  Why D wrong: Skin changes are not a fibroid symptom."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The menstrual symptom of fibroid classically progresses to excessive bleeding, which is termed:","options":["Amenorrhea","Menorrhagia","Oligomenorrhea","Metrorrhagia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Menstrual symptoms → excessive bleeding → menorrhagia.  Why A wrong: Amenorrhea is never seen in fibroid.  Why C wrong: Oligomenorrhea (scanty menses) is the opposite of the heavy bleeding seen.  Why D wrong: Metrorrhagia (intermenstrual bleeding) is not the term used here for the heavy bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Painful menstruation that can accompany a fibroid is termed:","options":["Dyspareunia","Dysmenorrhea","Dysuria","Dyschezia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A patient with fibroid can also complain of pain during menstruation = dysmenorrhea.  Why A wrong: Dyspareunia is pain during intercourse.  Why C wrong: Dysuria is painful urination.  Why D wrong: Dyschezia is painful defecation."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Which menstrual disturbance is NEVER seen in a fibroid?","options":["Menorrhagia","Dysmenorrhea","Amenorrhea","Excessive bleeding"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Amenorrhea is never seen in fibroid (fibroid causes bleeding, not absence of menses).  Why A/B/D wrong: Menorrhagia, dysmenorrhea, and excessive bleeding are all seen with fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A fibroid pressing on veins can lead to which complication?","options":["Edema","Varicose veins","Constipation","Urinary retention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A fibroid pressing on veins → varicose veins.  Why A wrong: Pressure on lymphatics causes edema.  Why C wrong: Pressure on the intestine causes constipation.  Why D wrong: Pressure on the urethra causes urinary retention."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A fibroid pressing on lymphatics leads to:","options":["Varicose veins","Edema","Constipation","Urinary retention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pressure on lymphatics → edema.  Why A wrong: Pressure on veins causes varicose veins.  Why C wrong: Pressure on intestine causes constipation.  Why D wrong: Pressure on urethra causes urinary retention."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A fibroid pressing on the intestine leads to:","options":["Diarrhea","Constipation","Edema","Varicose veins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pressure on the intestine → constipation.  Why A wrong: The notes specify constipation, not diarrhea.  Why C wrong: Edema results from lymphatic pressure.  Why D wrong: Varicose veins result from venous pressure."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A fibroid pressing on the urethra can lead to:","options":["Urinary incontinence","Urinary retention","Polyuria","Hematuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pressure on the urethra → urinary retention.  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specify urinary retention, not incontinence, polyuria, or hematuria."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Besides the pressure effects listed, a fibroid can also cause the patient to:","options":["Feel heaviness","Lose weight rapidly","Develop fever","Have night sweats"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One pressure/mass symptom is that the patient can feel heaviness.  Why B/C/D wrong: Rapid weight loss, fever, and night sweats are not listed fibroid pressure symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Urinary symptoms are more common in which type of fibroid?","options":["Submucous fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Cervical fibroid","Intramural fibroid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Urinary symptoms are more common in cervical fibroid (due to proximity to the bladder/urethra).  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the fibroids most associated with urinary symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The fibroid associated with maximum symptoms is:","options":["Intramural fibroid","Submucous fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Cervical fibroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fibroid with maximum symptoms is submucous fibroid.  Why A wrong: Intramural is most common but not the most symptomatic.  Why C/D wrong: Subserous and cervical fibroids are not the most symptomatic type."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The fibroid with the maximum chances of causing abortion is:","options":["Subserous fibroid","Intramural fibroid","Submucous fibroid","Pedunculated fibroid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Submucous fibroid (projecting into the cavity) has maximum chances of causing abortion.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the fibroids most likely to cause abortion."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The fibroid with the maximum chances of causing infertility is:","options":["Submucous fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Intramural fibroid","Cervical fibroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Submucous fibroid has the maximum chances of causing infertility.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the fibroids most associated with infertility."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The fibroid with the maximum chances of becoming malignant is:","options":["Intramural fibroid","Submucous fibroid","Subserous fibroid","Cervical fibroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Submucous fibroid has the maximum chances of becoming malignant.  Why A/C/D wrong: These types carry lower malignant transformation risk than submucous fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Which type of fibroid has the maximum chances of undergoing torsion?","options":["Submucous fibroid","Intramural fibroid","Pedunculated subserous fibroid","Cervical fibroid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A pedunculated subserous fibroid (having a stalk) has maximum chances of undergoing torsion.  Why A/B/D wrong: These fibroids lack the stalk that predisposes to torsion."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The investigation of choice for a uterine fibroid is:","options":["CT scan","MRI","Ultrasound","Plain X-ray"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The investigation of choice for fibroid is ultrasound.  Why A/B/D wrong: CT, MRI, and X-ray are not the first-line investigation of choice for fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"An asymptomatic fibroid should be managed how, according to the management chart?","options":["Immediate surgery","No management, irrespective of the size of the fibroid","Hysterectomy","Hormonal therapy for all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asymptomatic fibroid requires no management, irrespective of the size of the fibroid.  Why A/C wrong: Surgery/hysterectomy is reserved for symptomatic fibroids.  Why D wrong: Medical management is for those refusing/contraindicated for surgery, not routine asymptomatic cases."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A symptomatic fibroid in a woman whose family is NOT complete is best managed by:","options":["Hysterectomy","Myomectomy (remove fibroid but not uterus)","No management","Chemotherapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the family is not complete, remove the fibroid but not the uterus — this surgery is called myomectomy (preserves fertility).  Why A wrong: Hysterectomy removes the uterus and is chosen when the family is complete.  Why C wrong: A symptomatic fibroid needs treatment.  Why D wrong: Chemotherapy is not indicated for benign fibroid."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The surgery that removes the fibroid together with the uterus is called:","options":["Myomectomy","Hysterectomy","Oophorectomy","Salpingectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Removal of fibroid + uterus is hysterectomy, done when the patient's family is complete.  Why A wrong: Myomectomy removes only the fibroid, sparing the uterus.  Why C wrong: Oophorectomy is removal of the ovary.  Why D wrong: Salpingectomy is removal of the fallopian tube."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"For a symptomatic fibroid where the patient's family is complete, the preferred surgery is:","options":["Myomectomy","Hysterectomy","Conservative management","Uterine preservation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the family is complete, remove fibroid + uterus = hysterectomy.  Why A/D wrong: Myomectomy/uterine preservation is chosen when the family is not complete.  Why C wrong: A symptomatic fibroid warrants surgical management."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Medical (drug) management of a fibroid is indicated in which situation?","options":["All asymptomatic fibroids","If the patient refuses surgery or has a contraindication to surgery","Only in pregnancy","Only in adolescents"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Medical management with drugs is used if the patient refuses surgery or has a contraindication to surgery.  Why A wrong: Asymptomatic fibroids need no management.  Why C/D wrong: The indication is refusal/contraindication to surgery, not restricted to pregnancy or adolescents."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"In the fibroid drug mnemonic, \"Today = Tranexamic acid\" refers to a drug that is:","options":["A hormonal drug","A non-hormonal drug (fibrinolytic/antifibrinolytic)","A GnRH analogue","An aromatase inhibitor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tranexamic acid is listed as a non-hormonal drug (antifibrinolytic) used to reduce bleeding.  Why A wrong: It is specifically the non-hormonal option, unlike the others.  Why C wrong: GnRH (Got) is the hormonal analogue in the list.  Why D wrong: Letrozole (Late) is the aromatase inhibitor."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"In the fibroid drug mnemonic, \"Domino = Danazol\" is described as:","options":["The best hormonal drug","A non-hormonal drug","A fibrinolytic","An antibiotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Danazol is labelled \"(Best)\" among the hormonal drugs in the notes.  Why B/C wrong: Tranexamic acid is the non-hormonal/fibrinolytic drug, not Danazol.  Why D wrong: Danazol is a hormonal agent, not an antibiotic."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Which of the following drugs is included in the medical (hormonal) management of fibroid per the notes mnemonic?","options":["Progesterone","Aspirin","Metformin","Warfarin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Pizza = Progesterone\" is one of the listed hormonal drugs for fibroid.  Why B/C/D wrong: Aspirin, metformin, and warfarin are not in the fibroid drug list."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"In the fibroid drug mnemonic \"Got = GnRH\", how is GnRH administered to treat fibroid?","options":["Pulsatile","Continuous","Single dose only","Topical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GnRH is used continuously (\"Got = GnRH (Continuous)\"), which suppresses estrogen and shrinks the fibroid.  Why A wrong: Pulsatile GnRH stimulates rather than suppresses; continuous is used here.  Why C/D wrong: Single-dose/topical use is not described."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"In the fibroid drug mnemonic, \"Late = Letrozole.\" Letrozole belongs to which drug class?","options":["Antifibrinolytic","Aromatase inhibitor (reduces estrogen)","Progestin","Antibiotic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Letrozole is an aromatase inhibitor that lowers estrogen, consistent with the hormonal drugs' action of decreasing estrogen.  Why A wrong: Tranexamic acid is the antifibrinolytic.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is the progestin in the list.  Why D wrong: Letrozole is not an antibiotic."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The main mechanism of action of the hormonal drugs used for fibroid (Danazol, Progesterone, GnRH, Letrozole) is to:","options":["Increase estrogen","Decrease estrogen","Increase progesterone only","Stimulate ovulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hormonal drugs' action is to decrease estrogen (↓Estrogen), since fibroid is estrogen dependent.  Why A wrong: Increasing estrogen would worsen a fibroid.  Why C/D wrong: The unifying action is lowering estrogen, not merely raising progesterone or stimulating ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Which fibroid drug is fibrinolytic/antifibrinolytic and non-hormonal, acting to control bleeding rather than by lowering estrogen?","options":["Danazol","GnRH","Tranexamic acid","Letrozole"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Tranexamic acid is the non-hormonal (fibrinolytic-related) drug; the others act hormonally by decreasing estrogen.  Why A/B/D wrong: Danazol, GnRH, and Letrozole are hormonal drugs acting via ↓estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Endometriosis is best defined as:","options":["Endometrial tissue found within the myometrium","Occurrence of endometrial tissue outside the uterus","Overgrowth of the endometrium inside the uterus","Infection of the endometrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endometriosis is the occurrence of endometrial tissue outside the uterus.  Why A wrong: Endometrial tissue within the myometrium is adenomyosis.  Why C wrong: Overgrowth inside the uterus is endometrial hyperplasia, not endometriosis.  Why D wrong: Endometriosis is ectopic tissue, not an infection."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The most common site of endometriosis is:","options":["Pouch of Douglas","Ovary (in the form of a chocolate cyst)","GI tract","Lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of endometriosis is the ovary, in the form of a chocolate cyst.  Why A wrong: Pouch of Douglas is the 2nd most common site.  Why C/D wrong: GI tract and lungs are possible but not the most common sites."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Endometriosis occurring in the ovary characteristically forms a:","options":["Dermoid cyst","Chocolate cyst","Serous cyst","Corpus luteum cyst"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ovarian endometriosis forms a chocolate cyst (endometrioma).  Why A/C/D wrong: Dermoid, serous, and corpus luteum cysts are different entities, not the endometriotic cyst."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The second most common site of endometriosis is the Pouch of Douglas, which is also known as the:","options":["Vesicouterine pouch","Rectouterine pouch","Broad ligament","Rectovesical pouch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Pouch of Douglas is the rectouterine pouch, the 2nd most common site of endometriosis.  Why A wrong: The vesicouterine pouch is a different peritoneal recess.  Why C wrong: The broad ligament is a separate structure (a possible ectopic site).  Why D wrong: The rectovesical pouch is found in males."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Endometriosis can occur almost anywhere in the body EXCEPT which organ?","options":["Lungs","Kidney","Spleen","Urinary bladder"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Endometriosis can occur anywhere in the body except the spleen.  Why A/B/D wrong: Lungs, kidney, and urinary bladder are all listed as possible sites of endometriosis."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Which of the following are recognized ectopic sites where endometriosis can occur? (Select all that apply)","options":["GI tract","Urinary bladder","Lungs","Broad ligament","Spleen"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Endometriosis can occur in the GI tract, urinary bladder, lungs, pleura, nose, kidney, and broad ligament.  Why E wrong: The spleen is the one organ where endometriosis does not occur."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Endometriosis is related to which hormonal state?","options":["It is not related to estrogen","Excessive estrogen (more estrogen → more chances of endometriosis)","Low progesterone only","High prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endometriosis is related to excessive estrogen — if estrogen is more, chances of endometriosis increase.  Why A wrong: That statement describes adenomyosis, which is not related to estrogen.  Why C/D wrong: The linked hormone is estrogen, not progesterone or prolactin."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Endometriosis is more common in which type of female? (Select all that apply)","options":["Obese female","Nulliparous female","Multiparous female","Underweight female"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Endometriosis is more common in obese females and nulliparous females.  Why C wrong: Multiparity is associated with adenomyosis, not endometriosis.  Why D wrong: Obesity, not low weight, is the risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Endometriosis is more common in which age group?","options":["20–25 years","30–35 years","≥ 40 years","≥ 50 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endometriosis is more common at 30–35 years.  Why C wrong: Age ≥ 40 years is characteristic of adenomyosis.  Why A/D wrong: These age ranges are not the ones stated for endometriosis."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The chief complaint (most common symptom) of endometriosis is:","options":["Menorrhagia","Secondary dysmenorrhea (pain during menstruation)","Amenorrhea","Fever"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chief complaint / most common symptom of endometriosis is secondary dysmenorrhea (pain during menstruation).  Why A wrong: Menorrhagia is the most common complaint of adenomyosis.  Why C/D wrong: Amenorrhea and fever are not the chief complaint of endometriosis."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Which of the following are \"other complaints\" of endometriosis besides secondary dysmenorrhea? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dyspareunia (pain during intercourse)","Infertility","Amenorrhea","Excessive menstrual bleeding"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Other complaints of endometriosis include dyspareunia (pain during intercourse) and infertility.  Why C wrong: Amenorrhea is not a listed complaint.  Why D wrong: Excessive bleeding/menorrhagia is the hallmark of adenomyosis, not the other complaints of endometriosis."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Dyspareunia, a complaint in endometriosis, refers to:","options":["Painful urination","Pain during menstruation","Pain during intercourse","Painful defecation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dyspareunia is pain during intercourse.  Why A wrong: Painful urination is dysuria.  Why B wrong: Pain during menstruation is dysmenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Painful defecation is dyschezia."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Adenomyosis is defined as the condition where endometrium is found in the:","options":["Ovary","Myometrium","Pouch of Douglas","Fallopian tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adenomyosis is the condition where endometrium is found in the myometrium.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are sites of endometriosis, not adenomyosis."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Adenomyosis is also known by which alternative name?","options":["Endometriosis externa","Endometriosis interna","Chocolate cyst","Chronic endometritis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adenomyosis is also called endometriosis interna (endometrium inside the myometrium).  Why A wrong: \"Externa\" would refer to endometriosis outside the uterus.  Why C wrong: Chocolate cyst is ovarian endometriosis.  Why D wrong: Chronic endometritis is an infection, not adenomyosis."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Regarding its relationship to estrogen, adenomyosis is:","options":["Related to excessive estrogen","Not related to estrogen","Related to low progesterone","Related to high prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adenomyosis is NOT related to estrogen (unlike endometriosis).  Why A wrong: Excessive estrogen is linked to endometriosis, not adenomyosis.  Why C/D wrong: Neither progesterone nor prolactin defines the estrogen (non-)relationship of adenomyosis."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Adenomyosis is more common in which type of female?","options":["Nulliparous female","Multiparous female","Obese female only","Adolescent female"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adenomyosis is more common in multiparous females.  Why A wrong: Nulliparity is associated with endometriosis.  Why C wrong: Obesity is a risk for endometriosis; multiparity is the stated risk for adenomyosis.  Why D wrong: It is not primarily an adolescent condition (age ≥ 40 years)."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Adenomyosis is more common in which age group?","options":["20–25 years","30–35 years","≥ 40 years","< 20 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Adenomyosis is more common at age ≥ 40 years.  Why B wrong: 30–35 years is the age for endometriosis.  Why A/D wrong: These younger ranges are not stated for adenomyosis."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"The most common complaint of adenomyosis is:","options":["Secondary dysmenorrhea","Excessive bleeding (menorrhagia)","Dyspareunia","Infertility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common complaint of adenomyosis is excessive bleeding, called menorrhagia.  Why A/C/D wrong: Secondary dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and infertility are more characteristic complaints of endometriosis (secondary dysmenorrhea is a secondary complaint of adenomyosis)."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Besides menorrhagia, another complaint of adenomyosis is:","options":["Amenorrhea","Secondary dysmenorrhea","Dyspareunia","Infertility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adenomyosis also causes secondary dysmenorrhea.  Why A wrong: Amenorrhea is not a listed complaint.  Why C/D wrong: Dyspareunia and infertility are listed under endometriosis complaints, not adenomyosis."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"Which condition is estrogen-dependent: endometriosis or adenomyosis?","options":["Both are estrogen-dependent","Endometriosis is estrogen-related; adenomyosis is not","Adenomyosis is estrogen-related; endometriosis is not","Neither is estrogen-related"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endometriosis is related to excessive estrogen, whereas adenomyosis is not related to estrogen.  Why A/C/D wrong: The distinguishing point is that only endometriosis is estrogen-related."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A 32-year-old nulliparous obese woman presents with secondary dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and infertility. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":["Adenomyosis","Endometriosis","Uterine fibroid","Cervical cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Age 30–35, nulliparous, obese, secondary dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia and infertility all point to endometriosis.  Why A wrong: Adenomyosis occurs at ≥ 40 years, in multiparous women, presenting mainly with menorrhagia.  Why C wrong: Fibroid typically presents with menorrhagia and is usually asymptomatic.  Why D wrong: The presentation does not fit cervical cancer."},{"section":"Batch 079 · Uterine fibroid…","question":"A 42-year-old multiparous woman presents with heavy menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia) and secondary dysmenorrhea; imaging shows endometrium within the myometrium. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":["Endometriosis","Adenomyosis","Submucous fibroid","Ovarian chocolate cyst"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Age ≥ 40, multiparous, menorrhagia, and endometrium in the myometrium are classic for adenomyosis (endometriosis interna).  Why A wrong: Endometriosis is estrogen-related, in younger nulliparous women, with dysmenorrhea as chief complaint.  Why C wrong: A fibroid is a myometrial tumour, not ectopic endometrium within the myometrium.  Why D wrong: A chocolate cyst is ovarian endometriosis, not myometrial."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In adenomyosis, the endometrium is abnormally found within which structure of the uterus?","options":["Perimetrium","Myometrium","Cervix","Endocervical canal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In adenomyosis the endometrial tissue is found inside the myometrium (the muscular wall of the uterus).  Why A wrong: Perimetrium is the serosal outer covering, not the site of adenomyosis.  Why C wrong: Adenomyosis involves the uterine body wall, not the cervix.  Why D wrong: The endocervical canal is not the site of the ectopic endometrium in adenomyosis."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In adenomyosis, the uterus typically enlarges to approximately the size of a pregnancy of how many weeks?","options":["6–8 weeks","8–10 weeks","12–14 weeks","16–18 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In adenomyosis the uterus size increases to about that of a 12–14 weeks pregnancy.  Why A/B wrong: These are smaller than the described adenomyotic enlargement.  Why D wrong: Adenomyosis does not typically enlarge the uterus beyond the 12–14 week size."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The pattern of uterine enlargement in adenomyosis is best described as:","options":["Irregular and nodular","Uniformly (symmetrically) enlarged","Enlarged only at the fundus","Asymmetrically enlarged with a lateral mass"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In adenomyosis the uterus is uniformly (symmetrically) enlarged.  Why A wrong: Irregular/nodular enlargement suggests fibroids, not adenomyosis.  Why C wrong: Enlargement is uniform, not confined to the fundus.  Why D wrong: An asymmetric lateral mass is not the described feature."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"What is the most common complaint (presenting symptom) in adenomyosis?","options":["Dyspareunia","Menorrhagia","Amenorrhea","Post-coital bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common complaint in adenomyosis is menorrhagia (heavy menstrual bleeding).  Why A wrong: Dyspareunia is part of the endometriosis triad, not the most common adenomyosis complaint.  Why C wrong: Amenorrhea (absence of menses) is not typical of adenomyosis.  Why D wrong: Post-coital bleeding is more associated with cervical pathology."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In endometriosis, ectopic endometrial tissue located outside the uterus responds to hormones and, during menstruation, will:","options":["Remain inactive","Shed like normal endometrium","Turn malignant","Be reabsorbed completely without effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ectopic endometrial tissue responds to hormones and sheds during menstruation just like normal endometrium.  Why A wrong: It is hormonally active, not inactive.  Why C wrong: Malignant transformation is not the described mechanism.  Why D wrong: It sheds and, having no exit, becomes encysted rather than being harmlessly reabsorbed."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In endometriosis, because the shed ectopic endometrial tissue has no space to come out, the sequence that follows is:","options":["It is expelled through the vagina","It becomes encysted and adhesions are formed","It calcifies into a fibroid","It is absorbed into the bloodstream"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With no space to exit, the shed tissue becomes encysted and adhesions are formed.  Why A wrong: The ectopic tissue cannot exit through the vagina, which is the whole problem.  Why C wrong: Calcification into fibroid is not the described pathology.  Why D wrong: It becomes encysted locally, not absorbed into the blood."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In endometriosis, the adhesions that form ultimately lead to which complication?","options":["Menorrhagia","Infertility","Uterine rupture","Cervical stenosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The adhesions formed in endometriosis lead to infertility.  Why A wrong: Menorrhagia is the hallmark of adenomyosis, not the adhesion complication of endometriosis.  Why C wrong: Uterine rupture is not a described consequence of endometriosis adhesions.  Why D wrong: Cervical stenosis is not the described outcome."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The chocolate-colored (brown) cyst characteristic of endometriosis in the ovary derives its color from:","options":["Melanin deposition","Breakdown of RBCs releasing hemosiderin pigment","Bile pigment accumulation","Purulent (pus) collection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RBC breakdown releases hemosiderin pigment, giving the cyst its brown color, hence \"chocolate cyst.\"  Why A wrong: Melanin is not the source of the brown color.  Why C wrong: Bile pigment is not involved.  Why D wrong: A chocolate cyst is old blood, not pus."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"A brown cyst found in the ovary of a patient with endometriosis is specifically called a:","options":["Dermoid cyst","Chocolate cyst (endometrioma)","Corpus luteum cyst","Follicular cyst"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The brown ovarian cyst of endometriosis is called a chocolate cyst (endometrioma).  Why A wrong: A dermoid cyst is a teratoma containing skin/hair, not old blood.  Why C wrong: A corpus luteum cyst is a functional cyst, not the endometriotic brown cyst.  Why D wrong: A follicular cyst is a functional ovarian cyst, not an endometrioma."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In endometriosis affecting the peritoneum, the multiple small blue/black cysts are described as having which appearance?","options":["Cobble-stone appearance","Powder-burn / gunshot appearance","Ground-glass appearance","Snowstorm appearance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On the peritoneum, endometriosis produces multiple small blue/black cysts called powder-burn / gunshot appearance.  Why A wrong: Cobble-stone appearance describes the uterosacral ligament lesions, not the peritoneal lesions.  Why C wrong: Ground-glass is a radiologic term, not this description.  Why D wrong: Snowstorm appearance is associated with molar pregnancy on ultrasound."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"On per vaginal examination in a patient with endometriosis, the uterus size and mobility are typically:","options":["Enlarged and mobile","Normal in size and fixed (cannot be moved)","Enlarged and fixed","Small and mobile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In endometriosis, per vaginal exam shows the uterus normal in size but fixed (cannot be moved) due to adhesions.  Why A wrong: Enlarged mobile uterus describes adenomyosis, not endometriosis.  Why C wrong: In endometriosis the size is normal (fixed), not enlarged.  Why D wrong: The uterus is fixed, not mobile, in endometriosis."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"On per vaginal examination in endometriosis, the adnexa (tubes + ovaries) reveal:","options":["No mass","An adnexal mass (chocolate mass)","A pedunculated fibroid","A cervical polyp"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In endometriosis an adnexal mass (the chocolate/endometriotic mass involving tubes + ovaries) is palpable.  Why A wrong: Absence of adnexal mass describes adenomyosis.  Why C wrong: A pedunculated fibroid is a different entity.  Why D wrong: A cervical polyp is unrelated to the adnexal findings."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"On per vaginal examination in adenomyosis, the uterus is characteristically:","options":["Enlarged, symmetrical and mobile","Enlarged, irregular and fixed","Normal in size and fixed","Small and immobile"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In adenomyosis the uterus is enlarged (not more than a 12–14 weeks pregnant uterus size), symmetrical, and mobile.  Why B wrong: It is symmetrical and mobile, not irregular and fixed.  Why C wrong: Normal size and fixed describes endometriosis.  Why D wrong: The uterus is enlarged and mobile, not small and immobile."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"On per vaginal examination in adenomyosis, the enlarged uterus should not exceed the size of a pregnant uterus of how many weeks?","options":["8 weeks","10 weeks","12–14 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In adenomyosis the enlarged uterus is not more than 12–14 weeks pregnant uterus size.  Why A/B wrong: These underestimate the described maximum size.  Why D wrong: Adenomyosis does not enlarge the uterus to 20 weeks size."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Regarding the adnexa on per vaginal examination in adenomyosis:","options":["A chocolate mass is felt","No adnexal mass is felt","Bilateral adnexal masses are felt","A tender fixed mass is felt"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In adenomyosis no adnexal mass is felt (in contrast to endometriosis).  Why A wrong: A chocolate/adnexal mass is a feature of endometriosis.  Why C/D wrong: Palpable adnexal masses are not features of adenomyosis."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"What is the investigation of choice (IOC) for endometriosis?","options":["MRI","Transvaginal ultrasound","Laparoscopy","Hysteroscopy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Laparoscopy is the investigation of choice (IOC) for endometriosis as it allows direct visualization.  Why A wrong: MRI is the IOC for adenomyosis, not endometriosis.  Why B wrong: Transvaginal ultrasound is the first investigation for adenomyosis.  Why D wrong: Hysteroscopy examines the uterine cavity, not the pelvic peritoneal deposits of endometriosis."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"On laparoscopy for endometriosis, a lesion on the OVARY is seen as a:","options":["Gunshot appearance","Chocolate cyst","Cobble stone appearance","Powder-burn spot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On laparoscopy, the ovarian endometriotic lesion appears as a chocolate cyst.  Why A wrong: Gunshot appearance is seen on the peritoneum.  Why C wrong: Cobble stone appearance is seen on the uterosacral ligament.  Why D wrong: Powder-burn describes peritoneal lesions, not the ovarian chocolate cyst."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"On laparoscopy for endometriosis, a lesion on the PERITONEUM appears as:","options":["Chocolate cyst","Gunshot appearance","Cobble stone appearance","Ground-glass opacity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On laparoscopy, peritoneal endometriosis appears as a gunshot (powder-burn) appearance.  Why A wrong: Chocolate cyst is the ovarian lesion.  Why C wrong: Cobble stone appearance is the uterosacral ligament finding.  Why D wrong: Ground-glass opacity is a radiologic (CT) term, not this laparoscopic finding."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"On laparoscopy for endometriosis, small cysts on the UTEROSACRAL LIGAMENT are described as:","options":["Chocolate cyst","Cobble stone appearance","Gunshot appearance","Powder-burn appearance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Small cysts on the uterosacral ligament in endometriosis are called cobble stone appearance.  Why A wrong: Chocolate cyst is the ovarian lesion.  Why C/D wrong: Gunshot/powder-burn appearance describes peritoneal lesions."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"What is the investigation of choice (IOC) for adenomyosis?","options":["Laparoscopy","MRI","Hysteroscopy","Plain X-ray abdomen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MRI is the investigation of choice for adenomyosis.  Why A wrong: Laparoscopy is the IOC for endometriosis.  Why C wrong: Hysteroscopy is not the IOC for adenomyosis.  Why D wrong: Plain X-ray has no role in diagnosing adenomyosis."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"What is the FIRST investigation done in a suspected case of adenomyosis?","options":["MRI","Transvaginal ultrasound","Laparoscopy","CT scan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first investigation in adenomyosis is transvaginal ultrasound (MRI is the IOC).  Why A wrong: MRI is the IOC (confirmatory), not the first investigation.  Why C wrong: Laparoscopy belongs to endometriosis workup.  Why D wrong: CT scan is not the first-line investigation described."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Endometriosis is fundamentally caused by which hormonal state?","options":["Excessive estrogen","Estrogen deficiency","Excessive progesterone","Deficiency of GnRh"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Endometriosis is due to excessive estrogen — it is an estrogen-dependent (hyperestrogenic) condition.  Why B wrong: Estrogen deficiency would not drive endometriosis; low estrogen is actually protective.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is used therapeutically to oppose estrogen, not the cause.  Why D wrong: Continuous GnRh is used as treatment; GnRh deficiency is not the cause."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Which class of drug is used in endometriosis to decrease estrogen levels, remembered by the mnemonic \"Dominos\"?","options":["Progesterone","Danazol","Letrozole","GnRh analogue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the mnemonic (Dominos–Pizza–Got–Late), \"Dominos\" stands for Danazol, a drug used to decrease estrogen in endometriosis.  Why A wrong: Progesterone corresponds to \"Pizza\" in the mnemonic.  Why C wrong: Letrozole corresponds to \"Late.\"  Why D wrong: GnRh (continuous) corresponds to \"Got.\""},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Which of the following drugs used in endometriosis is a hormone that opposes estrogen and corresponds to \"Pizza\" in the mnemonic?","options":["Danazol","Progesterone","GnRh","Letrozole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone (\"Pizza\") is used to oppose/decrease estrogen effects in endometriosis.  Why A wrong: Danazol is \"Dominos.\"  Why C wrong: GnRh (continuous) is \"Got.\"  Why D wrong: Letrozole is \"Late.\""},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In the medical management of endometriosis, GnRh is used in which manner to suppress estrogen?","options":["Pulsatile administration","Continuous administration","Single dose only","Weekly intermittent doses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GnRh is given continuously in endometriosis, which downregulates the pituitary and reduces estrogen.  Why A wrong: Pulsatile GnRh would stimulate, not suppress, the gonadal axis.  Why C/D wrong: The source specifies continuous administration, not single or intermittent weekly dosing."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Which aromatase inhibitor, remembered as \"Late\" in the endometriosis drug mnemonic, decreases estrogen production?","options":["Danazol","Progesterone","Letrozole","Leuprolide"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Letrozole (\"Late\") is an aromatase inhibitor that lowers estrogen and is used in endometriosis.  Why A wrong: Danazol is \"Dominos.\"  Why B wrong: Progesterone is \"Pizza.\"  Why D wrong: Leuprolide is a GnRh analogue, but the mnemonic word \"Late\" specifically denotes Letrozole."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The full mnemonic for drugs used to decrease estrogen in endometriosis (\"Dominos Pizza Got Late\") stands for which set of drugs?","options":["Danazol, Progesterone, GnRh, Letrozole","Danazol, Prostaglandin, GnRh, Levonorgestrel","Diazepam, Progesterone, Gestodene, Letrozole","Danazol, Progesterone, Gonadotropin, Leuprolide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dominos = Danazol, Pizza = Progesterone, Got = GnRh (continuous), Late = Letrozole.  Why B wrong: Prostaglandin and Levonorgestrel are not the listed drugs.  Why C wrong: Diazepam and Gestodene are not on the list.  Why D wrong: Gonadotropin and Leuprolide are not the words used in this mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The primary surgical management goal in adenomyosis (with a bulky uterus) is to:","options":["Remove the ovaries only","Remove the uterus (hysterectomy)","Remove the fallopian tubes only","Perform myomectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because the uterus is bulky in adenomyosis, management is to remove the uterus by hysterectomy (removal of uterus and cervix).  Why A wrong: Removing only the ovaries does not treat the adenomyotic uterus.  Why C wrong: Removing only the tubes does not address the uterine disease.  Why D wrong: Myomectomy removes fibroids, not the diffuse adenomyotic uterus."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In adenomyosis, hysterectomy involves removal of which structures?","options":["Uterus only","Uterus and cervix","Uterus, cervix and both ovaries","Cervix only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines hysterectomy here as uterus and cervix removal.  Why A wrong: The cervix is also removed in this definition.  Why C wrong: Removal of ovaries depends on age; it is not included by default in the hysterectomy definition.  Why D wrong: Removing the cervix alone is not a hysterectomy."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"During hysterectomy for adenomyosis, if the patient's age is LESS than 45 years, the recommended approach regarding the ovaries is:","options":["Remove both ovaries","Do not remove the ovaries","Remove one ovary","Remove ovaries only if a cyst is present"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If age is less than 45 years, do not remove the ovaries (to preserve ovarian hormone function).  Why A wrong: Bilateral removal is reserved for patients ≥45 years.  Why C wrong: The source states not to remove ovaries, not to remove one.  Why D wrong: The criterion given is age (<45), not the presence of a cyst."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"During hysterectomy for adenomyosis, if the patient's age is 45 years or more, what is done with the ovaries?","options":["Ovaries are preserved","Both ovaries are removed (bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy)","Only one ovary is removed","Ovaries are biopsied but retained"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If age ≥45 years, remove the ovaries — bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy is performed.  Why A wrong: Ovaries are preserved only when age is <45 years.  Why C wrong: Both ovaries (bilateral) are removed, not just one.  Why D wrong: The source specifies removal, not biopsy-and-retain."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"\"Bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy\" refers to the removal of:","options":["Both fallopian tubes only","Both ovaries only","Both fallopian tubes and both ovaries","Uterus and cervix"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy = removal of both fallopian tubes (salpingo-) and both ovaries (-oophorectomy).  Why A wrong: That describes salpingectomy alone.  Why B wrong: That describes oophorectomy alone.  Why D wrong: Removal of uterus and cervix is hysterectomy, not salpingo-oophorectomy."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The classic symptom triad of endometriosis includes secondary dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and:","options":["Menorrhagia","Infertility","Amenorrhea","Post-menopausal bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The endometriosis symptom triad is secondary dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and infertility.  Why A wrong: Menorrhagia is the hallmark of adenomyosis, not the endometriosis triad.  Why C wrong: Amenorrhea is not part of the triad.  Why D wrong: Post-menopausal bleeding is not part of the triad."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Which symptom is the most common (m/c) presenting feature of endometriosis?","options":["Dyspareunia","Infertility","Secondary dysmenorrhea","Menorrhagia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary dysmenorrhea is noted as the most common (m/c) symptom in the endometriosis triad.  Why A wrong: Dyspareunia is part of the triad but not marked as most common.  Why B wrong: Infertility is part of the triad but not the most common symptom.  Why D wrong: Menorrhagia is an adenomyosis feature."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"\"Dyspareunia,\" one component of the endometriosis triad, refers to:","options":["Painful menstruation","Painful sexual intercourse","Absence of menstruation","Excessive menstrual bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dyspareunia means painful sexual intercourse.  Why A wrong: Painful menstruation is dysmenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Absence of menstruation is amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Excessive menstrual bleeding is menorrhagia."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"According to the notes, what is the recommended management category for endometriosis in the important questions?","options":["Immediate hysterectomy","Medical management","Radiation therapy","No treatment required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The management of endometriosis is medical management (drugs to reduce estrogen).  Why A wrong: Hysterectomy is the management for adenomyosis, not first-line for endometriosis.  Why C wrong: Radiation therapy is not used for endometriosis.  Why D wrong: Endometriosis requires management, not observation alone."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Should a chocolate cyst of the ovary be surgically removed in a patient with endometriosis?","options":["Yes, always remove it surgically","No, it disappears on its own when endometriosis is treated","Yes, but only after menopause","Yes, remove it along with the ovary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chocolate cyst need not be removed; it disappears on its own when the endometriosis is treated.  Why A wrong: Routine surgical removal is not required.  Why C wrong: Waiting until menopause is not the described approach.  Why D wrong: Removing the ovary is unnecessary as the cyst resolves with treatment."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Endometriosis is best described as which type of hormone-dependent condition?","options":["Hypoestrogenic","Hyperestrogenic (estrogen-dependent)","Hyperprogesteronic","Hyperandrogenic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endometriosis is a hyperestrogenic condition, dependent on estrogen.  Why A wrong: It is not hypoestrogenic; low estrogen protects against it.  Why C wrong: It is estrogen-dependent, not progesterone-driven.  Why D wrong: It is not androgen-dependent."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Why is smoking said to be protective against endometriosis?","options":["It increases progesterone","It decreases the level of estrogen","It increases GnRh secretion","It increases blood flow to the ovaries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smoking decreases the level of estrogen, and since endometriosis is estrogen-dependent, it therefore protects from endometriosis.  Why A wrong: The protective effect is via decreasing estrogen, not increasing progesterone.  Why C wrong: The mechanism cited is lowered estrogen, not increased GnRh.  Why D wrong: Increased ovarian blood flow is not the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"According to the definition given, infertility is the inability of a couple to conceive even after how long of unprotected intercourse?","options":["6 months","1 year","2 years","3 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infertility is defined as inability to conceive even after 1 year of unprotected intercourse.  Why A wrong: 6 months is shorter than the defined period.  Why C/D wrong: 2 or 3 years exceed the standard 1-year definition given."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The definition of infertility specifies that the intercourse must be:","options":["Protected","Unprotected","Timed to ovulation only","At least twice weekly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The definition requires unprotected intercourse for 1 year.  Why A wrong: Protected intercourse would not test fertility.  Why C wrong: The definition does not restrict to ovulation-timed intercourse.  Why D wrong: A specific twice-weekly frequency is not part of the definition."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"\"Primary infertility\" is defined as a situation in which the female:","options":["Was earlier able to conceive but now cannot","Has never conceived","Has had recurrent miscarriages","Conceives but delivers preterm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Primary infertility means the female has never conceived.  Why A wrong: That description is secondary infertility.  Why C wrong: Recurrent miscarriage is a separate entity, not the definition of primary infertility.  Why D wrong: Preterm delivery is unrelated to the infertility classification."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"\"Secondary infertility\" refers to a female who:","options":["Has never conceived","Was earlier able to conceive but is now unable to conceive","Is infertile due to her male partner only","Has primary amenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary infertility is when a female was earlier able to conceive but is now unable to conceive.  Why A wrong: Never having conceived is primary infertility.  Why C wrong: Secondary infertility is defined by the female's prior conception, not the cause.  Why D wrong: Primary amenorrhea is unrelated to this definition."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"According to the causes of infertility, female infertility accounts for approximately what percentage of cases?","options":["10%","30–40%","40–50%","60–70%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Female infertility accounts for about 40–50% of causes.  Why A wrong: 10% corresponds to combined or unknown causes.  Why B wrong: 30–40% corresponds to male-partner (male) infertility.  Why D wrong: 60–70% is not a listed figure."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"According to the causes of infertility, male infertility (male partner) accounts for approximately:","options":["10%","30–40%","40–50%","50–60%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The male partner (male infertility) accounts for about 30–40% of cases.  Why A wrong: 10% is for combined or unknown causes.  Why C wrong: 40–50% is for female infertility.  Why D wrong: 50–60% is not a listed value."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In the causes of infertility, \"combined\" causes account for what percentage?","options":["10%","20%","30%","40%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Combined causes account for about 10% of infertility.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values are not assigned to the combined category in the source."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"According to the source, \"unknown\" causes of infertility account for approximately:","options":["10%","25%","40%","50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Unknown causes account for about 10% of infertility.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the values assigned to the unknown category."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"When a couple complains of infertility, what is the 1st investigation to be done?","options":["Hysterosalpingography","Semen analysis","Laparoscopy","Serum FSH/LH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Whenever a couple complains of infertility, the first investigation to do is semen analysis.  Why A wrong: Hysterosalpingography evaluates tubal patency but is not the first test.  Why C wrong: Laparoscopy is more invasive and not the first step.  Why D wrong: Hormonal assays are not the described first investigation."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"For semen analysis, the recommended period of sexual abstinence before giving the sample is:","options":["1–2 days","3–5 days","7–10 days","14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The advised abstinence before semen analysis is 3–5 days.  Why A wrong: 1–2 days is shorter than advised.  Why C/D wrong: 7–10 or 14 days is longer than the recommended abstinence."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"How should the semen specimen for analysis be collected?","options":["In a condom during intercourse","By masturbation into a container at the clinic","By coitus interruptus into a plastic bag","By post-coital vaginal aspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient comes to the clinic and provides the specimen by masturbation into a container.  Why A wrong: The specimen should NOT be collected in a condom.  Why C wrong: Coitus interruptus into a bag is not the described method.  Why D wrong: Vaginal aspiration is not how semen samples are collected."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Which of the following is an INCORRECT method of semen specimen collection?","options":["Collecting by masturbation","Collecting into a clean container","Collecting in a condom","Coming to the clinic to give the sample"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source explicitly states the specimen should not be collected in a condom (spermicidal lubricants/latex affect results).  Why A wrong: Collection by masturbation is correct.  Why B wrong: Collecting into a container is correct.  Why D wrong: Coming to the clinic to give the sample is advised."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"During the period of abstinence before semen analysis, the patient should specifically avoid:","options":["Exercise and travel","Smoking and alcohol","Spicy food and caffeine","Any prescribed medication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smoking and alcohol should be avoided in the period of abstinence.  Why A wrong: Avoidance of exercise/travel is not the stated instruction.  Why C wrong: Spicy food/caffeine restriction is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Stopping prescribed medication is not the instruction given."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In a normal semen analysis report, the pH of semen should be:","options":["≥ 6.0","≥ 7.2","≥ 8.0","≤ 5.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal semen pH is ≥ 7.2.  Why A wrong: ≥6.0 is below the normal threshold.  Why C wrong: ≥8.0 overstates the normal lower limit.  Why D wrong: ≤5.5 is abnormally acidic."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The normal semen volume in a semen analysis report is:","options":["≥ 0.5 mL","≥ 1.5 mL","≥ 3.0 mL","≥ 5.0 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal semen volume is ≥ 1.5 mL.  Why A wrong: ≥0.5 mL is below normal.  Why C/D wrong: These overstate the normal lower reference limit."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The complete absence of semen (no semen) on analysis is termed:","options":["Azoospermia","Aspermia","Oligospermia","Necrospermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: No semen at all is called aspermia.  Why A wrong: Azoospermia is no sperms in the semen (semen is present but has no sperm).  Why C wrong: Oligospermia is low sperm concentration.  Why D wrong: Necrospermia is excess dead/non-living sperm."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The normal sperm concentration in a semen analysis report is:","options":["≥ 5 million/mL","≥ 15 million/mL","≥ 39 million/mL","≥ 40 million/mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal sperm concentration is ≥ 15 million/mL.  Why A wrong: <5 million/mL indicates severe oligospermia.  Why C wrong: ≥39 million is the total sperm count per ejaculate, not concentration.  Why D wrong: ≥40% refers to motility, not concentration."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"A sperm concentration less than 15 million/mL is termed:","options":["Azoospermia","Oligospermia","Asthenospermia","Teratospermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sperm concentration <15 million/mL is called oligospermia.  Why A wrong: Azoospermia is no sperm at all.  Why C wrong: Asthenospermia is reduced motility.  Why D wrong: Teratospermia is abnormal morphology."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"A sperm concentration of less than 5 million/mL is specifically called:","options":["Oligospermia","Severe oligospermia","Azoospermia","Necrospermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sperm concentration <5 million/mL is severe oligospermia.  Why A wrong: Ordinary oligospermia is <15 million/mL.  Why C wrong: Azoospermia is complete absence of sperm.  Why D wrong: Necrospermia refers to dead sperm."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The complete absence of sperms in the semen is called:","options":["Aspermia","Azoospermia","Oligospermia","Asthenospermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: No sperms in the semen is termed azoospermia.  Why A wrong: Aspermia is absence of semen (no ejaculate), not absence of sperm within semen.  Why C wrong: Oligospermia is low, not absent, sperm.  Why D wrong: Asthenospermia is reduced motility."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The normal total sperm count per ejaculate is:","options":["≥ 15 million/ejaculate","≥ 39 million/ejaculate","≥ 40 million/ejaculate","≥ 58 million/ejaculate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal total sperm count is ≥ 39 million/ejaculate.  Why A wrong: 15 million/mL is the concentration, not the total count.  Why C wrong: ≥40% is the motility value.  Why D wrong: ≥58% is the viability value."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The normal total sperm motility in a semen analysis is:","options":["≥ 32%","≥ 40%","≥ 58%","≥ 4%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal total motility is ≥ 40%.  Why A wrong: ≥32% is the normal progressive motility.  Why C wrong: ≥58% is normal viability.  Why D wrong: ≥4% is the normal morphology cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Reduced sperm motility on semen analysis is termed:","options":["Oligospermia","Asthenospermia","Teratospermia","Necrospermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Less motility is called asthenospermia.  Why A wrong: Oligospermia is low sperm concentration.  Why C wrong: Teratospermia is abnormal morphology.  Why D wrong: Necrospermia is excess dead sperm."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The normal progressive motility of sperm in a semen analysis is:","options":["≥ 4%","≥ 32%","≥ 40%","≥ 58%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal progressive motility is ≥ 32%.  Why A wrong: ≥4% is the normal morphology value.  Why C wrong: ≥40% is the total motility value.  Why D wrong: ≥58% is the viability value."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"For normal sperm morphology, what percentage of sperm should have normal forms?","options":["≥ 4%","≥ 15%","≥ 32%","≥ 40%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At least ≥ 4% of sperm should have normal morphology.  Why B wrong: 15 million/mL is the concentration; 15% is not the morphology cutoff.  Why C wrong: ≥32% is progressive motility.  Why D wrong: ≥40% is total motility."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"When more abnormal sperm forms than normal are present on morphology assessment, the condition is termed:","options":["Asthenospermia","Teratospermia / Teratozoospermia","Necrospermia","Azoospermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Excess abnormal forms is teratospermia / teratozoospermia.  Why A wrong: Asthenospermia is reduced motility.  Why C wrong: Necrospermia is excess dead sperm.  Why D wrong: Azoospermia is absence of sperm."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The normal percentage of living (viable) sperms in a semen analysis is:","options":["≥ 4%","≥ 32%","≥ 40%","≥ 58%"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Normal living/viable sperm should be ≥ 58%.  Why A wrong: ≥4% is the morphology cutoff.  Why B wrong: ≥32% is progressive motility.  Why C wrong: ≥40% is total motility."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"When there is an excess of non-living/dead sperm in the semen, the condition is called:","options":["Teratospermia","Necrospermia","Asthenospermia","Oligospermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: More non-living/dead sperm is termed necrospermia.  Why A wrong: Teratospermia is abnormal morphology.  Why C wrong: Asthenospermia is reduced motility.  Why D wrong: Oligospermia is low sperm concentration."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"According to the notes, what is the single most important criterion in evaluating male infertility?","options":["Serum testosterone","Semen analysis","Testicular biopsy","Scrotal ultrasound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most important criterion is semen analysis.  Why A wrong: Serum testosterone is a hormone level, not the most important overall criterion.  Why C wrong: Testicular biopsy is not the primary criterion.  Why D wrong: Scrotal ultrasound is not the described key criterion."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Among semen analysis parameters, which is ranked as the MOST important?","options":["Sperm concentration","Sperm motility","Sperm morphology","Semen volume"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sperm morphology is ranked the most important parameter.  Why A wrong: Sperm concentration is ranked 3rd most important.  Why B wrong: Sperm motility is ranked 2nd most important.  Why D wrong: Semen volume is not listed in this priority ranking."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In the ranking of semen analysis parameters, sperm motility is the:","options":["Most important","2nd most important","3rd most important","Least important"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sperm motility is the 2nd most important parameter.  Why A wrong: Morphology is the most important.  Why C wrong: Concentration is the 3rd most important.  Why D wrong: Motility is high on the list, not the least important."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In the ranking of semen analysis parameters, sperm concentration is the:","options":["Most important","2nd most important","3rd most important","Not important"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sperm concentration is the 3rd most important parameter.  Why A wrong: Morphology is most important.  Why B wrong: Motility is 2nd most important.  Why D wrong: Concentration is ranked 3rd, so it is still important."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In the hormonal axis controlling spermatogenesis, the hypothalamus secretes which hormone that acts on the anterior pituitary?","options":["FSH","LH","GnRh","Testosterone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The hypothalamus secretes GnRh, which acts on the anterior pituitary.  Why A/B wrong: FSH and LH are secreted by the anterior pituitary in response to GnRh.  Why D wrong: Testosterone is secreted by Leydig cells of the testis, not the hypothalamus."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In response to GnRh, the anterior pituitary secretes which two hormones acting on the testis?","options":["Testosterone and estrogen","FSH and LH","Prolactin and oxytocin","ACTH and TSH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior pituitary secretes FSH and LH, which act on the testis.  Why A wrong: Testosterone is a testicular product; estrogen is not the pituitary output here.  Why C wrong: Prolactin and oxytocin are not the gonadotropins in this axis.  Why D wrong: ACTH and TSH act on the adrenal and thyroid, not the testis."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In the male reproductive axis, FSH from the anterior pituitary acts primarily on which testicular cells?","options":["Leydig cells","Sertoli cells","Germ cells only","Interstitial cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: FSH acts on the Sertoli cells.  Why A wrong: LH acts on the Leydig cells.  Why C wrong: FSH targets Sertoli cells, which support germ cells; it does not act on germ cells directly in this diagram.  Why D wrong: Interstitial (Leydig) cells respond to LH, not FSH."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In the male reproductive axis, LH from the anterior pituitary acts on which testicular cells?","options":["Sertoli cells","Leydig cells","Spermatogonia","Peritubular cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: LH acts on the Leydig cells (which produce testosterone).  Why A wrong: Sertoli cells respond to FSH.  Why C wrong: Spermatogonia are germ cells, not the direct target of LH.  Why D wrong: Peritubular cells are not the described LH target."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Which hormone is produced by the Leydig cells of the testis in response to LH?","options":["Estrogen","Testosterone","Inhibin","Progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Leydig cells produce testosterone in response to LH.  Why A wrong: Estrogen is not the primary Leydig cell product in this axis.  Why C wrong: Inhibin is produced by Sertoli cells, not Leydig cells.  Why D wrong: Progesterone is not the testicular hormone described here."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Testosterone produced by the testis positively stimulates which process?","options":["Oogenesis","Spermatogenesis","Ovulation","Menstruation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Testosterone provides a positive (+) stimulus to spermatogenesis.  Why A wrong: Oogenesis is the female process of egg formation.  Why C/D wrong: Ovulation and menstruation are female reproductive events, not driven by testosterone in this axis."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In the male hormonal axis, testosterone exerts what type of feedback on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary?","options":["Positive feedback","Negative feedback","No feedback","Neutral feedback"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Testosterone provides negative (-ve) feedback to the hypothalamus/anterior pituitary, regulating GnRh, FSH, and LH.  Why A wrong: On the higher centers it is negative, not positive, feedback.  Why C/D wrong: There is a defined negative feedback loop, not an absent or neutral one."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Spermatogenesis refers to which process?","options":["Production of eggs","Production of sperms","Production of testosterone","Fusion of sperm and egg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogenesis is the production of sperms.  Why A wrong: Production of eggs is oogenesis.  Why C wrong: Testosterone production is steroidogenesis by Leydig cells.  Why D wrong: Fusion of sperm and egg is fertilization."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"In which specific structure of the testis does spermatogenesis occur?","options":["Epididymis","Seminiferous tubules","Rete testis","Vas deferens"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogenesis occurs in the testis, specifically in the seminiferous tubules.  Why A wrong: The epididymis is a site of sperm maturation/storage, not spermatogenesis.  Why C wrong: The rete testis is a collecting network, not the site of sperm production.  Why D wrong: The vas deferens transports sperm; it does not produce them."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"At what stage of life does spermatogenesis begin?","options":["Fetal life","Birth","Puberty","Adulthood after 25 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogenesis begins at puberty.  Why A/B wrong: It does not begin in fetal life or at birth.  Why D wrong: It begins at puberty, well before 25 years."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"The time taken to complete spermatogenesis (production of a mature sperm) is approximately:","options":["28–30 days","45–50 days","70–75 days","90–100 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Spermatogenesis takes approximately 70–75 days.  Why A/B wrong: These durations are shorter than the stated time.  Why D wrong: 90–100 days overestimates the time."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"Which hormone is the most important one needed for spermatogenesis?","options":["FSH","LH","Testosterone","Estrogen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Testosterone is the most important hormone needed for spermatogenesis.  Why A wrong: FSH supports Sertoli cells but is not named the single most important hormone here.  Why B wrong: LH stimulates testosterone but is not itself the most important for spermatogenesis.  Why D wrong: Estrogen is not the key hormone for male spermatogenesis."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"A 30-year-old woman with a symmetrically enlarged, mobile uterus (about 12–14 weeks size), heavy menstrual bleeding, and no palpable adnexal mass is most likely to have which condition, and the IOC would be:","options":["Endometriosis; laparoscopy","Adenomyosis; MRI","Fibroid; hysteroscopy","Chocolate cyst; transabdominal ultrasound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Symmetrical, mobile, uniformly enlarged uterus with menorrhagia and no adnexal mass is classic adenomyosis, for which MRI is the IOC.  Why A wrong: Endometriosis gives a fixed, normal-size uterus with an adnexal (chocolate) mass; IOC is laparoscopy.  Why C wrong: Fibroids give an irregular/nodular uterus rather than a uniformly symmetrical one.  Why D wrong: A chocolate cyst is an endometriosis finding, not the described uterine picture."},{"section":"Batch 080 · Adenomyosis pathology & clinical features…","question":"A woman with secondary dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and infertility has a normal-sized fixed uterus and a palpable adnexal mass. The single best confirmatory investigation is:","options":["Transvaginal ultrasound","MRI","Laparoscopy","Hysterosalpingography"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: This is the classic endometriosis triad with a fixed uterus and adnexal (chocolate) mass; laparoscopy is the IOC allowing direct visualization.  Why A wrong: Transvaginal ultrasound is the first test for adenomyosis, not the IOC for endometriosis.  Why B wrong: MRI is the IOC for adenomyosis.  Why D wrong: HSG evaluates tubal patency, not endometriotic deposits."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Besides testosterone, which two pituitary hormones are needed for normal spermatogenesis?","options":["LH and FSH","ACTH and TSH","GH and prolactin","Oxytocin and ADH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Other hormones needed: LH, FSH\" for spermatogenesis.  Why B wrong: ACTH and TSH regulate adrenal and thyroid function, not spermatogenesis.  Why C wrong: GH and prolactin are not the primary spermatogenesis hormones; excess prolactin actually causes infertility.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin and ADH are posterior-pituitary hormones unrelated to sperm production."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Male infertility can broadly be due to which three categories of problems?","options":["Problem in hypothalamus/pituitary, problem in testis, obstruction of sperm path","Problem in kidney, liver, and heart","Problem in bladder, urethra, and penis only","Nutritional, psychological, and environmental only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart divides male infertility into problem in hypothalamus/pituitary, problem in testis, and obstruction of sperm path.  Why B wrong: Kidney/liver/heart are not the listed categories.  Why C wrong: These are not the classification used in the notes.  Why D wrong: These factors are not the three categories shown."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"A problem in the hypothalamus/pituitary causing male infertility is classified as which type of azoospermia?","options":["Pretesticular azoospermia","Testicular azoospermia","Post-testicular azoospermia","Obstructive azoospermia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows \"Problem in hypothalamus pituitary → PRETESTICULAR AZOOSPERMIA.\"  Why B wrong: Testicular azoospermia arises from a problem in the testis.  Why C wrong: Post-testicular azoospermia is from obstruction of the sperm path.  Why D wrong: Obstructive azoospermia is a post-testicular (path obstruction) type."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which of the following is listed under \"problem in testis\" causes of male infertility?","options":["Torsion of testis","Vasectomy","Absent vas deferens","Kallman syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The testis-problem branch lists testicular azoospermia, torsion of testis, and testis tumor/orchitis.  Why B wrong: Vasectomy is an obstruction (post-testicular) cause.  Why C wrong: Absent vas deferens is a post-testicular/obstructive cause.  Why D wrong: Kallman syndrome is a hypothalamic (pretesticular) cause."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which conditions are grouped under \"problem in testis\" as testicular causes of male infertility? (Select all that apply)","options":["Testicular azoospermia","Torsion of testis","Testis tumor / orchitis","Vasectomy","Kallman syndrome"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list testicular azoospermia, torsion of testis, and testis tumor/orchitis under problem in testis.  Why D wrong: Vasectomy is an obstructive (post-testicular) cause.  Why E wrong: Kallman syndrome is a hypothalamic (pretesticular) cause."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Obstruction of the sperm path leads to which type of azoospermia?","options":["Post-testicular / obstructive azoospermia","Pretesticular azoospermia","Testicular azoospermia","Idiopathic azoospermia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows \"Obstruction of sperm path → Post-testicular azoospermia or obstructive azoospermia.\"  Why B wrong: Pretesticular is due to hypothalamic/pituitary problems.  Why C wrong: Testicular azoospermia is due to a testis problem.  Why D wrong: \"Idiopathic\" is not the labelled cause here."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which two examples are given for post-testicular / obstructive azoospermia?","options":["Vasectomy and absent vas deferens","PCOS and endometriosis","Orchitis and testicular torsion","Kallman syndrome and hyperprolactinaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give vasectomy and absent vas deferens as examples of obstructive azoospermia.  Why B wrong: PCOS and endometriosis are female infertility causes.  Why C wrong: Orchitis and torsion are testicular causes.  Why D wrong: These are pretesticular causes."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Kallman syndrome, a cause of male infertility, is best described as which type of failure?","options":["Hypothalamic failure","Testicular failure","Post-testicular obstruction","Prostatic dysfunction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Kallman syndrome (hypothalamic failure).\"  Why B wrong: It is not primarily a testicular failure.  Why C wrong: It is not an obstructive/post-testicular problem.  Why D wrong: It is not a prostatic disorder."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"An abnormally increased level of which hormone is listed as a cause of male infertility?","options":["Prolactin","Insulin","Cortisol","Aldosterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Increase prolactin level\" as a cause of male infertility.  Why B wrong: Insulin is not the listed cause.  Why C wrong: Cortisol is not mentioned as a cause here.  Why D wrong: Aldosterone is not related to this fertility point."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Among the causes of male infertility, the best prognosis is seen in which type of azoospermia?","options":["Post-testicular azoospermia","Pretesticular azoospermia","Testicular azoospermia","All have equal prognosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Best prognosis is seen in post-testicular azoospermia\" because spermatogenesis is normal, only the passage is obstructed.  Why B wrong: Pretesticular has a hormonal defect.  Why C wrong: Testicular azoospermia reflects a defect in sperm production itself.  Why D wrong: The notes specifically single out post-testicular as best prognosis."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"In post-testicular azoospermia, what is the status of LH, FSH, hypothalamus, pituitary and testosterone?","options":["All are normal","All are high","All are low","LH high but FSH low"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show LH, FSH normal and hypothalamus, pituitary, testosterone all normal (\"12 normal\") in post-testicular azoospermia.  Why B wrong: They are normal, not elevated.  Why C wrong: They are normal, not low.  Why D wrong: Both LH and FSH are normal."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Why does post-testicular azoospermia carry the best prognosis despite the absence of sperm in the ejaculate?","options":["Spermatogenesis is normal but sperms cannot come out due to obstruction in the passage","The testes are producing extra sperm","Hormone therapy alone always cures it","Sperm are being destroyed inside the testis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explain spermatogenesis is normal, sperms are formed normally, but due to some obstruction in passage they cannot come out.  Why B wrong: Production is normal, not excessive.  Why C wrong: The mechanism is obstruction, not a hormonal deficiency corrected by hormones.  Why D wrong: Sperm are formed normally, not destroyed."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"In the sperm pathway, sperm maturation (\"maturity in sperms\") occurs mainly in which structure?","options":["Epididymis","Prostate gland","Seminal vesicle","Ejaculatory duct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sperm pathway diagram labels the epididymis with \"Maturity, maturity in sperms.\"  Why B wrong: The prostate adds secretion, not maturation.  Why C wrong: Seminal vesicles add secretion and fructose.  Why D wrong: The ejaculatory duct is a conduit, not a maturation site."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which correctly orders the sperm pathway from origin toward ejaculation?","options":["Testis → epididymis → vas deferens → seminal vesicles → prostate → ejaculatory duct","Prostate → testis → epididymis → vas deferens → ejaculatory duct","Seminal vesicles → testis → epididymis → prostate → vas deferens","Vas deferens → testis → epididymis → prostate → ejaculatory duct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sperm pathway diagram runs testis → epididymis → vas deferens → seminal vesicles → prostate gland → ejaculatory duct.  Why B, C, D wrong: They scramble the correct anatomical sequence shown in the diagram."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"The seminal vesicles contribute approximately what proportion of the semen volume?","options":["60% of semen","10% of semen","90% of semen","30% of semen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state seminal vesicle secretion adds 60% of semen and is the major contribution.  Why B, C, D wrong: These percentages do not match the \"60% of semen\" stated."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which sugar contributed by the seminal vesicles is a major energy source in semen?","options":["Fructose","Glucose","Sucrose","Lactose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state seminal vesicle secretion adds fructose to semen.  Why B, C, D wrong: These sugars are not the ones named in the sperm-pathway note."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which gland's secretion is described as providing the \"major contribution to semen\"?","options":["Seminal vesicles","Prostate gland","Bulbourethral gland","Epididymis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state seminal vesicle secretion (60% + fructose) is the major contribution to semen and provides the maximum.  Why B wrong: The prostate adds secretion but is not the maximum contributor here.  Why C wrong: The bulbourethral gland adds a smaller secretion at ejaculation.  Why D wrong: The epididymis is for maturation, not the major semen volume."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"At the time of ejaculation, which gland adds its secretion to the passing sperm?","options":["Bulbourethral gland","Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pituitary gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that at the time of ejaculation the bulbourethral gland adds its secretion.  Why B, C, D wrong: These endocrine glands do not contribute to semen at ejaculation."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"According to the notes, semen is composed of secretions from which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Seminal vesicles (maximum)","Prostate fluid","Bulbourethral gland secretion","Adrenal cortex","Thyroid follicles"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list semen = secretions from seminal vesicles (maximum) + prostate fluid + bulbourethral gland secretion.  Why D wrong: The adrenal cortex does not contribute to semen.  Why E wrong: Thyroid follicles do not contribute to semen."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Testing which hormone is used to differentiate pretesticular, testicular and post-testicular causes of male infertility?","options":["FSH levels","TSH levels","Cortisol levels","Insulin levels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state all three can be differentiated by testing FSH levels.  Why B, C, D wrong: These hormones are not used for this differentiation in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"In a pretesticular cause of male infertility, the FSH level is expected to be:","options":["Low","High","Normal","Undetectable but with high LH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state pretesticular cause → FSH low.  Why B wrong: High FSH indicates a testicular cause.  Why C wrong: Normal FSH indicates a post-testicular/obstructive cause.  Why D wrong: The notes describe FSH as simply low, not undetectable with high LH."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"A high FSH level in an infertile male points to which cause?","options":["Testicular cause","Pretesticular cause","Post-testicular (obstructive) cause","Cervical factor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state testicular cause → FSH = high.  Why B wrong: Pretesticular cause has low FSH.  Why C wrong: Post-testicular/obstructive cause has normal FSH.  Why D wrong: Cervical factor is a female infertility cause."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"In a post-testicular / obstructive cause of male infertility, the FSH level is:","options":["Normal","Low","High","Fluctuating"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state post-testicular/obstructive cause → FSH = normal.  Why B wrong: Low FSH is pretesticular.  Why C wrong: High FSH is testicular.  Why D wrong: The notes describe FSH as normal, not fluctuating."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Oligospermia is defined in the notes as a sperm count in which range?","options":["Less than 15 million but more than 10 million/mL","Less than 5 million/mL","More than 20 million/mL","Exactly 15 million/mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The treatment table defines oligospermia as sperm count <15 million but >10 million/mL.  Why B wrong: <5 million/mL is severe oligospermia.  Why C wrong: >20 million/mL is not oligospermia.  Why D wrong: The definition is a range, not a single value."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"For oligospermia (sperm count <15 million but >10 million/mL), which treatment is recommended?","options":["IUI (Intrauterine insemination)","IVF (In vitro fertilization)","ICSI (Intracytoplasmic sperm injection)","No treatment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table pairs oligospermia (<15 but >10 million/mL) with IUI.  Why B wrong: IVF is for counts <10 but >5 million/mL.  Why C wrong: ICSI is for severe oligospermia (<5 million/mL).  Why D wrong: A specific treatment (IUI) is indicated."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"For a sperm count <10 million/mL but >5 million/mL, the recommended treatment is:","options":["IVF (In vitro fertilization)","IUI (Intrauterine insemination)","ICSI (Intracytoplasmic sperm injection)","Vasectomy reversal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table pairs a sperm count <10 million but >5 million/mL with IVF (the source prints \"IUF\" but means in vitro fertilization = IVF).  Why B wrong: IUI is for oligospermia (>10 million/mL).  Why C wrong: ICSI is for severe oligospermia (<5 million/mL).  Why D wrong: Vasectomy reversal is not listed in this table."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Severe oligospermia is defined as a sperm count of:","options":["<5 million/mL","<15 million/mL","<10 million/mL","<20 million/mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define severe oligospermia as <5 million/mL.  Why B wrong: <15 million/mL is ordinary oligospermia range boundary.  Why C wrong: <10 million/mL is the IVF threshold, not severe oligospermia.  Why D wrong: <20 million/mL is not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"The recommended treatment for severe oligospermia (<5 million/mL) is:","options":["ICSI (intracytoplasmic sperm injection)","IUI","IVF","Clomiphene citrate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table pairs severe oligospermia (<5 million/mL) with ICSI.  Why B wrong: IUI is for higher counts (oligospermia).  Why C wrong: IVF is for counts <10 but >5 million/mL.  Why D wrong: Clomiphene citrate is an ovulation-inducing drug used in female anovulation."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"ICSI stands for:","options":["Intracytoplasmic sperm injection","Intrauterine cervical sperm insertion","Intracervical sperm implantation","Intra-abdominal sperm injection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes expand ICSI as intracytoplasmic sperm injection.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of the abbreviation."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"The IUI (intrauterine insemination) procedure is performed at what point in the female's cycle?","options":["At the time of ovulation","During menstruation","On day 1 of the cycle","During the luteal phase after implantation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The method states IUI is done \"at the time of ovulation in female.\"  Why B wrong: It is not done during menstruation.  Why C wrong: Day 1 is menstruation onset, not ovulation.  Why D wrong: It is timed to ovulation, not post-implantation."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"What is the first step performed by the male partner in the IUI procedure?","options":["Ejaculate the semen into a container","Undergo a vasectomy","Take clomiphene citrate","Receive an HCG injection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Step 1 of the IUI method is that the male partner ejaculates in a container.  Why B wrong: Vasectomy is a cause of obstructive azoospermia, not a step.  Why C wrong: Clomiphene is used for female anovulation.  Why D wrong: HCG injection is part of ovulation induction, not IUI step 1."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"After the semen is collected for IUI, the next step is to:","options":["Wash and process the semen","Freeze it for one year","Discard the seminal plasma without processing","Inject it directly without preparation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Step 2 states \"Wash and process the semen,\" after which washed and processed sperms are taken.  Why B wrong: Freezing for a year is not the described step.  Why C wrong: The semen is washed and processed, not simply discarded.  Why D wrong: Direct injection without processing is not described."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"During IUI, the washed and processed sperms are introduced into the female using:","options":["A sterile catheter","A large-bore needle into the ovary","A laparoscope","An intramuscular injection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the washed sperms are taken with a sterile catheter and slowly introduced into the uterine cavity.  Why B wrong: The sperm are not injected into the ovary.  Why C wrong: A laparoscope is not used for IUI insertion here.  Why D wrong: It is not an intramuscular injection."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"In IUI, the processed semen is slowly introduced into which structure?","options":["Female's uterine cavity","Vagina only","Fallopian tube directly","Cervical canal only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Step 3 states the semen is slowly introduced in the female's uterine cavity.  Why B wrong: Intravaginal deposition is natural insemination, not IUI.  Why C wrong: Direct tubal introduction is not the described route.  Why D wrong: It is introduced into the uterine cavity, past the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"After IUI, for how long is the patient asked to lie supine?","options":["15 minutes","5 minutes","1 hour","24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Step 4 states the patient is asked to lay supine for 15 min.  Why B, C, D wrong: These durations do not match the \"15 min\" stated."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"IUI-H refers to intrauterine insemination performed with:","options":["Husband's semen","Donor semen","Frozen embryo","Cervical mucus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes distinguish IUI-H (with husband semen) from IUI-D (with donor semen); the source prints \"IUI-4\" but the intended letter is H for husband.  Why B wrong: Donor semen is IUI-D.  Why C wrong: A frozen embryo is not used in IUI.  Why D wrong: Cervical mucus is not the inseminate."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"IUI-D refers to intrauterine insemination using:","options":["Donor semen","Husband's semen","Both partners' semen mixed","Only the male's own frozen sperm from years earlier"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state IUI-D is intrauterine insemination with donor semen.  Why B wrong: Husband's semen is IUI-H.  Why C wrong: Mixed semen is not the described type.  Why D wrong: The distinction is donor vs husband, not timing of freezing."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"What is the minimum sperm concentration needed for IUI to be performed?","options":["10 million/mL","5 million/mL","15 million/mL","20 million/mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the minimum sperm concentration needed for IUI is 10 million/mL.  Why B wrong: 5 million/mL falls in the severe-oligospermia range where IUI is not done.  Why C wrong: 15 million/mL is above the minimum, not the minimum itself.  Why D wrong: 20 million/mL is not the stated minimum."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"According to the IUI indications table, IUI is indicated when the male partner's sperm concentration is:","options":["10–15 million/mL","<5 million/mL","Azoospermic (0)","Exactly 2 million/mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indication 1 lists male partner sperm concentration 10–15 million/mL.  Why B wrong: <5 million/mL (severe oligospermia) is a contraindication.  Why C wrong: Azoospermia is listed under \"not done.\"  Why D wrong: 2 million/mL is below the minimum needed for IUI."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which of the following is a listed indication for IUI? (Select all that apply)","options":["Male partner sperm concentration 10–15 million/mL","Females in whom the cervix has anti-sperm antibodies","Males with an ejaculatory problem","Unexplained infertility","Females whose tubes are blocked"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The indications table lists all four: concentration 10–15 million/mL, cervix with anti-sperm antibodies, ejaculatory problem, and unexplained infertility.  Why E wrong: Females with blocked tubes are listed under \"not done\" (contraindication), not indication."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"IUI is a suitable option for a female whose cervix has which of the following?","options":["Anti-sperm antibodies","Blocked fallopian tubes","Complete cervical stenosis with fused uterus","Active genital tuberculosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indication 2 lists females in whom the cervix has anti-sperm antibodies, since IUI bypasses the cervix.  Why B wrong: Blocked tubes are a contraindication (not done).  Why C wrong: This is not the listed indication.  Why D wrong: Genital TB is not the listed IUI indication."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"IUI is indicated for males who have which problem?","options":["Ejaculatory problem","Absent vas deferens","Azoospermia","Severe oligospermia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indication 3 lists males who have an ejaculatory problem.  Why B wrong: Absent vas deferens causes azoospermia — not an IUI indication here.  Why C wrong: Azoospermia is listed under \"not done.\"  Why D wrong: Severe oligospermia is under \"not done.\""},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"IUI is offered as a treatment for which fertility scenario?","options":["Unexplained infertility","Blocked fallopian tubes","Azoospermia","Sperm concentration <10 million/mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indication 4 lists unexplained infertility.  Why B wrong: Blocked tubes are a contraindication.  Why C wrong: Azoospermia is under \"not done.\"  Why D wrong: <10 million/mL is under \"not done.\""},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"IUI should NOT be done in which of the following situations? (Select all that apply)","options":["Sperm concentration <10 million/mL","Severe oligospermia","Azoospermia","Females whose tubes are blocked","Unexplained infertility"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Not done\" column lists <10 million/mL sperm concentration, severe oligospermia, azoospermia, and females whose tubes are blocked.  Why E wrong: Unexplained infertility is an indication for IUI, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"IUI is contraindicated (not done) if the female has which condition?","options":["Blocked fallopian tubes","Anti-sperm antibodies in the cervix","Anovulation treated with clomiphene","Regular ovulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Not done\" list includes females whose tubes are blocked, since sperm cannot reach the ovum for fertilization.  Why B wrong: Cervical anti-sperm antibodies are an indication, not a contraindication.  Why C wrong: Anovulation treated with clomiphene is not listed as a contraindication.  Why D wrong: Regular ovulation is not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"According to the notes, what is the MOST COMMON reason for female infertility among the listed causes?","options":["No ovulation / Anovulation","Tubal infection","Uterine factor","Cervical factor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes mark \"No ovulation/Anovulation\" as the most common reason (labelled ①), e.g., PCOS, increased prolactin levels.  Why B wrong: Tubal infection is a cause but not the most common reason listed.  Why C wrong: Uterine factor is a cause, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Cervical factor is a cause, not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which of the following are given as examples/associations of the anovulation cause of female infertility? (Select all that apply)","options":["PCOS","Increased prolactin levels","Fibroid","Salpingitis"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list PCOS and increased prolactin levels under no ovulation/anovulation.  Why C wrong: Fibroid is listed under uterine factor.  Why D wrong: Salpingitis is listed under tubal infection."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which conditions are listed under the \"tubal infection\" cause of female infertility? (Select all that apply)","options":["Adhesions","Salpingitis","PID (endometriosis)","Genital TB","Fibroid"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Under tubal infection the notes list adhesions, salpingitis, PID (endometriosis), and genital TB.  Why E wrong: Fibroid is a uterine factor, not a tubal infection."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which of the following is listed under the \"uterine factor\" cause of female infertility?","options":["Congenital malformation of uterus","Salpingitis","Anti-sperm antibodies","PCOS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Uterine factors listed are congenital malformation of uterus, fibroid, and endometritis.  Why B wrong: Salpingitis is a tubal infection.  Why C wrong: Anti-sperm antibodies are a cervical factor.  Why D wrong: PCOS is an ovulation (anovulation) cause."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which conditions fall under the \"uterine factor\" causes of female infertility? (Select all that apply)","options":["Congenital malformation of uterus","Fibroid","Endometritis","Cervicitis","Genital TB"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list congenital malformation of uterus, fibroid, and endometritis as uterine factors.  Why D wrong: Cervicitis is a cervical factor.  Why E wrong: Genital TB is listed under tubal infection."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"The cervical factor causing female infertility is characterized by:","options":["Antibodies present against sperm (cervicitis)","Fibroid in the uterine wall","Blocked fallopian tubes","Failure of ovulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the cervical factor as antibodies present against sperm — cervicitis.  Why B wrong: Fibroid is a uterine factor.  Why C wrong: Blocked tubes fall under tubal infection.  Why D wrong: Ovulation failure is the ovarian/anovulation cause."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Anovulation is described in the notes as the most common cause of:","options":["Female infertility","Male infertility","Postpartum haemorrhage","Preterm labour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under ovarian cause, anovulation is labelled the most common cause of female infertility.  Why B wrong: The notes attribute this to female infertility, not male.  Why C wrong: PPH is unrelated to this point.  Why D wrong: Preterm labour is unrelated to this point."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"What is the most common cause of anovulation according to the notes?","options":["PCOS / PCOD","Genital TB","Fibroid","Cervicitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states the most common cause of anovulation is PCOS/PCOD.  Why B wrong: Genital TB is a tubal cause of infertility.  Why C wrong: Fibroid is a uterine factor.  Why D wrong: Cervicitis is a cervical factor."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"What is the BEST test for anovulation according to the notes?","options":["Hormonal assessment","Hysterosalpingography","Endometrial biopsy","Semen analysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best test for anovulation is hormonal assessment (LH kits, serum progesterone).  Why B wrong: HSG assesses tubal patency, not ovulation.  Why C wrong: Endometrial biopsy is not named as the best test here.  Why D wrong: Semen analysis assesses male fertility."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which hormonal tests are used to assess for anovulation? (Select all that apply)","options":["LH kits","Serum progesterone level on day 22 of cycle","Serum sodium","Serum bilirubin"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list LH kits and serum progesterone level on day 22 of the cycle as the hormonal assessment for anovulation.  Why C wrong: Serum sodium is not used to assess ovulation.  Why D wrong: Serum bilirubin is a liver test, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Serum progesterone for ovulation assessment is measured on which day of the cycle?","options":["Day 22","Day 10","Day 1","Day 14"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify serum progesterone level on day 22 of the cycle.  Why B wrong: Day 10 is when follicular monitoring begins.  Why C wrong: Day 1 is menstruation onset.  Why D wrong: Day 14 is not the day specified in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"A HIGH serum progesterone level on day 22 of the cycle indicates:","options":["Ovulation has occurred","Ovulation has not occurred","Pregnancy is impossible","Anovulation is confirmed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show High → Ovulation occurred.  Why B wrong: A low level indicates ovulation not occurred.  Why C wrong: The test does not indicate impossibility of pregnancy.  Why D wrong: High progesterone indicates ovulation happened, the opposite of anovulation."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"A LOW serum progesterone level on day 22 of the cycle indicates:","options":["Ovulation has not occurred","Ovulation has occurred","Multiple ovulation","Successful implantation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show Low → Ovulation not occurred.  Why B wrong: A high level indicates ovulation occurred.  Why C wrong: Low progesterone does not indicate multiple ovulation.  Why D wrong: Low progesterone does not indicate implantation."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"The \"most commonly done test\" for monitoring follicle growth is a special transvaginal ultrasound called:","options":["Follicular monitoring","Hysterosalpingography","Doppler flow study","Sonohysterography"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes name the special transvaginal ultrasound as follicular monitoring.  Why B wrong: HSG assesses tubal patency.  Why C wrong: Doppler assesses blood flow, not the named test.  Why D wrong: Sonohysterography assesses the uterine cavity, not follicles here."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Follicular monitoring is started from which day of the cycle?","options":["Day 10","Day 1","Day 22","Day 28"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state to start follicular monitoring from day 10 of the cycle.  Why B wrong: Day 1 is the start of menstruation.  Why C wrong: Day 22 is when serum progesterone is measured.  Why D wrong: Day 28 is typically the cycle end."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"How frequently is follicular monitoring performed?","options":["Every alternate day","Every hour","Once a month","Only on day 28"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state to do follicular monitoring every alternate day.  Why B, C, D wrong: These frequencies do not match \"every alternate day.\""},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"During follicular monitoring, follicles are expected to grow in size by approximately:","options":["1–2 mm/day","5–6 mm/day","10 mm/day","0.5 mm/week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state follicles grow in size by 1–2 mm/day.  Why B, C, D wrong: These growth rates do not match \"1–2 mm/day.\""},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"On follicular monitoring, ovulation is anticipated when the follicles reach what size?","options":["18–20 mm","5–8 mm","25–30 mm","40 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state follicles reach 18–20 mm size (the mature/pre-ovulatory size).  Why B, C, D wrong: These sizes do not match the \"18–20 mm\" stated."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"On ultrasound, which of the following are signs that ovulation has occurred? (Select all that apply)","options":["Size of follicles decreases (suddenly)","Fluid in the pouch of Douglas","Endometrium shows a 3-layered (trilaminar) appearance","Follicles suddenly grow to 40 mm","Complete absence of endometrium"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list, as signs of ovulation on ultrasound: sudden decrease in follicle size, fluid in the pouch of Douglas, and a 3-layered (trilaminar) endometrium.  Why D wrong: A sudden decrease in size (follicle rupture), not enlargement to 40 mm, signals ovulation.  Why E wrong: A trilaminar endometrium, not absence of endometrium, is the sign."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"The \"trilaminar appearance\" seen at ovulation refers to which structure being 3-layered?","options":["Endometrium","Myometrium","Ovary","Cervix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Endometrium = 3 layered (TRILAMINAR appearance).\"  Why B wrong: It is the endometrium, not the myometrium.  Why C wrong: The ovary is not described as trilaminar.  Why D wrong: The cervix is not the trilaminar structure."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Anovulation is described as being \"easily treated\" by which category of drugs?","options":["Ovulation-inducing drugs","Antibiotics","Anticoagulants","Antihypertensives"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The management section states anovulation is easily treated by ovulation-inducing drugs.  Why B, C, D wrong: These drug classes do not induce ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which of the following are listed ovulation-inducing drugs for anovulation? (Select all that apply)","options":["Clomiphene citrate","Letrozole","hMG (Human menopausal gonadotropin) — synthetic LH and FSH","Pulsatile GnRH","Warfarin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list clomiphene citrate, letrozole, synthetic LH and FSH (hMG), and pulsatile GnRH as ovulation-inducing drugs.  Why E wrong: Warfarin is an anticoagulant, not an ovulation-inducing drug."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"hMG used for ovulation induction is described as synthetic:","options":["LH and FSH","Estrogen and progesterone","Prolactin and oxytocin","HCG and TSH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe hMG (human menopausal gonadotropin) as synthetic LH and FSH.  Why B, C, D wrong: hMG is not composed of these hormone pairs."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"What does the abbreviation hMG stand for?","options":["Human menopausal gonadotropin","Human maternal globulin","Highly modified gonadotropin","Human male gonadotropin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes expand hMG as human menopausal gonadotropin.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Which ovulation-inducing agent is given in a \"pulsatile\" fashion?","options":["GnRH","Clomiphene citrate","Letrozole","HCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Pulsatile GnRH\" as an ovulation-inducing drug.  Why B wrong: Clomiphene is given orally on set cycle days, not pulsatile.  Why C wrong: Letrozole is an oral agent, not pulsatile.  Why D wrong: HCG is given as a trigger injection, not pulsatile."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"According to the notes, which is the MOST COMMONLY USED drug for anovulation / PCOS treatment?","options":["Clomiphene citrate","Letrozole","hMG","Pulsatile GnRH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most commonly used drug for anovulation/PCOS treatment is clomiphene citrate.  Why B wrong: Letrozole is stated to be the best drug, not the most commonly used.  Why C wrong: hMG is a listed drug but not the most commonly used.  Why D wrong: Pulsatile GnRH is not the most commonly used."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"According to the notes, which is the BEST drug for anovulation / PCOS treatment?","options":["Letrozole","Clomiphene citrate","hMG","HCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best drug for anovulation/PCOS treatment is letrozole.  Why B wrong: Clomiphene is the most commonly used, but letrozole is named the best.  Why C wrong: hMG is not identified as the best.  Why D wrong: HCG is a trigger injection, not the best PCOS treatment."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"What is the starting dose of clomiphene according to the notes?","options":["50 mcg/day","100 mcg/day","25 mcg/day","150 mcg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the starting dose of clomiphene is 50 mcg/day (unit as printed in source).  Why B wrong: 100 mcg/day is the maximum dose.  Why C, D wrong: These doses are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"What is the maximum dose of clomiphene according to the notes?","options":["100 mcg/day","50 mcg/day","200 mcg/day","250 mcg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the maximum dose of clomiphene is 100 mcg/day (unit as printed in source).  Why B wrong: 50 mcg/day is the starting dose.  Why C, D wrong: These doses are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Clomiphene is given during which days of the cycle according to the notes?","options":["Day 5 to day 9 (or Day 2 to Day 6)","Day 10 to day 14","Day 20 to day 24","Day 1 to day 3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state clomiphene is given from Day 5–day 9, or Day 2–Day 6.  Why B, C, D wrong: These day ranges do not match the stated regimen."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"After starting clomiphene, follicular monitoring is done on which day of the cycle?","options":["Day 10","Day 2","Day 22","Day 28"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Day 10 = Do follicular monitoring\" after clomiphene.  Why B wrong: Day 2 may be a clomiphene start day, not monitoring day.  Why C wrong: Day 22 is for serum progesterone.  Why D wrong: Day 28 is not the monitoring day stated."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"Due to clomiphene, follicular monitoring typically shows:","options":["Multiple follicles growing","A single follicle only","No follicle growth","Complete follicular atresia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that due to clomiphene, multiple follicles grow.  Why B wrong: Multiple, not single, follicles develop.  Why C wrong: Growth is stimulated, not absent.  Why D wrong: Follicles grow rather than undergoing atresia."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"During clomiphene-induced ovulation, when follicles reach 18–20 mm in size, the next step is to:","options":["Give an injection of HCG to the patient","Stop all treatment","Perform a hysterectomy","Repeat clomiphene at day 20"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when follicles reach 18–20 mm, give inj. HCG to the patient.  Why B wrong: Treatment is not stopped at this point; HCG is the trigger.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not part of ovulation induction.  Why D wrong: Clomiphene is not repeated at day 20."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"HCG is used as an ovulation trigger because it is structurally and functionally similar to which hormone?","options":["LH (acts like the LH surge)","FSH","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HCG is structurally + functionally same to LH and acts like the LH surge.  Why B wrong: HCG mimics LH, not FSH.  Why C, D wrong: HCG mimics LH, not estrogen or progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"With clomiphene followed by HCG trigger, ovulation is achieved in approximately what percentage of cases?","options":["90%","50%","30%","100%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state ovulation occurs in 90% of cases.  Why B, C, D wrong: These percentages do not match the \"90%\" stated."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"What is the notable side effect of clomiphene therapy mentioned in the notes?","options":["Multifetal pregnancy / twins (5–8%)","Deep vein thrombosis","Hypothyroidism","Severe hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the side effect of clomiphene is multifetal pregnancy/twins in 5–8% of cases (due to multiple follicle development).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the side effect stated in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"The risk of twins/multifetal pregnancy with clomiphene is stated to be:","options":["5–8%","10–15%","20–25%","1%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the multifetal pregnancy/twin rate as 5–8%.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ranges do not match the \"5–8%\" stated."},{"section":"Batch 081 · Male infertility…","question":"A woman with anovulatory infertility due to PCOS is most likely to conceive multiples as a side effect because clomiphene:","options":["Causes multiple follicles to grow, increasing the chance of multifetal pregnancy","Directly implants two embryos","Prevents the corpus luteum from forming","Suppresses all follicular growth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes link clomiphene's effect of multiple follicle growth to the twin/multifetal pregnancy side effect (5–8%).  Why B wrong: Clomiphene does not implant embryos.  Why C wrong: It promotes ovulation and corpus luteum formation.  Why D wrong: It stimulates rather than suppresses follicular growth."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In a clomiphene-induced cycle, which hormone injection acts as the ovulation trigger?","options":["FSH","hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin)","Progesterone","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in a clomiphene-induced cycle, hCG acts like the ovulation trigger (it mimics the LH surge, causing final follicular maturation and rupture).  Why A wrong: FSH stimulates follicular growth but is not the trigger used here.  Why C wrong: Progesterone supports the luteal phase, it does not trigger ovulation.  Why D wrong: Prolactin, when elevated, actually suppresses ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"What percentage of females ovulate when given clomiphene?","options":["40%","50%","90%","100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 90% of females ovulate with clomiphene.  Why A wrong: 40% is the conception (pregnancy) figure range lower bound, not the ovulation rate.  Why B wrong: 50% is the upper conception figure, not ovulation rate.  Why D wrong: 100% is not stated; not all patients ovulate."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"What percentage of females conceive after being given clomiphene?","options":["10–20%","40–50%","60–70%","90%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 40–50% of females conceive after clomiphene.  Why A wrong: Too low; not stated.  Why C wrong: Not stated.  Why D wrong: 90% is the ovulation rate, not the conception rate — a classic distractor."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"How many hours after an injection of hCG does the patient ovulate?","options":["12–16 hrs","24–28 hrs","32–36 hrs","48–72 hrs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show Inj hCG → (32–36 hrs) → Ovulate.  Why A wrong: Too early; not stated.  Why B wrong: Not the stated interval.  Why D wrong: Too long; not stated."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Tubal infertility refers to which of the following?","options":["Absence of ovulation","Blockage of the fallopian tubes for some reason","Antibodies against sperm in the cervix","Low sperm count"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define tubal infertility as infertility due to some reason the tubes are blocked (blockage).  Why A wrong: That is anovulatory infertility.  Why C wrong: That is cervical/immunological infertility.  Why D wrong: That is a male factor (oligospermia)."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In tubal infertility, treatment is needed only under which condition?","options":["When one tube is blocked","When both tubes are blocked","When any tube is patent","Only if the uterus is absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state treatment is needed only if both tubes are blocked (one patent tube can still allow conception).  Why A wrong: A single blocked tube usually still permits natural conception through the other.  Why C wrong: A patent tube does not require treatment.  Why D wrong: Absent uterus is a separate IVF/surrogacy consideration, not tubal infertility treatment criteria."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"What is the investigation of choice (IOC) for tubal infertility?","options":["Ultrasound","Hysterosalpingography (HSG)","Laparoscopy","Endometrial biopsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Hysterosalpingography (HSG) as the IOC for tubal infertility.  Why A wrong: Ultrasound does not reliably assess tubal patency.  Why C wrong: Laparoscopy (with dye/chromopertubation) is used but is not the stated IOC here.  Why D wrong: Endometrial biopsy assesses the endometrium, not tubal patency."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In hysterosalpingography (HSG), what type of dye is instilled into the uterus?","options":["Methylene blue","Radiopaque dye","Fluorescein dye","Barium sulphate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a radiopaque dye is put into the uterus so it shows on serial X-rays.  Why A wrong: Methylene blue is used in laparoscopic chromopertubation, not X-ray HSG.  Why C wrong: Fluorescein is not used for HSG.  Why D wrong: Barium is a GI contrast, not used for HSG."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In HSG, the radiopaque dye is instilled into the uterus with the help of which cannula?","options":["Karman cannula","Leech Wilkinson cannula","Rubin cannula","Fallope ring cannula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify the dye is put in with a cannula called the Leech Wilkinson cannula.  Why A wrong: Karman cannula is used for suction/menstrual regulation and MTP.  Why C wrong: Rubin cannula is used for tubal insufflation (Rubin's test), not the HSG cannula named here.  Why D wrong: There is no \"Fallope ring cannula\"; Falope rings are for tubal ligation."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In HSG, after the dye is instilled, what imaging is performed to assess tubal patency?","options":["Single MRI","Serial X-rays","Doppler ultrasound","CT scan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state serial X-rays are done to see whether the dye comes out of the tube.  Why A wrong: MRI is not used in this HSG technique.  Why C wrong: Doppler assesses blood flow, not dye spill.  Why D wrong: CT is not the described imaging method."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a reason for tube blockage? (First cause)","options":["Infection – salpingitis","Ovarian cyst","Fibroid","Cervical polyp"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Infection – salpingitis as the first reason for tube blockage.  Why B wrong: Ovarian cyst is not listed as a cause of tubal blockage here.  Why C wrong: Fibroids are not listed.  Why D wrong: Cervical polyp is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Which pelvic infection is listed as a cause of tubal blockage?","options":["Cystitis","Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)","Vaginitis","Bartholinitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PID is listed as reason #2 for tube blockage.  Why A wrong: Cystitis is a bladder infection, not listed.  Why C wrong: Vaginitis is not listed.  Why D wrong: Bartholinitis is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Which infectious disease is listed as reason #3 for tubal blockage?","options":["Genital tuberculosis (TB)","Syphilis","Gonorrhoea only","Hepatitis B"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Genital TB as reason #3 for tube blockage.  Why B wrong: Syphilis is not listed here.  Why C wrong: Gonorrhoea is not the specifically listed cause.  Why D wrong: Hepatitis B is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Which condition, where endometrial tissue grows outside the uterus, is listed as reason #4 for tubal blockage?","options":["Adenomyosis","Endometriosis","Endometritis","Endometrial hyperplasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Endometriosis as reason #4 for tube blockage.  Why A wrong: Adenomyosis is endometrial tissue within the myometrium, not listed here.  Why C wrong: Endometritis is inflammation of the endometrium, not listed.  Why D wrong: Endometrial hyperplasia is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"On a normal HSG (labelled \"Normal HCG\" in the notes), which structures are visualized as the dye passes through?","options":["Uterus, both tubes, and spill of dye at fimbrial ends","Ovaries and cervix only","Bladder and rectum","Vagina and cervix only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The labelled normal HSG image shows the uterus, right tube, left tube, and spill of dye — indicating patent tubes.  Why B wrong: Ovaries are not directly outlined by intrauterine dye.  Why C wrong: Bladder/rectum are not the structures assessed.  Why D wrong: The vagina/cervix are not the focus; tubal spill is."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"On HSG, when dye spills out through the fimbrial end of the tube, what does it indicate?","options":["Tubes are blocked","Tubes are not blocked (patent)","Cornual block","Uterine perforation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that when spilling is present at the fimbrial end, the tubes are not blocked (patent).  Why A wrong: Blockage is indicated by absence of spill.  Why C wrong: Cornual block is a specific proximal obstruction with no spill.  Why D wrong: Spill is a normal finding, not perforation."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"On HSG, if the dye is NOT spilling from the tube, what does it indicate?","options":["Tubes are patent","Tubes are blocked","Normal study","Ovulation has occurred"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state if dye is not spilling, the tubes are blocked.  Why A wrong: Patency is shown by spill, not absence of it.  Why C wrong: No spill is an abnormal finding.  Why D wrong: HSG does not assess ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In the HSG images, an obstruction at the proximal (cornual) region of BOTH tubes preventing dye entry is termed:","options":["Bilateral cornual block","Unilateral hydrosalpinx","Cervical stenosis","Fimbrial phimosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label this finding as bilateral cornual block.  Why B wrong: Hydrosalpinx is fluid-filled distal tube dilation, not the labelled finding.  Why C wrong: Cervical stenosis is not shown.  Why D wrong: Fimbrial phimosis is a distal narrowing, not the labelled cornual block."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"An HSG image in the notes shows dye filling the uterus and one tube but not the other, labelled as:","options":["Left side tube is blocked","Right side tube is blocked","Both tubes patent","Uterine septum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly label the image \"Right side tube is blocked.\"  Why A wrong: It is the right tube, not the left, per the label.  Why C wrong: One tube is blocked, so not both patent.  Why D wrong: The finding is tubal, not a uterine septum."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"The cannula used in HSG is also known by which alternate name?","options":["Hysterosalpingography cannula","Insufflation cannula","Suction cannula","Epidural cannula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label it \"Leech Wilkinson's cannula/hysterosalpingography cannula.\"  Why B wrong: Insufflation cannula (Rubin's) is a different instrument for tubal insufflation.  Why C wrong: Suction cannula is for MTP/aspiration.  Why D wrong: Epidural cannula is for anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"On which day of the menstrual cycle is HSG ideally performed?","options":["Day 2–3","Day 7–10 (around day 10, pre-ovulatory phase)","Day 14","Day 21–24"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HSG is done on day 10 of cycle (day 7–day 10), in the pre-ovulatory phase.  Why A wrong: Day 2–3 is during menstruation, not ideal for HSG.  Why C wrong: Day 14 is around ovulation; HSG is never done after ovulation.  Why D wrong: Day 21–24 is the luteal (post-ovulatory) phase."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"HSG should NEVER be performed during which phase of the cycle?","options":["Menstrual phase","Pre-ovulatory phase","After ovulation (post-ovulatory / luteal phase)","Proliferative phase"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes emphasize HSG is never done after ovulation (to avoid disturbing a possible early pregnancy).  Why A wrong: HSG is done in the pre-ovulatory window, typically after menses stop.  Why B wrong: Pre-ovulatory is exactly when HSG is done.  Why D wrong: The proliferative (pre-ovulatory) phase is the appropriate time."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Regarding anaesthesia for HSG, which statement is correct?","options":["General anaesthesia is required","Spinal anaesthesia is required","No anaesthesia is needed","Deep sedation is mandatory"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state no anaesthesia is needed for HSG.  Why A wrong: General anaesthesia is not required.  Why B wrong: Spinal anaesthesia is not required.  Why D wrong: Deep sedation is not mandated."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"HSG is a painful procedure. What is given to the patient to manage the pain?","options":["General anaesthetics","Analgesics","Muscle relaxants","Antibiotics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HSG is painful → analgesics are given.  Why A wrong: The notes specifically say no anaesthesia is needed.  Why C wrong: Muscle relaxants are not indicated.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics are not the pain-management measure described."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication to HSG? (First listed)","options":["Pregnancy","Regular menses","Nulliparity","Age below 30"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pregnancy is listed as the first contraindication of HSG (radiation and dye hazard to the fetus).  Why B wrong: Regular menses is not a contraindication.  Why C wrong: Nulliparity is not a contraindication.  Why D wrong: Young age is not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Which infection is a contraindication to performing HSG?","options":["Active genital tuberculosis (TB)","Common cold","Oral thrush","Athlete's foot"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Active genital TB is listed as a contraindication (HSG could spread the infection).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are unrelated infections not listed as contraindications to HSG."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Which pelvic condition is a contraindication to HSG?","options":["Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)","Healed appendectomy","Ovarian reserve testing","Prior normal delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PID is listed as a contraindication to HSG (risk of flaring/spreading infection).  Why B, C, D wrong: None of these are listed contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Which patient-specific factor is a contraindication to HSG?","options":["Allergy to the (radiopaque) dye","Allergy to penicillin","Lactose intolerance","Gluten intolerance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Allergy to dye is listed as a contraindication to HSG (risk of anaphylaxis to contrast).  Why B, C, D wrong: These allergies/intolerances are not the contrast-related contraindication listed."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Select ALL contraindications of HSG listed in the notes.","options":["Pregnancy","Active genital TB","PID","Regular ovulatory cycles","Allergy to dye"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list exactly four contraindications — Pregnancy, Active genital TB, PID, and Allergy to dye.  Why D wrong: Regular ovulatory cycles is not a contraindication; it is a normal finding."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"For infertility caused by blocked tubes, what treatment is available to relieve the block itself?","options":["Tuboplasty always cures it","There is no treatment to relieve the block","Repeated HSG opens the tubes","Clomiphene opens the tubes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state there is no treatment to relieve the block; the only option is IVF.  Why A wrong: The notes do not offer tuboplasty as a reliable cure here.  Why C wrong: HSG is diagnostic, not a treatment to open tubes here.  Why D wrong: Clomiphene induces ovulation, it does not open tubes."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"For a woman with both fallopian tubes blocked, what is the only treatment option for achieving pregnancy?","options":["IUI (intrauterine insemination)","IVF (in vitro fertilization)","Clomiphene alone","HSG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the only option for blocked tubes is IVF, because fertilization occurs outside the body and bypasses the tubes.  Why A wrong: The notes state IUI is NOT useful in this case (sperm still cannot reach the egg through blocked tubes).  Why C wrong: Clomiphene addresses ovulation, not tubal blockage.  Why D wrong: HSG is a diagnostic test, not treatment."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Why is IUI (intrauterine insemination) NOT useful in infertility due to blocked tubes?","options":["Because IUI still requires patent tubes for the sperm and egg to meet","Because IUI needs a donor egg","Because IUI causes tubal block","Because IUI is only for men"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state IUI is not useful when tubes are blocked; IUI deposits sperm in the uterus but fertilization still needs a patent tube.  Why B wrong: IUI uses the woman's own egg, released naturally.  Why C wrong: IUI does not cause blockage.  Why D wrong: IUI is a female-directed procedure, not \"only for men.\""},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In cervical infertility, what is present in the cervix that causes infertility?","options":["Antibodies against sperms","Fibroids","Excess mucus only","Cervical polyps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in some females the cervix contains antibodies against sperms.  Why B wrong: Fibroids are not the described cervical cause.  Why C wrong: Mucus quality can matter but the notes specify antibodies.  Why D wrong: Polyps are not the described cause."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Cervical infertility, where antibodies against sperm are present, is also called:","options":["Mechanical cause of infertility","Immunological cause of infertility","Endocrine cause of infertility","Idiopathic infertility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes call antibodies against sperm the immunological cause of infertility.  Why A wrong: It is immunological, not mechanical.  Why C wrong: It is not hormonal/endocrine.  Why D wrong: The cause is identified (antibodies), so it is not idiopathic/unexplained."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"What is the management of cervical (immunological) infertility?","options":["Remove the cervix","Bypass the cervix by intrauterine insemination (IUI)","Perform IVF only","Give antibiotics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state management is to bypass the area of the cervix by intrauterine insemination, so sperm are placed directly in the uterus, avoiding cervical antibodies.  Why A wrong: Removal of the cervix is not the described management.  Why C wrong: IVF is not required; IUI bypasses the cervical barrier.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics do not address antisperm antibodies."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In the unexplained infertility treatment protocol, clomiphene + inj hCG is followed after 32–36 hrs by which procedure?","options":["IVF","Intrauterine insemination (IUI)","ICSI","HSG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show Clomiphene + inj hCG → after 32–36 hrs → Intrauterine insemination (IUI).  Why A wrong: IVF is reserved for other indications (e.g., blocked tubes).  Why C wrong: ICSI is for severe male factor/repeated IVF failure.  Why D wrong: HSG is diagnostic, not part of this treatment protocol."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In the unexplained infertility protocol, IUI is timed for when the follicle reaches what size?","options":["10–12 mm","14–16 mm","18–20 mm","22–24 mm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state IUI is done when follicles become 18–20 mm (mature follicle size).  Why A, B wrong: These are immature follicle sizes.  Why D wrong: Larger than the stated 18–20 mm threshold."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In the IVF protocol for the male partner, what are the two sequential steps?","options":["Semen specimen collection → sperm processing","Blood test → sperm freezing","Testicular biopsy → sperm processing","Semen specimen → oocyte pickup"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show Male partner → Semen specimen → Sperms process.  Why B wrong: Blood test and freezing are not the described steps.  Why C wrong: Testicular biopsy is not the routine described step.  Why D wrong: Oocyte pickup is part of the female partner pathway."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In the IVF protocol for the female partner, on which days is clomiphene given?","options":["Day 1 – Day 4","Day 5 – Day 9","Day 10 – Day 14","Day 15 – Day 20"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state clomiphene is given days 5 – Day 9 to the female partner.  Why A wrong: Not the stated window.  Why C wrong: Day 10 is when follicular monitoring begins, not clomiphene dosing.  Why D wrong: Not the stated window."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In the IVF female-partner protocol, from which day is follicular monitoring started?","options":["Day 5","Day 7","Day 10","Day 14"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state follicular monitoring is done from day 10.  Why A wrong: Day 5 is when clomiphene starts.  Why B wrong: Not stated.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In the IVF protocol, when the follicle reaches what size is hCG injected to trigger ovulation?","options":["12–14 mm","15–17 mm","18–20 mm","21–23 mm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state hCG is injected to trigger ovulation when the follicle reaches 18–20 mm in size.  Why A, B wrong: These follicles are immature.  Why D wrong: Larger than the stated 18–20 mm mature threshold."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In the IVF protocol, after the hCG trigger, ovulation occurs after how many hours?","options":["12–16 hrs","24–30 hrs","32–36 hrs","48 hrs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state after 32–36 hrs ovulation occurs following the hCG trigger.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the stated 32–36 hr interval."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In IVF, the secondary oocyte pickup (retrieval) is performed under the guidance of:","options":["Fluoroscopy","Ultrasonography (USG)","Laparotomy","MRI"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state secondary oocyte pickup is done under USG (ultrasound) guidance.  Why A wrong: Fluoroscopy is not used for oocyte retrieval.  Why C wrong: Modern oocyte pickup is transvaginal under USG, not open laparotomy.  Why D wrong: MRI is not used for oocyte pickup."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In IVF, how many sperms are placed per oocyte in the petri dish?","options":["500 to 1,000","5,000 to 10,000","50,000 to 1.5 lakh (1,50,000)","5 lakh to 10 lakh"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state per oocyte, 50,000 to 1.5 lakh (1,50,000) sperms are put.  Why A, B wrong: Too few compared to the stated number.  Why D wrong: More than the stated upper limit of 1.5 lakh."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In IVF, after retrieval, where does the fertilization (further action on the secondary oocyte) take place?","options":["In the fallopian tube","In the uterus","In a petri dish (outside the female's body)","In the cervix"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state further action occurs in a petri dish, outside the female's body — this is the \"in vitro\" step.  Why A wrong: IVF bypasses the tube entirely.  Why B wrong: Fertilization is outside the uterus; only the embryo is later transferred to the uterus.  Why D wrong: The cervix is not involved in IVF fertilization."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"After fertilization of the secondary oocyte in IVF, the immediate product formed is a:","options":["Morula","Zygote","Blastocyst","Gastrula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show that after fertilization in the petri dish, a zygote is formed.  Why A wrong: The morula forms later after cleavage.  Why C wrong: The blastocyst forms by day 5, not immediately.  Why D wrong: Gastrulation occurs much later in development."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In IVF embryo culture, Day 3 corresponds to which developmental stage?","options":["2-cell stage","8-cell stage","Blastocyst stage","Zygote stage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Day 3 = 8-cell stage.  Why A wrong: The 2-cell stage is earlier (~day 1–2).  Why C wrong: The blastocyst is Day 5.  Why D wrong: The zygote is the day-0 fertilized product."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In IVF embryo culture, Day 5 corresponds to which developmental stage?","options":["8-cell stage","Zygote stage","Blastocyst stage","Morula stage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Day 5 = Blastocyst stage.  Why A wrong: The 8-cell stage is Day 3.  Why B wrong: Zygote is day 0.  Why D wrong: The morula precedes the blastocyst; day 5 is the blastocyst per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In IVF, the cultured embryo is transferred back into the uterus at what position?","options":["At the level of the internal os","2 cm below the fundus (site of implantation)","Just above the cervix","Into the fallopian tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the embryo is transferred back into the uterus 2 cm below the fundus, described as the site of implantation.  Why A wrong: The internal os is too low and not the implantation site.  Why C wrong: Placement just above the cervix is not the stated site.  Why D wrong: Embryo transfer is into the uterine cavity, not the tube."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"What is the (single-cycle) success rate of IVF according to the notes?","options":["5–10%","20–30%","40–50%","60–70%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the success rate of IVF = 20–30%.  Why A wrong: Too low; not stated.  Why C wrong: 40–50% is the cumulative success rate, not the per-cycle rate.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"What is the cumulative success rate of IVF according to the notes?","options":["20–30%","40–50%","70–80%","90%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cumulative success rate is 40–50%.  Why A wrong: 20–30% is the single-cycle success rate.  Why C, D wrong: Higher than stated; not supported."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"IVF is indicated (IVF done) in which of the following situations?","options":["Both fallopian tubes are blocked","Complete absence of sperm (azoospermia)","Anovulation","Sperm concentration <5 million/mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"both tubes of female are blocked\" as an indication where IVF is done.  Why B wrong: Azoospermia is under \"IVF not done.\"  Why C wrong: Anovulation is listed under \"not done.\"  Why D wrong: Severe oligospermia (<5 million/mL) is under \"not done.\""},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"In IVF indications, absence of the uterus (e.g., Mullerian agenesis) is listed under which category?","options":["IVF done","IVF not done","IUI preferred","ICSI only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Female uterus absent e.g. Mullerian agenesis\" under IVF done (typically requiring a surrogate).  Why B wrong: It is listed under IVF done, not \"not done.\"  Why C wrong: IUI is not appropriate when the uterus is absent.  Why D wrong: ICSI is a fertilization technique, not the listed pathway here."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Congenital absence/underdevelopment of the uterus (and upper vagina) due to failed Müllerian duct development is termed:","options":["Turner syndrome","Mullerian agenesis","Endometriosis","Asherman syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes name Mullerian agenesis as the example of an absent uterus.  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome is a chromosomal (45,X) disorder, not specifically named here.  Why C wrong: Endometriosis is ectopic endometrial tissue.  Why D wrong: Asherman syndrome is intrauterine adhesions, not agenesis."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"For a male with a sperm concentration between 5–10 million/mL, IVF is:","options":["Done","Not done","Contraindicated","Replaced by IUI always"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"males with sperm concentration between 5–10 million/mL\" under IVF done.  Why B wrong: This range is in the \"IVF done\" column.  Why C wrong: It is an indication, not a contraindication.  Why D wrong: The notes place this under IVF, not universally IUI."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"IVF is indicated after IUI has failed how many times?","options":["1 time","2 times","3 times","5 times"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"If IUI has failed × 3 times\" as an indication for IVF.  Why A, B wrong: Fewer attempts than the stated 3.  Why D wrong: More than the stated 3 attempts."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"IVF is NOT done (not useful) when the male sperm concentration is:","options":["Between 5–10 million/mL","Less than 5 million/mL (severe oligospermia)","20 million/mL","40 million/mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state IVF is NOT done when sperm concentration is <5 million/mL (severe oligospermia) — such cases need ICSI instead.  Why A wrong: 5–10 million/mL is in the \"IVF done\" column.  Why C, D wrong: These are normal/near-normal counts, not the \"not done\" criterion."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"A sperm concentration below 5 million/mL is termed:","options":["Azoospermia","Severe oligospermia","Asthenospermia","Teratospermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label <5 million/mL as severe oligospermia.  Why A wrong: Azoospermia is complete absence of sperm.  Why C wrong: Asthenospermia is reduced sperm motility.  Why D wrong: Teratospermia is abnormal sperm morphology."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"The complete absence of sperms in the semen is called:","options":["Oligospermia","Azoospermia","Anovulation","Aspermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Absent sperms in semen (Azoospermia)\" — a situation where standard IVF is not done.  Why A wrong: Oligospermia is low count, not complete absence.  Why C wrong: Anovulation is a female ovulation problem.  Why D wrong: Aspermia is absence of semen (ejaculate) volume, distinct from azoospermia (absent sperm in semen)."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Which female-factor condition is listed under \"IVF not done\"?","options":["Blocked tubes","Absent uterus","Anovulation","Failed IUI × 3"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Anovulation under \"IVF not done\" (ovulation must be induced first; IVF cannot proceed without an oocyte).  Why A, B, D wrong: Blocked tubes, absent uterus, and failed IUI ×3 are all in the \"IVF done\" column."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Select ALL conditions in which IVF is done (indicated), per the notes.","options":["Both tubes blocked","Absent uterus (Mullerian agenesis)","Male sperm concentration 5–10 million/mL","Azoospermia","IUI failed × 3 times"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes' \"IVF done\" column lists blocked tubes, absent uterus (Mullerian agenesis), sperm 5–10 million/mL, and IUI failed ×3.  Why D wrong: Azoospermia is in the \"IVF not done\" column."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Select ALL conditions in which IVF is NOT done, per the notes.","options":["Sperm concentration <5 million/mL (severe oligospermia)","Azoospermia (absent sperms in semen)","Anovulation","Both tubes blocked","IUI failed × 3 times"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Not done\" column lists severe oligospermia (<5 million/mL), azoospermia, and anovulation.  Why D, E wrong: Both tubes blocked and IUI failed ×3 are in the \"IVF done\" column."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"What does the abbreviation ICSI stand for?","options":["Intra Cervical Sperm Insertion","Intra Cytoplasmic Sperm Injection","Intra Cavity Sperm Implantation","Intra Cornual Sperm Instillation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define ICSI as Intracytoplasmic Sperm Injection.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of the abbreviation."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"How does ICSI differ from conventional IVF?","options":["The sperm is injected directly into the cytoplasm of the oocyte instead of placing sperms in a petri dish","The embryo is transferred to the tube instead of the uterus","No hCG trigger is used","Fertilization occurs inside the body"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state ICSI's entire procedure is the same, but instead of putting sperms in the petri dish, a single sperm is taken and injected directly into the cytoplasm of the oocyte.  Why B wrong: Embryo transfer is still into the uterus.  Why C wrong: The hCG trigger step is unchanged.  Why D wrong: Fertilization still occurs in vitro."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"ICSI is indicated in which of the following cases? (First listed)","options":["Severe oligospermia","Mild oligospermia","Blocked tubes with normal sperm","Cervical infertility"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list severe oligospermia as the first indication for ICSI (very few sperm, so a single sperm is injected directly).  Why B wrong: Mild oligospermia is not the listed indication.  Why C wrong: Blocked tubes with normal sperm is a standard IVF indication.  Why D wrong: Cervical infertility is managed with IUI, not specifically ICSI here."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"According to the notes, how many sperms are needed per oocyte for ICSI?","options":["Only 1 sperm","50,000 sperms","1.5 lakh sperms","10,000 sperms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for ICSI, per oocyte only 1 sperm is needed (making it ideal for severe male-factor infertility).  Why B, C wrong: 50,000 to 1.5 lakh sperms is the requirement for conventional IVF, not ICSI.  Why D wrong: Not stated for ICSI; a single sperm suffices."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"ICSI is indicated after which of the following, per the notes?","options":["First IVF attempt","Repeated IVF failure","First IUI attempt","Successful IVF"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list repeated IVF failure as an indication for ICSI.  Why A wrong: ICSI is not routinely done on the very first IVF attempt per the notes; it follows repeated failure.  Why C wrong: A first IUI attempt is not an ICSI indication.  Why D wrong: A successful IVF needs no further intervention."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"Select ALL indications for ICSI listed in the notes.","options":["Severe oligospermia","Per oocyte only 1 sperm needed situations","Repeated IVF failure","Normal sperm count with patent tubes","Cervical mucus hostility"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list exactly three points under ICSI indications — severe oligospermia, per-oocyte only 1 sperm needed, and repeated IVF failure.  Why D wrong: Normal sperm with patent tubes does not require ICSI.  Why E wrong: Cervical mucus hostility is managed by IUI, not listed as an ICSI indication."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"A woman is found to have bilateral cornual block on HSG. Both tubes cannot be reopened. Which is the most appropriate treatment to help her conceive?","options":["IUI","IVF","Repeat HSG","Clomiphene alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With both tubes blocked and no way to relieve the block, the notes state IVF is the only option (fertilization occurs outside, bypassing the tubes).  Why A wrong: IUI is not useful when tubes are blocked.  Why C wrong: HSG is diagnostic, not therapeutic.  Why D wrong: Clomiphene induces ovulation but does not overcome tubal blockage."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"A couple has unexplained infertility. The woman is given clomiphene on days 5–9, then an hCG injection when the follicle is 18–20 mm. What is the next step and its timing?","options":["IUI immediately","IUI after 32–36 hrs (when ovulation occurs)","IVF after 12 hrs","ICSI after 48 hrs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes show that after the hCG trigger (follicle 18–20 mm), ovulation occurs at 32–36 hrs, and IUI is timed accordingly in unexplained infertility.  Why A wrong: IUI is not done immediately; it is timed to ovulation at 32–36 hrs.  Why C wrong: IVF is not the protocol for unexplained infertility here, and 12 hrs is the wrong interval.  Why D wrong: ICSI is for male-factor/repeated IVF failure, not first-line unexplained infertility."},{"section":"Batch 082 · Clomiphene-induced ovulation…","question":"A man's semen analysis shows azoospermia. According to the notes, which fertility procedure is NOT done for him?","options":["ICSI","IVF","Testicular sperm retrieval","Hormone therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly place \"absent sperms in semen (azoospermia)\" under the \"IVF not done\" column, because IVF in the petri dish requires available sperm.  Why A wrong: While ICSI is listed for severe oligospermia in these notes, standard IVF specifically is the procedure the notes mark \"not done\" for azoospermia.  Why C wrong: The notes do not address surgical sperm retrieval.  Why D wrong: Hormone therapy is not the procedure being classified here."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"In gynaecology, \"prolapse\" is best defined as:","options":["Displacement of an organ from its normal anatomical position","Complete removal of an organ","Inflammation of an organ","Abnormal growth of an organ"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define prolapse as displacement of an organ from its normal anatomical position.  Why B wrong: Removal of an organ is excision/extirpation, not prolapse.  Why C wrong: Inflammation is an -itis, not prolapse.  Why D wrong: Abnormal growth is a neoplasm/hyperplasia, not prolapse."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The uterus is kept in its normal anatomical position by how many kinds of supports?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The uterus remains in its normal position because of three kinds of supports — mechanical, ligamentous, and muscular.  Why A/C/D wrong: The notes specify exactly three kinds of support, not two, four, or five."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three recognised kinds of support that keep the uterus in position?","options":["Mechanical support","Ligaments that support uterus","Muscles supporting uterus","Vascular support"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The three supports listed are mechanical, ligaments, and muscles; vascular support is not listed.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are exactly the three kinds of support described."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"What is the angle of anteversion of the uterus in its normal anatomical position?","options":["60°","90°","120°","180°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the angle of anteversion is 90°.  Why A/D wrong: These values are not given for anteversion.  Why C wrong: 120° is the angle of anteflexion, not anteversion."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"What is the angle of anteflexion of the uterus in its normal anatomical position?","options":["90°","100°","120°","150°"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the angle of anteflexion is 120°.  Why A wrong: 90° is the angle of anteversion.  Why B/D wrong: These values are not given."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"In its normal anatomical position, the uterus is normally:","options":["Retroverted and retroflexed","Anteverted and anteflexed","Anteverted and retroflexed","Retroverted and anteflexed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Normally uterus is Anteverted & Anteflexed.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These combinations are abnormal; the normal position is anteverted AND anteflexed."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The angle of anteversion is the angle between the:","options":["Long axis of the uterus and the long axis of the vagina","Body and cervix of the uterus","Fundus and the pubic symphysis","Cervix and the sacrum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anteversion (90°) describes the forward tilt of the uterus relative to the vaginal axis, shown between uterus and vagina in the diagram.  Why B wrong: The angle between body and cervix is anteflexion, not anteversion.  Why C/D wrong: These describe fundal direction, not the anteversion angle."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The angle of anteflexion (120°) is measured between the:","options":["Uterus and the vagina","Body of the uterus and the cervix","Cervix and the pubic symphysis","Fundus and the bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anteflexion is the forward bend between the body and cervix of the uterus (120°).  Why A wrong: The uterus–vagina angle is anteversion.  Why C/D wrong: These are not how anteflexion is defined."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"According to the notes, the anteverted-anteflexed position of the uterus is important because it:","options":["Prevents prolapse","Facilitates menstruation","Increases fertility","Prevents infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that because the uterus is anteverted and anteflexed, \"this position prevents prolapse.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The notes attribute this position specifically to prevention of prolapse, not menstruation, fertility, or infection."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"What is described as the FIRST step in the development of uterine prolapse?","options":["Descent of the cervix","Retroversion of the uterus","Rupture of the round ligament","Weakening of the levator ani"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"The first step in Prolapse is → Retroversion of uterus.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the first step; retroversion of the uterus is."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"In an anteverted uterus, the fundus points towards the:","options":["Sacral promontory (posteriorly)","Pubic symphysis (anteriorly)","Rectum","Coccyx"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state anteverted uterus → fundus points towards pubic symphysis (anteriorly).  Why A wrong: That describes a retroverted uterus.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the directions given."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"In a retroverted uterus, the fundus points towards the:","options":["Pubic symphysis (anteriorly)","Bladder","Sacral promontory (posteriorly)","Anterior abdominal wall"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state retroverted uterus → fundus points towards sacral promontory (posteriorly).  Why A wrong: That describes an anteverted uterus.  Why B/D wrong: These are not the given directions."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The round ligament of the uterus arises from the ______ and is inserted into the ______.","options":["Cervix; sacrum","Angle of the uterus; labia majora","Fundus; pubic symphysis","Pelvic side wall; cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the round ligament \"arises from angle of uterus and gets inserted in Labia Majora.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These origins and insertions are not those of the round ligament."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"In the male, the round ligament of the uterus is homologous to the:","options":["Spermatic cord","Gubernaculum of testis","Vas deferens","Epididymis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state round ligament = \"Homologous to Gubernaculum of Testis in males.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated homologue."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"What is the primary role of the round ligament?","options":["To keep the uterus in the anteverted position","To attach the uterus to the sacrum","To form the primary support of the uterus","To prevent descent of the cervix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the round ligament \"Helps to keep the uterus in Anteverted position.\"  Why B wrong: Sacral attachment is the uterosacral ligament.  Why C wrong: The round ligament is a secondary (indirect), not primary, support.  Why D wrong: This is not its stated role."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The round ligament is classified as which type of support of the uterus?","options":["Primary support","Secondary (indirect) support","Muscular support","Non-supporting structure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the round ligament \"indirectly supports the uterus OR is it secondary support of uterus.\"  Why A wrong: Primary support is the three ligaments (pubocervical, cardinal, uterosacral).  Why C wrong: It is a ligament, not a muscle.  Why D wrong: It does support (indirectly), unlike the broad ligament."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which THREE ligaments together form the primary support of the uterus?","options":["Round, broad, and ovarian ligaments","Pubocervical, cardinal, and uterosacral ligaments","Broad, cardinal, and round ligaments","Suspensory, round, and uterosacral ligaments"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list pubocervical, cardinal, and uterosacral ligaments as the primary support of the uterus.  Why A/C/D wrong: The round, broad, ovarian, and suspensory ligaments are not part of the primary support triad."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The three ligaments forming the primary support of the uterus are collectively called the:","options":["Broad ligament","Triradiate ligament","Suspensory ligament","Cardinal complex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the pubocervical, cardinal, and uterosacral ligaments as the \"TRIRADIATE ligament.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the collective term used for the three primary ligaments."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which ligament is also known as the Mackenrodt ligament or transverse cervical ligament?","options":["Uterosacral ligament","Pubocervical ligament","Cardinal ligament","Round ligament"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the cardinal ligament the alternate names Mackenrodt ligament and transverse cervical ligament.  Why A/B/D wrong: These ligaments do not carry the Mackenrodt/transverse cervical synonyms."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which is the MOST important ligament supporting the uterus?","options":["Uterosacral ligament","Cardinal (Mackenrodt) ligament","Round ligament","Broad ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state cardinal ligament > uterosacral ligament, marking the cardinal ligament as the most important.  Why A wrong: The uterosacral ligament ranks second to the cardinal ligament.  Why C wrong: The round ligament is only a secondary support.  Why D wrong: The broad ligament does not support the uterus at all."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"According to the notes, the order of importance of the ligamentous supports is:","options":["Uterosacral > Cardinal","Round > Cardinal","Cardinal > Uterosacral","Pubocervical > Cardinal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly write \"Cardinal ligament > Uterosacral ligament.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the given order of importance."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The secondary support of the uterus is provided by the:","options":["Cardinal ligament","Round ligament","Uterosacral ligament","Levator ani"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list the round ligament as the secondary support.  Why A/C wrong: Cardinal and uterosacral ligaments are primary supports.  Why D wrong: The levator ani is a muscular support."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which ligament does NOT support the uterus at all?","options":["Cardinal ligament","Uterosacral ligament","Broad ligament","Round ligament"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the broad ligament is the \"ligament which does not support uterus at all.\"  Why A/B wrong: Cardinal and uterosacral ligaments are primary supports.  Why D wrong: The round ligament is a secondary (indirect) support."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The broad ligament is best described as a:","options":["Thick muscular band","Thin peritoneal fold extending from the sides of the uterus to the pelvic side wall","Fibromuscular cord attaching uterus to the sacrum","Fold connecting uterus to the labia majora"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the broad ligament as \"a thin peritoneal fold which extends from sides of uterus to the pelvic side wall.\"  Why A wrong: It is a peritoneal fold, not a muscular band.  Why C wrong: That describes the uterosacral ligament.  Why D wrong: That describes the round ligament."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which muscle is the MOST important muscle supporting the uterus?","options":["Obturator muscle","Levator ani","Ischiocavernosus","Bulbospongiosus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the levator ani is the most important muscle that supports the uterus.  Why A wrong: The obturator muscle is a wall muscle, not a uterine support.  Why C wrong: Ischiocavernosus does not support the uterus.  Why D wrong: Bulbospongiosus supports the uterus but is not the most important."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The levator ani muscle is composed of which TWO parts?","options":["Pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus","Pubococcygeus and ischiococcygeus","Iliococcygeus and ischiococcygeus","Pubococcygeus and obturator"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state levator ani = pubococcygeus + iliococcygeus.  Why B/C wrong: Ischiococcygeus is specifically noted NOT to be part of the levator ani.  Why D wrong: The obturator is not part of levator ani."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which muscle is specifically stated NOT to be a part of the levator ani muscle?","options":["Pubococcygeus","Iliococcygeus","Ischiococcygeus","Bulbospongiosus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Ischiococcygeus is not a part of Levator ani muscle.\"  Why A/B wrong: Pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus are the two parts of levator ani.  Why D wrong: Bulbospongiosus is a separate perineal muscle, not claimed to be part of levator ani."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which of the following muscles supports the uterus? (Select all that apply)","options":["Levator ani","Deep transverse perineal","Ischiocavernosus","Bulbospongiosus","External anal sphincter"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list levator ani, deep transverse perineal, superficial transverse perineal, bulbospongiosus, and external urinary/anal sphincters as muscles supporting the uterus.  Why C wrong: Ischiocavernosus is listed among muscles that do NOT support the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which of the following is NOT listed among the muscles that support the uterus?","options":["Superficial transverse perineal","Bulbospongiosus","External urinary sphincter","Ischiocavernosus"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Ischiocavernosus is listed among muscles that do NOT support the uterus.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are all listed as supporting muscles of the uterus."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The muscles supporting the uterus that are \"present in the midline and attached to the perineal body\" include which of the following?","options":["Levator ani, deep transverse perineal, superficial transverse perineal, bulbospongiosus","Obturator, ischiocavernosus, ischiococcygeus","Cardinal, uterosacral, pubocervical","Round, broad, ovarian"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes group levator ani, deep transverse perineal, superficial transverse perineal, and bulbospongiosus (with the sphincters) as present in midline and attached to the perineal body.  Why B wrong: These are non-supporting/wall muscles.  Why C/D wrong: These are ligaments, not muscles."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which TWO muscles are listed as muscles that do NOT support the uterus?","options":["Ischiocavernosus and ischiococcygeus","Pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus","Levator ani and bulbospongiosus","Deep and superficial transverse perineal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list ischiocavernosus and ischiococcygeus as muscles that do not support the uterus.  Why B wrong: These form the levator ani (a supporting muscle).  Why C/D wrong: These are supporting muscles."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"What is the overall MOST important support of the uterus?","options":["Cardinal ligament","Round ligament","Levator ani muscle","Broad ligament"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes conclude \"Overall most important support of uterus — Levator ani muscle.\"  Why A wrong: The cardinal ligament is the most important ligament, but the levator ani is the overall most important support.  Why B wrong: The round ligament is only a secondary support.  Why D wrong: The broad ligament does not support the uterus at all."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The urogenital diaphragm is made up of which muscle?","options":["Deep transverse perineal muscle","Superficial transverse perineal muscle","Bulbospongiosus","Ischiocavernosus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the urogenital diaphragm is \"made = deep transverse perinei muscle.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These muscles are not identified as forming the urogenital diaphragm."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"In the pelvic floor diagram, which muscle is labelled as clearly not supporting the uterus?","options":["Levator ani","Bulbocavernosus","Ischiocavernosus","Deep transverse perineal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The handwritten note beside ischiocavernosus states \"Doesn't support.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These are supporting muscles."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The bulbospongiosus muscle is also known as the:","options":["Ischiocavernosus","Bulbocavernosus","Pubococcygeus","Iliococcygeus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes write \"Bulbospongiosus / Bulbocavernosus\" as synonyms.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are distinct muscles, not synonyms of bulbospongiosus."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"In the perineal anatomy diagram, which structure is labelled as lying just deep to the levator ani and forming the roof of the pelvic floor space?","options":["Supra levator space","Colles fascia","Superficial perineal pouch","Deep perineal pouch"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the \"supra levator space\" above the levator ani.  Why B wrong: Colles fascia is superficial perineal fascia.  Why C/D wrong: These pouches lie below, not above, the levator ani."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"In the perineal diagram, Bartholin's gland is labelled in relation to which structure?","options":["Deep perineal pouch","The labium minus / vestibule region","Supra levator space","Obturator muscle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels Bartholin's gland near the labium minus in the vulvar/vestibular region.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are separate labelled structures, not the location of Bartholin's gland."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Colles fascia in the perineal diagram refers to the:","options":["Superficial (membranous) perineal fascia","Peritoneum","Levator ani","Obturator muscle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Colles fascia is the membranous superficial perineal fascia, labelled in the diagram of the perineum.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct labelled structures (peritoneum, muscle, wall muscle)."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which of the following structures is labelled forming the lateral wall of the pelvis in the muscle-support diagram?","options":["Obturator muscle","Levator ani","Bulbocavernosus","Bartholin's gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The obturator muscle is labelled as the lateral wall structure in the diagram.  Why B wrong: Levator ani forms the pelvic floor.  Why C/D wrong: These are perineal/vestibular structures, not the lateral pelvic wall."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"A clinician notes that a patient's uterine fundus points posteriorly toward the sacral promontory. This uterine position is termed:","options":["Anteverted","Anteflexed","Retroverted","Normal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A fundus pointing towards the sacral promontory (posteriorly) defines a retroverted uterus per the notes.  Why A/D wrong: A fundus pointing anteriorly (towards pubic symphysis) is the normal anteverted position.  Why B wrong: Anteflexion refers to the body–cervix angle, not fundal direction toward the sacrum."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Which ligament is regarded as the most important ligamentous support because it anchors the cervix and upper vagina to the pelvic side wall?","options":["Round ligament","Cardinal (transverse cervical) ligament","Broad ligament","Ovarian ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal (transverse cervical/Mackenrodt) ligament is stated to be the most important ligament (cardinal > uterosacral).  Why A wrong: The round ligament is only a secondary support.  Why C wrong: The broad ligament does not support the uterus.  Why D wrong: The ovarian ligament is not part of the support triad."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"The external urinary sphincter and external anal sphincter are grouped with which category of pelvic structures relative to the uterus?","options":["Muscles that support the uterus","Muscles that do not support the uterus","Ligaments of the uterus","Peritoneal folds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list external urinary sphincter and external anal sphincter among the muscles which support the uterus.  Why B wrong: They are supporting, not non-supporting.  Why C/D wrong: They are muscles, not ligaments or peritoneal folds."},{"section":"Batch 083 · Prolapse — definition, normal anatomical position of uterus…","question":"Because the round ligament keeps the uterus anteverted, it is best described as providing:","options":["Direct primary support","Indirect (secondary) support","No support at all","Purely vascular support"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the round ligament \"indirectly supports the uterus OR is it secondary support.\"  Why A wrong: Primary support is provided by the triradiate ligaments.  Why C wrong: The broad ligament, not the round ligament, provides no support.  Why D wrong: Support here is mechanical/positional, not vascular."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In uterovaginal prolapse, the descent of pelvic organs occurs primarily because of which underlying change?","options":["Weakening of the supporting muscles and ligaments","Hypertrophy of the myometrium","Excessive estrogen production","Increased tone of the pelvic floor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states prolapse occurs when muscles/ligaments become weak, leading to descent of the organs.  Why B wrong: Myometrial hypertrophy is not the cause; the support structures weaken.  Why C wrong: Prolapse is associated with estrogen deficiency (menopause), not excess.  Why D wrong: Increased pelvic-floor tone would prevent prolapse, not cause it."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Prolapse due to menopause reflects that uterovaginal prolapse is characteristically a problem of which population?","options":["Young nulliparous women","Elderly and multiparous females","Adolescent girls","Primigravida women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state prolapse \"is problem of Elderly & multiparous female,\" linked to menopause.  Why A wrong: Nulliparous young females only rarely develop prolapse (congenital type).  Why C wrong: Adolescents are not the typical group.  Why D wrong: Primigravidas are not the characteristic group; multiparity increases risk."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Repeated childbirth predisposes to prolapse. Which of the following intrapartum factors is specifically listed as contributing? (Select all that apply)","options":["Obstructed labor","Prolonged labor","Perineal tear","Instrumental delivery","Elective caesarean at term"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists obstructed labor, prolonged labor, perineal tear, and instrumental delivery as the factors \"especially\" causing prolapse after repeated childbirth.  Why E wrong: Elective caesarean is not listed as a contributing factor on the page."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Which of the following is listed as an \"other risk factor\" for uterovaginal prolapse?","options":["Cigarette smoking","High-fibre diet","Regular aerobic exercise","Low body mass index"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cigarette smoking is the first item in the \"Other Risk Factors\" list.  Why B, C, D wrong: None of these are listed; they are not identified risk factors on the page."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"A chronic increase in which of the following is an \"other risk factor\" for prolapse?","options":["Intra-abdominal pressure","Intracranial pressure","Serum estrogen","Renal blood flow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Chronic increase in intra-abdominal pressure\" as a risk factor (e.g., chronic cough, constipation, heavy lifting).  Why B wrong: Intracranial pressure is unrelated to pelvic support.  Why C wrong: Estrogen deficiency, not increase, contributes to prolapse.  Why D wrong: Renal blood flow is not a listed factor."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Which neurological/connective-tissue conditions are listed among the \"other risk factors\" for prolapse? (Select all that apply)","options":["Spinal cord injury","Connective tissue disorder","Migraine","Cigarette smoking","Chronic increase in intra-abdominal pressure"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The four \"other risk factors\" listed are cigarette smoking, chronic increase in intra-abdominal pressure, spinal cord injury, and connective tissue disorder.  Why C wrong: Migraine is not mentioned as a risk factor on the page."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"When prolapse rarely occurs in a young, nulliparous female, it is termed:","options":["Congenital prolapse","Acquired prolapse","Post-menopausal prolapse","Traumatic prolapse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that rarely prolapse occurs in young, nulliparous females and is \"Called as Congenital prolapse.\"  Why B wrong: Acquired prolapse is not the term used for the nulliparous/young presentation.  Why C wrong: Post-menopausal prolapse relates to elderly women, not young nulliparous.  Why D wrong: \"Traumatic prolapse\" is not the term given."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Which condition is identified as a risk factor for congenital prolapse in young nulliparous females?","options":["Connective tissue disorder / spina bifida","Diabetes mellitus","Menopause","Multiparity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart lists \"Connective tissue disorder / spina bifida\" as the risk factor for congenital prolapse.  Why B wrong: Diabetes is not the listed risk factor for congenital prolapse.  Why C wrong: Menopause relates to elderly, acquired prolapse.  Why D wrong: By definition congenital prolapse occurs in nulliparous women, so multiparity is not the cause."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Uterovaginal prolapse can be classified as uterocervical prolapse, vaginal prolapse, or:","options":["Both together","Neither","Only bladder prolapse","Only rectal prolapse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states prolapse \"can be of Uterocervical Prolapse or Vaginal, of Both.\"  Why B wrong: \"Neither\" contradicts the classification which includes a combined type.  Why C, D wrong: These are components (cysto/rectocele) but not the overall classification categories described."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In anterior vaginal wall prolapse, descent of the UPPER two-thirds of the anterior wall (with the bladder) produces which condition?","options":["Cystocele","Urethrocele","Rectocele","Enterocele"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows Anterior Vaginal Wall Prolapse — Upper 2/3 → CYSTOCELE (bladder descent).  Why B wrong: Urethrocele involves the lower 1/3 (urethra), not the upper 2/3.  Why C wrong: Rectocele is a posterior wall (mid 1/3) prolapse.  Why D wrong: Enterocele is a posterior wall (upper 1/3) prolapse."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In anterior vaginal wall prolapse, descent of the LOWER one-third of the anterior wall produces:","options":["Urethrocele","Cystocele","Enterocele","Rectocele"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows Anterior Vaginal Wall Prolapse — Lower 1/3 → URETHROCELE (urethral descent).  Why B wrong: Cystocele is the upper 2/3 anterior prolapse.  Why C, D wrong: Enterocele and rectocele are posterior wall prolapses."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In posterior vaginal wall prolapse, descent involving the UPPER one-third (containing the pouch of Douglas with intestines) is called:","options":["Enterocele","Rectocele","Cystocele","Laxed perineum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows Posterior Vaginal Wall Prolapse — Upper 1/3 → ENTEROCELE; the pouch of Douglas contains intestines.  Why B wrong: Rectocele is the mid 1/3 posterior prolapse.  Why C wrong: Cystocele is an anterior wall prolapse.  Why D wrong: Laxed perineum corresponds to the posterior/lower 1/3, not the upper 1/3."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In posterior vaginal wall prolapse, descent of the MID one-third (rectum) is termed:","options":["Rectocele","Enterocele","Urethrocele","Cystocele"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows Posterior Vaginal Wall Prolapse — Mid 1/3 → RECTOCELE (rectal descent into vagina).  Why B wrong: Enterocele is the upper 1/3 posterior prolapse.  Why C, D wrong: Urethrocele and cystocele are anterior wall prolapses."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In posterior vaginal wall prolapse, the posterior one-third defect is described as:","options":["Laxed perineum","Enterocele","Rectocele","Cystocele"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows Posterior Vaginal Wall Prolapse — Posterior 1/3 → LAXED PERINEUM.  Why B wrong: Enterocele is the upper 1/3 posterior defect.  Why C wrong: Rectocele is the mid 1/3 posterior defect.  Why D wrong: Cystocele is an anterior wall defect."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"According to the anterior vaginal wall diagram, which organs lie against the anterior vaginal wall?","options":["Bladder and urethra","Rectum and anal canal","Small intestine only","Ureters and kidneys"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The labelled diagram shows the bladder and urethra adjacent to the anterior vaginal wall (giving cystocele and urethrocele).  Why B wrong: Rectum and anal canal relate to the posterior wall.  Why C wrong: Intestines occupy the pouch of Douglas (posterior/upper), not the anterior wall.  Why D wrong: Ureters and kidneys are not depicted in this vaginal-wall diagram."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"The management (surgery) of vaginal prolapse is described as being:","options":["The same for all ages of females","Different for every decade of life","Only offered after menopause","Only medical, never surgical"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states management of vaginal prolapse is \"Same for all age of females.\"  Why B wrong: It is explicitly the same across ages, not age-dependent (unlike uterine prolapse).  Why C wrong: It is not restricted to post-menopause.  Why D wrong: The management listed is surgical."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"The surgical repair of a cystocele or ureterocele (anterior wall defect) is:","options":["Anterior colporrhaphy","Posterior colpoperineorrhaphy","Vaginal hysterectomy","Le Fort colpocleisis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The management table pairs Cystocele/Ureterocele with Anterior colporrhaphy.  Why B wrong: Posterior colpoperineorrhaphy repairs rectocele/laxed perineum.  Why C wrong: Vaginal hysterectomy is for uterine prolapse in older women.  Why D wrong: Le Fort colpocleisis is for elderly unfit patients with uterine prolapse."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Which surgical procedures are used to repair an enterocele? (Select all that apply)","options":["Moscowitz repair","Halban repair","McCall culdoplasty","Anterior colporrhaphy","Posterior colpoperineorrhaphy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists enterocele repair as Moscowitz repair / Halban repair / McCall culdoplasty.  Why D wrong: Anterior colporrhaphy repairs cystocele/ureterocele.  Why E wrong: Posterior colpoperineorrhaphy repairs rectocele/laxed perineum."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"The surgical repair for a rectocele or laxed perineum (posterior wall defect) is:","options":["Posterior colpoperineorrhaphy","Anterior colporrhaphy","McCall culdoplasty","Manchester surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table pairs Rectocele/Laxed Perineum with Posterior colpoperineorrhaphy.  Why B wrong: Anterior colporrhaphy is for cystocele/ureterocele.  Why C wrong: McCall culdoplasty is for enterocele.  Why D wrong: Manchester surgery is a uterine-prolapse operation, not for the posterior vaginal wall alone."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Uterine prolapse is defined on the page as:","options":["Utero + cervical prolapse","Bladder + urethral descent","Rectal + anal descent","Intestinal herniation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states uterine prolapse = \"UTERO + Cervical prolapse.\"  Why B wrong: That describes anterior vaginal wall prolapse.  Why C wrong: That describes posterior wall prolapse components.  Why D wrong: Intestinal herniation describes enterocele."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"For grading uterine descent, the reference point used is:","options":["The introitus (vaginal opening)","The cervix","The pubic symphysis","The ischial spines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Reference Point: Introitus (Vagina open).\"  Why B wrong: The cervix is the descending structure being measured, not the fixed reference.  Why C, D wrong: Pubic symphysis and ischial spines are not the stated reference point."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In first-degree uterine descent, the cervix descends:","options":["Into the vagina (but not to the introitus)","To the level of the introitus","Outside the introitus","Not at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The classification states 1° = descent of the cervix in the vagina.  Why B wrong: Descent to the introitus is second degree.  Why C wrong: Descent outside the introitus is third degree.  Why D wrong: No descent is the normal (Grade 0) state."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In second-degree uterine descent, the cervix descends:","options":["To the introitus","Into the vagina only","Outside the introitus","With the whole uterus outside"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The classification states 2° = descent of the cervix to the introitus.  Why B wrong: Descent within the vagina only is first degree.  Why C wrong: Descent outside the introitus is third degree.  Why D wrong: Whole uterus outside the introitus is procidentia."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In third-degree uterine descent, the cervix descends:","options":["Outside the introitus","To the introitus","Into the vagina only","Not at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The classification states 3° = descent of the cervix outside the introitus.  Why B wrong: To the introitus is second degree.  Why C wrong: Within the vagina is first degree.  Why D wrong: No descent is normal."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Procidentia refers to:","options":["All of the uterus lying outside the introitus","Cervix descending into the vagina","Cervix at the level of the introitus","Normal uterine position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines Procidentia as \"All of the Uterus outside the Introitus.\"  Why B wrong: That is first-degree descent.  Why C wrong: That is second-degree descent.  Why D wrong: Normal position is Grade 0."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In the grading table, GRADE 0 uterine prolapse indicates:","options":["Normal position of uterus and cervix — no prolapse","Cervix descends into vagina","Cervix at level of introitus","Cervix outside the vagina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines Grade 0 as \"Normal position of uterus and cervix, No Prolapse.\"  Why B wrong: Cervix descending into vagina (not to introitus) is Grade 1.  Why C wrong: Cervix up to the level of introitus is Grade 2.  Why D wrong: Cervix outside the vagina is Grade 3."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In the grading table, GRADE 1 uterine prolapse means:","options":["Cervix descends into vagina but not up to the level of introitus","Cervix is up to the level of introitus","Cervix is outside the vagina","Entire cervix/uterus outside the vagina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines Grade 1 as cervix descending into the vagina but not up to the level of the introitus.  Why B wrong: Cervix up to the introitus is Grade 2.  Why C wrong: Cervix outside the vagina is Grade 3.  Why D wrong: Entire uterus outside is Grade 4 (procidentia)."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In the grading table, GRADE 2 uterine prolapse means the cervix is:","options":["Up to the level of the introitus","Descended into the vagina only","Outside the vagina","In normal position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines Grade 2 as \"Cervix is up till the level of introitus.\"  Why B wrong: Descent within the vagina only is Grade 1.  Why C wrong: Cervix outside the vagina is Grade 3.  Why D wrong: Normal position is Grade 0."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In the grading table, GRADE 3 uterine prolapse means the cervix is:","options":["Outside the vagina (introitus)","Up to the level of the introitus","Within the vagina only","In normal position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines Grade 3 as \"Cervix is outside the vagina (introitus).\"  Why B wrong: Cervix at the introitus is Grade 2.  Why C wrong: Cervix within the vagina is Grade 1.  Why D wrong: Normal position is Grade 0."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In the grading table, GRADE 4 uterine prolapse corresponds to which term?","options":["Procidentia — entire cervix/uterus outside the vagina","First degree","No prolapse","Cystocele"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines Grade 4 as \"Entire cervix of uterus are outside vagina (PROCIDENTIA).\"  Why B wrong: First degree is a mild descent within the vagina.  Why C wrong: No prolapse is Grade 0.  Why D wrong: Cystocele is a vaginal (anterior wall) prolapse, not a uterine grade."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Which of the following is a symptom of uterine prolapse?","options":["Sensation of something coming out of the vagina","Sudden loss of vision","Chest pain radiating to arm","Epigastric burning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The symptom list begins with \"Something coming out of vagina.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not listed symptoms of prolapse on the page."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Backache in uterine prolapse is attributed to:","options":["Stretching of the uterosacral ligaments","Herniation of the bladder","Rectal compression","Venous congestion of the ovary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state backache \"is due to stretching of uterosacral ligaments.\"  Why B wrong: Bladder herniation (cystocele) causes urinary symptoms, not the listed backache mechanism.  Why C wrong: Rectal compression is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Ovarian venous congestion is not the listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"A decubitus ulcer in a prolapsed uterus is caused by:","options":["Venous congestion (concession) from hampered venous return","Friction between the prolapsed uterus and thigh","Bacterial infection alone","Arterial embolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pathophysiology chain states decubitus ulcer is due to venous congestion: uterus hangs outside → venous return is hampered → venous congestion → ulcer.  Why B wrong: The page explicitly states the ulcer is NOT due to friction between prolapsed uterus and thigh.  Why C wrong: Infection is not the primary listed cause; venous congestion is.  Why D wrong: Arterial embolism is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Arrange the correct sequence of decubitus-ulcer pathophysiology in a prolapsed uterus.","options":["Uterus hangs outside → venous return hampered → venous congestion → ulcer","Ulcer → venous congestion → uterus hangs outside","Venous congestion → uterus hangs inside → ulcer","Friction → arterial block → ulcer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart is: in prolapsed uterus → uterus hangs outside → venous return is hampered → venous congestion → ulcer.  Why B wrong: The order is reversed.  Why C wrong: The uterus hangs OUTSIDE, not inside.  Why D wrong: The ulcer is not due to friction/arterial block."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"The management of a decubitus ulcer includes daily packing of the uterus with:","options":["Sterile gauze pack","Dry cotton wool","Iodine dressing changed weekly","Alginate rope only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Daily packing of uterus with sterile gauze pack.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These specific materials/schedules are not the described management."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In decubitus ulcer management, acriflavine is used because it acts as a/an:","options":["Antiseptic agent","Hygroscopic agent","Analgesic","Anticoagulant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels Acriflavine as an \"Antiseptic agent.\"  Why B wrong: Glycerine is the hygroscopic agent, not acriflavine.  Why C, D wrong: Acriflavine is not an analgesic or anticoagulant."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In decubitus ulcer management, glycerine is combined with acriflavine because glycerine is a/an:","options":["Hygroscopic agent","Antiseptic agent","Antibiotic","Local anaesthetic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels Glycerine as a \"Hygroscopic agent\" (draws out fluid/reduces edema).  Why B wrong: Acriflavine is the antiseptic; glycerine is hygroscopic.  Why C, D wrong: Glycerine is neither an antibiotic nor an anaesthetic."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"The combination applied for decubitus ulcer packing is:","options":["Acriflavine + Glycerine","Povidone-iodine + saline","Silver nitrate + honey","Hydrogen peroxide + petroleum jelly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page pairs Acriflavine (antiseptic) + Glycerine (hygroscopic) for daily gauze packing.  Why B, C, D wrong: These combinations are not described on the page."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"The management of uterine prolapse (unlike vaginal prolapse) depends on:","options":["Patient's age and need for future pregnancy","Body weight and height only","Blood group","Parity alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states management \"Depends on 'age' + need for future pregnancy or not.\"  Why B, C wrong: Weight/height and blood group are not the decision factors.  Why D wrong: Parity is a risk factor but not the stated management decision basis."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"A young female with prolapse who wants future pregnancy (or has congenital prolapse) is managed with an artificial support in the form of a sling. This procedure is known as:","options":["Sling surgery / cervicopexy (using Mersilene tape)","Vaginal hysterectomy","Le Fort colpocleisis","Fothergill surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states young women desiring pregnancy get artificial support in the form of a \"SLING → MERSILENE TAPE, Sling surgery/Cervicopexy.\"  Why B wrong: Vaginal hysterectomy removes the uterus and precludes pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Le Fort colpocleisis is for elderly unfit patients.  Why D wrong: Fothergill/Manchester is for women <40 who want to keep menstruating but not conceive."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In sling surgery for uterine prolapse, the artificial support material used is:","options":["Mersilene tape","Silk suture","Steel wire","Absorbable catgut"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify the sling is a \"MERSILENE TAPE.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These materials are not specified for the sling on the page."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"A woman younger than 40 years who does not desire pregnancy but wishes to keep menstruating is best managed by:","options":["Fothergill (Manchester) surgery","Vaginal hysterectomy","Le Fort colpocleisis","Ring pessary only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table assigns Fothergill/Manchester surgery to women <40 who do not want pregnancy but want to keep menstruating.  Why B wrong: Vaginal hysterectomy would stop menstruation (uterus removed).  Why C wrong: Le Fort colpocleisis is for elderly unfit patients.  Why D wrong: Ring pessary is temporary, not the definitive surgery listed for this group."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In Fothergill (Manchester) surgery, which of the following is performed? (Select all that apply)","options":["The cervix is amputated","The uterus is stitched to the vagina","The entire uterus is removed","Both anterior and posterior vaginal walls are stitched closed","The bladder is removed"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Fothergill/Manchester surgery = cervix is amputated + uterus is stitched to vagina.  Why C wrong: The uterus is preserved (menstruation continues), not removed.  Why D wrong: Stitching both walls closed describes Le Fort colpocleisis.  Why E wrong: Bladder removal is not part of this surgery."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"A woman ≥40 years who does not want pregnancy and does not want to menstruate is managed by:","options":["Vaginal hysterectomy","Sling surgery","Fothergill surgery","Ring pessary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table assigns Vaginal Hysterectomy (removal of uterus and cervix) to women ≥40 who want neither pregnancy nor menstruation.  Why B wrong: Sling surgery is for young women wanting pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Fothergill preserves the uterus for continued menstruation.  Why D wrong: Ring pessary is temporary/conservative."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Vaginal hysterectomy for uterine prolapse involves removal of:","options":["Uterus and cervix","Only the cervix","Only the ovaries","Bladder and uterus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes vaginal hysterectomy as \"Uterus & Cervix removal.\"  Why B wrong: Cervix alone is amputated in Fothergill, not hysterectomy.  Why C wrong: Ovaries are not the removed organs described.  Why D wrong: The bladder is not removed."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Vaginal hysterectomy is classified as a major surgery done under which type of anaesthesia?","options":["General anaesthesia","Local anaesthesia","No anaesthesia","Topical anaesthesia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states vaginal hysterectomy is a \"Major Surgery – done under general Anesthesia.\"  Why B wrong: Local anaesthesia is used for Le Fort colpocleisis, not vaginal hysterectomy.  Why C, D wrong: A major surgery is not done without/with only topical anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"An elderly woman aged ≥60–65 years with diabetes, heart failure and hypertension who cannot receive general anaesthesia is best managed by:","options":["Le Fort colpocleisis (stitching anterior & posterior vaginal walls under local anaesthesia)","Vaginal hysterectomy under general anaesthesia","Sling surgery","Fothergill surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table assigns Le Fort colpocleisis — stitching the anterior and posterior vaginal walls, done under local anaesthesia — to elderly comorbid patients unfit for general anaesthesia.  Why B wrong: Vaginal hysterectomy needs general anaesthesia, which this patient cannot receive.  Why C wrong: Sling surgery is for young women wanting pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Fothergill is for women <40 who want to menstruate."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Le Fort colpocleisis is performed by:","options":["Stitching the anterior and posterior walls of the vagina together","Removing the uterus","Amputating the cervix","Inserting a sling of Mersilene tape"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes Le Fort colpocleisis as stitching the anterior & posterior wall of the vagina, done under local anaesthesia.  Why B wrong: Uterus removal is vaginal hysterectomy.  Why C wrong: Cervix amputation is Fothergill surgery.  Why D wrong: A Mersilene sling is used in sling surgery/cervicopexy."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Le Fort colpocleisis is carried out under which anaesthesia?","options":["Local anaesthesia","General anaesthesia","Spinal anaesthesia","No anaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states this procedure is \"Done under Local Anesthesia,\" suitable for elderly unfit patients.  Why B wrong: General anaesthesia is used for vaginal hysterectomy, and is contraindicated in these comorbid patients.  Why C, D wrong: Spinal or no anaesthesia is not what is specified."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Which of the following are situations described as \"young female with prolapse\" who may need conservative management? (Select all that apply)","options":["During pregnancy","Immediately after delivery","Unfit for surgery","Refusing surgery","After menopause"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists young female with prolapse scenarios as: during pregnancy, immediately after delivery, unfit for surgery, and refusing surgery.  Why E wrong: \"After menopause\" is not part of this young-female list."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"A ring pessary used in prolapse management is kept at the level of the:","options":["External os","Internal os","Introitus","Fornix only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the ring pessary is \"kept at the level of External os.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: The pessary level is specified as the external os, not the internal os, introitus, or fornix."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"A ring pessary is regarded as which kind of treatment, and how often is it changed?","options":["Temporary treatment, changed after 3 months","Permanent treatment, never changed","Temporary treatment, changed daily","Permanent treatment, changed yearly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the ring pessary is a \"Temporary Treatment, changed after 3 months.\"  Why B, D wrong: It is temporary, not permanent.  Why C wrong: It is changed after 3 months, not daily."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"How does a ring pessary work in uterine prolapse?","options":["It is a space-occupying device that does not allow the uterus to descend into the vagina","It surgically fixes the uterus to the sacrum","It removes the prolapsed tissue","It amputates the cervix"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the pessary as a \"space occupying device which does not allow uterus to enter into vagina.\"  Why B wrong: It is a non-surgical device, not a fixation operation.  Why C, D wrong: It does not remove tissue or amputate the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"According to the notes, the BEST method to prevent prolapse is:","options":["Perineal exercises to strengthen the perineal muscles","Prolonged bed rest","Early hysterectomy","Chronic pessary use"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states the best method to prevent prolapse is perineal exercises, by which the perineal muscles get strengthened.  Why B wrong: Bed rest is not the preventive method described.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is a treatment, not prevention.  Why D wrong: A pessary is temporary treatment, not the best prevention."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"The specific perineal exercise recommended to prevent prolapse and strengthen the pelvic floor is:","options":["Kegel's exercises","Valsalva maneuver","Straight leg raises","Deep squatting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart names \"KEGEL's exercises\" as the perineal-strengthening exercise.  Why B wrong: The Valsalva maneuver raises intra-abdominal pressure (a risk factor), not a preventive exercise.  Why C, D wrong: Leg raises and squatting are not the exercises named."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"According to the Kegel exercise information box, these exercises have been proven effective in reducing:","options":["Urinary incontinence","Constipation","Hypertension","Back pain only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The infographic states Kegel exercises \"have been proven effective in reducing urinary incontinence.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These specific benefits are not stated for Kegel exercises on the page."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"The Kegel exercise technique involves which action?","options":["Lifting the pelvic floor and contracting the muscles","Bearing down forcefully","Holding the breath and pushing","Rapid abdominal crunches"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The infographic's \"How to\" states: lift the pelvic floor and contract muscles.  Why B, C wrong: Bearing down/pushing increases intra-abdominal pressure and would worsen prolapse.  Why D wrong: Abdominal crunches are not the described technique."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Which statement about where Kegel exercises can be performed is correct per the notes?","options":["They can be done at any location — home, office, or in the car","They can only be done in a hospital","They require special gym equipment","They can only be done lying down"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The infographic states they \"can be done at any location: at home, office, or in the car.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These restrictions contradict the stated versatility of the exercise."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"For preventive perineal-exercise advice, a pregnant female is advised to begin exercises:","options":["After the 4th month of pregnancy","In the first month of pregnancy","Only in the last week before delivery","Never during pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under \"Advice,\" the page states pregnant females should exercise \"after 4 month of pregnancy.\"  Why B, C wrong: A different timing is specified (after 4 months).  Why D wrong: Exercises are advised, not prohibited, during pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"Besides pregnant women (after 4 months), the notes also advise perineal/Kegel exercises for women:","options":["After delivery","Before menarche","During menstruation only","Only if diabetic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Advice\" section lists \"After delivery\" as the second group advised to do the exercises.  Why B, C, D wrong: These groups/conditions are not mentioned in the advice."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"In the anterior vaginal wall diagram, the pouch of Douglas is noted to contain intestines. This anatomical relationship explains which type of prolapse?","options":["Enterocele","Cystocele","Urethrocele","Rectocele"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Because the pouch of Douglas contains intestine, herniation of the upper 1/3 posterior wall carries bowel and forms an enterocele.  Why B, C wrong: Cystocele/urethrocele involve the bladder/urethra (anterior wall).  Why D wrong: Rectocele involves the rectum (mid 1/3 posterior), not the intestine-containing pouch."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"A multiparous 68-year-old woman with hypertension and heart failure presents with the entire uterus lying outside the introitus. Which grade of prolapse and which surgery are most appropriate?","options":["Grade 4 (procidentia); Le Fort colpocleisis under local anaesthesia","Grade 1; sling surgery","Grade 2; Fothergill surgery","Grade 0; ring pessary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Entire uterus outside the introitus = Grade 4/procidentia; an elderly comorbid patient unfit for general anaesthesia is managed with Le Fort colpocleisis under local anaesthesia.  Why B wrong: Grade 1 is mild descent within the vagina, and sling surgery is for young women wanting pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Grade 2 is cervix at the introitus; Fothergill is for women <40 wanting to menstruate.  Why D wrong: Grade 0 is no prolapse."},{"section":"Batch 084 · Uterovaginal prolapse, types of prolapse…","question":"A 30-year-old woman with congenital prolapse who desires future childbearing should be offered:","options":["Sling surgery / cervicopexy using Mersilene tape","Vaginal hysterectomy","Le Fort colpocleisis","Fothergill surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Young women desiring pregnancy or with congenital prolapse receive an artificial sling support (Mersilene tape) — sling surgery/cervicopexy, which preserves the uterus.  Why B wrong: Hysterectomy removes the uterus and prevents pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Le Fort is for elderly unfit patients.  Why D wrong: Fothergill amputates the cervix and is for women <40 who do not want pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which country was the first in the world to initiate a national family planning programme, and in which year?","options":["USA in 1948","India in 1952","China in 1960","UK in 1955"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state India was the first country in 1952 to initiate a family planning programme.  Why A wrong: USA in 1948 is not stated and is incorrect.  Why C wrong: China's programme came later and is not the first.  Why D wrong: UK in 1955 is not the first country to launch such a programme."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"According to the concept of a \"Planned Family,\" the first child birth should ideally occur after the mother has attained which minimum age?","options":["≥ 16 years","≥ 18 years","≥ 20 years","≥ 25 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 1st child birth should be after the mother is ≥ 20 years of age.  Why A wrong: 16 years is below the recommended age.  Why B wrong: 18 years, though legal marriage age, is not the figure given here.  Why D wrong: 25 years is higher than the stated criterion."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"As per the \"Planned Family\" concept, what is the recommended minimum gap between two children?","options":["Minimum of 1 year","Minimum of 2 years","Minimum of 3 years","Minimum of 5 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes specify a minimum of 3 years of gap between 2 children.  Why A wrong: 1 year is too short and not stated.  Why B wrong: 2 years is not the figure given.  Why D wrong: 5 years exceeds the stated minimum."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"According to the \"Planned Family\" concept, the limited (ideal) family size recommended in India is:","options":["1 child","2 or 3 children","4 children","No limit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state limited size of family (India 2, 3).  Why A wrong: A single child is not the stated norm.  Why C wrong: 4 children exceeds the recommended limit.  Why D wrong: The concept specifically limits family size."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Contraceptive methods are designed to achieve two main aims. Which pair correctly represents these two aims?","options":["To increase fertility and to treat infertility","To have gap between children and to limit size of family","To promote menstruation and to enhance ovulation","To treat STDs and to reduce menopause symptoms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"2 Aims\" flowchart lists (1) to have gap between children and (2) to limit size of family.  Why A wrong: Increasing fertility is the opposite of contraception's purpose.  Why C wrong: Promoting ovulation is unrelated to the stated aims.  Why D wrong: Treating STDs/menopause is not one of the two stated aims."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Methods used \"to have a gap between children\" are collectively known as:","options":["Permanent / irreversible methods","Temporary / spacing / reversible methods","Emergency methods","Surgical methods"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aim 1 (gap between children) → Temporary methods/spacing methods/Reversible.  Why A wrong: Permanent/irreversible methods correspond to limiting family size, not spacing.  Why C wrong: Emergency methods are not the category named for spacing.  Why D wrong: Surgical methods are permanent methods, not spacing."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Methods used \"to limit the size of the family\" are classified as:","options":["Temporary / spacing methods","Reversible methods","Permanent / irreversible methods","Barrier methods"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Aim 2 (limit size of family) → Permanent methods/Irreversible methods.  Why A wrong: Temporary/spacing methods are for gap between children.  Why B wrong: Reversible methods correspond to the spacing category.  Why D wrong: Barrier methods are temporary/reversible, not permanent."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Some contraceptives offer additional benefits such as decreasing chances of STDs and decreasing bleeding. What are these extra benefits collectively called?","options":["Primary contraceptive benefits","Non-contraceptive benefits","Reproductive benefits","Prophylactic benefits"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state such benefits (decreasing STD chances, decreasing bleeding) are called Non-contraceptive benefits.  Why A wrong: These are additional, not the primary contraceptive effect.  Why C wrong: \"Reproductive benefits\" is not the term used.  Why D wrong: \"Prophylactic benefits\" is not the stated terminology."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which index is used to know the efficacy (failure rate) of a contraceptive?","options":["Body Mass Index","Pearl Index","Apgar Index","Bishop Index"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the indication used to know efficacy of a contraceptive is the Pearl Index.  Why A wrong: BMI measures body fat, not contraceptive efficacy.  Why C wrong: Apgar assesses newborn condition.  Why D wrong: Bishop score assesses cervical readiness for labour induction."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The Pearl Index is defined as the number of accidental pregnancies that occur if:","options":["1000 women use the same contraceptive for 1 month","100 women use the same contraceptive for 1 year","10 women use different contraceptives for 5 years","100 women use the same contraceptive for their lifetime"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pearl Index = number of accidental pregnancies which occur if 100 women use the same contraceptive for 1 year.  Why A wrong: The unit is per 100 women over 1 year, not 1000 women over 1 month.  Why C wrong: It is a single contraceptive over a defined period, not 10 women/5 years.  Why D wrong: The defined period is 1 year, not lifetime."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The unit of the Pearl Index is abbreviated as HWY. What does HWY stand for?","options":["Hundred Woman Weeks","Hundred Women Year","Half Women Yield","Highest Worst Yearly rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Unit = HWY = Hundred women year.  Why A wrong: It is \"year,\" not \"weeks.\"  Why C wrong: \"Half Women Yield\" is fabricated.  Why D wrong: \"Highest Worst Yearly rate\" is fabricated."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"According to the notes, which contraceptive is the MOST effective / has the LEAST failure rate?","options":["Implants","Vasectomy","Progesterone IUCD (Mirena)","Male condom"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Implants have a Pearl Index of 0.05, the lowest failure rate listed (most effective).  Why B wrong: Vasectomy has a Pearl Index of 0.1, higher than implants.  Why C wrong: Progesterone IUCD (Mirena) has 0.2, higher than implants.  Why D wrong: Male condom has a high failure rate (2–12), not the least."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"What is the Pearl Index (failure rate) of contraceptive implants as given in the notes?","options":["0.05","0.1","0.2","1.0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Implants 0.05.  Why B wrong: 0.1 is the value for Vasectomy.  Why C wrong: 0.2 is the value for Progesterone IUCD (Mirena).  Why D wrong: 1.0 is not listed for implants."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"What is the Pearl Index (failure rate) of vasectomy according to the notes?","options":["0.05","0.1","0.2","2.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Vasectomy 0.1.  Why A wrong: 0.05 corresponds to implants.  Why C wrong: 0.2 corresponds to Progesterone IUCD (Mirena).  Why D wrong: 2.0 is not listed for vasectomy."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"What is the Pearl Index (failure rate) of the Progesterone IUCD (Mirena) per the notes?","options":["0.05","0.1","0.2","0.5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Progesterone IUCD (Mirena) 0.2.  Why A wrong: 0.05 is for implants.  Why B wrong: 0.1 is for vasectomy.  Why D wrong: 0.5 is not the listed value."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The brand name \"Mirena\" refers to which type of contraceptive?","options":["Copper-containing IUCD","Progesterone (hormonal) IUCD","Combined oral contraceptive pill","Progesterone injection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes identify Mirena as the Progesterone IUCD.  Why A wrong: Mirena is hormonal, not a plain copper device.  Why C wrong: It is an intrauterine device, not an oral pill.  Why D wrong: It is an implanted IUCD, not an injection."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a category of temporary / spacing / reversible contraceptive methods?","options":["Barrier method","OCPs","Tubectomy","IUCD"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Tubectomy is a permanent/irreversible method for females, not a temporary method.  Why A wrong: Barrier method is listed under temporary methods.  Why B wrong: OCPs are listed under temporary methods.  Why D wrong: IUCD is listed under temporary methods."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"In the temporary methods classification, \"Centchroman\" falls under which category?","options":["Barrier method","Natural method","Others","Progesterone-only methods"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The flowchart places Centchroman under the \"Others\" category.  Why A wrong: Centchroman is not a barrier method.  Why B wrong: It is a pharmacological agent, not a natural method.  Why D wrong: Centchroman is a non-steroidal (SERM) agent, listed under Others, not progesterone-only."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which of the following are listed as barrier methods under the temporary methods branch? (Select all that apply)","options":["Condoms","Vaginal diaphragm","Today sponge","Progesterone injection","OCPs"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The barrier method box lists condoms, vaginal diaphragm, and Today sponge.  Why D wrong: Progesterone injection is a progesterone-only method, not a barrier.  Why E wrong: OCPs are a separate category, not barrier methods."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Condoms as a barrier method are available for which of the following?","options":["Only males","Only females","Both males and females","Neither, they are unisex disposables only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The barrier method box branches condoms into Male and Female.  Why A wrong: Female condoms also exist.  Why B wrong: Male condoms also exist.  Why D wrong: They are specifically male and female types."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which methods are listed under \"Progesterone only methods\"? (Select all that apply)","options":["Progesterone only pills","Progesterone injection","Progesterone implants","Copper IUCD","Male condom"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The progesterone-only methods box lists progesterone only pills, progesterone injection, and progesterone implants.  Why D wrong: Copper IUCD is a separate IUCD category, not progesterone-only.  Why E wrong: Male condom is a barrier method."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"LARC stands for Long Acting Reversible Contraceptive. Which of the following are classified as LARC? (Select all that apply)","options":["IUCD","Progesterone injection","Progesterone implant","Male condom","Coitus interruptus"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list LARC as (1) IUCD, (2) Progesterone injection, (3) Progesterone implant.  Why D wrong: Male condom is a short-acting barrier method, not LARC.  Why E wrong: Coitus interruptus is a natural method, not LARC."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Under permanent / irreversible methods, the procedure for females is:","options":["Vasectomy","Tubectomy","Diaphragm insertion","IUCD insertion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The permanent method flowchart lists Tubectomy for females.  Why A wrong: Vasectomy is the male permanent method.  Why C wrong: Diaphragm is a reversible barrier method.  Why D wrong: IUCD is a temporary/reversible method."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The notes mention a \"New method\" of permanent female contraception named:","options":["Essure / Ensure","Mirena","Centchroman","FC2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state under Females \"New method = Ensure\" (Essure hysteroscopic sterilization).  Why B wrong: Mirena is a progesterone IUCD (temporary), not a new permanent method.  Why C wrong: Centchroman is an oral non-steroidal contraceptive.  Why D wrong: FC2 is a female condom."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Under permanent / irreversible methods, the procedure for males is:","options":["Tubectomy","Vasectomy","Orchidectomy","Circumcision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The permanent method flowchart lists Vasectomy for males.  Why A wrong: Tubectomy is the female procedure.  Why C wrong: Orchidectomy is testis removal, not a contraceptive sterilization.  Why D wrong: Circumcision is not a contraceptive method."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"In the natural method of contraception, the couple prevents pregnancy by:","options":["Using barrier devices consistently","Not using any contraceptive but having timed intercourse based on knowledge of safe/unsafe days","Taking daily oral pills","Undergoing surgical sterilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the natural method the couple doesn't use any contraceptive; they have knowledge of when it is safe to have intercourse based on hormonal changes and apply timed intercourse to prevent pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Natural method uses no device.  Why C wrong: No pills are used in the natural method.  Why D wrong: Surgery is a permanent method, not natural."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which of the following are listed as disadvantages/requirements of the natural method of contraception? (Select all that apply)","options":["Requires an educated couple","Requires a motivated couple","Can be done only if the female's cycles are regular","High failure rate","Protects against STDs"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list disadvantages: educated couple, motivated couple, can be done if cycles are regular, and high failure rate.  Why E wrong: STD protection is not listed as a feature of the natural method; it belongs to barrier methods."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which of the following is one of the four natural methods of contraception listed?","options":["Safe period / Rhythm / Calendar method","Copper IUCD","Progesterone implant","Diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The four natural methods listed are safe period/rhythm/calendar, coitus interruptus, cervical mucus/Billing, and basal body temperature.  Why B wrong: Copper IUCD is a temporary device method, not natural.  Why C wrong: Progesterone implant is a hormonal LARC method.  Why D wrong: Diaphragm is a barrier method."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The Billing method of natural contraception is another name for which method?","options":["Basal body temperature method","Cervical mucus method","Calendar/rhythm method","Coitus interruptus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Cervical mucus method/Billing method\" as the same natural method.  Why A wrong: BBT method is a separate natural method.  Why C wrong: The calendar/rhythm method is a different natural method.  Why D wrong: Coitus interruptus is withdrawal, not the Billing method."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"In the safe period/calendar (rhythm) method, the menstruation phase (days 1–7) of the cycle is considered:","options":["Fertile","Infertile","Ovulatory","Unsafe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cycle diagram labels the menstruation days as \"Menstruation – Infertile.\"  Why A wrong: The fertile window is around the middle of the cycle (days 8–19), not menstruation.  Why C wrong: Ovulation occurs mid-cycle, not during menstruation.  Why D wrong: Menstruation is marked infertile (relatively safe), not the unsafe window."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"In the calendar/rhythm method diagram, which range of cycle days is labelled as the \"Fertile\" (unsafe) period?","options":["Day 1 – Day 7","Day 8 – Day 19","Day 20 – Day 26","Day 25 – Day 32"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Unsafe Period: Day 8 – Day 19 of the cycle (the fertile window in the diagram).  Why A wrong: Days 1–7 are the menstruation/infertile phase.  Why C wrong: Days 20 onward are labelled infertile.  Why D wrong: Days 25–32 are in the infertile (safe) phase."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The basis of the rhythm/safe period method regarding when intercourse is considered safe is:","options":["Only during menstruation","1 week before + 1 week after intercourse are avoided; safe otherwise","Only on the day of ovulation","Any day of the cycle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the basis as \"Intercourse is safe... 1st week before + 1 week after\" (defining the fertile/unsafe window around ovulation, i.e., days 8–19).  Why A wrong: The method does not restrict intercourse only to menstruation.  Why C wrong: The ovulation day itself is unsafe, not safe.  Why D wrong: Intercourse is not safe on any day; the fertile window must be avoided."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Coitus interruptus as a natural contraceptive method is best defined as:","options":["Male ejaculates outside the female vagina (withdrawal)","Timing intercourse to safe days","Measuring basal body temperature","Observing cervical mucus changes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define coitus interruptus as the male ejaculating outside the female vagina.  Why B wrong: Timing intercourse describes the rhythm/calendar method.  Why C wrong: BBT is a separate natural method.  Why D wrong: Cervical mucus observation is the Billing method."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"In the basal body temperature (BBT) method, ovulation is signalled by the release of which hormone, which raises the basal body temperature?","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone","FSH","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state progesterone is released at the time of ovulation and raises basal body temperature.  Why A wrong: Estrogen dominates the follicular phase and does not cause the post-ovulatory temperature rise.  Why C wrong: FSH stimulates follicle growth, not the temperature rise.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is for lactation, not ovulation temperature change."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"In the BBT method, by how much does the basal body temperature rise following ovulation?","options":["0.1 to 0.2°F","0.5 to 0.8°F","1.0 to 1.5°F","2.0 to 2.5°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state basal body temperature rises by 0.5 to 0.8°F.  Why A wrong: 0.1–0.2°F is too small and not the stated range.  Why C wrong: 1.0–1.5°F overstates the rise.  Why D wrong: 2.0–2.5°F is far too high."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"In the BBT method, the correct sequence of steps for a woman to follow is:","options":["Measure BBT daily → when it rises → she has ovulated → avoid intercourse","Measure BBT weekly → when it falls → she has ovulated → have intercourse","Measure BBT only during menses → when stable → ovulation","Do not measure BBT; rely on calendar only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows measure BBT daily → when it rises → she has ovulated → avoid intercourse.  Why B wrong: The temperature rises (not falls) after ovulation, and one should avoid, not have, intercourse.  Why C wrong: BBT is measured daily, and the rise indicates ovulation.  Why D wrong: The method specifically requires daily BBT measurement."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Under the influence of estrogen, the cervical mucus becomes:","options":["Thick, scanty, and non-elastic","Thin, watery, and elastic","Absent completely","Bloody and clotted"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state under Estrogen the cervical mucus is thin, watery, elastic.  Why A wrong: Thick, scanty, non-elastic mucus is the progesterone effect.  Why C wrong: Mucus is not absent; its quality changes.  Why D wrong: Bloody/clotted is not described for cervical mucus assessment."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Under the influence of progesterone, the cervical mucus becomes:","options":["Thin, watery, and elastic","Thick, scanty, and non-elastic","Profuse and clear","Stretchy with high spinnbarkeit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state under Progesterone the cervical mucus is thick, scanty, non-elastic.  Why A wrong: Thin/watery/elastic is the estrogen effect.  Why C wrong: Profuse/clear mucus reflects estrogen dominance.  Why D wrong: High stretchability (spinnbarkeit) reflects estrogen, not progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a barrier method of contraception? (Select all that apply)","options":["Male condom","Female condom","Diaphragm","Today sponge","Basal body temperature method"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The barrier methods listed are male condom, female condom, diaphragm, and Today sponge.  Why E wrong: BBT is a natural method, not a barrier method."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The mechanism of action by which barrier methods prevent pregnancy is:","options":["They stop ovulation","They do not let sperms come in contact with the vagina, preventing fertilization","They thin the endometrium","They increase cervical mucus viscosity hormonally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the barrier method does not let sperms come in contact with the vagina and thereby prevents fertilization.  Why A wrong: Ovulation suppression is the action of hormonal methods, not barriers.  Why C wrong: Endometrial thinning is a hormonal/IUCD effect.  Why D wrong: Barriers act mechanically, not by hormonally altering mucus."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which of the following protective (non-contraceptive) benefits apply to barrier methods as a group? (Select all that apply)","options":["Protect against sexually transmitted disease","Protect against pelvic inflammatory disease","Protect against ectopic pregnancy","Protect against ovarian cancer","Cure infertility"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list that all barrier methods protect against STD, PID, and ectopic pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Ovarian cancer protection is not listed for barriers.  Why E wrong: Barriers do not cure infertility."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which barrier methods specifically protect against HIV and HPV (and therefore also protect against cancer of the cervix)?","options":["Only the diaphragm","Only the Today sponge","Only male and female condoms","All barrier methods equally"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state only male & female condom protect against HIV and HPV, and also protect against cancer cervix.  Why A wrong: Diaphragm does not offer this protection.  Why B wrong: Today sponge does not offer HIV/HPV protection.  Why D wrong: Only condoms, not all barriers, protect against HIV/HPV."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Condoms protecting against HPV consequently also protect against which cancer?","options":["Cancer of the breast","Cancer of the cervix","Cancer of the ovary","Cancer of the endometrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state condoms (via HPV protection) also protect against cancer cervix.  Why A wrong: Breast cancer is not linked to HPV protection here.  Why C wrong: Ovarian cancer is not the one mentioned.  Why D wrong: Endometrial cancer is not the one mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"According to the notes, the overall failure rate of barrier methods is:","options":["Very low / negligible","High","Zero","Same as sterilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the failure rate of barrier methods is high.  Why A wrong: The notes describe it as high, not negligible.  Why C wrong: No method has a zero failure rate.  Why D wrong: Barriers are far less effective than sterilization."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which of the following barrier products are included in the family health programme? (Select all that apply)","options":["Male condom: NIRODH","Female condom: FC2","Sponge: Today","Diaphragm"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes bracket male condom (NIRODH), female condom (FC2), and sponge (Today) as included in the family health programme.  Why D wrong: The notes explicitly state the diaphragm is NOT included in the family planning programme."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The male condom marketed/included in the Government of India family planning programme is known by the brand name:","options":["FC2","Today","NIRODH","Mirena"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state male condom = NIRODH, included in the family health/planning programme.  Why A wrong: FC2 is the female condom.  Why B wrong: Today is the sponge.  Why D wrong: Mirena is the progesterone IUCD."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The female condom included in the family health programme is designated as:","options":["NIRODH","FC2","Today","Ensure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list female condom = FC2.  Why A wrong: NIRODH is the male condom.  Why C wrong: Today is the sponge.  Why D wrong: Ensure is the new permanent female sterilization method."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which barrier method is explicitly stated to be NOT included in the family planning programme?","options":["Male condom (NIRODH)","Female condom (FC2)","Today sponge","Diaphragm"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Diaphragm: Not included in family Planning program.\"  Why A wrong: NIRODH male condom is included.  Why B wrong: FC2 female condom is included.  Why C wrong: Today sponge is included."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Among barrier methods, which one is NOT a \"one time use\" device (i.e., can be used repeatedly within a defined period)?","options":["Male condom","Female condom","Diaphragm","Today sponge"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state all are \"one time use\" except the sponge, which can be left in the female body for 24 hrs allowing repeated intercourse.  Why A wrong: The male condom is one-time use.  Why B wrong: The female condom is one-time use.  Why C wrong: The exception noted is the sponge, not the diaphragm."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"For how long can the Today sponge be left in the female body, during which intercourse can occur any number of times?","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the sponge can be left in the female body for 24 hrs, and in these 24 hours intercourse can occur any number of times.  Why A wrong: 6 hours understates the stated duration.  Why B wrong: 12 hours is not the figure given.  Why D wrong: 48 hours overstates the stated duration."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The male condom was originally made of which material, which limited the use of oil-based lubricants?","options":["Polyurethane","Latex","Silicone","Nylon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the male condom was originally made of latex, so oil-based lubricant could not be used.  Why A wrong: Polyurethane is the newer material, not the original.  Why C wrong: Silicone is not the stated material.  Why D wrong: Nylon is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Why could oil-based lubricants not be used with the original (latex) male condom?","options":["Because oil dissolves progesterone","Because oil damages latex","Because oil increases fertility","Because oil is a spermicide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes link latex condoms to the caution that oil-based lubricants could not be used (oil degrades latex).  Why A wrong: There is no progesterone in a condom to dissolve.  Why C wrong: Oil does not increase fertility.  Why D wrong: Oil is not a spermicide; the issue is latex damage."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Modern male condoms are now made of which material (allowing use with oil-based lubricants)?","options":["Latex only","Polyurethane","Cotton","Rubber tree sap"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state condoms are now made of polyurethane.  Why A wrong: Latex was the original, not the current improved material.  Why C wrong: Cotton is not used.  Why D wrong: Rubber tree sap (latex) is the old material."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"\"NIRODH\" is significant because it is:","options":["A female sterilization device","A male condom included in the family planning programme of the Government of India","A progesterone injection","An emergency contraceptive pill"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 'NIRODH' is included in the family planning programme of the Government of India (as the male condom).  Why A wrong: NIRODH is a condom, not a sterilization device.  Why C wrong: It is not an injection.  Why D wrong: It is not an emergency pill."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"What is stated as the biggest advantage of the male condom?","options":["It permanently sterilizes","It is the best method to prevent transmission of HIV (a non-contraceptive benefit)","It increases sexual pleasure","It has a 100% contraceptive efficacy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the biggest advantage is that it is the best method to prevent transmission of HIV (now considered a contraceptive/non-contraceptive benefit).  Why A wrong: Condoms are reversible, not sterilizing.  Why C wrong: The notes note decreased sexual pleasure as a disadvantage.  Why D wrong: Condoms have a high failure rate (2–12 HMY), not 100% efficacy."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"Which of the following are listed as disadvantages of the male condom as a contraceptive? (Select all that apply)","options":["Decreased (↓) sexual pleasure","High failure rate (2–12 HMY)","Can cause contact dermatitis","Prevents HIV transmission","Permanent sterilization"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list disadvantages as ↓ sexual pleasure, high failure rate (2–12 HMY), and can cause contact dermatitis.  Why D wrong: Preventing HIV is the biggest advantage, not a disadvantage.  Why E wrong: The condom is reversible and does not sterilize."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The failure rate of the male condom as stated in the notes is:","options":["0.05 HMY","0.1 HMY","2–12 HMY","20–30 HMY"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give a high failure rate of 2–12 HMY for the male condom.  Why A wrong: 0.05 is the implant failure rate.  Why B wrong: 0.1 is the vasectomy failure rate.  Why D wrong: 20–30 HMY is not the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"According to the notes, the high failure rate of the male condom is most commonly due to which two causes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Incorrect use","Breakage (break)","Manufacturing defect in all condoms","Use with water-based lubricant","Expiry within 1 day"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes attribute condom failure most commonly to incorrect use and to breakage (break).  Why C wrong: A universal manufacturing defect is not stated.  Why D wrong: Water-based lubricants are actually safe with condoms; oil-based are the problem.  Why E wrong: 1-day expiry is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The male condom can cause which dermatological reaction (especially in latex-sensitive individuals)?","options":["Psoriasis","Contact dermatitis","Eczema herpeticum","Urticaria pigmentosa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Can cause contact dermatitis\" as a disadvantage of the male condom.  Why A wrong: Psoriasis is a chronic autoimmune skin disease unrelated to condoms.  Why C wrong: Eczema herpeticum is a herpes viral complication, not stated.  Why D wrong: Urticaria pigmentosa is a mast cell disorder, not stated."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"The female condom referred to in the notes is designated by which code?","options":["FC1 / FCI","NIRODH","Mirena","Centchroman"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes label the female condom as \"FCI\" (FC1), consistent with FC2 mentioned earlier as the family-programme female condom.  Why B wrong: NIRODH is the male condom.  Why C wrong: Mirena is a progesterone IUCD.  Why D wrong: Centchroman is an oral non-steroidal contraceptive."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"A rolled male condom must be applied over which part before intercourse, as shown by the \"unrolled/rolled\" birth control condom illustration?","options":["The erect penis, by unrolling it down the shaft","Inside the vagina like a diaphragm","Over the cervix only","Around the scrotum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The illustration shows the condom in rolled and unrolled states; a male condom is applied by unrolling it over the erect penis (the \"birth control condom\").  Why B wrong: A diaphragm/female condom goes in the vagina, not the male condom.  Why C wrong: A cervical cap covers the cervix, not the male condom.  Why D wrong: The condom covers the penile shaft, not the scrotum."},{"section":"Batch 085 · Family planning/contraception — India's family planning history…","question":"A young couple wants a reversible spacing method that also protects against HIV, HPV, STDs, PID and ectopic pregnancy. Which method is the MOST appropriate first choice based on these notes?","options":["Tubectomy","Male or female condom","Diaphragm","Coitus interruptus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Only male and female condoms are stated to protect against HIV and HPV (and all barriers protect against STD, PID, ectopic pregnancy) while being a reversible spacing method.  Why A wrong: Tubectomy is permanent/irreversible, unsuitable for spacing.  Why B is correct as above.  Why C wrong: The diaphragm does not protect against HIV/HPV and is not in the family programme.  Why D wrong: Coitus interruptus offers no STD/HIV protection and has a high failure rate."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The FC2 and FC3 varieties of female condom used in the Family Planning Programme are made of which material?","options":["Latex","Nitrile","Polyurethane","Silicone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state FC2/FC3 female condoms are \"Made of Nitrile.\"  Why A wrong: Latex is the classic male condom material, not the FC2/FC3 female condom.  Why C wrong: Polyurethane was used in the older FC1 version, not specified here for FC2/FC3.  Why D wrong: Silicone is not the material given for these female condoms."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which of the following is TRUE regarding the female (nitrile) condom compared with the male condom?","options":["It is cheaper than the male condom","It is more expensive than the male condom","It costs exactly the same","It is not lubricated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the female condom is \"Expensive than male condom.\"  Why A wrong: It is more expensive, not cheaper.  Why C wrong: The cost is not equal; it is higher.  Why D wrong: The female condom is described as prelubricated."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"A prelubricated nitrile female condom is contraindicated in a woman with which condition?","options":["Diabetes mellitus","Uterine (genital) prolapse","Hypertension","Anemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the female condom is \"Contraindicated in Female who has prolapse.\"  Why A wrong: Diabetes is not listed as a contraindication.  Why C wrong: Hypertension is not a listed contraindication.  Why D wrong: Anemia is not listed as a contraindication for the female condom."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The failure rate of the female condom is documented as:","options":["1–2 HWY","5–20 HWY","20–30 HWY","0.2 HWY"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give the female condom failure rate as \"5–20 HWY\" (Hundred Woman Years).  Why A wrong: 1–2 HWY is not the value given.  Why C wrong: 20–30 HWY is higher than the stated range.  Why D wrong: 0.2 is the Mirena failure rate, not the female condom's."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which additional protective benefit is attributed to the female condom?","options":["Decreases chances of HIV and HPV transmission","Prevents ectopic pregnancy","Reduces menstrual cramps","Treats pelvic inflammatory disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state female condoms \"decrease chances of HIV & HPV transmission.\"  Why B wrong: They do not prevent ectopic pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Reducing cramps is not a listed benefit.  Why D wrong: They do not treat PID."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Regarding the vaginal diaphragm, which statement is correct?","options":["It is included in the National Family Planning Programme","It is NOT included in the National Family Planning Programme","It decreases HIV transmission significantly","It must be removed immediately after intercourse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the vaginal diaphragm is \"Not included in National family planning programme.\"  Why C wrong: The notes state it \"Does not decrease HIV Transmission.\"  Why D wrong: It must be left in the vagina for at least 6 hours after intercourse, not removed immediately.  Why A wrong: It is specifically NOT included in the programme."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which statement about the vaginal diaphragm and HIV is TRUE?","options":["It halves HIV transmission","It does not decrease HIV transmission","It fully prevents HIV transmission","It increases HIV risk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the diaphragm \"Does not decrease HIV Transmission.\"  Why A wrong: No reduction is claimed.  Why C wrong: It does not prevent HIV transmission.  Why D wrong: The notes do not state it increases HIV risk."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"For how long must the vaginal diaphragm be left in place after intercourse?","options":["1 hour","3 hours","6 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state it \"Has to be left in vagina till 6 hrs after intercourse.\"  Why A wrong: 1 hour is too short.  Why B wrong: 3 hours is not the stated duration.  Why D wrong: 12 hours is longer than the required minimum of 6 hours."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Leaving a vaginal diaphragm in place for more than 24 hours can lead to which serious complication?","options":["Endometriosis","Toxic shock syndrome","Ectopic pregnancy","Cervical cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if left for more than 24 hours it \"leads to Toxic shock syndrome.\"  Why A wrong: Endometriosis is not caused by a retained diaphragm.  Why C wrong: Ectopic pregnancy is unrelated.  Why D wrong: Cervical cancer is not a consequence of a retained diaphragm."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the characteristic shape of the \"Today\" contraceptive sponge?","options":["Ring shaped","Mushroom shaped","T shaped","Disc shaped"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the Today sponge shape is \"Mushroom shaped.\"  Why A wrong: Ring shape describes a vaginal ring, not the sponge.  Why C wrong: T-shape describes the CuT IUCD.  Why D wrong: Disc shape describes the diaphragm, not the sponge."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Regarding the \"Today\" sponge and the National Family Planning Programme:","options":["It is not included in the programme","'Today' is included in the National Family Planning Programme","It is only available privately","It has been withdrawn from the programme"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"'Today' → Included in National family planning programme.\"  Why A wrong: It is included, unlike the diaphragm.  Why C wrong: The notes state it is part of the national programme.  Why D wrong: There is no mention of withdrawal."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The \"Today\" sponge contains 1 gm of Nonoxynol-9, which acts as a/an:","options":["Antibiotic","Spermicidal agent","Lubricant only","Hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state it \"Has 1 gm Non oxynol-9 → SPERMICIDAL agent.\"  Why A wrong: Nonoxynol-9 is not an antibiotic.  Why C wrong: It functions as a spermicide, not merely a lubricant.  Why D wrong: It is a spermicidal chemical, not a hormone."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the mechanism of action of Nonoxynol-9 in the Today sponge?","options":["Inhibits ovulation","Disrupts the cell membrane of sperm","Thickens the endometrium","Blocks the fallopian tubes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the action is to \"Disrupt the cell membrane of sperm.\"  Why A wrong: It does not inhibit ovulation.  Why C wrong: It does not thicken the endometrium.  Why D wrong: It does not block the fallopian tubes."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the maximum duration for which the Today sponge can be left in the female body?","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state it \"Can be left for maximum 24 hrs in female body.\"  Why A wrong: 6 hours is not the maximum.  Why B wrong: 12 hours is less than the stated 24-hour maximum.  Why D wrong: 48 hours exceeds the safe maximum."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"During the time the Today sponge is in place, the female can have intercourse:","options":["Only once","Any number of times using the same sponge","Only twice","Not at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the female can have intercourse \"using Same sponge any number of times.\"  Why A wrong: It is not limited to a single act.  Why C wrong: There is no two-time limit.  Why D wrong: Intercourse is permitted with the sponge in place."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What does the abbreviation IUCD stand for?","options":["Intrauterine contraceptive device","Intra-uterine cervical dilator","Internal urethral contraceptive device","Intra-uterine copper diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes define IUCD as \"Intrauterine contraceptive device.\"  Why B wrong: It is not a cervical dilator.  Why C wrong: It is intrauterine, not urethral.  Why D wrong: It is a device, not a diaphragm."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"How many generations of IUCD are described?","options":["2 generations","3 generations","4 generations","5 generations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"IUCD: 3 generation available.\"  Why A wrong: There are three, not two.  Why C wrong: Four is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Five is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which device represents the FIRST generation IUCD (now outdated)?","options":["CuT 380A","Lippes loop","Multiload","Mirena"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the 1st generation is the \"Lippes loop (Outdated).\"  Why A wrong: CuT 380A is a 2nd generation copper IUCD.  Why C wrong: Multiload is a 2nd generation device.  Why D wrong: Mirena is a 3rd generation hormonal IUCD."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which of the following are examples of SECOND generation IUCDs?","options":["Lippes loop and Mirena","CuT (CuT 380A) and Multiload","Mirena and Progestasert","Lippes loop only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list 2nd generation as \"1. CuT (CuT 380A)\" and \"2. MULTILOAD.\"  Why A wrong: Lippes loop is 1st gen and Mirena is 3rd gen.  Why C wrong: Mirena and Progestasert are 3rd generation (hormonal).  Why D wrong: Lippes loop is the 1st generation device."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The third-generation IUCDs are classified as which type?","options":["Copper-bearing","Hormonal","Inert/non-medicated","Silicone-based"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state 3rd generation IUCDs are \"Hormonal.\"  Why A wrong: Copper-bearing devices are 2nd generation.  Why C wrong: Inert/non-medicated devices (like Lippes loop) are 1st generation.  Why D wrong: They are hormonal, not silicone-based."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which two devices are given as examples of third-generation hormonal IUCDs?","options":["CuT 380A and Multiload","Mirena and Progestasert","Lippes loop and Mirena","Multiload and Progestasert"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list 3rd generation hormonal IUCDs as \"MIRENA\" and \"PROGESTASERT (no longer used).\"  Why A wrong: These are 2nd generation copper devices.  Why C wrong: Lippes loop is 1st generation.  Why D wrong: Multiload is 2nd generation copper."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which third-generation hormonal IUCD is noted to be \"no longer used\"?","options":["Mirena","Progestasert","Multiload","CuT 380A"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"PROGESTASERT (no longer used).\"  Why A wrong: Mirena is still in use.  Why C wrong: Multiload is a 2nd generation device.  Why D wrong: CuT 380A remains widely used."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the purpose of the thread (identification mark) attached to an IUCD?","options":["To release copper ions","To know that the IUCD is in place and to help removal of the device","To prevent perforation","To store hormones"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the identification mark (thread attached) is \"To know that IUCD is in place\" and for \"Removal of device.\"  Why A wrong: Copper release is a function of the copper wire, not the thread.  Why C wrong: Perforation prevention is the role of the ball tip, not the thread.  Why D wrong: The thread does not store hormones."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"IUCDs are inserted once and have a long duration of action; they are therefore classified as:","options":["Barrier contraceptives","Long acting reversible contraceptives (LARC)","Permanent methods","Emergency contraceptives only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state IUCDs \"are inserted once & have long action → Long acting reversible contraceptive (LARC).\"  Why A wrong: They are not barrier methods.  Why C wrong: They are reversible, not permanent.  Why D wrong: They provide ongoing (not merely emergency) contraception."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the duration of action of the CuT 380A IUCD?","options":["3 years","5 years","10 years","1 year"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give CuT 380A duration as \"10 years.\"  Why A wrong: 3 years is not the stated duration.  Why B wrong: 5–7 years is the Mirena duration.  Why D wrong: 1 year is the Progestasert duration."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the effective duration of the Mirena IUCD as given in the notes?","options":["10 years","5–7 years","1 year","3 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give Mirena (MINENA) duration as \"5–7 years.\"  Why A wrong: 10 years is the CuT 380A duration.  Why C wrong: 1 year is the Progestasert duration.  Why D wrong: 3 months is not stated for Mirena."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the duration of action of the Progestasert IUCD?","options":["10 years","5–7 years","1 year","7 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give Progestasert (PROGERT assert) duration as \"1 years.\"  Why A wrong: 10 years is the CuT 380A duration.  Why B wrong: 5–7 years is the Mirena duration.  Why D wrong: 7 years is not the stated Progestasert duration."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the shape of the CuT 380A device?","options":["Mushroom shaped","T shaped","Ring shaped","Coil shaped"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe CuT 380A as \"T shaped.\"  Why A wrong: Mushroom describes the Today sponge.  Why C wrong: Ring describes a vaginal ring.  Why D wrong: Coil (loop) describes the older Lippes loop."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"In the term \"CuT 380A\", what does the number 380 represent?","options":["The length in mm","The surface area of the copper wire = 380 mm²","The weight of copper in mg","The number of copper coils"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Cu wire: surface area = 380 mm2, Therefore CuT380.\"  Why A wrong: 380 is the copper surface area, not the length.  Why C wrong: It is not a weight in mg.  Why D wrong: It is not the number of coils."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"In the name \"CuT 380A\", what does the letter \"A\" signify?","options":["Copper present on the arms","Anemia indication","Age of the device","Antibiotic coating"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"A = Copper present on arms.\"  Why B wrong: A does not stand for anemia.  Why C wrong: A does not indicate age.  Why D wrong: There is no antibiotic coating."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"In the Family Planning Programme, the CuT 380A is known by which brand/name?","options":["Mirena","Paragard (PARAGAURD)","Multiload","Progestasert"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Family planning programme = Name of 'PARAGAURD'\" (Paragard).  Why A wrong: Mirena is a hormonal 3rd generation IUCD.  Why C wrong: Multiload is a different 2nd generation copper device.  Why D wrong: Progestasert is a 3rd generation hormonal device."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the function of the ball at the tip of the CuT 380A device?","options":["To release hormones","To prevent perforation","To thicken cervical mucus","To dilate the cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Ball: Prevent perforation.\"  Why A wrong: CuT does not release hormones.  Why C wrong: Cervical mucus thickening is a Mirena (progesterone) effect.  Why D wrong: The ball does not dilate the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the approximate length of the CuT 380A device shown in the diagram?","options":["22 mm / 0.87 in","32 mm / 1.26 in","42 mm / 1.65 in","52 mm / 2.05 in"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the CuT 380A as \"32 mm/1.26 in.\"  Why A wrong: 22 mm is not the labelled value.  Why C wrong: 42 mm is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 52 mm is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The PRIMARY (main) mechanism by which the CuT prevents pregnancy is:","options":["Inhibiting ovulation","Preventing fertilization (copper mixes with cervical discharge and does not allow sperm to come)","Blocking the fallopian tubes permanently","Causing early abortion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state copper \"Mix with cervical discharge → Does not allow sperm to come → Prevent fertilization (Mainly).\"  Why A wrong: The notes explicitly state CuT \"Never Inhibits Ovulation.\"  Why C wrong: It does not block tubes permanently.  Why D wrong: Its main action is preventing fertilization, not abortion."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"A key point about the CuT and ovulation is that the CuT:","options":["Completely inhibits ovulation","Never inhibits ovulation","Delays ovulation by one week","Triggers double ovulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state \"Never Inhibits Ovulation.\"  Why A wrong: It does not inhibit ovulation.  Why C wrong: It does not delay ovulation.  Why D wrong: It does not cause double ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Besides preventing fertilization, the copper released by the CuT is harmful for the endometrium, which leads to:","options":["Increased implantation","Prevention of implantation","Endometrial cancer","Ovulation suppression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state copper \"Is harmful for Endometrium → Prevents Implantation.\"  Why A wrong: It prevents, not increases, implantation.  Why C wrong: The notes do not state it causes endometrial cancer.  Why D wrong: It does not suppress ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The Multiload IUCD differs from the CuT in that the Multiload:","options":["Has no copper","Has copper but is NOT 'T' shaped","Releases hormones","Is made of nitrile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Multiload \"Also has copper but not 'T' shaped.\"  Why A wrong: Multiload does contain copper.  Why C wrong: Multiload is a copper device, not hormonal.  Why D wrong: It is not made of nitrile (that is the female condom)."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which features of the Multiload IUCD help keep it in position and decrease the rate of expulsion?","options":["Rigid T-frame and ball tip","Flexible arms and spur (fin) projections","Hormonal reservoir and thread","Latex coating and lubricant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Multiload \"Has flexible arms\" and \"Has spurs fins present\" which \"Helps to keep multiload in position and Decrease rate of expulsion.\"  Why A wrong: The Multiload is specifically not T-shaped.  Why C wrong: Multiload is copper, not hormonal.  Why D wrong: There is no latex coating; these features are the flexible arms and spurs."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The Mirena IUCD is characterized as:","options":["An IUCD with copper wire","An IUCD with NO copper wire (3rd generation hormonal)","A first generation inert device","A barrier method"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Mirena is an \"IUCD with No copper wire\" and a \"3rd generation IUCD.\"  Why A wrong: Mirena contains no copper.  Why C wrong: It is 3rd generation, not 1st.  Why D wrong: It is a hormonal IUCD, not a barrier method."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Mirena is also called LNG-20, referring to its release of:","options":["Estrogen 20 µg/day","LNG (levonorgestrel) 20 µg/day","Copper 20 mg/day","Progesterone 20 mg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Mirena is \"also called as LNG – 20 → Release of LNG 20µg/day.\"  Why A wrong: It releases levonorgestrel, not estrogen.  Why C wrong: It releases no copper.  Why D wrong: The dose is 20 µg/day of LNG, not 20 mg of progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The hormone levonorgestrel released by Mirena is a form of:","options":["Estrogen","Progesterone (progestin)","Testosterone","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Progesterone = Levonorgestrel\" and \"MIRENA: Releases Progesterone.\"  Why A wrong: Levonorgestrel is a progestin, not estrogen.  Why C wrong: It is not testosterone.  Why D wrong: It is not prolactin."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The progesterone released by Mirena leads to which sequence of endometrial changes?","options":["Endometrial hyperplasia then bleeding","Secretory change in endometrium which later becomes endometrial atrophy","Endometrial cancer","Immediate menstruation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Mirena \"Leads to secretory change in Endometrium & later becomes endometrium atrophy.\"  Why A wrong: It causes atrophy, not hyperplasia with bleeding.  Why C wrong: It does not cause cancer.  Why D wrong: It reduces bleeding rather than triggering menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Endometrial atrophy produced by Mirena results in \"No Endometrium = No bleeding\" and:","options":["Increases implantation","Prevents implantation","Causes ovulation","Increases menstrual flow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state endometrial atrophy \"Prevents implantation\" and \"No Endometrium = No bleeding.\"  Why A wrong: It prevents implantation.  Why C wrong: It does not cause ovulation.  Why D wrong: It decreases, not increases, menstrual flow."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The progesterone in Mirena makes the cervical mucus thick, which:","options":["Increases chances of PID","Decreases chances of PID","Has no effect on PID","Causes cervical cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Progesterone => Cervical mucous become thick => Decreases PID chances.\"  Why A wrong: It decreases, not increases, PID chances.  Why C wrong: It does have an effect (decreasing PID).  Why D wrong: It does not cause cervical cancer."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which of the following is a non-contraceptive benefit of Mirena related to menstrual bleeding?","options":["It increases menstrual bleeding","It decreases bleeding and is used in females with excessive bleeding","It causes amenorrhea only in older women","It has no effect on bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list as a non-contraceptive benefit: \"↓ Bleeding → used in female with excessive bleeding.\"  Why A wrong: It decreases bleeding.  Why C wrong: The benefit is not restricted to older women.  Why D wrong: It clearly reduces bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Because Mirena contains progesterone, it can be used in which of the following?","options":["Hormone replacement therapy","As an antibiotic","To increase estrogen levels","As emergency contraception"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Mirena \"Has progesterone = It can be used in Hormone replacement therapy\" and in conditions where estrogen is more.  Why B wrong: It is not an antibiotic.  Why C wrong: Progesterone opposes estrogen; it does not increase estrogen.  Why D wrong: The notes elsewhere state Mirena is NOT used as emergency contraception."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Mirena is useful in treating conditions where estrogen is in excess. Which of the following are such conditions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fibroid","Endometriosis","Wilson disease","Diabetes","Actinomyces infection"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Treatment of condition where estrogen is more\" as Fibroid and Endometriosis.  Why C wrong: Wilson disease is a contraindication for CuT, not an estrogen-excess condition treated by Mirena.  Why D wrong: Diabetes is not listed.  Why E wrong: Actinomyces is the organism in PID after IUCD, not an estrogen-excess condition."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"According to the CuT vs Mirena comparison, the CuT is associated with:","options":["Decreased chances of PID","Increased chances of PID","No change in PID risk","Prevention of all infections"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state CuT has \"↑ chances of PID.\"  Why A wrong: Decreased PID is a Mirena feature (due to thick cervical mucus).  Why C wrong: The CuT increases PID risk.  Why D wrong: It does not prevent infections."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the most common time for pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) to occur after CuT insertion?","options":["Within 1 week of insertion","Within 1 month of insertion","After 6 months","After 1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common time for PID is \"within 1 month of insertion.\"  Why A wrong: 1 week is not the stated time.  Why C wrong: 6 months is later than the peak period.  Why D wrong: 1 year is not the most common time."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which organism is the most common cause of PID following IUCD insertion?","options":["Chlamydia","Actinomyces","Candida","Trichomonas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"M/c PID after IUCD insertion: Actinomyces.\"  Why A wrong: Chlamydia is not the organism named here.  Why C wrong: Candida causes vaginal yeast infection, not the IUCD-related PID cited.  Why D wrong: Trichomonas is not the stated organism."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which IUCD can be used as an emergency contraceptive?","options":["CuT","Mirena","Both CuT and Mirena","Neither"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state CuT \"Can be used as Emergency contraception,\" while Mirena is \"Not to be used as Emergency Contraceptive.\"  Why B wrong: Mirena is explicitly NOT to be used as emergency contraceptive.  Why C wrong: Only CuT can be used as emergency contraception.  Why D wrong: CuT can be used for emergency contraception."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the most common side effect of the CuT IUCD?","options":["Irregular bleeding","Bleeding","Pain","Amenorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the \"Most common side effect: Bleeding\" for CuT.  Why A wrong: Irregular bleeding is the most common side effect of Mirena, not CuT.  Why C wrong: Pain is the 2nd most common side effect of CuT.  Why D wrong: Amenorrhea is more associated with Mirena."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the second most common side effect of the CuT, which is also the most common reason for its removal?","options":["Bleeding","Pain","Infection","Perforation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"2nd most common side effect: Pain\" and \"Most common reason for removal of IUCD = Pain.\"  Why A wrong: Bleeding is the most common side effect, not the 2nd.  Why C wrong: Infection is not stated as the removal reason.  Why D wrong: Perforation is not the stated 2nd most common side effect."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the failure rate of the CuT IUCD?","options":["0.2%","0.5 – 1%","5 – 20%","2 – 3%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give CuT \"Failure Rate → 0.5 – 1%.\"  Why A wrong: 0.2% is the Mirena failure rate.  Why C wrong: 5–20 is the female condom failure range (HWY).  Why D wrong: 2–3% is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the most common side effect of the Mirena IUCD?","options":["Pain","Irregular bleeding","Heavy bleeding","Perforation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Mirena \"Most common side effect: Irregular Bleeding.\"  Why A wrong: Pain is the CuT's 2nd most common side effect.  Why C wrong: Mirena decreases bleeding; heavy bleeding is not its most common effect.  Why D wrong: Perforation is not listed as most common for Mirena."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"What is the failure rate of the Mirena IUCD?","options":["0.2%","0.5 – 1%","5%","2%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes give Mirena \"Failure Rate = 0.2%.\"  Why B wrong: 0.5–1% is the CuT failure rate.  Why C wrong: 5% is too high.  Why D wrong: 2% is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which infection-related conditions are contraindications for IUCD (both CuT and Mirena) use? (Select all that apply)","options":["Current PID / History of PID","Puerperal sepsis","Fibroid","Currently pregnant","Wilson disease"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list under \"Infection related\" contraindications: \"Current PID/History of PID\" and \"Puerperal sepsis.\"  Why C wrong: Fibroid is listed under bleeding-related contraindications, not infection-related.  Why D wrong: Currently pregnant is a pregnancy-related contraindication.  Why E wrong: Wilson disease is a specific CuT contraindication, not an infection-related one."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which of the following are pregnancy-related contraindications for IUCD use? (Select all that apply)","options":["Currently pregnant","Past history of ectopic pregnancy","Past history of molar pregnancy","Cancer cervix","Puerperal sepsis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list under \"Pregnancy related\": Currently pregnant, Past history of ectopic pregnancy, Past history of molar pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Cancer cervix is a bleeding-related contraindication.  Why E wrong: Puerperal sepsis is an infection-related contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Which of the following are bleeding-related contraindications for IUCD use? (Select all that apply)","options":["Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding","Cancer cervix","Cancer endometrium","Fibroid","Ectopic pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list under \"Bleeding related\": Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding, Cancer cervix, Cancer endometrium, Fibroid.  Why E wrong: Ectopic pregnancy history is a pregnancy-related contraindication, not bleeding-related."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"The CuT IUCD is specifically contraindicated in which metabolic disorder?","options":["Wilson disease","Breast cancer","Diabetes mellitus","Hypothyroidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"CuT IUCD → Contraindicated in Wilson disease\" (a disorder of copper metabolism).  Why B wrong: Breast cancer is a Mirena contraindication.  Why C wrong: Diabetes is not listed as a CuT contraindication.  Why D wrong: Hypothyroidism is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Mirena is specifically contraindicated in which condition?","options":["Wilson disease","Breast cancer","Anemia","Diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"MIRENA → Contraindicated in Breast cancer\" (a hormone-sensitive cancer).  Why A wrong: Wilson disease is a CuT contraindication.  Why C wrong: Anemia is a caution for CuT, not a specific Mirena contraindication.  Why D wrong: Diabetes is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"According to the notes, the CuT should NOT be used in which of the following situations? (Select all that apply)","options":["Female with multiple partners","Severely anemic female","Female wanting long-acting contraception","Female with a single partner","Female desiring reversible contraception"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state CuT \"should not be used in female with multiple partner\" and \"should not be used in severely Anemic female.\"  Why C wrong: LARC is a benefit, not a contraindication.  Why D wrong: A single partner is not a contraindication.  Why E wrong: Desiring reversible contraception is not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Why should the CuT be avoided in a severely anemic female?","options":["Because CuT inhibits ovulation","Because its most common side effect is bleeding, which can worsen anemia","Because it releases progesterone","Because it prevents implantation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CuT's most common side effect is bleeding (page 10), so it is avoided in severely anemic females who cannot tolerate additional blood loss.  Why A wrong: CuT never inhibits ovulation.  Why C wrong: CuT does not release progesterone (that is Mirena).  Why D wrong: Prevention of implantation is not the reason for avoiding it in anemia."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"For a woman who is not pregnant and not planning delivery in the near future, the recommended time to insert an IUCD is:","options":["1st – 10 days of the cycle (best 1–5 days)","Mid-cycle at ovulation","Only during menstruation day 15–20","Any time in the luteal phase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state for such a female the timing is \"1st – 10 days of cycle (Best 1– 5 days).\"  Why B wrong: Ovulation (mid-cycle) is not the recommended window.  Why C wrong: Day 15–20 is not the stated window.  Why D wrong: Luteal phase is not the recommended time."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Insertion of an IUCD within 24 hours of delivery is termed:","options":["Interval IUCD insertion","Postpartum / post-placental IUCD insertion","Emergency IUCD insertion","Elective interval insertion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state insertion \"Within 24 hours of delivery → Post partum/IUCD insertion/post placental IUCD insertion.\"  Why A wrong: Interval IUCD insertion is done after 6 weeks.  Why C wrong: \"Emergency IUCD insertion\" is not the term used here.  Why D wrong: Elective interval insertion is the 6-week option, not within 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"Postpartum / post-placental IUCD insertion (within 24 hours of delivery) is recommended by the Government of India but carries which disadvantage?","options":["Higher failure rate due to hormone loss","Higher expulsion rate","Increased risk of Wilson disease","Increased risk of breast cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state postpartum insertion is \"Recommended by GI\" with the \"Disadvantage => Higher expulsion rate.\"  Why A wrong: The disadvantage cited is expulsion, not hormone loss.  Why C wrong: Wilson disease is unrelated to insertion timing.  Why D wrong: Breast cancer is a Mirena contraindication, not an expulsion-related disadvantage."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"When a woman has delivered recently, IUCD insertion done after 6 weeks of delivery is called:","options":["Post-placental insertion","Interval IUCD insertion","Postpartum insertion","Immediate insertion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"After 6 weeks delivery → Interval IUCD insertion.\"  Why A wrong: Post-placental insertion is within 24 hours of delivery.  Why C wrong: Postpartum insertion is within 24 hours.  Why D wrong: Immediate insertion is not the 6-week term."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"A nurse counsels a woman that her copper IUCD works \"mainly\" by which action, and importantly does NOT do which of the following?","options":["Works mainly by preventing fertilization; does NOT inhibit ovulation","Works mainly by inhibiting ovulation; does NOT prevent fertilization","Works mainly by releasing progesterone; does NOT affect sperm","Works mainly by thickening the endometrium; does NOT affect implantation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state CuT mainly \"Prevent fertilization\" and \"Never Inhibits Ovulation.\"  Why B wrong: CuT does not inhibit ovulation and does prevent fertilization.  Why C wrong: CuT is copper, not progesterone-releasing.  Why D wrong: Copper is harmful to the endometrium (thinning/preventing implantation), not thickening it."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"A woman with heavy menstrual bleeding and a fibroid wants a long-acting contraceptive. Which IUCD is most appropriate based on the notes?","options":["CuT 380A","Mirena","Lippes loop","Multiload"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mirena decreases bleeding (used in excessive bleeding) and treats estrogen-excess conditions such as fibroid; it is the appropriate choice.  Why A wrong: CuT's most common side effect is bleeding, which would worsen her heavy bleeding.  Why C wrong: Lippes loop is an outdated 1st generation inert device.  Why D wrong: Multiload is a copper device that can also increase bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 086 · Female condom properties and failure rate, Vaginal diaphragm…","question":"A woman who has had multiple sexual partners requests an IUCD. Based on the notes, which counseling point is correct?","options":["CuT is preferred because of its emergency contraceptive property","CuT should not be used in females with multiple partners (increased PID risk)","Mirena is contraindicated in all women with any partner history","IUCDs are ideal for women with multiple partners"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"CuT should not be used in female with multiple partner,\" reflecting the higher PID risk with copper IUCDs.  Why A wrong: Multiple partners increase infection risk, contraindicating CuT rather than favoring it.  Why C wrong: The notes do not contraindicate Mirena for all partner history.  Why D wrong: IUCDs (especially CuT) are not ideal in women with multiple partners due to PID risk."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Oral Combined Pills (OCP) contain which combination of hormones?","options":["Estrogen only","Progesterone only","Estrogen + Progesterone","Estrogen + Testosterone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: OCP (Oral Combined Pills) = Estrogen + Progesterone.  Why A wrong: Estrogen-only preparations are not combined pills.  Why B wrong: Progesterone-only is the POP (progesterone only pill), not the combined pill.  Why D wrong: Testosterone is not a component of OCPs."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Mala-D pack contains how many tablets in total and in what arrangement?","options":["21 hormonal tablets + 7 brown (iron) tablets","28 hormonal tablets only","21 iron tablets + 7 hormonal tablets","28 iron tablets only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mala-D = 21 tablets (estrogen + progesterone) + 7 brown tablets (iron = ferrous fumarate).  Why B wrong: The pack is not all-hormonal; 7 are iron tablets.  Why C wrong: The ratio is reversed — 21 hormonal + 7 iron, not vice versa.  Why D wrong: The pack is not all-iron."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In Mala-D, the 7 brown tablets contain which iron compound?","options":["Ferrous sulfate","Ferrous fumarate","Ferrous gluconate","Iron dextran"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Mala-D the iron tablet is ferrous fumarate.  Why A wrong: The source specifies ferrous fumarate, not ferrous sulfate.  Why C wrong: Ferrous gluconate is not the compound used in Mala-D.  Why D wrong: Iron dextran is a parenteral iron preparation, not the oral brown tablet in Mala-D."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Mala-D is distributed under which scheme?","options":["Free distribution by Government of India","Social marketing scheme (sold at low cost)","Prescription-only sale at market price","Distributed only in tertiary hospitals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mala-D is under the social marketing scheme — sold at low cost.  Why A wrong: Free distribution by Government of India applies to Mala-N, not Mala-D.  Why C wrong: It is sold at a subsidised low cost, not at full market price.  Why D wrong: Social marketing distributes it widely, not restricted to tertiary hospitals."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Mala-N contains how many tablets and of what type?","options":["21 tablets of estrogen + progesterone","21 hormonal + 7 iron tablets","28 progesterone-only tablets","21 iron tablets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mala-N = 21 tablets of estrogen + progesterone.  Why B wrong: The 21 + 7 (with iron) arrangement describes Mala-D, not Mala-N.  Why C wrong: Mala-N is a combined (estrogen + progesterone) pill, not progesterone-only.  Why D wrong: They are hormonal tablets, not iron tablets."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"How is Mala-N supplied in India?","options":["Sold at low cost under social marketing","Free distribution by Government of India","Only available on private prescription","Available only for lactating mothers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mala-N has no cost and is under free distribution by Government of India.  Why A wrong: Sold-at-low-cost social marketing describes Mala-D.  Why C wrong: It is a government free-supply product, not private prescription only.  Why D wrong: There is no such restriction to lactating mothers."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"How do the compositions of Mala-D and Mala-N compare?","options":["Mala-D has more estrogen","Mala-N has more progesterone","Both have the same composition","Mala-N contains no progesterone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Both Mala-D and Mala-N have the same composition; the difference is only distribution (low cost vs free) and iron tablets in Mala-D.  Why A/B wrong: Neither has higher hormone content than the other — composition is identical.  Why D wrong: Both are combined pills containing progesterone."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"What is the estrogen component and dose in Mala-D / Mala-N?","options":["Ethinyl estradiol 30 mcg (0.03 mg)","Mestranol 50 mcg","Ethinyl estradiol 50 mcg (0.05 mg)","Estradiol valerate 2 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: E = Ethinyl estradiol = 30 mcg (0.03 mg).  Why B wrong: The estrogen is ethinyl estradiol, not mestranol.  Why C wrong: The dose is 30 mcg, not 50 mcg.  Why D wrong: Estradiol valerate 2 mg is not the stated component."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"What is the progesterone component and dose in Mala-D / Mala-N?","options":["Levonorgestrel = 0.15 mg","Desogestrel = 0.075 mg","Norethisterone = 0.35 mg","Levonorgestrel = 1.5 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: P = Levonorgestrel = 0.15 mg.  Why B wrong: Desogestrel is the progestin in Cerazette, not in Mala-D/N.  Why C wrong: Norethisterone is not the stated component.  Why D wrong: 1.5 mg levonorgestrel is the emergency-contraception dose, not the 0.15 mg daily combined-pill dose."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Through which two hormonal effects do combined pills (estrogen + progesterone) primarily prevent pregnancy?","options":["−ve FSH and −ve LH leading to inhibition of ovulation","+ve FSH and +ve LH stimulating ovulation","Increasing GnRH secretion","Thickening the endometrium only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Estrogen + progesterone produce negative feedback (−ve FSH and −ve LH), which inhibits ovulation / causes anovulation.  Why B wrong: The pills suppress, not stimulate, FSH and LH.  Why C wrong: They suppress the pituitary/hypothalamic axis rather than increasing GnRH.  Why D wrong: The primary mechanism shown is suppression of ovulation, not endometrial thickening."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"The suppression of ovulation by OCP is best described as which state?","options":["Superovulation","Anovulation","Hyperovulation","Luteinization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inhibition of ovulation = anovulation, the mechanism of the combined pill.  Why A/C wrong: OCPs prevent, not increase, ovulation.  Why D wrong: Luteinization is corpus luteum formation, not the contraceptive mechanism described."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Overall, OCP has what effect on the body's natural estrogen and progesterone?","options":["Increases them","Decreases them","Has no effect","Only increases estrogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"OCP = ↓ Body's natural estrogen and progesterone\" — the pills suppress endogenous hormone production.  Why A wrong: Natural hormone levels fall, not rise.  Why C wrong: There is a definite suppressive effect on natural hormones.  Why D wrong: Both estrogen and progesterone decrease, not just estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"According to the classification of combined pills, \"high dose pills\" contain estrogen at what level?","options":["Estrogen ≥ 50 mcg","Estrogen < 50 mcg","Estrogen < 20 mcg","Estrogen 30–35 mcg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: High dose pills = estrogen ≥ 50 mcg.  Why B wrong: Estrogen < 50 mcg defines low dose pills.  Why C wrong: < 20 mcg defines very low dose pills.  Why D wrong: 30–35 mcg is the range for low dose pills (Mala-D/Mala-N)."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the classification of combined pills, low dose pills contain estrogen at what level, and which brands are examples?","options":["< 50 mcg (30–35 mcg); Mala-D and Mala-N","≥ 50 mcg; Mala-D and Mala-N","< 20 mcg; Cerazette","< 50 mcg; Antara only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low dose pills = estrogen < 50 mcg (30–35 mcg); Mala-D and Mala-N are examples.  Why B wrong: ≥ 50 mcg defines high dose pills.  Why C wrong: < 20 mcg is very low dose; Cerazette is a progesterone-only pill.  Why D wrong: Antara is a progesterone injection, not a low-dose combined pill."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"\"Very low dose\" combined pills contain estrogen at what level?","options":["Estrogen < 20 mcg","Estrogen < 50 mcg","Estrogen ≥ 50 mcg","Estrogen 30–35 mcg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Very low dose pills = estrogen < 20 mcg.  Why B wrong: < 50 mcg is the low dose category.  Why C wrong: ≥ 50 mcg is the high dose category.  Why D wrong: 30–35 mcg falls in the low dose category."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"How do OCP pills affect the body's natural hormones (LH, FSH, estrogen, progesterone)?","options":["All are increased","All are decreased","Only LH and FSH decrease","Only estrogen and progesterone increase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OCP pills affect natural hormones — Natural LH ↓, Natural FSH ↓, Natural Estrogen ↓, Natural Progesterone ↓ (all decreased).  Why A wrong: They are all suppressed, not increased.  Why C wrong: Estrogen and progesterone also decrease, not only the gonadotropins.  Why D wrong: Estrogen and progesterone decrease, not increase."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Because OCP decreases the body's overall estrogen level, it reduces the risk/incidence of all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Fibroid","Endometriosis","Cancer of cervix","Ovarian cancer"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: OCP is associated with an INCREASED risk of cancer cervix (side-effect table), so it is not among the conditions it decreases.  Why A wrong: OCP decreases fibroid.  Why B wrong: OCP decreases endometriosis.  Why D wrong: OCP decreases ovarian cancer."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"OCP use is associated with a decreased risk of which of the following (SATA)?","options":["Fibroid","Endometriosis","Ovarian cyst","Endometrial cancer","Colon cancer"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Because OCP decreases the body's natural estrogen, it decreases fibroid, endometriosis, ovarian cyst, endometrial cancer, ovarian cancer, colon cancer, and benign breast disease — all listed options are decreased.  Why not exclude any: All five options are among the conditions the source lists as decreased by OCP."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"OCP decreases the incidence of which type of breast condition?","options":["Benign breast diseases","Malignant breast cancer definitively","Fibroadenoma malignancy","Inflammatory breast cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"↓ Benign breast diseases\" among the effects of OCP-induced estrogen reduction.  Why B wrong: The effect on breast cancer risk is listed as \"±\" (uncertain), not a definite decrease.  Why C/D wrong: These specific malignant conditions are not listed as decreased by OCP."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Due to its anovulatory effect, OCP is therapeutically used for which conditions?","options":["Treating dysmenorrhea and irregular cycles","Treating infertility","Inducing ovulation","Treating menopausal osteoporosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Due to anovulation = used in treating dysmenorrhea, used in treating irregular cycles.\"  Why B wrong: OCP suppresses ovulation, so it is not used to treat infertility.  Why C wrong: OCP inhibits, not induces, ovulation.  Why D wrong: Menopausal osteoporosis is not the stated therapeutic use here."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"When should a woman start taking the OCP in her menstrual cycle?","options":["Day 1 of the cycle","Day 5 of the cycle","Day 14 of the cycle","Any random day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"How to take OCP: Day 1 of cycle start.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies Day 1 of the cycle as the start day."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"According to the OCP regimen described, how many pills are taken and for how long?","options":["1 pill everyday, 21 pills, taken for 21 days (3 weeks)","1 pill everyday, 28 pills, taken for 28 days","2 pills daily for 21 days","1 pill on alternate days for 6 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Everyday = 1 pill → 21 pills → take till 21 days (3 weeks).  Why B wrong: The active regimen described is 21 days (3 weeks), not 28.  Why C wrong: It is 1 pill daily, not 2.  Why D wrong: It is taken daily for 21 days, not alternate days for 6 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"After completing the 21-day OCP pack, what is the typical schedule before restarting?","options":["Roughly 3 weeks on + 1 week off, then restart when period comes","Continuous with no break","2 weeks on + 2 weeks off","Restart immediately the next day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Then stop: Whenever period comes then start again; Roughly = 3 week on + 1 week off.\"  Why B wrong: There is a pill-free interval, not continuous use.  Why C wrong: The described cycle is 3 weeks on + 1 week off, not 2 + 2.  Why D wrong: There is a one-week break before restarting."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"If a woman forgets ONE OCP pill, what should she do?","options":["Take 2 pills the next day at the same time","Stop the pack completely","Take 3 pills immediately","Wait until the next period to restart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 1 pill forgotten → take 2 pills the next day at the same time.  Why B wrong: The pack is continued, not stopped.  Why C wrong: The instruction is 2 pills, not 3.  Why D wrong: She does not wait for the next period."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"If a woman forgets 2 OR MORE OCP pills, the correct advice is:","options":["Take 2 pills the next day AND use a barrier method also for 1 week","Take 2 pills the next day only, no extra precaution","Discontinue the pack for that month","Take one extra pill only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For ≥ 2 pills forgotten → take 2 pills next day + for 1 week use barrier method also.  Why B wrong: An additional barrier method for 1 week is required with ≥ 2 missed pills.  Why C wrong: The pack is continued, not discontinued.  Why D wrong: Two pills are taken plus barrier backup, not just one extra pill."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Under the \"Metabolic\" side effects of OCP, weight gain is linked with which two associated effects?","options":["Abnormal lipid profile and depression","Anemia and infertility","Hyperkalemia and osteoporosis","Hypoglycemia and jaundice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The metabolic side-effect diagram shows Weight gain branching to \"Lipid profile abnormal\" and \"Depression.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the associations shown in the metabolic side-effect flow chart."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Which of the following are listed as metabolic side effects of OCP (SATA)?","options":["Weight gain","Blood pressure ↑","Cholestasis → gall stone ↑","Liver disease ↑","Increased fertility"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolic side effects listed are weight gain (→ abnormal lipid profile, depression), blood pressure ↑, cholestasis → gall stone ↑, and liver disease ↑.  Why E wrong: Increased fertility is not a metabolic side effect; OCP is a contraceptive."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"OCP-induced cholestasis increases the risk of which condition?","options":["Gall stones","Kidney stones","Peptic ulcer","Pancreatic pseudocyst"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Cholestasis → Gall stone ↑\" is listed among metabolic side effects.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cholestasis-related complication described."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Regarding the thrombotic side effects of OCP, what is the correct sequence?","options":["OCP ↑ clotting factor → thrombosis → ↑ risk of MI/stroke","OCP ↓ clotting factor → bleeding → ↓ risk of MI","OCP has no effect on clotting factors","OCP ↑ clotting factor → decreased stroke risk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: OCP ↑ clotting factor → thrombosis → MI/stroke risk ↑.  Why B wrong: OCP increases (not decreases) clotting factors, raising thrombotic risk.  Why C wrong: There is a definite effect increasing clotting factors.  Why D wrong: Stroke/MI risk increases, not decreases."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Which cancer risk is INCREASED with OCP use?","options":["Cancer of cervix","Ovarian cancer","Endometrial cancer","Colon cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the cancer column, \"Cancer cervix ↑\" is increased with OCP.  Why B/C/D wrong: Ovarian, endometrial, and colon cancers are decreased by OCP."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the OCP side-effect table, the risk of breast cancer and liver cancer is indicated as:","options":["Definitely increased","Definitely decreased","Uncertain / equivocal (±)","Not affected at all"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The cancer column marks \"Breast cancer ±\" and \"Liver cancer ±\" — equivocal/uncertain risk.  Why A wrong: They are marked ± (uncertain), not a definite increase.  Why B wrong: Only cervix is clearly ↑; breast and liver are ±.  Why D wrong: The ± sign indicates a possible effect, not none."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the contraindications mnemonic for OCP (\"Banks Have Various Schemes To Provide Home Loans During May\"), \"Banks\" stands for which contraindication?","options":["Breast cancer","Blood pressure","Bone loss","Bleeding disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Banks = Breast cancer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic expansion for \"Banks.\""},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the OCP contraindication mnemonic, \"Have\" stands for:","options":["Hypertriglyceridemia (hyper triglyceride)","Hyperthyroidism","Headache","Heart block"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Have = Hyper triglyceride (hypertriglyceridemia).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic expansion for \"Have.\""},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the OCP contraindication mnemonic, \"Various\" refers to which condition?","options":["Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding","Varicose veins","Vaginal candidiasis","Vitamin deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Various = Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic expansion for \"Various.\""},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"According to the OCP contraindication mnemonic, at what age is a smoker contraindicated for OCP?","options":["Smoker ≥ 35 years","Smoker ≥ 25 years","Smoker ≥ 45 years","Any smoker regardless of age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Schemes = Smoker ≥ age 35 years is a contraindication.  Why B/C wrong: The stated cut-off is 35 years, not 25 or 45.  Why D wrong: The contraindication is specifically for smokers ≥ 35 years."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the OCP contraindication mnemonic, \"To\" stands for a history of what?","options":["History of thrombosis (MI, stroke)","History of tuberculosis","History of thyroid disease","History of tonsillitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To = History of thrombosis (MI, stroke).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic expansion for \"To.\""},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Which physiological state is an absolute contraindication to OCP in the mnemonic (\"Provide\")?","options":["Pregnancy","Puberty","Perimenopause","Postpartum breastfeeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Provide = Pregnancy is a contraindication.  Why B/C wrong: Puberty and perimenopause are not listed as contraindications here.  Why D wrong: Postpartum breastfeeding relates to progesterone-only methods; the mnemonic item is Pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the OCP contraindication mnemonic, hypertension is a contraindication at what blood-pressure threshold?","options":["Hypertension > 160/110 mm Hg","Hypertension > 140/90 mm Hg","Hypertension > 120/80 mm Hg","Hypertension > 150/100 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Home = Hypertension (> 160/110 mm Hg).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the threshold as > 160/110 mm Hg."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the OCP contraindication mnemonic, \"Loams\" stands for:","options":["Liver disease","Lupus","Low platelets","Lymphoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Loams = Liver disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic expansion for \"Loams.\""},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the OCP contraindication mnemonic, \"During\" refers to which diabetic condition?","options":["Diabetes with vasculopathy","Gestational diabetes","Well-controlled type 2 diabetes","Diabetes insipidus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During = Diabetes with vasculopathy.  Why B/C wrong: The contraindication specified is diabetes with vasculopathy, not uncomplicated or gestational diabetes.  Why D wrong: Diabetes insipidus is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the OCP contraindication mnemonic, \"May\" refers to which type of migraine?","options":["Migraine with aura","Migraine without aura","Tension headache","Cluster headache"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: May = Migraine with aura is a contraindication.  Why B wrong: The specific contraindication is migraine WITH aura.  Why C/D wrong: Tension and cluster headaches are not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to OCP use (SATA)?","options":["Breast cancer","Smoker ≥ 35 years","Migraine with aura","Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding","Diabetes with vasculopathy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are in the OCP contraindication mnemonic — breast cancer, smoker ≥35 yrs, migraine with aura, undiagnosed vaginal bleeding, and diabetes with vasculopathy.  Why not exclude any: Each option maps to a mnemonic line (Banks, Schemes, May, Various, During)."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Which of the following is a category of progesterone-only contraceptive?","options":["POP (progesterone only pills)","Combined oral pill","Copper-T IUCD","Condom"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: POP (progesterone only pills) is one of the three progesterone-only contraceptive types (POP, injection, implant).  Why B wrong: Combined oral pill contains estrogen + progesterone, not progesterone only.  Why C wrong: Copper-T is a non-hormonal IUCD.  Why D wrong: Condom is a barrier method, not hormonal."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"The three forms of progesterone-only contraceptives shown are:","options":["Pills (POP), Injection (Depo-Provera), and Implant (Implanon)","Combined pill, patch, and ring","Copper-T, condom, and diaphragm","Estrogen pill, injection, and IUD"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart lists POP's (progesterone only pills), Injection → Depo-Provera, and Implant → Implanon.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the three progesterone-only forms shown."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"What is the primary mechanism of action of progesterone-only pills (POP)?","options":["Thickening of cervical mucus","Inhibition of ovulation only","Endometrial thinning only","Suppression of FSH surge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mechanism of action of POP is that it makes cervical mucus thick.  Why B wrong: Ovulation inhibition is more characteristic of the injection/implant (anovulation), whereas POP works mainly via thick cervical mucus.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the mechanism given for POP."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"The progesterone-only injection Depo-Provera contains which drug?","options":["Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA)","Levonorgestrel","Desogestrel","Etonogestrel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Injection → Depo-Provera → Inj. Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA).  Why B wrong: Levonorgestrel is the progestin in Mala-D/N combined pills.  Why C wrong: Desogestrel is the drug in Cerazette (POP), not the injection.  Why D wrong: Etonogestrel is not the drug named for this injection."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"The DMPA injection included in India's National Family Planning Programme is marketed as:","options":["ANTARA","Mala-N","Cerazette","Implanon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Inj. DMPA (included in national family planning programme) → ANTARA.  Why B wrong: Mala-N is a combined oral pill.  Why C wrong: Cerazette is the desogestrel POP.  Why D wrong: Implanon is the implant, not the DMPA injection."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"What is the overall mechanism by which the progesterone injection and implant primarily prevent pregnancy?","options":["Anovulation","Increasing menstrual flow","Fertilization enhancement","Increasing LH surge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The injection and implant group act via anovulation (as bracketed under the flow chart).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are contrary to a contraceptive mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"What is the MOST common side effect of progesterone-only contraceptives?","options":["Irregular bleeding","Weight loss","Hypertension","Deep vein thrombosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Most common side effect: Irregular bleeding.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the most common side effect listed for progesterone-only methods."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"What effect do all progesterone-only contraceptives have on the risk of pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)?","options":["Decrease chances of PID","Increase chances of PID","No effect on PID","Cause PID directly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"All of them: Decrease chances of PID.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states they decrease PID risk (via thick cervical mucus barrier)."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Regarding use in lactating (breastfeeding) women, progesterone-only contraceptives are:","options":["Safe — all can be used in lactating females","Contraindicated in lactation","Only the implant can be used","Only usable after weaning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"All of them: Can be used in lactating female\" — progesterone-only methods are safe in breastfeeding.  Why B wrong: They are specifically suitable during lactation (unlike estrogen-containing pills).  Why C wrong: All forms, not only the implant, can be used.  Why D wrong: They can be used during lactation, not only after weaning."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Can progesterone-only contraceptives (POP, injection, implant) be used as emergency contraception?","options":["No — none of them are used as emergency contraceptives","Yes — all of them","Only the injection","Only the implant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"None of them: Used as an emergency contraceptive.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The source clearly states none of these routine progesterone-only methods serve as emergency contraception."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the mnemonic for contraindications of ALL progesterone contraceptives (\"Virat Likes To Play (with) Ball\"), \"Virat\" stands for:","options":["Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding","Varicose veins","Viral hepatitis","Vomiting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Virat = Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic expansion for \"Virat.\""},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the progesterone-contraceptive contraindication mnemonic, \"Likes\" stands for:","options":["Liver disease","Lymphoma","Low BP","Lupus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Likes = Liver disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic expansion for \"Likes.\""},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the progesterone-contraceptive contraindication mnemonic, \"To\" stands for:","options":["Thrombosis","Thyroid disease","Tuberculosis","Tonsillitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To = Thrombosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic expansion for \"To.\""},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the progesterone-contraceptive contraindication mnemonic, \"Play (with)\" stands for:","options":["Pregnancy","Puberty","Palpitation","Pneumonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Play (with) = Pregnancy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic expansion for \"Play.\""},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In the progesterone-contraceptive contraindication mnemonic, \"Ball\" stands for:","options":["Breast cancer","Bleeding disorder","Blood pressure","Bone loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ball = Breast cancer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mnemonic expansion for \"Ball.\""},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to ALL progesterone-only contraceptives (SATA)?","options":["Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding","Liver disease","Thrombosis","Pregnancy","Breast cancer"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic \"Virat Likes To Play (with) Ball\" = Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding, Liver disease, Thrombosis, Pregnancy, Breast cancer.  Why not exclude any: All five are explicitly listed contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Antara (DMPA) injection is given at what dose?","options":["150 mg","100 mg","50 mg","300 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antara → Injection 150 mg.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated dose is 150 mg."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"By which route is the Antara (DMPA) injection administered?","options":["Intramuscular (i/m)","Intravenous","Subcutaneous only","Intradermal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antara is given i/m (intramuscular); the vial states \"For deep intramuscular use only.\"  Why B/D wrong: It is not given IV or intradermally.  Why C wrong: The label specifies deep intramuscular use."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"How frequently is the Antara (DMPA) injection given?","options":["Every 3 months","Every month","Every 6 months","Once a year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antara injection is given every 3 months.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated interval is every 3 months (quarterly)."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"The Antara (Medroxyprogesterone) contraceptive injection is supplied at what concentration and volume?","options":["150 mg/ml, sterile aqueous suspension 1 ml, single use only","100 mg/ml, 2 ml multi-dose vial","50 mg/ml, 5 ml vial","150 mg/ml, oral suspension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vial reads \"Medroxyprogesterone Injection I.P. 150 mg/ml, Sterile Aqueous Suspension 1 ml, Single Use Only, for deep intramuscular use.\"  Why B/C wrong: The stated concentration is 150 mg/ml in 1 ml.  Why D wrong: It is an injectable suspension, not oral."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Which are the TWO advantages of progesterone-only contraceptives listed?","options":["Decrease seizure frequency (good in epileptic patients) and good in sickle cell anemia","Prevent osteoporosis and increase fertility","Reduce blood pressure and prevent thrombosis","Reverse infertility and increase bone density"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 2 Advantages = ↓ decrease seizure frequency (very good in epileptic patients) and very good in sickle cell anemia.  Why B wrong: A disadvantage is bone loss/osteoporosis and delayed return of fertility, opposite to option B.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the listed advantages."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Progesterone-only contraceptives are especially useful in epileptic patients because they:","options":["Decrease the frequency of seizures","Increase seizure threshold drugs","Cause sedation","Enhance anticonvulsant metabolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: They decrease seizure frequency, therefore are very good in epileptic patients.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mechanism/benefit stated."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Progesterone-only contraceptives are considered very good in patients with which hematological condition?","options":["Sickle cell anemia","Thalassemia major","Hemophilia","Iron deficiency anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Very good in sickle cell anemia\" is a listed advantage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These specific conditions are not the stated advantage."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Which are the TWO disadvantages of progesterone-only contraceptives listed?","options":["Delayed return of fertility and bone loss → osteoporosis","Weight loss and hair fall","Increased seizures and hypertension","Nausea and vomiting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 2 Disadvantages = (1) Delayed return of fertility and (2) Bone loss → osteoporosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the disadvantages listed."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"A recognised disadvantage of progesterone-only injectable contraceptives is bone loss leading to:","options":["Osteoporosis","Osteomalacia","Rickets","Paget's disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Bone loss → osteoporosis\" is a listed disadvantage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These bone disorders are not the stated complication."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"In India, which progesterone-only pill (POP) is available?","options":["Cerazette","Mala-D","Antara","Saheli"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"India: Progesterone only pill available: CERAZETTE.\"  Why B wrong: Mala-D is a combined oral pill.  Why C wrong: Antara is the DMPA injection, not a POP.  Why D wrong: Saheli (centchroman) is not the POP named here."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Cerazette contains which progestin?","options":["Desogestrel","Levonorgestrel","Medroxyprogesterone acetate","Norethisterone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cerazette → Desogestrel progesterone (75 microgram film-coated tablets).  Why B wrong: Levonorgestrel is in Mala-D/N combined pills.  Why C wrong: Medroxyprogesterone acetate is the injection (Antara/DMPA).  Why D wrong: Norethisterone is not the Cerazette progestin."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"What is the dosing schedule for Cerazette (desogestrel POP)?","options":["1 tablet/day for 21 days","1 tablet weekly","2 tablets/day for 21 days","1 tablet monthly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cerazette = 1 tab/day (for 21 days).  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated schedule is 1 tablet daily for 21 days."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Cerazette film-coated tablets are available in what strength?","options":["75 microgram","30 microgram","150 microgram","0.15 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Cerazette pack states \"75 microgram film-coated tablets, Desogestrel.\"  Why B wrong: 30 mcg is the ethinyl estradiol dose in Mala-D/N.  Why C wrong: 150 mg (not mcg) refers to the DMPA injection dose.  Why D wrong: 0.15 mg is the levonorgestrel dose in the combined pill."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"The progesterone-only implant referred to as \"Implanon\" consists of:","options":["A single 4 cm rod","Two rods","Six capsules","A T-shaped device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Implanon → single 4 cm rod.  Why B wrong: It is a single rod, not two.  Why C wrong: Six capsules describe Norplant, not Implanon.  Why D wrong: A T-shape describes an IUCD, not the implant."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"Where is the Implanon implant inserted?","options":["Subcutaneously into the medial side of the left arm","Intramuscularly into the deltoid","Into the uterine cavity","Under the skin of the abdomen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Implanon is inserted subcutaneously into the medial side of the left arm.  Why B wrong: It is subcutaneous, not intramuscular.  Why C wrong: The uterine cavity is for an IUCD, not the implant.  Why D wrong: The stated site is the medial side of the left arm, not the abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"The progesterone-only implant marketed in the flow chart as an implant option is called:","options":["Implanon (Impassions)","Copper-T","Depo-Provera","Cerazette"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Implant branch of the progesterone-only flow chart → Implanon (written \"Impassions\" in the source, the intended term being Implanon).  Why B wrong: Copper-T is a non-hormonal IUCD.  Why C wrong: Depo-Provera is the injection.  Why D wrong: Cerazette is the oral POP."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"A lactating mother needs contraception and also has a history of migraine with aura. Which method is MOST appropriate?","options":["A progesterone-only method (safe in lactation)","Combined oral pill (OCP)","High dose estrogen pill","Mala-N combined pill"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Progesterone-only contraceptives can be used in lactating females; migraine with aura is a contraindication to estrogen-containing OCP, so a progesterone-only method is appropriate.  Why B/C/D wrong: All contain estrogen and are contraindicated in migraine with aura (and less preferred in lactation)."},{"section":"Batch 087 · Oral Combined Pills, Mala-D & Mala-N…","question":"A woman on OCP reports she missed her pill yesterday (1 missed pill). What is the priority nursing instruction?","options":["Take 2 pills today at the same time and continue the pack","Stop the pack and wait for menses","Start emergency contraception immediately","Take 2 pills plus use a barrier method for a week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 1 missed pill, take 2 pills next day at the same time and continue.  Why B wrong: The pack is continued, not stopped.  Why C wrong: Emergency contraception is not required for a single missed pill per this protocol.  Why D wrong: The barrier-method-for-a-week rule applies to ≥ 2 missed pills, not one."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Centchroman is marketed in India under which brand names?","options":["Saheli / Chhaya","Mala-D / Mala-N","Mirena / Copper-T","Nirodh / Kamal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Centchroman = Saheli / Chhaya.  Why B wrong: Mala-D / Mala-N are combined oral contraceptive pills, not Centchroman.  Why C wrong: Mirena is a hormonal IUD and Copper-T is a copper IUD.  Why D wrong: Nirodh is a condom brand; Kamal is not the brand for Centchroman."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Centchroman has been included in which national programme?","options":["National Family Planning Programme","National AIDS Control Programme","National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme","National Nutrition Mission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Centchroman has been included in the national family planning programme.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated national programmes; Centchroman is a contraceptive included in the family planning programme."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Centchroman was developed by which Indian research institute?","options":["CDRI, Lucknow","AIIMS, Delhi","ICMR, Delhi","NIN, Hyderabad"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state development by CDRI (Central Drug Research Institute), Lucknow.  Why B/C/D wrong: These institutes are not credited with developing Centchroman in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which statement best describes the hormonal nature of Centchroman?","options":["It is non-steroidal","It is a progesterone-only steroid","It is a combined estrogen-progesterone steroid","It is an androgenic steroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Centchroman is non-steroidal.  Why B/C/D wrong: Centchroman is specifically described as non-steroidal, not any type of steroid."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"The active ingredient of Centchroman (Saheli/Chhaya) is:","options":["Ormeloxifene","Levonorgestrel","Ulipristal acetate","Mifepristone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Centchroman has ORMELOXIFENE (written \"ORMILOXIFENE\").  Why B wrong: Levonorgestrel is a progestin used in emergency contraception, not Centchroman.  Why C wrong: Ulipristal is a selective progesterone receptor modulator used as emergency contraceptive.  Why D wrong: Mifepristone is an antiprogestin used medically for emergency contraception."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"What is the dosing frequency of Centchroman (Saheli)?","options":["Once weekly tablet","Once daily tablet","Once monthly injection","Once every three months injection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Centchroman is a once weekly tablet.  Why B wrong: Daily dosing describes standard OCPs, not Centchroman.  Why C/D wrong: Centchroman is an oral tablet, not an injectable."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Emergency contraceptives differ from normal contraceptives because they are given:","options":["After unprotected intercourse","Only before intercourse","Only during menstruation","Only after confirmed pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state normally contraceptives are given before intercourse, but emergency contraceptives are given after unprotected intercourse.  Why B wrong: That describes normal (routine) contraceptives.  Why C/D wrong: Emergency contraception timing relates to unprotected intercourse, not menstruation or established pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Emergency contraceptives are also known as:","options":["Post-coital pills","Pre-coital pills","Mini pills","Progesterone-only pills"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The heading reads \"Emergency Contraceptive/Post Coital Pills.\"  Why B wrong: \"Pre-coital\" would mean before intercourse, which is not emergency contraception.  Why C/D wrong: Mini pills / progesterone-only pills are routine methods and are explicitly NOT used as emergency contraceptives."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"What is the best (ideal) time window to give an emergency contraceptive after unprotected intercourse?","options":["Within 72 hours (3 days)","Within 12 hours","Within 7 days","Within 24 hours only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best time to give emergency contraceptive is within 72 hours (3 days).  Why B/D wrong: The stated ideal window is 72 hours, not 12 or only 24 hours.  Why C wrong: 7 days exceeds the maximum window of 5 days (120 hours)."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Up to what maximum time can an emergency contraceptive be given after unprotected intercourse?","options":["Until 5 days (120 hours)","Until 3 days (72 hours)","Until 7 days (168 hours)","Until 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state it can be given until 5 days (120 hours).  Why B wrong: 72 hours is the ideal/best time, not the maximum.  Why C/D wrong: The stated maximum is 120 hours (5 days), not 7 days or 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which is the BEST (most effective) emergency contraceptive method overall?","options":["Cu-T (copper IUD)","Levonorgestrel","Ulipristal","Mifepristone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Cu-T is the chief/best emergency contraceptive (\"Best emergency contraceptive = Cu-T\").  Why B wrong: Levonorgestrel is the most commonly used and second-best hormonal option, not the overall best.  Why C wrong: Ulipristal is the best hormonal option but Cu-T is best overall.  Why D wrong: Mifepristone is a medical option, not the best overall."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which is the BEST hormonal emergency contraceptive?","options":["Ulipristal","Levonorgestrel","Cu-T","Mifepristone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the best hormonal emergency contraceptive = ulipristal.  Why B wrong: Levonorgestrel is the second-best hormonal emergency contraceptive.  Why C wrong: Cu-T is not hormonal; it is the best overall.  Why D wrong: Mifepristone is not named as the best hormonal option in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which is the SECOND-best hormonal emergency contraceptive?","options":["Levonorgestrel","Ulipristal","Mifepristone","Cu-T"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state second-best hormonal emergency contraceptive = levonorgestrel.  Why B wrong: Ulipristal is the best hormonal option.  Why C wrong: Mifepristone is not named as the second best.  Why D wrong: Cu-T is not hormonal."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which is the MOST COMMONLY USED emergency contraceptive?","options":["Levonorgestrel","Ulipristal","Cu-T","Mifepristone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most commonly used emergency contraceptive = levonorgestrel.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are effective/best options but levonorgestrel is the most commonly used."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"The emergency contraceptive distributed free of cost by the Government of India is:","options":["Levonorgestrel E-pills","Ulipristal pills","Mifepristone","Cu-T"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the emergency contraceptive distributed free of cost by the Government of India = levonorgestrel E-pills.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the free-of-cost government-distributed emergency contraceptive."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In the emergency contraception methods, the medical (drug) option listed is:","options":["Mifepristone","Ormeloxifene","Oxytocin","Ergometrine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under \"Methods of emergency contraception,\" Medical → Mifepristone is listed.  Why B wrong: Ormeloxifene is Centchroman, a weekly contraceptive.  Why C/D wrong: Oxytocin and ergometrine are uterotonics, not emergency contraceptives."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"The \"Yuzpe method\" of emergency contraception uses which class of pills?","options":["OCP (combined oral contraceptive pills)","Progesterone-only mini pills","Ulipristal only","Mifepristone only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state OCP = Yuzpe method (written \"yurpee method\").  Why B wrong: Mini pills are progesterone-only and are not used as emergency contraceptives.  Why C/D wrong: The Yuzpe regimen uses combined OCPs, not ulipristal or mifepristone."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which of the following is NOT used as an emergency contraceptive?","options":["Progesterone-only pill / mini pill","Levonorgestrel","Ulipristal","Cu-T"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly list \"Progesterone only pill/mini pill\" as NOT used as emergency contraceptive.  Why B/C/D wrong: Levonorgestrel, ulipristal, and Cu-T are all listed as emergency contraceptive options."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which of the following is NOT used as an emergency contraceptive? (SATA)","options":["Progesterone-only pill / mini pill","Misoprost","Mirena","Levonorgestrel","Cu-T"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Progesterone-only pill/mini pill, Misoprost, and Mirena as NOT used as emergency contraceptives.  Why D wrong: Levonorgestrel is the most commonly used emergency contraceptive.  Why E wrong: Cu-T is the best emergency contraceptive."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Permanent (irreversible) methods of contraception in females and males respectively are:","options":["Tubectomy and Vasectomy","IUCD and Condom","OCP and Withdrawal","DMPA and Diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart lists Female → Tubectomy and Male → Vasectomy under permanent/irreversible methods.  Why B/C/D wrong: IUCD, condoms, OCP, withdrawal, DMPA, and diaphragms are all temporary/reversible methods."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Vasectomy involves cutting which structure?","options":["Vas deferens","Fallopian tube","Epididymis","Urethra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Vasectomy = cut the vas deferens.  Why B wrong: The fallopian tube is cut/ligated in tubectomy (female).  Why C/D wrong: The epididymis and urethra are not cut in vasectomy."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"A scalpel-free technique for male sterilization is called:","options":["Non-scalpel vasectomy","Pomeroy technique","Minilaparotomy","Essure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Non scalpel vasectomy\" as a vasectomy technique.  Why B/C wrong: Pomeroy technique and minilaparotomy are female tubal ligation methods.  Why D wrong: Essure is a female hysteroscopic sterilization device."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"After a vasectomy, another contraceptive method must be used for how long / how many ejaculates?","options":["3 months / ~9 weeks / 30 ejaculates","1 month / 4 weeks / 10 ejaculates","6 months / 24 weeks / 50 ejaculates","2 weeks / 5 ejaculates"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"For 3 months / 9 weeks / 30 ejaculates after vasectomy, use some other method of contraceptive.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations/counts do not match the stated 3 months / 9 weeks / 30 ejaculates."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"What confirms that no further contraception is needed after vasectomy?","options":["Semen analysis showing no sperms (azoospermia)","A negative urine pregnancy test in the partner","Resolution of scrotal swelling","Return of normal libido"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows: do semen analysis → no sperms → no contraceptive needed.  Why B wrong: A partner's pregnancy test does not confirm vasectomy success.  Why C/D wrong: Swelling resolution and libido are unrelated to confirming azoospermia."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In female tubectomy, at which part of the fallopian tube is the fallope ring applied?","options":["Isthmus","Ampulla","Fimbria","Infundibulum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the fallope ring is applied in the isthmus of the fallopian tube (\"Part of tube ligated → Isthmus\").  Why B wrong: The ampulla is the widest, distal part and is not the ligation site.  Why C/D wrong: The fimbria and infundibulum are the distal funnel-shaped portions, not the ligation site."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"The most common method used for tubal ligation is:","options":["Laparoscopic tubal ligation","Minilaparotomy","Essure","Pomeroy via open laparotomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Which method is most common for tubal ligation = laparoscopic tubal ligation.\"  Why B wrong: Minilaparotomy is used mainly in postpartum females.  Why C wrong: Essure is a hysteroscopic device, not the most common tubal ligation.  Why D wrong: The Pomeroy technique is used with minilaparotomy, not the most common overall."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which sterilization method is preferred in postpartum females?","options":["Minilaparotomy (Pomeroy method)","Laparoscopic tubal ligation","Essure","Non-scalpel vasectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Which method can be used in post partum females: Minilaparotomy (Pomeroy method).\"  Why B wrong: Laparoscopic tubal ligation is contraindicated in postpartum delivery.  Why C wrong: Essure is a hysteroscopic method, not used postpartum.  Why D wrong: Vasectomy is a male procedure."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"The technique used for tubal ligation via minilaparotomy is the:","options":["Pomeroy technique","Irving technique","Uchida technique","Madlener technique"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes repeatedly state the method used in minilaparotomy = Pomeroy technique.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other tubal ligation techniques but are not the one named in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"What is the best time to perform tubal ligation after delivery (postpartum)?","options":["After 48 hours of delivery and until 1 week of delivery","Immediately within the first hour of delivery","After 6 weeks of delivery","After 3 months of delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state best time to do tubal ligation after delivery (postpartum) = after 48 hours of delivery and until 1 week of delivery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 48 hours to 1 week postpartum window."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"A laparoscopic tubal ring procedure is performed using which applicator?","options":["Fallope ring applicator","Filshie clip applicator","Essure delivery catheter","Hulka clip applicator"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state it is done using a fallope ring applicator with a positive fallope ring.  Why B/D wrong: Filshie and Hulka clips are other devices but not named here.  Why C wrong: The Essure catheter is used for hysteroscopic sterilization, not the tubal ring."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"During a laparoscopic tubal ring procedure, a loop is made in which part of the tube and the fallope ring is applied?","options":["Isthmus of the tube","Ampulla of the tube","Fimbrial end","Cornual end"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state a loop is made in the isthmus of the tube and the fallope ring is applied.  Why B/C/D wrong: The loop and ring are applied at the isthmus, not the ampulla, fimbria, or cornua."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which gas is used to create pneumoperitoneum during laparoscopy?","options":["Carbon dioxide (CO2)","Oxygen (O2)","Nitrous oxide (N2O)","Room air"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state gas used to create pneumoperitoneum = CO2.  Why B/C/D wrong: CO2 is used because it is rapidly absorbed and non-combustible; oxygen, nitrous oxide, and room air are not the standard gas."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"What is the maximum volume of CO2 that can be used during laparoscopy?","options":["2 L","1 L","5 L","500 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state maximum volume of CO2 that can be used = 2 L.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated maximum of 2 L."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"What is the recommended intra-abdominal pressure during laparoscopy?","options":["8–12 mm Hg (maximum 15 mm Hg)","15–20 mm Hg (maximum 25 mm Hg)","2–5 mm Hg (maximum 8 mm Hg)","20–30 mm Hg (maximum 40 mm Hg)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state pressure during laparoscopy = 8–12 mm Hg (maximum = 15 mm Hg).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 8–12 mm Hg range or 15 mm Hg maximum."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Laparoscopy is CONTRAINDICATED in which of the following patients? (SATA)","options":["Heart disease patients","COPD patients","Postpartum tubal ligation candidates (for laparoscopic tubal ligation)","Healthy nulliparous women","Young, newly married couples"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list heart disease and COPD as contraindications of laparoscopy, and state laparoscopic tubal ligation is NOT done in postpartum delivery.  Why D wrong: Healthy nulliparous women are not listed as a contraindication.  Why E wrong: Marital status is not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Laparoscopic tubal ligation is contraindicated in which situation?","options":["Immediately post-partum (recently delivered)","In a woman who has not delivered recently","In a woman using OCP","In a woman with a history of appendectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state contraindication of laparoscopic tubal ligation = not done in postpartum delivery.  Why B wrong: A woman who has not delivered recently is a suitable candidate.  Why C/D wrong: OCP use and prior appendectomy are not listed as contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In a female who has NOT delivered recently, tubal ligation by minilaparotomy is done through an incision of what size and location?","options":["2–3 cm incision above the pubic symphysis","5 cm incision at the umbilicus","10 cm midline laparotomy","2 cm incision below the umbilicus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in a female who has not delivered recently, a 2–3 cm incision is made above the pubic symphysis.  Why B/C wrong: These incisions are too large / mislocated.  Why D wrong: A 2 cm below-umbilicus incision is used in recently-delivered (postpartum) females, not non-postpartum."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In a female who has RECENTLY delivered (postpartum), the minilaparotomy incision for tubal ligation is placed:","options":["2 cm below the umbilicus","2–3 cm above the pubic symphysis","At the level of the umbilicus","In the right iliac fossa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state in a recently delivered female, the incision is given 2 cm below the umbilicus (because the postpartum fundus is high).  Why B wrong: The above-pubic-symphysis incision is for non-postpartum females.  Why C/D wrong: Neither the umbilical level nor the right iliac fossa is described."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In the Pomeroy technique, what is done at the base of the loop of the tube?","options":["Tie a catgut at the base","Apply a metal clip at the base","Cauterize the base electrically","Leave the base intact"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the Pomeroy steps: make a loop in isthmus of tube, tie a catgut at the base, then cut the loop.  Why B/C wrong: The notes specify a catgut ligature, not a clip or electrocautery.  Why D wrong: The base is tied, not left intact."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Arrange the correct sequence of Pomeroy technique steps.","options":["Make a loop in isthmus → tie catgut at base → cut the loop","Cut the tube → tie catgut → make a loop","Tie catgut → cut the loop → make a loop","Make a loop in ampulla → cauterize → excise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list, in order: make a loop (isthmus of tube), tie a catgut at the base, cut the loop.  Why B/C wrong: These reorder the steps incorrectly.  Why D wrong: The loop is made in the isthmus, and catgut ligation (not cauterization) is used."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which statement about minilaparotomy is correct?","options":["It can be done in the postpartum period","It is contraindicated in the postpartum period","It always requires general anaesthesia and pneumoperitoneum","It uses CO2 insufflation like laparoscopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state \"Minilaparotomy can be done in post partum period.\"  Why B wrong: It is actually indicated (not contraindicated) in the postpartum period, unlike laparoscopic tubal ligation.  Why C/D wrong: Pneumoperitoneum and CO2 insufflation are features of laparoscopy, not minilaparotomy."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Essure, a device for female sterilization, is a coil made of which materials?","options":["Steel, nickel, and titanium","Copper and silver","Gold and platinum","Silicone and latex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Essure is a coil made of steel, nickel, and titanium.  Why B/C/D wrong: These material combinations do not match the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Where and how is the Essure coil inserted?","options":["Into the intramural part of the tube, hysteroscopically","Into the ampulla of the tube, laparoscopically","Into the uterine cavity, via laparotomy","Into the vas deferens, via scrotal incision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Essure is inserted into the intramural part of the tube hysteroscopically.  Why B wrong: It is placed in the intramural part hysteroscopically, not the ampulla laparoscopically.  Why C/D wrong: It is not placed in the uterine cavity via laparotomy nor in the vas deferens."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"How does the Essure device produce tubal occlusion?","options":["It acts like a foreign body causing inflammation → adhesions → blockage of the tube","It mechanically ties off the tube immediately","It releases progesterone locally","It cauterizes the tubal lining"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Essure acts like a foreign body → inflammation → adhesions → blockage of tube.  Why B wrong: Blockage develops gradually through inflammation, not immediate ligation.  Why C/D wrong: It does not release hormones or cauterize; it works via foreign-body reaction."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"After inserting Essure, the female is advised to use another contraceptive for how long?","options":["3 months","1 month","6 months","1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state after inserting Essure, advise the female to use some other contraceptive for 3 months.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated duration is 3 months."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"After the 3-month interval following Essure insertion, which test confirms that the tube is blocked?","options":["Hysterosalpingography (HSG)","Semen analysis","Pelvic ultrasound only","Serum beta-hCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state: then do hysterosalpingography and confirm that tube is blocked.  Why B wrong: Semen analysis confirms vasectomy success in males.  Why C/D wrong: Ultrasound and beta-hCG do not confirm tubal blockage in this protocol."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"After Essure insertion, once hysterosalpingography confirms tubal blockage, the nurse should advise the female to:","options":["Stop using any other contraceptive","Continue barrier contraception lifelong","Take OCPs daily","Repeat the HSG monthly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state: then ask female not to use any contraceptive (once blockage is confirmed).  Why B/C/D wrong: Once confirmed occluded, no further contraception is needed."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"For a young, newly married couple staying together, the best contraceptive is:","options":["OCP (oral contraceptive pills)","Barrier method","IUCD","Tubectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states for a young, newly married couple staying together → OCP.  Why B wrong: Barrier method is best for couples staying in two different cities.  Why C wrong: IUCD is best for a lactating female.  Why D wrong: Tubectomy is permanent and inappropriate for a young couple wanting future children."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"For a young married couple staying in two different cities (infrequent intercourse), the best contraceptive is:","options":["Barrier method","OCP","IUCD","DMPA injection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states for a young married couple staying in 2 different cities → barrier method (suited to infrequent, unpredictable intercourse).  Why B wrong: Daily OCPs are unnecessary when intercourse is infrequent.  Why C/D wrong: IUCD and DMPA are not the recommended option in this scenario."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"After evacuation of a molar pregnancy, the best contraceptive is:","options":["OCP","IUCD","Barrier method","Tubectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states after evacuation of molar pregnancy → OCP (IUCD is avoided due to perforation risk and interference with beta-hCG follow-up).  Why B wrong: IUCD is generally avoided after molar pregnancy evacuation.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the recommended option per the table."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In a lactating (breastfeeding) female, the BEST contraceptive is:","options":["IUCD","Progesterone-only pills","Combined OCP","DMPA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states in a lactating female → best: IUCD (second best: progesterone-only pills).  Why B wrong: Progesterone-only pills are the second best, not the best.  Why C wrong: Combined OCPs contain estrogen, which can reduce milk supply and are avoided during lactation.  Why D wrong: DMPA is not listed as the best option here."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In a lactating female, the SECOND-best contraceptive is:","options":["Progesterone-only pills","IUCD","Combined OCP","Barrier method"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states 2nd best in a lactating female = progesterone-only pills.  Why B wrong: IUCD is the best (first) choice.  Why C wrong: Combined OCPs (estrogen-containing) are avoided in lactation.  Why D wrong: Barrier is not named as the second best here."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In a female with heart disease, the best TEMPORARY contraceptive (new concept) is:","options":["IUCD","Combined OCP","DMPA","Barrier method"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states for a female with heart disease, temporary (new concept) = IUCD.  Why B wrong: Combined OCPs raise thromboembolic risk and are avoided in heart disease.  Why C/D wrong: The table specifies IUCD as the new-concept temporary option."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In a female with heart disease, the old-concept temporary contraceptive was:","options":["Barrier method","IUCD","Combined OCP","Injectable DMPA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states permanent/old concept for heart disease = barrier method (the older recommendation before IUCD).  Why B wrong: IUCD is the newer concept.  Why C/D wrong: Estrogen-containing and injectable methods are not the old-concept choice listed."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"For permanent contraception in a couple where the female has heart disease, the BEST option is:","options":["Vasectomy (male sterilization)","Tubal ligation (female sterilization)","Essure","Laparoscopic tubal ligation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states for a female with heart disease, permanent → best: vasectomy (safer, avoids putting the cardiac patient through surgery).  Why B/D wrong: Tubal ligation is chosen only if the husband refuses vasectomy.  Why C wrong: Essure is not named as the best permanent option here."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"If the husband refuses vasectomy in a couple where the female has heart disease, the permanent method chosen is:","options":["Tubal ligation (by minilaparotomy)","Laparoscopic tubal ligation","Essure","Copper-T"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states if husband refuses → tubal ligation, and the method used = minilaparotomy (laparoscopy is contraindicated in heart disease).  Why B wrong: Laparoscopy is contraindicated in heart disease patients.  Why C wrong: Essure is not named here.  Why D wrong: Copper-T is a temporary method."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"For a female with epilepsy or sickle cell anemia, the recommended contraceptive is:","options":["DMPA (depot medroxyprogesterone acetate)","Combined OCP","Copper IUCD","Barrier method"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states for a female with epilepsy and for a female with sickle cell anemia → DMPA (depot medroxyprogesterone acetate).  Why B wrong: Enzyme-inducing anti-epileptics reduce OCP efficacy, so DMPA is preferred.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the option named in the notes for these conditions."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"What does DMPA stand for?","options":["Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate","Depot methylprednisolone acetate","Depot medroxyprogesterone ascorbate","Dual medroxyprogesterone agent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes expand DMPA as depot medroxyprogesterone acetate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"What is the most common cancer in females in the world?","options":["Breast cancer","Cervical cancer","Ovarian cancer","Endometrial cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cancer in females in the world = breast cancer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are genital tract cancers but not the most common overall cancer in women worldwide."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"What is the most common cancer in females in India?","options":["Breast cancer","Cervical cancer","Ovarian cancer","Endometrial cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cancer in females in India = breast cancer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the single most common female cancer in India per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"What is the most common cancer of the female genital tract (overall / in the world)?","options":["Cancer of the cervix","Cancer of the endometrium","Cancer of the ovary","Cancer of the vagina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common cancer of the female genital tract = cancer cervix (and most common of the genital tract in the world = cancer cervix).  Why B wrong: Endometrial cancer is most common only in developed countries.  Why C/D wrong: Ovarian and vaginal cancers are not the most common genital tract cancers."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In developed countries, the most common cancer of the female genital tract is:","options":["Uterine cancer / endometrial cancer","Cervical cancer","Ovarian cancer","Vulvar cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state most common cancer of the female genital tract in developed countries = uterine cancer / cancer endometrium.  Why B wrong: Cervical cancer is most common worldwide but less so in developed countries due to screening.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the most common in developed countries."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which female genital tract cancer is most common in India (per the comparison table)?","options":["Cancer cervix","Cancer endometrium","Cancer ovary","Cancer vagina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table marks \"most common in India\" under Cancer cervix (with a tick).  Why B/C/D wrong: The tick for \"most common in India\" is placed under cervix, not endometrium, ovary, or vagina."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"The typical age group for endometrial (uterine) cancer is:","options":["55–70 years, mean 60 years (post-menopausal)","20–30 years","35–39 years","60–70 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states cancer endometrium age group = 55–70 years, mean age 60 years (post-menopausal).  Why B wrong: This is too young.  Why C wrong: 35–39 years is one of the cervical cancer age peaks.  Why D wrong: 60–70 years is the ovarian cancer age group."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"According to the table, the bimodal age peaks for cervical cancer are:","options":["35–39 years and 60–65 years","20–25 years and 40–45 years","55–70 years and 75–80 years","60–70 years only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows cervical cancer age = 35–39 years and 60–65 years.  Why B/C wrong: These ranges do not match the table.  Why D wrong: 60–70 years is the ovarian cancer age group."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"The typical age group for ovarian cancer per the table is:","options":["60–70 years","35–39 years","55–70 years","20–30 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states cancer ovary age group = 60–70 years.  Why B wrong: 35–39 years is a cervical cancer peak.  Why C wrong: 55–70 years describes endometrial cancer.  Why D wrong: Too young."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which genital tract cancers are more common in NULLIPAROUS women? (SATA)","options":["Endometrial cancer","Ovarian cancer","Cervical cancer","Vaginal cancer","Vulvar cancer"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists endometrium and ovary as nulliparous, whereas cervix is multiparous.  Why C wrong: Cervical cancer is more common in multiparous women.  Why D/E wrong: Not addressed in the table."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Cervical cancer is more common in which parity group?","options":["Multiparous women","Nulliparous women","Primiparous women only","Parity has no effect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists cervical cancer as occurring in multiparous women.  Why B wrong: Nulliparity is associated with endometrial and ovarian cancers.  Why C/D wrong: The table specifies multiparous, not primiparous or no effect."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which genital tract cancers are related to increased estrogen? (SATA)","options":["Endometrial cancer","Ovarian cancer","Cervical cancer","Vulvar cancer","Vaginal cancer"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The table marks \"related to increased estrogen\" with a tick (✓) for endometrium and ovary, and a cross (×) for cervix.  Why C wrong: Cervical cancer is marked × for increased estrogen relation.  Why D/E wrong: Not addressed in the table."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which genital tract cancers have a hereditary/genetic factor? (SATA)","options":["Endometrial cancer","Ovarian cancer","Cervical cancer","All genital tract cancers equally"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The table marks hereditary/genetic factor ✓ for endometrium and ovary and × for cervix.  Why C wrong: Cervical cancer is marked × for hereditary/genetic factor.  Why D wrong: The table distinguishes cervix as not hereditary."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which genital tract cancer is related to intercourse / multiple sexual partners?","options":["Cervical cancer","Endometrial cancer","Ovarian cancer","All three equally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table marks \"related to intercourse/multiple partners\" ✓ for cervix and × for endometrium and ovary.  Why B/C wrong: Endometrium and ovary are marked × for this factor.  Why D wrong: Only cervix is linked to intercourse/multiple partners."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"The mnemonic \"FHOLDAUNT\" for endometrial cancer risk factors: the letter \"H\" stands for:","options":["Hypertension","Hyperlipidemia","Hyperthyroidism","Hepatitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state Hes = Hypertension in the endometrial cancer risk-factor list.  Why B/C/D wrong: The notes specifically list hypertension, not these."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In the endometrial cancer risk-factor list, the letter \"O\" stands for:","options":["Obesity","Ovarian cyst","Oligomenorrhea","Old age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list O – Obesity as an endometrial cancer risk factor.  Why B/C/D wrong: The notes specify obesity, not these."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In the endometrial cancer risk-factor list, the letter \"L\" stands for:","options":["Late menopause and early menarche","Low socioeconomic status","Long menstrual cycle","Liver disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list L – Late menopause & early menarche (prolonged estrogen exposure).  Why B wrong: Low socioeconomic status is a cervical cancer risk factor.  Why C/D wrong: Not listed in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In the endometrial cancer risk-factor list, the letter \"D\" stands for:","options":["Diabetes","Dysmenorrhea","Dehydration","Depression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list D – Diabetes as an endometrial cancer risk factor.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not listed in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In the endometrial cancer risk-factor list, the letter \"A\" stands for:","options":["Atypical endometrial hyperplasia","Anemia","Adenomyosis","Amenorrhea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list A – Atypical endometrial hyperplasia as a risk factor.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not listed in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In the endometrial cancer risk-factor list, the letter \"U\" stands for:","options":["Unopposed estrogen","Uterine fibroid","Urinary tract infection","Undescended testis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list U – Unopposed estrogen (estrogen without progesterone) as a risk factor.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not listed in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In the endometrial cancer risk-factor list, the letter \"N\" stands for:","options":["Nulliparity","Night shift work","Neuropathy","Nephropathy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list N – Nulliparity as an endometrial cancer risk factor.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not listed in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In the endometrial cancer risk-factor list, the letter \"T\" stands for:","options":["Tamoxifen therapy","Tuberculosis","Thyroid disease","Thrombosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list T – Tamoxifen therapy as an endometrial cancer risk factor.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not listed in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Tamoxifen, used in the treatment of which cancer, can increase the long-term risk of endometrial cancer?","options":["Breast cancer","Ovarian cancer","Cervical cancer","Colon cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state tamoxifen is used in treatment of breast cancer but long-term use can lead to endometrial cancer.  Why B/C/D wrong: Tamoxifen is specifically a breast cancer treatment in this context."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"The \"Corpus cancer syndrome\" describes an endometrial (corpus) cancer patient who is typically:","options":["Obese, hypertensive, and diabetic","Thin, hypotensive, and hypoglycemic","Young, nulliparous, and lean","Anemic and immunocompromised"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe the endometrial/corpus cancer female as obese positive, hypertension positive, diabetic positive → corpus cancer syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the classic obese-hypertensive-diabetic triad of corpus cancer syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which of the following is a risk factor for cervical cancer? (SATA)","options":["Early age of intercourse","Early age of marriage","Multiple sex partners","Nulliparity","Late menopause"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list early age of intercourse, early age of marriage, and multiple sex partners as cervical cancer risk factors.  Why D wrong: Nulliparity is an endometrial/ovarian risk factor; cervix is linked to multiparity.  Why E wrong: Late menopause is an endometrial cancer risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which infection is the MOST IMPORTANT risk factor for cervical cancer?","options":["HPV (Human Papilloma Virus) infection","HIV infection","Herpes simplex virus","Chlamydia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes highlight \"Most important HPV infection\" among cervical cancer risk factors.  Why B wrong: HIV positivity (immunocompromised state) is a listed risk factor but HPV is the most important.  Why C/D wrong: These are STDs but HPV is specifically named as most important."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which of the following are risk factors for cervical cancer per the notes? (SATA)","options":["STDs","Low socioeconomic status","Immunocompromised condition (HIV positive)","Smoking","High socioeconomic status"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list STDs, low socioeconomic status, immunocompromised (HIV positive), and smoking as cervical cancer risk factors.  Why E wrong: Cervical cancer is more common in LOW socioeconomic status, not high."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which risk factors are shared by ovarian cancer per the notes? (SATA)","options":["Increased estrogen","Increased ovulation","Nulliparity","Obesity","Multiple sexual partners"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list increased estrogen, increased ovulation, nulliparity, obesity, early menarche, late menopause, and ovulation-inducing drugs as ovarian cancer risk factors.  Why E wrong: Multiple sexual partners is a cervical cancer risk factor, not ovarian."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which class of drugs is listed as a risk factor for ovarian cancer?","options":["Ovulation-inducing drugs (e.g., clomiphene citrate)","Beta-blockers","ACE inhibitors","Antiepileptics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list ovulation-inducing drugs (clomiphene citrate) as an ovarian cancer risk factor (repeated ovulation).  Why B/C/D wrong: These drug classes are not listed as ovarian cancer risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which ovarian cancer risk factors relate to prolonged estrogen exposure? (SATA)","options":["Early menarche","Late menopause","Obesity","Multiparity"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list early menarche, late menopause, and obesity as ovarian cancer risk factors (all increasing lifetime estrogen/ovulation).  Why D wrong: Ovarian cancer is associated with nulliparity, not multiparity."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Endometrial cancer is more common in which socioeconomic group?","options":["High socioeconomic status","Low socioeconomic status","Middle income only","Independent of socioeconomic status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states endometrial cancer is more common in high socioeconomic status.  Why B wrong: Low socioeconomic status is associated with cervical cancer.  Why C/D wrong: The table specifies high socioeconomic status."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Cervical cancer is more common in which socioeconomic group?","options":["Low socioeconomic status","High socioeconomic status","Middle income only","Independent of socioeconomic status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states cervical cancer is more common in low socioeconomic status.  Why B wrong: High socioeconomic status is associated with endometrial and ovarian cancers.  Why C/D wrong: The table specifies low socioeconomic status."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Ovarian cancer is more common in which socioeconomic group?","options":["High socioeconomic status","Low socioeconomic status","Middle income only","Independent of socioeconomic status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states ovarian cancer is more common in high socioeconomic status.  Why B wrong: Low socioeconomic status is linked to cervical cancer.  Why C/D wrong: The table specifies high socioeconomic status."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"In the endometrial cancer mnemonic, \"Family/Fes\" corresponds to which risk factor?","options":["Family history","Fatigue","Fever","Fasting hyperglycemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"Family = Family History\" as an endometrial cancer risk factor.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not listed in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 088 · Centchroman, emergency/post-coital contraceptives…","question":"Which of the following genital tract cancers is associated with increased ovulation as a risk factor? (SATA)","options":["Endometrial cancer","Ovarian cancer","Cervical cancer","Vulvar cancer"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list \"increased ovulation\" as a risk factor under both endometrial cancer and ovarian cancer.  Why C wrong: Cervical cancer risk is linked to HPV/intercourse factors, not ovulation.  Why D wrong: Not addressed in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"According to the comparison of gynaecological cancers, which cancer is typically associated with a \"cachexia look\" (thin, wasted body build)?","options":["Cancer endometrium","Cancer cervix","Cancer ovary","Cancer vulva"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the body build for cancer cervix as a \"cachexia look,\" while endometrium and ovary are associated with an obese build.  Why A wrong: Cancer endometrium is associated with an obese build, not cachexia.  Why C wrong: Cancer ovary is associated with an obese build.  Why D wrong: Cancer vulva is not listed in this comparison table."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In the gynaecological cancer comparison table, which two cancers share an \"obese\" body build?","options":["Cancer endometrium and cancer cervix","Cancer endometrium and cancer ovary","Cancer cervix and cancer ovary","Cancer cervix and cancer vulva"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"Obese\" as the build for both cancer endometrium and cancer ovary.  Why A wrong: Cancer cervix is associated with cachexia, not obesity.  Why C wrong: Cancer cervix is a cachexia look, not obese.  Why D wrong: Cancer cervix is cachexia and cancer vulva is not in the table."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a protective factor against cancer of the endometrium?","options":["Early age of intercourse","Multiple sexual partners","Pregnancy (multiparity)","Smoking increasing estrogen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pregnancy (multiparity) is listed as a protective factor for cancer endometrium.  Why A wrong: Early age of intercourse is a risk factor for cancer cervix, not a protective factor.  Why B wrong: Multiple partners is a risk factor for cancer cervix.  Why D wrong: Smoking is protective because it DECREASES estrogen, not increases it."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The notes state that smoking is protective against cancer endometrium and cancer ovary through which mechanism?","options":["Increased ovulation","Decreased estrogen","Increased progesterone","Increased HPV clearance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smoking is listed as protective because it decreases (↓) estrogen.  Why A wrong: Decreased (not increased) ovulation via OCPs is the protective mechanism cited for OCPs.  Why C wrong: The notes attribute smoking's protection to decreased estrogen, not increased progesterone.  Why D wrong: HPV clearance relates to cancer cervix, not the smoking/estrogen mechanism listed here."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) are listed as protective against endometrial and ovarian cancer primarily because they cause:","options":["Increased ovulation","Decreased ovulation","Increased estrogen","Increased menstruation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list OCPs as protective via ↓ ovulation.  Why A wrong: OCPs suppress rather than increase ovulation.  Why C wrong: The protective effect is linked to decreased ovulation, not increased estrogen.  Why D wrong: Menstruation change is not the cited mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which of the following are listed as protective factors for BOTH cancer endometrium and cancer ovary? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pregnancy (multiparity)","Exercise","Smoking","OCPs","HPV vaccine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Both the endometrium and ovary columns list pregnancy/multiparity, exercise, smoking, OCPs, and breast feeding as protective factors.  Why E wrong: HPV vaccine is the protective factor listed specifically for cancer cervix, not for endometrium/ovary."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"According to the table, the protective factor specific to cancer cervix is:","options":["Multiparity","HPV vaccine","Exercise","Breast feeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HPV vaccine is listed as the protective factor under cancer cervix.  Why A wrong: Multiparity is listed for endometrium and ovary, not cervix.  Why C wrong: Exercise is listed for endometrium and ovary.  Why D wrong: Breast feeding is listed for endometrium and ovary."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the most important risk factor for cancer cervix?","options":["Multiparity","Human papilloma virus (HPV)","Obesity","Nulliparity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states the most important risk factor for cancer cervix is Human papilloma virus.  Why A wrong: Multiparity is protective for endometrium/ovary, not the key cervical risk factor.  Why C wrong: Obesity is linked to endometrial/ovarian cancer builds, not the cervical risk factor.  Why D wrong: Nulliparity is not cited as the most important cervical cancer risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"HPV is divided into how many risk groups?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows HPV splits into 2 risk groups (high-risk and low-risk subtypes).  Why A wrong: There are two groups, not one.  Why C wrong: Only two groups are described.  Why D wrong: Only two groups are described."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which of the following are HIGH-RISK HPV subtypes as listed in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["HPV 16","HPV 18","HPV 6","HPV 33","HPV 11"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: The high-risk subtype list is HPV 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58 — so 16, 18, and 33 qualify.  Why C wrong: HPV 6 is a low-risk subtype.  Why E wrong: HPV 11 is a low-risk subtype."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The complete list of high-risk HPV subtypes given in the notes is:","options":["6, 11, 16, 18","16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58","16, 18, 45, 58 only","31, 33, 52, 58 only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list high-risk subtypes as HPV 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58.  Why A wrong: 6 and 11 are low-risk subtypes.  Why C wrong: This omits 31, 33, 52.  Why D wrong: This omits 16, 18, 45."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which HPV subtypes are classified as LOW-RISK and cause anogenital warts?","options":["HPV 16 and 18","HPV 31 and 33","HPV 6 and 11","HPV 45 and 52"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list low-risk subtype as HPV 6, 11 with the cause = anogenital warts.  Why A wrong: HPV 16 and 18 are high-risk subtypes.  Why B wrong: HPV 31 and 33 are high-risk subtypes.  Why D wrong: HPV 45 and 52 are high-risk subtypes."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Low-risk HPV subtypes (6, 11) are chiefly responsible for causing:","options":["Cancer cervix","Anogenital warts","Ovarian cancer","Endometrial hyperplasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state low-risk subtypes cause anogenital warts.  Why A wrong: Cancer cervix is caused by high-risk subtypes (16, 18).  Why C wrong: Ovarian cancer is not linked to HPV in these notes.  Why D wrong: Endometrial hyperplasia is not an HPV-caused lesion here."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In females, high-risk HPV subtypes are associated with cancers of the cervix, vagina, and:","options":["Endometrium","Vulva/cancer valve","Ovary","Fallopian tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list female cancers from high-risk HPV as cervix, cancer valve (vulva), and cancer vagina.  Why A wrong: Endometrium is not in the HPV-associated female cancer list.  Why C wrong: Ovary is not in the HPV-associated female cancer list.  Why D wrong: Fallopian tube is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In males, high-risk HPV subtypes are associated with which cancers? (Select all that apply)","options":["Carcinoma penis","Oral cancer","Anal cancer","Testicular cancer","Prostate cancer"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list male HPV-associated cancers as carcinoma penis, oral cancer, and anal cancer.  Why D wrong: Testicular cancer is not listed as HPV-associated.  Why E wrong: Prostate cancer is not listed as HPV-associated."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which HPV subtype(s) is/are the MOST COMMON associated with cancer cervix?","options":["HPV 16 and 18","HPV 6 and 11","HPV 31 and 33","HPV 45 and 52"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common HPV associated with cancer cervix = HPV 16 and HPV 18.  Why B wrong: HPV 6 and 11 are low-risk (anogenital warts).  Why C wrong: HPV 31 and 33 are high-risk but not the most common.  Why D wrong: HPV 45 and 52 are high-risk but not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The SECOND most common HPV associated with cancer cervix is:","options":["HPV 16","HPV 18","HPV 6","HPV 33"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the second most common HPV associated with cancer cervix = HPV 18.  Why A wrong: HPV 16 is the most common, not second.  Why C wrong: HPV 6 is low-risk.  Why D wrong: HPV 33 is high-risk but not cited as second most common."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The MOST SPECIFIC HPV subtype for cancer cervix is:","options":["HPV 16","HPV 18","HPV 11","HPV 31"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most specific HPV for cancer cervix = HPV 18.  Why A wrong: HPV 16 is the most common, not the most specific.  Why C wrong: HPV 11 is low-risk.  Why D wrong: HPV 31 is high-risk but not the most specific."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which HPV subtype is most commonly associated with SQUAMOUS CELL cancer of the cervix?","options":["HPV 16","HPV 18","HPV 6","HPV 45"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common HPV associated with squamous cell cancer of cervix = HPV 16.  Why B wrong: HPV 18 is most commonly linked to adenocarcinoma of cervix.  Why C wrong: HPV 6 is low-risk.  Why D wrong: HPV 45 is high-risk but not the cited answer."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which HPV subtype is most commonly associated with ADENOCARCINOMA of the cervix?","options":["HPV 16","HPV 18","HPV 11","HPV 33"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common HPV associated with adenocarcinoma of cervix = HPV 18.  Why A wrong: HPV 16 is linked to squamous cell cancer of cervix.  Why C wrong: HPV 11 is low-risk.  Why D wrong: HPV 33 is high-risk but not the cited answer."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Many young females acquire HPV infection. According to the notes, what is the usual outcome?","options":["It always progresses to cancer cervix","In most of them it resolves","It always persists lifelong","It converts to ovarian cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that in most young females who get HPV infection, it resolves.  Why A wrong: HPV infection does not always progress to cancer; presence of HPV does not mean cancer cervix will occur.  Why C wrong: In most it resolves; persistence occurs only in very few.  Why D wrong: HPV is not linked to ovarian cancer in these notes."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"A young female tests positive for HPV. Based on the notes, the most appropriate interpretation is:","options":["She definitely has cancer cervix","HPV infection does not mean she will have cancer cervix","She requires immediate hysterectomy","She is immune to all future HPV infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes explicitly state that if HPV infection is present in a female it does not mean she will have cancer cervix, since it resolves in most.  Why A wrong: A positive HPV test does not equal cancer.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not indicated for mere HPV positivity.  Why D wrong: The notes do not claim lifelong immunity from a single infection."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In the very few females in whom HPV infection PERSISTS, what can it lead to?","options":["Anogenital warts only","Dysplasia / CIN / cancer cervix","Ovarian cyst","Endometrial hyperplasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state that if HPV infection persists, it leads to dysplasia / CIN / cancer cervix.  Why A wrong: Anogenital warts are caused by low-risk subtypes, not the persistence-to-cancer pathway.  Why C wrong: Ovarian cyst is unrelated to HPV persistence.  Why D wrong: Endometrial hyperplasia is not the HPV persistence outcome."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"HPV infection is transmitted mainly by which route?","options":["Fecal-oral","Sexually transmitted","Airborne droplet","Blood transfusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HPV infection is sexually transmitted.  Why A wrong: HPV is not transmitted fecal-orally.  Why C wrong: HPV is not an airborne/droplet infection.  Why D wrong: HPV is not primarily blood-borne."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which of the following are risk factors for cancer cervix as stated in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Early age of intercourse","Multiple partners","Sexually transmitted diseases (STDs)","Late age of first intercourse","Celibacy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Because HPV is sexually transmitted, early age of intercourse, multiple partners, and STDs are risk factors for cancer cervix.  Why D wrong: Early (not late) age of intercourse is the risk factor.  Why E wrong: Celibacy reduces sexual exposure and is not a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"HPV vaccines are which type of vaccine?","options":["Live attenuated vaccines","Recombinant vaccines using capsid protein","Toxoid vaccines","Inactivated whole-virus vaccines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state HPV vaccines are recombinant vaccines made from CAPSID protein.  Why A wrong: They are not live attenuated.  Why C wrong: They are not toxoids.  Why D wrong: They are recombinant capsid protein, not inactivated whole virus."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which HPV vaccine is a BIVALENT vaccine active against HPV 16 and 18?","options":["Cervarix","Gardasil","Gardasil-9","BCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Cervarix as active against HPV 16, 18 — a bivalent vaccine.  Why B wrong: Gardasil is quadrivalent (16, 18, 6, 11).  Why C wrong: Gardasil-9 is nonavalent (9 types).  Why D wrong: BCG is a tuberculosis vaccine, unrelated to HPV."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Gardasil is a quadrivalent HPV vaccine active against which subtypes?","options":["HPV 16, 18 only","HPV 16, 18, 6, 11","HPV 6, 11 only","HPV 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 51, 58"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Gardasil as active against HPV 16, 18 and HPV 6, 11 — a quadrivalent vaccine.  Why A wrong: HPV 16, 18 only describes bivalent Cervarix.  Why C wrong: HPV 6, 11 alone is incomplete for Gardasil.  Why D wrong: That extended list describes Gardasil-9 (nonavalent)."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Gardasil-9 (Gardasil-39) is a nonavalent vaccine active against which HPV subtypes?","options":["16, 18 only","6, 11, 16, 18","6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 51, 58","31, 33, 45, 51, 58 only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Gardasil-9 as nonavalent, active against HPV 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 51, 58.  Why A wrong: This is bivalent Cervarix.  Why B wrong: This is quadrivalent Gardasil.  Why D wrong: This omits 6, 11, 16, 18."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Cervarix (bivalent HPV vaccine) is protective against:","options":["Cancer cervix only","Cancer cervix + genital warts","Genital warts only","Ovarian cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Cervarix as protective against cancer cervix only.  Why B wrong: Genital wart protection is provided by Gardasil (covers HPV 6, 11).  Why C wrong: Cervarix does not cover the wart-causing low-risk types.  Why D wrong: These vaccines are not indicated for ovarian cancer."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Gardasil (quadrivalent) provides protection against cancer cervix PLUS:","options":["Ovarian cancer","Anogenital / genital warts","Endometrial cancer","Breast cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Gardasil as protective against cancer cervix + anogenital/genital warts (because it covers HPV 6, 11).  Why A wrong: It does not protect against ovarian cancer.  Why C wrong: It does not protect against endometrial cancer.  Why D wrong: It does not protect against breast cancer."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Gardasil-9 provides the broadest protection, covering cancer cervix, cancer vagina, genital warts and additionally:","options":["Cancer valve (vulva)","Ovarian cancer","Endometrial cancer","Cancer of the fallopian tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Gardasil-9 as protective against cancer cervix, cancer valve (vulva), cancer vagina + genital warts.  Why B wrong: Ovarian cancer is not covered.  Why C wrong: Endometrial cancer is not covered.  Why D wrong: Fallopian tube cancer is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the IDEAL age for administering the HPV vaccine?","options":["9–26 years","11–12 years","26–30 years","Below 9 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the ideal age is 11–12 years.  Why A wrong: 9–26 years is the range in which the vaccine \"can be given,\" not the ideal age.  Why C wrong: 26–30 years is beyond the recommended range.  Why D wrong: Below 9 years is younger than the approved age."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The HPV vaccine can be given within which age range?","options":["5–15 years","9–26 years","11–12 years","15–45 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the HPV vaccine can be given from 9–26 years.  Why A wrong: The lower bound is 9, upper is 26.  Why C wrong: 11–12 is the ideal age, not the full range.  Why D wrong: The upper limit given is 26 years, not 45."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The HPV vaccine can be administered to:","options":["Females only","Males only","Both males and females","Pregnant women only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states both male and female can be given the HPV vaccine.  Why A wrong: It is not restricted to females.  Why B wrong: It is not restricted to males.  Why D wrong: It is not specific to pregnant women."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the dose of the HPV vaccine?","options":["0.1 ml","0.5 ml","1 ml","5 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the dose as 0.5 ml.  Why A wrong: 0.1 ml is the intradermal BCG dose, not HPV.  Why C wrong: The stated dose is 0.5 ml, not 1 ml.  Why D wrong: 5 ml is far larger than the stated dose."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"By which route is the HPV vaccine administered?","options":["Subcutaneous","Intradermal","Intramuscular (I/M)","Oral"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the route as I/M (intramuscular).  Why A wrong: It is not given subcutaneously.  Why B wrong: It is not intradermal.  Why D wrong: It is not given orally."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON side effect of the HPV vaccine?","options":["Anaphylaxis","Syncope","High fever","Seizures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the most common side effect as syncope (fainting).  Why A wrong: Anaphylaxis is rare, not the most common effect.  Why C wrong: High fever is not listed as the most common effect.  Why D wrong: Seizures are not the cited most common side effect."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The premalignant condition preceding cancer of the ENDOMETRIUM is:","options":["CIN (cervical intraepithelial neoplasia)","Endometrial hyperplasia","Ovarian cyst","Anogenital warts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists endometrial hyperplasia as the premalignant condition for cancer endometrium.  Why A wrong: CIN is the premalignant condition for cancer cervix.  Why C wrong: Ovarian cyst is the premalignant condition for cancer ovary.  Why D wrong: Anogenital warts are caused by low-risk HPV, not the endometrial premalignant lesion."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Endometrial hyperplasia WITHOUT atypical cells carries what risk and treatment?","options":["Increased chance of malignancy; treat with hysterectomy","Less chance of malignancy; treat with progesterone","No risk; no treatment needed","Less chance of malignancy; treat with radiotherapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states hyperplasia without atypical cells has less chances of malignancy and treatment is progesterone.  Why A wrong: Hysterectomy is for hyperplasia WITH atypical cells.  Why C wrong: Treatment (progesterone) is still indicated.  Why D wrong: Radiotherapy is not the treatment for this variant."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Endometrial hyperplasia WITH atypical cells carries increased chance of malignancy; the treatment is:","options":["Progesterone","Hysterectomy","Observation","HPV vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states hyperplasia with atypical cells has increased chances of malignancy and treatment is hysterectomy.  Why A wrong: Progesterone is for hyperplasia without atypical cells.  Why C wrong: Observation alone is inappropriate given increased malignancy risk.  Why D wrong: The HPV vaccine is a preventive measure for cervical cancer, not a treatment for endometrial hyperplasia."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The premalignant condition of cancer CERVIX is abbreviated as CIN, which stands for:","options":["Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia","Cervical invasive neoplasm","Cervical inflammatory nodule","Cervical intramural nodule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines CIN as cervical intraepithelial neoplasia.  Why B wrong: CIN is intraepithelial (pre-invasive), not invasive.  Why C wrong: It is a neoplasia, not an inflammatory nodule.  Why D wrong: It is not an intramural nodule."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The premalignant condition for cancer OVARY described in the notes is an ovarian cyst that is typically:","options":["Solid, unilateral, associated with ascites and lymph node enlargement","Cystic, bilateral, no ascites","Solid, bilateral, no lymph node involvement","Cystic, unilateral, associated with pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the ovarian cyst as solid, unilateral, associated with ascites and associated with LN (lymph node) enlargement.  Why B wrong: It is described as solid, not cystic, and associated with ascites.  Why C wrong: It is unilateral and associated with LN enlargement.  Why D wrong: The described features are solid/ascites/LN, not a pregnancy-linked cyst."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Among the three gynaecological cancers, which one has a recommended (✓) screening programme?","options":["Cancer endometrium","Cancer cervix","Cancer ovary","All three equally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The screening row marks cancer cervix with a ✓ (screening done), while endometrium and ovary are marked ✗.  Why A wrong: Endometrium is marked ✗ for screening.  Why C wrong: Ovary is marked ✗ for screening.  Why D wrong: Only cervix has an established screening tick."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which of the following are recognised screening methods for cancer CERVIX? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pap smear","HPV DNA testing (>30 years)","VIA / VILI in rural areas","CA-125 levels","Mammography"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Pap smear, HPV DNA testing (>30 years), and VIA/VILI (in rural areas) as cervical screening methods.  Why D wrong: CA-125 is a tumour marker for ovarian cancer, not cervical screening.  Why E wrong: Mammography screens for breast cancer, not cervix."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"HPV DNA testing as a cervical cancer screening method is recommended for women of what age?","options":["Below 18 years","Above 30 years (>30 years)","Exactly 26 years","Any age from birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table specifies HPV DNA testing for those >30 years.  Why A wrong: It is not recommended below 18 years.  Why C wrong: The cut-off is stated as greater than 30 years, not exactly 26.  Why D wrong: It is age-restricted to over 30 years."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In rural areas, which low-cost visual methods are used for cervical cancer screening?","options":["VIA and VILI","Pap smear and colposcopy","CA-125 and CT","Mammography and MRI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists VIA, VILI as the rural-area screening methods for cervix.  Why B wrong: Pap smear and colposcopy are listed separately, not as the rural low-cost visual methods.  Why C wrong: CA-125 and CT are not cervical rural screening methods.  Why D wrong: Mammography/MRI are not cervical screening methods."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The tumour marker used for ovarian cancer screening in a post-menopausal woman is considered positive when its value is:","options":["CA-125 > 35 IU","CA-125 < 10 IU","CEA > 5 IU","AFP > 35 IU"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states tumour marker CA-125 in a post-menopausal woman >35 IU indicates increased chances of ovarian cancer.  Why B wrong: A low value (<10) is not the positive threshold.  Why C wrong: CEA is not the marker cited for ovarian cancer here.  Why D wrong: AFP is not the marker cited for ovarian screening here."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON histological variety of cancer ENDOMETRIUM?","options":["Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Clear cell tumor","Papillary serous tumor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists adenocarcinoma as the most common variety of cancer endometrium.  Why B wrong: Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common variety of cancer cervix.  Why C wrong: Clear cell tumor is listed as the MOST MALIGNANT endometrial variety, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Papillary serous tumor is the \"2nd best\" (second most common) endometrial variety."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The MOST MALIGNANT histological variety of cancer endometrium is:","options":["Adenocarcinoma","Clear cell tumor","Papillary serous tumor","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists clear cell tumor as the most malignant variety of cancer endometrium.  Why A wrong: Adenocarcinoma is the most common, not most malignant.  Why C wrong: Papillary serous tumor is listed as the second (2nd best) variety.  Why D wrong: Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common cervical variety."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In the endometrial cancer varieties, the \"2nd best\" (second most common) other variety is:","options":["Clear cell tumor","Papillary serous tumor","Adenocarcinoma","Brenner tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the 2nd best other variety as papillary serous tumor.  Why A wrong: Clear cell tumor is the most malignant variety.  Why C wrong: Adenocarcinoma is the most common variety.  Why D wrong: Brenner tumor is an ovarian epithelial variety."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON histological variety of cancer CERVIX?","options":["Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Clear cell tumor","Epithelial cell tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists squamous cell carcinoma as the most common variety of cancer cervix.  Why A wrong: Adenocarcinoma of cervix is an other variety, most commonly with HPV 18.  Why C wrong: Clear cell tumor is an endometrial variety.  Why D wrong: Epithelial cell tumor is the most common ovarian variety."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON SITE of squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix?","options":["Endocervix","Transformation zone","Ectocervix tip","Uterine fundus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the most common site is the transformation zone, commonly seen with HPV 16 infection.  Why A wrong: Endocervix is the most common site for adenocarcinoma of cervix.  Why C wrong: Ectocervix tip is not the cited common site.  Why D wrong: The uterine fundus relates to endometrial cancer."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Adenocarcinoma of the cervix (an \"other variety\") has its most common site at the endocervix and is most commonly seen with which HPV infection?","options":["HPV 16","HPV 18","HPV 6","HPV 11"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states adenocarcinoma of cervix — most common site = endocervix — is most commonly seen with HPV 18 infection.  Why A wrong: HPV 16 is linked to squamous cell carcinoma (transformation zone).  Why C wrong: HPV 6 is low-risk (warts).  Why D wrong: HPV 11 is low-risk (warts)."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The most common variety of cancer OVARY is:","options":["Germ cell tumor","Epithelial cell tumor","Sex cord tumor","Metastatic tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists epithelial cell tumor as the most common ovarian variety, arising from the germinal epithelium of the ovary.  Why A wrong: Germ cell tumor is the most common ovarian tumor in young girls, not overall.  Why C wrong: Sex cord tumor is a less common category.  Why D wrong: Metastatic tumor (e.g., Krukenberg) is not the most common ovarian variety."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Epithelial cell tumors of the ovary arise from which structure?","options":["Germinal epithelium of the ovary","Corpus luteum","Fallopian tube mucosa","Endometrial glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the epithelial cell tumor arises from the germinal epithelium of the ovary.  Why B wrong: It does not arise from the corpus luteum.  Why C wrong: It arises from the ovary, not the fallopian tube mucosa.  Why D wrong: It does not arise from endometrial glands."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which of the following are the four main categories of ovarian tumors listed in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Epithelial cell tumor","Germ cell tumor","Sex cord tumor","Metastatic tumor","Squamous cell tumor"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The classification chart lists epithelial cell tumor, germ cell tumor, sex cord tumor, and metastatic as the four categories.  Why E wrong: Squamous cell tumor is a cervical variety, not an ovarian tumor category."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which of the following are epithelial cell tumors of the ovary listed in the notes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Serous tumor","Mucinous tumor","Clear cell tumor","Brenner tumor","Endometrial tumor"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists all five — serous tumor, mucinous tumor, clear cell tumor, Brenner tumor, and endometrial tumor — as epithelial cell tumors.  Why (none) wrong: All listed options belong to the epithelial cell tumor group."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Among ovarian epithelial cell tumors, the serous tumor accounts for what percentage?","options":["20–30%","40–50%","80–90%","100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The chart notes serous tumor accounts for 80–90% of epithelial cell tumors.  Why A wrong: 20–30% understates the serous proportion.  Why B wrong: 40–50% is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 100% overstates it; other subtypes exist."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"\"Teratoma\" is classified under which category of ovarian tumor?","options":["Epithelial cell tumor","Germ cell tumor","Sex cord tumor","Metastatic tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists teratoma under germ cell tumor.  Why A wrong: Teratoma is not an epithelial cell tumor.  Why C wrong: Teratoma is not a sex cord tumor.  Why D wrong: Teratoma is not metastatic."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which type of teratoma is immature and corresponds to a dermoid cyst per the notes?","options":["Immature teratoma / dermoid cyst","Mature teratoma (malignant)","Choriocarcinoma","Dysgerminoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists teratoma as immature: dermoid cyst, and mature: malignant.  Why B wrong: The mature form is labelled malignant in the chart.  Why C wrong: Choriocarcinoma is a separate germ cell tumor (C).  Why D wrong: Dysgerminoma is a separate germ cell tumor (D)."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In the germ cell tumor \"CDE\" mnemonic in the notes, C stands for:","options":["Choriocarcinoma","Clear cell tumor","Corpus luteum tumor","Cystadenoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists C = Choriocarcinoma among germ cell tumors.  Why B wrong: Clear cell tumor is an epithelial cell tumor.  Why C wrong: Corpus luteum tumor is not in the list.  Why D wrong: Cystadenoma is an epithelial benign tumor."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In the germ cell tumor list, D and E respectively stand for:","options":["Dysgerminoma and Embryonal tumors","Dermoid cyst and Endometrial tumor","Dysgerminoma and Epithelial tumor","Dermoid and Embryonal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists D = Dysgerminoma and E = Embryonal tumors.  Why B wrong: Dermoid cyst is the immature teratoma, and endometrial tumor is epithelial.  Why C wrong: Epithelial tumor is a separate category, not E here.  Why D wrong: D is Dysgerminoma, not \"Dermoid.\""},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Yolk sac tumor is classified as which category of ovarian tumor?","options":["Epithelial cell tumor","Germ cell tumor","Sex cord tumor","Metastatic tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists yolk sac tumor under germ cell tumor.  Why A wrong: It is not an epithelial cell tumor.  Why C wrong: It is not a sex cord tumor.  Why D wrong: It is not metastatic."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which of the following are FEMALE-hormone-producing sex cord tumors of the ovary? (Select all that apply)","options":["Granulosa cell tumor","Fibroma","Thecoma","Sertoli cell tumor","Leydig cell tumor"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists granulosa cell tumor, fibroma, and thecoma as female-hormone-producing sex cord tumors.  Why D wrong: Sertoli cell tumor is male-hormone-producing.  Why E wrong: Leydig cell tumor is male-hormone-producing."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which of the following are MALE-hormone-producing sex cord tumors of the ovary? (Select all that apply)","options":["Sertoli cell tumor","Leydig cell tumor","Hilus cell tumor","Granulosa cell tumor","Thecoma"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists Sertoli cell tumor, Leydig cell tumor, and Hilus cell tumor as male-hormone-producing.  Why D wrong: Granulosa cell tumor is female-hormone-producing.  Why E wrong: Thecoma is female-hormone-producing."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The metastatic ovarian tumor named in the notes is the:","options":["Krukenberg tumor","Granulosa cell tumor","Dysgerminoma","Brenner tumor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists the metastatic category → Krukenberg tumor.  Why B wrong: Granulosa cell tumor is a sex cord tumor.  Why C wrong: Dysgerminoma is a germ cell tumor.  Why D wrong: Brenner tumor is an epithelial cell tumor."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The most common BENIGN tumor of the ovary / most common benign epithelial cell tumor is:","options":["Serous cystadenoma","Serous cystadenocarcinoma","Mature teratoma","Dermoid cyst"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common benign tumor of ovary / most common benign epithelial cell tumor = serous cystadenoma.  Why B wrong: Serous cystadenocarcinoma is the most common MALIGNANT epithelial tumor.  Why C wrong: Mature teratoma is the most common germ cell tumor.  Why D wrong: Dermoid cyst is the most common tumor in pregnancy / to undergo torsion, not the benign epithelial one."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The most common MALIGNANT tumor of the ovary / most common malignant epithelial cell tumor is:","options":["Serous cystadenoma","Serous cystadenocarcinoma","Yolk sac tumor","Krukenberg tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common malignant epithelial cell tumor = serous cystadenocarcinoma.  Why A wrong: Serous cystadenoma is benign.  Why C wrong: Yolk sac tumor is the most malignant germ cell tumor, not the most common malignant epithelial one.  Why D wrong: Krukenberg tumor is a metastatic tumor."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The most common age for an epithelial tumor of the ovary / most common malignant epithelial cell tumor (serous cystadenocarcinoma) is:","options":["20–30 years","40–50 years","60–70 years","Below 20 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common age of epithelial tumor of ovary is 60–70 years.  Why A wrong: 20–30 years is not the cited age range.  Why B wrong: 40–50 years is not the cited age range.  Why D wrong: Below 20 years relates to germ cell tumors of young girls."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The most common ovarian tumor in a young girl (at puberty) is:","options":["Epithelial cell tumor","Germ cell tumor","Sex cord tumor","Metastatic tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common ovarian tumor in a young girl (puberty) = germ cell tumor.  Why A wrong: Epithelial cell tumor is most common overall/in older women (60–70 years).  Why C wrong: Sex cord tumor is not the puberty-age common tumor.  Why D wrong: Metastatic tumor is not the puberty-age common tumor."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The most common benign germ cell tumor of the ovary is:","options":["Dermoid cyst (immature teratoma)","Yolk sac tumor","Choriocarcinoma","Dysgerminoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common benign germ cell tumor = dermoid cyst (immature teratoma).  Why B wrong: Yolk sac tumor is the most malignant germ cell tumor.  Why C wrong: Choriocarcinoma is a malignant germ cell tumor.  Why D wrong: Dysgerminoma is the only radiosensitive ovarian tumor, not the most common benign germ cell tumor."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The most common germ cell tumor overall is:","options":["Dermoid cyst","Mature teratoma","Yolk sac tumor","Choriocarcinoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common germ cell tumor = mature teratoma.  Why A wrong: Dermoid cyst (immature teratoma) is the most common BENIGN germ cell tumor, a separate one-liner.  Why C wrong: Yolk sac tumor is the most malignant germ cell tumor.  Why D wrong: Choriocarcinoma is not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The most common ovarian tumor seen in PREGNANCY is:","options":["Serous cystadenoma","Dermoid cyst","Krukenberg tumor","Granulosa cell tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common ovarian tumor seen in pregnancy = dermoid cyst.  Why A wrong: Serous cystadenoma is the most common benign epithelial tumor overall, not the pregnancy-specific answer.  Why C wrong: Krukenberg tumor is metastatic from stomach cancer.  Why D wrong: Granulosa cell tumor is a sex cord tumor."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The most common ovarian tumor to undergo TORSION is:","options":["Serous cystadenocarcinoma","Dermoid cyst","Yolk sac tumor","Fibroma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most common ovarian tumor to undergo torsion = dermoid cyst.  Why A wrong: Serous cystadenocarcinoma is the most common malignant epithelial tumor, not the torsion answer.  Why C wrong: Yolk sac tumor is the most malignant germ cell tumor.  Why D wrong: Fibroma is a female-hormone-producing sex cord tumor."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The MOST MALIGNANT germ cell tumor of the ovary is:","options":["Mature teratoma","Dermoid cyst","Yolk sac tumor","Dysgerminoma"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the most malignant germ cell tumor = yolk sac tumor.  Why A wrong: Mature teratoma is the most common germ cell tumor, not the most malignant.  Why B wrong: Dermoid cyst is a benign germ cell tumor.  Why D wrong: Dysgerminoma is the only radiosensitive ovarian tumor, not labelled most malignant."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Regarding radiosensitivity, the ovary itself is described as a:","options":["Radioresistant organ","Radiosensitive organ","Radio-neutral organ","Organ unaffected by radiation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Remember\" box states the ovary is a radiosensitive organ.  Why A wrong: The ovary is radiosensitive; ovarian TUMORS are radioresistant.  Why C wrong: It is described specifically as radiosensitive.  Why D wrong: Radiation can lead to ovarian failure, so it is affected."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Because the ovary is radiosensitive, giving radiotherapy for any reason in the pelvis can lead to:","options":["Ovarian failure","Ovarian hypertrophy","Increased fertility","Immediate ovulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Remember\" box states that if radiotherapy is given for any reason in the pelvis, it leads to ovarian failure.  Why B wrong: It causes failure, not hypertrophy.  Why C wrong: It reduces, not increases, fertility.  Why D wrong: It does not trigger immediate ovulation."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Ovarian TUMORS are generally radioresistant. The only ovarian tumor that is radiosensitive is:","options":["Serous cystadenocarcinoma","Yolk sac tumor","Dysgerminoma","Krukenberg tumor"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Remember\" box states that the only ovarian tumor which is radiosensitive = dysgerminoma.  Why A wrong: Serous cystadenocarcinoma is not the radiosensitive one.  Why B wrong: Yolk sac tumor is radioresistant like most ovarian tumors.  Why D wrong: Krukenberg tumor is a metastatic tumor, not the radiosensitive one."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"A Krukenberg tumor of the ovary is a metastatic tumor originating from which primary site?","options":["Stomach cancer","Breast cancer","Lung cancer","Cervix cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state stomach cancer metastasizes to the ovary, forming a Krukenberg tumor.  Why B wrong: The cited primary is stomach cancer, not breast.  Why C wrong: The cited primary is stomach cancer, not lung.  Why D wrong: The cited primary is stomach cancer, not cervix."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"By which route does stomach cancer metastasize to the ovary to form a Krukenberg tumor?","options":["Retrograde lymphatics","Hematogenous arterial spread","Direct extension","Perineural spread"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state metastasis occurs by retrograde lymphatics from stomach cancer to the ovary.  Why B wrong: The cited route is retrograde lymphatic, not hematogenous.  Why C wrong: It is not direct extension.  Why D wrong: It is not perineural."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which of the following are characteristics of a Krukenberg tumor? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bilateral","Soft, waxy consistency","Freely mobile (no adhesion)","Shape of ovary is retained","Unilateral and fixed"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes list Krukenberg tumor characteristics as bilateral; soft, waxy consistency; freely mobile (no adhesion); and shape of ovary retained.  Why E wrong: The tumor is bilateral and freely mobile, not unilateral and fixed."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The tumor marker for epithelial cell ovarian cancer is:","options":["CA-125","CEA","AFP","beta-hCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states the tumor marker for epithelial cell ovarian cancer = CA-125.  Why B wrong: CEA is a colorectal/GI marker.  Why C wrong: AFP is a yolk sac tumor / hepatocellular marker.  Why D wrong: beta-hCG is a choriocarcinoma/pregnancy marker."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"CA-125 levels are most significant (clinically useful) in which group?","options":["Young pre-menopausal females","Post-menopausal females","Pregnant women","Adolescent girls"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states CA-125 is significant in post-menopausal females.  Why A wrong: CA-125 is not done in young females as it rises in many conditions.  Why C wrong: Pregnancy can nonspecifically alter markers; the notes emphasise post-menopausal use.  Why D wrong: Adolescents are among young females in whom CA-125 is not done."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In a post-menopausal female, a CA-125 value at or above which threshold is positive for ovarian cancer?","options":["≥ 5 IU","≥ 15 IU","≥ 35 IU","≥ 100 IU"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states if the value is ≥35 IU in a post-menopausal female, it is positive for ovarian cancer.  Why A wrong: 5 IU is below the stated threshold.  Why B wrong: 15 IU is below the stated threshold.  Why D wrong: The stated cut-off is 35 IU, not 100 IU."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Why are CA-125 levels NOT done in young females?","options":["It is too expensive","It is increased in many benign conditions in young females","It is only made after menopause","Young females never get ovarian tumors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state CA-125 is not done in young females because it is increased in young females in many conditions (non-specific).  Why A wrong: Cost is not the reason cited.  Why C wrong: The marker exists in young females but is non-specific.  Why D wrong: Young girls can develop germ cell tumors; the reason given is non-specificity."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON symptom of cancer ENDOMETRIUM?","options":["Post-coital bleeding","Post-menopausal bleeding","Vague abdominal symptoms","Appetite loss"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the most common symptom of cancer endometrium as post-menopausal bleeding.  Why A wrong: Post-coital bleeding is the most common symptom of cancer cervix.  Why C wrong: Vague symptoms characterise cancer ovary.  Why D wrong: Appetite loss is an ovarian cancer symptom."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON symptom of cancer CERVIX?","options":["Post-menopausal bleeding","Post-coital bleeding","Appetite loss","Abdominal distension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the most common symptom of cancer cervix as post-coital bleeding.  Why A wrong: Post-menopausal bleeding is the endometrial cancer symptom.  Why C wrong: Appetite loss is an ovarian cancer symptom.  Why D wrong: Abdominal distension is not the cited cervix symptom."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What are the most common symptoms of cancer OVARY according to the table?","options":["Post-coital bleeding","Post-menopausal bleeding","Vague symptoms — appetite loss, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain","Cyclical mastalgia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists cancer ovary symptoms as vague — appetite loss, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain.  Why A wrong: Post-coital bleeding is the cervix symptom.  Why B wrong: Post-menopausal bleeding is the endometrial symptom.  Why D wrong: Cyclical mastalgia is a breast symptom, not listed for ovary."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"For which cancer does the table indicate NO specific symptom (marked ✗ in the \"most specific symptom\" row)?","options":["Cancer endometrium","Cancer cervix","Cancer ovary","All three have specific symptoms"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The \"most specific symptom\" row marks cancer ovary with ✗, meaning no specific symptom (its symptoms are vague).  Why A wrong: Endometrium's row is not marked ✗.  Why B wrong: Cervix's row is not marked ✗.  Why D wrong: Ovary is specifically marked as lacking a specific symptom."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON route of spread of cancer ENDOMETRIUM?","options":["Direct spread","Lymphatic spread","Peritoneal spread","Hematogenous spread"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the most common route of spread of cancer endometrium as direct spread.  Why B wrong: Lymphatic spread is the route for cancer cervix.  Why C wrong: Peritoneal spread is the route for cancer ovary.  Why D wrong: Hematogenous spread is not the cited route for endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON route of spread of cancer CERVIX?","options":["Direct spread","Lymphatic spread","Peritoneal spread","Transcoelomic spread"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the most common route of spread of cancer cervix as lymphatic spread.  Why A wrong: Direct spread is the endometrial route.  Why C wrong: Peritoneal spread is the ovarian route.  Why D wrong: Transcoelomic is not the cited term for cervix here."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In lymphatic spread of cancer cervix, which is the MOST COMMONLY involved lymph node?","options":["Uterine LN","Obturator LN","Para-aortic LN","Inguinal LN"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the m/c (most common) lymph node involved in cervix cancer = obturator LN.  Why A wrong: Uterine LN is the FIRST lymph node involved, not the most common.  Why C wrong: Para-aortic LN is not cited as most common here.  Why D wrong: Inguinal LN is not cited as most common here."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In lymphatic spread of cancer cervix, the FIRST lymph node to be involved is the:","options":["Obturator LN","Uterine LN","Inguinal LN","Sentinel axillary LN"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the 1st LN involved in cervix cancer = uterine LN.  Why A wrong: Obturator LN is the most commonly involved, not the first.  Why C wrong: Inguinal LN is not cited as first.  Why D wrong: Axillary nodes relate to breast, not cervix."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON route of spread of cancer OVARY?","options":["Direct spread","Lymphatic spread","Peritoneal spread","Perineural spread"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the most common route of spread of cancer ovary as peritoneal spread.  Why A wrong: Direct spread is the endometrial route.  Why B wrong: Lymphatic spread is the cervical route.  Why D wrong: Perineural spread is not the cited ovarian route."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the FIRST investigation for suspected cancer ENDOMETRIUM?","options":["Trans-vaginal ultrasound","Pap smear","CA-125","Colposcopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists trans-vaginal ultrasound as the first investigation for cancer endometrium.  Why B wrong: Pap smear is the screening test for cervix.  Why C wrong: CA-125 is the ovarian marker.  Why D wrong: Colposcopy is used in cervical cancer work-up."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the Investigation of Choice (IOC) / gold standard for cancer ENDOMETRIUM, described as an OPD procedure needing no anesthesia?","options":["Dilatation and curettage","Endometrial sampling / endometrial aspiration biopsy","Histopathological examination under anesthesia","Trans-vaginal ultrasound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists endometrial sampling / endometrial aspiration biopsy as the IOC (OPD procedure, no anesthesia, gold standard).  Why A wrong: Dilatation and curettage requires the OT and anesthesia; the OPD gold-standard IOC is aspiration biopsy.  Why C wrong: HPE (OT procedure, anesthesia needed) is downstream, not the OPD IOC.  Why D wrong: Trans-vaginal ultrasound is the first investigation, not the gold-standard IOC."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In the endometrial cancer work-up, dilatation and curettage followed by histopathological examination (HPE) is described as which type of procedure?","options":["OPD procedure, no anesthesia","OT procedure, anesthesia needed","Bedside procedure, local anesthesia only","Home-based procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states dilatation and curettage → HPE is an OT procedure with anesthesia needed.  Why A wrong: The OPD, no-anesthesia procedure is endometrial aspiration biopsy.  Why C wrong: It requires OT and anesthesia, not merely local.  Why D wrong: It is not a home procedure."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the screening/first investigation for cancer CERVIX?","options":["CA-125","Screening Pap smear","Trans-vaginal ultrasound","Endometrial biopsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists screening Pap smear as the first investigation for cancer cervix.  Why A wrong: CA-125 is the ovarian marker.  Why B wrong: (Correct answer.)  Why C wrong: Trans-vaginal ultrasound is the first investigation for endometrium/ovary, not cervix screening.  Why D wrong: Endometrial biopsy is for endometrium."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In the cervix cancer pathway, after a positive Pap smear report, the DEFINITIVE test that follows is:","options":["Colposcopy","Trans-vaginal ultrasound","CA-125","Endometrial aspiration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows: screening Pap smear → positive report → definitive test → biopsy → colposcopy; colposcopy is the definitive test used to visualise and biopsy the cervix.  Why B wrong: Trans-vaginal ultrasound is not the cervical definitive test.  Why C wrong: CA-125 is the ovarian marker.  Why D wrong: Endometrial aspiration is for endometrial cancer."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the first investigation for cancer OVARY, and what follow-up is used in a post-menopausal female?","options":["Trans-vaginal ultrasound; then CA-125 levels","Pap smear; then colposcopy","Endometrial biopsy; then D&C","Mammography; then MRI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists trans-vaginal ultrasound as the first ovarian investigation, followed by CA-125 levels in a post-menopausal female.  Why B wrong: Pap smear/colposcopy is the cervical pathway.  Why C wrong: Endometrial biopsy/D&C is the endometrial pathway.  Why D wrong: Mammography/MRI is not the ovarian pathway described."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The staging procedure for cancer ENDOMETRIUM involves removing the uterus, both tubes and both ovaries, sending the specimen for HPE, and is a form of:","options":["Clinical staging","Surgical staging","Radiological staging","Cytological staging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes endometrial staging as remove uterus → both tubes and ovaries → specimen for HPE → pathologist tells stage → surgical staging.  Why A wrong: Clinical staging is used for cancer cervix.  Why C wrong: Radiological alone is not the described staging type.  Why D wrong: Cytological is not the described staging type."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"Which type of staging is used for cancer CERVIX?","options":["Surgical staging","Clinical staging","Cytological staging","Molecular staging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cervix staging pathway (Pap smear → colposcopy → CT/MRI/PET → staging) ends in clinical staging.  Why A wrong: Surgical staging is used for endometrium and ovary.  Why C wrong: Cytological staging is not the described method.  Why D wrong: Molecular staging is not described."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In staging of cancer cervix, which imaging modalities are used after a positive colposcopy? (Select all that apply)","options":["CT","MRI","PET scan","DEXA scan","Bone densitometry"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists CT, MRI, PET scan etc. as imaging used in cervical cancer staging.  Why D wrong: DEXA scan measures bone density, not used for cervical staging here.  Why E wrong: Bone densitometry is not listed for cervical staging."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In the cervix cancer staging pathway, colposcopy is described as the:","options":["Screening test","Definitive test","Tumor marker","Treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The staging flow labels colposcopy as the definitive test after a positive Pap smear.  Why A wrong: Pap smear is the screening test.  Why C wrong: Colposcopy is a visualisation procedure, not a tumor marker.  Why D wrong: It is diagnostic, not a treatment."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The staging procedure for cancer OVARY additionally requires removal of which structure beyond the uterus, tubes, and both ovaries?","options":["Omentum","Cervix stump only","Bladder","Rectum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes ovarian staging as remove uterus + both tubes + both ovaries + omentum → specimen for HPE → surgical staging.  Why B wrong: Cervix stump removal is not specified as the extra step.  Why C wrong: Bladder removal is not part of the described staging.  Why D wrong: Rectum removal is not part of the described staging."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the treatment (after surgery) for cancer ENDOMETRIUM?","options":["Radiotherapy","Chemotherapy","Hormone therapy only","No further treatment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists treatment after surgery for cancer endometrium as radiotherapy.  Why B wrong: Chemotherapy is the post-surgical treatment for cancer ovary.  Why C wrong: Hormone therapy alone is not the cited post-surgical treatment.  Why D wrong: Radiotherapy follows surgery; it is not \"no treatment.\""},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In EARLY-stage cancer cervix, the surgical treatment of choice is:","options":["Wertheim (radical) hysterectomy","Simple total hysterectomy only","Chemotherapy alone","Radiotherapy alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states earlier stages of cancer cervix are treated with advanced/radical hysterectomy (Wertheim hysterectomy).  Why B wrong: A simple hysterectomy is inadequate; a radical (Wertheim) hysterectomy is used.  Why C wrong: Chemotherapy alone is not the early-stage treatment.  Why D wrong: Radiotherapy is reserved for advanced stages, not early-stage surgery."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"The radical hysterectomy used in early-stage cancer cervix is also known as:","options":["Wertheim hysterectomy","Subtotal hysterectomy","Vaginal hysterectomy","Manchester repair"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table names the advanced/radical hysterectomy as Wertheim hysterectomy.  Why B wrong: A subtotal hysterectomy leaves the cervix and is not radical.  Why C wrong: Vaginal hysterectomy is a route, not the named radical procedure.  Why D wrong: Manchester repair is for prolapse, not cervical cancer."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In ADVANCED-stage cancer cervix, which of the following is TRUE regarding management?","options":["Surgery is the treatment of choice","No surgery is done; radiotherapy is used","Only chemotherapy is used with no radiotherapy","Only hormone therapy is used"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states in advanced stages there is no surgery and radiotherapy is done.  Why A wrong: Surgery is not done in advanced stages.  Why C wrong: Radiotherapy is the backbone; chemotherapy (cisplatin) is added as a radiosensitizer.  Why D wrong: Hormone therapy is not the described approach."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In advanced cancer cervix, which drug is given before radiotherapy to increase the sensitivity of cells to radiation (acting as a radiosensitizer)?","options":["Cisplatin","Methotrexate","Progesterone","Tamoxifen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states cisplatin is given to increase sensitivity of cells to radiotherapy, acting as a radiosensitizer.  Why B wrong: Methotrexate is not the cited radiosensitizer here.  Why C wrong: Progesterone treats endometrial hyperplasia without atypia.  Why D wrong: Tamoxifen is a breast cancer hormone therapy, not the cited radiosensitizer."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"When cisplatin is combined with radiotherapy in advanced cancer cervix, the combined modality is best called:","options":["Chemoradiation (the best treatment)","Neoadjuvant chemotherapy","Palliative radiotherapy only","Hormone therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states that instead of radiotherapy alone it is better to call the combination chemoradiation (the best answer), with radiotherapy alone being the 2nd best answer.  Why B wrong: The notes label the combination \"chemoradiation,\" not neoadjuvant chemotherapy.  Why C wrong: Radiotherapy alone is the 2nd best, not the best combined approach.  Why D wrong: Hormone therapy is not described here."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In advanced cancer cervix, if chemoradiation is the best treatment, what is the SECOND best answer?","options":["Radiotherapy (alone)","Surgery","Chemotherapy alone","Hormone therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table explicitly states 2nd best answer = radiotherapy.  Why B wrong: Surgery is not done in advanced stages.  Why C wrong: Chemotherapy alone is not the 2nd best; radiotherapy is.  Why D wrong: Hormone therapy is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the treatment (after surgery) for cancer OVARY?","options":["Radiotherapy","Chemotherapy","Progesterone","Cisplatin radiosensitization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists treatment after surgery for cancer ovary as chemotherapy (ovarian tumors are radioresistant).  Why A wrong: Radiotherapy is the endometrial post-surgical treatment; most ovarian tumors are radioresistant.  Why C wrong: Progesterone treats endometrial hyperplasia.  Why D wrong: Cisplatin radiosensitization is the cervical advanced-stage approach."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON cause of death in cancer CERVIX?","options":["Renal failure","Haemorrhage","Sepsis","Pulmonary embolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists renal failure as the most common cause of death in cancer cervix (due to ureteric obstruction).  Why B wrong: Haemorrhage is the 2nd most common cause of death.  Why C wrong: Sepsis is not cited as the most common cause.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary embolism is not cited as the most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"What is the SECOND most common cause of death in cancer cervix?","options":["Renal failure","Haemorrhage","Cardiac arrest","Infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the 2nd most common cause of death in cancer cervix as haemorrhage.  Why A wrong: Renal failure is the most common (first), not second.  Why C wrong: Cardiac arrest is not cited.  Why D wrong: Infection is not cited as the second most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"In the comparison table, the \"most common cause of death\" row is marked ✗ (not specified) for which cancers? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cancer endometrium","Cancer cervix","Cancer ovary","None of them"],"correct":"A,C","rationale":"Why correct: The row marks cancer endometrium and cancer ovary with ✗ (not specified), while cancer cervix has renal failure/haemorrhage listed.  Why B wrong: Cancer cervix HAS a specified cause of death (renal failure, then haemorrhage).  Why D wrong: Two cancers (endometrium, ovary) are marked ✗."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"A post-menopausal woman presents with painless vaginal bleeding. Trans-vaginal ultrasound is done first, followed by endometrial aspiration biopsy. Which cancer is most likely being investigated?","options":["Cancer cervix","Cancer endometrium","Cancer ovary","Vulvar cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Post-menopausal bleeding is the most common symptom of cancer endometrium, and trans-vaginal ultrasound + endometrial aspiration biopsy is its investigation pathway.  Why A wrong: Cancer cervix presents with post-coital bleeding and is screened by Pap smear.  Why C wrong: Cancer ovary presents with vague symptoms, not post-menopausal bleeding.  Why D wrong: Vulvar cancer is not part of this comparison and does not fit the pathway."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"A sexually active woman presents with post-coital bleeding. A Pap smear is positive. What is the next best step per the notes?","options":["Immediate hysterectomy","Colposcopy (definitive test) with biopsy","CA-125 measurement","Endometrial aspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Post-coital bleeding suggests cancer cervix; after a positive Pap smear the pathway proceeds to colposcopy (definitive test) with biopsy.  Why A wrong: Diagnosis must be confirmed by colposcopy/biopsy and staging before hysterectomy.  Why C wrong: CA-125 is the ovarian marker, not the cervical next step.  Why D wrong: Endometrial aspiration is for endometrial cancer."},{"section":"Batch 089 · Gynaecological cancers comparison — body build & protective factors…","question":"A 65-year-old woman has vague abdominal symptoms with appetite loss and a solid, unilateral ovarian mass with ascites. Trans-vaginal ultrasound is done and CA-125 is 60 IU. Which finding supports ovarian malignancy?","options":["CA-125 ≥ 35 IU in a post-menopausal female","CA-125 < 35 IU","Absence of ascites","A cystic bilateral mobile mass"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A CA-125 ≥35 IU in a post-menopausal female is positive for ovarian cancer, and the solid unilateral mass with ascites fits the premalignant/malignant ovarian description.  Why B wrong: <35 IU would not meet the positive threshold.  Why C wrong: Ascites is present and supports malignancy, so its absence would not.  Why D wrong: A cystic, freely mobile bilateral mass describes the benign/Krukenberg-type features, not this solid unilateral malignancy."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"The cervix is anatomically divided into how many parts?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state the cervix has 2 parts — the part near to the uterus and the part inside the vagina.  Why A wrong: A single undivided part does not match the anatomical division described.  Why C wrong: Three parts is not the described division.  Why D wrong: Four parts overstates the anatomical division."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"The part of the cervix that lies near to the uterus is known as the:","options":["Exocervix","Portio vaginalis","Endocervix","Transformation zone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The part of the cervix near to the uterus is called the endocervix.  Why A wrong: The exocervix is the part inside the vagina, not near the uterus.  Why B wrong: Portio vaginalis is another name for the exocervix (the vaginal part).  Why D wrong: The transformation zone is the junction between the two epithelia, not the uterine part."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"The part of the cervix that lies inside the vagina is called the exocervix or:","options":["Endocervix","Portio vaginalis","Isthmus","Internal os"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The part inside the vagina is the exocervix, also known as the portio vaginalis.  Why A wrong: The endocervix is the part near the uterus.  Why C wrong: The isthmus is not the term used for the vaginal part of the cervix here.  Why D wrong: The internal os is an opening, not the vaginal portion of the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"The lining epithelium of the endocervix is:","options":["Squamous epithelium","Columnar epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Cuboidal epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The endocervix is lined by columnar epithelium.  Why A wrong: Squamous epithelium lines the exocervix, not the endocervix.  Why C wrong: Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract, not the endocervix.  Why D wrong: Cuboidal epithelium is not the lining of the endocervix per the notes."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"On per speculum examination, the columnar epithelium of the endocervix appears which colour?","options":["Pink","White","Red","Yellow"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The columnar epithelium of the endocervix appears red in colour on per speculum examination.  Why A wrong: Pink is the colour of the squamous epithelium of the exocervix.  Why B wrong: White/pale is characteristic of the transformation zone, not the columnar endocervix.  Why D wrong: Yellow is not described for the endocervix."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"The lining epithelium of the exocervix (portio vaginalis) is:","options":["Columnar epithelium","Squamous epithelium","Ciliated epithelium","Glandular epithelium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The exocervix is lined by squamous epithelium.  Why A wrong: Columnar epithelium lines the endocervix.  Why C wrong: Ciliated epithelium lines structures like the fallopian tubes, not the exocervix.  Why D wrong: Glandular epithelium is not the described lining of the exocervix."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"On per speculum examination, the squamous epithelium of the exocervix appears which colour?","options":["Red","Pink","White","Blue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The squamous epithelium of the exocervix appears pink in colour on per speculum examination.  Why A wrong: Red is the colour of the columnar (endocervix) epithelium.  Why C wrong: White is characteristic of the transformation zone.  Why D wrong: Blue is not described for the exocervix."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"On per speculum examination, which structure is seen as the central opening surrounded by the pink-coloured exocervix?","options":["Internal os","External os","Isthmus","Fornix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the central opening as the external os, with the surrounding pink area being the exocervix and the reddish central canal the endocervix.  Why A wrong: The internal os is at the uterine end and is not visualized on speculum examination.  Why C wrong: The isthmus is above the cervix, not seen as the central speculum opening.  Why D wrong: The fornix is the vaginal recess around the cervix, not the central opening."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"The place where the columnar epithelium of the endocervix changes to the squamous epithelium of the exocervix is called the:","options":["External os","Squamocolumnar junction / Transformation zone","Internal os","Endometrial junction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This junction is called the squamocolumnar junction, also known as the transformation zone.  Why A wrong: The external os is the opening of the cervix, not the epithelial junction.  Why C wrong: The internal os is the uterine opening of the cervical canal.  Why D wrong: \"Endometrial junction\" is not a recognized term for this location."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Where does the transformation zone of the cervix lie?","options":["Near the internal os","Near the external os","In the uterine cavity","At the vaginal fornix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The transformation zone lies near the external os.  Why A wrong: It lies near the external os, not the internal os.  Why C wrong: It is a cervical structure, not located in the uterine cavity.  Why D wrong: It is at the external os region, not the vaginal fornix."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"On per speculum examination, the transformation zone appears which colour?","options":["Red","Pink","White/pale","Yellow"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The transformation zone appears white/pale on per speculum examination.  Why A wrong: Red is the columnar/endocervix colour.  Why B wrong: Pink is the squamous/exocervix colour.  Why D wrong: Yellow is not the described colour of the transformation zone."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which is the most common site of cancer cervix (squamous cell variety)?","options":["Endocervix","Internal os","Transformation zone","Vaginal fornix"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The transformation zone is the most common site of cancer cervix of the squamous cell variety.  Why A wrong: The endocervix is the most common site of adenocarcinoma, not the squamous cell variety.  Why B wrong: The internal os is not the most common site.  Why D wrong: The vaginal fornix is not the most common site of cancer cervix."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"The change from columnar epithelium of the endocervix to squamous epithelium is termed:","options":["Dysplasia","Metaplasia","Hyperplasia","Neoplasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This physiological change in epithelium type is called metaplasia.  Why A wrong: Dysplasia is disorganized (precancerous) change, occurring only when HPV is present.  Why C wrong: Hyperplasia is an increase in cell number, not a change in epithelial type.  Why D wrong: Neoplasia refers to new abnormal growth (cancer), not the physiological metaplastic change."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which of the following is TRUE about metaplasia in the cervix?","options":["It happens in only a few females","It is pathological and precancerous","It happens in all females and is physiological","It occurs only in HIV-positive females"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Metaplasia happens in all females, is physiological, and is not precancerous.  Why A wrong: It occurs in all females, not just a few (that describes dysplasia).  Why B wrong: Metaplasia is physiological and NOT precancerous.  Why D wrong: It is a universal physiological process, not limited to HIV-positive females."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"During metaplasia, which infection, if present, causes the metaplasia to occur in a disorganized manner (dysplasia)?","options":["Chlamydia","HPV (Human Papilloma Virus)","HIV","Herpes simplex virus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If HPV infection is present during metaplasia, the metaplasia occurs in a disorganized manner, called dysplasia.  Why A wrong: Chlamydia is not the causative infection described for dysplasia here.  Why C wrong: HIV affects screening frequency but is not the infection driving disorganized metaplasia.  Why D wrong: Herpes simplex virus is not the described trigger for dysplasia."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"When metaplasia occurs in a disorganized manner due to HPV, the resulting condition is called:","options":["Metaplasia","Hyperplasia","Dysplasia","Hypertrophy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Disorganized metaplasia caused by HPV is called dysplasia.  Why A wrong: Metaplasia is the organized physiological change; disorganized change is dysplasia.  Why B wrong: Hyperplasia is increased cell number, not disorganized metaplasia.  Why D wrong: Hypertrophy is increased cell size, not the described condition."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding cervical dysplasia?","options":["It happens in all females","It is physiological","It is precancerous and happens in few females","It is not precancerous"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dysplasia happens in few females, is not physiological, and is precancerous.  Why A wrong: It happens only in few females, unlike metaplasia which occurs in all.  Why B wrong: Dysplasia is NOT physiological.  Why D wrong: Dysplasia IS precancerous."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Cervical dysplasia is classified into how many grades/types?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The notes classify dysplasia into 4 grades/types.  Why A wrong: Two grades understates the classification.  Why B wrong: Three grades corresponds to the CIN 1/2/3 subset but the notes list 4 grades/types (adding carcinoma in situ on page 3).  Why D wrong: Five grades overstates the classification."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Dysplasia involving less than 1/3 of the cervical epithelium is classified as:","options":["CIN 1","CIN 2","CIN 3","Cancer in situ"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Involvement of less than 1/3 of the cervical epithelium is CIN 1.  Why B wrong: CIN 2 involves 1/3 to 2/3 of the epithelium.  Why C wrong: CIN 3 involves ≥2/3 of the epithelium.  Why D wrong: Cancer in situ involves the entire epithelium with intact overlying membrane."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Dysplasia involving 1/3 to 2/3 of the cervical epithelium is classified as:","options":["CIN 1","CIN 2","CIN 3","Cancer in situ"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Involvement of 1/3 to 2/3 of the cervical epithelium is CIN 2.  Why A wrong: CIN 1 involves less than 1/3.  Why C wrong: CIN 3 involves ≥2/3.  Why D wrong: Cancer in situ involves the full thickness of epithelium."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Dysplasia involving ≥2/3 of the cervical epithelium is classified as:","options":["CIN 1","CIN 2","CIN 3","Metaplasia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Involvement of ≥2/3 of the epithelium is CIN 3.  Why A wrong: CIN 1 involves less than 1/3.  Why B wrong: CIN 2 involves 1/3 to 2/3.  Why D wrong: Metaplasia is a physiological change, not a grade of dysplasia."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"CIN\" stand for in cervical pathology?","options":["Cervical Invasive Neoplasm","Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia","Cervical Inflammatory Nodule","Columnar Intraepithelial Necrosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CIN stands for Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia, the grading system for cervical dysplasia.  Why A wrong: CIN denotes intraepithelial (pre-invasive) neoplasia, not invasive neoplasm.  Why C wrong: CIN is not an inflammatory nodule.  Why D wrong: This expansion is fabricated and incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"When the entire cervical epithelium becomes dysplastic but the overlying (basement) membrane remains intact, the condition is called:","options":["Invasive cancer cervix","Cancer in situ","CIN 1","Metaplasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the entire epithelium is dysplastic and the overlying membrane is intact, it is cancer in situ.  Why A wrong: Invasive cancer cervix occurs when the overlying membrane is broken.  Why C wrong: CIN 1 involves less than 1/3 of the epithelium, not the entire thickness.  Why D wrong: Metaplasia is a physiological change, not full-thickness dysplasia."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"When the entire cervical epithelium is dysplastic AND the overlying membrane is broken, the condition is called:","options":["Cancer in situ","CIN 3","Invasive cancer cervix","Metaplasia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A broken overlying membrane with full-thickness dysplasia signifies invasive cancer cervix.  Why A wrong: Cancer in situ has an intact overlying membrane.  Why B wrong: CIN 3 is precancerous, not invasive cancer.  Why D wrong: Metaplasia is not cancer."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which of the following are grouped as precancerous conditions of the cervix? (Select All That Apply)","options":["CIN 1","CIN 2","CIN 3","Cancer in situ","Invasive cancer cervix"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The notes group CIN 1, CIN 2, CIN 3, and cancer in situ together as precancerous conditions.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four (CIN 1, CIN 2, CIN 3, and cancer in situ) are bracketed as precancerous.  Why E wrong: Invasive cancer cervix is actual cancer (membrane broken), not a precancerous condition."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"As per the new Bethesda classification, CIN-1 is called:","options":["HSIL (High grade squamous intraepithelial lesion)","LSIL (Low grade squamous intraepithelial lesion)","ASCUS","Carcinoma in situ"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the Bethesda classification, CIN-1 is called LSIL (Low squamous/low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion).  Why A wrong: HSIL corresponds to CIN 2 and CIN 3, not CIN 1.  Why C wrong: ASCUS is not the Bethesda term equated with CIN 1 in these notes.  Why D wrong: Carcinoma in situ is a separate entity, not the Bethesda name for CIN 1."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"As per the Bethesda classification, CIN-2 and CIN-3 together are called:","options":["LSIL","HSIL (High squamous intraepithelial lesion)","ASCUS","AGC"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CIN-2 and CIN-3 are grouped together as HSIL (High squamous/high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion).  Why A wrong: LSIL corresponds to CIN-1 only.  Why C wrong: ASCUS is not the term for CIN 2/3 in these notes.  Why D wrong: AGC (atypical glandular cells) is not equated with CIN 2/3 here."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"According to the notes, what is the typical age range for CIN 1, CIN 2, and CIN 3?","options":["15–20 years","21–30 years","30–35 years","35–39 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CIN 1, CIN 2, and CIN 3 occur at an age of 21–30 years.  Why A wrong: 15–20 years is not the range given.  Why C wrong: 30–35 years is the age for cancer in situ.  Why D wrong: 35–39 years is one of the age ranges for cancer cervix."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"According to the notes, the typical age for cancer in situ is:","options":["21–30 years","30–35 years","35–39 years","60–65 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cancer in situ occurs at an age of 30–35 years.  Why A wrong: 21–30 years corresponds to CIN 1/2/3.  Why C wrong: 35–39 years is an age range for cancer cervix (invasive).  Why D wrong: 60–65 years is the other peak age range for cancer cervix."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"According to the notes, what is the approximate time taken for progression from CIN/cancer-in-situ stages to cancer cervix?","options":["1–2 years","5–8 years","10–15 years","20–25 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The bracket spanning CIN stages and cancer in situ progression to cancer cervix is labeled 10–15 years.  Why A wrong: 1–2 years is too short for the described progression.  Why B wrong: 5–8 years is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 20–25 years overstates the described interval."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"According to the notes, the peak age ranges for invasive cancer cervix are:","options":["21–30 years and 30–35 years","35–39 years and 60–65 years","15–20 years and 40–45 years","45–50 years and 70–75 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cancer cervix age is given as 35–39 years and 60–65 years.  Why A wrong: These are the CIN and cancer-in-situ ages, not invasive cancer cervix.  Why C wrong: These ranges are not mentioned.  Why D wrong: These ranges are not mentioned in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Regarding screening for cancer cervix, which principle is emphasized in the notes?","options":["Only high-risk women should be screened","Universal screening — all females should be screened","Only women with symptoms should be screened","Only postmenopausal women should be screened"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes state universal screening — all females should be screened.  Why A wrong: Screening is universal, not limited to high-risk women.  Why C wrong: Screening is done in all females, not only symptomatic ones.  Why D wrong: Screening begins much earlier (at 21 years), not only postmenopausal."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"At what age should cervical cancer screening begin?","options":["18 years","21 years","25 years","30 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The age to begin screening is 21 years.  Why A wrong: 18 years is not the recommended starting age.  Why C wrong: 25 years is not the starting age in these notes.  Why D wrong: 30 years is when co-testing begins, not when screening begins."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which screening test is used to begin cervical cancer screening at age 21 years?","options":["HPV DNA test","Pap smear","Colposcopy","Endometrial biopsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Screening begins at 21 years with the Pap smear.  Why A wrong: HPV DNA testing is added only from age 30 (co-testing), not at the start.  Why C wrong: Colposcopy is a diagnostic follow-up, not the initial screening test.  Why D wrong: Endometrial biopsy assesses the endometrium, not cervical screening."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"If the Pap smear is normal in a woman aged 21–30 years, how often should it be repeated?","options":["Every year","Every 3 years","Every 5 years","Every 10 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A normal Pap smear should be repeated every 3 years (up to 30 years of age).  Why A wrong: Annual screening applies to HIV-positive women, not routine screening.  Why C wrong: The 5-year interval applies to co-testing from age 30 onwards.  Why D wrong: A 10-year interval is not recommended for Pap smear."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Screening with Pap smear alone (repeated every 3 years) is continued until what age?","options":["25 years","30 years","35 years","40 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pap smear every 3 years is done till 30 years of age.  Why A wrong: 25 years is not the cut-off for this interval.  Why C wrong: 35 years is not the transition point; co-testing starts at 30.  Why D wrong: 40 years is not the described cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"From 30 years of age, cervical cancer screening should be done with Pap smear PLUS which additional test (co-testing)?","options":["HPV DNA testing","CA-125 assay","Endometrial aspiration","Serum estrogen level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: From 30 years, Pap smear + HPV DNA testing (co-testing) should be done.  Why B wrong: CA-125 is an ovarian cancer marker, not part of cervical co-testing.  Why C wrong: Endometrial aspiration assesses the endometrium, not cervical screening.  Why D wrong: Serum estrogen level is not part of cervical co-testing."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"The combination of Pap smear and HPV DNA testing done from age 30 years is referred to as:","options":["Screening","Co-testing","Triple test","Reflex testing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Combining Pap smear with HPV DNA testing is called co-testing.  Why A wrong: \"Screening\" is the general term; the specific combined method is co-testing.  Why C wrong: \"Triple test\" is not the term used in the notes.  Why D wrong: \"Reflex testing\" is not the term used here."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"If co-testing (Pap smear + HPV DNA) is normal from age 30, how often should it be repeated?","options":["Every year","Every 3 years","Every 5 years","Every 10 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If co-testing is normal, it should be repeated every 5 years.  Why A wrong: Annual screening is specifically for HIV-positive women.  Why B wrong: The 3-year interval applies to Pap smear alone (age 21–30).  Why D wrong: A 10-year interval is not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Cervical cancer screening should be continued until what age?","options":["55 years","60 years","65 years","70 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Screening is to be done till 65 years of age.  Why A wrong: 55 years is not the stopping age.  Why B wrong: 60 years is not the described cut-off.  Why D wrong: 70 years is beyond the recommended stopping age."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"In HIV-positive females, how frequently should cervical cancer screening be done?","options":["Every 3 years","Every 5 years","Annual (every year) screening","Only once at age 30"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: HIV-positive females require annual (yearly) screening.  Why A wrong: The 3-year interval is for routine Pap smear in immunocompetent women.  Why B wrong: The 5-year interval is for co-testing in immunocompetent women.  Why D wrong: HIV-positive women need frequent (annual) screening, not a one-time test."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"In females who have had a hysterectomy for fibroid or prolapse, cervical cancer screening should be:","options":["Continued annually","Continued every 5 years","Stopped after hysterectomy","Started only after age 65"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In women who had a hysterectomy for fibroid/prolapse, screening should be stopped after hysterectomy.  Why A wrong: Screening is stopped, not continued annually.  Why B wrong: Screening is stopped, not continued at 5-year intervals.  Why D wrong: There is no recommendation to start at 65; screening is stopped."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"After a woman receives the HPV vaccine, what should happen to cervical cancer screening?","options":["Screening is no longer needed","Screening should be continued as usual","Screening should be done only every 10 years","Screening should stop at age 30"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After HPV vaccine, screening should be continued as usual.  Why A wrong: Vaccination does not eliminate the need for screening.  Why C wrong: No special 10-year interval applies after vaccination.  Why D wrong: Screening continues as usual, it does not stop at 30."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"For a Pap smear, \"Sample 1\" is intended to detect the most common site of cancer cervix. From which site is this sample taken?","options":["Endocervix","Transformation zone (near external os)","Internal os","Vaginal fornix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of cancer cervix is the transformation zone, located near the external os; Sample 1 is taken from there.  Why A wrong: The endocervix is the site sampled for adenocarcinoma (Sample 2).  Why C wrong: The internal os is not the sampling site.  Why D wrong: The vaginal fornix is not the described Pap smear sampling site."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"For a Pap smear, \"Sample 2\" targets the most common site of adenocarcinoma. From which site is this sample taken?","options":["Transformation zone","External os","Endocervix","Vaginal wall"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of adenocarcinoma is the endocervix, so Sample 2 is taken from the endocervix.  Why A wrong: The transformation zone is the site for the squamous cell cancer sample (Sample 1).  Why B wrong: The external os is where Sample 1 is taken.  Why D wrong: The vaginal wall is not the described sampling site."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which is the most common site of adenocarcinoma of the cervix?","options":["Transformation zone","Exocervix","Endocervix","External os"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The endocervix is the most common site of adenocarcinoma.  Why A wrong: The transformation zone is the most common site of squamous cell cancer cervix.  Why B wrong: The exocervix is the vaginal squamous portion, not the adenocarcinoma site.  Why D wrong: The external os is a sampling landmark, not the adenocarcinoma site."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which instrument is used to collect the cervical (squamous) sample during a Pap smear?","options":["Cusco speculum","Ayers spatula","Uterine sound","Punch biopsy forceps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ayers spatula, which has a bifid end, is used to take the cervical smear.  Why A wrong: A Cusco speculum exposes the cervix but does not collect the smear.  Why C wrong: A uterine sound measures uterine cavity depth, not for Pap smear.  Why D wrong: Punch biopsy forceps take a tissue biopsy, not a cytology smear."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"What is a distinctive feature of the Ayers spatula used for Pap smear?","options":["It has a pointed sharp end","It has a bifid end","It has a hollow needle tip","It has a balloon tip"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ayers spatula has a bifid end, used to collect the cervical smear near the external os.  Why A wrong: It is not a pointed sharp-ended instrument.  Why C wrong: It has no hollow needle; it is a solid spatula.  Why D wrong: It has no balloon tip."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"During Pap smear collection with the Ayers spatula, the bifid end is placed in the cervix near the external os and then rotated through:","options":["90°","180°","270°","360°"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The spatula is rotated 360° to obtain the cervical smear.  Why A wrong: 90° would not sample the full circumference.  Why B wrong: 180° is only half a rotation, insufficient.  Why C wrong: 270° is not the described rotation."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Arrange the correct sequence of Pap smear collection using the Ayers spatula.","options":["Rotate 360° → Take in cervix near external os → Put on slide → Cervical smear","Take it in cervix near the external os → Rotate 360° → Cervical smear → Put on slide","Put on slide → Rotate 360° → Take in cervix → Cervical smear","Cervical smear → Put on slide → Take in cervix → Rotate 360°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The correct order is: take it in the cervix near the external os, rotate 360°, obtain the cervical smear, then put on the slide.  Why A wrong: The rotation cannot precede placement in the cervix.  Why C wrong: Putting on the slide cannot be the first step.  Why D wrong: This reverses the entire logical order."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"In the cervical epithelium diagram for Pap smear, the metaplastic cells and squamous cells are located near which landmark?","options":["Internal os","External os","Uterine fundus","Vaginal introitus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels the external os (Ext os) with metaplastic cells and squamous cells in that region, with columnar cells toward the endocervical canal.  Why A wrong: The internal os is at the uterine end, not the labeled sampling landmark.  Why C wrong: The uterine fundus is unrelated to cervical smear sampling.  Why D wrong: The vaginal introitus is the vaginal opening, not the cervical sampling site."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which three cell types are labeled around the cervical os in the Pap smear cytology diagram? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Metaplastic cells","Squamous cells","Columnar cells","Transitional cells","Ciliated cells"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels metaplastic cells, squamous cells, and columnar cells around the cervical os.  Why A/B/C correct: These three cell types are explicitly labeled.  Why D wrong: Transitional cells are not labeled in the diagram.  Why E wrong: Ciliated cells are not labeled in the diagram."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which cytology technique is described as an alternative sampling/preparation method where the sample is placed into a liquid medium?","options":["Conventional Pap smear","Liquid-based cytology","Frozen section","Immunohistochemistry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The notes describe liquid-based cytology as the method taken (\"Now Take: Liquid Based Cytology\").  Why A wrong: The conventional Pap smear is the direct-on-slide method; this section highlights liquid-based cytology.  Why C wrong: Frozen section is an intraoperative histology technique, not cervical cytology sampling.  Why D wrong: Immunohistochemistry is a staining method, not a cervical sample collection technique."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"In liquid-based cytology, which instrument is used to collect the endocervical sample?","options":["Ayers spatula","Endocervical brush / cytobrush","Uterine curette","Cusco speculum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The endocervical brush/cytobrush is used to take the sample from the endocervix.  Why A wrong: The Ayers spatula is used for the ectocervical (squamous) smear, not the endocervical brush sample.  Why C wrong: A uterine curette scrapes the endometrium, not for cervical cytology.  Why D wrong: A Cusco speculum only exposes the cervix."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Arrange the correct sequence for endocervical brush/cytobrush sampling in liquid-based cytology.","options":["Rotate → Take it in Endocervix → Spread on slide → Send for examination","Take it in Endocervix → Rotate → Spread it on some slide → Send slide for histopathological examination","Spread on slide → Take in endocervix → Rotate → Send for examination","Send for examination → Rotate → Take in endocervix → Spread on slide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow is: take it in the endocervix → rotate → spread it on some slide → send the slide for histopathological examination.  Why A wrong: Rotation cannot occur before the brush is placed in the endocervix.  Why C wrong: Spreading on slide cannot be the first step.  Why D wrong: Sending for examination cannot be the first step."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which of the following are types of sampling devices shown for cervical cytology? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Cervex brush","Cytobrush","Plastic spatula","Wooden spatula","Bone marrow needle"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The figure \"Different types of sampling device\" shows the cervex brush, cytobrush, plastic spatula, and wooden spatula.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly illustrated as sampling devices.  Why E wrong: A bone marrow needle is not a cervical sampling device and is not shown."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"A nurse is preparing a woman for a Pap smear. Which of the following should NOT be done before the procedure? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Perform a per vaginal (bimanual) examination","Apply a lubricant","Air dry the slide","Keep the woman NPO for 8 hours","Collect a full history"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Before doing a Pap smear one should NOT do a per vaginal examination, NOT use lubricant, and NOT air dry the slide.  Why A/B/C correct: These three are the explicitly prohibited pre-Pap-smear actions.  Why D wrong: NPO status is not mentioned as a Pap smear requirement.  Why E wrong: Collecting a history is not prohibited before a Pap smear."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Why should a per vaginal examination and lubricant be avoided before performing a Pap smear?","options":["They cause bleeding that stops the test","They can interfere with/contaminate the cervical cell sample","They increase the risk of infection","They cause pain to the patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Avoiding per vaginal examination and lubricant prevents interference with or contamination of the cervical cytology sample, ensuring accurate results.  Why A wrong: The primary concern is sample contamination/interference, not bleeding halting the test.  Why C wrong: Infection risk is not the reason given for these precautions.  Why D wrong: Pain is not the stated rationale for these precautions."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"Which fixative is used in the Pap smear technique?","options":["10% formalin","95% ethyl alcohol + 5% ether","Bouin's solution","Absolute methanol only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fixative used in Pap smear is 95% ethyl alcohol + 5% ether.  Why A wrong: 10% formalin is a histopathology fixative, not the Pap smear cytology fixative described.  Why C wrong: Bouin's solution is not the Pap smear fixative in these notes.  Why D wrong: The correct fixative combines ethyl alcohol with ether, not methanol alone."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"In the Pap smear fixative, what is the percentage of ethyl alcohol and ether respectively?","options":["90% ethyl alcohol + 10% ether","95% ethyl alcohol + 5% ether","70% ethyl alcohol + 30% ether","50% ethyl alcohol + 50% ether"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fixative is composed of 95% ethyl alcohol plus 5% ether.  Why A wrong: The proportions are 95:5, not 90:10.  Why C wrong: The proportions are 95:5, not 70:30.  Why D wrong: The proportions are 95:5, not 50:50."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"After the cervical smear/slide is prepared, where is it sent?","options":["For culture and sensitivity","For histopathological examination","For blood grouping","For hormone assay"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The slide is sent for histopathological examination.  Why A wrong: Culture and sensitivity is for microbiology, not cervical cytology.  Why C wrong: Blood grouping is unrelated to Pap smear slides.  Why D wrong: Hormone assay is not the destination of the cervical smear slide."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"The title of this section of notes on cancer of the cervix falls under which broader topic?","options":["Physiology of pregnancy","Malignancy of Female Genital Tract","Contraception methods","Normal labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The heading is \"Malignancy of Female Genital Tract (Part-3),\" dealing with cancer cervix.  Why A wrong: These pages address genital tract malignancy, not pregnancy physiology.  Why C wrong: The content is on cancer cervix, not contraception.  Why D wrong: The content is on malignancy, not normal labour."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"A 28-year-old woman with a normal Pap smear at 21 has been screened every 3 years. She is now turning 30. What change in her screening should the nurse anticipate?","options":["Stop screening completely","Switch to Pap smear + HPV DNA co-testing every 5 years","Begin annual Pap smear only","Repeat Pap smear every 10 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: From 30 years, co-testing (Pap smear + HPV DNA) is done and, if normal, repeated every 5 years.  Why A wrong: Screening continues until age 65, not stopped at 30.  Why B: correct as above.  Why C wrong: Annual screening is for HIV-positive women, not the routine change at 30.  Why D wrong: A 10-year interval is not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 090 · Malignancy of female genital tract — anatomy of cervix…","question":"A precancerous cervical lesion where dysplasia involves the entire epithelial thickness but has NOT breached the basement membrane best corresponds to:","options":["Invasive cancer cervix","Carcinoma in situ","CIN 1 / LSIL","Physiological metaplasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Full-thickness dysplasia with an intact overlying (basement) membrane is carcinoma/cancer in situ.  Why A wrong: Invasion of the membrane defines invasive cancer, not in situ disease.  Why C wrong: CIN 1 (LSIL) involves less than 1/3 of the epithelium.  Why D wrong: Metaplasia is a physiological, non-cancerous change."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"According to the Pap smear reporting protocol, if the Pap smear report is NORMAL, how often should it be repeated and up to what age?","options":["Repeat yearly till 21 years of age","Repeat 3 yearly till 30 years of age","Repeat 5 yearly till 45 years of age","Repeat monthly till 25 years of age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states that for a NORMAL Pap smear report, repeat \"3 yearly till = 30 years of age.\"  Why A wrong: The interval is 3-yearly, not yearly, and the cut-off is 30 years, not 21.  Why C wrong: The source specifies a 3-year interval and 30-year cut-off, not 5 yearly till 45.  Why D wrong: Monthly repetition is not indicated for a normal Pap smear."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"An ABNORMAL Pap smear report may show which of the following findings?","options":["LSIL, HSIL, CIN","Only normal squamous cells","Endometrial hyperplasia only","Trophoblastic tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists LSIL, HSIL, CIN under an ABNORMAL Pap smear report.  Why B wrong: Normal squamous cells indicate a normal, not abnormal, report.  Why C wrong: Endometrial hyperplasia is not the finding listed for an abnormal Pap smear here.  Why D wrong: Trophoblastic tissue relates to gestational disease, not cervical Pap cytology abnormality."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"The Pap smear is best described as which type of test?","options":["A confirmatory (diagnostic) test","A treatment procedure","Only a screening test","A staging investigation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source explicitly states \"Pap smear is only a screening test.\"  Why A wrong: Confirmation is done by colposcopy/biopsy, not the Pap smear.  Why B wrong: A Pap smear does not treat; it screens.  Why D wrong: Pap smear does not stage cancer."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"After an abnormal Pap smear, when is treatment given?","options":["Immediately after the Pap smear","No treatment is given till a confirmatory test is done","Only after 3 years of observation","Never, since it is self-limiting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart states \"No treatment is given till confirmatory test is done.\"  Why A wrong: Treatment is withheld until confirmation.  Why C wrong: The delay is for confirmatory testing, not a fixed 3-year wait.  Why D wrong: Treatment may be needed once confirmed; it is not always self-limiting."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"What is the confirmatory test done after an abnormal Pap smear?","options":["Repeat Pap smear","Colposcopy (colposcopic directed biopsy)","Ultrasound","CT scan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the confirmatory test is COLPOSCOPY (colposcopic directed biopsy).  Why A wrong: Repeating a screening test is not confirmatory.  Why C wrong: Ultrasound is not the listed confirmatory test.  Why D wrong: CT scan is not the confirmatory test for an abnormal Pap smear here."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"A colposcope is best described as which kind of instrument?","options":["A cutting instrument","A magnifying instrument","A staining reagent","A suction device"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes the colposcope as a \"Magnifying instrument.\"  Why A wrong: It magnifies for visualization; cutting is done by other tools (e.g., LLETZ loop).  Why C wrong: It is an instrument, not a stain.  Why D wrong: It is not a suction device."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"By how many times does a colposcope magnify the cervix?","options":["2 times","5 times","10 times","20 times"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Magnification 5 times.\"  Why A, C, D wrong: These magnification values are not the figure given in the notes (5 times)."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In which setting is colposcopy performed?","options":["It requires major inpatient admission","It is an OPD (outpatient department) procedure","It requires ICU care","It is done only in an operation theatre under general anesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists colposcopy as an \"OPD procedure.\"  Why A wrong: It does not require major inpatient admission.  Why C wrong: ICU care is not required.  Why D wrong: It is not routinely a GA operation-theatre procedure."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"During colposcopy, what is the first step performed?","options":["Take a biopsy blindly","First see the cervix with the colposcope","Apply Lugol's iodine","Perform hysterectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"First see the cervix with colposcope.\"  Why A wrong: Biopsy is taken from a rough/abnormal area after visualization, not blindly first.  Why C wrong: Lugol's iodine (VILI) is a separate rural screening method, not the first colposcopy step described.  Why D wrong: Hysterectomy is a treatment, not the first colposcopy step."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"During colposcopy, if any rough area of the cervix is seen, what is done?","options":["It is ignored","Take a biopsy from that area","Immediate hysterectomy is performed","Only chemoradiation is started"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if you get any rough area → \"Take biopsy from that area.\"  Why A wrong: A rough area is suspicious and must be biopsied, not ignored.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is not the immediate colposcopy action.  Why D wrong: Chemoradiation is for advanced cancer, not a colposcopy step."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"The colposcope has a special filter that helps visualize abnormal blood vessels. Which filter is it?","options":["Red filter","Green filter","Blue filter","Yellow filter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Colposcope has green filter\" and abnormal blood vessels are seen with it.  Why A, C, D wrong: The notes specify a green filter, not red, blue, or yellow."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"When the colposcope's green filter reveals abnormal blood vessels, what is the next step?","options":["Take biopsy","Discharge the patient","Start antibiotics","Repeat Pap smear only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows abnormal blood vessels seen → \"Take Biopsy.\"  Why B wrong: Abnormal vessels are suspicious and require biopsy, not discharge.  Why C wrong: Antibiotics are not indicated by this finding.  Why D wrong: A biopsy (confirmatory), not a repeat screening Pap smear, is indicated."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"During colposcopy, what concentration of acetic acid is applied on the cervix?","options":["1-2%","3-5%","10-15%","20-25%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Apply Acetic acid on cervix (3-5%).\"  Why A, C, D wrong: The notes specify 3-5% acetic acid, not these concentrations."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"After applying 3-5% acetic acid on the cervix, dysplastic cells appear in which color?","options":["Pink","Brown","White","Yellow"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states dysplastic cells \"appear white in color\" after acetic acid.  Why A wrong: Pink is the color of normal cells.  Why B wrong: Brown corresponds to normal cervix after Lugol's iodine (VILI), not acetic acid.  Why D wrong: Yellow is not described."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"After acetic acid application, normal cervical cells appear in which color?","options":["White","Pink","Black","Unstained (colorless)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Normal cells appear pink in color.\"  Why A wrong: White is the dysplastic (abnormal) cell appearance.  Why C wrong: Black is not described.  Why D wrong: Unstained/colorless describes the VILI (Lugol's iodine) finding for dysplastic cells, not acetic acid for normal cells."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"The whitening of dysplastic cells seen after applying acetic acid on the cervix is termed:","options":["Schiller's positive test","Acetowhite appearance","Chadwick's sign","Goodell's sign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source labels the finding \"Acetowhite appearance.\"  Why A wrong: Schiller's test relates to iodine staining, not acetic acid.  Why C wrong: Chadwick's sign is bluish discoloration in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Goodell's sign is cervical softening in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"After the acetowhite appearance is identified during colposcopy, what is the next step?","options":["Biopsy","Discharge","Immediate chemoradiation","Repeat acetic acid application after 2 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow shows Acetowhite appearance → \"Biopsy.\"  Why B wrong: The suspicious acetowhite area must be biopsied.  Why C wrong: Chemoradiation is for advanced cancer, not the immediate step here.  Why D wrong: The next step is biopsy, not delayed re-application."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"According to the Colposcopy Report management, how is CIN 1 initially managed?","options":["Immediate hysterectomy","Wait for 2 years; if it does not resolve, cryoablation is done","LLETZ immediately","Chemoradiation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for CIN 1 → Wait for 2 years → if it does not resolve → CO2 introduced at very low temperature → CRYOABLATION.  Why A wrong: Hysterectomy is not done for CIN.  Why C wrong: LLETZ is the treatment for CIN 2, 3/HSIL, not first-line CIN 1.  Why D wrong: Chemoradiation is for cancer cervix (later stages)."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In cryoablation for CIN 1 that does not resolve, which gas is introduced at very low temperature?","options":["Oxygen","Nitrogen","CO2 (carbon dioxide)","Helium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"CO2 introduced at very low temperature → CRYOABLATION.\"  Why A, B, D wrong: The notes specify CO2, not oxygen, nitrogen, or helium."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"According to the Colposcopy Report, CIN 2, 3/HSIL is treated by which procedure?","options":["Cryoablation","LLETZ (Large Loop Excision of Transformation Zone)","Radical hysterectomy","Chemoradiation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CIN 2, 3/HSIL → treatment needed → LLETZ (Large Loop Excision of Transformation Zone).  Why A wrong: Cryoablation is used for non-resolving CIN 1.  Why C wrong: Radical hysterectomy is for early-stage cancer cervix.  Why D wrong: Chemoradiation is for later-stage cancer cervix."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"What does the abbreviation LLETZ stand for?","options":["Local Laser Excision of the Transformation Zone","Large Loop Excision of Transformation Zone","Low Level Electrocautery of Transition Zone","Longitudinal Loop Excision of Tumor Zone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source expands LLETZ as \"Large Loop Excision of Transformation Zone.\"  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the correct expansions given in the notes."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Which statement about surgical management of CIN is correct?","options":["Hysterectomy is the treatment of choice for CIN","Hysterectomy is not done for CIN","Radical hysterectomy is required for all CIN grades","Cryoablation is contraindicated in CIN"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source explicitly states \"Hysterectomy is not done for CIN.\"  Why A wrong: Hysterectomy is not the treatment for CIN.  Why C wrong: CIN is treated by LLETZ/cryoablation, not radical hysterectomy.  Why D wrong: Cryoablation is used for non-resolving CIN 1, not contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"For early-stage cancer of the cervix, what is the management according to the notes?","options":["Chemoradiation","Advanced (Radical/Wertheim) Hysterectomy","Cryoablation","LLETZ"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for cancer cervix Early stages → Advanced Hysterectomy (Radical/Wertheim).  Why A wrong: Chemoradiation is for later stages.  Why C wrong: Cryoablation is for non-resolving CIN 1.  Why D wrong: LLETZ is for CIN 2, 3/HSIL."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"The radical hysterectomy used for early-stage cervical cancer is also known by which eponym?","options":["Manchester","Wertheim","Fothergill","Le Fort"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Advanced Hysterectomy (Radical/Wertheim).\"  Why A wrong: Manchester (Fothergill) repair is for prolapse.  Why C wrong: Fothergill relates to Manchester repair, not radical hysterectomy here.  Why D wrong: Le Fort is a colpocleisis for prolapse."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"For later-stage cancer of the cervix, what is the management according to the notes?","options":["Radical hysterectomy","Chemoradiation","Cryoablation","Wait for 2 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cancer cervix Later stages → Chemoradiation.  Why A wrong: Radical hysterectomy is for early stages.  Why C wrong: Cryoablation is for non-resolving CIN 1.  Why D wrong: Waiting is the CIN 1 approach, not later-stage cancer."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Which of the following is TRUE about the LLETZ procedure?","options":["It requires general anesthesia and hospital admission","It is an OPD procedure done under local anesthesia with no admission needed","It is associated with heavy bleeding","It requires ICU monitoring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists LLETZ as an OPD procedure, under local anesthesia, no admission needed.  Why A wrong: It is under local anesthesia with no admission, not GA with admission.  Why C wrong: The notes state \"No bleeding.\"  Why D wrong: ICU monitoring is not required."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Which characteristic is listed for the LLETZ procedure regarding bleeding?","options":["Profuse bleeding","No bleeding","Delayed secondary hemorrhage","Requires blood transfusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source explicitly lists \"No bleeding\" for LLETZ.  Why A, C, D wrong: LLETZ is described as having no bleeding, contradicting these options."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"The LLETZ procedure uses a wire loop that performs which two functions simultaneously?","options":["Cuts and coagulates at the same time","Freezes and cauterizes","Suctions and irrigates","Stains and biopsies"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the wire loop \"cuts & coagulates at same time.\"  Why B wrong: Freezing is cryoablation, not LLETZ.  Why C wrong: Suction/irrigation is not the LLETZ loop function.  Why D wrong: The loop cuts and coagulates; staining is a separate step."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In LLETZ, what tissue is removed by the wire loop?","options":["The whole uterus","The Transformation Zone","Both ovaries","The endometrium only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states LLETZ removes \"the Transformation Zone.\"  Why A wrong: LLETZ does not remove the whole uterus.  Why C wrong: Ovaries are not removed in LLETZ.  Why D wrong: The transformation zone of the cervix, not the endometrium, is removed."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In a rural area where there is no facility for a Pap smear, which screening approach is used for cancer cervix?","options":["VIA and VILI","CT and MRI","Colposcopy and biopsy only","Tumor markers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source shows the rural screening procedure for cancer cervix as VIA and VILI.  Why B wrong: CT/MRI are not the rural screening methods described.  Why C wrong: Colposcopy needs equipment; the rural context specifically lacks Pap facilities and uses VIA/VILI.  Why D wrong: Tumor markers are not the described screening."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"What does VIA stand for in cervical cancer screening?","options":["Visual Inspection with Acetic acid","Visual Inspection of the Anus","Vaginal Iodine Application","Visual Identification of Abnormality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source expands VIA as \"Visual inspection with acetic acid.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the correct expansions given."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"What does VILI stand for in cervical cancer screening?","options":["Visual Inspection with Lugol Iodine","Vaginal Inspection of Lower Introitus","Visual Illumination of the Lower Isthmus","Vaginal Iodine Lavage Inspection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source expands VILI as \"Visual inspection with lugol iodine.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the correct expansions given."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In the VIA rural screening method, what concentration of acetic acid is applied to the examined cervix?","options":["1% acetic acid","3-5% acetic acid","10% acetic acid","Lugol's iodine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Apply 3-5% acetic acid\" under the VIA pathway.  Why A, C wrong: The concentration is 3-5%, not 1% or 10%.  Why D wrong: Lugol's iodine is used in VILI, not VIA."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In VIA (acetic acid) screening, how does a NORMAL cervix appear?","options":["White","Pink / Unstained","Brown","Yellow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in VIA, normal cervix → \"Pink/Unstained.\"  Why A wrong: White indicates dysplastic cells in VIA.  Why B: is correct.  Why C wrong: Brown is the VILI (Lugol's) appearance of normal cervix.  Why D wrong: Yellow is not described."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In VIA (acetic acid) screening, how do dysplastic cells of the cervix appear?","options":["Pink","White","Brown","Unstained"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in VIA, dysplastic cells → \"White.\"  Why A wrong: Pink/unstained is the normal cervix appearance in VIA.  Why C wrong: Brown is the VILI normal appearance.  Why D wrong: Unstained is the VILI dysplastic appearance, not VIA dysplastic."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In VILI (Lugol's iodine) screening, how does a NORMAL cervix appear?","options":["White","Brown","Pink","Unstained"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in VILI, normal cervix → \"Brown.\"  Why A wrong: White is the VIA dysplastic appearance.  Why C wrong: Pink is the VIA normal appearance.  Why D wrong: Unstained is the VILI dysplastic appearance."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In VILI (Lugol's iodine) screening, how do dysplastic cells appear?","options":["Brown","Unstained","Pink","White"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in VILI, dysplastic cells → \"Unstained.\"  Why A wrong: Brown is the VILI normal cervix appearance (glycogen uptake).  Why C wrong: Pink is the VIA normal appearance.  Why D wrong: White is the VIA dysplastic appearance."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In both VIA and VILI, what step is done first before applying the reagent?","options":["Cervix is examined","Biopsy is taken","Hysterectomy","Pap smear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows both VIA and VILI lead to \"Cervix Examined\" before reagent application.  Why B wrong: Biopsy is not the first step in these visual screening methods.  Why C wrong: Hysterectomy is a treatment, not a screening step.  Why D wrong: The rural context specifically lacks Pap smear facilities."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Primary amenorrhea is defined in the notes as no menstruation by which age in the ABSENCE of breast development?","options":["11 years","13 years","15 years","18 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states no menstruation \"by 13 years of age in absence of breast.\"  Why A, D wrong: These ages are not given.  Why C wrong: 15 years is the cut-off when breast development is present (irrespective of breast development), not the absence-of-breast cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Primary amenorrhea is defined as no menstruation by which age IRRESPECTIVE of breast development?","options":["13 years","14 years","15 years","16 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states no menstruation \"by 15 years of age irrespective of breast development.\"  Why A wrong: 13 years is the cut-off in the absence of breast development.  Why B, D wrong: These ages are not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Secondary amenorrhea is defined in the notes as a previously normally menstruating female now having no menstruation for how long?","options":["For 1 month or 2 cycles","For 3 months or for 6 cycles","For 12 months","For 2 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states secondary amenorrhea = no menstruation \"for 3 months or for 6 cycles.\"  Why A, C, D wrong: These durations do not match the source (3 months / 6 cycles)."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"According to the menstruation requirement axis, what is the correct sequence of structures/hormones?","options":["Pituitary → Hypothalamus → Ovary → Uterus","Hypothalamus → Pituitary (FSH, LH) → Ovary (Estrogen, Progesterone) → Uterus","Ovary → Pituitary → Hypothalamus → Uterus","Uterus → Ovary → Pituitary → Hypothalamus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows Hypothalamus → Pituitary → FSH & LH → Ovary → Estrogen & Progesterone → Uterus.  Why A, C, D wrong: These reverse or scramble the correct hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian-uterine axis."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Which hormone does the hypothalamus secrete (in a pulsatile manner) to stimulate the pituitary in the menstruation axis?","options":["FSH","LH","GnRH","Estrogen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The chart labels the hypothalamus-to-pituitary arrow \"GnRH (Pulsatile).\"  Why A, B wrong: FSH and LH are secreted by the pituitary, not the hypothalamus.  Why D wrong: Estrogen is an ovarian hormone."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Which two hormones are secreted by the pituitary in the menstruation axis?","options":["GnRH and Estrogen","FSH and LH","Progesterone and Estrogen","Oxytocin and Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows the pituitary secreting FSH and LH.  Why A wrong: GnRH is hypothalamic; estrogen is ovarian.  Why C wrong: Estrogen and progesterone are ovarian.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin/prolactin are not shown in this menstruation axis."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Which two hormones does the ovary secrete in the menstruation axis, acting on the uterus?","options":["FSH and LH","Estrogen and Progesterone","GnRH and FSH","Oxytocin and Estrogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart shows the ovary secreting Estrogen and Progesterone, which act on the uterus.  Why A wrong: FSH/LH are pituitary hormones.  Why C wrong: GnRH is hypothalamic; FSH is pituitary.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not shown in this ovarian secretion step."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"For normal menstruation to occur, what must be true of the hypothalamus-pituitary-ovary-uterus axis?","options":["Only the ovary needs to be normal","All components should be normal; if there is a problem in any one, the female will not menstruate","Only the uterus needs to be normal","The pituitary can be absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"All should be normal\" and \"If there is a problem in any of them, female will not menstruate.\"  Why A, C wrong: All components (not just ovary or uterus) must be normal.  Why D wrong: A problem in the pituitary would prevent menstruation."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"According to the notes, what is the secondary sexual characteristic in females, and which hormone is needed for its development?","options":["Breast; Estrogen","Pubic hair; Progesterone","Breast; Testosterone","Body hair; LH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the secondary sexual characteristic in female is breast, and estrogen is needed for breast development.  Why B wrong: Breast (not pubic hair) is the stated characteristic; estrogen (not progesterone) drives it.  Why C wrong: Estrogen, not testosterone, is needed for breast development.  Why D wrong: Breast development requires estrogen, not LH."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"According to the notes, in the absence of the Y chromosome:","options":["The individual is called male","The individual is called female and the gonad is the ovary","The gonad becomes a testis","No gonads develop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Y chromosome Absent → Then we call her female\" and \"Gonad = ovary.\"  Why A wrong: Absence of Y makes the individual female, not male.  Why C wrong: The gonad is the ovary, not testis, when Y is absent.  Why D wrong: The ovary develops as the gonad."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"According to the notes, what is required for the proper development of the ovary?","options":["Only one X chromosome","Both X chromosomes are needed","A Y chromosome","An extra X chromosome (three X)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"But for proper development of ovary Both X chromosome needed.\"  Why A wrong: One X chromosome is insufficient (leads to streak gonad, e.g., Turner).  Why C wrong: The Y chromosome is not needed for ovarian development.  Why D wrong: Two (both) X chromosomes are needed, not three."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Why should estrogen never be given alone to a female whose uterus is present?","options":["It can cause osteoporosis","It can lead to endometrial cancer","It causes primary amenorrhea","It leads to anosmia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Never give estrogen alone to a female if her uterus is present → it can lead to Endometrial cancer.\"  Why A wrong: Estrogen protects bone; the stated risk here is endometrial cancer.  Why C wrong: Amenorrhea is not the stated consequence.  Why D wrong: Anosmia relates to Kallman syndrome, not estrogen therapy."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"When hormone therapy is indicated in a female with an intact uterus, what combination should always be given?","options":["Estrogen alone","Progesterone alone","Estrogen and Progesterone","Testosterone and Estrogen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Always give → Estrogen & Progesterone.\"  Why A wrong: Estrogen alone risks endometrial cancer with a uterus present.  Why B wrong: The recommendation is a combination, not progesterone alone.  Why D wrong: The stated combination is estrogen and progesterone, not testosterone."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"What is the chromosome number/karyotype in Kallman Syndrome?","options":["45(XO)","46(XX) = Female","47(XXY)","46(XY)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Kallman Syndrome chromosome number is 46(XX) = Female.  Why A wrong: 45(XO) is Turner Syndrome.  Why C wrong: 47(XXY) is Klinefelter syndrome.  Why D wrong: 46(XY) is a normal male karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In Kallman Syndrome, what is the status of the gonads (ovaries)?","options":["Streak gonads","Ovary = Normal","Absent","Replaced by testes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in Kallman Syndrome \"Gonads = OVARY = Normal.\"  Why A wrong: Streak gonads occur in Turner Syndrome.  Why C wrong: Ovaries are present and normal.  Why D wrong: There are no testes; ovaries are normal."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"What is the primary underlying defect in Kallman Syndrome?","options":["Ovarian failure","Hypothalamus failure (leading to low GnRH)","Uterine agenesis","Pituitary tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source shows the problem starting with \"Hypothalamus failure\" leading to decreased GnRH.  Why A wrong: In Kallman, the ovary is normal; the defect is hypothalamic.  Why C wrong: Uterine agenesis is not the described problem.  Why D wrong: The defect is hypothalamic failure, not a pituitary tumor."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In Kallman Syndrome, the hormonal cascade following hypothalamic failure shows which pattern?","options":["GnRH ↑, FSH ↑, LH ↑, Estrogen ↑","GnRH ↓, FSH ↓, LH ↓, Estrogen ↓","GnRH normal, FSH ↑, LH ↑","Estrogen ↑ with normal GnRH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source shows Hypothalamus failure → GnRH↓ → FSH↓ LH↓ → Estrogen↓.  Why A wrong: All these hormones are decreased, not increased.  Why C, D wrong: GnRH is decreased and estrogen is decreased, not normal/increased."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Because of low estrogen in Kallman Syndrome, what happens to breast development?","options":["Breast develops normally","Breast will not be properly developed (secondary sexual characteristics absent)","Excessive breast development occurs","Breast becomes cancerous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Breast will not be properly developed (secondary sexual characteristics absent)\" due to low estrogen.  Why A wrong: Low estrogen prevents normal breast development.  Why C wrong: Development is impaired, not excessive.  Why D wrong: Cancer is not the described outcome."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In Kallman Syndrome, what is the effect on menstruation?","options":["Heavy menstruation","Primary amenorrhea (no menstruation)","Secondary amenorrhea only","Normal menstruation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source shows Kallman Syndrome leads to \"Primary amenorrhea → No Menstruation.\"  Why A wrong: There is no menstruation, not heavy menstruation.  Why C wrong: It is primary (never menstruated), not secondary amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Menstruation is absent."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"A characteristic distinguishing feature of Kallman Syndrome is:","options":["Webbing of the neck","Anosmia (inability to smell)","Shield-shaped chest","Micrognathia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"These patients have Anosmia (Inability to smell).\"  Why A, C, D wrong: Webbing of neck, shield-shaped chest, and micrognathia are additional features of Turner Syndrome, not the distinguishing feature of Kallman."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"A patient presents with primary amenorrhea, anosmia, absent secondary sexual characteristics (breast absent), and normal height. What is the diagnosis?","options":["Turner Syndrome","Kallman Syndrome","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Asherman syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states this exact combination (Primary Amenorrhea + Anosmia + secondary sexual characteristics absent + normal height) = Kallman Syndrome.  Why A wrong: Turner has short stature and no anosmia.  Why C wrong: PCOS presents with hyperandrogenism/oligomenorrhea, not this picture.  Why D wrong: Asherman is intrauterine adhesions causing secondary amenorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In Kallman Syndrome, what is the patient's height typically?","options":["Short stature","Normal height","Very tall stature","Progressively shortening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in the patient description \"Height is normal.\"  Why A wrong: Short stature is characteristic of Turner Syndrome, not Kallman.  Why C, D wrong: Height is normal, not tall or shortening."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"What is the chromosome number/genotype in Turner Syndrome?","options":["46(XX)","45(XO) = Female","47(XXX)","46(XY)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Turner Syndrome chromosome number/genotype is 45(XO) = Female.  Why A wrong: 46(XX) is a normal female / Kallman karyotype.  Why C wrong: 47(XXX) is triple X, not Turner.  Why D wrong: 46(XY) is normal male."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In Turner Syndrome, what are the gonads?","options":["Testes","Ovary","Absent gonads","Ovotestis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Turner Syndrome Gonads: Ovary.  Why A wrong: Testes are not present.  Why C wrong: Ovary is present (though dysgenetic/streak), not absent.  Why D wrong: Ovotestis is not described."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"What is the basic problem in Turner Syndrome that prevents proper ovarian development?","options":["Both X chromosomes are extra","Only one X chromosome is present","A Y chromosome is present","The uterus is absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the problem is \"Only one X chromosome is present, therefore ovaries are not properly developed.\"  Why A wrong: There is only one X (not extra); it is a single X (XO).  Why C wrong: There is no Y chromosome.  Why D wrong: The described problem is the single X leading to poorly developed ovaries, not uterine absence."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In Turner Syndrome, the poorly developed ovary (due to a single X chromosome) is called:","options":["Polycystic ovary","Streak gonad (Ovarian dysgenesis)","Corpus luteum cyst","Chocolate cyst"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source labels it \"STREAK Gonad (Ovarian Dysgenesis).\"  Why A wrong: Polycystic ovary is PCOS, not Turner.  Why C, D wrong: Corpus luteum cyst and chocolate cyst (endometrioma) are unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In Turner Syndrome, the streak gonad leads to low estrogen, which results in:","options":["Excessive breast growth","No breast development and absent secondary sexual characteristics","Precocious puberty","Normal menstruation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source shows Estrogen↓ → No breast development → Secondary sexual characteristics are absent.  Why A wrong: Breast does not develop with low estrogen.  Why C wrong: There is delayed/absent puberty, not precocious.  Why D wrong: Menstruation is absent (primary amenorrhea)."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In Turner Syndrome, what is the effect on menstruation?","options":["Menorrhagia","No menstruation → Primary Amenorrhea","Secondary amenorrhea after normal menses","Regular cycles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source shows the streak gonad leads to \"No menstruation → Primary Amenorrhea.\"  Why A wrong: There is no menstruation, not menorrhagia.  Why C wrong: It is primary amenorrhea (never menstruated), not secondary.  Why D wrong: Menstruation is absent."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"Which of the following is an additional physical feature of Turner Syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Low set ears","Low posterior hair line","Micrognathia","Webbing of neck","Shield-shaped chest"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all five as additional problems in Turner Syndrome: low ears, low posterior hair line, micrognathia, webbing of neck, and shield-shaped chest.  Why not exclude any: Each option is explicitly listed among the additional problems, so all apply."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"\"Micrognathia,\" listed as an additional problem in Turner Syndrome, refers to:","options":["A large jaw","An abnormally small lower jaw","Widely spaced nipples","A short neck"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Micrognathia means an abnormally small (under-developed) lower jaw, listed among Turner's additional features.  Why A wrong: That is macrognathia, the opposite.  Why C wrong: Widely spaced nipples relate to the shield-shaped chest, a separate feature.  Why D wrong: A short/webbed neck is a separate listed feature."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"A key difference between Kallman Syndrome and Turner Syndrome is that Turner Syndrome patients characteristically have:","options":["Anosmia and normal height","Short stature with features like webbed neck and shield-shaped chest, and a 45(XO) karyotype","A normal 46(XX) karyotype","Normal breast development"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Turner (45 XO) shows streak gonads plus additional features (webbing of neck, shield chest, micrognathia, low ears, low posterior hairline), unlike Kallman.  Why A wrong: Anosmia and normal height are Kallman features, not Turner.  Why C wrong: 46(XX) is the Kallman/normal female karyotype; Turner is 45(XO).  Why D wrong: Turner has no breast development (low estrogen)."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"In both Kallman Syndrome and Turner Syndrome, which clinical presentation is common?","options":["Secondary amenorrhea","Primary amenorrhea with absent secondary sexual characteristics (no breast development)","Normal menstruation with anosmia","Excess estrogen production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Both syndromes show low estrogen → no breast development (secondary sexual characteristics absent) and primary amenorrhea.  Why A wrong: Both present with primary (not secondary) amenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Menstruation is absent in both.  Why D wrong: Both have decreased, not excess, estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 091 · Pap smear reporting, Pap smear as screening test…","question":"According to the notes, \"Primary Amenorrhea\" was (incorrectly in the source) explained as the female having never had \"menopause.\" What is the factually intended meaning?","options":["The female has never had menstruation (menarche)","The female has never ovulated","The female has never had menopause","The female has never conceived"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source wrote \"never had menopause,\" but factually primary amenorrhea means the female has never had menstruation (menarche); this is a source wording error corrected here per fidelity guidelines.  Why B wrong: The definition centers on absent menstruation, not ovulation specifically.  Why C wrong: The printed word \"menopause\" is a mistake; menopause is cessation, not onset.  Why D wrong: It refers to absent menstruation, not conception."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In Turner syndrome, the breasts are described as:","options":["Enlarged with everted nipples","Absent with widely spaced nipples","Normal in development","Tender and swollen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists \"Breast absent - Nipples widely spaced\" as a feature of Turner syndrome.  Why A wrong: Breasts are absent, not enlarged, because ovarian dysgenesis causes estrogen deficiency.  Why C wrong: Breast development is deficient (absent secondary sexual characteristics).  Why D wrong: There is no tenderness/swelling; the finding is absence of breast tissue."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Which cardiac defect is classically associated with Turner syndrome as shown in the diagram?","options":["Ventricular septal defect","Tetralogy of Fallot","Coarctation of aorta","Patent ductus arteriosus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Turner syndrome figure labels \"Coarctation of aorta\" along with \"Congenital heart diseases.\"  Why A wrong: VSD is not the labelled classic Turner defect on this page.  Why B wrong: Tetralogy of Fallot is not listed for Turner syndrome.  Why D wrong: PDA is not the feature shown; coarctation of aorta is specified."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"A patient with Turner syndrome typically presents with which finding regarding feet and legs?","options":["Muscle wasting","Edema of feet & legs","Hyperpigmentation","Varicose veins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page explicitly lists \"Edema of feet & legs\" as a Turner feature (lymphedema).  Why A wrong: Muscle wasting is not a listed feature.  Why C wrong: Hyperpigmentation is not listed for Turner syndrome.  Why D wrong: Varicose veins are not mentioned; edema is the finding."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Regarding height and intelligence in Turner syndrome, which is TRUE?","options":["Height tall, IQ low","Height short, IQ normal","Height normal, IQ low","Height short, IQ high"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists \"Height short\" and \"IQ: Normal\" for Turner syndrome.  Why A wrong: Height is short, not tall.  Why C wrong: Height is short (not normal), though IQ is normal.  Why D wrong: IQ is normal, not high, and height is short."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Which of the following is a labelled physical feature of Turner syndrome in the diagram? (SATA)","options":["Low posterior hairline","Webbing of neck","Cubitus valgus","Long fingers with arachnodactyly","Broad chest with widely spaced nipples"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels low posterior hairline, webbing of neck, cubitus valgus, and broad chest with widely spaced nipples.  Why A/B/C/E correct: All four are explicitly marked on the Turner syndrome figure.  Why D wrong: Arachnodactyly (long fingers) is a Marfan feature, not labelled here."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The \"heart-shaped face\" and \"short stature\" seen in the Turner syndrome figure reflect:","options":["Normal pubertal development","Characteristic phenotypic features of the syndrome","Signs of pregnancy","Features of Cushing syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels \"Short stature\" and \"Heart-shaped face\" as characteristic Turner phenotype features.  Why A wrong: These are abnormal phenotypic features, not normal development.  Why C wrong: They are unrelated to pregnancy.  Why D wrong: These are Turner features, not Cushing syndrome findings."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The ovaries in Turner syndrome are described as:","options":["Polycystic ovaries","Streak ovaries with amenorrhea and infertility","Enlarged ovaries","Normal functioning ovaries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels \"Streak ovaries, amenorrhea, infertility.\"  Why A wrong: Polycystic ovaries are seen in PCOS, not Turner.  Why C wrong: Ovaries are streak (small/underdeveloped), not enlarged.  Why D wrong: Ovaries are dysgenetic and non-functional, causing amenorrhea and infertility."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"A female presents with primary amenorrhea, absent secondary sexual characteristics, and NORMAL height. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":["Turner syndrome","Kallmann syndrome","PCOS","Asherman syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Q1 on the page states: Female with primary amenorrhea + absent secondary sexual characteristics + normal height → Kallmann syndrome.  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome presents with SHORT height, not normal.  Why C wrong: PCOS presents with hyperandrogenism and oligo/secondary amenorrhea, not absent secondary sexual characteristics with primary amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Asherman causes secondary amenorrhea after uterine curettage, not this presentation."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"A female with primary amenorrhea, absent secondary sexual characteristics, and SHORT height is most likely to have:","options":["Kallmann syndrome","MRKH syndrome","Turner syndrome","Sheehan syndrome"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Q2 states: primary amenorrhea + absent secondary sexual characteristics + short height → Turner syndrome.  Why A wrong: Kallmann presents with NORMAL height.  Why B wrong: MRKH has normal breasts/secondary sexual characteristics.  Why D wrong: Sheehan causes secondary amenorrhea postpartum, not this presentation."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The recommended hormonal treatment to develop secondary sexual characteristics in Turner syndrome is:","options":["Estrogen + Progesterone","Testosterone","Thyroxine","Insulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Treatment section states \"Give them estrogen + Progesterone.\"  Why B wrong: Testosterone is an androgen and would not develop female characteristics.  Why C wrong: Thyroxine treats hypothyroidism, not the estrogen deficiency here.  Why D wrong: Insulin treats diabetes, not the ovarian dysgenesis."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"To increase height in Turner syndrome, which hormone is given?","options":["Estrogen","Growth Hormone","Progesterone","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Height increase → Growth Hormone given.\"  Why A wrong: Estrogen is used for secondary sexual characteristics, not primarily for height.  Why C wrong: Progesterone is combined with estrogen for hormone replacement, not for height.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is a lactation hormone, not used to increase height."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The most common cause of primary amenorrhea in a young female is:","options":["PCOS","Turner syndrome (ovarian dysgenesis)","Asherman syndrome","Sheehan syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most common cause of primary amenorrhea in a young female is Turner syndrome (ovarian dysgenesis).  Why A wrong: PCOS causes oligomenorrhea/secondary amenorrhea, not the most common primary amenorrhea.  Why C wrong: Asherman causes secondary amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Sheehan causes secondary amenorrhea postpartum."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Mullerian Agenesis is also known as which syndrome?","options":["Stein-Leventhal syndrome","Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome","Sheehan syndrome","Kallmann syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page equates Mullerian Agenesis with Mayer Rokitansky Kuster Hauser (MRKH) syndrome.  Why A wrong: Stein-Leventhal is the alternate name for PCOS.  Why C wrong: Sheehan is postpartum pituitary necrosis.  Why D wrong: Kallmann is hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"What is the genotype (karyotype) of a female with MRKH syndrome?","options":["45,XO","46,XY","46,XX","47,XXY"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Genotype = 46(XX) in MRKH syndrome.  Why A wrong: 45,XO is Turner syndrome.  Why B wrong: 46,XY is a normal male karyotype (or androgen insensitivity).  Why D wrong: 47,XXY is Klinefelter syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In MRKH syndrome, the gonads (ovaries) are:","options":["Absent","Normal and develop from the genital ridge","Streak ovaries","Polycystic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Gonad = Ovaries (Normal) = develop from Genital Ridge.  Why A wrong: Ovaries are present and normal in MRKH.  Why C wrong: Streak ovaries occur in Turner syndrome, not MRKH.  Why D wrong: Polycystic ovaries occur in PCOS."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Because the ovaries are normal in MRKH syndrome, the estrogen level and secondary sexual characteristics are:","options":["Both decreased","Both normal","Estrogen normal but characteristics absent","Estrogen low but characteristics normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Estrogen (Normal) and Secondary sexual characteristic = Normal in MRKH.  Why A wrong: Both are normal because ovaries function normally.  Why C wrong: Secondary sexual characteristics are normal, not absent.  Why D wrong: Estrogen is normal, not low."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The basic problem in MRKH syndrome is:","options":["Absence of both ovaries","Absence of both Mullerian ducts","Pituitary necrosis","Excess androgen production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Problem: Both mullerian ducts are absent.\"  Why A wrong: Ovaries are present and normal; it is the Mullerian ducts that are absent.  Why C wrong: Pituitary necrosis describes Sheehan syndrome.  Why D wrong: Excess androgen describes PCOS."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Which structures are ABSENT in MRKH syndrome due to absent Mullerian ducts? (SATA)","options":["Both Fallopian tubes","Uterus","Cervix","Ovaries","Lower 1/3 of vagina"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists both Fallopian tubes absent, uterus absent, and cervix absent.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are Mullerian derivatives and are absent.  Why D wrong: Ovaries are normal (develop from genital ridge, not Mullerian ducts).  Why E wrong: Lower 1/3 vagina is normal as it develops from the urogenital sinus."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In MRKH syndrome the upper 2/3 of the vagina is absent, leading to which complaint?","options":["Excessive menstrual bleeding","Difficulty during intercourse","Recurrent urinary infection","Galactorrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Upper 2/3rd vagina absent → Difficulty during intercourse.\"  Why A wrong: There is no menstruation (primary amenorrhea), so no excessive bleeding.  Why C wrong: UTI is not the stated consequence.  Why D wrong: Galactorrhea relates to prolactin, not vaginal agenesis."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In MRKH syndrome, why is the lower 1/3 of the vagina normal?","options":["It develops from the Mullerian ducts","It develops from the urogenital sinus","It develops from the ovary","It develops from the mesonephric duct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page notes the lower 1/3 vagina is normal as it develops from the urogenital sinus.  Why A wrong: The Mullerian ducts (which form upper vagina) are absent.  Why C wrong: The ovary does not form the vagina.  Why D wrong: The mesonephric (Wolffian) duct forms male structures, not the lower vagina here."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"A young female with MRKH syndrome will classically present with primary amenorrhea PLUS which combination?","options":["Absent breasts + normal uterus","Normal breasts + absent uterus + renal anomalies","Enlarged uterus + no breasts","Excess androgens + hirsutism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed \"Patient Will Say\" note lists primary amenorrhea + breast normal + renal anomalies + uterus absent.  Why A wrong: Breasts are normal and uterus is absent (opposite of this option).  Why C wrong: The uterus is absent, not enlarged.  Why D wrong: Hyperandrogenism/hirsutism describes PCOS, not MRKH."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"What is the management of amenorrhea itself in MRKH syndrome?","options":["Hysterectomy","No treatment for the amenorrhea; treat only the intercourse problem","Ovulation induction","Estrogen + Progesterone therapy for menstruation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"No Treatment for amenorrhea in this case but for her intercourse problem.\"  Why A wrong: There is no uterus to remove; hysterectomy is irrelevant.  Why C wrong: Ovulation induction cannot restore menstruation without a uterus.  Why D wrong: Hormones cannot produce menstruation as the uterus is absent."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The surgical management done for the intercourse problem in MRKH syndrome is:","options":["Myomectomy","Vaginoplasty","Salpingectomy","Oophorectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the management is to \"Do vaginoplasty.\"  Why A wrong: Myomectomy removes fibroids, not applicable here.  Why C wrong: Salpingectomy removes tubes, which are already absent.  Why D wrong: Oophorectomy removes ovaries, which are normal and needed."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"According to the notes, the appropriate timing for vaginoplasty in MRKH syndrome is:","options":["In early childhood","Just before or just after marriage","Immediately at birth","After menopause"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states time for vaginoplasty is \"Just before marriage Or Just after marriage.\"  Why A wrong: Childhood is not the stated timing.  Why C wrong: It is not done at birth.  Why D wrong: It is timed around marriage, not menopause."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Asherman syndrome is characterized by which intrauterine finding?","options":["Multiple ovarian cysts","Intrauterine adhesions with thin endometrium","Thick hyperplastic endometrium","Fibroid masses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in Asherman syndrome: inside uterus adhesions are present and endometrium is thin.  Why A wrong: Multiple ovarian cysts occur in PCOS.  Why C wrong: The endometrium is thin, not thick/hyperplastic.  Why D wrong: Fibroids are not the defining feature; adhesions are."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Because of thin endometrium, a patient with Asherman syndrome initially complains of:","options":["Menorrhagia (heavy bleeding)","Decreased blood loss during menstruation (hypomenorrhea)","Intermenstrual bleeding","Postmenopausal bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the patient complains of decreased blood loss during menstruation → hypomenorrhea.  Why A wrong: Bleeding decreases, not increases.  Why C wrong: Intermenstrual bleeding is not the described symptom.  Why D wrong: This occurs in reproductive-age women, not postmenopausal bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In Asherman syndrome, hypomenorrhea may later progress to:","options":["Menorrhagia","Secondary amenorrhea","Primary amenorrhea","Metrorrhagia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Later on Secondary amenorrhea occurs.\"  Why A wrong: Bleeding decreases to absence, not heavier bleeding.  Why C wrong: It is secondary (menses were present before), not primary amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Metrorrhagia (irregular bleeding) is not the progression described."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The MOST COMMON cause of Asherman syndrome is:","options":["Genital tuberculosis","Curettage of the uterus done after delivery","Oral contraceptive use","Ovarian cyst rupture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists \"Most common cause: Curettage of uterus done after delivery.\"  Why A wrong: Genital TB is listed as an \"other cause,\" not the most common.  Why C wrong: OCP use is not a listed cause.  Why D wrong: Ovarian cyst rupture is not a cause of intrauterine adhesions."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are listed as OTHER causes of Asherman syndrome? (SATA)","options":["Excessive curettage done anytime","Genital Tuberculosis","PCOS","Sheehan syndrome","Turner syndrome"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists other causes as excessive curettage done anytime and genital tuberculosis.  Why A/B correct: Both are explicitly listed as other causes.  Why C wrong: PCOS is an endocrine disorder, not a cause of intrauterine adhesions.  Why D wrong: Sheehan is pituitary necrosis, unrelated to uterine adhesions.  Why E wrong: Turner is a genetic ovarian dysgenesis, not a cause of adhesions."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The investigation of choice (IOC) for Asherman syndrome is:","options":["Laparoscopy","Hysteroscopy","Ultrasound of abdomen","Endometrial biopsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"IOC: HYSTEROSCOPY.\"  Why A wrong: Laparoscopy views the pelvis externally, not intrauterine adhesions directly.  Why C wrong: USG is not the stated investigation of choice.  Why D wrong: Endometrial biopsy is not the IOC named here."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The treatment of Asherman syndrome includes hysteroscopic adhesiolysis PLUS which hormones to build the endometrium?","options":["Androgens","Estrogen and Progesterone","Thyroxine","Growth hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states treatment = Hysteroscopic Adhesiolysis + Estrogen and Progesterone (to build the endometrium).  Why A wrong: Androgens would not rebuild endometrium.  Why C wrong: Thyroxine is for thyroid disorders.  Why D wrong: Growth hormone is used for Turner height, not endometrial rebuilding."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"PCOS is also known by which eponymous name?","options":["Stein-Leventhal syndrome","Sheehan syndrome","Meigs syndrome","Asherman syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states PCOS = Polycystic Ovarian Syndrome / Stein-Leventhal Syndrome.  Why B wrong: Sheehan is postpartum pituitary necrosis.  Why C wrong: Meigs is ovarian fibroma with ascites and pleural effusion.  Why D wrong: Asherman is intrauterine adhesions."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The core problem in PCOS is that the ovaries produce excessive:","options":["Estrogen only","Progesterone","Androgens (male hormone)","Prolactin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"In these females ovaries produce excessive Androgens (Male Hormone).\"  Why A wrong: The primary excess is androgens; estrogen rise is secondary (from adipose conversion).  Why B wrong: Progesterone is actually decreased in PCOS.  Why D wrong: Prolactin excess is not the defining PCOS problem."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Excess androgens in PCOS cause male-like hair distribution, a condition called:","options":["Alopecia","Hirsutism","Hypertrichosis","Acanthosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states male-like distribution of hair → Hirsutism (hair on chin, upper lips, chest).  Why A wrong: Alopecia is hair loss, not excess growth.  Why C wrong: The term used on the page is hirsutism (androgen-dependent pattern).  Why D wrong: Acanthosis (nigricans) is a skin pigmentation, not hair growth."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In PCOS, hirsutism causes hair growth in which areas per the notes?","options":["Scalp only","Chin, upper lips, chest","Palms and soles","Eyebrows only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page specifies hirsutism = hair on chin, upper lips, chest.  Why A wrong: Scalp is not listed; scalp typically thins in androgen excess.  Why C wrong: Palms and soles do not grow terminal hair.  Why D wrong: Only chin, upper lips, and chest are specified."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In PCOS, excess androgen is converted in adipose tissue to estrogen, increasing the chances of which cancers? (SATA)","options":["Uterine cancer","Ovarian cancer","Breast cancer","Cervical cancer","Bone cancer"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in adipose tissue ↑estrogen → increased chances of uterine, ovarian, and breast cancer.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed.  Why D wrong: Cervical cancer is not listed (it is HPV-related, not estrogen-driven here).  Why E wrong: Bone cancer is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"PCOS is more common in obese females and can lead in the future to:","options":["Metabolic X syndrome","Turner syndrome","Kallmann syndrome","Osteoporosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states PCOS is more common in obese females and in future \"Metabolic X Syndrome can occur.\"  Why B wrong: Turner is genetic (45,XO), not caused by PCOS.  Why C wrong: Kallmann is a genetic hypogonadotropic disorder.  Why D wrong: Osteoporosis is explicitly the complication NOT seen in PCOS."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In PCOS, excessive androgen leads to which reproductive consequence?","options":["Increased ovulation","Ovulation will not happen (anovulation)","Multiple ovulation","Early menopause"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states due to excessive androgen \"Ovulation will not happen.\"  Why A wrong: Ovulation fails to occur, it does not increase.  Why C wrong: There is anovulation, not multiple ovulation.  Why D wrong: Early menopause is not the described consequence."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Because ovulation does not occur in PCOS, the patient commonly complains of:","options":["Infertility","Heavy menstrual bleeding","Galactorrhea","Deafness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states anovulation leads to \"Complains of Infertility.\"  Why B wrong: Anovulation leads to oligomenorrhea/amenorrhea, not heavy bleeding.  Why C wrong: Galactorrhea is a prolactin-related symptom.  Why D wrong: Deafness is unrelated to PCOS."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In PCOS, absence of ovulation means no corpus luteum forms, which results in:","options":["Increased progesterone","No or decreased progesterone","Increased FSH","Decreased androgen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states no corpus luteum → no progesterone or decreased progesterone.  Why A wrong: Progesterone is decreased because the corpus luteum (its source) does not form.  Why C wrong: FSH is actually decreased in PCOS.  Why D wrong: Androgen is increased, not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The decreased progesterone from anovulation in PCOS results in which menstrual disturbance?","options":["Secondary amenorrhea or oligomenorrhea","Menorrhagia","Primary amenorrhea","Regular cycles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states no progesterone → no menstruation = secondary amenorrhea or oligomenorrhea.  Why B wrong: Bleeding is reduced/absent, not increased.  Why C wrong: It is secondary (menses previously present), not primary amenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Cycles become irregular/absent, not regular."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In PCOS there is insulin resistance, which produces a skin finding called:","options":["Vitiligo","Acanthosis nigricans","Petechiae","Erythema nodosum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states insulin resistance → Acanthosis Nigricans.  Why A wrong: Vitiligo is depigmentation, opposite of acanthosis nigricans.  Why C wrong: Petechiae are bleeding spots, unrelated.  Why D wrong: Erythema nodosum is inflammatory nodules, not related to insulin resistance here."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Acanthosis nigricans presents as darkly pigmented skin in which areas?","options":["Palms and soles","Nape of neck or axilla","Face and forehead","Lower back"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states acanthosis nigricans = darkly pigmented skin in nape of neck or in axilla.  Why A wrong: Palms and soles are not the described sites.  Why C wrong: Face/forehead are not the classic sites listed.  Why D wrong: Lower back is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Insulin resistance in PCOS predisposes the patient in the future to:","options":["Diabetes","Hypothyroidism","Addison disease","Cushing syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in future PCOS patients \"can have Diabetes.\"  Why B wrong: Hypothyroidism is not the stated future risk.  Why C wrong: Addison disease (adrenal insufficiency) is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Cushing syndrome is not the described future complication."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In PCOS, the LH:FSH ratio is characteristically:","options":["1:1","2:1 or 3:1 (LH increased)","1:2","1:3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in PCOS the LH:FSH ratio is 2:1 or 3:1 (LH is increased).  Why A wrong: A 1:1 ratio is normal, not the PCOS pattern.  Why C/D wrong: These are reversed ratios; in PCOS LH is elevated relative to FSH."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Due to excess androgens in PCOS, the lipid profile shows:","options":["LDL decreased, HDL increased","LDL increased, HDL decreased","Both LDL and HDL increased","Both LDL and HDL decreased"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Low Density lipid ↑ and High Density Lipid ↓ due to excess androgens.  Why A wrong: This is the reverse of the actual pattern.  Why C wrong: HDL is decreased, not increased.  Why D wrong: LDL is increased, not decreased."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The abnormal lipid changes in PCOS increase the future risk of:","options":["Heart disease","Peptic ulcer","Asthma","Renal stones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states with LDL↑ and HDL↓, \"Chances of Heart disease in future increases.\"  Why B wrong: Peptic ulcer is unrelated to the lipid changes.  Why C wrong: Asthma is not a consequence of dyslipidemia here.  Why D wrong: Renal stones are not the stated risk."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Which hormones are INCREASED in PCOS? (SATA)","options":["Androgens (Testosterone)","Estrogen","LH","Progesterone","Insulin"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists hormones INCREASED in PCOS as androgens (testosterone), estrogen, LH, LDL, and insulin.  Why A/B/C/E correct: All four are listed among the increased hormones.  Why D wrong: Progesterone is listed among the DECREASED hormones."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Which hormones are DECREASED in PCOS? (SATA)","options":["Progesterone","FSH","HDL","LH","Insulin"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists decreased in PCOS as Progesterone, FSH, and HDL.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are listed as decreased hormones/lipids.  Why D wrong: LH is increased in PCOS.  Why E wrong: Insulin is increased (insulin resistance) in PCOS."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"PCOS is diagnosed using which set of criteria?","options":["Amsel criteria","Rotterdam criteria","Bishop score","Modified Duke criteria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Diagnosis of PCOS: Rotterdam Criteria.\"  Why A wrong: Amsel criteria diagnose bacterial vaginosis.  Why C wrong: Bishop score assesses cervical readiness for labour induction.  Why D wrong: Duke criteria diagnose infective endocarditis."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"According to the Rotterdam criteria described, how many of the three features must be present to diagnose PCOS?","options":["All 3","Any 2","Any 1","At least 4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"If any 2 are present → Then we diagnose PCOS.\"  Why A wrong: Only any 2 of 3 are required, not all 3.  Why C wrong: One feature alone is insufficient.  Why D wrong: There are only 3 criteria; 4 is not possible."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are the three Rotterdam criteria for PCOS? (SATA)","options":["Increased androgen","Menstrual problem (oligomenorrhea/secondary amenorrhea)","USG of ovaries showing multiple cysts","Elevated prolactin","Elevated FSH"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists the three criteria as increased androgen, menstrual problem (oligomenorrhea/secondary amenorrhea), and USG of ovaries = multiple cyst.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are explicitly listed criteria.  Why D wrong: Prolactin elevation is not a Rotterdam criterion.  Why E wrong: FSH is decreased in PCOS and is not a criterion."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"On ultrasound, the multiple cysts of PCOS are arranged around the periphery of the ovary, giving what characteristic appearance?","options":["Snowstorm appearance","Necklace appearance","Ground-glass appearance","Honeycomb appearance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states multiple cysts arranged around periphery of ovary → Necklace appearance.  Why A wrong: Snowstorm appearance is seen in molar pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Ground-glass is a chest/CT term, not the PCOS USG pattern here.  Why D wrong: Honeycomb is not the described appearance."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The first management advice for a patient with PCOS is:","options":["Advise the patient to lose weight","Immediate hysterectomy","Radiotherapy","Bed rest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Management: 1. Advise patient to lose weight.  Why B wrong: Hysterectomy is not indicated for PCOS management.  Why C wrong: Radiotherapy has no role in PCOS.  Why D wrong: Weight loss/lifestyle is advised, not bed rest."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The drug used to treat insulin resistance in PCOS is:","options":["Metformin","Clomiphene citrate","Estrogen","Prolactin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The problem-treatment table lists insulin resistance → Metformin.  Why B wrong: Clomiphene citrate is for ovulation induction (infertility), not insulin resistance.  Why C wrong: Estrogen (OCP) is used for irregular cycles, not primarily insulin resistance.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is a hormone, not a treatment."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"For irregular menstrual cycles and hirsutism in PCOS, the treatment given is:","options":["OCP (Estrogen + Progesterone)","Growth hormone","Metformin","Insulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists irregular cycles → OCP (Estrogen + Progesterone) and hirsutism → OCP.  Why B wrong: Growth hormone is used in Turner syndrome for height.  Why C wrong: Metformin treats insulin resistance, not irregular cycles primarily.  Why D wrong: Insulin is not used to regulate cycles in PCOS."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Infertility in PCOS is due to anovulation; the BEST treatment is an ovulation-inducing drug called:","options":["Metformin","Clomiphene citrate","Bromocriptine","Danazol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states best treatment for infertility (due to anovulation) = ovulation inducing drug Clomiphene Citrate.  Why A wrong: Metformin treats insulin resistance, not the best ovulation inducer here.  Why C wrong: Bromocriptine is for hyperprolactinemia.  Why D wrong: Danazol is used for endometriosis, not ovulation induction."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Clomiphene citrate belongs to which drug class?","options":["SERM (Selective Estrogen Receptor Modulator)","Beta-blocker","Corticosteroid","Aromatase inhibitor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page labels clomiphene citrate as a \"SERM Drug.\"  Why B wrong: It is not a beta-blocker.  Why C wrong: It is not a corticosteroid.  Why D wrong: Aromatase inhibitors (e.g., letrozole) are a different class; clomiphene is a SERM."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"A known side effect of clomiphene citrate used for ovulation induction is:","options":["Twin (multiple) pregnancy","Osteoporosis","Deafness","Cataract"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states SIDE effect of clomiphene citrate = Twin Pregnancy.  Why B wrong: Osteoporosis is not the listed side effect.  Why C wrong: Deafness is not associated.  Why D wrong: Cataract is not the stated side effect."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The most common cause of hirsutism in a young female is:","options":["Turner syndrome","PCOS","Sheehan syndrome","MRKH syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Most common of Hirsutism in young female → PCOS.\"  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome causes absent secondary sexual characteristics, not hirsutism.  Why C wrong: Sheehan causes hormone deficiency, not hirsutism.  Why D wrong: MRKH has normal ovaries/estrogen without androgen excess."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Infertility in PCOS (as shown in the problem table) occurs due to:","options":["Anovulation","Blocked fallopian tubes","Uterine fibroids","Cervical stenosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states infertility in PCOS is \"Due to Anovulation.\"  Why B wrong: Tubal blockage is not the PCOS mechanism described.  Why C wrong: Fibroids are not the cause listed.  Why D wrong: Cervical stenosis is not the mechanism here."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Which of the following are FUTURE complications of PCOS? (SATA)","options":["Ovarian cancer","Breast cancer","Uterine cancer","Osteoporosis","Diabetes"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists future complications as ovarian cancer, breast cancer, uterine cancer, metabolic X syndrome, diabetes, and heart disease.  Why A/B/C/E correct: All four are listed among future PCOS complications.  Why D wrong: Osteoporosis is explicitly the complication NOT seen in PCOS (estrogen is elevated, protecting bone)."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In addition to cancers and diabetes, which are listed as future complications of PCOS? (SATA)","options":["Metabolic X syndrome","Heart disease","Anemia","Deep vein thrombosis"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists metabolic X syndrome and heart disease among the six future complications of PCOS.  Why A/B correct: Both are explicitly listed.  Why C wrong: Anemia is not a listed PCOS complication.  Why D wrong: DVT is not among the listed complications."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"Which complication is specifically NOT seen in PCOS?","options":["Diabetes","Osteoporosis","Uterine cancer","Heart disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Complication not seen in PCOS: OSTEOPOROSIS\" (because estrogen is elevated, which protects bone).  Why A wrong: Diabetes IS a PCOS complication.  Why C wrong: Uterine cancer IS a PCOS complication.  Why D wrong: Heart disease IS a PCOS complication."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In Sheehan syndrome, which gland undergoes necrosis?","options":["Anterior pituitary gland","Posterior pituitary gland","Thyroid gland","Adrenal gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the problem in Sheehan syndrome is that the Anterior Pituitary gland undergoes necrosis.  Why B wrong: It is the anterior, not posterior, pituitary that is affected.  Why C wrong: The thyroid is not the necrotic gland here.  Why D wrong: The adrenal gland is not the primary site in Sheehan."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"The cause (reason) of Sheehan syndrome is:","options":["Excessive blood loss after delivery (postpartum hemorrhage)","Genital tuberculosis","Uterine curettage","Excess androgen production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the reason is due to excessive blood loss after delivery (Post-partum Hemorrhage).  Why B wrong: Genital TB causes Asherman syndrome, not Sheehan.  Why C wrong: Curettage causes Asherman syndrome.  Why D wrong: Excess androgen relates to PCOS."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In Sheehan syndrome, the hormones produced by the anterior pituitary will:","options":["Increase","Decrease","Remain unchanged","Fluctuate randomly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in Sheehan syndrome all hormones produced by the anterior pituitary will decrease.  Why A wrong: Necrosis causes decreased, not increased, hormone output.  Why C wrong: Hormone levels fall due to gland necrosis.  Why D wrong: They decrease consistently, not randomly."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In Sheehan syndrome, decreased LH and FSH lead to which complaint?","options":["Secondary amenorrhea","Primary amenorrhea","Menorrhagia","Precocious puberty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows ↓LH & FSH → complains of secondary amenorrhea.  Why B wrong: It is secondary (the woman has delivered, so menses previously occurred), not primary.  Why C wrong: Bleeding stops (amenorrhea), it does not become heavy.  Why D wrong: Gonadotropin deficiency does not cause precocious puberty."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"In Sheehan syndrome, decreased prolactin leads to:","options":["Galactorrhea","Failure to breast feed the baby","Increased milk production","Early menopause"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows ↓Prolactin → failure to breast feed baby.  Why A wrong: Galactorrhea (excess milk) occurs with HIGH prolactin, opposite of Sheehan.  Why C wrong: Milk production fails because prolactin is decreased.  Why D wrong: Early menopause is not the described consequence of low prolactin."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"A postpartum woman with a history of severe hemorrhage during delivery now cannot breastfeed and has not resumed menses. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":["Sheehan syndrome","PCOS","Asherman syndrome","Turner syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Failure to lactate (↓prolactin) plus secondary amenorrhea (↓LH/FSH) following postpartum hemorrhage is classic Sheehan syndrome (anterior pituitary necrosis).  Why B wrong: PCOS is not caused by postpartum hemorrhage and does not cause lactation failure.  Why C wrong: Asherman causes amenorrhea but not lactation failure/pituitary hormone deficiency.  Why D wrong: Turner is a congenital condition presenting with primary amenorrhea, not postpartum."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"A 16-year-old girl presents with primary amenorrhea, short stature, webbed neck, and widely spaced nipples. The karyotype most likely shows:","options":["45,XO","46,XX","47,XXY","46,XY"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These are classic Turner syndrome features; Turner syndrome has the karyotype 45,XO (although the page focuses on clinical features, this is the standard genotype for the described phenotype).  Why B wrong: 46,XX is normal female / MRKH, which has normal height and normal secondary sexual characteristics.  Why C wrong: 47,XXY is Klinefelter (male) syndrome.  Why D wrong: 46,XY is a male karyotype."},{"section":"Batch 092 · Turner syndrome features & clinical signs, Kallmann syndrome…","question":"A young woman has primary amenorrhea but normal breast development and normal height; imaging shows absent uterus with normal ovaries and a renal anomaly. The diagnosis is:","options":["MRKH syndrome","Turner syndrome","PCOS","Kallmann syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primary amenorrhea with normal breasts, absent uterus, normal ovaries, and renal anomalies matches MRKH (Mullerian agenesis) as described in the \"Patient Will Say\" box.  Why B wrong: Turner has short stature and absent secondary sexual characteristics.  Why C wrong: PCOS presents with hyperandrogenism and oligomenorrhea, and the uterus is present.  Why D wrong: Kallmann has absent secondary sexual characteristics (breasts undeveloped)."}];
